// Generated by tools/ctx_build.py from ctx/batch*/. Reader context for planets, written from their key papers' abstracts. Do not edit.
const CONTEXT = {"Kepler-37 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-37 d. Kepler-37 is known to host three transiting planets, and it became a test case for pulling very small planet signals out of noise from the star itself. Using HARPS-N spectra, one team measured the mass of the outermost planet, Kepler-37 d, whose signal had long been too faint to detect (Rajpaul et al. 2021). The method separates the wobble caused by a planet from changes caused by spots and bright patches on the star's surface (Rajpaul et al. 2021). Keck spectra of the star were also used to measure the abundances of 15 elements (Vaz & Schuler 2014).","system":"The star hosts three transiting planets, and the outermost, Kepler-37 d, has a mass of about 5.4 Earth masses (Rajpaul et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler37c-Rajpauletal2021","cite":"Rajpaul et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"V. M. Rajpaul, L. A. Buchhave, G. Lacedelli et al.","title":"A HARPS-N mass for the elusive Kepler-37d: a case study in disentangling stellar activity and planetary signals","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13900","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler37c-VazSchuler2014","cite":"Vaz & Schuler 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Zachary Vaz, S. C. Schuler","title":"Metallicity Analysis of Planetary Hosts Kepler 37, 62, & 68","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #223","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-305 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No new observations are reported. Instead, computer simulations of the four planets were run over two million years to study how their orbits tug on each other (D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023). The study confirmed that planets b and c are locked in a resonance, a repeating pattern in their orbital periods (D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023). From the planet sizes and masses, the authors judge that planet e is probably rocky, while b and d are mini-Neptunes with thick gas envelopes (D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023).","system":"The K-type star has four transiting planets. Planets b and c are in resonance, and planet c has an inflated atmosphere (D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023).","history":"","open":"The surface temperatures and pressures are estimates built on assumed hydrogen-helium atmospheres (D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler305e-DAngiolilloMacDonald2023","cite":"D'Angiolillo & MacDonald 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Skylar D'Angiolillo, Mariah MacDonald","title":"Exploring Habitability within Exoplanetary System Kepler-305","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-444 d":{"text":{"why":"The discovery work used the star's ringing, measured with asteroseismology, to date it at 11.2 billion years (Campante et al. 2015).","observed":"Kepler observations of this star, poor in heavy elements, show five planets with sizes between Mercury and Venus (Campante et al. 2015). Because the planets orbit so close together, planets d and e tug on each other and shift their transit times, which gives their masses: a few hundredths of an Earth mass each, much less than the mass listed in the NASA Exoplanet Archive (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). Ultraviolet hydrogen light from the system varies; models of the star's spin show it is not active enough to cause this itself, which leaves escaping planetary atmospheres as a possibility (Pezzotti et al. 2021).","system":"The five planets circle the brightest star of a triple system. A pair of red dwarfs orbits it on a very elongated path (eccentricity 0.864) that brings them within about 5 au of the star (Dupuy et al. 2016). The system is stable in this arrangement (Dupuy et al. 2016).","history":"Its age makes it the oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets (Campante et al. 2015). Smooth migration through the disc is unlikely to explain the planets' orbits without later disturbances (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). The companion stars may have trimmed the disc the planets formed in (Dupuy et al. 2016).","open":"Whether the star's core stayed convective for nearly 8 billion years is disputed (Winther et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler444d-Campanteetal2015","cite":"Campante et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"T. L. Campante, T. Barclay, J. J. Swift et al.","title":"KOI-3158: The oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07869","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444d-MillsFabrycky2017","cite":"Mills & Fabrycky 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky","title":"Mass, Density, and Formation Constraints in the Compact, Sub-Earth Kepler-444 System including Two Mars-mass Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03417","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444d-Pezzottietal2021","cite":"Pezzotti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Pezzotti, P. Eggenberger, G. Buldgen et al.","title":"Revisiting Kepler-444. II. Rotational, orbital, and high-energy fluxes evolution of the system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.06061","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444d-Dupuyetal2016","cite":"Dupuy et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Trent J. Dupuy, Kaitlin M. Kratter, Adam L. Kraus et al.","title":"Orbital Architectures of Planet-hosting Binaries. I. Forming Five Small Planets in the Truncated Disk of Kepler-444A","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03428","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444d-Wintheretal2023","cite":"Winther et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mark Lykke Winther, Víctor Aguirre Børsen-Koch, Jakob Lysgaard Rørsted et al.","title":"Did Kepler-444 have a long-lived convective core?","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":18,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"8 UMi b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits a red giant that is burning helium in its core, a star that should already have swallowed so close a planet; it shows that such planets can survive (Hon et al. 2023).","observed":"The search that turned it up watched about 200 bright stars near the north celestial pole (Lee et al. 2015). The team checked that the star's activity could not cause the regular wobble, and attributed it to a giant planet (Lee et al. 2015). The planet sits only 0.5 au from a star that should once have swollen to 0.7 au, and its nearly circular orbit makes a distant starting orbit hard to believe; the discovery team proposed that a merger of two stars spared it, either by changing how the star evolved or by making the planet a second-generation one (Hon et al. 2023). Chen et al. (2024) tested this from the star's motion through the Galaxy and its chemistry. They found an age of about 3.5 billion years, far younger than a merger would need, which argues against the idea.","system":"","history":"","open":"How the planet avoided being swallowed is still unexplained (Chen et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-8umib-Honetal2023","cite":"Hon et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Hon, D. Huber, N. Z. Rui et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: 8 UMi radial velocity curve (Hon+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-8umib-Leeetal2015","cite":"Lee et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park, S.-M. Lee et al.","title":"Search for exoplanet around northern circumpolar stars. Four planets around HD 11755, HD 12648, HD 24064, and 8 Ursae Minoris","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.09012","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-8umib-Chenetal2024","cite":"Chen et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Huiling Chen, Yang Huang, Wei Zhu et al.","title":"The Kinematic and Chemical Properties of the Close-in Planet Host Star 8 UMi","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05120","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD-06 1339 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doubt surrounds this planet. Photometry from the TESS satellite shows brightness changes in the star that match the 3.9-day signal in the radial velocities (Simpson et al. 2022). The authors conclude that star activity, not a planet, probably caused the signal, though they call for more data (Simpson et al. 2022). Earlier work on the same star, listed there as GJ 221, reanalysed archive spectra and found a hot companion with a 3.87-day period (Arriagada et al. 2013).","system":"Papers on the star as GJ 221 report a second companion, with a 125-day period, and a further candidate with a 500-day period (Arriagada et al. 2013; Tuomi 2014).","history":"","open":"The 3.9-day signal may not be a planet at all (Simpson et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd061339b-Simpsonetal2022","cite":"Simpson et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emilie R. Simpson, Tara Fetherolf, Stephen R. Kane et al.","title":"Revisiting BD-06 1339b: A Likely False Positive Caused by Stellar Activity","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06191","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd061339b-Arriagadaetal2013","cite":"Arriagada et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Pamela Arriagada, Guillem Anglada-Escudé, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Two Planetary Companions around the K7 Dwarf GJ 221: A Hot Super-Earth and a Candidate in the Sub-Saturn Desert Range","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2203","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd061339b-Tuomi2014","cite":"Tuomi 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Tuomi","title":"A new cold sub-Saturnian candidate planet orbiting GJ 221.","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.4000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2011-BLG-262L b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first microlensing candidate for a moon orbiting a free-floating planet, though a star with a planet fits the data about as well (Bennett et al. 2014).","observed":"The brightening it caused fits two pictures almost equally well (Bennett et al. 2014). In the one favoured by a statistical comparison, a star in the Galaxy's central bulge has a planet as its companion; the catalogue's values, and the picture here, follow this reading (Bennett et al. 2014). In the other, the system lies much closer to the Sun, and the lens is a free-floating planet of about 4 Jupiter masses with a moon smaller than Earth (Bennett et al. 2014). Which is right depends on how far away the system is, which the data do not settle (Bennett et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"A planet and moon could end up adrift after a close pass by another star (Badoux & Portegies Zwart 2026). Simulations show a hot Jupiter and its moon could also be flung out together (Trani et al. 2019).","open":"Whether the lens is a moon, a planet, or a star in the bulge is not settled (Bennett et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg262lb-Bennettetal2014","cite":"Bennett et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. P. Bennett, V. Batista, I. A. Bond et al.","title":"MOA-2011-BLG-262Lb: A Sub-Earth-Mass Moon Orbiting a Gas Giant Primary or a High Velocity Planetary System in the Galactic Bulge","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3951","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg262lb-BadouxPortegiesZwart2026","cite":"Badoux & Portegies Zwart 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Y. Badoux, S. Portegies Zwart","title":"Planet-moon ejections in close stellar encounters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.16402","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg262lb-Tranietal2019","cite":"Trani et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Alessandro Alberto Trani, Adrian Hamers, Aaron Geller","title":"Can a hot Jupiter host exomoons?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-277 b":{"text":{"why":"It came out of a systematic search of 250 transit-like events in TESS data for hot Neptune candidates (Magliano et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet was one of two systems, with TOI-1288 b, that passed a statistical validation, meaning the chance of a false alarm was under 0.3 per cent (Magliano et al. 2023). This used two vetting tools and follow-up observations (Magliano et al. 2023). It is considered a high-priority target for radial velocity follow-up, to measure its mass directly (Magliano et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet has not yet been confirmed with radial velocity mass measurements (Magliano et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi277b-Maglianoetal2023","cite":"Magliano et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Christian Magliano, Giovanni Covone, Richa Dobal et al.","title":"A systematic validation of hot Neptunes in TESS data","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 20329 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits a star of the thick disk, an ancient part of the Galaxy where stars have less iron and more alpha elements (Martin et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet completes an orbit in under a day, which makes it an ultra-short-period planet (Murgas et al. 2022). These are rare, heavily irradiated, and their formation is unknown (Murgas et al. 2022). TESS light curves and HARPS-N radial velocities gave its size and mass (Murgas et al. 2022). It was first described as a super-Mercury rich in iron, which is the opposite of what a thick-disk star would suggest (Martin et al. 2026). New data are sharpening the density (Martin et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Thick-disk planets might be rich in silicate and poor in iron (Martin et al. 2026).","open":"Whether the planet is rich or poor in iron awaits the improved density (Martin et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd20329b-Martinetal2026","cite":"Martin et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Claire Martin, Emily Pass, Andrew Vanderburg","title":"A revised density for HD 20329b, an ultra-short-period super-Earth orbiting a thick disk star","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20329b-Murgasetal2022","cite":"Murgas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"F. Murgas, G. Nowak, T. Masseron et al.","title":"HD 20329b: An ultra-short-period planet around a solar-type star found by TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02547","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-72 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No new observations are reported. Computer simulations of the four-planet system over 100 million years, including tides, show the planets drift away from low-order resonances (Kuznetsov & Perminov 2026). The planets are not in a chain of resonances, and the system stays stable if the orbital eccentricities stay below 0.03 (Kuznetsov & Perminov 2026).","system":"The compact system is a small M dwarf with four planets, three of them Earth-like (Kuznetsov & Perminov 2024). Planets d and c are far from a 2-to-1 resonance (Kuznetsov & Perminov 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k272c-KuznetsovPerminov2026","cite":"Kuznetsov & Perminov 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. D. Kuznetsov, A. S. Perminov","title":"Dynamic evolution of the compact planetary system K2-72","journal":"Planetary Science and Exoplanets in the Era of JWST","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921324002709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k272c-KuznetsovPerminov2024","cite":"Kuznetsov & Perminov 2024","year":"2024","authors":"E. Kuznetsov, A. Perminov","title":"Investigation of the dynamical evolution of the compact planetary system K2-72","journal":"Modern Astronomy: From the Early Universe to Exoplanets and Black Holes","url":"https://doi.org/10.26119/VAK2024.136","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1633 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A reanalysis of small, long-period Kepler candidates found that false alarms are more common in this regime than once thought, so some confirmed planets no longer meet the 99 per cent confidence level (Burke et al. 2019). Kepler-1633 b is among the single-planet systems that the study names for closer scrutiny (Burke et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether it counts as a statistically confirmed planet is in question (Burke et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1633b-Burkeetal2019","cite":"Burke et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, F. Mullally, Susan E. Thompson et al.","title":"Re-evaluating Small Long-period Confirmed Planets from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.00506","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 536 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HARPS and HARPS-N spectra show the planet in a near-circular orbit (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). A second signal at 43 days was linked to the star's rotation through its activity indicators (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). The team also tentatively found a magnetic cycle of less than 3 years (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). In 2025, a second planet was found with a period of 32.8 days (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2025).","system":"The second planet, GJ 536 c, lies near 0.16 au and has an equilibrium temperature of 290.5 K (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2025).","history":"Tides should circularise close orbits quickly, yet a study of old M dwarfs of about 8 billion years found mildly eccentric close-in planets, and for GJ 536 b it implies tidal behaviour closer to the ice giants (Veyette & Muirhead 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj536b-Mascareoetal2017","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, J. I. González Hernández, R. Rebolo et al.","title":"A super-Earth orbiting the nearby M dwarf GJ 536","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.02122","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj536b-Mascareoetal2025","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, C. del Burgo, J.-B. Delisle et al.","title":"A second low-mass planet orbiting the nearby M-dwarf GJ 536","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj536b-VeyetteMuirhead2018","cite":"Veyette & Muirhead 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Mark J. Veyette, Philip S. Muirhead","title":"Chemo-kinematic Ages of Eccentric-planet-hosting M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-261 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host is a bright, metal-rich star that has begun to swell (Johnson et al. 2018). Near the end of its main-sequence life, the star's age can be measured fairly precisely at about 8.8 billion years (Johnson et al. 2018). Joint K2 and TESS photometry later tightened the timing of its transits, helping plan future JWST and other observations (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2261b-Johnsonetal2018","cite":"Johnson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. C. Johnson, F. Dai, A. B. Justesen et al.","title":"K2-260 b: a hot Jupiter transiting an F star, and K2-261 b: a warm Saturn around a bright G star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06099","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2261b-IkwutUkwaetal2020","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mma Ikwut-Ukwa, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"The K2 and TESS Synergy. I. Updated Ephemerides and Parameters for K2-114, K2-167, K2-237, and K2-261","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00678","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2020-BLG-135L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's mass ratio to its host star, with a separation near the Einstein ring, places it at the break and likely peak of the mass-ratio function from the MOA survey (Ishitani Silva et al. 2022). The microlensing light curve alone does not fix its mass or host. The estimate depends on assumptions about which stars host planets. A uniform assumption gives a host of about 0.23 solar masses, while one favouring heavier hosts gives about 0.53 (Ishitani Silva et al. 2022).","system":"The host may be a low-mass star in the Galactic bulge, about 8 kiloparsecs away (Ishitani Silva et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The planet's mass could be about 11 or 25 Earth masses depending on the assumption (Ishitani Silva et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2020blg135lb-Silvaetal2022","cite":"Silva et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Stela Ishitani Silva, Clément Ranc, David P. Bennett et al.","title":"MOA-2020-BLG-135Lb: A New Neptune-class Planet for the Extended MOA-II Exoplanet Microlens Statistical Analysis","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.03672","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2076 d":{"text":{"why":"It is part of a young system of about 200 million years that shares its motion through space with another young planetary system (Hedges et al. 2021).","observed":"TESS saw only two transits of this planet, separated by a two-year gap, so its period could not be fixed at first (Hedges et al. 2021). CHEOPS and ground-based telescopes later ruled out the alternatives and settled it on a period of about 35.1 days (Osborn et al. 2022). The star shows strong periodic dimming from starspots, typical of young stars (Hedges et al. 2021). All three outer planets are JWST targets (Barber et al. 2025).","system":"Three sub-Neptunes orbit on 10 to 35 day orbits, plus a smaller inner planet found later (Barber et al. 2025). Transit timing variations show they pull on each other (Frazier et al. 2023).","history":"Planet b's orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, which suggests the system formed by migrating together in a flat disc (Frazier et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2076d-Hedgesetal2021","cite":"Hedges et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christina Hedges, Alex Hughes, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2076 and TOI-1807: Two Young, Comoving Planetary Systems within 50 pc Identified by TESS that are Ideal Candidates for Further Follow Up","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01311","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076d-Osbornetal2022","cite":"Osborn et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"H. P. Osborn, A. Bonfanti, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Uncovering the true periods of the young sub-Neptunes orbiting TOI-2076","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03194","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076d-Barberetal2025","cite":"Barber et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Madyson G. Barber, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TESS Investigation—Demographics of Young Exoplanets (TI-DYE). III. An Inner Super-Earth in TOI 2076","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076d-Frazieretal2023","cite":"Frazier et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Robert C. Frazier, Gudmundur Stefánsson, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"NEID Reveals That the Young Warm Neptune TOI-2076 b Has a Low Obliquity","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06266","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 48948 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The detection rests on 189 HARPS-N velocity measurements collected over nearly ten years (Dalal et al. 2024). Star activity could mimic planets, so the team modelled it with Gaussian processes that used activity indicators (Dalal et al. 2024). The authors treat all three planets as candidates (Dalal et al. 2024). This outer one lies in the temperate habitable zone, a distance where liquid water could exist, and its projected separation from the star is 0.029 arcseconds (Dalal et al. 2024).","system":"Three super-Earth candidates orbit with periods of 7.3, 38, and 151 days (Dalal et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"All three planets remain candidates, and only a minimum mass is known (Dalal et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd48948d-Dalaletal2024","cite":"Dalal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Dalal, F. Rescigno, M. Cretignier et al.","title":"Trio of super-Earth candidates orbiting K-dwarf HD 48948: a new habitable zone candidate","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stae1367","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 d":{"text":{"why":"A citizen science project, Planet Hunters, gave an independent characterisation and added another candidate to the system (Schmitt et al. 2014).","observed":"The star has eight transiting planets, the most known (Shaw et al. 2025). A neural network helped find the eighth planet (Shallue & Vanderburg 2018). Planet Hunters volunteers added a seventh candidate before that (Schmitt et al. 2014). The system is compact, with every planet inside about 1 AU, and it resembles our own, with small inner planets and gas giants outside (Schmitt et al. 2014).","system":"The inner five planets range from Earth to mini-Neptune size, and the outer ones are gas giants (Schmitt et al. 2014). The outer pair is near a 2:3 resonance but not locked in it, and the arrangement is stable for billions of years (Liang et al. 2021). One of the outer planets has an unusually low density, making it a super-puff (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"Planet interactions may have broken the resonant lock after formation (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351d-Schmittetal2014","cite":"Schmitt et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Joseph R. Schmitt, Ji Wang, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"Planet Hunters. VI. An Independent Characterization of KOI-351 and Several Long Period Planet Candidates from the Kepler Archival Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5912","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi351d-Shawetal2025","cite":"Shaw et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"David E. Shaw, Lauren M. Weiss, Eric Agol et al.","title":"Updated Masses for the Gas Giants in the Eight-planet Kepler-90 System Via Transit-timing Variation and Radial Velocity Observations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.13588","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi351d-ShallueVanderburg2018","cite":"Shallue & Vanderburg 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Christopher J. Shallue, Andrew Vanderburg","title":"Identifying Exoplanets with Deep Learning: A Five-planet Resonant Chain around Kepler-80 and an Eighth Planet around Kepler-90","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.05044","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi351d-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":20,"about":20,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-52 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Measured masses for Kepler-52 b, Kepler-52 c, and Kepler-57 b imply densities higher than iron planets of the same size (Mocquet et al. 2013). The authors propose that these could be the bare solid cores of gas giants that lost their atmospheres, for instance while migrating toward the star (Mocquet et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"The planet may have once been a gas giant (Mocquet et al. 2013).","open":"The idea of a dense rocky core is only a proposal, and it is unclear whether such a core could stay so dense (Mocquet et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler52b-Mocquetetal2013","cite":"Mocquet et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"A. Mocquet, O. Grasset, C. Sotin","title":"Super-dense remnants of gas giant exoplanets","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 108236 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 108236 is the brightest Sun-like star in visible light known to host four or more transiting planets (Daylan et al. 2021). The innermost planet is a possibly rocky super-Earth, and the outer three are sub-Neptunes with possible gas envelopes (Daylan et al. 2021). The CHEOPS space telescope then caught a fifth planet by chance in one of its light curves, later confirmed in a 29.54-day orbit (Bonfanti et al. 2021; Hoyer et al. 2022). The bright star suits mass measurements and atmosphere studies (Daylan et al. 2021).","system":"Five small planets, all under 3 Earth radii, orbit this Sun-like star (Hoyer et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd108236b-Daylanetal2021","cite":"Daylan et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Tansu Daylan, Kartik Pinglé, Jasmine Wright et al.","title":"TESS Discovery of a Super-Earth and Three Sub-Neptunes Hosted by the Bright, Sun-like Star HD 108236","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.11314","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd108236b-Bonfantietal2021","cite":"Bonfanti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Bonfanti, L. Delrez, M. J. Hooton et al.","title":"CHEOPS observations of the HD 108236 planetary system: a fifth planet, improved ephemerides, and planetary radii","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.00663","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd108236b-Hoyeretal2022","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Hoyer, A. Bonfanti, A. Leleu et al.","title":"Characterization of the HD 108236 system with CHEOPS and TESS Confirmation of a fifth transiting planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08912","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 97233 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up monitored 166 bright giant stars in the southern sky to see how a star's evolution changes its planets (Jones et al. 2015).","observed":"Radial velocity measurements of the giant star show a repeating signal with a period of 1058.8 days (Jones et al. 2015). The authors describe the companion as a highly eccentric brown dwarf, an object between a planet and a star in mass (Jones et al. 2015). Planets around giant stars often differ in their orbits and properties from those around less evolved stars (Jones et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip97233b-Jonesetal2015","cite":"Jones et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. I. Jones, J. S. Jenkins, P. Rojo et al.","title":"A planetary system and a highly eccentric brown dwarf around the giant stars HIP 67851 and HIP 97233","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.7429","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 98736 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, N2K, targeted metal-rich stars, since these are more likely to host gas giants (Ment et al. 2018).","observed":"Keck HIRES radial velocities of 378 target stars were collected between 2004 and 2017 (Ment et al. 2018). They show a cold gas giant around HD 98736, with a minimum mass of 2.33 Jupiter masses (Ment et al. 2018). Across the new planets, orbits are between 1.0 and 6.2 au, with eccentricities from 0.13 to 0.71 (Ment et al. 2018). The authors highlight candidates for direct imaging and astrometry (Ment et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd98736b-Mentetal2018","cite":"Ment et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Kristo Ment, Debra A. Fischer, Gaspar Bakos et al.","title":"Radial Velocities from the N2K Project: Six New Cold Gas Giant Planets Orbiting HD 55696, HD 98736, HD 148164, HD 203473, and HD 211810","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01228","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-198 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a young K dwarf aged between 200 and 500 million years, and X-ray measurements from eROSITA gave the radiation the planets receive (Ketzer et al. 2024). Models suggest that the innermost planet, K2-198 c, lost its primordial gas envelope early (Ketzer et al. 2024). The outermost planet, K2-198 b, should stay stable against evaporation for a wide range of masses (Ketzer et al. 2024).","system":"Three transiting planets orbit the young K dwarf (Ketzer et al. 2024).","history":"Young stars are very active and strip atmospheres from nearby planets (Ketzer et al. 2024).","open":"The planets' masses are not yet measured, and these limit how much of an envelope remains (Ketzer et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2198b-Ketzeretal2024","cite":"Ketzer et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"L. Ketzer, K. Poppenhaeger, M. Baratella et al.","title":"Three young planets around the K-dwarf K2-198: high-energy environment, evaporation history, and expected future","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06897","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 2126 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found in 112 archived velocity measurements of its star taken with the HARPS spectrograph (Schorr et al. 2025). Its orbit is extremely stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.85, where 0 is a circle (Schorr et al. 2025). That puts it among the most eccentric planets known (Schorr et al. 2025). The host is a low-mass star (Schorr et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The authors say such an extreme orbit helps test theories of how planets form and evolve (Schorr et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj2126b-Schorretal2025","cite":"Schorr et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Schorr, A. Binnenfeld, S. Zucker","title":"GJ 2126 b: A highly eccentric Jovian exoplanet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.11139","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-107 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is unusually puffy for its mass. Models suggest a hydrogen and helium envelope of more than 85% of its mass, with a core under 4.6 Earth masses (Piaulet et al. 2021). Its mass sits between Neptune and Saturn (Anderson et al. 2017).","observed":"WASP-107 b is one of the most studied puffy planets. Its atmosphere holds water, and a thick layer of cloud or haze high up mutes the spectral features (Kreidberg et al. 2018). The study also hints at less methane than expected (Kreidberg et al. 2018). Helium is escaping from the planet, seen as extra absorption of 5.5% to 7.3% of the starlight during transits (Allart et al. 2019; Kirk et al. 2020). The signal did not change over two years (Kirk et al. 2020).","system":"Long Keck velocity monitoring revealed a second, more massive planet on a wide, eccentric orbit (Piaulet et al. 2021). The star turns once in about 17 days, and its spin axis is strongly tilted relative to the planet's orbit, between 40 and 140 degrees (Dai & Winn 2017).","history":"The authors argue the planet began gathering gas beyond about 1 au, in a dust-free, low-opacity atmosphere, then moved inward (Piaulet et al. 2021).","open":"The tilted orbit is hard to explain with some theories of how stellar tilts arise (Dai & Winn 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-Piauletetal2021","cite":"Piaulet et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Caroline Piaulet, Björn Benneke, Ryan A. Rubenzahl et al.","title":"WASP-107b's Density Is Even Lower: A Case Study for the Physics of Planetary Gas Envelope Accretion and Orbital Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13444","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-Andersonetal2017","cite":"Anderson et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, L. Delrez et al.","title":"The discoveries of WASP-91b, WASP-105b and WASP-107b: Two warm Jupiters and a planet in the transition region between ice giants and gas giants","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03776","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-Kreidbergetal2018","cite":"Kreidberg et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Laura Kreidberg, Michael R. Line, Daniel Thorngren et al.","title":"Water, High-altitude Condensates, and Possible Methane Depletion in the Atmosphere of the Warm Super-Neptune WASP-107b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.08635","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-Allartetal2019","cite":"Allart et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"R. Allart, V. Bourrier, C. Lovis et al.","title":"High-resolution confirmation of an extended helium atmosphere around WASP-107b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08073","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-Kirketal2020","cite":"Kirk et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"James Kirk, Munazza K. Alam, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"Confirmation of WASP-107b's Extended Helium Atmosphere with Keck II/NIRSPEC","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.07667","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107b-DaiWinn2017","cite":"Dai & Winn 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Fei Dai, Joshua N. Winn","title":"The Oblique Orbit of WASP-107b from K2 Photometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.04734","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":101,"about":46,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 504 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is cooler than earlier imaged planets, at about 510 K, with a bluer colour (Kuzuhara et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet is among the coldest ever photographed directly. Methane absorption was detected in it (Janson et al. 2013), and its spectrum fits an object with a surface temperature near 544 K and more heavy elements than the Sun (Skemer et al. 2016). JWST later gave the first direct spectrum, showing water, carbon monoxide, methane, carbon dioxide, ammonia and hydrogen sulfide, with clouds and possible metal enrichment (Baburaj et al. 2026).","system":"The planet lies at a projected 43.5 au from a Sun-like star, farther out than the roughly 30 au limit predicted for core accretion (Kuzuhara et al. 2013).","history":"Its wide orbit challenges the idea that planets build up from solid cores close to their stars (Kuzuhara et al. 2013).","open":"Its nature is debated because the star's age is uncertain. A young age of 160 million years gives a planet, while an age near 4 billion years would make it a brown dwarf (D'Orazi et al. 2017). Its mass is quoted as 3 to 35 Jupiter masses (Bonnefoy et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj504b-Kuzuharaetal2013","cite":"Kuzuhara et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Kuzuhara, M. Tamura, T. Kudo et al.","title":"Direct Imaging of a Cold Jovian Exoplanet in Orbit around the Sun-like Star GJ 504","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.2886","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj504b-Jansonetal2013","cite":"Janson et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Markus Janson, Timothy D. Brandt, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"Direct Imaging Detection of Methane in the Atmosphere of GJ 504 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.4183","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj504b-Skemeretal2016","cite":"Skemer et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Andrew J. Skemer, Caroline V. Morley, Neil T. Zimmerman et al.","title":"The LEECH Exoplanet Imaging Survey: Characterization of the Coldest Directly Imaged Exoplanet, GJ 504 b, and Evidence for Superstellar Metallicity","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.09183","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj504b-Baburajetal2026","cite":"Baburaj et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Aneesh Baburaj, Jean-Baptiste Ruffio, Marshall Perrin et al.","title":"JWST-TST High Contrast: First Direct Spectroscopy of GJ 504 b Reveals Clouds and Possible Metal Enrichment","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6919","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj504b-DOrazietal2017","cite":"D'Orazi et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"V. D'Orazi, S. Desidera, R. G. Gratton et al.","title":"A critical reassessment of the fundamental properties of GJ 504: chemical composition and age","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02530","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj504b-Bonnefoyetal2018","cite":"Bonnefoy et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. Bonnefoy, K. Perraut, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"The GJ 504 system revisited. Combining interferometric, radial velocity, and high contrast imaging data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.00657","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":42,"about":15,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-18 b became a leading target for atmosphere studies. Hubble detected water vapour and likely water clouds in its hydrogen-rich atmosphere (Benneke et al. 2019). The planet receives about as much starlight as Earth does, yet its thick hydrogen envelope means it is not a true Earth twin (Benneke et al. 2019). JWST later detected methane and carbon dioxide; one modelling study finds these fit a gas-rich mini-Neptune with no habitable surface better than an ocean world (Wogan et al. 2024). Methane and ammonia were found depleted in earlier data, pointing to chemical disequilibrium (Madhusudhan et al. 2020).","system":"A second, warm planet orbits closer in, at about nine days, on a non-coplanar orbit (Cloutier et al. 2017). A third transit seen by Spitzer confirmed the 33-day planet (Benneke et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The interior is debated. It might be an ocean world under hydrogen, a gas-rich mini-Neptune, or a world with a magma ocean (Wogan et al. 2024; Shorttle et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k218b-Bennekeetal2019","cite":"Benneke et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Björn Benneke, Ian Wong, Caroline Piaulet et al.","title":"Water Vapor and Clouds on the Habitable-zone Sub-Neptune Exoplanet K2-18b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04642","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218b-Woganetal2024","cite":"Wogan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Nicholas F. Wogan, Natasha E. Batalha, Kevin J. Zahnle et al.","title":"JWST Observations of K2-18b Can Be Explained by a Gas-rich Mini-Neptune with No Habitable Surface","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.11082","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218b-Madhusudhanetal2020","cite":"Madhusudhan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Nikku Madhusudhan, Matthew C. Nixon, Luis Welbanks et al.","title":"The Interior and Atmosphere of the Habitable-zone Exoplanet K2-18b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.11115","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218b-Cloutieretal2017","cite":"Cloutier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"R. Cloutier, N. Astudillo-Defru, R. Doyon et al.","title":"Characterization of the K2-18 multi-planetary system with HARPS. A habitable zone super-Earth and discovery of a second, warm super-Earth on a non-coplanar orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04292","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218b-Bennekeetal2017","cite":"Benneke et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Björn Benneke, Michael Werner, Erik Petigura et al.","title":"Spitzer Observations Confirm and Rescue the Habitable-zone Super-Earth K2-18b for Future Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07249","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218b-Shorttleetal2024","cite":"Shorttle et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Oliver Shorttle, Sean Jordan, Harrison Nicholls et al.","title":"Distinguishing Oceans of Water from Magma on Mini-Neptune K2-18b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05864","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":154,"about":82,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-46 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is photospherically and chromospherically active, and its brightness varies as it spins (Anderson et al. 2011). The planet's dayside glow was detected at near-infrared wavelengths, giving brightness temperatures of about 2300 to 2460 K (Chen et al. 2014). That is much hotter than the dayside-average equilibrium temperature, which points to very poor heat sharing with the night side (Chen et al. 2014). Repeated transit timing found no sign of orbital decay or of a second planet (Petrucci et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its measured size has varied between studies, and a possible trend of radius with wavelength needs more data (Ciceri et al. 2016; Moyano et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp46b-Andersonetal2011","cite":"Anderson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-44b, WASP-45b and WASP-46b: three short-period, transiting extrasolar planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3179","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp46b-Chenetal2014","cite":"Chen et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"G. Chen, R. van Boekel, H. Wang et al.","title":"Observed spectral energy distribution of the thermal emission from the dayside of <ASTROBJ>WASP-46b</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.7048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp46b-Petruccietal2018","cite":"Petrucci et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Petrucci, E. Jofré, L. V. Ferrero et al.","title":"A search for transit timing variations and orbital decay in WASP-46b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.04707","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp46b-Cicerietal2016","cite":"Ciceri et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. Ciceri, L. Mancini, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Physical properties of the planetary systems WASP-45 and WASP-46 from simultaneous multiband photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05171","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp46b-Moyanoetal2017","cite":"Moyano et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Moyano, L. A. Almeida, C. von Essen et al.","title":"Multi-band characterization of the hot Jupiters: WASP-5b, WASP-44b and WASP-46b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.05700","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-160 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-160 c shows substantial transit timing variations, meaning its transits arrive earlier or later than a clock would predict (Heller et al. 2020). These shifts were used to infer a third planet that does not transit (Heller et al. 2020). A search of the archive also found a new transit candidate on a 378-day orbit with Earth-like starlight, at 1.9 Earth radii (Heller et al. 2020). Radio searches from 1 to 8 GHz found no artificial signals (Perez et al. 2020).","system":"The star hosts two known transiting planets, b and c, plus the suspected extra planets (Heller et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The 378-day candidate is not confirmed, with a vetting reliability of about 85% against instrumental artifacts (Heller et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler160c-Helleretal2020","cite":"Heller et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"René Heller, Michael Hippke, Jantje Freudenthal et al.","title":"Transit least-squares survey. III. A 1.9 R<SUB>⊕</SUB> transit candidate in the habitable zone of Kepler-160 and a nontransiting planet characterized by transit-timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.02123","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler160c-Perezetal2020","cite":"Perez et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Karen Perez, Bryan Brzycki, Vishal Gajjar et al.","title":"Breakthrough Listen Search for Technosignatures toward the Kepler-160 System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13789","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2384 b":{"text":{"why":"Short-period giant planets are significantly rarer around stars less massive than the Sun (Bryant et al. 2024).","observed":"The host is an early M dwarf, a low-mass star (Bryant et al. 2024). Its planet is a Jupiter-sized world with about twice Jupiter's mass (Bryant et al. 2024). It has the third highest planet-to-star mass ratio of any transiting planet around a low-mass star (Bryant et al. 2024).","system":"A sister planet, TOI-2379 b, was announced in the same paper around a different low-mass star (Bryant et al. 2024).","history":"The authors call these planets key pieces for understanding the extremes of giant planet formation (Bryant et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2384b-Bryantetal2024","cite":"Bryant et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"TOI-2379 b and TOI-2384 b: two super-Jupiter mass planets transiting low-mass host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1803 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1803 has two mini-Neptunes near a 2:1 orbital resonance, with periods of about 6.3 and 12.9 days (Zingales et al. 2025). Follow-up photometry from the ground revealed significant transit timing variations (Zingales et al. 2025). Masses came from HARPS-N velocities, with a statistical model separating planet signals from stellar activity (Zingales et al. 2025). Simulated JWST observations were made for the less dense planet under different atmosphere assumptions (Zingales et al. 2025).","system":"The planets sit close to, but not exactly in, resonance. The host is a K0 star below 1 billion years old (Zingales & Malavolta 2025).","history":"The authors ran formation and evolution simulations to explain the orbital arrangement (Zingales et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1803c-Zingalesetal2025","cite":"Zingales et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"T. Zingales, L. Malavolta, L. Borsato et al.","title":"A joint effort to discover and characterize two resonant mini-Neptunes around TOI-1803 with TESS, HARPS-N, and CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05423","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1803c-ZingalesMalavolta2025","cite":"Zingales & Malavolta 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Tiziano Zingales, Luca Malavolta","title":"A joint effort to discover two resonant mini Neptunes around TOI-1803 with CHEOPS, HARPS-N and TESS","journal":"EAS2024, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-186 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most work on this planet looks at the whole system. Tidal models show the four inner planets must have small tilts of their spin axes and near-synchronous rotation, so one face stays toward the star (Bolmont et al. 2014). The star is an early M dwarf with slightly sub-solar metals (Souto et al. 2017).","system":"Five planets orbit this star with periods of 4 to 130 days (Bolmont et al. 2014). The outer planet f sits in the habitable zone (Bolmont et al. 2014).","history":"Simulations reproduce the planet masses if they grew from a dense disc shaped by earlier migration (Bolmont et al. 2014).","open":"Simulations of the system's tides and orbits predict one to two undetected planets between planets e and f (Bolmont et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler186e-Bolmontetal2014","cite":"Bolmont et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Emeline Bolmont, Sean N. Raymond, Philip von Paris et al.","title":"Formation, Tidal Evolution, and Habitability of the Kepler-186 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.4368","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler186e-Soutoetal2017","cite":"Souto et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Souto, K. Cunha, D. A. García-Hernández et al.","title":"Chemical Abundances of M-dwarfs from the APOGEE Survey. I. The Exoplanet Hosting Stars Kepler-138 and Kepler-186","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01598","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7041 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an oscillating red giant, and its pulsations let astronomers measure its mass and radius very precisely (Saunders et al. 2025). The star is about 4.1 times the Sun's radius, one of the largest stars with a TESS transiting planet (Saunders et al. 2025). The planet appears less inflated than similar planets receiving the same starlight (Saunders et al. 2025). Its nearly circular orbit means it is not being heated by tides (Saunders et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7041b-Saundersetal2025","cite":"Saunders et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Nicholas Saunders, Samuel K. Grunblatt, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"TESS Giants Transiting Giants. VII. A Hot Saturn Orbiting an Oscillating Red Giant Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11037","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 14067 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is an evolved giant, 2.4 times the Sun's mass (Wang et al. 2014). Two orbit fits work equally well, one with a 1455-day period and a slow velocity drift, and one with a 2850-day period (Wang et al. 2014). The orbit's eccentricity is among the highest seen around evolved stars (Wang et al. 2014). Imaging ruled out companions above 30 to 60 Jupiter masses at 1 to 7 arcseconds (Ryu et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"Distant companions could have stretched the orbit through the Kozai mechanism, a slow tug that raises eccentricity (Ryu et al. 2016).","open":"The true period and mass depend on which orbit model is right (Wang et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd14067b-Wangetal2014","cite":"Wang et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Liang Wang, Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"A long-period eccentric substellar companion to the evolved intermediate-mass star HD 14067","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.6081","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd14067b-Ryuetal2016","cite":"Ryu et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Tsuguru Ryu, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"High-contrast Imaging of Intermediate-mass Giants with Long-term Radial Velocity Trends","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-49 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an old G6 star, and the planet is slightly bloated (Lendl et al. 2012). High-resolution spectra resolved the sodium lines in its atmosphere, showing hot neutral sodium high up, at about 1.5 planet radii or 45,000 km (Wyttenbach et al. 2017). The atmosphere is free of cloud at the heights probed by sodium (Wyttenbach et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp49b-Lendletal2012","cite":"Lendl et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Lendl, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-42 b and WASP-49 b: two new transiting sub-Jupiters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2757","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp49b-Wyttenbachetal2017","cite":"Wyttenbach et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Wyttenbach, C. Lovis, D. Ehrenreich et al.","title":"Hot Exoplanet Atmospheres Resolved with Transit Spectroscopy (HEARTS). I. Detection of hot neutral sodium at high altitudes on WASP-49b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":33,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1235 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet sits in the radius gap, the range of sizes where planets switch from rocky to having thick envelopes (Cloutier et al. 2020). Its density is slightly above Earth's, consistent with a rocky Earth-like composition (Bluhm et al. 2020). Helium searches found no extended atmosphere (Krishnamurthy et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Models of atmosphere loss and of rocky planet formation in gas-poor discs predict different places for this transition, so the planet is a test case (Cloutier et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1235b-Cloutieretal2020","cite":"Cloutier et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ryan Cloutier, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Jonathan Irwin et al.","title":"TOI-1235 b: A Keystone Super-Earth for Testing Radius Valley Emergence Models around Early M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06682","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1235b-Bluhmetal2020","cite":"Bluhm et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Bluhm, R. Luque, N. Espinoza et al.","title":"Precise mass and radius of a transiting super-Earth planet orbiting the M dwarf TOI-1235: a planet in the radius gap?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06218","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1235b-Krishnamurthyetal2023","cite":"Krishnamurthy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vigneshwaran Krishnamurthy, Teruyuki Hirano, IRD Team","title":"NIR observation of TOI-1235b, GJ 9827b and GJ 9827d: Absence of extended atmosphere in the radius-gap planets","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-183 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is puffy for its mass, close to the upper edge of the mass-radius range of known planets (Turner et al. 2019). Its separation from its star falls in a transition zone between systems with well-aligned orbits and those with more scattered alignment (Turner et al. 2019). A later census found its host belongs to the Galactic thick disk, an old population of stars (Ferreira et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp183b-Turneretal2019","cite":"Turner et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Oliver D. Turner, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"Three hot-Jupiters on the upper edge of the mass-radius distribution: WASP-177, WASP-181, and WASP-183","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06622","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp183b-Ferreiraetal2026","cite":"Ferreira et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Thiago Ferreira, Jhon Yana Galarza, Henrique Reggiani et al.","title":"Giant Planets and Eccentric Orbits are Common around Galactic Thick Disk Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.17072","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2011-BLG-293L b":{"text":{"why":"The survey was a test of whether microlensing surveys alone can detect planets (Yee et al. 2012).","observed":"Keck adaptive optics later detected the host star's light, a G dwarf, and settled its mass at about 0.86 solar masses (Batista et al. 2014). That made the planet about 4.8 Jupiter masses, roughly 1.1 au from its star (Batista et al. 2014). Earlier estimates had suggested a super-Jupiter around a faint M dwarf (Batista et al. 2014).","system":"The planet lies in or near the habitable zone and could in principle have habitable moons (Batista et al. 2014). It is the first planet placed in the Galactic bulge with good confidence, 7.7 kpc away (Batista et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"A symmetry in the data leaves two possible orbit sizes, about 1.0 or 3.4 au in the discovery data (Yee et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg293lb-Yeeetal2012","cite":"Yee et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"J. C. Yee, Y. Shvartzvald, A. Gal-Yam et al.","title":"MOA-2011-BLG-293Lb: A Test of Pure Survey Microlensing Planet Detections","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.1002","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg293lb-Batistaetal2014","cite":"Batista et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"V. Batista, J.-P. Beaulieu, A. Gould et al.","title":"MOA-2011-BLG-293Lb: First Microlensing Planet Possibly in the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.3706","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1268 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a young K dwarf, between 120 and 670 million years old (Dong et al. 2022). The planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a projected tilt below 60 degrees (Dong et al. 2022). Helium absorption shows the atmosphere is escaping at about 10^10.2 grams per second (Pérez-González et al. 2024). That rate would last far longer than 10 billion years, so photoevaporation looks weak (Pérez-González et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The young age and wide star separation make it unlikely that the planet realigned its star, so the alignment may be primordial (Dong et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1268b-Dongetal2022","cite":"Dong et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jiayin Dong, Chelsea X. Huang, George Zhou et al.","title":"NEID Rossiter-McLaughlin Measurement of TOI-1268b: A Young Warm Saturn Aligned with Its Cool Host Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12836","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1268b-PrezGonzlezetal2024","cite":"Pérez-González et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jorge Pérez-González, Michael Greklek-McKeon, Shreyas Vissapragada et al.","title":"Detection of an Atmospheric Outflow from the Young Hot Saturn TOI-1268b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-266 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Timing of repeated transits gave planet d a mass of 6.0 Earth masses, which places it among volatile-rich planets (Jiang et al. 2025). Simulations show the system is stable (Jiang et al. 2025).","system":"The system holds several sub-Neptunes around a K star, including an ultra-short-period planet misaligned by more than 12 degrees from the others (Rodriguez et al. 2018). Planets d and e lie close to but not in a 4:3 resonance (Jiang et al. 2025). A co-moving companion star hosts its own transiting candidate (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2266d-Jiangetal2025","cite":"Jiang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ing-Guey Jiang, Li-Chin Yeh, Billy Edwards et al.","title":"Toward High-precision Mass Measurements of Two Sub-Neptunes in the K2-266 Planetary System Through Transit Timing","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.08905","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2266d-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Juliette Becker, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"K2-266: A Compact Multi-Planet System With A Significantly Misaligned Ultra Short Period Planet","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-16 is slightly active, and its brightness varies with a 12-day spin period (Ciceri et al. 2016). That is unexpectedly fast for a star about 10 billion years old (Ciceri et al. 2016). The planet is a very massive hot Jupiter (Ciceri et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"The fast spin might come from tidal interaction between the star and the planet (Ciceri et al. 2016).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats16b-Cicerietal2016","cite":"Ciceri et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. Ciceri, L. Mancini, T. Henning et al.","title":"HATS-15b and HATS-16b: Two Massive Planets Transiting Old G Dwarf Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 20794 b":{"text":{"why":"The search targeted quiet Sun-like stars, aiming at very low-mass planets in the habitable zone (Pepe et al. 2011).","observed":"The star was monitored by HARPS with three nightly observations to average out stellar pulsation and granulation noise (Pepe et al. 2011). A later analysis of over 20 years of data, joined with ESPRESSO, confirmed three planets, at 18.3, 89.7 and 647.6 days (Nari et al. 2025). The 18-day planet has a mass of about 2.15 Earth masses (Nari et al. 2025).","system":"The star is a nearby metal-poor G dwarf, about 9 billion years old, with a compact multi-planet system (Medhi et al. 2026). A 147-day and a 330-day signal were also proposed and need confirmation (Feng et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The system's number of planets has been debated in the literature (Nari et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd20794b-Pepeetal2011","cite":"Pepe et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Pepe, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Very low-mass planets around <ASTROBJ>HD 20794</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 85512</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 192310</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3447","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794b-Narietal2025","cite":"Nari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Nari, X. Dumusque, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"Revisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794: Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17092","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794b-Medhietal2026","cite":"Medhi et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mrinmay Medhi, Mami Deka, Krishna Saha et al.","title":"A theoretical study of the structure and elemental abundances of HD 20794","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794b-Fengetal2017","cite":"Feng et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"F. Feng, M. Tuomi, H. R. A. Jones","title":"Evidence for at least three planet candidates orbiting HD 20794","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.05124","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":14,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V1298 Tau e":{"text":{"why":"The star is a young Sun-like star, so its planets can be studied while they are still changing (David et al. 2019).","observed":"Planet e was seen to cross its star only once in the K2 satellite's light record, so its orbit was at first poorly known: the period had to be longer than 36 days but probably shorter than 223 days (David et al. 2019). It is 8.7 Earth radii in size, between Neptune and Saturn (David et al. 2019). X-ray observations of the host star with Chandra and ROSAT show it is X-ray bright, which matters for how much gas the planets may lose (Poppenhaeger et al. 2021). A full transit of planet c was also watched with the Gemini North telescope for signs of escaping gas, but the signal could be explained by starspots (Feinstein et al. 2021).","system":"The star is only about 23 million years old and hosts four transiting planets, all between the sizes of Neptune and Saturn (David et al. 2019). Planets c and d lie just outside a 3:2 orbital resonance, meaning they nearly complete three orbits for every two of the other (David et al. 2019).","history":"The system may be an early version of the compact multi-planet systems that Kepler found to be common, though its planets are much larger than those typical systems (David et al. 2019).","open":"Radial-velocity masses are hard to get because the young star is so variable, and one earlier mass estimate was judged to be overfitted and unreliable (Blunt et al. 2023). A newer long HARPS-N campaign found that the masses depend on the analysis choices (Damasso et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v1298taue-Davidetal2019","cite":"David et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Trevor J. David, Erik A. Petigura, Rodrigo Luger et al.","title":"Four Newborn Planets Transiting the Young Solar Analog V1298 Tau","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.04563","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taue-Poppenhaegeretal2021","cite":"Poppenhaeger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"K. Poppenhaeger, L. Ketzer, M. Mallonn","title":"X-ray irradiation and evaporation of the four young planets around V1298 Tau","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.10240","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taue-Feinsteinetal2021","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Benjamin T. Montet, Marshall C. Johnson et al.","title":"H-alpha and Ca II Infrared Triplet Variations During a Transit of the 23 Myr Planet V1298 Tau c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01213","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taue-Bluntetal2023","cite":"Blunt et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Sarah Blunt, Adolfo Carvalho, Trevor J. David et al.","title":"Overfitting Affects the Reliability of Radial Velocity Mass Estimates of the V1298 Tau Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08145","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taue-Damassoetal2026","cite":"Damasso et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. Damasso, P. Leonardi, L. Borsato et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXIX. New mass constraints for the infant planets in the V1298 Tau system through a long-term radial-velocity monitoring with HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":47,"about":46,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 29399 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an evolved star, and the discovery team studied it in depth using vibrations of the star seen by the TESS satellite (Pezzotti et al. 2022). A later study confirmed from these vibrations that the star is on the red giant branch, burning hydrogen in a shell around its core (Lin et al. 2024). An earlier survey had seen a strong repeating velocity signal in this star and judged it to be stellar variability rather than a planet (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"One study modelled how the orbit would have changed as the host star evolved, under dynamical and tidal effects (Pezzotti et al. 2022).","open":"The planet's signal was once attributed to the star itself, so the separation of stellar and planetary signals was a point of debate (Wittenmyer et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd29399b-Pezzottietal2022","cite":"Pezzotti et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"C. Pezzotti, G. Ottoni, G. Buldgen et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES). III. A new Jupiter host-star: in-depth analysis of HD 29399 using TESS data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01553","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd29399b-Linetal2024","cite":"Lin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Wen-Xu Lin, Sheng-Bang Qian, Li-Ying Zhu","title":"Revealing the Fate of Exoplanet Systems: Asteroseismic Identification of Host Star in the Red Clump or Red Giant Branch","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04947","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd29399b-Wittenmyeretal2017","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, M. I. Jones, Jinglin Zhao et al.","title":"The Pan-Pacific Planet Search. VI. Giant Planets Orbiting HD 86950 and HD 222076","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07648","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1238 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first flagged as a candidate by the TESS satellite, which watched the star in four sectors. The team then took 55 radial-velocity measurements with the CARMENES spectrograph over 11 months to confirm it and measure its mass (González-Álvarez et al. 2022). They also monitored the star's brightness from the ground to understand its activity (González-Álvarez et al. 2022). The star hosts two super-Earth-sized planets (González-Álvarez et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1238c-Gonzlezlvarezetal2022","cite":"González-Álvarez et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. González-Álvarez, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, J. Sanz-Forcada et al.","title":"A multi-planetary system orbiting the early-M dwarf TOI-1238","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.14602","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2095 c":{"text":{"why":"The planets were checked as part of the TESS aim to find small planets around stars bright enough for follow-up (Murgas et al. 2023).","observed":"Two independent teams confirmed the planet. One combined TESS photometry with CARMENES radial velocities and placed only an upper limit on the mass of planet c, below 7.4 Earth masses (Murgas et al. 2023). The other used 24 sectors of TESS data and described the planets as sitting in the Venus zone, with insolation of 1.73 times Earth's for the outer planet (Quintana et al. 2023). The two studies differ a little on temperature: roughly 300 to 350 K (Murgas et al. 2023) versus about 400 K (Quintana et al. 2023).","system":"The star is a nearby M dwarf, and the pair of planets have similar sizes (Murgas et al. 2023). The outer planet is on a 28-day orbit, near the inner edge of the star's habitable zone (Murgas et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The planets lie in the size range where compositions switch from rock-dominated to rich in volatile materials, so measured masses would help show which they are (Quintana et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2095c-Murgasetal2023","cite":"Murgas et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Murgas, A. Castro-González, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Two super-Earths at the edge of the habitable zone of the nearby M dwarf TOI-2095","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09220","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2095c-Quintanaetal2023","cite":"Quintana et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, Emily A. Gilbert, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Two Warm Super-Earths Transiting the Nearby M Dwarf TOI-2095","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09189","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-180 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed by detecting its Doppler shadow, a distortion of the star's spectrum as the planet crosses it, and by measuring its mass with radial velocities (Temple et al. 2019). That shadow shows the planet circles its star backwards, in a misaligned, retrograde orbit (Temple et al. 2019). Later helium observations at Palomar found strong signs of atmospheric escape from the planet, which is being stripped by its F-type star (Saidel et al. 2026).","system":"The host star is the brighter member of a visual binary. The companion star is about 5 arcseconds away, roughly 1200 au, and supplies about 30 per cent of the light (Temple et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp180ab-Templeetal2019","cite":"Temple et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Y. Temple, C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-180Ab: Doppler tomography of a hot Jupiter orbiting the primary star in a visual binary","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08002","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp180ab-Saideletal2026","cite":"Saidel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Morgan Saidel, Shreyas Vissapragada, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"The First Dedicated Survey of Atmospheric Escape from Planets Orbiting F Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05240","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-952 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of 11 long-period giant planet candidates from transit surveys that were followed up with years of Doppler spectroscopy. The measurements only placed an upper limit on its mass (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass is still not measured, only bounded from above (Dalba et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler952b-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 70849 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a late K dwarf and has a long period, between 5 and 75 years in the discovery paper (Ségransan et al. 2011). Later work combining radial velocities with absolute astrometry (the star's motion on the sky) found a mass in the planetary range (Philipot et al. 2023). Imaging searches with the SPHERE instrument found no companion but set upper mass limits (Zurlo et al. 2018).","system":"A cool T-type brown dwarf was found at 6.3 arcminutes (about 9000 au) from the star, and the star is described as hosting a planet of about 9 Jupiter masses on an eccentric orbit (Lodieu et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"The planet's true mass depended on the orbit's tilt, which radial velocities alone cannot give (Philipot et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip70849b-Sgransanetal2011","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. Ségransan, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIX. Four new planets in orbit around the moderatly active dwarfs <ASTROBJ>HD 63765</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 104067</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 125595</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HIP 70849</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.0339","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip70849b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, P. Rubini et al.","title":"Updated characterization of long-period single companion by combining radial velocity, relative astrometry, and absolute astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01263","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip70849b-Zurloetal2018","cite":"Zurlo et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. Zurlo, D. Mesa, S. Desidera et al.","title":"Imaging radial velocity planets with SPHERE","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01324","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip70849b-Lodieuetal2014","cite":"Lodieu et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"N. Lodieu, A. Pérez-Garrido, V. J. S. Béjar et al.","title":"Binary frequency of planet-host stars at wide separations. A new brown dwarf companion to a planet-host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.1208","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-1 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS photometry of the system was searched for transits, and a possible shallow signal appeared at 3.285 days that does not match any of the known planets (Jones et al. 2020). The signal could come from a small, catastrophically disintegrating planet that has too little mass to show up in radial velocities (Jones et al. 2020). The known planets themselves were not seen to transit, with limits below 100 ppm (Jones et al. 2020).","system":"The star hosts hot super-Earths and a warm Neptune, and the system was found because of circumstellar gas thought to come from the ablation of hot planets (Jones et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"More observations are needed to confirm the extra transit signal (Jones et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp1c-Jonesetal2020","cite":"Jones et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mark H. Jones, Carole A. Haswell, John R. Barnes et al.","title":"A Possible Transit of a Disintegrating Exoplanet in the Nearby Multiplanet System DMPP-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.07086","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-35 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind it was designed to measure how often stars heavier than the Sun host close-in giant planets (Sebastian et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was followed up with radial velocities alongside two other objects from the CoRoT survey, which looked at 17 promising transit candidates around stars 1.3 to 3.2 times the Sun's mass (Sebastian et al. 2022). The team describes the planet as inflated, meaning larger than its mass would normally give (Sebastian et al. 2022).","system":"CoRoT-35 b may be part of a planetary system around a metal-poor star (Sebastian et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot35b-Sebastianetal2022","cite":"Sebastian et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"D. Sebastian, E. W. Guenther, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"Sub-stellar companions of intermediate-mass stars with CoRoT: CoRoT-34b, CoRoT-35b, and CoRoT-36b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08742","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-22 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three transits in Kepler data gave a planet radius of 2.38 Earth radii around a Sun-like star, in a 290-day orbit in the habitable zone (Borucki et al. 2012). Sixteen radial velocities from Keck were not enough to pin down a mass, but they set an upper limit of 124 Earth masses (Borucki et al. 2012). Climate models of the planet explored an Earth-like atmosphere and an ocean planet with half its mass in water (Neubauer et al. 2012). Other studies used orbit simulations to check whether moons could be stable around it (Fuse & Bokorney 2015).","system":"The star's mass and radius were pinned down using its vibrations seen in the Kepler data (Borucki et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"Its composition is unknown, and the climate models show that surface temperature depends strongly on the atmosphere, clouds and surface reflectivity (Neubauer et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler22b-Boruckietal2012","cite":"Borucki et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"William J. Borucki, David G. Koch, Natalie Batalha et al.","title":"Kepler-22b: A 2.4 Earth-radius Planet in the Habitable Zone of a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1640","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler22b-Neubaueretal2012","cite":"Neubauer et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"David Neubauer, Aron Vrtala, Johannes J. Leitner et al.","title":"The life supporting zone of Kepler-22b and the Kepler planetary candidates: KOI268.01, KOI701.03, KOI854.01 and KOI1026.01","journal":"Planetary and Space Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pss.2012.07.020","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler22b-FuseBokorney2015","cite":"Fuse & Bokorney 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Christopher R. Fuse, Jake Bokorney","title":"The Properties of Exomoons Around the Habitable Zone Planet, Kepler 22b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-19 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet does not transit. Its presence was inferred from shifts in the transit times of Kepler-19 b, which a second planet's gravity pulls early or late (Ballard et al. 2011). Later work combined transit timing with 91 radial velocities from HARPS-N and found a 28.7-day orbit and a mass of 13.1 Earth masses (Malavolta et al. 2017).","system":"The velocity data also revealed a third planet, a Neptune-like world of 20.3 Earth masses on a 63-day orbit (Malavolta et al. 2017). The innermost planet has a thick gas envelope (Malavolta et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler19c-Ballardetal2011","cite":"Ballard et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Sarah Ballard, Daniel Fabrycky, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"The Kepler-19 System: A Transiting 2.2 R <SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet and a Second Planet Detected via Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.1561","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler19c-Malavoltaetal2017","cite":"Malavolta et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Luca Malavolta, Luca Borsato, Valentina Granata et al.","title":"The Kepler-19 System: A Thick-envelope Super-Earth with Two Neptune-mass Companions Characterized Using Radial Velocities and Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06885","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-165 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with three other hot planets from the WASP survey, and the study calls it a classical hot Jupiter around a metal-rich star, with a mass of 0.66 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.26 Jupiter radii (Lendl et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp165b-Lendletal2019","cite":"Lendl et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Lendl, D. R. Anderson, A. Bonfanti et al.","title":"WASP-147b, 160Bb, 164b, and 165b: two hot Saturns and two Jupiters, including two planets with metal-rich hosts","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06973","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2094 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was validated by comparing transit depths in several colours, which reveals false alarms and allows quick vetting of habitable-zone candidates before costly radial-velocity work (Jiang et al. 2026).","observed":"Light curves from TESS and ground telescopes, including HiPERCAM at the 10.4-m Gran Telescopio Canarias, were fitted together to show the transit looks the same at different wavelengths, as a real planet's would (Jiang et al. 2026). The planet orbits an M3 dwarf and receives about 0.98 times Earth's sunlight, well inside the habitable zone (Jiang et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2094b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Chengzi Jiang, Aleksandra Selezneva, Hannu Parviainen et al.","title":"Multicolour validation of two temperate mini-Neptunes around M-dwarf habitable zones","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06817","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11964 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A re-analysis of the radial-velocity data using a Bayesian method found evidence for three planets rather than two, with periods of about 38, 360 and 1924 days (Gregory 2007). The three-planet model was over 600 times more probable than the next best model (Gregory 2007). The 360-day period is within 1.3 sigma of one year, which hints at a possible error in the correction for Earth's motion (Gregory 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the extra signals are real planets is not settled (Gregory 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11964b-Gregory2007","cite":"Gregory 2007","year":"2007","authors":"P. C. Gregory","title":"A Bayesian periodogram finds evidence for three planets in HD 11964","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.0970","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-6 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a metal-poor star, and the planet gets almost the same incident light as HD 209458 b, so the two can be compared at different metallicity (Collins et al. 2014).","observed":"The initial transit signal came from the KELT-North survey, and the planet was confirmed with follow-up photometry, imaging and spectroscopy. It is a mildly inflated Saturn-mass planet (Collins et al. 2014). Its orbit is prograde and slightly misaligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of -36 degrees (Damasso et al. 2015). Its mass and low density are known (Collins et al. 2014).","system":"A long-period companion was found with a minimum mass of 3.71 Jupiter masses on an orbit of about 3.5 years (Damasso et al. 2015). The system is described as interesting for studying how multi-planet systems form and evolve (Damasso et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt6b-Collinsetal2014","cite":"Collins et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Karen A. Collins, Jason D. Eastman, Thomas G. Beatty et al.","title":"KELT-6b: A P ~ 7.9 Day Hot Saturn Transiting a Metal-poor Star with a Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt6b-Damassoetal2015","cite":"Damasso et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Damasso, M. Esposito, V. Nascimbeni et al.","title":"The GAPS programme with HARPS-N at TNG. IX. The multi-planet system KELT-6: Detection of the planet KELT-6 c and measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect for KELT-6 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.06520","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 222237 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a promising target for direct imaging with the JWST MIRI instrument (Xiao et al. 2024). A JWST proposal aims to image it as a cold planet that could be the coolest ever imaged (Balmer et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"It would fill a gap between imaged planets and cold brown dwarfs, and imaging could constrain water-ice clouds and ammonia (Balmer et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd222237b-Xiaoetal2024","cite":"Xiao et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Guang-Yao Xiao, Fabo Feng, Stephen A. Shectman et al.","title":"HD 222237 b: a long-period super-Jupiter around a nearby star revealed by radial-velocity and Hipparcos-Gaia astrometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08067","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd222237b-Balmeretal2025","cite":"Balmer et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"William Balmer, Daniella Carolina Bardalez Gagliuffi, Evelyn Bruinsma et al.","title":"Direct Detection and Characterization of a Nearby Temperate Giant Planet","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 724 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found with 153 CARMENES spectra plus archival HARPS data, and its orbit is eccentric, with e = 0.577 (Gorrini et al. 2023). That makes it the most eccentric single planet known around an M dwarf at the time of the study (Gorrini et al. 2023).","system":"The planet orbits very close to its star, at less than 0.05 au (Gorrini et al. 2023).","history":"The team discusses a high-eccentricity tidal migration as a way to explain its orbit (Gorrini et al. 2023).","open":"An earlier conference report asked whether the signal could be two planets in a 2:1 resonance rather than one eccentric planet (Gorrini & Dreizler 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj724b-Gorrinietal2023","cite":"Gorrini et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"P. Gorrini, J. Kemmer, S. Dreizler et al.","title":"Planetary companions orbiting the M dwarfs GJ 724 and GJ 3988. A CARMENES and IRD collaboration","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05599","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj724b-GorriniDreizler2023","cite":"Gorrini & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paula Gorrini, Stefan Dreizler","title":"Planetary system around GJ 724: A planet in an eccentric orbit or two planets in 2:1 resonance?","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LTT 1445 A c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host star had to be identified out of three M dwarfs that sit on one TESS pixel, which needed a dedicated Hubble proposal (Winters et al. 2022). Its radius is uncertain because the light curve allows both grazing and non-grazing transits, with an 85 per cent probability that the transit is grazing (Winters et al. 2022; Lavie et al. 2023). JWST secondary eclipse measurements found a brightness temperature of 625 K, consistent with a dark bare rock, though the planet's eclipse depth is poorly constrained (Ih et al. 2026).","system":"The system is a triple M-dwarf star system 6.9 pc away. The host star hosts a second transiting planet and a third, non-transiting candidate on a 24-day orbit (Lavie et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The radius of planet c is still not well known (Lavie et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ltt1445ac-Wintersetal2022","cite":"Winters et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jennifer G. Winters, Ryan Cloutier, Amber A. Medina et al.","title":"A Second Planet Transiting LTT 1445A and a Determination of the Masses of Both Worlds","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14737","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt1445ac-Lavieetal2023","cite":"Lavie et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"B. Lavie, F. Bouchy, C. Lovis et al.","title":"Planetary system around LTT 1445A unveiled by ESPRESSO: Multiple planets in a triple M-dwarf system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09713","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt1445ac-Ihetal2026","cite":"Ih et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jegug Ih, Shubham Kanodia, Nicholas Wogan et al.","title":"JWST Rocky Worlds DDT Observations of LTT 1445 A b and c in Secondary Eclipse Using MIRI/F1500W","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17300","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6034 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is part of the Searching for GEMS survey, which targets giant planets around M dwarfs, which are rare because of the low-mass hosts (Kanodia et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was measured using radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and MAROON-X, along with ground photometry and high-contrast imaging (Kanodia et al. 2024). Its mass was precisely measured (Kanodia et al. 2024).","system":"The star has a late F-type companion about 40 arcseconds away, making it the first GEMS host star with an earlier main-sequence binary companion (Clark 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6034b-Kanodiaetal2024","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Arvind F. Gupta, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Characterizing Six Giant Planets Around Cool Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14694","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi6034b-Clark2024","cite":"Clark 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Catherine Clark","title":"Big Planets around Small Stars: Masses and Upper Limits for Six Giant Planets","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.RHF4LF","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-69 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was the smallest found by Kepler in or near the habitable zone of a Sun-like star at the time (Barclay et al. 2013). With an Earth-like reflectivity it has an equilibrium temperature of 299 K (Barclay et al. 2013). A follow-up study found that it is unlikely to lie in the habitable zone and receives 1.91 times the sunlight Earth does, similar to Venus. The authors suggest it is a super-Venus rather than a super-Earth (Kane et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has a Venus-like or Earth-like atmosphere is not known, and follow-up observations were proposed to tell them apart (Kane et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler69c-Barclayetal2013","cite":"Barclay et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Thomas Barclay, Christopher J. Burke, Steve B. Howell et al.","title":"A Super-Earth-sized Planet Orbiting in or Near the Habitable Zone around a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4941","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler69c-Kaneetal2013","cite":"Kane et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Thomas Barclay, Dawn M. Gelino","title":"A Potential Super-Venus in the Kepler-69 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2933","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-68 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with follow-up transit photometry, radial velocities from three spectrographs and speckle imaging (Lindor et al. 2021). It circles a K dwarf on a circular orbit (Lindor et al. 2021). The star sits outside the field of view of both TESS and K2 (Lindor et al. 2021). The deep transit makes the planet a promising target for atmospheric study (Lindor et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp68b-Lindoretal2021","cite":"Lindor et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Bethlee M. Lindor, Joel D. Hartman, Gáspár Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-68b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter around a K5 Dwarf Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.16026","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-129 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Six years of Keck radial velocities revealed a massive outer planet, Kepler-129 d, at about 8.3 Jupiter masses with a 7.2-year orbit, and gave planet c a mass of about 43 Earth masses (Zhang et al. 2021). Analysis of the star's vibrations gave tentative evidence that both inner planets are misaligned with the star's spin axis by at least 38 degrees (Zhang et al. 2021).","system":"Kepler-129 is a hierarchical system with two tightly packed inner planets and a massive outer companion (Zhang et al. 2021). The outer planet makes the inner orbits tilt back and forth over time (Zhang et al. 2021).","history":"The outer giant may have torqued the inner planets out of line with the star's spin, if it is inclined by at least 19 degrees to them (Zhang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler129c-Zhangetal2021","cite":"Zhang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jingwen Zhang, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"Long-period Jovian Tilts the Orbits of Two sub-Neptunes Relative to Stellar Spin Axis in Kepler-129","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03446","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 96992 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a survey of 122 stars of about one solar mass, from the main sequence to the red giant branch. The survey aims to relate stellar metallicity and luminosity to how often planets occur (Niedzielski et al. 2021).","observed":"The star was watched for up to 14 years with two high-resolution spectrographs, one on the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and one on the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo (Niedzielski et al. 2021). The tiny back-and-forth motion of the star reveals a companion with a minimum mass of 1.14 Jupiter masses. It circles at 1.24 au on a stretched orbit with an eccentricity of 0.41 (Niedzielski et al. 2021). The true mass is unknown because the tilt of the orbit is not measured.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd96992b-Niedzielskietal2021","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Niedzielski, E. Villaver, M. Adamów et al.","title":"Tracking Advanced Planetary Systems (TAPAS) with HARPS-N. VII. Elder suns with low-mass companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.10410","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-970 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet and its star belong to a young group of stars called MELANGE-3 in the Kepler field. The group has an age of 105 million years, found from the stars' spin rates, lithium levels and colours (Barber et al. 2022). Knowing the age lets the authors revise the star's and planet's properties. They find a planet of 2.8 Earth radii on a 16.73-day orbit (Barber et al. 2022). Such an age is rare for a known planet, so it shows a planet early in its life.","system":"The young group of stars it belongs to also hosts a second Kepler planetary system, KOI-3876 (Barber et al. 2022).","history":"The group may be part of a larger stream of stars called Theia 316 (Barber et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler970b-Barberetal2022","cite":"Barber et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Madyson G. Barber, Andrew W. Mann, Jonathan L. Bush et al.","title":"Transit Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). VIII. A Pleiades-age Association Harboring Two Transiting Planetary Systems from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08383","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-110 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Two transits were observed with the FORS2 instrument on the Very Large Telescope. They give a spectrum of how much starlight the planet's atmosphere blocks at 46 colours from 400 to 833 nanometres (Nikolov et al. 2021). The planet looks larger at redder wavelengths. The sodium and potassium absorption lines expected for a clear atmosphere are missing (Nikolov et al. 2021). The best explanation is a cloud layer at the planet's day-night edge, together with bright patches (faculae) on the star that were not covered by the planet (Nikolov et al. 2021). Clear and hazy atmospheres are rejected with high confidence (Nikolov et al. 2021).","system":"A close A-type star seen nearby in the sky was fully resolved and does not affect the measured transit depths (Nikolov et al. 2021). The host star shows low to moderate activity (Nikolov et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"A study of seven very similar planets finds a tentative link between a host star's metal content and how much aerosol (cloud or haze) its planet has. WASP-110 b is one of three planets with a measured spectrum used for this (McGruder et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp110b-Nikolovetal2021","cite":"Nikolov et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nikolay Nikolov, Gracjan Maciejewski, Savvas Constantinou et al.","title":"Ground-based Transmission Spectroscopy with VLT FORS2: Evidence for Faculae and Clouds in the Optical Spectrum of the Warm Saturn WASP-110b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06522","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp110b-McGruderetal2023","cite":"McGruder et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Chima D. McGruder, Mercedes López-Morales, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Similar Seven: A Set of Very Alike Exoplanets to Test Correlations between System Parameters and Atmospheric Properties","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04174","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 125612 d":{"text":{"why":"The planets of this star were found in a HARPS survey of a volume-limited sample of nearby stars, which supports the view that low-mass planets are mostly found in multiple systems (Lo Curto et al. 2010).","observed":"The star is one of three multiple-planet systems reported in the HARPS survey, which together hold six planets (Lo Curto et al. 2010). A separate imaging survey of planet host stars found a faint red dwarf of 0.18 solar masses that moves through space together with the planet's star (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009).","system":"The companion star, an M4 dwarf, lies about 1.5 arcminutes away, a projected distance of about 4750 au (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009). The system is therefore a wide binary star with planets around the main star.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd125612d-Curtoetal2010","cite":"Curto et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Lo Curto, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HARPS RV curves of HD125612, HD215497, HIP5158 (Lo Curto+, 2010)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35120048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd125612d-MugrauerNeuhuser2009","cite":"Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser","title":"The multiplicity of exoplanet host stars. New low-mass stellar companions of the exoplanet host stars HD 125612 and HD 212301","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.2561","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6038 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's mass was measured with the PARAS-2 spectrograph and combined with TESS transit data. It has a circular orbit and a density like that of dense planets in the 'Neptunian ridge' (about 1.5 to 2.0 grams per cubic centimetre) (Baliwal et al. 2025). Models of its interior suggest about 74% of its mass is rock and iron forming a core. The rest is a low-density hydrogen and helium envelope (Baliwal et al. 2025). It sits at the border between the ridge and the 'savanna', two regions of the planet population (Baliwal et al. 2025).","system":"The star is bright and metal-rich. A second star, an early K dwarf, orbits far out at a projected distance of about 3217 au (Baliwal et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6038ab-Baliwaletal2025","cite":"Baliwal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Sanjay Baliwal, Rishikesh Sharma, Abhijit Chakraborty et al.","title":"TOI-6038 A b: A Dense Sub-Saturn in the Transition Regime between the Neptunian Ridge and Savanna","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02272","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5322 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host star was analysed in a study of 268 stars that host hot Jupiters, using spectra and Gaia data. It turns out to be a member of the Milky Way's thick disk, an older population of stars with more alpha elements, that was not recognised before (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The thick-disk planets in the study tend to be less massive than the typical planet around thin-disk stars. None has an orbital period shorter than 2.7 days, while 22% of the thin-disk planets do. This may reflect differences in giant-planet formation across galactic environments, later evolution, or differing stellar properties (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","open":"The authors treat the difference between the populations as a possible indication, not an established result (DiTomasso et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5322b-DiTomassoetal2026","cite":"DiTomasso et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Victoria DiTomasso, Samuel W. Yee, Romy Rodriguez Martinez et al.","title":"A Sample of Thick-Disk Stars Hosting Hot Jupiters","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22254","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-117 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its transits arrive a few minutes early or late from one orbit to the next, known as transit-timing variations. These come from the pull of a heavier neighbour, Kepler-117 c (Bruno et al. 2015). The planet's own pull on its star is too weak to measure well with radial velocities. So the timing variations are what fix its mass, at about 0.094 Jupiter masses (Bruno et al. 2015). The orbit is nearly circular (Bruno et al. 2015).","system":"The star hosts two transiting planets. Planet c has a period of about 50.8 days and a mass of 1.84 Jupiter masses, about twenty times heavier than b. The two are not in a simple resonance (Bruno et al. 2015). The best-fit solution is dynamically stable (Bruno et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler117b-Brunoetal2015","cite":"Bruno et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"G. Bruno, J.-M. Almenara, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. XIV. A joint photometric, spectroscopic, and dynamical analysis of the Kepler-117 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.3934","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kapteyn c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with three radial-velocity spectrographs (HARPS, HIRES and PFS). Two signals, at periods of about 48 and 120 days, were attributed to two super-Earths (Anglada-Escudé et al. 2014). A later study found that the 48-day signal follows the star's own activity and is an artifact. The star spins in 143 days, and 48 days is one third of that (Robertson et al. 2015).","system":"Kapteyn's star is an old, metal-poor red dwarf from the Galaxy's halo. It is the nearest halo star, moving at about 245 km/s (Durbin et al. 2014). Its age is estimated at 10 to 12 billion years (Durbin et al. 2014).","history":"The discovery paper noted that a planetary system surviving around a star with such an unusual origin and motion would be remarkable (Anglada-Escudé et al. 2014).","open":"Papers disagree on the star's spin period, 143 days in one case and 195 days in another (Robertson et al. 2015; Durbin et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kapteync-AngladaEscudetal2014","cite":"Anglada-Escudé et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"G. Anglada-Escudé, P. Arriagada, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"Two planets around Kapteyn's star: a cold and a temperate super-Earth orbiting the nearest halo red dwarf","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.0818","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kapteync-Robertsonetal2015","cite":"Robertson et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Paul Robertson, Arpita Roy, Suvrath Mahadevan","title":"Stellar Activity Mimics a Habitable-zone Planet around Kapteyn's Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.02778","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kapteync-Durbinetal2014","cite":"Durbin et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Allyn J. Durbin, E. F. Guinan, S. G. Engle","title":"X-ray and Hubble/COS UV Measures of Kapteyn's Star: A Crucial Proxy of X-UV Irradiances for Old Red Dwarf Stars that May Host Habitable Zone Planets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #223","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":11,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 205739 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of the first two planets from the N2K survey on the Magellan telescopes (Lopez-Morales et al. 2008).","observed":"The radial-velocity signal gives a minimum mass of 1.37 Jupiter masses on an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.27 (Lopez-Morales et al. 2008). The star is metal-rich and shows little magnetic activity in its calcium lines (Lopez-Morales et al. 2008). The best orbit fit also shows a slow drift in the star's velocity, which hints at an additional distant body (Lopez-Morales et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":"The cause of the velocity drift, a possible outer companion, is not identified (Lopez-Morales et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd205739b-LopezMoralesetal2008","cite":"Lopez-Morales et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Mercedes López-Morales, R. Paul Butler, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"Two Jupiter-Mass Planets Orbiting HD 154672 and HD 205739","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1037","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1386 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A total of 64 velocity measurements with the HIRES spectrograph on Keck, spread over 2.5 years, showed a second planet that does not transit. It has a minimum mass of 0.309 Jupiter masses and a period of about 227.6 days (Hill et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet is a warm sub-Saturn of 0.540 Jupiter radii and 0.148 Jupiter masses (Hill et al. 2024). The two planets form a long-term stable system, though the inner planet's orbit may swing substantially in eccentricity because of the outer one (Hill et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1386c-Hilletal2024","cite":"Hill et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Michelle L. Hill, Stephen R. Kane, Paul A. Dalba et al.","title":"The TESS─Keck Survey. XIX. A Warm Transiting Sub-Saturn-mass Planet and a Nontransiting Saturn-mass Planet Orbiting a Solar Analog","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03498","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6008 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It is one of three short-period Earth-sized planets around nearby M dwarfs reported together. The planets were confirmed through TESS data, ground-based multicolour light curves, spectroscopy, high-resolution imaging and statistical tests, together with Subaru radial velocities (Barkaoui et al. 2024). The planet has a radius close to Earth's and a very short orbit. Its equilibrium temperature, the heat from the star alone, is about 707 K (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6008b-Barkaouietal2024","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. Barkaoui, R. P. Schwarz, N. Narita et al.","title":"Three short-period Earth-sized planets around M dwarfs discovered by TESS: TOI-5720 b, TOI-6008 b, and TOI-6086 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.06350","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-544 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit. It has a minimum mass of 21.5 Earth masses and a period of about 50 days (Osborne et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-544 b, orbits in 1.55 days and has a low density. It may be a rocky core with a hydrogen atmosphere or have a lot of water, and the two options cannot be told apart. It sits in the 'radius valley', where few planets are found (Osborne et al. 2024). The masses of both planets are among the most precisely measured by radial velocity (Osborne et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi544c-Osborneetal2024","cite":"Osborne et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. L. M. Osborne, V. Van Eylen, E. Goffo et al.","title":"TOI-544 b: a potential water-world inside the radius valley in a two-planet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14908","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi544c-Osborneetal2022","cite":"Osborne et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Hannah Osborne","title":"TOI544b: a new small planet inside the radius valley.","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2006-BLG-284L A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was seen as a short blip in a microlensing event, where the gravity of a foreground system magnifies a background star. The lens consists of two stars plus a gas giant, with a planet-to-primary mass ratio of about 1.26 thousandths (Bennett et al. 2020). The two stars are about 2.1 au apart in projection and the planet about 2.2 au from the primary (Bennett et al. 2020). Only rough masses can be estimated because the lens's brightness and parallax were not measured (Bennett et al. 2020).","system":"The planet could orbit the primary star alone or circle both stars. Stable orbits need the real three-dimensional separations to be much larger than those seen in projection, and which case holds is unknown (Bennett et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The lens and the source star were expected to be about 90 milliarcseconds apart by mid-2020, so that adaptive optics or Hubble imaging could detect the host stars (Bennett et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2006blg284lab-Bennettetal2020","cite":"Bennett et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. Bennett, A. Udalski, I. Bond et al.","title":"A Gas Giant Planet in the OGLE-2006-BLG-284L Stellar Binary System","journal":"Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.07199","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is only 6.5 parsecs away, and the discovery paper found four low-mass planets around it. The inner planet transits, which makes it the nearest known transiting planet (Motalebi et al. 2015). An independent Keck and APF study found six planetary candidates, including one at 6.8 days with a minimum mass of 3.5 Earth masses (Vogt et al. 2015). Models of stellar-wind sputtering suggest planets b and c can grow dense exospheres, thin outer atmospheres of heavy elements such as oxygen and magnesium, reaching several planetary radii (Vidotto et al. 2018).","system":"The star has several planets. The 22.8-day signal that Vogt et al. assigned to a planet matches the star's rotation period, so it may be caused by star spots instead (Johnson et al. 2016). The star also has an 11.7-year activity cycle like the Sun's (Johnson et al. 2016).","history":"Planet b may be less dense than c by about 10 per cent, which could be explained if b formed from material rich in calcium and aluminium. The density uncertainties are too large to be sure (Dorn et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134c-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134c-Vogtetal2015","cite":"Vogt et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Steven S. Vogt, Jennifer Burt, Stefano Meschiari et al.","title":"Six Planets Orbiting HD 219134","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07912","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134c-Vidottoetal2018","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, H. Lichtenegger, L. Fossati et al.","title":"Characterization of the HD 219134 multi-planet system II. Stellar-wind sputtered exospheres in rocky planets b & c","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00404","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134c-Johnsonetal2016","cite":"Johnson et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Marshall C. Johnson, Michael Endl, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"A 12-year Activity Cycle for the Nearby Planet Host Star HD 219134","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.05200","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134c-Dornetal2019","cite":"Dorn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Dorn, J. H. D. Harrison, A. Bonsor et al.","title":"A new class of Super-Earths formed from high-temperature condensates: HD219134 b, 55 Cnc e, WASP-47 e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07222","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":24,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 475 b":{"text":{"why":"A Chandra proposal notes that LHS 475 b was the first target in a JWST campaign of five rocky planets around moderately active stars (Garraffo 2023).","observed":"Two transits were observed with the JWST NIRSpec instrument over 2.9 to 5.3 micrometres. The spectrum has no clear features, ruling out a hydrogen-dominated atmosphere and a cloudless pure methane one (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2023). The planet has either little or no atmosphere, or an atmosphere with a high-altitude cloud layer like that of Venus (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2023). The planet's equilibrium temperature is about 586 K (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a nearby M3 dwarf. Its X-ray and extreme-ultraviolet output could only be estimated from indirect indicators before a Chandra observation was requested (Garraffo 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether this rocky planet keeps an atmosphere around a small red star is still open (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs475b-Garraffo2023","cite":"Garraffo 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cecilia Garraffo","title":"The Radiation Environment of JWST's Key Planet Host LHS 475","journal":"Chandra Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs475b-LustigYaegeretal2023","cite":"Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Lustig-Yaeger, G. Fu, E. May et al.","title":"A JWST transmission spectrum of the nearby Earth-sized exoplanet LHS 475 b","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41550-023-02064-z","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 195019 b":{"text":{"why":"It was announced from the Lick Observatory planet search, which added 82 new stars to its sample (Fischer et al. 1999).","observed":"The Doppler signal gives a minimum mass of 3.51 Jupiter masses on an almost circular orbit. The star shows no strong chromospheric activity, and its metallicity is about solar (Fischer et al. 1999).","system":"The star is part of a binary system. The composition of the two stars was compared in a thesis on planet-hosting binaries (Carande 2013).","history":"An earlier analysis of Hipparcos data suggested that the companion might be a 0.7 solar-mass K dwarf in a nearly pole-on orbit. Interferometry at the Palomar Testbed rules this out at 99% confidence, ruling out any companion above 0.35 solar masses (Koresko et al. 2002).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd195019b-Fischeretal1999","cite":"Fischer et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions around Two Solar-Type Stars: HD 195019 and HD 217107","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9810420","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd195019b-Carande2013","cite":"Carande 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Bryce Carande","title":"The Chemical Composition of Exoplanet-hosting Binary Star Systems","journal":"Masters Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd195019b-Koreskoetal2002","cite":"Koresko et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"C. D. Koresko, B. Mennesson, A. F. Boden et al.","title":"Long baseline interferometric observations of HD 195019: no K dwarf companion detected","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"mu Leo b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. It is a K2 giant with an effective temperature of about 4540 K, and it is richer in heavy elements than the Sun, with iron about +0.29 dex above solar (Smith & Ruck 2000). Earlier work also found it metal-rich, with most element ratios close to solar and extra sodium and aluminium (Gratton & Sneden 1990). Its carbon-12 to carbon-13 ratio is about 18 (Tomkin & Lambert 1974).","system":"","history":"","open":"How metal-rich the star really is has been debated, which is why a critical review of earlier results was needed (Smith & Ruck 2000)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-muleob-SmithRuck2000","cite":"Smith & Ruck 2000","year":"2000","authors":"G. Smith, M. J. Ruck","title":"A new abundance analysis of the super-metal-rich K2 giant, mu Leonis","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-muleob-GrattonSneden1990","cite":"Gratton & Sneden 1990","year":"1990","authors":"R. G. Gratton, C. Sneden","title":"The chemical composition of MU Leonis.","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-muleob-TomkinLambert1974","cite":"Tomkin & Lambert 1974","year":"1974","authors":"J. Tomkin, D. L. Lambert","title":"The <SUP>12</SUP>C/<SUP>13</SUP>C ratio in stellar atmospheres. III. Alpha Tauri, Beta Geminorum, and Mu Leonis.","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/153200","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":31,"about":15,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-139 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter of 0.387 Jupiter masses, one of the least massive known among transiting warm Jupiters. Its density can be explained with a core of about 50 Earth masses (Barragán et al. 2018). Four CHEOPS transit observations made its transit times predictable to within 10 minutes for the next decade. They also rule out an outer planet with a period under 150 days and a brown dwarf with a period under a year (Smith & Csizmadia 2022). Searching for water absorption with ESPRESSO found nothing (Prinoth et al. 2024).","system":"The host is an active K0 dwarf (Barragán et al. 2018). The planet's orbit is slightly misaligned with the star's spin, by about 15 to 25 degrees on the sky and 15 to 30 degrees in three dimensions (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","history":"The study of eight warm giants suggests that warm Jupiters around single stars form in disks that were aligned with the star's spin (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2139b-Barragnetal2018","cite":"Barragán et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"O. Barragán, D. Gandolfi, A. M. S. Smith et al.","title":"K2-139 b: a low-mass warm Jupiter on a 29-d orbit transiting an active K0 V star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00691","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2139b-SmithCsizmadia2022","cite":"Smith & Csizmadia 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alexis M. S. Smith, Szilárd Csizmadia","title":"A CHEOPS Search for Massive, Long-period Companions to the Warm Jupiter K2-139 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09355","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2139b-Prinothetal2024","cite":"Prinoth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Bibiana Prinoth, Elyar Sedaghati, Julia V. Seidel et al.","title":"High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy of Warm Jupiters: An ESPRESSO Sample with Predictions for ANDES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08558","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2139b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5799 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It was validated with the SHERLOCK pipeline and statistical methods. It is a super-Earth of 1.76 Earth radii (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-5799 b, has a radius of 1.73 Earth radii and an orbit of 4.17 days (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5799c-Yalinkayaetal2025","cite":"Yalçinkaya et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Yalçinkaya, K. Barkaoui, Ö. Baștürk et al.","title":"TOI-1743 b, TOI-5799 b, TOI-5799 c, and TOI-6223 b: TESS discovery and validation of four super-Earth to Neptune-sized planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-223 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star has four planets whose orbital periods are in the ratios 3:4:6:8. They are linked by 4:3, 3:2 and 2:1 resonances that cause measurable shifts in transit times (Mills et al. 2015). The system is an outstanding example of a resonant chain (Mills et al. 2015).","system":"Four sub-Neptune planets form the chain. Their detailed layout is too finely tuned for formation by scattering (Mills et al. 2016). Tidal dissipation or wide-scale instability might eventually turn such chains into the more common architecture (Mills et al. 2015).","history":"The resonances point to formation by migration in the gas disk, and this system is among the few that appear to require it (Mills et al. 2016). Modelling the chain limits the viscosity and density of the disk, which sets how turbulent it was (Hühn et al. 2021). About a Mars mass of orbit-crossing debris could pull a system like this out of resonance (Moore et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler223d-Millsetal2015","cite":"Mills et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel Clark Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223d-Millsetal2016","cite":"Mills et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Sean Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Transiting, Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #47","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223d-Hhnetal2021","cite":"Hühn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L.-A. Hühn, G. Pichierri, B. Bitsch et al.","title":"Kepler-223 resonance holds information about turbulence during the gas-disk phase","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13835","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223d-Mooreetal2013","cite":"Moore et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Alexander Moore, Imran Hasan, Alice C. Quillen","title":"Limits on orbit-crossing planetesimals in the resonant multiple planet system, KOI-730","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOINTREAU-1 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a Keck adaptive-optics imaging survey of young stars in the Taurus and rho Ophiuchi star-forming regions (Walker et al. 2026).","observed":"The faint companion was shown to be bound to its host by relative astrometry. Near-infrared spectra give an average spectral type of M9, with a gravity class typical of very young objects (Walker et al. 2026). Its spectral type changed between two observations, from M7 in one to L1 in the other (Walker et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a Taurus member about 3 million years old. The companion lies at a projected separation of 690 au (Walker et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kointreau1b-Walkeretal2026","cite":"Walker et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Samuel A. U. Walker, Michael C. Liu, Dimitri Mawet et al.","title":"Keck Observations in the INfrared of Taurus and ρ Oph Exoplanets And Ultracool Dwarfs (KOINTREAU) I: A Planetary-mass Companion and a Disk-obscured Stellar Companion Discovered in Taurus","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05191","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 35843 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transits were first spotted by the citizen-science project Planet Hunters TESS. Radial velocities from ESPRESSO then confirmed the planet and gave its mass of 11.32 Earth masses and a mildly eccentric orbit (e = 0.15). The bulk density is 3.80 grams per cubic centimetre (Hesse et al. 2025). The planet might be rocky with a thick hydrogen atmosphere, or a 'water world' (Hesse et al. 2025). At about 480 K it is among the coolest 5% of planets found by TESS (Hesse et al. 2025).","system":"The metal-poor Sun-like star has a second planet, HD 35843 b, found in the velocity data, with a 9.90-day period and a minimum mass of 5.84 Earth masses (Hesse et al. 2025). The star is bright (V = 9.4), so the planet is a good target for atmosphere studies (Hesse et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The composition, whether rock with hydrogen or a water world, is not settled (Hesse et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd35843c-Hesseetal2025","cite":"Hesse et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Katharine Hesse, Ismael Mireles, François Bouchy et al.","title":"HD 35843: A Sun-like Star Hosting a Long-period Sub-Neptune and Inner Super-Earth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00898","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-1843.03":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its orbit takes only 4.245 hours, the shortest period known for a planet at the time. A planet that close must stay outside its Roche limit, the distance inside which tides tear it apart. That requires a mean density above about 7 grams per cubic centimetre, so the planet must be mostly iron with at most about 30% silicates (Rappaport et al. 2013). Later 3D models suggest it may be shaped like an American football, stretched toward the star with an aspect ratio of up to 1.79 (Price & Rogers 2020). To survive it would need to be about as iron-rich as Mercury, around 66% iron by mass (Price & Rogers 2020).","system":"A planet in the K2 data, K2-137 b, orbits an M dwarf in a period just 4 minutes longer (Smith et al. 2018). The host here is a 0.46 solar-mass star (Price & Rogers 2020).","history":"Objects that break up this close to a star may leave co-orbiting debris, as seen at a white dwarf (Veras et al. 2016). High iron content could point to past tidal or impact stripping of the mantle, as proposed for Mercury (Noviello et al. 2024).","open":"It is still called a candidate, and its true composition and shape are not measured (Price & Rogers 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi184303-Rappaportetal2013","cite":"Rappaport et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Saul Rappaport, Roberto Sanchis-Ojeda, Leslie A. Rogers et al.","title":"The Roche Limit for Close-orbiting Planets: Minimum Density, Composition Constraints, and Application to the 4.2 hr Planet KOI 1843.03","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4080","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi184303-PriceRogers2020","cite":"Price & Rogers 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ellen M. Price, Leslie A. Rogers","title":"Tidally Distorted, Iron-enhanced Exoplanets Closely Orbiting Their Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.10666","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi184303-Smithetal2018","cite":"Smith et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, J. Cabrera, Sz Csizmadia et al.","title":"K2-137 b: an Earth-sized planet in a 4.3-h orbit around an M-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04549","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi184303-Verasetal2016","cite":"Veras et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Dimitri Veras, Thomas R. Marsh, Boris T. Gänsicke","title":"Dynamical mass and multiplicity constraints on co-orbital bodies around stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00440","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi184303-Novielloetal2024","cite":"Noviello et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jessica Noviello, Steven J. Desch, Jason Lee","title":"Inferring Redox State of Close-in Rocky Exoplanets","journal":"AGU Fall Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-2 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial-velocity monitoring over 153 days showed that the star also has a second companion in a wider orbit (Bryan et al. 2011). Orbit fits gave a period of about a year and a mass of at least several Jupiter masses, though more observations were needed to pin it down (Bryan et al. 2011). Spitzer watched the planet through a full orbit at two infrared wavelengths. Its night side gave off no detectable light at 4.5 microns, but gave off significant light at 3.6 microns (Fraine et al. 2019). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1350 K, similar to WASP-43 b (Fraine et al. 2019). Cloud-free models would predict more night-side light than was seen (Fraine et al. 2019). It was also included in a uniform reanalysis of Spitzer phase curves for hot Jupiters (May et al. 2022).","system":"A hot Jupiter with a much more massive companion on a long orbit differs sharply from the Kepler multi-planet systems, which are mostly small planets on tightly spaced, nearly flat orbits (Bryan et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"The outer companion's orbit and mass are still uncertain (Bryan et al. 2011). The cause of the night-side light pattern is open; one idea is high-altitude hazes forming near the day-night boundary (Fraine et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar2b-Bryanetal2011","cite":"Bryan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Marta L. Bryan, Khalid A. Alsubai, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Qatar-2: A K Dwarf Orbited by a Transiting Hot Jupiter and a More Massive Companion in an Outer Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5912","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar2b-Fraineetal2019","cite":"Fraine et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jonathan D. Fraine, Kevin Stevenson, Carlos Eduardo Romero-Mirza et al.","title":"Spitzer Phase Curves with Clouds: A New Population of Anomalous Infared Phase Curves","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-2609L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The signal came from the background source star passing near a planet's own caustic, the region where lensing magnification changes sharply. The planet lies at a projected distance from its host larger than the Einstein radius, the natural length scale of the lens (Han et al. 2024). The light curve could be explained in several ways. One is the usual inner-outer degeneracy, in which two different planet positions give nearly the same signal. A second is a choice between solutions in which the source partly or fully envelops the caustic (Han et al. 2024). A twin event, KMT-2022-BLG-0303, showed a similar anomaly but without the extra ambiguity (Han et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's exact separation from its host is not settled, because several solutions fit the data (Han et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg2609lb-Hanetal2024","cite":"Han et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Cheongho Han, Michael D. Albrow, Chung-Uk Lee et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-2609Lb and KMT-2022-BLG-0303Lb: Microlensing planets identified through signals produced by major-image perturbations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.17002","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet masses here come from transit timing variations, small shifts in transit times caused by the planets pulling on each other. Fits to these shifts show that the outer five planets sit in successive three-body resonances, where three neighbouring planets have orbital periods in a linked ratio (Weisserman et al. 2023). The planets do not appear to be in resonance in pairs (Weisserman et al. 2023). Adding the newly found planet g changes some of the masses derived from the timing data, so the authors urge caution when a system may be incomplete (Weisserman et al. 2023).","system":"The system has an extreme three-body resonance configuration and is very compact (Ragozzine et al. 2015). Radial-velocity surveys have not found any systems this tightly packed (Ragozzine et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Two analyses of the same system used different methods and made different assumptions, and their results differ (Weisserman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80d-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80d-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Pr0201 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits a star in the Praesepe (Beehive) open cluster. It is one of the first two known hot Jupiters in an open cluster and one of the only planets known around Sun-like main-sequence stars in a cluster (Quinn et al. 2012).","observed":"The signal was a Doppler wobble of the star. Line-shape measurements and activity indicators showed no link to orbital phase, which supports a planetary cause (Quinn et al. 2012). Two planets turned up among 53 single member stars surveyed, which sets a lower limit of a few per cent on how common hot Jupiters are in this metal-rich cluster (Quinn et al. 2012). A later study of six cluster stars found the cluster rich in metals and chemically uniform (Vejar et al. 2021).","system":"A second planet, Pr0211 b, was announced in the same paper around another cluster star (Quinn et al. 2012).","history":"Because the cluster's age is known precisely, at least two giant planets must have migrated inward within 600 million years of forming (Quinn et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pr0201b-Quinnetal2012","cite":"Quinn et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Samuel N. Quinn, Russel J. White, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Two \"b\"s in the Beehive: The Discovery of the First Hot Jupiters in an Open Cluster","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0818","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pr0201b-Vejaretal2021","cite":"Vejar et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"George Vejar, Simon C. Schuler, Keivan G. Stassun","title":"Detailed Abundances of Planet-hosting Open Clusters. The Praesepe (Beehive) Cluster","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01472","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 60532 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper's dynamical analysis was later corrected, because it had treated the orbits as seen pole-on instead of edge-on (Desort et al. 2008). A follow-up study found that the two giant planets are in a 3:1 mean-motion resonance, meaning the outer planet orbits once for every three orbits of the inner one. The best fit to the data is a resonant one, and the system stays stable for at least 5 billion years (Laskar & Correia 2009). Stability would hold even if the orbital plane were tilted as far as 15 degrees from the plane of the sky (Laskar & Correia 2009).","system":"Two giant planets share the resonance (Laskar & Correia 2009).","history":"","open":"The true masses depend on the tilt of the orbits. Letting the tilt float in the fit gives about 20 degrees, which would make the planets about 3.1 and 7.4 Jupiter masses (Laskar & Correia 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd60532c-Desortetal2008","cite":"Desort et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"M. Desort, A.-M. Lagrange, F. Galland et al.","title":"Extrasolar planets and brown dwarfs around A-F type stars. V. A planetary system found with HARPS around the F6IV-V star <ASTROBJ>HD 60532</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200810241e","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd60532c-LaskarCorreia2009","cite":"Laskar & Correia 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. Laskar, A. C. M. Correia","title":"HD 60532, a planetary system in a 3:1 mean motion resonance","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 341 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was flagged by TESS and has already been observed with the James Webb Space Telescope in a search for an atmosphere (DiTomasso et al. 2025). This study used 57 HARPS radial velocities to model the magnetic cycle and activity of the host star. The activity cycle is what limits the mass measurement, which is only an upper limit (DiTomasso et al. 2025). The data rule out further companions above 15.1 Earth masses with periods under 1750 days, and rule out contamination by background objects during the TESS and JWST observations (DiTomasso et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Only an upper limit on the planet's mass exists, which affects how the JWST atmosphere data can be interpreted (DiTomasso et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj341b-DiTomassoetal2025","cite":"DiTomasso et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Victoria DiTomasso, Mercedes López-Morales, Sarah Peacock et al.","title":"The Magnetically Induced Radial Velocity Variation of Gliese 341 and an Upper Limit to the Mass of Its Transiting Earth-sized Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07867","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-18 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper described it as a very low-density Saturn-mass planet around a metal-rich K star (Hartman et al. 2011).","observed":"The planet's orbit runs against the star's spin. Radial-velocity measurements during a transit gave an angle of 132 degrees between the star's spin axis and the orbital axis, on one of the coolest stars with such a measurement (Esposito et al. 2014). A survey of helium absorption found a strong signal, which shows gas streaming away from the planet (Vissapragada et al. 2022). The planet was also observed for about seven hours with the JWST NIRISS instrument (Fu et al. 2024). Its mean density is about 0.25 g per cubic centimetre (Hartman et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"The retrograde orbit bears on how such planets reach close orbits (Esposito et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp18b-Hartmanetal2011","cite":"Hartman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, B. Sato et al.","title":"HAT-P-18b and HAT-P-19b: Two Low-density Saturn-mass Planets Transiting Metal-rich K Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4850","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp18b-Espositoetal2014","cite":"Esposito et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Esposito, E. Covino, L. Mancini et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. III: The retrograde orbit of HAT-P-18b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.6728","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp18b-Vissapragadaetal2022","cite":"Vissapragada et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Shreyas Vissapragada, Heather A. Knutson, Michael Greklek-McKeon et al.","title":"The Upper Edge of the Neptune Desert Is Stable Against Photoevaporation","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11865","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp18b-Fuetal2024","cite":"Fu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Fu, N. Espinoza, D. K. Sing et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HAT-P-18b JWST NIRISS transmission spectrum (Fu+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 3651 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Saturn-mass world with a 62-day orbit of high eccentricity, about 0.6 (Brewer et al. 2020). The star is chromospherically quiet, meaning it has little magnetic activity. This made it a benchmark for testing new high-precision radial-velocity spectrographs (Brewer et al. 2020). The orbit's eccentricity and moderate period mean any inner planets would have been dynamically cleared (Brewer et al. 2020).","system":"A faint brown dwarf, HD 3651B, orbits far from the star, at about 480 au projected distance. It was the first brown dwarf directly imaged as a companion to a star with a known planet (Mugrauer et al. 2006). It has a spectral type of T7.5 to T8, a temperature of 780 to 840 K and a mass of 40 to 72 Jupiter masses (Liu et al. 2007). Spitzer also found it independently (Luhman et al. 2007). It shows that wide brown dwarfs and giant planets can share a star (Liu et al. 2007). It might disturb any terrestrial planets in the habitable zone (Burgasser 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd3651b-Breweretal2020","cite":"Brewer et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"John M. Brewer, Debra A. Fischer, Ryan T. Blackman et al.","title":"EXPRES. I. HD 3651 as an Ideal RV Benchmark","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.02303","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3651b-Mugraueretal2006","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"M. Mugrauer, A. Seifahrt, R. Neuhäuser et al.","title":"HD3651B: the first directly imaged brown dwarf companion of an exoplanet host star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0608484","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3651b-Liuetal2007","cite":"Liu et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Michael C. Liu, S. K. Leggett, Kuenley Chiu","title":"The Late-T Dwarf Companion to the Exoplanet Host Star HD 3651: A New Benchmark for Gravity and Metallicity Effects in Ultracool Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0701111","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3651b-Luhmanetal2007","cite":"Luhman et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"K. L. Luhman, B. M. Patten, M. Marengo et al.","title":"Discovery of Two T Dwarf Companions with the Spitzer Space Telescope","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609464","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3651b-Burgasser2007","cite":"Burgasser 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Adam J. Burgasser","title":"The Physical Properties of HD 3651B: An Extrasolar Nemesis?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0611542","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2023-BLG-0119L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet showed up as a short dip in the light curve near the peak of a highly magnified event. The dip came when the background source passed through the region of weakened magnification behind the central caustic, along the line between planet and host (Han et al. 2025). The planet-to-host mass ratio is about 3.6 in 10,000 (Han et al. 2025). The signal is subject to an inner-outer degeneracy, so the projected distance of the planet from its host is uncertain (Han et al. 2025). Physical properties came from statistical modelling based on the lensing measurements (Han et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's separation from its host is uncertain because two different orbit solutions fit the data (Han et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2023blg0119lb-Hanetal2025","cite":"Han et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cheongho Han, Ian A. Bond, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"MOA-2022-BLG-033Lb, KMT-2023-BLG-0119Lb, and KMT-2023-BLG-1896Lb: Three low mass-ratio microlensing planets detected through dip signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02193","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-34 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper singled out this planet because of its high eccentricity of 0.441, a very elongated orbit for a hot planet. It also noted hints of an outer companion (Bakos et al. 2012). The other three planets announced alongside it have properties typical of hot Jupiters (Bakos et al. 2012). The planet was among 14 systems with new Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements. This effect traces how the star's spin lines up with the planet's orbit (Albrecht et al. 2012). That study argues that star-planet tides realign many hot Jupiter orbits over time (Albrecht et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp34b-Bakosetal2012","cite":"Bakos et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-34b-HAT-P-37b: Four Transiting Planets More Massive than Jupiter Orbiting Moderately Bright Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.0659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp34b-Albrechtetal2012","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John A. Johnson et al.","title":"Obliquities of Hot Jupiter Host Stars: Evidence for Tidal Interactions and Primordial Misalignments","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6105","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 87646 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is in a close binary with another star about 22 au away. The system is the first close binary found with more than one companion of planetary or brown-dwarf mass around the primary star (Ma et al. 2016). Follow-up Doppler measurements from several other instruments confirmed it and revealed a long-period brown dwarf candidate (Ma et al. 2016). The companion star was located with archive data and an adaptive-optics image (Ma et al. 2016).","system":"A second, more massive companion, the brown dwarf candidate MARVELS-7c, orbits the same star on a longer orbit (Ma et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The second companion is only a candidate brown dwarf (Ma et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd87646b-Maetal2016","cite":"Ma et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"B. Ma, J. Ge, A. Wolszczan et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Solar-type stars from SDSS-III MARVELS. VI. HD 87646 (Ma+, 2016)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51520112","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-32 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star, WASP-32, is depleted in lithium. The discovery paper found that some stars of about 1.2 solar masses with hot Jupiters are lithium-poor, though most such stars have lithium close to field-star levels (Maxted et al. 2010). The planet is one of a small group of hot Jupiters with masses above 3 Jupiter masses (Maxted et al. 2010). The transit repeats every 2.72 days and takes about 0.10 day, with a depth of about 1 per cent (Maxted et al. 2010). A high-resolution spectrograph confirmed that the companion is a planet (Maxted et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp32b-Maxtedetal2010","cite":"Maxted et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-32b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter Planet Orbiting a Lithium-Poor, Solar-Type Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1742","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-954 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey paper paired it with a second Saturn-mass planet to test whether strong irradiation inflates planets of this mass (Sha et al. 2021).","observed":"TOI-954 b is about 30 times more irradiated than its sibling discovery K2-329 b, yet both planets are about the same size (Sha et al. 2021). That pairing offers a test of whether heating from the star puffs up Saturn-mass planets. The two planets are comparison cases for Saturn-mass planets at different points in their lives (Sha et al. 2021). The planet transits a G subgiant, a star starting to evolve off the main sequence, observed by TESS in sectors 4 and 5 (Sha et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether irradiation inflates Saturn-mass planets is still being tested (Sha et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi954b-Shaetal2021","cite":"Sha et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Lizhou Sha, Chelsea X. Huang, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"TOI-954 b and K2-329 b: Short-period Saturn-mass Planets that Test whether Irradiation Leads to Inflation","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.14436","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5205 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a mid-M dwarf of 0.392 solar masses and has one of the highest planet-to-star mass ratios for M-dwarf planets, almost 0.3 per cent (Kanodia et al. 2023). Its large size against a small star gives a transit depth of about 7 per cent, one of the deepest for a confirmed planet around a main-sequence star (Kanodia et al. 2023). It is the first transiting Jupiter with a measured mass around such a low-mass star (Kanodia et al. 2023). JWST observed three transits. The spectrum was strongly affected by starlight from the star's uneven surface, yet the planet's atmosphere was clearly detected, with signs of carbon- and sulfur-bearing molecules (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Its high mass stretches current ideas of planet formation and the disc scaling relations, which cannot easily produce a planet like this (Kanodia et al. 2023).","open":"Constraints on atmospheric metallicity and carbon-to-oxygen ratio, which could trace how it formed, are still being worked out (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5205b-Kanodiaetal2023","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Suvrath Mahadevan, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"TOI-5205b: A Short-period Jovian Planet Transiting a Mid-M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.11160","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5205b-LustigYaegeretal2025","cite":"Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacob Lustig-Yaeger, Caleb Cañas, Dana Louie et al.","title":"Looking at Giant Exoplanets around M-dwarfs (GEMS) with JWST: Constraining the Atmosphere of TOI-5205 b Through Strong Stellar Contamination","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-9 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-9 was the first system with multiple confirmed transiting planets and the first to clearly show transit timing variations, shifts in transit times caused by planets pulling on one another (Ragozzine & Holman 2018). The two Saturn-size planets have periods near a 2:1 resonance. Their transit times changed in opposite ways (Holman et al. 2010). A reanalysis with six times more data found lower masses and very low densities, about 0.14 g per cubic centimetre for planet c, about a third of the earlier value (Dreizler & Ofir 2014). A search for oblateness, or flattening from spin, in its transits found its light curve consistent with a spherical planet (Lammers & Winn 2026).","system":"A small third planet with a 1.6-day period, Kepler-9 d, was found after removing the two giants' signals (Holman et al. 2010). Radial velocity data hint at a possible outer non-transiting planet (Dreizler & Ofir 2014).","history":"","open":"The masses changed substantially between analyses, so the authors warn that formal errors in timing fits can mislead if the data cover too little of the interaction time (Dreizler & Ofir 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler9c-RagozzineHolman2018","cite":"Ragozzine & Holman 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Matthew J. Holman","title":"Kepler-9: The first multi-transiting system and the first transit timing variations","journal":"New Astronomy Reviews","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newar.2019.03.003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9c-Holmanetal2010","cite":"Holman et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Matthew J. Holman, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Darin Ragozzine et al.","title":"Kepler-9: A System of Multiple Planets Transiting a Sun-Like Star, Confirmed by Timing Variations","journal":"Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1195778","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9c-DreizlerOfir2014","cite":"Dreizler & Ofir 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Stefan Dreizler, Aviv Ofir","title":"Kepler-9 revisited 60% the mass with six times more data","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1372","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9c-LammersWinn2026","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Constraining the Planetary Obliquity Distribution of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-95 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with six other hot Jupiters, WASP-96 b to WASP-101 b, all with periods of 2.1 to 5.7 days (Hellier et al. 2014). Its orbit is compatible with zero eccentricity, meaning circular (Hellier et al. 2014). The host star shows a possible rotational modulation with a period of 20.7 days, a brightness change as spots rotate in and out of view (Hellier et al. 2014). The star is one of five in the batch brighter than V = 10.8, which helps follow-up studies (Hellier et al. 2014). A later reanalysis of TESS data gave improved physical properties for the planet (Saha & Sengupta 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp95b-Hellieretal2014","cite":"Hellier et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Transiting hot Jupiters from WASP-South, Euler and TRAPPIST: WASP-95b to WASP-101b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5630","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp95b-SahaSengupta2021","cite":"Saha & Sengupta 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Suman Saha, Sujan Sengupta","title":"Critical Analysis of Tess Transit Photometric Data: Improved Physical Properties for Five Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.11366","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102272 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a red giant, found in a survey of subgiant and giant stars with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope using an iodine cell to measure precise radial velocities (Nowak et al. 2010). Long-period radial velocity changes in red giants can also have other causes than planets, such as pulsations or starspots rotating in and out of view (Nowak et al. 2010). The team therefore measured the bisector of the spectral lines, a test of line shape, to check for such causes (Nowak et al. 2010). This was the second red giant with planets found in the survey (Nowak et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102272b-Nowaketal2010","cite":"Nowak et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Nowak, A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan","title":"The Pennsylvania-Torun Search for Planets: Bisector Measurements of HD 102272","journal":"EAS Publications Series","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/eas/1042017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-149 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is described as a typical hot Jupiter around a G6 dwarf (Brown et al. 2014). WASP photometry of the system was contaminated by light from a nearby star. The team corrected the transit depth for this dilution (Brown et al. 2014). It was announced alongside WASP-85 A b and WASP-116 b in the original discovery paper (Brown et al. 2014). It was later revisited, with the help of TESS, in a paper reporting nine WASP planets with newly updated transit parameters (Schanche et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp149b-Brownetal2014","cite":"Brown et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, D. R. Anderson, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Three transiting planet discoveries from the Wide Angle Search for Planets: WASP-85 A b; WASP-116 b, and WASP-149 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.7761","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp149b-Schancheetal2025","cite":"Schanche et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Schanche, G. Hébrard, K. G. Stassun et al.","title":"A Swarm of WASP Planets: Nine Giant Planets Identified by the WASP Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08091","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-356 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Volunteer amateur astronomers searching K2 data found 42 planet candidates, 18 of them new (Castro-González et al. 2022). The professional team then validated four new planets, including this one (Castro-González et al. 2022). The study highlights K2-356 b and a candidate, EPIC 211537087.02, as being near a 2:1 period commensurability, meaning one orbits about twice for each orbit of the other (Castro-González et al. 2022).","system":"It has a neighbouring candidate close to a 2:1 period ratio (Castro-González et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2356b-CastroGonzlezetal2022","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. Castro-González, E. Díez Alonso, J. Menéndez Blanco et al.","title":"The K2-OjOS Project: New and revisited planets and candidates in K2 campaigns 5, 16, & 18","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03346","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3323 c":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper reports a dozen planets around five M dwarfs from HARPS monitoring, aimed at finding lower-mass, longer-period planets (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017).","observed":"Low-mass stars are among the best targets for finding rocky planets in the habitable zone. Over 13 years HARPS has identified over a dozen super-Earths and Earth-mass planets around M dwarfs (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017). This planet comes from dense, long-term radial-velocity monitoring of a small sample of M dwarfs. The authors searched the data for periodic signals and tested them against activity indicators of the stars (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3323c-AstudilloDefruetal2017","cite":"Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"N. Astudillo-Defru, T. Forveille, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLI. A dozen planets around the M dwarfs GJ 3138, GJ 3323, GJ 273, GJ 628, and GJ 3293","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05386","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-94 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits one star of a wide pair of near-twin stars, and the other star also hosts a hot Jupiter (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect shows a retrograde orbit, running against the star's spin (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). A ground-based transmission spectrum suggested sodium absorption and a slope from Rayleigh scattering, which points to a fairly clear atmosphere (Ahrer 2021). JWST spectra of its two limbs show muted water features on the morning limb and clear features on the evening limb, which indicates high-altitude aerosols on the morning side (Fu et al. 2025).","system":"The companion star WASP-94 B is an F9 star about 2700 au away, projected. It hosts a second hot Jupiter with a 2.008-day period (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014).","history":"The two planets' orbital planes are inclined to each other, so at least one is tilted relative to the plane of the stellar binary (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp94ab-NeveuVanMalleetal2014","cite":"Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Neveu-VanMalle, D. Queloz, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-94 A and B planets: hot-Jupiter cousins in a twin-star system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.7566","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp94ab-Ahrer2021","cite":"Ahrer 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Eva-Maria Ahrer","title":"LRG-BEASTS: sodium absorption and Rayleigh slope in the atmosphere of WASP-94A b using NTT/EFOSC2","journal":"Atmospheres, Atmospheres! Do I Look Like I Care About Atmospheres?","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5544521","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp94ab-Fuetal2025","cite":"Fu et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Guangwei Fu, Sagnick Mukherjee, Kevin B. Stevenson et al.","title":"Overcast Mornings and Clear Evenings in Hot Jupiter Exoplanet Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15854","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148797 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Both planets show anti-correlated transit timing variations, with one arriving early as the other arrives late. This gives enough information to derive masses from the timings alone (Almenara et al. 2026). The two planets have nearly equal radii and masses and low bulk densities, which makes them low-density sub-Jovian planets of the Neptunian savanna (Almenara et al. 2026).","system":"The second planet orbits every 68.2 days, so the period ratio is 1.619, close to the golden mean and near several strong resonances (Almenara et al. 2026). Most multi-planet systems in the savanna have at least one adjacent pair with a period ratio under 3 (Almenara et al. 2026).","history":"The calm layout is consistent with smoother migration, such as disc-driven migration (Almenara et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148797b-Almenaraetal2026","cite":"Almenara et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. M. Almenara, A. Castro-González, R. Mardling et al.","title":"HD 148797: A bright F-type star with two moderate-period low-density sub-Jovian planets. Compact multi-planet architectures are common in the Neptunian savanna","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09656","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+48 738 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a K0 giant found in the Penn State-Torun survey with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Gettel et al. 2012). The star also shows a nonlinear long-term radial velocity trend. This indicates another, more distant companion, which may be a low-mass star or of planetary or brown-dwarf mass (Gettel et al. 2012). The study also notes that noise in giant-star radial velocities, if it comes from solar-type oscillations, is anti-correlated with stellar metallicity (Gettel et al. 2012).","system":"A more distant second companion is suspected from the long-term trend (Gettel et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"The nature of the outer companion is unknown (Gettel et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd48738b-Getteletal2012","cite":"Gettel et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S. Gettel, A. Wolszczan, A. Niedzielski et al.","title":"Substellar-mass Companions to the K-giants HD 240237, BD +48 738, and HD 96127","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.1641","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 192310 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper comes from a program built to find tiny signals from very low-mass planets in the habitable zones of nearby, quiet, slowly rotating Sun-like stars (Pepe et al. 2011).","observed":"Ten stars were picked from the most stable stars of the HARPS program. For each star about 50 data points were collected per season, in three short nightly visits two hours apart. This approach was chosen to reduce noise from stellar pulsation and granulation (Pepe et al. 2011). The study reports five new and one confirmed low-mass planets around three stars, HD 20794, HD 85512 and HD 192310 (Pepe et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd192310b-Pepeetal2011","cite":"Pepe et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Pepe, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Very low-mass planets around <ASTROBJ>HD 20794</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 85512</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 192310</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3447","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-19 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper announced it as the shortest-period transiting planet found so far (Hebb et al. 2010).","observed":"The star's rotation period, 10.5 days, came from regular brightness changes. It is fairly short, so either the star is young, about 600 million years, or tides have made the planet spiral inward and spin the star up (Hebb et al. 2010). Transit timing over many years shows no significant variations, which limits any perturbing planet to small masses in resonances (Cortés-Zuleta et al. 2020). Transmission spectra of the planet are best fit by aerosols that scatter light like a haze (Barstow et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The age is still uncertain, and a more precise age would constrain how strongly stars and planets exchange tidal energy (Hebb et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp19b-Hebbetal2010","cite":"Hebb et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"L. Hebb, A. Collier-Cameron, A. H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"WASP-19b: The Shortest Period Transiting Exoplanet Yet Discovered","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp19b-CortsZuletaetal2020","cite":"Cortés-Zuleta et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Pía Cortés-Zuleta, Patricio Rojo, Songhu Wang et al.","title":"TraMoS. V. Updated ephemeris and multi-epoch monitoring of the hot Jupiters WASP-18Ab, WASP-19b, and WASP-77Ab","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.11112","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp19b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":39,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-622 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Saturn-mass world orbiting an F star. Despite strong irradiation, it shows no sign of radius inflation (Psaridi et al. 2023). The host is hotter than the Kraft break at 6200 K, above which stars rotate fast and have fewer spectral lines, which makes measuring planet masses harder (Psaridi et al. 2023). A later Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement found an aligned orbit, with a true spin-orbit angle of about 16 degrees, consistent with quiet migration through a disc (Zak et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The aligned orbit is consistent with quiet disc migration (Zak et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi622b-Psaridietal2023","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Angelica Psaridi, Francois Bouchy, Monika Lendl et al.","title":"Exploring the densities of planets and brown dwarfs transiting hot stars above the Kraft break","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2022-643","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi622b-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, P. Kabath, H. M. J. Boffin et al.","title":"PLATOSpec's first results: Planets WASP-35b and TOI-622b are on aligned orbits, and K2-237b is on a polar orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.10145","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2524 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper describes it as a warm Jupiter near the transition to hot Jupiters, among three warm Jupiters around solar-like stars (Eberhardt et al. 2023). A later study measured its stellar obliquity with the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and found it aligned with the star's spin, like five other warm-Jupiter systems studied alongside it (Wang et al. 2024). The study argues that misalignments are neither universal nor primordial among Jovian planets, since single-star warm-Jupiter systems tend to be aligned, even around hot stars (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters are tidally detached enough to likely preserve their primordial obliquity (Wang et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2524b-Eberhardtetal2023","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Melissa J. Hobson, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"Three Warm Jupiters around Solar-analog Stars Detected with TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17592","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2524b-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice, Songhu Wang et al.","title":"Single-star Warm-Jupiter Systems Tend to Be Aligned, Even around Hot Stellar Hosts: No T <SUB>eff</SUB>-λ Dependency","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-53 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up photometry for the system came from the Keplercam on the 1.2 m telescope at Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory, in addition to the HATNet survey (Hartman et al. 2015). A search of mid-transit times for 144 hot Jupiters flagged HAT-P-53 b as a potential orbital-decay candidate, a planet whose orbit may be shrinking (Yeh et al. 2024). Such a result is from timing fits only, and remains a candidate (Yeh et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the orbit is truly decaying is not settled (Yeh et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp53b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti, G. A. Bakos et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Spectroscopy and photometry for HAT-P-50--HAT-P-53 (Hartman+, 2015)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51500168","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp53b-Yehetal2024","cite":"Yeh et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Li-Chin Yeh, Ing-Guey Jiang, Napaporn A-thano","title":"Searching for candidates of orbital decays among transit exoplanets","journal":"New Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08953","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1140 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The inner planet's orbit is coplanar with that of its sibling LHS 1140 b. Its density, 4.7 g per cubic centimetre, is consistent with Earth's ratio of iron to magnesium silicate (Ment et al. 2019). Later ESPRESSO analysis fixed its mass to about 3 per cent precision (Cadieux et al. 2024). A metastable helium search found a signal for planet b but no detection for planet c (Cherubim et al. 2026).","system":"The outer planet b is more complex. Its density is lower than purely rocky, so it is a temperate mini-Neptune or a water world (Cadieux et al. 2024). Its transmission spectrum rules out a hydrogen-rich atmosphere (Damiano et al. 2024). Compact systems of small planets around M dwarfs are common, so more planets may exist (Ment et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1140c-Mentetal2019","cite":"Ment et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Kristo Ment, Jason A. Dittmann, Nicola Astudillo-Defru et al.","title":"A Second Terrestrial Planet Orbiting the Nearby M Dwarf LHS 1140","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00485","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140c-Cadieuxetal2024","cite":"Cadieux et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Charles Cadieux, Mykhaylo Plotnykov, René Doyon et al.","title":"New Mass and Radius Constraints on the LHS 1140 Planets: LHS 1140 b Is either a Temperate Mini-Neptune or a Water World","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15490","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140c-Cherubimetal2026","cite":"Cherubim et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Collin Cherubim, Shreyas Vissapragada, Tim Cunningham et al.","title":"Escaping helium from a terrestrial planet atmosphere in a nearby habitable-zone","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140c-Damianoetal2024","cite":"Damiano et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Mario Damiano, Aaron Bello-Arufe, Jeehyun Yang et al.","title":"LHS 1140 b Is a Potentially Habitable Water World","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13265","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-153 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper was aimed at pinning down giant-planet migration and the upper boundary of the Neptunian desert, the shortage of Neptune-size planets on close orbits (Demangeon et al. 2018).","observed":"Mass and radius were measured to better than 15 per cent by combining SuperWASP transits with SOPHIE radial velocities. This precision is needed to learn about the origin of features in the planet population (Demangeon et al. 2018). The planet is a hot Saturn with a low density and a radius of about 1.55 Jupiter radii, notably puffy for its mass (Demangeon et al. 2018). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1700 K (Demangeon et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp153b-Demangeonetal2018","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, F. Faedi, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"The discovery of WASP-151b, WASP-153b, WASP-156b: Insights on giant planet migration and the upper boundary of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06321","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known comes from long monitoring of the star. Over 1000 hours of Spitzer Space Telescope observations between 2016 and 2019 caught 188 transits of the seven planets, which pinned down their masses, sizes and orbits (Ducrot et al. 2020). Planet e is regarded as the most promising of the seven for surface liquid water, since it gets about 66 % of the sunlight Earth does (Fauchez et al. 2020). No atmosphere has been measured. Published work so far is modelling of what the James Webb Space Telescope could detect (Mikal-Evans 2022).","system":"All seven planets are Earth-sized and at least three lie in the star's habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). They appear to be locked in a chain of orbital resonances, where their orbital periods form simple ratios, and simulations suggest such a chain can form as planets migrate inward through a disc (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether it has an atmosphere at all, and what its interior holds. Interior models allow water mass fractions from none to substantial across the system (Dorn et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1e-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1e-Fauchezetal2020","cite":"Fauchez et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Thomas J. Fauchez, Martin Turbet, Eric T. Wolf et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1 Habitable Atmosphere Intercomparison (THAI): motivations and protocol version 1.0","journal":"Geoscientific Model Development","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10950","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1e-MikalEvans2022","cite":"Mikal-Evans 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Thomas Mikal-Evans","title":"Detecting the proposed CH<SUB>4</SUB>-CO<SUB>2</SUB> biosignature pair with the James Webb Space Telescope: TRAPPIST-1e and the effect of cloud/haze","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09685","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1e-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1e-Dornetal2018","cite":"Dorn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Klaus Mosegaard, Simon L. Grimm et al.","title":"Interior Characterization in Multiplanetary Systems: TRAPPIST-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 d":{"text":{"why":"The planetary nature of the signals was checked with ground-based photometry, archival images, radial velocities and high-resolution imaging (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020).","observed":"The star is bright and quiet, which made it a good target for weighing the planets. Spectrographs measured the planet's mass at 4.6 sigma confidence, after only an upper limit had been set before (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). This confirmed the earlier suspicion that the planet has a low density (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","system":"Three sub-Neptunes of similar size orbit close to the star, near orbital resonances of 1:3 and 3:4 (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020). Farther out are a Saturn-mass planet, a planet of at least 13.5 Earth masses in the habitable zone, and a very long-period companion (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). A spin-orbit measurement for planet b shows alignment, and all planets lie within about 10 degrees of one shared plane, which looks more like our Solar System than most systems do (Lubin & Petigura 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939d-BadenasAgustietal2020","cite":"Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mariona Badenas-Agusti, Maximilian N. Günther, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"HD 191939: Three Sub-Neptunes Transiting a Sun-like Star Only 54 pc Away","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939d-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939d-LubinPetigura2024","cite":"Lubin & Petigura 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Lubin, Erik Petigura","title":"HD 191939: a multi-planet system with a shared and aligned orbital plane","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-8 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper also introduced a new way to extract precise radial velocities from noisy spectra (Fulton et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet is one of the most inflated known, with a density of only 0.167 g per cubic centimetre (Fulton et al. 2015). Near-infrared spectra from the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo detected water vapour in its atmosphere (Basilicata et al. 2025). A search of TESS data found its transit depth seems to vary, but this most likely comes from light of a nearby star mixed into the measurement (Wang & Espinoza 2024).","system":"The host is a mildly evolved, metal-rich G dwarf, larger than the Sun (Fulton et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"A planned JWST eclipse-mapping programme aims to test whether a misaligned magnetic field shifts the hottest spot north or south, which would be a first (Valentine et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt8b-Fultonetal2015","cite":"Fulton et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Benjamin J. Fulton, Karen A. Collins, B. Scott Gaudi et al.","title":"KELT-8b: A Highly Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiter and a New Technique for Extracting High-precision Radial Velocities from Noisy Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.06738","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt8b-Basilicataetal2025","cite":"Basilicata et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"M. Basilicata, P. Giacobbe, M. Brogi et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG: LXVII. Detection of water and preliminary characterisation of the atmospheres of the two hot Jupiters: KELT-8 b and KELT-23 Ab","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05056","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt8b-WangEspinoza2024","cite":"Wang & Espinoza 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gavin Wang, Néstor Espinoza","title":"A Blind Search for Transit Depth Variability with TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02154","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt8b-Valentineetal2024","cite":"Valentine et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daniel Valentine, Ryan Challener, David Grant et al.","title":"Unlocking New Dimensions in Eclipse Mapping with KELT-8b","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 3","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-78 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has Earth's size but circles its star in 8.5 hours. It is the smallest planet with measured mass, radius and dayside brightness (Winn et al. 2018). The star is young and active, so its own variations had to be removed from the radial velocity data before the planet's tug could be seen, and different methods gave masses from 1.31 to 1.87 Earth masses (Hatzes 2014; Grunblatt et al. 2015). Its dayside glow was detected in visible light at 9.5 sigma (Singh et al. 2022).","system":"It is the best-known example of ultra-short-period planets, which are about as common as hot Jupiters and are almost always smaller than twice Earth's radius (Winn et al. 2018). Such planets often have companions in compact systems (Winn et al. 2018).","history":"Ultra-short-period planets might be the exposed rocky cores of larger gas-rich planets (Winn et al. 2018).","open":"Models disagree on whether it keeps an atmosphere; one study ranks it among planets likely to have a dense secondary atmosphere or an icy low-density interior (Gupta & Schlichting 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler78b-Winnetal2018","cite":"Winn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, Roberto Sanchis-Ojeda, Saul Rappaport","title":"Kepler-78 and the Ultra-Short-Period planets","journal":"New Astronomy Reviews","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03303","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler78b-Hatzes2014","cite":"Hatzes 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. P. Hatzes","title":"The detection of Earth-mass planets around active stars. The mass of Kepler-78b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.0853","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler78b-Grunblattetal2015","cite":"Grunblatt et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Samuel K. Grunblatt, Andrew W. Howard, Raphaëlle D. Haywood","title":"Determining the Mass of Kepler-78b with Nonparametric Gaussian Process Estimation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.00369","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler78b-Singhetal2022","cite":"Singh et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"V. Singh, A. S. Bonomo, G. Scandariato et al.","title":"Probing Kepler's hottest small planets via homogeneous search and analysis of optical secondary eclipses and phase variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.05716","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler78b-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 238090 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey searched M dwarfs, small cool stars, for planets with CARMENES (Stock et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet was found together with two similar warm super-Earths around other stars (Stock et al. 2020). From the star's light and the orbit, its equilibrium temperature comes out near 469.6 K (Stock et al. 2020). The star's activity indicators show no signal at the planet's period, which supports a real planet. The star itself spins every 96.7 days (Stock et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd238090b-Stocketal2020","cite":"Stock et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Stock, E. Nagel, J. Kemmer et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Three temperate-to-warm super-Earths","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.00474","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2016-BLG-1195L b":{"text":{"why":"The lens star is an ultracool dwarf, and the planet is the lowest-mass one found by microlensing so far (Shvartzvald et al. 2017).","observed":"It lies about 1.16 au from a 0.078 solar-mass star (Shvartzvald et al. 2017). The planet's signal lasted only about 2.5 hours (Bond et al. 2017). A separate analysis of the same data gave a larger, cooler solution: roughly 3 Earth masses at about 2 au around a 0.2 solar-mass star (Bond et al. 2017).","system":"It is the third case in a row from Spitzer's survey where the lens lies in the Galactic disc, not the bulge (Shvartzvald et al. 2017).","history":"Together with TRAPPIST-1, it suggests Earth-sized planets may be common around ultracool dwarfs (Shvartzvald et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2016blg1195lb-Shvartzvaldetal2017","cite":"Shvartzvald et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Y. Shvartzvald, J. C. Yee, S. Calchi Novati et al.","title":"An Earth-mass Planet in a 1 au Orbit around an Ultracool Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08548","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogle2016blg1195lb-Bondetal2017","cite":"Bond et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"I. A. Bond, D. P. Bennett, T. Sumi et al.","title":"The lowest mass ratio planetary microlens: OGLE 2016-BLG-1195Lb","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08639","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 42012 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is low in heavy elements, and the planet is a good target for Gaia astrometry, which tracks a star's tiny sideways wobble (Rey et al. 2017).","observed":"Years of SOPHIE and ELODIE velocity data revealed one giant companion, with a period between 1.7 and 3.7 years for the three planets studied (Rey et al. 2017). It is the only planet detected around its star. The study discusses early astrometric results from Gaia's first data release (Rey et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass is unknown, since only a minimum is measured; Gaia may settle it (Rey et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd42012b-Reyetal2017","cite":"Rey et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Rey, G. Hébrard, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XII. Three giant planets suitable for astrometric mass determination with Gaia","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.06393","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5143 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"During transit, the planet blocks part of the star's spinning surface, and the distorted starlight (the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect) showed that its orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Radzom et al. 2025). It is only the third hot Jupiter with a nearby companion to get such a measurement (Radzom et al. 2025).","system":"A sub-Neptune orbits inside it with a period of about 2.4 days (Rodriguez & Quinn 2024).","history":"Hot Jupiters on tilted orbits are often blamed on violent inward migration, which should clear out neighbours. A hot Jupiter with an aligned orbit and a close companion points to a gentler history, such as forming close in (Radzom et al. 2025; Rodriguez & Quinn 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5143c-Radzometal2025","cite":"Radzom et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Brandon T. Radzom, Jiayin Dong, Malena Rice et al.","title":"Evidence for Primordial Alignment II: Insights from Stellar Obliquity Measurements for Hot Jupiters in Compact Multiplanet Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03745","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5143c-RodriguezQuinn2024","cite":"Rodriguez & Quinn 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Samuel Quinn","title":"Hot Jupiters with Friends as a Guide for Planetary Evolution","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 210277 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit is strongly eccentric, at 0.45 (Marcy et al. 1999). Adaptive optics images and earlier studies indicate the host is a single star (Marcy et al. 1999). Herschel found a faint cold dust disc, point-like in its images and among the coldest and faintest found around mature stars, with a brightness near that of the Kuiper belt (Eiroa et al. 2011). The star is rich in heavy elements, as are many planet hosts (Santos et al. 2001).","system":"The star has a faint cold dust disc at about 22 K or less, a debris disc (Eiroa et al. 2011).","history":"The study lists several ways an orbit becomes eccentric: pushes from other planets, passing stars in the birth cluster, or the gas disc (Marcy et al. 1999).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd210277b-Marcyetal1999","cite":"Marcy et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"Two New Candidate Planets in Eccentric Orbits","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9904275","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd210277b-Eiroaetal2011","cite":"Eiroa et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Eiroa, J. P. Marshall, A. Mora et al.","title":"Herschel discovery of a new class of cold, faint debris discs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.4826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd210277b-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, G. Israelian, M. Mayor","title":"The metal-rich nature of stars with planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0105216","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-730 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is Earth-sized and was confirmed statistically rather than by weighing it. The team used APOGEE-2N velocities, Robo-AO high-resolution imaging, and Gaia distances to rule out false alarms (Cañas et al. 2019). Its transit signal is faint, and the study checks for a chance alignment with another star (Zhu et al. 2018).","system":"A hot Jupiter circles outside it in a 6.49 day orbit. This is only the second compact system with a hot Jupiter and an inner transiting planet (Cañas et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"One possibility remains that the small planet transits a different star than the hot Jupiter (Zhu et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler730c-Caasetal2019","cite":"Cañas et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Songhu Wang, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"Kepler-730: A Hot Jupiter System with a Close-in, Transiting, Earth-sized Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler730c-Zhuetal2018","cite":"Zhu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Wei Zhu, Fei Dai, Kento Masuda","title":"Kepler-730b is Probably a Hot Jupiter with a Small Companion","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00007","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-47 d":{"text":{"why":"The planet had first appeared as a single unexplained transit in the earlier data (Welsh et al. 2015).","observed":"Extra transits seen later confirmed it. Its orbit is tilted slightly and precesses, which made its transits grow deeper over the Kepler observations (Welsh et al. 2015). Its mass is only loosely known, between 7 and 43 Earth masses, with a density below 0.68 g per cubic centimetre, which is very low (Orosz et al. 2019).","system":"It orbits two stars: a Sun-like one and a companion about one-third its size, circling each other every 7.45 days (Orosz et al. 2012). Planet d lies between planets b and c, and the outer two are tightly packed, so no other planet could fit stably between them (Orosz et al. 2019). All orbits are nearly coplanar, within about 2 degrees (Welsh et al. 2015).","history":"The low eccentricities and flat layout argue against violent scattering and suggest gentle migration within the disc (Orosz et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler47d-Welshetal2015","cite":"Welsh et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"William Welsh, Jerome Orosz, Billy Quarles et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Three-Planet Circumbinary System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47d-Oroszetal2019","cite":"Orosz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Nader Haghighipour et al.","title":"Discovery of a Third Transiting Planet in the Kepler-47 Circumbinary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07255","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47d-Oroszetal2012","cite":"Orosz et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Joshua A. Carter et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Transiting Circumbinary Multiplanet System","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5489","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6041 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey, called GOT'EM, targets giant planets on wide orbits that transit only rarely (Heidari et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet first showed up as a single transit in TESS data. Later TESS and CHEOPS observations found more transits and strong timing variations, with a swing of about one hour from the expected arrival times (Heidari et al. 2026). Radial velocities from the APF spectrograph limit its mass to under 28.9 Earth masses (Heidari et al. 2026).","system":"A second planet, TOI-6041 c, orbits every 88 days with a minimum mass of 0.25 Jupiter masses (Heidari et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Explaining the timing swings through planet c requires an eccentricity of about 0.3, and the study also mentions an alternative explanation (Heidari et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6041b-Heidarietal2026","cite":"Heidari et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"N. Heidari, A. Alnajjarine, H. P. Osborn et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT'EM) survey: VII. TOI-6041: A multi-planet system including a warm Neptune exhibiting strong transit-timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE2-TR-L9 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was picked out of archival OGLE-II microlensing survey data, which had not been designed for transit searches (Snellen et al. 2009).","observed":"The transit was re-observed more than 7 years after the old data, and it arrived only 8 minutes from the predicted time (Snellen et al. 2009). The host is a rapidly rotating F3 star (Snellen et al. 2009). It is one of the few known transiting planets that could host a moon above 0.4 Earth masses, within reach of current instruments (Weidner & Horne 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2trl9b-Snellenetal2009","cite":"Snellen et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"I. A. G. Snellen, J. Koppenhoefer, R. F. J. van der Burg et al.","title":"OGLE2-TR-L9b: an exoplanet transiting a rapidly rotating F3 star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.0599","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogle2trl9b-WeidnerHorne2010","cite":"Weidner & Horne 2010","year":"2010","authors":"C. Weidner, K. Horne","title":"Limits on the orbits and masses of moons around currently-known transiting exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4500","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Lupus-TR-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey stared at one field in the Galactic plane for 53 nights to test whether deep, single-field surveys could find hot Jupiters (Weldrake et al. 2008; Bayliss et al. 2009).","observed":"It is the faintest ground-based transiting-planet detection to date (Weldrake et al. 2008). A faint red neighbour 0.4 arcseconds away cannot be fully ruled out as a blended binary, but simulations show only an unusual binary could mimic the data (Weldrake et al. 2008). Of four candidates in the survey, this was the only true planet; two were blends and one was a binary (Bayliss et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":"The velocity signal is only 2.4 sigma, so the mass is not firmly set (Weldrake et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lupustr3b-Weldrakeetal2008","cite":"Weldrake et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"David T. F. Weldrake, Daniel D. R. Bayliss, Penny D. Sackett et al.","title":"Lupus-TR-3b: A Low-Mass Transiting Hot Jupiter in the Galactic Plane?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.1746","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lupustr3b-Baylissetal2009","cite":"Bayliss et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Daniel D. R. Bayliss, David T. F. Weldrake, Penny D. Sackett et al.","title":"The Lupus Transit Survey for Hot Jupiters: Results and Lessons","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.5121","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5120 b":{"text":{"why":"The aim was to expand the sample of well-characterised warm Jupiters, whose orbits are thought to keep clues about formation and migration (Knudstrup et al. 2026).","observed":"Warm Jupiters orbit far enough out that tides have largely not reshaped them, so their eccentricity and tilt should still reflect their past (Knudstrup et al. 2026). TOI-5120 b has a slightly eccentric orbit, with e = 0.10 (Knudstrup et al. 2026). TESS light curves were fitted together with velocities from two ground-based spectrographs.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5120b-Knudstrupetal2026","cite":"Knudstrup et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. Knudstrup, D. Gandolfi, W. D. Cochran et al.","title":"TS23/McDonald and FIES/NOT strike again: Two new warm Jupiter systems and outer companions in a third","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30915","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2447 b":{"text":{"why":"Long-period transiting planets let astronomers study cool atmospheres below 700 K and see how planets migrate inward from farther out than the usual hot, close-in worlds (Gill et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet first showed up as a single 7.29-hour dip of 1.3 per cent in TESS data, together with an earlier dip from 2017 in NGTS ground-based data (Gill et al. 2024). Four more NGTS transits pinned down the long orbit (Gill et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is 414 K, much cooler than most planets found by TESS (Gill et al. 2024). Radial velocity measurements gave a mass close to that of Saturn (Gill et al. 2024).","system":"The star is a bright, Sun-like star (Gill et al. 2024). The team also found a second transit signal in the NGTS data that does not come from this planet, along with changes in the timing of its transits (Gill et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2447b-Gilletal2024","cite":"Gill et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Samuel Gill, Daniel Bayliss, Solène Ulmer-Moll et al.","title":"Correction to: TOI-2447 b / NGTS-29 b: a 69-day Saturn around a Solar analogue","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.07367","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-693 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Small shifts in when this planet crosses its star, and in how long each crossing lasts, revealed a second, non-transiting body in the system (Masuda 2017). Computer modelling of those shifts gives the outer companion a mass of about 150 Jupiter masses and an eccentric orbit (e = 0.47) that comes within 1.5 au of the star (Masuda 2017). The inner planet itself has a moderately eccentric orbit (Masuda 2017).","system":"The inner and outer orbits are tilted against each other by about 53 or 134 degrees (Masuda 2017). Kepler-693 b was one of 23 single-transiting warm Jupiters whose timings were checked, and one of only three with clearly non-regular timing shifts (Masuda 2017).","history":"The large tilt is likely to drive slow swings in the shape of the inner orbit, pulling it close enough to the star to matter for how warm Jupiters form (Masuda 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler693b-Masuda2017","cite":"Masuda 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Eccentric Companions to Kepler-448b and Kepler-693b: Clues to the Formation of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-5 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a low density of 0.66 g per cubic centimetre and a mass near the average of known transiting planets (Bakos et al. 2007). Spitzer measured its infrared brightness at two wavelengths as it passed behind its star, and the orbit was confirmed to be circular (Wallack et al. 2021). Measurements of the transit depth in several colours gave a flat spectrum across optical wavelengths (Turner et al. 2017). A separate observing run instead saw signs of Rayleigh scattering, where small particles or gas scatter blue light (PeQueen 2018).","system":"Infrared excess emission from the star suggests warm dust in rings between about 0.1 and 25 au, a rare find for a system with a transiting planet (Krivov et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"The two optical studies disagree about the atmosphere, so its haze or cloud content is not settled (Turner et al. 2017; PeQueen 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp5b-Bakosetal2007","cite":"Bakos et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, A. Shporer, A. Pál et al.","title":"HAT-P-5b: A Jupiter-like Hot Jupiter Transiting a Bright Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.1841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp5b-Wallacketal2021","cite":"Wallack et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Drake Deming","title":"Trends in Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp5b-Turneretal2017","cite":"Turner et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Robin M. Leiter, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Investigating the physical properties of transiting hot Jupiters with the 1.5-m Kuiper Telescope","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07909","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp5b-PeQueen2018","cite":"PeQueen 2018","year":"2018","authors":"David Jeffrey PeQueen, Jackson Lane Cole, Cristilyn N. Gardner et al.","title":"Characterizing Giant Exoplanets through Multiwavelength Transit Observations: HAT-P-5 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp5b-Krivovetal2011","cite":"Krivov et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. V. Krivov, M. Reidemeister, S. Fiedler et al.","title":"Warm dust in systems with transiting planets","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2011","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-29 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 0.78 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.11 Jupiter radii, so it is lighter than Jupiter but of similar size (Buchhave et al. 2011). Radial velocity monitoring with Keck found a steady drift in the star's motion, which means something massive and far away is pulling on the system (Knutson et al. 2014). Later work counted this system among eccentric planetary systems and measured the star's spin alignment with HARPS-N spectra (Mancini et al. 2022). Extra photometry from a 35 cm telescope gave more precise planet parameters (Rocchetto & Fossey 2013).","system":"A survey for distant companions of hot Jupiters, including this one, estimates such companions at 1 to 500 Jupiter masses, usually 1 to 75 au away (Knutson et al. 2014). The orbit of the hot Jupiter is eccentric, with e between about 0.1 and 0.3 in this group of systems (Mancini et al. 2022).","history":"A massive distant companion could have shaped the inner planet's orbit (Knutson et al. 2014).","open":"The nature of the distant companion is not pinned down (Knutson et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp29b-Buchhaveetal2011","cite":"Buchhave et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"L. A. Buchhave, G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"Hat-P-28b and Hat-P-29b: Two Sub-Jupiter Mass Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.1813","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp29b-Knutsonetal2014","cite":"Knutson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Heather A. Knutson, Benjamin J. Fulton, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"Friends of Hot Jupiters. I. A Radial Velocity Search for Massive, Long-period Companions to Close-in Gas Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.2954","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp29b-Mancinietal2022","cite":"Mancini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXXVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and revising the physical and orbital parameters of the HAT-P-15, HAT-P-17, HAT-P-21, HAT-P-26, HAT-P-29 eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10549","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp29b-RocchettoFossey2013","cite":"Rocchetto & Fossey 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Rocchetto, S. Fossey","title":"Improved characterisation of exoplanets discovered in wide-field surveys: HAT-P-29b and HAT-P-31b","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1295 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed as one of four hot Jupiters using spectrographs at Wendelstein, Kitt Peak and the Fred Lawrence Whipple Observatory (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). The four planets have orbits of 2.4 to 4.0 days, radii of 1.29 to 1.64 Jupiter radii and masses of 0.6 to 1.5 Jupiter masses (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). The team looked for other planets in these systems and found none (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). The sizes of all four fit a current empirical study of why hot Jupiters are puffed up (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1295b-Ehrhardtetal2024","cite":"Ehrhardt et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Juliana Ehrhardt, Luis Thomas, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"Confirmation of four hot Jupiters detected by TESS using follow-up spectroscopy from MaHPS at Wendelstein together with NEID and TRES","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04383","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4379 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looks for giant planets around evolved giant stars (Saunders et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was part of the first systematic study of how hot Jupiters orbiting subgiant stars are aligned with their star's spin, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, which reveals the tilt as the planet crosses the star (Saunders et al. 2024). Hot Jupiters around subgiants that have cooled below about 6250 K are found aligned or nearly aligned with the star's spin (Saunders et al. 2024). Observations from space gave it 2-minute-cadence light curves in two TESS sectors (Saunders et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The alignment suggests that once a star grows a deep outer convective layer, tides quickly straighten out the orbit (Saunders et al. 2024).","open":"Only an upper limit on how long the realignment takes has been set (Saunders et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4379b-Saundersetal2024","cite":"Saunders et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Nicholas Saunders, Samuel K. Grunblatt, Ashley Chontos et al.","title":"TESS Giants Transiting Giants. VI. Newly Discovered Hot Jupiters Provide Evidence for Efficient Obliquity Damping after the Main Sequence","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21650","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 45364 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Precise HARPS measurements show two planets orbiting a Sun-like star, with minimum masses of 0.187 and 0.658 Jupiter masses at 0.681 and 0.897 au (Correia et al. 2009). This planet is the inner one (Correia et al. 2009). The two orbits are slightly eccentric, with e of 0.168 and 0.097 (Correia et al. 2009).","system":"The two planets are locked in a 3:2 resonance: the inner planet orbits three times while the outer orbits twice (Correia et al. 2009). The resonance prevents close encounters and keeps the system stable for 5 billion years (Correia et al. 2009). It was the first such resonance seen among exoplanets, with a Solar System analogue in Neptune and Pluto (Correia et al. 2009).","history":"The resonance gives clues to how the planets formed and migrated (Correia et al. 2009).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd45364b-Correiaetal2009","cite":"Correia et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. C. M. Correia, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XVI. HD 45364, a pair of planets in a 3:2 mean motion resonance","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0597","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-287 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a Saturn-like mass of 0.317 Jupiter masses and a very eccentric orbit, with e of 0.476 (Jordan et al. 2019). Its time-averaged equilibrium temperature is about 800 K (Jordan et al. 2019). That is cool enough that its structure should not be puffed up by starlight, which makes it one of few such giants around nearby stars (Jordan et al. 2019). The CHEOPS space telescope also observed its transits, as part of a set of seven planets (Borsato et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a metal-rich G dwarf, with [Fe/H] = 0.20 (Jordan et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Follow-up studies could help test ideas about how giant planets migrate into close orbits (Jordan et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2287b-Jordanetal2019","cite":"Jordan et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm, Néstor Espinoza et al.","title":"K2-287 b: An Eccentric Warm Saturn Transiting a G-dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08879","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2287b-Borsatoetal2021","cite":"Borsato et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L. Borsato, G. Piotto, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: 7 transiting exoplanets CHEOPS light curves (Borsato+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-77 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No eclipse of the planet behind its star was detected, down to a depth of about 10 parts per million (Gandolfi et al. 2013). That limits its geometric albedo to 0.087 or less and its Bond albedo to 0.058 or less, so the planet reflects very little light (Gandolfi et al. 2013). It is moderately inflated, with a density of 0.603 g per cubic centimetre (Gandolfi et al. 2013). Follow-up spectroscopy was also used to rule out false positives (Endl et al. 2014).","system":"The host is a slowly rotating G5 dwarf with a spin period of 36 days, an age of about 7.5 billion years and a high metal content (Gandolfi et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler77b-Gandolfietal2013","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"D. Gandolfi, H. Parviainen, M. Fridlund et al.","title":"Kepler-77b: a very low albedo, Saturn-mass transiting planet around a metal-rich solar-like star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3891","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler77b-Endletal2014","cite":"Endl et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Michael Endl, Douglas A. Caldwell, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Kepler-424 b: A \"Lonely\" Hot Jupiter that Found a Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8156","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 117618 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with two others from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search, all of them low-mass, with minimum masses from about half of Saturn's to half of Jupiter's (Tinney et al. 2005). The parent stars are quiet main-sequence G dwarfs, and HD 117618 has roughly solar metal content (Tinney et al. 2005). These planets cause Doppler shifts of 10.6 to 19 metres per second, similar to those of Jupiter analogues (Tinney et al. 2005). Data spanning 6 years gave confidence in the survey's ability to find such planets (Tinney et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd117618b-Tinneyetal2005","cite":"Tinney et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Three Low-Mass Planets from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/428661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-30 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter on a nearly circular 9.06-day orbit around a G3V star, about 0.08 au away (Bordé et al. 2020). It has about the size of Jupiter but a mass of 2.90 Jupiter masses, giving it a density of 3.45 g per cubic centimetre (Bordé et al. 2020). It is about ten times denser than the second planet in the same paper, CoRoT-31 b, even though both have similar radii (Bordé et al. 2020). The team also tested a claim that stars with planets are more depleted in lithium, and found that neither system supports it (Bordé et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot30b-Bordetal2020","cite":"Bordé et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Bordé, R. F. Díaz, O. Creevey et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXIX. The hot Jupiters CoRoT-30 b and CoRoT-31 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201732393","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5789 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of the discovery paper covers a second planet, TOI-5789 c, which crosses the star every 12.93 days (Bonomo et al. 2026). The work used 141 radial velocity measurements from HARPS-N, plus TESS photometry (Bonomo et al. 2026). The star is a bright, magnetically inactive K1 dwarf, with V = 7.3 (Bonomo et al. 2026). Planet c is a sub-Neptune of 2.86 Earth radii and 5.00 Earth masses, a good target for JWST and Ariel (Bonomo et al. 2026).","system":"The system holds several planets whose orbits are mutually inclined, meaning they do not lie in a single flat plane (Bonomo et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"It is unclear whether sub-Neptunes like planet c are mostly gas-rich worlds or water worlds (Bonomo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5789b-Bonomoetal2026","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, L. Naponiello, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXI. A sub-Neptune suitable for atmospheric characterization in a multiplanet and mutually inclined system orbiting the bright K dwarf TOI-5789 (HIP 99452)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"ZTF J1230-2655 b":{"text":{"why":"Pairs of a white dwarf and a close companion are thought to form when the star that became the white dwarf swelled up and swallowed its companion, in a stage called common envelope evolution (Parsons et al. 2025).","observed":"The object is one of two eclipsing systems in which a white dwarf, the dead core of a Sun-like star, is paired with a brown dwarf of very low mass (Parsons et al. 2025). Earlier white dwarf and brown dwarf pairs usually had companions heavier than about 50 Jupiter masses (Parsons et al. 2025). The companion has survived the common envelope stage, which is an event that often destroys such objects (Parsons et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ztfj12302655b-Parsonsetal2025","cite":"Parsons et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. G. Parsons, A. J. Brown, S. L. Casewell et al.","title":"Two almost planetary mass survivors of common envelope evolution","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14333","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HU Aqr AB c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's existence rests on eclipse timing, which tracks the exact times that the two stars eclipse each other (Gozdziewski et al. 2017). The analysis gathered timing data from the literature, including new measurements published by Bours et al. in 2014, and added new data (Gozdziewski et al. 2017). Adding those measurements changed the best-fitting parameters only slightly (Gozdziewski et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's existence has been questioned."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-huaqrabc-Gozdziewskietal2017","cite":"Gozdziewski et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"K. Gozdziewski, A. Slowikowska, D. Dimitrov et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HU Aqr planetary system mid-egress moments (Gozdziewski+, 2015)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1011 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a small rocky world confirmed using 187 high-precision radial velocities from the MAROON-X and KPF spectrographs (Brinkman et al. 2025). It was studied alongside five other rocky planets whose masses were updated, to test whether such planets really differ so much in composition (Brinkman et al. 2025). The new masses point to compositions closer to Earth's (Brinkman et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The planets' ratio of iron to rock-building elements was compared with the same ratio in their host stars, and the two appear similar (Brinkman et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1011b-Brinkmanetal2025","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"The Compositions of Rocky Planets in Close-in Orbits Tend to Be Earth-like","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00213","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-82 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is inflated, with a radius of 1.7 to 1.8 Jupiter radii, as are two other hot Jupiters reported in the same paper (West et al. 2016). It orbits a hot F-type star in 1.8 to 3.9 days, and the star has already left the main sequence (West et al. 2016). The planet fits a known trend that strongly irradiated hot Jupiters are the most puffed up (West et al. 2016).","system":"Hot stars with radii of 1.7 to 2.2 times the Sun's are the hosts of this group (West et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Non-inflated planets around such evolved stars may be missed, because their transits are too shallow for ground-based surveys (West et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp82b-Westetal2016","cite":"West et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. G. West, C. Hellier, J.-M. Almenara et al.","title":"Three irradiated and bloated hot Jupiters:. WASP-76b, WASP-82b, and WASP-90b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5607","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164922 b":{"text":{"why":"Researchers want to know how common Solar System analogues are, with small inner planets and outer gas giants (Benatti et al. 2020).","observed":"This planet is the outer giant, at about 1200 days and roughly 2 au from its star (Benatti et al. 2020). More than 300 HARPS-N spectra of the bright G9 star, combined with older data into almost 700 radial velocities, revealed a close-in super-Earth, HD 164922 d (Benatti et al. 2020). The team measured the star's spin period and used a statistical model to separate stellar activity from planet signals (Benatti et al. 2020).","system":"The system has a Saturn-mass long-period companion, a Neptune-mass planet with a period of about 76 days, and a close-in super-Earth (Benatti et al. 2020). A dynamical analysis tested the stability of the system and where extra planets could fit (Benatti et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"How often Solar System analogues occur is still an open question (Benatti et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164922b-Benattietal2020","cite":"Benatti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Benatti, M. Damasso, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XXIII. HD 164922 d: close-in super-Earth discovered with HARPS-N in a system with a long-period Saturn mass companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.03368","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 1397 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Jovian planet on an eccentric 11.5-day orbit around a bright subgiant, with e of 0.216 (Brahm et al. 2019). Two teams measured its mass independently: 0.415 Jupiter masses (Nielsen et al. 2019) and 0.367 Jupiter masses (Brahm et al. 2019). High-resolution imaging ruled out nearby eclipsing binaries as a false alarm (Nielsen et al. 2019). It is among the brightest systems known to host a transiting planet, making detailed follow-up possible (Brahm et al. 2019).","system":"The host star has left the main sequence, with a radius of 2.34 times the Sun's and an age of about 4.5 billion years (Brahm et al. 2019).","history":"Characterising such eccentric giants helps test theories of giant planet formation and migration (Nielsen et al. 2019).","open":"A second, longer radial velocity signal is probably stellar activity, but a second planet cannot be ruled out without long-term monitoring (Brahm et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd1397b-Brahmetal2019","cite":"Brahm et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Néstor Espinoza, Andrés Jordán et al.","title":"HD 1397b: A Transiting Warm Giant Planet Orbiting A V = 7.8 mag Subgiant Star Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.02156","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd1397b-Nielsenetal2019","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, F. Bouchy, O. Turner et al.","title":"A Jovian planet in an eccentric 11.5 day orbit around HD 1397 discovered by TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.01882","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-68 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with two other hot Jupiters, WASP-73 b and WASP-88 b, in one paper (Delrez et al. 2014). Its mass is close to that of Jupiter and its radius is a little larger. Its size is typical of hot Jupiters with similar masses (Delrez et al. 2014). The star is an evolved G0-type star, one that has begun to swell with age (Delrez et al. 2014). Follow-up used brightness measurements from the TRAPPIST and Euler telescopes and velocity measurements from the Euler/CORALIE spectrograph (Delrez et al. 2014). The three planets orbit evolved late-F or early-G stars every 4 to 5 days, yet they differ greatly in bulk properties (Delrez 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp68b-Delrezetal2014","cite":"Delrez et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"L. Delrez, V. Van Grootel, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Transiting planets from WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST. WASP-68 b, WASP-73 b, and WASP-88 b, three hot Jupiters transiting evolved solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1827","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp68b-Delrez2016","cite":"Delrez 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Laetitia Delrez","title":"Detection and Characterization of Transiting Hot Jupiters","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1115 b":{"text":{"why":"Close-in giant planets often show tilted orbits, while the solar system's planets are aligned with the Sun's spin (Barnes 2015).","observed":"The host star spins fast enough that its disc is brighter at the poles than at the equator, an effect called gravity darkening. This shapes the transit light curve and shows the planet's path relative to the star's spin. The measured tilt of the orbit, as seen on the sky, is 1 degree, give or take 13 degrees, so the orbit is aligned with the star's equator (Barnes et al. 2015). Only one other super-Earth had such a measurement, 55 Cancri e, which is misaligned. This planet is the first aligned one (Barnes et al. 2015). The result was first reported while the planet was still a candidate (Barnes et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"At 23.55 days the orbit lies beyond the reach of tides, so the alignment is probably the original one from formation (Barnes & Ahlers 2015).","open":"Whether many solar-system-like rocky planets with periods longer than 10 days orbit near their stars' equators is still to be tested (Barnes & Ahlers 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1115b-Barnes2015","cite":"Barnes 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Jason W. Barnes, Jonathon Ahlers","title":"KOI2138.01: First of the Intermediate-Period Spin-Orbit Aligned Super-Earths","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1115b-Barnesetal2015","cite":"Barnes et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Jason W. Barnes, Jonathon Ahlers","title":"KOI2138.01: First of the Intermediate-Period Spin-Orbit Aligned Super-Earths","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1115b-BarnesAhlers2015","cite":"Barnes & Ahlers 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Jason W. Barnes, Jonathon Ahlers","title":"KOI2138.01: First of the Intermediate-Period Spin-Orbit Aligned Super-Earths","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2379 b":{"text":{"why":"Short-period gas giants are significantly rarer around stars less massive than the Sun, so a giant planet around an early M dwarf is unusual (Bryant et al. 2024).","observed":"Radial velocity measurements, which track the small back-and-forth motion of the star, gave its mass. It is about 5.8 times the mass of Jupiter, yet only about the size of Jupiter, so it is very dense for a gas giant (Bryant et al. 2024). The host is a low-mass star of early M type (Bryant et al. 2024). A second giant, TOI-2384 b, was reported in the same paper around a similar star (Bryant et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2379b-Bryantetal2024","cite":"Bryant et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"TOI-2379 b and TOI-2384 b: two super-Jupiter mass planets transiting low-mass host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28109 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star sits in the part of the sky that TESS watches continuously, which helps in spotting planets with long periods (Dransfield et al. 2022). TESS first identified the two outer planets, and the planet with a 22.89-day period was found later in the same data (Dransfield et al. 2022). Ground-based photometry from Hazelwood, ASTEP in Antarctica and the Las Cumbres network confirmed all three, including a full 9-hour transit seen from Antarctica (Dransfield et al. 2022). Masses came from timing changes in the transits and from precise velocity measurements with ESPRESSO and HARPS (Dransfield et al. 2022).","system":"The star hosts three transiting Neptune-sized planets. The two outer ones have periods close to a 3:2 ratio, which makes their transit times drift by up to 60 minutes (Dransfield et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28109b-Dransfieldetal2022","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Georgina Dransfield, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Tristan Guillot et al.","title":"HD 28109 hosts a trio of transiting Neptunian planets including a near-resonant pair, confirmed by ASTEP from Antarctica","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09046","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-190 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with Doppler tomography, which tracks how the planet's shadow moves across the spinning star's disc. It is the first hot Jupiter confirmed this way with an orbital period longer than 5 days (Temple et al. 2019). The same method shows that the orbit is tilted slightly against the star's spin, by 21 degrees, give or take 6 degrees (Temple et al. 2019). The planet is mildly inflated, meaning a little larger than its mass alone would predict (Temple et al. 2019). It orbits a mildly evolved F6 star (Temple et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp190b-Templeetal2019","cite":"Temple et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Y. Temple, C. Hellier, Y. Almleaky et al.","title":"WASP-190b: Tomographic Discovery of a Transiting Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05742","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-22 b":{"text":{"why":"The orbital tilt of hot Jupiters can hold clues to how they migrated inward (Mancini et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet's transit was observed in the near-ultraviolet, and these were the first ground-based light curves in that range for it. No asymmetry was seen in the transits of any of the 15 planets in that study (Turner et al. 2016). The tilt of its orbit against the star's equator was measured with HARPS-N, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a small distortion in the star's velocity signal as the planet crosses it (Mancini et al. 2018). Radial velocity data from the Keck telescope show the star accelerating, which points to another body farther out. Its mass is only roughly known (Knutson et al. 2014).","system":"HAT-P-22 is one of six systems where an acceleration of the star had not been reported before. Companions in such systems typically lie between 1 and 75 AU from the star (Knutson et al. 2014).","history":"A distant massive companion could have shaped the inner giant's orbit (Knutson et al. 2014).","open":"What the distant companion is, and how heavy, is not pinned down (Knutson et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp22b-Mancinietal2018","cite":"Mancini et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect of transiting planetary systems HAT-P-3, HAT-P-12, HAT-P-22, WASP-39, and WASP-60","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.03859","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp22b-Turneretal2016","cite":"Turner et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Kyle A. Pearson, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Ground-based near-UV observations of 15 transiting exoplanets: constraints on their atmospheres and no evidence for asymmetrical transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02587","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp22b-Knutsonetal2014","cite":"Knutson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Heather A. Knutson, Benjamin J. Fulton, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"Friends of Hot Jupiters. I. A Radial Velocity Search for Massive, Long-period Companions to Close-in Gas Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.2954","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 393 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The CHARA Array, a set of telescopes linked to act as one very large one, resolved the star's disc. Its width is 0.564 milliarcseconds, and its temperature comes out near 3515 K, which agrees with spectroscopic measurements (Schaefer et al. 2018). The star has a mass of 0.42 solar masses (Schaefer et al. 2018).","system":"The star belongs to the AB Doradus moving group, a set of young stars that move together through space (Schaefer et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"The star lies close to the zero-age main sequence, so it is unclear whether it is still contracting or expanding. Its age is therefore uncertain, with a lower limit of 0.06 billion years (Schaefer et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj393b-Schaeferetal2018","cite":"Schaefer et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"G. H. Schaefer, R. J. White, E. K. Baines et al.","title":"AB Dor Moving Group Stars Resolved with the CHARA Array","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/aaba71","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-59 c":{"text":{"why":"The inner and outer planets together make a system that is valuable for understanding planet migration (Sarkis et al. 2018).","observed":"This is the first multi-planet system found by the HATSouth network. HATS-59 b is a transiting hot Jupiter, and HATS-59 c is a cold, massive giant farther out (Sarkis et al. 2018). The outer planet shows up only through the star's velocity wobble, not as a transit. Its minimum mass is about 12.7 times that of Jupiter, and its orbit is circular (Sarkis et al. 2018).","system":"The inner planet is on a slightly eccentric orbit with a 5.4-day period. Most systems with a hot Jupiter hold only that one planet, so a massive outer companion is unusual (Sarkis et al. 2018). The star is Sun-like (Sarkis et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats59c-Sarkisetal2018","cite":"Sarkis et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"P. Sarkis, Th. Henning, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-59b,c: A Transiting Hot Jupiter and a Cold Massive Giant Planet around a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.05925","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-419 c":{"text":{"why":"The system is peculiar. It holds two nearly coplanar, highly eccentric gas giants whose orbits are stretched in nearly opposite directions (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","observed":"Work on this system so far uses simulations rather than new observations. They ask how two giants could end up on such orbits, and what that says about the disc of gas and dust in which they formed (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","system":"The system's two giant planets move on highly eccentric orbits in nearly the same plane. The long axes of their orbits swing back and forth around an anti-aligned position (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","history":"The orbits may result from the pull of a massive disc as it slowly dispersed. Simulations need an initial disc of at least 95 Jupiter masses lasting at least 10,000 years (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020). Such discs are stable against collapse, so both planets probably grew by core accretion and then migrated through the disc (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler419c-AliDibPetrovich2020","cite":"Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mohamad Ali-Dib, Cristobal Petrovich","title":"Constraining protoplanetary discs with exoplanetary dynamics: Kepler-419 as an example","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-12 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about the mass of Jupiter but is much larger. Its density is only about 0.2 grams per cubic centimetre, making it one of the most inflated giant planets known (Stevens et al. 2017). The host star is mildly evolved and hot (Stevens et al. 2017). The system was also found independently by another survey as WASP-86. The two teams measured very different properties, with the planet's radius differing by a factor of three (Southworth & Faedi 2022). TESS data gave values in between, a radius of 1.38 Jupiter radii (Southworth & Faedi 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"The transit is long and shallow, which ground telescopes handle poorly. That caused the disagreement over its depth and duration, and so the planet's true size (Southworth & Faedi 2022). It is a good target for studying the atmosphere by transmission spectroscopy (Southworth & Faedi 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt12b-Stevensetal2017","cite":"Stevens et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Daniel J. Stevens, Karen A. Collins, B. Scott Gaudi et al.","title":"KELT-12b: A P ∼ 5 day, Highly Inflated Hot Jupiter Transiting a Mildly Evolved Hot Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04714","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt12b-SouthworthFaedi2022","cite":"Southworth & Faedi 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Southworth, F. Faedi","title":"The transiting planetary system WASP-86/KELT-12: TESS provides the casting vote","journal":"The Observatory","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-55 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery used the HATNet network of small telescopes, with velocity measurements from SOPHIE and the Nordic Optical Telescope (Juncher et al. 2015). Later teams have followed its transits. Follow-up with the Las Cumbres telescope network refined its transit times for future observations (Edwards et al. 2021). Four-colour photometry with the MuSCAT2 camera, together with TESS data, aimed to improve its transit parameters and to search for timing variations. The study also ranked targets for follow-up transmission spectroscopy (Kang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp55b-Juncheretal2015","cite":"Juncher et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. Juncher, L. A. Buchhave, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-55b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a Sun-Like Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.03734","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp55b-Edwardsetal2021","cite":"Edwards et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Billy Edwards, Cynthia Ho, Hannah Osborne et al.","title":"Original Research By Young Twinkle Students (ORBYTS): Ephemeris Refinement of Transiting Exoplanets III","journal":"Astronomy: Theory, Observations and Methods","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10350","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp55b-Kangetal2024","cite":"Kang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. Kang, G. Chen, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Simultaneous multicolour transit photometry of hot Jupiters HAT-P-19b, HAT-P-51b, HAT-P-55b, and HAT-P-65b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03715","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-13 b":{"text":{"why":"Its eccentricity could tell astronomers about the planet's interior (Hardy et al. 2013).","observed":"The discovery paper reported a close-in hot Jupiter on a near-circular orbit together with a massive outer planet on a highly eccentric one. The outer planet was only seen through the star's wobble, and its transits have not been observed and may not occur (Bakos et al. 2009). The host star is rather metal-rich (Bakos et al. 2009). Spitzer observed two secondary eclipses, when the planet passes behind the star. These suggest a solar-abundance composition with no temperature inversion in its atmosphere (Hardy et al. 2013). Searches for timing variations found little evidence of a third planet (Sutaria 2012).","system":"The outer planet has a period of 428.5 days and a minimum mass of about 15 Jupiter masses (Bakos et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"If the orbits of the two planets are aligned, the small eccentricity of planet b could reveal how centrally condensed it is, which constrains models of its interior (Hardy et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp13b-Hardyetal2013","cite":"Hardy et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ryan A. Hardy, J. Harrington, M. R. Hardin et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipses of HAT-P-13b","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #45","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp13b-Bakosetal2009","cite":"Bakos et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, A. W. Howard, R. W. Noyes et al.","title":"HAT-P-13b,c: A Transiting Hot Jupiter with a Massive Outer Companion on an Eccentric Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3525","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp13b-Sutaria2012","cite":"Sutaria 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Firoza Sutaria","title":"Search for transit timing variations in some Exoplanet systems.","journal":"39th COSPAR Scientific Assembly","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5882 b":{"text":{"why":"The companion is a brown dwarf, an object between a planet and a star in mass. It belongs to a sample of more than 50 transiting brown dwarfs used to test how planets and stars form (Vowell et al. 2025).","observed":"It is a massive brown dwarf of about 22 Jupiter masses on a 7.1-day orbit (Kotten et al. 2026). Its host is a subgiant, a star beginning to swell as it ages, with an unusually high lithium content. Its lithium ranks in the 98.4th percentile of a comparison group of 61 subgiants (Kotten et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The lithium could come from the star swallowing a super-Earth or Neptune-mass planet (Kotten et al. 2026). Simulations find engulfment likely, but only 5% of runs engulf a planet within the short time that the lithium signal stays visible (Charalambous et al. 2026).","open":"Whether a planet was really swallowed is not settled (Charalambous et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5882b-Vowelletal2025","cite":"Vowell et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Noah Vowell, Joseph E. Rodriguez, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Eleven New Transiting Brown Dwarfs and Very-low-mass Stars from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09795","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5882b-Kottenetal2026","cite":"Kotten et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Brooke Kotten, Melinda Soares-Furtado, Ricardo Yarza et al.","title":"Lithium Enrichment in a Subgiant Star with a Brown Dwarf Companion: A Planetary Engulfment Candidate","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21407","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5882b-Charalambousetal2026","cite":"Charalambous et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"C. Charalambous, C. Aguilera-Gómez, S. Urrutia et al.","title":"Dynamical constraints on planet engulfment as the origin of lithium enhancement in TOI-5882","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05566","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-138 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has an unusually low density, which makes it a target for atmosphere studies (Dragomir et al. 2014). A Spitzer observing proposal planned transit-depth measurements, adding to approved Hubble observations. The aim was to tell a clear, hydrogen-dominated atmosphere from a compact atmosphere rich in heavy elements, and to constrain any high clouds (Dragomir et al. 2014). A model of core-powered mass loss lists it among planets that could be losing their atmospheres today, at a rate of at least 10 million grams per second (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler138d-Dragomiretal2014","cite":"Dragomir et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Diana Dragomir, Bjoern Benneke, Ian Crossfield et al.","title":"A Comparative Study of Super-Earth Atmospheres","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138d-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1468 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with radial velocity measurements from the CARMENES and MAROON-X spectrographs, plus ground-based transit photometry (Chaturvedi et al. 2022). Its density fits a mostly rocky composition (Chaturvedi et al. 2022). The James Webb Space Telescope observed three eclipses at 15 micrometres (Meier Valdes et al. 2025). Such measurements probe whether low-temperature rocky planets around nearby stars have atmospheres (Connors et al. 2025). A reanalysis compared the eclipse data of TOI-1468 b with different atmospheric and surface compositions (Connors et al. 2025).","system":"A second planet, TOI-1468 c, has a 15.5-day period and a rocky core with a hydrogen and helium envelope. The two planets lie on opposite sides of the radius valley, the gap between rocky planets and gas-rich ones (Chaturvedi et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1468b-Chaturvedietal2022","cite":"Chaturvedi et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. Chaturvedi, P. Bluhm, E. Nagel et al.","title":"TOI-1468: A system of two transiting planets, a super-Earth and a mini-Neptune, on opposite sides of the radius valley","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10351","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1468b-Valdesetal2025","cite":"Valdes et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. A. Meier Valdes, B.-O. Demory, H. Diamond-Lowe et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Hot Rocks Survey II. TOI-1468 b fluxes (Meier Valdes+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1468b-Connorsetal2025","cite":"Connors et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Nicholas J. Connors, Christopher Monaghan, Björn Benneke et al.","title":"Uniform Reanalysis of JWST MIRI 15 μm Exoplanet Eclipse Observations Using Frame-normalized Principal Component Analysis","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02052","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2023-BLG-0469L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet shows up as a short dip in the lensing light curve near the event's peak (Han et al. 2024). The mass ratio between planet and host is about 2.5 in 10,000. Bayesian analysis gives a planet of roughly 0.12 Jupiter masses, orbiting a star of about 0.47 solar masses at about 7 kiloparsecs (Han et al. 2024). The planet-host separation is ambiguous because of a degeneracy, where an inner and an outer solution fit equally well (Han et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The projected separation from the host remains uncertain, so no orbital period is known (Han et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2023blg0469lb-Hanetal2024","cite":"Han et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Cheongho Han, Youn Kil Jung, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"Three sub-Jovian-mass microlensing planets: MOA-2022-BLG-563Lb, KMT-2023-BLG-0469Lb, and KMT-2023-BLG-0735Lb","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202347951","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-21 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive hot Jupiter with about 2.4 times Jupiter's mass, orbiting a metal-poor K dwarf. Among known hot Jupiters at similar temperatures it has one of the largest radii despite its large mass (Alves et al. 2022). Structure models without inflation suggest its atmosphere is puffed up by about 21 per cent. Inflation models reproduce the observed radius, which points to starlight heating as the likely cause (Alves et al. 2022). Its density is among the largest of giant planets around metal-poor hosts (Alves et al. 2022).","system":"The host star is about 10 billion years old, rotates every 17.9 days and shows low activity (Alves et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts21b-Alvesetal2022","cite":"Alves et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Douglas R. Alves, James S. Jenkins, Jose I. Vines et al.","title":"NGTS-21b: an inflated Super-Jupiter orbiting a metal-poor K dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.01027","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 f":{"text":{"why":"Convergent disc migration is thought to build chains of planets locked in orbital resonances, which later disruption can destroy. TOI-1136 may preserve one almost untouched (Dai et al. 2023).","observed":"Timing changes in the transits gave the masses of the planets, which range from 3 to 8 Earth masses (Dai et al. 2023). They show the planets are in true resonances, with resonant angles that oscillate regularly. A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement of planet d shows the star's spin is aligned with the planets' orbital planes (Dai et al. 2023).","system":"The star is a young G star, about 700 million years old, with at least six transiting planets of 2 to 5 Earth radii. Period ratios near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2 depart from exact values by only about one part in 10,000. It is the first resonant chain known to include the second-order 7:5 resonance between two first-order ones (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"The aligned orbits and the lack of a stellar companion suggest the chain formed in a quiet, isolated disc. No flyby, disc warp or major asymmetry disturbed it (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136f-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-17 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits an old, main-sequence star with an age of about 10.7 billion years, which makes it a useful object for studying how planets evolve (Csizmadia et al. 2011). Its orbit is circular (Csizmadia et al. 2011). Its mass is about 2.4 times that of Jupiter. Its internal composition is not well constrained and could range from pure hydrogen and helium to a mix with about 380 Earth masses of heavier elements (Csizmadia et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"How much of the planet is made of heavy elements is unknown (Csizmadia et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot17b-Csizmadiaetal2011","cite":"Csizmadia et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Sz. Csizmadia, C. Moutou, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XVII. The hot Jupiter CoRoT-17b: a very old planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.4393","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AU Mic b":{"text":{"why":"The star is only about 22 million years old, so the planets sit in a rare young system where irradiation and mass loss can be studied together (Feinstein et al. 2022).","observed":"The Hubble Space Telescope watched the host star in far-ultraviolet light for over 5 hours and caught 13 flares, each releasing 10^29 to 10^31 erg per second (Feinstein et al. 2022). Flares brighten the shortest ultraviolet wavelengths a hundredfold (Feinstein et al. 2022). From this radiation the authors estimate that AU Mic b loses atmosphere at a baseline rate of about 10^8 grams per second (Feinstein et al. 2022). Earlier ground photometry showed the star is covered in star-spots and flares often (Hebb et al. 2007).","system":"The star is an active, young M dwarf, and its planets orbit inside a debris disc of leftover dust (Feinstein et al. 2022). The disc is seen almost edge-on (Arnold et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"How strongly the star's ultraviolet and X-ray output strips the atmospheres of the planets at different distances remains to be measured (Feinstein et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-aumicb-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Kevin France, Allison Youngblood et al.","title":"AU Microscopii in the Far-UV: Observations in Quiescence, during Flares, and Implications for AU Mic b and c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09606","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumicb-Hebbetal2007","cite":"Hebb et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Leslie Hebb, Larry Petro, Holland C. Ford et al.","title":"A search for planets transiting the M-dwarf debris disc host, AU Microscopii","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.3584","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumicb-Arnoldetal2022","cite":"Arnold et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessica A. Arnold, Alycia J. Weinberger, Gorden Videen et al.","title":"Stumbling over Planetary Building Blocks: AU Microscopii as an Example of the Challenge of Retrieving Debris-disk Dust Properties","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.12264","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 41004 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found while following up a long-term drift in the motion of the brighter star, and the new measurements fit a planet of about 2.5 Jupiter masses on an eccentric orbit (Zucker et al. 2004). The system is unusual because it holds both a planet around one star and a brown dwarf around the other (Zucker et al. 2004). Computer studies of the orbit find it stable and regular for at least ten million years in a flat configuration, despite the pull of the companion star (Satyal & Musielak 2016).","system":"The host star has a fainter red-dwarf companion about 22 AU away, and that red dwarf is itself orbited closely by a brown dwarf of about 18.64 Jupiter masses, 0.0177 AU away (Satyal & Musielak 2016). Satellite photometry shows a 31-hour brightness cycle, probably from the red dwarf spun up by its brown dwarf (Ayres & Buzasi 2021).","history":"The red dwarf may someday swallow its brown dwarf partner (Ayres & Buzasi 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd41004ab-Zuckeretal2004","cite":"Zucker et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"S. Zucker, T. Mazeh, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"Multi-order TODCOR: Application to observations taken with the CORALIE echelle spectrograph. II. A planet in the system <ASTROBJ>HD 41004</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20040384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd41004ab-SatyalMusielak2016","cite":"Satyal & Musielak 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. Satyal, Z. E. Musielak","title":"Stability of a planet in the HD 41004 binary system","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/asna.201512305","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd41004ab-AyresBuzasi2021","cite":"Ayres & Buzasi 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Tom Ayres, Derek Buzasi","title":"HD 41004: A Photometric Sheep in Wolf's Clothing?","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ac3255","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63935 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it also ranks planets by how well their atmospheres could be observed with the James Webb Space Telescope (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet first showed up in ground-based velocity measurements of the star and was later confirmed as a transit in satellite photometry (Scarsdale et al. 2021). It is nearly the same size as its sibling planet b, so the two are called twin sub-Neptunes (Scarsdale et al. 2021). Their densities suggest that hydrogen and helium make up only a few percent of each planet's mass (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","system":"The inner planet, HD 63935 b, has a 9.06-day orbit, and the system's star is bright and Sun-like (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63935c-Scarsdaleetal2021","cite":"Scarsdale et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Scarsdale, J. M. Murphy, N. Batalha","title":"HD 63935's Space Oddities: Two Atmospheric Targets in Sparse Regions of Mass-Radius Space","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1452 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies near the size boundary between rocky super-Earths and larger hydrogen-rich mini-Neptunes, which helps show how one turns into the other (Cadieux et al. 2022).","observed":"The star has a twin 3.2 arcseconds away, so ground-based telescopes had to work out which star the transits came from (Cadieux et al. 2022). A near-infrared spectrograph then measured the planet's mass (Cadieux et al. 2022). The density suggests a rocky core wrapped in a volatile-rich layer, perhaps with a water-mass fraction of about 22 percent (Cadieux et al. 2022).","system":"It orbits one star of a nearby visual pair of M dwarfs, with an equilibrium temperature of 326 K (Cadieux et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"With a thin atmosphere the surface may be rocky, but with a thick one it could be too hot for liquid water (Daspute et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1452b-Cadieuxetal2022","cite":"Cadieux et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Charles Cadieux, René Doyon, Mykhaylo Plotnykov et al.","title":"TOI-1452 b: SPIRou and TESS Reveal a Super-Earth in a Temperate Orbit Transiting an M4 Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.06333","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1452b-Dasputeetal2025","cite":"Daspute et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mangesh Daspute, Amri Wandel, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Potential Interior Structures and Habitability of Super-Earth Exoplanets LHS 1140 b, K2-18 b, TOI-1452 b, and TOI-1468 c","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08476","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 160691 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A fresh analysis of velocity data on the star finds the hot Neptune on its 9-day orbit alongside two Jupiter-like planets near 630 and 2500 days (Goździewski et al. 2007). The same analysis finds hints of a fourth Jupiter-like planet, not yet confirmed, near 307 days (Goździewski et al. 2007). Only minimum masses are known, because the orbit tilts are not measured (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd160691d-PerminovKuznetsov2019","cite":"Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Alexander Perminov, Eduard Kuznetsov","title":"Dynamical evolution of extrasolar planetary systems HD 141399, HD 160691 and HD 39194","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 68988 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A small telescope in Crimea took blue, visual and red photometry of this star, along with two others with close-in giant planets (Golovin et al. 2007). The authors suspect that tides raised by the giant planet may cause variable chromospheric emission, which is activity in the star's thin lower atmosphere, and that the light curves hint at such variability (Golovin et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":"The link between close giant planets and stellar activity was only suspected, not shown (Golovin et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd68988b-Golovinetal2007","cite":"Golovin et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Alex V. Golovin, Viktoriia N. Krushevska, Yuliana G. Kuznyetsova","title":"Photometric Detection of Chromospheric Activity of Extrasolar Planets with Short Orbital Periods: HD 187123, HD 68988, HD168746","journal":"JENAM-2007, \"Our Non-Stable Universe\"","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A velocity series across the transit of planet c shows its orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, within about 6 degrees with an uncertainty of roughly 25 degrees (Zak et al. 2024). The authors read this to mean the system reached its current layout by gentle migration with no major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"The star hosts a chain of sub-Neptunes locked in orbital resonances, so each planet's orbit period is a simple ratio of its neighbour's (Zak et al. 2024). The star is also not alone: it has a wide companion, HD 110106, 13,400 au away, which is itself a pair of K stars orbiting each other every 8 years (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"Resonant systems are thought to form by dissipative processes, and the alignment fits convergent migration (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067g-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067g-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11977 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey monitored an intermediate-mass giant star, a type that few planet searches cover (Hartmann et al. 2008).","observed":"Five years of velocity measurements of this giant star show a 711-day cycle with a stable shape (Setiawan et al. 2005). The cycle is longer than the star's estimated spin, and activity indicators do not track it, so spots are an unlikely cause (Setiawan et al. 2005). The minimum mass is 6.54 Jupiter masses, but satellite position data limit it only to below 65.5 Jupiter masses, so a brown dwarf is not ruled out (Setiawan et al. 2005).","system":"The host is a G5 giant of 1.91 solar masses, and the planet orbits about 1.93 AU away on an elongated path with eccentricity near 0.4 (Setiawan et al. 2005).","history":"","open":"Whether the companion is a planet or a brown dwarf is open (Setiawan et al. 2005)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11977b-Hartmannetal2008","cite":"Hartmann et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"M. Hartmann, A. P. Hatzes, E. W. Guenther et al.","title":"A Survey for Extrasolar Planets Around A F Type Stars","journal":"Precision Spectroscopy in Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-75485-5_70","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd11977b-Setiawanetal2005","cite":"Setiawan et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"J. Setiawan, J. Rodmann, L. da Silva et al.","title":"A substellar companion around the intermediate-mass giant star HD 11977","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0505510","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106315 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The satellite CHEOPS observed this planet's transits during its early science programme, and the precision of the transit measurements matched that of the larger Spitzer and Kepler telescopes (Maxted et al. 2022). The authors used the results to tighten limits on the planet's internal structure (Maxted et al. 2022).","system":"The star also hosts a larger Neptune on a 21-day orbit, and its brightness and fast spin make that outer planet a good target for measuring the tilt of its orbit against the star's spin (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"Simulations show that the two planets could have formed farther out and migrated inward through the disc, ending in a near 2:1 resonance in about 65,000 years (Huang et al. 2021).","open":"Tidal pull from the star may slowly pull the pair out of resonance, but the simulations do not show whether it does (Huang et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106315b-Maxtedetal2022","cite":"Maxted et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. Ehrenreich, T. G. Wilson et al.","title":"Analysis of Early Science observations with the CHaracterising ExOPlanets Satellite (CHEOPS) using PYCHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08828","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106315b-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Andrew Vanderburg, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"HD 106315 and GJ 9827: Understanding the Formation and Evolution of Small Planets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106315b-Huangetal2021","cite":"Huang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Xiu-Min Huang, Jiang-Hui Ji, Yao Dong","title":"Near 2:1 Mean Motion Resonance Capture and Orbital Evolution of a Hot-Neptune System: HD 106315","journal":"Chinese Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chinastron.2021.02.007","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4504 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity data from the star revealed this third planet of the system and gave the planets' masses (Vítková et al. 2025). Planet d has an orbital period of 40.56 days and a mass of 1.42 Jupiter masses (Vítková et al. 2025).","system":"Planets c and d are warm gas giants locked in an orbital resonance (Vítková et al. 2025). Planet c has a mass of 3.77 Jupiter masses and a period near 82.5 days (Vítková et al. 2025). A small inner planet, b, orbits in 2.43 days (Vítková et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4504d-Vtkovetal2025","cite":"Vítková et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michaela Vítková, Rafael Brahm, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"TOI-4504: Exceptionally Large Transit Timing Variations Induced by Two Resonant Warm Gas Giants in a Three-planet System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05609","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-431 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a dense super-Earth with a density of 8.0 grams per cubic centimetre, and it is thought to be a bare, stripped core (Osborn et al. 2021). Its short orbit makes it a prime target for measuring how its brightness changes around the orbit (Osborn et al. 2021). X-ray measurements of the star suggest the planet loses mass at about 10^10.5 grams per second and should be a naked solid planet (Jiang 2024). It is one of five targets for a planned JWST programme on lava worlds, and most models favour a bare-rock outcome (Sastre et al. 2026).","system":"Two more planets share the system: a sub-Neptune on a 12.46-day orbit and a non-transiting planet at 4.85 days, so the three straddle the radius gap between rocky and gas-rich worlds (Osborn et al. 2021).","history":"Lava planets like this one likely migrated inward from farther out in the disc, and the best fits need very eccentric starting orbits (Herath et al. 2026). One study places its birthplace inside that of the outer planet (Jiang 2024).","open":"Whether the planet keeps any atmosphere is not yet known (Sastre et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi431b-Osbornetal2021","cite":"Osborn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ares Osborn, David J. Armstrong, Bryson Cale et al.","title":"TOI-431/HIP 26013: a super-Earth and a sub-Neptune transiting a bright, early K dwarf, with a third RV planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02310","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi431b-Jiang2024","cite":"Jiang 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jonathan Jiang","title":"Estimate the Mass Escaping Rates of Radius-valley-spanning Planets in TOI-431 System via XUV Evaporation","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.FT6MA5","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi431b-Sastreetal2026","cite":"Sastre et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mariana Sastre, Tim Lichtenberg, Lisa Dang et al.","title":"Thermal Evolution of Lava Planets Across System Ages: Predictions for Hell of a Survey","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20155","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi431b-Herathetal2026","cite":"Herath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mahesh Herath, Nicolas B. Cowan, Charles-Édouard Boukaré et al.","title":"Coupled Orbital and Interior Structure Evolution of Lava Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18682","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1516 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was confirmed with 2 to 3 metre telescopes, showing that mid-sized telescope networks can follow up gas giants from TESS and the future PLATO mission (Kabáth et al. 2022).","observed":"Velocity follow-up shows a planet of 3.16 Jupiter masses orbiting an ordinary F main-sequence star, an ordinary example of a hot Jupiter (Kabáth et al. 2022). A later study of its transit times finds a strong signal that a model of orbital decay fits best, which points to a dynamical cause that warrants further study (Sonbas et al. 2025).","system":"The host star is 2 to 5 billion years old (Kabáth et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether the timing changes come from the orbit shrinking or from another body is unresolved (Sonbas et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1516b-Kabthetal2022","cite":"Kabáth et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Petr Kabáth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"TOI-2046b, TOI-1181b, and TOI-1516b, three new hot Jupiters from TESS: planets orbiting a young star, a subgiant, and a normal star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01860","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1516b-Sonbasetal2025","cite":"Sonbas et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Sonbas, K. Kaplan, M. Tanriver et al.","title":"A Combined TESS and Ground-based Study of Transit Timing Variations in HAT-P-16, TOI-1516 and TOI-2046 Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.21551","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1135 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is young, with an age of 125 to 1000 million years, judged from its 5.13-day spin and its lithium content (Mallorquín et al. 2024). The star is active, with strong brightness and spectral variability that the team modelled to isolate the planet (Mallorquín et al. 2024). The planet is Saturn-sized but inflated, with a mass limit below 51.4 Earth masses (Mallorquín et al. 2024). A conference report on this system reports that sub-Saturns on warm orbits are all misaligned with their stars' spin (Dugan et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The misaligned orbits of sub-Saturns pose a puzzle for how such planets form and evolve (Dugan et al. 2024).","open":"The planet's mass is only bounded from above (Mallorquín et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1135b-Mallorqunetal2024","cite":"Mallorquín et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Mallorquín, N. Lodieu, V. J. S. Béjar et al.","title":"TOI-1135 b: A young hot Saturn-size planet orbiting a solar-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17448","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1135b-Duganetal2024","cite":"Dugan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Emma Dugan, Songhu Wang, Xian-Yu Wang","title":"TOI-1135: Sub-Saturns are Universally Misaligned","journal":"TESS Science Conference III","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13525818","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-11 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the smallest-radius transiting planet found from the ground at that time and the first hot Neptune found by transit surveys (Bakos et al. 2010).","observed":"The transit signal is the shallowest from a confirmed ground-based discovery, and the star is active, making the mass measurement hard (Bakos et al. 2010). The orbit is eccentric, with e near 0.2 (Bakos et al. 2010). Hubble spectra covering water and methane bands have been taken across several transits (Chachan et al. 2020). The planet's orbit is tilted against the star's spin, about 106 degrees in one fit (Lu et al. 2024).","system":"A second, Jupiter-like planet orbits every 9.3 years on a very eccentric path (Yee et al. 2018).","history":"A planet-planet Kozai effect cannot tilt planet b because relativity interferes, while slow orbital twisting over million-year spans can (Yee et al. 2018). Another model suggests scattering and a related slow orbital pumping explain the tilt (Lu et al. 2024).","open":"Why planet c has such an eccentric orbit remains open (Yee et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp11b-Bakosetal2010","cite":"Bakos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres, A. Pál et al.","title":"HAT-P-11b: A Super-Neptune Planet Transiting a Bright K Star in the Kepler Field","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.0282","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp11b-Chachanetal2020","cite":"Chachan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Y. Chachan, H. A. Knutson, P. Gao et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HAT-P-11b spectroscopic light curve fit results (Chachan+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51580244","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp11b-Luetal2024","cite":"Lu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Tiger Lu, Qier An, Gregory Brandt et al.","title":"HAT-P-11: Orbital Misalignment through Planet-Planet Scattering and ZLK Migration","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp11b-Yeeetal2018","cite":"Yee et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Erik A. Petigura, Benjamin J. Fulton et al.","title":"HAT-P-11: Discovery of a Second Planet and a Clue to Understanding Exoplanet Obliquities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09352","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":24,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-174 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spitzer watched transits at 4.5 micrometres and, combined with the original K2 light curves, gave improved radii and multi-wavelength information that helps confirm the signal is a planet (Livingston et al. 2019). The observations also sharpened the predicted transit times, cutting the timing uncertainty for mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes (Livingston et al. 2019). The planet is listed as a promising target for velocity follow-up (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2174b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 73583 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is young, about 500 million years old, active and bright, which makes both planets strong candidates for transmission spectroscopy (Barragán et al. 2022). Planet c has a density of about 3.9 grams per cubic centimetre, consistent with a solid core surrounded by a volatile envelope (Barragán et al. 2022). A search with CARMENES found hints of hydrogen-alpha absorption for planets b and c, still to be confirmed (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"The two planets lie close to a rare 1:3 orbital resonance, and planet b has a moderately eccentric orbit (El Mufti et al. 2023). The inner planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Zhang et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the hints of escaping atmosphere are real needs confirming (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd73583c-Barragnetal2022","cite":"Barragán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"O. Barragán, D. J. Armstrong, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"The young HD 73583 (TOI-560) planetary system: two 10-M<SUB>⊕</SUB> mini-Neptunes transiting a 500-Myr-old, bright, and active K dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13069","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583c-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583c-Muftietal2023","cite":"Mufti et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mohammed El Mufti, Peter P. Plavchan, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"TOI 560: Two Transiting Planets Orbiting a K Dwarf Validated with iSHELL, PFS, and HIRES RVs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.13448","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583c-Zhangetal2026","cite":"Zhang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Elina Y. Zhang, Fei Dai, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"Spin-Orbit Alignment of Two Neptune-size Planets Younger Than 500 Myr: TOI-560 b and TOI-5082 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22611","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-167 d":{"text":{"why":"The system is the only one known with both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant beyond 1 au (Chachan et al. 2022).","observed":"Long-term velocity monitoring measured the mass of the outer giant, Kepler-167e, at about 1 Jupiter mass on a 1071-day orbit, making it a Jupiter analogue (Chachan et al. 2022). The team also refit the Kepler light curves to update the radii of all four planets, including this inner one (Chachan et al. 2022).","system":"The giant holds about 66 Earth masses of solids, far more than its solar-metallicity star would suggest (Chachan et al. 2022).","history":"Simple disc models suggest that massive, metal-rich discs can form a giant planet's core and still deliver enough solids to the inner disc to make super-Earths (Chachan et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler167d-Chachanetal2022","cite":"Chachan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yayaati Chachan, Paul A. Dalba, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-167e as a Probe of the Formation Histories of Cold Giants with Inner Super-Earths","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00747","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 4308 b":{"text":{"why":"The star has a marked sub-solar metallicity, meaning it holds fewer heavy elements than the Sun. Most planet-host stars are richer in them, so light planets may follow a different pattern than gas giants (Udry et al. 2006).","observed":"The planet's pull makes the star's velocity change by only 4.1 m/s over each orbit, a very small signal (Udry et al. 2006). The velocity variation follows a circular orbit, and the leftover scatter is just 1.3 m/s, which shows how steady the HARPS measurements are (Udry et al. 2006). Checks of the star's activity rule out starspots or similar stellar effects as the cause (Udry et al. 2006). The measured minimum mass is about 14 Earth masses (Udry et al. 2006).","system":"","history":"Together with Neptune-mass planets close to their stars, it sits in a region of mass and orbital distance that constrains theories of planet formation and evolution (Udry et al. 2006).","open":"It is not settled whether such planets could be what remains of evaporated gas giants (Udry et al. 2006)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd4308b-Udryetal2006","cite":"Udry et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. V. A 14 Earth-masses planet orbiting HD 4308","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510354","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GSC 06214-00210 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble Space Telescope optical photometry showed extra light from gas still falling onto this companion, which sits at the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Zhou et al. 2014). The rate of infall is 10^-9 to 10^-11 solar masses per year, measured jointly with two similar objects, GQ Lup b and DH Tau b (Zhou et al. 2014). That is about ten times higher than expected if such companions formed like small stars, by the fragmenting of a gas cloud (Zhou et al. 2014).","system":"The companion lies far from a solar-mass star, and the high infall rate points to a massive disc of gas around the companion itself (Zhou et al. 2014).","history":"Models of how wide planetary-mass companions form and evolve need to explain these high infall rates (Zhou et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gsc0621400210b-Zhouetal2014","cite":"Zhou et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Yifan Zhou, Gregory J. Herczeg, Adam L. Kraus et al.","title":"Accretion onto Planetary Mass Companions of Low-mass Young Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.6545","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 330075 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is faint for a planet search (V = 9.36), so the efficiency of the new HARPS instrument mattered: fewer than 10 observing nights and 3 hours of total exposure were enough to find the planet and fix its orbit (Pepe et al. 2004).","observed":"Brightness measurements of the star rule out a transit, so the planet's size is unknown (Pepe et al. 2004). The velocity wobble exceeds 100 m/s, which is easy to detect (Pepe et al. 2004). Tests of the star's light and its activity show no sign that starspots cause the signal, so a low-mass companion is the best explanation (Pepe et al. 2004). The minimum mass is about 0.62 Jupiter masses (Pepe et al. 2004).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd330075b-Pepeetal2004","cite":"Pepe et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"F. Pepe, M. Mayor, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. I. HD 330075 b: A new ``hot Jupiter''","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0405252","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-186 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A later re-examination of small, long-period Kepler planets used the final Kepler candidate catalogue and a better understanding of false alarms caused by instrument noise (Burke et al. 2019). It found that Kepler-186 f no longer reaches the 99% confidence level needed to count as a statistically confirmed planet (Burke et al. 2019). The boost in odds from belonging to a multi-planet system turned out smaller than first assumed (Burke et al. 2019).","system":"The planet is a member of a multiple-planet system (Burke et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The authors note that many factors in the analysis make it hard to say whether any single small, long-period candidate meets a strict confirmation standard (Burke et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler186f-Burkeetal2019","cite":"Burke et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, F. Mullally, Susan E. Thompson et al.","title":"Re-evaluating Small Long-period Confirmed Planets from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.00506","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Early TESS data suggested that two planets might share one orbit in a horseshoe pattern (Hooton et al. 2022). Follow-up with CHEOPS, NGTS and SPECULOOS showed instead a compact system of six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune (Hooton et al. 2022). Radial-velocity measurements with ESPRESSO revealed unusual jumps in density from one planet to the next (Hooton et al. 2022). JWST time was awarded to study the atmospheres of planets b, d and g with transmission spectroscopy (Hooton et al. 2022).","system":"Five of the six planets form a Laplacian resonant chain, in which each planet's orbit is linked by simple ratios to the orbits of two others (Hooton et al. 2022). Chains like this are rare and are also seen in TRAPPIST-1 and in Jupiter's Galilean moons (Hooton et al. 2022). The host is a cool K dwarf (Hooton et al. 2022).","history":"A fragile chain like this limits the paths by which the planets can have formed and evolved (Hooton et al. 2022). The measurements of carbon-to-oxygen ratios planned with JWST could show where each planet formed relative to different ice lines (Hooton et al. 2021).","open":"The large density differences between neighbouring planets appear hard to explain with current theory (Hooton et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178b-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178b-Hootonetal2021","cite":"Hooton et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 433 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A separate observing proposal treats the host star as a suspected very low-mass binary, GJ 433 AB (Dieterich et al. 2009). The duplicity was first hinted at by a perturbation in Hipparcos satellite data, later by speckle imaging, and then seen in Hubble NICMOS images (Dieterich et al. 2009).","system":"The suspected companion has a mass near the hydrogen-burning limit, between 0.088 and 0.122 solar masses (Dieterich et al. 2009). Its orbit around the main star would last about 1.5 years, based on the Hipparcos data (Dieterich et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"The exact dividing line between the lowest-mass stars and the most massive brown dwarfs is still poorly tested, and this object lies close to it (Dieterich et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj433d-Dieterichetal2009","cite":"Dieterich et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Sergio Dieterich, Todd Henry, David Golimowski","title":"GJ 433: Resolving a Suspected Binary Near the Stellar/Substellar Boundary","journal":"NOAO Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-405 b":{"text":{"why":"It was validated as part of a batch of 60 new K2 planets in 46 systems, chosen partly to find targets for further study (Christiansen et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed statistically rather than by measuring its mass, using reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging, shifts in the star's image centre and the likelihood of the signal (Christiansen et al. 2022). The authors single it out as suitable for both transmission spectroscopy, which probes starlight filtered through the atmosphere, and emission spectroscopy, which reads the planet's own heat glow (Christiansen et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2405b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2443 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A year and a half of HARPS-N radial-velocity monitoring, combined with TESS and ground-based photometry, confirmed the planet and gave it a mass (Naponiello et al. 2025). The host is a quiet K5 dwarf (Naponiello et al. 2025). An independent team used infrared and optical spectrographs together to fit starspot noise, since the star varies by nearly 15 m/s (Geneser et al. 2024).","system":"A second, outer candidate with a mass similar to a sub-Neptune may orbit the same star (Naponiello et al. 2025).","history":"Sub-Neptunes may be a link between terrestrial planets and gas giants, so their orbits and multiplicity help test formation and migration ideas (Naponiello et al. 2025).","open":"The outer object is still only a candidate (Naponiello et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2443b-Naponielloetal2025","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"L. Naponiello, A. S. Bonomo, L. Mancini et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXIV. An inner eccentric sub-Neptune and an outer sub-Neptune-mass candidate around BD+00 444 (TOI-2443)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.09417","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2443b-Geneseretal2024","cite":"Geneser et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Claire Geneser, Angelle Tanner, Peter Plavchan et al.","title":"Tune into GJ 105.5: Validating a transiting planet with doppler spectroscopy","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4666 b":{"text":{"why":"It was confirmed within two programmes dedicated to finding giant planets around low-mass stars: GATOS, which uses the NIRPS instrument, and MANGOS (Frensch et al. 2026; Dransfield et al. 2026).","observed":"Radial velocities in optical and near-infrared light, taken with HARPS and NIRPS, were fitted together with TESS and ground-based photometry (Frensch et al. 2026). The team used a new processing step to reduce contamination from Earth's atmosphere in the infrared data (Frensch et al. 2026). The host is an M2.5 dwarf with a temperature of 3512 K (Frensch et al. 2026). The planet is one of five giants with radii of 0.99 to 1.12 Jupiter radii and periods between 1.43 and 2.91 days (Dransfield et al. 2026). The authors say these planets are suitable for studies of their atmospheres and orbit tilts (Dransfield et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Giant planets around low-mass stars are uncommon outcomes of planet formation (Frensch et al. 2026). In the most extreme cases their existence cannot be reconciled with current core-accretion models (Dransfield et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4666b-Frenschetal2026","cite":"Frensch et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yolanda G. C. Frensch, François Bouchy, Gaspare Lo Curto et al.","title":"TOI-3288 b and TOI-4666 b: Two gas giants transiting low-mass stars characterised by NIRPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11703","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4666b-Dransfieldetal2026","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Dransfield, M. Timmermans, D. Sebastian et al.","title":"MANGOS ─ II. Five new giant planets orbiting low-mass stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WTS-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up used the UKIRT telescope in near-infrared light and was aimed at M dwarf stars; the host turned out to be a late-F star instead (Cappetta et al. 2012; Sipőcz et al. 2013).","observed":"Light curves of about 60,000 stars were searched for transits, and spectra from the Hobby-Eberly Telescope then measured the host and its velocity changes (Cappetta et al. 2012). The planet is swollen: its radius is about 1.49 Jupiter radii for a mass of about 4 Jupiter masses (Cappetta et al. 2012). It has one of the largest radius anomalies among hot Jupiters of 3 to 5 Jupiter masses, meaning it is larger than models predict (Cappetta et al. 2012). The strong radiation from its star places it in the hot pM atmospheric class (Cappetta et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a late-F dwarf with possibly slightly sub-solar metallicity (Cappetta et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wts1b-Cappettaetal2012","cite":"Cappetta et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Cappetta, R. P. Saglia, J. L. Birkby et al.","title":"The first planet detected in the WTS: an inflated hot Jupiter in a 3.35 d orbit around a late F star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1217","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-6 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is one of the lowest-mass stars known to have a close-in gas giant (Hartman et al. 2015).","observed":"The transits are among the deepest of any known planet because the star is small (Hartman et al. 2015). Two transits observed with JWST's NIRSpec covered 0.6 to 5.3 micrometres (Guzmán Caloca et al. 2026). The spectrum indicates a low-metallicity atmosphere, below the Sun's, with a carbon-to-oxygen ratio also below the Sun's, and shows water, methane and ammonia (Guzmán Caloca et al. 2026). The planet is warm, with an equilibrium temperature of about 700 K (Guzmán Caloca et al. 2026).","system":"The planet is Saturn-mass and Jupiter-sized, and orbits a roughly 0.6 solar-mass M dwarf (Guzmán Caloca et al. 2026).","history":"It belongs to a JWST survey of giants around M dwarfs, designed to learn how such planets form close to low-mass stars (Guzman Caloca et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats6b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, D. Bayliss, R. Brahm et al.","title":"HATS-6b: A Warm Saturn Transiting an Early M Dwarf Star, and a Set of Empirical Relations for Characterizing K and M Dwarf Planet Hosts","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.1758","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats6b-Calocaetal2026","cite":"Caloca et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Giannina Guzman Caloca, Caleb Cañas, Erin May et al.","title":"Looking at Giant Exoplanets around M-dwarfs (GEMS) with JWST: NIRSpec Transmission Spectroscopy of the Warm Saturn HATS-6 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats6b-Calocaetal2025","cite":"Caloca et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Giannina Guzman Caloca, Caleb Cañas, Erin May et al.","title":"Looking at Giant Exoplanets around M-dwarfs (GEMS) with JWST: NIRSpec Transmission Spectroscopy of the Warm Saturn HATS-6 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KPS-1 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first transiting planet found with wide-field CCD data collected by an amateur astronomer using affordable, readily available equipment (Burdanov et al. 2018).","observed":"The detection came from the prototype Kourovka Planet Search project (Burdanov et al. 2018). Spectra from the SOPHIE spectrograph and precise brightness measurements from 1-metre-class telescopes then confirmed it (Burdanov et al. 2018). The amateur telescope that took the data later became part of the Galactic Plane eXoplanet Survey, which tests whether sharper images with less sky coverage can find transits in crowded Milky Way fields (Benni et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a K1 main-sequence star (Burdanov et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kps1b-Burdanovetal2018","cite":"Burdanov et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Artem Burdanov, Paul Benni, Eugene Sokov et al.","title":"KPS-1b: The First Transiting Exoplanet Discovered Using an Amateur Astronomer's Wide-field CCD Data","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05551","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kps1b-Bennietal2020","cite":"Benni et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Benni, A. Burdanov, V. Krushinsky et al.","title":"First Discoveries of the Galactic Plane Exoplanet Survey (GPX)","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #236","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5789 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper focuses mostly on planet c, a sub-Neptune that crosses the bright, magnetically inactive K1 dwarf HIP 99452 every 12.93 days (Bonomo et al. 2026). It used TESS photometry and 141 HARPS-N radial velocities (Bonomo et al. 2026). That planet is a promising target for atmosphere studies with JWST and, later, Ariel (Bonomo et al. 2026).","system":"The system has several planets whose orbits are inclined to one another (Bonomo et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5789e-Bonomoetal2026","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, L. Naponiello, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXI. A sub-Neptune suitable for atmospheric characterization in a multiplanet and mutually inclined system orbiting the bright K dwarf TOI-5789 (HIP 99452)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1227 b":{"text":{"why":"Young stars may host Jupiter-sized planets that later shrink into the common sub-Neptunes and super-Earths, so finding one is a test of this idea (Mann et al. 2022).","observed":"The star belongs to a young group called Musca, in Lower Centaurus Crux, which was dated to 11 million years from lithium, rotation and colour-magnitude data (Mann et al. 2022). Multi-wavelength transit observations and infrared radial velocities showed the signal is planetary and put an upper limit of about 0.5 Jupiter masses on its mass (Mann et al. 2022). Chandra X-ray observations later suggested an age nearer 8 million years and rapid atmospheric loss of about 10^12 g/s (Varga et al. 2025). Transits arrive up to about 40 minutes off schedule, which suggests other planets are tugging on it (Almenara et al. 2024).","system":"Timing shifts point to additional unseen planets, possibly near orbital resonances (Almenara et al. 2024). The host is a very-low-mass star of 0.17 solar masses (Mann et al. 2022).","history":"The planet is thought to be still contracting and will probably become one of the more common planets smaller than 5 Earth radii (Mann et al. 2022).","open":"Several orbital layouts for the extra planets fit the timing data, and more observations are needed to measure the masses (Almenara et al. 2024). The planet's future depends strongly on its true mass (Varga et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1227b-Mannetal2022","cite":"Mann et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. W. Mann, M. L. Wood, S. P. Schmidt et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: THYME. VI. TOI-1227 radial velocity (Mann+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51630156","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1227b-Vargaetal2025","cite":"Varga et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Attila Varga, Joel H. Kastner, Alexander S. Binks et al.","title":"The Age and High-energy Environment of the Very Young Transiting Exoplanet TOI 1227b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.04440","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1227b-Almenaraetal2024","cite":"Almenara et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. M. Almenara, X. Bonfils, T. Guillot et al.","title":"Evidence for transit-timing variations of the 11 Myr exoplanet TOI-1227 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05047","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-39 b":{"text":{"why":"It was first spotted as a single transit event in TESS data, which does not reveal the orbital period (Apergis et al. 2026).","observed":"NGTS photometry and radial velocities from CORALIE and HARPS confirmed the planet and its 58.2-day orbit (Apergis et al. 2026). The orbit is clearly eccentric, with e = 0.386 (Apergis et al. 2026). The equilibrium temperature is about 519 K, between hot Jupiters and cold Jupiter-like giants (Apergis et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a bright F9 dwarf with a temperature of about 6053 K (Apergis et al. 2026).","history":"The mix of high eccentricity and moderate temperature makes it a useful test of formation and migration models for long-period giants (Apergis et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts39b-Apergisetal2026","cite":"Apergis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ioannis Apergis, Daniel Bayliss, Solène Ulmer-Moll et al.","title":"NGTS-39 b: a 58 d transiting warm Jupiter in an eccentric orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02061","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-540 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is slightly smaller than Earth and likely rocky (Ment et al. 2021). Its star spins once every 17.4 hours, very fast, and gives off strong X-rays, seen by chance with XMM-Newton (Ment et al. 2021). The strong X-rays make the planet a good target to study atmosphere erosion (Ment et al. 2021). A radio search with MeerKAT for signs of star-planet magnetic interaction found nothing (Ortiz Ceballos et al. 2025). It is among the best small targets for JWST (Ment et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a low-mass red dwarf of 0.159 solar masses (Ment et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The radio limits would still have revealed the signal even for a planetary magnetic field of only about 1 gauss, if the emission pointed at Earth (Ortiz Ceballos et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi540b-Mentetal2021","cite":"Ment et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kristo Ment, Jonathan Irwin, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"TOI 540 b: A Planet Smaller than Earth Orbiting a Nearby Rapidly Rotating Low-mass Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.13623","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi540b-Ceballosetal2025","cite":"Ceballos et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Kevin N. Ortiz Ceballos, Yvette Cendes, Edo Berger","title":"A Radio Search for Star-Planet Interaction in TOI-540 and SPECULOOS-3","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16852","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-155 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Speckle imaging and high-dispersion spectra, including radial velocities, were used to confirm the planets around this bright, metal-poor early M dwarf (Hirano et al. 2018). The planet's radius of about 1.95 Earth radii and 13.85-day period were measured from the K2 light curve (Hirano et al. 2018).","system":"Three super-Earths transit the star, with periods of 6.3, 13.9 and 40.7 days (Hirano et al. 2018). The outermost planet receives about 1.67 times Earth's sunlight, near the habitable zone (Hirano et al. 2018). Three-dimensional climate simulations suggest that planet could stay moderate if it is tidally locked and Earth-like (Hirano et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2155c-Hiranoetal2018","cite":"Hirano et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Fei Dai, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"K2-155: A Bright Metal-poor M Dwarf with Three Transiting Super-Earths","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06957","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-6 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble and Spitzer measured the planet's transmission spectrum from 0.29 to 4.5 micrometres, and it looks larger at shorter wavelengths (Nikolov et al. 2015). That points to haze or dust that scatters blue light, with a temperature of 973 K at the day-night edge (Nikolov et al. 2015). Ground-based observations found a similarly featureless spectrum (Jordán et al. 2013). A comparison of ten hot Jupiters found aerosol extending to low pressures (Barstow et al. 2017). Spitzer eclipse measurements added it to a sample of cool giants used to study how heat is carried to the night side (Kammer et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":"A test of orbit shapes found no evidence that its orbit is eccentric (Husnoo et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp6b-Nikolovetal2015","cite":"Nikolov et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"N. Nikolov, D. K. Sing, A. S. Burrows et al.","title":"HST hot-Jupiter transmission spectral survey: haze in the atmosphere of WASP-6b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4567","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp6b-Jordnetal2013","cite":"Jordán et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Andrés Jordán, Néstor Espinoza, Markus Rabus et al.","title":"A Ground-based Optical Transmission Spectrum of WASP-6b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp6b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp6b-Kammeretal2015","cite":"Kammer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Joshua A. Kammer, Heather A. Knutson, Michael R. Line et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipse Observations of Five Cool Gas Giant Planets and Empirical Trends in Cool Planet Emission Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.00902","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp6b-Husnooetal2012","cite":"Husnoo et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Nawal Husnoo, Frédéric Pont, Tsevi Mazeh et al.","title":"Observational constraints on tidal effects using orbital eccentricities","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.6379","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"SPECULOOS-3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radio telescopes searched this system for signs that the planet interacts magnetically with its star. The Very Large Array made deep, repeated observations at 4-8 GHz and detected nothing, down to a limit of about 7.5 microjansky (Ortiz Ceballos et al. 2025). That rules out observable interaction emission for most of the planet's orbit (Ortiz Ceballos et al. 2025). The team says the observations would have caught such emission even for a planetary magnetic field of only about 1 gauss, if it pointed our way (Ortiz Ceballos et al. 2025).","system":"The host is one of four planet-hosting ultracool dwarfs whose near-infrared spectra show intermediate-gravity signatures, even though other indicators point to field-age stars (Davoudi et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"A study of the gravity class of such cool stars' spectra found no significant link between it and having planets (Davoudi et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-speculoos3b-Ceballosetal2025","cite":"Ceballos et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Kevin N. Ortiz Ceballos, Yvette Cendes, Edo Berger","title":"A Radio Search for Star-Planet Interaction in TOI-540 and SPECULOOS-3","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16852","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-speculoos3b-Davoudietal2025","cite":"Davoudi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Fatemeh Davoudi, Benjamin V. Rackham, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Gravity-sensitive Spectral Indices in Ultracool Dwarfs: Investigating Correlations with Metallicity and Planet Occurrence Using SpeX and Fire Observations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19928","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-11 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Part of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect was caught: a distortion of the star's spectrum as the planet crosses its spinning surface. It showed the planet orbits in the same direction as the star rotates (Gandolfi et al. 2010). The star spins unusually fast, among the fastest known planet hosts (Gandolfi et al. 2010). The planet's low density can be explained by a puffed-up hydrogen planet with a high level of energy dissipation in its interior (Gandolfi et al. 2010).","system":"If the star is seen equator-on, it turns once in about 1.73 days, faster than the planet's roughly 3-day orbit (Gandolfi et al. 2010). A faint companion candidate near the star was re-observed in a high-resolution imaging survey (Wöllert et al. 2015).","history":"Strong tides would have pushed the planet outward from an orbit once nearly in step with the star's spin (Gandolfi et al. 2010). The system is considered well suited for measuring tidal dissipation in F-type stars (Lanza et al. 2011).","open":"Timing transits to about 1 second over some 25 years could measure how efficiently the star dissipates tides (Gandolfi et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot11b-Gandolfietal2010","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Gandolfi, G. Hébrard, R. Alonso et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XIV. CoRoT-11b: a transiting massive ``hot-Jupiter'' in a prograde orbit around a rapidly rotating F-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.2597","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot11b-Wllertetal2015","cite":"Wöllert et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maria Wöllert, Wolfgang Brandner, Carolina Bergfors et al.","title":"A Lucky Imaging search for stellar companions to transiting planet host stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01938","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot11b-Lanzaetal2011","cite":"Lanza et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. F. Lanza, C. Damiani, D. Gandolfi","title":"Constraining tidal dissipation in F-type main-sequence stars: the case of CoRoT-11","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.5791","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2114 b":{"text":{"why":"Massive hot-Jupiter candidates from the TESS mission often go unconfirmed, because most ground-based follow-up time goes to low-mass candidates (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","observed":"The CAFE2 spectrograph at Calar Alto Observatory monitored the star's radial velocity, meaning the small back-and-forth motion the planet causes in it. Together with the TESS brightness data, this confirmed that the transiting object is a planet of about 1 Jupiter mass on a 6.2-day orbit (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). It was one of seven hot-Jupiter candidates checked this way (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2114b-LilloBoxetal2026","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, C. Cifuentes, O. Balsalobre-Ruza et al.","title":"CAFE follow-up of TESS hot Jupiter candidates left behind: I. Five newly confirmed planets and a false positive","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19756","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 132406 b":{"text":{"why":"The search that found it was biased toward metal-rich stars, which are more likely to host giant planets (da Silva et al. 2007).","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements with the ELODIE spectrograph, later followed up with SOPHIE, showed a giant planet candidate with a minimum mass of 5.6 Jupiter masses. Its orbit takes about 974 days and is clearly eccentric, with e = 0.34 (da Silva et al. 2007). The host star is slightly metal-rich (da Silva et al. 2007). The programme was originally designed to look for transiting hot Jupiters, so this planet is an intermediate-period by-product (da Silva et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd132406b-daSilvaetal2007","cite":"da Silva et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. da Silva, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"ELODIE metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. IV. Intermediate period planets orbiting the stars HD 43691 and HD 132406","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.0958","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOBE-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The KOBE survey is a blind search of 50 late K dwarfs, using an observing strategy designed to find planets in their habitable zones (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","observed":"Three years of CARMENES spectra, 82 in all, showed two periodic signals in the star's motion, at 8.5 and 29.7 days (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). Statistical model comparison and checks against other causes confirmed them as planets (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). Two TESS sectors were also inspected for transits (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). Both planets likely lie in the sub-Neptune mass range (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a K7 dwarf, a star type between Sun-like stars and red dwarfs. The study also uses the data to constrain which planets could exist in its habitable zone (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kobe1b-BalsalobreRuzaetal2025","cite":"Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"O. Balsalobre-Ruza, J. Lillo-Box, A. M. Silva et al.","title":"KOBE-1: The first planetary system from the KOBE survey: Two planets likely residing in the sub-Neptune mass regime around a late K-dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01249","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1332 b":{"text":{"why":"The search assumed that a civilisation might time a radio signal to a planet's transit, a natural shared moment that could act as a meeting point (Agosto Reyes 2023).","observed":"The planet has been studied not for its own properties but as a target of radio searches for technosignatures, narrow-band signals that might come from intelligent life. A first campaign with the Green Bank Telescope watched 12 Kepler planets during transit and found none, but two signals of interest could not be told apart from transient or localised interference (Agosto Reyes 2023). Kepler-1332 b was re-observed during transit with the Allen Telescope Array for 30 minutes around mid-transit, using one beam on target and one slightly off it (Agosto Reyes 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1332b-Reyes2023","cite":"Reyes 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Alanice Agosto Reyes","title":"A Technosignature Follow-Up Search for Two Kepler Planets with the Allen Telescope Array","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 108236 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet turned up by chance in one of the CHEOPS space telescope's light curves, while the telescope was characterising the four planets already known (Bonfanti et al. 2021). Combining TESS and CHEOPS data gives only a tentative orbital period of about 29.5 days (Bonfanti et al. 2021). Later CHEOPS and TESS observations covered transits of planets b to f (Hoyer et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a bright, Sun-like star with a super-Earth and three mini-Neptunes already known, making this the fifth planet (Bonfanti et al. 2021). Its age is uncertain, about 6.7 billion years with a wide range (Bonfanti et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The 29.5-day period is still only tentative (Bonfanti et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd108236f-Bonfantietal2021","cite":"Bonfanti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Bonfanti, L. Delrez, M. J. Hooton et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 108236 CHEOPS light curves (Bonfanti+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36460157","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd108236f-Hoyeretal2022","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Hoyer, A. Bonfanti, A. Leleu et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 108236 system observed with CHEOPS and TESS (Hoyer+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1420 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a close match to WASP-107 b in mass, radius, density and orbital period, so comparing the two may reveal how such low-density planets form (Yoshida et al. 2023).","observed":"Transit observations from TESS and many ground telescopes, plus radial velocities from HARPS-N and NEID, give a density of just 0.08 g/cm3. It is the largest known planet with a mass under 50 Earth masses (Yoshida et al. 2023). About 82 percent of its mass is a hydrogen and helium envelope (Yoshida et al. 2023). Its extended atmosphere and a predicted Rossiter-McLaughlin signal of about 17 m/s make it an excellent target for follow-up (Yoshida et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"The envelope fraction suggests runaway gas accretion began when the core was at most four to five Earth masses (Yoshida et al. 2023).","open":"A population-wide structure study finds TOI-1420 b inconsistent with core accretion theory, even with an extra heat source, so a nonstandard explanation such as rings may be needed (Marston et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1420b-Yoshidaetal2023","cite":"Yoshida et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Stephanie Yoshida, Shreyas Vissapragada, David W. Latham et al.","title":"TESS Spots a Super-puff: The Remarkably Low Density of TOI-1420b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.09945","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1420b-Marstonetal2026","cite":"Marston et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nicholas T. Marston, Juliette Becker, Alex R. Howe","title":"Do Super-puffs Defy Core Accretion? Population-wide Interior Structure Constraints","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11330","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is massive and orbits in under a day. Its tides appear to have spun up the star, which rotates faster than expected for its age (Penev et al. 2016). Together with the similar system WASP-19, this constrains how efficiently Sun-like stars dissipate tides (Penev et al. 2016). In theory the planet will spiral into its star, but timing of 131 transits found no convincing sign of this (Cao & Zheng 2026). Earlier work gave a similar result (Southworth et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether tidal decay is happening remains unresolved, though current limits are close to constraining enhanced tidal dissipation by internal gravity waves (Southworth et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats18b-Penevetal2016","cite":"Penev et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"K. Penev, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-18b: An Extreme Short-period Massive Transiting Planet Spinning Up Its Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00848","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats18b-CaoZheng2026","cite":"Cao & Zheng 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Kaidong Cao, Yumeng Zheng","title":"Search for orbital decay in the HATS-18 system","journal":"Society of Photo-Optical Instrumentation Engineers (SPIE) Conference Series","url":"https://doi.org/10.1117/12.3116602","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats18b-Southworthetal2022","cite":"Southworth et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"John Southworth, A. J. Barker, T. C. Hinse et al.","title":"A search for transit timing variations in the HATS-18 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05873","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-59 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper judged the planet likely to have a large, dense core, in contrast to its inflated sibling planets announced alongside it (Hébrard et al. 2013). Archived HARPS spectra of a transit later measured its orbit's tilt relative to the star's spin. The orbit is consistent with alignment, with a sky-projected angle of 1 degree and an uncertainty of about 20 degrees (Zak et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp59b-Hbrardetal2013","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Hebrard, Cameron A. Collier, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Four new WASP transiting close-in giant planets (Hebrard+, 2013)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35490134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp59b-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquities of eight close-in gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.05615","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-11 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet confirmed after being spotted as a single transit in TESS data. Seventy-nine nights of monitoring caught a second transit a year later, which narrowed the period to 13 possible values (Gill et al. 2020).","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements fixed the true 35-day period and measured the mass (Gill et al. 2020). With an equilibrium temperature of only about 435 K, it is one of the coolest known transiting gas giants (Gill et al. 2020). Later data lowered its mass to about 0.63 Saturn masses (Anderson et al. 2025). JWST is planned to measure its transmission spectrum (Claringbold et al. 2025).","system":"A Neptune-mass planet, NGTS-11 c, orbits closer in on a 12.8-day orbit. Radial velocities also hint at a third, more distant companion of more than 3.6 Jupiter masses (Anderson et al. 2025).","history":"The near-circular, compact orbits make high-eccentricity migration unlikely. The planets probably formed in place or moved inward through the gas disc (Anderson et al. 2025). Such warm Jupiters with inner companions were probably only weakly migrated (Claringbold et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts11b-Gilletal2020","cite":"Gill et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Samuel Gill, Peter J. Wheatley, Benjamin F. Cooke et al.","title":"NGTS-11 b (TOI-1847 b): A Transiting Warm Saturn Recovered from a TESS Single-transit Event","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00006","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts11b-Andersonetal2025","cite":"Anderson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"David R. Anderson, Jose I. Vines, Katharine Hesse et al.","title":"NGTS-11 c: a transiting Neptune-mass planet interior to the warm Saturn NGTS-11 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.14083","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts11b-Claringboldetal2025","cite":"Claringbold et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Alastair Claringbold, James Edward Owen, Eva-Maria Ahrer et al.","title":"Warm Jupiters: the next step in uncovering giant planet formation and migration","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of the most massive transiting planets known and the densest one with an orbit shorter than 3 days, at about 4.6 g/cm3 (Joshi et al. 2009). Its orbit is slightly eccentric (Joshi et al. 2009). The star has a high lithium abundance, and its density, temperature and rotation suggest an age of about 0.5 to 1 billion years (Joshi et al. 2009). Timing of 19 later transit light curves over seven years found no sign of a second planet pulling on it (Raetz et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp14b-Joshietal2009","cite":"Joshi et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Y. C. Joshi, D. Pollacco, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-14b: 7.3 M<SUB>J</SUB> transiting planet in an eccentric orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1478","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp14b-Raetzetal2015","cite":"Raetz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"St. Raetz, G. Maciejewski, M. Seeliger et al.","title":"WASP-14 b: transit timing analysis of 19 light curves","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01609","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-10 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit is highly eccentric, with e = 0.53. As a result, the planet receives 10.6 times more starlight at its closest than at its farthest (Bonomo et al. 2010). Its density is about 2.8 times Jupiter's, and its core could hold up to 240 Earth masses of heavy elements (Bonomo et al. 2010). A reported mid-infrared excess suggests dust around the star (Krivov et al. 2011). Amateur observers also report a transit arriving more than four hours early (Libotte et al. 2025).","system":"The star's infrared excess is modelled as dust rings between about 0.1 and 25 AU (Krivov et al. 2011).","history":"The long tidal circularisation time, over 7 billion years, means no resonant perturber is needed to explain the eccentricity (Bonomo et al. 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot10b-Bonomoetal2010","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Santerne, R. Alonso et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. X. CoRoT-10b: a giant planet in a 13.24 day eccentric orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2949","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot10b-Krivovetal2011","cite":"Krivov et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. V. Krivov, M. Reidemeister, S. Fiedler et al.","title":"Warm dust in systems with transiting planets","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2011","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot10b-Libotteetal2025","cite":"Libotte et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Florence Libotte, Mercedes Correa, Antelm Ginard et al.","title":"Exoplanet observations and TTV, transit timing variation interesting cases","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2025","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc-dps2025-173","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-66 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It was announced together with nine other HATSouth planets, and is the most massive of the ten, a super-Jupiter of 5.33 Jupiter masses (Hartman et al. 2019). Its faint host star was also checked using Gaia distances and photometry for hidden stellar companions. Such companions were flagged for three other systems in the batch, not this one (Hartman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats66b-Hartmanetal2019","cite":"Hartman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-60b-HATS-69b: 10 Transiting Planets from HATSouth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3894 b":{"text":{"why":"It belongs to the MEEP survey, which aims to find every hot Jupiter around bright stars (Gaia magnitude below 12.5) using the same methods each time. This builds a complete sample for testing theories of how hot Jupiters form and migrate (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"TESS flagged it as a candidate, and ground-based imaging and spectroscopy then confirmed it as a planet (Schulte et al. 2024). It is one of nine new hot Jupiters in the first MEEP paper. Their masses range from 0.55 to 3.88 Jupiter masses (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3894b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-1 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found by the Qatar Exoplanet Survey, and the discovery paper also describes the survey's candidate selection and false-positive rate (Alsubai et al. 2011).","observed":"The host is a metal-rich K dwarf older than 4 billion years (Alsubai et al. 2011). Near-infrared observations of the planet passing behind its star gave a brightness temperature of about 1885 K (Cruz et al. 2016). Transit timings in 2013 hinted at a roughly 190-day variation (von Essen et al. 2013). It is also a target for ground-based transit observations by small telescopes and student programmes (Krause & Young 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The timing variations might come from a weak perturber in resonance or a brown dwarf, but need more data to confirm (von Essen et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar1b-Alsubaietal2011","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"K. A. Alsubai, N. R. Parley, D. M. Bramich et al.","title":"Qatar-1b: a hot Jupiter orbiting a metal-rich K dwarf star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.3027","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar1b-Cruzetal2016","cite":"Cruz et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Patricia Cruz, David Barrado, Jorge Lillo-Box et al.","title":"Detection of the secondary eclipse of Qatar-1b in the Ks band","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.06263","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar1b-vonEssenetal2013","cite":"von Essen et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"C. von Essen, S. Schröter, E. Agol et al.","title":"Qatar-1: indications for possible transit timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.1457","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar1b-KrauseYoung2026","cite":"Krause & Young 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Hanna Krause, Timothy Young","title":"Transit Depth Variability and Observational Effects in the Hot Jupiter Qatar-1b","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2012-BLG-0358L b":{"text":{"why":"Earlier planetary-mass companions to brown dwarfs had large mass ratios and wide separations, suggesting they formed like stellar binaries (Han et al. 2013).","observed":"The host is an old field brown dwarf of 0.022 solar masses, and the companion weighs about 1.9 Jupiter masses (Han et al. 2013). The mass ratio is low, 0.08, and the separation is only about 0.87 AU (Han et al. 2013).","system":"There is no true star in the system, only a brown dwarf and its companion (Han et al. 2013).","history":"The small mass ratio and tight separation suggest the companion may have formed in a disc around the brown dwarf, as planets do (Han et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2012blg0358lb-Hanetal2013","cite":"Han et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"C. Han, Y. K. Jung, A. Udalski et al.","title":"Microlensing Discovery of a Tight, Low-mass-ratio Planetary-mass Object around an Old Field Brown Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6335","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 224538 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from the Calan-Hertfordshire Extrasolar Planet Search, which reported eight new giant planets using the CORALIE, HARPS and MIKE spectrographs (Jenkins et al. 2017).","observed":"The study calls it a rare 14 Herculis analogue, referring to a well-known long-period giant planet system (Jenkins et al. 2017). Planets in the whole sample range from 1.1 to 5.4 Jupiter masses, with periods of 40 to 2900 days (Jenkins et al. 2017). The sample shows that low-mass planets tend to orbit more metal-poor stars than giant planets (Jenkins et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd224538b-Jenkinsetal2017","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, H. R. A. Jones, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"New planetary systems from the Calan-Hertfordshire Extrasolar Planet Search","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09391","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-18 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed by combining large transit timing variations, radial velocities, warm-Spitzer photometry and statistical checks (Cochran et al. 2011). Its density is only 0.59 g/cm3, so it is a low-density, Neptune-mass planet (Cochran et al. 2011). It is a benchmark for such planets, since it was cited in a Spitzer proposal to study another low-density Neptune (Demory et al. 2012).","system":"Kepler-18 hosts three planets around a Sun-like star rich in iron. The innermost is a super-Earth, and planets c and d are both low-density Neptune-mass worlds. Planets c and d orbit near a 2:1 resonance, producing the large timing variations (Cochran et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler18c-Cochranetal2011","cite":"Cochran et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"William D. Cochran, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"Kepler-18b, c, and d: A System of Three Planets Confirmed by Transit Timing Variations, Light Curve Validation, Warm-Spitzer Photometry, and Radial Velocity Measurements","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.0820","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler18c-Demoryetal2012","cite":"Demory et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Brice-Olivier Demory, Michael Gillon, Xavier Bonfils et al.","title":"Characterization of a Low-density Exo-Neptune","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-261.02":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The first TESS list gave this candidate a period of 13 days. Follow-up with the HARPS spectrograph found a different period of 10.2 days (Grziwa et al. 2022). The spectrograph measures the small wobble the planets cause in their star. It confirmed the planets and gave masses between 8 and 11 Earth masses for all three mini-Neptunes in the system (Grziwa et al. 2022).","system":"The F8 V star hosts three mini-Neptunes. The inner two orbit every 3.4 and 10.2 days, and the outer one every 22.8 days (Grziwa et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi26102-Grziwaetal2022","cite":"Grziwa et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sascha Grziwa, Davide Gandolfi, Luisa Maria Serrano et al.","title":"One for all and all for one: the TESS-HARPS synergy unveils three mini-Neptunes transiting TOI-261","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 169142 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet is a candidate still embedded in the gas and dust disc around its young star. ALMA, a radio telescope array, mapped the disc nearly face-on. It shows two dust rings, one at 20-35 au and one at 56-83 au, with an empty gap between them (Fedele et al. 2017). Gas was still found inside that gap and inside the central cavity (Fedele et al. 2017). The authors read this pattern as the mark of two giant protoplanets (Fedele et al. 2017). Infrared polarised-light images with SPHERE show an inner hole, a bright ring near 21 au and a wide gap from 29 to 55 au (Bertrang et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a young, hot star of the Herbig Ae/Be type with an age estimated at about 6 million years (Grady et al. 2007; Bertrang et al. 2018).","history":"The ring and gap structure fits dust being trapped by a massive planet and carved by planets still forming (Fedele et al. 2017).","open":"Whether the disc features are made by one planet, two planets or something else is still being tested (Fedele et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd169142b-Fedeleetal2017","cite":"Fedele et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Fedele, M. Carney, M. R. Hogerheijde et al.","title":"ALMA unveils rings and gaps in the protoplanetary system <ASTROBJ>HD 169142</ASTROBJ>: signatures of two giant protoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.02844","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd169142b-Bertrangetal2018","cite":"Bertrang et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"G. H.-M. Bertrang, H. Avenhaus, S. Casassus et al.","title":"HD 169142 in the eyes of ZIMPOL/SPHERE","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09040","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd169142b-Gradyetal2007","cite":"Grady et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"C. A. Grady, G. Schneider, K. Hamaguchi et al.","title":"The Disk and Environment of a Young Vega Analog: HD 169142","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/519757","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":36,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 112640 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a radial velocity survey of northern circumpolar stars. Its aim is to search for planetary companions (Lee et al. 2020).","observed":"The host is an evolved giant star. Its brightness shows a wobble that repeats every 613 days. The authors argued that spots or other features on the star's surface do not explain this wobble. They conclude that an orbiting planet is the most likely cause (Lee et al. 2020). The minimum mass they found is about 5 Jupiter masses, using a star mass of 1.8 times the Sun's (Lee et al. 2020). The detection is described as a planet candidate (Lee et al. 2020).","system":"It was reported together with a similar companion around another giant star, HD 60292, from the same survey (Lee et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd112640b-Leeetal2020","cite":"Lee et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Myeong-Gu Park, Inwoo Han et al.","title":"A Search for Exoplanets around Northern Circumpolar Stars. VI. Detection of Planetary Companions Orbiting the Giants HD 60292 and HD 112640","journal":"Journal of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.5303/JKAS.2020.53.1.27","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-82 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was studied with a network of telescopes around the world that tracks Kepler planets whose transits arrive early or late. These timing shifts are called transit timing variations. Kepler-82 b and c show smooth, wave-like shifts because they pull on each other near a 2:1 orbital resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Extra jitter in the timing of planet c pointed to another planet. A combined model of gravity and transit light curves found a non-transiting planet of about 21 Earth masses, named Kepler-82 f (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","system":"The system holds at least three planets that interact by gravity. The outer one, Kepler-82 f, does not transit and shows itself only through its pull on the others (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler82b-Freudenthaletal2019","cite":"Freudenthal et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Freudenthal, C. von Essen, A. Ofir et al.","title":"Kepler Object of Interest Network. III. Kepler-82f: a new non-transiting 21 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> planet from photodynamical modelling","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.06534","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 81208 C b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a re-analysis of old data from a survey of young B-type stars. That survey, called BEAST, asks how massive giant planets can form around such stars (Chomez et al. 2023).","observed":"A new image-processing method called PACO was used on archive images from the SPHERE instrument. It recovered a faint source very close to the star HIP 81208 C (Chomez et al. 2023). Its motion across the sky shows that it is bound to C (Chomez et al. 2023). It orbits the low-mass star C, not the big central star.","system":"The 2.6 solar-mass central star has a brown dwarf at about 50 au and a late M star, C, at about 230 au (Chomez et al. 2023). The new companion orbits C, which makes this a four-body system arranged in a hierarchy (Chomez et al. 2023). The system is about 17 million years old (Chomez et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip81208cb-Chomezetal2023","cite":"Chomez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Chomez, V. Squicciarini, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"An imaged 15 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> companion within a hierarchical quadruple system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.01195","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-198 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet was one of four used as case studies in a paper on why planet searches miss signals. Its transits were strong enough to detect, with a signal-to-noise ratio of at least 10. Yet it went unnoticed in several large searches of K2 data (Hedges et al. 2019). The study used fast open-source tools to pull the signal out of instrumental noise and star variability, and confirmed the planet (Hedges et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2198c-Hedgesetal2019","cite":"Hedges et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christina Hedges, Nicholas Saunders, Geert Barentsen et al.","title":"Four Small Planets Buried in K2 Systems: What Can We Learn for TESS?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.08244","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-18 b is the inner member of a three-planet system. The planets were confirmed with timing shifts in their transits, Doppler velocity measurements, Warm-Spitzer observations and statistical checks (Cochran et al. 2011). Planet b has a mean density of about 4.9 g per cubic centimetre. Its two outer neighbours are far fluffier, at 0.59 and 0.27 g per cubic centimetre (Cochran et al. 2011). Modelling suggests that b holds less than 10 per cent of its mass as hydrogen and helium (Valencia et al. 2013).","system":"The two outer planets, c and d, are low-density Neptune-mass worlds near a 2:1 orbital resonance. This causes large transit timing variations (Cochran et al. 2011). The star is Sun-like and richer in iron than the Sun (Cochran et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler18b-Cochranetal2011","cite":"Cochran et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"William D. Cochran, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"Kepler-18b, c, and d: A System of Three Planets Confirmed by Transit Timing Variations, Light Curve Validation, Warm-Spitzer Photometry, and Radial Velocity Measurements","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.0820","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler18b-Valenciaetal2013","cite":"Valencia et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Diana Valencia, Tristan Guillot, Vivien Parmentier et al.","title":"Bulk Composition of GJ 1214b and Other Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2629","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-7 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits the brightest star found by the WASP-South survey, and is currently the brightest transiting system in the southern sky (Hellier et al. 2008).","observed":"WASP-7 b is one of the densest known planets of Jupiter's mass, which suggests it has a massive core (Hellier et al. 2008). Spitzer measured the planet passing behind its star at two infrared wavelengths. The timing of this eclipse shows evidence for a small non-zero orbital eccentricity, unlike most similar planets (Wallack et al. 2021). The planet's orbit is tilted by roughly 90 degrees to the star's spin when seen on the sky (Serrano et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"The tilted orbit makes this a promising target for studying how different latitudes on the star rotate at different speeds. A noise level near 2 m/s or better would be needed (Serrano et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp7b-Hellieretal2008","cite":"Hellier et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, M. Gillon et al.","title":"Wasp-7: A Bright Transiting-Exoplanet System in the Southern Hemisphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.2600","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp7b-Wallacketal2021","cite":"Wallack et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Drake Deming","title":"Trends in Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp7b-Serranoetal2020","cite":"Serrano et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. M. Serrano, M. Oshagh, H. M. Cegla et al.","title":"Can we detect the stellar differential rotation of WASP-7 through the Rossiter-McLaughlin observations?","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.08227","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 59686 A b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a giant star that was watched for over 12 years in a survey of 373 giant stars with the Lick Observatory (Ortiz et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet is found in a close binary. The giant star has a stellar companion on a highly eccentric orbit with a semi-major axis of 13.6 au (Ortiz et al. 2016). Infrared velocities checked that the signal is not caused by star pulsations or spots (Ortiz et al. 2016). Imaging with the Large Binocular Telescope helped describe the system (Ortiz et al. 2016). Computer models show that the planet is stable only if its orbit lies nearly in the plane of the binary and is locked in step with it, or if it orbits backward (Trifonov et al. 2018; Lee et al. 2024).","system":"The companion star has a minimum mass of 0.53 Suns and a very eccentric orbit, with eccentricity 0.73 (Trifonov et al. 2018). The planet circles the giant at about 1.09 au in a nearly round orbit (Trifonov et al. 2018). At closest approach, the stars come within about 3.7 au (Trifonov et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet moves with the binary or against it is not known (Lee et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd59686ab-Ortizetal2016","cite":"Ortiz et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Mauricio Ortiz, Sabine Reffert, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"Precise radial velocities of giant stars. IX. HD 59686 Ab: a massive circumstellar planet orbiting a giant star in a  13.6 au eccentric binary system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00963","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd59686ab-Trifonovetal2018","cite":"Trifonov et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Trifon Trifonov, Man Hoi Lee, Sabine Reffert et al.","title":"Dynamical Analysis of the Circumprimary Planet in the Eccentric Binary System HD 59686","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.01434","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd59686ab-Leeetal2024","cite":"Lee et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Man Hoi Lee, Ka Ho Wong, Ho Wan Cheng et al.","title":"Dynamics of Circumstellar Planets in Binary Star Systems","journal":"Complex Planetary Systems II: Latest Methods for an Interdisciplinary Approach","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921323004787","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd59686ab-Trifonovetal2015","cite":"Trifonov et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Trifon Trifonov, Man Hoi Lee, Sabine Reffert et al.","title":"New Evidence for Planets on S-type Orbits in Close Binary Systems","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-7913 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a group of stars that move through space together. The group is called the Cep-Her complex and stretches from Cepheus to Hercules (Bouma et al. 2022).","observed":"Motions from Gaia, spin periods from TESS and spectra show that the host star is about 36 million years old. It sits in a loose region of the Cep-Her group called CH-2 (Bouma et al. 2022). That makes the planet one of very few small planets with a reliable young age. The transit shape and high-resolution images support that it is a real planet (Bouma et al. 2022).","system":"The planet is one of three mini-Neptunes in different parts of the same young association. The study names it KOI-7913 Ab, which points to a stellar companion (Bouma et al. 2022).","history":"Young ages let astronomers see planets soon after birth, before they evolve much (Bouma et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi7913b-Boumaetal2022","cite":"Bouma et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. G. Bouma, R. Kerr, J. L. Curtis et al.","title":"Kepler and the Behemoth: Three Mini-Neptunes in a 40 Million Year Old Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01112","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"55 Cnc d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Interferometry gave the star's radius as 0.943 times the Sun's and its age as about 10.2 billion years (von Braun et al. 2011). That interferometry also shows the habitable zone lies at 0.67-1.32 au. Planet f spends most of its eccentric orbit in that zone (von Braun et al. 2011). Computer models suggest the whole system is probably tilted against the star's spin because of a distant binary companion (Kaib et al. 2011).","system":"The system is packed with planets. Planets b to f were known by 2011, including the transiting super-Earth 55 Cnc e (von Braun et al. 2011). The planets are probably coplanar with one another, yet inclined to the star's equator (Kaib et al. 2011).","history":"Tidal evolution of planet e may have pushed the inner planets through two secular resonances. That would explain the large tilt between the innermost and outermost planets (Hansen & Zink 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-55cncd-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Theo A. ten Brummelaar et al.","title":"55 Cancri: Stellar Astrophysical Parameters, a Planet in the Habitable Zone, and Implications for the Radius of a Transiting Super-Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncd-Kaibetal2011","cite":"Kaib et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nathan A. Kaib, Sean N. Raymond, Martin J. Duncan","title":"55 Cancri: A Coplanar Planetary System That is Likely Misaligned with Its Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5911","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncd-HansenZink2015","cite":"Hansen & Zink 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Bradley M. S. Hansen, Jonathon Zink","title":"On the potentially dramatic history of the super-Earth ρ 55 Cancri e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05539","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"47 UMa c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet has not been imaged yet. It is a high-priority target for the coronagraph on the Roman Space Telescope, which will image giant planets in reflected light (Smith et al. 2022). Simulations combine velocity data with mock Roman images. They show that the true orbit tilt and the timing of observations strongly affect what can be learned (Smith et al. 2022). The star is Sun-like in age and metallicity and is magnetically quiet (Gonzalez 1996; Henry et al. 1997).","system":"Studies of the system's stability check whether a terrestrial planet could sit in its habitable zone (Asghari et al. 2004).","history":"","open":"The planet's true orbit tilt, and so its true mass, is still unknown (Smith et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-47umac-Smithetal2022","cite":"Smith et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adam JRW Smith, Eric Nielsen, Bruce Macintosh et al.","title":"47 Ursae Majoris c: Exploring Roman CGI Observation Strategies","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umac-Gonzalez1996","cite":"Gonzalez 1996","year":"1996","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez","title":"Spectroscopic Analyses of the Planetary System Candidates: 55 Cnc, 51 Peg, 47 UMa, 70 Vir, and HD 114762","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9609148","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umac-Henryetal1997","cite":"Henry et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Gregory W. Henry, Sallie L. Baliunas, Robert A. Donahue et al.","title":"Properties of Sun-like Stars with Planets: 51 Pegasi, 47 Ursae Majoris, 70 Virginis, and HD 114762","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/303451","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umac-Asgharietal2004","cite":"Asghari et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"N. Asghari, C. Broeg, L. Carone et al.","title":"Stability of terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of Gl 777 A, HD 72659, Gl 614, 47 Uma and HD 4208","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0403152","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 12961 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a HARPS survey that monitors nearby M dwarfs, partly to improve statistics on giant planets around low-mass stars (Forveille et al. 2011).","observed":"The host is an early M-type dwarf. This planet has a minimum mass of 1 Saturn mass. Its host is a slightly metal-rich star ([Fe/H] = -0.07) (Forveille et al. 2011). Giant planets are found less often around M dwarfs than around heavier stars (Forveille et al. 2011). Together with Gl 676 A b, it supports the view that giant planets are more common around metal-rich M dwarfs (Forveille et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"The most massive planets are thought to form in discs with a large supply of solid material (Forveille et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip12961b-Forveilleetal2011","cite":"Forveille et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"T. Forveille, X. Bonfils, G. Lo Curto et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXVI. Two giant planets around M0 dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.1168","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1478 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of five hot and warm Jupiters found in TESS full-frame images and confirmed by a follow-up group (Rodriguez et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet was confirmed through ground-based photometry from many observatories and spectroscopy with the TRES spectrograph (Rodriguez et al. 2021). The host star is bright, which makes the planet well suited to further study (Rodriguez et al. 2021). Its star was also included in a later chemical abundance study of 25 Sun-like stars (Sun et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an F or G type star in a sample with temperatures between about 5600 and 6500 K (Rodriguez et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1478b-Rodriguezetal2021","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, George Zhou et al.","title":"TESS Delivers Five New Hot Giant Planets Orbiting Bright Stars from the Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01726","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1478b-Sunetal2025","cite":"Sun et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Q. Sun, C. Ji, S. X. Wang et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: PASTA II. Abundances for 25 solar twins and analogs (Sun+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-131 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet circles its star in just 8.9 hours, so it counts as an ultra-short-period planet (Dai et al. 2017). Spectra showed stellar activity mixed in with the orbital motion. The team measured the mass with two methods, one comparing velocities within single nights and one using a Gaussian process that also uses the light curve (Dai et al. 2017). The results agree on a mass of 6.5 Earth masses and a density of 6.0 g per cubic centimetre (Dai et al. 2017). A separate search found a phase curve where reflected and thermal light both contribute (Niraula et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2131b-Daietal2017","cite":"Dai et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Fei Dai, Joshua N. Winn, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"The Discovery and Mass Measurement of a New Ultra-short-period Planet: K2-131b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.00076","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2131b-Niraulaetal2018","cite":"Niraula et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Seth Redfield, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Discovery of Six Optical Phase Curves with K2","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09227","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-87 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spitzer watched the planet transit at 4.5 micrometres. Combined with K2 data, this improved its radius and added colour information that helped confirm it as a planet (Livingston et al. 2019). The new measurements sharpened the timing of future transits. The typical uncertainty in mid-2021 shrank from several hours to about a dozen minutes (Livingston et al. 2019). The study lists it as a promising target for radial velocity follow-up (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k287b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 50554 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler measurements at Lick Observatory confirmed an earlier announced planet around this star (Fischer et al. 2002). The orbit takes about 1254 days and is clearly eccentric, with e = 0.51 (Fischer et al. 2002). The minimum mass is 3.7 Jupiter masses at a distance of 2.2 au from the star (Fischer et al. 2002). The study reports similar confirmations for two other stars (Fischer et al. 2002).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd50554b-Fischeretal2002","cite":"Fischer et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to HD 136118, HD 50554, and HD 106252","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/341677","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-771 c":{"text":{"why":"The system is a target of the THIRSTEE project. The project studies sub-Neptunes around different stars to learn about their origins, using JWST (Lacedelli et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet was found in 31 ESPRESSO velocity measurements taken over about 94 days (Lacedelli et al. 2025). It does not transit, so only its minimum mass is known. The inner planet b was already known from TESS and has a radius of 1.36 Earth radii and a mass of 2.47 Earth masses (Lacedelli et al. 2025). The study describes c as a sub-Neptune orbiting an M dwarf (Lacedelli et al. 2025).","system":"The M dwarf lies at about 25 pc. Planet b is a rocky super-Earth with a 2.3-day orbit (Lacedelli et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi771c-Lacedellietal2025","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"G. Lacedelli, E. Palle, Y. T. Davis et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-771 RV datasets (Lacedelli+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-51 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was inferred, not seen. Kepler-51 d transited at a time that JWST measured about 10 years after Kepler, and it came out far from the three-planet prediction (Masuda et al. 2024). Adding a fourth outer planet explains the shift (Masuda et al. 2024). Its mass could be anything up to about Jupiter's, and its period up to about 10 years (Masuda et al. 2024). In every coplanar solution the inner planets keep masses below 10 Earth masses and so remain very low in density (Masuda et al. 2024).","system":"The star is under 1 billion years old and hosts three transiting super-puffs, planets with very low densities of about 0.1 g per cubic centimetre or less (Masuda et al. 2024). JWST found hazes on Kepler-51 d, or possibly a tilted ring (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The orbit and mass of this planet are still not pinned down (Masuda et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler51e-Masudaetal2024","cite":"Masuda et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kento Masuda, Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"A Fourth Planet in the Kepler-51 System Revealed by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01625","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51e-LibbyRobertsetal2026","cite":"Libby-Roberts et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, Aaron Bello-Arufe, Zachory K. Berta-Thompson et al.","title":"The James Webb Space Telescope NIRSpec-PRISM Transmission Spectrum of the Super-puff, Kepler-51d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21358","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 96700 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of 12 super-Earths reported around five Sun-like stars from over 10 years of HARPS velocity data (Unger et al. 2021). The team used Bayesian model comparison to decide how many planets each star hosts (Unger et al. 2021). For some stars they also compared different noise models to separate planet signals from stellar activity (Unger et al. 2021). The survey can detect planets down to super-Earths on orbits up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd96700d-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0909L b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a project that publishes all planets found by eye in 2021 data of the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network (Ryu et al. 2023).","observed":"This planet was one of four reported in the second paper of the series. Their planet-to-host mass ratios lie between about 0.0005 and 0.006 (Ryu et al. 2023). The by-eye sample brings the total of secure 2021 planets to 16 (Ryu et al. 2023). It is meant as a completeness check for the semi-automated searches used for statistics (Ryu et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0909lb-Ryuetal2023","cite":"Ryu et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Yoon-Hyun Ryu, In-Gu Shin, Hongjing Yang et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets II","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3998 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with the HARPS-N spectrograph for 869 days, giving 136 velocity measurements (Affer et al. 2016). Alongside the velocities, the team tracked activity indicators called S and H-alpha indices (Affer et al. 2016). These help tell planet signals from star spots. A later study reduced 206 HARPS-N spectra of this star (Stefanov et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3998b-Afferetal2016","cite":"Affer et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"L. Affer, G. Micela, M. Damasso et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 3998 RVs, S and Halpha indexes (Affer+, 2016)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35930117","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3998b-Stefanovetal2025","cite":"Stefanov et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. K. Stefanov, A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 3998 RVs, SMW and Halpha indexes (Stefanov+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 16175 b":{"text":{"why":"The host was found in the Lick Subgiants Planet Search, which monitors evolved stars (Peek et al. 2009).","observed":"The host is a G0 subgiant with 1.3 times the Sun's mass and very rich in metals ([Fe/H] above +0.3) (Peek et al. 2009). The planet has a minimum mass of 4.4 Jupiter masses and a highly eccentric orbit (e = 0.59) that takes 2.71 years (Peek et al. 2009). Later work merged SOPHIE data with Lick measurements to improve the orbit (Díaz et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd16175b-Peeketal2009","cite":"Peek et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Kathryn M. G. Peek, John Asher Johnson, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"Old, Rich, and Eccentric: Two Jovian Planets Orbiting Evolved Metal-Rich Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0904.2786","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd16175b-Dazetal2016","cite":"Díaz et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. F. Díaz, J. Rey, O. Demangeon et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XI. Three new companions and an orbit update: Giant planets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.07610","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is the brightest star known with a transiting giant planet on a 5 to 10 day orbit (Rodriguez et al. 2019). Doppler tomography shows the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, and no large eccentricity is seen (Rodriguez et al. 2019). The planet was found independently by a second team as MASCARA-3 b (Hjorth et al. 2019). The two teams reported notably inconsistent stellar parameters (Giovinazzi et al. 2024). XMM-Newton X-ray observations detected the star and caught a flare lasting about 2 ks (Pillitteri et al. 2023). Transit searches found no other transiting planets (Maciejewski 2020).","system":"The star is young at under 1 billion years. A single transit-like event in TESS data is thought to come from an eclipsing binary bound to the star, and needs follow-up to confirm (Rodriguez et al. 2019; Giovinazzi et al. 2024).","history":"The tidy, aligned orbit is surprising because it lacks the signs expected from migration caused by dynamical interactions (Rodriguez et al. 2019).","open":"The properties of the host star are still debated (Giovinazzi et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt24b-Rodriguezetal2019","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Jason D. Eastman, George Zhou et al.","title":"KELT-24b: A 5M <SUB>J</SUB> Planet on a 5.6 day Well-aligned Orbit around the Young V = 8.3 F-star HD 93148","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.03276","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt24b-Hjorthetal2019","cite":"Hjorth et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Hjorth, S. Albrecht, G. J. J. Talens et al.","title":"MASCARA-3b. A hot Jupiter transiting a bright F7 star in an aligned orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.05254","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt24b-Giovinazzietal2024","cite":"Giovinazzi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Mark R. Giovinazzi, Bryson Cale, Jason D. Eastman et al.","title":"Trials and Tribulations in the Reanalysis of KELT-24 b: A Case Study for the Importance of Stellar Modeling","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04288","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt24b-Pillitterietal2023","cite":"Pillitteri et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"I. Pillitteri, S. Colombo, G. Micela et al.","title":"The X-ray activity of F stars with hot Jupiters: KELT-24 versus WASP-18","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.00854","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt24b-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-79 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"All four transiting planets show timing shifts in their transits that match their distances from resonance with neighbours. Over 1282 days of Kepler data, these shifts allowed masses to be measured and confirmed that all four planets orbit the same star (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). All four have low densities for their sizes (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","system":"The orbital periods lie near a 1:2:4:6 chain of resonances, running from 13.5 to 81.1 days (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). The largest, Kepler-79 d, has the lowest density yet found for a sub-Saturn mass planet (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","history":"Simulations suggest the planets probably formed farther out and migrated inward, stopping at the inner edge of the gas disc (Wang et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler79b-JontofHutteretal2014","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jack J. Lissauer, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-79's Low Density Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2642","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler79b-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Su Wang, Jianghui Ji, Ji-Lin Zhou","title":"Predicting the Configuration of a Planetary System: KOI-152 Observed by Kepler","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2431","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1233.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star, also called HD 108236, is one of the brightest known to host five small transiting planets (Hoyer et al. 2022). The fifth planet, with a 29.54 day period, was found serendipitously by CHEOPS (Hoyer et al. 2022). CHEOPS and TESS then followed 16 and 43 transits, and a timing analysis looked for transit timing variations (Hoyer et al. 2022). The star is Sun-like (G3V). Its range of planets makes it a good test case for comparing atmospheres (Daylan 2021).","system":"The planets all have radii below 3 Earth radii. They span a broad range of sizes and equilibrium temperatures (Hoyer et al. 2022; Daylan 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi123301-Hoyeretal2022","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Hoyer, A. Bonfanti, A. Leleu et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 108236 system observed with CHEOPS and TESS (Hoyer+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi123301-Daylan2021","cite":"Daylan 2021","year":"2021","authors":"T. Daylan","title":"Towards comparative atmospheric characterization using multiplanetary systems from TESS","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2416 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the WINE collaboration, which systematically studies TESS warm Jupiters with periods of 10 to 300 days (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","observed":"This planet is more massive than Jupiter at 3.0 Jupiter masses, but smaller at 0.88 Jupiter radii. It is therefore dense, at 5.4 g per cubic centimetre (Eberhardt et al. 2023). Its orbit is moderately eccentric, with e = 0.32 (Eberhardt et al. 2023). The three planets in the study sit in a thinly populated zone between hot and warm Jupiters (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Such planets are useful for testing theories of formation, migration and radius inflation (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2416b-Eberhardtetal2023","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Melissa J. Hobson, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"Three Warm Jupiters around Solar-analog Stars Detected with TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17592","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-75 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet and its sibling WASP-65 b were reported as hot Jupiters without highly inflated radii (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013).","observed":"The radius of WASP-75 b is only slightly inflated, by less than about 10 per cent compared with models without a core (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013). Its density is similar to Saturn's (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013). That is notable because many planets in its mass range appear puffed up. The host is an F9 star, about 3 billion years old, with slightly more metals than the Sun (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp75b-Chewetal2013","cite":"Chew et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Y. Gómez Maqueo Chew, F. Faedi, D. Pollacco et al.","title":"Discovery of WASP-65b and WASP-75b: Two hot Jupiters without highly inflated radii","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6532","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3341 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 3341 is a 0.47 solar-mass M dwarf observed with HARPS for about five years, with a dense series of velocity measurements (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2015). The authors compared each signal with several stellar activity indicators. This tests whether a signal is a real planet or an effect of spots and activity cycles on the star (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2015). M dwarfs suit such searches because planets of a given mass cause larger wobbles around lighter stars (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3341b-AstudilloDefruetal2015","cite":"Astudillo-Defru et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"N. Astudillo-Defru, X. Bonfils, X. Delfosse et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXVI. Planetary systems and stellar activity of the M dwarfs GJ 3293, GJ 3341, and GJ 3543","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.7048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-32 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-32 hosts planets with periods of about 8.99, 20.66 and 31.7 days, close to a 3:2:1 ratio (Petigura et al. 2017). They were first confirmed by multiplicity arguments (Petigura et al. 2017). Masses came from radial velocities, with 43 HARPS and 6 PFS spectra from an earlier study, plus 31 Keck HIRES spectra (Petigura et al. 2017). HARPS velocities were also analysed jointly with K2 photometry in a later study (Lillo-Box et al. 2020).","system":"The planets lie near a 3:2:1 commensurability of their periods (Petigura et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k232c-Petiguraetal2017","cite":"Petigura et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"E. A. Petigura, E. Sinukoff, E. D. Lopez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Radial velocities of systems hosting sub-Saturns (Petigura+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51530142","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k232c-LilloBoxetal2020","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, T. Lopez, A. Santerne et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2-32 and K2-233 light and RV curves (Lillo-Box+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36400048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"POTS-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from the pre-OmegaTranS survey, which watched one dense field in the Carina region of the Milky Way disc. It took images about every 2 minutes in 2006-2008 (Koppenhoefer et al. 2013). The team searched 16,000 light curves for transits (Koppenhoefer et al. 2013).","observed":"Two planet candidates came out of the survey and POTS-1 b was confirmed by extensive follow-up (Koppenhoefer et al. 2013). The host is a mid-K dwarf with a radius of 0.59 times the Sun's (Koppenhoefer et al. 2013). It is faint, at I = 16.1, which made follow-up and confirmation difficult (Koppenhoefer et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pots1b-Koppenhoeferetal2013","cite":"Koppenhoefer et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. Koppenhoefer, R. P. Saglia, L. Fossati et al.","title":"A hot Jupiter transiting a mid-K dwarf found in the pre-OmegaCam Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.6574","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-26 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-26 b is one of two low-density giants reported together. It has a density of about 0.28 g per cubic centimetre, with a mass of 0.52 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.26 Jupiter radii (Almenara et al. 2013). The host is a slightly evolved G5 star about 9 billion years old, with solar metallicity (Almenara et al. 2013). Evolution models estimate a core of a few tens of Earth masses (Almenara et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot26b-Almenaraetal2013","cite":"Almenara et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. M. Almenara, F. Bouchy, P. Gaulme et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXIV. CoRoT-25b and CoRoT-26b: two low-density giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201321462","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 44219 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of eight companions reported together from HARPS radial-velocity data on low-activity solar-type stars, chosen so that starspots would disturb the measurements as little as possible (Naef et al. 2010). Across the group, minimum masses run from 0.58 to 2.54 Jupiter masses, which puts them in the planetary range (Naef et al. 2010). Orbital periods run from just under one year to almost seven years (Naef et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd44219b-Naefetal2010","cite":"Naef et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, G. Lo Curto et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extrasolar planets. XXIII. 8 planetary companions to low-activity solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.4600","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-232 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. That planet is a warm Saturn with a radius close to Jupiter's, on an eccentric 11.2-day orbit, and its equilibrium temperature is about 1030 K (Brahm et al. 2018). Its host star is bright, one of only four well-characterised warm Jupiters with hosts brighter than V = 10 (Brahm et al. 2018).","system":"The inner planet K2-232 b has an orbit that is noticeably eccentric, with e = 0.258 (Brahm et al. 2018). It holds an estimated 20 Earth masses of heavy elements (Brahm et al. 2018). The bright star makes the system a good target for studying giant-planet atmospheres and orbital evolution (Brahm et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2232c-Brahmetal2018","cite":"Brahm et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Brahm, N. Espinoza, A. Jordán et al.","title":"K2-232 b: a transiting warm Saturn on an eccentric P = 11.2 d orbit around a V = 9.9 star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-10 c":{"text":{"why":"The signal was first seen in the Kepler data but could not be confirmed as a planet at the time Kepler-10 b was announced (Fressin et al. 2011).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-10 b. Planet c is a 45-day signal that stood unconfirmed for a while. To rule out a false alarm from a blended eclipsing binary star, astronomers watched two transits with the Spitzer Space Telescope at 4.5 microns. The transit depth there matched the depth in Kepler's visible light, which excludes blends of a different colour (Fressin et al. 2011). Together with high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy, this left a false-alarm chance of 1.6 in 100,000, low enough to validate the planet (Fressin et al. 2011).","system":"The inner planet Kepler-10 b was the first rocky planet found by Kepler (Batalha et al. 2011). The star is old, about 10.4 billion years, making this the oldest known rocky-planet system when measured (Fogtmann-Schulz et al. 2014). The star's chemistry has been used to estimate the iron content of its rocky planets (Santos et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler10c-Fressinetal2011","cite":"Fressin et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"François Fressin, Guillermo Torres, Jean-Michel Désert et al.","title":"Kepler-10 c: a 2.2 Earth Radius Transiting Planet in a Multiple System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.4647","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10c-Batalhaetal2011","cite":"Batalha et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Natalie M. Batalha, William J. Borucki, Stephen T. Bryson et al.","title":"Kepler's First Rocky Planet: Kepler-10b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0605","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10c-FogtmannSchulzetal2014","cite":"Fogtmann-Schulz et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Alexandra Fogtmann-Schulz, Brian Hinrup, Vincent Van Eylen et al.","title":"Accurate Parameters of the Oldest Known Rocky-exoplanet Hosting System: Kepler-10 Revisited","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6336","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10c-Santosetal2015","cite":"Santos et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"N. C. Santos, V. Adibekyan, C. Mordasini et al.","title":"Constraining planet structure from stellar chemistry: the cases of CoRoT-7, Kepler-10, and Kepler-93","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08081","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2022-BLG-0440L b":{"text":{"why":"The planetary signal was weak and short-lived, so it needed both survey and follow-up telescopes to catch it (Zhang et al. 2023).","observed":"The event was a high-magnification lensing event, where the background star brightened strongly. The planet's brief signal was covered by the KMTNet survey and by follow-up observations (Zhang et al. 2023). Two competing explanations of the signal, a different caustic shape and a brown-dwarf companion, were ruled out, and so was a model with a second background star (Zhang et al. 2023). The host is likely a K or M dwarf in the Galactic disc, and the planet is probably Neptune-mass (Zhang et al. 2023).","system":"The planet-star separation is either about 1.9 or 4.6 au, and the data cannot yet choose between the two (Zhang et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The close or wide separation is unresolved (Zhang et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2022blg0440lb-Zhangetal2023","cite":"Zhang et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jiyuan Zhang, Weicheng Zang, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"KMT-2022-BLG-0440Lb: A new q < 10<SUP>-4</SUP> microlensing planet with the central-resonant caustic degeneracy broken","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06779","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"YZ Cet d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star's radial velocities have been measured with HARPS and later with CARMENES combined with HARPS (Stock et al. 2020). Radio observers have claimed signals from this system that might come from magnetic interaction between a planet and its star. A model of such interaction finds that YZ Cet b is most likely in the regime where interaction should occur, though the predicted radio emission from one type of model is generally very low (Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025).","system":"The star is a small, cool M dwarf, and the radio work focuses on its close-in planets (Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether the claimed radio signals are really caused by planets is not settled (Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-yzcetd-Stocketal2020","cite":"Stock et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Stock, J. Kemmer, S. Reffert et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: YZ Ceti CARMENES and HARPS radial velocity curve (Stock+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36360119","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-yzcetd-PeaMoinoPrezTorres2025","cite":"Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Luis Peña-Moñino, Miguel Pérez-Torres","title":"Modelling magnetic star─planet interaction in the iconic M dwarfs Proxima Centauri, YZ Ceti, and GJ 1151","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20891","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 806 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aim was to find small transiting planets around the closest and brightest stars (Palle et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is an ultra-short-period world that transits its star every 0.93 days. Its mean density is 4.40 g per cubic centimetre and its equilibrium temperature is 940 K (Palle et al. 2023). Its mass was measured with the CARMENES, MAROON-X and HIRES spectrographs, and the discovery paper also reports one transit observed with CARMENES (Palle et al. 2023). The authors describe it as a hot, low-density super-Earth (Palle et al. 2023).","system":"A second, non-transiting super-Earth, GJ 806 c, circles the star every 6.6 days (Palle et al. 2023). A third signal at 13.6 days could be another planet or stellar activity (Palle et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the 13.6-day signal is a planet is not decided (Palle et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj806b-Palleetal2023","cite":"Palle et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Palle, J. Orell-Miquel, M. Brady et al.","title":"GJ 806 (TOI-4481): A bright nearby multi-planetary system with a transiting hot low-density super-Earth","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06873","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102843 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This star was included in a study of 34 nearby Sun-like stars that each host one giant planet found by radial velocity, to see whether Earth-like planets could survive in their habitable zones (Kokaia et al. 2020). Computer simulations of the orbits found that stable habitable-zone orbits are rarer once a past instability, which ejected other planets, is assumed (Kokaia et al. 2020). HD 102843 is among seven systems listed as good candidates for future searches (Kokaia et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"No habitable-zone planet is known in this system; the study only suggests where one could survive (Kokaia et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102843b-Kokaiaetal2020","cite":"Kokaia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Giorgi Kokaia, Melvyn B. Davies, Alexander J. Mustill","title":"Resilient habitability of nearby exoplanet systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07573","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-265 b":{"text":{"why":"The short orbit and small size put the planet below the photoevaporation gap, the size range where intense starlight strips away planets' gas envelopes (Lam et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with HARPS radial-velocity follow-up. Its bulk density is 7.1 g per cubic centimetre, and composition models give it an Earth-like rocky interior with about 80 per cent rock by mass (Lam et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a bright G8 dwarf (Lam et al. 2018).","history":"The authors suggest it may be an exposed core left after its envelope eroded under strong stellar irradiation (Lam et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2265b-Lametal2018","cite":"Lam et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"K. W. F. Lam, A. Santerne, S. G. Sousa et al.","title":"K2-265 b: a transiting rocky super-Earth","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08869","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-238 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The team analysed TESS photometry together with ESPRESSO and HARPS radial velocities, using statistical models to separate the star's activity from planetary signals. The second transiting planet, TOI-238 c, appears in both the radial-velocity and TESS data (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet TOI-238 b is a super-Earth of 1.40 Earth radii and 3.40 Earth masses, at 0.02118 au from the star (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2024). The study describes the pair as a super-Earth and a mini-Neptune around a K dwarf (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi238c-Mascareoetal2024","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, V. M. Passegger, J. I. González Hernández et al.","title":"TESS and ESPRESSO discover a super-Earth and a mini-Neptune orbiting the K-dwarf TOI-238*","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04113","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-30 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet c is a warm Jupiter whose pull on its neighbours makes their transit times shift by more than 2 days (Panichi et al. 2018). Those timing shifts give its mass, about 536 Earth masses in one study and 549 in another (Panichi et al. 2018; Wu et al. 2018). Its light curve also shows no sign of flattening from spin, so it is consistent with a spherical planet (Lammers & Winn 2026).","system":"Kepler-30 is a very compact, quasi-resonant but long-term stable system. A massive Jovian planet sits between two Neptune-like companions. The inner pair lies near a 2:1 orbital resonance (Panichi et al. 2018). The outer Neptune-like planet has a very low density, 0.19 g per cubic centimetre (Panichi et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"The tilt of a warm Jupiter's spin axis could show how such planets form, and Kepler-30 c is one of six targets used to test this (Lammers & Winn 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler30c-Panichietal2018","cite":"Panichi et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"F. Panichi, K. Goździewski, C. Migaszewski et al.","title":"The architecture and formation of the Kepler-30 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04962","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler30c-Wuetal2018","cite":"Wu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Dong-Hong Wu, Songhu Wang, Ji-Lin Zhou et al.","title":"TTV-determined Masses for Warm Jupiters and Their Close Planetary Companions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02217","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler30c-LammersWinn2026","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Constraining the Planetary Obliquity Distribution of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2018-BLG-0748L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event was short, lasting about 4.4 days, and its light curve showed small asymmetric deviations in the centre. These were explained by a binary-lens model with a planet-to-host mass ratio near 2 in 1000 (Han et al. 2020). A small angular Einstein radius and the short timescale together point to a very low-mass host, near the boundary between stars and brown dwarfs (Han et al. 2020).","system":"The estimated host mass is 0.087 solar masses and the planet is about 0.18 Jupiter masses, a sub-Saturn (Han et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The host mass depends on an assumption about how often planets occur around different hosts, so its nature is unclear until high-resolution follow-up (Han et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg0748lb-Hanetal2020","cite":"Han et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Cheongho Han, In-Gu Shin, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-0748Lb: sub-Saturn microlensing planet orbiting an ultracool host","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05204","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 167042 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its host is a K1 subgiant of about 1.5 solar masses, a star that was probably an early F or A-type dwarf earlier in its life (Sato et al. 2008). The orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.10 (Sato et al. 2008).","system":"The host has solar or sub-solar metal content, supporting a lack of the metal-rich tendency seen for planets around Sun-like stars (Sato et al. 2008).","history":"","open":"Debate over how often planets orbit intermediate-mass stars depends on getting these stars' masses right (White et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd167042b-Satoetal2008","cite":"Sato et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eri Toyota, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: 14 Andromedae, 81 Ceti, 6 Lyncis, and HD167042","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.0268","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd167042b-Whiteetal2018","cite":"White et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"T. R. White, D. Huber, A. W. Mann et al.","title":"Interferometric diameters of five evolved intermediate-mass planet-hosting stars measured with PAVO at the CHARA Array","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05976","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-93 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Kepler-93 has high-quality asteroseismic data, meaning its sound-wave oscillations measure its density (Ballard et al. 2014). Kepler-93 b, a terrestrial world, was measured to within 120 km using Kepler and Spitzer transits (Ballard et al. 2014).","system":"The star is a Sun-like star of 0.911 solar masses, one of the lowest-mass stars studied with asteroseismology at the time (Ballard et al. 2014). It is a benchmark target for the future PLATO mission (Bétrisey et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler93c-Ballardetal2014","cite":"Ballard et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Sarah Ballard, William J. Chaplin, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"Kepler-93b: A Terrestrial World Measured to within 120 km, and a Test Case for a New Spitzer Observing Mode","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.3659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler93c-Btriseyetal2022","cite":"Bétrisey et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Bétrisey, C. Pezzotti, G. Buldgen et al.","title":"Kepler-93: A testbed for detailed seismic modelling and orbital evolution of super-Earths around solar-like stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.13686","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3998 d":{"text":{"why":"The planet had earlier been dismissed as stellar activity (Stefanov et al. 2025).","observed":"The signal was found by modelling HARPS-N velocities together with the star's rotation, which the team put at 30.2 days, and a long-term activity cycle near 316 days (Stefanov et al. 2025). The 41.78-day signal was previously attributed to stellar activity and was recovered as a planet by the joint model (Stefanov et al. 2025). It receives about 1.2 times Earth's sunlight, one of the few known planets with an Earth-like insolation (Stefanov et al. 2025).","system":"Two inner super-Earths, GJ 3998 b and c, were confirmed in the same data (Stefanov et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3998d-Stefanovetal2025","cite":"Stefanov et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. K. Stefanov, A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 3998 RVs, SMW and Halpha indexes (Stefanov+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came from a wide-field transit survey, SuperWASP, followed up with radial velocities (Collier Cameron et al. 2007).","observed":"The host was flagged as a likely transit host by the 2004 SuperWASP survey, and the SOPHIE spectrograph then detected radial-velocity variations in phase with the transits. Line-bisector studies ruled out a blended binary star as the cause (Collier Cameron et al. 2007). At discovery the radius was only loosely known, between 0.65 and 1.26 Jupiter radii (Collier Cameron et al. 2007). Its spin-orbit alignment was later measured through the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, where a planet crossing the star distorts the star's light as the planet blocks part of its spinning disc (Triaud et al. 2010).","system":"The host is a K1 dwarf (Collier Cameron et al. 2007).","history":"The alignment measurement was meant to test how hot Jupiters came to be so close to their stars (Triaud et al. 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp2b-Cameronetal2007","cite":"Cameron et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"A. Collier Cameron, F. Bouchy, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"WASP-1b and WASP-2b: two new transiting exoplanets detected with SuperWASP and SOPHIE","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609688","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp2b-Triaudetal2010","cite":"Triaud et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"A. H. M. J. Triaud, A. Collier Cameron, D. Queloz et al.","title":"Spin-orbit angle measurements for six southern transiting planets. New insights into the dynamical origins of hot Jupiters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.2353","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":28,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 41378 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has been seen to transit only twice, three years apart, so its true period is uncertain. The best estimate of 278.36 days rests on stability arguments and a partial detection of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Sulis et al. 2024). CHEOPS looked at the predicted transit time and saw no transit (Sulis et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Either large transit timing variations of over 22.4 hours hide the transit, or the period is wrong. Alternative periods that minimise eccentricity are 101.22 and 371.14 days, and TESS was to test the shortest in 2025 (Sulis et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip41378d-Sulisetal2024","cite":"Sulis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Sulis, L. Borsato, S. Grouffal et al.","title":"HIP 41378 observed by CHEOPS: Where is planet d?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20077","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-150 f":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came from a search for planets that could have been lost when the Kepler pipeline masked known transits, leaving holes in the light curves (Schmitt et al. 2017).","observed":"The team examined 114 stars with three or more confirmed planets, subtracted the known transit signals and searched the cleaned light curves again. The Kepler pipeline found no credible new transits, but a follow-up visual search through the cleaned data produced the long-period, Neptune-sized planet Kepler-150 f (Schmitt et al. 2017).","system":"The simulation suggests only a few per cent of transiting planets are lost to masking, peaking near 3.3 per cent at periods of 400 to 500 days (Schmitt et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler150f-Schmittetal2017","cite":"Schmitt et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Joseph R. Schmitt, Jon M. Jenkins, Debra A. Fischer","title":"A Search for Lost Planets in the Kepler Multi-planet Systems and the Discovery of the Long-period, Neptune-sized Exoplanet Kepler-150 f","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09229","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MWC 758 c":{"text":{"why":"Its star is very young, and the planet is one of the youngest known, which makes it a chance to study planet formation at its earliest stage (Wagner et al. 2023).","observed":"JWST's NIRCam camera imaged the star's disk and searched outside about 1 arcsecond for planets. It saw the two-armed spiral disk, a background star and a background galaxy, but no clear companions (Wagner et al. 2023). The two reported candidates lie at separations where the data were not sensitive enough to detect them (Wagner et al. 2023). The images still rule out companions above about 2 Jupiter masses at 150 au and about 0.5 Jupiter masses beyond 600 au (Wagner et al. 2023). A separate JWST proposal aimed to measure how much circumplanetary dust hides this planet (Wagner et al. 2023).","system":"The star is a young one with a spiral protoplanetary disk, and the spirals are likely driven by companions (Wagner et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Ground-based data suggest dust dims the planet by more than 8 magnitudes in visible light, and the true amount could be far higher (Wagner et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-mwc758c-Wagneretal2023","cite":"Wagner et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Wagner, Korash Assani, Ammar Bayyari et al.","title":"Imaging Planet Formation at its Earliest Stages: Measuring The Extinction Level of an Enshrouded Protoplanet","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 2","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":32,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 212771 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host is a red giant, an evolved star that has swollen after leaving the main sequence. NASA's TESS satellite recorded its solar-like oscillations, tiny brightness ripples caused by sound waves inside the star (Campante et al. 2019). These were the first detections of such oscillations in a previously known planet-host star by TESS (Campante et al. 2019). The ripples let the team estimate the star's fundamental properties with a grid of stellar models (Campante et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd212771b-Campanteetal2019","cite":"Campante et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Tiago L. Campante, Enrico Corsaro, Mikkel N. Lund et al.","title":"TESS Asteroseismology of the Known Red-giant Host Stars HD 212771 and HD 203949","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.05961","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-54 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed by combining ground-based light curves with radial velocity measurements, which track the star's wobble (Bakos et al. 2015). Its radius is smaller than that of 92% of known transiting planets heavier than Saturn (Bakos et al. 2015). The host is one of the lowest-mass stars known to host a hot Jupiter (Bakos et al. 2015). The star's brightness also varies every 15.6 days, which the team reads as its rotation (Bakos et al. 2015). Later multiband transit observations from India refined the planet's properties (Saha et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp54b-Bakosetal2015","cite":"Bakos et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti et al.","title":"HAT-P-54b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a 0.6 M<SUB>☉</SUB> Star in Field 0 of the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.4417","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp54b-Sahaetal2021","cite":"Saha et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Suman Saha, Aritra Chakrabarty, Sujan Sengupta","title":"Multiband Transit Follow-up Observations of Five Hot Jupiters with Critical Noise Treatments: Improved Physical Properties","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-62 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper described a system of five planets around a K-type star, of which this is the innermost (Borucki et al. 2013). Two outer planets, e and f, orbit in the star's habitable zone, where liquid water could exist, and models suggest both could be solid, either rocky or mostly water ice (Borucki et al. 2013).","system":"Five planets orbit this star, with periods from 5.7 to 267.3 days (Borucki et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler62b-Boruckietal2013","cite":"Borucki et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"William J. Borucki, Eric Agol, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Kepler-62: A Five-Planet System with Planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth Radii in the Habitable Zone","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7387","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-30 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity measurements from several spectrographs confirmed the transit signal as a planet (Johnson et al. 2016). Two further teams measured it independently, one finding a circular orbit around a G dwarf (Brahm et al. 2016). The star has a low metal content for a hot Jupiter host (Johnson et al. 2016). One team found the planet puffed up beyond normal size, orbiting a slowly rotating star (Lillo-Box et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":"The star is a good target for measuring the tilt of the orbit with the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, and for atmospheric studies (Lillo-Box et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k230b-Johnsonetal2016","cite":"Johnson et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Marshall C. Johnson, Davide Gandolfi, Malcolm Fridlund et al.","title":"Two Hot Jupiters from K2 Campaign 4","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.07844","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k230b-Brahmetal2016","cite":"Brahm et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Matías Jones, Néstor Espinoza et al.","title":"An Independent Discovery of Two Hot Jupiters from the K2 Mission","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.01721","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k230b-LilloBoxetal2016","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, O. Demangeon, A. Santerne et al.","title":"K2-30 b and K2-34 b: Two inflated hot Jupiters around solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.07635","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"G 196-3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion is a young, low-mass object with an L-type spectrum. Its spectrum shows features that are sensitive to surface gravity, and these point to a low gravity and an age of roughly 20 to 300 million years (McGovern et al. 2004). It was one of the first brown dwarf companions to be confirmed by both spectroscopy and proper motion (Guenther et al. 2001). A search at 1.3 mm for a leftover debris disk around it found nothing (Zakhozhay et al. 2023).","system":"It orbits an M-type dwarf at a wide separation of at least 100 au (Zakhozhay et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Its mass lies on the borderline between a planet and a brown dwarf (Zakhozhay et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-g1963b-McGovernetal2004","cite":"McGovern et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"Mark R. McGovern, J. Davy Kirkpatrick, Ian S. McLean et al.","title":"Identifying Young Brown Dwarfs Using Gravity-Sensitive Spectral Features","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0309634","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-g1963b-Guentheretal2001","cite":"Guenther et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"E. W. Guenther, R. Neuhäuser, N. Huélamo et al.","title":"Infrared spectrum and proper motion of the brown dwarf companion of HR 7329 in Tucanae","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20000051","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-g1963b-Zakhozhayetal2023","cite":"Zakhozhay et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Olga V. Zakhozhay, María Rosa Zapatero Osorio, Víctor J. S. Béjar et al.","title":"New constraints on the presence of debris disks around G 196-3 B and VHS J125601.92-125723.9 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.12163","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 181720 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is one of about 100 moderately metal-poor stars searched for giant planets (Santos et al. 2010).","observed":"Its minimum mass is 0.37 Jupiter masses, and the orbit is moderately eccentric (Santos et al. 2010). The full survey of 104 stars suggests that giant planets around metal-poor stars may be more common than thought (Santos et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":"The survey behind it finds the chance of having a planet rises steeply with the star's metal content (Santos et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd181720b-Santosetal2010","cite":"Santos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXI. Three new giant planets orbiting the metal-poor stars HD 5388, HD 181720, and HD 190984","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3216","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-332 b":{"text":{"why":"It sits deep in the Neptunian desert, a range of short orbits and intermediate sizes where planets should be easy to find but few are known (Osborn et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is smaller than Neptune but has an unusually large mass, giving it one of the highest densities of any Neptune-sized planet found so far (Osborn et al. 2023). A four-layer model of its interior suggests it holds a negligible hydrogen-helium envelope (Osborn et al. 2023). Few planets this massive are known to lack such an envelope (Osborn et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"The planet would have been expected to gather a Jupiter-like envelope, but photoevaporation, the star blowing gas away, cannot account for its loss (Osborn et al. 2023).","open":"How it formed and lost its envelope remains a challenge for planet formation theory (Osborn et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi332b-Osbornetal2023","cite":"Osborn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ares Osborn, David J. Armstrong, Jorge Fernández Fernández et al.","title":"TOI-332 b: a super dense Neptune found deep within the Neptunian desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12137","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gaia22dkvL b":{"text":{"why":"The event was alerted by Gaia and points toward a disk star rather than the usual bulge fields (Wu et al. 2024).","observed":"Its host is a turnoff star of about 1.1 solar masses at roughly 1.3 kiloparsecs (Wu et al. 2024). The host is far brighter than any earlier microlensing planet host (Wu et al. 2024). This makes radial velocity follow-up possible, which could give the planet's orbital period and eccentricity (Wu et al. 2024). Gaia's astrometric microlensing should also measure the system's properties directly (Wu et al. 2024).","system":"Radial velocities could search for inner planets, as expected from a link between inner and outer planets seen in Kepler and radial velocity surveys (Wu et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"A geometric ambiguity in the light curve is still unresolved, but Gaia data could break it (Wu et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gaia22dkvlb-Wuetal2024","cite":"Wu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Zexuan Wu, Subo Dong, Tuan Yi et al.","title":"Gaia22dkvLb: A Microlensing Planet Potentially Accessible to Radial-velocity Characterization","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.03944","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 3167 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is bright and nearby, and one of the closest known to host several transiting planets (Vanderburg et al. 2016). Radial velocity follow-up gave the planet a density of about 8 g per cubic centimetre, typical of a rocky ultra-short-period planet (Gandolfi et al. 2017). Spectra taken during transit show its orbit is aligned with the star's spin, while planet c orbits at a steep angle to it (Bourrier et al. 2021). Models suggest its dayside is hot enough to evaporate rock, so its atmosphere could be probed (Piette et al. 2023).","system":"The star hosts at least two transiting planets, and the outer one, a mini-Neptune, has a thick gas envelope (Gandolfi et al. 2017). The two orbits are close to perpendicular (Bourrier et al. 2021).","history":"Lava planets like this one probably migrated inward from orbits of 1 to 10 days (Herath et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd3167b-Vanderburgetal2016","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Allyson Bieryla, Dmitry A. Duev et al.","title":"Two Small Planets Transiting HD 3167","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.05248","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167b-Gandolfietal2017","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Davide Gandolfi, Oscar Barragán, Artie P. Hatzes et al.","title":"The Transiting Multi-planet System HD 3167: A 5.7 M <SUB>⊕</SUB> Super-Earth and an 8.3 M <SUB>⊕</SUB> Mini-Neptune","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167b-Bourrieretal2021","cite":"Bourrier et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"V. Bourrier, C. Lovis, M. Cretignier et al.","title":"The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect revolutions: an ultra-short period planet and a warm mini-Neptune on perpendicular orbits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14214","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167b-Pietteetal2023","cite":"Piette et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Anjali A. A. Piette, Peter Gao, Kara Brugman et al.","title":"Rocky Planet or Water World? Observability of Low-density Lava World Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.10100","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167b-Herathetal2026","cite":"Herath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mahesh Herath, Nicolas B. Cowan, Charles-Édouard Boukaré et al.","title":"Coupled Orbital and Interior Structure Evolution of Lava Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18682","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 32518 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was re-examined in a long-running Okayama survey of 32 planetary systems around evolved stars (Teng et al. 2023). Its radial velocity variations may indicate an additional planet, stellar activity, or another understudied source (Teng et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"What causes the extra variation in the star's motion is not settled (Teng et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd32518b-Tengetal2023","cite":"Teng et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"Revisiting planetary systems in the Okayama Planet Search Program: A new long-period planet, RV astrometry joint analysis, and a multiplicity-metallicity trend around evolved stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05343","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-50 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came from the HATSouth network of automated telescopes on three continents (Henning et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet is one of four hot Jupiters announced together (Henning et al. 2018). It has a low density, with a radius larger than Jupiter's for under half its mass (Henning et al. 2018). Its star is metal-rich, larger and more massive than the Sun (Henning et al. 2018). The light curve shows a marginal additional transit feature (Henning et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The extra transit feature in the light curve is only marginal (Henning et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats50b-Henningetal2018","cite":"Henning et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Th. Henning, L. Mancini, P. Sarkis et al.","title":"HATS-50b through HATS-53b: Four Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting G-type Stars Discovered by the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.04324","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2046 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with radial velocities from mid-sized 2 to 3 metre telescopes (Kabáth et al. 2022). Its star is an F-type main sequence star among the youngest known to host a hot Jupiter, with an age of 100 to 400 million years (Kabáth et al. 2022). Spectra taken during transit show the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin (Zak et al. 2025). It was one of 20 planets searched for escaping helium and hydrogen, though new helium detections were reported for two other planets (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its short period makes it a target for searches for tidal decay, where the orbit slowly shrinks (Huchmala et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2046b-Kabthetal2022","cite":"Kabáth et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Petr Kabáth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"TOI-2046b, TOI-1181b, and TOI-1516b, three new hot Jupiters from TESS: planets orbiting a young star, a subgiant, and a normal star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01860","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2046b-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquities of eight close-in gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.05615","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2046b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2046b-Huchmalaetal2024","cite":"Huchmala et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Rachel Huchmala, Hailey Stubbers, Dallon Carlson et al.","title":"SuPerPiG @ Boise State: Ground-Based Transit Searches for Tidal Decay","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #243","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-2 S d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The two stars are nearly identical, with very similar spectra and brightness, and both are metal-rich (Burke et al. 2007). Radial velocities of the planet-hosting star also showed a long-term variation that remains unexplained (Narita et al. 2011).","system":"The star belongs to a pair of near-twin stars about 31 arcseconds apart on the sky, moving together through space (Burke et al. 2007).","history":"The pair's Galactic motion suggests they formed in the metal-rich inner disk and were later scattered into the solar neighbourhood (Burke et al. 2007).","open":"The cause of the long-term velocity variation needs more measurements (Narita et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo2sd-Burkeetal2007","cite":"Burke et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, P. R. McCullough, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-2b: Transiting Hot Jupiter in a Metal-rich Common Proper Motion Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo2sd-Naritaetal2011","cite":"Narita et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Norio Narita, Teruyuki Hirano, Bun'ei Sato et al.","title":"XO-2b: a Prograde Planet with Negligible Eccentricity and an Additional Radial Velocity Variation","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.6136","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":8,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-10 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-10 b. The star's pulsations were used to find that the system is old, about 10.41 billion years, the oldest known to host a rocky planet (Fogtmann-Schulz et al. 2014). The inner planet, Kepler-10 b, was Kepler's first rocky planet (Batalha et al. 2011). A second planet, Kepler-10 c, was validated as a 2.2 Earth-radius planet (Fressin et al. 2011). The star's composition suggests the inner planets have iron fractions matching the star's iron abundance (Santos et al. 2015).","system":"At least two inner planets orbit the star, with periods of 0.84 and 45 days (Batalha et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler10d-FogtmannSchulzetal2014","cite":"Fogtmann-Schulz et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Alexandra Fogtmann-Schulz, Brian Hinrup, Vincent Van Eylen et al.","title":"Accurate Parameters of the Oldest Known Rocky-exoplanet Hosting System: Kepler-10 Revisited","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6336","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10d-Batalhaetal2011","cite":"Batalha et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Natalie M. Batalha, William J. Borucki, Stephen T. Bryson et al.","title":"Kepler's First Rocky Planet: Kepler-10b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0605","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10d-Fressinetal2011","cite":"Fressin et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"François Fressin, Guillermo Torres, Jean-Michel Désert et al.","title":"Kepler-10 c: a 2.2 Earth Radius Transiting Planet in a Multiple System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.4647","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10d-Santosetal2015","cite":"Santos et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"N. C. Santos, V. Adibekyan, C. Mordasini et al.","title":"Constraining planet structure from stellar chemistry: the cases of CoRoT-7, Kepler-10, and Kepler-93","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08081","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 65891 b":{"text":{"why":"The search that turned it up is the EXPRESS program, which aims to find companions around evolved stars and to study how the host star's mass and evolution affect their orbits (Jones et al. 2015).","observed":"The host is an intermediate-mass giant star, a star that has left the main sequence and expanded. Giants like this show thousands of spectral lines, which helps precise velocity measurements even though the stars are hard targets (Jones et al. 2015). Velocity data from the CHIRON and FEROS spectrographs gave an orbit of about 1085 days with a fairly round shape (Jones et al. 2015). A later study used Gaia position data to estimate the true mass, since velocities alone give only a minimum. It found the object is probably heavier than 13.5 Jupiter masses, which would put it in the brown dwarf range (Kiefer et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether this is a true planet or a brown dwarf depends on its real mass, which the Gaia analysis puts above the usual planet limit (Kiefer et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip65891b-Jonesetal2015","cite":"Jones et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. I. Jones, J. S. Jenkins, P. Rojo et al.","title":"Giant planets around two intermediate-mass evolved stars and confirmation of the planetary nature of HIP 67851c","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.06718","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65891b-Kieferetal2021","cite":"Kiefer et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"F. Kiefer, G. Hébrard, A. Lecavelier des Etangs et al.","title":"Determining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia. Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14164","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2147 b":{"text":{"why":"Its orbit is eccentric and its period falls in the 10 to 200 day range of warm Jupiters, a group whose wide spread of orbit shapes points to several different formation and migration paths (Thomas et al. 2026).","observed":"TESS light curves were combined with ground-based photometry from LCOGT and MuSCAT2, speckle imaging to rule out close stellar companions, and velocity data from the MaHPS spectrograph (Thomas et al. 2026). The orbit is clearly eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.29, and the host is a G-type star slightly poorer in metals than the Sun (Thomas et al. 2026). Models of the planet's interior suggest that heating from tides, caused by the changing pull of the star along the eccentric orbit, probably helps explain its mildly inflated radius (Thomas et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2147b-Thomasetal2026","cite":"Thomas et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Luis Thomas, Louise D. Nielsen, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"TOI-2147 b and TOI-6019 b: Two eccentric warm Jupiters detected and characterized with TESS and MaHPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.20224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2017-BLG-1434L b":{"text":{"why":"It was the eighth microlensing planet with a planet-to-star mass ratio below 1 in 10,000, and the sample was used to confirm a turnover in how common planets are at different mass ratios (Udalski et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet is a cold super-Earth orbiting a dwarf star of about 0.23 solar masses. Its projected separation from the star is about 1.18 au, roughly 1.9 times the distance of the snow line, where water ice can form (Udalski et al. 2018). The system is quite nearby for a microlensing planet, at about 0.86 kiloparsecs. The star is close and light, which made the 'microlens parallax' large and well measured, and that allowed precise mass measurements (Udalski et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2017blg1434lb-Udalskietal2018","cite":"Udalski et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. Udalski, Y.-H. Ryu, S. Sajadian et al.","title":"OGLE-2017-BLG-1434Lb: Eighth q<1×10<SUP>-4</SUP> Mass-Ratio Microlens Planet Confirms Turnover in Planet Mass-Ratio Function","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.02582","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"91 Aqr b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet comes from a Lick Observatory survey of 182 K giant stars, using the Hamilton echelle spectrograph with an iodine cell, a gas cell that gives a steady wavelength reference. Velocity precision was typically 5 m/s (Mitchell et al. 2003). The companion has a minimum mass of 2.9 Jupiter masses and a period of about 182 days (Mitchell et al. 2003). Because giant stars can pulse and carry spots that mimic a planet, the team modelled both effects and concluded they do not cause the velocity changes seen in this star (Mitchell et al. 2003).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-91aqrb-Mitchelletal2003","cite":"Mitchell et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"D. S. Mitchell, S. Frink, A. Quirrenbach et al.","title":"Four Substellar Companions Found Around K Giant Stars","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 168746 b":{"text":{"why":"At the time it was among the lightest planets ever found, with a minimum mass of only 0.77 that of Saturn (Pepe et al. 2002).","observed":"Spotting a Saturn-mass planet by its pull on the star needs very precise velocity measurements. The CORALIE spectrograph, which uses a simultaneous thorium-argon lamp as a wavelength reference, reached a precision better than 3 m/s (Pepe et al. 2002). The discovery paper also introduced a weighted cross-correlation method that lowers photon noise in the velocities (Pepe et al. 2002). Small-telescope photometry later reported brightness changes in the star with periods matching the planet's orbit, and the authors suggest a possible link to chromospheric activity, the glow of the star's outer atmosphere (Kuznyetsova et al. 2014). Its composition is close to the Sun's (Sadakane et al. 2002).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet really drives activity in its star, through tides or magnetic effects, is only suggested by the photometry (Kuznyetsova et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd168746b-Pepeetal2002","cite":"Pepe et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"F. Pepe, M. Mayor, F. Galland et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets VII. Two short-period Saturnian companions to <ASTROBJ>HD 108147</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 168746</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202457","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd168746b-Kuznyetsovaetal2014","cite":"Kuznyetsova et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Yu. Kuznyetsova, V. Krushevska, M. Andreev et al.","title":"Photometric researches of chromospheric activity variations for star systems with exoplanets using small telescopes","journal":"Contributions of the Astronomical Observatory Skalnate Pleso","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd168746b-Sadakaneetal2002","cite":"Sadakane et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Kozo Sadakane, Michiko Ohkubo, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Abundance Analyses of 12 Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets Observed with the SUBARU/HDS","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/54.6.911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86226 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This small planet shares its system with a giant planet on a roughly 1600-day orbit, which was known before the transiting planet was found (Teske et al. 2020). The measured density of 3.97 g per cubic centimetre is low enough to suggest at least a small envelope of volatile gases, but the mix of rock, iron and water is not well constrained (Teske et al. 2020). It lies near the radius gap and the hot Neptune desert, two features of the planet population, and the bright host star makes it a good target for studying its atmosphere (Teske et al. 2020). Models treat it as a low-density lava world whose dayside could be hot enough to evaporate its surface (Piette et al. 2023).","system":"The outer giant planet turned out to be less eccentric and less massive than first reported (Teske et al. 2020).","history":"If planets lose their atmospheres through heat escaping from the core, this planet is on a shortlist of those that may be losing gas today (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","open":"The mix of rock, iron, water and gas inside it is not well constrained (Teske et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86226c-Teskeetal2020","cite":"Teske et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Johanna Teske, Matías R. Díaz, Rafael Luque et al.","title":"TESS Reveals a Short-period Sub-Neptune Sibling (HD 86226c) to a Known Long-period Giant Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.13927","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd86226c-Pietteetal2023","cite":"Piette et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Anjali A. A. Piette, Peter Gao, Kara Brugman et al.","title":"Rocky Planet or Water World? Observability of Low-density Lava World Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.10100","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd86226c-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-17 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour HAT-P-17 b. This outer planet was reported together with an inner transiting planet, HAT-P-17 b, in a paper that describes a multi-planet system (Howard et al. 2012). Planet c takes about 4.4 years to orbit and its path is nearly circular, with a minimum mass of 1.31 Jupiter masses (Howard et al. 2012). No transits of c have been seen and it may not transit at all, so its tilt and true mass are unknown (Howard et al. 2012). The inner planet is a hot Saturn on an eccentric, 10.3-day orbit, which makes the system an unusual pairing (Howard et al. 2012).","system":"The inner planet, HAT-P-17 b, transits a bright early K dwarf and has an eccentric orbit with e = 0.342 (Howard et al. 2012). Its spin-orbit alignment was later targeted with HARPS-N observations (Mancini et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The orbital tilt of planet c is unknown because transits have not been observed (Howard et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp17c-Howardetal2012","cite":"Howard et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"A. W. Howard, G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-17b,c: A Transiting, Eccentric, Hot Saturn and a Long-period, Cold Jupiter","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3898","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp17c-Mancinietal2022","cite":"Mancini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXXVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and revising the physical and orbital parameters of the HAT-P-15, HAT-P-17, HAT-P-21, HAT-P-26, HAT-P-29 eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1077L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The microlensing event had a subtle and complicated anomaly near its peak. Simple models with a binary lens or a binary source left clear residuals, and only a model with three lensing bodies explained all the major features (Han et al. 2022). In that model the two companions have mass ratios to the host of about 1.56 and 1.75 thousandths, which is 1.6 and 1.8 times the Jupiter-to-Sun ratio, so both are giant planets (Han et al. 2022). The system was the fifth confirmed multi-planet system seen through microlensing (Han et al. 2022).","system":"The lens is a system of two giant planets around one host star (Han et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1077lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrew Gould, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-1077L: The fifth confirmed multiplanetary system detected by microlensing","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16734","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-180 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Another team found this planet independently and made the first mass measurement of it, using velocity data (Korth et al. 2019). Its density suggests a rocky composition (Korth et al. 2019). It sits above the so-called planetary radius gap, the dip in the number of planets with sizes between rocky super-Earths and puffier mini-Neptunes (Korth et al. 2019). It is one of the densest mini-Neptune-sized planets known, and one of the few such planets that transit a metal-poor star (Korth et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a metal-poor K dwarf, with an iron content well below the Sun's (Korth et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2180b-Korthetal2019","cite":"Korth et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Korth, Sz Csizmadia, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"K2-140b and K2-180b - Characterization of a hot Jupiter and a mini-Neptune from the K2 mission","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-453 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a pair of eclipsing stars of 0.94 and 0.195 solar masses that circle each other every 27.32 days (Welsh et al. 2015). The planet's orbital plane is rapidly precessing, meaning it wobbles, with a cycle of about 103 years. Only in the second half of the Kepler data did the tilt line up so that transits appeared, and three conjunctions in the first half produced none (Welsh et al. 2015). Transits are visible only about 8.9 percent of the time, so for every such system seen there are about 11.5 more that exist but do not now show transits (Welsh et al. 2015). The planet is too light to disturb the binary, so only an upper limit of 16 Earth masses exists (Welsh et al. 2015).","system":"The planet's period is 8.8 times the binary's, well outside the zone where its orbit would be unstable, and it lies within the habitable zone of the binary (Welsh et al. 2015).","history":"Circumbinary planets are thought to have formed far from their stars and migrated inward, partly because attempts to build them close to the stability limit have failed (Haghighipour 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler453b-Welshetal2015","cite":"Welsh et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"William F. Welsh, Jerome A. Orosz, Donald R. Short et al.","title":"Kepler 453 b - The 10th Kepler Transiting Circumbinary Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.1605","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler453b-Haghighipour2017","cite":"Haghighipour 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Nader Haghighipour","title":"Dynamical Evolution of Circumbinary Disks and Embedded Planets","journal":"NASA XRP proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Early TESS data hinted that two planets might share a horseshoe-shaped coorbital path. Follow-up with CHEOPS, NGTS and SPECULOOS instead revealed a compact system of six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune, orbiting a cool K dwarf (Hooton et al. 2022). Five of them form a chain of Laplacian resonance, in which each planet's orbit is locked in rhythm with its neighbours (Hooton et al. 2022). Velocities from the ESPRESSO spectrograph showed unusual swings in density from planet to planet, rather than the steady decline with distance seen in most systems (Hooton et al. 2022). Later CHEOPS, NGTS and EulerCam visits tracked timing shifts of the five outer planets (Leleu et al. 2024).","system":"The resonant chain makes it a tight test of how the planets formed and moved (Hooton et al. 2021).","history":"Resonant chains are fragile, so their existence limits the ways such a system can have formed and evolved (Hooton et al. 2022).","open":"Why the densities vary so much from planet to planet is hard to explain with current theory (Hooton et al. 2021). JWST time was awarded to study planets b, d and g (Hooton et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178d-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178d-Leleuetal2024","cite":"Leleu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Leleu, J.-B. Delisle, L. Delrez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-178 light curves (Leleu+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178d-Hootonetal2021","cite":"Hooton et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-36 c":{"text":{"why":"It is one of two planets whose orbits differ in distance by only about 10 percent while their densities differ by a factor of 8 (Carter et al. 2012).","observed":"The two planets are about 20 times more closely spaced, and have a larger density contrast, than any adjacent pair in the solar system (Carter et al. 2012). Their gravity pulls on each other shift the transit times, which allowed the planet masses to be measured (Rogers et al. 2015). The planet is Neptune-like, while its inner neighbour is a likely rocky super-Earth (Carter et al. 2012). A later infrared observation from the ground showed that a single new transit can cut the uncertainty on such dynamical masses by up to a factor of 3 (Vissapragada et al. 2020). Kepler-36 c has less than 10 percent hydrogen and helium by mass (Valencia et al. 2013).","system":"The inner neighbour, Kepler-36 b, is much denser, with a density about eight times higher, and the host is a sub-giant star (Rogers et al. 2015).","history":"One model has the gassy planet form beyond the ice line and the rocky one within it. The heavier outer planet then migrates inward and captures its neighbour into a 2:1 resonance. Collisions with small bodies then raise the inner planet's density and strip its atmosphere (Rimlinger & Hamilton 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler36c-Carteretal2012","cite":"Carter et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Joshua A. Carter, Eric Agol, William J. Chaplin et al.","title":"Kepler-36: A Pair of Planets with Neighboring Orbits and Dissimilar Densities","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.4718","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36c-Rogersetal2015","cite":"Rogers et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Leslie Anne Rogers, Katherine Deck, Jack Lissauer et al.","title":"Compositional Constraints on the Best Characterized Rocky Exoplanet, Kepler-36 b","journal":"IAU General Assembly","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36c-Vissapragadaetal2020","cite":"Vissapragada et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Shreyas Vissapragada, Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"Diffuser-assisted Infrared Transit Photometry for Four Dynamically Interacting Kepler Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.04445","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36c-Valenciaetal2013","cite":"Valencia et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Diana Valencia, Tristan Guillot, Vivien Parmentier et al.","title":"Bulk Composition of GJ 1214b and Other Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2629","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36c-RimlingerHamilton2018","cite":"Rimlinger & Hamilton 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Thomas Rimlinger, Douglas Hamilton","title":"The Origin and Evolution of the Kepler-36 System","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #50","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-3 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits the brighter star of a pair of stars less than 1 arcsecond apart that cannot be told apart in ordinary images. The two stars are NGTS-3A, a G6 dwarf, and NGTS-3B, a K1 dwarf (Günther et al. 2018). To work out which star hosts the planet, the team combined multicolour photometry, shifts in the star's image centre during transit, and the shape of the velocity cross-correlation profiles, using software called BLENDFITTER (Günther et al. 2018). The planet is large for its mass and is potentially inflated (Günther et al. 2018). The authors stress that single-colour photometry and velocities alone cannot untangle such systems (Günther et al. 2018).","system":"The host belongs to an unresolved pair of stars, a G6 dwarf and a K1 dwarf (Günther et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts3ab-Gntheretal2018","cite":"Günther et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Maximilian N. Günther, Didier Queloz, Edward Gillen et al.","title":"Unmasking the hidden NGTS-3Ab: a hot Jupiter in an unresolved binary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.01378","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5542 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS saw a single transit in one sector, and a second transit 376 days later in a later sector, so the period was not obvious at first (Grieves et al. 2022). A third transit was caught by NGTS, EulerCam and SAAO (Grieves et al. 2022). The host is an old G dwarf of about 10.8 billion years and is metal-poor, with an iron content of -0.21 on the usual scale, which does not follow the usual trend of metal-rich hosts for giant planets (Grieves et al. 2022). The orbit is circular, which suggests disk migration or in-place formation rather than high-eccentricity migration (Grieves et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Its circular orbit most likely points to formation by disk migration or in place, rather than by being flung inward onto a stretched orbit (Grieves et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5542b-Grievesetal2022","cite":"Grieves et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Nolan Grieves, François Bouchy, Solène Ulmer-Moll et al.","title":"An old warm Jupiter orbiting the metal-poor G-dwarf TOI-5542","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.14830","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3984 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a gas giant orbiting an M4 dwarf, and the star has a companion white dwarf at a wide separation (Cañas et al. 2023). The planet's equilibrium temperature is about 563 K, which makes it one of the coolest gas giants among hot Jupiter-sized planets around M dwarfs (Cañas et al. 2023). It is a favourable target for atmospheric characterization and for measuring its three-dimensional orbit tilt relative to the star's spin, which would help test how such systems formed and migrated (Cañas et al. 2023). The data included TESS photometry, speckle imaging and velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID spectrographs (Cañas et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"How a giant planet formed around such a low-mass star in a wide binary is still a question (Cañas et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3984ab-Caasetal2023","cite":"Cañas et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Shubham Kanodia, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"TOI-3984 A b and TOI-5293 A b: Two Temperate Gas Giants Transiting Mid-M Dwarfs in Wide Binary Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07714","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"18 Del b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looked at G and K giants at Okayama Astrophysical Observatory (Sato et al. 2008).","observed":"The host is a G6 giant in the red clump, the quiet stage after the star has expanded, with a mass of about 2.1 solar masses (Sato et al. 2008). The planet's orbit is nearly circular, with a period of 993 days, which at the time was the longest period around an evolved star (Sato et al. 2008). A search for a distant companion with Subaru's high-contrast camera excluded objects more massive than 30 to 60 Jupiter masses at projected separations of 1 to 7 arcseconds, so the star's long-term velocity trend is not explained by a bright companion (Ryu et al. 2016).","system":"No planets around these intermediate-mass clump giants have been found closer than 0.68 au, which hints at a lack of short-period planets (Sato et al. 2008).","history":"Jupiter-mass planets within roughly 0.5 to 1 au of stars of 2 to 3 solar masses could be swallowed when the star expands at the tip of the red giant branch (Sato et al. 2008).","open":"It is not clear whether the lack of short-period planets is primordial or due to engulfment (Sato et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-18delb-Satoetal2008","cite":"Sato et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Hideyuki Izumiura, Eri Toyota et al.","title":"Planetary Companions around Three Intermediate-Mass G and K Giants: 18 Delphini, ξ Aquilae, and HD 81688","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.2590","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-18delb-Ryuetal2016","cite":"Ryu et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Tsuguru Ryu, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"High-contrast Imaging of Intermediate-mass Giants with Long-term Radial Velocity Trends","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-72 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is one of 76 low-mass stars whose properties were improved with near-infrared spectra, followed by new fits to the transit light curves to update planet sizes (Dressing et al. 2017). Most planets in that sample are highly irradiated, but K2-72 e is named as an exception to that (Dressing et al. 2017). It is among the smallest planets in the study, with a radius near 1.29 Earth radii, in a class the authors call super-Earths for sizes between 1.25 and 2 Earth radii (Dressing et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k272e-Dressingetal2017","cite":"Dressing et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Courtney D. Dressing, Andrew Vanderburg, Joshua E. Schlieder et al.","title":"Characterizing K2 Candidate Planetary Systems Orbiting Low-mass Stars. II. Planetary Systems Observed During Campaigns 1-7","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.07416","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+49 828 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up is the Penn State-Toruń Planet Search with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Niedzielski et al. 2015).","observed":"The host is a K0 giant of about 1.52 solar masses. The companion has a minimum mass of 1.6 Jupiter masses and a wide orbit of about 4.2 au, with an eccentricity near 0.35 (Niedzielski et al. 2015). It came out of a paper that also reported two other giant stars with companions in much tighter orbits, which lie close to the engulfment zone and may be swallowed in the near future (Niedzielski et al. 2015). By contrast, this planet's wide orbit keeps it far from that zone.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd49828b-Niedzielskietal2015","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan, G. Nowak et al.","title":"Three Red Giants With Substellar-Mass Companions","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07076","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-66 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star was studied as one of five young stars with planets, using Kepler data to map the temperature differences across the stellar surface, which come from dark starspots, and to follow how the active regions change position over time (Dmitrienko & Savanov 2018). The study measured how spotted each star is and concluded that the stars follow the general trends found earlier for thousands of other stars (Dmitrienko & Savanov 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler66b-DmitrienkoSavanov2018","cite":"Dmitrienko & Savanov 2018","year":"2018","authors":"E. S. Dmitrienko, I. S. Savanov","title":"Activity of Young Cold Dwarves with Planets. (Research on the Data of the Kepler Cosmic Telescope)","journal":"Modern Star Astronomy","url":"https://doi.org/10.31361/eaas.2018-1.030","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1289 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was watched with the SPIRou spectropolarimeter 300 times over 1686 days, and the weak velocity signal only showed up after a line-by-line processing step (Moutou et al. 2024). The planet is a potential temperate sub-Neptune orbiting a fully convective M4.5 dwarf on a circular orbit (Moutou et al. 2024). The same data mapped the star's large-scale magnetic field, which is mostly axisymmetric and dipolar with polar strengths of 200 to 240 G. The star's rotation period, measured from the magnetic field, differs from the planet's orbital period, and stellar activity is not seen in the near-infrared velocities (Moutou et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1289b-Moutouetal2024","cite":"Moutou et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Ould-Elhkim, J.-F. Donati et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 1289 and GJ 3378 RV, B and DetlaT (Moutou+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2207 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a survey that aims to build a uniform sample of hot Jupiters for studies of how they form (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"Candidates were identified from periodic dips in TESS brightness, then confirmed with ground-based time-series photometry, high-angular-resolution imaging and high-resolution spectroscopy (Yee et al. 2022). The survey merges new detections with earlier ground-based work to give a consistent sample (Yee et al. 2022). The ten new planets orbit fairly bright F and G stars, with periods from 2 to 10 days and masses from 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiter masses (Yee et al. 2022). Velocities for this star came from the Magellan II PFS spectrograph (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2207b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"SWEEPS-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It is one of sixteen transiting planet candidates found in a deep sequence of Hubble images of the Galactic bulge taken in 2004, of which at least seven are likely true planets (Clarkson et al. 2009). The star is almost certainly in the Galactic disk rather than the bulge itself, and follow-up velocity measurements showed it has a planetary companion (Clarkson et al. 2009). That makes it one of the few planets confirmed with velocities at such large distances.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-sweeps4b-Clarksonetal2009","cite":"Clarkson et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Will Clarkson, Kailash Sahu, Jay Anderson et al.","title":"Kinematics of the SWEEPS transiting planet candidates","journal":"Transiting Planets","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921308027087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-100 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star belongs to the young Praesepe cluster. Its HARPS-N velocities were analysed with a Gaussian-process method to separate the planet's pull from the star's activity, giving the first mass measurement of a planet around a young active star (Barragán 2019). The planet's radius implies a significant volatile envelope, but it receives about 1900 times Earth's sunlight, so photo-evaporation is expected to matter (Barragán 2019). Keck helium searches found no excess absorption, with a mass-loss limit of under 1.25 x 10^11 g/s, lower than hydrodynamic predictions (Alam et al. 2024). A further survey also reports non-detections for it (Dos Santos et al. 2024). The host is bright enough for precise velocity work (Stefansson et al. 2018).","system":"The host star is a member of the Praesepe cluster (Stefansson et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Whether it is losing its atmosphere is still unclear, since helium searches show no sign of an outflow (Alam et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2100b-Barragn2019","cite":"Barragán 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Oscar Barragán","title":"Radial velocity confirmation of K2-100b: a young transiting hot Neptune with a likely evaporating atmosphere","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2100b-Alametal2024","cite":"Alam et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Munazza K. Alam, James Kirk, Leonardo A. Dos Santos et al.","title":"Nondetections of Helium in the Young Sub-Jovian Planets K2-100b, HD 63433b, and V1298 Tau c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17294","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2100b-Santosetal2024","cite":"Santos et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Leonardo Dos Santos, Munazza Alam, James Kirk","title":"A Keck II/NIRSPEC survey of atmospheric escape in young transiting exoplanets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #243","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2100b-Stefanssonetal2018","cite":"Stefansson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Gudmundur Stefansson, Yiting Li, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"Diffuser-assisted Photometric Follow-up Observations of the Neptune-sized Planets K2-28b and K2-100b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04420","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 62509 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star, also called Beta Geminorum, is an evolved giant, and its planet was later used to test a new radial-velocity programme at Weihai Observatory. That programme measured the star's motion with a scatter of 7.3 m/s around the planet's orbit fit (Wittenmyer et al. 2015). Its goal is to find out whether planets close to giant stars are truly missing or have been swallowed (Wittenmyer et al. 2015). Computer simulations with the precision of the Gaia satellite showed that the planet's orbit could be recovered from position shifts of the star on the sky (Huang & Ji 2017).","system":"The star is a K giant. Hubble spectra show that its boron is greatly reduced compared with the Sun, a sign of deep mixing inside the star (Duncan et al. 1994).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd62509b-Wittenmyeretal2015","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Dongyang Gao, Shao Ming Hu et al.","title":"The Weihai Observatory Search for Close-in Planets Orbiting Giant Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.06051","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd62509b-HuangJi2017","cite":"Huang & Ji 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ping-hui Huang, Jiang-hui Ji","title":"Analogue Simulation and Orbit Solution Algorithm of Astrometric Exoplanet Detection","journal":"Chinese Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chinastron.2017.08.008","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd62509b-Duncanetal1994","cite":"Duncan et al. 1994","year":"1994","authors":"D. K. Duncan, R. C. Peterson, J. A. Thorburn et al.","title":"The Boron Abundance of the Hyades Giants","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #184","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1266 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Timing shifts in the planet's transits, measured with ground telescopes and new space-telescope data, were combined with earlier radial-velocity data to refine its mass and size (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). The planet has a low density, so it must carry a hydrogen-rich gas envelope (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Its sibling planet is denser and could hold either a hydrogen-rich or a water-rich envelope (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","system":"The star is a nearby red dwarf with an unusual layout: the larger planet sits closer in than the smaller one (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). A third planet that does not transit was confirmed from the timing shifts (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). The inner and outer planets lie near a 3:1 period ratio with a non-resonant planet between them, which is rare (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1266b-GreklekMcKeonetal2025","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Shreyas Vissapragada, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Tidally Heated Sub-Neptunes, Refined Planetary Compositions, and Confirmation of a Third Planet in the TOI-1266 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5398 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a programme that characterises young giant planets (Mantovan et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed by modelling the star's activity and the planetary signals together, using a space-telescope light curve and ground-based radial velocities from HARPS-N (Mantovan et al. 2022). Its transits were also followed in detail with a long run of spectra covering the full 4.3-hour transit (Mantovan et al. 2022). The measurements gave both its radius and mass, which constrain its bulk composition (Mantovan et al. 2022).","system":"This is the youngest known compact system with a small inner planet inside a giant one, around a G-type star about 650 million years old (Mantovan et al. 2022). The inner planet is a hot sub-Neptune on a 4.77-day orbit (Mantovan et al. 2022). Such small companions to short-period giants are known in only five other compact systems (Mantovan et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5398b-Mantovanetal2022","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Mantovan, L. Malavolta, S. Desidera et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-5398 spectroscopic time-series (Mantovan+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4515 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was studied to help explain why warm Jupiters have such a wide range of orbital shapes (Carleo et al. 2024).","observed":"Radial-velocity follow-up with three spectrographs, combined with space-telescope light curves, showed that the planet is an eccentric warm Jupiter orbiting a star about 1.2 billion years old (Carleo et al. 2024). Later observations of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the tiny distortion in starlight as the planet crosses the star, showed that its orbit is close to aligned with the star's spin (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025). Its true three-dimensional tilt is about 15 to 30 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The aligned orbits of warm Jupiters around single stars suggest that they form in discs that were aligned from the start (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":"How warm Jupiters reach such varied eccentricities is still not explained by any one theory (Carleo et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4515b-Carleoetal2024","cite":"Carleo et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"I. Carleo, L. Malavolta, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. L. TOI-4515 b: An eccentric warm Jupiter orbiting a 1.2 Gyr-old G-star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11903","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4515b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-43 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's parameters were refined by modelling the published radial velocities together with Kepler short-cadence data, which sample each transit much more finely than the earlier long-cadence data (Bonomo et al. 2015). The team also re-analysed the host star's spectra and used the star's spin rate to estimate its age (Bonomo et al. 2015). Another team also measured the star and ruled out false positives for it, using spectra from three telescopes (Endl et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler43b-Bonomoetal2015","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Improved parameters of seven Kepler giant companions characterized with SOPHIE and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler43b-Endletal2014","cite":"Endl et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Michael Endl, Douglas A. Caldwell, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Kepler-424 b: A \"Lonely\" Hot Jupiter that Found a Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8156","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 34445 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up adds to the statistics of giant planets orbiting near or beyond the ice line (Howard et al. 2010).","observed":"Ten years of Doppler measurements from Keck Observatory showed a single planet in a Keplerian orbit, and measurements from the Hobby-Eberly Telescope confirmed the signal and sharpened the orbit (Howard et al. 2010). The planet has a minimum mass of 0.79 Jupiter masses and an eccentricity of 0.27 (Howard et al. 2010). The authors say it merits follow-up with astrometry, imaging and space-based spectroscopy (Howard et al. 2010).","system":"The star is old and G-type (Howard et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd34445b-Howardetal2010","cite":"Howard et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The California Planet Survey. I. Four New Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3488","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-47 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's transits were later watched by the K2 mission, which revealed two more transiting planets: a Neptune-sized outer one and a super-Earth inner one (Becker et al. 2015). Variations in the timing of the hot Jupiter's transits let the planet masses be measured (Becker et al. 2015). The star is bright, so precise radial-velocity measurements were possible, and an outer giant planet on a long orbit was also found (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","system":"This was the first hot Jupiter found with close companion planets, at periods of about 0.8 and 9 days (Becker & Adams 2016). Dynamical analysis suggests the outer giant orbits near the same plane as the inner planets (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","history":"The flat, close-knit layout disfavours hot-Jupiter origins that involve violent gravitational interactions, such as Kozai-Lidov migration (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp47b-Beckeretal2015","cite":"Becker et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Juliette C. Becker, Andrew Vanderburg, Fred C. Adams et al.","title":"WASP-47: A Hot Jupiter System with Two Additional Planets Discovered by K2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02411","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47b-Vanderburgetal2018","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Juliette Becker, David W. Latham et al.","title":"WASP-47 and the Origin of Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47b-BeckerAdams2016","cite":"Becker & Adams 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Juliette C. Becker, Andrew Vanderburg, Fred C. Adams et al.","title":"WASP-47: A Hot Jupiter System with Two Additional Planets Discovered by K2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02411","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1054.01":{"text":{"why":"The study aimed to measure the gas content of low-mass planets around F-type stars (Cabrera et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is also known as HD 190622 b. It was first reported as a candidate by the TESS satellite, then validated and followed up (Cabrera et al. 2023). The follow-up used three transit observations with the CHEOPS satellite to pin down the radius, and 117 high-resolution HARPS spectra taken over more than three years to measure the mass (Cabrera et al. 2023). Mass and radius together constrain how much gas the planet holds (Cabrera et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"The amount of volatile material in such planets relates to how they formed and evolved (Cabrera et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi105401-Cabreraetal2023","cite":"Cabrera et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Cabrera, D. Gandolfi, L. M. Serrano et al.","title":"The planetary system around HD 190622 (TOI-1054). Measuring the gas content of low-mass planets orbiting F-stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202245774","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 h":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three transits of this outermost planet were observed with the Hubble Space Telescope to look for a cold atmosphere (Garcia et al. 2022). The star's dark and bright patches contaminate the signal, so the teams modelled them first (Garcia et al. 2022). The spectrum is flat, which rules out a clear, hydrogen-dominated atmosphere (Garcia et al. 2022). A heavy atmosphere, no atmosphere, or a layer of opaque haze remain possible (Garcia et al. 2022). A second analysis reached the same conclusion about the presence or absence of an atmosphere (Gressier et al. 2022). The planet's equilibrium temperature is 173 K (Gressier et al. 2022).","system":"The star hosts seven Earth-sized planets in a chain of resonances, where orbital periods are linked by simple ratios (Teyssandier et al. 2022). Spitzer observed 188 transits of the system (Ducrot et al. 2020). Their masses suggest rocky planets (Fauchez et al. 2020).","history":"Simulations show that planets migrating through a disc can form a resonant chain like the observed one (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"Interior models give possible water mass fractions that span a wide range (Dorn et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Garciaetal2022","cite":"Garcia et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. J. Garcia, S. E. Moran, B. V. Rackham et al.","title":"HST/WFC3 transmission spectroscopy of the cold rocky planet TRAPPIST-1h","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.13698","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Gressieretal2022","cite":"Gressier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. Gressier, M. Mori, Q. Changeat et al.","title":"Near-infrared transmission spectrum of TRAPPIST-1 h using Hubble WFC3 G141 observations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.05510","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Fauchezetal2020","cite":"Fauchez et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Thomas J. Fauchez, Martin Turbet, Eric T. Wolf et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1 Habitable Atmosphere Intercomparison (THAI): motivations and protocol version 1.0","journal":"Geoscientific Model Development","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10950","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1h-Dornetal2018","cite":"Dorn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Klaus Mosegaard, Simon L. Grimm et al.","title":"Interior Characterization in Multiplanetary Systems: TRAPPIST-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"55 Cnc f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Interferometry gave a direct measurement of the host star's size and temperature, and from these a habitable zone of 0.67 to 1.32 AU (von Braun et al. 2011). Planet f, with a period near 260 days, spends most of its elliptical orbit inside that zone (von Braun et al. 2011). It is too massive to have liquid water on its own surface, but the authors discuss a large moon or a low-mass planet nearby that could (von Braun et al. 2011).","system":"The star has a distant binary companion that makes the planetary system precess like a rigid body (Kaib et al. 2011). The planets are probably coplanar but likely tilted by about 50 degrees from the star's spin axis (Kaib et al. 2011). The innermost planet, 55 Cnc e, may have been strongly altered by tides and passages through secular resonances (Hansen & Zink 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-55cncf-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Theo A. ten Brummelaar et al.","title":"55 Cancri: Stellar Astrophysical Parameters, a Planet in the Habitable Zone, and Implications for the Radius of a Transiting Super-Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncf-Kaibetal2011","cite":"Kaib et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nathan A. Kaib, Sean N. Raymond, Martin J. Duncan","title":"55 Cancri: A Coplanar Planetary System That is Likely Misaligned with Its Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5911","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncf-HansenZink2015","cite":"Hansen & Zink 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Bradley M. S. Hansen, Jonathon Zink","title":"On the potentially dramatic history of the super-Earth ρ 55 Cancri e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05539","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4562 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-4562 b. The planet is known only through its effect on the transit times of the inner planet, TOI-4562 b. That planet shows tentative timing variations of about 20 minutes across five transits, which favour a companion (Heitzmann et al. 2023). The authors say such a companion could explain the dynamical history of the system if confirmed (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-4562 b, is a Jupiter-like world on a 225-day orbit with a high eccentricity of 0.76, around a young F-type star (Heitzmann et al. 2023). The star is younger than 700 million years (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The timing variations are tentative and need more transits to confirm (Heitzmann et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4562c-Heitzmannetal2023","cite":"Heitzmann et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Alexis Heitzmann, George Zhou, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"TOI-4562b: A Highly Eccentric Temperate Jupiter Analog Orbiting a Young Field Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10854","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LTT 1445 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The first JWST transmission spectrum of the planet, covering 3 to 5 micrometres, shows no statistically significant features (Batalha et al. 2026). It rules out atmospheres with a metal content below about 350 times solar when cloud tops are high, and about 500 times solar when combined with Hubble data (Batalha et al. 2026). An earlier Hubble spectrum was not flat, which led to a proposal for eight further JWST transits (Bennett et al. 2025).","system":"The planet sits in a triple M-dwarf system 6.9 parsecs away and is the closest transiting rocky planet around an M dwarf (Bennett et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether it has any atmosphere is unresolved, and further analysis may find features (Batalha et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ltt1445ab-Batalhaetal2026","cite":"Batalha et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Natasha E. Batalha, Nicole L. Wallack, Tyler A. Gordon et al.","title":"JWST COMPASS: The 3─5 μm Transmission Spectrum of LTT 1445 A b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt1445ab-Bennettetal2025","cite":"Bennett et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Katherine Bennett, Munazza Alam, Natalie Allen et al.","title":"Does Our Closest M-Dwarf Rocky Neighbor Have An Atmosphere? We Need to Find Out.","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2589 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet first showed up as a single transit in light curves built from TESS full-frame images, with a depth of about 9000 parts per million lasting 0.28 days. Radial-velocity measurements and extra light curves were then needed to find its orbital period (Brahm et al. 2023). The planet has a mass of 3.50 Jupiter masses, a radius of 1.08 Jupiter radii and a strongly eccentric orbit (Brahm et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a slightly metal-rich early G-type star (Brahm et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2589b-Brahmetal2023","cite":"Brahm et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Melissa J. Hobson et al.","title":"Three Long-period Transiting Giant Planets from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02139","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KIC 10068024 b":{"text":{"why":"The study aimed to confirm planets that do not transit, and so validate a detection technique (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","observed":"This planet does not transit its star. Radial velocities from two spectrographs and high-resolution imaging then confirmed it as a hot Jupiter and ruled out blended binary stars as the source (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kic10068024b-LilloBoxetal2021","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, S. Millholland, G. Laughlin","title":"Follow-up of non-transiting planets detected by Kepler. Confirmation of three hot-Jupiters and validation of three other planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14621","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-18 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-18 d was confirmed as a planet by four lines of evidence: large shifts in transit timing, small changes in the star's motion, Warm-Spitzer infrared observations, and a statistical check against false alarms (Cochran et al. 2011). Its mean density is only 0.27 g per cubic centimetre, so it is a very fluffy Neptune-mass planet (Cochran et al. 2011).","system":"Kepler-18 hosts three transiting planets with periods of about 3.5, 7.6 and 14.9 days (Cochran et al. 2011). The inner planet is a super-Earth, and the two outer ones are low-density Neptune-mass worlds (Cochran et al. 2011). The star is Sun-like, with a slightly higher iron content than the Sun (Cochran et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler18d-Cochranetal2011","cite":"Cochran et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"William D. Cochran, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"Kepler-18b, c, and d: A System of Three Planets Confirmed by Transit Timing Variations, Light Curve Validation, Warm-Spitzer Photometry, and Radial Velocity Measurements","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.0820","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 137496 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from NCORES, a HARPS programme that measures masses of small transiting candidates to learn their internal makeup (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with K2 space photometry together with radial velocities from the HARPS and CORALIE spectrographs (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022). The authors call it a dense, hot super-Mercury, meaning an iron-rich world (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022). Planets this dense are rare in the known sample (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022).","system":"The star is a bright, slightly evolved Sun-like star, a little larger than the Sun (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022). The system also holds a cold Jupiter on a much wider orbit (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd137496b-Silvaetal2022","cite":"Silva et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"T. Azevedo Silva, O. D. S. Demangeon, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"The HD 137496 system: A dense, hot super-Mercury and a cold Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08764","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4633 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet was spotted by citizen scientists in the Planet Hunters TESS project (Eisner et al. 2024).","observed":"Its 272-day orbit gives it an Earth-like year, and the starlight it receives puts it in the optimistic habitable zone (Eisner et al. 2024). Doppler measurements gave only an upper limit on its mass (Eisner et al. 2024). They also revealed a second, non-transiting planet candidate with a period of about 34 days (Eisner et al. 2024).","system":"The star has a stellar companion on a very elongated orbit, with a period of about 230 years and an eccentricity of about 0.9 (Eisner et al. 2024). Archival data back to 1905 and new high-resolution imaging showed this (Eisner et al. 2024).","history":"The long planetary period, the elongated companion orbit and its close approach make the system a useful test of how planets form and stay stable in binaries (Eisner et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4633c-Eisneretal2024","cite":"Eisner et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Nora L. Eisner, Samuel K. Grunblatt, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"Planet Hunters TESS. V. A Planetary System Around a Binary Star, Including a Mini-Neptune in the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.18997","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-136 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-136 b is a hot Jupiter that orbits an F9 subgiant, a star that has begun to swell as it ages (Lam et al. 2017). The star is about 2.2 times the Sun's radius (Lam et al. 2017). Its mass and radius are about 1.5 and 1.4 times Jupiter's (Lam et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The authors suggest that the planet could help show what giant planets around evolved stars are like (Lam et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp136b-Lametal2017","cite":"Lam et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"K. W. F. Lam, F. Faedi, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"From dense hot Jupiter to low-density Neptune: The discovery of WASP-127b, WASP-136b, and WASP-138b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.07859","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 75784 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from the N2K survey, which began as a search for short-period planets and has since turned to worlds far from their stars (Giguere et al. 2015). The aim is to learn how common giant planets are beyond the ice line, where gas giants are thought to form (Giguere et al. 2015).","observed":"Radial velocities show a giant planet with an orbital period of about 342 days around HD 75784 (Giguere et al. 2015). The study notes evidence for a longer-period companion as well (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"HD 75784 was not known to host planets before this work (Giguere et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd75784b-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 25463 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 25463 b is one of eight sub-Neptunes presented as good targets for JWST atmosphere studies (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). The planet orbits a bright star with a V magnitude of 6.9 (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). Its mass comes from more than two years of Keck-HIRES and Automated Planet Finder radial velocities, and it is measured to better than 3-sigma precision (Akana Murphy et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd25463b-Murphyetal2023","cite":"Murphy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Natalie Batalha, TESS-Keck Survey","title":"The Masses and Radii of Eight Sub-Neptunes Amenable to Atmospheric Characterization","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-908 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-908 b lies in the Neptune desert, a sparsely populated region of size and period where few planets are seen (Hawthorn et al. 2023). Its bulk density is about 2.7 g per cubic centimetre (Hawthorn et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"It probably began as a sub-Saturn planet and then lost much of its gas to photoevaporation by X-rays and extreme ultraviolet light from its star (Hawthorn et al. 2023).","open":"It is likely to keep evaporating, losing a significant fraction of its remaining envelope (Hawthorn et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi908b-Hawthornetal2023","cite":"Hawthorn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Faith Hawthorn, Daniel Bayliss, David J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-908: a planet at the edge of the Neptune desert transiting a G-type star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09758","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-102 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-102 b is a bloated, Saturn-like planet with a mass of 0.62 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.27 Jupiter radii (Faedi et al. 2016). Its radius is larger than models predict (Faedi et al. 2016). The authors say the planet receives strong incident flux, which helps to inflate it (Faedi et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a G0 star about 1 billion years old (Faedi et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp102b-Faedietal2016","cite":"Faedi et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"F. Faedi, Y. Gómez Maqueo Chew, D. Pollacco et al.","title":"WASP-86b and WASP-102b: super-dense versus bloated planets","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04225","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-20 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-20 f is one of two Earth-size candidates in the Kepler-20 system, picked out from five distinct transit signals (Gautier et al. 2012). The study demonstrates that the other three signals are unlikely to be false alarms (Gautier et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a Sun-like star with three sub-Neptune planets and the two Earth-size candidates (Gautier et al. 2012). The star has a temperature of about 5455 K and a metal content close to the Sun's (Gautier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler20f-Gautieretal2012","cite":"Gautier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas N., III Gautier, David Charbonneau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-20: A Sun-like Star with Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets and Two Earth-size Candidates","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.4514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6054.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-6054.01 is one of a pair of candidate hot sub-Neptunes, with periods of about 7.5 and 12.6 days (Kroft & Beatty 2024). Mass measurements are under way with the NEID spectrograph and the Keck Planet Finder (Kroft & Beatty 2024). Preliminary fits suggest both planets have low bulk densities, which could mean atmospheres rich in hydrogen (Kroft & Beatty 2024).","system":"The host is an F-type star (Kroft & Beatty 2024).","history":"","open":"The planets may be good targets for atmosphere studies because they lie close to their star (Kroft & Beatty 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi605401-KroftBeatty2024","cite":"Kroft & Beatty 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Max Kroft, Thomas Beatty","title":"A Pair of Hot Sub-Neptunes Around a Sun-like Star","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #244","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-10 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-10 b is one of the more massive transiting planets, with about 3 Jupiter masses and little data for planets above 2 Jupiter masses at the time (Christian et al. 2009). Its host star is a faint late-type K star (Christian et al. 2009). Spitzer infrared eclipse observations showed that planets heavier than Jupiter, like this one, are best matched by models with inefficient heat flow to the night side and low reflectivity (Kammer et al. 2015).","system":"Radial velocities show a steady acceleration of the star, which points to a more distant massive companion (Knutson et al. 2014). Its mass and orbit are only loosely constrained (Knutson et al. 2014).","history":"Tidal evolution of the orbit may help explain the planet's large radius (Miller et al. 2009). Another study found no good evidence that its orbit is eccentric (Husnoo et al. 2012).","open":"Citizen-science timing data show hints that its orbital period may be drifting, which needs follow-up to confirm (Hagey et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Christianetal2009","cite":"Christian et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. J. Christian, N. P. Gibson, E. K. Simpson et al.","title":"WASP-10b: a 3M<SUB>J</SUB>, gas-giant planet transiting a late-type K star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1482","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Kammeretal2015","cite":"Kammer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Joshua A. Kammer, Heather A. Knutson, Michael R. Line et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipse Observations of Five Cool Gas Giant Planets and Empirical Trends in Cool Planet Emission Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.00902","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Knutsonetal2014","cite":"Knutson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Heather A. Knutson, Benjamin J. Fulton, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"Friends of Hot Jupiters. I. A Radial Velocity Search for Massive, Long-period Companions to Close-in Gas Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.2954","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Milleretal2009","cite":"Miller et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"N. Miller, J. J. Fortney, B. Jackson","title":"Inflating and Deflating Hot Jupiters: Coupled Tidal and Thermal Evolution of Known Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1268","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Husnooetal2012","cite":"Husnoo et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Nawal Husnoo, Frédéric Pont, Tsevi Mazeh et al.","title":"Observational constraints on tidal effects using orbital eccentricities","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.6379","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp10b-Hageyetal2022","cite":"Hagey et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Simone R. Hagey, Billy Edwards, Aaron C. Boley","title":"Evidence of Long-term Period Variations in the Exoplanet Transit Database (ETD)","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10752","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-904 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity checks and high-resolution speckle imaging showed no sign of an eclipsing binary or a nearby companion star, which supports the planetary nature of the signals (Harris et al. 2023).","system":"The host is an early M dwarf with a second planet of similar size, about 2.6 Earth radii, on an 84-day orbit (Harris et al. 2023). That outer planet has an estimated equilibrium temperature of about 219 K and is the longest-period M dwarf planet found by TESS (Harris et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The outer planet could be the coldest M dwarf planet reachable for atmosphere follow-up, because its star is brighter than those in similar systems (Harris et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi904b-Harrisetal2023","cite":"Harris et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mallory Harris, Diana Dragomir, Karen Collins et al.","title":"TOI 904: A multiplanet system around an M dwarf with cold sub-Neptune","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 1666 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a survey of metal-rich stars that used three telescopes and an analysis method tuned for long time spans (Harakawa et al. 2015).","observed":"HD 1666 is an F7 main-sequence star with a mass of 1.5 Suns (Harakawa et al. 2015). The planet has a minimum mass of 6.4 Jupiter masses and an orbit of 0.94 AU with an eccentricity of 0.63 (Harakawa et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"The authors say such a massive, eccentric planet can be explained by planet-planet interactions among giant planets (Harakawa et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd1666b-Harakawaetal2015","cite":"Harakawa et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Hiroki Harakawa, Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Five New Exoplanets Orbiting Three Metal-rich, Massive Stars: Two-planet Systems Including Long-period Planets and an Eccentric Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07636","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 327 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Wolf 327 b is an ultra-short-period planet, with a year of only 13.7 hours (Murgas et al. 2024). Of about 5500 confirmed planets, only about 130 are this close in, and about 40 have both mass and radius measured (Murgas et al. 2024). The star is a faint M dwarf, and CARMENES radial velocities confirmed the planet (Murgas et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Planets with periods under one day are rare, and their formation histories are not fully understood (Murgas et al. 2024). Such planets are often found in multi-planet systems, although none are known here (Murgas et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf327b-Murgasetal2024","cite":"Murgas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Murgas, E. Pallé, J. Orell-Miquel et al.","title":"Wolf 327b: A new member of the pack of ultra-short-period super-Earths around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.12150","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4336 A c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-4336 A c is an inner super-Earth with a radius of 1.25 Earth radii and a mass of 1.55 Earth masses, measured with ESPRESSO radial velocities (Parc et al. 2026). It sits inside a sub-Neptune on a 16.34-day orbit, which was validated earlier (Parc et al. 2026).","system":"The host, TOI-4336 A, is an M3.5 dwarf that belongs to a hierarchical triple M-dwarf system about 22 parsecs away (Timmermans et al. 2024). The outer sub-Neptune sits near the inner edge of the habitable zone and is a promising target for atmosphere studies (Timmermans et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4336ac-Parcetal2026","cite":"Parc et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Léna Parc, Charles Cadieux, Nolan Grieves et al.","title":"Densities of small planets around the M dwarfs TOI-4336 A and TOI-4342 with ESPRESSO: Three sub-Neptunes, one super-Earth, and a Neptune-mass candidate","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22115","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4336ac-Timmermansetal2024","cite":"Timmermans et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Timmermans, G. Dransfield, M. Gillon et al.","title":"TOI-4336 A b: A temperate sub-Neptune ripe for atmospheric characterization in a nearby triple M-dwarf system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12722","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-33 b":{"text":{"why":"Planet surveys have mostly targeted Sun-like stars, and massive stars have been avoided because they are hard to measure and very active (Alves et al. 2025).","observed":"NGTS-33 b is a super-Jupiter with a mass of 3.6 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.64 Jupiter radii (Alves et al. 2025). The study calls the planet young (Alves et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a hot A9V star with a temperature of 7437 K (Alves et al. 2025). It spins very fast, with a rotation period of about 0.67 days (Alves et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts33b-Alvesetal2025","cite":"Alves et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Douglas R. Alves, James S. Jenkins, Jose I. Vines et al.","title":"NGTS-33b: a young super-Jupiter hosted by a fast-rotating massive hot star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.08960","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7510 c":{"text":{"why":"The system first looked like a two-planet case in a machine-learning search of TESS light curves (Almenara et al. 2025).","observed":"Follow-up photometry from ASTEP, CHEOPS and EulerCam showed that one transit had been wrongly assigned, so the system holds three transiting giant planets, not two (Almenara et al. 2025). The three have orbital periods of 11.5, 22.6 and 48.9 days (Almenara et al. 2025). The planets pull on each other enough to shift their transit times by large amounts, and modelling these shifts gives each planet's mass (Almenara et al. 2025). The middle planet, this one, has a radius of 0.96 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.41 Jupiter masses (Almenara et al. 2025).","system":"The periods lie near a 4:2:1 chain, meaning each planet orbits about twice as slowly as the one inside it (Almenara et al. 2025). The three are the largest known trio of transiting planets, and the modelling favours low eccentricities and small tilts between their orbits (Almenara et al. 2025).","history":"The compact layout makes the system a test case for how tightly packed giant planets form and interact (Almenara et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7510c-Almenaraetal2025","cite":"Almenara et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. M. Almenara, A. Leleu, T. Guillot et al.","title":"TOI-7510: A solar-analog system of three transiting giant planets near a Laplace resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20074","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4427 b":{"text":{"why":"It was announced together with four other hot and warm Jupiters from TESS (Guenther et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed using TESS data combined with ground-based observations, which gave its mass and radius (Guenther et al. 2026). It has a low density and a large transmission spectroscopy metric, a score for how well a planet's atmosphere can be studied by starlight filtering through it (Guenther et al. 2026). This makes it, with TOI-2578 b, a particularly interesting target for future atmospheric work (Guenther et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Hot Jupiters still have several competing formation theories, and atmospheric and dynamical studies of well-characterised planets can help choose between them (Guenther et al. 2026).","open":"How hot Jupiters form, possibly in more than one way, is still debated (Guenther et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4427b-Guentheretal2026","cite":"Guenther et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Eike W. Guenther, William D. Cochran, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"The Discovery of Five Hot Jupiters by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6449","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 64121 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a long radial-velocity survey of 641 red giants followed since 2006 with the CORALIE spectrograph (Ottoni et al. 2022).","observed":"The host is an evolved giant star of intermediate mass, and the planet was found by tracking small, regular shifts in the star's light over many years (Ottoni et al. 2022). The team separated planet-driven shifts from stellar surface noise by checking the line shapes and activity indicators of the star (Ottoni et al. 2022). It is one of the first three planets from this survey, which aims to study the statistics of planets around giants (Ottoni et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd64121b-Ottonietal2022","cite":"Ottoni et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Ottoni, S. Udry, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES). I. Sample definition and first results: Three new planets orbiting giant stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOINTREAU-4 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from an infrared adaptive-optics survey of young stars in the Taurus and Rho Ophiuchi star-forming regions (Walker et al. 2026).","observed":"The object was imaged with the Keck telescope using adaptive optics, a technique that sharpens the view by correcting for atmospheric blur, and a pyramid wavefront sensor (Walker et al. 2026). It moves across the sky together with its host, the young star 2MASS J16262785-2625152, so the two are bound (Walker et al. 2026). It lies at a projected separation of 180 au from the star (Walker et al. 2026). Its mass depends on assumptions, and the study gives two possible values (Walker et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass is uncertain, with two possible values in the discovery paper (Walker et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kointreau4b-Walkeretal2026","cite":"Walker et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Samuel A. U. Walker, Michael C. Liu, Dimitri Mawet et al.","title":"Keck Observations in the INfrared of Taurus and ρ Oph Exoplanets And Ultracool Dwarfs (KOINTREAU). II. Two Young Bound Companions to Ophiuchus Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5799 b":{"text":{"why":"It was validated statistically, with multi-colour light curves and other supporting observations, not by measuring its mass (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet is a super-Earth, with a radius of 1.733 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of 505 K, orbiting an M2 dwarf in 4.17 days (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025). A statistical test, combined with TESS and ground-based photometry, was used to rule out false alarms (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","system":"The star hosts a second transiting super-Earth, TOI-5799 c, of 1.76 Earth radii, found in the TESS data with the SHERLOCK search pipeline (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5799b-Yalinkayaetal2025","cite":"Yalçinkaya et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Yalçinkaya, K. Barkaoui, Ö. Baștürk et al.","title":"TOI-1743 b, TOI-5799 b, TOI-5799 c, and TOI-6223 b: TESS discovery and validation of four super-Earth to Neptune-sized planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-5 b":{"text":{"why":"It was one of five planets in the first six weeks of Kepler data, and it helped show that some giants are less dense than expected (Borucki et al. 2010).","observed":"Kepler-5 b, with Kepler-6 b and Kepler-8 b, confirmed that planets with lower densities than predicted for gas giants exist (Borucki et al. 2010). Later work found that it shows both a secondary eclipse, when it passes behind its star, and brightness changes through its orbit (Esteves et al. 2013). Its geometric albedo, how much light it reflects, is low, below 0.3 (Esteves et al. 2013). A statistical study also flagged it as a system where the star's spin axis may be tilted relative to the orbit along the line of sight (Schlaufman 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":"The apparent tilt between star spin and orbit comes from a statistical argument, not from a direct measurement for this star (Schlaufman 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler5b-Boruckietal2010","cite":"Borucki et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"William J. Borucki, David Koch, Gibor Basri et al.","title":"Kepler Planet-Detection Mission: Introduction and First Results","journal":"Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1185402","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler5b-Estevesetal2013","cite":"Esteves et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Lisa J. Esteves, Ernst J. W. De Mooij, Ray Jayawardhana","title":"Optical Phase Curves of Kepler Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3271","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler5b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 63242 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first planet from a radial-velocity survey of 166 giant stars aimed at close-in planets around evolved stars (Jones et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet orbits an intermediate-mass giant at 0.57 AU, on an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.23 (Jones et al. 2013). It is the innermost planet found around any intermediate-mass giant star (Jones et al. 2013). Among the more than 40 planets known around giants, none were previously found inside about 0.6 AU (Jones et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"The missing close-in planets may come from orbital changes through interaction with the evolving host star, or from a different way of forming and migrating around more massive stars (Jones et al. 2013).","open":"Why planets are rare close to giant stars is still unresolved (Jones et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip63242b-Jonesetal2013","cite":"Jones et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. I. Jones, J. S. Jenkins, P. Rojo et al.","title":"Study of the impact of the post-MS evolution of the host star on the orbits of close-in planets. II. A giant planet in a close-in orbit around the RGB star HIP 63242","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3939","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3362 b":{"text":{"why":"It is a candidate proto hot Jupiter, a giant planet caught while its orbit is still shrinking through high-eccentricity tidal migration (Dong et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet has an extremely eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.815, around an F-type main-sequence star (Dong et al. 2021). Tides are expected to shrink its orbit to about 0.051 au after complete circularisation (Dong et al. 2021). An independent fit of the TESS light curve gave a similar eccentricity of 0.8 (Freeman 2025). Its temperature swings as it moves along the orbit, and tidal heating at closest approach makes it a target for atmospheric modelling (Dong et al. 2021). A search for water with ESPRESSO found nothing (Prinoth et al. 2024). Models of its brightness after closest approach were also made (Vanrespaille et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Planet-planet scattering or secular interactions could explain the extreme eccentricity (Dong et al. 2021). Once circularised, it would become a hot Jupiter with a period of 3.5 days (Freeman 2025).","open":"Whether the star's spin is aligned with the orbit and whether other companions exist remain to be tested (Dong et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3362b-Dongetal2021","cite":"Dong et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jiayin Dong, Chelsea X. Huang, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-3362b: A Proto Hot Jupiter Undergoing High-eccentricity Tidal Migration","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03771","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3362b-Freeman2025","cite":"Freeman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jasmine Freeman","title":"Deriving Orbital Eccentricity of Transiting Exoplanets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3362b-Prinothetal2024","cite":"Prinoth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Bibiana Prinoth, Elyar Sedaghati, Julia V. Seidel et al.","title":"High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy of Warm Jupiters: An ESPRESSO Sample with Predictions for ANDES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08558","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3362b-Vanrespailleetal2024","cite":"Vanrespaille et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Vanrespaille, R. Baeyens, A. Schneider et al.","title":"Examining the detectability of ringing on highly eccentric exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.01026","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2011-BLG-322L b":{"text":{"why":"It came from a second-generation microlensing survey that monitors a large area of the Galactic bulge around the clock (Shvartzvald et al. 2014).","observed":"The event was seen by the survey groups MOA, OGLE and the Wise Observatory, without alerts that would have triggered dedicated follow-up (Shvartzvald et al. 2014). The light curve shows an anomaly caused by a second lensing mass, with a mass ratio between star and companion of 0.028 (Shvartzvald et al. 2014). Finite-source effects are only marginally detected (Shvartzvald et al. 2014). A statistical estimate gives a host mass of about 0.39 solar masses at 7.56 kpc, and a companion of roughly 11.6 Jupiter masses at a projected separation of 4.3 AU (Shvartzvald et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":"The masses are estimated statistically, with wide ranges (Shvartzvald et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2011blg322lb-Shvartzvaldetal2014","cite":"Shvartzvald et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Y. Shvartzvald, D. Maoz, S. Kaspi et al.","title":"MOA-2011-BLG-322Lb: a `second generation survey' microlensing planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.0008","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1224 b":{"text":{"why":"It belongs to a young system, found as part of a search for young and maturing planets (Thao et al. 2024).","observed":"The host sits in a young population called MELANGE-5, with an age of 210 Myr from isochrone fitting, lithium abundance, gyrochronology and Gaia excess variability (Thao et al. 2024). Planet b was first flagged by the TESS pipeline (Thao et al. 2024). The host is bright and small, with a radius of 0.44 solar radii and a temperature of 3326 K (Thao et al. 2024).","system":"A second planet, TOI-1224 c, was found with search tools tuned for young stars (Thao et al. 2024). The planets measure 2.10 and 2.88 Earth radii and orbit every 4.18 and 17.95 days (Thao et al. 2024).","history":"Young multi-planet systems let researchers control for variables when studying how planets form and evolve (Thao et al. 2024).","open":"The edges of the MELANGE-5 group and its neighbours still need to be mapped (Thao et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1224b-Thaoetal2024","cite":"Thao et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Pa Chia Thao, Andrew W. Mann, Madyson G. Barber et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). X. A Two-planet System in the 210 Myr MELANGE-5 Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05234","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1759 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery used infrared spectropolarimetry, which also measures stellar magnetism, to study the star (Martioli et al. 2022).","observed":"Mass came from radial velocities taken with SPIRou, an infrared spectrograph, and analysed in two ways (Martioli et al. 2022). The host is a moderately active M0 dwarf with a rotation period of about 35.65 days (Martioli et al. 2022). Polarised spectra revealed Zeeman signatures, signs of the star's magnetic field, and the large-scale surface field was reconstructed (Martioli et al. 2022). The planet is also named a favourable target for atmospheric study with JWST and Ariel, as a temperate world most likely rich in hydrogen (Lavvas et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1759b-Martiolietal2022","cite":"Martioli et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. Martioli, G. Hébrard, P. Fouqué et al.","title":"TOI-1759 b: A transiting sub-Neptune around a low mass star characterized with SPIRou and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01259","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1759b-Lavvasetal2024","cite":"Lavvas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Panayotis Lavvas, Athena Coustenis, Therese Encrenaz et al.","title":"A favorable sub-Neptune target for JWST and Ariel observations: TOI 1759 b","journal":"European Geosciences Union General Assembly 2024 (EGU24)","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu24-7609","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1140 b":{"text":{"why":"It orbits in the habitable zone of a nearby M dwarf, which makes it a leading target for atmosphere studies (Lillo-Box et al. 2020).","observed":"JWST NIRSpec observations between 1.7 and 5.2 micrometres rule out a hydrogen-rich atmosphere, since methane or carbon dioxide features would show and do not (Damiano et al. 2024). The data modestly favour a heavy atmosphere, possibly nitrogen-dominated with water and carbon dioxide (Damiano et al. 2024). Reanalysis of ESPRESSO velocities gives a mass of 5.60 Earth masses and a radius of 1.730 Earth radii (Cadieux et al. 2024). Stellar abundances suggest it is not a rocky super-Earth, but either a mini-Neptune with a thin hydrogen-helium layer or a water world with 9 to 19 percent water by mass (Cadieux et al. 2024).","system":"A second planet, LHS 1140 c, orbits every 3.78 days, and both orbits are coplanar and consistent with circular (Ment et al. 2019). A radio search for technosignatures at this system found nothing of astronomical origin (Pinchuk et al. 2019).","history":"Forming the planet by gathering ices that carry carbon and nitrogen could give a carbon dioxide or nitrogen atmosphere (Damiano et al. 2024).","open":"Whether it is a mini-Neptune or a water world is still debated, and climate simulations suggest sea ice could expand over its surface (Cadieux et al. 2024; Yang & Ji 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-LilloBoxetal2020","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, P. Figueira, A. Leleu et al.","title":"Planetary system LHS 1140 revisited with ESPRESSO and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.06928","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-Damianoetal2024","cite":"Damiano et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Mario Damiano, Aaron Bello-Arufe, Jeehyun Yang et al.","title":"LHS 1140 b Is a Potentially Habitable Water World","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13265","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-Cadieuxetal2024","cite":"Cadieux et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Charles Cadieux, Mykhaylo Plotnykov, René Doyon et al.","title":"New Mass and Radius Constraints on the LHS 1140 Planets: LHS 1140 b Is either a Temperate Mini-Neptune or a Water World","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15490","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-Mentetal2019","cite":"Ment et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Kristo Ment, Jason A. Dittmann, Nicola Astudillo-Defru et al.","title":"A Second Terrestrial Planet Orbiting the Nearby M Dwarf LHS 1140","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00485","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-Pinchuketal2019","cite":"Pinchuk et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Pavlo Pinchuk, Jean-Luc Margot, Adam H. Greenberg et al.","title":"A Search for Technosignatures from TRAPPIST-1, LHS 1140, and 10 Planetary Systems in the Kepler  Field with the Green Bank Telescope at 1.15-1.73 GHz","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.04057","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1140b-YangJi2018","cite":"Yang & Ji 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Yang, W. Ji","title":"Increased Ice Coverages on TRAPPIST-1e, LHS 1140b, and Proxima b by Sea Ice Dynamics","journal":"Comparative Climatology of Terrestrial Planets III: From Stars to Surfaces","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":23,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+05 4868 A b":{"text":{"why":"It is a disintegrating planet, the brightest known and the latest of four, with a comet-like dust tail (Hon et al. 2025; Walker 2026).","observed":"The planet is Mercury-sized and orbits at 0.02 AU with a period of 30.5 hours (Walker 2026). Dust and gas stream off it in tails, which can be studied by looking at starlight filtered through them (Tusay et al. 2026). A JWST MIRI phase-curve observation in October 2025 included two transits of the central core (Tusay et al. 2026). Ground-based photometry showed uneven, changing dimming that fits dust tails on two sides, and one spectrum feature may point to iron-peak elements (Walker 2026).","system":"The host is a binary of a K star and an M star, and the K star has an apparent magnitude of about 10 (Walker 2026; Hon et al. 2025).","history":"It is unclear whether it is the leftover core of a former gas giant or a small rocky planet that migrated too close, and an X-ray study of the host aims to find out (Rappaport 2025).","open":"The composition of the planet's interior is still unknown, and the dust spectra are meant to reveal it (Hon et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd054868ab-Honetal2025","cite":"Hon et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marc Teng Yen Hon, Nick Tusay, Saul A. Rappaport et al.","title":"Precise Direct Interior Composition Measurements of a New Disintegrating Terrestrial Planet","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd054868ab-Walker2026","cite":"Walker 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Kody Walker","title":"Spectroscopy and Photometry of BD+05 4868 Ab's Dual Dust Tails","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd054868ab-Tusayetal2026","cite":"Tusay et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nick Tusay, Marc Hon, Saul Rappaport et al.","title":"JWST Observations of the Brightest Disintegrating Exoplanet, BD+05 4868 Ab","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd054868ab-Rappaport2025","cite":"Rappaport 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Saul Rappaport","title":"Revealing The Origins of a Disintegrating Exoplanet","journal":"Chandra Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CD Cet b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper mainly tests how well the CARMENES instrument can measure precise velocities in the near infrared (Bauer et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet is a super-Earth of about 4.0 Earth masses on a 2.29-day orbit around the nearby M5.0 dwarf CD Cet (Bauer et al. 2020). It was used to show that both the visible and the near-infrared channels of CARMENES can detect small planets (Bauer et al. 2020). The near-infrared channel is a cryogenically cooled instrument and is harder to calibrate (Bauer et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-cdcetb-Baueretal2020","cite":"Bauer et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"F. F. Bauer, M. Zechmeister, A. Kaminski et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Measuring precise radial velocities in the near infrared: The example of the super-Earth CD Cet b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01684","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2194 b":{"text":{"why":"It was validated statistically as part of the VaTEST project, which tests TESS candidates with a false-positive tool (Mistry et al. 2023).","observed":"The tool TRICERATOPS was used to compute false-positive probabilities after checking the light curves (Mistry et al. 2023). The planet is a super-Earth that orbits a bright star, V = 8.42, that is also metal-poor, with an iron abundance of -0.3720 (Mistry et al. 2023). It is named as suitable for atmospheric study by transmission spectroscopy, where starlight passes through the planet's atmosphere (Mistry et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2194b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Aniket Prasad et al.","title":"VaTEST. II. Statistical Validation of 11 TESS-detected Exoplanets Orbiting K-type Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 221416 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first transiting TESS planet whose host star can be studied with asteroseismology, the study of starquakes (Huber et al. 2019).","observed":"The host is a bright subgiant that oscillates at about 430 microhertz and shows a clear signature of mixed modes (Huber et al. 2019). The oscillations give a robust radius of 2.943 solar radii, mass of 1.212 solar masses and age of 4.9 billion years, and show that the star has just begun climbing the red-giant branch (Huber et al. 2019). With transits and velocities, the planet is a hot Saturn with low density, 0.431 grams per cubic centimetre (Huber et al. 2019). The oscillation amplitude supports TESS's expected yield of thousands of solar-like oscillators (Huber et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd221416b-Huberetal2019","cite":"Huber et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Daniel Huber, William J. Chaplin, Ashley Chontos et al.","title":"A Hot Saturn Orbiting an Oscillating Late Subgiant Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.01643","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3714 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of only nine known transiting hot Jupiters around M dwarfs, a group that challenges planet-formation theory (Cañas et al. 2022).","observed":"The host is an M2 dwarf with a resolved white dwarf companion (Cañas et al. 2022). The planet is warm, with an equilibrium temperature of 750 K, and is amenable to transmission spectroscopy (Cañas et al. 2022). A JWST programme observed it three times with NIRSpec and detected large absorption features from several molecules, even though haze was expected to mask features (Stevenson & Giant Exoplanets around M-dwarfs (GEMS) 2025). Its host star is metal-rich, like other M dwarfs with close-in giants (Hartman et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The star's spin relative to the orbit could probe how it formed, and has not been measured (Cañas et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3714b-Caasetal2022","cite":"Cañas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Shubham Kanodia, Chad F. Bender et al.","title":"TOI-3714 b and TOI-3629 b: Two Gas Giants Transiting M Dwarfs Confirmed with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.09963","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3714b-Hartmanetal2023","cite":"Hartman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, Z. Csubry et al.","title":"TOI 4201 b and TOI 5344 b: Discovery of Two Transiting Giant Planets around M-dwarf Stars and Revised Parameters for Three Others","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06809","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-10 b":{"text":{"why":"Its planetary nature was long uncertain, as a faint signal could also come from a blended eclipsing binary (Konacki et al. 2003).","observed":"Keck spectra showed no radial-velocity variation at the level of 100 to 200 metres per second, so any companion would have a mass similar to Jupiter's (Konacki et al. 2003). The same data could not rule out a blend, light from an eclipsing binary on the same line of sight (Konacki et al. 2003). A later follow-up of OGLE candidates in the Galactic bulge listed it as a possible exoplanet (Bouchy et al. 2005). The host's age was also examined through lithium abundance and activity (Melo et al. 2006).","system":"","history":"It came from the OGLE-III survey, which announced dozens of short-period transiting objects in the Galactic bulge, many of which proved false positives (Bouchy et al. 2005).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr10b-Konackietal2003","cite":"Konacki et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Maciej Konacki, Guillermo Torres, Dimitar D. Sasselov et al.","title":"High-Resolution Spectroscopic Follow-up of OGLE Planetary Transit Candidates in the Galactic Bulge: Two Possible Jupiter-Mass Planets and Two Blends","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0306542","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr10b-Bouchyetal2005","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"F. Bouchy, F. Pont, C. Melo et al.","title":"Doppler follow-up of OGLE transiting companions in the Galactic bulge","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0410346","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr10b-Meloetal2006","cite":"Melo et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"C. Melo, N. C. Santos, F. Pont et al.","title":"On the age of stars harboring transiting planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609259","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 41378 f":{"text":{"why":"It is a super-puff, a planet far less dense than expected, in a system with otherwise ordinary planets (Belkovski et al. 2022).","observed":"Radial-velocity monitoring from 2015 to 2024 with HARPS, HARPS-N, HIRES and ESPRESSO gave this planet a mass of 25 Earth masses, confirming its super-puff nature (Grouffal et al. 2026). The system hosts five transiting planets with periods up to 1.5 years, so many transits are single events (Grouffal et al. 2026). Planet d's period has been hard to pin down, and a CHEOPS attempt to see it found no transit (Sulis et al. 2024).","system":"It is the only super-puff in a system of otherwise more normal densities (Belkovski et al. 2022). K2 found it to be the first K2 system with more than three planets (Kohler 2016).","history":"Changes in the star's light over time could have altered the planets' densities (Belkovski et al. 2022).","open":"Whether rings explain the low density is still a proposal under study (Belkovski et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip41378f-Belkovskietal2022","cite":"Belkovski et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Michelle Belkovski, Juliette Becker, Alex Howe et al.","title":"A Multiplanet System's Sole Super-puff: Exploring Allowable Physical Parameters for the Cold Super-puff HIP 41378 f","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.17180","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378f-Grouffaletal2026","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, X. Dumusque et al.","title":"A decade of monitoring the HIP 41378 planetary system: Masses and orbital periods of six planets and a planet candidate","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202659666","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378f-Sulisetal2024","cite":"Sulis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Sulis, L. Borsato, S. Grouffal et al.","title":"HIP 41378 observed by CHEOPS: Where is planet d?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20077","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378f-Kohler2016","cite":"Kohler 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"K2's First Five-Planet System","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-444 c":{"text":{"why":"The host is among the oldest known planet systems, and its planets are terrestrial in size (Campante et al. 2015).","observed":"Kepler observations show five planets with sizes between Mercury and Venus around the metal-poor, Sun-like star KOI-3158 (Campante et al. 2015). Asteroseismology, the study of starquakes, gives the star an age of 11.2 billion years (Campante et al. 2015). The star belongs to the old thick-disk population of the Galaxy (Campante et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"Small planets can form even with few heavy elements, so Earth-size planets could have formed through most of the Universe's 13.8-billion-year history (Campante et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler444c-Campanteetal2015","cite":"Campante et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"T. L. Campante, T. Barclay, J. J. Swift et al.","title":"KOI-3158: The oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07869","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2017-BLG-1146L b":{"text":{"why":"It was one of two planetary events from the 2017 bulge season of the KMTNet microlensing survey (Shin et al. 2019).","observed":"The event shows a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 2.0 times 10 to the minus 3 (Shin et al. 2019). A statistical analysis assuming no stellar-remnant host gives a sub-Jupiter planet of about 0.71 Jupiter masses around a mid-M dwarf of 0.33 solar masses (Shin et al. 2019). The system lies about 6.5 kpc away toward the Galactic bulge (Shin et al. 2019). The planet is likely gaseous and orbits beyond the star's snow line (Shin et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The masses and distance are statistical estimates with wide uncertainty (Shin et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2017blg1146lb-Shinetal2019","cite":"Shin et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"I.-G. Shin, Y.-H. Ryu, J. C. Yee et al.","title":"Two Jupiter-mass Planets Discovered by the KMTNet Survey in 2017","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12505","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2018-BLG-0029L b":{"text":{"why":"It had the lowest planet-to-host mass ratio of any microlensing planet at its discovery (Gould et al. 2020).","observed":"The mass ratio is 1.81 times 10 to the minus 5, lower than the pile-up of earlier values, making it the first to probe below it (Gould et al. 2020). The Spitzer space telescope viewed the event from a different angle, and the discovery team found a 7.59 Earth-mass planet around a 1.14 solar-mass host (Gould et al. 2020). Later Keck adaptive-optics imaging found excess light that matches ground-based parallax, not Spitzer's, giving 4.2 Earth masses around a 0.70 solar-mass host at 3.1 au (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The earlier and later analyses disagree on the lens distance and mass (Gould et al. 2020; Zhang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg0029lb-Gouldetal2020","cite":"Gould et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Andrew Gould, Yoon-Hyun Ryu, Sebastiano Calchi Novati et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-0029Lb: A Very Low Mass-Ratio Spitzer Microlens Planet","journal":"Journal of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.11183","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg0029lb-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Keming Zhang, Sean K. Terry, Joshua S. Bloom et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-0029Lb and OGLE-2019-BLG-0960Lb: Mass Measurements for Two Super-Earth Microlensing Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18343","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-108 b":{"text":{"why":"The host was among 45 transit hosts imaged with VLT/SPHERE to study how stellar companions affect hot-Jupiter formation (Bohn et al. 2020).","observed":"The high-contrast images found a new candidate companion near WASP-108 (Bohn et al. 2020). The team tested whether each candidate is gravitationally bound by checking common proper motion and simulated star fields (Bohn et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"Stellar companions may affect how hot Jupiters form and migrate (Bohn et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp108b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164509 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a metal-rich G5V star observed with the Keck telescope (Giguere et al. 2012).","observed":"Velocity measurements show a planet with a period near 282 days, an eccentricity of 0.26 and a minimum mass of 0.48 Jupiter masses (Giguere et al. 2012). The star's velocities also have a slow linear trend of -5.1 metres per second per year, which points to an additional, longer-period companion (Giguere et al. 2012).","system":"A longer-period companion is suggested by the trend (Giguere et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"The extra companion is not yet identified (Giguere et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164509b-Giguereetal2012","cite":"Giguere et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"A High-eccentricity Component in the Double-planet System around HD 163607 and a Planet around HD 164509","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.2955","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 76700 b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from a sample of metal-enriched stars added to the Anglo-Australian Planet Search in 1999 (Tinney et al. 2003).","observed":"The host was chosen for metal enrichment from Stromgren photometry, and detailed spectroscopy confirmed it (Tinney et al. 2003). The planet has a circular orbit with a period of 3.98 days (Tinney et al. 2003). With a minimum mass of 0.197 Jupiter masses, or 0.69 Saturn masses, it was one of the lowest-minimum-mass planets known then (Tinney et al. 2003).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd76700b-Tinneyetal2003","cite":"Tinney et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Four New Planets Orbiting Metal-enriched Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207128","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-68 c":{"text":{"why":"Confirming that an Earth-sized planet is real posed unique challenges here (Gilliland et al. 2013).","observed":"Kepler-68 c has a radius near that of Earth and orbits in 9.6 days (Gilliland et al. 2013). Validating it was hard, and Doppler measurements revealed a third, Jovian-mass planet beyond the two transiting ones (Gilliland et al. 2013). The star shows a rich set of pulsation modes in one-minute Kepler data, enabling asteroseismic modelling of its interior and precise stellar properties (Gilliland et al. 2013).","system":"Planet b has a density between that of the ice giants and Earth, and planet d has a period of about 580 days and a minimum mass of 0.947 Jupiter masses (Gilliland et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler68c-Gillilandetal2013","cite":"Gilliland et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ronald L. Gilliland, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-68: Three Planets, One with a Density between that of Earth and Ice Giants","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2596","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 23472 f":{"text":{"why":"It is one of three Earth-size planets confirmed with ESPRESSO in a compact system that studies how planets from one disc compare (Barros et al. 2022).","observed":"The system holds five planets with periods of about 3.98, 7.90, 12.16, 17.67 and 29.80 days, and this planet is the 12.16-day one (Barros et al. 2022). Radial velocities from ESPRESSO were used to estimate masses, and this planet has a radius of about 1.13 Earth radii and a mass of about 0.64 Earth masses (Barros et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Comparing planets around the same star helps constrain theories of formation (Barros et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd23472f-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"HD 23472: a multi-planetary system with three super-Earths and two potential super-Mercuries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"11 UMi b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looked at 62 K giant stars with the aim of learning how planet formation depends on the mass of the host star (Döllinger et al. 2009).","observed":"The host is a K giant, an evolved star that has swollen up, and it was watched with the 2 m telescope of the Thüringer Landessternwarte starting in February 2004 (Döllinger et al. 2009). An iodine gas cell in the telescope's spectrograph gave very accurate velocity readings (Döllinger et al. 2009). The star's velocity repeats every 516.22 days, with a swing of 189.70 metres per second (Döllinger et al. 2009). Brightness data from the Hipparcos satellite and measurements of the star's chromospheric activity show no change on that cycle, so orbital motion is the most likely cause (Döllinger et al. 2009).","system":"The orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.083 (Döllinger et al. 2009).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-11umib-Dllingeretal2009","cite":"Döllinger et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. P. Döllinger, A. P. Hatzes, L. Pasquini et al.","title":"Planetary companions around the K giant stars 11 Ursae Minoris and HD 32518","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1753","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 248847494 b":{"text":{"why":"Only a single transit was seen, so the orbital period is not directly measured and ordinary follow-up methods had to be adapted (Giles et al. 2018).","observed":"The long, unknown period meant the transit was fitted with the Namaste tool, and the slope of the star's radial velocity was compared with a grid of possible planet orbits (Giles et al. 2018). The star is a sub-giant with a radius of 2.70 solar radii (Giles et al. 2018). The period is 3650 days, with a large uncertainty, which puts the planet at about 4.5 AU (Giles et al. 2018). Velocity data limit the mass to under 13 Jupiter masses, so the object is planet-like (Giles et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet still needs a full radial velocity campaign for confirmation. If confirmed, it would be the longest-period transiting planet known (Giles et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic248847494b-Gilesetal2018","cite":"Giles et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"H. A. C. Giles, H. P. Osborn, S. Blanco-Cuaresma et al.","title":"Transiting planet candidate from K2 with the longest period","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.08757","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1842 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was picked out as a target for atmosphere studies, as space telescopes built to probe exoplanet atmospheres were about to launch (Wittenmyer et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is a warm Saturn with an extremely low density of 0.252 grams per cubic centimetre (Wittenmyer et al. 2022). Its large atmospheric scale height of 893 km, combined with a bright host star (J = 8.747 mag), makes it one of the best known targets for atmosphere work (Wittenmyer et al. 2022). The transit lasts only 4.3 hours, which makes ground-based follow-up easy to schedule (Wittenmyer et al. 2022). Follow-up came from MINERVA-Australis, NRES and the Las Cumbres Observatory (Wittenmyer et al. 2022).","system":"The host star is beginning to evolve off the main sequence (Wittenmyer et al. 2022).","history":"The planet is expected to be a good test of models in which a gas giant swells up again as its aging star brightens (Wittenmyer et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1842b-Wittenmyeretal2022","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jake T. Clark, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"TOI-1842b: A Transiting Warm Saturn Undergoing Reinflation around an Evolving Subgiant","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1670 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a warm Jupiter with a smaller planet closer in, and such systems help test how warm Jupiters formed (Tran et al. 2022).","observed":"Radial velocity data from the Tull Coudé Spectrograph and HARPS-N gave a mass of 0.63 Jupiter masses and a mean density of 0.81 grams per cubic centimetre (Tran et al. 2022). A seven-hour observation of a transit with the NEID spectrograph on the 3.5 m WIYN Telescope measured the spin-orbit angle, the tilt between the orbit and the star's spin, as -0.3 ± 2.2 degrees, so the planet is well aligned (Lubin et al. 2023). Among planets with a measured angle, it has one of the longest orbital periods (Lubin et al. 2023). The transit shape is consistent with a spherical planet, and its oblateness (how flattened it is) is not constrained (Lammers & Winn 2026).","system":"An inner sub-Neptune orbits every 10.9 days, and its mass is too small to detect in the velocity data (Tran et al. 2022). The outer planet's orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.09 (Tran et al. 2022).","history":"The near-circular orbit and inner companion fit gentle formation by disk migration or in-place formation better than migration on a stretched, eccentric orbit (Tran et al. 2022).","open":"Warm Jupiters have uncertain origins, and their tilt could separate the formation routes (Lammers & Winn 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1670c-Tranetal2022","cite":"Tran et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Quang H. Tran, Brendan P. Bowler, Michael Endl et al.","title":"TOI-1670 b and c: An Inner Sub-Neptune with an Outer Warm Jupiter Unlikely to Have Originated from High-eccentricity Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.04334","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1670c-Lubinetal2023","cite":"Lubin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jack Lubin, Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice et al.","title":"TOI-1670 c, a 40 day Orbital Period Warm Jupiter in a Compact System, Is Well Aligned","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16237","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1670c-LammersWinn2026","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Constraining the Planetary Obliquity Distribution of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 98-59 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is an M3 dwarf with four confirmed planets and one planetary candidate (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). The three inner planets transit, and the transiting ones have been observed with the Wide Field Camera 3 on the Hubble Space Telescope for atmosphere studies (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). Planets c and d lie very close to a 2:1 orbital resonance, which makes the system useful for studying composition and architecture (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). Combined fits of TESS and Hubble transits with ESPRESSO and HARPS velocities aim at the best possible masses (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023).","system":"The outer planet and the candidate show up only in radial velocities (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). A possible signal near 23 days could be a candidate companion or stellar activity, since the star's rotation period is either 39.6 or 78 days (Schwarz et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether the 23-day signal is a planet or stellar activity is not settled (Schwarz et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l9859c-SchwarzDreizler2023","cite":"Schwarz & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler","title":"L98-59 (TOI-175) a case study for a multiple exoplanet system with small planets near resonance","journal":"EAS2023, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859c-Schwarzetal2022","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler, René Heller","title":"Disentangling the stellar activity and the radial velocity signatures of L 98-59 (TOI-175)","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an inflated hot Jupiter with a radius of 1.52 Jupiter radii and a mass of 1.196 Jupiter masses (Rodriguez et al. 2016). Its orbit is consistent with circular (Rodriguez et al. 2016). Spectra from the ESPRESSO instrument show the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a tilt of 0.09 degrees (Stangret et al. 2024). The same spectra found no sign of the atoms and molecules searched for, including iron, sodium, titanium oxide and water, possibly because the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect or low signal masked them (Stangret et al. 2024). Phase-curve analysis of TESS data measured a significant eclipse depth or brightness variation around the orbit (Wong et al. 2020). A high-contrast imaging search found new candidate companions around the host star (Bohn et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a G2 star near the main-sequence turnoff, about 5 billion years old (Rodriguez et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt14b-Rodriguezetal2016","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Knicole D. Colón, Keivan G. Stassun et al.","title":"KELT-14b and KELT-15b: An Independent Discovery of WASP-122b and a New Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.08953","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt14b-Stangretetal2024","cite":"Stangret et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Stangret, E. Palle, E. Esparza-Borges et al.","title":"The obliquity and atmosphere of the hot Jupiter WASP-122b (KELT-14b) with ESPRESSO: An aligned orbit and no sign of atomic or molecular absorption","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00800","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt14b-Wongetal2020","cite":"Wong et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ian Wong, Avi Shporer, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"Systematic Phase Curve Study of Known Transiting Systems from Year One of the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06407","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt14b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219828 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Neptune with a minimum mass of 21 Earth masses, orbiting every 3.83 days (Santos et al. 2016). Velocity data from HARPS, SOPHIE and ELODIE confirmed it and revealed a much larger companion on a long orbit (Santos et al. 2016). Dynamical modelling shows the system is stable (Santos et al. 2016). Planet b's equilibrium eccentricity, the value tides would settle it at, is close to zero (Santos et al. 2016). The star is bright and metal-rich (Santos et al. 2016).","system":"A companion, HD 219828 c, has a minimum mass of 15.1 Jupiter masses, a 13.1-year period and a very eccentric orbit (e = 0.81) (Santos et al. 2016). The authors call the system extreme, with a long-period super Jupiter and a hot-Neptune host star (Santos et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219828b-Santosetal2016","cite":"Santos et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. C. Santos, A. Santerne, J. P. Faria et al.","title":"An extreme planetary system around HD 219828. One long-period super Jupiter to a hot-Neptune host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.06942","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a K3 dwarf, among the nearest and brightest with several planets (Bailey 2023). Radial velocity data show six planets, and only the two innermost, b and c, transit (Bailey 2023). The ASTERIA CubeSat and TESS searched for transits of planets d and f, because the long transit duration (about 5 hours), shallow depth and uncertain timing make ground-based searches impractical (Bailey 2023). Planet f's transit was ruled out with TESS, and for planet d a transit upper limit was set (Bailey 2023). Its minimum mass is 16.7 Earth masses (Bailey 2023).","system":"The first four planets were announced together, and the innermost, b, is a transiting rocky planet (Motalebi et al. 2015). Planet b is thought to be rocky from its density of 5.76 grams per cubic centimetre (Motalebi et al. 2015). Strong stellar wind hitting planet b may sputter material from its surface (Folsom et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether planet d transits is not known (Bailey 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134d-Bailey2023","cite":"Bailey 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vanessa Bailey","title":"HD 219134 Revisited: Planet d Transit Upper Limit and Planet f Transit Non-Detection with ASTERIA and TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.MTOJHC","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134d-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134d-Folsometal2022","cite":"Folsom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Colin P. Folsom, Aline A. Vidotto, Luca Fossati","title":"Stellar magnetic fields for star-planet interactions: HD 219134 and 55 Cnc","journal":"The 21st Cambridge Workshop on Cool Stars, Stellar Systems, and the Sun","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7592884","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-150 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a mass of 8.5 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.1 Jupiter radii, which makes it very dense, at 6.4 times Jupiter's density (Cooke et al. 2020). The orbit is eccentric, with e = 0.38 (Cooke et al. 2020). The host is an F8 main-sequence star of 1.4 solar masses (Cooke et al. 2020). The star is about 3 billion years old, well below the time tides would need to circularise the orbit, which supports the eccentric orbit being real (Cooke et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp150b-Cookeetal2020","cite":"Cooke et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Benjamin F. Cooke, Don Pollacco, Y. Almleaky et al.","title":"Two Transiting Hot Jupiters from the WASP Survey: WASP-150b and WASP-176b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06622","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2022-BLG-0371L b":{"text":{"why":"The event is one of four whose light curves showed brief central anomalies that were only partly covered by data (Han et al. 2023).","observed":"Several models were tested for the anomaly, including a planet orbiting a star and a pair of source stars (Han et al. 2023). Four degenerate planetary solutions with different mass ratios fit the data (Han et al. 2023). One with a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 4 × 10^-4 is strongly favoured on fit quality and relative proper motion, and the two-source explanation is clearly ruled out (Han et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host star is poorly known, so the planet's mass depends on the favoured solution (Han et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2022blg0371lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Weicheng Zang et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-2010Lb, KMT-2022-BLG-0371Lb, and KMT-2022-BLG-1013Lb: Three microlensing planets detected via partially covered signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03871","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 2562 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The first ALMA detection of the star's debris disc, a ring of leftover dust and rocky debris, shows it nearly edge-on and well resolved (Zhang et al. 2019). The full extent of the disc is about 200 AU (Zhang et al. 2019). The size of the inner hole is not resolved, and a range of sizes fits the data (Zhang et al. 2019). The companion is a brown dwarf, an object between planet and star in mass, that lies in a cleared inner hole of the disc (Zhang et al. 2019).","system":"An inner hole as wide as the distance between the companion and the star could be evidence for dynamical sculpting, meaning the companion's gravity clears the dust (Zhang et al. 2019).","history":"The disc properties may help show how the companion formed and what its true mass is (Zhang et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr2562b-Zhangetal2019","cite":"Zhang et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Yimiao Zhang, Gaspard Duchene, Quinn Konopacky et al.","title":"Characterizing the Debris Disk of Substellar Companion Host HR 2562 with ALMA","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 22496 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found by a blind radial velocity search using ESPRESSO alone, meant to show what the instrument can do over long observing spans (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","observed":"The detection used 41 ESPRESSO measurements spread over 895 days, with a median photon noise of 18 centimetres per second (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). The analysis also accounted for stellar activity on longer timescales for this K5-K7 main-sequence star (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). For comparison, 43 archive HARPS measurements were analysed, and four TESS sectors were searched for transits (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). The velocities fit a close-in planet orbiting every 5.09071 days (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd22496b-LilloBoxetal2021","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, J. P. Faria, A. Suárez Mascareño et al.","title":"HD 22496 b: The first ESPRESSO stand-alone planet discovery","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.00226","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-98 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star has a metallicity of -0.60, exceptionally low for a star with a transiting planet (Hellier et al. 2014). A fainter star 12 arcseconds away was identified as a white dwarf, a dead stellar core, and its distance and motion match a physical link to the planetary system (Southworth et al. 2020). Fits to its colours and parallax show the system is old, at least 3.6 billion years (Southworth et al. 2020). The motion of the system is consistent with the thick disc of the Galaxy, though the thin disc cannot be ruled out (Southworth et al. 2020).","system":"The planet orbits a late-G dwarf, and the white dwarf is a bound companion star (Southworth et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The age of the system cannot be pinned down more precisely because of theoretical uncertainties (Southworth et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp98b-Hellieretal2014","cite":"Hellier et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Transiting hot Jupiters from WASP-South, Euler and TRAPPIST: WASP-95b to WASP-101b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5630","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp98b-Southworthetal2020","cite":"Southworth et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"John Southworth, Pier-Emmanuel Tremblay, Boris T. Gänsicke et al.","title":"A white dwarf bound to the transiting planetary system WASP-98","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.02533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-45 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet passes in front of the limb of its K2V host star, so its path crosses the edge of the stellar disc (Anderson et al. 2011). Weak Ca II H and K emission in the star's spectra suggests it is chromospherically active (Anderson et al. 2011). The orbit was fitted as circular because the velocity data are consistent with near-circular orbits, as is expected for short-period Jupiter-mass planets (Anderson et al. 2011). Anderson et al. (2011) point out that the derived planet and star radii depend on whether eccentricity is fitted. Transits were also observed with the MINERVA-Australis telescopes to demonstrate their photometric capability (Addison et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp45b-Andersonetal2011","cite":"Anderson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-44b, WASP-45b and WASP-46b: three short-period, transiting extrasolar planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3179","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp45b-Addisonetal2019","cite":"Addison et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Brett Addison, Duncan J. Wright, Robert A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"Minerva-Australis. I. Design, Commissioning, and First Photometric Results","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.11231","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-238 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a mass of 0.86 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.30 Jupiter radii (Soto et al. 2018). Data came from K2 Campaigns 11 and 12 plus velocities from HARPS, FEROS and CORALIE (Soto et al. 2018). Its radius is consistent with theoretical models once the high incident flux falling on the planet is considered (Soto et al. 2018). The planet was announced together with K2-237 b, which is highly inflated (Soto et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2238b-Sotoetal2018","cite":"Soto et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. G. Soto, M. R. Díaz, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"K2-237 b and K2-238 b: discovery and characterization of two new transiting hot Jupiters from K2","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.07959","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 215497 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The detection came from the HARPS volume-limited sample, in which three multiple-planet systems were announced together (Lo Curto et al. 2010). Among the six planets, one is a super-Earth with a minimum mass of 6.6 Earth masses and one is a Neptune-like planet of 18 Earth masses (Lo Curto et al. 2010). Both orbit within 0.05 AU with periods of about four days (Lo Curto et al. 2010). The finds strengthen the argument that low-mass planets are mostly found in multiple-planet systems (Lo Curto et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd215497b-Curtoetal2010","cite":"Curto et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Lo Curto, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HARPS RV curves of HD125612, HD215497, HIP5158 (Lo Curto+, 2010)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35120048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-75 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the SOPHIE and HARPS-N spectrographs revealed that the Kepler candidate is a giant planet (Bonomo et al. 2015). The system parameters were refined with Kepler short-cadence data, which sample transits much better than the earlier long-cadence data (Bonomo et al. 2015). A massive close-in planet like this one raises tides on its star, and Kepler-75 is among the systems modelled for how those tides and stellar wind braking change the star's rotation (Ferraz-Mello et al. 2015). For companions closer than about 0.03-0.04 AU, tidal evolution means a star's age cannot be estimated from its spin rate (Ferraz-Mello et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler75b-Bonomoetal2015","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Improved parameters of seven Kepler giant companions characterized with SOPHIE and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler75b-FerrazMelloetal2015","cite":"Ferraz-Mello et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"S. Ferraz-Mello, M. Tadeu dos Santos, H. Folonier et al.","title":"Interplay of Tidal Evolution and Stellar Wind Braking in the Rotation of Stars Hosting Massive Close-In Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04369","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1266 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Timing variations of transits of the inner two planets, measured with ground-based follow-up and new TESS data, confirmed a third planet that does not transit (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Planet d has a period of 32.5 days and a mass of 3.68 Earth masses (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Combining these timings with archival velocity data refined all the planet masses and radii (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","system":"The star is a nearby M dwarf with two temperate sub-Neptunes below 450 K, in a rare inverted architecture where the inner planet is larger (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). The inner and outer planets sit near a 3:1 period ratio, with a nonresonant planet between them (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Planet b has a low density that requires a hydrogen-rich envelope (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1266d-GreklekMcKeonetal2025","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Shreyas Vissapragada, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Tidally Heated Sub-Neptunes, Refined Planetary Compositions, and Confirmation of a Third Planet in the TOI-1266 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2015 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's transits arrive early or late in a pattern with an amplitude of about 100 minutes (Jones et al. 2024). Such transit timing variations point to a neighbour tugging on the planet. Radial velocities, 63 transit light curves and 49 transit epochs show a non-transiting companion, TOI-2015 c, in strong gravitational interaction with it (Barkaoui et al. 2025). The planet's density is only about a quarter of Earth's (Barkaoui et al. 2025), which suggests a volatile-rich world (Jones et al. 2024).","system":"The host is an active M4 dwarf that spins once every 8.7 days, which gives an age of about 1.1 billion years (Jones et al. 2024). No other transiting planets are seen around it (Jones et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The period ratio of the two planets is debated. Early modelling favoured an outer planet near a 2:1 resonance but could not exclude 3:2 or 4:3 (Jones et al. 2024). Later photodynamical modelling strongly favours a 5:3 resonance (Barkaoui et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2015b-Jonesetal2024","cite":"Jones et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sinclaire E. Jones, Guđmundur Stefánsson, Kento Masuda et al.","title":"TOI-2015 b: A Warm Neptune with Transit Timing Variations Orbiting an Active Mid-type M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11775","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2015b-Barkaouietal2025","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"K. Barkaoui, J. Korth, E. Gaidos et al.","title":"TOI-2015 b: A sub-Neptune in strong gravitational interaction with an outer non-transiting planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07074","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-115 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up was a search for warm Jupiters, gas giants receiving little starlight (Shporer et al. 2017).","observed":"The planet is one of the longest-period transiting gas giants with a measured mass (Shporer et al. 2017). Its mass is 0.84 Jupiter masses and its radius is 1.115 Jupiter radii (Shporer et al. 2017). Its radius is not inflated and agrees with theoretical expectations (Shporer et al. 2017). Radial velocities from the Keck HIRES spectrograph gave the mass, and ground-based telescopes in a robotic network watched extra transits (Shporer et al. 2017).","system":"The host star is relatively old (Shporer et al. 2017). High-resolution imaging shows it to be isolated, with no close companion star (Shporer et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Where planets receive less starlight than about 10^8 erg per second per square centimetre, the link between irradiation and planet size appears to level off. Stellar irradiation may then play little role in setting the radius of warm Jupiters (Shporer et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2115b-Shporeretal2017","cite":"Shporer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Avi Shporer, George Zhou, Benjamin J. Fulton et al.","title":"K2-114b and K2-115b: Two Transiting Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07128","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 160691 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planets in this four-planet system were found from Doppler shifts, so only lower limits on their masses are known (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019). Orbital tilts and node directions are unknown. Orbital evolution was computed over 1 million years, with the unknown orbital elements varied within allowed limits (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019). The orbits were studied for long-term dynamical behaviour.","system":"The host star is the G3 subgiant Cervantes, also called mu Arae, 15.57 parsecs from the Sun (Diputra et al. 2026). Its model has a core out to 0.28 solar radii, a radiative zone to 0.99 solar radii and a convective envelope to 1.349 solar radii, at an age of 6 billion years (Diputra et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd160691e-PerminovKuznetsov2019","cite":"Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Alexander Perminov, Eduard Kuznetsov","title":"Dynamical evolution of extrasolar planetary systems HD 141399, HD 160691 and HD 39194","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd160691e-Diputraetal2026","cite":"Diputra et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Adi Syahwal Ganda Diputra, Farahhati Mumtahana, Gerhana Puannandra Putri et al.","title":"Peering Inside the Planet-Hosting Cervantes Star (\\mu Arae)","journal":"Bulgarian Astronomical Journal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-91 b":{"text":{"why":"Most Kepler planets with measured phase curves reflect little light, with optical albedos below 0.25, but Kepler-91 b is an exception (Esteves et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet orbits a red giant star, one that has already left the main sequence (Lillo-Box et al. 2014). Its planetary nature was shown by slight brightness changes in the star's light as the planet orbits, and by asteroseismology, which reads the star's oscillations (Lillo-Box et al. 2014). Radial velocities from the Calar Alto CAFE spectrograph then gave a mass of 1.09 Jupiter masses (Lillo-Box et al. 2014). An independent Subaru study found 0.66 Jupiter masses (Sato et al. 2015). Other work had claimed the object was self-luminous, not a planet (Lillo-Box et al. 2014).","system":"Subaru radial velocities taken about a year apart differ by around 20 m/s, which suggests an additional companion in the system (Sato et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Only a few tens of planets are known around stars climbing the red giant branch, so how planets die is still an open question (Lillo-Box et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler91b-Estevesetal2015","cite":"Esteves et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Lisa J. Esteves, Ernst J. W. De Mooij, Ray Jayawardhana","title":"Changing Phases of Alien Worlds: Probing Atmospheres of Kepler Planets with High-precision Photometry","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.2245","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler91b-LilloBoxetal2014","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, D. Barrado, A. Moya et al.","title":"Kepler-91b: a planet at the end of its life. Planet and giant host star properties via light-curve variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3943","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler91b-Satoetal2015","cite":"Sato et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Teruyuki Hirano, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Precise Radial Velocity Measurements for Kepler Giants Hosting Planetary Candidates: Kepler-91 and KOI-1894","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.05415","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 99770 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's spectrum shows strong carbon dioxide absorption relative to carbon monoxide, which indicates about three times the star's heavy-element content (Balmer et al. 2026). Such enrichment suggests formation within a disk (Balmer et al. 2026).","observed":"Gaia and Hipparcos measurements of the star's motion revealed a pull from an unseen giant, and direct imaging then picked out the planet (Currie et al. 2023). The planet orbits about 17 astronomical units from its star and receives about as much light as Jupiter does (Currie et al. 2023). Its dynamical mass is 13.9 to 16.1 Jupiter masses (Currie et al. 2023). Its spectrum looks like an older, less cloudy version of the HR 8799 planets (Currie et al. 2023). JWST images show carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide absorption (Balmer et al. 2026).","system":"The host star, also called 29 Cygni, is an early-type star (Balmer et al. 2026). Its spin axis is consistent with the planet's orbit at the 2 sigma level, with a difference of 12° ± 6° (Balmer et al. 2026). Follow-up imaging prefers a semimajor axis of about 15.7 to 15.8 astronomical units and an eccentricity near 0.28 to 0.29, disfavouring a circular orbit (Bovie et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The mass is uncertain and straddles the deuterium-burning limit, at about 15 ± 5 Jupiter masses (Balmer et al. 2026). How such objects form is unclear (Balmer et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip99770b-Balmeretal2026","cite":"Balmer et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"William O. Balmer, Laurent Pueyo, Ashley Messier et al.","title":"Direct Images of CO<SUB>2</SUB> Absorption in the Atmosphere of a Super-Jupiter: Enhanced Metallicity Suggestive of Formation in a Disk","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09785","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip99770b-Currieetal2023","cite":"Currie et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Thayne Currie, G. Mirek Brandt, Timothy D. Brandt et al.","title":"Direct imaging and astrometric detection of a gas giant planet orbiting an accelerating star","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.00034","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip99770b-Bovieetal2025","cite":"Bovie et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Danielle Bovie, Thayne Currie, Mona El Morsy et al.","title":"Multi-band Spectral and Astrometric Characterization of the HIP 99770 b Planet with SCExAO/CHARIS and Gaia<SUP></SUP>","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02665","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86226 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A small transiting planet was later found on a 4-day orbit around the same star, so the system holds a long-period giant and a short-period sub-Neptune (Teske et al. 2020). Updated radial velocities show the giant, which is not known to transit, to be less eccentric and less massive than previously reported (Teske et al. 2020). The giant's orbit takes about 1600 days (Teske et al. 2020).","system":"The inner planet, HD 86226 c, has a radius of 2.16 Earth radii and a mass of 7.25 Earth masses (Teske et al. 2020). The host is a bright, solar-type star (Teske et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The inner planet's density suggests at least a small volatile envelope, but the shares of rock, iron and water are not well constrained (Teske et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86226b-Teskeetal2020","cite":"Teske et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Johanna Teske, Matías R. Díaz, Rafael Luque et al.","title":"TESS Reveals a Short-period Sub-Neptune Sibling (HD 86226c) to a Known Long-period Giant Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.13927","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-25 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The tilt between the star's spin axis and the planetary orbits was measured in three dimensions. The analysis joined asteroseismology, which reads stellar oscillations, the transit light curve, and the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in the star's spectrum during transit (Benomar et al. 2014). Kepler-25 is one of the few systems with several transiting planets that has a measured spin-orbit angle (Benomar et al. 2014).","system":"The system holds two short-period transiting planets and one outer planet that does not transit (Benomar et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler25c-Benomaretal2014","cite":"Benomar et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Othman Benomar, Kento Masuda, Hiromoto Shibahashi et al.","title":"Determination of three-dimensional spin-orbit angle with joint analysis of asteroseismology, transit lightcurve, and the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect: Cases of HAT-P-7 and Kepler-25","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.7332","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4465 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is enriched in heavy elements at a level that fits the core accretion theory of giant planet formation (Essack et al. 2025).","observed":"Only a single transit was seen in TESS Sector 40, so the orbit had to be pinned down from radial velocities taken with the Automated Planet Finder telescope, which gave a period of about 102 days and an eccentricity of 0.24 (Essack et al. 2025). A global campaign then caught another transit of about 12 hours from many sites, including 24 citizen scientists, confirming the period (Essack et al. 2025). The planet has a mass of 5.89 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.25 Jupiter radii (Essack et al. 2025). It is dense, at 3.9 g per cubic centimetre (Essack et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a G dwarf star (Essack et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Confirming more single-transit planets is crucial for learning how common long-period planets are in the outer parts of planetary systems (Essack et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4465b-Essacketal2025","cite":"Essack et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Zahra Essack, Diana Dragomir, Paul A. Dalba et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. VI. Confirmation of a Long-period Giant Planet Discovered with a Single TESS Transit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20019","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1859 b":{"text":{"why":"Warm Jupiters feel weak tides from their stars, so their orbit tilts may still reflect how they first formed and moved (Dong et al. 2023).","observed":"The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in the star's spectrum during transit, shows the planet's orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin by 38.9° (Dong et al. 2023). The planet's orbital eccentricity is 0.57, inferred from transit light curves alone (Dong et al. 2023). Spectroscopic observations confirmed it as a planet (Dong et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a late F dwarf (Dong et al. 2023).","history":"The eccentric and misaligned orbit is likely an outcome of dynamical interactions, such as planet-planet scattering or planet-disk resonance crossing (Dong et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1859b-Dongetal2023","cite":"Dong et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jiayin Dong, Songhu Wang, Malena Rice et al.","title":"TOI-1859b: A 64 Day Warm Jupiter on an Eccentric and Misaligned Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16495","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-51 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a low density, a mass of 0.768 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.41 Jupiter radii (Henning et al. 2018). It was one of four hot Jupiters announced together, and three of them have low density (Henning et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a G-type dwarf that is metal-rich, larger and more massive than the Sun (Henning et al. 2018). Its planet circles it every 3.3489 days (Henning et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats51b-Henningetal2018","cite":"Henning et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Th. Henning, L. Mancini, P. Sarkis et al.","title":"HATS-50b through HATS-53b: Four Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting G-type Stars Discovered by the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.04324","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 216437 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit is eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.33, and has a semi-major axis of 2.4 astronomical units (Jones et al. 2002). Its minimum mass is 2.1 Jupiter masses (Jones et al. 2002). Its host is a solar-type star (Jones et al. 2002). The planet was also found in a separate CORALIE survey, among ten typical Jupiter-like planets on intermediate or long orbits with eccentricities of 0.2 to 0.5 (Mayor et al. 2004).","system":"The star is one of several metal-rich Sun-like stars with mildly eccentric, long-period planets (Jones et al. 2002).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd216437b-Jonesetal2002","cite":"Jones et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Hugh R. A. Jones, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Extrasolar planets around HD 196050, HD 216437 and HD 160691","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0206216","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd216437b-Mayoretal2004","cite":"Mayor et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mayor, S. Udry, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XII. Orbital solutions for 16 extra-solar planets discovered with CORALIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0310316","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-231 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's host belongs to the Ruprecht 147 star cluster, which is about 3 billion years old and 300 parsecs away (Curtis et al. 2018).","observed":"The host star is a solar twin in the cluster, and the planet has a radius of 2.5 Earth radii (Curtis et al. 2018). Six transits in the K2 light curve, each lasting about 4 hours, gave a depth of about 0.06 percent (Curtis et al. 2018). The star appears to be single, based on photometry, radial velocity variations over nearly 10 years, and adaptive optics imaging (Curtis et al. 2018). Statistical modelling validated the planet (Curtis et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass was only estimated from a mass-radius relation, at 7 Earth masses with a large uncertainty (Curtis et al. 2018). Radial velocity of around 2 m/s may be measurable (Curtis et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2231b-Curtisetal2018","cite":"Curtis et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Jason Lee Curtis, Andrew Vanderburg, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"K2-231 b: A Sub-Neptune Exoplanet Transiting a Solar Twin in Ruprecht 147","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-96 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is active and solar-type, and flares showed up on the planet's transit light curves (Estrela & Valio 2018). Fitting a transit model with a flare added gave the duration and energy of each flare (Estrela & Valio 2018). Three superflares were found, and the biggest had an energy of 1.81 × 10^29 joules (Estrela & Valio 2018). Calculations for a hypothetical Earth in the star's habitable zone found that bacteria like E. coli could survive on its surface only if an ozone layer were present (Estrela & Valio 2018).","system":"The star is about 2.3 billion years old, the same as the Sun when oxygen rose in Earth's air (Estrela & Valio 2018). The planet is a close-in super-Earth (Estrela & Valio 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler96b-EstrelaValio2018","cite":"Estrela & Valio 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Raissa Estrela, Adriana Valio","title":"Superflare Ultraviolet Impact on Kepler-96 System: A Glimpse of Habitability When the Ozone Layer First Formed on Earth","journal":"Astrobiology","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.05400","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 29442 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The faint transits of the star's planets were found in the TESS light curve (Damasso et al. 2023). CHEOPS later measured the radius of planet d as 1.538 Earth radii, differing by more than 3 sigma from the TESS-based value (Egger et al. 2024). Caution is needed when using TESS photometry to size small planets with low per-transit signal-to-noise and large gaps between observations (Egger et al. 2024).","system":"The host is an evolved K0V star with two super-Earths and one sub-Neptune on orbits of 13.6, 3.5 and 6.4 days (Egger et al. 2024). The system spans the radius valley, the gap in the sizes of close-in planets (Egger et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip29442d-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, J. Rodrigues, A. Castro-González et al.","title":"A compact multi-planet system transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) discovered by TESS and ESPRESSO. Radial velocities lead to the detection of transits with low signal-to-noise ratio","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.13310","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip29442d-Eggeretal2024","cite":"Egger et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. A. Egger, H. P. Osborn, D. Kubyshkina et al.","title":"Unveiling the internal structure and formation history of the three planets transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) with CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18653","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is bright, with a V magnitude of 8.54, and spins at 73 km/s, which makes the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect unusually large, with an amplitude of several hundred m/s (Bieryla et al. 2015). The planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Bieryla et al. 2015), and Doppler tomography confirms an angle of 2.7° ± 0.6° (Zhou et al. 2016). Hubble spectra show a transmission spectrum consistent with a cloud-free atmosphere, with water and H- (Pluriel et al. 2020). The emission spectrum lacks strong absorption features and is not a simple blackbody (Pluriel et al. 2020).","system":"The host star is an F star with a mass of 1.535 solar masses, the fifth most massive and fifth hottest known to host a transiting planet (Bieryla et al. 2015). Its rotation period is shorter than the planet's orbital period (Zhou et al. 2016).","history":"Tidal interactions show no sign of having changed the star's rotation, so the aligned orbit may be the original configuration (Zhou et al. 2016).","open":"Further Hubble or next-generation observations are needed to confirm what sets the planet's optical opacity (Pluriel et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt7b-Bierylaetal2015","cite":"Bieryla et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Allyson Bieryla, Karen Collins, Thomas G. Beatty et al.","title":"KELT-7b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a Bright V = 8.54 Rapidly Rotating F-star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.05565","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt7b-Zhouetal2016","cite":"Zhou et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"George Zhou, David W. Latham, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Spin-orbit alignment for KELT-7b and HAT-P-56b via Doppler tomography with TRES","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.01991","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt7b-Plurieletal2020","cite":"Pluriel et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"William Pluriel, Niall Whiteford, Billy Edwards et al.","title":"ARES. III. Unveiling the Two Faces of KELT-7 b with HST WFC3","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14199","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-27 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is massive and dense, with a mass of 10.39 Jupiter masses, a radius of 1.01 Jupiter radii and a mean density of 12.6 g per cubic centimetre (Parviainen et al. 2014). Its effective temperature is 1500 K (Parviainen et al. 2014). Ground-based follow-up between 2012 and 2016 found no transit in the predicted window, so the ephemeris was not matched (St Raetz et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a 4.2 billion-year-old G2 star (Parviainen et al. 2014). The planet orbits at 0.048 astronomical units (Parviainen et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot27b-Parviainenetal2014","cite":"Parviainen et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"H. Parviainen, D. Gandolfi, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXV. CoRoT-27b: a massive and dense planet on a short-period orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1122","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot27b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 139357 b":{"text":{"why":"The search aimed to learn how planet formation depends on stellar mass, by looking at giant stars of intermediate mass (Döllinger et al. 2009).","observed":"The host is a K4 giant that was one of 62 K giants watched for several years with a 2 m telescope in Germany (Döllinger et al. 2009). An iodine cell in the spectrograph gave a steady wavelength reference for the velocity measurements (Döllinger et al. 2009). The star's velocity rises and falls every 1125.7 days with a semiamplitude of 159.9 m/s (Döllinger et al. 2009). The orbit is only slightly eccentric (Döllinger et al. 2009). The authors call the companion a candidate, not a confirmed planet (Döllinger et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd139357b-Dllingeretal2009","cite":"Döllinger et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. P. Döllinger, A. P. Hatzes, L. Pasquini et al.","title":"Planetary companion candidates around the K giant stars 42 Draconis and HD 139 357","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.3593","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-672 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits in the Neptunian desert, a lack of Neptune-sized planets on short orbits, which is thought to be carved by photoevaporation and tidal circularisation after high-eccentricity migration (Osborn et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was first checked statistically: light curves were examined and false-positive odds were computed, and it was judged good for both transmission and emission spectroscopy (Mistry et al. 2023). Later, two spectrographs working together on one telescope in Chile measured the star's wobble, and TESS and ExTrA photometry were added (Osborn et al. 2026). This confirmed a massive super-Neptune around an M0 dwarf (Osborn et al. 2026). It lies in the Neptunian ridge, a pile-up of planets with 3 to 5 day periods at the desert edge (Osborn et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The study compared the desert boundaries for planets around FGK stars and M dwarfs for the first time, and found the ridge shifts slightly inward for M dwarfs (Osborn et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi672b-Osbornetal2026","cite":"Osborn et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ares Osborn, Ryan Cloutier, Vincent Bourrier et al.","title":"Confirmation of the hot super-Neptune TOI-672 b with NIRPS and HARPS: Insights into the Neptunian desert around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12345","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi672b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Aniket Prasad et al.","title":"VaTEST. II. Statistical Validation of 11 TESS-detected Exoplanets Orbiting K-type Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-41 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is very massive for a hot Jupiter, with a mass near 9.7 Jupiter masses, and only a few hot Jupiters above 5 Jupiter masses are known (Bento et al. 2018). Its host star is the most metal-rich star known to host a transiting planet (Bento et al. 2018). The orbit is likely eccentric (Bento et al. 2018). It is one of four planets reported together around F stars, whose masses and densities differ by more than a factor of ten (Bento et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"The high mass, together with the host star's young age of about 1.34 billion years, may explain why the orbit has not yet become circular (Bento et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats41b-Bentoetal2018","cite":"Bento et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Bento, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-39b, HATS-40b, HATS-41b, and HATS-42b: three inflated hot Jupiters and a super-Jupiter transiting F stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01623","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-31 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet from the HAT telescope network confirmed without any follow-up photometry, showing a new mode of operation was possible (Kipping et al. 2011).","observed":"The planet has an unusually high eccentricity of about 0.245, measured with Keck, FIES and Subaru velocities (Kipping et al. 2011). The velocities also show a steady drift, which the authors read as a more distant companion, HAT-P-31 c (Kipping et al. 2011). Later small-telescope photometry gave more precise parameters (Rocchetto & Fossey 2013). Transit times drifted by about 2 hours from the old prediction, so the timing was refined (Mallonn et al. 2019).","system":"The outer companion has a minimum mass of 3.4 Jupiter masses and a period of at least 2.8 years, but the data cover less than half an orbit (Kipping et al. 2011). Simulations show the pair can be stable (Kipping et al. 2011).","history":"If the outer body has an eccentric orbit, simulations show it actively drives the eccentricity of the inner planet (Kipping et al. 2011).","open":"The properties of the outer companion are not well fixed yet (Kipping et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp31b-Kippingetal2011","cite":"Kipping et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. M. Kipping, J. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-31b,c: A Transiting, Eccentric, Hot Jupiter and a Long-period, Massive Third Body","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1169","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp31b-RocchettoFossey2013","cite":"Rocchetto & Fossey 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Rocchetto, S. Fossey","title":"Improved characterisation of exoplanets discovered in wide-field surveys: HAT-P-29b and HAT-P-31b","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp31b-Mallonnetal2019","cite":"Mallonn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Mallonn, C. von Essen, E. Herrero et al.","title":"Ephemeris refinement of 21 hot Jupiter exoplanets with high timing uncertainties","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.05882","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 184010 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it watches late-G and early-K giant stars, looking for planets around intermediate-mass stars in their evolved stages (Teng et al. 2022).","observed":"The host is a K0 star at the start of the red giant branch, with a radius of 4.86 solar radii (Teng et al. 2022). Velocity data taken over 17 years at Okayama show three giant planets (Teng et al. 2022). The three have periods of 286.6, 484.3 and 836.4 days and minimum masses between 0.30 and 0.45 Jupiter masses (Teng et al. 2022).","system":"The planets are packed closely, with period ratios near 21 : 12 : 7, so neighbouring planets are closer than 2 : 1 (Teng et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd184010b-Tengetal2022","cite":"Teng et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Takuya Takarada et al.","title":"A trio of giant planets orbiting evolved star HD 184010","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09426","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-51 d":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the lowest-density planets known, and most formation theories do not readily explain it (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026).","observed":"Timing shifts in its transits and those of its neighbours gave very low masses and densities for all three transiting planets (Masuda 2014). Webb Space Telescope spectra from 0.6 to 5.3 microns show a sloped, featureless line, best matched by a low-metallicity atmosphere with high-altitude haze of tiny particles (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). A tilted ring could also fit (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). Webb transits show no sign of flattening, so the planet spins slowly, with a period of at least 40 hours (Lammers & Winn 2024).","system":"The star is about 500 million years old (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). Three transiting planets lie near a 1 : 2 : 3 resonance chain (Masuda 2014). A fourth, non-transiting planet was later inferred from transit timing (Masuda et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The cause of the low density is unsettled: a thick hydrogen-helium envelope, high haze or a tilted ring are all possible (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). A ring search in Kepler data finds no sign of large, thick rings around planet d (Zuluaga et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler51d-LibbyRobertsetal2026","cite":"Libby-Roberts et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, Aaron Bello-Arufe, Zachory K. Berta-Thompson et al.","title":"The James Webb Space Telescope NIRSpec-PRISM Transmission Spectrum of the Super-puff, Kepler-51d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51d-Masuda2014","cite":"Masuda 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Very Low Density Planets around Kepler-51 Revealed with Transit Timing Variations and an Anomaly Similar to a Planet-Planet Eclipse Event","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2885","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51d-LammersWinn2024","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Slow Rotation for the Super-puff Planet Kepler-51d","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.06697","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51d-Masudaetal2024","cite":"Masuda et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kento Masuda, Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"A Fourth Planet in the Kepler-51 System Revealed by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01625","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51d-Zuluagaetal2026","cite":"Zuluaga et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jorge I. Zuluaga, Sebastian Numpaque, David M. Kipping et al.","title":"Probing Exoplanetary Rings with Asterodensity Profiling: A PhotoRing Analysis of Kepler-51","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25234","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":28,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-124 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Optical transmission spectra from two ground-based telescopes cover 4570 to 9940 angstroms (McGruder et al. 2023). No strong spectral features were found, so the data do not show a clear atmospheric signature (McGruder et al. 2023). Models give a temperature that agrees with the planet's equilibrium temperature, but with wide uncertainties (McGruder et al. 2023). The atmosphere could be opaque anywhere from 1.6 microbars to 32 bars (McGruder et al. 2023).","system":"The planet belongs to a set of seven alike planets chosen to test whether aerosol levels follow stellar metallicity (McGruder et al. 2023). It is predicted to have lower aerosol levels than WASP-25 b (McGruder et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp124b-McGruderetal2023","cite":"McGruder et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Chima D. McGruder, Mercedes López-Morales, James Kirk et al.","title":"ACCESS, LRG-BEASTS, and MOPSS: Featureless Optical Transmission Spectra of WASP-25b and WASP-124b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06263","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 224693 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper mainly presents a Doppler method that needs no separate star template, saving telescope time for the Next 2000 Stars (N2K) quick-look survey (Johnson et al. 2006).","observed":"The planet has 0.71 Jupiter masses and a circular orbit of 26.73 days (Johnson et al. 2006). It was found with Keck spectra using a synthetic template for the star, which reached a precision of 3 m/s over short times and 5 m/s over many runs (Johnson et al. 2006).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd224693b-Johnsonetal2006","cite":"Johnson et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"The N2K Consortium. VI. Doppler Shifts without Templates and Three New Short-Period Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0604348","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-411 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-411 hosts four planets (Sun et al. 2019). Transit timing variations gave planet c a mass of 26.4 Earth masses and a density of 1.71 g/cm3 (Sun et al. 2019). The host is an active K2 dwarf that spins in 10.52 days (Araújo & Valio 2021). Its spots were mapped as planets crossed them, and 198 spots were found (Araújo & Valio 2021). Planet c crossed spots on three occasions (Tuomi et al. 2026). The star also had 65 superflares, with energies of 10^33 to 10^35 erg (Araújo & Valio 2021).","system":"The planets have periods of 3, 7.8 and 58 days, and a non-transiting fourth planet orbits at 31.5 days and interacts strongly with the 58-day one (Sun et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler411c-Sunetal2019","cite":"Sun et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Sun, P. Ioannidis, S. Gu et al.","title":"Kepler-411: a four-planet system with an active host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.09719","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler411c-ArajoValio2021","cite":"Araújo & Valio 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alexandre Araújo, Adriana Valio","title":"Kepler-411 Differential Rotation from Three Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.07692","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler411c-Tuomietal2026","cite":"Tuomi et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mikko Tuomi, András Haris, Thomas Hackman","title":"Characterisation of starspot structure and differential rotation of Kepler-411","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6383 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star, TOI-6383 A, is an M3 dwarf with a temperature of about 3500 K (Bernabò et al. 2024). It has a giant planet with a mass of 1.04 Jupiter masses (Bernabò et al. 2024). It orbits a second M dwarf, TOI-6383 B, at a projected distance of 3126 au (Bernabò et al. 2024). The planet is large compared to its small star, which makes it a useful case for studying planet evolution (Bernabò et al. 2024).","system":"The companion star has a temperature of about 3100 K (Bernabò et al. 2024).","history":"Making such a planet needs a massive, dust-rich disc and high accretion efficiency (Bernabò et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6383ab-Bernabetal2024","cite":"Bernabò et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Lia Marta Bernabò, Shubham Kanodia, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-6383Ab, a Giant Planet Transiting an M3-dwarf Star in a Binary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16889","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-192 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a post-main-sequence G0 star, 4 to 8 billion years old (Hellier et al. 2019). It is larger than the Sun, so the transits are shallow, at 0.7 to 0.9 per cent (Hellier et al. 2019). The planet's radius is moderately inflated, at 1.2 to 1.3 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp192b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"WASP-South hot Jupiters: WASP-178b, WASP-184b, WASP-185b, and WASP-192b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5292 A b":{"text":{"why":"It comes from the MANGOS programme, which searches for giant planets, brown dwarfs and low-mass stars around M dwarfs (Dransfield et al. 2026).","observed":"Giant planets on short orbits around low-mass stars are hard to explain with core accretion models (Dransfield et al. 2026). TOI-5292 is a wide binary, and the authors were able to characterise both stars (Dransfield et al. 2026). The planet is among five in the study that suit further study of their obliquities and atmospheres (Dransfield et al. 2026).","system":"The system has two stars, which form a wide binary (Dransfield et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5292ab-Dransfieldetal2026","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Dransfield, M. Timmermans, D. Sebastian et al.","title":"MANGOS ─ II. Five new giant planets orbiting low-mass stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-157 d":{"text":{"why":"The aim was to confirm the ultra-short-period Earth-sized planet K2-157 b, which orbits in 8.8 hours, and to look for companions (Castro-González et al. 2025).","observed":"Forty-nine velocity measurements from the ESPRESSO spectrograph revealed two further planets, c and d (Castro-González et al. 2025). Planet d has a minimum mass of 23.3 Earth masses and a 66.5 day period (Castro-González et al. 2025). It lies in the warm Neptunian savanna, a region of orbits for Neptune-sized planets (Castro-González et al. 2025).","system":"Planet c has a 25.9 day period and a minimum mass of 30.8 Earth masses (Castro-González et al. 2025). The inner planet b is only about 1.1 Earth masses (Castro-González et al. 2025).","history":"Ultra-short-period rocky planets are widely thought to have migrated inward through interactions with outer neighbours (Castro-González et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2157d-CastroGonzlezetal2025","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Castro-González, F. Bouchy, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"Two neighbours of the ultra-short-period Earth-sized planet K2-157 b in the warm Neptunian savanna","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20999","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2107 b":{"text":{"why":"It belongs to the MEEP survey, which aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters around FGK stars brighter than Gaia magnitude 12.5, found and analysed in the same way (Schulte et al. 2024). Such a sample can test whether hot Jupiters formed in place, migrated through the disc, or moved by high-eccentricity tidal migration (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","observed":"The planet is one of nine new hot Jupiters from TESS, confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy (Schulte et al. 2024). The nine span 0.55 to 3.88 Jupiter masses and 0.967 to 1.438 Jupiter radii (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2107b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2107b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 18599 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a young K dwarf, 300 to 400 million years old, bright enough for detailed study (Desidera et al. 2023; Vines et al. 2023). The planet has a high density of 7.1 g/cm3 and lies at the edge of the Neptune Desert, the first young planet of its type found there (Vines et al. 2023). Structure models suggest 23 per cent water and 77 per cent rock and iron (Vines et al. 2023). Its orbit is significantly eccentric, near 0.34 (Desidera et al. 2023). Another team could only set an upper mass limit of 30.5 Earth masses, since young stars are noisy (de Leon et al. 2023).","system":"High-contrast imaging found a companion on the boundary between brown dwarfs and very low-mass stars, at about 83 Jupiter masses and a projected separation of 3.3 au (Desidera et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd18599b-Desideraetal2023","cite":"Desidera et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"S. Desidera, M. Damasso, R. Gratton et al.","title":"TOI-179: A young system with a transiting compact Neptune-mass planet and a low-mass companion in outer orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07933","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd18599b-Vinesetal2023","cite":"Vines et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jose I. Vines, James S. Jenkins, Zaira Berdiñas et al.","title":"A dense mini-Neptune orbiting the bright young star HD 18599","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07945","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd18599b-deLeonetal2023","cite":"de Leon et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. P. de Leon, J. H. Livingston, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"A sub-Neptune transiting the young field star HD 18599 at 40 pc","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08179","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"USco1621 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aimed to find analogues of gas giant planets as companions at wide separations from very young stars (Chinchilla et al. 2020).","observed":"Spectra gave it a spectral type of M8.5 and confirmed its youth and membership in the group (Chinchilla et al. 2020).","system":"It orbits an early-M star at a very wide separation, in a group about 5 to 10 million years old and about 145 pc away (Chinchilla et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-usco1621b-Chinchillaetal2020","cite":"Chinchilla et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Patricia Chinchilla, Víctor J. S. Béjar, Nicolas Lodieu et al.","title":"USco1621 B and USco1556 B: Two wide companions at the deuterium-burning mass limit in Upper Scorpius","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.04150","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 187123 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was not seen directly. A slow curve in the velocity residuals of a star known to host a planet pointed to a more distant body (Wright et al. 2007). Its minimum mass is between 3 and 7 Jupiter masses, with a period over 8 years (Wright et al. 2007). The orbit is incomplete in the data (Wright et al. 2007).","system":"The inner planet, HD 187123 b, was detected through water in its thermal emission (Buzard et al. 2020). That detection gives it a mass of 1.4 Jupiter masses (Buzard et al. 2020). The host is metal rich and nearly identical to 51 Peg (Gonzalez et al. 1999).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd187123c-Wrightetal2007","cite":"Wright et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"J. T. Wright, G. W. Marcy, D. A. Fischer et al.","title":"Four New Exoplanets and Hints of Additional Substellar Companions to Exoplanet Host Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0611658","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd187123c-Buzardetal2020","cite":"Buzard et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Cam Buzard, Luke Finnerty, Danielle Piskorz et al.","title":"Simulating the Multi-epoch Direct Detection Technique to Isolate the Thermal Emission of the Non-transiting Hot Jupiter HD187123b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.03020","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd187123c-Gonzalezetal1999","cite":"Gonzalez et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, George Wallerstein, Steven H. Saar","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. IV. 14 Herculis, HD 187123, and HD 210277","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/311847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-462 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-462 b is also called KOI-89.01 and orbits an early-type star (Masuda & Tamayo 2020). A 2015 study claimed from stellar gravity darkening that the system was strongly tilted against the star's spin (Ahlers et al. 2015). A later reanalysis found no firm evidence for gravity darkening, so the stellar obliquity is not constrained (Masuda & Tamayo 2020).","system":"A second candidate, KOI-89.02, has a 207.6 day period and transit timing variations (Masuda & Tamayo 2020). The dynamics point to low orbital eccentricities (Masuda & Tamayo 2020).","history":"","open":"Whether the planets and the star's spin are misaligned remains unsettled (Masuda & Tamayo 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler462b-MasudaTamayo2020","cite":"Masuda & Tamayo 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Kento Masuda, Daniel Tamayo","title":"Revisiting the Architecture of the KOI-89 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06850","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler462b-Ahlersetal2015","cite":"Ahlers et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"John P. Ahlers, Jason W. Barnes, Rory Barnes","title":"Spin-Orbit Misalignment of Two-Planet-System KOI-89 Via Gravity Darkening","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07930","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-68 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is about 8.3 Earth masses with a density of 3.32 g/cm3, between that of the ice giants and Earth (Gilliland et al. 2013). The host star shows strong, rich pulsation modes that allow its interior to be studied and its properties to be measured precisely (Gilliland et al. 2013). Models suggest the planet holds less than 10 per cent hydrogen and helium by mass (Valencia et al. 2013).","system":"Planet c is Earth-sized with a 9.6 day orbit, and a Jovian-mass planet d orbits every 580 days (Gilliland et al. 2013). The outer planet lies in the habitable zone, and stable orbits for an Earth-mass planet exist there, most notably near the 2:3 resonance with it (Kane 2015).","history":"Simulations show that a distant giant planet can trap the two inner planets in a near 5:3 or 7:4 resonance (Pan et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler68b-Gillilandetal2013","cite":"Gilliland et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ronald L. Gilliland, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-68: Three Planets, One with a Density between that of Earth and Ice Giants","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler68b-Valenciaetal2013","cite":"Valencia et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Diana Valencia, Tristan Guillot, Vivien Parmentier et al.","title":"Bulk Composition of GJ 1214b and Other Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2629","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler68b-Kane2015","cite":"Kane 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Stephen R. Kane","title":"Stability of Earth-mass Planets in the Kepler-68 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.02882","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler68b-Panetal2020","cite":"Pan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mengrui Pan, Su Wang, Jianghui Ji","title":"Near mean motion resonance of terrestrial planet pair induced by giant planet: application to Kepler-68 system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14506","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2257 b":{"text":{"why":"Few long-period planets are known around M dwarfs, so this one offers clues about how such systems form and evolve (Schanche et al. 2022).","observed":"The long transit duration suggests a very eccentric orbit of about 0.5, which would make it the most eccentric planet known to transit an M dwarf (Schanche et al. 2022). The transit is deep enough to be seen with medium-size ground-based telescopes (Schanche et al. 2022). Simulations place the planet near the orbital distance beyond which tidal effects and atmospheric escape become negligible (Olohoy et al. 2026). If rocky, it could lose about 10 Earth ocean masses of water early on, and tidal heating would be similar to Io's (Olohoy et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"It is not known whether it is a mini-Neptune or a rocky super-Earth (Olohoy et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2257b-Schancheetal2022","cite":"Schanche et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"N. Schanche, F. J. Pozuelos, M. N. Günther et al.","title":"TOI-2257 b: A highly eccentric long-period sub-Neptune transiting a nearby M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01749","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2257b-Olohoyetal2026","cite":"Olohoy et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Michael Kent A. Olohoy, Yukari Aubrey D. Gelani, Rory Barnes","title":"The Limits of Tidal Circularization and Atmospheric Escape for Mini-Neptunes and Super-Earths: The Case of TOI-2257 b","journal":"The Planetary Science Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19280","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-140 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transit is grazing, so the planet's radius is uncertain (Hellier et al. 2017). The orbit is slightly eccentric, with e = 0.047 (Hellier et al. 2017). The host's 10.4 day spin suggests a young star, and the time for tidal circularisation is likely the shortest of any known eccentric hot Jupiter (Hellier et al. 2017). The orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Zak et al. 2025). No timing variations or additional planets have been found (North & Banks 2022; Maciejewski 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp140b-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp140b-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquities of eight close-in gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.05615","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp140b-NorthBanks2022","cite":"North & Banks 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. North, T. Banks","title":"Photometry and Transit Modeling of Exoplanet WASP-140b","journal":"Journal of the American Association of Variable Star Observers (JAAVSO)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10582","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp140b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5789 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This sub-Neptune orbits a bright, magnetically quiet K1 dwarf and was characterised with 141 velocity measurements from HARPS-N (Bonomo et al. 2026). Its radius is 2.86 Earth radii, its mass 5.00 Earth masses and its density 1.16 g/cm3 (Bonomo et al. 2026). The host star is bright, so the planet is a promising target for atmospheric study with JWST and later Ariel (Bonomo et al. 2026).","system":"The system has several planets whose orbits are inclined to one another (Bonomo et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether sub-Neptunes are gas dwarfs or water worlds is unclear, and an atmosphere measurement could settle it (Bonomo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5789c-Bonomoetal2026","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, L. Naponiello, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXI. A sub-Neptune suitable for atmospheric characterization in a multiplanet and mutually inclined system orbiting the bright K dwarf TOI-5789 (HIP 99452)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-216.02":{"text":{"why":"The system is in or near 2:1 orbital resonance, which suggests a gentle history of formation in place or migration in the disc (Dawson et al. 2019).","observed":"This planet, TOI-216 b, was first seen only in grazing transits, so its size was unknown (McKee & Montet 2023). Its orbit has since precessed, and it now transits fully (McKee & Montet 2023). The radius is about 7.84 Earth radii and the density 0.201 g/cm3, making it a super-puff (McKee & Montet 2023).","system":"It is in 2:1 resonance with TOI-216 c, a warm Jupiter (Dawson et al. 2021). Its orbit is eccentric, and the pair have a small mutual inclination (Dawson et al. 2021).","history":"Simulations suggest the inner planet's migration halted near its current orbit while the outer one moved in to the resonance (Nesvorný et al. 2022). Super-puffs in resonance point to smooth disc migration (McKee & Montet 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi21602-Dawsonetal2019","cite":"Dawson et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Rebekah I. Dawson, Chelsea X. Huang, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"TOI-216b and TOI-216 c: Two Warm, Large Exoplanets in or Slightly Wide of the 2:1 Orbital Resonance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.11852","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi21602-McKeeMontet2023","cite":"McKee & Montet 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Brendan J. McKee, Benjamin T. Montet","title":"Transit Depth Variations Reveal TOI-216 b to be a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07450","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi21602-Dawsonetal2021","cite":"Dawson et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Rebekah I. Dawson, Chelsea X. Huang, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"Precise Transit and Radial-velocity Characterization of a Resonant Pair: The Warm Jupiter TOI-216c and Eccentric Warm Neptune TOI-216b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06754","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi21602-Nesvornetal2022","cite":"Nesvorný et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"David Nesvorný, Ondřej Chrenko, Mario Flock","title":"TOI-216: Resonant Constraints on Planet Migration","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09577","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 111232 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-mass, Jupiter-like body on an eccentric orbit of long period (Minniti et al. 2009). The host stars are nearby and have low chromospheric activity (Minniti et al. 2009). The companion had been reported earlier by the CORALIE survey (Minniti et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd111232b-Minnitietal2009","cite":"Minniti et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Dante Minniti, R. Paul Butler, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"Low-Mass Companions for Five Solar-Type Stars From the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.5348","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1000 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this planet so far is theoretical, not new observations. They ask whether the planet could keep a moon for a long time. Calculations of how a moon's orbit would slowly change found that the long-term motion of possible moons of Kepler-1000 b is stable (Melnikov 2023). The same work estimated which orbits such moons could have (Melnikov 2022). No moon has been detected, so these are only possible companions.","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has a moon at all is not known (Melnikov 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1000b-Melnikov2023","cite":"Melnikov 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. V. Melnikov","title":"Secular Orbital Dynamics of Exoplanet Satellite Candidates","journal":"Solar System Research","url":"https://doi.org/10.1134/S0038094623030061","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1000b-Melnikov2022","cite":"Melnikov 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. V. Melnikov","title":"On the orbital stability of exoplanet satellites","journal":"Publications of the Pulkovo Observatory","url":"https://doi.org/10.31725/0367-7966-2022-227-8","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-2 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star has starspots, dark patches on its surface, and these have been used to learn about the system. Two transit light curves taken in four colours at once both contained anomalies that match the planet crossing starspots along the same path (Mohler-Fischer et al. 2013). A later study combined transits spanning more than ten years, from ground telescopes and the TESS space telescope. Anomalies showed up in most of the light curves and were modelled as spots the planet passed over (Biagiotti et al. 2024). Several sets of spot crossings happened within five days of each other (Biagiotti et al. 2024).","system":"The star is a K-type dwarf that shows starspot activity (Mohler-Fischer et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"The later study also set out to test how the orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin, and to look for shifts in transit times and for atmospheric signs (Biagiotti et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats2b-MohlerFischeretal2013","cite":"Mohler-Fischer et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Mohler-Fischer, L. Mancini, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-2b: A transiting extrasolar planet orbiting a K-type star showing starspot activity","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.2140","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats2b-Biagiottietal2024","cite":"Biagiotti et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Biagiotti, L. Mancini, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Star-spot activity, orbital obliquity, transmission spectrum, physical properties, and transit time variations of the HATS-2 planetary system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.04476","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":6,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11964 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A statistical re-analysis of earlier velocity measurements tested models with one to four planets. The three-planet model came out at least 600 times more probable than the next best, a two-planet model (Gregory 2007). In that model a signal near 38 days, the one that matches this planet, has a slightly eccentric orbit (Gregory 2007).","system":"The best model has three signals with periods of about 38, 360 and 1924 days. If all are planets, their minimum masses are about 0.09, 0.21 and 0.77 Jupiter masses (Gregory 2007).","history":"","open":"The 360-day signal is close to one year, which suggests the correction for Earth's own motion in the data should be checked (Gregory 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11964c-Gregory2007","cite":"Gregory 2007","year":"2007","authors":"P. C. Gregory","title":"A Bayesian periodogram finds evidence for three planets in HD 11964","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.0970","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1749 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS watched the star in 12 sectors, and ground telescopes followed up with transit photometry in several colours, adaptive optics imaging and low-resolution spectroscopy to confirm the planets (Fukui et al. 2021). Variations in the times of transits gave only an upper limit on the planet's mass, 57 Earth masses at 95% confidence (Fukui et al. 2021).","system":"Three planets orbit this early M dwarf, with periods of 2.39, 4.49 and 9.05 days. The outer pair has a period ratio within 1% of 2, as in TOI-175 and TOI-270 (Fukui et al. 2021).","history":"The sizes and periods fit the idea that all three planets began with a hydrogen envelope on a rocky core, and only the innermost lost its envelope, by photoevaporation or core-powered mass loss (Fukui et al. 2021).","open":"The masses are still only upper limits (Fukui et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1749b-Fukuietal2021","cite":"Fukui et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Fukui, J. Korth, J. H. Livingston et al.","title":"TOI-1749: an M dwarf with a Trio of Planets including a Near-resonant Pair","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 2952 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper reports three planets around evolved stars of intermediate mass, all found in one radial-velocity programme (Sato et al. 2013).","observed":"The star is an evolved giant of spectral type K0 III with a mass of 2.5 Suns, and the planet has a minimum mass of 1.6 Jupiter masses on a nearly circular orbit (Sato et al. 2013). The same paper showed that the jitter from a giant star's own oscillations can be averaged out to a few metres per second over a week of frequent observations. This makes smaller planets detectable around such stars (Sato et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd2952b-Satoetal2013","cite":"Sato et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya, Hiroki Harakawa et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to Three Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: HD 2952, HD 120084, and ω Serpentis","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4328","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2017-BLG-1038L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was noticed as a short distortion in a lensing event, which gave a planet-to-star mass ratio of about 5.3 thousandths. Turning that ratio into masses needs a model of the Galaxy, assuming the host is not a stellar remnant. The result is a planet of about 2 Jupiter masses orbiting a mid-M dwarf of about 0.37 Suns, some 6 kiloparsecs away toward the Galactic bulge (Shin et al. 2019).","system":"The planet is probably gaseous and lies beyond its host star's snow line (Shin et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The masses and distance come from a statistical model, not direct measurement, so they are uncertain by a factor of about two (Shin et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2017blg1038lb-Shinetal2019","cite":"Shin et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"I.-G. Shin, Y.-H. Ryu, J. C. Yee et al.","title":"Two Jupiter-mass Planets Discovered by the KMTNet Survey in 2017","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12505","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1439 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a dedicated survey of planets around subgiant stars, which are stars just past the main sequence (Chontos et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is one of 12 transiting planets confirmed with about 16 nights of Keck/HIRES radial-velocity measurements (Chontos et al. 2024). The survey also looked for outer companions in the 21 subgiant systems and found that at least 19% have them, a rate comparable to main-sequence stars (Chontos et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1439b-Chontosetal2024","cite":"Chontos et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ashley Chontos, Daniel Huber, Samuel K. Grunblatt et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey XXI: 13 New Planets and Homogeneous Properties for 21 Subgiant Systems","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07893","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 2039 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up added 20 faint, metal-enriched stars to its main sample in 1999, chosen from their colours in Stromgren photometry (Tinney et al. 2003).","observed":"Its orbit is elliptical, not circular (Tinney et al. 2003). Heavy-element-rich stars appear to host planets more often. At least 25% of metal-rich stars in this sample seem to have planets within 2.5 au, compared with about 8% of stars within 3.5 au in the main sample (McCarthy et al. 2003).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd2039b-Tinneyetal2003","cite":"Tinney et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Four New Planets Orbiting Metal-enriched Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207128","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd2039b-McCarthyetal2003","cite":"McCarthy et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"C. McCarthy, C. Tinney, H. Jones et al.","title":"Recent Discoveries from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search","journal":"Scientific Frontiers in Research on Extrasolar Planets","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5388.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The TESS candidate was confirmed with ground-based photometry and radial-velocity follow-up with CARMENES. The planet is Earth-sized, with a radius of about 0.99 Earth radii, but the velocity data give only an upper limit on its mass of 2.2 Earth masses and on its density of 2.2 times Earth's (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). Given its Earth-like radius, it is most probably rocky (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026).","system":"The host is an M3.0 dwarf, Wolf 346 (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The mass is only an upper limit, so the planet's exact composition is not pinned down (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi538801-Poultourtzidisetal2026","cite":"Poultourtzidis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. Poultourtzidis, G. Lacedelli, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Characterization of two new transiting sub-Neptunes and a terrestrial planet around M-dwarf hosts","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07414","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 149143 b":{"text":{"why":"Two surveys aimed at metal-rich stars both reached this star, because planets are more common around them (da Silva et al. 2006; Fischer et al. 2005).","observed":"Keck velocities gave an orbital period of 4.072 days and a minimum mass of 1.33 Jupiter masses (Fischer et al. 2006). Brightness monitoring showed no transits down to about 0.02%, ruling out a transiting planet and limiting the tilt of the orbit to under 83 degrees (Fischer et al. 2006). An independent set of ELODIE velocities gave a similar orbit, with a marginally significant eccentricity of 0.08 (da Silva et al. 2006).","system":"The host is a metal-rich, slightly evolved G0 star with a radius of about 1.49 Suns. It shows no chromospheric activity, so its outer atmosphere is quiet (Fischer et al. 2006).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd149143b-daSilvaetal2006","cite":"da Silva et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"R. da Silva, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"Elodie metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. I. Two Hot Jupiters orbiting the slightly evolved stars <ASTROBJ>HD 118203</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 149143</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd149143b-Fischeretal2005","cite":"Fischer et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"D. A. Fischer, G. W. Marcy, R. P. Butler et al.","title":"N2K: A Targeted Search for Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd149143b-Fischeretal2006","cite":"Fischer et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Gregory Laughlin, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The N2K Consortium. III. Short-Period Planets Orbiting HD 149143 and HD 109749","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/498557","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2018-BLG-0516L b":{"text":{"why":"It came from a systematic search of KMTNet 2018-2019 light curves with an automated algorithm called AnomalyFinder (Hwang et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet's signal is a subtle dip in the light curve, produced as the background source star crosses the line joining host and planet, on the side opposite the planet. Such dips were missed in earlier searches done by eye (Hwang et al. 2022). The planet-to-host mass ratio is below 2 in 10,000, which makes it one of 11 low-mass-ratio planets found in the search (Hwang et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2018blg0516lb-Hwangetal2022","cite":"Hwang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Kyu-Ha Hwang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. II. Six New q < 2 × 10<SUP>-4</SUP> Mass-ratio Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06686","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-107 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The study studied the two innermost planets of this compact, near-resonant system, Kepler-107 b and c, not planet e itself. It found they have nearly identical radii of about 1.5 to 1.6 Earth radii. Even so, c is more than twice as dense as b, at about 12.6 and 5.3 grams per cubic centimetre (Bonomo et al. 2019).","system":"The two inner planets must differ in iron content, since c needs a larger iron core fraction than b (Bonomo et al. 2019).","history":"The authors conclude that a giant collision between planets is the likely origin of the difference, because X-ray and ultraviolet heating from the star cannot explain it (Bonomo et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler107e-Bonomoetal2019","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Aldo S. Bonomo, Li Zeng, Mario Damasso et al.","title":"A giant impact as the likely origin of different twins in the Kepler-107 exoplanet system","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.01316","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1075 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of 13 hot, potentially rocky TESS planets validated as good targets for James Webb Space Telescope emission spectroscopy (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"Radial velocities from the Magellan II telescope gave the planet a mass of 9.95 Earth masses. With its radius this means a density of about 9.3 grams per cubic centimetre, almost twice Earth's (Essack et al. 2023). The planet's size puts it near the radius gap, so it would be expected to hold a hydrogen and helium atmosphere, but its high density likely rules that out (Essack et al. 2023). It circles its star every 14.5 hours (Essack et al. 2023).","system":"The velocity data show a long-term trend. Preliminary results point to a long-period sub-Saturn planet, TOI-1075 c, in the system (Essack et al. 2024).","history":"The authors say the planet defies current planet formation and evolution models and explore ways it could have formed (Essack et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1075b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1075b-Essacketal2023","cite":"Essack et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Zahra Essack, Avi Shporer, Jennifer A. Burt et al.","title":"TOI-1075 b: A Dense, Massive, Ultra-short-period Hot Super-Earth Straddling the Radius Gap","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14901","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1075b-Essacketal2024","cite":"Essack et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Zahra Essack, Avi Shporer, Jennifer A. Burt et al.","title":"TOI-1075 b: A Dense, Massive, Ultra-short-period Hot Super-Earth Straddling the Radius Gap","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14901","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6002 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was validated, not weighed, using TESS data, ground-based photometry in many colours and Keck/NIRC2 adaptive optics imaging. It receives about 1.8 times the sunlight Earth does, and it lies near the inner edge of its star's habitable zone (Ghachoui et al. 2024). Its size puts it near the radius valley, a range of sizes that separates two kinds of small planets (Ghachoui et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a mid-M dwarf about 32 parsecs away (Ghachoui et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Both planets in the study are described as targets for future studies of how such planets form (Ghachoui et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6002b-Ghachouietal2024","cite":"Ghachoui et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Ghachoui, B. V. Rackham, M. Dévora-Pajares et al.","title":"TESS discovery of two super-Earths orbiting the M-dwarf stars TOI-6002 and TOI-5713 near the radius valley","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00709","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 87816 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, CASCADES, began in 2006 to learn how common planets are around intermediate-mass stars, by watching them after they leave the main sequence (Fontanet et al. 2025).","observed":"The study reports planets around giant stars of intermediate mass. It used CORALIE radial velocities from the Euler telescope at La Silla, collected over more than ten years for each target (Fontanet et al. 2025). Only around 200 planets were known around giant stars at that time, so each addition helps (Fontanet et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd87816b-Fontanetetal2025","cite":"Fontanet et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Fontanet, S. Udry, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES): IV. New planetary systems around HD 87816, HD 94890, and HD 102888 and an update on HD 121056","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-138 c":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper was a search for moons around eight Kepler planet candidates orbiting M dwarfs. It found no compelling evidence for a moon in any of them (Kipping et al. 2014).","observed":"Small shifts in transit times, caused by the planets pulling on each other, were used to weigh the planets of Kepler-138. This gave the first density measurement for an exoplanet smaller than Earth, the Mars-sized inner planet. The middle planet, c, has a density similar to Earth's, while the outer planet is less than half as dense (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015). A later model fitted the light curves directly and made the densities about twice as precise (Almenara et al. 2018).","system":"The star is a cool M dwarf with slightly sub-solar metals (Souto et al. 2017). At least three planets smaller than 2 Earth radii orbit it. The outer planet may be a water world, since its low density and flat transmission spectrum fit that without a hydrogen atmosphere (Piaulet et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler138c-Kippingetal2014","cite":"Kipping et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. M. Kipping, D. Nesvorný, L. A. Buchhave et al.","title":"The Hunt for Exomoons with Kepler (HEK). IV. A Search for Moons around Eight M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1210","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138c-JontofHutteretal2015","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jason F. Rowe, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"The mass of the Mars-sized exoplanet Kepler-138 b from transit timing","journal":"Nature","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.07067","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138c-Almenaraetal2018","cite":"Almenara et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. M. Almenara, R. F. Díaz, C. Dorn et al.","title":"Absolute densities in exoplanetary systems: photodynamical modelling of Kepler-138","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.12520","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138c-Soutoetal2017","cite":"Souto et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Souto, K. Cunha, D. A. García-Hernández et al.","title":"Chemical Abundances of M-dwarfs from the APOGEE Survey. I. The Exoplanet Hosting Stars Kepler-138 and Kepler-186","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01598","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138c-Piauletetal2021","cite":"Piaulet et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Piaulet, B. Benneke, J. Almenara et al.","title":"Spectroscopic Characterization of Kepler-138d: a temperate Earth-mass exoplanet that might be a water world","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Barnard b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A set of 156 ESPRESSO velocity measurements over four years was modelled with a Gaussian process to remove stellar activity. That analysis found a long activity cycle of about 3200 days and a rotation signal at 140 days. It then revealed a 3.15-day signal, confirmed as a sub-Earth-mass planet with a velocity semi-amplitude of only 55 centimetres per second (Gonzalez Hernandez et al. 2024).","system":"Three other short-period candidate signals appear, at 4.12, 2.34 and 6.74 days (Gonzalez Hernandez et al. 2024).","history":"The name Barnard b was first given to a candidate at 233 days. The new data do not support that candidate (Gonzalez Hernandez et al. 2024). Another team argued it was stellar activity, an alias of the 145-day rotation period (Lubin et al. 2021).","open":"The other short-period signals still need to be confirmed (Gonzalez Hernandez et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-barnardb-Hernandezetal2024","cite":"Hernandez et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. I. González Hernández, A. Suárez Mascareño, A. M. Silva et al.","title":"A sub-Earth-mass planet orbiting Barnard's star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00569","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-barnardb-Lubinetal2021","cite":"Lubin et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jack Lubin, Paul Robertson, Gudmundur Stefansson et al.","title":"Stellar Activity Manifesting at a One-year Alias Explains Barnard b as a False Positive","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ac0057","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":61,"about":59,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-444 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a metal-poor, Sun-like star from the old thick-disc population of the Galaxy. Asteroseismology, which reads a star's age from its oscillations, gave 11.2 billion years. This makes the five planets, with sizes between Mercury and Venus, the oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets (Campante et al. 2015). Transit-timing analysis found that planets d and e are Mars-mass (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","system":"The planets orbit one star of a triple system. The other two stars are M dwarfs about 66 au away in projection, on a very eccentric orbit that brings them within about 5 au of the planets. The system is dynamically stable (Dupuy et al. 2016).","history":"The timing of the planets suggests smooth migration through the disc alone is unlikely to explain their orbits (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). The tight M dwarf pair may have truncated the disc the planets formed in (Dupuy et al. 2016).","open":"Variations seen in the star's hydrogen Lyman-alpha light raise the question of escaping atmospheres. One model finds no need for an unusually active star to explain them (Pezzotti et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler444f-Campanteetal2015","cite":"Campante et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"T. L. Campante, T. Barclay, J. J. Swift et al.","title":"KOI-3158: The oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07869","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444f-MillsFabrycky2017","cite":"Mills & Fabrycky 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky","title":"Mass, Density, and Formation Constraints in the Compact, Sub-Earth Kepler-444 System including Two Mars-mass Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03417","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444f-Dupuyetal2016","cite":"Dupuy et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Trent J. Dupuy, Kaitlin M. Kratter, Adam L. Kraus et al.","title":"Orbital Architectures of Planet-hosting Binaries. I. Forming Five Small Planets in the Truncated Disk of Kepler-444A","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03428","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler444f-Pezzottietal2021","cite":"Pezzotti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Pezzotti, P. Eggenberger, G. Buldgen et al.","title":"Revisiting Kepler-444. II. Rotational, orbital, and high-energy fluxes evolution of the system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.06061","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":18,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V1298 Tau c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A full transit was observed with the GRACES spectrograph on Gemini North. The planet's shadow on the star gave a projected obliquity of 5 degrees, plus or minus 15, meaning the orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Feinstein et al. 2021). The star's hydrogen-alpha absorption fell smoothly during the transit, which could be hot gas escaping. It is also consistent with similar changes seen in young stars with no planets, and with starspots (Feinstein et al. 2021). X-ray observations with Chandra show the star is bright in X-rays (Poppenhaeger et al. 2021).","system":"Four planets orbit a young star of about 23 million years. Planets c and d lie 0.25% outside a 3:2 orbital resonance, and all are far larger than most Kepler planets (David et al. 2019).","history":"The system may be a precursor to the compact multiplanet systems that Kepler found to be common (David et al. 2019).","open":"Mass estimates are disputed. A study found that earlier velocity masses were unreliable because of overfitting (Blunt et al. 2023), and a newer one finds the masses depend on the analysis method (Damasso et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v1298tauc-Feinsteinetal2021","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Benjamin T. Montet, Marshall C. Johnson et al.","title":"H-alpha and Ca II Infrared Triplet Variations During a Transit of the 23 Myr Planet V1298 Tau c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01213","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298tauc-Poppenhaegeretal2021","cite":"Poppenhaeger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"K. Poppenhaeger, L. Ketzer, M. Mallonn","title":"X-ray irradiation and evaporation of the four young planets around V1298 Tau","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.10240","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298tauc-Davidetal2019","cite":"David et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Trevor J. David, Erik A. Petigura, Rodrigo Luger et al.","title":"Four Newborn Planets Transiting the Young Solar Analog V1298 Tau","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.04563","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298tauc-Bluntetal2023","cite":"Blunt et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Sarah Blunt, Adolfo Carvalho, Trevor J. David et al.","title":"Overfitting Affects the Reliability of Radial Velocity Mass Estimates of the V1298 Tau Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08145","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298tauc-Damassoetal2026","cite":"Damasso et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. Damasso, P. Leonardi, L. Borsato et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXIX. New mass constraints for the infant planets in the V1298 Tau system through a long-term radial-velocity monitoring with HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.30038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":53,"about":46,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocities from Keck/HIRES and the APF telescope over 415 days revealed a planet that does not transit, with a minimum mass of about 0.34 Jupiter masses and a period near 101 days. Its gravitational pull on the inner transiting planets shows it orbits in the same plane as they do, within 10 degrees (Lubin et al. 2022).","system":"Three sub-Neptunes of 3 to 3.4 Earth radii transit the star, with periods of 9, 29 and 38 days. A further planet f with 2 to 11 Jupiter masses lies far out. Later work added a planet g near 280 days, close to a 1:3 resonance with planet e (Lubin et al. 2022; Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Planet f's mass is only known within a wide range, from combined velocities and Gaia and Hipparcos astrometry (Lubin et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939e-Lubinetal2022","cite":"Lubin et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jack Lubin, Judah Van Zandt, Rae Holcomb et al.","title":"TESS-Keck Survey. IX. Masses of Three Sub-Neptunes Orbiting HD 191939 and the Discovery of a Warm Jovian plus a Distant Substellar Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02208","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939e-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3512 c":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets in orbits shorter than 1000 days are rarer around late-type stars than around Sun-like ones. This agrees with core and pebble accretion models of planet formation (Morales et al. 2025).","observed":"Radial velocities fitted with several Keplerian orbits constrained two giant gas planets around this tiny star (Lopez-Santiago et al. 2020). Later CARMENES and IRD data pinned down the orbit and minimum mass of planet c, where only lower limits existed before (Morales et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an M5.5 dwarf of intermediate age, 2 to 8 billion years. It shows a likely magnetic activity cycle, and a long-lasting 70 to 100 minute brightness oscillation, probably from its corona (Lopez-Santiago et al. 2020; Lopez-Santiago et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3512c-Moralesetal2025","cite":"Morales et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. C. Morales, I. Ribas, S. Reffert et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs: Revisiting the GJ 317, GJ463, and GJ 3512 systems and two newly discovered planets orbiting GJ9773 and GJ 508.2","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15516","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3512c-LopezSantiagoetal2020","cite":"Lopez-Santiago et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Javier Lopez-Santiago, Luca Martino, Joaquín Míguez et al.","title":"A Likely Magnetic Activity Cycle for the Exoplanet Host M Dwarf GJ 3512","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07715","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3512c-LopezSantiagoetal2026","cite":"Lopez-Santiago et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. López-Santiago, F. Reale, G. Micela et al.","title":"A recurrent 70─100 min quasi-periodic pulsation in the intermediate-aged mid-M dwarf GJ 3512","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18086","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-93 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The system has enough angular momentum for the planet to be tidally migrating outwards if the orbit is near alignment with the star's spin. This is the opposite of most hot Jupiters (Hay et al. 2016). A search found no significant timing variations, so no hidden planet is detected (Gajdos et al. 2019). Observations in the metastable helium line found tentative signs of an escaping atmosphere (Saidel et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a hot F4 star (Hay et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The helium detection is only tentative, above 2 sigma (Saidel et al. 2026). A discrepancy in the planet's radius was resolved with TESS data (Alexoudi 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp93b-Hayetal2016","cite":"Hay et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"K. L. Hay, A. Collier-Cameron, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"WASP-92b, WASP-93b and WASP-118b: three new transiting close-in giant planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00774","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp93b-Gajdosetal2019","cite":"Gajdos et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Pavol Gajdoš, Martin Vaňko, Marián Jakubík et al.","title":"WASP-92, WASP-93, and WASP-118: transit timing variations and long-term stability of the systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02804","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp93b-Saideletal2026","cite":"Saidel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Morgan Saidel, Shreyas Vissapragada, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"The First Dedicated Survey of Atmospheric Escape from Planets Orbiting F Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05240","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp93b-Alexoudi2022","cite":"Alexoudi 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Xanthippi Alexoudi","title":"On the parameter refinement of inflated exoplanets with large radius uncertainty based on TESS observations","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02133","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-323 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck/HIRES gave 79 new velocity measurements, combined with 48 from HARPS-N. Planet b has a mass of about 2.0 Earth masses and c about 6.5, and both are consistent with rocky compositions (Thomas et al. 2024). The study compared them with the more massive, gas-wrapped planets of the Kepler-104 system (Thomas et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Planet b's mass is uncertain by more than half its value (Thomas et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler323b-Thomasetal2024","cite":"Thomas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Alexander Thomas, Lauren M. Weiss, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"A Tale of Two Peas in a Pod: The Kepler-323 and Kepler-104 Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13386","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HN Lib b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HN Lib was monitored with the CARMENES spectrograph on the 3.5 m telescope at Calar Alto Observatory in Spain. The instrument records high-resolution spectra in both visible and near-infrared light. Between January 2016 and December 2020 it gathered 94 radial-velocity measurements, each a 1800-second exposure, the longest used for precise measurements with this instrument (Gonzalez-Alvarez et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hnlibb-GonzalezAlvarezetal2023","cite":"Gonzalez-Alvarez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Gonzalez-Alvarez, J. Kemmer, P. Chaturvedi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HN Lib radial velocities (Gonzalez-Alvarez+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1520 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-1520 b is being disintegrated by its host star. Dust and debris pulled from its surface form tails that trail and precede the planet in its orbit, which makes it hard to tell what the solid body underneath is really like (Baka & Schlawin 2021). Earlier studies tried to find the true size by picking out shallow transits. A newer approach models the starlight noise and the changing dust signal together, to place a limit on the size of the underlying object (Baka & Schlawin 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true radius of the solid body under the dust is still hard to pin down (Baka & Schlawin 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1520b-BakaSchlawin2021","cite":"Baka & Schlawin 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Keith Baka, Everett Schlawin","title":"Disintegrating Exoplanets: Creating Size Constraints by Statistically Peering Through the Debris","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-32 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is a typical red dwarf, the most common kind of star in the Galaxy, so the system serves as a model for many others (Swift et al. 2013).","observed":"Kepler-32 has five transiting planets, the richest such system known around a red dwarf. All five orbit within a distance one-third the size of Mercury's orbit, and the innermost sits only 4.3 stellar radii from the star's surface (Swift et al. 2013). New observations ruled out false-positive explanations for the signals, which allowed the whole system to be validated (Swift et al. 2013).","system":"Three neighbouring planets have orbital periods close to simple ratios (Swift et al. 2013).","history":"Arguments based on the distance at which dust would evaporate, on a minimum-mass disc of gas and dust, and on the near-ratio periods of three planets were used to propose how the system formed (Swift et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler32b-Swiftetal2013","cite":"Swift et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jonathan J. Swift, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. IV. Kepler-32 as a Prototype for the Formation of Compact Planetary Systems throughout the Galaxy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4559 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-4559 b is one of eight planets confirmed together by statistical means rather than by measuring the star's wobble. Ground-based telescope data, high-resolution imaging and the statistical tool TRICERATOPS were combined to show that the transit signals come from planets (Mistry et al. 2024). The planet is a super-Earth about 1.42 times the radius of Earth in that study (Mistry et al. 2024). Model spectra of the eight planets were also computed for the Hubble and James Webb space telescopes (Mistry et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4559b-Mistryetal2024","cite":"Mistry et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Aniket Prasad, Mousam Maity et al.","title":"VaTEST III: Validation of eight potential super-earths from TESS data","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-387 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was confirmed in a project to find promising targets for further study, and it is among those suited to transmission spectroscopy, which probes a planet's atmosphere using starlight filtered through it (Christiansen et al. 2022).","observed":"K2-387 b was confirmed together with 59 other planets by analysing reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution images, shifts in the position of the star's light centre during transits, and the statistical likelihood of the signals (Christiansen et al. 2022). Of the 91 candidates examined, 60 were validated as planets in 46 systems (Christiansen et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2387b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-42 b":{"text":{"why":"Its star is relatively bright, which makes further follow-up studies easier (Boisse et al. 2013).","observed":"HAT-P-42 b is described as an inflated hot Jupiter on a circular orbit around an early-G or late-F main-sequence star (Boisse et al. 2013). The discovery added to the sample of planets with measured densities, which are needed to test theories of planetary interiors and atmospheres (Boisse et al. 2013). A later search of multi-sector TESS photometry found no additional transiting planets in this system down to 2 to 4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"No other planet has been seen in the system. The TESS search covered transiting companions only (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp42b-Boisseetal2013","cite":"Boisse et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"I. Boisse, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-42b and HAT-P-43b. Two inflated transiting hot Jupiters from the HATNet Survey","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.6448","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp42b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3278 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a programme of follow-up on TESS giant-planet candidates around Sun-like stars, using the University of Tasmania Greenhill Observatory (Plunkett et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-3278 b, also known as HATS-78 b, is a hot Saturn-mass planet of about 0.30 Jupiter masses with a highly inflated atmosphere and a low density of 0.21 grams per cubic centimetre (Plunkett et al. 2026). Its host is a thin-disc G dwarf with roughly solar metallicity (Plunkett et al. 2026). Ground-based photometry, reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging and radial velocities were used to rule out false positives and measure the mass (Plunkett et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3278b-Plunkettetal2026","cite":"Plunkett et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Thomas Plunkett, Erica Thygesen, Andrew A. Cole et al.","title":"The TASSIE program II: Three close-in companions orbiting sun-like stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05500","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 45652 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a planet search biased toward metal-rich stars, and it was the seventh giant planet found in that programme (Santos et al. 2008).","observed":"HD 45652 b has about 0.47 Jupiter masses and follows an eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.39 (Santos et al. 2008). Its host star is metal-rich (Santos et al. 2008). Its 44-day period puts it in the middle of the gap in the period distribution of known extrasolar planets (Santos et al. 2008). Higher-precision velocities from the CORALIE and SOPHIE spectrographs confirmed the signal (Santos et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd45652b-Santosetal2008","cite":"Santos et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"N. C. Santos, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"ELODIE metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. V. An intermediate-period Jovian planet orbiting HD 45652","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.1019","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1743 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1743 b orbits a mid-type red dwarf every 4.27 days and has a radius of about 1.83 Earth radii (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025). Its equilibrium temperature, the temperature set by starlight alone, is about 485 K (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025). The planet was confirmed using a statistical validation method, multi-colour light curves and other supporting observations, not by measuring its mass directly (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025). It was announced alongside three other planets around red dwarfs (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1743b-Yalinkayaetal2025","cite":"Yalçinkaya et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Yalçinkaya, K. Barkaoui, Ö. Baștürk et al.","title":"TOI-1743 b, TOI-5799 b, TOI-5799 c, and TOI-6223 b: TESS discovery and validation of four super-Earth to Neptune-sized planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-66 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-66 b is a sub-Neptune with a mass of 21.3 Earth masses, measured with radial velocities, K2 photometry and adaptive-optics imaging (Sinukoff et al. 2017). The star is evolving up the subgiant branch, so the planet receives about twice the starlight a planet around a main-sequence star would (Sinukoff et al. 2017). Its mass and radius imply an envelope of gas of less than 5 percent of the planet, and perhaps none at all, making it one of the largest planets without a significant envelope (Sinukoff et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet may lie inside the photoevaporation desert, a range of size and starlight where almost no planets are found (Sinukoff et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k266b-Sinukoffetal2017","cite":"Sinukoff et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Evan Sinukoff, Andrew W. Howard, Erik A. Petigura et al.","title":"K2-66b and K2-106b: Two Extremely Hot Sub-Neptune-size Planets with High Densities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.03491","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-784 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits at the lower edge of the radius valley, a dip in the number of planets between about 1.5 and 2 Earth radii, which matches predictions from photoevaporation or core-powered mass loss, two ways that planets lose gas (Hua et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-784 b has a mass of 9.67 Earth masses from radial velocities and a radius of 1.93 Earth radii, giving a density of about 7.4 grams per cubic centimetre (Hua et al. 2023). Although its size matches a sub-Neptune, the density suggests a rocky planet (Hua et al. 2023).","system":"A second planet candidate probably orbits farther out and probably does not transit. Its period is between about 20 and 63 days, and its minimum mass is between 12.6 and 31.1 Earth masses (Hua et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The outer candidate is not yet confirmed (Hua et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi784b-Huaetal2023","cite":"Hua et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Xinyan Hua, Sharon Xuesong Wang, Johanna K. Teske et al.","title":"A Transiting Super-Earth in the Radius Valley and an Outer Planet Candidate Around HD 307842","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14655","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-211 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from about twenty promising transit candidates in a 2005 survey campaign, which were checked spectroscopically with the VLT/FLAMES instrument (Udalski et al. 2008).","observed":"OGLE-TR-211 b orbits an F7-8 dwarf star. Its radial velocity varies by only 82 metres per second in step with the transits, and the planetary nature was confirmed with further photometry and high-resolution spectra (Udalski et al. 2008). The planet is about 20 percent larger than typical hot Jupiters of similar mass (Udalski et al. 2008). It was one of a small group of only seven known inflated planets, with large radii and unusually low densities, which are a challenge to models of exoplanets (Udalski et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr211b-Udalskietal2008","cite":"Udalski et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"A. Udalski, F. Pont, D. Naef et al.","title":"OGLE-TR-211 - a new transiting inflated hot Jupiter from the OGLE survey and ESO LP666 spectroscopic follow-up program","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.3978","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 107148 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 107148 hosts two planets, confirmed by an independent Doppler analysis using HIRES, HARPS and CARMENES velocities (Eberhardt et al. 2022). The analysis found no link between the velocity signals and stellar activity (Eberhardt et al. 2022). Planet b is a Saturn-mass planet with a period of about 77.2 days in that fit (Eberhardt et al. 2022).","system":"A second planet, c, is Neptune-mass on an eccentric orbit of about 18.3 days, with an eccentricity near 0.4. The two planets form a moderately compact system (Eberhardt et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd107148b-Eberhardtetal2022","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov, Martin Kürster et al.","title":"Dynamical Architecture of the HD 107148 Planetary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.06702","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 5583 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 5583 is a cool giant star. It was observed for 2313 days with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope, giving 14 epochs of precise velocities, and for 976 days with the HARPS-N spectrograph, giving 22 epochs of ultra-precise velocities (Niedzielski et al. 2016). The planet is described as a warm companion of the giant star (Niedzielski et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd5583b-Niedzielskietal2016","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"A. Niedzielski, E. Villaver, G. Nowak et al.","title":"Tracking Advanced Planetary Systems (TAPAS) with HARPS-N. III. HD 5583 and BD+15 2375 - two cool giants with warm companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.06832","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-62 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-62 is a five-planet system around a K2V star, with planets b, c, d, e and f orbiting every 5.7, 12.4, 18.2, 122.4 and 267.3 days (Borucki et al. 2013). Planet c, at 0.54 Earth radii, is the smallest of the five (Borucki et al. 2013). The outer planets e and f are super-Earths in the habitable zone, receiving 1.2 and 0.41 times the sunlight Earth gets (Borucki et al. 2013).","system":"Models suggest that planets e and f, for a stellar age of about 7 billion years, could be solid, either rocky or made mostly of solid water (Borucki et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler62c-Boruckietal2013","cite":"Borucki et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"William J. Borucki, Eric Agol, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Kepler-62: A Five-Planet System with Planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth Radii in the Habitable Zone","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7387","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-82 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The Kepler-82 system was analysed for the first time with a photodynamical model, which fits Kepler data and new ground-based transit data from the Kepler Object of Interest Network together with the planets' gravitational interactions (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Planets b and c are near a 2:1 resonance and show sinusoidal shifts in transit times. Extra short-term wiggles in the timing of planet c hint at a further planet near a 3:2 or 3:1 resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","system":"The timing analysis of the system led to a new non-transiting planet, Kepler-82 f, of about 21 Earth masses (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler82e-Freudenthaletal2019","cite":"Freudenthal et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Freudenthal, C. von Essen, A. Ofir et al.","title":"Kepler Object of Interest Network. III. Kepler-82f: a new non-transiting 21 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> planet from photodynamical modelling","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.06534","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1696 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1696 b is a sub-Neptune orbiting a nearby M4 dwarf every 2.5 days, with a radius of 3.09 Earth radii (Mori et al. 2022). Its host star has a temperature of 3185 K and is rich in heavy elements (Mori et al. 2022). Radial velocities from the Subaru IRD instrument set a 2-sigma upper limit of 48.8 Earth masses, and a separate analysis with the Habitable-Zone Planet Finder gave an upper limit of 56.6 Earth masses (Mori et al. 2022; Beard et al. 2022). The planet lies in the Neptunian desert, where few planets of this size are found close to their stars (Mori et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass is still only an upper limit. The planet is a promising target for atmospheric study with the James Webb Space Telescope (Beard et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1696b-Morietal2022","cite":"Mori et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Mayuko Mori, John H. Livingston, Jerome de Leon et al.","title":"TOI-1696: A Nearby M4 Dwarf with a 3 R <SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet in the Neptunian Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02694","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1696b-Beardetal2022","cite":"Beard et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Corey Beard, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"TOI-1696 and TOI-2136: Constraining the Masses of Two Mini-Neptunes with the Habitable-Zone Planet Finder","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.09063","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5398 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Both planets in the system were confirmed by combining a TESS light curve with HARPS-N radial velocities (Mantovan et al. 2024). Gaussian processes, a statistical tool for smooth variations, were used to separate the star's own activity from the planets' signals (Mantovan et al. 2024). Radius and mass were measured for both planets, which limits what they are made of (Mantovan et al. 2024). The star is only about 650 million years old, so TOI-5398 is the youngest known compact multi-planet system with a hot sub-Neptune inside a short-period Saturn (Mantovan et al. 2024).","system":"Short-period giant planets are frequently found alone (Mantovan et al. 2024). Small inner companions to such giants, with periods under about 15 days, are known in only five other compact systems: WASP-47, Kepler-730, WASP-132, TOI-1130 and TOI-2000 (Mantovan et al. 2024). The outer planet, TOI-5398 b, is a Saturn-like world that circles the star every 10.59 days (Mantovan et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5398c-Mantovanetal2024","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Mantovan, L. Malavolta, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XLIX. TOI-5398, the youngest compact multi-planet system composed of an inner sub-Neptune and an outer warm Saturn","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.16888","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-92 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits an F7 star and is moderately inflated, meaning its radius is larger than expected for its mass (Hay et al. 2016). Its radius is 1.461 times Jupiter's and its mass is 0.805 times Jupiter's (Hay et al. 2016). The host star is fairly faint, at magnitude 13.18 (Hay et al. 2016). Later timing studies of the transits found no significant deviation from a regular schedule, so no sign of a second planet pulling on it (Gajdos et al. 2019).","system":"Simulations of long-term stability show where another planet could survive for a long time in this system, and upper mass limits were placed on hypothetical extra planets (Gajdos et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp92b-Hayetal2016","cite":"Hay et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"K. L. Hay, A. Collier-Cameron, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"WASP-92b, WASP-93b and WASP-118b: three new transiting close-in giant planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00774","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp92b-Gajdosetal2019","cite":"Gajdos et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Pavol Gajdoš, Martin Vaňko, Marián Jakubík et al.","title":"WASP-92, WASP-93, and WASP-118: transit timing variations and long-term stability of the systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02804","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1489 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a search of Kepler data that used a graphics-card algorithm to fold light curves, paired with a neural network (Wang et al. 2024). Only a few hundred known planets have radii below Earth's, and how they form is not understood (Wang et al. 2024).","observed":"Kepler-1489 c is an ultra-short-period planet with a radius of 0.51 Earth radii, orbiting a G-type dwarf star in 0.680741 days (Wang et al. 2024). It is one of five new ultra-short-period planets found in the same search (Wang et al. 2024). Planets this small and this close to their stars are rare, so each new one adds to a small sample used to study sub-Earth formation (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"How sub-Earth-sized planets form is still not understood (Wang et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1489c-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kaitlyn Wang, Jian Ge, Kevin Willis et al.","title":"Discovery of small ultra-short-period planets orbiting Kepler KG dwarfs with GPU phase folding and deep learning","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17382","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28109 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three Neptune-sized planets found around an F8 star that sits in the continuous viewing zone of TESS, where the satellite stares for long stretches (Dransfield et al. 2022). Its period is close to a 3:2 ratio with the next planet in, which makes the two tug on each other and shift their transit times by up to 60 minutes (Dransfield et al. 2022). Those timing shifts, plus radial velocities from ESPRESSO and HARPS, gave masses for all three planets (Dransfield et al. 2022). Planet d has a radius of 3.25 Earth radii and a mass of 5.7 Earth masses (Dransfield et al. 2022).","system":"Ground-based confirmation included ASTEP in Antarctica, which recorded a full transit of the middle planet, HD 28109 c, lasting about 9 hours (Dransfield et al. 2022). The innermost planet is dense and massive for its size (Dransfield et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28109d-Dransfieldetal2022","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Georgina Dransfield, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Tristan Guillot et al.","title":"HD 28109 hosts a trio of transiting Neptunian planets including a near-resonant pair, confirmed by ASTEP from Antarctica","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09046","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-76 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first hot Jupiter spotted in Kepler data by the BEER method, which looks for the faint brightening and stretching of a star caused by an orbiting companion (Faigler et al. 2013).","observed":"Follow-up spectroscopy gave the planet a mass of about 2.0 Jupiter masses (Faigler et al. 2013). The mass implied by the star's Doppler beaming, a brightening as the star moves toward us, came out much larger than the spectroscopic mass (Faigler et al. 2013). The mismatch was explained as superrotation, where winds push the hottest part of the atmosphere east of the point facing the star (Faigler et al. 2013). Kepler data spanning over 1000 days show a dayside effective temperature of 2830 K (Jackson et al. 2019). The brightness variation around the orbit changes from 35 to 70 parts per million over tens of days, and the hot spot sits about 9 degrees east of the point directly under the star (Jackson et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The changing glow may come from thermal structures and clouds that advance and retreat in the atmosphere (Jackson et al. 2019). A magnetic field of 4 to 19 gauss could also explain wind variations, which would shift the hot spot (Hindle et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler76b-Faigleretal2013","cite":"Faigler et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"S. Faigler, L. Tal-Or, T. Mazeh et al.","title":"BEER Analysis of Kepler and CoRoT Light Curves. I. Discovery of Kepler-76b: A Hot Jupiter with Evidence for Superrotation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler76b-Jacksonetal2019","cite":"Jackson et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Brian Jackson, Elisabeth Adams, Wesley Sandidge et al.","title":"Variability in the Atmosphere of the Hot Jupiter Kepler-76b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.07781","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler76b-Hindleetal2021","cite":"Hindle et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. W. Hindle, P. J. Bushby, T. M. Rogers","title":"Observational Consequences of Shallow-water Magnetohydrodynamics on Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.10848","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-904 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-904 c is a sub-Neptune with an estimated equilibrium temperature of 217 K (Harris et al. 2023). It is the longest-period planet around an M dwarf found by TESS (Harris et al. 2023). It was first seen with a twin of similar size, TOI-904 b, which has a 10.887-day orbit (Harris et al. 2023). Its orbit of 83.999 days was pinned down using five more TESS sectors across the primary and two extended missions (Harris et al. 2023). Radial velocity checks and high-resolution speckle imaging showed no sign of an eclipsing binary or a nearby star (Harris et al. 2023).","system":"Three other validated planets with similar hosts and periods were found by Kepler around much dimmer stars, so TOI-904 c, orbiting a brighter star, could be the coldest M dwarf planet suited to atmospheric follow-up (Harris et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi904c-Harrisetal2023","cite":"Harris et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mallory Harris, Diana Dragomir, Karen Collins et al.","title":"TOI 904: A multiplanet system around an M dwarf with cold sub-Neptune","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1333 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter of about 2.4 Jupiter masses on a 4.72-day orbit (Knudstrup et al. 2026). Its host is a subgiant, a star beginning to swell as it ages (Rodriguez et al. 2021). Spectra taken during transit measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of the star's light as the planet crosses its spinning surface (Knudstrup et al. 2026). The planet's orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of -5 +/- 10 degrees (Knudstrup et al. 2026). Its orbit is modestly eccentric, with e of about 0.07 (Knudstrup et al. 2026).","system":"A stellar companion orbits the system on a wide path that is tilted relative to the planet's orbit (Knudstrup et al. 2026). Alignment was also found in a separate study of four hot Jupiters in binary systems, which includes this planet (Polanski et al. 2026). The star's temperature lies close to the dividing line, put at 6193 K, between cooler stars whose planets tend to be aligned and hotter ones with random tilts (Polanski et al. 2026).","history":"The low tilt and modest eccentricity fit either migration through the planet-forming disc, or migration on a stretched orbit followed by efficient circularising and realigning by tides (Knudstrup et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1333b-Knudstrupetal2026","cite":"Knudstrup et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. Knudstrup, M. L. Marcussen, S. H. Albrecht et al.","title":"TOI-1333Ab is on a well-aligned orbit: An aligned hot Jupiter around an F-type star with a mutually inclined stellar companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19129","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1333b-Rodriguezetal2021","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, George Zhou et al.","title":"TESS Delivers Five New Hot Giant Planets Orbiting Bright Stars from the Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01726","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1333b-Polanskietal2026","cite":"Polanski et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alex S. Polanski, Malena Rice, Catherine A. Clark et al.","title":"CHIANTE I: Obliquity Measurements of Four High-Priority Ariel Targets in Binaries","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23662","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-102 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-102 is an active star, which can disturb radial velocity mass measurements (Brinkman et al. 2023). New measurements with Keck/HIRES and HARPS-N, 111 in all, were modelled with a Gaussian process, a flexible statistical model for the star's activity (Brinkman et al. 2023). Planet d has a best-fit mass of 2.5 +/- 1.4 Earth masses, with an upper limit of 5.3 Earth masses (Brinkman et al. 2023). Its density of 5.6 +/- 3.2 grams per cubic centimetre is consistent with an Earth-like rocky composition (Brinkman et al. 2023). Earlier work had found a Mercury-like high density (Brinkman et al. 2023).","system":"Five tightly packed planets transit the star, with periods between 5 and 28 days (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The planets lie near, but not locked into, orbital resonances (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The star is estimated to be over 2 billion years old, so the system must have avoided early instabilities (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler102d-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, James Cadman, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"Kepler-102: Masses and Compositions for a Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Orbiting an Active Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05196","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler102d-VolkMalhotra2020","cite":"Volk & Malhotra 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Volk, R. Malhotra","title":"Kepler-102: a case study for using dynamical constraints to characterize exoplanet systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-113 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-113 b is a hot Jupiter with a radius of 0.93 times Jupiter's and a mass of 1.29 times Jupiter's, which gives it a high density of 1.97 grams per cubic centimetre (Espinoza et al. 2017). Among planets below 2 Jupiter masses with equilibrium temperatures above 1000 K, it is one of the densest known (Espinoza et al. 2017). It sits just above the temperature at which mechanisms that puff up hot Jupiters are believed to begin working (Espinoza et al. 2017). Its heavy element content is estimated at about 110 Earth masses or more (Espinoza et al. 2017). The host is a solar analogue, a star much like the Sun, with V = 13.68 (Espinoza et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2113b-Espinozaetal2017","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Néstor Espinoza, Markus Rabus, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"K2-113: a dense hot-Jupiter transiting a solar analogue","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07614","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 110067 hosts a chain of six sub-Neptunes locked in orbital resonances, where the planets' periods form simple ratios (Zak et al. 2024). Spectra from ESPRESSO taken during the transit of planet c measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, which shows how a planet's orbit is tilted against the star's spin (Zak et al. 2024). Planet c is well aligned, with a sky-projected angle of 6 degrees, plus or minus about 25 degrees (Zak et al. 2024). Planets in a stable resonant chain are likely to share one orbital plane, so the alignment probably applies to the whole system (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"The host star is not alone. A companion star, HD 110106, lies at a projected distance of 13,400 au and is itself an 8-year double-lined spectroscopic binary, so the system holds three stars (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"The alignment suggests the resonant architecture formed by convergent migration, with the planets drifting inward together, and without any major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067d-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067d-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 9827 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 9827 hosts the nearest planetary system found by Kepler or K2, at about 30 parsecs, and its star is bright enough, V = 10.35, for detailed study (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). The three planets have periods of 1.2, 3.6 and 6.2 days, close to a 1:3:5 ratio (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). Radial velocities from FIES, HARPS and HARPS-N gave masses for all three (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). A search at the helium line at 1083.3 nm with the Subaru telescope found no escaping atmosphere around planet d, with absorption below 18.39 milli-angstroms at 99 per cent confidence (Krishnamurthy et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a K6 dwarf, and planet d lies in the radius gap, the scarcity of planets between 1.5 and 2 Earth radii seen around Sun-like stars (Krishnamurthy et al. 2023). Planet d is one of five planets with tentative atmosphere detections used to anchor a revised empirical line separating worlds that keep atmospheres from those that lose them (Meni-Gallardo & Palle 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet holds an atmosphere is not settled: the helium search found no extended outflow, while other tentative hints of an atmosphere exist (Krishnamurthy et al. 2023; Meni-Gallardo & Palle 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj9827d-PrietoArranzetal2018","cite":"Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Prieto-Arranz, E. Palle, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Mass determination of the 1:3:5 near-resonant planets transiting GJ 9827 (K2-135)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.09557","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj9827d-Krishnamurthyetal2023","cite":"Krishnamurthy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vigneshwaran Krishnamurthy, Teruyuki Hirano, Eric Gaidos et al.","title":"Absence of extended atmospheres in low-mass star radius-gap planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01479","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj9827d-MeniGallardoPalle2026","cite":"Meni-Gallardo & Palle 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Meni-Gallardo, E. Pallé","title":"An empirical determination of the cosmic shoreline","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.12865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-52 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of four hot Jupiters announced together from the HATSouth survey, a network of automated telescopes on three continents (Henning et al. 2018).","observed":"HATS-52 b is the odd one out of its four sister planets. The other three have low density and orbital periods near 3.4 to 3.9 days, while HATS-52 b is denser and has a much shorter period of 1.3667 days (Henning et al. 2018). Its mass is 2.24 Jupiter masses and its radius 1.382 times Jupiter's (Henning et al. 2018). The planet receives intense radiation from its host, a G-type star, giving an equilibrium temperature of 1834 K (Henning et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats52b-Henningetal2018","cite":"Henning et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Th. Henning, L. Mancini, P. Sarkis et al.","title":"HATS-50b through HATS-53b: Four Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting G-type Stars Discovered by the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.04324","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-114 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a search for transiting warm Jupiters, gas giants receiving less stellar heat than hot Jupiters, with orbits beyond about 10 days around Sun-like stars (Shporer et al. 2017). The project aims to explain why some giant planets are puffed up, to study how hot Jupiters move inward, and to find targets for atmosphere studies (Shporer et al. 2017).","observed":"K2-114 b has a mass of 1.85 Jupiter masses, a radius of 0.942 times Jupiter's and an 11.4-day period (Shporer et al. 2017). It is among the longest-period transiting gas giants with a measured mass (Shporer et al. 2017). The host star is relatively old (Shporer et al. 2017). The planet is not inflated, since its radius matches theoretical expectations (Shporer et al. 2017). It was also a Guest Investigator target for TESS, which gave further light curves (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's position suggests that below about 10^8 erg per second per square centimetre of starlight, the link between irradiation and planet size levels off, so stellar heating may not set the size of warm Jupiters (Shporer et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2114b-Shporeretal2017","cite":"Shporer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Avi Shporer, George Zhou, Benjamin J. Fulton et al.","title":"K2-114b and K2-115b: Two Transiting Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07128","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2114b-IkwutUkwaetal2021","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"M. Ikwut-Ukwa, J. E. Rodriguez, A. Bieryla et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2 & TESS Synergy. I. Parameters & LC, 4 stars (Ikwut-Ukwa+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51600209","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 118203 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It lies beyond a close-in eccentric hot Jupiter with a 6.135-day period and a mass of 2.14 Jupiter masses (Zhang et al. 2024). An earlier study had already noticed a linear trend in the velocities that a longer-period companion could explain (da Silva et al. 2006). Hipparcos and Gaia astrometry, which tracks a star's tiny sideways motion, helped to limit the mutual tilt between the two planets' orbits along the line of sight (Zhang et al. 2024). The two planets are possibly aligned (Zhang et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet's true spin-orbit angle is below 33.5 degrees at 2 sigma, so its orbit lies nearly along the star's spin axis (Zhang et al. 2024). Stellar oscillations detected in the host confirm that the star is evolved (Zhang et al. 2024).","history":"The system is one of the first hot Jupiter systems where the full three-dimensional orbit geometry of both an inner and an outer planet has been measured, making it a testbed for theories of tidal migration (Zhang et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd118203c-Zhangetal2024","cite":"Zhang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jingwen Zhang, Daniel Huber, Lauren M. Weiss et al.","title":"A Testbed for Tidal Migration: The 3D Architecture of an Eccentric Hot Jupiter HD 118203 b Accompanied by a Possibly Aligned Outer Giant Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21377","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd118203c-daSilvaetal2006","cite":"da Silva et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"R. da Silva, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"Elodie metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. I. Two Hot Jupiters orbiting the slightly evolved stars <ASTROBJ>HD 118203</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 149143</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6692 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first flagged by volunteer and community groups, the Visual Survey Group and Planet Hunters, as a single-transit candidate in TESS data (Bieryla et al. 2026).","observed":"TESS caught only one transit, so the period was unknown until radial velocities from the Planet Finder Spectrograph on the Magellan telescope showed it to be about 130 days (Bieryla et al. 2026). Radial velocities from CHIRON, FEROS and CORALIE were added (Bieryla et al. 2026). Ground-based photometry from LCOGT over two weeks did not see the start or end of the 11.04-hour transit, but showed a possible in-transit signal and gave an updated ephemeris, the schedule for catching future transits (Bieryla et al. 2026). The orbit is eccentric, with e of about 0.54 (Bieryla et al. 2026).","system":"A long-term radial velocity trend points to a further companion that is not yet characterised (Bieryla et al. 2026).","history":"Giant planets with orbits near 100 days or more usually show eccentricities below what high-eccentricity tidal migration would give, and TOI-6692 b fits planet-planet scattering better (Bieryla et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6692b-Bierylaetal2026","cite":"Bieryla et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Allyson Bieryla, Karen A. Collins, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-6692 b: An Eccentric 130 Day Period Giant Planet with a Single Transit from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.16357","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-223 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-223 has four sub-Neptune planets in a resonance chain, linked by 4:3, 3:2 and 2:1 resonances (Mills et al. 2015). Their periods lie in ratios of 3:4:6:8 to within a part in 1000 (Fabrycky et al. 2011). The planets' mutual tugs cause transit timing variations, which were modelled as resonant libration, a slow back-and-forth swing of the orbits (Mills et al. 2016). Combining these with stability tests constrained the planets' masses (Mills et al. 2016).","system":"Orbits as tightly tuned as these are rare among Kepler's many multi-planet systems, which only occasionally sit exactly on resonances (Mills et al. 2016).","history":"The architecture is too finely tuned to have formed by scattering, while simulations show it is a natural result of inward migration through the planet-forming disc (Mills et al. 2016). The resonances also constrain the disc's turbulence and surface density (Hühn et al. 2021). A mass of orbit-crossing planetesimals about equal to Mars could pull such a system out of resonance (Moore et al. 2013).","open":"Whether similar systems later become destabilised, turning into the more common type of architecture, is not settled (Mills et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler223c-Millsetal2015","cite":"Mills et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel Clark Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223c-Fabryckyetal2011","cite":"Fabrycky et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Daniel C. Fabrycky, M. J. Holman, J. A. Carter et al.","title":"Koi-730 As A System Of Four Planets In A Chain Of Resonances","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223c-Millsetal2016","cite":"Mills et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Sean Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Transiting, Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #47","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223c-Hhnetal2021","cite":"Hühn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L.-A. Hühn, G. Pichierri, B. Bitsch et al.","title":"Kepler-223 resonance holds information about turbulence during the gas-disk phase","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13835","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223c-Mooreetal2013","cite":"Moore et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Alexander Moore, Imran Hasan, Alice C. Quillen","title":"Limits on orbit-crossing planetesimals in the resonant multiple planet system, KOI-730","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 95872 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Computer simulations tested 34 nearby Sun-like dwarf stars, each with one giant planet found by Doppler wobble outside the habitable zone, to see whether an Earth-like planet could orbit stably in the zone (Kokaia et al. 2020). The star is one of seven systems picked out as best bets for future searches (Kokaia et al. 2020).","system":"The star hosts one known giant planet beyond its habitable zone, and the simulations asked whether smaller planets could share the system (Kokaia et al. 2020).","history":"The simulations assumed that a giant planet's elongated orbit may come from an instability in which other planets were thrown out of the system (Kokaia et al. 2020). Systems where habitable-zone planets would survive such an event were called resiliently habitable (Kokaia et al. 2020).","open":"Whether any Earth-like planet exists in the habitable zone of this star is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd95872b-Kokaiaetal2020","cite":"Kokaia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Giorgi Kokaia, Melvyn B. Davies, Alexander J. Mustill","title":"Resilient habitability of nearby exoplanet systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07573","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wendelstein-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, Pan-Planets, aimed to find hot Jupiters around low-mass stars, because such planets seem to get rarer as stars get smaller (Obermeier et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet was confirmed in two ways. Doppler measurements with two spectrographs, HIRES and the Habitable-zone Planet Finder, gave its mass (Obermeier et al. 2020). A second check used the Transit Color Signature: the transit was observed from 4000 to 24 000 angstroms, and a real planet blocks nearly the same fraction of light at every colour (Obermeier et al. 2020). Secondary eclipse observations, when the planet passes behind its star, also measured its thermal glow (Obermeier et al. 2020). Its radius is 1.16 Jupiter radii and its mass 0.73 Jupiter masses (Obermeier et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a late K dwarf, a cool orange star. A second hot Jupiter, Wendelstein-1b, was announced alongside it from the same survey (Obermeier et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wendelstein2b-Obermeieretal2020","cite":"Obermeier et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"C. Obermeier, J. Steuer, H. Kellermann et al.","title":"Following the TraCS of exoplanets with Pan-Planets: Wendelstein-1b and Wendelstein-2b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13560","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 30562 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Years of Doppler measurements from Lick Observatory show a planet with a minimum mass of 1.29 Jupiter masses on a very eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.76 and an average distance of 2.3 au (Fischer et al. 2009). Later measurements with the CORALIE spectrograph refined its orbit as part of a survey of long-period, massive planets (Marmier et al. 2013). Eccentricities among such massive, distant planets are often higher than the average for known exoplanets (Fischer et al. 2009).","system":"The host star is a metal-rich F8 dwarf (Fischer et al. 2009).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd30562b-Fischeretal2009","cite":"Fischer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Debra Fischer, Peter Driscoll, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Five Planets and an Independent Confirmation of HD 196885Ab from Lick Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd30562b-Marmieretal2013","cite":"Marmier et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Marmier, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVII. New and updated long period and massive planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6444","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"UZ For b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is inferred from timing of eclipses in UZ Fornacis, a close pair of a white dwarf and a small companion star where gas flows between the two. Four new eclipse timings showed that the orbital period of the pair is growing (Dai et al. 2010). The eclipse times also show a possible repeating change with a period of about 23 years (Dai et al. 2010). Magnetic activity cycles in the companion star and the light travel-time effect, caused by an unseen body pulling the pair back and forth, were both named as probable causes (Dai et al. 2010).","system":"UZ For is a cataclysmic variable, a white dwarf pulling gas from a companion, with an orbital period inside the so-called period gap (Dai et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether the timing cycle comes from a planet or from magnetic cycles in the companion star is not settled (Dai et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-uzforb-Daietal2010","cite":"Dai et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Z.-B. Dai, S.-B. Qian, E. Fernández Lajús et al.","title":"Orbital period analyses for two cataclysmic variables: UZ Fornacis and V348 Puppis inside the period gap","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4070","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 163 c":{"text":{"why":"The search was designed to find planets of 1 to 10 Earth masses in the habitable zone of cool stars (Bonfils et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet was found in 150 Doppler measurements made with HARPS over eight years (Bonfils et al. 2013). Five repeating signals appeared, and stellar activity cast doubt on two of them, so at least three planets are accepted (Bonfils et al. 2013). The planet is a super-Earth with a minimum mass of 6.8 Earth masses and a period of 25.63 days (Bonfils et al. 2013). It was named among the first potentially habitable planets found by HARPS around M dwarfs (Bonfils et al. 2013).","system":"The star is an M3 dwarf. Planet b has a period of 8.632 days and the two inner planets have a period ratio of 2.97, but the system lies outside the 3:1 resonance (Bonfils et al. 2013). A distant planet d has a period of 604 days (Bonfils et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Two further signals, at 19.4 and 108 days, are not yet accepted as planets (Bonfils et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj163c-Bonfilsetal2013","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"X. Bonfils, G. Lo Curto, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXIV. A planetary system around the nearby M dwarf <ASTROBJ>GJ 163</ASTROBJ>, with a super-Earth possibly in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.0904","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-28 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transits were observed from optical to near-infrared colours, along with spectroscopy and sharp adaptive-optics imaging, to rule out false alarms (Hirano et al. 2016). The transit depth is nearly the same in every colour, which points to a planet and not a background eclipsing star (Hirano et al. 2016). The chance of a background eclipsing pair is below 2 in 100 000 (Hirano et al. 2016). Later, a diffuser on a 3.5 m telescope gave sharper transit timing and a radius of 2.56 Earth radii, a mini-Neptune (Stefansson et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a bright, metal-rich M4 dwarf (Hirano et al. 2016; Stefansson et al. 2018). A stellar companion later than M4 cannot be present (Hirano et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Current radius measurements are too imprecise to say anything about the atmosphere. The James Webb Space Telescope, using NIRISS, is expected to be able to tell more (Stefansson et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k228b-Hiranoetal2016","cite":"Hirano et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Akihiko Fukui, Andrew W. Mann et al.","title":"The K2-ESPRINT Project III: A Close-in Super-Earth around a Metal-rich Mid-M Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.08508","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k228b-Stefanssonetal2018","cite":"Stefansson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Gudmundur Stefansson, Yiting Li, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"Diffuser-assisted Photometric Follow-up Observations of the Neptune-sized Planets K2-28b and K2-100b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04420","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-712 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three transiting mini-Neptunes were found in the light curve from the TESS satellite, and the middle one, planet c, has the largest radius of the three at 2.70 Earth radii (Vach et al. 2022). A fourth, Earth-sized candidate with a period of 4.32 days was also identified in the data (Vach et al. 2022). The star's rotation period of 12.48 days, measured from the light curve, gives its age (Vach et al. 2022).","system":"The star is a bright K4.5 dwarf. Planets b, c and d circle it every 9.5, 51.7 and 84.8 days (Vach et al. 2022). The habitable zone lies between 0.339 and 0.844 au (Vach et al. 2022).","history":"The system is young, between about 500 million and 1.1 billion years old, so its planets are still in an early phase of evolution (Vach et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi712c-Vachetal2022","cite":"Vach et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sydney Vach, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TOI-712: A System of Adolescent Mini-Neptunes Extending to the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02416","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2020-BLG-0414L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet-to-host mass ratio is 0.9 to 1.2 parts in 100 000, the lowest of any microlensing planet to date, equal to about 3 to 4 Earth masses at 1 sigma (Zang et al. 2021). The light curve was extremely magnified, by a factor of about 1450, which also revealed a second companion (Zang et al. 2021). Together with two other recent finds, the result fills an empty region in the diagram of mass ratio against separation and questions a suggested break in the planet mass-ratio function (Zang et al. 2021).","system":"The host is estimated at about 0.3 solar masses, the planet at about 1 Earth mass, and the second companion at about 17 Jupiter masses (Zang et al. 2021). The second companion lies roughly ten times closer or farther from the host, in projection (Zang et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2020blg0414lb-Zangetal2021","cite":"Zang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Weicheng Zang, Cheongho Han, Iona Kondo et al.","title":"An Earth-mass planet in a time of COVID-19: KMT-2020-BLG-0414Lb","journal":"Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01896","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-813 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found by the Planet Hunters TESS project, in which citizen scientists searched TESS data by eye (Eisner et al. 2020).","observed":"Four transits were seen in the first year of TESS observations (Eisner et al. 2020). Spectroscopy and speckle imaging from the ground set an upper limit of 2 Jupiter masses on the companion and validated it as a planet (Eisner et al. 2020). Its period of 83.89 days and its evolved host put it in a relatively little explored corner of parameter space (Eisner et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a bright subgiant with a radius of 1.94 Suns and a mass of 1.32 Suns (Eisner et al. 2020). The planet is at 0.423 au and tugs its star by only about 6 metres per second (Eisner et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi813b-Eisneretal2020","cite":"Eisner et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. L. Eisner, O. Barragán, S. Aigrain et al.","title":"Planet Hunters TESS I: TOI 813, a subgiant hosting a transiting Saturn-sized planet on an 84-day orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.09094","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is known only from Doppler measurements. Searches for a transit used the small ASTERIA cubesat and TESS (Bailey 2023). TESS covered the predicted transit window and definitively rules out a transit of this planet (Bailey 2023). The star's innermost planet, b, does transit and is the nearest known transiting planet, which made this system a prime target (Motalebi et al. 2015).","system":"HD 219134 is a K3 dwarf with six reported planets, only 6.5 parsecs away (Bailey 2023). Planets b and c transit, so the system could be the nearest bright compact system of transiting planets (Bailey 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the signal is a planet at all is still questioned. In one search for exotic objects, it was listed among Doppler-only candidates never seen to transit or imaged, as a possible primordial black hole (Halpern et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134f-Bailey2023","cite":"Bailey 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vanessa Bailey","title":"HD 219134 Revisited: Planet d Transit Upper Limit and Planet f Transit Non-Detection with ASTERIA and TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.MTOJHC","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134f-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134f-Halpernetal2025","cite":"Halpern et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Paul Halpern, Erik Cauley, Max Stoltzmann et al.","title":"Probing for primordial black hole candidates in exoplanet search data","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.03118","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-370 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of 60 statistically validated K2 planets, noted as a good target for emission spectroscopy (Christiansen et al. 2022). A later study combined K2, TESS and CHEOPS transits with HARPS-N and HARPS Doppler data to find a radius of 2.67 Earth radii (Sozzetti et al. 2024). The star rotates every 13.5 days and is very active, so its spots had to be modelled to pull out the planet's 5.6 metre per second signal (Sozzetti et al. 2024).","system":"The host is HD 284521, an active G-type dwarf (Sozzetti et al. 2024). The planet orbits in 2.14 days and is strongly irradiated (Sozzetti et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2370b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2370b-Sozzettietal2024","cite":"Sozzetti et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Sozzetti, M. Damasso, J. Fernández Fernández et al.","title":"K2-370 b: a strongly irradiated sub-Neptune transiting a very active solar-type star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stae2323","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-26 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The first detection of the planet's occultation, when it passes behind its star, came from the warm Spitzer telescope at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres (Mahtani et al. 2013). The occultation depths imply brightness temperatures of 1825 and 1725 K (Mahtani et al. 2013). The orbit is consistent with circular (Mahtani et al. 2013). The data fit atmospheric models with or without a thermal inversion, a layer where temperature rises with height, and the atmosphere may be nearly isothermal (Mahtani et al. 2013). Its alignment with its star's spin was also measured with the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Albrecht et al. 2012).","system":"The host is in a visual double with a fainter K-type star, and the two share motion through space at a distance of about 250 parsecs and an age of 6 billion years (Smalley et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere has a thermal inversion needs more data (Mahtani et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp26b-Mahtanietal2013","cite":"Mahtani et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"D. P. Mahtani, P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer occultation photometry of WASP-26b at 3.6 and 4.5 μm","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.4596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp26b-Albrechtetal2012","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John A. Johnson et al.","title":"Obliquities of Hot Jupiter Host Stars: Evidence for Tidal Interactions and Primordial Misalignments","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6105","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp26b-Smalleyetal2010","cite":"Smalley et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"B. Smalley, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-26b: a 1-Jupiter-mass planet around an early-G-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.1542","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1259 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transits are 2.7 per cent deep, among the deepest known, which makes the planet a good target for atmosphere studies (Martin et al. 2021). Helium absorption was seen during transit with Palomar and Keck telescopes, with an excess of 0.395 per cent and a 5.5 kilometre per second blueshift, which indicates an extended, escaping atmosphere (Saidel et al. 2025). The mass-loss rate is relatively low (Saidel et al. 2025). Using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the true 3D tilt between orbit and stellar equator is 24 degrees, a low obliquity (Veldhuis et al. 2026).","system":"The planet, with a radius of 1.0 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.44 Jupiter masses, orbits a K dwarf (Martin et al. 2021). A bound white dwarf companion lies about 1600 au away (Martin et al. 2021). The white dwarf has hydrogen lines only and shows no heavy-element pollution (Fitzmaurice et al. 2023).","history":"The white dwarf's age of about 4.05 billion years matches the age from the K dwarf's spin (Fitzmaurice et al. 2023). The planet may have formed in this aligned state, or the white dwarf's evolution may have triggered inward migration (Veldhuis et al. 2026).","open":"Whether the planet moved inward through Lidov-Kozai oscillations or formed quietly in place is not settled (Veldhuis et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1259ab-Martinetal2021","cite":"Martin et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"David V. Martin, Kareem El-Badry, Vedad Kunovac Hodžić et al.","title":"TOI-1259Ab - a gas giant planet with 2.7 per cent deep transits and a bound white dwarf companion","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.02707","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1259ab-Saideletal2025","cite":"Saidel et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Morgan Saidel, Shreyas Vissapragada, Jessica Spake et al.","title":"Atmospheric Mass Loss from TOI-1259 A b, a Gas Giant Planet with a White Dwarf Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.08736","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1259ab-Veldhuisetal2026","cite":"Veldhuis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Hugo Veldhuis, Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Gudmundur Stefansson et al.","title":"TOI-1259Ab: A warm Jupiter orbiting a K-dwarf white-dwarf binary on a low-obliquity orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07737","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1259ab-Fitzmauriceetal2023","cite":"Fitzmaurice et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Evan Fitzmaurice, David V. Martin, Romy Rodríguez Martínez et al.","title":"Spectroscopy of TOI-1259B - an unpolluted white dwarf companion to an inflated warm Saturn","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.01259","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-20 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Five repeating transit signals appeared in the Kepler light curve of the star (Gautier et al. 2012). For three of them, analysis of possible blends and of imaging, colours and spectra strongly disfavours astrophysical false positives (Gautier et al. 2012). The system is described as three sub-Neptune planets and two Earth-size candidates (Gautier et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a Sun-like star with a temperature of about 5455 K and a mass of 0.912 Suns (Gautier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler20c-Gautieretal2012","cite":"Gautier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas N., III Gautier, David Charbonneau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-20: A Sun-like Star with Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets and Two Earth-size Candidates","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.4514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-431 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet shows up only in the High Accuracy Radial velocity Planet Searcher Doppler data and is not seen to transit in TESS light curves (Osborn et al. 2021). Its minimum mass is 2.83 Earth masses and its period is 4.85 days (Osborn et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a bright, early K dwarf that rotates every 30.5 days (Osborn et al. 2021). Planet b is a super-Earth with a period of 0.49 days and a density of 8.0 grams per cubic centimetre, and may be a stripped core (Osborn et al. 2021). Planet d is a sub-Neptune with a period of 12.46 days and a density of 1.36 (Osborn et al. 2021). The planets straddle the radius gap, the dip in planet sizes between rocky and puffy worlds (Osborn et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi431c-Osbornetal2021","cite":"Osborn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ares Osborn, David J. Armstrong, Bryson Cale et al.","title":"TOI-431/HIP 26013: a super-Earth and a sub-Neptune transiting a bright, early K dwarf, with a third RV planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02310","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-2 S b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host belongs to a pair of nearly identical twin K dwarfs that move through space together and are 31 arcseconds apart (Burke et al. 2007). Both stars are metal-rich, with [Fe/H] near 0.45 (Burke et al. 2007). A transiting hot Jupiter, XO-2b, orbits one of the two stars every 2.6 days (Burke et al. 2007).","system":"The two stars have similar spectra and apparent magnitudes (Burke et al. 2007). Their motion suggests an origin in the metal-rich inner disc of the Galaxy and later scattering into the solar neighbourhood (Burke et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo2sb-Burkeetal2007","cite":"Burke et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, P. R. McCullough, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-2b: Transiting Hot Jupiter in a Metal-rich Common Proper Motion Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0003","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1243 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ground-based photometry and Doppler follow-up with the CARMENES spectrograph confirmed the planet (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The radius is 2.33 Earth radii and the mass is 7.7 Earth masses, giving a mean density of 0.61 times Earth's (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The mass is known to a precision of 19 per cent (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The planet sits in a degenerate region of the mass-radius diagram, so its make-up cannot be pinned down (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026).","system":"The star is an early M dwarf of type M2.0 V (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is rocky with a thick atmosphere, or rich in water or other volatile material, is not known (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1243b-Poultourtzidisetal2026","cite":"Poultourtzidis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. Poultourtzidis, G. Lacedelli, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Characterization of two new transiting sub-Neptunes and a terrestrial planet around M-dwarf hosts","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07414","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 12648 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the find targeted about 200 northern circumpolar stars, using the 1.8 m telescope at Bohyunsan Observatory (Lee et al. 2015).","observed":"A Doppler signal with a period of 133 days points to a companion with a minimum mass of 2.9 Jupiter masses on a nearly circular orbit with an eccentricity of 0.04 (Lee et al. 2015). Later, asteroseismology confirmed the host as a red-clump giant, a star that has already swollen on the red giant branch and shrunk after igniting helium (Lin et al. 2026). The planet now orbits closer than the star's maximum radius, so it should not have survived (Lin et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a red-clump giant, one of three such systems with survivors (Lin et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Survival of a planet this close to an evolved giant defies current models of how stars and planets evolve together (Lin et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd12648b-Leeetal2015","cite":"Lee et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park, S.-M. Lee et al.","title":"Search for exoplanet around northern circumpolar stars. Four planets around HD 11755, HD 12648, HD 24064, and 8 Ursae Minoris","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.09012","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd12648b-Linetal2026","cite":"Lin et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Wen-Xu Lin, Sheng-Bang Qian, Qi-Bin Sun et al.","title":"The Survival Paradox of Close-In Planets Orbiting Red Clump Giants","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04544","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"YZ Cet b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Near-infrared spectropolarimetry of the host shows its large-scale magnetic field changing strongly over just a few stellar rotations, from a weaker complex field of about 201 gauss to a stronger dipole-dominated field of about 276 gauss (Biswas et al. 2026). The star has also shown polarized radio bursts that may come from aurorae driven by the planet's magnetic interaction with the star (Biswas et al. 2026). Models place the planet most likely inside the star's sub-Alfvenic region, where star-planet interaction should be at work (Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025).","system":"The star is a nearby M4.5 dwarf (Biswas et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The predicted radio emission from the planet is very low in the Alfven wing model and could be higher if magnetic reconnection occurs (Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-yzcetb-Biswasetal2026","cite":"Biswas et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ayan Biswas, Colin P. Folsom, James A. Barron et al.","title":"Discovery of a Rapidly Evolving Global Magnetic Field in the M Dwarf YZ Cet and Constraints on the Magnetic Field of Its Planet YZ Cet b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16298","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-yzcetb-PeaMoinoPrezTorres2025","cite":"Peña-Moñino & Pérez-Torres 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Luis Peña-Moñino, Miguel Pérez-Torres","title":"Modelling magnetic star─planet interaction in the iconic M dwarfs Proxima Centauri, YZ Ceti, and GJ 1151","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.20891","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 10697 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler data alone give a minimum mass of 6.35 Jupiter masses and an orbital period of about three years (Zucker & Mazeh 2000). Using Hipparcos astrometry, a semimajor axis of 2.1 milliarcseconds implies a true mass of about 38 Jupiter masses, so the object is probably a brown dwarf at 2 au (Zucker & Mazeh 2000). A separate study of the star finds [Fe/H] of +0.16 and also notes that its companion lies in the brown-dwarf mass range (Gonzalez et al. 2001).","system":"The host is a nearby main-sequence star with a metallicity of [Fe/H] = +0.16 (Gonzalez et al. 2001).","history":"","open":"Whether the object is a true planet or a brown dwarf depends on its actual mass, which the Hipparcos analysis puts well above the planet range (Zucker & Mazeh 2000)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd10697b-ZuckerMazeh2000","cite":"Zucker & Mazeh 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Shay Zucker, Tsevi Mazeh","title":"Analysis of the Hipparcos Measurements of HD 10697: A Mass Determination of a Brown Dwarf Secondary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0001282","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10697b-Gonzalezetal2001","cite":"Gonzalez et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, Chris Laws, Sudhi Tyagi et al.","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. VI. Abundance Analyses of 20 New Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0010197","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-427 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was shown to be a moderately oblate spheroid, a slightly flattened ball, rather than a perfect sphere (Carado & Knuth 2020). A method for modelling the transit light curves of non-spherical planets uses it as an example (Carado & Knuth 2020). Mid-transit times from Kepler fit a steady, linear ephemeris with no strong timing variations (Püsküllü & Ozturk 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler427b-CaradoKnuth2020","cite":"Carado & Knuth 2020","year":"2020","authors":"B. Carado, K. H. Knuth","title":"Modeling transit and reflected light curves for non-spherical exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Computing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ascom.2020.100406","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler427b-PskllOzturk2023","cite":"Püsküllü & Ozturk 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Çağlar Püsküllü, Oguz Ozturk","title":"Investigating the Variation of Selected Kepler Objects Mid-Transit Times","journal":"Journal of Advanced Research in Natural and Applied Sciences","url":"https://doi.org/10.28979/jarnas.1201714","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4406 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first noticed as a single transit in TESS full-frame images, so its orbital period had to be found with follow-up measurements (Brahm et al. 2023).","observed":"Doppler measurements and further light curves fixed the period at 30.08 days (Brahm et al. 2023). The mass is 0.30 Jupiter masses and the radius is 1.00 Jupiter radii (Brahm et al. 2023). The orbit has a low eccentricity of 0.15 (Brahm et al. 2023). The planet is enriched in heavy elements compared with its host star (Brahm et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a slightly metal-rich late F star (Brahm et al. 2023). Two similar warm giants, TOI-2338 b and TOI-2589 b, were announced in the same study, both on highly eccentric orbits (Brahm et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4406b-Brahmetal2023","cite":"Brahm et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Melissa J. Hobson et al.","title":"Three Long-period Transiting Giant Planets from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02139","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 222076 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey, the Pan-Pacific Planet Search, looks at evolved stars, using three spectrographs (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","observed":"Doppler data show a planet with a period of 871 days, an orbit at 1.83 au and a minimum mass of 1.56 Jupiter masses (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). It is typical of the planets known around evolved stars (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). Across 164 evolved stars in the survey, planets are more common around metal-rich hosts (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a K giant (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd222076b-Wittenmyeretal2017","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, M. I. Jones, Jinglin Zhao et al.","title":"The Pan-Pacific Planet Search. VI. Giant Planets Orbiting HD 86950 and HD 222076","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07648","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOINTREAU-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey, KOINTREAU, uses adaptive optics at Keck to search for planets and ultracool dwarfs around young stars in the Taurus and rho Ophiuchi star-forming regions (Walker et al. 2026).","observed":"The companion was spotted as a faint object moving together with its star ISO-Oph 96, at a projected separation of 340 au (Walker et al. 2026). From infrared photometry and evolutionary models, assuming the same dust dimming as its host, the mass is 3.4 Jupiter masses (Walker et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a young star in the rho Ophiuchi region (Walker et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kointreau3b-Walkeretal2026","cite":"Walker et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Samuel A. U. Walker, Michael C. Liu, Dimitri Mawet et al.","title":"Keck Observations in the INfrared of Taurus and ρ Oph Exoplanets And Ultracool Dwarfs (KOINTREAU). II. Two Young Bound Companions to Ophiuchus Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-125 b":{"text":{"why":"The system is studied as a rare chance to compare planets formed in the same disc (Chai et al. 2026).","observed":"Observations of one full transit with the James Webb Space Telescope, using the NIRSpec G395H mode, gave a transmission spectrum with a median error of 50 parts per million (Chai et al. 2026). The spectrum fits either an atmosphere about 150 times richer in heavy elements than the Sun's or clouds above about 3 millibar (Chai et al. 2026). The result may support recent evidence for metal enrichment in sub-Neptunes (Chai et al. 2026).","system":"The sub-Neptunes b and c are similar in size and orbit a Sun-like star, with equilibrium temperatures of 1100 and 825 K (Chai et al. 2026). Larger-than-predicted transit timing variations meant that only 70 per cent of planet c's transit was observed (Chai et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere is metal-rich or cloudy cannot be told apart yet (Chai et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi125b-Chaietal2026","cite":"Chai et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yiwei Chai, Néstor Espinoza, Chloe Fisher et al.","title":"JWST NIRSpec G395H Transmission Spectra of Two Similar Sub-Neptunes in the TOI-125 System","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.35607","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2010-BLG-073L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event was unusual because the background source star was already known to vary irregularly over more than 200 days, and a model of the lens showed a source radius of 14.7 Suns, a red giant (Street et al. 2013). The mass ratio of the lens is 0.0654 (Street et al. 2013). The event lasted about 44 days, long enough to show parallax effects and the orbital motion of the lens system (Street et al. 2013).","system":"The host is an M dwarf with a companion at the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Street et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2010blg073lb-Streetetal2013","cite":"Street et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"R. A. Street, J.-Y. Choi, Y. Tsapras et al.","title":"MOA-2010-BLG-073L: An M-dwarf with a Substellar Companion at the Planet/Brown Dwarf Boundary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.3782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 93963 A c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CHEOPS observed the star in nine visits, eight aimed at planet b and one at planet c (Serrano et al. 2022). A transit of planet c was then observed on 2024 May 3 with the Keck Planet Finder, capturing the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect that shows the alignment of its orbit with the stellar spin (Teng et al. 2026). A total of 43 spectra were taken, 25 of them during transit (Teng et al. 2026).","system":"A binary companion star lies 5.9 arcseconds away, far enough that its light could be excluded (Teng et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd93963ac-Serranoetal2022","cite":"Serrano et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. M. Serrano, D. Gandolfi, S. Hoyer et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD93963 CHEOPS light curves (Serrano+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd93963ac-Tengetal2026","cite":"Teng et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"H.-Y. Teng, F. Dai, A. W. Howard et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 93963 radial velocities from Keck/KPF (Teng+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2015 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit. Its existence follows from transit timing variations of its neighbour TOI-2015 b, in which transits arrive earlier or later than a steady clock predicts (Barkaoui et al. 2025). A photodynamical analysis of 63 transit light curves strongly favours a 5:3 orbital resonance, with planet b at 3.34 days (Barkaoui et al. 2025). The mass of planet b is 9.02 Earth masses and its radius 3.31 Earth radii (Barkaoui et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an M4 dwarf with a temperature of about 3200 K and subsolar metallicity (Barkaoui et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"An earlier study found that an outer planet near a 2:1 resonance could explain the timing variations, and that 3:2 and 4:3 could not be excluded (Jones et al. 2024). The 5:3 solution is strongly favoured by the later analysis (Barkaoui et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2015c-Barkaouietal2025","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"K. Barkaoui, J. Korth, E. Gaidos et al.","title":"TOI-2015 b: A sub-Neptune in strong gravitational interaction with an outer non-transiting planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07074","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2015c-Jonesetal2024","cite":"Jones et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sinclaire E. Jones, Guđmundur Stefánsson, Kento Masuda et al.","title":"TOI-2015 b: A Warm Neptune with Transit Timing Variations Orbiting an Active Mid-type M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11775","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-712 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three transiting mini-Neptunes were found in the TESS light curve (Vach et al. 2022). The outermost transiting planet, d, has a period of 84.8 days and a radius of 2.47 Earth radii (Vach et al. 2022). The habitable zone falls between 82.7 and 325.3 days, so planet d lies near its inner edge (Vach et al. 2022). The planet is named among examples of planets near the upper edge of the radius gap, used to test models of possible ocean worlds (Scheucher & Hu 2022).","system":"The star is a bright K4.5 dwarf. Planets b and c have periods of 9.5 and 51.7 days, and a fourth, Earth-sized candidate may also be present (Vach et al. 2022).","history":"The system is young, between about 500 million and 1.1 billion years old (Vach et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi712d-Vachetal2022","cite":"Vach et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sydney Vach, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TOI-712: A System of Adolescent Mini-Neptunes Extending to the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02416","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi712d-ScheucherHu2022","cite":"Scheucher & Hu 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Markus Scheucher, Renyu Hu","title":"Towards equilibrium climate states of potential ocean worlds through radiative-convective-photochemical modeling","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting #240","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2018-BLG-1990L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet shows that the survey's low-cadence fields can reveal Jovian planets, though caustic crossings are best followed up with faster observations (Ryu et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet is a Jovian world of about 0.57 Jupiter masses orbiting a late M dwarf of 0.14 Suns, at a distance of about 1.23 kiloparsecs (Ryu et al. 2019). It lies at 2.6 times the snow line distance, in projection (Ryu et al. 2019). It is the second Jovian planet found by the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network in its low-cadence fields (Ryu et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a late M dwarf (Ryu et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg1990lb-Ryuetal2019","cite":"Ryu et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Yoon-Hyun Ryu, Kyu-Ha Hwang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-1990Lb: A Nearby Jovian Planet From A Low-cadence Microlensing Field","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.05509","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2007-BLG-192L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The mass ratio between planet and host is about 2 in 10 000 (Bennett et al. 2008). Combining parallax and finite-source effects gives a host of 0.060 solar masses and a planet of 3.3 Earth masses (Bennett et al. 2008). Coverage of the planetary signal is sparse, so the limits are weak (Bennett et al. 2008). Adaptive optics images with the VLT NACO instrument fit a brown dwarf or a star at the bottom of the main sequence (Bennett et al. 2008).","system":"The host may be a brown dwarf, not a normal star (Bennett et al. 2008).","history":"","open":"Follow-up with the VLT or Hubble will either confirm a brown-dwarf host or detect the low-mass star (Bennett et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2007blg192lb-Bennettetal2008","cite":"Bennett et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"D. P. Bennett, I. A. Bond, A. Udalski et al.","title":"A Low-Mass Planet with a Possible Sub-Stellar-Mass Host in Microlensing Event MOA-2007-BLG-192","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.0025","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4994 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with Doppler measurements from five spectrographs and ground-based photometry from Las Cumbres Observatory and ASTEP in Antarctica (Rodríguez Martínez et al. 2025). Its 21.5-day orbit is eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.32 (Rodríguez Martínez et al. 2025). The bulk density is Saturn-like (Rodríguez Martínez et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a G-type solar analog with V of 12.6 magnitudes (Rodríguez Martínez et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The star is a potentially viable candidate for measuring the tilt between its spin and the planet's orbit (Rodríguez Martínez et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4994b-Martnezetal2025","cite":"Martínez et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Romy Rodríguez Martínez, Jason D. Eastman, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"Discovery and Characterization of an Eccentric, Warm Saturn Transiting the Solar Analog TOI-4994","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02769","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-79 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Changes in the times when the planets cross their star were tracked over 1282 days of Kepler photometry (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). The planets tug on each other, so the crossings come early or late in step with how close each planet sits to a resonance, a simple ratio between orbital periods (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Fitting these shifts gave tight masses for all four planets and showed that they orbit the same star (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). All four have low densities for their sizes (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","system":"Four planets, 3.5 to 7 times the size of Earth, are packed close in, with periods near a 1:2:4:6 chain (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Orbital periods run from 13.5 to 81.1 days (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","history":"Planets in such a chain were unlikely to have formed where they are now, and probably moved inward through the gas disc and stopped near its inner edge (Wang et al. 2012). Computer models suggest slow inward drift, about a tenth of the simple theory rate, favours this arrangement (Wang et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler79c-JontofHutteretal2014","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jack J. Lissauer, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-79's Low Density Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2642","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler79c-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Su Wang, Jianghui Ji, Ji-Lin Zhou","title":"Predicting the Configuration of a Planetary System: KOI-152 Observed by Kepler","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2431","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+14 4559 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a survey of stars with the 9.2 m Hobby-Eberly Telescope, the Penn State-Torun Planet Search (Niedzielski et al. 2009).","observed":"The star is a K2 dwarf, and its companion has a minimum mass of 1.5 Jupiter masses on a 269-day orbit (Niedzielski et al. 2009). Its true mass is not known, since the tilt of the orbit is not measured (Niedzielski et al. 2009). Its orbit has also been used in model studies of how warm a large moon would be, since the planet crosses the region where liquid water could exist (Hinkel & Kane 2013).","system":"The star's motion shows a slow, steady drift on top of the 269-day signal, which hints at another planet-mass body further out (Niedzielski et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"No moon has been seen. Whether another body is present in the system is not settled (Niedzielski et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd144559b-Niedzielskietal2009","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. Niedzielski, G. Nowak, M. Adamów et al.","title":"Substellar-mass Companions to the K-dwarf BD+14 4559 and the K-giants HD 240210 and BD+20 2457","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.1804","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd144559b-HinkelKane2013","cite":"Hinkel & Kane 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Natalie R. Hinkel, Stephen R. Kane","title":"Habitability of Exomoons at the Hill or Tidal Locking Radius","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4760","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-2 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looks for planets around stars with unusual activity signs, the kind expected when a close-in planet is boiling away and shedding gas around the star (Standing et al. 2026).","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements of a sample of stars were searched for planet signals, and DMPP-2 c and d are two of seven new signals reported in five systems (Standing et al. 2026). Detection limits were worked out for 24 stars, and planets with periods under 50 days and masses of 10 to 30 Earth masses were found around 27 per cent of them, with a wide uncertainty (Standing et al. 2026).","system":"The star hosts at least three known planets in the updated orbital fits (Standing et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the unusual activity of these stars really comes from evaporating close-in planets is what the survey tests (Standing et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp2c-Standingetal2026","cite":"Standing et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, John R. Barnes, Carole A. Haswell et al.","title":"The dispersed matter planet project sample ─ detection limits, occurrence rates, and new planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18207","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164604 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial-velocity data were combined with Hipparcos and Gaia position measurements to pin down the orbits of this planet and a newly found outer giant planet (Xiao et al. 2026). The inner planet has a very elongated orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.479, and a mass of 13.2 Jupiter masses or 8.8 depending on the orbit's tilt (Xiao et al. 2026). Its distance from the star is 1.362 au (Xiao et al. 2026).","system":"A second giant, HD 164604 c, orbits at 5.556 au with a mass of 9.5 or 7.6 Jupiter masses (Xiao et al. 2026). The two orbits are either nearly flat with each other, tilted by 5 degrees, or run in opposite directions, tilted by 162 degrees (Xiao et al. 2026).","history":"A retrograde pair would point to a violent past, with gravitational kicks between the planets or slow tilting cycles driven by a distant body (Xiao et al. 2026). A flat, same-direction pair would fit calm migration in the gas disc (Xiao et al. 2026).","open":"Which orientation is right is not known. Gaia DR4 astrometry is expected to settle it (Xiao et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164604b-Xiaoetal2026","cite":"Xiao et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Guang-Yao Xiao, Fabo Feng, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"HD 164604 c: a second giant planet on a 15-yr orbit and the constraint of the planet-planet mutual inclination","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10919","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-60 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Atmospheric-escape models, which take the planet's mass, radius and star as input, were run for many known planets (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). Kepler-60 d is on a short list of planets that could be losing gas right now at a rate of at least 10 million grams per second, if gas loss is driven by heat leaking from the planet's own core (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). No escaping gas has been measured.","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet really loses gas today depends on whether this core-powered process dominates its history (Gupta & Schlichting 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler60d-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-69 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is an active K dwarf about a billion years old (Anderson et al. 2014).","observed":"Helium absorption at 10830 angstroms during transit shows the planet is losing its upper atmosphere. Keck/NIRSPEC followed the outflow for 7.5 planet radii, starting 1.28 hours after the planet left the stellar disc (Tyler et al. 2024). The gas is blueshifted by 23 km/s and the loss rate is about 1 Earth mass per billion years (Tyler et al. 2024). Earlier work saw an outflow of only about 2.2 planet radii a year before (Tyler et al. 2024). Other estimates give 0.9 times 10^11 g/s at 5250 K (Lampon et al. 2023).","system":"The planet is a bloated, Saturn-mass world, with 0.26 Jupiter masses and 1.06 Jupiter radii (Anderson et al. 2014). X-rays were seen close to the star, which would mean heavy mass loss if the star is their source (Anderson et al. 2014). JWST also observed it in the 2.4 to 14 micron range (Schlawin et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The shape of the outflow is disputed. A 3D model finds no prominent comet-like tail (Wang & Dai 2021), while Keck data favour a tail shaped by the stellar wind (Tyler et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp69b-Andersonetal2014","cite":"Anderson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, L. Delrez et al.","title":"Three newly discovered sub-Jupiter-mass planets: WASP-69b and WASP-84b transit active K dwarfs and WASP-70Ab transits the evolved primary of a G4+K3 binary","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5654","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp69b-Tyleretal2024","cite":"Tyler et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Dakotah Tyler, Erik A. Petigura, Antonija Oklopčić et al.","title":"WASP-69b's Escaping Envelope Is Confined to a Tail Extending at Least 7 R<SUB>p</SUB>","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.02381","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp69b-Lamponetal2023","cite":"Lampon et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Lampón, M. López-Puertas, J. Sanz-Forcada et al.","title":"Characterisation of the upper atmospheres of HAT-P-32 b, WASP-69 b, GJ 1214 b, and WASP-76 b through their He I triplet absorption","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03839","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp69b-Schlawinetal2024","cite":"Schlawin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"E. Schlawin, S. Mukherjee, K. Ohno et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-69b JWST NIRCam & MIRI eclipse depth sp. (Schlawin+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp69b-WangDai2021","cite":"Wang & Dai 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Lile Wang, Fei Dai","title":"Metastable Helium Absorptions with 3D Hydrodynamics and Self-consistent Photochemistry. I. WASP-69b, Dimensionality, X-Ray and UV Flux Level, Spectral Types, and Flares","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.00042","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":26,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 115954 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"More than 20 years of radial-velocity data leave the tilt of such long orbits unknown, and so the true mass. Combining the velocities with position measurements of the star from space gave a true mass in the planetary range for HD 115954 b (Philipot et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd115954b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, P. Rubini et al.","title":"Updated characterization of long-period single companion by combining radial velocity, relative astrometry, and absolute astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01263","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-22 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with z-band photometry from the Faulkes Telescope South and with CORALIE and HARPS spectra (Maxted et al. 2010). Its star shows a steady drift of 40 m/s per year in its velocity, which points to a third body on a long orbit (Maxted et al. 2010). Follow-up transit light curves showed no timing variations (Southworth et al. 2016). The planet is puffier than gas-giant models predict (Southworth et al. 2016).","system":"The system is a hierarchical triple, with the extra companion possibly a low-mass M dwarf, a white dwarf or a second planet (Maxted et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"The nature of the outer body is not known (Maxted et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp22b-Maxtedetal2010","cite":"Maxted et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-22 b: A Transiting \"Hot Jupiter\" Planet in a Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.1514","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp22b-Southworthetal2016","cite":"Southworth et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"John Southworth, J. Tregloan-Reed, M. I. Andersen et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VIII. WASP-22, WASP-41, WASP-42 and WASP-55","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-371 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Validation of the planet's signal used reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging and statistical checks (Christiansen et al. 2022). The planet is listed as suitable for emission spectroscopy, which measures light from the planet's own heat (Christiansen et al. 2022). No atmosphere measurement has been reported.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2371b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NY Vir c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is inferred from the timing of eclipses, when one star passes in front of the other. In 2018, 18 nights of primary-eclipse timing were taken with a 0.5 m telescope in Arizona (Song et al. 2019). The signal is a small, regular drift in when the eclipses arrive (Song et al. 2019). The planet's existence has been questioned.","system":"The central pair is a very short-period eclipsing binary, a pulsating hot subdwarf B star with an M5 dwarf companion (Vuckovic et al. 2009). The hot star pulsates with periods of about 3 minutes, and its pulsations change over time (Kilkenny et al. 2003). It probably formed in a common-envelope phase, when the companion was swallowed by the expanding giant, and had a helium flash, with a mass between 0.39 and 0.48 solar masses (Hu et al. 2007).","history":"","open":"The exact masses of the two stars could not be fixed uniquely, which makes timing models harder (Vuckovic et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-nyvirc-Songetal2019","cite":"Song et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"S. Song, X. Mai, R. L. Mutel et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: NY Virginis primary mid-eclipse times (Song+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51570184","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nyvirc-Vuckovicetal2009","cite":"Vuckovic et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Vuckovic, R. Ostensen, J. H. Telting et al.","title":"Detection of line-profile variations in high-resolution VLT/UVES spectroscopy of the subdwarf B pulsator PG 1336018 (NY Virginis)","journal":"Communications in Asteroseismology","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nyvirc-Kilkennyetal2003","cite":"Kilkenny et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"D. Kilkenny, M. D. Reed, D. O'Donoghue et al.","title":"A Whole Earth Telescope campaign on the pulsating subdwarf B binary system PG 1336-018 (NY Vir)","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-8711.2003.07007.x","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nyvirc-Huetal2007","cite":"Hu et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Haili Hu, G. Nelemans, R. Østensen et al.","title":"An evolutionary study of the pulsating subdwarf B eclipsing binary <ASTROBJ>PG 1336-018</ASTROBJ> (NY Virginis)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.0106","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nyvirc-Vuckovicetal2007","cite":"Vuckovic et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Vučković, C. Aerts, R. Östensen et al.","title":"The binary properties of the pulsating subdwarf B eclipsing binary <ASTROBJ>PG 1336-018</ASTROBJ> (NY Virginis)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.3363","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 46375 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Starlight reflected by the planet was tentatively picked up in a 34-day CoRoT run: a faint signal of 13 to 26.8 parts per million matching the planet's phase (Gaulme et al. 2010). That would mean an albedo of 0.16 to 0.33, or a dayside visible brightness temperature of about 1880 to 2030 K (Gaulme et al. 2010). A competing signal from a 1-day period, blamed on Earth's atmosphere, blends with it (Gaulme et al. 2010).","system":"The star is an unevolved K0 star with high metallicity and a weak magnetic field of about 5 gauss (Gaulme et al. 2010). A wide stellar companion is confirmed by position and brightness measurements (Mugrauer et al. 2006).","history":"","open":"The reflected-light detection is only possible, not firm (Gaulme et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd46375b-Gaulmeetal2010","cite":"Gaulme et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"P. Gaulme, S. Deheuvels, W. W. Weiss et al.","title":"HD 46375: seismic and spectropolarimetric analysis of a young Sun hosting a Saturn-like planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.2671","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd46375b-Mugraueretal2006","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser, T. Mazeh et al.","title":"A search for wide visual companions of exoplanet host stars: The Calar Alto Survey","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0602067","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-3 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New radial-velocity data and Spitzer transit follow-up gave improved masses and radii for the planets of this bright M dwarf (Kosiarek et al. 2019). The Spitzer transits sharpened the transit times by more than a factor of 10 (Kosiarek et al. 2019). Stellar rotation and activity were modelled with ground-based photometry to separate them from the planet signals (Kosiarek et al. 2019). Planet c has a mass of 2.14 Earth masses, with a wide uncertainty (Kosiarek et al. 2019).","system":"Three planets are known, b, c and d, and d is only an upper limit of 2.8 Earth masses (Kosiarek et al. 2019). The planets have densities below 3.7 g per cubic centimetre (Kosiarek et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The planets are among the best targets for transmission spectroscopy, which would reveal their atmospheres (Kosiarek et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k23c-Kosiareketal2019","cite":"Kosiarek et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Molly R. Kosiarek, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman et al.","title":"Bright Opportunities for Atmospheric Characterization of Small Planets: Masses and Radii of K2-3 b, c, and d and GJ3470 b from Radial Velocity Measurements and Spitzer Transits","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08241","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-640 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of only three known hot Jupiters with a highly inflated radius above 1.7 Jupiter radii and a period over 5 days (Rodriguez et al. 2021). HARPS spectra taken during transit measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, giving a projected obliquity of 184 degrees (Knudstrup et al. 2023). The star's rotation shows it is viewed nearly pole-on, with a true tilt between star and orbit of 104 degrees (Knudstrup et al. 2023). The orbit therefore passes over the star's poles (Knudstrup et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved F star, a subgiant (Rodriguez et al. 2021). The planet has a mass of 0.57 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.72 Jupiter radii (Knudstrup et al. 2023).","history":"The large radius may result from the star's evolution (Rodriguez et al. 2021). The origin of such polar orbits is not well understood (Knudstrup et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi640b-Rodriguezetal2021","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, George Zhou et al.","title":"TESS Delivers Five New Hot Giant Planets Orbiting Bright Stars from the Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01726","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi640b-Knudstrupetal2023","cite":"Knudstrup et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Emil Knudstrup, Simon H. Albrecht, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"A puffy polar planet. The low density, hot Jupiter TOI-640 b is on a polar orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01702","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"70 Vir b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star's wobble was followed for 8 years, giving a minimum mass of 6.6 Jupiter masses and an eccentricity of 0.40 (Marcy & Butler 1996). Hipparcos data placed an upper mass limit of 38 to 65 Jupiter masses (Perryman et al. 1996). Marcy & Butler 1996 estimate its temperature at about 90 degrees Celsius. The first planet in a clearly eccentric orbit was this one (Fischer et al. 2014).","system":"The host is a quiet, solar-type star with roughly solar metallicity (Gonzalez 1996). Magnetically quiet stars like it favour planet detection by the Doppler method (Henry et al. 1997).","history":"The formation of giant planets in eccentric orbits is not explained by the theory of the time (Marcy & Butler 1996).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-70virb-MarcyButler1996","cite":"Marcy & Butler 1996","year":"1996","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler","title":"A Planetary Companion to 70 Virginis","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/310096","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-70virb-Perrymanetal1996","cite":"Perryman et al. 1996","year":"1996","authors":"M. A. C. Perryman, L. Lindegren, F. Arenou et al.","title":"HIPPARCOS distances and mass limits for the planetary candidates: 47 Ursae Majoris, 70 Virginis, and 51 Pegasi","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-70virb-Fischeretal2014","cite":"Fischer et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Julien F. P. Spronck","title":"The Twenty-five Year Lick Planet Search","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7315","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-70virb-Gonzalez1996","cite":"Gonzalez 1996","year":"1996","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez","title":"Spectroscopic Analyses of the Planetary System Candidates: 55 Cnc, 51 Peg, 47 UMa, 70 Vir, and HD 114762","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9609148","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-70virb-Henryetal1997","cite":"Henry et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Gregory W. Henry, Sallie L. Baliunas, Robert A. Donahue et al.","title":"Properties of Sun-like Stars with Planets: 51 Pegasi, 47 Ursae Majoris, 70 Virginis, and HD 114762","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/303451","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106515 A b":{"text":{"why":"The CORALIE survey that found it aims at Jupiter-period planets beyond the ice line (Marmier et al. 2013).","observed":"Its orbit lasts 9.8 years, with an eccentricity of 0.57 and a minimum mass of 9.33 Jupiter masses (Desidera et al. 2012). Combining velocities with positions from Gaia gives a true mass of 18.9 Jupiter masses, which puts it in the low-mass brown dwarf range (Li et al. 2021). Five of the seven new long-period planets in the same survey have very high eccentricities above 0.57 (Marmier et al. 2013).","system":"The star has a companion star about 7 arcseconds away, with an orbit of about 4800 years at 345 au (Rica et al. 2017). The planet orbits the primary at 4.59 au (Rica et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The companion star could allow a true mass measurement (Desidera et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106515ab-Marmieretal2013","cite":"Marmier et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Marmier, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVII. New and updated long period and massive planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6444","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106515ab-Desideraetal2012","cite":"Desidera et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S. Desidera, R. Gratton, E. Carolo et al.","title":"A long-period massive planet around HD 106515A","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.3963","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106515ab-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106515ab-Ricaetal2017","cite":"Rica et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"F. M. Rica, R. Barrena, J. A. Henríquez et al.","title":"Dynamical Study of the Exoplanet Host Binary System HD 106515","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/pasa.2016.59","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gliese 12 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the few Earth-sized worlds with mild temperatures that orbit a star this close, which makes it a good target for studying atmospheres (Kuzuhara et al. 2024).","observed":"Only three transits were seen at first, and gaps in the observations left the orbital period unclear (Dholakia et al. 2024). Follow-up photometry from the ground and from the CHEOPS satellite settled the period at 12.76 days (Kuzuhara et al. 2024; Dholakia et al. 2024). Radial velocity data from three spectrographs later gave the first precise mass, 0.95 Earth masses, alongside a radius of 0.93 Earth radii (Turner et al. 2026). The planet has been called an exo-Venus because of its size (Turner et al. 2026). Its equilibrium temperature is about 315 K (Dholakia et al. 2024).","system":"The star is an inactive, metal-poor red dwarf with one of the lowest activity levels known for such stars (Dholakia et al. 2024). X-ray observations confirmed the quiet behaviour (Kuzuhara et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet kept an atmosphere around its cool star is not known (Dholakia et al. 2024). A Hubble program was proposed to look for escaping hydrogen, a sign of a Venus-like runaway greenhouse (Vissapragada et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gliese12b-Kuzuharaetal2024","cite":"Kuzuhara et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Masayuki Kuzuhara, Akihiko Fukui, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"Gliese 12 b: A Temperate Earth-sized Planet at 12 pc Ideal for Atmospheric Transmission Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14708","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gliese12b-Dholakiaetal2024","cite":"Dholakia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shishir Dholakia, Larissa Palethorpe, Alexander Venner et al.","title":"Gliese 12 b, a temperate Earth-sized planet at 12 parsecs discovered with TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13118","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gliese12b-Turneretal2026","cite":"Turner et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Daisy A. Turner, Yoshi Nike Emilia Eschen, Felipe Murgas et al.","title":"The mass of the exo-Venus Gliese 12 b, as revealed by HARPS-N, ESPRESSO, and CARMENES","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20564","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gliese12b-Vissapragadaetal2024","cite":"Vissapragada et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shreyas Vissapragada, Shishir Dholakia, Annelies Mortier et al.","title":"The Exosphere of an Venus-Like Exoplanet","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110082 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits a young star, so it offers a view of a planetary system early in its life (Tofflemire et al. 2021).","observed":"Transits seen by TESS were confirmed with two transits observed by the Spitzer Space Telescope (Tofflemire et al. 2021). High-contrast imaging and high-resolution spectra of the star were also taken to rule out other explanations for the dips (Tofflemire et al. 2021). The host star turns out to be young, based on its fast rotation and lithium absorption (Tofflemire et al. 2021). The planet is a sub-Neptune, a size between Earth and Neptune (Tofflemire et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a late-F dwarf of 1.2 solar masses (Tofflemire et al. 2021). A red dwarf of 0.26 solar masses orbits it at a distance of about 6200 au, nearly one arcminute apart on the sky (Tofflemire et al. 2021).","history":"The star belongs to a previously unknown young group of stars, named MELANGE-1, with an age of about 250 million years (Tofflemire et al. 2021). The age was found by searching for stars moving together with it (Tofflemire et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110082b-Tofflemireetal2021","cite":"Tofflemire et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Benjamin M. Tofflemire, Aaron C. Rizzuto, Elisabeth R. Newton et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). V. A Sub-Neptune Transiting a Young Star in a Newly Discovered 250 Myr Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06066","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"55 Cnc c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Direct interferometry of the host star gave a radius of 0.943 solar radii and a temperature of 5196 K (von Braun et al. 2011). Isochrone fitting put the star's age at 10.2 billion years (von Braun et al. 2011). The habitable zone, where liquid water could exist on a planet's surface, was worked out to span 0.67 to 1.32 au (von Braun et al. 2011). The innermost planet, 55 Cnc e, transits the star, and the star's size allowed its diameter to be set at about 2.05 Earth radii (von Braun et al. 2011).","system":"The star hosts five planets in the work on its habitable zone (von Braun et al. 2011). A distant binary companion star pulls on the planetary system so that it precesses like a rigid body (Kaib et al. 2011). The planets are likely highly inclined to the star's spin axis, with a most likely angle of about 50 degrees and a 30 per cent chance of a retrograde arrangement (Kaib et al. 2011).","history":"Tidal evolution of planet e may have taken it through two secular resonances, where slow gravitational tugs from other planets build up (Hansen & Zink 2015). This could explain the large tilt between the innermost and outermost planets (Hansen & Zink 2015).","open":"The tilt between the planetary orbits and the star's spin has not been measured directly (Kaib et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-55cncc-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Theo A. ten Brummelaar et al.","title":"55 Cancri: Stellar Astrophysical Parameters, a Planet in the Habitable Zone, and Implications for the Radius of a Transiting Super-Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncc-Kaibetal2011","cite":"Kaib et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nathan A. Kaib, Sean N. Raymond, Martin J. Duncan","title":"55 Cancri: A Coplanar Planetary System That is Likely Misaligned with Its Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5911","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncc-HansenZink2015","cite":"Hansen & Zink 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Bradley M. S. Hansen, Jonathon Zink","title":"On the potentially dramatic history of the super-Earth ρ 55 Cancri e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05539","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1638 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No observation of the planet's atmosphere or surface has been reported. The planet appears in a theoretical study of the photosynthetic habitable zone, the range of distances where both liquid water and oxygen-producing photosynthesis could exist (Hall et al. 2023). Kepler-1638 b is one of five planets that sit inside this zone under a wide range of assumptions about life and atmosphere (Hall et al. 2023). Its day length is predicted to be between 9 and 11 hours (Hall et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has an atmosphere, liquid water or life is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1638b-Halletal2023","cite":"Hall et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"C. Hall, P. C. Stancil, J. P. Terry et al.","title":"A New Definition of Exoplanet Habitability: Introducing the Photosynthetic Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.13836","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 167768 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits a star that has already swollen into a giant, which is rare for a planet this close in (Teng et al. 2023).","observed":"The host is a G-type giant of 1.08 solar masses and 9.70 solar radii, with a low iron content (Teng et al. 2023). The planet's period of 20.65 days is among the shortest found by radial velocity around such deeply evolved stars (Teng et al. 2023). It has a minimum mass of 0.85 Jupiter masses and an orbit of 0.151 au (Teng et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 1874 K, as high as that of a hot Jupiter (Teng et al. 2023).","system":"The radial velocities show two more regular variations, at 41 days and 95 days, which may point to outer companions (Teng et al. 2023).","history":"Orbital calculations show that the planet will be engulfed by its expanding star within 0.15 billion years (Teng et al. 2023).","open":"Whether the 41-day and 95-day signals come from real outer planets is not known, and more monitoring is needed to check (Teng et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd167768b-Tengetal2023","cite":"Teng et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Masanobu Kunitomo et al.","title":"A close-in planet orbiting giant star HD 167768","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06576","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"G 9-40 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is called a keystone planet, a name for planets whose size and orbit help test how sub-Neptunes form and evolve (Luque et al. 2022).","observed":"A total of 69 new radial velocity measurements from the CARMENES spectrograph gave the first mass of the planet, 4.00 Earth masses, with a precision of 16 per cent (Luque et al. 2022). New TESS data narrowed the radius to 1.900 Earth radii, with an uncertainty of 3.4 per cent (Luque et al. 2022). The resulting density is too low for a purely rocky planet (Luque et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a mid-type red dwarf (Luque et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The planet may have a water-rich core or a significant hydrogen-rich envelope, and the data cannot yet tell which (Luque et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-g940b-Luqueetal2022","cite":"Luque et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"R. Luque, G. Nowak, T. Hirano et al.","title":"Precise mass determination for the keystone sub-Neptune planet transiting the mid-type M dwarf G 9-40","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07287","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1470 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is a nearby M1.5 red dwarf that TESS watched in four different sectors (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). A total of 44 radial velocity measurements from the CARMENES spectrograph were taken over eight months to separate the planets' signals from the star's activity (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). Photometry from ground-based telescopes in Spain and from the MuSCAT instruments helped confirm the transits (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). The star rotates once every 29 days or so (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). The system holds two sub-Neptunes (González-Álvarez et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-1470 b, transits every 2.5 days (González-Álvarez et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1470c-Gonzlezlvarezetal2023","cite":"González-Álvarez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. González-Álvarez, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, J. A. Caballero et al.","title":"Two sub-Neptunes around the M dwarf TOI-1470","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08516","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KIC 8121913 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found among a set of 60 candidates whose light curves showed small brightness changes from close-in companions that do not cross their star (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). Following up some of them tested whether that technique works (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","observed":"Radial velocity monitoring with the CAFE and HERMES spectrographs checked ten of the candidates along their orbits (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). KIC 8121913 b was confirmed as one of three new hot Jupiters from that group (Lillo-Box et al. 2021). High-resolution images from AstraLux were also taken for some candidates to rule out blended binary stars that can mimic a planet's signal (Lillo-Box et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kic8121913b-LilloBoxetal2021","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, S. Millholland, G. Laughlin","title":"Follow-up of non-transiting planets detected by Kepler. Confirmation of three hot-Jupiters and validation of three other planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14621","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-269 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit is strongly eccentric, with e = 0.425, one of the highest values among planets with periods under 10 days (Cointepas et al. 2021). The planet lies close to the transition between super-Earths and sub-Neptunes (Cointepas et al. 2021). It is among the best targets of its kind for atmospheric study by transmission spectroscopy (Cointepas et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"The star is likely a few billion years old, so the system does not look dynamically young (Cointepas et al. 2021). The high eccentricity is therefore not a leftover from a recent upheaval (Cointepas et al. 2021).","open":"How the planet came to have such an eccentric orbit is not settled (Cointepas et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi269b-Cointepasetal2021","cite":"Cointepas et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"M. Cointepas, J. M. Almenara, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"TOI-269 b: an eccentric sub-Neptune transiting a M2 dwarf revisited with ExTrA","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.14782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110113 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet does not transit, so it was found only through its pull on the star (Osborn et al. 2021).","observed":"Radial velocities from the HARPS spectrograph, gathered by the NCORES program, showed a second planet with a minimum mass of 10.5 Earth masses and a period of about 6.74 days (Osborn et al. 2021). The star's own rotation signal, with a period of about 20.8 days, is strong and had to be removed first (Osborn et al. 2021). A new fit to several activity indicators at once modelled that rotation signal (Osborn et al. 2021).","system":"The inner planet, HD 110113 b, is a transiting mini-Neptune on a 2.5-day orbit around a Sun-like star (Osborn et al. 2021). It has a radius of 2.05 Earth radii and a mass of 4.55 Earth masses, and it still holds a thin hydrogen-helium atmosphere despite sitting in the radius valley (Osborn et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110113c-Osbornetal2021","cite":"Osborn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"H. P. Osborn, D. J. Armstrong, V. Adibekyan et al.","title":"A hot mini-Neptune in the radius valley orbiting solar analogue HD 110113","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.04745","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-67 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was chosen for a direct comparison with HAT-P-38 b, which has nearly the same equilibrium temperature of about 1050 K and gravity (Bruno et al. 2018). Differences in their spectra can then be traced to something other than those two factors (Bruno et al. 2018).","observed":"Hubble's WFC3 camera measured the planet's transmission spectrum in infrared light (Bruno et al. 2018). WASP-67 b has 0.27 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.83 Jupiter radii (Bruno et al. 2018). Its water absorption is more muted than that of HAT-P-38 b, which shows a clear water detection (Bruno et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"The difference between the two planets supports the idea that giant planet atmospheres carry traces of how the planets formed (Bruno et al. 2018).","open":"The muted water feature could mean a higher cloud layer or a more metal-rich atmosphere (Bruno et al. 2018). Visible and mid-infrared observations are needed to tell which (Bruno et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp67b-Brunoetal2018","cite":"Bruno et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Giovanni Bruno, Nikole K. Lewis, Kevin B. Stevenson et al.","title":"A Comparative Study of WASP-67 b and HAT-P-38 b from WFC3 Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.03384","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TIC 139270665 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity measurements from the Automated Planet Finder showed signs of a second planet beyond the inner transiting one (Peluso et al. 2024). Its period is about 1010 days, with a wide uncertainty from about 790 to 1790 days, and its minimum mass is 4.89 Jupiter masses (Peluso et al. 2024). Only evidence for it was found, not a firm confirmation (Peluso et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet, TIC 139270665 b, is a warm, dense sub-Saturn with a period of 23.6 days (Peluso et al. 2024). It circles a metal-rich G2 star (Peluso et al. 2024). Citizen scientists first spotted its transit in TESS data (Peluso et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The orbit of the outer planet is poorly known because only part of one orbit has been covered (Peluso et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tic139270665c-Pelusoetal2024","cite":"Peluso et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daniel O'Conner Peluso, Paul A. Dalba, Duncan Wright et al.","title":"Confirming the Warm and Dense Sub-Saturn TIC 139270665 b with the Automated Planet Finder and Unistellar Citizen Science Network","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ad2772","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1680 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was checked with TESS and with ground-based photometry from TRAPPIST, SPECULOOS and LCO (Ghachoui et al. 2023). High-resolution adaptive optics images from Keck and Shane ruled out nearby stars that could mimic the signal (Ghachoui et al. 2023). The mass has not been measured; the figure used comes from mass-radius relations (Ghachoui et al. 2023). The equilibrium temperature is 404 K, assuming no reflected light and perfect heat sharing around the planet (Ghachoui et al. 2023). The planet is a promising target for atmospheric study with the James Webb Space Telescope (Ghachoui et al. 2023).","system":"The host is an inactive M4.5 dwarf with a mass of 0.18 solar masses (Ghachoui et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1680b-Ghachouietal2023","cite":"Ghachoui et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Ghachoui, A. Soubkiou, R. D. Wells et al.","title":"TESS discovery of a super-Earth orbiting the M-dwarf star TOI-1680","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05368","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 219134 is a K3 dwarf only 6.53 parsecs away with six reported planets found by radial velocity (Bailey 2023). The inner planet, HD 219134 b, was seen transiting by the Spitzer Space Telescope and was the nearest known transiting planet at that time (Motalebi et al. 2015). Planet c also transits (Bailey 2023). Searches with TESS and the small ASTERIA satellite ruled out transits of planet f (Bailey 2023).","system":"The star is bright and close, so the system could be the nearest compact multiple transiting system (Bailey 2023). Planet b has a mass of 4.36 Earth masses and a radius of 1.606 Earth radii, which suggests a rocky composition (Motalebi et al. 2015).","history":"Planet b has a bulk density about 10 per cent lower than planet c, which could be explained by minerals rich in calcium and aluminium that condense early from the disc (Dorn et al. 2019). The uncertainties are too large to rule out equal densities (Dorn et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134g-Bailey2023","cite":"Bailey 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vanessa Bailey","title":"HD 219134 Revisited: Planet d Transit Upper Limit and Planet f Transit Non-Detection with ASTERIA and TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.MTOJHC","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134g-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134g-Dornetal2019","cite":"Dorn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Dorn, J. H. D. Harrison, A. Bonsor et al.","title":"A new class of Super-Earths formed from high-temperature condensates: HD219134 b, 55 Cnc e, WASP-47 e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07222","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TrES-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first transiting planet found in the field of view of the Kepler mission, so it could be followed with great precision by Kepler (O'Donovan et al. 2006). The survey that turned it up is a network of three small-aperture telescopes (O'Donovan et al. 2006).","observed":"High-precision radial velocities confirmed the signal with the period and phase predicted by the photometry (O'Donovan et al. 2006). The planet has a mass of 1.28 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.24 Jupiter radii, with transits 1.4 per cent deep (O'Donovan et al. 2006). It was the most massive of the nearby transiting planets known at the time (O'Donovan et al. 2006). Later timing of ten transits refined the orbital period to 2.470614 days (Raetz et al. 2009). A second dip after the transit was also reported (Raetz et al. 2009).","system":"The second dip could come from a blended variable star, the passing of a second star, or an additional object in the system (Raetz et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"The cause of the second dip after the transit is not known (Raetz et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tres2b-ODonovanetal2006","cite":"O'Donovan et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Francis T. O'Donovan, David Charbonneau, Georgi Mandushev et al.","title":"TrES-2: The First Transiting Planet in the Kepler Field","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609335","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres2b-Raetzetal2009","cite":"Raetz et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"St. Raetz, M. Mugrauer, T. O. B. Schmidt et al.","title":"Planetary transit observations at the University Observatory Jena: TrES-2","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.1842","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":54,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-12 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has the longest period of any planet found by the main NGTS survey (Bryant et al. 2020). Radial velocities from HARPS and FEROS gave a mass of 0.208 Jupiter masses, and a radius of 1.048 Jupiter radii, so the density is a low 0.223 g per cubic centimetre (Bryant et al. 2020). When the stellar properties are taken into account, the planet sits on the edge of the Neptunian desert, a region where planets of this size are rare (Bryant et al. 2020). Its long period and low density make it a good target for transmission spectroscopy (Bryant et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts12b-Bryantetal2020","cite":"Bryant et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss, Louise D. Nielsen et al.","title":"NGTS-12b: A sub-Saturn mass transiting exoplanet in a 7.53 day orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10620","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New observations refined the planet's orbital period to a precision 19 times better than before (Boyle et al. 2022). The expected transit timing variation, a shift in transit times caused by neighbouring planets, is about 7 minutes (Boyle et al. 2022). Timing uncertainty in the K2 data was about 10 minutes, and the new data were too sparse to confirm a variation (Boyle et al. 2022).","system":"K2-138 d is one of five planets in a chain of near 3:2 orbital resonances (Boyle et al. 2022). A sixth planet, K2-138 g, was confirmed by Spitzer with a period of 41.966 days and is not part of the chain (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Citizen scientists in the Exoplanet Explorers project helped find the inner planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Radial velocity masses for the inner four have been obtained (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","history":"Dynamical evidence points to the first known first-order three-planet mean-motion resonance in a real system (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024). Capture into it during migration is favoured by short-period planets of a few Earth masses and by a disc of lower surface density (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024).","open":"A large gap lies between the fifth and sixth planets, and non-transiting planets there are possible (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138d-Boyleetal2022","cite":"Boyle et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Andrew Boyle, Jessie L. Christiansen, Shreyas Vissapragada et al.","title":"An Updated Ephemeris for K2-138 d","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ac64fe","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138d-HardegreeUllmanetal2021","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie L. Christiansen, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Planet for the Citizen Science System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08978","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138d-CerioniBeaug2024","cite":"Cerioni & Beaugé 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cerioni, C. Beaugé","title":"K2-138 and the capture in 1st-order 3P-MMRs","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1478 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is part of the Hot Rocks Survey, which asks whether close-in rocky planets around red dwarfs can keep any atmosphere at all (August et al. 2025).","observed":"The James Webb Space Telescope's MIRI instrument watched two secondary eclipses at 15 micrometres, when the planet passes behind its star, to measure heat from the planet's day side (August et al. 2025). One eclipse gave a depth of 138 ± 53 ppm, a moderate 2.8 sigma detection (August et al. 2025). The second observation gave no detection because of larger unexplained systematics (August et al. 2025). Earlier work found a density of 6.67 g per cubic centimetre, consistent with a rocky iron and silicate composition (Soto et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a quiet M3 red dwarf (Soto et al. 2021). The planet receives 21 times the instellation of Earth and has an equilibrium temperature of 585 K (August et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has an atmosphere is not settled, and more eclipse data are needed (August et al. 2025). Models favour a non-eccentric orbit (August et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1478b-Sotoetal2021","cite":"Soto et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"M. G. Soto, G. Anglada-Escudé, S. Dreizler et al.","title":"Mass and density of the transiting hot and rocky super-Earth LHS 1478 b (TOI-1640 b)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.11640","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WD 1856+534 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The Mid-Infrared Instrument on JWST saw extra mid-infrared light coming from the white dwarf, matching a Jupiter-sized planet in a close orbit. It is the coldest exoplanet from which light has ever been directly observed (Limbach et al. 2025). The measurement limits the planet's mass to no more than six times that of Jupiter, which rules out a low-mass brown dwarf (Limbach et al. 2025). A Gemini spectrum built from ten transits showed no prominent absorption features. Because the planet is 8 times larger than its star, it covers over half of the stellar disc at mid-transit (Xu et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a white dwarf that shows no detectable metals in its atmosphere, although many white dwarfs with planetary systems do (Zhang et al. 2026). Simulations of asteroids heading for the star test whether a close-in planet can shield it from such pollution (Zhang et al. 2026).","history":"The planet is the first intact exoplanet confirmed inside a white dwarf's forbidden zone, where planets should have been engulfed when the star swelled into a red giant. Its presence is direct evidence that planets can migrate into close orbits around white dwarfs (Limbach et al. 2025).","open":"A tentative sign that the orbit is growing has been measured, but it is not yet treated as real until longer follow-up exists (Alvarado et al. 2024). Whether clouds hide the atmosphere is not known (Xu et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wd1856534b-Limbachetal2025","cite":"Limbach et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mary Anne Limbach, Andrew Vanderburg, Ryan J. MacDonald et al.","title":"Thermal Emission and Confirmation of the Frigid White Dwarf Exoplanet WD 1856+534 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16982","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd1856534b-Xuetal2021","cite":"Xu et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Siyi Xu, Hannah Diamond-Lowe, Ryan J. MacDonald et al.","title":"Gemini/GMOS Transmission Spectroscopy of the Grazing Planet Candidate WD 1856+534 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14106","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd1856534b-Zhangetal2026","cite":"Zhang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Xin-Yue Zhang, Ji-Wei Xie, Di-Chang Chen et al.","title":"How a close-in planet protects its white dwarf host from pollution","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15005","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd1856534b-Alvaradoetal2024","cite":"Alvarado et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Efrain Alvarado, Kate B. Bostow, Kishore C. Patra et al.","title":"Searching for tidal orbital decay in hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04660","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2024-BLG-1281L b":{"text":{"why":"The search that turned it up picked out events with anomalies shorter than one day, which can be signs of low-mass planets (Han et al. 2025).","observed":"The brightening of a background star by the planet's host showed a very brief anomaly. Modelling the light curve shows that a planet orbiting the lens causes it. The anomaly was short because the planet's caustics, the zones where the lens magnifies the source most strongly, are small and elongated. They limited the region where the background star could interact with them (Han et al. 2025). The physical properties of the lens system were estimated statistically (Han et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The distance and the host star are only estimated statistically, so they are not pinned down by direct measurement (Han et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2024blg1281lb-Hanetal2025","cite":"Han et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"Six microlensing planets detected via sub-day signals during the 2023─2024 season","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05522","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2537 b":{"text":{"why":"Warm Jupiters orbit far enough from their stars that effects of close proximity are small, so they give cleaner clues to how planets form and evolve (Heidari et al. 2025).","observed":"TOI-2537 b is one of three newly found warm Jupiters, a group with orbital periods between 30 and 94 days, masses of 0.9 to 2.9 Jupiter masses and radii of 1.0 to 1.5 Jupiter radii. It was studied with space photometry, radial velocities from the SOPHIE spectrograph and high-resolution imaging (Heidari et al. 2025). Transiting warm Jupiters are still few in number (Heidari et al. 2025).","system":"The star hosts at least one more planet, TOI-2537 c, on a much longer orbit of about 1920 days. Its minimum mass is about 7.2 times that of Jupiter (Heidari et al. 2025).","history":"Warm Jupiters are generally believed to have formed beyond the snowline and then moved inward to their present orbits (Heidari et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2537b-Heidarietal2025","cite":"Heidari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Heidari, G. Hébrard, E. Martioli et al.","title":"Characterization of seven transiting systems, including four warm Jupiters from SOPHIE and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08527","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-32 f":{"text":{"why":"The star is a typical red dwarf of the kind that makes up about 70% of all stars in the Galaxy, so its planets may be a blueprint for the most common planets (Swift et al. 2013).","observed":"Five planets cross the face of this star, which makes it the richest known system of transiting planets around a red dwarf. All five orbit within one-third the size of Mercury's orbit, and the innermost sits only 4.3 stellar radii from the star's surface. New observations ruled out false alarms and validated the whole system (Swift et al. 2013).","system":"Three adjacent planets have orbital periods close to simple ratios of each other (Swift et al. 2013).","history":"The system is proposed as a prototype for how compact planetary systems formed throughout the Galaxy. The argument rests on the dust sublimation radius, a minimum-mass protoplanetary nebula and the near-matching periods of three neighbouring planets (Swift et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler32f-Swiftetal2013","cite":"Swift et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jonathan J. Swift, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. IV. Kepler-32 as a Prototype for the Formation of Compact Planetary Systems throughout the Galaxy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-37 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-37 A b is a Neptune-sized planet whose mass is only loosely measured, at about 31.5 plus or minus 13.4 Earth masses (Jordán et al. 2020). It lies in the Neptune desert, a region where such planets are rarely found, and is among the lowest-mass planets found by ground-based wide-field surveys (Jordán et al. 2020).","system":"The host star appears to have an unresolved companion star, HATS-37 B, with an estimated mass of 0.654 solar masses (Jordán et al. 2020). The star is old, with an estimated age of about 11.5 billion years (Jordán et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats37ab-Jordnetal2020","cite":"Jordán et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. Jordán, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-37Ab and HATS-38b: Two Transiting Hot Neptunes in the Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07135","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer giants Kepler-90 g and Kepler-90 h. Their transit times are shifted by up to 25 hours by their mutual gravity, which allowed their masses to be measured. Kepler-90 g has an unusually low apparent density of 0.15 grams per cubic centimetre, which makes it a super-puff (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"The orbital periods of Kepler-90 g and h are close to a 2:3 ratio, but they are not locked in resonance. The arrangement is stable over billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"Interactions between the planets appear able to raise their orbital eccentricities and break a resonant lock that formed early on (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351e-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2018-BLG-1025L b":{"text":{"why":"The target events were those with high peak magnification (Han et al. 2021).","observed":"The event lasted about 10 days and at first drew little attention, because its light curve looked like that of an ordinary event with one lens and one source (Han et al. 2021). A small anomaly in the light curve points to a companion with a very low mass ratio to the lens star. Three solutions fit the data. One of them belongs to a new type of ambiguity that had not been reported before (Han et al. 2021). Solution A gives a planet of about 6.1 Earth masses around a host of 0.22 solar masses at about 6.7 kiloparsecs (Han et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The three solutions give different mass ratios, so the planet's mass is uncertain (Han et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg1025lb-Hanetal2021","cite":"Han et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Chung-Uk Lee et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-1025Lb: microlensing super-Earth planet orbiting a low-mass star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.01890","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2022-BLG-249L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The microlensing event showed a brief bump that lasted about one day and deviated by up to about 0.2 magnitudes from a single-lens model (Han et al. 2023). A planet around the lens explains the bump better than a faint companion to the background star does (Han et al. 2023). Two solutions fit equally well because of an inner-outer ambiguity in the planet's position, and both give a very small mass ratio of about 8 in 100,000 (Han et al. 2023). The host is a low-mass star of about 0.18 solar masses in the Galactic disc (Han et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2022blg249lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrew Gould, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"MOA-2022-BLG-249Lb: Nearby microlensing super-Earth planet detected from high-cadence surveys","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02815","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 133131 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is one of two Sun-like twins in a binary about 360 au wide. The twin HD 133131 B hosts a long-period planet of its own, and HD 133131 A hosts two eccentric planets (Teske et al. 2016). Their orbits were traced by years of Doppler measurements from the MIKE and PFS spectrographs on the Magellan telescopes (Teske et al. 2016). The inner planet has a minimum mass of about 1.43 Jupiter masses at about 1.44 au (Teske et al. 2016). These are the first planets reported mainly from PFS data (Teske et al. 2016).","system":"Both stars are old, likely about 9.5 billion years, and metal-poor. A small depletion of about 0.03 dex in refractory elements was detected in HD 133131 A (Teske et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd133131ab-Teskeetal2016","cite":"Teske et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Johanna K. Teske, Stephen A. Shectman, Steve S. Vogt et al.","title":"The Magellan PFS Planet Search Program: Radial Velocity and Stellar Abundance Analyses of the 360 au, Metal-poor Binary “Twins” HD 133131A & B","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.06216","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-98 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Neptune-like world whose mass and radius were both measured with a precision better than 25%. Only a small group of Neptune-size planets have measurements that good (Barragán et al. 2016). Ground-based photometry and spectroscopy confirmed the planet and gave its mass (Barragán et al. 2016).","system":"The host is an F-type star of about 1.07 solar masses and an age of about 5.2 billion years (Barragán et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The star is evolving toward the red giant stage, and the planet is estimated to be swallowed by it in about 3 billion years (Barragán et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k298b-Barragnetal2016","cite":"Barragán et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Oscar Barragán, Sascha Grziwa, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"EPIC 211391664b: A 32 M <SUB>⊕</SUB> Neptune-size Planet in a 10 Day Orbit Transiting an F8 Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.01165","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 52265 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck Doppler measurements showed a period of 119 days, an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.29 and a minimum mass of 1.13 Jupiter masses at 0.49 au (Butler et al. 2000). The star is metal-rich and shows little magnetic activity (Butler et al. 2000).","system":"The star was also observed for 117 days by the CoRoT space telescope, which found solar-like oscillations and allowed precise measurements of its properties (Ballot et al. 2011). Its rotation speed along the line of sight is about 3.6 km per second (Ballot et al. 2011).","history":"The star belongs to the group of planet hosts that are richer in heavy elements than typical stars nearby (Butler et al. 2000). A volume-limited comparison later confirmed this metal-rich nature (Santos et al. 2001).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd52265b-Butleretal2000","cite":"Butler et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to the Metal-rich Stars BD -10°3166 and HD 52265","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/317796","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd52265b-Ballotetal2011","cite":"Ballot et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. Ballot, L. Gizon, R. Samadi et al.","title":"Accurate p-mode measurements of the G0V metal-rich CoRoT target HD 52265","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3551","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd52265b-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, G. Israelian, M. Mayor","title":"The metal-rich nature of stars with planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0105216","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 422 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet candidate came from a re-analysis of public Doppler data of 41 nearby red dwarfs, aimed at finding low-amplitude signals and estimating how common low-mass planets are (Tuomi et al. 2014).","observed":"The signal was picked out with statistical criteria that account for noise correlated in time. It is one of eight new planet candidates reported around nearby red dwarfs in that sample (Tuomi et al. 2014). The star was also one of three observed in a search for laser flashes from an alien civilisation. No signal was reported, and upper limits on the power of any laser were derived (Welsh et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj422b-Tuomietal2014","cite":"Tuomi et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Mikko Tuomi, Hugh R. A. Jones, John R. Barnes et al.","title":"Bayesian search for low-mass planets around nearby M dwarfs - estimates for occurrence rate based on global detectability statistics","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.0430","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj422b-Welshetal2018","cite":"Welsh et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Barry Welsh, John Vallerga, Marissa Kotze et al.","title":"Novel Optical SETI Observations of Three Exoplanets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2407 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Neptune-sized world with an equilibrium temperature of about 705 K, orbiting an early red dwarf (Janó Muñoz et al. 2025). It was validated with ground-based follow-up at several wavelengths. One observation used a new infrared camera with a CMOS detector on the SPECULOOS telescope, which shows how well such detectors can work for red dwarfs (Janó Muñoz et al. 2025). The planet lies inside the Neptune desert, where Neptune-sized planets on short orbits are scarce (Janó Muñoz et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass has not been measured. Radial-velocity follow-up is expected to refine its place in the Neptune desert (Janó Muñoz et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2407b-Muozetal2025","cite":"Muñoz et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"C. Janó Muñoz, M. J. Hooton, P. P. Pedersen et al.","title":"TOI-2407 b: a warm Neptune in the desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06195","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 103891 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a search of fifteen years of public HARPS data, aimed at long-period massive candidates around Sun-like stars (Sreenivas et al. 2022).","observed":"The signal repeats every 1919 days. The minimum mass is about 1.44 Jupiter masses, which makes the planet a Jupiter analogue. Its orbit is slightly eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.31 (Sreenivas et al. 2022). Checks against stellar activity indicators showed no matching signal, which argues against a false detection (Sreenivas et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved F8 dwarf star (Sreenivas et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd103891b-Sreenivasetal2022","cite":"Sreenivas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"K. R. Sreenivas, V. Perdelwitz, L. Tal-Or et al.","title":"Analysis of the public HARPS/ESO spectroscopic archive. Jupiter-like planets around HD103891 and HD105779","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.09029","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-47 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-47 d is a Neptune-sized planet whose transit times drift from their expected schedule through gravitational tugs from its neighbours. These shifts confirmed that it is a planet and helped to measure its mass (Becker et al. 2015). The system serves as a test case for finding planets through transit-timing shifts (Becker et al. 2015). ESPRESSO measurements refined the mass of WASP-47 d (Bryant & Bayliss 2022).","system":"The star hosts a hot Jupiter with a period of 4.159 days, a super-Earth at 0.7896 days, this Neptune at 9.031 days and a giant planet at 588.4 days (Bryant & Bayliss 2022). The hot Jupiter was the only one known to have such close companions (Vanderburg et al. 2018). The outer giant likely orbits close to the plane of the inner planets (Vanderburg et al. 2018). The star rotates in about 32.5 days (Bryant & Bayliss 2022).","history":"Orbits this flat disfavour hot Jupiter models that rely on strong dynamical interactions such as Kozai-Lidov migration (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp47d-Beckeretal2015","cite":"Becker et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Juliette C. Becker, Andrew Vanderburg, Fred C. Adams et al.","title":"WASP-47: A Hot Jupiter System with Two Additional Planets Discovered by K2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02411","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47d-BryantBayliss2022","cite":"Bryant & Bayliss 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss","title":"Revisiting WASP-47 with ESPRESSO and TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47d-Vanderburgetal2018","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Juliette Becker, David W. Latham et al.","title":"WASP-47 and the Origin of Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5573 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Saturn-like world, with a radius of 9.75 Earth radii and a low density of about 0.66 grams per cubic centimetre, akin to Saturn's. It orbits an early red dwarf (Fernandes et al. 2025). It was confirmed using 11 transits from four TESS sectors, ground-based photometry and radial velocities from two Doppler spectrographs (Fernandes et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5573b-Fernandesetal2025","cite":"Fernandes et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Rachel B. Fernandes, Shubham Kanodia, Megan Delamer et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Confirmation of TOI-5573 b, a Cool, Saturn-like Planet Orbiting an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.08947","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2120 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of four sub-Neptunes around red dwarfs studied together. Doppler monitoring with the Subaru InfraRed Doppler instrument set a 2-sigma upper limit on its mass of 6.8 Earth masses (Hori et al. 2024). Its radius is about 2.12 Earth radii (Hori et al. 2024). The mass-radius relation of the four planets shows volatile material in their interiors (Hori et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The four planets lie above the radius valley, a dip in the number of planets at certain sizes. They probably keep a significant atmosphere or an icy mantle over the core, as in a water world (Hori et al. 2024).","open":"Only an upper limit on the mass exists, so the planet's makeup is not known (Hori et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2120b-Horietal2024","cite":"Hori et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Yasunori Hori, Akihiko Fukui, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"The Discovery and Follow-up of Four Transiting Short-period Sub-Neptunes Orbiting M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12637","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-bin-29 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event was seen in 2006 and fits five different solutions, all with a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 1 in 100 (Kondo et al. 2019). The short event time of 4 to 7 days points to a low-mass host (Kondo et al. 2019). Only finite-source effects were detected, so the properties come from statistical analysis. The planet is likely a gas giant of about 0.63 Jupiter masses orbiting a brown dwarf of about 0.06 solar masses in the Galactic bulge. Their projected separation is about 0.53 au (Kondo et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The masses and distance are statistical estimates and carry large uncertainties (Kondo et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moabin29b-Kondoetal2019","cite":"Kondo et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Iona Kondo, Takahiro Sumi, David P. Bennett et al.","title":"MOA-bin-29b: A Microlensing Gas-giant Planet Orbiting a Low-mass Host Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.01239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-38 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found after an upgrade to the SuperWASP-North cameras. It has the longest period of any planet found by SuperWASP-North (Barros et al. 2010).","observed":"The orbit is slightly eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.0314, measured precisely from transit timing and the shape of the velocity signal (Barros et al. 2010). The planet's equilibrium temperature is about 1292 K (Barros et al. 2010). Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements, which track how the planet crosses the spinning star, show the orbit is aligned with the star's rotation (Brown et al. 2012). Earlier work reached the same result with a larger uncertainty (Simpson et al. 2011). The CHEOPS space telescope timed its transits to within about 13 to 16 seconds (Borsato et al. 2021).","system":"The host star is bright and of spectral type F8 (Barros et al. 2010).","history":"Massive planets on moderately long, eccentric orbits are often misaligned, so the aligned orbit stands out (Simpson et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp38b-Barrosetal2010","cite":"Barros et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, F. Faedi, Cameron A. Collier et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Transits of WASP-38b (Barros+, 2011)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35250054","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp38b-Brownetal2012","cite":"Brown et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. Collier Cameron, R. F. Díaz et al.","title":"Analysis of Spin-Orbit Alignment in the WASP-32, WASP-38, and HAT-P-27/WASP-40 Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5649","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp38b-Simpsonetal2011","cite":"Simpson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"E. K. Simpson, D. Pollacco, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"The spin-orbit angles of the transiting exoplanets WASP-1b, WASP-24b, WASP-38b and HAT-P-8b from Rossiter-McLaughlin observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.5664","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp38b-Borsatoetal2021","cite":"Borsato et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L. Borsato, G. Piotto, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Exploiting timing capabilities of the CHEOPS mission with warm-Jupiter planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11331","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up scanned for transits across bright stars, and the transit was clear enough that no extra photometry was needed to confirm it (Raynard et al. 2018).","observed":"Its density is only about 0.23 grams per cubic centimetre, which makes it one of the lowest-density exoplanets known (Raynard et al. 2018). The host star is bright and the transit is deep, so the planet is a good target for atmospheric study (Raynard et al. 2018). One transit with JWST's NIRSpec instrument gave the first look at its atmosphere (Fairman et al. 2026). The spectrum is muted, with only weak water and carbon dioxide features (Fairman et al. 2026). Fits to these weak features favour a heavy, metal-rich atmosphere, but some fits exceed the 43.5 times solar limit set by interior models (Fairman et al. 2026). Clouds and metal content can mimic each other, and the wavelength range was too short to separate them (Fairman et al. 2026).","system":"The star rotates quickly, and the planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin axis (Raynard et al. 2018; Fairman et al. 2026).","history":"The JWST observation belongs to a programme that compares hot Jupiters on aligned and misaligned orbits around F stars. It aims to link atmospheric makeup to how such planets formed (Fairman et al. 2026).","open":"Whether clouds or a very metal-rich atmosphere flatten the spectrum is not settled (Fairman et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts2b-Raynardetal2018","cite":"Raynard et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Liam Raynard, Michael R. Goad, Edward Gillen et al.","title":"NGTS-2b: an inflated hot-Jupiter transiting a bright F-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.10449","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts2b-Fairmanetal2026","cite":"Fairman et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Charlotte Fairman, Hannah R. Wakeford, Alastair B. Claringbold et al.","title":"BOWIE-ALIGN: exploring degeneracies in the muted transmission spectrum of the aligned hot Jupiter NGTS-2b with NIRSpec/G395H","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18332","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 75302 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its orbit and mass come from combining archival HARPS velocities with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of the star's motion; it is one of six new planets found this way, with separations between 1 and 6 au and masses between 0.6 and 100 Jupiter masses across the whole group (Philipot et al. 2023). Its host star was also included in a study of the chemical makeup of 25 solar-like stars (Sun et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd75302b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, F. Kiefer et al.","title":"A multi-technique approach to identifying and/or constraining radial-velocity substellar companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05417","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75302b-Sunetal2025","cite":"Sun et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Q. Sun, C. Ji, S. X. Wang et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: PASTA II. Abundances for 25 solar twins and analogs (Sun+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-157 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Observations from the K2 mission show a moderately inflated planet with a density similar to Saturn's (Močnik et al. 2016). The star's light shows no sign of rotational variation or of a phase curve, the slow brightening and dimming as the planet's day and night sides turn toward us (Močnik et al. 2016). The secondary eclipse, when the planet passes behind the star, was also not detected (Močnik et al. 2016). Upper limits on these signals are 250 parts per million for the modulations and 20 parts per million for the eclipse (Močnik et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp157b-Moniketal2016","cite":"Močnik et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"T. Močnik, D. R. Anderson, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"WASP-157b, a Transiting Hot Jupiter Observed with K2","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.05638","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5704 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed as real by statistical validation: its light curve was checked by eye and a statistical tool computed the chance of a false alarm (Mistry et al. 2023). It is a short-period Neptune-like planet lying in the hot-Neptune desert, a region where such planets are rare (Mistry et al. 2023). Its brightness makes it suitable for emission spectroscopy, which measures light from the planet itself (Mistry et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5704b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Aniket Prasad et al.","title":"VaTEST. II. Statistical Validation of 11 TESS-detected Exoplanets Orbiting K-type Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5938 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a survey of K-dwarf stars. Such stars are less often searched than the smaller M dwarfs, which are easier to observe (Morello et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed by statistical validation using photometric, spectroscopic and high-resolution imaging data (Morello et al. 2026). It sits at the edge of the Neptune desert, a region where Neptune-sized planets are rare on very short orbits, which makes it a rare addition to the known sub-Neptune population (Morello et al. 2026). Its size and host star make it suitable for precise mass measurement and atmospheric study with current facilities (Morello et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass has not yet been measured precisely (Morello et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5938b-Morelloetal2026","cite":"Morello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Morello, A. Peláez-Torres, F. J. Pozuelos et al.","title":"The K-dwarfs survey ─ I. Four validated planets from the radius valley to the Neptune desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag183","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 81040 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities alone leave a planet's mass uncertain, because the tilt of its orbit is unknown. Combining them with Gaia and Hipparcos measurements of the star's motion resolves this. The method gave HD 81040 b a mass of about 7.2 Jupiter masses, confirming it as a planet (Li et al. 2021). A separate study found that the orbit from Gaia's own two-body solution agrees with the Doppler data, and fitted them jointly for the best orbit and mass (Winn 2022). Two orbit tilts remain possible, since prograde and retrograde motion are hard to tell apart (Li et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the orbit runs prograde or retrograde is not settled (Li et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd81040b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd81040b-Winn2022","cite":"Winn 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Joshua N. Winn","title":"Joint Constraints on Exoplanetary Orbits from Gaia DR3 and Doppler Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.05516","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd81040b-Lietal2022","cite":"Li et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TrES-5 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is one of the faintest stars with a transiting planet found from the ground at that time, which showed that precise photometry and spectroscopy are possible even for such faint stars (Mandushev et al. 2011).","observed":"Several studies of transit times have asked whether the planet's orbit is shrinking. TrES-5 b is one of only three ultrahot Jupiters where a decreasing orbital period has persisted through multiple independent analyses (Rothmeier et al. 2025). Analyses of citizen-science transit data and of a large database of hot Jupiters also flagged it as showing period changes (Hagey et al. 2022; Yeh et al. 2024). New ground-based transits and TESS data weakened the case: the fitted rate is now 5.3 milliseconds per year, less than half the earlier value (Rothmeier et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The cause of the apparent period change has not been explained, and the orbital decay itself is not confirmed (Rothmeier et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tres5b-Mandushevetal2011","cite":"Mandushev et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Georgi Mandushev, Samuel N. Quinn, Lars A. Buchhave et al.","title":"TrES-5: A Massive Jupiter-sized Planet Transiting a Cool G Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3572","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres5b-Rothmeieretal2025","cite":"Rothmeier et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marvin Rothmeier, Elisabeth R. Adams, Karsten Schindler et al.","title":"Doomed Worlds. II. Reassessing Suggestions of Orbital Decay for TrES-5 b","journal":"The Planetary Science Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.13937","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres5b-Hageyetal2022","cite":"Hagey et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Simone R. Hagey, Billy Edwards, Aaron C. Boley","title":"Evidence of Long-term Period Variations in the Exoplanet Transit Database (ETD)","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10752","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres5b-Yehetal2024","cite":"Yeh et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Li-Chin Yeh, Ing-Guey Jiang, Napaporn A-thano","title":"Searching for candidates of orbital decays among transit exoplanets","journal":"New Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08953","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 222155 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up reused ELODIE data from about 15 years and added new SOPHIE measurements, in order to find long-period planets around Sun-like stars (Boisse et al. 2012).","observed":"The planet is a candidate Jupiter analogue orbiting a bright, Sun-like star. Its orbit takes 10.9 years at about 5.1 au, and its minimum mass is about 1.9 Jupiter masses (Boisse et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd222155b-Boisseetal2012","cite":"Boisse et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"I. Boisse, F. Pepe, C. Perrier et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. V. Follow-up of ELODIE candidates: Jupiter-analogs around Sun-like stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.5835","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 163 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the outermost of three that the discovery team accepted around this nearby M dwarf, based on 150 HARPS velocity measurements taken over eight years (Bonfils et al. 2013). Its orbit takes about 604 days and its minimum mass is about 29 Earth masses (Bonfils et al. 2013).","system":"The inner two planets have periods of 8.6 and 25.6 days, close to a ratio of 3, but a dynamical analysis shows they lie outside the 3:1 resonance (Bonfils et al. 2013). Two further signals, at 19.4 and 108 days, were not accepted as planets (Bonfils et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Whether the two extra signals are planets or stellar activity is not known (Bonfils et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj163d-Bonfilsetal2013","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"X. Bonfils, G. Lo Curto, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXIV. A planetary system around the nearby M dwarf <ASTROBJ>GJ 163</ASTROBJ>, with a super-Earth possibly in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.0904","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2017-BLG-0173L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet-to-star mass ratio is either about 2.5 or about 6.5 in units of one hundred-thousandth, among the lowest ever detected (Hwang et al. 2018). The signal is very strong because a bright, large background star passed completely over the planet's caustic, the region where lensing is sharply amplified (Hwang et al. 2018). Events like this are nicknamed Hollywood events (Hwang et al. 2018). The two possible answers come from a previously unrecognised degeneracy between cases where the caustic is fully covered and cases where only one flank is covered (Hwang et al. 2018). The degeneracy arises from gaps in the data (Hwang et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Which of the two mass ratios is correct is not settled (Hwang et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2017blg0173lb-Hwangetal2018","cite":"Hwang et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"K.-H. Hwang, A. Udalski, Y. Shvartzvald et al.","title":"OGLE-2017-BLG-0173Lb: Low-mass-ratio Planet in a “Hollywood” Microlensing Event","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.08476","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2076 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS saw only two transits of this planet, separated by a two-year gap with no data, so its period could not be determined uniquely at first (Hedges et al. 2021). Later spectroscopic follow-up fixed the periods of the three transiting planets near 10, 21 and 35 days (Damasso et al. 2024). The star is very active, with radial-velocity scatter above 30 metres per second, so measuring planet masses required modelling out the starspot signal (Damasso et al. 2024).","system":"The star hosts three transiting planets and is young, about 200 to 300 million years old (Hedges et al. 2021; Damasso et al. 2024). It moves through space together with another young star, TOI-1807, that also hosts a planet (Hedges et al. 2021). Starspots cause strong, periodic dimming (Hedges et al. 2021).","history":"The follow-up work estimated how much atmosphere each planet will lose to evaporation by the star's radiation up to an age of 5 billion years (Damasso et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2076c-Hedgesetal2021","cite":"Hedges et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christina Hedges, Alex Hughes, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2076 and TOI-1807: Two Young, Comoving Planetary Systems within 50 pc Identified by TESS that are Ideal Candidates for Further Follow Up","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01311","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076c-Damassoetal2024","cite":"Damasso et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Locci, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG: LIX. Characterisation study of the ∼300 Myr-old multi-planetary system orbiting the star BD+40 2790 (TOI-2076)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10629","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-669 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of eight sub-Neptunes presented as especially suited to atmospheric follow-up with JWST (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). Its mass was measured to better than 5-sigma precision using more than two years of Keck-HIRES and Automated Planet Finder radial velocities from the TESS-Keck Survey (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). The survey exists because measuring masses is expected to become the bottleneck for studying small-planet atmospheres (Akana Murphy et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi669b-Murphyetal2023","cite":"Murphy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Natalie Batalha, TESS-Keck Survey","title":"The Masses and Radii of Eight Sub-Neptunes Amenable to Atmospheric Characterization","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1749 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of the system's measurements come from three planets seen together. Transit timing gave only upper limits on their masses, which for planet c is 14 Earth masses at 95% confidence (Fukui et al. 2021). Follow-up included multiband transit photometry, adaptive optics imaging and low-resolution spectroscopy (Fukui et al. 2021).","system":"The star is an early M dwarf with three planets. The outer pair, c and d, has a period ratio within 1% of 2, like other trios around M dwarfs such as TOI-175 and TOI-270 (Fukui et al. 2021).","history":"Planet c is consistent with having kept a hydrogen envelope over a rocky core, while only the innermost planet appears to have lost its envelope to photoevaporation or core-powered mass loss (Fukui et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1749c-Fukuietal2021","cite":"Fukui et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Fukui, J. Korth, J. H. Livingston et al.","title":"TOI-1749: an M dwarf with a Trio of Planets including a Near-resonant Pair","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three super-Jupiters announced together, adding to the small number of known planets near twice Jupiter's mass (Bento et al. 2017). Its radius is inflated, larger than expected for its mass (Bento et al. 2017). The host star is an F dwarf of moderate brightness (Bento et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats24b-Bentoetal2017","cite":"Bento et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Bento, B. Schmidt, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-22b, HATS-23b and HATS-24b: three new transiting super-Jupiters from the HATSouth project","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-333 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies in the Neptune desert, where Neptune-sized planets are rarely found on orbits shorter than about 4 days around Sun-like stars (Alves et al. 2026).","observed":"The host star is probably younger than 1 billion years, supported by lithium absorption compared with stars in open clusters of known age (Alves et al. 2026). Its density is 1.42 grams per cubic centimetre (Alves et al. 2026). Interior models allow a small hydrogen-helium envelope of about 8.5% of the core mass, or an irradiated ocean world with about 20% water by mass (Alves et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet holds a thin hydrogen envelope or a water-rich interior is not settled (Alves et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi333b-Alvesetal2026","cite":"Alves et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Douglas R. Alves, James S. Jenkins, José I. Vinés et al.","title":"TOI-333b: A Neptune-desert planet around an F7V star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13832","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1652 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Interior models of 28 rocky planets in their stars' habitable zones suggest that Kepler-1652 b has an extremely high water content (Boldog et al. 2024). The models assume an iron core, a rock mantle, a high-pressure ice layer and a surface layer of ice or water, and include heating from tides and radioactivity (Boldog et al. 2024). Its host star is a cool red dwarf, and Hubble imaging of other Kepler hosts showed that unseen companion stars can change a planet's size and habitability (Cartier et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1652b-Boldogetal2024","cite":"Boldog et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ádám Boldog, Vera Dobos, László L. Kiss et al.","title":"Water content of rocky exoplanets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.01893","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1652b-Cartieretal2015","cite":"Cartier et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Kimberly M. S. Cartier, Ronald L. Gilliland, Jason T. Wright et al.","title":"Revision of Earth-sized Kepler Planet Candidate Properties with High-resolution Imaging by the Hubble Space Telescope","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.1057","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5678 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a search for long-period transiting planets in TESS data, which tend to be missed because of how the satellite observes (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2023; Tuson 2023).","observed":"TESS saw only two transits, separated by a long gap, so the true period was unknown. The CHEOPS satellite observed the most probable period options one by one to find the right one (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2023). HARPS radial velocities gave the mass, and the planet turned out to be a Neptune-mass world with a 48-day orbit (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2023). Cooler giant planets like this one are unaltered by intense starlight and tides, so they are easier to compare with formation models than hot Jupiters (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5678b-UlmerMolletal2023","cite":"Ulmer-Moll et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"S. Ulmer-Moll, H. P. Osborn, A. Tuson et al.","title":"TOI-5678b: A 48-day transiting Neptune-mass planet characterized with CHEOPS and HARPS★","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04295","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5678b-Tuson2023","cite":"Tuson 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Amy Tuson","title":"Discovery and Characterisation of Long-Period Exoplanets with TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"https://doi.org/10.17863/CAM.109981","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-63 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit passes almost over the star's poles, and the star's spin axis is tilted 104 degrees to the orbit (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2013). The star is young and Sun-like, and its large starspots change the depth of the transits (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2013). Because the planet crosses the star at high latitudes, it revealed a large, persistent polar starspot (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2013). The orbit also let astronomers map spots at different latitudes across about 150 transits (Netto & Valio 2020). Mass could not be measured because the star is so active, and only an upper limit of 120 Earth masses was set (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2013).","system":"The star is about 210 million years old and magnetically active (Netto & Valio 2020). Its X-ray output is far above that of the active Sun, so the planet receives strong high-energy radiation (Lalitha et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler63b-SanchisOjedaetal2013","cite":"Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Roberto Sanchis-Ojeda, Joshua N. Winn, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Kepler-63b: A Giant Planet in a Polar Orbit around a Young Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.8128","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler63b-NettoValio2020","cite":"Netto & Valio 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Y. Netto, A. Valio","title":"Stellar magnetic activity and the butterfly diagram of Kepler-63","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.08661","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler63b-Lalithaetal2018","cite":"Lalitha et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Sairam Lalitha, J. H. M. M. Schmitt, Spandan Dash","title":"Atmospheric mass-loss of extrasolar planets orbiting magnetically active host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08684","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86081 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came with a new Doppler technique that builds a synthetic stellar template instead of observing one, saving telescope time for the quick-look N2K planet search (Johnson et al. 2006).","observed":"With an orbit of about 2.14 days, the planet's period is remarkably short, bridging the gap between the extremely short-period planets found by the OGLE transit survey and the 16 hot Jupiters known from Doppler surveys at that time (Johnson et al. 2006). Imaging with Keck/NIRC2 later showed that its host star has a stellar companion, a newly found multi-star system (Ngo et al. 2017).","system":"The star is part of a multi-star system, newly found in an imaging survey of 144 giant-planet hosts (Ngo et al. 2017). That survey found no evidence that stellar companions change the orbits of planets between 0.1 and 5 au (Ngo et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86081b-Johnsonetal2006","cite":"Johnson et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"The N2K Consortium. VI. Doppler Shifts without Templates and Three New Short-Period Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0604348","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd86081b-Ngoetal2017","cite":"Ngo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Henry Ngo, Heather A. Knutson, Marta L. Bryan et al.","title":"No Difference in Orbital Parameters of RV-detected Giant Planets between 0.1 and 5 au in Single versus Multi-stellar Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02326","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4507 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities taken during transits show the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a small distortion in the star's spectrum that reveals how a planet's orbit lines up with the star's spin. The planet orbits in the same direction as the star spins, with a sky-projected angle of about -15 degrees and a range of -59 to +35 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). Its density is below 0.2 g/cm3, so it is a very fluffy \"superpuff\" (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). Its mass is only limited to below 20 Earth masses at 95% confidence (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026).","system":"The host is an F star about 700 million years old (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). Of all systems where the angle between orbit and stellar spin has been measured, TOI-4507 is one of those with the longest orbital period (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026).","history":"The planet is among the longest-period and youngest superpuff planets known (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026).","open":"The mass is not pinned down, only limited from above (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4507b-EspinozaRetamaletal2026","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Rafael Brahm, Cristobal Petrovich et al.","title":"A Cold and Superpuffy Planet on a Prograde Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00102","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-13 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the CORALIE spectrograph confirmed the planet, giving a mass of 4.84 Jupiter masses, a radius of 1.142 Jupiter radii and a slightly eccentric orbit with e = 0.086 (Grieves et al. 2021). Because its mass lies just above 4 Jupiter masses, it sits near a proposed dividing line between giant planets and brown dwarfs, which are failed stars (Grieves et al. 2021).","system":"The host star is a subgiant, a star that has started to swell as it ages, with a radius of 1.79 solar radii (Grieves et al. 2021).","history":"About 4 Jupiter masses has been suggested as the border between two ways of building giant planets, core accretion and disk instability (Grieves et al. 2021). Massive giants may also form in the same way as lower-mass brown dwarfs (Grieves et al. 2021).","open":"More planets near this mass are needed to tell the classes apart statistically (Grieves et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts13b-Grievesetal2021","cite":"Grieves et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nolan Grieves, Louise D. Nielsen, Jose I. Vines et al.","title":"NGTS-13b: a hot 4.8 Jupiter-mass planet transiting a subgiant star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.04245","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-47 e":{"text":{"why":"The mass and radius do not fit an Earth-like rock-and-iron mix, and the cause is debated. One study finds a volatile-rich envelope around an Earth-like core and mantle (Vanderburg et al. 2017). Another proposes a planet with no core, rich in calcium and aluminium minerals that condense early from a cooling disc, with density 10-20 per cent below Earth-like values (Dorn et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet orbits in about 19 hours, and its mass and size have been measured precisely. Its mass is 6.83 Earth masses and its radius 1.810 Earth radii (Vanderburg et al. 2017). Newer radial velocities give 6.77 Earth masses and a density of 6.29 g/cm3, the second most precisely measured density of any super-Earth (Bryant & Bayliss 2022). Unlike most ultra-short-period planets, its composition is not Earth-like (Vanderburg et al. 2017).","system":"WASP-47 is the only known hot Jupiter with close-in companions: a super-Earth in a 0.79-day orbit, a Neptune-like planet in 9.03 days and a Jupiter-like planet in 588 days (Bryant & Bayliss 2022; Vanderburg et al. 2018). The outer giant likely orbits close to the same plane as the inner planets, which suggests a quiet history (Vanderburg et al. 2017).","history":"The calm, flat layout argues against hot Jupiters being thrown inward by violent interactions such as Kozai-Lidov migration (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","open":"Whether the planet has a gas envelope or a calcium- and aluminium-rich rocky interior is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp47e-Vanderburgetal2017","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Juliette C. Becker, Lars A. Buchhave et al.","title":"Precise Masses in the WASP-47 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.00026","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47e-Dornetal2019","cite":"Dorn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Dorn, J. H. D. Harrison, A. Bonsor et al.","title":"A new class of Super-Earths formed from high-temperature condensates: HD219134 b, 55 Cnc e, WASP-47 e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07222","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47e-BryantBayliss2022","cite":"Bryant & Bayliss 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss","title":"Revisiting WASP-47 with ESPRESSO and TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47e-Vanderburgetal2018","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Juliette Becker, David W. Latham et al.","title":"WASP-47 and the Origin of Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-532 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a metal-rich M dwarf with [Fe/H] = 0.38, and the planet follows the known trend that gas giants around M dwarfs favour metal-rich stars (Kanodia et al. 2021).","observed":"Near-infrared radial velocities with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder gave a mass of 61.5 Earth masses and a radius of 5.82 Earth radii (Kanodia et al. 2021). It is the largest and most massive super Neptune around an M dwarf with both mass and radius measured (Kanodia et al. 2021). It bridges the gap between Neptune-sized planets and the heavier Jupiter-like planets known around M dwarfs (Kanodia et al. 2021). High-contrast imaging was also used to check for nearby stars (Kanodia et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a faint, cool M dwarf with a temperature of 3957 K (Kanodia et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The planet sits at the edge of the Neptune desert, a region of close-in orbits where planets of this size are scarce, so it helps test what carves that region out (Kanodia et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi532b-Kanodiaetal2021","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Gudmundur Stefansson, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"TOI-532b: The Habitable-zone Planet Finder confirms a Large Super Neptune in the Neptune Desert orbiting a metal-rich M-dwarf host","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13670","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7510 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet b is the innermost of three transiting giant planets, with a radius of 0.65 Jupiter radii and a mass estimate of 0.057 Jupiter masses (Almenara et al. 2025). Modelling of the light curves and the timing of the transits favours low eccentricities and small mutual tilts between the orbits (Almenara et al. 2025).","system":"The three planets have orbital periods of 11.5, 22.6 and 48.9 days and radii of 0.65, 0.96 and 0.94 Jupiter radii (Almenara et al. 2025). They are the largest known trio of transiting planets (Almenara et al. 2025). The orbits lie near a 4:2:1 chain of mean motion resonance, which causes large changes in transit times for all three planets (Almenara et al. 2025). The host is a solar analog (Almenara et al. 2025).","history":"The system was first flagged as a two-planet candidate by a machine-learning search, and one transit had been assigned to the wrong planet (Almenara et al. 2025).","open":"The compact giant-planet layout is a good test of how such systems form and interact (Almenara et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7510b-Almenaraetal2025","cite":"Almenara et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. M. Almenara, A. Leleu, T. Guillot et al.","title":"TOI-7510: A solar-analog system of three transiting giant planets near a Laplace resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20074","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+15 2940 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 1.1 Jupiter masses and circles its star at 0.54 AU (Nowak et al. 2013). It is the closest-in planet known around a giant star (Nowak et al. 2013). Its star is a K0 giant of 1.1 solar masses (Nowak et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"As the star ages and swells, the planet could be swallowed, a fate called engulfment (Nowak et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd152940b-Nowaketal2013","cite":"Nowak et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Nowak, A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan et al.","title":"BD+15 2940 and HD 233604: Two Giants with Planets Close to the Engulfment Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6755","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-82 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet f does not transit its star. Its existence and mass of about 21 Earth masses come from modelling the small shifts in the transit times of its neighbours (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Planets b and c are near a 2:1 resonance and show regular, wave-like timing shifts. Extra short-term wobble in the timings of planet c hinted at a further planet near the 3:2 or 3:1 resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","system":"Kepler-82 is the first system in which this combined light-curve and orbit modelling was applied to give a full dynamical analysis (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler82f-Freudenthaletal2019","cite":"Freudenthal et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Freudenthal, C. von Essen, A. Ofir et al.","title":"Kepler Object of Interest Network. III. Kepler-82f: a new non-transiting 21 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> planet from photodynamical modelling","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.06534","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-72 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-72 b is a super-Neptune with a mass of 0.1254 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.7224 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2020). Radial velocities of high precision gave an exceptionally precise mass (Hartman et al. 2020). Three surveys, HATSouth, WASP and TESS, each independently picked it out as a transit candidate, and the data from all three were combined (Hartman et al. 2020). The planet is also known as WASP-191 b and TOI-294 b (Hartman et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a main-sequence K dwarf of 0.7311 solar masses (Hartman et al. 2020). The planet orbits in 7.3279 days, a warm orbit that is farther out than a typical hot Jupiter (Hartman et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats72b-Hartmanetal2020","cite":"Hartman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. D. Hartman, Andrés Jordán, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-47b, HATS-48Ab, HATS-49b, and HATS-72b: Four Warm Giant Planets Transiting K Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4468 c":{"text":{"why":"Almost all hot Jupiters have no nearby transiting companions, which points to violent formation by dynamical interactions (Hord et al. 2024). About 10 systems break that rule, and most of those have the companion on an inner orbit, unlike here (Hord et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a sub-Neptune with a radius of 3.22 Earth radii and a 7.01-day orbit (Hord et al. 2024). Ground-based photometry supports its statistical validation as a real planet (Hord et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a K dwarf. An inner hot Jupiter with a 2.77-day orbit and a radius of 11.43 Earth radii lies closer to the star than planet c (Hord et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"What the few known hot Jupiters with close neighbours say about how all hot Jupiters form is still being worked out (Hord et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4468c-Hordetal2024","cite":"Hord et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Benjamin Hord, Joseph Livesey, Juliette Becker","title":"TOI-4468 and the Emerging Trends in Systems Hosting Hot Jupiters with Nearby Companion Planets","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-132 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself. Work so far aims to measure its mass and radius to see whether it was puffed up by its star's growing brightness or has been inflated since formation (Gary & Grunblatt 2021). The same study found another hot Jupiter, K2-97 b, with a mass of 0.49 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.63 Jupiter radii, very likely re-inflated by its host (Gary & Grunblatt 2021).","system":"Modelling of K2-132 has been used to show that changes in transit duration could be large and observable in some multi-planet systems (Van Laerhoven et al. 2018).","history":"As a star evolves and brightens, its irradiation can swell a hot Jupiter that was smaller before (Gary & Grunblatt 2021). In the other possibility, the planet was already inflated when it formed and is cooling slowly (Gary & Grunblatt 2021).","open":"The planet's mass and radius were still being measured, so which kind of inflation applies is not yet known (Gary & Grunblatt 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2132b-GaryGrunblatt2021","cite":"Gary & Grunblatt 2021","year":"2021","authors":"S. Gary, S. Grunblatt","title":"Confirmation of Hot Jupiter Re-Inflation","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2132b-VanLaerhovenetal2018","cite":"Van Laerhoven et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Christa Van Laerhoven, Aaron Boley, Agueda Paula Granados Contreras","title":"Transit Duration Variations in Multi-Planet Systems","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #50","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LkCa 15 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The existence of this planet is questioned. Early imaging with aperture masking, which combines light from parts of the telescope mirror, found a faint source inside a gap in the disc around the star (Kraus & Ireland 2012). Later infrared imaging at the same wavelengths and positions did not reveal the claimed planets. Instead, it resolved extended emission from dust whose brightness and location match the earlier signals (Currie et al. 2019).","system":"The star is a solar analog of about 2 million years, still surrounded by a protoplanetary disc with a large gap, called a transitional disc (Kraus & Ireland 2012).","history":"Planets embedded in such a gap would be caught during assembly, which is why the claim drew attention (Kraus & Ireland 2012).","open":"Dust close to the star, called an inner disc, is a more convincing explanation for the detections than orbiting planets (Currie et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lkca15c-KrausIreland2012","cite":"Kraus & Ireland 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Adam L. Kraus, Michael J. Ireland","title":"LkCa 15: A Young Exoplanet Caught at Formation?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.3808","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lkca15c-Currieetal2019","cite":"Currie et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Thayne Currie, Christian Marois, Lucas Cieza et al.","title":"No Clear, Direct Evidence for Multiple Protoplanets Orbiting LkCa 15: LkCa 15 bcd are Likely Inner Disk Signals","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04322","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 810 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Years of radial velocity monitoring revealed a planet with a minimum mass of 2.26 Jupiter masses, an eccentricity of 0.161 and a distance of 0.925 AU. At the time, it moved on the most Earth-like orbit found among exoplanets (Kürster et al. 2000). The star also shows extra velocity scatter of 27 m/s, probably from stellar activity (Kürster et al. 2000).","system":"The star, also called iota Horologii, was the youngest known to host a planet at the time (Kürster et al. 2000). A chemical analysis finds it matches the Hyades cluster for many elements but has about half the Hyades lithium (Ramírez et al. 2017). Its rotation period is about 5 days (Ramírez et al. 2017).","history":"The star was suggested to be an escaped Hyades member (Laymand & Vauclair 2007). Galactic orbit models place it hundreds of parsecs from the cluster at formation, so a shared birthplace is hard to justify (Ramírez et al. 2017).","open":"Stable Trojan orbits, where a small planet would share the giant's path, were found possible in the system (Schwarz et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr810b-Krsteretal2000","cite":"Kürster et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"M. Kürster, M. Endl, S. Els et al.","title":"An extrasolar giant planet in an Earth-like orbit. Precise radial velocities of the young star iota Horologii = HR 810","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr810b-Ramrezetal2017","cite":"Ramírez et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"I. Ramírez, D. Yong, E. Gutiérrez et al.","title":"Iota Horologii Is Unlikely to Be an Evaporated Hyades Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05930","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr810b-LaymandVauclair2007","cite":"Laymand & Vauclair 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Laymand, S. Vauclair","title":"Asteroseismology of exoplanets host stars: the special case of ι Horologii (HD 17051)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0610501","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr810b-Schwarzetal2007","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. Schwarz, R. Dvorak, Á. Süli et al.","title":"Survey of the stability region of hypothetical habitable Trojan planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20077994","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102956 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 0.96 Jupiter masses and orbits at 0.081 AU from a subgiant star of 1.68 solar masses (Johnson et al. 2010). The host is the most massive star known to have a hot Jupiter (Johnson et al. 2010). Only two other planets are known within 0.6 AU of a star above 1.5 solar masses (Johnson et al. 2010).","system":"In a sample of 137 subgiants above 1.45 solar masses, 0.5% to 2.3% of A-type stars host a close-in planet of over 1 Jupiter mass, similar to the rate around Sun-like stars (Johnson et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Planets at 0.1 to 1.0 AU are scarce around stars of intermediate mass. The gap may be an exaggerated version of the \"period valley\" seen around Sun-like stars (Johnson et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102956b-Johnsonetal2010","cite":"Johnson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"John Asher Johnson, Brendan P. Bowler, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"A Hot Jupiter Orbiting the 1.7 M <SUB>sun</SUB> Subgiant HD 102956","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4555","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 135344 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of about 10 Jupiter masses and orbits at roughly 15 to 20 au (Stolker et al. 2025). The planet's brightness resembles a mid-L-type object, a cool and dim object like a brown dwarf (Stolker et al. 2025). Several observations over time show it shares the star's motion and distance, which rules out a background star (Stolker et al. 2025).","system":"The star is the A0 primary in a young visual binary. Its companion star is known for a protoplanetary disc, but the primary's own surroundings are depleted of dust, so a planet is easy to see (Stolker et al. 2025).","history":"The planet is young and recently formed, with properties that confirm its youth (Stolker et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd135344ab-Stolkeretal2025","cite":"Stolker et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"T. Stolker, J. Kammerer, M. Benisty et al.","title":"Searching for low-mass companions at small separations in transition disks with aperture masking interferometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11989","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the whole system rather than planet e alone. The star hosts six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune (Hooton et al. 2022). Their densities vary from planet to planet, which departs from the usual steady fall in density with distance from the star (Hooton et al. 2022).","system":"Five of the six planets form a chain of Laplace resonance, in which the orbital periods are linked in a repeating pattern (Hooton et al. 2022). Similar chains exist among the TRAPPIST-1 planets and the Galilean moons of Jupiter (Hooton et al. 2022). The host is a cool K dwarf (Hooton et al. 2022).","history":"Early TESS data suggested that two planets might share one orbit in a horseshoe pattern, but follow-up showed a compact system of six (Hooton et al. 2022). A chain this fragile limits how the planets can have formed and moved (Hooton et al. 2022).","open":"JWST was awarded time to study the atmospheres of planets b, d and g, to compare their formation. The large density differences are hard to explain (Hooton et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178e-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1422 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a sub-Neptune with a radius of 2.61 Earth radii and a 34.563-day orbit (Naponiello et al. 2026). Its density is 4.3 g/cm3 (Naponiello et al. 2026). Three transits, two from newly released TESS sectors, were combined with new and old radial velocities to confirm it (Naponiello et al. 2026). Earlier work had hinted at an outer companion from a signal of about 29 days and a possible single transit, but it was unclear whether the two signals came from one planet (Naponiello et al. 2026).","system":"The star is a G2 dwarf. Inner planet b is a warm Neptune-sized world with a mass of about 9 Earth masses, a radius of about 4 Earth radii and a circular 12.997-day orbit (Naponiello et al. 2026). It was earlier described as puffy (Naponiello et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1422c-Naponielloetal2026","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"L. Naponiello, P. Leonardi, M. Damasso et al.","title":"A 34.6-d transiting sub-Neptune in the TOI-1422 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11492","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1422c-Naponielloetal2022","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Naponiello, L. Mancini, M. Damasso et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XL. A puffy and warm Neptune-sized planet and an outer Neptune-mass candidate orbiting the solar-type star TOI-1422","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.03293","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1513 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a radius of about 8 Earth radii and orbits at 0.53 au (Yahalomi et al. 2024). Its transit times drift in a way that first looked like the pull of a moon (Yahalomi et al. 2024). New transits nearly a decade after the last Kepler transit added a second, slower drift. Only a non-transiting planet tugging on it reproduces the timings (Yahalomi et al. 2024). Radial velocities so far give only an upper limit on its mass (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a late G-type dwarf (Yahalomi et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The perturbing planet has not been seen directly (Yahalomi et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1513b-Yahalomietal2024","cite":"Yahalomi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daniel A. Yahalomi, David Kipping, David Nesvorný et al.","title":"Not-so-fast Kepler-1513: a perturbing planetary interloper in the exomoon corridor","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03802","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1513b-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-262 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was observed by both TESS and CHEOPS, a European space telescope. It is one of nine planets studied in this way to help map the radius valley, a dip in the number of planets at certain sizes that tells how small planets gain and lose atmospheres (Oddo et al. 2023). The CHEOPS transits typically give uncertainties equal to between two and 12 TESS transits (Oddo et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi262b-Oddoetal2023","cite":"Oddo et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Dominic Oddo, Diana Dragomir, Alexis Brandeker et al.","title":"Characterization of a Set of Small Planets with TESS and CHEOPS and an Analysis of Photometric Performance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08162","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 3.47 Jupiter masses, a radius of 1.31 Jupiter radii and a density of 2.2 g/cm3 (Hébrard et al. 2011). It orbits in the same direction as the star spins and nearly aligned, with a sky-projected angle of -10 degrees and a true tilt of 20 degrees (Hébrard et al. 2011). The planet crosses dark starspots during transit, and tracking one gave a rotation period of 5.19 days and an alignment of 6 degrees (Raetz et al. 2019).","system":"The star's age is uncertain. Stellar models point to a few tens of millions of years or several billion, while spin and lithium suggest a few hundred million years (Hébrard et al. 2011). Starspot work supports a young star (Raetz et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The mismatch of ages may point to a gap in how young stars are understood, which affects planet sizes around them (Hébrard et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot18b-Hbrardetal2011","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"G. Hébrard, T. M. Evans, R. Alonso et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XVIII. CoRoT-18b: a massive hot Jupiter on a prograde, nearly aligned orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.2032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot18b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-146 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 7.50 Earth masses and a radius of 2.19 Earth radii (Hamann et al. 2019). It is locked in a 3:2 orbital resonance with planet b, and the two planets pull on each other so much that their transit times shift in opposite directions (Hamann et al. 2019). Its transit was missed at first because it only grazed the star, and the orbit later tilted into full view, tripling the transit depth (Hamann et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a mid-M dwarf of 0.331 solar masses (Hamann et al. 2019). The two orbits are tilted 2.40 degrees from each other (Hamann et al. 2019). The masses are among the most precise known for small planets (Hamann et al. 2019). Another study finds moderate eccentricities near 0.16 (Lam et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2146c-Hamannetal2019","cite":"Hamann et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Aaron Hamann, Benjamin T. Montet, Daniel C. Fabrycky et al.","title":"K2-146: Discovery of Planet c, Precise Masses from Transit Timing, and Observed Precession","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10620","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2146c-Lametal2020","cite":"Lam et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Kristine W. F. Lam, Judith Korth, Kento Masuda et al.","title":"It Takes Two Planets in Resonance to Tango around K2-146","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11141","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-158 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a tiny world with a radius of 0.43 Earth radii, orbiting a K dwarf every 0.645088 days (Wang et al. 2024). Only a few hundred planets with sub-Earth radii are known (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"How sub-Earth planets form is still unclear (Wang et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler158d-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kaitlyn Wang, Jian Ge, Kevin Willis et al.","title":"Discovery of small ultra-short-period planets orbiting Kepler KG dwarfs with GPU phase folding and deep learning","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17382","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-20 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 7.246 Jupiter masses, a radius of 0.867 Jupiter radii and a density of 13.78 g/cm3, so it is very dense (Bakos et al. 2010). Four secondary eclipses, when the planet passes behind the star, were measured with Spitzer. They give a temperature of 1134 K, a sign of poor heat transfer around the planet, and show no strong molecular absorption (Deming et al. 2015). The star's spin alignment was also measured with the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Esposito et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a metal-rich K3 dwarf with [Fe/H] = +0.35 and a temperature of 4595 K (Bakos et al. 2010). The orbit is circular (Bakos et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"The planet varies at 4.5 micrometres, and is a high priority for JWST spectroscopy (Deming et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp20b-Bakosetal2010","cite":"Bakos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-20b-HAT-P-23b: Four Massive Transiting Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3388","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp20b-Demingetal2015","cite":"Deming et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Drake Deming, Heather Knutson, Joshua Kammer et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipses of the Dense, Modestly-irradiated, Giant Exoplanet HAT-P-20b Using Pixel-level Decorrelation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.7404","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp20b-Espositoetal2017","cite":"Esposito et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Esposito, E. Covino, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XIII. The orbital obliquity of three close-in massive planets hosted by dwarf K-type stars: WASP-43, HAT-P-20 and Qatar-2","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03136","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the whole system rather than planet c alone. The star hosts six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune (Hooton et al. 2022). Their densities vary from planet to planet, which departs from the usual steady fall in density with distance from the star (Hooton et al. 2022).","system":"Five of the six planets form a chain of Laplace resonance, in which the orbital periods are linked in a repeating pattern (Hooton et al. 2022). Similar chains exist among the TRAPPIST-1 planets and the Galilean moons of Jupiter (Hooton et al. 2022). The host is a cool K dwarf (Hooton et al. 2022).","history":"Early TESS data suggested that two planets might share one orbit in a horseshoe pattern, but follow-up showed a compact system of six (Hooton et al. 2022). A chain this fragile limits how the planets can have formed and moved (Hooton et al. 2022).","open":"JWST was awarded time to study the atmospheres of planets b, d and g, to compare their formation. The large density differences are hard to explain (Hooton et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178c-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-561 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a mini-Neptune with a mass of 5.40 Earth masses and a radius of 2.88 Earth radii (Lacedelli et al. 2021). It, with planets d and e, likely kept a hydrogen-helium envelope (Lacedelli et al. 2021). Three of the system's planets are mini-Neptunes beyond an ultra-short-period inner planet, which is unusually low in density (Lacedelli et al. 2021).","system":"The star is an old, metal-poor member of the Galactic thick disc, about 10 billion years old (Brinkman et al. 2023). At least four planets transit, with periods of 0.44, 10.8, 25.7 and 77.1 days, and a fifth, non-transiting candidate orbits every 433 days (Piotto et al. 2024). Two transits first assigned to an outer candidate belong instead to planets found by radial velocity (Lacedelli et al. 2021).","history":"Old, metal-poor thick-disc stars are less likely to host ultra-short-period super-Earths (Piotto et al. 2024).","open":"A long-period signal in the radial velocities could be another planet or stellar activity (Lacedelli et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi561c-Lacedellietal2021","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"G. Lacedelli, L. Malavolta, L. Borsato et al.","title":"An unusually low density ultra-short period super-Earth and three mini-Neptunes around the old star TOI-561","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.02332","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561c-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-561 b: A Low-density Ultra-short-period \"Rocky\" Planet around a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06665","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561c-Piottoetal2024","cite":"Piotto et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Piotto, T. Zingales, L. Borsato et al.","title":"Architecture of TOI-561 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18169","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-2 N b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a radius of 0.98 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.57 Jupiter masses (Burke et al. 2007). Radial velocities show a prograde orbit, with a sky-projected angle of 10 degrees and an eccentricity consistent with zero (Narita et al. 2011). The star also shows a long-term velocity drift whose cause is not known (Narita et al. 2011). Transit times vary in ways that no simple model explains, and unseen perturbing planets may be present (Wang et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a metal-rich early K dwarf with a near-identical twin companion star at 31 arcseconds (Burke et al. 2007). The star's Galactic orbit and position suggest it formed in the metal-rich inner thin disc and was scattered outward (Burke et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo2nb-Burkeetal2007","cite":"Burke et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, P. R. McCullough, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-2b: Transiting Hot Jupiter in a Metal-rich Common Proper Motion Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo2nb-Naritaetal2011","cite":"Narita et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Norio Narita, Teruyuki Hirano, Bun'ei Sato et al.","title":"XO-2b: a Prograde Planet with Negligible Eccentricity and an Additional Radial Velocity Variation","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.6136","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo2nb-Wangetal2025","cite":"Wang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Weilong Wang, Shenghong Gu, Xiaobin Wang et al.","title":"Observations and Studies on the Transiting Systems HAT-P-36, XO-2 and WASP-76","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/add1de","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-94 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the system, called Kepler-89, which has four transiting planets (Mayo et al. 2017). The timing of transits of the outer planet, Kepler-89e, shows large changes that point to a fifth planet (Mayo et al. 2017). That planet could also affect the mass of Kepler-89d, whose radial velocity and timing masses disagree (Mayo et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The fifth planet is hypothetical, and more transits of planet e could pin down its properties (Mayo et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi94e-Mayoetal2017","cite":"Mayo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Andrew Mayo, Katherine Deck, Heather Knutson et al.","title":"A Possible 5th Planet in the Kepler-89 System","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #229","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2267 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a cool binary of an M5 and an M6 star (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025). The two stars are only 0.384 arcseconds apart, a projected distance of about 8 au at 22 pc (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025). The system has been described as holding three Earth-sized planets (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2267c-ZunigaFernandezetal2025","cite":"Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Zuniga-Fernandez, F. J. Pozuelos, M. Devora-Pajares et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-2267 light curves (Zuniga-Fernandez+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 95086 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits at about 57 au, between an inner and an outer debris belt (Desgrange et al. 2022). Its mass is 5 Jupiter masses, estimated from its brightness and models of a young planet that begins hot (Rameau et al. 2013). The spectrum is very red and featureless, which fits a cloudy atmosphere (De Rosa et al. 2016). JWST detected it at 10.575 and 11.3 micrometres (Mâlin et al. 2024).","system":"The star is young, with an age of about 17 million years, and has an extended debris disc (Rameau et al. 2013). Other planets may maintain the broad gap between the belts (Desgrange et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"A search for a second planet has found none so far (Desgrange et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd95086b-Desgrangeetal2022","cite":"Desgrange et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"C. Desgrange, G. Chauvin, V. Christiaens et al.","title":"In-depth direct imaging and spectroscopic characterization of the young Solar System analog HD 95086","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.00425","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95086b-Rameauetal2013","cite":"Rameau et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. Rameau, G. Chauvin, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"Confirmation of the Planet around HD 95086 by Direct Imaging","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7483","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95086b-DeRosaetal2016","cite":"De Rosa et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Robert J. De Rosa, Julien Rameau, Jenny Patience et al.","title":"Spectroscopic Characterization of HD 95086 b with the Gemini Planet Imager","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.01411","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95086b-Mlinetal2024","cite":"Mâlin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Mathilde Mâlin, Anthony Boccaletti, Clément Perrot et al.","title":"Unveiling the HD 95086 system at mid-infrared wavelengths with JWST/MIRI","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.16843","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"ITG 15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star, a young stellar object still surrounded by a disc of gas and dust. Light scattered by an inclined disc is slightly polarized in the near infrared, and that signal was detected from the ITG 15 disc even though the disc cannot be seen directly (Clemens et al. 2022).","system":"The star lies in the Heiles Cloud 2 dark cloud in Taurus. It sits in a wide pair, about 5200 au from the brown dwarf ITG 17, or possibly in a four-star system (Clemens et al. 2022). The discs of ITG 15 and ITG 17 point the same way, perpendicular to the local magnetic field (Clemens et al. 2022).","history":"The multiple-star nature of the system could result from formation in a place where the magnetic field was misaligned with the cloud's other structure (Clemens et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-itg15b-Clemensetal2022","cite":"Clemens et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Dan P. Clemens, Thushara G. S. Pillai, Anneliese M. Rilinger et al.","title":"Near-infrared Polarization from Unresolved Disks around Brown Dwarfs and Young Stellar Objects","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.02977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2411 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a group of hot, possibly rocky TESS planets picked as targets for emission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope, which could reveal atmospheres and surface properties (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"Ground-based follow-up observations and two vetting tools, DAVE and TRICERATOPS, were used to judge how likely each of 18 candidates was to be a real planet; 13 were validated, including this one (Giacalone et al. 2022). Its orbit is so tight that it is classed as an ultra-short-period planet, one of seven among the 13 (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2411b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 27442 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.058 (Butler et al. 2001). It was the first planet beyond 0.2 au known on a circular orbit like those of the planets in the Solar System (Butler et al. 2001). Its minimum mass is 1.43 times Jupiter's, and its distance from the star is 1.18 au (Butler et al. 2001).","system":"The host star has a distant stellar companion, HD 27442 B, which was confirmed by its spectrum and shares the star's motion across the sky (Mugrauer et al. 2007). Computer simulations of orbits found that a terrestrial planet could in principle stay on a stable orbit sharing the giant planet's path in the star's habitable zone (Schwarz et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd27442b-Butleretal2001","cite":"Butler et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"R. Paul Butler, C. G. Tinney, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Two New Planets from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/321467","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd27442b-Mugraueretal2007","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser, T. Mazeh","title":"The multiplicity of exoplanet host stars. Spectroscopic confirmation of the companions GJ 3021 B and HD 27442 B, one new planet host triple-star system, and global statistics","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703795","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd27442b-Schwarzetal2007","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. Schwarz, R. Dvorak, Á. Süli et al.","title":"Survey of the stability region of hypothetical habitable Trojan planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20077994","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 220842 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was one of eight dwarf stars followed with the SOPHIE spectrograph, with spectral types between F5 and K0 (Hébrard et al. 2016). The velocity measurements of HD 220842 allow only a single planet to be found around it (Hébrard et al. 2016). Planets in the full set have minimum masses of 0.4 to 3.8 times Jupiter's (Hébrard et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd220842b-Hbrardetal2016","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"G. Hébrard, L. Arnold, T. Forveille et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. X. Detection and characterization of giant planets by the dozen","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04622","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 30669 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with the HARPS spectrograph over ten years, which was needed to catch planets in distant orbits (Moutou et al. 2015). The five new giant planets in the same report have periods of 492 to 1684 days and eccentricities from 0 to 0.85 (Moutou et al. 2015). Stellar activity limits what such measurements can detect, even in quiet stars, at a precision of about 2 m/s (Moutou et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd30669b-Moutouetal2015","cite":"Moutou et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"C. Moutou, G. Lo Curto, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXVII. Five new long-period giant planets and a system update","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6591","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2485 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was studied as part of a survey of warm Jupiters with the CHIRON spectrograph. It may help bridge the gap between hot Jupiters (periods under 10 days) and warm Jupiters, whose orbits differ and whose origins are not settled (Carleo et al. 2024).","observed":"The star is slightly evolved. The planet has a mass of 2.4 times Jupiter's and a radius of 1.1 times Jupiter's, giving a density of 2.36 g/cm3, so it is compact for a gas giant (Carleo et al. 2024). The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in the star's light as the planet crosses its face, showed that the planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a typical uncertainty of 13 degrees across six systems (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters on single-star systems tend to be aligned, which suggests misalignment is not a universal sign of how giant planets form (Wang et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2485b-Carleoetal2024","cite":"Carleo et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Oscar Barrágan, Carina M. Persson et al.","title":"Mass determination of two Jupiter-sized planets orbiting slightly evolved stars: TOI-2420 b and TOI-2485 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05612","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2485b-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice, Songhu Wang et al.","title":"Single-star Warm-Jupiter Systems Tend to Be Aligned, Even around Hot Stellar Hosts: No T <SUB>eff</SUB>-λ Dependency","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-278 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star has left the main sequence and climbed the red giant branch. It is one of only three such stars known to have several short-period transiting planets (Jofré et al. 2020). The planets around it are among the smallest found around red giant branch stars (Jofré et al. 2020). Detailed spectra from the Gemini telescope gave refined star properties and abundances of 25 elements, and showed the star is about 15% less massive than previously reported (Jofré et al. 2020).","system":"The star's chemistry matches that of nearby evolved stars of the Galaxy's thin disc (Jofré et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler278c-Jofretal2020","cite":"Jofré et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Jofré, J. M. Almenara, R. Petrucci et al.","title":"Gemini-GRACES high-quality spectra of Kepler evolved stars with transiting planets. I. Detailed characterization of multi-planet systems Kepler-278 and Kepler-391","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10278","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-159 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a bloated hot Jupiter, with a radius of 1.4 times Jupiter's but a mass of only 0.55 times Jupiter's (Hellier et al. 2019). It orbits an F9 star of apparent magnitude 12.9 (Hellier et al. 2019). It was announced together with seven other hot Jupiters from the WASP-South survey, using follow-up from the Euler/CORALIE and TRAPPIST-South telescopes (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp159b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3862 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits in the middle of the hot Neptune desert, a region of short orbital periods and mid-sized radii where few planets are found. That makes it a useful test of theories of migration and atmospheric loss (Carleo et al. 2026).","observed":"A joint fit to transit and radial velocity data gave a mass of 53.7 Earth masses and a radius of 5.53 Earth radii, a density of 1.7 g/cm3 (Carleo et al. 2026). The planet is a super-Neptune, and it orbits a Sun-like star with an effective temperature of 5300 K (Carleo et al. 2026). Its density places it among the rare planets of this kind in the desert (Carleo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Close-in planets are shaped by atmospheric loss and by migration, and the boundaries of the desert, ridge and savanna regions of period and radius reflect those processes (Carleo et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3862b-Carleoetal2026","cite":"Carleo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Amadeo Castro-González, Enric Pallé et al.","title":"TOI-3862 b: A dense super-Neptune deep in the hot Neptune desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10450","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-136 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the first Neptune-sized planet found in a binary system within an open cluster (Ciardi et al. 2018). The Hyades cluster is the nearest to the Sun and anchors the distance scale, so its planets help show how planetary systems form and change (Ciardi et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet is the middle of three small planets and lies above the radius-period valley, the gap in sizes where few close-in planets are found (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). Models of its structure suggest a gas envelope of 1 to 1.7 per cent of its mass, which has survived evaporation because the rocky core is massive (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). A mass measurement for the planet has been presented, adding it to the small group of young planets with precisely known masses (Dressing 2022).","system":"The host star is a K dwarf in the Hyades, about 700 million years old. A very faint M dwarf companion lies about 40 au away in projection (Ciardi et al. 2018). The other two planets have radii of 1.0 and 1.5 Earth radii (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). The star's X-ray activity is lower than models predict but typical of Hyades members (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023).","history":"The three planets must share the same evaporation history, and the results are consistent with all three starting out as similar planets (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2136c-Ciardietal2018","cite":"Ciardi et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"David R. Ciardi, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"K2-136: A Binary System in the Hyades Cluster Hosting a Neptune-sized Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.10398","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2136c-Fernndezetal2023","cite":"Fernández et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jorge Fernández Fernández, Peter J. Wheatley, George W. King","title":"The shared evaporation history of three sub-Neptunes spanning the radius-period valley of a hyades star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12705","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2136c-Dressing2022","cite":"Dressing 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Courtney Dressing","title":"A Trio of Multiplanet Systems and Pathways to Habitable Worlds","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting #240","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 5608 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an intermediate-mass giant star in a long-running velocity survey at Okayama Observatory, where some giants show slow, steady velocity drifts that point to an outer companion (Ryu et al. 2016). High-contrast imaging with the Subaru Telescope found a co-moving companion, HD 5608 B, of 0.10 solar masses (Ryu et al. 2016).","system":"The outer companion is a low-mass star moving through space with the host (Ryu et al. 2016).","history":"The Kozai mechanism, a slow exchange of tilt and eccentricity driven by a distant companion, can explain the high eccentricity of the planet's orbit (Ryu et al. 2016).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd5608b-Ryuetal2016","cite":"Ryu et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Tsuguru Ryu, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"High-contrast Imaging of Intermediate-mass Giants with Long-term Radial Velocity Trends","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-13 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an inflated hot Jupiter, with a mass of 0.543 times Jupiter's and a radius of 1.212 times Jupiter's (Mancini et al. 2015). It orbits at about 0.04 au with a period of about 3 days (Mancini et al. 2015). Follow-up used light curves of four transits from several telescopes and radial velocities from various instruments (Mancini et al. 2015).","system":"A near twin of the host star, HATS-14, hosts a second planet found by the same survey, with a similar orbit and irradiation (Mancini et al. 2015). That planet, HATS-14b, has 1.071 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.039 times Jupiter's, so it is only slightly larger than Jupiter (Mancini et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"The two planets receive about the same heat from similar stars yet have very different sizes, and the reason is not known (Mancini et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats13b-Mancinietal2015","cite":"Mancini et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"L. Mancini, J. D. Hartman, K. Penev et al.","title":"HATS-13b and HATS-14b: two transiting hot Jupiters from the HATSouth survey","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.03469","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1533 b":{"text":{"why":"The system is a rare case of a small inner planet alongside a hot giant. Only eight such hot Jupiters with small inner-orbit companions were known as of May 2026, which suggests gentle migration through the disc (Mantovan et al. 2026).","observed":"The star is an active K dwarf, and its spots and flares disturb both the transit and velocity data. Modelling the activity together with simultaneous photometry and spectroscopic indicators let the planetary signals be pulled out at about 10 sigma (Mantovan et al. 2026). The inner planet is a sub-Neptune on a 3.63-day orbit, with a radius of 3.15 Earth radii in that analysis (Mantovan et al. 2026).","system":"An outer giant planet, TOI-1533 c, orbits every 8.06 days, with a radius above 7.5 Earth radii and a density below 0.48 g/cm3, so it has a large hydrogen-helium envelope (Mantovan et al. 2026). The two planets have similar masses but very different radii (Mantovan et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1533b-Mantovanetal2026","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Mantovan, V. Nascimbeni, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXVI. TOI-1533: A compact system hosting a super-Neptune-mass pair with disparate radii","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30799","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Barnard d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns Barnard's star. Before any planet was found, monitoring with the VLT spectrograph UVES over two and a half years ruled out planets above 7.5 Earth masses in the habitable zone, at 0.034 to 0.082 au (Kürster et al. 2003). The tightest limit was 2.9 Earth masses at a period of about 2.5 days (Kürster et al. 2003). Radial velocities also tracked stellar activity, with a link between velocity and emission in the H-alpha line (Kürster et al. 2003).","system":"Barnard's star is the nearest star after the alpha Centauri system, at 1.82 parsecs, and has the largest proper motion known, 10.4 arcseconds per year (Riedel et al. 2005). It is an old star, of 7 to 12 billion years, and yet still magnetically active, with X-ray and ultraviolet emission (Riedel et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-barnardd-Krsteretal2003","cite":"Kürster et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"M. Kürster, M. Endl, F. Rouesnel et al.","title":"The low-level radial velocity variability   in Barnard's star (= GJ 699).  Secular acceleration, indications for convective redshift,  and planet mass limits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0303528","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-barnardd-Riedeletal2005","cite":"Riedel et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"A. R. Riedel, E. F. Guinan, L. E. DeWarf et al.","title":"Barnard's Star as a Proxy for Old Disk dM Stars: Magnetic Activity, Light Variations, XUV Irradiances, and Planetary Habitable Zones","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #206","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2010-BLG-353L b":{"text":{"why":"The planetary signal was noticed only after the event had ended, during a systematic search of all archived binary-lens events (Rattenbury et al. 2015).","observed":"Only the high-cadence OGLE and MOA survey groups recorded the event, so the data are limited (Rattenbury et al. 2015). Assuming the lens star's chance of hosting such a planet does not depend on its mass or distance, and that the background star is in the Galactic bulge, the planet has about 0.9 times Saturn's mass. It orbits a red dwarf of about 0.18 solar masses at 6.43 kpc, with a projected separation of 1.72 au (Rattenbury et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet is only a possible one, and its mass and distance rest on assumptions. If the background star lies on the far side of the bulge, a slightly lighter planet is favoured (Rattenbury et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2010blg353lb-Rattenburyetal2015","cite":"Rattenbury et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"N. J. Rattenbury, D. P. Bennett, T. Sumi et al.","title":"MOA-2010-BLG-353Lb: a possible Saturn revealed","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.01393","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-32 e":{"text":{"why":"The star is a typical M dwarf, the most common kind of star, so its system may be a template for planets around the Galaxy's many small stars (Swift et al. 2013).","observed":"Five planets transit the star, making it the richest known system of transiting planets around an M dwarf (Swift et al. 2013). New high-resolution observations limited false-positive explanations and allowed the whole system to be validated (Swift et al. 2013).","system":"All five planets lie within one-third the size of Mercury's orbit. The innermost is only 4.3 stellar radii from the star's surface (Swift et al. 2013).","history":"Dust sublimation radius, a minimum-mass protoplanetary nebula and near-matching orbital periods of three adjacent planets were used to propose how such compact systems formed (Swift et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler32e-Swiftetal2013","cite":"Swift et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jonathan J. Swift, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. IV. Kepler-32 as a Prototype for the Formation of Compact Planetary Systems throughout the Galaxy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 83443 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from four instruments over more than two decades revealed a second planet, HD 83443c, with a minimum mass of 1.35 times Jupiter's, a distance of 8.0 au and a high eccentricity of 0.76 (Errico et al. 2022). Orbital simulations show stable regions where further planets, including some in the habitable zone, could exist (Errico et al. 2022).","system":"HD 83443 is a rare system with a hot Jupiter and an exterior planet (Errico et al. 2022). An earlier analysis gave the inner planet the smallest orbit of any then-known planet and an eccentricity of 0.079, kept up by gravitational interaction with an outer planet (Wu & Goldreich 2002).","history":"The outer planet's high eccentricity suggests a scattering event may have thrown the hot Jupiter into its tight orbit, leaving the outer planet on a wide, eccentric path (Errico et al. 2022).","open":"Whether further planets sit in the stable regions is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd83443b-Erricoetal2022","cite":"Errico et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adriana Errico, Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jonathan Horner et al.","title":"HD 83443c: A Highly Eccentric Giant Planet on a 22 yr Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.05711","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd83443b-WuGoldreich2002","cite":"Wu & Goldreich 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Yanqin Wu, Peter Goldreich","title":"Tidal Evolution of the Planetary System around HD 83443","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0108499","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-150.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star lies in the TESS southern continuous viewing zone, so it was watched almost without a break (Cañas et al. 2019). Radial velocities from the APOGEE-2 South spectrograph confirmed the planet, showing that large spectroscopic surveys now reach the precision to confirm giant planets directly (Cañas et al. 2019). Follow-up also used the Carnegie Planet Finder Spectrograph, a robotic telescope at Las Campanas and Gaia distances (Cañas et al. 2019).","system":"The star is middle-aged, about halfway through its main-sequence life (Cañas et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Further monitoring may reveal additional planets in the system (Cañas et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi15001-Caasetal2019","cite":"Cañas et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Gudmundur Stefansson, Andrew J. Monson et al.","title":"TOI-150: A Transiting Hot Jupiter in the TESS Southern CVZ","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.09710","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-47 b":{"text":{"why":"The 2016 report describes it as one of the two lowest-mass planets known with a radius larger than Jupiter's (Bakos et al. 2016).","observed":"Starlight filtered through the planet's atmosphere during transits was studied at optical wavelengths. Thirteen TESS transits refined the orbit, and two further transits were observed from the ground with the Large Binocular Telescope and the Gran Telescopio Canarias (Wang et al. 2026). One telescope's spectrum gives moderate evidence for absorption by titanium oxide, a molecule that can strongly shape an atmosphere's temperature. The other spectrum shows no firm sign of any specific absorber. Both show a slope with wavelength that points to aerosols, meaning haze or cloud particles (Wang et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether titanium oxide is really present is still tentative (Wang et al. 2026). Measuring the tilt of the orbit relative to the star's spin would test whether tides between star and planet pull giant planets into aligned orbits around stars like this one (Bakos et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp47b-Bakosetal2016","cite":"Bakos et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-47b AND HAT-P-48b: Two Low Density Sub-Saturn-Mass Transiting Planets on the Edge of the Period--Mass Desert","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04556","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp47b-Wangetal2026","cite":"Wang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Wan-Hao Wang, Guo Chen, Fei Yan et al.","title":"Optical transmission spectrum of HAT-P-47b: Evidence for aerosols and tentative TiO absorption","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29445","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63935 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits at the 'occurrence cliff', a drop in how common short-period planets are at around 3 Earth radii, which separates sub-Neptunes from giant planets (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet was picked out as one of the best targets for James Webb Space Telescope atmosphere studies. Among planets of its kind it ranks as the one with the most clearly visible atmosphere, judged by a score called the transmission spectroscopy metric (Scarsdale et al. 2021). Its density is consistent with a few percent of its mass being a hydrogen and helium envelope around a denser interior (Scarsdale et al. 2021). The planet was first flagged at twice its true orbital period, and later data corrected this (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","system":"A second planet of nearly the same size, HD 63935 c, orbits farther out. It was first noticed in radial velocity data and later seen to transit (Scarsdale et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The thickness and make-up of the atmosphere have not yet been measured (Scarsdale et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63935b-Scarsdaleetal2021","cite":"Scarsdale et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Scarsdale, J. M. Murphy, N. Batalha","title":"HD 63935's Space Oddities: Two Atmospheric Targets in Sparse Regions of Mass-Radius Space","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-963 c":{"text":{"why":"Sub-Earth planets are rare, and how they form is not understood. Only a few hundred are known (Wang et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was one of five new ultra-short-period planets, meaning planets that circle their star in under a day, found by re-searching Kepler data. The search used a graphics-card algorithm that folds the light curve at trial periods, combined with a neural network that recognises transit signals (Wang et al. 2024). Its radius of 0.6 Earth radii makes it smaller than Earth. Its star is a G or K dwarf (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"A second planet is known around the same star.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler963c-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kaitlyn Wang, Jian Ge, Kevin Willis et al.","title":"Discovery of small ultra-short-period planets orbiting Kepler KG dwarfs with GPU phase folding and deep learning","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17382","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-132 b":{"text":{"why":"With periods of 1.2 to 1.7 days, the first planets found by transit surveys sit well inside the 3 to 4 day cutoff seen in radial velocity surveys. OGLE-TR-132 b was grouped with them as a new class of 'very hot Jupiters' (Konacki et al. 2004).","observed":"Follow-up with the VLT and NTT gave very precise timing of the transits and a refined radius of 1.18 Jupiter radii and mass of 1.14 Jupiter masses (Gillon et al. 2007). The planet's equilibrium temperature is nearly 2000 K. It orbits at only 0.03 AU (Gillon et al. 2007). A smaller transit depth from an earlier analysis was traced to the method of reducing the data, so the new work gives a slightly larger planet and a slightly smaller, lighter star (Gillon et al. 2007).","system":"The star is a very metal-rich F dwarf about 2000 parsecs away, in the Galactic disc toward Carina (Gillon et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr132b-Konackietal2004","cite":"Konacki et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"Maciej Konacki, Guillermo Torres, Dimitar D. Sasselov et al.","title":"The Transiting Extrasolar Giant Planet around the Star OGLE-TR-113","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0404541","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr132b-Gillonetal2007","cite":"Gillon et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Gillon, F. Pont, C. Moutou et al.","title":"The transiting planet OGLE-TR-132b revisited with new spectroscopy and deconvolution photometry<SUP>, </SUP>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0702192","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 208527 b":{"text":{"why":"The star was re-classified as an M giant, so the planet is among the first candidates found around a star of that type (Lee et al. 2013).","observed":"A radial velocity survey with the BOES spectrograph at Bohyunsan Observatory, run from September 2008 to June 2012, found the star's velocity varying every 875.5 days. The variation does not match surface features on the star, so an orbiting companion is the likely cause (Lee et al. 2013). Assuming a stellar mass of 1.6 solar masses, the minimum mass is 9.9 Jupiter masses, at about 2.1 AU with a nearly circular orbit of eccentricity 0.08 (Lee et al. 2013).","system":"The host is a giant star. A second M giant, HD 220074, was reported with a planet in the same study (Lee et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"The true mass is not known, because radial velocities give only a minimum (Lee et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd208527b-Leeetal2013","cite":"Lee et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"B.-C. Lee, I. Han, M.-G. Park","title":"Planetary companions orbiting M giants HD 208527 and HD 220074","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.2051","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 152079 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet came from the Magellan velocity survey, which turned up five new Jupiter-mass planets on eccentric, intermediate- to long-period orbits around G and K dwarfs (Arriagada et al. 2010). The star is a G6 dwarf. The planet has a minimum mass of about 3 Jupiter masses and orbits at 3.2 AU. Its orbit is quite eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.60 (Arriagada et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass is not known, because radial velocities give only a minimum (Arriagada et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd152079b-Arriagadaetal2010","cite":"Arriagada et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Pamela Arriagada, R. Paul Butler, Dante Minniti et al.","title":"Five Long-period Extrasolar Planets in Eccentric Orbits from the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63433 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is only about 400 million years old, so the planet is seen early in its life (Damasso et al. 2023). Radial velocity monitoring with HARPS-N aimed at the first mass measurement and at how much atmosphere the planet loses to heating by its star. Searches for escaping gas have given mixed results. Keck observations found no helium absorption, with an upper limit on mass loss of 7.9 x 10^10 g/s (Alam et al. 2024). A search for escaping hydrogen found no signal either (Milburn et al. 2025). A separate survey saw hints of helium that need confirming (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Small shifts in the timing of its transits have also been flagged as a candidate (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026).","system":"At least one more planet, HD 63433 c, orbits closer in than three weeks, and both are sub-Neptunes (Damasso et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is losing atmosphere now is not settled. Long-term monitoring is needed to confirm the timing shifts (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63433b-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Locci, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XLII. A characterisation study of the multi-planet system around the 400 Myr-old star HD 63433 (TOI-1726)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15242","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433b-Alametal2024","cite":"Alam et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Munazza K. Alam, James Kirk, Leonardo A. Dos Santos et al.","title":"Nondetections of Helium in the Young Sub-Jovian Planets K2-100b, HD 63433b, and V1298 Tau c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17294","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433b-Milburnetal2025","cite":"Milburn et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Reilly P. Milburn, Andrew W. Mann, Keighley Rockcliffe et al.","title":"Ever Elusive Exospheres: One Probable Detection and Two Nondetections of Hα Transits in Young Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18820","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433b-Murilloetal2026","cite":"Murillo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ana Isabel Lopez Murillo, Andrew W. Mann, Madyson G. Barber et al.","title":"Searching for Transit Timing Variations in Young Transiting Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"2M0437 b":{"text":{"why":"A super-Jupiter around a very young, very low-mass star is hard to explain with current planet formation ideas (Gaidos et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was seen as a faint companion 0.9 arcseconds from its star, and three years of adaptive-optics images show it moves with the star (Gaidos et al. 2022). Comparison with models gives a mass of 3 to 5 Jupiter masses, well below the limit for fusing deuterium, and a temperature of 1400 to 1500 K, typical of a late L-type object. Its colour in the infrared is redder than a typical L dwarf but similar to other imaged young planets, such as those around HR 8799 (Gaidos et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a single mid-M dwarf of about 3100 K in the Taurus star-forming region, aged 2 to 5 million years (Gaidos et al. 2022). The star shows no infrared excess from a disc, yet K2 saw quasi-periodic dimming from close-in dust (Gaidos et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-2m0437b-Gaidosetal2022","cite":"Gaidos et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. Gaidos, T. Hirano, A. L. Kraus et al.","title":"Zodiacal exoplanets in time (ZEIT) XII: a directly imaged planetary-mass companion to a young Taurus M dwarf star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.08655","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-445 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three small, short-period planets seen transiting the same mid-M dwarf. The star is a metal-rich M4 dwarf (Muirhead et al. 2015). A strict prior on the star's density was used when fitting the transits, which removes a correlation between the fitted impact parameter and planet-to-star radius ratio for short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"Kepler-445 hosts three transiting planets. Kepler-445 c is similar to GJ 1214 b in size and host-star properties (Muirhead et al. 2015). A study of Kepler mid-M dwarfs puts the share hosting compact multiple-planet systems at about 21 percent (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler445d-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1774.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is also known as HD 85426 b. Its mass was measured by combining HARPS-N radial velocities with TESS and CHEOPS transit data. The result depends on how stellar activity is removed, giving 6.0 or 8.5 Earth masses (Lienhard et al. 2026). Its blackbody equilibrium temperature is 824 K (Lienhard et al. 2026).","system":"The star is a bright, 7.4-billion-year-old solar analogue of 0.99 solar masses.","history":"","open":"The mass is uncertain between two values that depend on how stellar activity is handled (Lienhard et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi177401-Lienhardetal2026","cite":"Lienhard et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"F. Lienhard, A. Mortier, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"HARPS-N, TESS, and CHEOPS discover a transiting sub-Neptune and two outer companions around the bright solar analogue HD 85426","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08473","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1279 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A homogeneous analysis of TESS planets from the TESS-Keck Survey found significant transit-timing variations for TOI-1279 b, meaning its transits do not arrive on a perfectly regular schedule. It is among at least one single-planet system with this effect. The others with the effect in the sample are in multi-planet systems (MacDougall et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1279b-MacDougalletal2023","cite":"MacDougall et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mason G. MacDougall, Erik A. Petigura, Gregory J. Gilbert et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. XV. Precise Properties of 108 TESS Planets and Their Host Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00251","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-106 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"ESPRESSO measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the distortion in starlight as the planet crosses the spinning star, and found the orbit nearly aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of 0 to 10 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025). A search for water in its atmosphere with the same spectrograph found none (Prinoth et al. 2024). Planets like this, with signals that are small compared with the noise, are hard to probe from the ground (Prinoth et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Across warm Jupiters around single stars, 95 percent have tilts below about 25 degrees, so they likely formed in discs aligned with their star's spin. Hot Jupiters are more often misaligned (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp106b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp106b-Prinothetal2024","cite":"Prinoth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Bibiana Prinoth, Elyar Sedaghati, Julia V. Seidel et al.","title":"High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy of Warm Jupiters: An ESPRESSO Sample with Predictions for ANDES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08558","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 128717 b":{"text":{"why":"Objects between giant planets and brown dwarfs are poorly understood, and few long-period ones have well-measured orbits (Pinamonti et al. 2026).","observed":"The companion was first flagged as a candidate in Gaia DR3 astrometry, the measured sideways wobble of the star on the sky. HARPS-N radial velocities taken at high cadence refined the orbit. The authors combined them with the star's proper motion anomaly to get the full three-dimensional orbit (Pinamonti et al. 2026). The study calls it HD 128717 B and describes it as a long-period, eccentric, low-mass brown dwarf (Pinamonti et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The study also looked for lower-mass planets orbiting closer in (Pinamonti et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd128717b-Pinamontietal2026","cite":"Pinamonti et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. Pinamonti, A. Sozzetti, D. Barbato et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXX. HD128717 B/Gaia-6 B: A long-period eccentric low-mass brown dwarf from astrometry and radial velocities","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04606","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-169 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-density Jupiter with about 0.56 Jupiter masses and a radius of about 1.3 Jupiter radii (Nielsen et al. 2019). It orbits a V = 12.17 F8 subgiant. Follow-up used CORALIE and HARPS velocities and photometry from EulerCam, TRAPPIST-North and -South, and SPECULOOS (Nielsen et al. 2019).","system":"WASP-169 b was reported along with three other giant planets from the same survey: WASP-171 b, WASP-175 b and WASP-182 b (Nielsen et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp169b-Nielsenetal2019","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, F. Bouchy, O. D. Turner et al.","title":"WASP-169, WASP-171, WASP-175, and WASP-182: three hot Jupiters and one bloated sub-Saturn mass planet discovered by WASP-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 65426 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was the first exoplanet imaged by JWST. JWST images from 2 to 16 microns detected it in all seven filters and gave the first direct detection of an exoplanet beyond 5 microns (Carter et al. 2023). It lies about 0.82 arcseconds from its star. Earlier SPHERE spectra show a warm, dusty atmosphere like that of young, low-gravity L5 to L7 dwarfs (Chauvin et al. 2017). Models give a mass of 6 to 12 Jupiter masses and a temperature of 1300 to 1600 K (Chauvin et al. 2017). VLT 4 to 5 micron photometry was also used to study its atmosphere (Stolker et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a young A2 star in the roughly 17-million-year-old Lower Centaurus-Crux association (Chauvin et al. 2017). The star pulses as a delta Scuti variable, and its spin is plausibly aligned with the planet's orbit (Sepulveda et al. 2024).","history":"Its atmosphere is enriched in heavy elements, suggesting it took in large amounts of solids early on (Wang 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip65426b-Carteretal2023","cite":"Carter et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Aarynn L. Carter, Sasha Hinkley, Jens Kammerer et al.","title":"The JWST Early Release Science Program for Direct Observations of Exoplanetary Systems I: High-contrast Imaging of the Exoplanet HIP 65426 b from 2 to 16 μm","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.14990","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65426b-Chauvinetal2017","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"G. Chauvin, S. Desidera, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"Discovery of a warm, dusty giant planet around HIP 65426","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01413","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65426b-Stolkeretal2020","cite":"Stolker et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"T. Stolker, S. P. Quanz, K. O. Todorov et al.","title":"MIRACLES: atmospheric characterization of directly imaged planets and substellar companions at 4-5 μm. I. Photometric analysis of β Pic b, HIP 65426 b, PZ Tel B, and HD 206893 B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.13316","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65426b-Sepulvedaetal2024","cite":"Sepulveda et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Aldo G. Sepulveda, Daniel Huber, Timothy R. Bedding et al.","title":"HIP 65426 is a High-frequency Delta Scuti Pulsator in Plausible Spin─Orbit Alignment with its Directly Imaged Exoplanet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05310","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65426b-Wang2025","cite":"Wang 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ji Wang","title":"Early Accretion of Large Amounts of Solids for Directly Imaged Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.00088","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-445 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three small, short-period planets seen transiting the same mid-M dwarf. The star is a metal-rich M4 dwarf (Muirhead et al. 2015). A strict prior on the star's density was used when fitting the transits, which removes a correlation between the fitted impact parameter and planet-to-star radius ratio for short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"Kepler-445 hosts three transiting planets. Kepler-445 c is similar to GJ 1214 b in size and host-star properties (Muirhead et al. 2015). A study of Kepler mid-M dwarfs puts the share hosting compact multiple-planet systems at about 21 percent (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler445b-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-142 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit. Its existence was inferred from the large transit-timing variations of KOI-142 b, which reach about 12 hours, and from changes in that planet's transit duration (Nesvorny et al. 2013). The analysis gave a mass of about 0.63 Jupiter masses. SOPHIE radial velocities later confirmed it, the first such confirmation of a non-transiting planet found from transit timing (Barros et al. 2014).","system":"Planet c orbits just wide of a 2:1 resonance with the transiting planet b, with a period ratio of 2.03 (Nesvorny et al. 2013). A third, more distant planet, Kepler-88 d, has a 1403-day period and a mass of about 3 Jupiter masses on an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.41 (Weiss et al. 2020).","history":"The system's dynamics can be used to test ideas for how near-resonant planet pairs form (Nesvorny et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi142c-Nesvornyetal2013","cite":"Nesvorny et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"David Nesvorný, David Kipping, Dirk Terrell et al.","title":"KOI-142, The King of Transit Variations, is a Pair of Planets near the 2:1 Resonance","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4283","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi142c-Barrosetal2014","cite":"Barros et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, R. F. Díaz, A. Santerne et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. X. KOI-142 c: first radial velocity confirmation of a non-transiting exoplanet discovered by transit timing","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4335","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi142c-Weissetal2020","cite":"Weiss et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Eric Agol et al.","title":"The Discovery of the Long-Period, Eccentric Planet Kepler-88 d and System Characterization with Radial Velocities and Photodynamical Analysis","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.02427","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2578 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS data were combined with ground-based observations to determine the planet's mass and radius. Its low density and large transmission spectroscopy metric, a score for how easily its atmosphere can be studied, make it a particularly good target for atmospheric work (Guenther et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2578b-Guentheretal2026","cite":"Guenther et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Eike W. Guenther, William D. Cochran, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"The Discovery of Five Hot Jupiters by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6449","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-105 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up found its host to be a metal-rich K2 star, in line with the tendency of giant planets to favour stars rich in heavy elements (Anderson et al. 2017).","observed":"WASP-105 b is a warm Jupiter, a giant planet in a 7.9-day orbit (Anderson et al. 2017). Its orbit is wide enough that tides from the star are weak. Measuring how the planet's orbit lines up with the star's spin could therefore help show how such giant planets moved inward (Anderson et al. 2017). That measurement was later made from archived spectra of the planet crossing its star, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in the star's light as the planet blocks part of its spinning surface. The orbit appears aligned with the star's spin (Zak et al. 2024). A search for atomic absorption in the planet's atmosphere found nothing (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Its aligned orbit, together with weak tides at this distance, leaves open how the planet reached its close orbit (Anderson et al. 2017; Zak et al. 2024).","open":"No atmospheric features were detected, most likely because high clouds or scattering by small particles mute the signal (Zak et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp105b-Andersonetal2017","cite":"Anderson et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, L. Delrez et al.","title":"The discoveries of WASP-91b, WASP-105b and WASP-107b: Two warm Jupiters and a planet in the transition region between ice giants and gas giants","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03776","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp105b-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, A. Bocchieri, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquity measurements of six gas giants. Orbital misalignment of WASP-101b and WASP-131b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15631","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-101 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Forty radial velocity measurements with the HARPS-N spectrograph gave the planet's mass, which was combined with the transit data (Bonomo et al. 2014). The result made it the first fully characterised super-Neptune, a planet larger than Neptune (Bonomo et al. 2014). Its density of about 1.45 g per cubic centimetre suggests that heavy elements make up more than 60% of its mass (Bonomo et al. 2014). Its host is a slightly evolved, metal-rich G-type star (Bonomo et al. 2014).","system":"A second planet, Kepler-101 c, is Earth-sized and orbits in 6.03 days. Its radius implies it has no hydrogen-helium envelope (Bonomo et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler101b-Bonomoetal2014","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, C. Lovis et al.","title":"Characterization of the planetary system Kepler-101 with HARPS-N. A hot super-Neptune with an Earth-sized low-mass companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.4592","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158259 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 158259 was monitored with the SOPHIE spectrograph, a search for Neptunes and super-Earths, giving 290 radial velocity measurements (Hara et al. 2020). The data support five planets, each with a minimum mass of about 6 Earth masses, in orbits of 3.4, 5.2, 7.9, 12 and 17.4 days (Hara et al. 2020). The 12-day planet is among the five. The outermost signal is classed only as a planet candidate, because it is slightly less significant and lies close to the star's expected rotation period (Hara et al. 2020).","system":"The five planets form a compact system close to a chain of 3:2 orbital resonances, where neighbouring orbits repeat in simple ratios (Hara et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Slow changes in the star's velocity, probably from a magnetic cycle and instrument drift, complicate the measurements. A new correction for instrument changes in SOPHIE data was later built from pressure and temperature records (Grouffal et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158259f-Haraetal2020","cite":"Hara et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. C. Hara, F. Bouchy, M. Stalport et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVI. HD 158259: A compact planetary system in a near-3:2 mean motion resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd158259f-Grouffaletal2024","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 158259 SOPHIE correction of RV systematics (Grouffal+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-102 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-102 has five tightly packed transiting planets and an active star, whose spots can disturb velocity measurements (Brinkman et al. 2023). New radial velocities, 111 in all, came from Keck/HIRES and the HARPS-N spectrograph, and the star's activity was modelled with a statistical method that fits repeating patterns (Brinkman et al. 2023). The planet's mass came out at 4.7 Earth masses, with an uncertainty of 1.7 (Brinkman et al. 2023). Earlier work found a density typical of sub-Neptune planets (Brinkman et al. 2023).","system":"The planets orbit in 5 to 28 days. They lie near, but not locked into, orbital resonances (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The inner super-Earth, Kepler-102 d, was found to have a mass consistent with a rocky composition like Earth's (Brinkman et al. 2023).","history":"Packed systems like this one tend to become unstable within 100 million years when simulated. The host star is thought to be older than 2 billion years, so the planets probably avoided that fate (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","open":"The masses of the smallest inner planets are not well constrained (Volk & Malhotra 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler102e-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, James Cadman, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"Kepler-102: Masses and Compositions for a Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Orbiting an Active Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05196","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler102e-VolkMalhotra2020","cite":"Volk & Malhotra 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Volk, R. Malhotra","title":"Kepler-102: a case study for using dynamical constraints to characterize exoplanet systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 192310 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up targeted a few nearby, quiet, slowly rotating Sun-like stars, aiming to find very low-mass planets in their habitable zones (Pepe et al. 2011).","observed":"HARPS monitored ten of the most stable stars from its high-precision programme, taking three 10-minute observations a night, two hours apart, to average out pulsation and surface granulation noise (Pepe et al. 2011). Each star got about 50 data points in a season (Pepe et al. 2011). HD 192310 was one of three stars from this campaign that yielded planets: five new low-mass planets and one confirmed one were reported across HD 20794, HD 85512 and HD 192310 (Pepe et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd192310c-Pepeetal2011","cite":"Pepe et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Pepe, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Very low-mass planets around <ASTROBJ>HD 20794</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 85512</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 192310</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3447","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-36 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-36 b orbits a metal-poor G2 dwarf in 1.54 days, and its mass and radius are 2.30 and 1.28 times Jupiter's (Smith et al. 2012). Eight full or partial transit light curves from four observatories let astronomers test how much a single light curve can mislead. Each curve alone gave results consistent with the combined fit, despite correlated noise in at least two (Smith et al. 2012). Near-ultraviolet transit observations later showed no lopsided transit shape for it or for 14 other planets (Turner et al. 2016). Further transit timing showed no sign of timing variations (Maciejewski et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp36b-Smithetal2012","cite":"Smith et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-36b: A New Transiting Planet around a Metal-poor G-dwarf, and an Investigation into Analyses Based on a Single Transit Light Curve","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5313","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp36b-Turneretal2016","cite":"Turner et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Kyle A. Pearson, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Ground-based near-UV observations of 15 transiting exoplanets: constraints on their atmospheres and no evidence for asymmetrical transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02587","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp36b-Maciejewskietal2016","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"G. Maciejewski, D. Dimitrov, L. Mancini et al.","title":"New Transit Observations for HAT-P-30 b, HAT-P-37 b, TrES-5 b, WASP-28 b, WASP-36 b and WASP-39 b","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.03268","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2580 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2580 b is one of four hot Jupiters whose planetary nature was confirmed using the MaHPS spectrograph at Wendelstein Observatory, together with NEID and TRES (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). The four orbit in 2.4 to 4.0 days and have radii of 1.29 to 1.64 Jupiter radii (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). They are among the first planets confirmed with MaHPS data, which shows it can work on stars as faint as V = 12 (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). A search for other planets in the systems found none (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","system":"No other planets were found around the star (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2580b-Ehrhardtetal2024","cite":"Ehrhardt et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Juliana Ehrhardt, Luis Thomas, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"Confirmation of four hot Jupiters detected by TESS using follow-up spectroscopy from MaHPS at Wendelstein together with NEID and TRES","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04383","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 486 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 486 b is the third closest transiting planet, at 8.1 parsecs (Caballero 2023). Its star is bright, quiet and visible from both hemispheres, which makes the planet a prime target for atmosphere studies (Caballero 2023). The star's angular size was measured directly with the CHARA Array, and velocity data from CARMENES and MAROON-X were combined with TESS and CHEOPS transits (Caballero 2023). Observations of the star in X-rays and ultraviolet light show flares, despite the star looking inactive at optical wavelengths (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2024). The planet is not expected to keep a thick hydrogen atmosphere, but could hold a pure carbon dioxide one if it began with about half of Earth's total supply of that gas (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The star is at least 6.6 billion years old (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2024).","open":"Whether the planet has any atmosphere at all is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj486b-Caballero2023","cite":"Caballero 2023","year":"2023","authors":"José A. Caballero","title":"Here comes the GJ 486","journal":"Highlights on Spanish Astrophysics XI","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj486b-DiamondLoweetal2024","cite":"Diamond-Lowe et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. Diamond-Lowe, G. W. King, A. Youngblood et al.","title":"High-energy spectra of LTT 1445A and GJ 486 reveal flares and activity","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.00165","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOBE-1 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, KOBE, is a blind search of 50 late K-dwarf stars with the CARMENES spectrograph, planned to detect planets in the habitable zone (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","observed":"KOBE-1 is the first planetary system from the KOBE survey (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). K dwarfs offer a trade-off between the chance of habitability and ease of detection (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). A total of 82 CARMENES spectra over three years showed two signals, at 8.5 and 29.7 days, which were confirmed as planets after other explanations were ruled out (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025). Both planets likely lie in the sub-Neptune mass range, and the star is a K7 dwarf (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","system":"The inner planet orbits in 8.5 days and the outer one, KOBE-1 c, in 29.7 days (Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The planet's true size is not known because it has not been seen to transit."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kobe1c-BalsalobreRuzaetal2025","cite":"Balsalobre-Ruza et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"O. Balsalobre-Ruza, J. Lillo-Box, A. M. Silva et al.","title":"KOBE-1: The first planetary system from the KOBE survey: Two planets likely residing in the sub-Neptune mass regime around a late K-dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01249","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-28 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-28 b is a Jupiter-sized planet with a mass of 0.484 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.955 Jupiter radii, in a 5.2-day orbit (Cabrera et al. 2015). Its host is an evolved star, one that has aged past the main sequence (Cabrera et al. 2015). Spectra from HARPS established the planet's nature and gave its mass, while ground-based follow-up measured the star's temperature, gravity and spin (Cabrera et al. 2015). The shape of the transit constrained the star's density, which was linked to stellar evolution models to fix the star's properties (Cabrera et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot28b-Cabreraetal2015","cite":"Cabrera et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. Cabrera, Sz. Csizmadia, G. Montagnier et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXVII. CoRoT-28b, a planet orbiting an evolved star, and CoRoT-29b, a planet showing an asymmetric transit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.01532","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2012-BLG-0950L b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery stands out for how its mass was measured: it was the first mass measurement from only the microlens parallax and the lens's own light, without a finite source effect (Koshimoto et al. 2017).","observed":"A slow distortion in the light curves seen by MOA and OGLE came from the Earth's orbital motion around the Sun, which gives the microlens parallax (Koshimoto et al. 2017). Keck adaptive optics imaging captured the lens's own light (Koshimoto et al. 2017). Together these show a planet of about 35 Earth masses around an M dwarf of 0.56 solar masses, 2.7 au apart in projection, about 3.0 kiloparsecs from us (Koshimoto et al. 2017). The planet-to-host mass ratio is about 2 in 10,000 (Koshimoto et al. 2017).","system":"The host is an M dwarf (Koshimoto et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Its orbit is not known, since only the projected separation was measured (Koshimoto et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2012blg0950lb-Koshimotoetal2017","cite":"Koshimoto et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"N. Koshimoto, A. Udalski, J. P. Beaulieu et al.","title":"OGLE-2012-BLG-0950Lb: The First Planet Mass Measurement from Only Microlens Parallax and Lens Flux","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03267","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 2685 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 2685 b is an inflated hot Jupiter in a low-eccentricity orbit around an early F-type star, with a mass of 1.17 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.44 Jupiter radii (Jones et al. 2019). Its star is one of the brightest known to host a transiting hot Jupiter, making it a good target for atmosphere studies (Jones et al. 2019). New HARPS velocities taken during and outside transit detected the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in starlight as the planet crosses the spinning star, and found an intermediate projected obliquity of about 56 degrees (Csizmadia et al. 2026).","system":"A search of TESS data found no additional planets down to sub-Neptune size (Maciejewski 2022). The star is single, evolved and hot (Csizmadia et al. 2026).","history":"The absence of companions fits with giant planets reaching close orbits by high-eccentricity migration, in which a planet is flung onto an elongated path that shrinks (Maciejewski 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd2685b-Jonesetal2019","cite":"Jones et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Matías I. Jones, Rafael Brahm, Nestor Espinoza et al.","title":"HD 2685 b: a hot Jupiter orbiting an early F-type star detected by TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05518","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd2685b-Csizmadiaetal2026","cite":"Csizmadia et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Szilárd Csizmadia, Alexis M. S. Smith, J. V. Harre et al.","title":"A comprehensive Rossiter─McLaughlin modelling framework in TLCM: application to HD 2685 = TOI-135 system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03456","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd2685b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5349 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, Searching for GEMS, hunts giant planets around M dwarfs (Sandoval et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5349 b is a warm, Saturn-like planet around an early M dwarf rich in heavy elements (Sandoval et al. 2026). It was confirmed with velocity measurements from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and MAROON-X, plus ground-based transit observations from 0.6 m and 1.0 m telescopes (Sandoval et al. 2026). Its mass is 0.40 Jupiter masses and its radius 0.91 Jupiter radii (Sandoval et al. 2026). Such giants around M dwarfs allow comparison with hot Jupiter analogues around Sun-like stars (Sandoval et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"How short-period gas giants form and migrate is not fully understood, and planets like this one help to test theories (Sandoval et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5349b-Sandovaletal2026","cite":"Sandoval et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Angeli Sandoval, Caleb I. Cañas, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-5349 b is a Saturn-like Planet Orbiting a Metal-rich Early M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19492","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-101 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-101 c is an Earth-sized planet, with a radius of 1.25 Earth radii, which implies it has no hydrogen-helium envelope (Bonomo et al. 2014). Its mass could not be measured, because the host star is faint for precise velocity work and few measurements exist (Bonomo et al. 2014). An upper limit of 3.8 Earth masses rules out a pure iron composition (Bonomo et al. 2014).","system":"Its neighbour Kepler-101 b is a hot super-Neptune with a density of about 1.45 g per cubic centimetre (Bonomo et al. 2014). The two planets orbit a slightly evolved, metal-rich G-type star in 3.49 and 6.03 days (Bonomo et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"The planet's mass and composition are not known beyond the upper limit."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler101c-Bonomoetal2014","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, C. Lovis et al.","title":"Characterization of the planetary system Kepler-101 with HARPS-N. A hot super-Neptune with an Earth-sized low-mass companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.4592","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-297 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-297 has three transiting planets (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015). Variations in their transit times, from Kepler data and new Spitzer data on Kepler-297 c, were used to study the system (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015). The planets pull on each other, which lets their mass ratios be derived (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015). The best fit includes a fourth, non-transiting planet outside the three (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015).","system":"The outermost transiting candidate was confirmed as a planet, Kepler-297 d (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler297c-DiamondLoweetal2015","cite":"Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Hannah Diamond-Lowe, Kevin B. Stevenson, Daniel Fabrycky et al.","title":"Determining the architecture of the Kepler-297 system using transit timing variations","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 190647 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up aims to improve knowledge of planet orbits among Sun-like stars, to constrain how planets form (Naef et al. 2007).","observed":"HARPS has monitored the radial velocities of about 1400 Solar-type stars to search for low-mass companions (Naef et al. 2007). The survey covers a volume-limited sample, meaning all stars within a set distance, which gives a fair picture of how common planets are (Naef et al. 2007). HD 190647 is one of three stars for which orbital solutions were presented, along with HD 100777 and HD 221287 (Naef et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd190647b-Naefetal2007","cite":"Naef et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IX. Exoplanets orbiting <ASTROBJ>HD 100777</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 190647</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 221287</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.0917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3021 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 3021 b is one of three planets reported from the CORALIE survey, all in longer orbits than 51 Peg-like planets, with fairly eccentric orbits and metal-rich host stars (Naef et al. 2001). Imaging at the Very Large Telescope showed that the star has a companion, GJ 3021 B (also called HD 1237 B), which moves with it and is a low-mass star of type M4 (Chauvin et al. 2007). Simulations of the star's wind examined how particles might reach the planet's atmosphere (Alvarado-Gómez et al. 2016). Binary companions can change the true mass, and fits of velocity data for GJ 3021 differ by more than 0.2 Jupiter masses depending on the model used (Huang et al. 2025).","system":"The star has a low-mass stellar companion (Chauvin et al. 2007).","history":"","open":"The planet's true mass is not known, since only its minimum mass has been measured. Precise position measurements of the star would be needed (Huang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3021b-Naefetal2001","cite":"Naef et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, F. Pepe et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. V. 3 new extrasolar planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0106255","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3021b-Chauvinetal2007","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Chauvin, A.-M. Lagrange, S. Udry et al.","title":"Characterization of the long-period companions of the exoplanet host stars: HD 196885, HD 1237 and HD 27442. VLT/NACO and SINFONI near-infrared, follow-up imaging and spectroscopy","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20067046","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3021b-AlvaradoGmezetal2016","cite":"Alvarado-Gómez et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. D. Alvarado-Gómez, G. A. J. Hussain, O. Cohen et al.","title":"Simulating the environment around planet-hosting stars. II. Stellar winds and inner astrospheres","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.08405","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3021b-Huangetal2025","cite":"Huang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Xiumin Huang, Jianghui Ji, Chunhui Bao et al.","title":"Closeby Habitable Exoplanet Survey (CHES). III. Retrieval of Planetary Masses in Binaries Using the N-body Model with Radial Velocity and Astrometry Synergy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17090","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2322 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2322 is a K4 star with strong activity signals. Its rotation period of 21.28 days was measured from ESPRESSO data and TESS and ASAS-SN photometry (Hobson et al. 2025). Spots on a rotating star can mimic planets in velocity data (Hobson et al. 2025). A joint model of photometry, velocities and activity indicators separated planets from activity (Hobson et al. 2025). TOI-2322 b has a period of 11.31 days, close to half the star's rotation period, a radius of 0.994 Earth radii, and a mass of no more than 2.03 Earth masses (Hobson et al. 2025).","system":"A second planet, TOI-2322 c, orbits in 20.2 days, close to the rotation period, with a mass of 18.10 Earth masses (Hobson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2322b-Hobsonetal2025","cite":"Hobson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"M. J. Hobson, A. Suárez Mascareño, C. Lovis et al.","title":"TOI-2322: Two transiting rocky planets close to the stellar rotation period and its first harmonic","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18094","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-68 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-68 b. Kepler-68 d is a Jupiter-mass planet that lies beyond two smaller planets whose transits were seen by the Kepler space telescope. Its orbit was found from Doppler measurements, which gave a period of 580 ± 15 days and a minimum mass of 0.947 ± 0.035 Jupiter masses (Gilliland et al. 2013). Kepler also recorded strong, rich pulsations of the host star, which allowed a detailed study of its interior and precise stellar properties (Gilliland et al. 2013).","system":"Two planets orbit much closer in. Kepler-68 b has a 5.4 day orbit, 8.3 Earth masses and a density between that of Earth and the ice giants (Gilliland et al. 2013). Kepler-68 c is Earth-sized and orbits in 9.6 days (Gilliland et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler68d-Gillilandetal2013","cite":"Gilliland et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ronald L. Gilliland, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-68: Three Planets, One with a Density between that of Earth and Ice Giants","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2596","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-199 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its transiting neighbour TOI-199 b, a 104.854 day warm giant with 0.17 Jupiter masses, the first warm exo-Saturn with a precisely measured mass and radius (Hobson et al. 2023). Planet c does not transit. Its presence shows up as strong transit timing variations, shifts in when planet b crosses the star, seen by TESS and by the ASTEP telescope in Antarctica (Hobson et al. 2023). Combining those shifts with radial velocities gives a unique solution for planet c, with a 273.69 day period and a mass of about 0.28 Jupiter masses (Hobson et al. 2023). Its orbit lies within the conservative habitable zone (Hobson et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi199c-Hobsonetal2023","cite":"Hobson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Melissa J. Hobson, Trifon Trifonov, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"TOI-199 b: A Well-characterized 100 day Transiting Warm Giant Planet with TTVs Seen from Antarctica","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14915","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1656 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from Keck/HIRES gave a mass of 48 ± 4 Earth masses and an orbital eccentricity of 0.84 ± 0.01, the largest measured for any planet under 100 Earth masses (Brady et al. 2018). Roughly 80% of the planet's mass is in dense rock and iron, with the rest in a light hydrogen-helium envelope (Brady et al. 2018). Later measurements of the angle between the star's spin and the planet's orbit suggest the orbit is aligned with the star's rotation, despite the extreme eccentricity (Rubenzahl et al. 2024).","system":"A second planet, with 0.40 ± 0.09 Jupiter masses and a 1919 day period, orbits farther out (Angelo et al. 2022).","history":"Scattering and merging of planets during or after the gas disc's dispersal may explain the high density and eccentricity (Brady et al. 2018). Simulations favour a planet that formed where it is now, with the outer planet gently stretching its orbit through slow Kozai-Lidov oscillations. There is no evidence that it has migrated inward on its eccentric orbit (Angelo et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1656b-Bradyetal2018","cite":"Brady et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Madison T. Brady, Erik A. Petigura, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-1656b: A Dense Sub-Saturn with an Extreme Eccentricity","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08436","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1656b-Rubenzahletal2024","cite":"Rubenzahl et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ryan Rubenzahl, Fei Dai, Samuel Halverson et al.","title":"Hot Jupiter Formation Clues from Three Extreme Solar Systems with the Keck Planet Finder","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1656b-Angeloetal2022","cite":"Angelo et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Isabel Angelo, Smadar Naoz, Erik Petigura et al.","title":"Kepler-1656b's Extreme Eccentricity: Signature of a Gentle Giant","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.00019","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 98-59 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Models of how the planet formed and changed over time have been compared with recent observations of its atmosphere. The observations can be explained by sulfur dioxide made in place by light-driven chemistry in a hydrogen-rich atmosphere (Nicholls et al. 2026). Such an atmosphere points to a chemically reducing mantle holding more than 1.8 mass% of sulfur and hydrogen early on (Nicholls et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a small red dwarf with several planets. The three innermost planets transit, and two of them have been observed with the Hubble Space Telescope's WFC3 camera for atmospheric studies (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). Planets c and d lie very close to a 2:1 orbital resonance, where one completes two orbits for each orbit of the other (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). A possible further signal near 23 days could instead come from stellar activity (Schwarz et al. 2022).","history":"Evolution models suggest a permanent magma ocean that keeps volatile gases locked in the mantle for billions of years (Nicholls et al. 2026). Cooling, atmospheric erosion and photochemistry have shaped it since (Nicholls et al. 2026). The results fit neither a rocky core with a hydrogen-helium atmosphere nor a water-dominated world (Nicholls et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l9859d-Nichollsetal2026","cite":"Nicholls et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Harrison Nicholls, Tim Lichtenberg, Richard D. Chatterjee et al.","title":"Volatile-rich evolution of molten super-Earth L 98-59 d","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02656","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859d-SchwarzDreizler2023","cite":"Schwarz & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler","title":"L98-59 (TOI-175) a case study for a multiple exoplanet system with small planets near resonance","journal":"EAS2023, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859d-Schwarzetal2022","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler, René Heller","title":"Disentangling the stellar activity and the radial velocity signatures of L 98-59 (TOI-175)","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2009-BLG-387L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The microlensing event showed pronounced deviations over a 12 day interval, one of the longest for any planetary event (Batista et al. 2011). The planet-to-star mass ratio of 0.0132 ± 0.003 was measured very well (Batista et al. 2011). The host star is an M dwarf with a mass between 0.07 and 0.49 solar masses at 90% confidence, which puts the planet near 2.6 Jupiter masses for a median host mass of 0.19 solar masses (Batista et al. 2011). Its distance is between 3.5 and 7.9 kiloparsecs, and its orbit has a semi-major axis between 1.1 and 2.7 AU (Batista et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host mass depends on a statistical argument. The effect of Earth's own orbital motion could not be separated from the planet's orbital motion, so a model of the Galaxy was used to resolve it (Batista et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2009blg387lb-Batistaetal2011","cite":"Batista et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"V. Batista, A. Gould, S. Dieters et al.","title":"MOA-2009-BLG-387Lb: a massive planet orbiting an M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0558","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 h":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its innermost planet, HD 219134 b. Spitzer observations caught it crossing its star, making it the nearest known transiting planet at the time (Motalebi et al. 2015). Its mass is 4.36 ± 0.44 Earth masses and its radius is 1.606 ± 0.086 Earth radii, giving a density that suggests a rocky composition (Motalebi et al. 2015). Planet h itself has no transit and no direct observation beyond its radial velocity signal.","system":"The star is a bright K dwarf only 6.53 parsecs away with six reported planets (Bailey 2023). Planets b and c transit, so the system may be the nearest, brightest star with a compact set of transiting planets (Bailey 2023). TESS data rule out transits of planet f (Bailey 2023). A search with the ASTERIA CubeSat and TESS also placed an upper limit on transits of planet d (Bailey 2023).","history":"Planet b is about 10% less dense than planet c, which could mean it is rich in calcium and aluminium minerals that condense early from a cooling disc, though the uncertainties are too large to be sure (Dorn et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134h-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134h-Bailey2023","cite":"Bailey 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vanessa Bailey","title":"HD 219134 Revisited: Planet d Transit Upper Limit and Planet f Transit Non-Detection with ASTERIA and TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.MTOJHC","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134h-Dornetal2019","cite":"Dorn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Dorn, J. H. D. Harrison, A. Bonsor et al.","title":"A new class of Super-Earths formed from high-temperature condensates: HD219134 b, 55 Cnc e, WASP-47 e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07222","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WTS-2 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits only 1.5 times the distance from its star at which it would be torn apart by Roche lobe overflow, where its outer layers spill onto the star (Birkby et al. 2014). Its remaining lifetime is predicted to be about 40 million years, if the star dissipates tidal energy at a rate of Q' = 10^6, a value observations barely constrain (Birkby et al. 2014). Orbital decay would shift transit times by about 17 seconds after 15 years (Birkby et al. 2014). An attempt to detect the decay with the Subaru Telescope's MOIRCS instrument, using transits and occultations, has been reported (Kawai 2026).","system":"The K2V host star may have a gravitationally bound M dwarf companion 0.6 arcseconds away, contributing about 20 per cent of the light in the infrared light curve (Birkby et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is spiralling inward has not been shown yet. A measured shift smaller than the predicted one would set a lower limit on how efficiently the star dissipates tidal energy (Birkby et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wts2b-Birkbyetal2014","cite":"Birkby et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. L. Birkby, M. Cappetta, P. Cruz et al.","title":"WTS-2 b: a hot Jupiter orbiting near its tidal destruction radius around a K dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.5416","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wts2b-Kawai2026","cite":"Kawai 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yugo Kawai","title":"Chasing the Demise of a Hot Jupiter with Transits and Occulations","journal":"Astronomical Herald","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3512 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with the CARMENES spectrograph in visible and near-infrared light, together with brightness measurements from the Montsec, Sierra Nevada and Las Cumbres observatories (Morales et al. 2019). Gas giants around very low mass stars like this one are puzzling, because planet formation in such systems is thought to be problematic (Curone 2021).","system":"","history":"Models of planet formation around very low mass stars struggle to build gas giants, and some exclude them entirely (Curone 2021). GJ 3512 b is cited as one of several detections that challenge those models (Curone 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3512b-Moralesetal2019","cite":"Morales et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. C. Morales, A. J. Mustill, I. Ribas et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 3512 radial velocity and light curves (Morales+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3512b-Curone2021","cite":"Curone 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Pietro Curone","title":"Planet formation around very low mass stars: the case of CIDA 1","journal":"The Star-Planet Connection","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5607750","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-171 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a typical hot Jupiter, with 1.084 ± 0.094 Jupiter masses and 0.98 Jupiter radii, orbiting a V = 13.05 G0 star (Nielsen et al. 2019). Radial velocities of the star drift in a straight line over 3.5 years, which points to a possible additional outer planet or a stellar companion (Nielsen et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the drift comes from a distant planet or a companion star is not known (Nielsen et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp171b-Nielsenetal2019","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, F. Bouchy, O. D. Turner et al.","title":"WASP-169, WASP-171, WASP-175, and WASP-182: three hot Jupiters and one bloated sub-Saturn mass planet discovered by WASP-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 5388 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is metal-deficient, and the discovery supported the idea that giant planets on long orbits are not uncommon around moderately metal-poor stars (Santos et al. 2010).","observed":"Radial velocities gave a minimum mass of 1.96 Jupiter masses (Santos et al. 2010). Hipparcos astrometry, which tracks the star's motion across the sky, showed an almost face-on orbit with an inclination of 178.3 degrees (Sahlmann et al. 2011). That tilt implies a true mass of 69 ± 20 Jupiter masses, near the upper end of the brown dwarf range, and masses below 13 Jupiter masses were excluded at 3 sigma (Sahlmann et al. 2011). A newer analysis combining radial velocities with Hipparcos and Gaia astrometry finds a likely planet of 3.2 Jupiter masses (Piccinini et al. 2026). The tilt of the stellar rotation axis was also found to differ significantly from the tilt of the companion's orbit (Sahlmann et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a brown dwarf is disputed, because different astrometric analyses give very different true masses (Sahlmann et al. 2011; Piccinini et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd5388b-Santosetal2010","cite":"Santos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXI. Three new giant planets orbiting the metal-poor stars HD 5388, HD 181720, and HD 190984","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3216","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd5388b-Sahlmannetal2011","cite":"Sahlmann et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. Sahlmann, C. Lovis, D. Queloz et al.","title":"HD 5388 b is a 69 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> companion instead of a planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.3372","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd5388b-Piccininietal2026","cite":"Piccinini et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Piccinini, A. Petralia, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"True masses using radial velocity data with HIPPARCOS and Gaia astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09401","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6324 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"An orbit of only about 6.7 hours means the planet is intensely irradiated by its M dwarf host and is expected to have lost any thick hydrogen-helium envelope (Lee et al. 2025). Radial velocities from the Keck Planet Finder gave a mass of 1.17 ± 0.22 Earth masses (Lee et al. 2025). The iron-core mass fraction of 27% ± 37% is consistent with Earth's (about 33%) and with other confirmed ultra-short-period planets (Lee et al. 2025). It is the closest to Earth-sized among confirmed planets of this kind (Lee et al. 2025). TESS data hint at a variation in brightness over the orbit with an amplitude of 42 ± 28 ppm, but the detection is tentative (Lee et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"JWST phase-curve and secondary eclipse observations could reveal the surface mineralogy, the day-night temperature contrast and possible tidal deformation (Lee et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6324b-Leeetal2025","cite":"Lee et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Rena A. Lee, Fei Dai, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"TOI-6324 b: An Earth-mass Ultra-short-period Planet Transiting a Nearby M Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 196067 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Combining radial velocities with absolute astrometry from Hipparcos and Gaia gave a mass of 12.5 Jupiter masses, which places the companion on the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Li et al. 2021).","system":"A planet of 10.4 Earth masses was found orbiting every 4.6 days, much closer to the star than the giant (Delisle et al. 2025). It is one of the first inner planets detected in a long-term programme comparing systems with and without outer giant planets (Delisle et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether outer giant planets and inner light planets tend to occur together is debated. Detections so far in this sample seem to contradict some previous studies (Delisle et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd196067b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196067b-Delisleetal2025","cite":"Delisle et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J.-B. Delisle, J. P. Faria, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"Architecture of planetary systems with and without outer giant planets: I. Inner planet detections around HD 23079, HD 196067, and HD 86226","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26232","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5616 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the Searching for GEMS survey, which looks for giant planets crossing M dwarfs, rare because such stars have so little mass (Kanodia et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was statistically validated, and radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and MAROON-X gave only an upper limit on its mass (Kanodia et al. 2024). The host star is an M dwarf (Clark 2024). Photometry and high-contrast imaging from several ground-based telescopes supported the result (Kanodia et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass is not measured precisely, only limited from above (Kanodia et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5616b-Kanodiaetal2024","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Arvind F. Gupta, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Characterizing Six Giant Planets Around Cool Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14694","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5616b-Clark2024","cite":"Clark 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Catherine Clark","title":"Big Planets around Small Stars: Masses and Upper Limits for Six Giant Planets","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.RHF4LF","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-6 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits 18 stellar radii, or 0.08 AU, from an F9V star about 3.0 billion years old, and has close to 3 Jupiter masses (Fridlund et al. 2010). The star has a metallicity 0.2 dex lower than the Sun and a relatively high lithium abundance (Fridlund et al. 2010). Its light curve is much more active than the Sun's, although spectra show no sign of that activity (Fridlund et al. 2010). A secondary eclipse, when the planet passes behind the star, was detected with statistical significance in CoRoT data (Parviainen et al. 2013). Starspot crossings during its transits have been used to study the star's spots (Valio 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot6b-Fridlundetal2010","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"M. Fridlund, G. Hébrard, R. Alonso et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. IX. CoRoT-6b: a transiting ``hot Jupiter'' planet in an 8.9d orbit around a low-metallicity star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1426","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot6b-Parviainenetal2013","cite":"Parviainen et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"H. Parviainen, H. J. Deeg, J. A. Belmonte","title":"Secondary eclipses in the CoRoT light curves. A homogeneous search based on Bayesian model selection","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.5361","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot6b-Valio2017","cite":"Valio 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Adriana Valio","title":"Starspots properties and stellar activity from planetary transits","journal":"Living Around Active Stars","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921317004094","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-36 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New Spitzer transit observations, combined with K2 and TESS data, gave updated radii and orbital timing for the planet (Duck et al. 2021). Spitzer's signal was too weak to validate the planet by checking that its transit depth is the same in both telescopes' colours (Duck et al. 2021). A joint fit of radii from K2 and Spitzer independently confirmed the K2 radius (Duck et al. 2021). The long gap between K2 and Spitzer sharpened the orbital period by a factor of 3 (Duck et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k236c-Ducketal2021","cite":"Duck et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alison Duck, Caleb K. Harada, Justin Harrell et al.","title":"K2, Spitzer, and TESS Transits of Four Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08889","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-69 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was announced together with HAT-P-70 b as a chance to study how often hot Jupiters occur around stars of different masses (Zhou et al. 2019). In a sample of 47,126 main-sequence stars brighter than Tmag = 10, the net hot Jupiter occurrence rate is 0.41 ± 0.10% (Zhou et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet has 3.58 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.676 Jupiter radii (Zhou et al. 2019). It orbits in the same direction as the star spins, in a prograde orbit lasting 4.79 days (Zhou et al. 2019). The WASP survey independently identified it as a planet candidate (Zhou et al. 2019).","system":"The host star is a hot A-type star (Zhou et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp69b-Zhouetal2019","cite":"Zhou et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"G. Zhou, C. X. Huang, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"Two New HATNet Hot Jupiters around A Stars and the First Glimpse at the Occurrence Rate of Hot Jupiters from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00462","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-105 b":{"text":{"why":"The system is a good test of whether stellar radiation can strip a planet's atmosphere, because its layout is rare (Householder & Weiss 2023).","observed":"Astronomers combined 92 radial velocity measurements from the HIRES spectrograph on the Keck I telescope with timing shifts in the transits seen by Kepler, to weigh the planets directly. Kepler-105 b came out at 10.8 ± 2.3 Earth masses and its outer neighbour Kepler-105 c at 5.6 ± 1.2 Earth masses (Householder & Weiss 2023). A follow-up set of 93 HIRES velocity measurements has also been published (Householder et al. 2024).","system":"The two planets sit on either side of the radius gap, a dip in how often planets of about 1.5 to 2.0 Earth radii occur. Unusually, the larger planet is the inner one (Householder & Weiss 2023).","history":"A common explanation for the radius gap is photoevaporation, where high-energy light from the star strips away planetary atmospheres. Closer planets receive more of it, so the inner planet keeping its thick envelope while the outer one lost its own is surprising (Householder & Weiss 2023).","open":"Whether photoevaporation can explain this unusual order of planet sizes is still being tested (Householder & Weiss 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler105b-HouseholderWeiss2023","cite":"Householder & Weiss 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Aaron Householder, Lauren Weiss","title":"Probing the Limits of Photoevaporation: Measuring the Masses of the Kepler-105 planets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler105b-Householderetal2024","cite":"Householder et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Householder, L. M. Weiss, J. E. Owen et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Kepler-105 radial velocity follows up (Householder+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-101 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was once proposed as a target for early James Webb Space Telescope observations of strong molecular features (Wakeford et al. 2017).","observed":"A single Hubble observation of the planet's near-infrared transmission spectrum showed no significant water absorption, and a clear atmosphere is ruled out at 13 sigma, so clouds are present (Wakeford et al. 2017). Its near twin WASP-31 b has similar temperature-pressure profiles and near-infrared features (Wakeford et al. 2017). Transit spectra from HARPS and HARPS-N found no strong atomic absorbers, probably because of high clouds or scattering of light by small particles (Zak et al. 2024). The orbit is moderately tilted against the star's spin, by 34° ± 3 (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"A tilted orbit like this is one of the clues used to test how hot Jupiters formed and migrated inward (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp101b-Wakefordetal2017","cite":"Wakeford et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"H. R. Wakeford, K. B. Stevenson, N. K. Lewis et al.","title":"HST PanCET program: A Cloudy Atmosphere for the Promising JWST Target WASP-101b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.00843","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp101b-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, A. Bocchieri, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquity measurements of six gas giants. Orbital misalignment of WASP-101b and WASP-131b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15631","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 890-9 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is a nearby, very cool red dwarf of type M6V, about 32 parsecs away, a kind of host that makes atmosphere searches on temperate rocky planets possible (Delrez et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is a super-Earth that was first spotted in four sectors of space-telescope data. Ground-based monitoring then revealed a second, outer planet that the space telescope had missed (Delrez et al. 2022). Eight transits of the inner planet have been observed with the James Webb Space Telescope's NIRSpec PRISM instrument, as part of a program testing whether rocky planets around red dwarfs keep atmospheres (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2026).","system":"A second temperate super-Earth, LP 890-9 c, orbits farther out (Delrez et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet holds an atmosphere at all is the open question of the Webb program, which tests the idea of a cosmic shoreline separating planets with and without air (Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp8909b-Delrezetal2022","cite":"Delrez et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Delrez, C. A. Murray, F. J. Pozuelos et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: LP 890-9 (TOI-4306) light curves and RVs (Delrez+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36670059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lp8909b-LustigYaegeretal2026","cite":"Lustig-Yaeger et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jacob Lustig-Yaeger, Kevin Stevenson, Erin May et al.","title":"Charting the Cosmic Shoreline: Overview and Initial Results from a Large JWST Program Searching for Rocky Exoplanet Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrobiology Science Conference (AbSciCon) 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 136352 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star, also called nu2 Lupi, was watched with the CHEOPS space telescope in six visits between 4 April and 6 July 2020. The visits caught four transits of planet b and three of planet c, and the last visit contained one transit of each (Delrez et al. 2021).","system":"Three planets are known around this star. Planets b and c both transit (Delrez et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd136352c-Delrezetal2021","cite":"Delrez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L. Delrez, D. Ehrenreich, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: nu2 Lupi CHEOPS light curves (Delrez+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 2638 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Adaptive optics images from Palomar showed a new, faint companion that moves through space together with HD 2638. Its projected separation is less than 30 AU, and its estimated orbital period is 130 years (Roberts et al. 2015). The companion has a mass of roughly 0.5 solar masses (Roberts et al. 2015).","system":"HD 2638 is a multiple star system that hosts a hot Jupiter. The new companion strengthens the link between hot Jupiters and binary stars (Roberts et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd2638b-Robertsetal2015","cite":"Roberts et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Lewis C., Jr. Roberts, Andrei Tokovinin, Brian D. Mason et al.","title":"Know the Star, Know the Planet. III. Discovery of Late-Type Companions to Two Exoplanet Host Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.01211","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-558 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet adds to the group of hot Jupiters on longer orbits of 5 to 15 days, which tend to be more massive than shorter-period ones. Whether this is a detection bias or a result of formation is not clear (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet orbits an F-type star in an eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.30 (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2022). Spectrograph measurements of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of starlight as the planet crosses the star, show that its orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, within about 0 to 10 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters around single stars appear to be more aligned than hot Jupiters, which suggests they form in discs that were aligned from the start (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi558b-IkwutUkwaetal2022","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Mma Ikwut-Ukwa, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"Two Massive Jupiters in Eccentric Orbits from the TESS Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.02222","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi558b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-469.01":{"text":{"why":"The system is a good place to test explanations for the radius valley, the gap in how often close-in planets of certain sizes occur (Egger et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is now named HIP 29442 b. Its mass was measured with ESPRESSO radial velocities, and the same data revealed two more close-in planets (Damasso et al. 2023). New CHEOPS space telescope observations over 9.6 days gave a radius of 3.410 ± 0.046 Earth radii for planet b (Egger et al. 2024).","system":"The star is an evolved K0 dwarf with three planets. Planets c and d have radii of about 1.55 and 1.54 Earth radii, and orbital periods of 3.5 and 6.4 days, while planet b orbits every 13.6 days (Egger et al. 2024). Planets c and d transit with low signal-to-noise, and were first suspected from the velocity data (Damasso et al. 2023).","history":"Internal structure models were used to investigate how the planets formed (Egger et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi46901-Eggeretal2024","cite":"Egger et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. A. Egger, H. P. Osborn, D. Kubyshkina et al.","title":"Unveiling the internal structure and formation history of the three planets transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) with CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi46901-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, J. Rodrigues, A. Castro-González et al.","title":"A compact multi-planet system transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) discovered by TESS and ESPRESSO. Radial velocities lead to the detection of transits with low signal-to-noise ratio","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.13310","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Ross 458 c":{"text":{"why":"The object lies about 1100 au from its host pair of stars, in a class of very low-mass companions found at hundreds to thousands of au (Meynardie et al. 2025).","observed":"Spectra from the James Webb Space Telescope (0.8 to 3.1 micrometres) were fitted with atmosphere models to measure its chemical makeup (Meynardie et al. 2025). Model grids prefer more heavy elements in its atmosphere than in the host stars, but retrievals, a different fitting approach, give a result that is consistent with the host (Meynardie et al. 2025).","system":"It orbits a close pair of stars, Ross 458 AB (Meynardie et al. 2025).","history":"If its makeup matches the stars, it probably formed like a star by fragmentation of the same cloud. If it holds extra heavy elements, it more likely formed in a disc and moved outward (Meynardie et al. 2025).","open":"Whether it is a gas giant planet or a brown dwarf is not settled (Meynardie et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ross458c-Meynardieetal2025","cite":"Meynardie et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"William W. Meynardie, Michael R. Meyer, Ryan J. MacDonald et al.","title":"Ross 458 C: Gas Giant or Brown Dwarf?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 104067 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about 3.6 Neptune masses and a 55.8-day period, and the first analysis found a circular orbit at 0.26 AU (Ségransan et al. 2011). The star's velocity shows a cycle of about 500 days that reveals magnetic activity (Ségransan et al. 2011). Later work added a Uranus-mass planet on an eccentric orbit of about 14 days (Kane et al. 2024).","system":"Two more planets were found later. TESS data show a terrestrial planet candidate of 1.30 ± 0.12 Earth radii with a period of about 2.2 days (Kane et al. 2024). Calculations show that the two outer planets strongly excite the eccentricity of the inner one (Kane et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The inner candidate is not yet confirmed (Kane et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd104067b-Sgransanetal2011","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. Ségransan, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIX. Four new planets in orbit around the moderatly active dwarfs <ASTROBJ>HD 63765</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 104067</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 125595</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HIP 70849</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.0339","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd104067b-Kaneetal2024","cite":"Kane et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Tara Fetherolf, Zhexing Li et al.","title":"A Perfect Tidal Storm: HD 104067 Planetary Architecture Creating an Incandescent World","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.17062","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1338 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits both stars of an eclipsing binary (Standing et al. 2023).","observed":"It was the first circumbinary planet found using radial velocities alone, with the HARPS and ESPRESSO spectrographs (Standing et al. 2023). The system was already known to have an inner transiting planet, and radial velocities cannot detect that one, only limit its mass to under 21.8 Earth masses (Standing et al. 2023). Only the second confirmed multiplanet circumbinary system is known (Standing et al. 2023).","system":"The two stars have masses of 1.1 and 0.3 solar masses and orbit each other every 14.6 days (Kostov et al. 2020). The inner planet has a radius of about 6.9 Earth radii. Its orbit is aligned with the binary to within about 1 degree (Kostov et al. 2020). The main star's spin is also aligned with the binary and planet orbits, which suggests formation from a single disc (Kunovac Hodžić et al. 2020).","history":"Simulations with pebble accretion, where planets grow from small drifting solids, find that only discs with low turbulence can produce a similar system (Coleman et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1338c-Standingetal2023","cite":"Standing et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, Lalitha Sairam, David V. Martin et al.","title":"Radial-velocity discovery of a second planet in the TOI-1338/BEBOP-1 circumbinary system","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10794","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1338c-Kostovetal2020","cite":"Kostov et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Veselin B. Kostov, Jerome A. Orosz, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"TOI-1338: TESS' First Transiting Circumbinary Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07783","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1338c-Colemanetal2024","cite":"Coleman et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gavin A. L. Coleman, Richard P. Nelson, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"Constraining the formation history of the TOI-1338/BEBOP-1 circumbinary planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11898","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5110 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the few known transiting warm Jupiters, with orbital periods of 10 to 200 days (Heidari et al. 2025).","observed":"It is one of three newly discovered systems among seven studied with TESS light curves, SOPHIE radial velocities and high-resolution imaging (Heidari et al. 2025). The three new warm Jupiters have periods of 30 to 94 days, masses of 0.9 to 2.9 Jupiter masses and radii of 1.0 to 1.5 Jupiter radii (Heidari et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters are generally thought to have formed beyond the snowline and migrated inward. Being far from their stars, they avoid the strong effects of closeness (Heidari et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5110b-Heidarietal2025","cite":"Heidari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Heidari, G. Hébrard, E. Martioli et al.","title":"Characterization of seven transiting systems, including four warm Jupiters from SOPHIE and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08527","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-18 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is hot, massive and bright, which makes the planet useful for atmosphere studies. Few known transiting planets orbit such stars (McLeod et al. 2017).","observed":"The planet is one of the most inflated known around a hot star, with a density of 0.377 g/cm3 and a scale height estimated at about 600 km (McLeod et al. 2017). A transit measurement with the Keck Planet Finder showed a polar orbit, with a projected angle of -94.8° ± 0.7 against the star's spin (Rubenzahl et al. 2024). A first low-resolution transmission spectrum was taken with the 2 m Himalayan Chandra Telescope (Unni et al. 2024).","system":"A stellar companion lies at a projected distance of about 1100 au (McLeod et al. 2017).","history":"The planet probably moved inward on an eccentric orbit, and the binary companion may have tilted its orbit (Rubenzahl et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt18b-McLeodetal2017","cite":"McLeod et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kim K. McLeod, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Ryan J. Oelkers et al.","title":"KELT-18b: Puffy Planet, Hot Host, Probably Perturbed","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.01657","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt18b-Rubenzahletal2024","cite":"Rubenzahl et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ryan A. Rubenzahl, Fei Dai, Samuel Halverson et al.","title":"KPF Confirms a Polar Orbit for KELT-18 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21196","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt18b-Unnietal2024","cite":"Unni et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Athira Unni, Thirupathi Sivarani, Jayesh Goyal et al.","title":"Low-resolution transit spectroscopy of three hot Jupiters using the 2 m Himalayan Chandra Telescope","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18450","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 78530 b":{"text":{"why":"The object lies in the young Upper Scorpius association, about 5 to 10 million years old (Lachapelle et al. 2015).","observed":"Near-infrared spectra and photometry from Gemini North show a spectral type of M7 and a temperature of 2700 ± 100 K (Lachapelle et al. 2015). Evolution models give a mass of 21 to 25 Jupiter masses, above the usual planet range (Lachapelle et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip78530b-Lachapelleetal2015","cite":"Lachapelle et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"François-René Lachapelle, David Lafrenière, Jonathan Gagné et al.","title":"Characterization of Low-mass, Wide-separation Substellar Companions to Stars in Upper Scorpius: Near-infrared Photometry and Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07586","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-126 b":{"text":{"why":"WASP-126 is the brightest star in the group of five systems announced alongside it (Maxted et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet is low in mass for its size, at 0.3 Jupiter masses and 0.95 Jupiter radii, which makes it a good target for transmission spectroscopy (Maxted et al. 2016). Photometry and spectroscopy confirmed it (Maxted et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp126b-Maxtedetal2016","cite":"Maxted et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Five transiting hot Jupiters discovered using WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST: WASP-119 b, WASP-124 b, WASP-126 b, WASP-129 b, and WASP-133 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.01740","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6223 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is slightly larger than Neptune and orbits an early M dwarf every 3.86 days. Its equilibrium temperature, the heat from starlight alone, is 714 ± 14 K (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025). It was validated statistically with multi-colour light curves and other follow-up (Yalçinkaya et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6223b-Yalinkayaetal2025","cite":"Yalçinkaya et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Yalçinkaya, K. Barkaoui, Ö. Baștürk et al.","title":"TOI-1743 b, TOI-5799 b, TOI-5799 c, and TOI-6223 b: TESS discovery and validation of four super-Earth to Neptune-sized planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V2376 Ori b":{"text":{"why":"Only a dozen young planetary-mass companions had been directly imaged before (Vila et al. 2025).","observed":"The object was spotted with the James Webb Space Telescope near the Orion Nebula, and a spectrum from the MUSE instrument confirmed it. Its mass is about 20 Jupiter masses (Vila et al. 2025). The spectrum shows signs of ongoing growth, with an accretion rate of about 10^-6.6 Jupiter masses per year, comparable to PDS 70 b (Vila et al. 2025). Extended emission suggests an interaction between the object and its host's disc (Vila et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an M star in Orion D, about 7 ± 3 million years old and around 80 pc in front of the Trapezium cluster (Vila et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v2376orib-Vilaetal2025","cite":"Vila et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Vila, P. Amiot, O. Berné et al.","title":"Observation of an accreting planetary-mass companion with signs of disc─disc interaction in Orion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04944","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0192L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is about 2 Neptune masses and orbits an M dwarf (Shin et al. 2023). It was spotted by eye in the survey data, and the by-eye detections serve as a comparison to planets picked out by the AnomalyFinder algorithm (Shin et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0192lb-Shinetal2023","cite":"Shin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"In-Gu Shin, Jennifer C. Yee, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets. III. Analysis of Three Giant Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.03886","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-290 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a mini-Neptune of 3.06 ± 0.16 Earth radii, above the radius valley, with an incident flux of about 400 times Earth's (Hjorth et al. 2019). Because the flux is that high, it sits safely outside the super-Earth desert (Hjorth et al. 2019).","system":"The star belongs to a triple system, with two M-dwarf companions at 113 and about 2467 au. An outer warm Jupiter orbits every 48.4 days (Hjorth et al. 2019).","history":"The mild eccentricity of the warm Jupiter and the presence of a mini-Neptune sibling point to in situ formation or disc migration (Hjorth et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2290b-Hjorthetal2019","cite":"Hjorth et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Hjorth, A. B. Justesen, T. Hirano et al.","title":"K2-290: a warm Jupiter and a mini-Neptune in a triple-star system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.03716","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-26 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble and Spitzer spectra from 0.5 to 5 micrometres show water absorption of up to 525 parts per million (Wakeford et al. 2017). The atmosphere holds heavy elements at only 4.8 times solar levels, low for a planet of Neptune's mass (Wakeford et al. 2017). The planet has a radius about 65% larger than Neptune's, so its density is low (Hartman et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"The low metal content suggests that the atmosphere is primordial and that the planet gathered its gas late in its disc's life, with little contamination from metal-rich planetesimals (Wakeford et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp26b-Wakefordetal2017","cite":"Wakeford et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Hannah R. Wakeford, David K. Sing, Tiffany Kataria et al.","title":"HAT-P-26b: A Neptune-mass exoplanet with a well-constrained heavy element abundance","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04354","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp26b-Hartmanetal2011","cite":"Hartman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. M. Kipping et al.","title":"HAT-P-26b: A Low-density Neptune-mass Planet Transiting a K Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1008","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-23 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Both a clearly eccentric orbit, with e = 0.16 ± 0.02, and a fairly high density are uncommon for a hot Jupiter (Rouan et al. 2012). The planet has about 2.8 Jupiter masses, a radius near 1.05 Jupiter radii and a density of about 3 g/cm3 (Rouan et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a mature G0 star about 7 billion years old, close to turning into a subgiant (Rouan et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot23b-Rouanetal2012","cite":"Rouan et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. Rouan, H. Parviainen, C. Moutou et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission . XIX. CoRoT-23b: a dense hot Jupiter on an eccentric orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.0584","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter that orbits at 0.039 to 0.052 au, like the three others announced with it. Its radius and mass lie between 1.10 and 1.30 Jupiter radii and 0.41 and 0.76 Jupiter masses (Tilbrook et al. 2021). All four are highly irradiated, a regime where processes that puff up planets work well (Tilbrook et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"What controls the swelling of hot Jupiters remains poorly understood (Tilbrook et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts15b-Tilbrooketal2021","cite":"Tilbrook et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Rosanna H. Tilbrook, Matthew R. Burleigh, Jean C. Costes et al.","title":"NGTS 15b, 16b, 17b, and 18b: four hot Jupiters from the Next-Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10302","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-395 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself. Work so far has studied its star, KIC 8890150, which shows an excess of infrared light, a sign of a circumstellar debris disc of dust from collisions (Du et al. 2025). Kepler-395 c is described as a promising terrestrial-sized planet in the habitable zone (Du et al. 2025).","system":"The star has a debris disc, built from leftover solid material after planet formation (Du et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler395c-Duetal2025","cite":"Du et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Julian Du, Jian Ge, Kevin Willis","title":"Debris Disks Around Stars Hosting Habitable Zone Terrestrial-sized Planet Candidates","journal":"246th Meeting of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"PZ Tel b":{"text":{"why":"The object is a young brown dwarf in the beta Pictoris moving group. Its age is known from group membership (Franson & Bowler 2023).","observed":"A Hipparcos-to-Gaia acceleration of the star, plus 15 years of imaging, gave a dynamical mass of 27 (+25, -9) Jupiter masses and an eccentricity of 0.52 (Franson & Bowler 2023). The mass agrees within 1.1 sigma with four sets of hot-start evolution models (Franson & Bowler 2023). Its colours indicate spectral type M7 (Biller et al. 2010).","system":"The star PZ Tel A has an excess of 70 micrometre emission, evidence of circumstellar dust (Biller et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pztelb-FransonBowler2023","cite":"Franson & Bowler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kyle Franson, Brendan P. Bowler","title":"Dynamical Mass of the Young Brown Dwarf Companion PZ Tel B","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01302","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pztelb-Billeretal2010","cite":"Biller et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Beth A. Biller, Michael C. Liu, Zahed Wahhaj et al.","title":"The Gemini NICI Planet-finding Campaign: Discovery of a Close Substellar Companion to the Young Debris Disk Star PZ Tel","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4808","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":26,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-47 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm giant of about 0.369 Jupiter masses and 1.117 Jupiter radii that orbits a K dwarf every 3.9228 days (Hartman et al. 2020). The host has a mass of about 0.674 solar masses (Hartman et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats47b-Hartmanetal2020","cite":"Hartman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. D. Hartman, Andrés Jordán, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-47b, HATS-48Ab, HATS-49b, and HATS-72b: Four Warm Giant Planets Transiting K Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 168443 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star hosts two massive companions. Both may be brown dwarfs, or both may be superplanets that formed together in the protoplanetary disc (Udry et al. 2002). The host is rich in heavy elements, like most stars with detected planets (Udry et al. 2002). Calculations of the system's long-term orbits show that its habitable zone is mostly chaotic (Érdi et al. 2004).","system":"","history":"","open":"The nature of the companions is not settled (Udry et al. 2002)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd168443b-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4860 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Jupiter-sized planet around a small M dwarf of about 0.34 solar masses. Radius and orbital period are about 0.76 Jupiter radii and 1.52 days (Triaud et al. 2023). Infrared photometry around the secondary eclipse detected no light from the planet itself (Triaud et al. 2023). Mass estimates differ, with 0.67 ± 0.14 Jupiter masses from Subaru IRD and 0.273 ± 0.006 from SPIRou and ESPRESSO (Triaud et al. 2023; Almenara et al. 2024). Its density suggests the planet is rich in heavy elements, like its star (Triaud et al. 2023).","system":"Radial velocities hint at an eccentric outer planet candidate, with a 427-day period and a minimum mass of 1.66 Jupiter masses (Almenara et al. 2024).","history":"Giant planets around M dwarfs challenge formation theory (Almenara et al. 2024).","open":"The outer candidate needs more data (Almenara et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4860b-Triaudetal2023","cite":"Triaud et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Georgina Dransfield, Taiki Kagetani et al.","title":"An M dwarf accompanied by a close-in giant orbiter with SPECULOOS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.02253","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4860b-Almenaraetal2024","cite":"Almenara et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. M. Almenara, X. Bonfils, E. M. Bryant et al.","title":"TOI-4860 b, a short-period giant planet transiting an M3.5 dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01454","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-146 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transit times of K2-146 b shift by large amounts because a neighbour pulls on it. The neighbour, planet c, has a 4.0-day orbit and is locked in a 3:2 resonance with planet b, so b goes round three times for every two orbits of c (Hamann et al. 2019). Fitting the timing shifts gave very precise masses of 5.77 and 7.50 Earth masses for b and c, a precision record for small planets (Hamann et al. 2019). Both are sub-Neptunes, meaning planets between Earth and Neptune in size (Hamann et al. 2019).","system":"The two orbits are tilted by 2.40 degrees relative to each other (Hamann et al. 2019). Gravity from planet b has torqued planet c's orbit closer to our line of sight. Planet c only grazed its star at first and was missed, but its transits are now about three times deeper (Hamann et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2146b-Hamannetal2019","cite":"Hamann et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Aaron Hamann, Benjamin T. Montet, Daniel C. Fabrycky et al.","title":"K2-146: Discovery of Planet c, Precise Masses from Transit Timing, and Observed Precession","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10620","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1648 b":{"text":{"why":"The three planets in the report, with radii of 2.5 to 4.1 Earth radii and orbital periods of 6 to 15 days, lie in a region where planet formation and atmospheric evolution are still poorly understood (Carleo et al. 2026).","observed":"Transit photometry from TESS was combined with ground-based radial velocities, which track the small wobble of the star, to measure the planet's mass and radius precisely (Carleo et al. 2026). TOI-1648 b is a sub-Neptune, a planet between Earth and Neptune in size, orbiting a G dwarf star (Carleo et al. 2026). It was announced together with TOI-1472 c and an improved mass for the known planet TOI-1472 b, as part of the KESPRINT collaboration (Carleo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1648b-Carleoetal2026","cite":"Carleo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Grzegorz Nowak, Felipe Murgas et al.","title":"Precise mass and radius determination for two new and one known Neptune-sized planets around G dwarf hosts","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20119","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-44 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-44 b is one of five giant planets whose nature was confirmed with radial velocities from the SOPHIE and HARPS-N spectrographs (Bonomo et al. 2015). Its transits had first been measured with Kepler's long-cadence data. The system was then reanalysed using Kepler's short-cadence data, which sample each transit much more finely, together with the published radial velocities (Bonomo et al. 2015). New spectral analyses of the star were also made, and stellar rotation periods were used to estimate ages where possible (Bonomo et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler44b-Bonomoetal2015","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Improved parameters of seven Kepler giant companions characterized with SOPHIE and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02653","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-80 c belongs to a tightly packed system whose outer five planets are linked in successive three-body resonances, where three neighbouring planets share a repeating orbital rhythm (Weisserman et al. 2023). The planets do not appear to be in two-body resonances (Weisserman et al. 2023). Shifts in the transit times were used to weigh the planets. Adding the newly found planet Kepler-80 g to the fit gives it a mass of about 0.8 to 1.0 Earth masses (Weisserman et al. 2023).","system":"Kepler-80 is an extreme case of a tightly packed system, with about four planets whose periods all lie within a factor of about 3 (Ragozzine et al. 2015). Radial velocity surveys have not detected any such system (Ragozzine et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Adding the extra planet does not change the character of the resonances much, but it can change the masses read from the timing shifts. Conclusions drawn from incomplete systems therefore need caution (Weisserman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80c-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80c-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2431 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2431 b circles its star in 5 hours and 22 minutes, one of the shortest orbits known (Taș et al. 2026). Its density is compatible with an Earth-like rocky makeup, and the strong heating makes it likely a lava world with an equilibrium temperature of 2063 K (Taș et al. 2026). The Hubble Space Telescope caught the planet passing behind its star, the first Hubble detection of this kind for a rocky planet (Zieba et al. 2026). The planet gave off 37 parts per million of the star's light in TESS optical data and 69 in Hubble near-infrared data. These values constrain the dayside temperature, reflected light and heat redistribution (Zieba et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Lava planets probably did not form on such tight orbits but moved inward over time (Herath et al. 2026). A model of seven lava planets, this one included, finds that moving in from about 0.1 au needs starting eccentricities of at least 0.9 (Herath et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2431b-Ziebaetal2026","cite":"Zieba et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sebastian Zieba, Billy Edwards, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"The First Hubble Detection of a Secondary Eclipse from a Rocky Exoplanet: The 5.4 hr Planet TOI-2431 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31966","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2431b-Herathetal2026","cite":"Herath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mahesh Herath, Nicolas B. Cowan, Charles-Édouard Boukaré et al.","title":"Coupled Orbital and Interior Structure Evolution of Lava Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18682","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 207496 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is part of a programme that aims to characterise naked core planets, worlds that have lost their gas envelopes (Barros et al. 2023).","observed":"The host is an active early K dwarf only about 0.52 billion years old, so the planet can be seen at an early stage of its evolution (Barros et al. 2023). Its mass and radius give a density of 3.27 grams per cubic centimetre (Barros et al. 2023). Interior modelling allows a water-rich envelope, a gas-rich envelope, or a mixture of both (Barros et al. 2023). The planet is described as either a naked core or on the verge of becoming one (Barros et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"If the planet has a gas-rich envelope, it has lost a significant fraction of it and its radius has shrunk (Barros et al. 2023). Heat from the core is not an efficient way to strip a gas envelope from a rocky core here. If the planet is instead an ocean world with a steam atmosphere, core heat could have removed much of its original hydrogen-helium gas within tens of millions of years (Evdokimov & Shematovich 2025).","open":"Whether the planet carries water, gas or both is not known (Barros et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd207496b-Barrosetal2023","cite":"Barros et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"The young mini-Neptune HD 207496b that is either a naked core or on the verge of becoming one","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.03775","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd207496b-EvdokimovShematovich2025","cite":"Evdokimov & Shematovich 2025","year":"2025","authors":"R. A. Evdokimov, V. I. Shematovich","title":"Estimate of Core-Powered Mass-Loss of Primary Atmosphere of the Mini-Neptune (Ocean Planet) HD 207496b","journal":"Solar System Research","url":"https://doi.org/10.1134/S0038094624602172","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 48265 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-mass, Jupiter-like world on an eccentric orbit of intermediate to long period (Minniti et al. 2009). It was reported from the Magellan precision Doppler survey, which measures stellar velocity shifts to find planets, alongside companions to four other solar-type stars (Minniti et al. 2009). The host stars range from mildly metal-poor to metal-rich and show low chromospheric activity, meaning they are magnetically quiet (Minniti et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd48265b-Minnitietal2009","cite":"Minniti et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Dante Minniti, R. Paul Butler, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"Low-Mass Companions for Five Solar-Type Stars From the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.5348","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"24 Boo b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an evolved giant star with 0.99 solar masses and a radius of 10.64 times the Sun's (Takarada et al. 2018). The planet has a minimum mass of 0.91 Jupiter masses and one of the shortest periods found around evolved stars with radial velocities (Takarada et al. 2018). The star is also one of the most metal-poor stars known to host a planet (Takarada et al. 2018). Asteroseismology, the study of stellar vibrations, confirms the star is a red-clump giant, a star that burns helium in its core. At 0.183 au, the planet is the closest known around such a star (Lin et al. 2026).","system":"The star also shows a 150-day signal, probably from stellar activity (Takarada et al. 2018). Further variations may point to extra planet candidates, stellar activity or other sources (Teng et al. 2023).","history":"The star expanded during its red giant phase to a size larger than the planet's present orbit, then contracted again, so the planet should not have survived (Lin et al. 2026).","open":"Current models of stars and planets evolving together do not explain how the planet survived (Lin et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-24boob-Takaradaetal2018","cite":"Takarada et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Takuya Takarada, Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Planets around the evolved stars 24 Boötis and γ Libra: A 30 d-period planet and a double giant-planet system in possible 7:3 MMR","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.04008","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-24boob-Linetal2026","cite":"Lin et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Wen-Xu Lin, Sheng-Bang Qian, Qi-Bin Sun et al.","title":"The Survival Paradox of Close-In Planets Orbiting Red Clump Giants","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04544","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-24boob-Tengetal2023","cite":"Teng et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"Revisiting planetary systems in the Okayama Planet Search Program: A new long-period planet, RV astrometry joint analysis, and a multiplicity-metallicity trend around evolved stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05343","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"eps Ind A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities of long-period companions give only a minimum mass, because the tilt of the orbit is not known (Philipot et al. 2023). Combining radial velocities with astrometry, which tracks tiny shifts of the star on the sky, lifted the uncertainty (Philipot et al. 2023). The result is a true mass in the planetary range for the companion of Epsilon Indi A (Philipot et al. 2023).","system":"The star has distant brown dwarf companions. Epsilon Indi B lies about 1459 AU from the star and is a T2.5 brown dwarf of about 40 to 60 Jupiter masses, the nearest brown dwarf to the Sun (Scholz et al. 2003). It was later resolved as a pair, Ba and Bb (King et al. 2009). A faint companion was seen only 2.1 AU apart from the brighter brown dwarf (Volk et al. 2003).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epsindab-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, P. Rubini et al.","title":"Updated characterization of long-period single companion by combining radial velocity, relative astrometry, and absolute astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01263","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epsindab-Scholzetal2003","cite":"Scholz et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"R.-D. Scholz, M. J. McCaughrean, N. Lodieu et al.","title":"varepsilon  Indi B: A new benchmark T dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0212487","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epsindab-Kingetal2009","cite":"King et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"R. R. King, M. J. McCaughrean, D. Homeier et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Epsilon Indi Ba and Bb IR spectra (King+, 2010)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35100099","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epsindab-Volketal2003","cite":"Volk et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"K. Volk, R. Blum, G. Walker et al.","title":"epsilon Indi B","journal":"International Astronomical Union Circular","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-223 e":{"text":{"why":"No other multi-planet system among hundreds of sub-Neptune systems had been identified that requires migration. The detailed architecture of Kepler-223 is too finely tuned for formation by scattering, while simulations show it arises naturally from migration (Mills et al. 2016).","observed":"Four planets orbit in a chain of resonances, with periods linked by 4:3, 3:2 and 2:1 ratios that cause measurable dynamical effects (Mills et al. 2015). The periods stand in the ratio 3:4:6:8 to within a part in 1000 (Fabrycky et al. 2011). Variations in the transit times were modelled as swinging resonant angles to constrain planetary masses and long-term stability (Mills et al. 2016). All four planets are sub-Neptunes (Mills et al. 2015).","system":"The ratios are so close that they would be extremely improbable if the four signals came from separate eclipsing systems blended together (Fabrycky et al. 2011).","history":"A resonant chain implies that the planets moved inward through the gas disc (Mills et al. 2015). The chain can constrain how turbulent the disc was and how dense (Hühn et al. 2021). About a Mars mass of planetesimals crossing the planets' orbits would be enough to pull the system out of resonance (Moore et al. 2013).","open":"Tidal dissipation in the planets or wide-scale instability may eventually turn resonant chains like this one into the more common type of architecture (Mills et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler223e-Millsetal2016","cite":"Mills et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Sean Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Transiting, Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #47","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223e-Millsetal2015","cite":"Mills et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel Clark Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223e-Fabryckyetal2011","cite":"Fabrycky et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Daniel C. Fabrycky, M. J. Holman, J. A. Carter et al.","title":"Koi-730 As A System Of Four Planets In A Chain Of Resonances","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223e-Hhnetal2021","cite":"Hühn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L.-A. Hühn, G. Pichierri, B. Bitsch et al.","title":"Kepler-223 resonance holds information about turbulence during the gas-disk phase","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13835","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223e-Mooreetal2013","cite":"Moore et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Alexander Moore, Imran Hasan, Alice C. Quillen","title":"Limits on orbit-crossing planetesimals in the resonant multiple planet system, KOI-730","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-177 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-177 b is a hot Jupiter of about 0.5 Jupiter masses and about 1.6 Jupiter radii, orbiting a K2 star of magnitude 12.6 (Turner et al. 2019). It sits close to the upper edge of the mass-radius range, so it is very puffy for its mass (Turner et al. 2019). Radial velocities came from the CORALIE spectrograph, with extra photometry from EulerCam and the TRAPPIST telescopes (Turner et al. 2022).","system":"It was announced together with two similar planets, WASP-181 b and WASP-183 b (Turner et al. 2019). The three have scaled orbital sizes between 9.6 and 12.1, a range where star-planet alignment changes from mostly well aligned to more widely scattered (Turner et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp177b-Turneretal2019","cite":"Turner et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Oliver D. Turner, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"Three hot-Jupiters on the upper edge of the mass-radius distribution: WASP-177, WASP-181, and WASP-183","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06622","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp177b-Turneretal2022","cite":"Turner et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"O. D. Turner, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-177, WASP-181, WASP-183 RV and phot. data (Turner+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-94 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its giant planet KOI-94 d. Planet c has a radius of 4.32 Earth radii, and its mass of 15.6 Earth masses is poorly measured, with an uncertainty reaching down to zero (Weiss et al. 2013). The mass came from 26 Keck radial velocities fitted together with the Kepler light curve (Weiss et al. 2013). Planet c is accepted as a planet because of its gravitational interactions seen in transit timing, the statistical robustness of multi-planet systems against false positives, and evidence that no other star lies inside the photometric aperture (Weiss et al. 2013).","system":"KOI-94 hosts at least four transiting planets. The giant KOI-94 d has a 22-day orbit and receives 2700 times the flux Jupiter does, yet it is not inflated like many hot Jupiters (Weiss et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi94c-Weissetal2013","cite":"Weiss et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Lauren M. Weiss, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"The Mass of KOI-94d and a Relation for Planet Radius, Mass, and Incident Flux","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.2150","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-77 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets are rarer around low-mass stars than around solar-type stars, a key prediction of core-accretion theory, so finding them tests that theory (Jordán et al. 2022).","observed":"HATS-77 b orbits a late K or early M dwarf, one of four gas giants announced around such stars (Jordán et al. 2022). Its radius of 1.165 Jupiter radii is the largest of the four, and its 3.0876-day orbit the longest (Jordán et al. 2022). The ESPRESSO spectrograph on the Very Large Telescope was used to confirm the planets and measure their masses (Jordán et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"The aim is to quantify how often giant planets occur around low-mass stars with further discoveries (Jordán et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats77b-Jordnetal2022","cite":"Jordán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Andrés Jordán, J. D. Hartman, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-74Ab, HATS-75b, HATS-76b, and HATS-77b: Four Transiting Giant Planets Around K and M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01928","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 116454 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was the first discovery from the two-wheeled Kepler mission, known as K2 (Vanderburg et al. 2015). K2 watched the star for only 9 days and saw just a single transit (Vanderburg et al. 2015). A new photometric analysis technique corrected the small drifts of the telescope and recovered the dip with high confidence (Vanderburg et al. 2015). Radial velocities from the HARPS-N spectrograph show a planet of about 11.8 Earth masses in a 9.1-day orbit, matching the transit (Vanderburg et al. 2015). The MOST satellite confirmed the transit and refined the timing (Vanderburg et al. 2015).","system":"The host star is a bright, metal-poor K1 dwarf with high proper motion, which makes the planet well suited to follow-up observations (Vanderburg et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip116454b-Vanderburgetal2015","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Benjamin T. Montet, John Asher Johnson et al.","title":"Characterizing K2 Planet Discoveries: A Super-Earth Transiting the Bright K Dwarf HIP 116454","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.5674","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-251 b":{"text":{"why":"Young planets help astronomers study the early stage of planet evolution (Sun et al. 2023).","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The host is a young G dwarf, first estimated to be 40 to 320 million years old (Sun et al. 2023). Astronomers searched for stellar siblings, stars that share its motion and origin, and measured rotation periods for 321 candidates within 50 parsecs (Sun et al. 2023). The rotation pattern puts the group between Pleiades-age and Group X-age stars, at about 200 million years. A rotation-based age gives 204 million years, with an uncertainty of 45 million years (Sun et al. 2023).","system":"A large share of the sibling candidates, 68.1%, rotate fast, compared with 14% to 16% for typical field stars. The excess suggests a young association of stars in the Phoenix-Grus constellation (Sun et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi251b-Sunetal2023","cite":"Sun et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Qinghui Sun, Sharon Xuesong Wang, Andrew W. Mann et al.","title":"A Search for Stellar Siblings of the 200 Myr TOI-251 b Planetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.03255","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 137496 c":{"text":{"why":"The measurements come from NCORES, a HARPS programme that confirms small transiting candidates and measures their masses to constrain their interior composition (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022).","observed":"Radial velocities from the HARPS and CORALIE spectrographs, together with K2 photometry, revealed a system with a dense, hot inner planet described as a super-Mercury and a cold Jupiter, which is this outer planet (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022). The host is a bright, slightly evolved Sun-like star with a radius of 1.587 times the Sun's (Azevedo Silva et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd137496c-Silvaetal2022","cite":"Silva et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"T. Azevedo Silva, O. D. S. Demangeon, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"The HD 137496 system: A dense, hot super-Mercury and a cold Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08764","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-20 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-20 b is an inflated, Saturn-mass planet of 0.31 Jupiter masses and 1.46 Jupiter radii (Anderson et al. 2015). Its orbit is near-aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected obliquity of 12.7 degrees plus or minus 4.2 (Anderson et al. 2015). Observations with the CRIRES+ spectrograph tentatively detect water vapour in its atmosphere, at a signal-to-noise ratio of 4.2 to 4.7 (Maimone et al. 2022). Its low density and bright host star make it a good target for studying the atmosphere (Anderson et al. 2015).","system":"The star has a companion, found only later: a second star about 60 au away in projection, with a K-band flux ratio of 0.4639 (Evans et al. 2016). The planet almost certainly orbits the brighter star (Evans et al. 2016). Including the companion's light raises the planet's measured mass and radius by 4 and 1 standard deviations (Evans et al. 2016).","history":"The near-alignment around an old, cool star fits the idea that close-in giants are scattered onto eccentric orbits with random alignments, then circularised and aligned by tides (Anderson et al. 2015).","open":"Measured properties differ greatly depending on whether binarity is ignored or the planet transits the brighter or the fainter star (Southworth et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp20b-Andersonetal2015","cite":"Anderson et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-20b and WASP-28b: a hot Saturn and a hot Jupiter in near-aligned orbits around solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1482","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp20b-Maimoneetal2022","cite":"Maimone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"M. C. Maimone, M. Brogi, A. Chiavassa et al.","title":"Detecting H<SUB>2</SUB>O with CRIRES+: WASP-20b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.11506","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp20b-Evansetal2016","cite":"Evans et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Daniel F. Evans, John Southworth, Barry Smalley","title":"WASP-20 Is a Close Visual Binary with a Transiting Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.08735","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp20b-Southworthetal2020","cite":"Southworth et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Southworth, A. J. Bohn, M. A. Kenworthy et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. II. Revised properties of transiting planetary systems with companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08225","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3629 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-3629 b is a warm gas giant orbiting an M1 dwarf, with a mass of 0.26 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.74 Jupiter radii (Cañas et al. 2022). It is one of only nine hot Jupiters known to transit M dwarfs (Cañas et al. 2022). Its equilibrium temperature is 690 K (Cañas et al. 2022). A transmission spectroscopy metric of 80 marks it as a promising target for probing atmospheric chemistry through transmission spectroscopy (Cañas et al. 2022).","system":"The host star has a metallicity above the Sun's, as do four other M dwarfs with giant planets, supporting the idea that close-in giants favour metal-rich M dwarfs (Hartman et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3629b-Caasetal2022","cite":"Cañas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Shubham Kanodia, Chad F. Bender et al.","title":"TOI-3714 b and TOI-3629 b: Two Gas Giants Transiting M Dwarfs Confirmed with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.09963","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3629b-Hartmanetal2023","cite":"Hartman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, Z. Csubry et al.","title":"TOI 4201 b and TOI 5344 b: Discovery of Two Transiting Giant Planets around M-dwarf Stars and Revised Parameters for Three Others","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06809","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191806 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 191806 b is one of three new companions too light to be a star found in radial velocity data from the SOPHIE spectrograph combined with its predecessor ELODIE (Díaz et al. 2016). The three have orbital periods of three to five years and minimum masses between 1.6 and 19 Jupiter masses (Díaz et al. 2016). Data from more than ten years were combined, with Bayesian priors handling offsets between the instruments (Díaz et al. 2016). A search for astrometric signatures used Hipparcos data (Díaz et al. 2016).","system":"The star is accelerating by more than 11 metres per second each year, possibly because of an additional companion that may be a star or something lighter (Díaz et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Whether a further companion exists is not known (Díaz et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191806b-Dazetal2016","cite":"Díaz et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. F. Díaz, J. Rey, O. Demangeon et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XI. Three new companions and an orbit update: Giant planets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.07610","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 152843 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system comes from a joint study of this planet and its neighbour HD 152843 c. An intensive run of radial-velocity measurements (tiny wobbles in the star's motion) with the HARPS-N spectrograph, plus two sectors of TESS data, gave the masses and orbital periods of both planets (Nicholson et al. 2024). The two planets have almost equal masses of around 9 Earth masses, yet very different sizes (Nicholson et al. 2024). This planet is the smaller one, at 3.05 Earth radii, so it is a sub-Neptune (Nicholson et al. 2024).","system":"The outer planet c is very puffy, with a radius of 5.94 Earth radii, which puts it in the lowest fifth-percentile in density of known planets (Nicholson et al. 2024). The two orbital periods, 11.62 and 19.50 days, lie near a 5:3 resonance (Nicholson et al. 2024).","history":"The near-resonance hints that the pair may have interacted with a third planet at some point in the system's past (Nicholson et al. 2024).","open":"Because of the near-resonance, the planets' transit times may drift in a measurable way; this has not been settled (Nicholson et al. 2024). The bright host star and the low density of planet c make the system a key target for atmosphere studies (Nicholson et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd152843b-Nicholsonetal2024","cite":"Nicholson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"B. A. Nicholson, S. Aigrain, N. L. Eisner et al.","title":"HD152843 b & c: the masses and orbital periods of a sub-Neptune and a superpuff Neptune","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15068","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CWISEP J193518.59-154620.3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This object is a companion to a free-floating Y dwarf, one of the coldest known objects, and it is only the second Y-dwarf pair found (De Furio et al. 2025). The James Webb Space Telescope's MIRI camera resolved the two at a separation of 172 milliarcseconds, or 2.48 au at a distance of 14.43 pc (De Furio et al. 2025). Evolutionary models give the companion 7 to 24 Jupiter masses, for an assumed age of 1 to 10 billion years, and the primary 12 to 39 Jupiter masses (De Furio et al. 2025).","system":"The primary, a Y dwarf with no host star, has a temperature near 482 K and shows strong methane emission at 3.326 microns, which points to a temperature inversion of about 300 K in its upper atmosphere (Faherty et al. 2024). Such an inversion is seen here without any host star to heat the atmosphere (Faherty et al. 2024). An earlier survey found it to be one of the reddest brown dwarfs known (Marocco et al. 2019).","history":"Objects this small are thought to form by turbulent fragmentation of a molecular cloud, so a pair of them tests how low in mass such fragmentation can go (De Furio et al. 2025).","open":"The origin of the methane emission is not clear; aurorae have been suggested (De Furio et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-cwisepj193518591546203b-DeFurioetal2025","cite":"De Furio et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Matthew De Furio, Jacqueline Kelly Faherty, Daniella C. Bardalez Gagliuffi et al.","title":"Discovery of the Second Y+Y Dwarf Binary System: CWISEP J193518.59-154620.3","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17176","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-cwisepj193518591546203b-Fahertyetal2024","cite":"Faherty et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jacqueline Faherty, Ben Burningham, Jonathan Gagné et al.","title":"JWST Identifies Methane Emission in a Cold Extrasolar World","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-cwisepj193518591546203b-Maroccoetal2019","cite":"Marocco et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Federico Marocco, Dan Caselden, Aaron M. Meisner et al.","title":"CWISEP J193518.59-154620.3: An Extremely Cold Brown Dwarf in the Solar Neighborhood Discovered with CatWISE","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08913","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 67522 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS watched the star in three observing sectors, in 2019, 2021 and 2023, and caught five transits of this planet (Barber et al. 2026). Two further transits were recorded from the ground in April and May 2024 with the MuSCAT4 camera and with three 1-metre telescopes of the Las Cumbres Observatory network (Barber et al. 2026). MuSCAT4 imaged the star in four colour bands at once (Barber et al. 2026).","system":"The star also hosts the inner planet HIP 67522 b, described as the youngest Jupiter-sized planet with a well-aligned orbit, meaning its orbit follows the star's spin (Heitzmann et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip67522c-Barberetal2026","cite":"Barber et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. G. Barber, Pa C. Thao, A. W. Mann et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TI-DYE. II. HIP 67522 light curves & transit data (Barber+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip67522c-Heitzmannetal2022","cite":"Heitzmann et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alexis Heitzmann, Stephen Marsden, George Zhou et al.","title":"Young planets are key: HIP 67522 b and TOI-4562 b","journal":"The 21st Cambridge Workshop on Cool Stars, Stellar Systems, and the Sun","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7322896","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2006-BLG-109L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Microlensing events usually give only a rough mass, but this one also showed a parallax signature and finite-source effects, which gave direct masses for the star and both planets (Bennett et al. 2010). The star is 0.51 solar masses, and this planet has 231 Earth masses (Bennett et al. 2010). Keck adaptive-optics imaging confirmed the brightness expected for the host star (Bennett et al. 2010). No orbital information is available for this planet (Bennett et al. 2010).","system":"The system is a Jupiter and Saturn analogue, the first double-planet system found by microlensing (Bennett et al. 2010). It is the only multi-planet system found by any method with measured masses for the star and both planets (Bennett et al. 2010). The Saturn-like planet c has 86 Earth masses, and its 11-day light-curve deviation shows its orbital motion, which constrains four of its six orbital parameters (Bennett et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2006blg109lb-Bennettetal2010","cite":"Bennett et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. P. Bennett, S. H. Rhie, S. Nikolaev et al.","title":"Masses and Orbital Constraints for the OGLE-2006-BLG-109Lb,c Jupiter/Saturn Analog Planetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0911.2706","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219666 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is an old, inactive G7 dwarf, about 10 billion years old (Esposito et al. 2019).","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements with the HARPS spectrograph confirmed the planet and gave its mass (Esposito et al. 2019). It belongs to a rare class called hot Neptunes, and is a member of the so-called Neptune desert, a region with few planets of this size on close orbits (Esposito et al. 2019; Hellier et al. 2019). The star is bright, at magnitude 9.9, so the planet is a prime target for in-transit spectroscopy and atmosphere studies (Esposito et al. 2019; Hellier et al. 2019). Later transits recovered in WASP-South data cover 8 years and cut the uncertainty on the orbital period by a factor of 60 (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219666b-Espositoetal2019","cite":"Esposito et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Esposito, D. J. Armstrong, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"HD 219666 b: a hot-Neptune from TESS Sector 1","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.05881","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219666b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, S. Gill et al.","title":"WASP-South Detection of HD219666b Transits Provides an Accurate Ephemeris","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07276","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"tau Cet g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Direct imaging has looked for planets here, without success. A search at 10 microns with the VISIR instrument on the Very Large Telescope, using fast-chopping and adaptive optics, covered five nearby stars including Tau Ceti and made no detections (Pathak et al. 2021). The search was sensitive to planets of a few Jupiter masses, far heavier than this planet (Pathak et al. 2021).","system":"Tau Ceti is a 10-billion-year-old G8 star and the closest Sun-like star (Di Folco et al. 2014). It has an exo-Kuiper belt, a ring of leftover icy debris, with a total mass only ten times that of the Kuiper belt, and Herschel images show a ring-like structure about 15 au across (Di Folco et al. 2014). Hot dust is also seen closer to the star (Di Folco et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the debris disc has been worn down steadily by collisions or was recently regenerated by an instability among its bodies is not known (Di Folco et al. 2014). The dust points to planets that have not yet been detected (Di Folco et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-taucetg-Pathaketal2021","cite":"Pathak et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Pathak, D. J. M. Petit dit de la Roche, M. Kasper et al.","title":"High-contrast imaging at ten microns: A search for exoplanets around Eps Indi A, Eps Eri, Tau Ceti, Sirius A, and Sirius B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-taucetg-DiFolcoetal2014","cite":"Di Folco et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"E. Di Folco, J. Péricaud, J.-C. Augereau et al.","title":"Tau Ceti: our nearest cousin","journal":"SF2A-2014: Proceedings of the Annual meeting of the French Society of Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 200964 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a massive, evolved subgiant, and its brightness stays constant to within 0.002 magnitudes, which supports the planetary reading of the radial-velocity variations (Johnson et al. 2010). The planet has a minimum mass of 1.99 Jupiter masses, assuming a star of 1.44 solar masses, and orbits at 1.601 au (Johnson et al. 2010).","system":"A second planet, c, orbits at 1.95 au, so the two orbits are close together (Johnson et al. 2010). Dynamical simulations find that most stable solutions include crossing orbits, so the pair is probably locked in a mean-motion resonance that prevents close encounters (Johnson et al. 2010). A later study found the pair in a 4:3 resonance, with the resonant angles librating around a constant value (Mia & Kushvah 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd200964b-Johnsonetal2010","cite":"Johnson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"John Asher Johnson, Matthew Payne, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"Retired A Stars and Their Companions. VI. A Pair of Interacting Exoplanet Pairs Around the Subgiants 24 Sextanis and HD 200964","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4552","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd200964b-MiaKushvah2016","cite":"Mia & Kushvah 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Rajib Mia, Badam Singh Kushvah","title":"Orbital dynamics of exoplanetary systems Kepler-62, HD 200964 and Kepler-11","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02110","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-11 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey target was chosen for follow-up by the Kepler space telescope in its K2 Campaign 7 (Rabus et al. 2016).","observed":"The host star has less iron than the Sun, with a metallicity of -0.39 dex, and is among the most metal-poor stars known to host a hot Jupiter (Rabus et al. 2016). The planet is on a circular orbit (Rabus et al. 2016). K2 data later revealed a phase curve, the rise and fall of light as the planet turns, and a secondary eclipse of 62 parts per million (Niraula et al. 2018). Modelling of its reflected light finds a high optical albedo, which fits bright clouds of Mg2SiO4 (a magnesium silicate) reaching very low pressures (Adams et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats11b-Rabusetal2016","cite":"Rabus et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"M. Rabus, A. Jordán, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-11b AND HATS-12b: Two Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting Subsolar Metallicity Stars Selected for the K2 Campaign 7","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02894","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats11b-Niraulaetal2018","cite":"Niraula et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Seth Redfield, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Discovery of Six Optical Phase Curves with K2","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09227","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats11b-Adamsetal2022","cite":"Adams et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Danica J. Adams, Tiffany Kataria, Natasha E. Batalha et al.","title":"Spatially Resolved Modeling of Optical Albedos for a Sample of Six Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00041","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 8574 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up has monitored Sun-like stars from Haute-Provence Observatory with the ELODIE spectrograph since 1994 (Perrier et al. 2003).","observed":"The planet has a moderate orbital period of 227.55 days and a semi-major axis of 0.77 au (Perrier et al. 2003). It was one of six companions announced together, whose minimum masses range from just over 2 to 10.6 Jupiter masses (Perrier et al. 2003). Most of the others have much longer periods, over 3 years (Perrier et al. 2003). Orbits in the group are all eccentric, and four of the six have eccentricities near 0.5 (Perrier et al. 2003).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd8574b-Perrieretal2003","cite":"Perrier et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"C. Perrier, J.-P. Sivan, D. Naef et al.","title":"The ELODIE survey for northern extra-solar planets. I. Six new extra-solar planet candidates","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0308281","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2013-BLG-605L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits 9 to 14 times farther out than the expected snow line, the distance beyond which water freezes, which is much like Neptune's 11 times (Sumi et al. 2016). Three different physical solutions fit the data equally well, and two of them come from a new kind of degeneracy in the parallax measurement, named the wide degeneracy (Sumi et al. 2016). The solutions give a Neptune-mass planet of 21 Earth masses around a low-mass M dwarf, a mini-Neptune of 7.9 Earth masses around a brown dwarf, or a super-Earth of 3.2 Earth masses around a low-mass brown dwarf (Sumi et al. 2016).","system":"The planet-to-host mass ratio is (3.6 +/- 0.7) x 10^-4 (Sumi et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Which of the three solutions is right is not known, so neither the planet's mass nor the type of its host is settled (Sumi et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2013blg605lb-Sumietal2016","cite":"Sumi et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"T. Sumi, A. Udalski, D. P. Bennett et al.","title":"The First Neptune Analog or Super-Earth with a Neptune-like Orbit: MOA-2013-BLG-605Lb","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.00134","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 190228 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion orbits a subgiant star and has a long period of more than 3 years (Perrier et al. 2003). Analysis of the star's chemistry found it to be metal-poor, with [Fe/H] = -0.40, at the low end for stars known to host planets (Chen & Zhao 2001). Its other elements show no clear anomalies, and nothing suggests that planet formation or the star's expansion has altered its composition (Chen & Zhao 2001).","system":"","history":"Planets tend to form around metal-rich stars (Santos et al. 2001). The low metal content here led to the idea that the companion may be a brown dwarf instead of a planet (Chen & Zhao 2001).","open":"Whether the companion is a planet or a brown dwarf was questioned (Chen & Zhao 2001)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd190228b-Perrieretal2003","cite":"Perrier et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"C. Perrier, J.-P. Sivan, D. Naef et al.","title":"The ELODIE survey for northern extra-solar planets. I. Six new extra-solar planet candidates","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0308281","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd190228b-ChenZhao2001","cite":"Chen & Zhao 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Y. Q. Chen, G. Zhao","title":"The companion of HD 190228: Planet or brown dwarf?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20010790","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd190228b-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, G. Israelian, M. Mayor","title":"The metal-rich nature of stars with planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0105216","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 6061 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the TESS-Keck Survey, which picked its targets with care to serve atmosphere studies (Akana Murphy et al. 2023).","observed":"More than two years of radial-velocity measurements with Keck-HIRES and the Automated Planet Finder gave a mass for the planet (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). It is one of eight newly confirmed sub-Neptunes chosen as suited to atmosphere studies with the James Webb Space Telescope, and one of four with masses measured to better than 5 sigma (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). The host star is bright, at magnitude 8.8 (Akana Murphy et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd6061b-Murphyetal2023","cite":"Murphy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Natalie Batalha, TESS-Keck Survey","title":"The Masses and Radii of Eight Sub-Neptunes Amenable to Atmospheric Characterization","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three sub-Neptunes of similar size that cross the face of a bright, nearby Sun-like star (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020). Their orbits are consistent with a stable, circular, coplanar layout near mean-motion resonances of 1:3 and 3:4 (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020). Radial velocities from several instruments later gave a mass for this planet with 3 sigma precision (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","system":"The system has at least five planets, with the three transiting sub-Neptunes inside two distant Jupiter-like planets (Lubin & Petigura 2024). Further planets include a Saturn-mass planet e, a non-transiting planet g near 280 days with a minimum mass of 13.5 Earth masses in the habitable zone, and hints of a very long-period companion f (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). A measurement of the spin-orbit angle for planet b is consistent with alignment, and all planets lie within about 10 degrees of one shared orbital plane (Lubin & Petigura 2024).","history":"","open":"Evidence for the very long-period companion f is only suggestive (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939c-BadenasAgustietal2020","cite":"Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mariona Badenas-Agusti, Maximilian N. Günther, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"HD 191939: Three Sub-Neptunes Transiting a Sun-like Star Only 54 pc Away","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939c-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939c-LubinPetigura2024","cite":"Lubin & Petigura 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Lubin, Erik Petigura","title":"HD 191939: a multi-planet system with a shared and aligned orbital plane","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-50 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's size is consistent with basic models of irradiated giant planets (Gillon et al. 2011). Occultation measurements from TESS give only an upper limit on its geometric albedo, the fraction of starlight it reflects, of 0.445 (Blažek et al. 2022). High-resolution spectroscopy found no significant absorption signals above 3 sigma in its atmosphere, in a study of five hot Jupiters at the edge of the Neptune desert (Jiang et al. 2026).","system":"The host star is moderately active, and its rotation and magnetic activity suggest an age of 0.8 billion years, while stellar evolution favours 7 billion years (Gillon et al. 2011).","history":"The age mismatch could come from tidal and magnetic effects of the planet on its star, in line with the faster rotation and stronger activity seen in stars that host hot Jupiters (Gillon et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp50b-Gillonetal2011","cite":"Gillon et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"M. Gillon, A. P. Doyle, M. Lendl et al.","title":"WASP-50 b: a hot Jupiter transiting a moderately active solar-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.2641","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp50b-Blaeketal2022","cite":"Blažek et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Martin Blažek, Petr Kabáth, Anjali A. A. Piette et al.","title":"Constraints on TESS albedos for five hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.03327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp50b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Zewen Jiang, Wei Wang, Shumei Jia et al.","title":"High-resolution Spectroscopic Atmospheric Studies of Five Hot Jupiters Across the Edge of the Neptune Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21273","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"PH2 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this planet so far consists of atmosphere models rather than direct measurements. The planet is treated as a temperate, gas-rich world with a thick hydrogen-dominated atmosphere, orbiting a G or K star (Hu 2021). Models trace how starlight breaks up water, methane, ammonia and hydrogen sulphide, and how the pieces react (Hu 2021). Ammonia is likely destroyed by sunlight down to the cloud layer on planets around G and K stars (Hu 2021). Hydrogen cyanide is the main product of ammonia breaking up in the presence of methane, and methane with water gives carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide (Hu 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ph2b-Hu2021","cite":"Hu 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Renyu Hu","title":"Photochemistry and Spectral Characterization of Temperate and Gas-rich Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04419","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-206 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a group of 18 candidate planets chosen as ideal targets for emission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based follow-up from the TESS Follow-up Observing Program and the vetting tools DAVE and TRICERATOPS (Giacalone et al. 2022). It is one of 13 planets validated this way, and one of seven of them with ultra-short orbits (Giacalone et al. 2022). Planets in the group are hot and potentially terrestrial (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi206b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Barnard c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Early radial-velocity monitoring of Barnard's star with the VLT and UVES instrument excluded planets with minimum masses above 7.5 Earth masses throughout the habitable zone (Kürster et al. 2003). The lowest limit was 2.9 Earth masses, at an orbital period of 2.5 days (Kürster et al. 2003). Stellar activity adds variability that can be mistaken for planetary signals (Kürster et al. 2003).","system":"Barnard's star is a nearby, old red dwarf of about 7 to 12 billion years, and the second-nearest star system to the Sun after Alpha Centauri (Riedel et al. 2005). It is moderately active, with X-ray emission from its corona and chromospheric ultraviolet emission (Riedel et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-barnardc-Krsteretal2003","cite":"Kürster et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"M. Kürster, M. Endl, F. Rouesnel et al.","title":"The low-level radial velocity variability   in Barnard's star (= GJ 699).  Secular acceleration, indications for convective redshift,  and planet mass limits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0303528","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-barnardc-Riedeletal2005","cite":"Riedel et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"A. R. Riedel, E. F. Guinan, L. E. DeWarf et al.","title":"Barnard's Star as a Proxy for Old Disk dM Stars: Magnetic Activity, Light Variations, XUV Irradiances, and Planetary Habitable Zones","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #206","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-6 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an ultrashort-period hot Jupiter, orbiting in 21.17 hours around an old K dwarf of 9.6 billion years (Vines et al. 2019). Its star is metal-rich, like the small group of other known ultrashort-period hot Jupiters (Vines et al. 2019). Models of photoevaporation, the loss of gas heated by starlight, suggest the planet has lost about 5 percent of its atmosphere over the life of the system (Vines et al. 2019). A later search for tidal decay, a shrinking orbital period, used 33 new transit light curves for NGTS-6 and three other hot Jupiters (Griffiths et al. 2026). A steady orbit fits three of the four systems best, with the strongest hint of decay in WASP-173 (Griffiths et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Ultrashort-period gas giants may help show which formation and evolution processes dominate this population (Vines et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts6b-Vinesetal2019","cite":"Vines et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jose I. Vines, James S. Jenkins, Jack S. Acton et al.","title":"NGTS-6b: an ultrashort period hot-Jupiter orbiting an old K dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07997","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts6b-Griffithsetal2026","cite":"Griffiths et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. W. Griffiths, J. Southworth, L. Alegre et al.","title":"A search for transit-timing variations in the transiting hot-Jupiter systems HIP 65, NGTS-6, NGTS-10, and WASP-173","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14634","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-56 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-56 b has a mass of about 0.57 Jupiter masses and a radius of about 1.09 Jupiter radii (Faedi et al. 2013). It was announced together with two other planets of lower than Jupiter mass. Its radius is not larger than expected for its mass (Faedi et al. 2013). Its density is high, which may point to a large core of heavy elements (Faedi et al. 2013).","system":"The planet circles a main-sequence star of spectral type G6 every 4.67 days (Faedi et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Whether the high density really means a heavy-element core is not settled; the possibility is raised only tentatively (Faedi et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp56b-Faedietal2013","cite":"Faedi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"F. Faedi, D. Pollacco, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-54b, WASP-56b, and WASP-57b: Three new sub-Jupiter mass planets from SuperWASP","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2329","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-615 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of three Saturn-mass planets around hot, F-type stars. Such stars are poorly sampled by transit searches, and the planets let astronomers study planet formation and strong heating by the star (Psaridi et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-615 b is a bloated, Saturn-mass planet of about 1.69 Jupiter radii and 0.43 Jupiter masses (Psaridi et al. 2023). It orbits a 6850 K star every 4.66 days and receives about 1277 times the sunlight that Earth does (Psaridi et al. 2023). Telescopes on the ground and radial velocity spectrographs confirmed the planet after the space telescope first flagged it (Psaridi et al. 2023).","system":"A sibling from the same study, TOI-622 b, receives about 600 times Earth's sunlight but shows no sign of a swollen radius (Psaridi et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi615b-Psaridietal2023","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Angelica Psaridi, François Bouchy, Monika Lendl et al.","title":"Three Saturn-mass planets transiting F-type stars revealed with TESS and HARPS: TOI-615b, TOI-622b, and TOI-2641b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15080","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-33 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is a hot, fast-spinning A-type star. Such stars were out of reach for the usual radial-velocity method, so the planet extended planet searches to a new kind of host (Collier Cameron et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet was confirmed by watching its shadow cross the star's spectral lines during three transits (Collier Cameron et al. 2010). That signal gave the planet's size and showed that it orbits backwards compared with the star's spin (Collier Cameron et al. 2010). The orbit lies only about 5.5 stellar radii from the star's surface, and the equilibrium temperature was estimated at 2700 K (Guenther et al. 2011). Infrared spectra of its dayside show carbon monoxide, water and the hydroxyl molecule OH (Choi et al. 2025).","system":"The star pulsates in a complex pattern of the delta Scuti type, the first transiting-planet host known to do so (Herrero et al. 2011). Planetary tides may help excite some of these pulsations (Collier Cameron et al. 2010). The orbit slowly swings around, and the planet is expected to stop transiting its star around 2090 (Stephan et al. 2022).","history":"A high-eccentricity route, in which tides shrink a stretched-out orbit, could explain how the planet reached its tight orbit (Stephan et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp33b-Cameronetal2010","cite":"Cameron et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"A. Collier Cameron, E. Guenther, B. Smalley et al.","title":"Line-profile tomography of exoplanet transits - II. A gas-giant planet transiting a rapidly rotating A5 star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.4551","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp33b-Guentheretal2011","cite":"Guenther et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eike W. Guenther, Andrew Collier Cameron, Barry Smalley et al.","title":"A Planet of an A-Star: HD15082b","journal":"Planetary Systems Beyond the Main Sequence","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0500","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp33b-Choietal2025","cite":"Choi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Yeon-Ho Choi, Ueejeong Jeong, Jae-Joon Lee et al.","title":"An Early Look at the Performance of IGRINS-2 at Gemini-North with Application to the Ultrahot Jupiter, WASP-33 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12736","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp33b-Herreroetal2011","cite":"Herrero et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"E. Herrero, J. C. Morales, I. Ribas et al.","title":"WASP-33: the first δ Scuti exoplanet host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1173","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp33b-Stephanetal2022","cite":"Stephan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alexander P. Stephan, Ji Wang, P. Wilson Cauley et al.","title":"Nodal Precession and Tidal Evolution of Two Hot Jupiters: WASP-33 b and KELT-9 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02546","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":45,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-263 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-263 b is a sub-Neptune with a long orbit for a small planet, about 50 days (Mortier et al. 2018). Its mass was measured with 67 radial velocity readings. Few small planets with such long periods have precisely measured masses (Mortier et al. 2018). Its density of about 5.7 grams per cubic centimetre points to a rocky core wrapped in a thick envelope of water or other volatiles (Mortier et al. 2018). A later joint study that adds new space-telescope transits finds it needs a steam-rich or mixed volatile envelope (Palethorpe et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a quiet G9 dwarf star (Mortier et al. 2018).","history":"The planet probably formed like the cores of the four giant planets of the Solar System but gathered little gas (Mortier et al. 2018).","open":"Why it did not collect more gas is not known (Mortier et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2263b-Mortieretal2018","cite":"Mortier et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. Mortier, A. S. Bonomo, V. M. Rajpaul et al.","title":"K2-263 b: a 50 d period sub-Neptune with a mass measurement using HARPS-N","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.08187","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2263b-Palethorpeetal2026","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Larissa Palethorpe, Annelies Mortier, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Constraining small planet compositions for future missions","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14552","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WISPIT 2 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits inside a gap in a young disc, which suggests it is carving that gap while it forms (van Capelleveen et al. 2025).","observed":"WISPIT 2 b is a young planet still embedded in a disc of gas and dust, seen in direct images of the star (van Capelleveen et al. 2025). It moves together with its star and shows orbital motion that fits an orbit within the gap (van Capelleveen et al. 2025). Its infrared brightness matches the glow of a young planet (van Capelleveen et al. 2025). Images in the Halpha line show it is still gathering gas, with emission about 54 au from the star (Close et al. 2025).","system":"The star is about 5 million years old and has a disc of 380 au in scattered light with several rings (van Capelleveen et al. 2025). A second protoplanet was later reported, which makes the system the closest known analogue to PDS 70 (Swastik et al. 2026). Light from the star shows little sign of gas accretion onto the star itself (Swastik et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wispit2b-vanCapelleveenetal2025","cite":"van Capelleveen et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Richelle F. van Capelleveen, Christian Ginski, Matthew A. Kenworthy et al.","title":"WIde Separation Planets In Time (WISPIT): A Gap-clearing Planet in a Multi-ringed Disk around the Young Solar-type Star WISPIT 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19053","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2b-Closeetal2025","cite":"Close et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Laird M. Close, Richelle F. van Capelleveen, Gabriel Weible et al.","title":"Wide Separation Planets in Time (WISPIT): Discovery of a Gap Hα Protoplanet WISPIT 2b with MagAO-X","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19046","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2b-Swastiketal2026","cite":"Swastik et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"C. Swastik, M. Bestha, L. S. Sonith et al.","title":"A quiet host in an active planet-forming disk: Optical spectroscopy of WISPIT 2","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.20649","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 147379 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first found with the radial velocity survey CARMENES, which looks at M dwarfs (Reiners et al. 2018).","observed":"HD 147379 b is a Neptune-mass world, about 1.5 times the mass of Neptune at minimum (Reiners et al. 2018). It sits in the temperate zone of its star, where water could be liquid (Reiners et al. 2018). The signal appears in CARMENES data from 2016 and 2017 and in Keck data going back to 2000 (Reiners et al. 2018). A later reanalysis with another spectrograph refined the orbit and found no additional planets (Stalport et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a bright M0 dwarf about 10.7 parsecs away that spins once in about 21 days (Reiners et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd147379b-Reinersetal2018","cite":"Reiners et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. Reiners, I. Ribas, M. Zechmeister et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. HD147379 b: A nearby Neptune in the temperate zone of an early-M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.05797","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd147379b-Stalportetal2023","cite":"Stalport et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Stalport, M. Cretignier, S. Udry et al.","title":"A review of planetary systems around HD 99492, HD 147379, and HD 190007 with HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05669","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-17 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about Kepler-17 b concerns the starspots it crosses. The planet orbits an active, young Sun-like star with many dark spots (Namekata et al. 2020). Each time it passes over a spot, the light briefly brightens, which maps spots at fine scale (Namekata et al. 2020). Two light dips per rotation hide the fact that many spots exist (Namekata et al. 2020). The planet's orbit runs in the same direction as the star's spin (Holczer et al. 2015). Spot motion also gives the star's differential rotation and a magnetic cycle of about 1.1 years (Bonomo & Lanza 2012; Estrela & Valio 2016). Spots lie near latitudes of plus or minus 15 degrees (Sagynbayeva & Farr 2026).","system":"The star is a G2 dwarf and a young solar analogue (Bonomo & Lanza 2012). The planet's mass is about 2.5 Jupiter masses (Bonomo & Lanza 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler17b-Namekataetal2020","cite":"Namekata et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Kosuke Namekata, James R. A. Davenport, Brett M. Morris et al.","title":"Temporal Evolution of Spatially Resolved Individual Star Spots on a Planet-hosting Solar-type Star: Kepler-17","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01086","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler17b-Holczeretal2015","cite":"Holczer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Tomer Holczer, Avi Shporer, Tsevi Mazeh et al.","title":"Time Variation of Kepler Transits Induced by Stellar Spots—A Way to Distinguish between Prograde and Retrograde Motion. II. Application to KOIs","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04028","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler17b-BonomoLanza2012","cite":"Bonomo & Lanza 2012","year":"2012","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. F. Lanza","title":"Starspot activity and rotation of the planet-hosting star Kepler-17","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.1676","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler17b-EstrelaValio2016","cite":"Estrela & Valio 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Raissa Estrela, Adriana Valio","title":"Stellar Magnetic Cycles in the Solar-like Stars Kepler-17 and Kepler-63","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/0004-637X/831/1/57","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler17b-SagynbayevaFarr2026","cite":"Sagynbayeva & Farr 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sabina Sagynbayeva, Will M. Farr","title":"Polka-dotted Stars. II. Starspots and Obliquities of Kepler-17 and Kepler-63","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07130","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-178 g belongs to a compact system of six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune (Hooton et al. 2022). It was singled out as the one possible temperate sub-Neptune target for a future infrared space mission, Ariel (Encrenaz et al. 2021). JWST time was awarded to observe its transit and those of planets b and d, to measure the makeup of their atmospheres (Hooton et al. 2021).","system":"Five of the six planets form a Laplace resonant chain, a rare pattern of linked orbits also seen in the TRAPPIST-1 planets and Jupiter's Galilean moons (Hooton et al. 2022). Planet densities jump up and down from one planet to the next rather than falling steadily with distance (Hooton et al. 2022). The host is a nearby K dwarf (Hooton et al. 2022).","history":"Early data hinted that two planets might share one orbit in a horseshoe pattern, but later observations showed six separate planets (Hooton et al. 2022). The resonant chain restricts how the system can have formed (Hooton et al. 2022).","open":"The large density differences between neighbouring planets are hard to explain with current theory (Hooton et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178g-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178g-Encrenazetal2021","cite":"Encrenaz et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Therese Encrenaz, Athena Coustenis, Gabriella Gilli et al.","title":"Observability of temperate exoplanets with Ariel","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #53","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178g-Hootonetal2021","cite":"Hooton et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 876 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Fits that assume all orbits lie in the same plane give a tilt of about 50 degrees, which raises its mass from a minimum of 5.89 to 7.53 Earth masses (Rivera et al. 2005). At the time it was by far the lowest-mass planet found around a main-sequence star other than the Sun (Rivera et al. 2005). Brightness data give the star a rotation period of 96.7 days (Rivera et al. 2005).","system":"The three outer planets lie in a Laplace resonance with rapid chaotic motion, while the innermost planet is not in the resonance and has a high eccentricity (Puranam & Batygin 2016). The star is the closest known to host several planets (Puranam & Batygin 2016).","history":"","open":"How the innermost planet keeps its high eccentricity despite tidal dissipation had not been clear. Simulations suggest angular momentum from the resonant chain stirs it, and imply a tidal Q of order a few thousand (Puranam & Batygin 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj876d-Riveraetal2005","cite":"Rivera et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, Jack J. Lissauer, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"A ~7.5 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet Orbiting the Nearby Star, GJ 876","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510508","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876d-PuranamBatygin2016","cite":"Puranam & Batygin 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Abhijit Krishna Puranam, Konstantin Batygin","title":"Tidal Q of a Super Earth: Dynamical Constraints from the GJ 876 System","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #47","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-85 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-85 A b is a hot Jupiter in a visual binary. A K-dwarf companion at about 1.5 arcseconds blends its light with the host star (Brown et al. 2014). Without correcting for the extra light, the transit depth would be about half what it should be, the planet radius about 18 percent too small, and some radial velocity readings would underestimate its mass (Brown et al. 2015). Strong calcium emission shows both stars are active (Brown et al. 2014). Spots on the host star have been studied with simultaneous observations from several telescopes (Kunovac 2024).","system":"The binary shows a long-term trend in position angle that points to a misalignment in the binary's orbit (Brown et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp85ab-Brownetal2014","cite":"Brown et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, D. R. Anderson, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Three transiting planet discoveries from the Wide Angle Search for Planets: WASP-85 A b; WASP-116 b, and WASP-149 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.7761","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp85ab-Brownetal2015","cite":"Brown et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, D. R. Anderson, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Three transiting planet discoveries from the Wide Angle Search for Planets: WASP-85 A b; WASP-116 b, and WASP-149 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.7761","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp85ab-Kunovac2024","cite":"Kunovac 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Vedad Kunovac","title":"Unveiling stellar spots in WASP-85A: Simultaneous transit observations with ESPRESSO, HARPS-N, CHEOPS, NGTS and SPECULOOS","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about TRAPPIST-1 f comes from studies of the whole system. Over 1000 hours of Spitzer observation from 2016 to 2019 monitored its transits to constrain its mass, size and makeup (Ducrot et al. 2020). The system is regarded as well suited to atmospheric study with JWST (Ducrot et al. 2020). Transit timing suggests the planets are rocky, with possibly extra volatiles (Fauchez et al. 2020).","system":"The system holds seven Earth-sized planets around a nearby ultracool dwarf star (Ducrot et al. 2020). At least three lie in the classical habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). The planets sit in a resonant chain, with every planet tied to its neighbours in two-planet and three-planet resonances (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","history":"Simulations of planets migrating in a disc can reproduce the resonant chain and the observed timing shifts (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"How much water the planets hold is not pinned down (Dorn et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1f-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1f-Fauchezetal2020","cite":"Fauchez et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Thomas J. Fauchez, Martin Turbet, Eric T. Wolf et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1 Habitable Atmosphere Intercomparison (THAI): motivations and protocol version 1.0","journal":"Geoscientific Model Development","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10950","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1f-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1f-Dornetal2018","cite":"Dorn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Klaus Mosegaard, Simon L. Grimm et al.","title":"Interior Characterization in Multiplanetary Systems: TRAPPIST-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-408 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of 60 K2 candidates confirmed statistically in a single project aiming to find good targets for follow-up study (Christiansen et al. 2022).","observed":"K2-408 b is a super-Earth around a moderately bright star (V = 11.93) that is poor in heavy elements ([Fe/H] = -0.579) (Christiansen et al. 2022). It was confirmed by checking spectra, high-resolution images, position shifts in the star's image and the statistical odds, rather than by a mass measurement (Christiansen et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2408b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2018-BLG-1011L b":{"text":{"why":"The light curve showed a double bump near the peak that two lenses or two sources could not fully explain. A third lens component was needed (Han et al. 2019).","observed":"OGLE-2018-BLG-1011L b is the inner of two giant planets of about 1.8 and 2.8 Jupiter masses around a low-mass star of about 0.18 solar masses (Han et al. 2019). The lens lies about 7.1 kiloparsecs away, the farthest system of its kind in the sample (Han et al. 2019). The planet-to-star mass ratios are about 0.0095 and 0.015 (Han et al. 2019). Two other models fit the data less well, including one with a single planet in a binary (Han et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The masses are only roughly known, with wide uncertainty ranges, because they come from a statistical estimate (Han et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2018blg1011lb-Hanetal2019","cite":"Han et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Cheongho Han, David P. Bennett, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"OGLE-2018-BLG-1011Lb,c: Microlensing Planetary System with Two Giant Planets Orbiting a Low-mass Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.01741","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 175607 b":{"text":{"why":"The search that found it targeted metal-poor stars, because models suggest such stars may favour small planets (Mortier et al. 2016).","observed":"HD 175607 b is a Neptune-like planet of at least 9 Earth masses, found from 119 radial velocity measurements across more than nine years (Mortier et al. 2016). Its host is the most metal-poor G dwarf known with a sub-Neptune (Mortier et al. 2016). The orbit is slightly eccentric (Mortier et al. 2016). The orbital period is close to the star's estimated rotation period, so the team checked activity signals with several methods (Mortier et al. 2016).","system":"The host is an old G6 star with a metallicity of -0.62 (Mortier et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd175607b-Mortieretal2016","cite":"Mortier et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"A. Mortier, J. P. Faria, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXIX. HD 175607, the most metal-poor G dwarf with an orbiting sub-Neptune","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.03941","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1815 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first known in the thick disc of the Galaxy that TESS detected (Gan et al. 2020).","observed":"LHS 1815 b is an Earth-sized planet around a bright, quiet M dwarf (Gan et al. 2020). Its mass was only constrained to under 8.7 Earth masses (Gan et al. 2020). The star's motion through the Galaxy makes it very likely a member of the thick disc, and it reaches about 1.8 kiloparsecs above the Galactic plane, much higher than other TESS hosts (Gan et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"Planets in the thick disc let astronomers compare how efficiently planets form and evolve in different parts of the Galaxy (Gan et al. 2020).","open":"The interior and atmosphere of the planet are unknown (Gan et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1815b-Ganetal2020","cite":"Gan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Tianjun Gan, Avi Shporer, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"LHS 1815b: The First Thick-disk Planet Detected by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04525","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-54 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-54 b is a heavily bloated planet with a mass of about 0.64 Jupiter masses (Faedi et al. 2013). Its orbit appears slightly eccentric, with e about 0.067, and further tests suggest this could be real (Faedi et al. 2013). Observing its secondary eclipse, when it passes behind its star, would settle the eccentricity and show its thermal structure (Faedi et al. 2013).","system":"The planet circles an F9 star that is evolving off the main sequence (Faedi et al. 2013). The star is bright (V = 10.42) (Faedi et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Whether the orbit is truly eccentric is not yet settled (Faedi et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp54b-Faedietal2013","cite":"Faedi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"F. Faedi, D. Pollacco, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-54b, WASP-56b, and WASP-57b: Three new sub-Jupiter mass planets from SuperWASP","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2329","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-41 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HAT-P-41 b was the first exoplanet observed with Hubble's WFC3/UVIS G280 grism, which covers 200 to 800 nm in one go (Wakeford et al. 2020). A transmission spectrum from the Gran Telescopio Canarias shows strong evidence for titanium oxide, with weaker hints of chromium hydride and magnesium hydride, and no high clouds or haze (Jiang et al. 2024). Hubble and Spitzer emission data give a near blackbody-like dayside spectrum (Fu et al. 2022). The orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Zak et al. 2025). The planet sits between hot and ultra-hot Jupiters (Fu et al. 2022).","system":"The star has a close stellar companion candidate (Wollert et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Hints of a temperature inversion in the upper atmosphere need confirming with dayside observations (Jiang et al. 2024). Emission data do not favour a metal-rich atmosphere (Fu et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp41b-Wakefordetal2020","cite":"Wakeford et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"H. R. Wakeford, D. K. Sing, K. B. Stevenson et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HAT-P-41b transmission spectra with HST WFC3/UVIS (Wakeford+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51590204","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp41b-Jiangetal2024","cite":"Jiang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Jiang, G. Chen, F. Murgas et al.","title":"Confirmation of TiO absorption and tentative detection of MgH and CrH in the atmosphere of HAT-P-41b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13840","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp41b-Fuetal2022","cite":"Fu et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Guangwei Fu, David K. Sing, Drake Deming et al.","title":"The Hubble PanCET Program: Emission Spectrum of Hot Jupiter HAT-P-41b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12314","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp41b-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquities of eight close-in gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.05615","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp41b-Wollertetal2015","cite":"Wollert et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maria Wöllert, Wolfgang Brandner, Carolina Bergfors et al.","title":"A Lucky Imaging search for stellar companions to transiting planet host stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01938","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4034 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of three giant planets found around stars nearing the end of their main-sequence life, a stage of hot Jupiter evolution that is little explored (Battley et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-4034 b is a short-period hot Jupiter with a radius of about 1.58 Jupiter radii and a mass of about 0.87 Jupiter masses (Battley et al. 2026). Its orbit takes 1.80 days (Battley et al. 2026). Follow-up spectrographs confirmed the planet after the space telescope flagged it (Battley et al. 2026).","system":"The host star is approaching the end of its main-sequence life (Battley et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4034b-Battleyetal2026","cite":"Battley et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew P. Battley, Marina Lafarga, Edward Gillen et al.","title":"TOI-3664 b, TOI-4034 b, and TOI-6564 b: three new hot Jupiters around stars approaching the terminal age main sequence","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.05295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3568 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits near the sub-Jovian desert, a region of short orbital periods where planets between super-Earths and hot Jupiters are unusually scarce (Martioli et al. 2024).","observed":"Radial-velocity monitoring with the MAROON-X spectrograph at Gemini North, plus extra data from the SPIRou spectrograph at CFHT, showed the signal was a real planet. A joint fit of the transit light curves and the velocities gave a bulk density of 0.98 g/cm3, low for its size (Martioli et al. 2024). The host is a K dwarf with solar metallicity, meaning it has about the Sun's share of heavy elements (Martioli et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The scarcity of planets in the desert is likely shaped by photoevaporation, where starlight strips gas from giant planets that have migrated inward (Martioli et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3568b-Martiolietal2024","cite":"Martioli et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"E. Martioli, R. P. Petrucci, E. Jofré et al.","title":"TOI-3568 b: A super-Neptune in the sub-Jovian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.03704","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-454 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's size placed it between the two groups of small planets, so its make-up was in question (Kohler 2016).","observed":"Planets smaller than 1.6 Earth radii with measured masses follow one mass-radius curve of Earth-like rock, while planets between 2 and 2.7 Earth radii are less dense and do not follow a single curve (Kohler 2016). Mass measurements place Kepler-454 b among the six larger, lower-density planets of that sample (Kohler 2016). Low densities suggest a significant share of volatiles, likely a thick gas envelope of water, hydrogen or helium (Kohler 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether planets in this size range are rocky or gas-rich is still debated, and only 12 planets between 1 and 2.7 Earth radii had precise masses at the time (Kohler 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler454b-Kohler2016","cite":"Kohler 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"Kepler-454b: Rocky or Not?","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 73534 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements at Keck Observatory show the star wobbling with a semi-amplitude of 16 m/s, which implies a Jupiter-like planet with a minimum mass of 1.1 Jupiter masses on a nearly circular orbit of 4.85 years (Valenti et al. 2009). The star is chromospherically inactive, meaning it shows little magnetic activity that could mimic a planet, and it is metal-rich (Valenti et al. 2009).","system":"The host is a G5 subgiant, a star that has begun to swell as it ages (Valenti et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"Orbits fitted to weak signals tend to look more eccentric than they are, a long-known bias that affects planets whose wobble is close to the measurement error (Valenti et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd73534b-Valentietal2009","cite":"Valenti et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Jeff A. Valenti, Debra Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Two Exoplanets Discovered at Keck Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 1061 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits inside the star's habitable zone, the range of distances where liquid water could exist on a surface (Wright et al. 2016).","observed":"Archival spectra from the HARPS spectrograph were reanalysed with a template tailored to the star, which removed a strong yearly variation in the velocities. The reanalysis revealed three planets with minimum masses low enough that they may be rocky (Wright et al. 2016). No signs of stellar activity appear near the planetary periods (Wright et al. 2016). The planet lies about 14 light-years away and orbits a solar-age M3 dwarf at 0.084 AU (Guinan & Engle 2017).","system":"Wolf 1061 has an inner planet with a 4.888-day orbit and an outer planet with a 67.274-day orbit that lies just outside the habitable zone (Wright et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The planet's radius is not measured and its atmosphere and surface are not known. Climate models of tidally locked planets around stars like Wolf 1061 find that haze depends on how fast the planet rotates (Cohen et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf1061c-Wrightetal2016","cite":"Wright et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Wright, R. A. Wittenmyer, C. G. Tinney et al.","title":"Three Planets Orbiting Wolf 1061","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05154","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wolf1061c-GuinanEngle2017","cite":"Guinan & Engle 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Edward F. Guinan, Scott G. Engle","title":"Beyond Proxima b: Investigating the next nearest Potentially Habitable Exoplanets: Kapteyn b (13 LY) and Wolf 1061 c (14 LY) - Assessing their Suitabilty for Life","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #229","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wolf1061c-Cohenetal2024","cite":"Cohen et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Maureen Cohen, Paul Palmer, Massimo Bollasina et al.","title":"Haze Optical Depth in Exoplanet Atmospheres Varies with Rotation Rate: Implications for Observations","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2024-168","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-519 b":{"text":{"why":"Brown dwarfs and massive planets on short orbits around M dwarfs are rare, yet more have been found than planet formation models expected (Parviainen et al. 2021).","observed":"Its host is a faint M dwarf with a visual magnitude of 17.35, and the object was first confirmed using multicolour transit photometry and a phase curve model (Parviainen et al. 2021). Radial velocities from the Subaru IRD instrument then gave a mass of 0.463 Jupiter masses, showing it is a planet and not a brown dwarf (Kagetani et al. 2023). The host star is metal-rich and has the lowest effective temperature, 3322 K, of any star known to host a close-in giant planet (Kagetani et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Models of the planet's thermal evolution allow a core of 0 to about 30 Earth masses, which fits both core accretion and disk instability formation (Kagetani et al. 2023). The metal-rich host fits a trend that favours core accretion (Kagetani et al. 2023).","open":"How a giant planet formed around such a low-mass star is not settled (Kagetani et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi519b-Parviainenetal2021","cite":"Parviainen et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"H. Parviainen, E. Palle, M. R. Zapatero-Osorio et al.","title":"TOI-519 b: A short-period substellar object around an M dwarf validated using multicolour photometry and phase curve analysis","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.11458","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi519b-Kagetanietal2023","cite":"Kagetani et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Taiki Kagetani, Norio Narita, Tadahiro Kimura et al.","title":"The mass of TOI-519 b: A close-in giant planet transiting a metal-rich mid-M dwarf","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.14703","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 5278 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The ESPRESSO spectrograph on the VLT took 43 high-resolution spectra of the bright late-F star, and the planet's mass came from a joint fit of those velocities with the TESS light curve (Sozzetti et al. 2021). Spectroscopic checks and the light curve were used to gauge how much stellar activity affects the velocities (Sozzetti et al. 2021).","system":"A second, non-transiting planet of about Neptune's mass orbits the same star (Sozzetti et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd5278b-Sozzettietal2021","cite":"Sozzetti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Sozzetti, M. Damasso, A. S. Bonomo et al.","title":"A sub-Neptune and a non-transiting Neptune-mass companion unveiled by ESPRESSO around the bright late-F dwarf HD 5278 (TOI-130)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.12300","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63765 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the HARPS spectrograph at La Silla show a planet with a minimum mass of 0.64 Jupiter masses on an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.24, at 0.94 AU from its star (Ségransan et al. 2011). The host is a G9 dwarf with moderate magnetic activity (Ségransan et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63765b-Sgransanetal2011","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. Ségransan, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIX. Four new planets in orbit around the moderatly active dwarfs <ASTROBJ>HD 63765</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 104067</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 125595</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HIP 70849</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.0339","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-19 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was too small and its star too faint for radial-velocity confirmation, so it was confirmed statistically (Ballard 2011).","observed":"A planet can be argued for statistically if the combined likelihood of all false-positive explanations is much smaller than the planet explanation (Ballard 2011). An authentic planet shows the same transit depth at different colours, and this check was used with space-based Kepler and Spitzer observations of the system (Ballard 2011). The system hosts a transiting planet of 2.2 Earth radii (Ballard 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler19b-Ballard2011","cite":"Ballard 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Sarah Ashley Ballard","title":"In Pursuit of New Worlds: Searches for and Studies of Transiting Exoplanets from Three Space-Based Observatories","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-538 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS 2-minute data were used to update the planet's transit timing, which sharpened the orbital period considerably (Battley et al. 2021). The new ephemeris, a table of predicted transit times, is at least seven years fresher than the archival one (Battley et al. 2021). Accurate predictions matter for follow-up observations of the planet (Battley et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler538b-Battleyetal2021","cite":"Battley et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Matthew P. Battley, Michelle Kunimoto, David J. Armstrong et al.","title":"Revisiting the Kepler field with TESS: Improved ephemerides using TESS 2 min data","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.03259","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 332231 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's long period and the star's brightness make it a compelling target for transmission spectroscopy, because its low surface gravity and relatively low stellar flux favour a detectable atmosphere (Dalba et al. 2020).","observed":"TESS caught only one transit at first, and a second transit was hidden because the data processing had masked it for scattered light (Dalba et al. 2020). Radial velocities from the Automated Planet Finder, Keck I and SONG telescopes gave the orbital period, which led to the second transit being recovered (Dalba et al. 2020). A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement with HARPS-N found a projected obliquity of -2 +/- 6 degrees, so the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin (Knudstrup & Albrecht 2022). The orbit is circular (Knudstrup & Albrecht 2022).","system":"Transit timing shows a coherent pattern with a period of about 6.7 years and an amplitude of about 45 minutes, which could be caused by an unseen additional planet (Maciejewski 2026).","history":"The aligned, circular orbit at an 18.7-day period is useful for tracing formation and migration, because tides at that distance are weaker than for planets on orbits shorter than about 10 days (Knudstrup & Albrecht 2022).","open":"Many orbital configurations reproduce the timing pattern, so the properties of any companion are not known (Maciejewski 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd332231b-Dalbaetal2020","cite":"Dalba et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. A. Dalba, A. F. Gupta, J. E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: The TESS-Keck Survey. I. HD332231 Radial Velocities (Dalba+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51590241","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd332231b-KnudstrupAlbrecht2022","cite":"Knudstrup & Albrecht 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. Knudstrup, S. H. Albrecht","title":"Orbital alignment of HD 332231 b. The warm Saturn HD 332231 b/TOI-1456 b travels on a well-aligned, circular orbit around a bright F8 dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.14968","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd332231b-Maciejewski2026","cite":"Maciejewski 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Gracjan Maciejewski","title":"Detection of periodic transit timing variations in the warm sub-Saturn planet HD 332231 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.10497","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-59 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, HATSouth, had never before found a multi-planet system (Sarkis et al. 2018).","observed":"Radial velocities revealed a second, much more massive planet beyond the hot Jupiter, a rare companion for a star with a hot Jupiter (Sarkis et al. 2018). The inner planet moves on a slightly eccentric orbit with e = 0.129 and has a density of 0.70 g/cm3 (Sarkis et al. 2018).","system":"HATS-59 c has a minimum mass of 12.70 Jupiter masses on a circular orbit of 1422 days (Sarkis et al. 2018).","history":"The architecture of the system is valuable for understanding how planets migrate inward (Sarkis et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats59b-Sarkisetal2018","cite":"Sarkis et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"P. Sarkis, Th. Henning, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-59b,c: A Transiting Hot Jupiter and a Cold Massive Giant Planet around a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.05925","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 33564 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Infrared interferometry gave a directly measured radius and temperature for the star, and the planet spends at least part of its orbit in the habitable zone (von Braun et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd33564b-vonBraunetal2014","cite":"von Braun et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Gerard T. van Belle et al.","title":"Stellar diameters and temperatures - V. 11 newly characterized exoplanet host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1792","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DE CVn b":{"text":{"why":"The signal appears in the timing of eclipses of the host binary, not in the light of the planet or a direct wobble of the star (Han et al. 2018).","observed":"The host is an eclipsing pair of an M3 red dwarf and a white dwarf of about 8000 K, orbiting each other every 8.7 hours (van den Besselaar et al. 2007). Eclipse times show a cyclic change with an amplitude of 28.08 seconds and a period of 11.22 years (Han et al. 2018). Gravitational pull from a third body best explains it, because a magnetic cycle in the red dwarf cannot produce such a large change (Han et al. 2018). The third body has a minimum mass of 0.011 solar masses (Han et al. 2018).","system":"The binary's orbit is shrinking faster than gravitational radiation and magnetic braking can explain, and a circumbinary disk of a few times 10^-4 to 10^-3 solar masses may be responsible (Han et al. 2018). The red dwarf has flares (Brecklin et al. 2012).","history":"The white dwarf's progenitor was a low-mass star that went through a common-envelope phase, in which the stars shared an outer gas layer and drew closer together (van den Besselaar et al. 2007).","open":"Whether the third body is a planet or a brown dwarf, and whether it exists, is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-decvnb-Hanetal2018","cite":"Han et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Z.-T. Han, S.-B. Qian, L.-Y. Zhu et al.","title":"DE CVn: An Eclipsing Post-common Envelope Binary with a Circumbinary Disk and a Giant Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08625","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-decvnb-Besselaaretal2007","cite":"Besselaar et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"E. J. M. van den Besselaar, R. Greimel, L. Morales-Rueda et al.","title":"DE CVn: A Bright, Eclipsing Red Dwarf -- White Dwarf Binary","journal":"Binary Stars as Critical Tools & Tests in Contemporary Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921307003882","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-decvnb-Brecklinetal2012","cite":"Brecklin et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Caitlin Brecklin, V. Strelnitski, G. Walker et al.","title":"On the Nature of Continuum and Hα Emission Variations of V471 Tau-type Stars","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #219","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 27894 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the HARPS spectrograph show a planet with a minimum mass of 0.62 Jupiter masses and a period of 17.991 days, at 0.122 AU from its star (Moutou et al. 2005). The data were checked for activity-induced effects, and the variations were judged to come from a planet (Moutou et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"The planet adds to evidence that metal-rich stars are more likely to host planets, especially close-in ones, which points to formation and migration of hot Jupiters (Moutou et al. 2005).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd27894b-Moutouetal2005","cite":"Moutou et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Mayor, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IV. Three close-in planets around HD 2638, HD 27894 and HD 63454","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20052826","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 125595 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the HARPS spectrograph at La Silla show a planet of about 0.77 Neptune masses on a circular orbit of 9.67 days (Ségransan et al. 2011). The host is a K4 dwarf with moderate magnetic activity (Ségransan et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd125595b-Sgransanetal2011","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. Ségransan, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIX. Four new planets in orbit around the moderatly active dwarfs <ASTROBJ>HD 63765</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 104067</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 125595</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HIP 70849</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.0339","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 82943 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is one of only seven, counting the Sun, known to host multiple planets and a debris disk, a belt of dust made by colliding rocky bodies (Moro-Martin et al. 2010). Two Spitzer infrared spectra, modelled together with the dust's energy distribution, were used to constrain where the dust-producing bodies orbit (Moro-Martin et al. 2010). Simulations show the two planets undergo apsidal alignment or antialignment, in which the long axes of their orbits point in the same or opposite directions (Ji et al. 2003).","system":"The planets' gravity limits where stable planetesimal belts can lie (Moro-Martin et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd82943b-MoroMartinetal2010","cite":"Moro-Martin et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Amaya Moro-Martín, Renu Malhotra, Geoffrey Bryden et al.","title":"Locating Planetesimal Belts in the Multiple-planet Systems HD 128311, HD 202206, HD 82943, and HR 8799","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd82943b-Jietal2003","cite":"Ji et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Jianghui Ji, Lin Liu, Hiroshi Kinoshita et al.","title":"The Librating Companions in HD 37124, HD 12661, HD 82943, 47 Ursa Majoris, and GJ 876: Alignment or Antialignment?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0305448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-2010L b":{"text":{"why":"The microlensing signal was only partly covered by observations, so its nature needed careful testing (Han et al. 2023).","observed":"The light curve shows a short central anomaly. Tests of binary-lens and binary-source models found that only a planetary lens explains it, with a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 2.8 x 10^-3 (Han et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host star is poorly known, so the planet's own mass and distance are not well pinned down."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg2010lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Weicheng Zang et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-2010Lb, KMT-2022-BLG-0371Lb, and KMT-2022-BLG-1013Lb: Three microlensing planets detected via partially covered signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03871","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-700 c":{"text":{"why":"The outer planet TOI-700 d was the first Earth-sized habitable-zone planet found by TESS, and the larger sub-Neptune c is an excellent target for JWST (Gilbert et al. 2020).","observed":"Radial velocities from the ESPRESSO spectrograph indicate that the planet is warm and low in mass, and carries a hydrogen-helium atmosphere (Gilbert et al. 2024). The star's irradiation did not completely remove its primordial atmosphere (Gilbert et al. 2024).","system":"TOI-700 is an inactive M dwarf hosting four small planets (Gilbert et al. 2024). The system includes two Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone, d and e (Gilbert et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether rocky planets around low-mass M dwarfs can keep atmospheres is an open question, because frequent flares may erode them (Gilbert et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi700c-Gilbertetal2020","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Emily A. Gilbert, Thomas Barclay, Joshua E. Schlieder et al.","title":"The First Habitable-zone Earth-sized Planet from TESS. I. Validation of the TOI-700 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.00952","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi700c-Gilbertetal2024","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Emily A. Gilbert, Andrew Vanderburg, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"A Second Earth-sized Planet in the Habitable Zone of the M Dwarf, TOI-700","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03617","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi700c-Gilbertetal2023","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Emily A. Gilbert, Andrew Vanderburg, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"A Second Earth-sized Planet in the Habitable Zone of the M Dwarf, TOI-700","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03617","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-406 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star that hosts this planet was studied as part of a project on how a star's metal content may shape the planets that form around it. The team took Keck/HIRES spectra of Kepler-406 and five other Kepler host stars and measured the amounts of 17 elements in each (Vaz et al. 2015). Earlier work had suggested that such abundance patterns might point to stars with planets, especially rocky ones (Vaz et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler406c-Vazetal2015","cite":"Vaz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Zachary A. Vaz, Simon C. Schuler, Orlando J. Katime Santrich et al.","title":"Metallicity Analysis of Kepler-65, Kepler-93, Kepler-99, Kepler-102, Kepler-406, and Kepler-409","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-12 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper notes that this is the least irradiated planet among the group of super-inflated ones, which matters for the physics of why giant planets swell (Fortney et al. 2011).","observed":"Kepler-12 b is one of a handful of planets with radii above 1.65 Jupiter radii, and its density is very low, about 0.11 g per cubic centimetre (Fortney et al. 2011). The data favour an atmosphere with no temperature inversion on the dayside, though this conclusion is preliminary (Fortney et al. 2011). The planet is fairly dark, reflecting only a small share of light (Fortney et al. 2011). Its brightest spot is shifted west of the point facing the star, which reflective clouds on the western side could explain (Shporer & Hu 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":"Which materials absorb light at visible wavelengths in hot Jupiter atmospheres is not well known, so the temperature structure remains uncertain (Fortney et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler12b-Fortneyetal2011","cite":"Fortney et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jonathan J. Fortney, Brice-Olivier Demory, Jean-Michel Désert et al.","title":"Discovery and Atmospheric Characterization of Giant Planet Kepler-12b: An Inflated Radius Outlier","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.1611","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler12b-ShporerHu2015","cite":"Shporer & Hu 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Avi Shporer, Renyu Hu","title":"Studying Atmosphere-dominated Hot Jupiter Kepler Phase Curves: Evidence that Inhomogeneous Atmospheric Reflection Is Common","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.00498","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-79 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-79 d is a super-puff: a planet with a few Earth masses of material but a radius like a giant planet's (Chachan et al. 2020). Its transits show timing shifts caused by the pull of its neighbours, and these gave tight limits on the masses of all four planets (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Planet d has the lowest bulk density found so far among planets below Saturn's mass (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Hubble spectra between 1.1 and 1.7 microns show no molecular features, which points to hazes or clouds in the atmosphere (Chachan et al. 2020).","system":"The four planets have periods near a 1:2:4:6 chain, and each one's timing shifts match its distance from resonance with its neighbours (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","history":"A system of planets this close to their star probably did not form where it is. A model study suggests the planets migrated inward and stopped near the inner edge of the gas disc (Wang et al. 2012). The large envelope may have been gathered beyond the water snow line (Chachan et al. 2020).","open":"Whether the planet is losing its envelope is still debated. Photochemical hazes could both flatten the spectrum and lower the inferred mass-loss rate (Chachan et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler79d-Chachanetal2020","cite":"Chachan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Yayaati Chachan, Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"A Featureless Infrared Transmission Spectrum for the Super-puff Planet Kepler-79d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.05480","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler79d-JontofHutteretal2014","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jack J. Lissauer, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-79's Low Density Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2642","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler79d-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Su Wang, Jianghui Ji, Ji-Lin Zhou","title":"Predicting the Configuration of a Planetary System: KOI-152 Observed by Kepler","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2431","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4492 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that confirmed it targeted hot Jupiter candidates that follow-up teams had passed over, because most ground-based effort goes to low-mass candidates (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-4492 was one of seven stars with hot Jupiter candidates that were monitored with the CAFE2 spectrograph at Calar Alto Observatory (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). The team measured the star's radial velocity, meaning the small back-and-forth motion caused by an orbiting body, and modelled it together with the TESS brightness dips (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). This gave a mass and confirmed that the transiting object is a planet (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). Five planets were confirmed in this study, with one false positive (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4492b-LilloBoxetal2026","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, C. Cifuentes, O. Balsalobre-Ruza et al.","title":"CAFE follow-up of TESS hot Jupiter candidates left behind: I. Five newly confirmed planets and a false positive","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19756","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 109749 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in the N2K survey, which picked metal-rich stars because the share of stars with planets rises with metal content (Fischer et al. 2006).","observed":"The host is a G3 subgiant star that shows little magnetic activity and is rich in iron compared with the Sun (Fischer et al. 2006). Its radial velocities fit a 5.24 day orbit with a signal of 28.7 m/s (Fischer et al. 2006). The star was later found to have a stellar companion, making it one of the planet hosts that belong to a binary (Desidera & Barbieri 2007). A chemical comparison of the two stars was also made (Carande 2013).","system":"The host belongs to a binary system (Desidera & Barbieri 2007). Across such systems, the main clear difference from single stars is in the masses of short-period planets (Desidera & Barbieri 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd109749b-Fischeretal2006","cite":"Fischer et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Gregory Laughlin, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The N2K Consortium. III. Short-Period Planets Orbiting HD 149143 and HD 109749","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/498557","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd109749b-DesideraBarbieri2007","cite":"Desidera & Barbieri 2007","year":"2007","authors":"S. Desidera, M. Barbieri","title":"Properties of planets in binary systems. The role of binary separation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0610623","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd109749b-Carande2013","cite":"Carande 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Bryce Carande","title":"The Chemical Composition of Exoplanet-hosting Binary Star Systems","journal":"Masters Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2016-BLG-526L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was missed in the original by-eye search of the data. It surfaced when a semi-automatic algorithm combed the KMTNet archive for hidden anomalies (Shin et al. 2024).","observed":"The microlensing event came from the 2016 observing season of the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network (Shin et al. 2024). It is one of four new planets found in the survey's lower-priority fields, and the study describes such planets as typical of microlensing: giant planets orbiting M dwarfs beyond their snow lines (Shin et al. 2024). Together with an earlier paper in the series, the search recovered nine hidden planets, about half as many as the by-eye search turned up (Shin et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2016blg526lb-Shinetal2024","cite":"Shin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"I. Shin, J. Yee, W. Zang et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. XI. Complete Sample of 2016 Subprime Field Planets","journal":"Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04256","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1232 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1232 hosts a warm Jupiter-mass planet, TOI-1232 b, found in TESS data and confirmed by ground-based transit follow-up (Mihaylov et al. 2026). The TESS light curve is complex, because a background eclipsing binary with a 1.37 day period contaminates it (Mihaylov et al. 2026). The transits of planet b arrive earlier or later than a steady clock would predict, and this timing variation, together with Doppler spectroscopy, revealed a second massive planet (Mihaylov et al. 2026).","system":"The study describes a warm, massive pair of planets around a G dwarf with slightly more metals than the Sun (Mihaylov et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1232c-Mihaylovetal2026","cite":"Mihaylov et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Deyan P. Mihaylov, Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"A Warm Massive Pair of Planets around TOI-1232 Revealed with Transit-timing Variations and Doppler Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18395","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-139 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A 2025 study of the Kepler-139 system found a further planet that does not transit, Kepler-139f, with a period of about 355 days and a mass of about 36 Earth masses (Lammers & Winn 2025). Kepler-139 c sits among the transiting planets, interior to this new planet and to an outer giant (Lammers & Winn 2025).","system":"The system has three transiting planets, then nontransiting planet f, then an outer giant (Lammers & Winn 2025). Simulations show that the giant's pull on f makes it less likely that a distant observer would see transits of all four inner planets (Lammers & Winn 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler139c-LammersWinn2025","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Discovery and Dynamics of the Nontransiting Planet Kepler-139f","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13160","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GU Psc b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looked for companions at very large separations (over 100 au) around young low-mass stars, a region earlier imaging surveys left mostly unstudied (Naud et al. 2017).","observed":"GU Psc b is a T3.5 object, cool enough for methane-rich atmospheres, with a temperature of 1000-1100 K and signs of low gravity that point to youth (Naud et al. 2014). Its mass is 9-13 Jupiter masses, based on the age of the AB Doradus Moving Group (Naud et al. 2014). Hubble spectra show its brightness changes by 2.7 percent over a rotation of at least 8 hours, and the changes are nearly the same at all wavelengths, which suggests patchy clouds (Lew et al. 2020). Ground-based work saw a 4 percent change on a timescale of about 6 hours on one night out of three (Naud et al. 2017). A radio search found no emission (Cendes et al. 2022).","system":"The companion lies about 42 arcseconds from its M3 host, roughly 2000 au away (Naud et al. 2014). The host is a young star in the AB Doradus Moving Group (Naud et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the cloud patches behave like those of brown dwarfs is still being tested, and more measurements are called for (Naud et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gupscb-Naudetal2017","cite":"Naud et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Marie-Eve Naud, Étienne Artigau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"A Search for Photometric Variability in the Young T3.5 Planetary-mass Companion GU Psc b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.01714","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gupscb-Naudetal2014","cite":"Naud et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Marie-Eve Naud, Étienne Artigau, Lison Malo et al.","title":"Discovery of a Wide Planetary-mass Companion to the Young M3 Star GU Psc","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.2932","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gupscb-Lewetal2020","cite":"Lew et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ben W. P. Lew, Dániel Apai, Yifan Zhou et al.","title":"Cloud Atlas: Weak Color Modulations Due to Rotation in the Planetary-mass Companion GU Psc b and 11 Other Brown Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02812","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gupscb-Cendesetal2022","cite":"Cendes et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Y. Cendes, P. K. G. Williams, E. Berger","title":"A Pilot Radio Search for Magnetic Activity in Directly Imaged Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14994","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1689L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was caught by HighMagFinder, a new system that automatically alerts observers to high-magnification events so follow-up can be organised (Yang et al. 2022).","observed":"The signal comes from a high-magnification microlensing event, with follow-up telescopes adding coverage (Yang et al. 2022). The light curve allows two solutions, a central-caustic and a resonant-caustic one, which give different planet-to-host mass ratios of about 2.5 and 1.8 times ten to the minus four (Yang et al. 2022). Four such degenerate cases appeared in the 2021 season alone, so earlier studies may have missed some (Yang et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"The two solutions cannot be separated by the data alone. The authors propose a quantitative factor to weigh them (Yang et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1689lb-Yangetal2022","cite":"Yang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Hongjing Yang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-0171Lb and KMT-2021-BLG-1689Lb: two microlensing planets in the KMTNet high-cadence fields with followup observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12584","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-69 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-69 b was one of ten planets reported in a single paper from the HATSouth survey (Hartman et al. 2019). It is the Saturn of the group, with a radius of 0.94 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2019). Its host star, with 0.89 solar masses, is the lowest-mass star among the ten hosts (Hartman et al. 2019). Follow-up included spectra from several telescopes and ground-based transit photometry (Hartman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats69b-Hartmanetal2019","cite":"Hartman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-60b-HATS-69b: 10 Transiting Planets from HATSouth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WD 0806-661 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion was spotted in Spitzer images because it moves across the sky together with the white dwarf (Luhman et al. 2011). It is extremely cold, about 300-345 K (Luhman et al. 2011). It was detected at 3.6 and 4.5 microns, but not in the near-infrared from the ground (Luhman et al. 2011). Hubble later made the first near-infrared detection (Luhman et al. 2014). It is the coldest companion imaged outside the solar system and a contender for the coldest brown dwarf (Luhman et al. 2011). It lies about 130 arcseconds away, or 2500 au, making it the widest known definite planetary-mass companion (Meisner et al. 2018).","system":"The primary is a white dwarf 19.2 pc away with an age of about 1.5 Gyr (Luhman et al. 2011). Its progenitor star probably had about 2 solar masses (Luhman et al. 2011).","history":"It may be a brown dwarf formed like a binary star, or a giant planet born in a disc and scattered outward (Luhman et al. 2011). Its mass fits formation by fragmentation of a cloud (Rodriguez et al. 2011).","open":"Its nature is unsettled, and spectroscopy would test models better but needs about ten Hubble orbits (Luhman et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wd0806661b-Luhmanetal2011","cite":"Luhman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"K. L. Luhman, A. J. Burgasser, J. J. Bochanski","title":"Discovery of a Candidate for the Coolest Known Brown Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.5411","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd0806661b-Luhmanetal2014","cite":"Luhman et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"K. L. Luhman, C. V. Morley, A. J. Burgasser et al.","title":"Near-infrared Detection of WD 0806-661 B with the Hubble Space Telescope","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.5897","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd0806661b-Meisneretal2018","cite":"Meisner et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. Meisner, M. Cushing, R. Cutri et al.","title":"A Secure W2 Detection of WD 0806-661B from CatWISE","journal":"Research Notes of the AAS","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/AAD7C0","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wd0806661b-Rodriguezetal2011","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"David R. Rodriguez, B. Zuckerman, Carl Melis et al.","title":"The Ultra Cool Brown Dwarf Companion of WD 0806-661B: Age, Mass, and Formation Mechanism","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.3544","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TIC 241249530 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet is described as a super-eccentric hot Jupiter progenitor: its orbit has an eccentricity of 0.94 and runs against the star's spin (Liveoak et al. 2026). It is the most eccentric transiting planet known so far (Marchis et al. 2025). Its long transit was confirmed through the Unistellar citizen-science network of amateur astronomers (Marchis et al. 2025). Its orbit is direct evidence that giant planets can reach close orbits by high-eccentricity migration (Millholland & Liveoak 2025).","system":"A distant binary star companion belongs to the system (Liveoak et al. 2026).","history":"The planet's properties can be explained by migration triggered by von Zeipel-Lidov-Kozai oscillations from the distant binary star, but only if the tidal heating inside the planet is of the steady equilibrium kind (Liveoak et al. 2026). Chaotic dynamical tides fail to explain the system (Liveoak et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tic241249530b-Liveoaketal2026","cite":"Liveoak et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Donald Liveoak, Sarah C. Millholland, Michelle Vick","title":"Case Study of a Super-eccentric Warm Jupiter Migrating via Equilibrium and Dynamical Tides","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.16406","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tic241249530b-Marchisetal2025","cite":"Marchis et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Franck Marchis, Lauren Sgro, Thomas Esposito et al.","title":"Confirming Giant Planets: Collaborative Discoveries through the Unistellar & SETI Institute Citizen Science Network","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tic241249530b-MillhollandLiveoak2025","cite":"Millholland & Liveoak 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Sarah Millholland, Donald Liveoak","title":"Exploring Hot Jupiter Formation via Chaotic Tidal Migration","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5422 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-5422 b is a transiting brown dwarf, an object between planets and stars in mass (Zhang et al. 2026). It was reported together with a similar one, TOI-4776 b, and the pair sit in an underpopulated region of the brown dwarf mass and period plane (Zhang et al. 2026). The two have comparable masses but different ages, and TOI-5422 b is the older (Zhang et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5422b-Zhangetal2026","cite":"Zhang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Elina Yuchen Zhang, Theron W. Carmichael, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"An Oasis in the Brown Dwarf Desert: Confirmation of Two Low-mass Transiting Brown Dwarfs Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05115","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 41378 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HIP 41378 is a bright star with five transiting planets, and planet e was long poorly constrained in period and mass (Grouffal et al. 2026). A decade of radial velocity and space photometry, spanning 2015 to 2024, refined its period to about 393 days (Grouffal et al. 2026). A transit of planet f arrived 1.5 hours earlier than predicted, and a timing model shows that interactions between e and f can explain it (Bryant et al. 2021).","system":"The system has planets with periods up to 1.5 years, including the ultra-low-density planet f (Grouffal et al. 2026). The outer planets showed only single transits at first, so their periods were uncertain (Becker et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip41378e-Grouffaletal2026","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, X. Dumusque et al.","title":"A decade of monitoring the HIP 41378 planetary system: Masses and orbital periods of six planets and a planet candidate","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202659666","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378e-Bryantetal2021","cite":"Bryant et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss, Alexandre Santerne et al.","title":"A transit timing variation observed for the long-period extremely low-density exoplanet HIP 41378 f","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03159","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378e-Beckeretal2019","cite":"Becker et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Juliette C. Becker, Andrew Vanderburg, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"A Discrete Set of Possible Transit Ephemerides for Two Long-period Gas Giants Orbiting HIP 41378","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.10688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 178911 B b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper compared lithium in the system's stars with 16 Cyg, another planet-hosting wide binary (Zucker et al. 2002).","observed":"The planet orbits the distant component B of a stellar triple system, and its radial-velocity signal was first seen with Keck, then followed up with ELODIE (Zucker et al. 2002). In both this system and 16 Cyg, lithium is undetected in the star that hosts the planet but easy to see in its visual companion (Zucker et al. 2002). The system is one of at least three exoplanet hosts known to lie in triple-star systems (Raghavan et al. 2006).","system":"The triple lies in a wide, hierarchical arrangement (Boss 2003).","history":"Tidal forces from a binary companion may help trigger clumps in a marginally unstable disc, which is one way giant planets might form quickly (Boss 2003).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd178911bb-Zuckeretal2002","cite":"Zucker et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Zucker, D. Naef, D. W. Latham et al.","title":"A Planet Candidate in the Stellar Triple System HD 178911","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0111550","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd178911bb-Raghavanetal2006","cite":"Raghavan et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Deepak Raghavan, Todd J. Henry, Brian D. Mason et al.","title":"Two Suns in The Sky: Stellar Multiplicity in Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0603836","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd178911bb-Boss2003","cite":"Boss 2003","year":"2003","authors":"A. P. Boss","title":"Rapid Formation of Giant Planets in Binary Star Systems","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #35","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1518 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1518 b is an ultra-hot Jupiter with an inflated radius, and its transit is nearly grazing across the edge of a fast-rotating F-type star (Cabot et al. 2021). Its orbit is strongly misaligned with the star's spin (Cabot et al. 2021). Transmission spectra reveal neutral iron (Cabot et al. 2021), and later work found 14 species including iron, with VO seen only with a new line list and TiO not detected (Simonnin et al. 2025). The time-resolved absorption indicates strong drag slowing the winds (Simonnin et al. 2025). The transit path across the star has shifted slowly, as expected from nodal precession of the orbit (Watanabe et al. 2024). Emission spectra showed iron and titanium (Kamen et al. 2026).","system":"The host rotates at about 85 km/s, fast enough to reshape the transit (Watanabe et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1518b-Cabotetal2021","cite":"Cabot et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Samuel H. C. Cabot, Aaron Bello-Arufe, João M. Mendonça et al.","title":"TOI-1518b: A Misaligned Ultra-hot Jupiter with Iron in Its Atmosphere","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.11403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1518b-Simonninetal2025","cite":"Simonnin et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Simonnin, V. Parmentier, J. P. Wardenier et al.","title":"Time-resolved absorption of six chemical species with MAROON-X points to a strong drag in the ultra-hot Jupiter TOI-1518 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.01472","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1518b-Watanabeetal2024","cite":"Watanabe et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Noriharu Watanabe, Norio Narita, Yasunori Hori","title":"Nodal precession of a hot Jupiter transiting the edge of a late A-type star TOI-1518","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17325","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1518b-Kamenetal2026","cite":"Kamen et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jake Kamen, Marshall Johnson, Victoria Bonidie et al.","title":"Chemistry and dynamics from high-resolution emission spectra of the ultra-hot Jupiters KELT-20b and TOI-1518b from the PIRANGA project","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2141 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from HARPS-N revealed two companions that do not transit, at 5.46 and 60.45 days (Luque et al. 2025). Planet d is the outer one, at 60.45 days (Luque et al. 2025). The transiting planet b has a radius of 3.15 Earth radii and a mass of 20.1 Earth masses (Luque et al. 2025). Despite a high transit probability for the innermost planet, no transits of it were seen (Luque et al. 2025).","system":"The system has a dense sub-Neptune with a volatile envelope on an Earth-like core, plus non-transiting inner and outer planets (Luque et al. 2025). Its envelope is hydrogen-rich and may have lost a little mass to photoevaporation (Luque et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2141d-Luqueetal2025","cite":"Luque et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"R. Luque, K. W. F. Lam, J. Cabrera et al.","title":"Improved characterization of the TOI-2141 system: A dense sub-Neptune with non-transiting inner and outer companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00762","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2013-BLG-1721L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event was too short and faint to measure a reliable parallax, so the mass is a statistical estimate (Mróz et al. 2017). It is a Saturn-mass planet orbiting an M dwarf, one of a growing group of sub-Jupiter-mass planets beyond the snow line of such stars (Mróz et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The lens mass was not measured, so the planet's mass remains a Bayesian estimate (Mróz et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2013blg1721lb-Mrzetal2017","cite":"Mróz et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"P. Mróz, A. Udalski, I. Bond et al.","title":"OGLE-2013-BLG-0132Lb and OGLE-2013-BLG-1721Lb: Two Saturn-mass Planets Discovered around M-dwarfs","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01058","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102888 b":{"text":{"why":"The search that produced it, CASCADES, began in 2006 to learn how planet populations around intermediate-mass stars look once those stars have evolved off the main sequence (Fontanet et al. 2025).","observed":"The CORALIE spectrograph on the Euler telescope in Chile tracked the star's radial velocity for more than ten years (Fontanet et al. 2025). The team searched for periodic signals and fitted orbits to them (Fontanet et al. 2025). The host is an evolved giant star of 1.5-5 solar masses, a kind of star around which only about 200 planets were known (Fontanet et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102888b-Fontanetetal2025","cite":"Fontanet et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Fontanet, S. Udry, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES): IV. New planetary systems around HD 87816, HD 94890, and HD 102888 and an update on HD 121056","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-28 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transit timing over three years from 15 transits showed no timing variations that could point to another planet-sized body (Petrucci et al. 2015). The orbit is nearly aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of 8 degrees give or take 18 (Anderson et al. 2015). The planet is an inflated Jupiter-mass world around an aged F8 star about 5 Gyr old (Anderson et al. 2015). The host is a thin-disc star (Petrucci et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"The aligned orbit fits the idea that close-in giant planets are scattered onto eccentric orbits and later circularised and aligned by tidal forces (Anderson et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp28b-Petruccietal2015","cite":"Petrucci et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"R. Petrucci, E. Jofré, M. Melita et al.","title":"Transit timing variation analysis in southern stars: the case of WASP-28","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stu2152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp28b-Andersonetal2015","cite":"Anderson et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-20b and WASP-28b: a hot Saturn and a hot Jupiter in near-aligned orbits around solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1482","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":50,"about":20,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-198 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A study of TESS and CHEOPS data showed that the planet is real (Oddo et al. 2023). With 87 ESPRESSO radial velocities, a mass of 3.17 Earth masses and radius of 1.36 Earth radii were obtained (Zapatero Osorio et al. 2026). The planet is dense and rocky, within the Earth-like region of the mass-radius diagram (Zapatero Osorio et al. 2026). It orbits inside the inner edge of its star's habitable zone (Zapatero Osorio et al. 2026). The eccentricity is limited to 0.15 or less (Zapatero Osorio et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a nearby M0 dwarf, also known as GJ 7 (Zapatero Osorio et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi198b-Oddoetal2023","cite":"Oddo et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Dominic Oddo, Diana Dragomir, Alexis Brandeker et al.","title":"Characterization of a Set of Small Planets with TESS and CHEOPS and an Analysis of Photometric Performance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08162","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi198b-Osorioetal2026","cite":"Osorio et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. R. Zapatero Osorio, H. Tabernero, A. Suárez Mascareño et al.","title":"A super-Earth orbiting the early-M dwarf GJ7","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202449545","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 184010 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 184010 hosts three giant planets with minimum masses of 0.30 to 0.45 Jupiter masses, found through radial velocity monitoring at Okayama Astrophysical Observatory between 2004 and 2021 (Teng et al. 2022). The star is a K0 star at the beginning of the red giant branch, with a radius of 4.86 solar radii (Teng et al. 2022).","system":"The orbital periods are about 287, 484 and 836 days, close to a 7:12:21 ratio, so both neighbouring period ratios are below 2:1 (Teng et al. 2022). The planets are in a compact arrangement (Teng et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd184010c-Tengetal2022","cite":"Teng et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Takuya Takarada et al.","title":"A trio of giant planets orbiting evolved star HD 184010","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09426","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 37605 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The outer planet has a period of about 7.5 years, a low eccentricity and a minimum mass of about 3.4 Jupiter masses (Wang et al. 2012). It came from almost eight years of radial velocity follow-up at the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and Keck, including data taken to provide precise transit times for long-period planets (Wang et al. 2012). The star is an old, inactive K dwarf (Wang et al. 2012).","system":"The inner planet b has a 55-day orbit with a high eccentricity of 0.737, and it does not transit (Cochran et al. 2004; Wang et al. 2012). The two planets interact only very little (Wang et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd37605c-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Xuesong, Sharon Wang, Jason T. Wright, William Cochran et al.","title":"The Discovery of HD 37605c and a Dispositive Null Detection of Transits of HD 37605b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.6985","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd37605c-Cochranetal2004","cite":"Cochran et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"William D. Cochran, Michael Endl, Barbara McArthur et al.","title":"The First Hobby-Eberly Telescope Planet: A Companion to HD 37605","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0407146","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 10180 f":{"text":{"why":"The HARPS survey behind it aimed to characterise the ice giants and super-Earths around nearby Sun-like stars (Lovis et al. 2011).","observed":"Planet f is one of at least five Neptune-like planets, with minimum masses of 12 to 25 Earth masses, orbiting between 0.06 and 1.4 au (Lovis et al. 2011). The system was then the most populated exoplanet system known (Lovis et al. 2011). Because the planets do not transit, their inclination is unknown, which leaves their true masses uncertain (Kane & Gelino 2014).","system":"The planets are in a dense but well-separated arrangement (Lovis et al. 2011). A sixth signal at longer period probably comes from a 65 Earth mass object, and another body of about 1.4 Earth masses may lie at 0.02 au (Lovis et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"A reanalysis found evidence for up to nine planets, which would exceed the solar system's count (Tuomi 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd10180f-Lovisetal2011","cite":"Lovis et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Lovis, D. Ségransan, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXVIII. Up to seven planets orbiting HD 10180: probing the architecture of low-mass planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.4994","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10180f-KaneGelino2014","cite":"Kane & Gelino 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Dawn M. Gelino","title":"On the Inclination and Habitability of the HD 10180 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.4150","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10180f-Tuomi2012","cite":"Tuomi 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Tuomi","title":"Evidence for nine planets in the HD 10180 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1254","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":13,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 174205 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the find looks for planets around stars near the northern celestial pole, using a Korean observatory (Jeong et al. 2022).","observed":"The signal is a slow wobble in the star's velocity, with a period of 582 days and a minimum planet mass of 4.2 Jupiter masses (Jeong et al. 2022). The team checked the star's brightness and the shapes of its spectral lines. Neither varied with the same period, so starspots or star pulsations are unlikely causes and a planet is the most likely explanation (Jeong et al. 2022). The authors call the detection likely rather than certain.","system":"The host is a giant star of intermediate mass (Jeong et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The authors note that some unclear interpretations and recent studies may call some planet claims around giant stars into question (Jeong et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd174205b-Jeongetal2022","cite":"Jeong et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Jeong, B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park et al.","title":"Search for exoplanets around northern circumpolar stars. V. Three likely planetary companions to the giant stars HD 19615, HD 150010, and HD 174205","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 202206 c":{"text":{"why":"Its host also hosts a much more massive inner companion, which makes the pair unusual (Correia et al. 2005).","observed":"Long-term Doppler measurements show two companions with minimum masses of 17.4 and 2.44 Jupiter masses and orbits of 0.83 and 2.55 au (Correia et al. 2005). Hubble astrometry later showed the system is nearly face-on. The inner companion is a star of 0.089 solar masses, so the host is really a close pair of stars. The outer body then has a true mass of about 17.9 Jupiter masses, which makes it a brown dwarf (Benedict & Harrison 2017). The two orbits are close to coplanar, with a mutual tilt of about 6 degrees (Benedict & Harrison 2017).","system":"The two companions sit in a 5:1 orbital resonance, and stability studies say the system survives only in or near that resonance (Correia et al. 2005; Benedict & Harrison 2017). Infrared data show the star also has a dusty disc of planetesimals, and the study sets limits on where that belt lies (Moro-Martín et al. 2010).","history":"Either the inner companion formed in the planet-forming disc as a very massive planet, or the outer one formed in a disc around the pair (Correia et al. 2005). The outer companion was first noticed as a drift in the velocities (Udry et al. 2002).","open":"Dynamical work showed that the range of tilts to our line of sight allowed by stable orbits limits the masses of both companions (Couetdic et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd202206c-Correiaetal2005","cite":"Correia et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"A. C. M. Correia, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XIII. A pair of planets around HD 202206 or a circumbinary planet?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0411512","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206c-BenedictHarrison2017","cite":"Benedict & Harrison 2017","year":"2017","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, Thomas E. Harrison","title":"HD 202206: A Circumbinary Brown Dwarf System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.00659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206c-MoroMartnetal2010","cite":"Moro-Martín et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Amaya Moro-Martín, Renu Malhotra, Geoffrey Bryden et al.","title":"Locating Planetesimal Belts in the Multiple-planet Systems HD 128311, HD 202206, HD 82943, and HR 8799","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206c-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206c-Couetdicetal2010","cite":"Couetdic et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"J. Couetdic, J. Laskar, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"Dynamical stability analysis of the HD 202206 system and constraints to the planetary orbits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0911.1963","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 179949 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit, but it has still been studied in detail (Tinney et al. 2001). Infrared spectra taken at the Very Large Telescope while the planet was near the far side of its orbit revealed carbon monoxide and water vapour in its atmosphere (Brogi et al. 2014). A later method called Doppler tomography confirmed both molecules, added a new water detection at 2.1 micrometres, and found a tentative sign of carbon monoxide on the night side. This would be the first evidence of molecules in a night-side emission spectrum (Matthews et al. 2024).","system":"The planet orbits about 8 stellar radii from an F8 star, which has a weak magnetic field of a few gauss that is tilted like a dipole by about 70 degrees (Fares et al. 2012). The star turns faster at its equator (7.62 days) than at its poles (10.3 days) (Fares et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"Several teams looked for the planet heating the star's atmosphere in step with its orbit. The effect appeared in some seasons but not in all of them (Scandariato et al. 2013). A McDonald Observatory study again saw planet-linked changes in the star's calcium emission (Gurdemir et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Tinneyetal2001","cite":"Tinney et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"First Results from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search: A Brown Dwarf Candidate and a 51 Peg-like Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0012204","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Brogietal2014","cite":"Brogi et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Brogi, R. J. de Kok, J. L. Birkby et al.","title":"Carbon monoxide and water vapor in the atmosphere of the non-transiting exoplanet HD 179949 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.3769","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Matthewsetal2024","cite":"Matthews et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. M. Matthews, C. A. Watson, E. J. W. de Mooij et al.","title":"Doppler tomography as a tool for characterizing exoplanet atmospheres - II. An analysis of HD 179949 b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.05652","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Faresetal2012","cite":"Fares et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"R. Fares, J.-F. Donati, C. Moutou et al.","title":"Magnetic field, differential rotation and activity of the hot-Jupiter-hosting star HD 179949","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.4472","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Scandariatoetal2013","cite":"Scandariato et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Scandariato, A. Maggio, A. F. Lanza et al.","title":"A coordinated optical and X-ray spectroscopic campaign on HD 179949: searching for planet-induced chromospheric and coronal activity","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7748","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd179949b-Gurdemiretal2012","cite":"Gurdemir et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"L. Gurdemir, S. Redfield, M. Cuntz","title":"Planet-Induced Emission Enhancements in HD 179949: Results from McDonald Observations","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.3612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":70,"about":18,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-9 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey targets stars whose spectra show unusual activity signs, which fit matter streaming off close-in evaporating planets (Standing et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet is one of seven new planetary signals reported in five systems from this radial velocity survey. Its host star is HD 200133 (Standing et al. 2026). The survey used its detections and non-detections in 24 stars to test whether such activity signs really point to close-in planets (Standing et al. 2026). It finds that planets of 3 to 10 Earth masses with periods under 50 days are very common in the sample, at about 83 percent (Standing et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp9b-Standingetal2026","cite":"Standing et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, John R. Barnes, Carole A. Haswell et al.","title":"The dispersed matter planet project sample ─ detection limits, occurrence rates, and new planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18207","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot super-Neptune around a quiet K dwarf. The star shows no spots in the survey data, little velocity jitter and no sign of chromospheric activity (Bakos et al. 2015). It is the second smallest planet found by a wide-field ground-based transit survey, and one of only a handful of Neptune-size planets with mass and radius known to 10 percent (Bakos et al. 2015). Models give it a hydrogen-helium envelope of 9 to 18 percent of its mass, close to Uranus and Neptune and unlike Saturn at 75 percent (Bakos et al. 2015). Hubble also observed it in transmission as part of a study of giant planet atmospheres (Wilkins 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats7b-Bakosetal2015","cite":"Bakos et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, K. Penev, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-7b: A Hot Super Neptune Transiting a Quiet K Dwarf Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01024","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats7b-Wilkins2017","cite":"Wilkins 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ashlee Wilkins","title":"Atmospheric Characterization of Giant Exoplanets in Extreme Environments","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2427 b":{"text":{"why":"It was picked as a hot, possibly rocky planet that the James Webb Space Telescope could study through its thermal emission (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"It was one of 13 planets validated together using ground-based follow-up and two statistical vetting tools (Giacalone et al. 2022). A later intensive campaign with the HARPS spectrograph measured its mass at 5.69 Earth masses and radius at 1.64 Earth radii. That makes it a dense, short-period super-Earth (Schmerling et al. 2025).","system":"The velocity data also revealed a second planet that does not transit, with a period of about 5.15 days, close to four times the inner planet's period (Schmerling et al. 2025). The host is a late K dwarf (Schmerling et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2427b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2427b-Schmerlingetal2025","cite":"Schmerling et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"H. Schmerling, E. Goffo, S. Grziwa et al.","title":"The TOI-2427 system: Two close-in planets orbiting a late K-dwarf star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202452620","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 887 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A new campaign of 101 HARPS and 12 ESPRESSO velocity measurements was designed to settle whether a 50-day signal was real. It modelled the star's activity with Gaussian processes and confirmed a four-planet system (Hartogh et al. 2026). The 50.77-day planet is a super-Earth in the habitable zone, and the second closest such planet to us after Proxima Cen b (Hartogh et al. 2026). The signal had first been listed as unconfirmed (Jeffers et al. 2020).","system":"The other planets have periods of 4.4, 9.26 and 21.78 days (Hartogh et al. 2026). A further signal at 2.2 days lies in a 2:1 resonance with the 4.4-day planet and may be another planet (Hartogh et al. 2026). The star is a bright M dwarf whose brightness varies by under 500 parts per million, unusually quiet for a red dwarf (Jeffers et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The 2.2-day signal remains a candidate, and other explanations are possible (Hartogh et al. 2026). The star looks calm in visible light, but Hubble caught two strong ultraviolet flares in under three hours, which could erode planetary atmospheres (Parke Loyd et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj887d-Hartoghetal2026","cite":"Hartogh et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"C. Hartogh, S. V. Jeffers, S. Dreizler et al.","title":"RedDots: Multiplanet system around M dwarf GJ 887 in the solar neighborhood","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08929","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj887d-Jeffersetal2020","cite":"Jeffers et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. V. Jeffers, S. Dreizler, J. R. Barnes et al.","title":"A multiplanet system of super-Earths orbiting the brightest red dwarf star GJ 887","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16372","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj887d-Loydetal2020","cite":"Loyd et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. O. Parke Loyd, Evgenya L. Shkolnik, Kevin France et al.","title":"When \"Boring\" Stars Flare: The Ultraviolet Activity of GJ 887, a Bright M Star Hosting Newly Discovered Planets","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/aba94a","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2260 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A mass measurement with the HARPS-N spectrograph confirmed this ultra-short-period planet. With an equilibrium temperature above 2500 K, it is the hottest small planet with a measured density (Quinn & HARPS-N Collaboration 2023). Its host is a young G dwarf of 321 million years, and one of the most metal-rich stars known to host a planet this close in (Giacalone et al. 2022). The planet gives a view of early evolution in an extreme environment (Quinn & HARPS-N Collaboration 2023).","system":"The star is so large in the planet's sky that more than 70 percent of the planet receives its light (Carter 2026).","history":"","open":"It is a target of James Webb Space Telescope program 8864. Models of lava planets predict that most targets in that program tend toward bare rock, though some outcomes remain open (Sastre et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2260b-QuinnHARPSNCollaboration2023","cite":"Quinn & HARPS-N Collaboration 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Samuel Quinn, HARPS-N Collaboration","title":"A young, highly irradiated, small planet from TESS and HARPS-N","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2260b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2260b-Carter2026","cite":"Carter 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jennifer Carter","title":"Calculating Instellation of Exoplanets by Extended Sources using ExoHype","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2260b-Sastreetal2026","cite":"Sastre et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mariana Sastre, Tim Lichtenberg, Lisa Dang et al.","title":"Thermal Evolution of Lava Planets Across System Ages: Predictions for Hell of a Survey","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20155","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 98-59 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A velocity campaign with the ESPRESSO spectrograph, combined with HARPS and TESS data, characterised the planets of this system (Demangeon et al. 2021). Planet e does not transit. Only velocity measurements can reveal it, unlike the three inner planets, which show transits (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023).","system":"The M3 dwarf hosts four confirmed planets and a further candidate. Planets c and d are very close to a 2:1 orbital resonance (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). The three inner planets were observed with Hubble for atmospheric studies (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023).","history":"","open":"A candidate signal at about 23 days could be an artifact of stellar activity, since the star's rotation period is still uncertain at either 39.6 or 78 days (Schwarz et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l9859e-Demangeonetal2021","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: L 98-59 (TOI-175) ESPRESSO observations (Demangeon+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36530041","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859e-SchwarzDreizler2023","cite":"Schwarz & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler","title":"L98-59 (TOI-175) a case study for a multiple exoplanet system with small planets near resonance","journal":"EAS2023, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859e-Schwarzetal2022","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler, René Heller","title":"Disentangling the stellar activity and the radial velocity signatures of L 98-59 (TOI-175)","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1704 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it looks for transiting giant planets on wide orbits. Highly eccentric ones are useful for testing ideas about how giants move inward (Dalba et al. 2021).","observed":"This 4.15 Jupiter-mass planet is one of few eccentric giants that also transit. A decade of Keck velocity data gave its orbit and its bulk heavy-element content (Dalba et al. 2021). The orbit has an eccentricity of 0.921, taking it from 0.16 to 3.9 au from its star. The resulting difference in equilibrium temperature is over 700 K (Dalba et al. 2021). A simulated phase curve during its close approach shows it would make a remarkable target for further study (Dalba et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"It is called a failed hot Jupiter. Scattering events, possibly starting while it was still gathering gas, probably pushed it onto this orbit. Its closest approach was too far out for tides to make the orbit circular (Dalba et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1704b-Dalbaetal2021","cite":"Dalba et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Zhexing Li et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. II. Discovery of a Failed Hot Jupiter on a 2.7 Yr, Highly Eccentric Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.06901","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1062 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HARPS velocity measurements gave the planet a mass of 10.15 Earth masses and a mean density of 4.85 grams per cubic centimetre. Its interior structure allows at most a thin volatile envelope of 0.35 percent of its mass (Otegi et al. 2021). The star's brightness and the planet's position near what is known as the radius valley make it a good target for further study (Otegi et al. 2021).","system":"A second planet that does not transit orbits every 7.97 days, close to a 2:1 resonance with the inner planet (Otegi et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"A 2026 study of flares in TESS data singled out this planet's eccentric orbit (e = 0.18) as showing flares at closest approach to the star. They may be induced by the planet, but this is not certain (Whitsett & Daylan 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1062b-Otegietal2021","cite":"Otegi et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. F. Otegi, F. Bouchy, R. Helled et al.","title":"TESS and HARPS reveal two sub-Neptunes around TOI 1062","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.01945","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1062b-WhitsettDaylan2026","cite":"Whitsett & Daylan 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nathan Whitsett, Tansu Daylan","title":"Planet-induced Stellar Flare Candidates from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ae6446","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-118 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a moderately inflated hot Jupiter around a hot F6 star, and was observed by the K2 mission in campaign 8 (Hay et al. 2016). K2 data show no transit timing variations, so no other planet is pulling on it (Gajdoš et al. 2019). No secondary eclipse was detected either, which limits the planet's geometric albedo to under 0.2 (Močnik et al. 2017).","system":"The host star shows variability like that of gamma Doradus pulsators, with several periods and a semi-amplitude of about 200 parts per million (Močnik et al. 2017). High-contrast imaging found a new candidate companion near the star, not yet confirmed as bound (Bohn et al. 2020). Simulations mapped where another planet could survive in a stable orbit (Gajdoš et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp118b-Hayetal2016","cite":"Hay et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"K. L. Hay, A. Collier-Cameron, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"WASP-92b, WASP-93b and WASP-118b: three new transiting close-in giant planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00774","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp118b-Gajdoetal2019","cite":"Gajdoš et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Pavol Gajdoš, Martin Vaňko, Marián Jakubík et al.","title":"WASP-92, WASP-93, and WASP-118: transit timing variations and long-term stability of the systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02804","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp118b-Moniketal2017","cite":"Močnik et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"T. Močnik, C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Starspots on WASP-107 and pulsations of WASP-118","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.05078","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp118b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3837 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS light curves, ground-based photometry and spectroscopy confirmed the planet. It is a warm Jupiter of 0.59 Jupiter masses and 0.97 Jupiter radii (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). Its orbit has a moderate eccentricity of 0.221 (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). It was announced together with two other warm giants and a warm sub-Saturn (Tala Pinto et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3837b-Pintoetal2025","cite":"Pinto et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marcelo Tala Pinto, Andrés Jordán, Lorena Acuña et al.","title":"Three warm Jupiters orbiting TOI-6628, TOI-3837, and TOI-5027 and one sub-Saturn orbiting TOI-2328","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02069","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-62 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet is one of two super-Earth-size planets in the habitable zone of a K star, and it gets 1.2 times the sunlight Earth does. Models for a star about 7 billion years old suggest it could be solid and rocky, or made mostly of solid water (Borucki et al. 2013). If it is a water planet, models predict a distinctive spectral fingerprint in transit, unlike a rocky Earth-like world (Kaltenegger et al. 2013). Tidal models suggest its rotation could be fast, unlike the slow rotation of the four inner planets (Bolmont et al. 2015).","system":"Five planets orbit the star, with periods from 5.7 to 267 days (Borucki et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler62e-Boruckietal2013","cite":"Borucki et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"William J. Borucki, Eric Agol, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Kepler-62: A Five-Planet System with Planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth Radii in the Habitable Zone","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7387","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler62e-Kalteneggeretal2013","cite":"Kaltenegger et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"L. Kaltenegger, D. Sasselov, S. Rugheimer","title":"Water-planets in the Habitable Zone: Atmospheric Chemistry, Observable Features, and the Case of Kepler-62e and -62f","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.5058","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler62e-Bolmontetal2015","cite":"Bolmont et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Emeline Bolmont, Sean N. Raymond, Jeremy Leconte et al.","title":"Mercury-T: A new code to study tidally evolving multi-planet systems. Applications to Kepler-62","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.04751","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":14,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 207832 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck radial velocities show two Jupiter-like planets in Keplerian orbits around this G star (Haghighipour et al. 2012). Brightness measurements show the star rotates every 17.8 days, well apart from the velocity periods, which supports a planetary origin (Haghighipour et al. 2012).","system":"The planets have minimum masses of 0.56 and 0.73 Jupiter masses, periods of about 162 and 1156 days, and eccentricities of 0.13 and 0.27 (Haghighipour et al. 2012). Orbital simulations show that small rocky bodies could survive only in a small region inside the inner planet (Haghighipour et al. 2012).","history":"Their orbit sizes suggest the planets moved inward from where giant planets form (Haghighipour et al. 2012).","open":"The data show no sign of additional objects (Haghighipour et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd207832b-Haghighipouretal2012","cite":"Haghighipour et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Nader Haghighipour, R. Paul Butler, Eugenio J. Rivera et al.","title":"The Lick-Carnegie Survey: A New Two-planet System around the Star HD 207832","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.2806","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5126 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS and the CHEOPS satellite revealed an F dwarf with two planets, a hot super-Neptune and this exterior warm Neptune. Planet c has an equilibrium temperature of 971 K and is suited to follow-up observation (Fairnington et al. 2024). The planets' orbits are 5.46 and 17.9 days (Fairnington et al. 2024). The star was also observed during transit with the NEID spectrograph to study the spin-orbit geometry (Radzom et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet lies in the hot Neptune desert, a region where such planets are rare (Fairnington et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Tentative transit timing variations hint at at least one more planet, but starspot crossings could also cause them (Fairnington et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5126c-Fairningtonetal2024","cite":"Fairnington et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Tyler R. Fairnington, Emma Nabbie, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-5126: a hot super-Neptune and warm Neptune pair discovered by TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08890","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5126c-Radzometal2024","cite":"Radzom et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"B. T. Radzom, J. Dong, M. Rice et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: NEID radial velocities of TOI-5126 and TOI-5398 (Radzom+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 77338 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is one of the most metal-rich known, and the planet is the lowest-mass one known around so metal-rich a star (Jenkins et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet is a hot Uranus-like world with a minimum mass of 15.9 Earth masses. Its velocity signal is only about 6 metres per second (Jenkins et al. 2013). A search found no transit (Jenkins et al. 2013). A later abundance study found that most elements in the host star follow its iron content, while some iron-peak elements are over-abundant (Kushniruk et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"The authors point to a trend among low-mass planets: higher masses are found around more metal-rich stars. That fits the core accretion picture of planet formation (Jenkins et al. 2013).","open":"Two studies measured the star's metal content as +0.35 and +0.47 dex, so the exact value is uncertain (Jenkins et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd77338b-Jenkinsetal2013","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, H. R. A. Jones, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"A Hot Uranus Orbiting the Super Metal-rich Star HD 77338 and the Metallicity-Mass Connection","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.1012","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd77338b-Kushniruketal2014","cite":"Kushniruk et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"I. O. Kushniruk, Ya. V. Pavlenko, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"Abundances in the atmosphere of the metal-rich planet-host star HD 77338","journal":"Advances in Astronomy and Space Physics","url":"https://doi.org/10.17721/2227-1481.4.20-24","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 86 b":{"text":{"why":"Hubble was used to learn how a giant planet could form so close to a companion star (Farihi 2011).","observed":"The planet's host has a white dwarf companion. Hubble imaging and spectroscopy show it is a helium-rich white dwarf at 8180 K with 0.59 solar masses. The white dwarf's orbit is at least 28 au wide, larger than earlier estimates (Farihi et al. 2013). If the planet's orbit is in the same plane as the binary's, its mass is 4.4 to 4.7 Jupiter masses (Farihi et al. 2013).","system":"The white dwarf once was an A star of 1.9 solar masses at 14 au or more from the planet's host. The system resembles Procyon (Farihi et al. 2013). The planet orbits at about 0.1 au around an early K dwarf (Farihi 2011).","history":"It remains unclear whether the planet formed with the stars, or later while the companion evolved (Farihi 2011).","open":"For GJ 86 and three other close binaries, a full N-body model gives minimum masses that differ by more than 0.2 Jupiter masses from simple orbital fits (Huang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj86b-Farihi2011","cite":"Farihi 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jay Farihi","title":"Constraining Planet Formation in the Unique Evolved Binary HR 637 {GJ 86}","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj86b-Farihietal2013","cite":"Farihi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. Farihi, Howard E. Bond, P. Dufour et al.","title":"Orbital and evolutionary constraints on the planet hosting binary GJ 86 from the Hubble Space Telescope","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.5601","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj86b-Huangetal2025","cite":"Huang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Xiumin Huang, Jianghui Ji, Chunhui Bao et al.","title":"Closeby Habitable Exoplanet Survey (CHES). III. Retrieval of Planetary Masses in Binaries Using the N-body Model with Radial Velocity and Astrometry Synergy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17090","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-24 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its planet pair lies in a nearly perfect 2:1 orbital resonance with large transit timing variations. Thirteen new CHEOPS light curves gave radii and masses at 1 and 5 percent precision (Nascimbeni et al. 2024). The orbits have small but clearly non-zero eccentricities, 0.0282 for planet c, so something besides smooth migration in the disc must have added eccentricity (Nascimbeni et al. 2024). Planet c is about 40 percent larger than b yet 20 percent less massive (Petigura et al. 2018).","system":"Planets between the size of Uranus and Saturn are absent in our Solar System. The two planets here have radii of 5.4 and 7.5 Earth radii and orbit just 1 percent outside the resonance (Petigura et al. 2018). Their low masses imply large envelope fractions, about 26 and 52 percent of their mass (Petigura et al. 2018).","history":"A model with capture into resonance in a disc, followed by eccentricity excitation as the disc dispersed with help from a third planet, reproduces the timing variations (Teyssandier & Libert 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k224c-Nascimbenietal2024","cite":"Nascimbeni et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"V. Nascimbeni, L. Borsato, P. Leonardi et al.","title":"The K2-24 planetary system revisited by CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.02995","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k224c-Petiguraetal2018","cite":"Petigura et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, Björn Benneke, Konstantin Batygin et al.","title":"Dynamics and Formation of the Near-resonant K2-24 System: Insights from Transit-timing Variations and Radial Velocities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.08959","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k224c-TeyssandierLibert2020","cite":"Teyssandier & Libert 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Jean Teyssandier, Anne-Sophie Libert","title":"Transit timing variation signature of planet migration: the case of K2-24","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.05054","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet is not seen in transit. Instead, years of velocity data were combined with astrometry from Gaia and Hipparcos. This gives a mass between 2 and 11 Jupiter masses and a period between 1700 and 7200 days (Lubin et al. 2022). Hubble's later transmission spectrum of the inner planet b showed no significant features (Gapp 2026).","system":"Three transiting sub-Neptunes with periods of 9, 29 and 38 days orbit close to resonances (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020). A Saturn-mass planet e at 101 days lies in the same plane as the transiting planets (Lubin et al. 2022). Another non-transiting planet, g, of at least 13.5 Earth masses orbits in the habitable zone at about 280 days (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The mass and period of planet f are only loosely constrained (Lubin et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939f-Lubinetal2022","cite":"Lubin et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jack Lubin, Judah Van Zandt, Rae Holcomb et al.","title":"TESS-Keck Survey. IX. Masses of Three Sub-Neptunes Orbiting HD 191939 and the Discovery of a Warm Jovian plus a Distant Substellar Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02208","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939f-Gapp2026","cite":"Gapp 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Cyril Gapp, Lorena Acuña-Aguirre, Reza Ashtari et al.","title":"The SPACE Program. II. No Discernible Spectral Features in the Transmission Spectrum of the Sub-Neptune HD 191939 b Observed with HST/WFC3","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05962","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939f-BadenasAgustietal2020","cite":"Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mariona Badenas-Agusti, Maximilian N. Günther, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"HD 191939: Three Sub-Neptunes Transiting a Sun-like Star Only 54 pc Away","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939f-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1554L b":{"text":{"why":"The survey searched high-cadence microlensing data for short-lived planetary signals (Han et al. 2022).","observed":"The planetary signal lasted from a few hours to a couple of days and was clearly detected in the combined survey data. The planet has about 0.12 times Jupiter's mass (Han et al. 2022). Its host star has about 0.08 solar masses, which is right at the boundary between a star and a brown dwarf (Han et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1554lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Doeon Kim, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Four sub-Jovian-mass planets detected by high-cadence microlensing surveys","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10510","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4336 A b":{"text":{"why":"Few small planets around bright nearby stars suit atmospheric study, and fewer still are in multiple star systems (Timmermans et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a temperate sub-Neptune with a 16.3-day orbit, placing it at the inner edge of its star's habitable zone (Timmermans et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is 319 K (Timmermans et al. 2022). Follow-up with the ESPRESSO spectrograph gave it a mass of 3.33 Earth masses and a radius of 2.14 Earth radii (Parc et al. 2026).","system":"The host is an M3.5 dwarf, the brightest of an inner binary, in a hierarchical triple M-dwarf system 22 parsecs away (Timmermans et al. 2024). An inner super-Earth orbits every 7.59 days (Parc et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4336ab-Timmermansetal2024","cite":"Timmermans et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Timmermans, G. Dransfield, M. Gillon et al.","title":"TOI-4336 A b: A temperate sub-Neptune ripe for atmospheric characterization in a nearby triple M-dwarf system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12722","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4336ab-Timmermansetal2022","cite":"Timmermans et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Mathilde Timmermans, Georgina Dransfield, Elsa Ducrot et al.","title":"TOI-4336.01: A mini-Neptune ripe for atmospheric characterization in a nearby triple M-dwarf system","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4336ab-Parcetal2026","cite":"Parc et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Léna Parc, Charles Cadieux, Nolan Grieves et al.","title":"Densities of small planets around the M dwarfs TOI-4336 A and TOI-4342 with ESPRESSO: Three sub-Neptunes, one super-Earth, and a Neptune-mass candidate","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22115","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-186 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive Jupiter of 4.22 Jupiter masses and 1.11 Jupiter radii, orbiting a mid-F star. The orbit is eccentric, with e of 0.327 (Schanche et al. 2020). SOPHIE spectroscopy established its planetary nature, and TESS added more photometry (Schanche et al. 2020). It was announced together with WASP-187b, a lower-density planet in a circular orbit (Schanche et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp186b-Schancheetal2020","cite":"Schanche et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. Schanche, G. Hébrard, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-186 and WASP-187: two hot Jupiters discovered by SuperWASP and SOPHIE with additional observations by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15205","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1512 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The host star is peculiar: infrared spectra show that its sodium and calcium lines look like those of a dwarf, while its carbon monoxide lines look like a giant (Muirhead et al. 2014). Adaptive optics imaging reveals two objects within 1 arcsecond with peculiar colours (Muirhead et al. 2014). Kepler data show strong rotational modulation and 47 flares with energies from 2e31 to 2e32 erg (Savanov 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"It is unclear whether the star is better classed as K or M, but the later study suggests K (Savanov 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1512b-Muirheadetal2014","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Juliette Becker, Gregory A. Feiden et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. VI. H- and K-band Spectra of Kepler M Dwarf Planet-candidate Hosts","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2718","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1512b-Savanov2022","cite":"Savanov 2022","year":"2022","authors":"I. Savanov","title":"Активность звезд с планетами в зоне обитаемости","journal":"Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.54503/0571-7132-2022.65.3-371","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-97 b":{"text":{"why":"It was used to test whether giant planets swell up again when their star turns into a red giant (Grunblatt et al. 2016).","observed":"Asteroseismology, spectroscopy and granulation modelling pinned down the star, a red giant of 4.2 solar radii. The planet has 1.31 Jupiter radii (Grunblatt et al. 2016). While the star was on the main sequence, the planet received 170 times Earth's sunlight, probably too little to inflate it. Its size now suits its current stronger irradiation, which favours re-inflation (Grunblatt et al. 2016; Grunblatt et al. 2016). Later work gave a mass of 0.49 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.63 Jupiter radii (Gary & Grunblatt 2021).","system":"A similar planet orbits another red giant, K2-132 (Grunblatt et al. 2016).","history":"Heating efficiency of the planets is only about 0.03 percent, lower than earlier estimates for re-inflation (Grunblatt et al. 2016).","open":"The planet radius is uncertain because the transit depth differs by up to 30 percent between light-curve reductions (Grunblatt et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k297b-Grunblattetal2016","cite":"Grunblatt et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Samuel K. Grunblatt, Daniel Huber, Eric J. Gaidos et al.","title":"K2-97b: A (Re-?)Inflated Planet Orbiting a Red Giant Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.05818","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k297b-GaryGrunblatt2021","cite":"Gary & Grunblatt 2021","year":"2021","authors":"S. Gary, S. Grunblatt","title":"Confirmation of Hot Jupiter Re-Inflation","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 50499 b":{"text":{"why":"The long-running survey aims to find giant companions on wide orbits that could later be imaged directly (Rickman et al. 2019).","observed":"More than 20 years of CORALIE velocity data gave updated orbital parameters for HD 50499 b (Rickman et al. 2019). The same paper also reports a new companion around this star. It belongs to a set of newly reported companions with periods of 15.6 to 40.4 years and masses of 2.93 to 26.77 Jupiter masses, straddling the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Rickman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd50499b-Rickmanetal2019","cite":"Rickman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"E. L. Rickman, D. Ségransan, M. Marmier et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVIII. Three new massive planets and two low-mass brown dwarfs at greater than 5 AU separation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.01573","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 8673 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet follows a very elongated orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.723 (Roberts et al. 2015). Its host star has a faint stellar companion, an early M dwarf of 0.33-0.45 solar masses, seen at a projected separation of only 10 AU (Roberts et al. 2015). A first candidate companion, spotted in speckle imaging (a way of sharpening images blurred by the atmosphere), turned out to be a false detection (Roberts et al. 2015; Mason et al. 2011).","system":"The companion star moves on an orbit with a semimajor axis of 35-60 AU, an eccentricity of 0.5 or less and an inclination of 75-85 degrees (Roberts et al. 2015). Its projected separation is one of the smallest known for a planet-hosting binary (Roberts et al. 2015).","history":"The companion star has likely shaped the planet's orbit strongly and quite possibly explains its high eccentricity (Roberts et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd8673b-Robertsetal2015","cite":"Roberts et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Lewis C., Jr. Roberts, Brian D. Mason, Christopher R. Neyman et al.","title":"Know the Star, Know the Planet. IV. A Stellar Companion to the Host Star of the Eccentric Exoplanet HD 8673b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.06630","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd8673b-Masonetal2011","cite":"Mason et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Brian D. Mason, William I. Hartkopf, Deepak Raghavan et al.","title":"Know the Star, Know the Planet. II. Speckle Interferometry of Exoplanet Host Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.4569","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164922 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system's newest member concerns HD 164922 d, a close-in super-Earth found in a long series of HARPS-N spectra (Benatti et al. 2020). More than 300 spectra were collected, and almost 700 high-precision velocity measurements were analysed together with a model of the star's activity (Benatti et al. 2020). Planet c, a Neptune-mass world, circles its star every 76 days (Benatti et al. 2020).","system":"The G9V star hosts a gas giant on a wide orbit of about 1200 days (about 2 au), the Neptune-mass planet c with a period of about 76 days, and a close-in super-Earth (Benatti et al. 2020). Such a mix of inner small planets and an outer giant resembles the layout of the Solar System, whose frequency among other stars is still an open question (Benatti et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164922c-Benattietal2020","cite":"Benatti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Benatti, M. Damasso, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XXIII. HD 164922 d: close-in super-Earth discovered with HARPS-N in a system with a long-period Saturn mass companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.03368","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DP Leo b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is not seen directly. Its presence is inferred from small timing shifts in the eclipses of the star it orbits, an eclipsing polar with a very short period of 1.4967 hours (Qian et al. 2010). The eclipse times vary in a cycle of 23.8 years with a semiamplitude of 31.5 seconds, which is plausibly explained by a third body tugging the binary back and forth (Qian et al. 2010). The mass is at least 6.28 Jupiter masses, and about 6.39 Jupiter masses at 8.6 AU if its orbit lies in the same plane as the binary (Qian et al. 2010).","system":"The host is a polar, a binary in which a strongly magnetic white dwarf pulls gas from a companion star. The magnetic field is strong enough to stop an accretion disc from forming (Qian et al. 2014). The white dwarf spins slightly faster than the binary orbits, and the orbital period is shrinking faster than gravitational radiation alone can explain (Pandel et al. 2002; Schwope et al. 2002).","history":"A giant planet around such a binary could give insight into how circumbinary planets form and into the late evolution of such systems (Qian et al. 2010).","open":"Whether the timing signal truly comes from a planet is not settled, and the orbital tilt is not known (Qian et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dpleob-Qianetal2010","cite":"Qian et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"S.-B. Qian, W.-P. Liao, L.-Y. Zhu et al.","title":"Detection of a Giant Extrasolar Planet Orbiting the Eclipsing Polar DP Leo","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/2041-8205/708/1/L66","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dpleob-Qianetal2014","cite":"Qian et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"S.-B. Qian, L.-Y. Zhu, E. Fernández-Lajús et al.","title":"Dark-Spot Activity on the Secondary as the Origin of Variable Mass Accretion in Cataclysmic Variables","journal":"Tenth Pacific Rim Conference on Stellar Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dpleob-Pandeletal2002","cite":"Pandel et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Dirk Pandel, France A. Cordova, Robert E. Shirey et al.","title":"First XMM-Newton observations of strongly magnetic cataclysmic variables - II. Timing studies of DP Leo and WW Hor","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0112567","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dpleob-Schwopeetal2002","cite":"Schwope et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"A. D. Schwope, V. Hambaryan, R. Schwarz et al.","title":"A multiwavelength timing analysis of the eclipsing polar DP Leo","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0111457","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TrES-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet has one of the shortest orbital periods of any transiting planet, about 31 hours, which makes it a good target for studies of orbital decay and loss of atmosphere by evaporation (O'Donovan et al. 2007).","observed":"Transits dim the star by 2.5 percent, and radial velocity measurements give a mass of 1.92 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.295 Jupiter radii (O'Donovan et al. 2007). Spectra show no sign of a blended eclipsing binary star that could fake the signal (O'Donovan et al. 2007). Space-telescope photometry of seven transits found slow brightness changes in the star that may be star spots (Christiansen et al. 2011). Multi-colour ground-based photometry found a flat transit depth across optical wavelengths (Saeed et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's radius is larger than models of strongly irradiated giants allow, and the cause may lie in its atmosphere's composition or in a small orbital eccentricity (O'Donovan et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tres3b-ODonovanetal2007","cite":"O'Donovan et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Francis T. O'Donovan, David Charbonneau, Gáspár Á. Bakos et al.","title":"TrES-3: A Nearby, Massive, Transiting Hot Jupiter in a 31 Hour Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.2004","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres3b-Christiansenetal2011","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sarah Ballard, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"System Parameters, Transit Times, and Secondary Eclipse Constraints of the Exoplanet Systems HAT-P-4, TrES-2, TrES-3, and WASP-3 from the NASA EPOXI Mission of Opportunity","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.2229","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres3b-Saeedetal2020","cite":"Saeed et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. I. Saeed, S. N. Goderya, F. A. Chishtie","title":"Multi-color photometry and parameters estimation of Jupiter-sized exoplanets; TRES-3b, WASP-2b and HATP-30b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.07169","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":43,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 129445 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of five found by the Magellan velocity survey, all giant planets in eccentric orbits of intermediate and long period (Arriagada et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet orbits a metal-rich G6 dwarf star. It has a minimum mass of 1.6 Jupiter masses, a semimajor axis of 2.9 AU and an eccentricity of 0.70 (Arriagada et al. 2010). Only a minimum mass is known, because the tilt of the orbit is not measured.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd129445b-Arriagadaetal2010","cite":"Arriagada et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Pamela Arriagada, R. Paul Butler, Dante Minniti et al.","title":"Five Long-period Extrasolar Planets in Eccentric Orbits from the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-824 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits on the lower edge of the hot Neptune desert, a region where planets of Neptune size are rare on close, hot orbits (Burt et al. 2020). It is the most irradiated small planet at that edge known to have kept its atmosphere (Burt et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet is more than twice as dense as Neptune, at 4.03 grams per cubic centimetre (Burt et al. 2020). Its day side was detected as heat given off during secondary eclipses (when the planet passes behind its star) with the Spitzer space telescope, with brightness temperatures near 1463 and 1484 K (Roy et al. 2022). Heat is poorly spread to the night side and the planet reflects little light, with a Bond albedo below 0.26 (Roy et al. 2022). A cloud-free atmosphere is likely, which makes it a good target for atmospheric studies (Burt et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a K-type dwarf that is rich in carbon but mildly poor in metals (Sun 2026).","history":"The planet could be an exposed Neptune mantle, a world with a water-rich interior that never gathered a hydrogen envelope or later lost it (Roy et al. 2022).","open":"JWST emission observations were planned to measure the day-side temperature, the metal content and the carbon-to-oxygen ratio of the atmosphere (Roy et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi824b-Burtetal2020","cite":"Burt et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Jennifer A. Burt, Louise D. Nielsen, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"TOI-824 b: A New Planet on the Lower Edge of the Hot Neptune Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.11732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi824b-Royetal2022","cite":"Roy et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Pierre-Alexis Roy, Björn Benneke, Caroline Piaulet et al.","title":"Is the Hot, Dense Sub-Neptune TOI-824 b an Exposed Neptune Mantle? Spitzer Detection of the Hot Dayside and Reanalysis of the Interior Composition","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11793","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi824b-Sun2026","cite":"Sun 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Qinghui Sun","title":"JWST Exoplanetary Worlds and Elemental Survey (JEWELS). I. High-precision Chemical Abundances of 20 FGK Planet-hosting Stars from JWST Cycle 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.04420","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi824b-Royetal2023","cite":"Roy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Pierre-Alexis Roy, Björn Benneke, Caroline Piaulet et al.","title":"Is the Hot, Dense Sub-Neptune TOI-824 b an Exposed Neptune Mantle? Spitzer Detection of the Hot Dayside and Reanalysis of the Interior Composition","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11793","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1442 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is an ultra-short-period world, with an orbit shorter than one day. These planets are relatively rare products of planet formation and evolution (Morello et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet orbits an M dwarf every 0.4090682 days and has a radius of 1.15 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of 1357 K (Morello et al. 2023). Space-telescope and ground-based light curves and radial velocities from the Subaru telescope were used to confirm it is a planet (Morello et al. 2023). It was one of 18 potentially terrestrial candidates picked as ideal targets for emission spectroscopy with JWST (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1442b-Morelloetal2023","cite":"Morello et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Morello, H. Parviainen, F. Murgas et al.","title":"TOI-1442 b and TOI-2445 b: Two potentially rocky ultra-short period planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.13274","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1442b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-46 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is highly inflated, with a Jupiter-like radius of 0.95 Jupiter radii but a mass of only 0.16 Jupiter masses (Ahrer et al. 2023). Its transmission spectrum (starlight filtered through its atmosphere during a transit) was measured over 3900-9000 angstroms from the ground, using one transit seen with the New Technology Telescope (Ahrer et al. 2023). The spectrum shows no strong absorption features, and a cloudy atmosphere is favoured over a clear one with 3.0 sigma confidence (Ahrer et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 1100 K (Ahrer et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a faint G-type star with a visual magnitude of 13.6 (Ahrer et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats46b-Ahreretal2023","cite":"Ahrer et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Ahrer, P. J. Wheatley, S. Gandhi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HATS-46 b EFOSC2 light curve (Ahrer+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TAP 26 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The host is a young T Tauri star in the Taurus-Auriga star-forming cloud (Bouvier et al. 1993). The star was monitored for over 5 months to pin down its rotation period, and three different periods had been reported: 2.51 days, 13 hours and 0.715 days (Petreshock et al. 1995). Starspots on such young stars change their brightness and could disturb precise position measurements (Donar & Herbst 2001).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true rotation period of the star is still debated, since monitoring over 5 months gave a period of 0.715 days that differs from the two earlier values (Petreshock et al. 1995)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tap26b-Bouvieretal1993","cite":"Bouvier et al. 1993","year":"1993","authors":"J. Bouvier, S. Cabrit, M. Fernandez et al.","title":"COYOTES I : the photometric variability and rotational evolution of T Tauri stars.","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tap26b-Petreshocketal1995","cite":"Petreshock et al. 1995","year":"1995","authors":"J. Petreshock, S. Wolk, N. Adams et al.","title":"Observational Results from Mount Stony Brook Observatory","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #186","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tap26b-DonarHerbst2001","cite":"Donar & Herbst 2001","year":"2001","authors":"A. Donar, W. Herbst","title":"Photometric and Astrometric Effects of Spots on T Tauri Stars","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #198","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-312 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a cold Jupiter on a wide orbit, with its period refined to 871.32 days (Naponiello et al. 2026). It follows a highly eccentric orbit, so monitoring its closest approach to the star was the aim of the new work (Naponiello et al. 2026). The study added 237 HARPS-N spectra, extending the observing baseline by almost 4 years (Naponiello et al. 2026).","system":"The star also hosts a rocky ultra-short-period super-Earth that orbits in 0.720 days (Naponiello et al. 2026). Systems with such inner planets and outer giants are used to test ideas about dynamical effects and pebble accretion, the growth of planets from small solid grains (Naponiello et al. 2026).","history":"Formation and dynamical evolution of the system were modelled (Naponiello et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2312c-Naponielloetal2026","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"L. Naponiello, E. Poretti, K. Rice et al.","title":"Refined parameters, formation, and dynamical stability of the wild exoplanet system K2-312≡HD 80653","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19325","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-68 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planets. Kepler-68 b is a planet of 2.31 Earth radii with a density between that of Earth and the ice giants, and Kepler-68 c is Earth-sized (Gilliland et al. 2013). A third planet, Kepler-68 d, is a Jupiter-mass planet with a period of 580 days found by Doppler measurements (Gilliland et al. 2013).","system":"The star shows strong, rich pulsation modes in Kepler data, which allowed the star's mass and radius to be measured precisely (Gilliland et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler68e-Gillilandetal2013","cite":"Gilliland et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ronald L. Gilliland, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-68: Three Planets, One with a Density between that of Earth and Ice Giants","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2596","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-635 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star pulsates, so its brightness varies on its own. A search of Kepler short-cadence data for transits around such A-F type pulsating stars removed the pulsation signals and still found the planet's transits (Sowicka et al. 2017). The transit period found this way agrees with the earlier value (Sowicka et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler635b-Sowickaetal2017","cite":"Sowicka et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Paulina Sowicka, Gerald Handler, Bartłomiej Dębski et al.","title":"Search for exoplanets around pulsating stars of A-F type in Kepler short-cadence data and the case of KIC 8197761","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.05158","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3779 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet has a near twin: GJ 1265 b, around another mid-type M dwarf, has a very similar mass and orbit of 3.651 days (Luque et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet orbits a M4.0V star, with a minimum mass of 8.0 Earth masses on a low-eccentricity orbit (Luque et al. 2018). The roughly 3-day signal has no counterpart in activity indicators of the star, which rules out starspots as the cause (Luque et al. 2018). Photometry shows that a separate Doppler signal near 95 days comes from the star's rotation (Luque et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Adding these two planets to the mass-period diagram of known planets around M dwarfs suggests a bimodal distribution, with a lack of short-period planets of 2-5 Earth masses (Luque et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3779b-Luqueetal2018","cite":"Luque et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Luque, G. Nowak, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. The warm super-Earths in twin orbits around the mid-type M dwarfs Ross 1020 (GJ 3779) and LP 819-052 (GJ 1265)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.07572","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1130 c":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters are rarely accompanied by smaller close-in companions, and this is one of those rare systems (Korth 2022).","observed":"Radial velocities from HARPS and PFS were modelled together with transit light curves, in a method called photodynamical modelling that follows how the planets pull on each other (Korth et al. 2023). The planet has a mass of 325.59 Earth masses and a radius of 13.32 Earth radii (Korth et al. 2023). Its orbit is nearly circular, locked in a 2:1 resonance with the inner planet, so the inner planet circles the star about twice for each orbit of the giant (Korth et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-1130 b, is Neptune-sized, with a mass of 19.28 Earth masses and an orbital period of 4.1 days (Korth et al. 2023). It is the first known system with a hot Jupiter and an inner lower-mass planet locked in a mean-motion resonance (Korth et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1130c-Korth2022","cite":"Korth 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Judith Korth","title":"Not alone in solitude: a look into the surprising world of TOI-1130","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2022-14","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1130c-Korthetal2023","cite":"Korth et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Korth, D. Gandolfi, J. Šubjak et al.","title":"TOI-1130: A photodynamical analysis of a hot Jupiter in resonance with an inner low-mass planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15565","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 136352 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The space telescope CHEOPS observed the star in six visits between 4 April and 6 July 2020, catching four transits of planet b and three of planet c (Delrez et al. 2021). One visit caught a transit of each planet (Delrez et al. 2021). The raw and detrended light curves from those visits have been released for study (Delrez et al. 2021).","system":"The star, also called nu2 Lupi, has several planets, and at least two of them transit (Delrez et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd136352b-Delrezetal2021","cite":"Delrez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L. Delrez, D. Ehrenreich, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: nu2 Lupi CHEOPS light curves (Delrez+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"tau Cet f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. A search for heat from planets at 10 microns with the VISIR instrument at the Very Large Telescope reached sub-mJy sensitivity, enough to detect a few Jupiter-mass planets, but found none around tau Ceti (Pathak et al. 2021). Such nearby stars are targets for future imaging of rocky planets (Pathak et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a 10 billion year old G8V star and the closest Sun-like star (Di Folco et al. 2014). It has a ring of dust about 15 au across, the least massive such belt found around a debris-disc star, with a mass only ten times that of the Kuiper belt (Di Folco et al. 2014). Hot dust is also seen closer in (Di Folco et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the dust belt has been steadily ground down by collisions or recently regenerated by an upset in the planets' orbits is not known (Di Folco et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-taucetf-Pathaketal2021","cite":"Pathak et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Pathak, D. J. M. Petit dit de la Roche, M. Kasper et al.","title":"High-contrast imaging at ten microns: A search for exoplanets around Eps Indi A, Eps Eri, Tau Ceti, Sirius A, and Sirius B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-taucetf-DiFolcoetal2014","cite":"Di Folco et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"E. Di Folco, J. Péricaud, J.-C. Augereau et al.","title":"Tau Ceti: our nearest cousin","journal":"SF2A-2014: Proceedings of the Annual meeting of the French Society of Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"2MASS J12073346-3932539 b":{"text":{"why":"The object orbits a brown dwarf rather than a star and is classed as a planetary-mass companion rather than a planet because of its large mass ratio to its host (Aoyama et al. 2024).","observed":"JWST spectra detected hydrogen emission lines (Paschen and Brackett), a sign of gas still falling onto a forming planet-like object of about 5 Jupiter masses (Aoyama et al. 2024). Modelling of the lines with an accretion-shock model was used to estimate how fast it gathers gas (Aoyama et al. 2024). Time-resolved JWST spectra over 12.56 hours show its brightness changing, consistent with a changing cover of clouds made of silicate and iron condensates (Adams et al. 2025). Its temperature is about 1300 K (Adams et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a brown dwarf of about 2500 K in a roughly 10 million year old system, and the companion lies about 0.77 arcseconds from it (Adams et al. 2025; Birkmann et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-2massj120733463932539b-Aoyamaetal2024","cite":"Aoyama et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Yuhiko Aoyama, Gabriel-Dominique Marleau, Jun Hashimoto","title":"Analyzing JWST/NIRSpec Hydrogen Line Detections at TWA 27B: Constraining Accretion Properties and Geometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15922","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-2massj120733463932539b-Adamsetal2025","cite":"Adams et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Arthur D. Adams, Yifan Zhou, Gabriel-Dominique Marleau et al.","title":"Characterizing the Time Variability of 2M1207 A + b with JWST NIRSpec/PRISM","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13544","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-2massj120733463932539b-Birkmannetal2017","cite":"Birkmann et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Stephan Birkmann, Pierre Ferruit, Pierre-Olivier Lagage","title":"Characterizing the TWA 27 system","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a very bright naked-eye F star (V about 5.7), and the planet is a candidate found in a repeating velocity signal of about 10 m/s that lasted over 4 years (Barnes et al. 2023). The preferred model has one planet with a period of 3.4982 days and a minimum mass of 12.2 Earth masses (Barnes et al. 2023). A two-planet alternative cannot yet be ruled out without more observations (Barnes et al. 2023).","system":"The star shows unusually low calcium emission, which suggests mass loss from planets orbiting a quiet star (Barnes et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the signal comes from one planet or two, and how far starspots affect it, is not settled (Barnes et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp4b-Barnesetal2023","cite":"Barnes et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. R. Barnes, M. R. Standing, C. A. Haswell et al.","title":"DMPP-4: candidate sub-Neptune mass planets orbiting a naked-eye star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.07530","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1855 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a survey that aims to build a complete, homogeneous sample of hot Jupiters orbiting FGK stars brighter than a Gaia magnitude of 12.5, to test how such planets reach their close orbits (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is one of nine new hot Jupiters from the survey, which span 0.55-3.88 Jupiter masses and 0.967-1.438 Jupiter radii (Schulte et al. 2024). It was confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Three ideas for how hot Jupiters form are tested by the survey: forming in place, migrating through the gas disc, and high-eccentricity tidal migration (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1855b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1855b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-172 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first planet confirmed from the Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite full frame images alone, because its star was not chosen for two-minute cadence observations (Rodriguez et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet is a massive hot Jupiter of 5.42 Jupiter masses and 0.965 Jupiter radii with a 9.48-day period (Rodriguez et al. 2019). It moves on an eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.3806 (Rodriguez et al. 2019). It is one of the few known massive giant planets on a highly eccentric short-period orbit (Rodriguez et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved G star, with a radius of 1.777 solar radii and an age of 7.4 billion years (Rodriguez et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi172b-Rodriguezetal2019","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"An Eccentric Massive Jupiter Orbiting a Subgiant on a 9.5-day Period Discovered in the Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite Full Frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.09950","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-91 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter with 1.34 Jupiter masses and 1.03 Jupiter radii, orbiting a metal-rich K3 star (Anderson et al. 2017). A later search of TESS data for extra planets in 12 hot-Jupiter systems, including this one, found no additional planets down to planets smaller than Neptune (Maciejewski 2022).","system":"The star is one of three metal-rich, late-type hosts announced together with WASP-105 b and WASP-107 b, a notable fact because giant planets seem more common around metal-rich stars (Anderson et al. 2017). No companion planets were found, in line with high-eccentricity migration as the way such giant planets reached tight orbits (Maciejewski 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp91b-Andersonetal2017","cite":"Anderson et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, L. Delrez et al.","title":"The discoveries of WASP-91b, WASP-105b and WASP-107b: Two warm Jupiters and a planet in the transition region between ice giants and gas giants","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03776","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp91b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-421 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up from the ground confirmed the 16-day signal as a planet, using transit photometry, adaptive-optics imaging and Doppler measurements from five spectrographs (Carleo et al. 2020). The planet is a warm Neptune with a very low density of 0.685 g/cm3, so it holds a thick gas envelope for its size (Carleo et al. 2020). Both planets in the system are well suited to studies of their atmospheres (Carleo et al. 2020).","system":"The star is a bright G9 dwarf that sits in a visual binary (Carleo et al. 2020). A second planet, a mini-Neptune, circles it every 5 days (Carleo et al. 2020). Space-telescope spectra of that inner planet show a hot atmosphere free of haze (Davenport et al. 2025).","history":"Low-density Neptunes are thought to reach close orbits mostly through gradual migration inside the gas disc, and the system serves as the first target of a survey testing this idea (Bourrier et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi421c-Carleoetal2020","cite":"Carleo et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Davide Gandolfi, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"The Multiplanet System TOI-421","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10095","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi421c-Davenportetal2025","cite":"Davenport et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Brian Davenport, Eliza M.-R. Kempton, Matthew C. Nixon et al.","title":"TOI-421 b: A Hot Sub-Neptune with a Haze-free, Low Mean Molecular Weight Atmosphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.01498","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi421c-Bourrieretal2025","cite":"Bourrier et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"V. Bourrier, M. Steiner, A. Castro-González et al.","title":"ATREIDES: I. Embarking on a trek across the exo-Neptunian landscape with the TOI-421 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15746","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28471 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The signal comes from 19 years of Doppler measurements with one spectrograph, searched with an algorithm that lets the number of planets vary freely (Stevenson et al. 2025). Checks of the star's activity indicate that three short-period signals are genuine planets (Stevenson et al. 2025). The planet has a minimum mass of 5.7 Earth masses and sits in the middle of the three (Stevenson et al. 2025).","system":"The three planets circle the star every 3.16, 6.12 and 11.68 days, a compact and near-resonant arrangement (Stevenson et al. 2025). A fourth signal of about 1500 days is strongly detected (Stevenson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The 1500-day signal may come from a short magnetic cycle of the star rather than a planet, and more data are needed to tell (Stevenson et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28471c-Stevensonetal2025","cite":"Stevenson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. T. Stevenson, C. A. Haswell, J. R. Barnes et al.","title":"HD 28471: a near-resonant compact multiplanet system with a possible cold giant planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-879 c":{"text":{"why":"Only a few hundred planets with radii below Earth's are known, so each new one adds to a small sample (Wang et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet turned up in a new search of Kepler light curves. A graphics-card algorithm folds the data at trial periods and a neural network picks out the transit signals, which allowed the full Kepler candidate list to be searched within days (Wang et al. 2024). With a radius of 0.4 Earth radii, the planet is smaller than Earth and circles a G dwarf every 0.646716 days (Wang et al. 2024). It is one of five new ultra-short-period planets reported in that search (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"How such sub-Earth planets form is not known (Wang et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler879c-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kaitlyn Wang, Jian Ge, Kevin Willis et al.","title":"Discovery of small ultra-short-period planets orbiting Kepler KG dwarfs with GPU phase folding and deep learning","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17382","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 98649 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler measurements from the CORALIE spectrograph, collected over more than 14 years, revealed a massive planet on a long orbit (Marmier et al. 2013). The survey aimed to find Jupiter-like planets on Jupiter-like periods, beyond the ice line (Marmier et al. 2013). Combining the Doppler data with precise star positions from the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites gives a mass of about 9.7 Jupiter masses and confirms it as a planet (Li et al. 2021). Its star is a Sun-like star with its chemical makeup measured in a study of 22 planet-hosting solar twins and analogues (Sun et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Two orbit orientations still fit the data, because the motion on the sky cannot yet be separated from its mirror image (Li et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd98649b-Marmieretal2013","cite":"Marmier et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Marmier, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVII. New and updated long period and massive planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6444","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd98649b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd98649b-Sunetal2025","cite":"Sun et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Q. Sun, C. Ji, S. X. Wang et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: PASTA II. Abundances for 25 solar twins and analogs (Sun+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd98649b-Lietal2022","cite":"Li et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star has a nearly equally bright companion star only 0.3 arcseconds away on the sky (Buchhave et al. 2011). The companion's light dilutes the transit signal. Left uncorrected, the dilution would make the planet's radius appear about 10% too small and its mass about 60% too small (Buchhave et al. 2011). After correction, the planet has a mass of 8.4 Jupiter masses, a radius of 1.136 Jupiter radii and a density of 7.1 g/cm3 (Buchhave et al. 2011). Starquake measurements (asteroseismology) were also used to refine the properties of the host star (Huber et al. 2013).","system":"The planet's star is part of a close visual binary (Buchhave et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"Other known planets that lack high-resolution imaging could have unseen companion stars and wrong sizes (Buchhave et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler14b-Buchhaveetal2011","cite":"Buchhave et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Lars A. Buchhave, David W. Latham, Joshua A. Carter et al.","title":"Kepler-14b: A Massive Hot Jupiter Transiting an F Star in a Close Visual Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.5510","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler14b-Huberetal2013","cite":"Huber et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Daniel Huber, William J. Chaplin, Jørgen Christensen-Dalsgaard et al.","title":"Fundamental Properties of Kepler Planet-candidate Host Stars using Asteroseismology","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2624","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2449 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aimed to enlarge the small sample of warm giant planets with precise radii and masses (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025).","observed":"Only a handful of transiting giant planets with orbits longer than 100 days are known (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025). Follow-up with the Next Generation Transit Survey caught extra transits to pin down the period, and several spectrographs measured the mass and the orbit's shape (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025). A planet evolution code was used to estimate the planet's heavy-element content (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"At such large distances and low starlight, the orbit and atmosphere of a warm giant stay relatively unaltered, which gives clues to how giants form and evolve (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2449b-UlmerMolletal2025","cite":"Ulmer-Moll et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Ulmer-Moll, S. Gill, R. Brahm et al.","title":"Detection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15424","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1105L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was among four picked out by eye from the 2021 data of the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network (Ryu et al. 2023). The host star may be a relatively bright star blended with the lensing system, seen at magnitudes of about 18.9 in the infrared and 21.6 in visible light (Ryu et al. 2023). If that star is the host, it is an early G dwarf (Ryu et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Imaging at high resolution could check immediately whether the bright star is the host (Ryu et al. 2023). If so, a 30-metre-class telescope could measure the planet by its Doppler signal (Ryu et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1105lb-Ryuetal2023","cite":"Ryu et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Yoon-Hyun Ryu, In-Gu Shin, Hongjing Yang et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets II","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-905 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up from the ground used photometry in several filters, speckle imaging to rule out close companion stars, and Doppler measurements (Davis et al. 2020). The planet is a classical hot Jupiter of 0.667 Jupiter masses and 1.171 Jupiter radii (Davis et al. 2020). It crosses fully in front of its mid-G dwarf star, which is Sun-like and moderately bright (Davis et al. 2020). The planet was announced together with TOI 564 b, which only grazes its own star (Davis et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi905b-Davisetal2020","cite":"Davis et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Allen B. Davis, Songhu Wang, Matias Jones et al.","title":"TOI 564 b and TOI 905 b: Grazing and Fully Transiting Hot Jupiters Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10186","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A later analysis finds a single giant with a minimum mass of about 69 Earth masses, or 0.72 Saturn masses, and an orbit of 4.93 days (Barnes et al. 2026). The host is a slightly evolved star (Barnes et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"A second signal of 21 to 22 days cannot yet be told apart from the star's spin or a further planet (Barnes et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp7b-Barnesetal2026","cite":"Barnes et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. R. Barnes, C. A. Haswell, Z. O. B. Ross et al.","title":"Three new exoplanet systems from the Dispersed Matter Planet Project","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11902","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-789 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Seven years of space-telescope data (42 sectors) and ground-based follow-up allowed statistical validation of the planet as a terrestrial world with a radius of 1.18 Earth radii (Pagés Navarrete 2026). The host star is an M3 dwarf (Pagés Navarrete 2026).","system":"The system is compact and near-resonant, with three validated planets at 5.45, 8.04 and 12.97 days (Pagés Navarrete 2026). A fourth candidate at 19.74 days and about 0.88 Earth radii sits farthest out, with an equilibrium temperature near 292 K (Pagés Navarrete 2026). The system stays stable only if orbits are nearly circular, with eccentricities below about 0.05 (Pagés Navarrete 2026).","history":"","open":"The fourth planet is not yet confirmed because its signal is weak (Pagés Navarrete 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi789b-Navarrete2026","cite":"Navarrete 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Juan F. Pagés Navarrete","title":"Characterization of the Compact Multiplanet System TOI-789: Validation of Three Terrestrial Planets, Dynamical Characterization, and Detection of a Fourth Temperate Candidate","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22945","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"81 Cet b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a clump giant of 2.4 solar masses, which was probably an early F-type or A-type dwarf while on the main sequence (Sato et al. 2008). The planet has a minimum mass of 5.3 Jupiter masses and a moderately eccentric orbit of 953 days (Sato et al. 2008).","system":"The host star has solar or sub-solar metallicity, which means heavy-element content (Sato et al. 2008).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-81cetb-Satoetal2008","cite":"Sato et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eri Toyota, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: 14 Andromedae, 81 Ceti, 6 Lyncis, and HD167042","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.0268","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102117 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three low-mass planets reported together from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search (Tinney et al. 2005). Its host is a chromospherically quiet G dwarf, and is metal enriched (Tinney et al. 2005). The planet has the shortest orbit of the three, 20.8 days, and the smallest eccentricity, within a range of 0.08 to 0.37 (Tinney et al. 2005). The three planets produce Doppler signals of 10.6 to 19 metres per second, similar to those of Jupiter analogues (Tinney et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102117b-Tinneyetal2005","cite":"Tinney et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Three Low-Mass Planets from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/428661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-10 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot, sub-Jupiter-mass world with an equilibrium temperature of about 2000 K, a mass of 0.7 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.54 Jupiter radii (Alsubai et al. 2019). Its orbit puts it at the edge of the sub-Jupiter desert, a region short of planets of this mass (Alsubai et al. 2019). A search of space-telescope data found no additional planets in the system down to sub-Neptune size (Maciejewski 2022).","system":"","history":"The lack of companions fits the idea that hot Jupiters reach tight orbits by high-eccentricity migration (Maciejewski 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar10b-Alsubaietal2019","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Khalid Alsubai, Zlatan I. Tsvetanov, Stylianos Pyrzas et al.","title":"Qatar Exoplanet Survey: Qatar-8b, 9b, and 10b—A Hot Saturn and Two Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.09258","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar10b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-83 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Saturn-mass world of 0.3 Jupiter masses, with a radius of 1.0 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2015). It circles a G8 star that is fairly faint, at magnitude 12.9 (Hellier et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp83b-Hellieretal2015","cite":"Hellier et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Three WASP-South Transiting Exoplanets: WASP-74b, WASP-83b, and WASP-89b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.6358","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-61 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler tomography, which follows the planet's shadow across the star's spinning surface, shows that the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, at an angle of 4.0 degrees with an uncertainty of about 18 degrees (Brown et al. 2017). Photometry from the Las Cumbres Observatory network has been used to refine the timing of its transits, and the planet is a viable target for atmospheric study (Edwards et al. 2021).","system":"The planet is one of seven typical hot Jupiters reported together, with densities ranging from Jupiter-like to inflated (Hellier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp61b-Brownetal2017","cite":"Brown et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. H. M. J. Triaud, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Rossiter-McLaughlin models and their effect on estimates of stellar rotation, illustrated using six WASP systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00600","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp61b-Edwardsetal2021","cite":"Edwards et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Billy Edwards, Cynthia Ho, Hannah Osborne et al.","title":"Original Research By Young Twinkle Students (ORBYTS): Ephemeris Refinement of Transiting Exoplanets III","journal":"Astronomy: Theory, Observations and Methods","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10350","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp61b-Hellieretal2012","cite":"Hellier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Seven transiting hot Jupiters from WASP-South, Euler and TRAPPIST: WASP-47b, WASP-55b, WASP-61b, WASP-62b, WASP-63b, WASP-66b and WASP-67b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5095","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 4760 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion has a minimum mass of 13.9 Jupiter masses on an orbit of 1.14 au, with an eccentricity of 0.23 (Niedzielski et al. 2021). The star is a solar-mass evolved star that was observed for up to 14 years with two telescopes (Niedzielski et al. 2021). The star belongs to a sample of 122 stars, from main sequence to red giant branch, used to study how planet occurrence relates to a star's metallicity and luminosity (Niedzielski et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd4760b-Niedzielskietal2021","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Niedzielski, E. Villaver, M. Adamów et al.","title":"Tracking Advanced Planetary Systems (TAPAS) with HARPS-N. VII. Elder suns with low-mass companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.10410","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-32 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the first exoplanet with absorption measured in both hydrogen and helium lines at once, with a depth of about 5% in both (Shaikhislamov et al. 2026). Absorption starts long before the transit, which points to gas streaming around the star (Shaikhislamov et al. 2026). Modelling suggests that metals in the upper atmosphere strongly affect the absorption (Shaikhislamov et al. 2026). The planet is highly inflated, at about 1.79 Jupiter radii for 0.86 Jupiter masses, assuming a circular orbit (Hartman et al. 2011).","system":"The star shows strong jitter, so its orbit is poorly constrained by Doppler data (Hartman et al. 2011). Doppler data show a steady acceleration, which points to a distant massive companion (Knutson et al. 2014). Transit timing shows no sign of an extra close-in planet (Wang et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp32b-Shaikhislamovetal2026","cite":"Shaikhislamov et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"F. Shaikhislamov I., Sharipov S. S, L. Khodachenko M. et al.","title":"HAT-P-32 b: what can be deduced from transit observations in Hα and He I lines?","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp32b-Hartmanetal2011","cite":"Hartman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-32b and HAT-P-33b: Two Highly Inflated Hot Jupiters Transiting High-jitter Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1212","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp32b-Knutsonetal2014","cite":"Knutson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Heather A. Knutson, Benjamin J. Fulton, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"Friends of Hot Jupiters. I. A Radial Velocity Search for Massive, Long-period Companions to Close-in Gas Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.2954","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp32b-Wangetal2019","cite":"Wang et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Yong-Hao Wang, Songhu Wang, Tobias C. Hinse et al.","title":"Transiting Exoplanet Monitoring Project (TEMP). V. Transit Follow Up for HAT-P-9b, HAT-P-32b, and HAT-P-36b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aaf6b6","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-107 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour WASP-107 b. The outer planet was found from four years of Doppler measurements, and has a minimum mass of 0.36 Jupiter masses on a wide, eccentric orbit (Piaulet et al. 2021). It does not transit, so its size is not known.","system":"The inner planet WASP-107 b is a Neptune-mass world with extremely low density (Piaulet et al. 2021). Its orbit is polar and retrograde, so it circles its star roughly over the poles and against its spin (Rubenzahl et al. 2023).","history":"Eccentric orbits and gas-shedding atmospheres point to formation far from the star and migration inward (Rubenzahl et al. 2023). The inner planet may have begun gathering gas at 1 au or more and then moved inward (Piaulet et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp107c-Piauletetal2021","cite":"Piaulet et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Caroline Piaulet, Björn Benneke, Ryan A. Rubenzahl et al.","title":"WASP-107b's Density Is Even Lower: A Case Study for the Physics of Planetary Gas Envelope Accretion and Orbital Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13444","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp107c-Rubenzahletal2023","cite":"Rubenzahl et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ryan Rubenzahl, Fei Dai, Andrew Howard","title":"Squad up: WASP-107 b joins an emerging population of hot polar Neptunes around cool stars","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-217 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a ground-based survey run before the Kepler mission launched (Howell et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet was one of three transit candidates found before launch and then watched by Kepler during its first observing quarter (Howell et al. 2010). The host star is faint, so the usual high-precision velocity checks were hard to make. Instead, the Kepler light curves and pixel data were used, together with medium-resolution spectra and speckle imaging, to rule out false alarms (Howell et al. 2010). The other two candidates turned out to be binary stars. The planet's star is a G8 dwarf, and the planet has a radius of 1.12 Jupiter radii (Howell et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi217b-Howelletal2010","cite":"Howell et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Steve B. Howell, Jason F. Rowe, William Sherry et al.","title":"Kepler Observations of Three Pre-launch Exoplanet Candidates: Discovery of Two Eclipsing Binaries and a New Exoplanet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.4106","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2373 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a collaboration that studies TESS transiting giant planets with orbital periods between 10 and 300 days (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","observed":"Its mass is about 9.3 Jupiter masses and its radius about 0.93 Jupiter radii (Eberhardt et al. 2023). The mean density is 14.4 grams per cubic centimetre, which makes it one of the densest planets known (Eberhardt et al. 2023). It is also one of the most massive planets with a precisely measured mass and radius around a Sun-like star (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","system":"The planet lies in a sparsely populated zone between hot and warm Jupiters. It is the most massive planet in that region around Sun-like G stars (Eberhardt et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Planets like it are useful for testing ideas about how giant planets form, move inward, and become puffed up (Eberhardt et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2373b-Eberhardtetal2023","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Melissa J. Hobson, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"Three Warm Jupiters around Solar-analog Stars Detected with TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17592","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"51 Peg b":{"text":{"why":"The announcement was made at a conference in Florence on 6 October 1995 (Mayor et al. 1995).","observed":"The planet was inferred from 18 months of precise velocity measurements of the star (Mayor et al. 1995). Pulsation and starspots could in principle have produced the same signal, but the star showed no matching change in brightness, so both were ruled out (Mayor et al. 1995). Teams at the Lick Observatory and at the Harvard-Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics confirmed the 4.2-day signal in mid-October 1995 (Mayor et al. 1995). Theory suggests that planets this close to their stars are locked by tides to show one face to the star, with a day-to-night temperature difference that could reach about 500 K and winds of about 2 kilometres per second (Showman & Guillot 2002).","system":"The star is metal-rich, with a heavy-element content about 1.6 times that of the Sun, and about 4 billion years old (Fuhrmann et al. 1997).","history":"Planets with circular orbits, Jupiter-like masses and orbital radii under about 0.2 astronomical units were proposed as a new class soon after its discovery (Butler et al. 1997).","open":"Clouds on planets like this one could sit mainly on the day side or the night side, depending on the winds (Showman & Guillot 2002)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-51pegb-Mayoretal1995","cite":"Mayor et al. 1995","year":"1995","authors":"M. Mayor, D. Queloz, G. Marcy et al.","title":"51 Pegasi","journal":"International Astronomical Union Circular","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-51pegb-ShowmanGuillot2002","cite":"Showman & Guillot 2002","year":"2002","authors":"A. P. Showman, T. Guillot","title":"Atmospheric circulation and tides of ``51 Pegasus b-like'' planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202236","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-51pegb-Fuhrmannetal1997","cite":"Fuhrmann et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"K. Fuhrmann, M. J. Pfeiffer, J. Bernkopf","title":"Solar-type stars with planetary companions: 51 Pegasi and 47 Ursae Majoris.","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-51pegb-Butleretal1997","cite":"Butler et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Eric Williams et al.","title":"Three New ``51 Pegasi-Type'' Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/310444","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":59,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1448 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of four short-period sub-Neptunes around M dwarfs studied together, to learn how close-in small planets form and evolve (Hori et al. 2024).","observed":"Velocity monitoring with the Subaru InfraRed Doppler instrument gave only an upper limit on the planet's mass: below 19.5 Earth masses at 2 sigma (Hori et al. 2024). Together with its size, the mass limits for the four planets in the study point to volatile material in their interiors (Hori et al. 2024). Because the planets sit above the so-called radius valley, they are likely to keep a thick atmosphere, an icy mantle on the core, or both, like a water world (Hori et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The exact make-up of the planet is not known, since only an upper limit on the mass has been measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1448b-Horietal2024","cite":"Hori et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Yasunori Hori, Akihiko Fukui, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"The Discovery and Follow-up of Four Transiting Short-period Sub-Neptunes Orbiting M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12637","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-8 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's orbit runs backwards compared with the spin of its star, which is unusual (Queloz et al. 2010).","observed":"The orbit is strongly tilted and eccentric, and the planet moves in the opposite direction to the rotation of its late-G host star (Queloz et al. 2010). Infrared observations with the Spitzer Space Telescope caught the planet passing behind its star in three wavelength bands (Cubillos et al. 2013). They gave brightness temperatures of 1552, 1131 and 938 K (Cubillos et al. 2013). The planet is one of the least-irradiated hot Jupiters observed with Spitzer, with a time-averaged equilibrium temperature of 948 K, and its atmosphere shows no temperature inversion (Cubillos et al. 2013).","system":"The star appears to belong to a multiple stellar system with two other companions (Queloz et al. 2010).","history":"The complex orbits suggest that the planet may have been shaped by slow gravitational interactions such as the Kozai mechanism, or that it formed in a way that differs from the classical picture of moving inward through a disc (Queloz et al. 2010).","open":"The very high brightness temperature at 3.6 micrometres (1552 K) is not explained, because no model spectrum fits all three measurements well (Cubillos et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp8b-Quelozetal2010","cite":"Queloz et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Queloz, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-8b: a retrograde transiting planet in a multiple system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.5089","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp8b-Cubillosetal2013","cite":"Cubillos et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Patricio Cubillos, Joseph Harrington, Nikku Madhusudhan et al.","title":"WASP-8b: Characterization of a Cool and Eccentric Exoplanet with Spitzer","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5468","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 261-75 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion is listed among several known objects whose age and brightness fall in a strip where models of how young bodies cool give overlapping masses, because of the burning of deuterium (Bowler et al. 2013). In that strip, brightness alone cannot pin down whether an object is a planet or a brown dwarf. The mass of the companion is therefore uncertain (Bowler et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the companion is a giant planet or a brown dwarf is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp26175b-Bowleretal2013","cite":"Bowler et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Brendan P. Bowler, Michael C. Liu, Evgenya L. Shkolnik et al.","title":"Planets around Low-mass Stars. III. A Young Dusty L Dwarf Companion at the Deuterium-burning Limit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.2237","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1824 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is unusually dense for its size, which makes it a test case for how such planets form (Lange et al. 2024).","observed":"TESS first flagged the planet in April 2020, after two transits in one observing sector were matched with a single transit in the previous sector (Lange et al. 2024). Velocity monitoring with Keck-HIRES and the APF-Levy spectrograph gave a mass of 18.5 Earth masses and a radius of 2.63 Earth radii (Lange et al. 2024). The bulk density is 5.6 grams per cubic centimetre, so the planet is an unusually dense sub-Neptune (Lange et al. 2024). It sits near a small group of superdense sub-Neptunes, which are smaller than about 3 Earth radii yet heavier than about 20 Earth masses (Lange et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"One suggested route to such planets is the collision of icy cores that formed early. Giant impacts of this kind would drive away gas and could explain why these planets seem to lack massive envelopes (Lange et al. 2024).","open":"How superdense sub-Neptunes form is a mystery, and it is not clear whether they are a distinct population (Lange et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1824b-Langeetal2024","cite":"Lange et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sarah Lange, Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. VII. A Superdense Sub-Neptune Orbiting TOI-1824","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ad34d9","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1824b-Langeetal2022","cite":"Lange et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sarah Lange, Joseph Akana Murphy, Natalie Batalha","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. A Warm, Superdense Sub-Neptune Orbiting TOI-1824","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting #240","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2981 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets show unexpected bulk densities. Hot Jupiters look larger than models predict for their mass, and warm Jupiters look denser (Mantovan et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet has a radius of about 1.2 Jupiter radii and a mass of about 2 Jupiter masses, on a nearly circular orbit (Mantovan et al. 2024). It was confirmed with velocity follow-up from the HARPS spectrograph (Mantovan et al. 2024). The star is metal-poor, with an iron abundance of -0.11 relative to the Sun, which is lower than for most stars known to host giant planets (Mantovan et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"It is not clear whether the density differences between hot and warm Jupiters come from different formation paths, or whether planet density follows the chemical make-up of the host star (Mantovan et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2981b-Mantovanetal2024","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Mantovan, T. G. Wilson, L. Borsato et al.","title":"The inflated, eccentric warm Jupiter TOI-4914 b orbiting a metal-poor star, and the hot Jupiters TOI-2714 b and TOI-2981 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.07520","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3884 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the first sub-Saturn known to transit a mid-M dwarf (Almenara et al. 2022). Its star is bright and small, so the planet is a top target for atmosphere studies with the James Webb Space Telescope (Almenara et al. 2022).","observed":"The star carries a giant dark spot near its pole. The planet crosses this spot during every transit, which is rare (Almenara et al. 2022; Mori et al. 2025). Each transit shows a different spot-crossing shape, and these changes reveal that the star rotates once every 11.1 days and is seen almost pole-on (Murray et al. 2026). The spot is not exactly on the pole (Mori et al. 2025). Seven transits observed with the James Webb Space Telescope span 0.6 to 5.3 micrometres, giving a spectrum of the spot itself (Murray et al. 2026). Brightness changes seen over many transits show that the orbit is misaligned with the star's spin, by about 77 degrees (Tamburo et al. 2025).","system":"The star is a fully convective M4 dwarf. Its spin axis points far from the planet's orbit, which is nearly polar (Murray et al. 2026; Mori et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The temperature and shape of starspots on M dwarfs are not well understood. Observations from the ground, taken at the same time as the Webb transits, aim to measure the spot temperature (Stephens et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3884b-Almenaraetal2022","cite":"Almenara et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. M. Almenara, X. Bonfils, T. Forveille et al.","title":"TOI-3884 b: A rare 6-R<SUB>E</SUB> planet that transits a low-mass star with a giant and likely polar spot","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.10909","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3884b-Morietal2025","cite":"Mori et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mayuko Mori, Akihiko Fukui, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"Multiband, Multiepoch Photometry of the Spot-crossing System TOI-3884: Refined System Geometry and Spot Properties","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06445","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3884b-Murrayetal2026","cite":"Murray et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"C. A. Murray, L. Garcia, B. V. Rackham et al.","title":"A Panchromatic JWST Spectrum of a Giant Starspot on the Fully Convective M Dwarf TOI-3884","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15414","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3884b-Tamburoetal2025","cite":"Tamburo et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Patrick Tamburo, Samuel W. Yee, Juliana García-Mejía et al.","title":"Spot-crossing Variations Confirm a Misaligned Orbit for a Planet Transiting an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.11998","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3884b-Stephensetal2026","cite":"Stephens et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Autumn Stephens, Zachory Berta-Thompson, Catriona Murray","title":"The Chromatic Contaminant: Ground-Based Spectrophotometry of TOI-3884's Persistent Spot","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 41378 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour HIP 41378 d. That planet was seen to transit only twice, three years apart (Sulis et al. 2024). A search for another transit with the CHEOPS space telescope, at the time predicted by the most likely 278.36-day period, found nothing (Sulis et al. 2024). Large timing shifts of more than 22.4 hours caused by planetary tugs could explain the miss (Sulis et al. 2024). If the period is not 278.36 days, the likeliest alternatives are 101.22 or 371.14 days. TESS was due to test the shortest of these in Sector 88, starting in January 2025 (Sulis et al. 2024).","system":"The system holds several planets. Planet d has an uncertain orbit that affects what is known about the whole system (Sulis et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The orbital period of planet d is not settled, and large transit timing variations are possible (Sulis et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip41378c-Sulisetal2024","cite":"Sulis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Sulis, L. Borsato, S. Grouffal et al.","title":"HIP 41378 observed by CHEOPS: Where is planet d?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20077","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-423 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about half the mass of Jupiter, 0.595 Jupiter masses, and a radius of 1.192 Jupiter radii, so its density is low at 0.459 grams per cubic centimetre (Gandolfi et al. 2015). Velocity measurements from the FIES spectrograph at the Nordic Optical Telescope were combined with the full Kepler light curve (Gandolfi et al. 2015). The transit depth in the Kepler data varies from quarter to quarter by about 4.3 percent, repeating every four quarters. Wrong estimates of how much light from nearby stars contaminates the measurement are the likely cause (Gandolfi et al. 2015).","system":"The host star is a G4 dwarf aged 11 billion years (Gandolfi et al. 2015). The star is very old for a Sun-like star.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler423b-Gandolfietal2015","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. Gandolfi, H. Parviainen, H. J. Deeg et al.","title":"Kepler-423b: a half-Jupiter mass planet transiting a very old solar-like star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8245","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1224 c":{"text":{"why":"Young planets help show how planets form and evolve. Multi-planet systems allow comparison between planets under the same conditions (Thao et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is 2.88 Earth radii in size and orbits every 17.95 days (Thao et al. 2024). The host star is small and cool, with a radius of 0.44 solar radii and a temperature of 3326 K (Thao et al. 2024).","system":"The star belongs to a young group called MELANGE-5, which is 210 million years old (Thao et al. 2024). The age comes from four methods: isochrone fitting, lithium abundance, gyrochronology and Gaia excess variability (Thao et al. 2024). The group lies close to younger groups, and its boundaries are not yet mapped (Thao et al. 2024). The inner planet, TOI-1224 b, is 2.10 Earth radii and orbits every 4.18 days (Thao et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1224c-Thaoetal2024","cite":"Thao et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Pa Chia Thao, Andrew W. Mann, Madyson G. Barber et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). X. A Two-planet System in the 210 Myr MELANGE-5 Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05234","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer planets Kepler-90 g and h. Their transits arrive up to 25 hours early or late because the two planets pull on each other (Liang et al. 2021). Fitting these shifts gives masses of 15.0 and 203 Earth masses (Liang et al. 2021). Kepler-90 g has an unusually low density of 0.15 grams per cubic centimetre, which makes it a super-puff (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"The periods of planets g and h are close to a 2:3 ratio, but the pair is not locked in resonance. The system is stable for billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"Planet interactions may have raised the orbits' eccentricities and broken the resonant lock after the planets formed (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351c-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-119 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with photometry from the EulerCam and TRAPPIST telescopes and with CORALIE spectra (Maxted et al. 2016). A later search of multi-sector TESS photometry found no sign of additional planets in the system. Transiting companions are ruled out down to the size of mini-Neptunes or Neptunes, and the timing of transits shows no trends or periodic shifts (Maciejewski 2020).","system":"The planet appears to be alone. The same is true of seven other hot Jupiter systems in the same search (Maciejewski 2020).","history":"The lack of companions favours high-eccentricity migration, in which a giant planet is thrown onto a stretched orbit that shrinks to a tight orbit, as the way these planets reached their close orbits (Maciejewski 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp119b-Maxtedetal2016","cite":"Maxted et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Five transiting hot Jupiters discovered using WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST: WASP-119 b, WASP-124 b, WASP-126 b, WASP-129 b, and WASP-133 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.01740","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp119b-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 19994 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a triple system, and the planet circles the main star. A far-infrared excess shows that dust surrounds the stars (Wiegert et al. 2016). The best-fitting model puts the dust in a disc around the secondary pair of stars, held within 40 astronomical units by gravity (Wiegert et al. 2016). Computer simulations show that the whole system is dynamically stable (Wiegert et al. 2016). Seismology with the HARPS spectrograph has also been used to refine the parameters of the F-type main star (Deal et al. 2017).","system":"The star is slightly enriched in heavy elements, with an iron abundance of +0.09 relative to the Sun, as are most stars with planets (Smith et al. 2001).","history":"The planet is one of ten Jupiter-like planets on intermediate or long orbits with a noticeable eccentricity found by the CORALIE survey (Mayor et al. 2004). Models of other planets in similar systems suggest that giant planet migration can bring much more water to rocky planets as they form (Carter-Bond et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd19994b-Wiegertetal2016","cite":"Wiegert et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. Wiegert, V. Faramaz, F. Cruz-Saenz de Miera","title":"94 Ceti: a triple star with a planet and dust disc","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03038","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd19994b-Dealetal2017","cite":"Deal et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Deal, M. E. Escobar, S. Vauclair et al.","title":"Asteroseismology of the exoplanet-host F-type star 94 Ceti: Impact of atomic diffusion on the stellar parameters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03170","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd19994b-Smithetal2001","cite":"Smith et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Verne V. Smith, Katia Cunha, Daniela Lazzaro","title":"The Abundance Distribution in the Extrasolar-Planet Host Star HD 19994","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0103063","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd19994b-Mayoretal2004","cite":"Mayor et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mayor, S. Udry, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XII. Orbital solutions for 16 extra-solar planets discovered with CORALIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0310316","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd19994b-CarterBondetal2014","cite":"Carter-Bond et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Jade C. Carter-Bond, David P. O'Brien, Sean N. Raymond","title":"Migration & Extra-solar Terrestrial Planets: Watering the Planets","journal":"Formation, Detection, and Characterization of Extrasolar Habitable Planets","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S174392131301288X","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"55 Cnc b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its innermost planet 55 Cnc e and the star itself. The star's radius is 0.943 solar radii, and its age is about 10.2 billion years (von Braun et al. 2011). The planets are probably in a flat, coplanar arrangement, but a distant stellar companion makes the whole set of orbits precess, so the planets are likely tilted relative to the star's spin (Kaib et al. 2011). The most likely projected tilt is about 50 degrees, with about a 30 percent chance that the orbits run against the star's spin (Kaib et al. 2011).","system":"Planet e is an ultra-short-period super-Earth. A large tilt between the innermost and outermost planets may result from tidal evolution of planet e (Hansen & Zink 2015). Planet f spends most of its eccentric orbit in the star's habitable zone (von Braun et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-55cncb-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Theo A. ten Brummelaar et al.","title":"55 Cancri: Stellar Astrophysical Parameters, a Planet in the Habitable Zone, and Implications for the Radius of a Transiting Super-Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncb-Kaibetal2011","cite":"Kaib et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nathan A. Kaib, Sean N. Raymond, Martin J. Duncan","title":"55 Cancri: A Coplanar Planetary System That is Likely Misaligned with Its Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5911","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncb-HansenZink2015","cite":"Hansen & Zink 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Bradley M. S. Hansen, Jonathon Zink","title":"On the potentially dramatic history of the super-Earth ρ 55 Cancri e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05539","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-561 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits one of the oldest and most metal-poor stars known to host a planet this small, a member of the Galaxy's thick disc (Brinkman et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet's mass was measured at 2.24 Earth masses and its radius at 1.37 Earth radii (Brinkman et al. 2023). Its density, 4.8 grams per cubic centimetre, makes it one of the lowest-density super-Earths measured (Brinkman et al. 2023). Both an iron-poor rocky make-up and a rocky planet with a gas envelope fit the numbers (Brinkman et al. 2023). The equilibrium temperature of about 2300 K means that any envelope would be heavy gases such as water vapour, carbon dioxide or silicate vapour. It is likely not primordial (Brinkman et al. 2023).","system":"The star is about 10 billion years old and has an iron abundance of -0.41 relative to the Sun (Weiss et al. 2021). Further planets orbit farther out. Mini-Neptunes TOI-561 c, d and e have periods of 10.78, 25.6 and 77.2 days (Lacedelli et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has a volatile envelope or a rocky iron-poor interior is not settled, since the composition depends on the chosen stellar parameters (Brinkman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi561b-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-561 b: A Low-density Ultra-short-period \"Rocky\" Planet around a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06665","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561b-Weissetal2021","cite":"Weiss et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Lauren M. Weiss, Fei Dai, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. II. An Ultra-short-period Rocky Planet and Its Siblings Transiting the Galactic Thick-disk Star TOI-561","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03071","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561b-Lacedellietal2021","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"G. Lacedelli, L. Malavolta, L. Borsato et al.","title":"An unusually low density ultra-short period super-Earth and three mini-Neptunes around the old star TOI-561","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.02332","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 45364 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Precise measurements of the star's back-and-forth motion revealed two planets, with the outer one (HD 45364 c) having a minimum mass of 0.658 Jupiter masses at 0.897 AU (Correia et al. 2009). The two planets are locked in a 3:2 mean motion resonance, meaning their orbital periods stand in a ratio of 3 to 2. Computer simulations show that this resonance prevents close encounters and keeps the system stable for 5 billion years (Correia et al. 2009).","system":"The inner planet has a minimum mass of 0.187 Jupiter masses and orbits at 0.681 AU. The orbits are slightly eccentric, with values of 0.168 for the inner and 0.097 for the outer planet (Correia et al. 2009).","history":"A resonant pair like the two planets of HD 45364 had not been seen before around another star, though the Solar System has an analogue in Neptune and Pluto (Correia et al. 2009). Resonant pairs like this may place limits on how planets form and migrate (Correia et al. 2009).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd45364c-Correiaetal2009","cite":"Correia et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. C. M. Correia, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XVI. HD 45364, a pair of planets in a 3:2 mean motion resonance","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0597","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1052 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed as a real planet with precise velocity measurements of its star from HARPS, and its properties were fitted jointly from those and the transit light curve (Armstrong et al. 2023). Its mean density is 3.9 g per cubic centimetre, consistent with an internal structure similar to Neptune's (Armstrong et al. 2023). The orbit may be somewhat eccentric, at 0.18, though this is only marginally significant (Armstrong et al. 2023).","system":"A second planet, TOI-1052 c, shows up only in the star's velocity data and is not seen to transit. It has a minimum mass of 34.3 Earth masses and a 35.8-day period, placing it just inside the 4:1 mean-motion resonance with planet b (Armstrong et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1052b-Armstrongetal2023","cite":"Armstrong et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"David J. Armstrong, Ares Osborn, Vardan Adibekyan et al.","title":"Discovery and characterization of two Neptune-mass planets orbiting HD 212729 with TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.11566","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-102 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its larger neighbours Kepler-102 d and e, whose masses were measured with 111 new velocity measurements of the star (Brinkman et al. 2023). Kepler-102 b is the innermost of five tightly packed transiting planets with periods of 5 to 28 days (Volk & Malhotra 2020). Simulations show that its orbit is the weak point of the system. Slow transfer of orbital disturbance inward can stretch its orbit and lead to planet collisions in about 80% of simulations. To stay stable, the planet likely needs a mass of at least about 0.1 Earth masses (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","system":"The star is active, which complicates velocity measurements (Brinkman et al. 2023). The planets lie near, but are not locked in, mutual resonances. The period of planet b falls between the 2:1 resonance of planet d and the 4:3 resonance of planet c (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The star is estimated to be more than 2 billion years old (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","history":"","open":"The mass of Kepler-102 b has not been measured; only the stability limit on its mass is known (Volk & Malhotra 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler102b-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, James Cadman, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"Kepler-102: Masses and Compositions for a Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Orbiting an Active Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05196","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler102b-VolkMalhotra2020","cite":"Volk & Malhotra 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Volk, R. Malhotra","title":"Kepler-102: a case study for using dynamical constraints to characterize exoplanet systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-70 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits the larger star of a binary whose two stars are 3.3 arcseconds apart, or at least 800 AU. The host is an evolved G4 star aged 9 to 10 billion years, and its companion is a K3 star (Anderson et al. 2014). Starlight from the companion leaks into the measurements. A later reanalysis that corrected for it found properties very similar to those obtained when ignoring it (Southworth et al. 2020).","system":"The companion K3 star is a second sun-like neighbour, resolved on the sky (Anderson et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp70ab-Andersonetal2014","cite":"Anderson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, L. Delrez et al.","title":"Three newly discovered sub-Jupiter-mass planets: WASP-69b and WASP-84b transit active K dwarfs and WASP-70Ab transits the evolved primary of a G4+K3 binary","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5654","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp70ab-Southworthetal2020","cite":"Southworth et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Southworth, A. J. Bohn, M. A. Kenworthy et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. II. Revised properties of transiting planetary systems with companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08225","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-237 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's very large radius makes it a prime target for follow-up study, and its star is fairly bright (V = 11.6) (Soto et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet has a radius of 1.65 Jupiter radii and a mass of 1.60 Jupiter masses, which leads to the conclusion that it is a highly inflated hot Jupiter, puffed up well beyond what its mass would normally give (Soto et al. 2018). Later TESS data combined with the original K2 data refined the timing of its transits, which cuts the uncertainty in predicting future transits. That helps planning of observations with the James Webb Space Telescope (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2237b-Sotoetal2018","cite":"Soto et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. G. Soto, M. R. Díaz, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"K2-237 b and K2-238 b: discovery and characterization of two new transiting hot Jupiters from K2","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.07959","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2237b-IkwutUkwaetal2020","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mma Ikwut-Ukwa, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"The K2 and TESS Synergy. I. Updated Ephemerides and Parameters for K2-114, K2-167, K2-237, and K2-261","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00678","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-108 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-108 holds two giant planets of roughly Saturn mass, with periods of about 49 and 190 days. Their orbits are tilted relative to each other by about 24 degrees, and are moderately eccentric (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). Such a tilt is unusual, since most well-studied giant-planet systems, including the Solar System, are nearly flat (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","system":"The star has a wide binary companion at about 300 AU (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","history":"The orbital layout is inconsistent with a purely disk-migration origin. Planet-planet scattering, which often gives tilted, eccentric orbits, is one way to explain it (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler108c-MillsFabrycky2017","cite":"Mills & Fabrycky 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky","title":"Kepler-108: A Mutually Inclined Giant Planet System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04485","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"rho CrB b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little direct observation of the planet's atmosphere exists. A computer model of the heating of close-in giant planets by their stars was extended to this planet, placed at 0.23 AU, the widest orbit in the study (Goukenleuque et al. 1999). The model considered masses of 0.5 to 2 Jupiter masses and an atmosphere made of water, carbon monoxide, methane, ammonia and hydrogen-helium gas. In none of the cases did the model temperatures rise with height in the upper atmosphere (Goukenleuque et al. 1999).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-rhocrbb-Goukenleuqueetal1999","cite":"Goukenleuque et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"C. Goukenleuque, B. Bezard, E. Lellouch","title":"A Radiative Equilibrium Model for 51-Peg Type Planets","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #31","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-329 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of two warm Saturns in a study of eight warm gas giants whose spin-orbit alignment was measured. The star's orbit alignment showed a slightly misaligned planet, with a sky-projected angle of about 15 to 25 degrees and a true three-dimensional angle of about 15 to 30 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025). Searches for water in its atmosphere with the ESPRESSO spectrograph found nothing. The planet's motion during transit changes by too little to separate its signal from the star's, and its absorption lines are weak (Prinoth et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters around single stars appear mostly aligned, which suggests they form in disks aligned with their star's spin. The two lighter warm Saturns, including K2-329 b, are slightly misaligned (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":"Whether water is present in the atmosphere is not known (Prinoth et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2329b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2329b-Prinothetal2024","cite":"Prinoth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Bibiana Prinoth, Elyar Sedaghati, Julia V. Seidel et al.","title":"High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy of Warm Jupiters: An ESPRESSO Sample with Predictions for ANDES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08558","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 43691 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up searched ELODIE data and favoured stars rich in heavy elements (da Silva et al. 2007).","observed":"The host star is metal-rich, and the planet has a minimum mass of 2.5 Jupiter masses and an eccentricity of 0.14 (da Silva et al. 2007). Extra velocity data improved its orbit, and photometry searched for transits at the predicted times.","system":"Infrared imaging confirmed that the star has a companion star that moves through space with it (Ngo et al. 2017). A survey of 144 giant-planet systems found no difference in orbital properties between planets in single-star and multi-star systems (Ngo et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd43691b-daSilvaetal2007","cite":"da Silva et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. da Silva, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"ELODIE metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. IV. Intermediate period planets orbiting the stars HD 43691 and HD 132406","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.0958","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd43691b-Ngoetal2017","cite":"Ngo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Henry Ngo, Heather A. Knutson, Marta L. Bryan et al.","title":"No Difference in Orbital Parameters of RV-detected Giant Planets between 0.1 and 5 au in Single versus Multi-stellar Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02326","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5076 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of about 16 Earth masses, a radius of 3.2 Earth radii and a density of 2.8 g per cubic centimetre. The density suggests a large share by volume of volatiles on top of a rocky core (Montalto et al. 2024). The host is a metal-rich K dwarf that belongs to the old thick-disk or thin-to-thick transition population of stars in the Galaxy (Montalto et al. 2024).","system":"The star forms a pair with an M-dwarf companion at a projected separation of 2178 AU (Montalto et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5076b-Montaltoetal2024","cite":"Montalto et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Montalto, N. Greco, K. Biazzo et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. LVII. TOI-5076b: A warm sub-Neptune planet orbiting a thin-to-thick-disk transition star in a wide binary system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.18950","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-63 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was a candidate target for the James Webb Space Telescope early science programme, since it is inflated and gives a large signal (Kilpatrick et al. 2017).","observed":"The Hubble Space Telescope recorded its near-infrared transmission spectrum, which is starlight filtered through the planet's atmosphere during transit. Several independent analyses found a water absorption feature, with significance from 1 to 3 sigma. The feature is weaker than a clear atmosphere of solar composition would give. Clouds or a different composition could explain it, and the two cannot be told apart (Kilpatrick et al. 2018). A small bump near 1.55 microns fits a high abundance of hydrogen cyanide, though that is questionable (Kilpatrick et al. 2018).","system":"A search of TESS data found no further transiting planets down to 2 to 4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp63b-Kilpatricketal2017","cite":"Kilpatrick et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Brian Kilpatrick, Patricio Cubillos, Giovanni Bruno et al.","title":"Community Targets for JWST's Early Release Science Program: Evaluation of Transiting Exoplanet WASP-63b.","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #230","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp63b-Kilpatricketal2018","cite":"Kilpatrick et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Brian M. Kilpatrick, Patricio E. Cubillos, Kevin B. Stevenson et al.","title":"Community Targets of JWST’s Early Release Science Program: Evaluation of WASP-63b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.07421","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp63b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2017-BLG-0673L b":{"text":{"why":"The signal was found in a search of peripheral bulge fields, where observations are sparse, and was missed earlier for that reason (Han et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet's signal came from the background star crossing a region of strongly boosted brightness created by the planet, known as a caustic. Because coverage of the event was sparse, the signal had gone unnoticed (Han et al. 2022). Analysis gave a single, unique planetary solution, unlike the second planet in the same study, which has several possible solutions (Han et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2017blg0673lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"KMT-2017-BLG-0673Lb and KMT-2019-BLG-0414Lb: Two microlensing planets detected in peripheral fields of KMTNet survey","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.00112","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 229 A c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Optical photometry and spectra of the star from the 1980s found a low level of flare activity and no sign of a large-scale uneven spot pattern (Byrne et al. 1985). X-ray emission from the corona and energy losses from the lower chromosphere point the same way (Byrne et al. 1985).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj229ac-Byrneetal1985","cite":"Byrne et al. 1985","year":"1985","authors":"P. B. Byrne, J. G. Doyle, J. W. Menzies","title":"Optical photometry and spectroscopy of the flare star Gliese 229 (=HD42581).","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/214.2.119","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-858 B b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits on a polar path. Two transits observed with CORALIE showed a sky-projected angle of 99.3 degrees between the planet's orbit and the star's spin, from the Rossiter-McLaughlin signal. The planet has a mass of about 1.1 Jupiter masses (Hagelberg et al. 2023).","system":"The host is the secondary of a loose binary. The primary is a 1.15 solar-mass F9 star about 11 arcseconds away, and it was also monitored for planets, setting upper limits on any companions (Hagelberg et al. 2023).","history":"Simulations show that the neighbouring star is too distant to have trapped the planet in a Kozai-Lidov resonance, in which a companion star slowly tilts a planet's orbit. A different dynamical history or a primordial origin must explain the misalignment (Hagelberg et al. 2023).","open":"How the orbit became polar is not known (Hagelberg et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi858bb-Hagelbergetal2023","cite":"Hagelberg et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Hagelberg, L. D. Nielsen, M. Attia et al.","title":"TOI-858 B b: A hot Jupiter on a polar orbit in a loose binary","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.11390","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 213885 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit, so it is seen only through the star's velocity wobble. The signal has a period of 4.78 days and a minimum mass of 19.9 Earth masses, consistent with a Neptune-mass planet (Espinoza et al. 2020).","system":"The inner planet HD 213885 b is an ultra-short-period super-Earth with a 1.008-day orbit, 8.8 Earth masses and 1.74 Earth radii. Its composition is similar to Earth's but enriched in iron (Espinoza et al. 2020). It resembles 55 Cancri e. The star is the second brightest known to host such a transiting planet, making the system good for atmospheric study (Espinoza et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd213885c-Espinozaetal2020","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Néstor Espinoza, Rafael Brahm, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"HD 213885b: a transiting 1-d-period super-Earth with an Earth-like composition around a bright (V = 7.9) star unveiled by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.07694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 103197 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a HARPS survey that reported three new planets together, around HD 85390, HD 90156 and HD 103197 (Mordasini et al. 2011).","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 31.2 Earth masses on a circular orbit of 47.8 days (Mordasini et al. 2011). That is an intermediate mass between Neptune-like and Jupiter-like planets. Core accretion models of planet formation predict that planets of 30 to 100 Earth masses should be rare inside a few AU (Mordasini et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"How such intermediate-mass planets form is a question raised by the discovery (Mordasini et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd103197b-Mordasinietal2011","cite":"Mordasini et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Mordasini, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIV. Companions to HD 85390, HD 90156, and HD 103197: a Neptune analog and two intermediate-mass planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.0856","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6628 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 0.74 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.98 Jupiter radii. Its orbit has an eccentricity of 0.670, one of the most eccentric orbits of all known warm giants (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). The host star is metal-rich (Tala Pinto et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6628b-Pintoetal2025","cite":"Pinto et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marcelo Tala Pinto, Andrés Jordán, Lorena Acuña et al.","title":"Three warm Jupiters orbiting TOI-6628, TOI-3837, and TOI-5027 and one sub-Saturn orbiting TOI-2328","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02069","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-35 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A high-resolution imaging survey of 74 transiting-planet host stars with the AstraLux Norte Lucky Imaging camera found a new stellar source within 1 arcsecond of HAT-P-35 (Wöllert & Brandner 2015). Such close stars matter because unrecognised light from them can bias the planet and star parameters derived from the transit (Wöllert & Brandner 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp35b-WllertBrandner2015","cite":"Wöllert & Brandner 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maria Wöllert, Wolfgang Brandner","title":"A Lucky Imaging search for stellar sources near 74 transit hosts","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.05456","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2022-BLG-563L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet showed up as a short dip near the peak of the lensing event's brightness curve. The planet-to-host mass ratio is about 8 times 10 to the minus 4 (Han et al. 2024). Statistical estimates put the planet at about 0.40 Jupiter masses, orbiting a host of 0.48 solar masses about 6.53 kiloparsecs away (Han et al. 2024). A common ambiguity between an inner and outer solution leaves the planet's projected distance from its star uncertain (Han et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's separation from its host star is uncertain because two solutions fit the data (Han et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2022blg563lb-Hanetal2024","cite":"Han et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Cheongho Han, Youn Kil Jung, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"Three sub-Jovian-mass microlensing planets: MOA-2022-BLG-563Lb, KMT-2023-BLG-0469Lb, and KMT-2023-BLG-0735Lb","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202347951","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3321 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of nine hot Jupiters reported together in the first paper of the Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets survey. That survey aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters around FGK stars brighter than Gaia magnitude 12.5, using the same methods for every planet (Schulte et al. 2024). A uniform sample is meant to test competing ideas for how hot Jupiters formed, such as forming in place, migrating through the gas disk, or moving inward on a highly eccentric orbit (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3321b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3321b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2338 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey data first showed only one transit of the planet, so its orbital period was not known at first (Brahm et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-2338 b was first noticed as a single dip in the light of its star, seen in full-frame images from the TESS space telescope (Brahm et al. 2023). Follow-up velocity measurements of the star and extra light curves were needed to pin down the 22.65-day orbit (Brahm et al. 2023). The planet has about 5.98 Jupiter masses but only about one Jupiter radius, so it is very dense (Brahm et al. 2023). Its orbit is highly eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.676 (Brahm et al. 2023). It holds more heavy elements than its host star does (Brahm et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a slightly metal-rich late F or early G type star (Brahm et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2338b-Brahmetal2023","cite":"Brahm et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Melissa J. Hobson et al.","title":"Three Long-period Transiting Giant Planets from TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02139","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-445 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a close match to GJ 1214 b in size and in the kind of star it orbits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","observed":"Kepler-445 c belongs to a system of three small, short-period transiting planets around a mid-type M dwarf (Muirhead et al. 2015). The star is rich in heavy elements compared with the Sun (Muirhead et al. 2015). The planet is similar to GJ 1214 b, both in radius and in the properties of its host star (Muirhead et al. 2015). Careful fitting of the transits, with the star's density held to a tight range, removed a known mix-up between planet size and transit geometry for short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"Three planets crowd close to the star, in what is called a compact multiple system. About 21 percent of mid-type M dwarfs are estimated to host such compact systems (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler445c-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 220674823 c":{"text":{"why":"The system holds two planets of almost the same mass but very different densities, which makes it a good test of how composition and distance from the star are linked (Guenther et al. 2017).","observed":"This planet orbits the star also called K2-106, together with an inner planet on a 0.57-day orbit (Guenther et al. 2017). Its mass was measured from velocity wobbles of the star and found to be about 5.8 Earth masses, with a radius of about 2.5 Earth radii (Guenther et al. 2017). Its density is low, about 2.0 grams per cubic centimetre (Guenther et al. 2017). The inner planet is dense, about 13.1 grams per cubic centimetre, and has a similar mass (Guenther et al. 2017).","system":"The inner planet is metal-rich and very dense, while the outer planet is much fluffier (Guenther et al. 2017).","history":"Planets between 2 and 15 Earth masses differ widely in density, and their make-up gives clues to how they formed and lost atmosphere (Guenther et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic220674823c-Guentheretal2017","cite":"Guenther et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"E. W. Guenther, O. Barragán, F. Dai et al.","title":"K2-106, a system containing a metal-rich planet and a planet of lower density","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04163","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 433 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The star was suspected to have a very faint companion, first hinted at by a wobble in Hipparcos satellite data and later by speckle imaging (Dieterich et al. 2009). The companion is estimated to have a mass between 0.088 and 0.122 solar masses, right at the boundary between stars and brown dwarfs (Dieterich et al. 2009). Its orbit around the main star was expected to take about 1.5 years (Dieterich et al. 2009).","system":"A faint companion sits close to the star, at the dividing line between low-mass stars and brown dwarfs (Dieterich et al. 2009).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj433b-Dieterichetal2009","cite":"Dieterich et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Sergio Dieterich, Todd Henry, David Golimowski","title":"GJ 433: Resolving a Suspected Binary Near the Stellar/Substellar Boundary","journal":"NOAO Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-20 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-20 b is among the most compact planets known, with a mean density of about 8.87 grams per cubic centimetre (Deleuil et al. 2012). Models suggest about 800 Earth masses of heavy elements if they sit in a central core, which would require changes to planet formation or structure models (Deleuil et al. 2012). More realistic models, with heavy elements mixed through the gas envelope, need less (Deleuil et al. 2012). The orbit is quite eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.56 (Deleuil et al. 2012). Later transit timing checks refined the orbital period (Raetz et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a G-type star that spins slowly, with a spin period near 11.5 days if its axis is aligned with the orbit (Deleuil et al. 2012).","history":"Tidal evolution calculations show the planet is one of very few that is stable against tides and is evolving toward a state where the orbit, planet spin and star spin all share one period (Deleuil et al. 2012).","open":"How a planet this dense formed remains unclear (Deleuil et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot20b-Deleuiletal2012","cite":"Deleuil et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Deleuil, A. S. Bonomo, S. Ferraz-Mello et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XX. CoRoT-20b: A very high density, high eccentricity transiting giant planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.3203","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot20b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is hot and the planet orbits close to it, so the planet is among the hottest known (Pollacco et al. 2008).","observed":"WASP-3 b is a gas giant strongly heated by an F-type star (Pollacco et al. 2008). Observations of its eclipses behind the star by CHEOPS, TESS and Spitzer measured how bright its day side is, with a depth of 92 parts per million in the CHEOPS band and 83 parts per million in the TESS band (Scandariato et al. 2024). The orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a small sky-projected angle consistent with zero (Simpson et al. 2010). A claimed second planet that tugs on the timing of its transits was not confirmed, though the period is not perfectly constant either (Nascimbeni et al. 2013).","system":"Adaptive optics imaging shows no nearby stellar companions (Pollacco et al. 2008).","history":"An aligned orbit is thought to point to a gentle migration, such as through the disc of gas around the young star (Simpson et al. 2010).","open":"Small changes in transit timing remain unexplained, and a perturber has not been ruled in or out (Nascimbeni et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp3b-Pollaccoetal2008","cite":"Pollacco et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"D. Pollacco, I. Skillen, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-3b: a strongly irradiated transiting gas-giant planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.0126","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp3b-Scandariatoetal2024","cite":"Scandariato et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Scandariato, L. Carone, P. E. Cubillos et al.","title":"The CHEOPS view of the climate of WASP-3 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16268","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp3b-Simpsonetal2010","cite":"Simpson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"E. K. Simpson, D. Pollacco, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"The spin-orbit alignment of the transiting exoplanet WASP-3b from Rossiter-McLaughlin observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3643","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp3b-Nascimbenietal2013","cite":"Nascimbeni et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"V. Nascimbeni, A. Cunial, S. Murabito et al.","title":"TASTE. III. A homogeneous study of transit time variations in WASP-3b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.3045","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":23,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 68475 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 68475 b is a cold giant planet on a very long orbit, with a period of about 7832 days, or roughly 21 years (Wu et al. 2026). Its mass was fixed by combining velocity data with Gaia and Hipparcos measurements of how the star's position shifts over 25 years (Wu et al. 2026). Combining the two methods gives a true mass of about 5.16 Jupiter masses, rather than only a minimum (Wu et al. 2026). It is the first confirmed planet in its system (Wu et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"How such cold Jupiters form is still uncertain (Wu et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd68475b-Wuetal2026","cite":"Wu et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yiyun Wu, Guang-Yao Xiao, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Detection of Four Cold Jupiters through Combined Analyses of Radial Velocity and Astrometry Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11280","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3235 b":{"text":{"why":"Planet formation models do not predict gas giants around stars this small (Hobson et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-3235 b is a Jupiter-sized planet around an M4 dwarf, a red dwarf star with a mass close to the point where stars become fully convective (Hobson et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is about 604 K, well below the level thought needed to puff up hot Jupiters (Hobson et al. 2023). It is one of the best-suited giants around red dwarfs for studying its atmosphere (Hobson et al. 2023). JWST observations of its atmosphere were planned, to measure its heavy-element content and carbon-to-oxygen ratio (Jordan et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Slow growth and low disc masses around small stars should block giant planet formation, yet such planets exist (Jordan et al. 2023).","open":"How the planet formed is not known (Jordan et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3235b-Hobsonetal2023","cite":"Hobson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Melissa J. Hobson, Andrés Jordán, E. M. Bryant et al.","title":"TOI-3235 b: A Transiting Giant Planet around an M4 Dwarf Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10008","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3235b-Jordanetal2023","cite":"Jordan et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Andres Jordan, Daniel Bayliss, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"Problem Planets: Understanding the Formation of Giant Planets around Low Mass Stars","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 2","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-176 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is a sub-giant, already somewhat evolved, which makes the planet's heating differ from a Sun-like host (Cooke et al. 2020).","observed":"WASP-176 b is a hot Jupiter with a mass of 0.86 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.5 Jupiter radii (Cooke et al. 2020). The result is a low bulk density, about 0.23 times that of Jupiter (Cooke et al. 2020). It orbits in 3.9 days around an F9 sub-giant star with 1.9 times the Sun's radius (Cooke et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp176b-Cookeetal2020","cite":"Cooke et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"B. F. Cooke, D. Pollacco, Y. Almleaky et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Observation & radial velocity of WASP-150 & WASP-176 (Cooke+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51590255","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BEBOP-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the first circumbinary system to be found by radial velocities in a previously unknown case (Baycroft et al. 2025).","observed":"BEBOP-3 b orbits two stars rather than one (Baycroft et al. 2025). Its mass is about 0.56 Jupiter masses and its orbit takes about 550 days, with an eccentricity of 0.25 (Baycroft et al. 2025). Compared with most known circumbinary planets, its period is long relative to the binary's, and its orbit is more eccentric (Baycroft et al. 2025). A possible outer planet with a period near 1400 days is also suggested (Baycroft et al. 2025).","system":"The two stars eclipse each other every 13.2 days, as a ninth-magnitude F9 V star totally hides a faint M dwarf (Maxted et al. 2025). The larger star has a radius of 1.386 solar radii and the smaller 0.274 solar radii (Maxted et al. 2025).","history":"Stable orbits for further inner planets are possible near the instability region where the Kepler circumbinary planets sit (Baycroft et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bebop3b-Baycroftetal2025","cite":"Baycroft et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Thomas A. Baycroft, Alexandre Santerne, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"BEBOP VII. SOPHIE discovery of BEBOP-3b, a circumbinary giant planet on an eccentric orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.14615","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bebop3b-Maxtedetal2025","cite":"Maxted et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, N. J. Miller, T. A. Baycroft et al.","title":"Fundamental effective temperature measurements for eclipsing binary stars ─ VI. Improved methodology and application to the circumbinary planet host star BEBOP-3","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23218","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1194 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1194 b is a sub-Saturn-size planet with a mass of about 0.456 Jupiter masses (Wang et al. 2024). Its confirmation came from multi-band ground-based photometry, gathered in a Sino-German campaign that uses small and medium telescopes to check candidates from TESS (Wang et al. 2024). The star also has a very low mass companion star, TOI-1251 B, with a mass of about 96.5 Jupiter masses (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a G dwarf (Wang et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1194b-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jia-Qi Wang, Xiao-Jun Jiang, Jie Zheng et al.","title":"Confirmation of a Sub-Saturn-size Transiting Exoplanet Orbiting a G Dwarf: TOI-1194 b and a Very Low Mass Companion Star: TOI-1251 B from TESS","journal":"Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12458","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2022-BLG-1818L b":{"text":{"why":"The microlensing survey that turned it up used follow-up of events where the background star is strongly magnified (Li et al. 2026).","observed":"KMT-2022-BLG-1818L b is the more massive of two planets found in one system, the sixth microlensing two-planet system known (Li et al. 2026). It has a super-Jovian mass ratio to its star of about 5 times 10 to the power of minus 3 (Li et al. 2026). The host is most likely a K dwarf in the Galactic disk (Li et al. 2026). The planet is probably a super-Jupiter on a Jupiter-like orbit (Li et al. 2026).","system":"A second, Saturn-class planet sits in the system, on either a Mercury-like or a Saturn-like orbit (Li et al. 2026). It was spotted because a one-planet model gave implausible physical values (Li et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The second planet's mass ratio differs by a factor of about 10 between the close and wide versions of the first planet's orbit, a new kind of degeneracy that calls for caution (Li et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2022blg1818lb-Lietal2026","cite":"Li et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Hongyu Li, Jiyuan Zhang, Cheongho Han et al.","title":"KMT-2022-BLG-1818Lb,c: A Cold Super-Jupiter with a Saturn Sibling","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.05093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"UKIRT-2017-BLG-001L b":{"text":{"why":"The event lies behind thick dust close to the Galactic centre, so only an infrared survey could have seen it (Shvartzvald et al. 2018).","observed":"UKIRT-2017-BLG-001L b is a giant planet with a mass ratio to its star of about 1.5 times that of Jupiter to the Sun (Shvartzvald et al. 2018). The event lies 0.35 degrees from the Galactic centre and suffers heavy dust dimming (Shvartzvald et al. 2018). The planet's mass is estimated at about 1.28 Jupiter masses, orbiting a star of about 0.81 solar masses at a projected separation of about 4.18 au (Shvartzvald et al. 2018). The system is about 6.3 kiloparsecs away and likely lies in the Galactic bulge (Shvartzvald et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The background source star may lie in the far side of the Galactic disk, which would make it the first microlensing source known there (Shvartzvald et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ukirt2017blg001lb-Shvartzvaldetal2018","cite":"Shvartzvald et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Y. Shvartzvald, S. Calchi Novati, B. S. Gaudi et al.","title":"UKIRT-2017-BLG-001Lb: A Giant Planet Detected through the Dust","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.06795","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1751 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1751 b is a warm sub-Neptune with a radius of about 2.77 Earth radii and a mass of about 14.5 Earth masses, giving a density of 3.6 grams per cubic centimetre (Desai et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is about 820 K (Desai et al. 2024). A signal at about 400 days appeared only in data from one observatory, and was judged to be an artefact of when the star was observed rather than a planet (Desai et al. 2024). Combining two observatories helped exclude it (Desai et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved, metal-poor, solar-type star (Desai et al. 2024).","history":"The metal-poor star suggests the planet may have formed in a water-enriched environment (Desai et al. 2024).","open":"Whether the interior is rocky or water-rich is not settled, though a volatile-rich interior is favoured (Desai et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1751b-Desaietal2024","cite":"Desai et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Desai, E. V. Turtelboom, C. K. Harada et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS-Keck Survey. XVIII. TOI-1751 radial velocity (Desai+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2267 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2267 is a cool binary of an M5 and an M6 star, and the system hosts three Earth-sized planets (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025). The two stars are only 0.384 arcseconds apart on the sky, a projected distance of about 8 au (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025). Most of what is known about the planets comes from light curves of the pair of stars (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025).","system":"The planets orbit a very tight pair of very small stars, with a projected separation of only about 8 au between them (Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2267d-ZunigaFernandezetal2025","cite":"Zuniga-Fernandez et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Zuniga-Fernandez, F. J. Pozuelos, M. Devora-Pajares et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-2267 light curves (Zuniga-Fernandez+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 203030 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 203030 b is a very red, young object far from its star, at 11.9 arcseconds, or 487 au in projection (Metchev & Hillenbrand 2006). Its infrared spectrum places it near the transition from L-type to T-type brown dwarfs, where dust clouds vanish from the atmosphere (Metchev & Hillenbrand 2006). It is only about 1206 K, much cooler than the roughly 1440 K of older objects of the same type (Metchev & Hillenbrand 2006). Hubble monitoring found a rotation period of about 7.5 hours, with brightness variations of 1.1 percent and 1.7 percent in two infrared bands (Miles-Páez et al. 2019). A patchy cloud layer sinking in the atmosphere explains these (Miles-Páez et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"Spectra show features of very low gravity, consistent with an age near 100 million years, and the object is likely of planetary mass (Miles-Páez et al. 2017).","open":"Whether the cool temperature comes from the object's young age, or from older field objects having been misjudged, was left open (Metchev & Hillenbrand 2006)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd203030b-MetchevHillenbrand2006","cite":"Metchev & Hillenbrand 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Stanimir A. Metchev, Lynne A. Hillenbrand","title":"HD 203030B: An Unusually Cool Young Substellar Companion near the L/T Transition","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0607514","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd203030b-MilesPezetal2019","cite":"Miles-Páez et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Paulo A. Miles-Páez, Stanimir Metchev, Dániel Apai et al.","title":"Cloud Atlas: Variability in and out of the Water Band in the Planetary-mass HD 203030B Points to Cloud Sedimentation in Low-gravity L Dwarfs","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd203030b-MilesPezetal2017","cite":"Miles-Páez et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Paulo A. Miles-Páez, Stanimir Metchev, Kevin L. Luhman et al.","title":"The Prototypical Young L/T-Transition Dwarf HD 203030B Likely Has Planetary Mass","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11274","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 142245 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Imaging with adaptive optics found a new companion star near the host star HD 142245 (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015). Its projected distance is hundreds of au from the host star (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015). The companion itself looks like a close binary, with a projected separation of only about 4 au between its two parts (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015). If so, the host star belongs to a hierarchical triple stellar system that also holds a planet (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015).","system":"Triple-star systems with planets, like this one, are few in number (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd142245b-MugrauerGinski2015","cite":"Mugrauer & Ginski 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Mugrauer, C. Ginski","title":"High-contrast imaging search for stellar and substellar companions of exoplanet host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stv771","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V1298 Tau b":{"text":{"why":"The star is only about 20 to 30 million years old, so its four planets show how planets change in size soon after forming (Feinstein et al. 2022).","observed":"V1298 Tau b is the largest of four transiting planets around a very young star, with a period near 24 days (Sozzetti 2024). Observations of its orbit across the star gave a precise spin-orbit angle (Sozzetti 2024). Metastable helium absorption was detected, a sign that its atmosphere is escaping (Sozzetti 2024). JWST found extremely low methane, and models suggest an inner temperature near 450 K, far above the 70 K expected from planet evolution models (Barat et al. 2026). More than 200 velocity measurements gave its mass despite the star's strong activity (Suárez Mascareño 2022).","system":"The system has four transiting planets with sizes between 0.5 and 0.9 Jupiter radii (Feinstein et al. 2022). The outer planet V1298 Tau e may sit in a near 2:1 orbital resonance with planet b (Feinstein et al. 2022).","history":"Young giant planets are expected to have large radii and low densities before shrinking over hundreds of millions of years (Suárez Mascareño 2022).","open":"Why the inner temperature is so high is not settled (Barat et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v1298taub-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Trevor J. David, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"V1298 Tau with TESS: Updated Ephemerides, Radii, and Period Constraints from a Second Transit of V1298 Tau e","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taub-Sozzetti2024","cite":"Sozzetti 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alessandro Sozzetti","title":"V1298 Tau b reloaded: updated spin-orbit alignment determination and detection of Helium absorption","journal":"EAS2024, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taub-Baratetal2026","cite":"Barat et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Saugata Barat, Jean-Michel Désert, Allona Vazan et al.","title":"Investigating potential causes for high inferred intrinsic temperatures of warm transiting Neptune and Sub-Neptune planets","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32044","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taub-Mascareo2022","cite":"Mascareño 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alejandro Suárez Mascareño","title":"V1298 Tau - Rapid contraction of giant planets?","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-9 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HAT-P-9 b has a radius of 1.40 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.78 Jupiter masses, so its density of 0.35 grams per cubic centimetre falls in the low-density tail of known transiting planets (Shporer et al. 2009). Models that couple tidal and thermal evolution can reproduce its large radius (Miller et al. 2009). The orbit is prograde and aligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of -16 degrees plus or minus 8 degrees (Moutou et al. 2011). Searches found no companions that cause timing shifts in the transits of about 1.5 minutes (Dittmann et al. 2011).","system":"The host is a moderately faint, late F-type star (Shporer et al. 2009).","history":"Tidal heating while the orbit became circular may have inflated the planet (Miller et al. 2009).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp9b-Shporeretal2009","cite":"Shporer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Avi Shporer, Gáspár Á. Bakos, Francois Bouchy et al.","title":"HAT-P-9b: A Low-Density Planet Transiting a Moderately Faint F Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.4008","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp9b-Milleretal2009","cite":"Miller et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"N. Miller, J. J. Fortney, B. Jackson","title":"Inflating and Deflating Hot Jupiters: Coupled Tidal and Thermal Evolution of Known Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1268","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp9b-Moutouetal2011","cite":"Moutou et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Moutou, R. F. Díaz, S. Udry et al.","title":"Spin-orbit inclinations of the exoplanetary systems HAT-P-8b, HAT-P-9b, HAT-P-16b, and HAT-P-23b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3849","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp9b-Dittmannetal2011","cite":"Dittmann et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jason Dittmann, L. Close, L. Scuderi","title":"On the Frequency of Additional Planets in Short Period Hot Jupiter Systems from Transit Timing Variations","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #218","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5713 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies near the radius valley, the gap between small rocky and larger gassy planets, and near the inner edge of its star's habitable zone (Ghachoui et al. 2024).","observed":"TOI-5713 b is a super-Earth with a radius of about 1.77 Earth radii that receives about 2.42 times the sunlight Earth does (Ghachoui et al. 2024). It orbits a mid-type M dwarf, with a period of 10.44 days (Ghachoui et al. 2024). Its planetary nature was checked with TESS data, ground-based multi-wavelength photometry, and adaptive optics imaging from Keck (Ghachoui et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether such planets near the radius valley are rocky or hold gas envelopes is a target for future study (Ghachoui et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5713b-Ghachouietal2024","cite":"Ghachoui et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Ghachoui, B. V. Rackham, M. Dévora-Pajares et al.","title":"TESS discovery of two super-Earths orbiting the M-dwarf stars TOI-6002 and TOI-5713 near the radius valley","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00709","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1470 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1470 b is a sub-Neptune on a 2.5-day orbit around an M1.5 dwarf (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). The transit signal was announced by TESS and then confirmed with 44 radial velocity measurements from CARMENES over eight months (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). Photometry from MuSCAT instruments confirmed the transits (González-Álvarez et al. 2023). The star rotates once every 29 days or so, found from light and spectra (González-Álvarez et al. 2023).","system":"A second sub-Neptune orbits the same star (González-Álvarez et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1470b-Gonzlezlvarezetal2023","cite":"González-Álvarez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. González-Álvarez, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, J. A. Caballero et al.","title":"Two sub-Neptunes around the M dwarf TOI-1470","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08516","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-39 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters this large for their mass are an emerging group: masses between 0.4 and 1.5 Jupiter masses with radii above 1.5 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2012).","observed":"HAT-P-39 b was announced together with two similar planets, all described as highly inflated hot Jupiters (Hartman et al. 2012). It has 0.60 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.57 Jupiter radii, and it orbits a moderately bright F star (Hartman et al. 2012). Later monitoring with the TESS space telescope found no additional transiting planets in the system down to 2 to 4 Earth radii, and its transit times show no drift away from a steady orbital period (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp39b-Hartmanetal2012","cite":"Hartman et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, B. Béky et al.","title":"HAT-P-39b-HAT-P-41b: Three Highly Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.3344","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp39b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5716 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-5716 b is an Earth-sized planet, with a radius of 0.96 Earth radii, on a 6.766 day orbit (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). Its star is a small, cool M dwarf that is poor in heavy elements and belongs to the thin disc of the Galaxy (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). Its planetary nature was established statistically, by combining TESS light curves, colour-resolved observations from Palomar and Las Cumbres Observatory, high-resolution imaging and stellar spectra (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). Because of its longer orbit, its equilibrium temperature is 400 K or less, which makes it a useful target for studying the atmospheres and make-up of rocky worlds (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5716b-Barrientosetal2026","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Heather A. Knutson, Morgan Saidel et al.","title":"From Earths to Super-Earths: Five New Small Planets Transiting M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11971","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2445 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets with orbits shorter than one day are relatively rare, and were known to number 125 when the first study was written (Morello et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-2445 b circles its M dwarf in about 0.37 days and was confirmed as a potentially rocky planet using TESS data, ground-based light curves and radial velocities from the Subaru telescope (Morello et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1330 K (Morello et al. 2023). JWST recorded a full orbit of its light with the NIRSpec instrument. From that phase curve, daytime and night-time brightness temperatures were derived, which constrain how much light the planet reflects and how well heat is carried around it (Xue et al. 2026). Intense starlight can strip away a rocky planet's atmosphere, and the level at which this happens is still uncertain (Xue et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet keeps any atmosphere under such strong irradiation is the question the JWST measurements address (Xue et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2445b-Morelloetal2023","cite":"Morello et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Morello, H. Parviainen, F. Murgas et al.","title":"TOI-1442 b and TOI-2445 b: Two potentially rocky ultra-short period planets around M dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.13274","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2445b-Xueetal2026","cite":"Xue et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Qiao Xue, Michael Zhang, Jacob Bean","title":"Two rocky planets through NIRSpec's eyes: what we learned from phase curves","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6716 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it, TEMPOS, aims to build a catalogue of precise radii for temperate planets around M dwarfs cooler than 3400 K, where temperate means receiving 0.1 to 5 times Earth's starlight (Scott et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-6716 b is a planet of about Earth's size, with a radius of 0.98 Earth radii, orbiting a small M4 star every 4.72 days (Scott et al. 2026). Its host is a fully convective star, meaning energy moves through its whole interior by churning gas (Scott et al. 2026). A later study of tidal heating, the warming caused by a planet being squeezed and stretched on its orbit, found that TOI-6716 b may have an exceptionally high tidal heat flux (Kane & Miles 2026). That heating alone could push the planet past the limit for a runaway greenhouse, like that of Venus (Kane & Miles 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The high tidal heating estimate rests on a catalogue value of the orbit's eccentricity and on a simple tidal model, so the true heating is not known (Kane & Miles 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6716b-Scottetal2026","cite":"Scott et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Madison G. Scott, Georgina Dransfield, Mathilde Timmermans et al.","title":"Two temperate Earth- and Neptune-sized planets orbiting fully convective M dwarfs","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05799","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi6716b-KaneMiles2026","cite":"Kane & Miles 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Emma L. Miles","title":"The Tidal Venus Phenomenon: Demographics and Case Studies","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18290","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 15337 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 15337 is a bright K dwarf that the HARPS spectrograph had watched for 14 years, collecting 85 precise velocity measurements before TESS found two transiting signals, at 4.76 and 17.18 days (Dumusque et al. 2019). The velocity data confirmed that both signals are planets, even though the star's activity, similar to the Sun's with a magnetic cycle of similar size, disturbs the measurements (Dumusque et al. 2019). The system holds a hot, rocky planet and a warm, puffy one (Dumusque et al. 2019). The CHEOPS space telescope later recorded six transits of the star (Rosario et al. 2024).","system":"Two planets are known, a hot, rocky inner one and the warm, puffy outer one (Dumusque et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd15337c-Dumusqueetal2019","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Xavier Dumusque, Oliver Turner, Caroline Dorn et al.","title":"Hot, rocky and warm, puffy super-Earths orbiting TOI-402 (HD 15337)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05419","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd15337c-Rosarioetal2024","cite":"Rosario et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"N. M. Rosario, O. D. S. Demangeon, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CHEOPS light curves of HD 15337 (TOI-402) (Rosario+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-139 b":{"text":{"why":"Its host star appears unusually dense for its type, like HAT-P-11 (Hellier et al. 2017).","observed":"WASP-139 b is a super-Neptune, the lowest-mass planet found by the WASP survey, at 0.12 Jupiter masses with a radius of 0.80 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2017). Its star is a metal-rich K0 dwarf (Hellier et al. 2017). Observing the planet crossing its star with the ESPRESSO spectrograph showed that its orbit is tilted by about 86 degrees from the star's spin, so it passes close to the star's poles (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024). It also has a small leftover eccentricity of about 0.10 (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"WASP-139 b joins a growing group of low-density hot Neptunes on polar orbits (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp139b-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp139b-EspinozaRetamaletal2024","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Guđmundur Stefánsson, Cristobal Petrovich et al.","title":"HATS-38 b and WASP-139 b Join a Growing Group of Hot Neptunes on Polar Orbits","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18631","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-65 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters of about 1.5 Jupiter masses are surprisingly rare among those known (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013).","observed":"WASP-65 b is a hot Jupiter that is not puffed up. Its density is 1.13 times Jupiter's, making it one of the densest known planets between 0.1 and 2.0 Jupiter masses (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013). In that mass range a large fraction of planets are inflated compared with theoretical models (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013). Its mass is 1.55 Jupiter masses and its radius 1.11 Jupiter radii (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013).","system":"The host is a G6 star with 5600 K and an age of at least 8 billion years (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp65b-Chewetal2013","cite":"Chew et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Y. Gomez Maqueo Chew, F. Faedi, D. Pollacco et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Exoplanets WASP-65b and WASP-75b (Gomez Maqueo Chew+, 2013)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35590036","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-23 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters are rare around stars that are leaving the main sequence, which made some planets of the same discovery set interesting (Jackson et al. 2023).","observed":"NGTS-23 b orbits a G-type star at a very small separation, between 0.037 and 0.050 au like its three companions in the discovery set (Jackson et al. 2023). Its mass is 0.61 Jupiter masses and its radius 1.27 Jupiter radii, and it is likely inflated (Jackson et al. 2023). Starlight at this distance is above the minimum level at which inflation mechanisms are expected to work (Jackson et al. 2023). What physically drives the inflation of hot Jupiters remains poorly understood (Jackson et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts23b-Jacksonetal2023","cite":"Jackson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"David G. Jackson, Christopher A. Watson, Ernst J. W. de Mooij et al.","title":"The discovery of three hot Jupiters, NGTS-23b, 24b, and 25b, and updated parameters for HATS-54b from the Next Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.01044","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-13 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of the interest in this planet comes from its fast-spinning host star. The transit curve is lopsided, which indicates a planet on a tilted orbit around a rapidly rotating, possibly elongated star (Szabó et al. 2011). Modelling of the transit alone, with no spectroscopy, found a spin-orbit misalignment of 40.3 degrees (Barnes 2011). The star has a visual companion 1.15 arcseconds away (Shporer et al. 2014). Measurements of the planet's glow found a dayside temperature of 2750 K and a relatively high reflectivity of 0.33, and comparison with atmosphere models suggests a temperature inversion (Shporer et al. 2014).","system":"The host belongs to a common proper motion double star whose two components both spin rapidly, at 65 to 70 km/s (Szabó et al. 2011).","history":"A Kozai resonance, a slow exchange of tilt and eccentricity driven by the stellar companion, was suggested to explain the tilted orbit (Barnes 2011).","open":"The early finding that its radius of 2.2 Jupiter radii might mean a hot brown dwarf rather than a planet was raised in the first study of the system (Szabó et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi13b-Szabetal2011","cite":"Szabó et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Gy. M. Szabó, R. Szabó, J. M. Benkő et al.","title":"Asymmetric Transit Curves as Indication of Orbital Obliquity: Clues from the Late-type Dwarf Companion in KOI-13","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.2524","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi13b-Barnes2011","cite":"Barnes 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. W. Barnes","title":"KOI-13.01: A Spin-Orbit Misaligned Giant Planet Orbiting a Fast-Rotating Star","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2011","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi13b-Shporeretal2014","cite":"Shporer et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Avi Shporer, Joseph G. O'Rourke, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Atmospheric Characterization of the Hot Jupiter Kepler-13Ab","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.6831","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":20,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1533 c":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters usually travel alone. Only eight are known with small inner companions as of May 2026, which points to gentle migration through the gas disc (Mantovan et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-1533 c is a hot giant planet with a radius of at least 7.5 Earth radii and a density below 0.48 g per cubic centimetre, so it holds a lot of hydrogen and helium (Mantovan et al. 2026). The host is an active K dwarf (Mantovan et al. 2026).","system":"An inner sub-Neptune, TOI-1533 b, orbits every 3.63 days with a radius of 3.15 Earth radii (Mantovan et al. 2026). The two planets have similar masses but very different radii (Mantovan et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1533c-Mantovanetal2026","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Mantovan, V. Nascimbeni, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXVI. TOI-1533: A compact system hosting a super-Neptune-mass pair with disparate radii","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30799","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CI Tau c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour CI Tau b, a planet of about 12 Jupiter masses at about 0.1 au (Muley & Dong 2021). CI Tau c shows up as a signal of about 25.2 days that appears in both brightness data from K2 and LCOGT and radial velocities from ESPaDOnS and SPIRou (Manick et al. 2024). The host is a young, accreting T Tauri star about 2 million years old with a strong magnetic field (Skinner 2023).","system":"The star is surrounded by a protoplanetary disc that reaches very close to the star (Muley & Dong 2021). Simulations show that a massive planet at 0.1 au has little effect on the disc's near-infrared glow (Muley & Dong 2021).","history":"At about 2 million years, the system is among the youngest known to host a close giant planet, which constrains how fast giant planets form (Skinner 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-citauc-MuleyDong2021","cite":"Muley & Dong 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Dhruv Muley, Ruobing Dong","title":"CI Tau: A Controlled Experiment in Disk-Planet Interaction","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13182","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-citauc-Manicketal2024","cite":"Manick et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"R. Manick, A. P. Sousa, J. Bouvier et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CI Tau light and radial velocity curves (Manick+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-citauc-Skinner2023","cite":"Skinner 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Stephen Skinner","title":"CI Tau: An Accreting T Tauri Star Hosting a Massive Planet","journal":"Chandra Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3693 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to build a uniform sample of hot Jupiters for demographic study, to address open questions about where they come from (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-3693 b was one of ten new hot Jupiters reported together from TESS data (Yee et al. 2022). Each began as a periodic dip in TESS brightness and was confirmed with ground-based time-series photometry, high-resolution imaging and high-resolution spectroscopy (Yee et al. 2022). The ten planets orbit relatively bright F and G stars, with orbital periods of 2 to 10 days and masses of 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiter masses (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3693b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-375 b":{"text":{"why":"Systems with several giant planets help show whether planets moved gently through the disc or were thrown around in more violent ways (Reinarz et al. 2026).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-375 c. TOI-375 b is a hot Jupiter with a mass of 0.745 Jupiter masses, a radius of 0.961 Jupiter radii and a slightly eccentric orbit of 0.087 (Reinarz et al. 2026). Radial velocities from FEROS and CHIRON revealed two further giant planet candidates in the system (Reinarz et al. 2026). The host is an intermediate-mass star that has already left the main sequence (Reinarz et al. 2026).","system":"The outer two planets, TOI-375 c and TOI-375 d, take 115.5 and 297.9 days to orbit, with minimum masses of 2.11 and 1.40 Jupiter masses (Reinarz et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi375b-Reinarzetal2026","cite":"Reinarz et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Y. Reinarz, M. I. Jones, R. Brahm et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-375 velocity and light curves (Reinarz+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6695 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-6695 b is a warm planet with a radius of 0.85 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.21 Jupiter masses (Eberhardt et al. 2025). It has a much more massive outer partner, with a minimum mass of 1.45 Jupiter masses and an orbit of about 242.4 days (Eberhardt et al. 2025). The two planets pull on each other, which shifts the times of the transits (Eberhardt et al. 2025). The host is a Sun-like star (Eberhardt et al. 2025).","system":"The pair lies near a 3:1 mean-motion resonance, meaning the outer planet orbits about three times for each orbit of the inner one (Eberhardt et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6695b-Eberhardtetal2025","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"TOI-6695: A Pair of Near-resonant Massive Planets Observed with TESS from the WINE Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/adc44e","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-138 c belongs to a system of six planets whose first five were found with the help of citizen scientists in the Exoplanet Explorers project (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). The five inner planets orbit near 3:2 mean-motion resonances, a chain in which each orbit is close to 1.5 times its inner neighbour's (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). A sixth planet, K2-138 g, was added with Spitzer, and a large gap lies between planets f and g (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Radial velocity masses for the inner four planets have been measured (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"Dynamical work finds evidence of a three-planet resonance in this system, which would be the first of its kind to be linked to a real system. Capture into such a resonance is shown to be easy during migration through the disc (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024).","open":"Further non-transiting planets may sit in the gap between planets f and g (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138c-HardegreeUllmanetal2021","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie L. Christiansen, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Planet for the Citizen Science System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08978","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138c-CerioniBeaug2024","cite":"Cerioni & Beaugé 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cerioni, C. Beaugé","title":"K2-138 and the capture in 1st-order 3P-MMRs","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AB Pic b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Studies of AB Pic b have mainly asked whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf, a body too light to be a star but heavier than a planet. A comparison with GQ Lup b and 2M1207 b gave AB Pic b a wider temperature range than earlier work (Neuhaeuser 2005). Models of how young objects cool allow either a planet or a brown dwarf (Neuhaeuser 2005). Near-infrared spectra give it a spectral type of L0 ± 1, and show no significant difference from young isolated brown dwarfs of the same type (Bonnefoy et al. 2014).","system":"Its host star has a solar-like carbon-to-oxygen ratio of 0.50 ± 0.14 (Baburaj et al. 2026).","history":"A formation model puts the companion's mass below the limit at which deuterium burns (Neuhaeuser 2005).","open":"Whether it counts as a planet is not settled. Its age and brightness fall in a strip where models of young objects overlap because of deuterium burning (Bowler et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-abpicb-Neuhaeuser2005","cite":"Neuhaeuser 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Ralph Neuhaeuser","title":"Homogeneous comparison of directly detected planet candidates: GQ Lup, 2M1207, AB Pic","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0509906","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abpicb-Bonnefoyetal2014","cite":"Bonnefoy et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Bonnefoy, G. Chauvin, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"A library of near-infrared integral field spectra of young M-L dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abpicb-Baburajetal2026","cite":"Baburaj et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Aneesh Baburaj, Quinn M. Konopacky, Christopher A. Theissen et al.","title":"A High-resolution Spectroscopic Survey of Directly Imaged Companion Hosts. II. Diversity in C/O Ratios among Host Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17774","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abpicb-Bowleretal2013","cite":"Bowler et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Brendan P. Bowler, Michael C. Liu, Evgenya L. Shkolnik et al.","title":"Planets around Low-mass Stars. III. A Young Dusty L Dwarf Companion at the Deuterium-burning Limit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.2237","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-22 b":{"text":{"why":"Spectra of its dust cloud can reveal what the planet is made of inside (Tusay et al. 2025).","observed":"Transits of K2-22 b vary from one passage to the next, with depths ranging from about 0% to 1.3% (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2015). Ground-based follow-up confirmed the changes, and once the depth differed with wavelength, which fits dust blocking the starlight rather than solid bodies (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2015). A small brightening just after transit, and a weaker one just before, suggest a dust tail behind the planet and a more prominent trail ahead of it (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2015). The planet is too small to be detected itself, so only its dust clouds show (Tusay et al. 2025). JWST observed four predicted transits in the mid-infrared. One was detected clearly, and its spectrum from 4.3 to 11.8 μm argues against iron-dominated core material and fits magnesium silicate minerals (Tusay et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an M dwarf with an effective temperature of about 3800 K (Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2015). K2-22 b belongs to a small group of disintegrating planets with comet-like dust tails (Budaj et al. 2020).","history":"The surface is thought to be molten and to mix with deeper layers. Material boils off into space, where radiation pressure erodes the cloud (Wright & Tusay 2025).","open":"An unexpected feature near 5 μm, consistent with MgO or NO gas, is not yet explained (Tusay et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k222b-Tusayetal2025","cite":"Tusay et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Nicholas Tusay, Jason Wright, Thomas Beatty et al.","title":"K2-22b: JWST Exposes Hot Rock Entrails","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k222b-SanchisOjedaetal2015","cite":"Sanchis-Ojeda et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"R. Sanchis-Ojeda, S. Rappaport, E. Pallè et al.","title":"The K2-ESPRINT Project I: Discovery of the Disintegrating Rocky Planet K2-22b with a Cometary Head and Leading Tail","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04379","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k222b-Budajetal2020","cite":"Budaj et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Jan Budaj, Petr Kabáth, Enric Palle","title":"Extrasolar Enigmas: From Disintegrating Exoplanets to Exoasteroids","journal":"Reviews in Frontiers of Modern Astrophysics; From Space Debris to Cosmology","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10370","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k222b-WrightTusay2025","cite":"Wright & Tusay 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jason Wright, Nicholas Tusay","title":"Hot Rocks Spitting in the Eye of JWST","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4010 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-4010 c. TOI-4010 b is a sub-Neptune on a 1.3-day orbit. That puts it in the hot-Neptune desert, a range of short orbits where planets of this size are scarce, and it is one of the few such planets with known companions (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Its mass was measured to about 8-12% precision with radial velocities from the HARPS-N spectrograph (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a metal-rich K dwarf. Two sub-Saturns, TOI-4010 c and d, orbit in 5.4 and 14.7 days, and a companion of more than Jupiter's mass, TOI-4010 e, moves on a long eccentric orbit of about 762 days with eccentricity about 0.26 (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Few systems with several short-period sub-Saturns are known (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4010b-Kunimotoetal2023","cite":"Kunimoto et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michelle Kunimoto, Andrew Vanderburg, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-4010: A System of Three Large Short-period Planets with a Massive Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05308","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158259 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known comes from 290 radial velocity measurements of the bright star HD 158259 with the SOPHIE spectrograph. They support five planets, each with a minimum mass near 6 Earth masses (Hara et al. 2020). Orbital periods are 3.4, 5.2, 7.9, 12 and 17.4 days (Hara et al. 2020). The 17.4-day signal is listed only as a candidate, because it is slightly less significant and lies close to the expected stellar rotation period (Hara et al. 2020). Later work improved the correction for instrumental drifts in SOPHIE data (Grouffal et al. 2024).","system":"The five planets sit close to a chain of 3:2 orbital resonances, in which neighbouring planets make about three orbits for every two of the next one out (Hara et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158259c-Haraetal2020","cite":"Hara et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. C. Hara, F. Bouchy, M. Stalport et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVI. HD 158259: A compact planetary system in a near-3:2 mean motion resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd158259c-Grouffaletal2024","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 158259 SOPHIE correction of RV systematics (Grouffal+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2420 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was studied to help bridge hot and warm Jupiters, two groups with different orbit patterns whose origins are puzzling (Carleo et al. 2024).","observed":"TOI-2420 b is a hot Jupiter, meaning a giant planet with an orbit shorter than 10 days. It was followed up with CHIRON and Minerva-Australis spectroscopy and ground-based photometry (Carleo et al. 2024). It is puffy: its radius is 1.3 times Jupiter's for a mass of 0.9 Jupiter masses, giving a density of 0.477 g per cubic centimetre (Carleo et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved star (Carleo et al. 2024).","history":"Hot and warm Jupiters might share one formation and migration path, yet their orbit distributions differ (Carleo et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2420b-Carleoetal2024","cite":"Carleo et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Oscar Barrágan, Carina M. Persson et al.","title":"Mass determination of two Jupiter-sized planets orbiting slightly evolved stars: TOI-2420 b and TOI-2485 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GQ Lup b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GQ Lup b sits close to the line between planets and brown dwarfs. Fits to its optical and near-infrared brightness gave a temperature of 2335 ± 100 K and a radius of 0.38 solar radii, larger than first derived and larger than models predict (Marois et al. 2007). Its mass is model-dependent, with 10 to 20 Jupiter masses favoured by one set of models (Marois et al. 2007). Common proper motion with its star is significant, and the chance of an unrelated look-alike is at most 3 in 10 billion (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2005). No orbital motion has yet been detected (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2005). Spectra show a late-M or early-L type (Neuhauser et al. 2005).","system":"A second, wider companion, GQ Lup C, is a low-mass accreting star with a disc seen almost edge-on. The disc is roughly aligned with the disc of the primary, and both follow the Lupus I dust filament (Lazzoni et al. 2020).","history":"The system is only a few million years old, so it can show how planets and brown dwarfs form (Neuhauser et al. 2005).","open":"Its true mass, and so whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf, depends on the models used (Marois et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gqlupb-Maroisetal2007","cite":"Marois et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Christian Marois, Bruce Macintosh, Travis Barman","title":"GQ Lup B Visible and Near-Infrared Photometric Analysis","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0610058","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gqlupb-MugrauerNeuhuser2005","cite":"Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2005","year":"2005","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser","title":"GQ Lup and its common proper motion companion","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0509162","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gqlupb-Neuhauseretal2005","cite":"Neuhauser et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"R. Neuhauser, M. Mugrauer, E. Guenther","title":"Direct Imaging of Sub-Stellar Companions around Young Stars","journal":"The Messenger","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gqlupb-Lazzonietal2020","cite":"Lazzoni et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"C. Lazzoni, R. Gratton, J. M. Alcalá et al.","title":"Disk of 2MASS 15491331-3539118 = GQ Lup C as seen by HST and WISE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00743","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":49,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1752 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work so far is a preliminary validation of a two-planet system around a faint, cool M dwarf. Ground-based photometry was used to check that the signals come from planets, not from look-alikes such as brown dwarfs or eclipsing binary stars (Pelaez Torres et al. 2025). The system holds an ultra-short-period super-Earth and, farther out, a sub-Neptune in the star's habitable zone (Pelaez Torres et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1752c-Torresetal2025","cite":"Torres et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Pelaez Torres, F. J. Pozuelos, G. Morello et al.","title":"TOI-1752: a GEM System with AN Ultra-Short Super-Earth and a Habitable Zone Sub-Neptune","journal":"Proceedings of the VIII Iberian Congress on Planetary Sciences and Solar System Exploration","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5027 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-5027 b is a warm Jupiter with a mass of 2.02 ± 0.13 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.96 Jupiter radii. It moves on an eccentric orbit, with eccentricity 0.385, every 10.24 days (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). Its transits were combined with ground-based photometry and spectroscopy to confirm it (Tala Pinto et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5027b-Pintoetal2025","cite":"Pinto et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marcelo Tala Pinto, Andrés Jordán, Lorena Acuña et al.","title":"Three warm Jupiters orbiting TOI-6628, TOI-3837, and TOI-5027 and one sub-Saturn orbiting TOI-2328","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02069","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1294 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1294 c is an outer planet that does not transit its star. It was reported as part of a survey of planets around subgiants, stars that have begun to swell after leaving the main sequence (Chontos et al. 2024). In that sample, 19 ± 8% of subgiants have outer planets, similar to main-sequence stars (Chontos et al. 2024).","system":"The planet orbits a star that is leaving the main sequence. A transiting planet closer in to the same star was also part of the survey (Chontos et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1294c-Chontosetal2024","cite":"Chontos et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ashley Chontos, Daniel Huber, Samuel Grunblatt et al.","title":"13 New TESS Planets and Homogeneous Properties for 21 Evolved Systems","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-210 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-210 is an active star with two Neptune-sized planets whose transit times drift from the expected schedule (Ioannidis et al. 2014). Star properties deduced from the two planets disagree with each other and with expectations, which suggests substantial eccentricities for the inner planets (Ioannidis et al. 2014). Mapping starspots crossed by the planets found that Kepler-210 c never covers spots at longitudes beyond 38°, while Kepler-210 b covers spots at all longitudes (Valio & Araújo 2022). The best explanation is that planet c has an inclined orbit and b lies parallel to the star's equator, giving planet c an obliquity of 18° to 45° (Valio & Araújo 2022).","system":"N-body simulations suggest the transit-time drifts come from another, somewhat longer-period planet (Ioannidis et al. 2014). A third, more massive and mutually inclined perturber was also proposed (Ioannidis et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler210c-Ioannidisetal2014","cite":"Ioannidis et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"P. Ioannidis, J. H. M. M. Schmitt, Ch. Avdellidou et al.","title":"Kepler-210: An active star with at least two planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.3238","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler210c-ValioArajo2022","cite":"Valio & Araújo 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adriana Valio, Alexandre Araújo","title":"Stellar Obliquity from Spot Transit Mapping of Kepler-210","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4357/ac9af6","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler210c-Ioannidisetal2013","cite":"Ioannidis et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"P. Ioannidis, J. Schmitt, C. Avdellidou et al.","title":"KOI-676: An active star with two transiting planets and a third possible candidate detected with TTV","journal":"11th Hellenic Astronomical Conference","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"alf Tau b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star, the bright red giant Aldebaran. Its angular diameter was measured from lunar occultations as 20.9 ± 2.0 milliarcseconds at 7460 Å and 18.4 ± 2.1 at 5680 Å (Beavers & Eitter 1979). Chandra X-ray observations failed to detect it, with an upper limit far below the Sun's relative X-ray output (Ayres et al. 2003). Carbon isotopes in the K giant were measured at 12 ± 2 for the 12C/13C ratio (Tomkin & Lambert 1974). Aboriginal oral traditions in South Australia describe its changing brightness (Hamacher 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-alftaub-BeaversEitter1979","cite":"Beavers & Eitter 1979","year":"1979","authors":"W. I. Beavers, J. Eitter","title":"The diameter of Aldebaran and Hyades occultations.","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/182914","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-alftaub-Ayresetal2003","cite":"Ayres et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Thomas R. Ayres, Alexander Brown, Graham M. Harper","title":"Buried Alive in the Coronal Graveyard","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/378699","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-alftaub-TomkinLambert1974","cite":"Tomkin & Lambert 1974","year":"1974","authors":"J. Tomkin, D. L. Lambert","title":"The <SUP>12</SUP>C/<SUP>13</SUP>C ratio in stellar atmospheres. III. Alpha Tauri, Beta Geminorum, and Mu Leonis.","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/153200","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-alftaub-Hamacher2018","cite":"Hamacher 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Duane W. Hamacher","title":"Observations of red-giant variable stars by Aboriginal Australians","journal":"The Australian Journal of Anthropology","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.04634","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 171028 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first to come out of a HARPS program that searches for planets around metal-poor stars (Santos et al. 2007).","observed":"HD 171028 b is a giant planet of at least 1.8 Jupiter masses on a 538-day orbit (Santos et al. 2007). Its host is metal-poor, with an iron abundance of -0.49 on a logarithmic scale, yet it is one of the least metal-poor stars in the sample (Santos et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"The discovery was placed in the context of models of planet formation and evolution (Santos et al. 2007).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd171028b-Santosetal2007","cite":"Santos et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XII. A giant planet orbiting the metal-poor star HD 171028","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.0954","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 73344 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 73344 d is a Jupiter analog, found in 27 years of radial velocity data and refined with Hipparcos and Gaia astrometry, which traces the star's slow acceleration (Zhang et al. 2025). The combined data give a three-dimensional orbit. The mutual inclination to the innermost transiting planet lies between 46° and 134° at 1σ, strongly disfavouring a flat system (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"The star also has a compact inner system, with a transiting sub-Neptune on a 15.61-day orbit and a non-transiting Saturn-mass planet on a 65.936-day orbit (Zhang et al. 2025). The star's rotation period is 9.03 ± 1.3 days (Zhang et al. 2025). The two inner planets are strongly coupled to each other (Zhang et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd73344d-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jingwen Zhang, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"Discovery of a Jupiter Analog Misaligned to the Inner Planetary System in HD 73344","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.09614","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-32 d":{"text":{"why":"Kepler-32 is offered as a prototype for how compact planetary systems form around M dwarfs, the most common stars in the Galaxy (Swift et al. 2013).","observed":"Kepler-32 is the richest known system of transiting planets around an M dwarf, with five planets (Swift et al. 2013). All five orbit within a third of the size of Mercury's orbit, and the innermost is only 4.3 stellar radii from the star's surface (Swift et al. 2013). New observations ruled out false-positive explanations and validated the whole system (Swift et al. 2013).","system":"M dwarfs make up about 70% of all stars in the Galaxy (Swift et al. 2013). Three adjacent planets have orbital periods that are nearly in simple ratios (Swift et al. 2013).","history":"A model of the formation of the system rests on the radius at which dust sublimates, a minimum-mass protoplanetary disc, and the near-matching periods of three neighbouring planets (Swift et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler32d-Swiftetal2013","cite":"Swift et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jonathan J. Swift, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. IV. Kepler-32 as a Prototype for the Formation of Compact Planetary Systems throughout the Galaxy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TRAPPIST-1 was watched by the Spitzer telescope for over 1000 hours between 2016 and 2019 (Ducrot et al. 2020). The data include 188 transits, along with 29 occultations of planet b, when the planet passes behind the star, at 4.5 μm (Ducrot et al. 2020). The occultations constrain the brightness temperature of the planet's day side (Ducrot et al. 2020).","system":"Seven Earth-sized planets orbit this ultracool dwarf star, and at least three lie in the habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). The planets are linked in a chain of orbital resonances, apart from the innermost pair, whose two-planet resonant angles circulate (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","history":"Simulations of planets migrating inward through a disc can build a resonant chain like the observed one (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"Whether planet b has an atmosphere is not known. Models of its possible atmospheres, from pure water to pure carbon dioxide to solar composition, predict different temperature profiles (Malik et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1b-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1b-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1b-Maliketal2019","cite":"Malik et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Matej Malik, Eliza M.-R. Kempton, Daniel D. B. Koll et al.","title":"Analyzing Atmospheric Temperature Profiles and Spectra of M Dwarf Rocky Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.13135","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 202206 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler measurements showed a companion of at least 17.5 Jupiter masses around HD 202206, near the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs, on an orbit with eccentricity between 0.22 and 0.43 (Udry et al. 2002). Hubble astrometry later showed the system is a nearly face-on G + M star binary, with the inner companion HD 202206 B a star of 0.089 solar masses and a brown dwarf of 17.9 Jupiter masses farther out (Benedict & Harrison 2017).","system":"The two companions have a period ratio of 4.92 ± 0.04, close to 5:1, and a mutual inclination of 6° ± 2° (Benedict & Harrison 2017). Stability appears to need both the resonance and coplanar orbits (Benedict & Harrison 2017). The star also hosts a debris disc, and modelling constrains where its planetesimal belt can lie (Moro-Martín et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether the inner companion counts as a planet is in doubt, since astrometry gives it a stellar mass (Benedict & Harrison 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd202206b-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206b-BenedictHarrison2017","cite":"Benedict & Harrison 2017","year":"2017","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, Thomas E. Harrison","title":"HD 202206: A Circumbinary Brown Dwarf System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.00659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202206b-MoroMartnetal2010","cite":"Moro-Martín et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Amaya Moro-Martín, Renu Malhotra, Geoffrey Bryden et al.","title":"Locating Planetesimal Belts in the Multiple-planet Systems HD 128311, HD 202206, HD 82943, and HR 8799","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2971","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-187 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-187 b is a low-density hot Jupiter of 0.80 ± 0.09 Jupiter masses and 1.64 ± 0.05 Jupiter radii (Schanche et al. 2020). Spectroscopy with SOPHIE confirmed its planetary nature, and TESS added more photometry (Schanche et al. 2020). It moves on a circular 5.15-day orbit (Schanche et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved early F-type star (Schanche et al. 2020). A second hot Jupiter, WASP-186 b, was announced in the same work around a different star (Schanche et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp187b-Schancheetal2020","cite":"Schanche et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. Schanche, G. Hébrard, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-186 and WASP-187: two hot Jupiters discovered by SuperWASP and SOPHIE with additional observations by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15205","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 45350 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 45350 b is a planet found in Keck Doppler data covering 4-6 years. Its host is one of five stars that are all metal-rich (Marcy et al. 2005). The system is among six that hold a very eccentric planet, with eccentricity above 0.6 (Wittenmyer et al. 2007). Searches of radial velocity data rule out additional planets down to a few Neptune masses (Wittenmyer et al. 2007).","system":"Dynamical simulations show that short-period planets could exist in the system without being thrown out of stable orbits (Wittenmyer et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd45350b-Marcyetal2005","cite":"Marcy et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"Five New Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/426384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd45350b-Wittenmyeretal2007","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Michael Endl, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"Dynamical and Observational Constraints on Additional Planets in Highly Eccentric Planetary Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1962","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1214 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 1214 b is a low-density planet orbiting a nearby M dwarf, and its gas layer has been probed from several sides. Its helium triplet absorption shows an escaping outflow with a relatively cold temperature of 3750 ± 750 K, losing material at (1.3 ± 1.1) × 10^11 grams per second (Lampón et al. 2023). Precise astrometry placed the star about 10% farther away than thought before (Anglada-Escudé et al. 2013).","system":"The host star rotates every 125 ± 5 days, with starspots about 370 K cooler than the surrounding surface, and these spots affect the measured transmission spectrum (Mallonn et al. 2018).","history":"The gas layer could come from gas captured from the birth disc, from melting ices, or from outgassing of rocky material (Rogers & Seager 2010).","open":"A unique composition cannot be inferred. Its low density means a significant gas component, and liquid water is unlikely under most conditions considered (Rogers & Seager 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1214b-Lampnetal2023","cite":"Lampón et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Lampón, M. López-Puertas, J. Sanz-Forcada et al.","title":"Characterisation of the upper atmospheres of HAT-P-32 b, WASP-69 b, GJ 1214 b, and WASP-76 b through their He I triplet absorption","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03839","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1214b-AngladaEscudetal2013","cite":"Anglada-Escudé et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Anglada-Escudé, B. Rojas-Ayala, A. P. Boss et al.","title":"GJ 1214 reviewed. Trigonometric parallax, stellar parameters, new orbital solution, and bulk properties for the super-Earth GJ 1214b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.8087","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1214b-Mallonnetal2018","cite":"Mallonn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. Mallonn, E. Herrero, I. G. Juvan et al.","title":"GJ 1214: Rotation period, starspots, and uncertainty on the optical slope of the transmission spectrum","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.05677","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1214b-RogersSeager2010","cite":"Rogers & Seager 2010","year":"2010","authors":"L. A. Rogers, S. Seager","title":"Three Possible Origins for the Gas Layer on GJ 1214b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3243","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":56,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3378 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"An earlier radial velocity discovery put GJ 3378 b on a 24.73 ± 0.06 day orbit with a minimum mass of 5.26 Earth masses (Moutou et al. 2024). New measurements, joined with the earlier data, give a significantly different orbit, with a shorter period of 21.45 ± 0.01 days and a revised mass (Robertson et al. 2026). Two infrared instruments added data: HPF and NEID (Robertson et al. 2026).","system":"The host is an M4 dwarf of about 0.26 solar masses, 7.7 parsecs away, with a mostly dipolar large-scale magnetic field of 20-30 G at its poles (Moutou et al. 2024). Stellar activity is not seen in its infrared radial velocities (Moutou et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The planet is described as straddling the cosmic shoreline, the dividing line between worlds that can hold an atmosphere and those that cannot, so whether it has one is open (Robertson et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3378b-Moutouetal2024","cite":"Moutou et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Ould-Elhkim, J.-F. Donati et al.","title":"Characterising planetary systems with SPIRou: Temperate sub-Neptune exoplanet orbiting the nearby fully convective star GJ 1289 and a candidate around GJ 3378","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3378b-Robertsonetal2026","cite":"Robertson et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Paul Robertson, Michael Endl, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"A Revised Mass and Period for the Habitable Zone super-Earth GJ 3378 b: A Planet Straddling the Cosmic Shoreline","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.16499","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"ZTF J1828+2308 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"ZTF J1828+2308 b is an eclipsing brown dwarf of 19.5 ± 0.8 Jupiter masses orbiting a hot white dwarf of 0.610 solar masses and 15900 ± 75 K in a 2.7-hour binary (Parsons et al. 2025). Such white dwarf binaries with companions too light to be a star are thought to form by common envelope evolution, in which the progenitor star swells and engulfs its companion (Parsons et al. 2025). Known companions of this kind typically have masses above about 50 Jupiter masses (Parsons et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The brown dwarf survived being engulfed when the white dwarf's progenitor swelled (Parsons et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ztfj18282308b-Parsonsetal2025","cite":"Parsons et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. G. Parsons, A. J. Brown, S. L. Casewell et al.","title":"Two almost planetary mass survivors of common envelope evolution","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14333","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-101 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-101 b is a young transiting planet, in a sample of 39 young systems searched for transit timing variations, which are shifts in transit times that can reveal planet masses (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026). The search found new candidate timing variations for K2-101 b (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026). Star spots have a weak-to-negligible effect on the results (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Across the sample, 28.3% ± 6.2% of young planets show timing variations, above the 7.3% ± 0.6% found by Kepler for mostly older systems (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026).","open":"Longer monitoring is needed to confirm the signals, and the planet's mass is not yet measured this way (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2101b-Murilloetal2026","cite":"Murillo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ana Isabel Lopez Murillo, Andrew W. Mann, Madyson G. Barber et al.","title":"Searching for Transit Timing Variations in Young Transiting Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-173 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The angle between the planet's orbit and its star's spin was measured from archival HARPS spectra taken during transits, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect. The projected angle is 9° ± 5°, consistent with an aligned orbit (Zak et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp173ab-Zaketal2025","cite":"Zak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquities of eight close-in gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.05615","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 23472 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 23472 d is the innermost of five planets around a K dwarf, with a period near 3.98 days and a radius near 0.75 Earth radii (Barros et al. 2022). ESPRESSO radial velocities gave it a mass of 0.54 ± 0.22 Earth masses (Barros et al. 2022).","system":"The compact system has three Earth-sized to super-Earth planets and two potential super-Mercuries, with periods of 3.98, 7.90, 12.16, 17.67 and 29.80 days (Barros et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd23472d-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"HD 23472: a multi-planetary system with three super-Earths and two potential super-Mercuries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NSVS 14256825 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"NSVS 14256825 is an eclipsing pair of a hot subdwarf and a red dwarf with a 2.65-hour orbit (Zhu et al. 2019). Eclipse times drift in a cycle of 8.83 years and 46.31 seconds in amplitude (Zhu et al. 2019). Magnetic activity of the red dwarf cannot supply the energy for that cycle, so a third body is invoked, with a minimum mass of 14.15 Jupiter masses at about 3 AU (Zhu et al. 2019).","system":"The host is an evolved binary, and the object too light to be a star orbits both stars (Zhu et al. 2019). Another analysis finds that two circumbinary companions are needed to fit the timing data (Zervas & Christopoulou 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether any circumbinary body exists is disputed. Newer observations fit none of 18 recent models, and other mechanisms such as magnetic effects may be involved (Pulley et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-nsvs14256825b-Zhuetal2019","cite":"Zhu et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Li-Ying Zhu, Sheng-Bang Qian, Eduardo Fernández Lajús et al.","title":"A close-in substellar object orbiting the sdOB-type eclipsing-binary system NSVS 14256825","journal":"Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.11664","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nsvs14256825b-ZervasChristopoulou2024","cite":"Zervas & Christopoulou 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. Zervas, P.-E. Christopoulou","title":"NSVS 14256825: Period variation and orbital stability analysis of two possible substellar companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nsvs14256825b-Pulleyetal2025","cite":"Pulley et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Pulley, I. D. Sharp, J. Mallett et al.","title":"Eclipse timing variations in seven post-common envelope binaries: an update and analysis of their recently proposed circumbinary models","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06748","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3053 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the TASSIE program, which follows up TESS giant-planet candidates at the University of Tasmania's Greenhill Observatory (Plunkett et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-3053 b is a typical hot Jupiter, with a mass of 0.85 ± 0.12 Jupiter masses and a density of 0.64 ± 0.10 g per cubic centimetre (Plunkett et al. 2026). False-positive explanations were ruled out with ground-based photometry, reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging and precise radial velocities (Plunkett et al. 2026).","system":"The star is a thin-disc G dwarf on the main sequence with roughly solar metallicity (Plunkett et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3053b-Plunkettetal2026","cite":"Plunkett et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Thomas Plunkett, Erica Thygesen, Andrew A. Cole et al.","title":"The TASSIE program II: Three close-in companions orbiting sun-like stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05500","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2016-BLG-2397L b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up searched for planet signals in lensing events with faint source stars, where weak signals can escape detection (Han et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet was one of four unpublished microlensing planets found when 2016 and 2017 data were searched for planetary signals in faint-source events (Han et al. 2020). For the group, host stars have masses between 0.50 and 0.85 solar masses and planets between 0.5 and 13.2 Jupiter masses, so all are giant planets around stars lighter than the Sun (Han et al. 2020). The lenses lie 3.8 to 6.4 kiloparsecs away (Han et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2016blg2397lb-Hanetal2020","cite":"Han et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Doeon Kim et al.","title":"Four microlensing planets with faint-source stars identified in the 2016 and 2017 season data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.09258","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-32 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-32 b is an inflated, Neptune-mass planet of 15.0 Earth masses, measured from 199 new HARPS radial velocities combined with K2 photometry (Lillo-Box et al. 2020). Keck/HIRES data give 16.5 ± 2.7 Earth masses, the lowest of four sub-Saturns of similar size and very different masses (Petigura et al. 2017).","system":"K2-32 is a compact, scaled-down version of the Solar System, with a small rocky inner planet of 2.1 Earth masses on a 4.35-day orbit followed by the inflated Neptune-mass planet (Lillo-Box et al. 2020). The four planets form a near-resonant chain, with K2-32 b close to two sub-Neptunes in a 3:2:1 period pattern (Petigura et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k232b-LilloBoxetal2020","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, T. Lopez, A. Santerne et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2-32 and K2-233 light and RV curves (Lillo-Box+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36400048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k232b-Petiguraetal2017","cite":"Petigura et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, Evan Sinukoff, Eric D. Lopez et al.","title":"Four Sub-Saturns with Dissimilar Densities: Windows into Planetary Cores and Envelopes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00013","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-270 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity measurements with the ESPRESSO and HARPS spectrographs gave planet b a mass of 1.58 ± 0.26 Earth masses (Van Eylen et al. 2021). Together with its size, that points to a rocky composition similar to Earth's (Van Eylen et al. 2021). A campaign with eight observatories between 2018 and 2020 followed its transit times; modelling them with the velocity data gave a mass of 1.48 ± 0.18 Earth masses and a small orbital eccentricity of 0.0167 ± 0.0084 (Kaye et al. 2022). Interior modelling also finds a dry, rocky planet (Deb et al. 2026).","system":"Planet b is a super-Earth in a system of three transiting planets around a nearby M dwarf; the other two are sub-Neptunes (Kaye et al. 2022). The planets orbit near low-order resonances of 5:3 and 2:1, and planets c and d show transit time shifts of about 10 minutes over a cycle of about 3 years (Kaye et al. 2022). Planet b and the two outer planets lie on opposite sides of the radius valley, a region with few planets (Van Eylen et al. 2021).","history":"Modelling of the interiors suggests the outer planets c and d formed beyond the ice line and gathered plenty of volatile material, while planet b formed dry (Deb et al. 2026).","open":"Systems with several planets spanning the radius valley are rare around M dwarfs, and the reason is not known (Günther 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi270b-VanEylenetal2021","cite":"Van Eylen et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"V. Van Eylen, N. Astudillo-Defru, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"Masses and compositions of three small planets orbiting the nearby M dwarf L231-32 (TOI-270) and the M dwarf radius valley","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01593","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270b-Kayeetal2022","cite":"Kaye et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Laurel Kaye, Shreyas Vissapragada, Maximilian N. Günther et al.","title":"Transit timings variations in the three-planet system: TOI-270","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10763","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270b-Debetal2026","cite":"Deb et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sushmita Deb, Kaushal Sharma, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Structural composition and atmospheric analysis of two planetary systems : TOI-270 and TOI-776","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag2095","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270b-Gnther2022","cite":"Günther 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Maximilian N. Günther","title":"TOI-270 as a unique testbed for exoplanet formation & evolution","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2022-1183","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 73583 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Helium absorption from an escaping atmosphere was seen during transit with Keck/NIRSPEC, with an average depth of 0.68% ± 0.08% in the line core (Zhang et al. 2022). The signal repeated in a partial transit a month later, though stronger and bluer (Zhang et al. 2022). The outflow looks slightly asymmetric and confined to regions close to the planet (Zhang et al. 2022). The absorption is redshifted by 4 km/s, where a blueshift was expected (Zhang et al. 2022). Hints of hydrogen-alpha absorption also need confirming (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements gave a projected obliquity of -25° ± 16°, so the orbit is not strongly tilted against the star's spin (Zhang et al. 2026).","system":"The system holds two transiting mini-Neptunes around a young, active K dwarf about 500 million years old that spins in 12.08 days (Barragán et al. 2022). The planets lie near a rare 1:3 orbital resonance, and planet b has a moderately eccentric orbit (El Mufti et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Computer models of the outflow overpredict the absorption, the blueshift, or both (Zhang et al. 2022). A modest orbital eccentricity could explain the redshift, but the velocity data disfavour it (Zhang et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd73583b-Zhangetal2022","cite":"Zhang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Michael Zhang, Heather A. Knutson, Lile Wang et al.","title":"Escaping Helium from TOI 560.01, a Young Mini-Neptune","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13150","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583b-Zhangetal2026","cite":"Zhang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Elina Y. Zhang, Fei Dai, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"Spin-Orbit Alignment of Two Neptune-size Planets Younger Than 500 Myr: TOI-560 b and TOI-5082 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22611","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583b-Barragnetal2022","cite":"Barragán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"O. Barragán, D. J. Armstrong, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"The young HD 73583 (TOI-560) planetary system: two 10-M<SUB>⊕</SUB> mini-Neptunes transiting a 500-Myr-old, bright, and active K dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13069","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73583b-Muftietal2023","cite":"Mufti et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mohammed El Mufti, Peter P. Plavchan, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"TOI 560: Two Transiting Planets Orbiting a K Dwarf Validated with iSHELL, PFS, and HIRES RVs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.13448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1431 b":{"text":{"why":"Its star is one of the hottest and brightest known to host a planet, which makes the planet well suited to detailed atmospheric study (Addison et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet's glow was measured from the full phase curve and the secondary eclipse in TESS light, giving a dayside temperature of 2983 ± 68 K and a nightside temperature of 2556 ± 65 K (Addison et al. 2021). The small contrast of about 400 K suggests very efficient heat transport between the two hemispheres (Addison et al. 2021). Emission lines of iron on the dayside were confirmed at 5.5 sigma, and the temperature profile is inverted, rising from about 2000 K to about 4500 K with height (Sikora et al. 2026). Transit spectroscopy found no sign of calcium, iron, magnesium, sodium, vanadium, TiO, VO or hydrogen-alpha (Stangret et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a young Am-type star, about 0.29 billion years old, with an effective temperature of 7690 K (Addison et al. 2021). Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements show a polar, misaligned orbit with a projected obliquity of about -155 degrees (Stangret et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The lack of absorption in transit may come from the planet's large surface gravity, but iron is seen in emission (Stangret et al. 2021; Sikora et al. 2026). Observations at more orbital phases are wanted to pin down its chemistry and winds (Sikora et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1431b-Addisonetal2021","cite":"Addison et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Brett C. Addison, Emil Knudstrup, Ian Wong et al.","title":"TOI-1431b/MASCARA-5b: A Highly Irradiated Ultrahot Jupiter Orbiting One of the Hottest and Brightest Known Exoplanet Host Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.12078","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1431b-Sikoraetal2026","cite":"Sikora et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"James T. Sikora, Joe Llama, Rachael M. Roettenbacher et al.","title":"Confirmation of Fe I on MASCARA-5 b's Dayside Observed With EXPRES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae5a29","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1431b-Stangretetal2021","cite":"Stangret et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"M. Stangret, E. Pallé, N. Casasayas-Barris et al.","title":"The obliquity and atmosphere of the ultra-hot Jupiter TOI-1431b (MASCARA-5b): A misaligned orbit and no signs of atomic or molecular absorptions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.12414","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LTT 9779 b":{"text":{"why":"Its orbit lasts only 19 hours and its equilibrium temperature exceeds 2000 K, making it the first Neptune-like ultra-short-period planet (Jenkins 2020). The host star is rich in heavy elements (Jenkins 2020).","observed":"Spitzer and TESS secondary eclipses show a dayside brightness temperature of 2305 ± 141 K, with a spectrum that prefers carbon monoxide absorption over a plain blackbody and shows no thermal inversion (Dragomir et al. 2020). The orbit is close to circular, with eccentricity below 0.01 (Dragomir et al. 2020). JWST transmission spectra from 0.63 to 5.165 microns show muted features consistent with clouds and a cool terminator, about 560 K or 910-950 K depending on the chemistry assumed (Stamer et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Planets this close to their stars are expected to lose their gas. The host star is unusually faint in X-rays, by a factor of 15 below what is expected for its age, which may be why the atmosphere survived (Fernández Fernández et al. 2024). Other work suggests the hot Neptune desert holds remnants of destroyed gas giants, and LTT 9779 b has long been proposed as one (Hallatt et al. 2026).","open":"Studies disagree on the planet's atmospheric content (Stamer et al. 2026). Measuring the star's obliquity could reveal whether the planet is a gas giant remnant (Hallatt et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ltt9779b-Jenkins2020","cite":"Jenkins 2020","year":"2020","authors":"James Jenkins","title":"TESS Discovery of the First Ultra Hot Neptune, LTT9779b","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt9779b-Dragomiretal2020","cite":"Dragomir et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Diana Dragomir, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Björn Benneke et al.","title":"Spitzer Reveals Evidence of Molecular Absorption in the Atmosphere of the Hot Neptune LTT 9779b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12744","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt9779b-Stameretal2026","cite":"Stamer et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sarah Stamer, Diana Dragomir, Annabella Meech et al.","title":"Cool, Cloudy Nights on an Ultrahot Neptune: A Panchromatic NIRISS/NIRSpec Transmission Spectrum of LTT 9779 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36206","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt9779b-Fernndezetal2024","cite":"Fernández et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jorge Fernández Fernández, Peter J. Wheatley, George W. King et al.","title":"Survival in the Neptune desert: LTT 9779 b kept its atmosphere thanks to an unusually X-ray faint host star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13171","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt9779b-Hallattetal2026","cite":"Hallatt et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Tim Hallatt, James Owen, Sarah Millholland","title":"Revealing the Origins of Desert Dwellers via Stellar Obliquities","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 217107 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"More than 20 years of radial velocity measurements, including 42 new ones from the MINERVA telescope array, confirm a long-period companion and refine its orbit (Giovinazzi et al. 2020). Its period is about 5059 days and its orbit is clearly eccentric, with e of about 0.40 (Giovinazzi et al. 2020).","system":"The inner planet, HD 217107 b, is a hot Jupiter with a period of about 7.13 days and an eccentric orbit of e about 0.13 (Giovinazzi et al. 2020). Both planets are eccentric (Giovinazzi et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"General relativity and perturbations from other planets should make the inner planet's orbit precess by about 0.8 degrees per century, but it has not been clearly detected (Giovinazzi et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd217107c-Giovinazzietal2020","cite":"Giovinazzi et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mark R. Giovinazzi, Cullen H. Blake, Jason D. Eastman et al.","title":"The HD 217107 planetary system: Twenty years of radial velocity measurements","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.12356","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1710 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity measurements with SOPHIE and HARPS-N established that the transit signal comes from a planet, with a nearly circular orbit of e = 0.16 ± 0.08 (König et al. 2022). Star spots complicate the measurement, because the orbital period is close to the star's rotation period of 22.5 ± 2.0 days (König et al. 2022). A later reanalysis that modelled the stellar activity found a mass of 18.4 ± 4.5 Earth masses, a radius of 5.15 ± 0.12 Earth radii and a density of 0.73 ± 0.18 g/cm3 (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Those values agree with the first measurement within 1.5 sigma (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Adding more velocity data did not by itself make the mass more precise, so the planet's mass is still uncertain (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1710b-Knigetal2022","cite":"König et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P.-C. König, M. Damasso, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"A warm super-Neptune around the G-dwarf star TOI-1710 revealed with TESS, SOPHIE, and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.08984","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1710b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, I. Carleo, F. Murgas et al.","title":"Revisiting the warm sub-Saturn TOI-1710b. The impact of stellar activity on the mass measurement","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13574","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-78 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transit photometry from WASP and TRAPPIST combined with CORALIE velocities gave a mass of 0.89 ± 0.08 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.70 ± 0.11 Jupiter radii, so the planet is highly bloated (Smalley et al. 2012). Its equilibrium temperature of 2350 ± 80 K made it one of the hottest planets known when it was announced (Smalley et al. 2012).","system":"The host is an F-type star, and the planet circles it every 2.175 days (Smalley et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp78b-Smalleyetal2012","cite":"Smalley et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"B. Smalley, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-78b and WASP-79b: two highly-bloated hot Jupiter-mass exoplanets orbiting F-type stars in Eridanus","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1177","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-136 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its host star and its neighbouring planets. An XMM-Newton X-ray observation shows the star is fainter than activity relations suggest, from either variability or intrinsically low X-ray output (Fernandez & Wheatley 2022). Mass-loss modelling finds planet b most likely rocky, with any past envelope evaporated within tens of millions of years (Fernandez & Wheatley 2022).","system":"Three planets with radii of 1.0, 3.0 and 1.5 Earth radii orbit within 0.15 AU of a young, active K dwarf in the Hyades cluster (Fernandez & Wheatley 2022). The star has a companion, an M7/8 dwarf at a projected separation of 40 au, and the Hyades are 625-750 million years old (Ciardi et al. 2018).","history":"All three planets have had the same X-ray exposure, so they form a test of different evaporation models (Fernandez & Wheatley 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2136b-FernandezWheatley2022","cite":"Fernandez & Wheatley 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jorge Fernandez, Peter Wheatley","title":"The Evaporation History of Three Planets around K2-136","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2136b-Ciardietal2018","cite":"Ciardi et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"David R. Ciardi, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"K2-136: A Binary System in the Hyades Cluster Hosting a Neptune-sized Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.10398","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 38529 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble Space Telescope astrometry combined with over 11 years of radial velocities gives the orbit an inclination of 48.3 ± 3.7 degrees and a period of 2136.1 ± 0.3 days (Benedict et al. 2010). The resulting mass is 17.6 Jupiter masses, 3 sigma above the 13 Jupiter masses where deuterium burning begins, so the body may be a brown dwarf rather than a planet (Benedict et al. 2010). Earlier Hipparcos astrometry had already pointed to brown dwarf masses for the outer companion (Reffert & Quirrenbach 2006).","system":"The star hosts two companions, and orbit simulations place the system within a large zone of stable orbits (Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002). The star spins in 31.65 ± 0.17 days, and its spots probably explain the leftover scatter in the velocities (Benedict et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd38529c-Benedictetal2010","cite":"Benedict et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, Barbara E. McArthur, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"The Mass of HD 38529c from Hubble Space Telescope Astrometry and High-precision Radial Velocities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0421","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd38529c-ReffertQuirrenbach2006","cite":"Reffert & Quirrenbach 2006","year":"2006","authors":"S. Reffert, A. Quirrenbach","title":"Hipparcos astrometric orbits for two brown dwarf companions: HD 38529 and HD 168443","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20054611","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd38529c-KiselevaEggletonetal2002","cite":"Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ludmila Kiseleva-Eggleton, Eric Bois, Nicolas Rambaux et al.","title":"Global Dynamics and Stability Limits for Planetary Systems around HD 12661, HD 38529, HD 37124, and HD 160691","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207367","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-117 b":{"text":{"why":"The orbit lasts 10 days, the longest period found by WASP at the time, and the star is bright enough for atmospheric follow-up (Lendl et al. 2014).","observed":"Hubble transmission spectra show a water feature, detected at 3 sigma (Carone et al. 2021). The spectrum fits either a high metallicity with clear skies or a lower metallicity with clouds, and methane is low (Carone et al. 2021). A separate analysis found a fully opaque cloud layer and a terminator temperature of 833 K (+260/-156 K) (Anisman et al. 2020). High-resolution spectra give marginal signals of hydrogen-alpha and lithium at 3.2 and 3.1 sigma (Jiang et al. 2026).","system":"The orbit is eccentric, with e of 0.302 ± 0.023, and tilted against the star's spin by about 70 degrees (Lendl et al. 2014). The planet's temperature swings across 970 K, which separates two chemistry regimes, during each orbit (Carone 2017).","history":"Tides at this distance are weak, so they have done little to round off or realign the orbit over the system's life (Lendl et al. 2014).","open":"Whether the atmosphere is clear and metal-rich or cloudy is not settled (Carone et al. 2021; Anisman et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp117b-Lendletal2014","cite":"Lendl et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Lendl, A. H. M. J. Triaud, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-117b: a 10-day-period Saturn in an eccentric and misaligned orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.6942","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp117b-Caroneetal2021","cite":"Carone et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ludmila Carone, Paul Mollière, Yifan Zhou et al.","title":"Indications for very high metallicity and absence of methane in the eccentric exo-Saturn WASP-117b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.05382","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp117b-Anismanetal2020","cite":"Anisman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Lara O. Anisman, Billy Edwards, Quentin Changeat et al.","title":"WASP-117 b: An Eccentric Hot Saturn as a Future Complex Chemistry Laboratory","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.08916","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp117b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Zewen Jiang, Wei Wang, Shumei Jia et al.","title":"High-resolution Spectroscopic Atmospheric Studies of Five Hot Jupiters Across the Edge of the Neptune Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21273","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp117b-Carone2017","cite":"Carone 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ludmila Carone","title":"Now you see me - the WASP-117b version","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1634 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits inside the M-dwarf radius valley, a gap in the sizes of close-in planets, so its composition can test competing ideas for how the valley arises (Cloutier et al. 2021).","observed":"Velocity measurements with HARPS-N and Subaru's IRD spectrograph confirm the planet, with a rocky, Earth-like composition (Cloutier et al. 2021; Hirano et al. 2021). The two teams measured different masses, about 4.9 and 10.14 ± 0.95 Earth masses (Cloutier et al. 2021; Hirano et al. 2021). A search for helium in its atmosphere found none, with an upper limit on the line depth of 3.00% (Kawauchi et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a nearby M2 dwarf, and the planet circles it in less than one day, making it an ultra-short-period planet (Cloutier et al. 2021).","history":"One idea says rocky planets form late in gas-poor discs, while another says they start with a gas envelope that heat later strips away (Cloutier et al. 2021).","open":"Mass measurements by different teams do not agree, so its density is not firmly known (Cloutier et al. 2021; Hirano et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1634b-Cloutieretal2021","cite":"Cloutier et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ryan Cloutier, David Charbonneau, Keivan G. Stassun et al.","title":"TOI-1634 b: An Ultra-short-period Keystone Planet Sitting inside the M-dwarf Radius Valley","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.12790","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1634b-Hiranoetal2021","cite":"Hirano et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, John H. Livingston, Akihiko Fukui et al.","title":"Two Bright M Dwarfs Hosting Ultra-Short-Period Super-Earths with Earth-like Compositions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.12760","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1634b-Kawauchietal2026","cite":"Kawauchi et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Kiyoe Kawauchi, Norio Narita, Yuichi Ito et al.","title":"A search for helium in the atmospheres of three sub-Neptunes and a super-Earth around M-dwarfs","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19030","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 80869 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the SOPHIE spectrograph taken over up to 15 years revealed a cold Jupiter, one of six new planets announced together (Demangeon et al. 2021). The six have minimum masses between 4.8 and 8.3 Jupiter masses and periods between 200 days and 10 years (Demangeon et al. 2021). Stellar activity indicators confirm the signal is a planet, and no extra planets show up in the data (Demangeon et al. 2021).","system":"The star is one of six bright, solar-like stars with a single detected giant planet each (Demangeon et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd80869b-Demangeonetal2021","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, S. Dalal, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVIII. Six new cold Jupiters, including one of the most eccentric exoplanet orbits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05089","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1296 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity measurements with SOPHIE gave a mass of 0.298 ± 0.039 Jupiter masses, and the radius of 1.2 Jupiter radii gives a low density of 0.198 g/cm3 (Moutou et al. 2021). A similar Saturn-mass planet around TOI-1298 has a much higher density of 0.743 g/cm3 (Moutou et al. 2021). The different irradiation from the two stars might explain why the radii differ (Moutou et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a subgiant star strongly enriched in heavy elements, with a metallicity of +0.44 dex (Moutou et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1296b-Moutouetal2021","cite":"Moutou et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Moutou, J. M. Almenara, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"TOI-1296b and TOI-1298b observed with TESS and SOPHIE: two hot Saturn-mass exoplanets with different densities around metal-rich stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.09252","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 201170410.02":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet turned up in K2 Campaign 1 data cleaned with a wavelet-based denoising method, TFAW, one of two new Earth-sized planets validated that way (del Ser & Fors 2020). TFAW improved the median photometric precision by about 30 per cent over a previous correction, and simulated Earth-Sun-like transits were found about 8.5 times more efficiently (del Ser & Fors 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic20117041002-delSerFors2020","cite":"del Ser & Fors 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. del Ser, O. Fors","title":"TFAW survey - I. Wavelet-based denoising of K2 light curves. Discovery and validation of two new Earth-sized planets in K2 campaign 1","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.09285","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-33 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the youngest transiting planet known, at about 10 million years (Thao et al. 2023). It orbits just inside the corotation radius, where migrating planets are expected to halt (David 2016).","observed":"Transit data covering 33 transits from K2, MEarth, Hubble and Spitzer, spanning 0.6 to 4.5 microns, show optical transits nearly twice as deep as near-infrared ones (Thao et al. 2023). Star spots cannot explain this, and photochemical haze fits better (Thao et al. 2023). The radius is about 5.0 Earth radii and the mass is below 17.5 Earth masses at 3 sigma (Mallorquín Díaz et al. 2024).","system":"The host star belongs to the Upper Scorpius association and is 5-10 million years old (David 2016).","history":"The orbit may be evidence of migration through the gas disc, though forming in place cannot be ruled out (David 2016). The planet is probably still contracting and should become a typical close-in sub-Neptune (David 2016).","open":"The mass is only an upper limit (Mallorquín Díaz et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k233b-Thaoetal2023","cite":"Thao et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Pa Chia Thao, Andrew W. Mann, Peter Gao et al.","title":"Hazy with a Chance of Star Spots: Constraining the Atmosphere of Young Planet K2-33b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.07728","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k233b-David2016","cite":"David 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Trevor J. David","title":"Observational constraints on planet formation and migration timescales","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #229","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k233b-Dazetal2024","cite":"Díaz et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Manuel Mallorquín Díaz, Nicolas Lodieu, Víctor J. Sánchez Béjar et al.","title":"Mass and radius determination of young planets and the implications for their gas envelope evolution","journal":"EAS2024, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit probably has a small eccentricity of 0.052 (+0.022/-0.017), favoured over a circular orbit with a false alarm probability of 5.8% (Kipping et al. 2010). If nothing keeps pumping it up, the planet experiences less tidal dissipation than Jupiter (Kipping et al. 2010). A search in TESS photometry and transit timing found no additional transiting planets down to 2-4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023). A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement was made as part of a study of hot Jupiter obliquities (Albrecht et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a hot F8 dwarf with a low metal content, [Fe/H] of -0.16 ± 0.08 (Kipping et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp24b-Kippingetal2010","cite":"Kipping et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. M. Kipping, G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-24b: An Inflated Hot Jupiter on a 3.36 Day Period Transiting a Hot, Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3389","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp24b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp24b-Albrechtetal2012","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John A. Johnson et al.","title":"Obliquities of Hot Jupiter Host Stars: Evidence for Tidal Interactions and Primordial Misalignments","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6105","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 900 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Speckle observations revealed GJ 900 to be a hierarchical triple star, about 200 million years old (Malogolovets et al. 2007). The primary is a K5-K7 star of about 0.64 solar masses, with two small companions of about 0.21 and 0.13 solar masses (Malogolovets et al. 2007).","system":"The companions orbit with possible periods of about 80 and 20 years, and the configuration may be dynamically unstable (Malogolovets et al. 2007). More very low mass components may exist (Malogolovets et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj900b-Malogolovetsetal2007","cite":"Malogolovets et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"E. V. Malogolovets, Yu. Yu. Balega, D. A. Rastegaev et al.","title":"GJ 900: A new hierarchical system with low-mass components","journal":"Astrophysical Bulletin","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.2193","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-8 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of 32 transiting-planet candidates in a single survey field, all followed up with spectra and extra photometry (Latham et al. 2009).","observed":"Two transits were observed with a 2.3-metre telescope in four colour bands, and the depth of the dip appears to change with wavelength, a preliminary hint about the atmosphere (Jarka et al. 2018). Warm Spitzer watched the planet pass behind its star at 3.6 and 4.5 microns (Todorov et al. 2012). The eclipse was deeper at 3.6 microns, and models without a temperature inversion, a layer where the upper air grows hotter with height, fit best (Todorov et al. 2012). Its radius is somewhat inflated for its mass (Latham et al. 2009).","system":"Two faint companion stars, with colours and brightnesses like M dwarfs, orbit the planet's host star and each other, so the planet lives in a triple star system (Bechter et al. 2014). The two are 65.3 milliarcseconds apart, about 15 AU (Bechter et al. 2014). The lighter one, HAT-P-8 C, is estimated at 0.18 solar masses (Bechter et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Tracking how the two companions orbit each other may give the first measured mass for stellar companions of a hot Jupiter host, and the system may have complex dynamics (Bechter et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp8b-Lathametal2009","cite":"Latham et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"David W. Latham, Gáspár Á. Bakos, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"Discovery of a Transiting Planet and Eight Eclipsing Binaries in HATNet Field G205","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.1161","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp8b-Jarkaetal2018","cite":"Jarka et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Kyla L. Jarka, Jackson Lane Cole, Cristilyn N. Gardner et al.","title":"Characterizing Giant Exoplanets through Multiwavelength Transit Observations: HAT-P-8b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp8b-Todorovetal2012","cite":"Todorov et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Kamen 0. Todorov, D. Deming, H. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Secondary Transit Photometry of Hot Jupiters HAT-P-6b, HAT-P-8b and XO-4b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #218","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp8b-Bechteretal2014","cite":"Bechter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Eric B. Bechter, Justin R. Crepp, Henry Ngo et al.","title":"WASP-12b and HAT-P-8b are Members of Triple Star Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6857","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-79 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's radius was uncertain at first because of too little follow-up photometry. It was at least 1.70 Jupiter radii and could have been as large as 2.09, which would have made it the largest known planet (Smalley et al. 2012).","observed":"The sky-projected angle between the star's spin and the planet's orbit is -106 degrees, so the planet circles nearly over the star's poles (Addison et al. 2013). Transmission spectra from 0.6 to 5.0 microns, taken with Hubble, Magellan and Spitzer, show a water feature, with water abundance constrained to between 10^-2.20 and 10^-1.55 (Sotzen et al. 2020). Models that include iron hydride and the negative hydrogen ion fit best (Sotzen et al. 2020). Hubble data alone also detect water vapour, with iron hydride in the best-fit models (Skaf et al. 2020).","system":"The host is an F-type star, and it fits the pattern that stars hotter than 6250 K often host hot Jupiters with misaligned orbits (Addison et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"A tentative hint that the orbital period is shortening needs more observations to confirm (Maciejewski et al. 2023). The planet sits in the transition between clear and cloudy atmospheres and is a target for the James Webb Space Telescope (Sotzen et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp79b-Smalleyetal2012","cite":"Smalley et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"B. Smalley, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-78b and WASP-79b: two highly-bloated hot Jupiter-mass exoplanets orbiting F-type stars in Eridanus","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1177","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp79b-Addisonetal2013","cite":"Addison et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"B. C. Addison, C. G. Tinney, D. J. Wright et al.","title":"A Nearly Polar Orbit for the Extrasolar Hot Jupiter WASP-79b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.0878","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp79b-Sotzenetal2020","cite":"Sotzen et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Kristin S. Sotzen, Kevin B. Stevenson, David K. Sing et al.","title":"Transmission Spectroscopy of WASP-79b from 0.6 to 5.0 μm","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02051","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp79b-Skafetal2020","cite":"Skaf et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Nour Skaf, Michelle Fabienne Bieger, Billy Edwards et al.","title":"ARES. II. Characterizing the Hot Jupiters WASP-127 b, WASP-79 b, and WASP-62b with the Hubble Space Telescope","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.09615","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp79b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2009-BLG-319L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet-to-star mass ratio places it in a predicted planet desert, a mass-ratio range where the runaway gas accretion model of core accretion expects few planets (Terry et al. 2021). Seven of the 30 planets in one earlier sample fall in this range, and this is the third with a measured host mass (Terry et al. 2021).","observed":"A survey telescope flagged the event as high magnification about 24 hours before its peak, so 20 different telescopes watched the planet's signal at the peak, the most for any planet discovery up to then (Miyake et al. 2011). About ten years later, Keck adaptive optics images detected the host star 66.5 milliarcseconds from the background source star, matching the prediction from the light curve (Terry et al. 2021). The combined data give a host star of 0.524 solar masses, a planet of 67.3 Earth masses, a distance of 7.1 kiloparsecs and a projected separation of 2.03 au (Terry et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a K or M dwarf in the inner Galactic disk or the Galactic bulge (Miyake et al. 2011).","history":"All three planets in the desert with measured host masses orbit stars of 0.5 to 0.7 solar masses, which suggests the gap is filled around stars within a factor of two of the Sun's mass (Terry et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2009blg319lb-Terryetal2021","cite":"Terry et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Sean K. Terry, Aparna Bhattacharya, David P. Bennett et al.","title":"MOA-2009-BLG-319Lb: A Sub-Saturn Planet inside the Predicted Mass Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.08461","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-moa2009blg319lb-Miyakeetal2011","cite":"Miyake et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"N. Miyake, T. Sumi, Subo Dong et al.","title":"A Sub-Saturn Mass Planet, MOA-2009-BLG-319Lb","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1809","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 206893 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the outer companion HD 206893 B, not planet c. Keck Planet Imager and Characterizer spectra of HD 206893 B show it is an exceptionally red object at the change from L-type to T-type spectra, and it was detected at more than 8 sigma in two epochs (Sappey et al. 2025). Its carbon-to-oxygen ratio is 0.57 +/- 0.02, close to the Sun's, and evolution models give an age of 112 million years and a mass of 22.7 Jupiter masses (Sappey et al. 2025). Little is known about planet c itself.","system":"HD 206893 hosts more than one companion, and HD 206893 B is part of a multi-planet system (Sappey et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd206893c-Sappeyetal2025","cite":"Sappey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ben Sappey, Quinn Konopacky, Clarissa R. Do Ó et al.","title":"HD 206893 B at High Spectral Resolution with the Keck Planet Imager and Characterizer","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-912 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a follow-up programme called THIRSTEE, which aims to understand the sub-Neptune population through detailed measurements of many planets (Lacedelli et al. 2026).","observed":"TESS light curves, ground-based photometry and radial velocities from the ESPRESSO, HARPS and IRD spectrographs were used to confirm the planet, measure its orbit and size, and look for further planets (Lacedelli et al. 2026). The star, with a temperature of 3572 K, has a near twin in TOI-521, and the two stars host nearly identical sub-Neptunes in mass and radius (Lacedelli et al. 2026).","system":"The host is an M dwarf. TOI-521 is a similar M dwarf of 3544 K that hosts a sub-Neptune in a 1.5-day orbit (Lacedelli et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Sub-Neptunes are absent in the Solar System yet are the most common planets in the Galaxy, and their internal makeup and origin remain uncertain (Lacedelli et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi912b-Lacedellietal2026","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Lacedelli, E. Pallé, R. Luque et al.","title":"Sibling sub-Neptunes around sibling M dwarfs: TOI-521 and TOI-912","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-29 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the smallest found by the WASP survey at the time of discovery, and the exoplanet most similar in mass and radius to Saturn (Hellier et al. 2010). The host star has above-solar metal content, which fits a possible trend that Saturn-mass planets around metal-rich stars have larger cores and so smaller radii (Hellier et al. 2010).","observed":"Spitzer watched the planet pass behind its star three times: twice at 3.6 microns and once at 4.5 microns. The eclipse was strongly detected at 3.6 microns and not detected at 4.5 microns (Hardin et al. 2012). The same pattern was seen on GJ 436 b, where it suggested abundant carbon monoxide, so WASP-29 b may also be rich in it (Hardin et al. 2012). Its equilibrium temperature is about 980 K (Hardin et al. 2012). Eclipse timings suggest a slightly eccentric orbit, with eccentricity between 0.037 and 0.056 (Challener et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether carbon monoxide is abundant is not settled, and the low signal-to-noise Spitzer data make the eclipse depths hard to pin down (Challener et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp29b-Hellieretal2010","cite":"Hellier et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-29b: A Saturn-sized Transiting Exoplanet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5318","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp29b-Hardinetal2012","cite":"Hardin et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Matthew Hardin, J. Harrington, K. Stevenson et al.","title":"WASP-29b: Another Cool Exoplanet With Abundant CO?","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #44","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp29b-Challeneretal2018","cite":"Challener et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Ryan Challener, Joseph Harrington, Patricio E. Cubillos et al.","title":"A Comparison of BLISS and PLD on Low-SNR WASP-29b Spitzer Observations","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD-10 3166 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Astrometry, which tracks tiny sideways shifts of a star on the sky, was used to search the host star for further companions over baselines of three to thirteen years. It found no planets of several Jupiter masses and no brown dwarfs with periods up to twice the length of coverage (Lurie et al. 2013). The host is a K dwarf (Lurie et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd103166b-Lurieetal2013","cite":"Lurie et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"John C. Lurie, T. J. Henry, W. Jao et al.","title":"Little Stars Don't Like Big Planets: An Astrometric Search for Super-Jupiters Around Red Dwarfs","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #221","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd103166b-Lurieetal2014","cite":"Lurie et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"John C. Lurie, Todd J. Henry, Wei-Chun Jao et al.","title":"The Solar Neighborhood. XXXIV. a Search for Planets Orbiting Nearby M Dwarfs Using Astrometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.4820","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-544 b":{"text":{"why":"Giacalone et al. 2022 picked the planet as one of 18 hot, potentially terrestrial TESS candidates that would make ideal targets for emission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope, and validated 13 of them as planets (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"A total of 122 HARPS and HARPS-N spectra over nearly three years confirmed the planet and measured its mass (Osborne et al. 2024). The planet has a radius of 2.018 Earth radii, a mass of 2.89 Earth masses and a bulk density of about 1.93 g per cubic centimetre (Osborne et al. 2024). The low density fits either a rocky core with a hydrogen atmosphere or a composition with a large share of water, and the two cannot be told apart with current interior models (Osborne et al. 2024). With an equilibrium temperature of 999 K and a 1.55-day orbit, it lies within the radius valley, where few planets are expected (Osborne et al. 2024).","system":"The velocities also revealed a non-transiting companion, TOI-544 c, with a minimum mass of 21.5 Earth masses and an orbital period of about 50 days (Osborne et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether planet b is a water world or a rocky core with hydrogen is not known. At this temperature a rocky core would struggle to keep a hydrogen layer (Osborne 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi544b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi544b-Osborneetal2024","cite":"Osborne et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. L. M. Osborne, V. Van Eylen, E. Goffo et al.","title":"TOI-544 b: a potential water-world inside the radius valley in a two-planet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14908","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi544b-Osborne2022","cite":"Osborne 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Hannah Osborne","title":"TOI544b: a new small planet inside the radius valley.","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2010-BLG-477L b":{"text":{"why":"Microlensing finds cool planets beyond the snow line, where giant planets are thought to form, and such detections help build an unbiased sample of how common they are (Bachelet et al. 2012).","observed":"Its mass ratio to the star is 2.181 x 10^-3, and the projected separation is 1.1228 Einstein radii (Bachelet et al. 2012). The angular Einstein radius is unusually large, 1.38 mas (Bachelet et al. 2012). A strong degeneracy between microlensing parallax and the planet's orbital motion limits the host mass to between 0.13 and 1.0 solar masses (Bachelet et al. 2012). Galactic-model and orbit priors each favour the upper end, giving a star of about 0.67 solar masses and a planet of about 1.5 Jupiter masses (Bachelet et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host and planet masses remain uncertain because of the parallax-orbit degeneracy (Bachelet et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2010blg477lb-Bacheletetal2012","cite":"Bachelet et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"E. Bachelet, I.-G. Shin, C. Han et al.","title":"MOA 2010-BLG-477Lb: Constraining the Mass of a Microlensing Planet from Microlensing Parallax, Orbital Motion, and Detection of Blended Light","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.6323","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AU Mic e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about planet e itself; work so far has studied its star and the system's other planets. Hubble far-ultraviolet observations of AU Mic over more than 5 hours caught 13 flares with energies from 10^29 to 10^31 erg (Feinstein et al. 2022). The baseline atmospheric mass-loss rate for planet b is about 10^8 g per second, and it can be much higher during flares (Feinstein et al. 2022). Star spots modulate the star's light every 4.847 days (Hebb et al. 2007).","system":"AU Mic is a young M dwarf of about 22 million years. Its planets orbit inside a debris disc that is seen nearly edge-on (Feinstein et al. 2022; Hebb et al. 2007). A search for transits found no Jupiter-mass planets in the disc's plane with periods of 5 days or less (Hebb et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-aumice-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Kevin France, Allison Youngblood et al.","title":"AU Microscopii in the Far-UV: Observations in Quiescence, during Flares, and Implications for AU Mic b and c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09606","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumice-Hebbetal2007","cite":"Hebb et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Leslie Hebb, Larry Petro, Holland C. Ford et al.","title":"A search for planets transiting the M-dwarf debris disc host, AU Microscopii","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.3584","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-415 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first spotted in K2 light curves and is also seen in recent TESS data (Hirano et al. 2023). Follow-up high-resolution imaging and near-infrared spectroscopy ruled out false alarms, leaving a false-positive probability of 2 x 10^-4 (Hirano et al. 2023). Sparse velocities over three years with the IRD spectrograph give a mass of 3.0 +/- 2.7 Earth masses, with a 95 per cent upper limit of 7.5 Earth masses (Hirano et al. 2023). The host is an M5 dwarf, one of the lowest-mass stars known to host an Earth-sized transiting planet (Hirano et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass is poorly pinned down, and more velocity monitoring and transit spectroscopy are planned follow-up (Hirano et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2415b-Hiranoetal2023","cite":"Hirano et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Fei Dai, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"An Earth-sized Planet around an M5 Dwarf Star at 22 pc","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00699","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 16141 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck HIRES velocities of the G5 subgiant host show a repeating signal of 11 metres per second with a period of 75.8 days, which gives a minimum mass of 0.22 Jupiter masses and an orbit of 0.35 au (Marcy et al. 2000). Along with a similar companion at HD 46375, it adds to the rising number of planets as mass drops (Marcy et al. 2000).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd16141b-Marcyetal2000","cite":"Marcy et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt","title":"Sub-Saturn Planetary Candidates of HD 16141 and HD 46375","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0004326","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 73344 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Space and ground telescopes were combined to study the star: K2 saw it in two campaigns in 2017-2018, and TESS in two sectors in 2021-2022, with SOPHIE and HIRES velocities gathered through 2021 (Sulis et al. 2024). The transiting sub-Neptune has an orbital period of 15.61 days (Zhang et al. 2025). TESS light curves give a stellar rotation period of 9.03 +/- 1.3 days (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"HD 73344 also hosts a non-transiting Saturn-mass planet with a 65.936-day period and a Jupiter-like planet, HD 73344 d, found from 27 years of velocities (Zhang et al. 2025). Combining velocities with Hipparcos-Gaia astrometry, the outer giant's orbit is tilted relative to the innermost transiting planet by between 46 and 134 degrees (1 sigma), which strongly disfavours a flat system (Zhang et al. 2025). The two inner planets are strongly coupled with each other (Zhang et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd73344b-Sulisetal2024","cite":"Sulis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Sulis, I. J. M. Crossfield, A. Santerne et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 73344 RV and light curves (Sulis+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd73344b-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jingwen Zhang, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"Discovery of a Jupiter Analog Misaligned to the Inner Planetary System in HD 73344","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.09614","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-161 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities confirmed the planet, which was first found as a candidate in K2 light curves from Campaign 14 (Brahm et al. 2019). Described as a low-density super-Neptune, it orbits a slightly evolved G-type star with a significantly eccentric orbit, e = 0.420 +/- 0.034 (Brahm et al. 2019). The star is bright, which makes the planet one of the best super-Neptune candidates for transmission spectroscopy (Brahm et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved G-type star of 1.669 solar radii (Brahm et al. 2019).","history":"Tides should circularise the orbit in about 1.8 billion years, but the host star is expected to engulf the planet in about 1 billion years, before that happens (Brahm et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2161b-Brahmetal2019","cite":"Brahm et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"R. Brahm, N. Espinoza, M. Rabus et al.","title":"K2-161b: a low-density super-Neptune on an eccentric orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.04073","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2076 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its three outer planets, which have orbits of about 10, 21 and 35 days (Damasso et al. 2024). The star is young, about 300 million years old, and its surface activity makes it jitter by more than 30 m/s in radial velocity, which hides the small tugs of the planets (Damasso et al. 2024). TESS watched the star in four separate observing seasons between 2019 and 2024 (Barber et al. 2025).","system":"The star belongs to a loose group of stars that move together through space. Earlier surveys placed it in groups of 26 and 76 members, aged 0.1 to 0.7 billion years and 300 million years respectively (Barber et al. 2025). Its three outer planets are sub-Neptune size, about 3 to 4 times the Earth's radius (Hedges et al. 2021).","history":"Its young age makes the system a test of how planets and their atmospheres change early on; the expected loss of atmosphere through heating by the star was calculated up to an age of 5 billion years (Damasso et al. 2024).","open":"Little is known about this innermost planet itself; work so far has mostly studied the star and its outer planets."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2076e-Damassoetal2024","cite":"Damasso et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Locci, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG: LIX. Characterisation study of the ∼300 Myr-old multi-planetary system orbiting the star BD+40 2790 (TOI-2076)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10629","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076e-Barberetal2025","cite":"Barber et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"M. G. Barber, A. W. Mann, A. Vanderburg et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TI-DYE. III. TOI-2076 light curves & comoving stars (Barber+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076e-Hedgesetal2021","cite":"Hedges et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christina Hedges, Alex Hughes, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2076 and TOI-1807: Two Young, Comoving Planetary Systems within 50 pc Identified by TESS that are Ideal Candidates for Further Follow Up","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01311","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5795 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was studied to help explain how Neptune-size planets end up on orbits shorter than ten days (Manni et al. 2025).","observed":"The star was watched with the HARPS spectrograph for two months to measure the planet's mass from the wobble it causes in the star's motion (Manni et al. 2025). Sharp-imaging checks with speckle and adaptive-optics cameras ruled out a hidden nearby star as the source of the signal (Manni et al. 2025). The host is a metal-poor G-type star, with less iron than the Sun, at [Fe/H] = -0.27 (Manni et al. 2025). The planet is described as a hot super-Neptune (Manni et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Three routes are considered for hot Neptunes: forming like longer-period Neptunes, coming from rare collisions of smaller planets, or being the leftover cores of giant planets stripped of their gas (Manni et al. 2025).","open":"How such close-in Neptunes form is still uncertain, and many more must be studied to settle it (Manni et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5795b-Mannietal2025","cite":"Manni et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"F. Manni, L. Naponiello, L. Mancini et al.","title":"The Hot Neptune Initiative (HONEI): II. TOI-5795 b: A hot super-Neptune orbiting a metal-poor star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.23413","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-19 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a very low density, similar to a puffed-up Saturn (Hartman et al. 2011). Spitzer observed it passing behind its star at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres, to read its dayside glow (Kammer et al. 2015). Planets lighter than Jupiter in that sample needed efficient heat sharing to the night side, or a brighter reflective surface, to match the models (Kammer et al. 2015). Ground-based transit observations found no significant timing changes (Seeliger et al. 2015). Citizen-science timings hint at a slow drift in its orbital period that still needs confirmation (Hagey et al. 2022).","system":"The star's velocity shows a steady linear trend that points to a third body in the system (Hartman et al. 2011). Searches in TESS data found no small planets close to the planet, and transit timing set tighter limits on that outer body (Maciejewski 2023).","history":"The lack of close companions fits the idea that hot Jupiters reach their orbits by high-eccentricity migration (Maciejewski 2023).","open":"The identity of the third body is not known. The period drift is not confirmed (Hagey et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp19b-Hartmanetal2011","cite":"Hartman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, B. Sato et al.","title":"HAT-P-18b and HAT-P-19b: Two Low-density Saturn-mass Planets Transiting Metal-rich K Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4850","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp19b-Kammeretal2015","cite":"Kammer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Joshua A. Kammer, Heather A. Knutson, Michael R. Line et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipse Observations of Five Cool Gas Giant Planets and Empirical Trends in Cool Planet Emission Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.00902","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp19b-Seeligeretal2015","cite":"Seeliger et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Seeliger, M. Kitze, R. Errmann et al.","title":"Ground-based transit observations of the HAT-P-18, HAT-P-19, HAT-P-27/WASP40 and WASP-21 systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.06215","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp19b-Hageyetal2022","cite":"Hagey et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Simone R. Hagey, Billy Edwards, Aaron C. Boley","title":"Evidence of Long-term Period Variations in the Exoplanet Transit Database (ETD)","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10752","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp19b-Maciejewski2023","cite":"Maciejewski 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, M. Fernández, A. Sota et al.","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. III. A Study of Ten Systems Enhanced with New Ground-Based Photometry","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00538","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-426 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert, a region of short orbits where Neptune-size planets are scarce (Castro-González et al. 2026).","observed":"Seventy-seven HARPS velocity measurements were combined with TESS photometry to weigh the planet (Castro-González et al. 2026). Stellar activity adds strong signals to the velocities, so the team modelled them with Gaussian processes, statistical curves that separate star noise from planet signals (Castro-González et al. 2026). The planet is a very strongly irradiated, volatile-rich sub-Neptune, a world rich in water or gas rather than bare rock (Castro-González et al. 2026).","system":"The system also holds a companion planet (Castro-González et al. 2026).","history":"Two ideas explain the desert edge: sub-Neptunes torn apart by tides after reaching tight orbits on stretched paths, and loss of their hydrogen and helium envelopes to space (Castro-González et al. 2026).","open":"The origin of the lower desert edge remains an open question (Castro-González et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi426b-CastroGonzlezetal2026","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Castro-González, O. Barragán, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"Two extremely irradiated volatile-rich sub-Neptunes with companions in the TOI-426 and TOI-1839 systems: Insights into arrival and survival near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05413","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-418 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour Kepler-418 b. Planet b was the first planet confirmed mainly through the transit colour signature, meaning how the dip in starlight differs between colours (Tingley et al. 2014). The star's light is blended with an unresolved source adding about 30 percent to the target, found only through multicolour photometry (Tingley et al. 2014). Kepler-418 c is one of the two proposed planets in the system (Tingley et al. 2014).","system":"The host star is significantly blended with another light source, so planet sizes depend on correcting for it (Tingley et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Little is known about planet c itself; work so far has focused on planet b."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler418c-Tingleyetal2014","cite":"Tingley et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"B. Tingley, H. Parviainen, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Confirmation of an exoplanet using the transit color signature: Kepler-418b, a blended giant planet in a multiplanet system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.5354","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-76 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an ultra-hot Jupiter with an equilibrium temperature of 2200 K (May et al. 2026). Hubble spectra of the planet's dayside suggest titanium oxide, a thermal inversion where the air warms with height, and a lot of water (Edwards et al. 2020). ESPRESSO spectra detected lithium, sodium, magnesium, calcium, manganese, potassium and iron in the atmosphere (Tabernero et al. 2021). Observations hint at iron absorbing differently on its two limbs, nicknamed iron rain (May et al. 2026). Spitzer found a large day-night contrast with small offsets, consistent with strong drag from ionised gas on the hot dayside (May et al. 2026). New JWST data cover 0.2 to 14 micrometres (May et al. 2026).","system":"A fainter star sits near the host, and removing its light from the Hubble data removed the earlier hints of titanium oxide and vanadium oxide (Edwards et al. 2020).","history":"The planet is inflated, about 1.8 Jupiter radii, a known trend for strongly irradiated hot Jupiters (West et al. 2016).","open":"Whether titanium oxide is truly present is still debated (Edwards et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp76b-Edwardsetal2020","cite":"Edwards et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Billy Edwards, Quentin Changeat, Robin Baeyens et al.","title":"ARES I: WASP-76 b, A Tale of Two HST Spectra","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.02374","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp76b-Taberneroetal2021","cite":"Tabernero et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"H. M. Tabernero, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, R. Allart et al.","title":"ESPRESSO high-resolution transmission spectroscopy of WASP-76 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.12197","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp76b-Westetal2016","cite":"West et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. G. West, C. Hellier, J.-M. Almenara et al.","title":"Three irradiated and bloated hot Jupiters:. WASP-76b, WASP-82b, and WASP-90b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5607","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":37,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106906 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is seen directly in infrared images, about 650 AU from its star, and moves with the star across the sky (Bailey et al. 2014). Models of its brightness give a mass of about 11 Jupiter masses, and its spectrum resembles a young, cool L-type object (Bailey et al. 2014). It is one of the most widely separated planet-mass companions known (Bailey et al. 2014). Millimetre observations place a low upper limit on any disc of dust around the planet (Marino et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a 13 million year old F-type star in the Sco-Cen association with a near edge-on debris disc, cleared inside about 50 AU and reaching beyond 500 AU (Kalas et al. 2015). The outer disc is lopsided, shaped like a needle (Kalas et al. 2015). The star is a binary, and the planet is outside the disc and appears to disturb it (Marino et al. 2020). The planet's position is about 21 degrees off the disc's plane, so the orbit is probably tilted against the disc (Kalas et al. 2015).","history":"Simulations show the disc's lopsided shape is consistent with pushes from the outer planet (Nesvold et al. 2017). A giant collision between solid bodies is another way to make such lopsided discs (Jones et al. 2023).","open":"The orbit is not known; an eccentric orbit above 0.65 would let it reach the disc edge (Bailey et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106906b-Baileyetal2014","cite":"Bailey et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Vanessa Bailey, Tiffany Meshkat, Megan Reiter et al.","title":"HD 106906 b: A Planetary-mass Companion Outside a Massive Debris Disk","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1265","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106906b-Marinoetal2020","cite":"Marino et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. Marino, A. Hughes, E. Nesvold et al.","title":"HD106906 Debris Disk Morphology and Origin of an External Perturber","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106906b-Kalasetal2015","cite":"Kalas et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Paul G. Kalas, Abhijith Rajan, Jason J. Wang et al.","title":"Direct Imaging of an Asymmetric Debris Disk in the HD 106906 Planetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.02747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106906b-Nesvoldetal2017","cite":"Nesvold et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Erika R. Nesvold, Smadar Naoz, Michael P. Fitzgerald","title":"HD 106906: A Case Study for External Perturbations of a Debris Disk","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.06578","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106906b-Jonesetal2023","cite":"Jones et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joshua W. Jones, Eugene Chiang, Gaspard Duchêne et al.","title":"Giant Impacts and Debris Disk Morphology","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10189","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2007-BLG-349L AB c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits two stars at once, and is the first circumbinary planet found through microlensing (Bennett et al. 2016). The light curve showed a clear planet signal plus an extra lens mass, either a second planet or a second star (Bennett et al. 2016). Hubble images resolved the lens and found excess flux from star light, which fits two stars but not a single star with two planets (Bennett et al. 2016). The planet is about 80 Earth masses (Bennett et al. 2016).","system":"The two host stars are M dwarfs of about 0.41 and 0.30 solar masses, which makes this the lowest-mass circumbinary planet system known (Bennett et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2007blg349labc-Bennettetal2016","cite":"Bennett et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. P. Bennett, S. H. Rhie, A. Udalski et al.","title":"The First Circumbinary Planet Found by Microlensing: OGLE-2007-BLG-349L(AB)c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.06720","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour HD 110067 c. ESPRESSO watched planet c cross the star and found its orbit lined up with the star's spin, with a sky-projected tilt of 6 degrees (+24, -26) (Zak et al. 2024). Planet e is one of the resonant sub-Neptunes in the same system, which the work suggests all share one flat orbital plane (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"The planets sit in a resonant chain, orbits linked by simple period ratios (Zak et al. 2024). The planet host is not alone: a K3 star, HD 110106, lies 13,400 au away and is itself an 8-year double-lined spectroscopic binary (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"The aligned orbit suggests the system reached its layout by migrating together without major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067e-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067e-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1801 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first listed with a period of 21.3 days, and follow-up measurements showed the true period is 10.6 days (Mallorquín et al. 2023).","observed":"Ground-based photometry and radial velocities from CARMENES and HIRES gave a mass of 5.74 Earth masses (Mallorquín et al. 2023). The planet is most probably made of water and rock, with at most 2 percent hydrogen by mass in its atmosphere (Mallorquín et al. 2023). The star rotates every 16 days, a signal found in both velocities and photometry (Mallorquín et al. 2023). The host star is young, 600 to 800 million years old, making the planet the least massive young mini-Neptune with precise mass and size (Mallorquín et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Any larger atmosphere in the past must have been removed in under 1 billion years (Mallorquín et al. 2023).","open":"The process that removed the gas is not known (Mallorquín et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1801b-Mallorqunetal2023","cite":"Mallorquín et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Mallorquín, E. Goffo, E. Pallé et al.","title":"TOI-1801 b: A temperate mini-Neptune around a young M0.5 dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10244","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3493 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Eighty-three HARPS spectra taken from February 2022 to August 2023 were used to measure the planet's mass (Chaturvedi et al. 2025). The orbit is nearly circular (Chaturvedi et al. 2025). The planet has a bulk density of about 1.47 g/cm3, similar to Neptune's, which fits a small solid core wrapped in a thick hydrogen-helium atmosphere (Chaturvedi et al. 2025). The host is a bright G0 star (Chaturvedi et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3493b-Chaturvedietal2025","cite":"Chaturvedi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"P. Chaturvedi, E. Goffo, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"TOI-3493 b: A planet with a Neptune-like density transiting a bright G0-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12884","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 202772 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first hot Jupiter confirmed from TESS data (Wang et al. 2019).","observed":"The host is among the brightest and most massive stars known to have a hot Jupiter, and it is mildly evolved (Wang et al. 2019). The planet is inflated and one of the most strongly irradiated hot Jupiters known (Wang et al. 2019). Spitzer eclipse depths at 3.6 micrometres were 680 ppm, giving a weak day-night contrast at that band, and a strong contrast at 4.5 micrometres (Adams et al. 2024). JWST follow-up is recommended (Adams et al. 2024).","system":"The star is in a wide visual binary with HD 202772 B, and no planet has been found around B (Jofré et al. 2025). Star A is hot, about 6440 K, and near the end of its main-sequence phase (Jofré et al. 2025). The planet is the most irradiated hot Jupiter known in a wide binary (Jofré et al. 2025).","history":"Whether the companion star helped move the planet inward through Kozai-Lidov cycles is being tested (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd202772ab-Wangetal2019","cite":"Wang et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Songhu Wang, Matias Jones, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"HD 202772A b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter around a Bright, Mildly Evolved Star in a Visual Binary Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.02341","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202772ab-Adamsetal2024","cite":"Adams et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Arthur D. Adams, Kimberly Bott, Paul A. Dalba et al.","title":"Modeling Spitzer 3.6 and 4.5 μm Eclipse Depths for the Inflated Hot Jupiter in the Evolved Binary System HD 202772","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.13680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202772ab-Jofretal2025","cite":"Jofré et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Emiliano Jofré, Yilen Gómez Maqueo Chew, Romina Petrucci et al.","title":"Detailed spectroscopic and photometric analysis of the remarkable planet-hosting wide binary system HD 202772A/B","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01595","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd202772ab-VazquezEastman2025","cite":"Vazquez & Eastman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacqueline Vazquez, Jason Eastman","title":"Understanding Hot Jupiter Migration for Six Binary Star Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 1069 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was detected in 262 CARMENES observations covering almost four years (Kossakowski et al. 2023). The minimum mass is 1.26 Earth masses (Kossakowski et al. 2023). The orbit is at 0.0672 au and puts it in the habitable zone, with 0.652 times the Earth's incoming starlight (Kossakowski et al. 2023). The star rotates slowly, with a period of 150 to 170 days, found by new photometric monitoring (Kossakowski et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a very low-mass M5 dwarf of 0.167 solar masses, about 9.6 parsecs away (Kossakowski et al. 2023). The velocities show extra long-period signals, one linked to the star's rotation (Kossakowski et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf1069b-Kossakowskietal2023","cite":"Kossakowski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Kossakowski, M. Kürster, T. Trifonov et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Wolf 1069 b: Earth-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby, very low-mass star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.02477","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-35 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aimed at long-period giant planets, since beyond 10 days few transiting gas giants are known (Kendall et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet is one of two Saturn-size worlds on orbits of about 25 days, reported with a 43-day Neptune, NGTS-34 b (Kendall et al. 2026). Each was first seen as a single transit in TESS data, then solved with more transits from NGTS and ASTEP (Kendall et al. 2026). CORALIE and HARPS radial velocities confirmed the masses (Kendall et al. 2026). On wide orbits, planets are less affected by their star, so they probe how planets form and migrate (Kendall et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts35b-Kendalletal2026","cite":"Kendall et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alicia Kendall, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Samuel Gill et al.","title":"A 43 d transiting Neptune and two 25 d Saturns from TESS, NGTS, and ASTEP","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07716","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3942 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found behind strong stellar activity signals, using 145 HARPS-N observations over five years (Perger et al. 2017). The star rotates every 16.3 days (Perger et al. 2017). An extra signal of 10.4 days may be a second planet near a 3:2 resonance with GJ 3942 b, if it is confirmed (Perger et al. 2017). A later reanalysis asks whether the star's true rotation is 32 days with 16 days as a harmonic, and TESS photometry cannot settle it (Fonseca & Dodson-Robinson 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The second signal is not yet significant enough to claim as a planet (Perger et al. 2017). The rotation period of the star is ambiguous (Fonseca & Dodson-Robinson 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3942b-Pergeretal2017","cite":"Perger et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Perger, I. Ribas, M. Damasso et al.","title":"HADES RV Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. VI. GJ 3942 b behind dominant activity signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.06851","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3942b-FonsecaDodsonRobinson2024","cite":"Fonseca & Dodson-Robinson 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Andrew Fonseca, Sarah Dodson-Robinson","title":"Time Series Analysis of GJ 3942","journal":"Zenodo","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14199669","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1649 c":{"text":{"why":"The Kepler pipeline labelled the planet a false positive, and a visual inspection of all rejected candidates rescued it (Vanderburg et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet is about 1.06 times Earth's size and receives 74 percent of Earth's incoming starlight, giving an equilibrium temperature of 234 K (Vanderburg et al. 2020). It sits in the habitable zone of a mid M-dwarf star of 0.1977 solar masses (Vanderburg et al. 2020). The discovery shows that human inspection still matters as automated methods improve (Vanderburg et al. 2020).","system":"An inner planet orbits every 8.7 days and is about Venus-like in size and incoming light (Vanderburg et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1649c-Vanderburgetal2020","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Pamela Rowden, Steve Bryson et al.","title":"A Habitable-zone Earth-sized Planet Rescued from False Positive Status","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.06725","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-20 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three signals were shown to be sub-Neptune planets, strongly disfavouring false positives such as blended stars, while two were left as Earth-size candidates (Gautier et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a Sun-like star with a temperature of 5455 K and about the Sun's metal content (Gautier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler20e-Gautieretal2012","cite":"Gautier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas N., III Gautier, David Charbonneau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-20: A Sun-like Star with Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets and Two Earth-size Candidates","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.4514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 100777 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a HARPS survey of about 1400 Solar-type stars, meant to improve statistics on planet orbits (Naef et al. 2007).","observed":"The velocities fit a planet of 1.16 Jupiter masses on a 384-day eccentric orbit with e = 0.36 (Naef et al. 2007). A later study ran long orbital simulations of hypothetical Earth-mass and Luna-mass moons around the planet, mapping stable and chaotic orbits (Gautam et al. 2022). No exomoon has yet been found around any planet (Gautam et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has moons is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd100777b-Naefetal2007","cite":"Naef et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IX. Exoplanets orbiting <ASTROBJ>HD 100777</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 190647</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 221287</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.0917","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd100777b-Gautametal2022","cite":"Gautam et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Aayush Gautam, Angel Bashyal, Shree Krishna Bhattarai et al.","title":"Orbital stability analysis of hypothetical Earth-mass and Luna-mass moons in the Sagarmatha (HD 100777) star system","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/psac037","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4603 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive hot Jupiter of 12.8 Jupiter masses and one of the highest-density planets known (Bence et al. 2025). The orbit is moderately eccentric, with e = 0.3, so it has not fully circularised (Bence et al. 2025). Radial velocities combined with astrometry point to a long-period outer companion of about 49.9 Jupiter masses, in the brown dwarf range, at about 6.7 AU (Bence et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Outer companions of hot Jupiters hold clues about how those planets reached their orbits (Bence et al. 2025).","open":"The outer companion is only potential, and its mass has large uncertainties (Bence et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4603b-Benceetal2025","cite":"Bence et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ciana-Lei Bence, Jingwen Zhang, Daniel Huber","title":"Constraining the properties of a long-period giant companion outside a massive hot Jupiter","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 15337 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star had 85 HARPS velocities taken over 14 years before TESS found two transit signals at 4.76 and 17.18 days (Dumusque et al. 2019). The star's activity is similar to the Sun's, with a magnetic cycle of similar size, and was modelled using the calcium activity index (Dumusque et al. 2019). The inner planet is described as hot and rocky and the outer one as warm and puffy (Dumusque et al. 2019). CHEOPS later observed 6 transits of the star (Rosario et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd15337b-Dumusqueetal2019","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Xavier Dumusque, Oliver Turner, Caroline Dorn et al.","title":"Hot, rocky and warm, puffy super-Earths orbiting TOI-402 (HD 15337)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05419","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd15337b-Rosarioetal2024","cite":"Rosario et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"N. M. Rosario, O. D. S. Demangeon, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CHEOPS light curves of HD 15337 (TOI-402) (Rosario+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-62 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet shows a clear atmosphere with no clouds or haze at the depths probed (Alam et al. 2021). Hubble and Spitzer transmission spectra detected sodium at 5.1 sigma, with the pressure-broadened wings of the sodium lines seen from space for the first time (Alam et al. 2021). A feature at 0.4 micrometres may be silicon hydride (Alam et al. 2021). It is the only known transiting gas giant in the JWST continuous viewing zone (Alam et al. 2021). The orbit is slightly misaligned with the star's spin, by 19.4 degrees (Brown et al. 2017). Swift ultraviolet observations placed only upper limits on its transit depth (Corrales et al. 2021).","system":"No transiting companions were found down to terrestrial size (Maciejewski 2020).","history":"The loneliness of such planets favours high-eccentricity migration (Maciejewski 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp62b-Alametal2021","cite":"Alam et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Munazza K. Alam, Mercedes López-Morales, Ryan J. MacDonald et al.","title":"Evidence of a Clear Atmosphere for WASP-62b: The Only Known Transiting Gas Giant in the JWST Continuous Viewing Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.06424","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp62b-Brownetal2017","cite":"Brown et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. H. M. J. Triaud, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Rossiter-McLaughlin models and their effect on estimates of stellar rotation, illustrated using six WASP systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00600","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp62b-Corralesetal2021","cite":"Corrales et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Lia Corrales, Sasikrishna Ravi, George W. King et al.","title":"Five New Hot Jupiter Transits Investigated with Swift-UVOT","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.01579","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp62b-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1513 c":{"text":{"why":"The planet was not seen to cross its star. Its presence was inferred from timing changes in Kepler-1513 b (Yahalomi et al. 2024).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-1513 b. Kepler-1513 b is an 8.05 Earth-radii planet at 0.53 au whose transit timing variations first looked like a moon (Yahalomi et al. 2024). New transits nearly a decade after Kepler showed an extra long-period timing change (Yahalomi et al. 2024). Only a non-transiting perturbing planet reproduced the timings, ruling out a moon and stellar activity (Yahalomi et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1513c-Yahalomietal2024","cite":"Yahalomi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daniel A. Yahalomi, David Kipping, David Nesvorný et al.","title":"Not-so-fast Kepler-1513: a perturbing planetary interloper in the exomoon corridor","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03802","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 1061 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planets were detected in archival HARPS spectra, using a custom cross-correlation template tailored to the star (Wright et al. 2016). The velocities do not suffer from the strong annual variation seen with the standard pipeline (Wright et al. 2016). No signs of stellar activity were found near the planetary periods (Wright et al. 2016).","system":"Three planets orbit the M3 dwarf, with minimum masses of 1.36, 4.25 and 5.21 Earth masses (Wright et al. 2016). The 17.9-day planet is in the habitable zone and the 67-day one lies just beyond it (Wright et al. 2016). All three may be rocky (Wright et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf1061b-Wrightetal2016","cite":"Wright et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Wright, R. A. Wittenmyer, C. G. Tinney et al.","title":"Three Planets Orbiting Wolf 1061","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05154","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gaia-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of two confirmed among 28 M and K stars with astrometric candidate companions, and 21 were refuted as stellar binaries (Stefánsson et al. 2025).","observed":"Gaia measures the tiny sideways wobble of stars, but velocity data are needed to confirm a companion (Stefánsson et al. 2025). Gaia-4 b is a massive planet of 11.8 Jupiter masses on a 571-day orbit around a 0.644 solar-mass star (Stefánsson et al. 2025). It is one of the first exoplanets found by astrometry and one of the most massive known around a low-mass star (Stefánsson et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gaia4b-Stefnssonetal2025","cite":"Stefánsson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Gudmundur Stefánsson, Suvrath Mahadevan, Joshua N. Winn et al.","title":"Gaia-4b and 5b: Radial Velocity Confirmation of Gaia Astrometric Orbital Solutions Reveal a Massive Planet and a Brown Dwarf Orbiting Low-mass Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05654","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-419 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to the Searching for GEMS survey of giant planets around M dwarfs, which are rare because the stars are small (Kanodia et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet has an equilibrium temperature of only 380 K, one of the coolest well-characterised transiting planets (Kanodia et al. 2024). It was observed by K2 in Campaigns 5 and 18 as well as TESS, which fixed the orbit despite a faint star and a period of 20.4 days (Kanodia et al. 2024). Radial velocities came from HPF and MAROON-X (Kanodia et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2419ab-Kanodiaetal2024","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Arvind F. Gupta, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Characterizing Six Giant Planets Around Cool Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 9827 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star hosts three small transiting planets with periods of 1.2, 3.6 and 6.2 days, close to a 1:3:5 ratio (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). FIES, HARPS and HARPS-N velocities were combined with K2 photometry to measure all three masses (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). The system is the nearest found by Kepler or K2, about 30 parsecs away, with a bright star (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018).","system":"The planet closest in, b, has a density of 5.11 g/cm3 (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj9827c-PrietoArranzetal2018","cite":"Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Prieto-Arranz, E. Palle, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Mass determination of the 1:3:5 near-resonant planets transiting GJ 9827 (K2-135)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.09557","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-132 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its hot Jupiter WASP-132 b and a small inner planet. WASP-132 b is a cool giant on a 7.1-day orbit around a K4 star (Hellier et al. 2017). A 1.78 Earth-radii planet on a 1.01-day orbit was validated inside it (Hord et al. 2022). CHEOPS and TESS photometry were combined with CORALIE and HARPS velocities (Grieves et al. 2025).","system":"The inner planet validated beside the hot Jupiter is stable for about 100 million years in simulations (Hord et al. 2022).","history":"A close companion makes high-eccentricity migration of the hot Jupiter highly unlikely (Hord et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp132d-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp132d-Hordetal2022","cite":"Hord et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Benjamin Hord, Knicole Colon, Travis Berger et al.","title":"TOI-822.02 is a Nearby Planetary Companion to Hot Jupiter WASP-132 b","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp132d-Grievesetal2025","cite":"Grieves et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Grieves, F. Bouchy, J. D. Armstrong et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-132 RV and TESS and TESS light curves (Grieves+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-13 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ground-based telescopes (CFHT and IAC80) confirmed the dips in starlight first recorded by the CoRoT space mission. The planet's mass was measured with the HARPS spectrograph (Cabrera et al. 2010). Its density is 2.34 g per cubic centimetre, which is extreme for a planet of this mass. Heavy elements are estimated to make up between about 140 and 300 Earth masses of it (Cabrera et al. 2010). Keck spectra of the star show solar metallicity and a lithium content higher than the Sun's, which fits the star's temperature, activity and age (Cabrera et al. 2010).","system":"The host is a G0 dwarf star of about solar size, with an estimated age between 0.12 and 3.15 billion years (Cabrera et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot13b-Cabreraetal2010","cite":"Cabrera et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"J. Cabrera, H. Bruntt, M. Ollivier et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission . XIII. CoRoT-13b: a dense hot Jupiter in transit around a star with solar metallicity and super-solar lithium content","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.5481","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-50 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with three other transiting giants from the same ground-based survey. Its orbit is consistent with circular, and its mass and size are both known to better than 10% (Hartman et al. 2015). Its star is larger than the Sun, with a radius of 1.70 solar radii (Hartman et al. 2015). A later search of space-telescope light curves and transit timings found no additional transiting planets in the system down to 2 to 4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"No second planet has been detected. Searches of TESS light curves reach down to planets 2 to 4 times the size of Earth (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp50b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-50b, HAT-P-51b, HAT-P-52b, and HAT-P-53b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters and a Transiting Hot Saturn From the HATNet Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04149","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp50b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 77946 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS first flagged the planet as the candidate TOI-1778 b. Follow-up used HARPS-N radial velocities and CHEOPS photometry, giving a mass of 8.38 Earth masses and a radius of 2.705 Earth radii (Palethorpe et al. 2024). Together with the star's chemical makeup, these values suggest a sub-Neptune with an atmosphere of hydrogen and helium making up about 1 per cent of its mass (Palethorpe et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1248 K (Palethorpe et al. 2024).","system":"The host is an F5 star slightly larger and heavier than the Sun, with no other planet known (Palethorpe et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is a sub-Neptune or a water world is still open. At 1248 K, water inside it could be in a supercritical state, a hot dense fluid that is neither liquid nor gas (Palethorpe et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd77946b-Palethorpeetal2024","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"L. Palethorpe, John A. Anna, A. Mortier et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 77946 RV curves (Palethorpe+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-181 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was validated as part of the VaTEST project, which confirms TESS planet candidates with statistical tools (Mistry et al. 2023).","observed":"TESS transit data and HARPS radial velocities show a sub-Saturn on a narrow 4.5-day orbit around a K dwarf. The orbit is slightly eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.15 (Mistry et al. 2023). A later survey of Neptunes measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, which shows how the orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin. The sky-projected misalignment is about 32 degrees, and a low eccentricity below 0.12 was found (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The planet is a useful test of how close-in Neptune-sized planets reach their tight orbits (Mistry et al. 2023). The tilted orbit is among the first results of a survey that uses stellar obliquity to probe where transiting Neptunes come from (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026).","open":"Two studies give different eccentricities, 0.15 and below 0.12 (Mistry et al. 2023; Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi181b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Georgios Lekkas et al.","title":"VaTEST I: validation of sub-Saturn exoplanet TOI-181b in narrow orbit from its host star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.07957","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi181b-EspinozaRetamaletal2026","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Joshua N. Winn, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"POSEIDON I: The Dynamical Origins of Transiting Neptunes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18553","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1438 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HARPS-N and HIRES radial velocities gathered over five years measured the masses of two transiting sub-Neptunes in this system. Planet b has a radius of 3.04 Earth radii and a mass of 9.4 Earth masses, giving a density of 1.8 g per cubic centimetre, which suggests a volatile-rich interior (Persson et al. 2025). Interior models allow its high-metallicity envelope to hold up to 2.5% hydrogen and helium by mass (Persson et al. 2025). Planet b receives 145 times the sunlight that Earth does (Persson et al. 2025).","system":"The K0 star hosts two short-period sub-Neptunes, with periods of 5.1 and 9.4 days for planets b and c (Persson et al. 2025). A long-period Jupiter-like planet is also tentatively indicated (Persson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The long-period Jupiter-like planet is only a tentative detection (Persson et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1438b-Perssonetal2025","cite":"Persson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Carina M. Persson, Emil Knudstrup, Ilaria Carleo et al.","title":"TOI-1438: A rare system with two short-period sub-Neptunes and a tentative long-period Jupiter-like planet orbiting a K0V star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-5 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"At discovery, the planet was the densest known Jovian-mass planet. It is about 7 times denser than TrES-4 and about 3 times denser than WASP-4 b (Anderson et al. 2008). Its 1.6-day orbit gives a predicted equilibrium temperature of 1750 K (Anderson et al. 2008). Radial velocity data show no evidence for an eccentric orbit (Husnoo et al. 2012). Multi-colour photometry shows a mostly flat broad-band spectrum (Moyano et al. 2017).","system":"Transit timings have been checked for signs of an unseen second planet. Observations from New Zealand found timing offsets with an amplitude of about 50 seconds, at 3.7 sigma significance (Fukui et al. 2011). A later campaign found no clear departure from a linear trend (Moyano et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Whether the timing variations are real or come from observing errors is not settled. If real, only an additional body could explain them (Fukui et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp5b-Andersonetal2008","cite":"Anderson et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"D. R. Anderson, M. Gillon, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-5b: a dense, very hot Jupiter transiting a 12th-mag Southern-hemisphere star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.1685","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp5b-Husnooetal2012","cite":"Husnoo et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Nawal Husnoo, Frédéric Pont, Tsevi Mazeh et al.","title":"Observational constraints on tidal effects using orbital eccentricities","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.6379","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp5b-Moyanoetal2017","cite":"Moyano et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Moyano, L. A. Almeida, C. von Essen et al.","title":"Multi-band characterization of the hot Jupiters: WASP-5b, WASP-44b and WASP-46b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.05700","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp5b-Fukuietal2011","cite":"Fukui et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Akihiko Fukui, Norio Narita, Paul J. Tristram et al.","title":"Measurements of Transit Timing Variations for WASP-5b","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5769","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 160691 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the multi-planet system as a whole. All of its planets were found by the Doppler shift of the star's light, so only minimum masses are known and the orbital tilts are not (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019). A reanalysis of the velocity data found two Jupiter-like planets near a 2:1 orbital resonance, a hot Neptune on a roughly 9-day orbit, and a possible further Jupiter-like planet at about 307 days (Goździewski et al. 2007).","system":"The star, also called mu Arae or Cervantes, is a G3 subgiant 15.57 parsecs away. The system has been compared to the solar system, and one planet is reported to lie in the habitable zone (Diputra et al. 2026). The architecture recalls the solar system (Goździewski et al. 2007).","history":"","open":"Simulations over 1 million years explore orbital evolution for the range of orbital tilts and angles that observations leave open (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd160691c-PerminovKuznetsov2019","cite":"Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Alexander Perminov, Eduard Kuznetsov","title":"Dynamical evolution of extrasolar planetary systems HD 141399, HD 160691 and HD 39194","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd160691c-Diputraetal2026","cite":"Diputra et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Adi Syahwal Ganda Diputra, Farahhati Mumtahana, Gerhana Puannandra Putri et al.","title":"Peering Inside the Planet-Hosting Cervantes Star (\\mu Arae)","journal":"Bulgarian Astronomical Journal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-174 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery was made in part with Doppler tomography, which follows the planet's shadow as it crosses the star's spinning surface (Temple et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet crosses the edge of its star's disc in a near-grazing transit, so its radius was first uncertain, anywhere from 0.8 to 1.8 Jupiter radii (Temple et al. 2018). Its sky-projected spin-orbit angle is 31 degrees, a moderate misalignment (Temple et al. 2018). WASP-174 is the faintest hot-Jupiter system confirmed with tomography (Temple et al. 2018). Follow-up radial velocities and multi-colour photometry confirmed the grazing nature of the transits and described the planet as highly inflated (Mancini et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"The misalignment matches the known tendency for planets around hotter stars to have tilted orbits (Temple et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp174b-Templeetal2018","cite":"Temple et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"L. Y. Temple, C. Hellier, Y. Almleaky et al.","title":"Discovery of WASP-174b: Doppler tomography of a near-grazing transit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.00766","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp174b-Mancinietal2020","cite":"Mancini et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. Mancini, P. Sarkis, Th. Henning et al.","title":"The highly inflated giant planet WASP-174b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08674","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4364 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a search for young and maturing planets (Distler et al. 2025).","observed":"TESS transits and ground-based photometry revealed a young mini-Neptune orbiting a bright M dwarf in the tidal tails of the Hyades cluster. The star's age is well constrained at 710 million years (Distler et al. 2025). The planet's radius puts it near the upper edge of the radius valley, which suggests it keeps a modest hydrogen-helium envelope (Distler et al. 2025). Its transmission spectroscopy metric of 44.2 makes it a candidate for atmospheric study with JWST (Distler et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a bright M dwarf 44 parsecs away and a member of the Hyades stream (Distler et al. 2025).","history":"The planet sits at the transition between rocky super-Earths and gas-rich mini-Neptunes, at an early age (Distler et al. 2025).","open":"Its radius may still change as the atmosphere cools, contracts and is lost to photoevaporation (Distler et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4364b-Distleretal2025","cite":"Distler et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Adam Distler, Melinda Soares-Furtado, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). XII. A Young Mini-Neptune on the Upper Edge of the Radius Valley in the Hyades Cluster","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 130322 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Jupiter-mass body on a nearly circular orbit of 10.7 days, among the early planets found with the CORALIE spectrograph (Udry et al. 2000). A search for transits using 14 years of photometry found none. Transits of a full planet-sized dip to 0.017 magnitude were excluded, and the chance that the planet transits at all is only 4.7% (Hinkel et al. 2015). Models of the star's wind suggest the orbit is supermagnetosonic, so a bow shock may form around the planet (Vidotto et al. 2015).","system":"The planet is the only known companion of its star. The star's abundances of many elements match the Sun's (Sadakane et al. 2002).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd130322b-Udryetal2000","cite":"Udry et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. II. The short-period planetary companions to <ASTROBJ>HD 75289</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 130322</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd130322b-Hinkeletal2015","cite":"Hinkel et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Natalie R. Hinkel, Stephen R. Kane, Gregory W. Henry et al.","title":"Refined Properties of the HD 130322 Planetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03441","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd130322b-Vidottoetal2015","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, R. Fares, M. Jardine et al.","title":"On the environment surrounding close-in exoplanets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.05711","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd130322b-Sadakaneetal2002","cite":"Sadakane et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Kozo Sadakane, Michiko Ohkubo, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Abundance Analyses of 12 Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets Observed with the SUBARU/HDS","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/54.6.911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-880 c":{"text":{"why":"Measuring how this planet's orbit is tilted relative to its star's spin can tell apart a single tilted planet orbit from a whole misaligned protoplanetary disc (Zhang et al. 2025).","observed":"The Keck Planet Finder measured the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect for this Neptune-sized planet, which gives the angle between its orbit and the star's spin. The system is reported to be aligned and coplanar, meaning all three planets orbit in the same plane and along the star's equator (Zhang et al. 2025). Planet c has a radius of 4.95 Earth radii and a 6.4-day period (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"The K-type star hosts three transiting planets. Planet b has a radius of 2.19 Earth radii and a 2.6-day period. Planet d has a radius of 3.4 Earth radii and a 14.3-day period (Zhang et al. 2025).","history":"Because the multi-planet system is aligned, a tilt of a single planet is not needed to explain the geometry (Zhang et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi880c-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Elina Y. Zhang, Huan-Yu Teng, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-880 is an Aligned, Coplanar, Multiplanet System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16194","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-181 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Saturn of about 0.3 Jupiter masses and 1.2 Jupiter radii, close to the upper bound of the mass-radius range (Turner et al. 2019). HARPS data were added because the planet's low mass was hard to measure with the survey spectrograph alone (Turner et al. 2019). Its orbit lies in the transition zone where systems shift from well aligned with the star's spin to a wider spread of tilts (Turner et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp181b-Turneretal2019","cite":"Turner et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Oliver D. Turner, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"Three hot-Jupiters on the upper edge of the mass-radius distribution: WASP-177, WASP-181, and WASP-183","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06622","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 503 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from four instruments gave a mass of 6.26 Earth masses and a bulk density of 2.92 g per cubic centimetre. A Spitzer transit refined the orbit's timing, and the orbit is quite eccentric, at 0.41 (Polanski et al. 2021). Interior models fit either an Earth-like core with a large water fraction of about 45%, or a thin hydrogen-helium envelope of 0.5% (Polanski et al. 2021). The host is a metal-poor K dwarf about 11 billion years old (Polanski et al. 2021).","system":"The host star is bright, with J = 8.3, and quiet (Polanski et al. 2021).","history":"The planet's size places it just above the radius gap, and it may be in the process of having its atmosphere stripped by its star (Polanski 2020).","open":"The planet's low gas fraction and old host make it a good test of mass loss driven by X-ray and ultraviolet light (Polanski et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf503b-Polanskietal2021","cite":"Polanski et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alex S. Polanski, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Jennifer A. Burt et al.","title":"Wolf 503 b: Characterization of a Sub-Neptune Orbiting a Metal-poor K Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.08092","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wolf503b-Polanski2020","cite":"Polanski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. Polanski","title":"Wolf503b: Mass Determination of a 2 Earth Radii Planet Orbiting a k Dwarf Star","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #236","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-41 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler photometry detected the planet passing behind its star, with a depth of 64 parts per million, and the optical brightness variation over its orbit. The geometric albedo was found to be 0.30, higher than for most transiting hot Jupiters measured (Santerne et al. 2011). The day-side temperature is 1730 K if no heat is carried to the night side (Santerne et al. 2011). Later short-cadence Kepler data favoured a lower orbital inclination, making the planet larger and less dense than first found, and changing the albedo (Bonomo et al. 2015).","system":"The host is a G dwarf about 650 million years old. Its starspots cover about 0.5% of the visible hemisphere (Huang et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler41b-Santerneetal2011","cite":"Santerne et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. Santerne, A. S. Bonomo, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. IV. KOI-196b: a non-inflated hot Jupiter with a high albedo","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.0550","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler41b-Bonomoetal2015","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Improved parameters of seven Kepler giant companions characterized with SOPHIE and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler41b-Huangetal2014","cite":"Huang et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Li-Ching Huang, Wing-Huen Ip, Chi-Ju Wu","title":"Star Spot Activities of Solar-type Stars with Hot-Jupiters in Kepler Data","journal":"EGU General Assembly Conference Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2018 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck/HIRES velocities gave a mass of 9.2 Earth masses. Mass and radius fit an Earth-like core with about 1% hydrogen-helium by mass, or an ice-rock mixture (Dai et al. 2023). Helium absorption was detected in the planet's atmosphere with high-resolution spectroscopy, a sign of escaping gas (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Models of that escape have been fitted to the absorption (Sánchez-Sánchez et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a metal-poor K dwarf with [Fe/H] of -0.58, and it is bright, with a K magnitude of 7.1 (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"The planet's mass is close to the threshold for runaway accretion, where a core quickly grows into a giant. The threshold is predicted to be lower in a low-metallicity disc. The planet may be a core that failed to become a giant, which could explain why giant planets are rare around metal-poor stars (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2018b-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kevin C. Schlaufman, Henrique Reggiani et al.","title":"A Mini-Neptune Orbiting the Metal-poor K Dwarf BD+29 2654","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08179","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2018b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2018b-SnchezSnchezetal2025","cite":"Sánchez-Sánchez et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Sánchez-Sánchez, M. Lampón, J. Orell-Miquel et al.","title":"The Hydrodynamic Atmospheric Escape of 5 Gas Giant Exoplanets with He Absorption Signal","journal":"Highlights of Spanish Astrophysics XII","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-175 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an inflated hot Jupiter of about 0.99 Jupiter masses and 1.208 Jupiter radii on a 3.07-day orbit around an F7 star. Follow-up used CORALIE and HARPS velocities and photometry from EulerCam, TRAPPIST and SPECULOOS (Nielsen et al. 2019).","system":"The star possibly belongs to a binary, with another star 7.9 arcseconds away (Nielsen et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Whether the neighbouring star is truly bound to WASP-175 is not known (Nielsen et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp175b-Nielsenetal2019","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, F. Bouchy, O. D. Turner et al.","title":"WASP-169, WASP-171, WASP-175, and WASP-182: three hot Jupiters and one bloated sub-Saturn mass planet discovered by WASP-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1422 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HARPS-N monitored the star for 1.5 years and AstraLux high-resolution imaging ruled out blended companions. The planet is a puffy, warm Neptune-sized body of about 9 Earth masses and 4 Earth radii on a circular 12.997-day orbit (Naponiello et al. 2022; Naponiello et al. 2026).","system":"The G2 star has at least two further planets. A transiting sub-Neptune, TOI-1422 c, has a radius of 2.61 Earth radii and a 34.563-day period, confirmed from three TESS transits and new and archival velocities (Naponiello et al. 2026). An earlier velocity signal of about 29 days had suggested a Neptune-mass candidate (Naponiello et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"It was not clear at first whether the roughly 29-day velocity signal and a single transit-like event came from the same companion (Naponiello et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1422b-Naponielloetal2022","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Naponiello, L. Mancini, M. Damasso et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XL. A puffy and warm Neptune-sized planet and an outer Neptune-mass candidate orbiting the solar-type star TOI-1422","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.03293","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1422b-Naponielloetal2026","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"L. Naponiello, P. Leonardi, M. Damasso et al.","title":"A 34.6-d transiting sub-Neptune in the TOI-1422 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11492","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2025 b":{"text":{"why":"Bright host stars make planets like this one good targets for further study of how hot and warm Jupiters form and migrate (Knudstrup et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with two ground-based spectrographs, FIES and Tull, which tracked the star's wobble (Knudstrup et al. 2022). Its orbit is clearly stretched: the eccentricity is 0.394 (Rodriguez et al. 2023). Spin-orbit measurements show the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of about 9 degrees and an uncertainty of roughly 30 degrees either way (Knudstrup et al. 2022). A later measurement also found alignment (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"A slow drift in the star's motion points to an outer companion in the system (Knudstrup et al. 2022).","history":"An aligned but eccentric orbit is a clue to how the planet reached its place. Warm Jupiters like this one are thought to keep the spin direction they were born with (Wang et al. 2024).","open":"The outer companion has not been characterised (Knudstrup et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2025b-Knudstrupetal2022","cite":"Knudstrup et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emil Knudstrup, Luisa M. Serrano, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"Confirmation and characterisation of three giant planets detected by TESS from the FIES/NOT and Tull/McDonald spectrographs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.13956","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2025b-Rodriguezetal2023","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Another shipment of six short-period giant planets from TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2025b-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice, Songhu Wang et al.","title":"Single-star Warm-Jupiter Systems Tend to Be Aligned, Even around Hot Stellar Hosts: No T <SUB>eff</SUB>-λ Dependency","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2031 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter whose orbit was checked against the spin of its star using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a small distortion in starlight as the planet crosses the star (Wang et al. 2024). The orbit was found to be aligned with the star's spin. The measurement was one of six new ones, with a median uncertainty of 13 degrees (Wang et al. 2024). The host is a hot star, yet the alignment held, unlike many hot Jupiters around hot stars (Wang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Warm Jupiters are thought to keep the spin direction they started with, so an aligned orbit argues against violent scattering in this case (Wang et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2031ab-Wangetal2024","cite":"Wang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice, Songhu Wang et al.","title":"Single-star Warm-Jupiter Systems Tend to Be Aligned, Even around Hot Stellar Hosts: No T <SUB>eff</SUB>-λ Dependency","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10038","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-220 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's mass and radius are known precisely, which allows a detailed look at what it is made of (Hoyer et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet is a warm sub-Neptune with a measured mass of 13.8 Earth masses and radius of 3.03 Earth radii, giving a density of about 2.7 grams per cubic centimetre (Hoyer et al. 2021). That is low for its size. Models of its interior suggest the low density could come from a steam atmosphere and a layer of supercritical water (water hotter and denser than ordinary liquid or gas) on top of a rocky mantle and a small iron core (Hoyer et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a bright, old K dwarf star about 10 billion years old (Hoyer et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The interior model is one explanation of the low density; the strong irradiation is the reason a steam atmosphere is plausible (Hoyer et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi220b-Hoyeretal2021","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"S. Hoyer, D. Gandolfi, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-220 b: a warm sub-Neptune discovered by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.01944","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 89744 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It swings from about 0.88 au on average, and its closest approach to the star was caught in autumn 1999 with a second telescope (Korzennik et al. 2000). The minimum mass is 7.2 Jupiter masses (Korzennik et al. 2000). Later work found hints of another long-period companion in the system (Wittenmyer et al. 2019).","system":"A faint companion, a very low-mass star or heavy brown dwarf, lies far out at a separation of about 63 arcseconds and moves through space with the star (Mugrauer et al. 2004). The star is metal-rich (Korzennik et al. 2000).","history":"The high eccentricity continues a trend that planets farther than about 0.15 au have much more stretched orbits than the Solar System's (Korzennik et al. 2000).","open":"A single stretched orbit can sometimes be two planets on near-circular orbits that were poorly sampled, and the extra signal here is not settled (Wittenmyer et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd89744b-Korzenniketal2000","cite":"Korzennik et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Sylvain G. Korzennik, Timothy M. Brown, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"A High-Eccentricity Low-Mass Companion to HD 89744","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0003045","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd89744b-Wittenmyeretal2019","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jake T. Clark, Jinglin Zhao et al.","title":"Truly eccentric - I. Revisiting eight single-eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08471","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd89744b-Mugraueretal2004","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser, T. Mazeh et al.","title":"Astrometric confirmation of a wide low-mass companion to the planet host star HD 89744","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0404320","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 685 b":{"text":{"why":"The star was monitored as part of a survey that aims to find small planets around M dwarfs with a few Earth masses (Pinamonti et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet was found in 106 spectra of its star taken over five years (Pinamonti et al. 2019). The host is an active M0 dwarf, so its spots and rotation can mimic planet signals. Brightness measurements were combined with the spectra, and a statistical model of the star's activity was used to separate stellar signals from real ones (Pinamonti et al. 2019). Three periodic signals appear in the velocities, at about 9, 18 and 24 days, and the planet is described as a warm super-Earth (Pinamonti et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj685b-Pinamontietal2019","cite":"Pinamonti et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Pinamonti, A. Sozzetti, P. Giacobbe et al.","title":"The HADES RV programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XI. GJ 685 b: a warm super-Earth around an active M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.11853","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-663 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet comes from a study of the whole three-planet system. The planet is a mini-Neptune of 2.26 Earth radii with a mass of 3.65 Earth masses, orbiting just above the radius valley, the gap that separates rocky planets from volatile-rich ones (Cointepas et al. 2024). The low mass for such a size means volatiles such as water must make up a large share (Cointepas et al. 2024).","system":"Three transiting mini-Neptunes circle an early M dwarf, and a dynamical analysis shows the system is stable (Cointepas et al. 2024). The sister planets b and d have radii of 2.27 and 1.92 Earth radii (Cointepas et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi663c-Cointepasetal2024","cite":"Cointepas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cointepas, F. Bouchy, J. M. Almenara et al.","title":"TOI-663: A newly discovered multi-planet system with three transiting mini-Neptunes orbiting an early M star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202346899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-2294L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was spotted by eye in the data, not by the search algorithm, so it helps compare the two methods of picking out planets (Shin et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is about 1.25 Neptune masses and orbits a very-low-mass M dwarf or a brown dwarf (Shin et al. 2023). It is the one planet of its survey sample that a by-eye search caught and the AnomalyFinder algorithm did not (Shin et al. 2023).","system":"The host star is very faint and low in mass, and its exact nature is not settled (Shin et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Planets around very-low-mass hosts found by microlensing cover the full range of masses, while close-in planets (within about 0.1 au) strongly favour small ones (Shin et al. 2023). The reason for the difference is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg2294lb-Shinetal2023","cite":"Shin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"In-Gu Shin, Jennifer C. Yee, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets. III. Analysis of Three Giant Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.03886","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4641 b":{"text":{"why":"Spin-orbit measurements for warm Jupiters are still few, so this one may shed light on how such planets form (Bieryla et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter of 0.73 Jupiter radii on a circular orbit around a bright (magnitude 7.5), fast-spinning F star (Bieryla et al. 2024). Two transits were watched with a spectrograph, showing the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of about 1.4 degrees (Bieryla et al. 2024). Its mass is only limited to under 3.87 Jupiter masses (Bieryla et al. 2024).","system":"The star has a radius of 1.73 Suns and a mass of 1.41 Suns (Bieryla et al. 2024).","history":"Hot Jupiters around hot stars are more often misaligned, but this warm Jupiter is not (Bieryla et al. 2024).","open":"Its mass is not yet pinned down beyond an upper limit (Bieryla et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4641b-Bierylaetal2024","cite":"Bieryla et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Allyson Bieryla, George Zhou, Juliana García-Mejía et al.","title":"TOI-4641b: an aligned warm Jupiter orbiting a bright (V=7.5) rapidly rotating F-star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03971","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1411 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a set of hot, possibly terrestrial planets picked as good targets for emission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed as real, or validated, along with 12 others using ground-based follow-up and two vetting tools (Giacalone et al. 2022). The goal of the set was to find hot, small planets whose thermal glow and surface could be studied with JWST (Giacalone et al. 2022). The set covers radii from about 0.6 to 2.0 Earth radii (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has any atmosphere or what its surface is like has not been measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1411b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 463 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ten years of precise velocity measurements from the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and Keck show a planet with a 10-year orbit and a minimum mass of 1.55 Jupiter masses (Endl et al. 2022). That makes it a Jupiter analog, a planet with an orbit like Jupiter's, around a low-mass star. The signal is not caused by a magnetic cycle of the star, and TESS found no transits (Endl et al. 2022). A pull on the star seen in Gaia and Hipparcos positions fits a distance of 3.53 au (Endl et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Giant planets in orbits shorter than 1000 days are less common around late-type stars than around Sun-like ones, in line with core and pebble accretion models (Morales et al. 2025).","open":"The planet is a candidate for direct imaging with the Roman Space Telescope's coronagraph (Endl et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj463b-Endletal2022","cite":"Endl et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Michael Endl, Paul Robertson, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"A Jupiter Analog Orbiting The Nearby M Dwarf GJ 463","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ac8e05","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj463b-Moralesetal2025","cite":"Morales et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. C. Morales, I. Ribas, S. Reffert et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs: Revisiting the GJ 317, GJ463, and GJ 3512 systems and two newly discovered planets orbiting GJ9773 and GJ 508.2","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15516","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-23 A b":{"text":{"why":"The star is close to the TESS and JWST continuous viewing zones, so it can be watched for long stretches (Johns et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a radius of 1.322 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.938 Jupiter masses, giving a low density (Johns et al. 2019). The strong starlight it receives has likely puffed it up (Johns et al. 2019). A TESS search for further planets found no transiting companions down to terrestrial size (Maciejewski 2020). Transit timing showed no sign of any pull from other planets (Maciejewski 2020).","system":"The host star is a near twin of the Sun and probably has a fainter companion star in a long-period orbit (Johns et al. 2019).","history":"Lone hot Jupiters like this one favour high-eccentricity migration as the way they reached tight orbits (Maciejewski 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt23ab-Johnsetal2019","cite":"Johns et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Daniel Johns, Phillip A. Reed, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"KELT-23Ab: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a Near-solar Twin Close to the TESS and JWST Continuous Viewing Zones","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.00031","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt23ab-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-17 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was announced as the least dense known planet, at 6% to 14% of Jupiter's density (Anderson et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet has 1.6 Saturn masses but 1.5 to 2 Jupiter radii, and the atmosphere has the largest scale height, the thickness of the gas layer, of any known planet (Anderson et al. 2010). Hubble spectra of its atmosphere were taken with STIS and WFC3, and Spitzer measured it as well (Ballester et al. 2014). A retrieval analysis fit its spectrum best with Rayleigh scattering aerosols, tiny particles that scatter blue light (Barstow et al. 2017).","system":"The star is a metal-poor F6 star (Anderson et al. 2010).","history":"An early spin-orbit measurement suggested a retrograde orbit, at about -150 degrees, pointing to a violent past with scattering (Anderson et al. 2010). Tidal heating from a stretched orbit being rounded off could explain the swollen radius (Anderson et al. 2010).","open":"The current eccentricity is not well known and would help test tidal-heating models (Anderson et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp17b-Andersonetal2010","cite":"Anderson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. R. Anderson, C. Hellier, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-17b: An Ultra-Low Density Planet in a Probable Retrograde Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1553","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp17b-Ballesteretal2014","cite":"Ballester et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"G. E. Ballester, N. Nikolov, H. Wakeford et al.","title":"Wasp-17b versus other hot-Jupiters being surveyed with HST: A clear versus dusty/hazy atmospheres","journal":"Search for Life Beyond the Solar System. Exoplanets, Biosignatures & Instruments","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp17b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":37,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3473 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the hottest known transiting Earth-sized planets, which makes it a good target for thermal emission spectroscopy (Kemmer et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet is Earth-sized, at 1.264 Earth radii, with a mass of 1.86 Earth masses measured from the star's wobble (Kemmer et al. 2020). Its mass was measured with three spectrographs and its transits followed up with ground-based photometry (Kemmer et al. 2020). It is presumably rocky (Kemmer et al. 2020).","system":"A second, temperate planet that does not transit orbits every 15.5 days with a minimum mass of 7.41 Earth masses (Kemmer et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3473b-Kemmeretal2020","cite":"Kemmer et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Kemmer, S. Stock, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"Discovery of a hot, transiting, Earth-sized planet and a second temperate, non-transiting planet around the M4 dwarf GJ 3473 (TOI-488)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10432","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 8799 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet comes from studies of the whole system. Keck images taken in 2005 detected planets b, c and d (Currie et al. 2012). Its orbit is likely tilted at least 25 degrees from face-on (Currie et al. 2012). Few orbits make it coplanar with b and c (Currie et al. 2012).","system":"The star has several planets, and orbits for b, c and d are consistent with a 4:2:1 resonance, with eccentricities below 0.18 to 0.3 (Currie et al. 2012). The star is a metal-weak lambda Bootis star with a dust disc, shown by excess light at 60 microns (Gray & Kaye 1999).","history":"The wide orbits fit formation by gravitational instability, where a disc breaks into clumps, which needs a disc of about 0.1 solar masses (Nero & Bjorkman 2009).","open":"Whether the planets lie in one plane is not settled (Currie et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr8799d-Currieetal2012","cite":"Currie et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thayne Currie, Misato Fukagawa, Christian Thalmann et al.","title":"Direct Detection and Orbital Analysis of the Exoplanets HR 8799 bcd from Archival 2005 Keck/NIRC2 Data","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.0483","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799d-GrayKaye1999","cite":"Gray & Kaye 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Richard O. Gray, Anthony B. Kaye","title":"HR 8799: A Link between γ Doradus Variables and λ Bootis Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/301134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799d-NeroBjorkman2009","cite":"Nero & Bjorkman 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Nero, J. E. Bjorkman","title":"Did Fomalhaut, HR 8799, and HL Tauri Form Planets Via the Gravitational Instability? Placing Limits on the Required Disk Masses","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1108","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 47536 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the star, a K-type giant with lower metallicity than the Sun (Sadakane et al. 2005). Its planet was confirmed by a reanalysis of FEROS spectra, though with orbital parameters different from earlier ones (Soto et al. 2015). A suspected second planet in the system was not confirmed (Soto et al. 2015). Hubble astrometry was used to try to measure the true mass of HD 47536's companion, beyond the minimum mass (Benedict et al. 2008).","system":"The star is a giant.","history":"Planets around giant stars seem less common around metal-rich stars than for dwarfs (Sadakane et al. 2005). Very metal-poor stars can form giant planets (Soto et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd47536b-Sadakaneetal2005","cite":"Sadakane et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Kozo Sadakane, Takashi Ohnishi, Michiko Ohkubo et al.","title":"Metallicities in Four Planet-Harbouring K-Type Giants: HD 47536, HD 59686, HD 137759, and HD 219449","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/57.1.127","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd47536b-Sotoetal2015","cite":"Soto et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. G. Soto, J. S. Jenkins, M. I. Jones","title":"RAFT - I. Discovery of new planetary candidates and updated orbits from archival FEROS spectra","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.04796","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd47536b-Benedictetal2008","cite":"Benedict et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, B. E. McArthur, E. Martioli et al.","title":"Exoplanet Masses from HST/FGS Astrometry - Progress Report","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #40","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 103720 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a 4.5557-day period and a minimum mass of 0.62 Jupiter masses (Moutou et al. 2015). It was found in HARPS velocities taken over ten years (Moutou et al. 2015). The star is active, and stellar activity limits how well planets can be detected in such stars at 2 metres per second precision (Moutou et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd103720b-Moutouetal2015","cite":"Moutou et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"C. Moutou, G. Lo Curto, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXVII. Five new long-period giant planets and a system update","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6591","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 b":{"text":{"why":"Systems locked in resonance let astronomers study how planets formed and migrated (Zak et al. 2024).","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet concerns the system. HD 110067 has six sub-Neptunes in a resonant chain, a pattern in which orbital periods are linked (Zak et al. 2024). An ESPRESSO spectrograph measurement during the transit of planet c shows its orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a projected angle of 6 degrees (Zak et al. 2024). The star is a K0 star and has a distant companion pair (Apps & Luque 2023).","system":"The host star is a wide hierarchical triple: a companion binary, HD 110106, lies 13,400 au away on the sky (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"The aligned orbit indicates that the system reached its architecture through convergent migration without major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067b-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067b-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-12 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of two hot Jupiters from a survey selected for follow-up observation by the K2 mission (Rabus et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet has 2.38 Jupiter masses and 1.35 Jupiter radii and moves on a circular orbit with a period of 3.1428 days (Rabus et al. 2016). Its host is among the most evolved stars known to host a hot Jupiter (Rabus et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved F star with a subsolar metal content (Rabus et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats12b-Rabusetal2016","cite":"Rabus et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"M. Rabus, A. Jordán, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-11b AND HATS-12b: Two Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting Subsolar Metallicity Stars Selected for the K2 Campaign 7","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02894","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spectra taken with Keck-HIRES during a transit showed that the planet's orbit is strongly tilted relative to the spin of its star, with a projected angle of about 76 degrees (Zhou et al. 2015). The star spins slowly, so the detection depends on an assumed rotation speed, and the uncertainty may be underestimated (Zhou et al. 2015). Another hot Jupiter announced alongside it, HATS-13b, is inflated, while HATS-14b is only slightly larger than Jupiter (Mancini et al. 2015).","system":"The host star is rich in heavy elements (Mancini et al. 2015).","history":"Tides were expected to align the orbits of hot Jupiters around stars with deep outer convection zones with the star's spin, yet HATS-14b is a clear outlier (Zhou et al. 2015).","open":"The tilt challenges the idea that close-in planets reshape the spin axes of stars like this one (Zhou et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats14b-Zhouetal2015","cite":"Zhou et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"G. Zhou, D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"A High Obliquity Orbit for the Hot-Jupiter HATS-14b Transiting a 5400K Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.08575","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats14b-Mancinietal2015","cite":"Mancini et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"L. Mancini, J. D. Hartman, K. Penev et al.","title":"HATS-13b and HATS-14b: two transiting hot Jupiters from the HATSouth survey","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.03469","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 80606 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet follows one of the most stretched-out orbits known, with an eccentricity of 0.927 when it was announced, the highest of any planet then (Naef et al. 2001). It swings from far from its star to very close in a 111-day orbit. The 12-hour transit and the eclipse were both recorded in January 2010 by the MOST space telescope (Roberts et al. 2010). Its distance from the star varies so much that its atmosphere is likely to behave unlike any other known (Roberts et al. 2010).","system":"The star is the brighter member of a wide binary, with a near-identical partner about 2000 AU away (Naef et al. 2001). The partner star has no detected planets (Mack et al. 2016).","history":"Several processes could have produced the extreme eccentricity (Naef et al. 2001). Chemical studies of the two stars found matching patterns, so the planet has not left a clear mark of rocky material swallowed by its star; up to 2.5 Earth masses of rock could still be hidden (Mack et al. 2016; Saffe et al. 2015).","open":"How the orbit became so elongated is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd80606b-Naefetal2001","cite":"Naef et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"D. Naef, D. W. Latham, M. Mayor et al.","title":"HD 80606 b, a planet on an extremely elongated orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0106256","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd80606b-Robertsetal2010","cite":"Roberts et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Jessica Roberts, J. W. Barnes, J. F. Rowe et al.","title":"HD80606b Transit Observed by MOST","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #42","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd80606b-Macketal2016","cite":"Mack et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Claude E., III Mack, Keivan G. Stassun, Simon C. Schuler et al.","title":"Detailed Abundances of Planet-hosting Wide Binaries. II. HD80606+HD80607","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.00018","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd80606b-Saffeetal2015","cite":"Saffe et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"C. Saffe, M. Flores, A. Buccino","title":"HD 80606: searching for the chemical signature of planet formation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08125","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":33,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 95127 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an evolved red giant about 20 times the Sun's radius, found in the Penn State-Torun survey with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Niedzielski et al. 2015). The planet orbits at 1.28 AU with a period of 482 days, and its minimum mass is about 5 times Jupiter's (Niedzielski et al. 2015). Its orbit is close to the zone where a swelling star engulfs its planets, so it may be swallowed in the near future (Niedzielski et al. 2015).","system":"Two other giant stars studied in the same work also host planets, one of them in an even tighter orbit that faces the same risk (Niedzielski et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"The true mass is not known, because only a minimum mass has been measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd95127b-Niedzielskietal2015","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan, G. Nowak et al.","title":"Three Red Giants With Substellar-Mass Companions","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07076","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-880.02":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer neighbour TOI-880 c. Observations with the Keck Planet Finder measured the angle between the star's spin and the orbit of planet c, and found the three transiting planets to be aligned with the star and lying in nearly the same plane (Zhang et al. 2025). Planet b is the innermost, at 2.19 Earth radii and a 2.6-day orbit, with planet c at 4.95 Earth radii on a 6.4-day orbit and planet d at about 3.4 Earth radii on a 14.3-day orbit (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"The star is a K-type dwarf with three transiting planets of different sizes (Zhang et al. 2025).","history":"A system where all planets are aligned suggests the whole protoplanetary disk was not tilted, rather than a single planet being pushed off course (Zhang et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi88002-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Elina Y. Zhang, Huan-Yu Teng, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-880 is an Aligned, Coplanar, Multiplanet System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16194","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"PDS 70 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet sits inside a gap in the disk of gas and dust around a young star, and is still growing. Hydrogen-alpha imaging with MUSE first suggested it, and SPHERE observations confirmed it; ALMA then resolved a disk of material around the planet itself (Chauvin et al. 2020). The planet's millimetre flux implies 0.007 to 0.031 Earth masses of dust at about 26 K in that disk, if the dust is thin (Mosqueira 2026). That dust is spread over an area equivalent to a radius of roughly 0.5 AU (Mosqueira 2026).","system":"The star is about 5 million years old, and PDS 70 is the only known system with protoplanets sitting in the cavity of the disk they formed from (Hsu et al. 2024). The inner planet, PDS 70 b, is a giant at 22 AU (Chauvin et al. 2020). Dust shows a gap from 15 to 60 AU (Long et al. 2018).","history":"Both planets feed from one shared gap, much like Jupiter and Saturn in the Solar System, and their final masses are linked (Mosqueira 2026). Moons could form in the disk around the planet (Mosqueira 2026).","open":"Whether the planet's disk will form satellites is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pds70c-Chauvinetal2020","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Gael Chauvin, Miriam Keppler, André Muller et al.","title":"PDS70, witnessing a young solar system analog in formation with SPHERE/ALMA","journal":"Planets 2020, Ground and Space Observatories: a Joint Venture to Planetary Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4435565","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70c-Mosqueira2026","cite":"Mosqueira 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ignacio Mosqueira","title":"PDS 70 c and SR 12 c: Observational Constraints on Giant-Planet and Satellite Formation","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70c-Hsuetal2024","cite":"Hsu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Chih-Chun Hsu, Jason J. Wang, Geoffrey A. Blake et al.","title":"PDS 70b Shows Stellar-like Carbon-to-oxygen Ratio","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15117","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70c-Longetal2018","cite":"Long et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Zachary C. Long, Eiji Akiyama, Michael Sitko et al.","title":"Differences in the Gas and Dust Distribution in the Transitional Disk of a Sun-like Young Star, PDS 70","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00529","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-11 holds six transiting planets, all orbiting closer to the star than Venus is to the Sun, which made it unique among 160,000 stars watched by Kepler at the time (Lissauer & Kepler Science Team 2011). A new spectrum showed the star is a young solar twin, which raised the densities of all six planets by 20% to 95% (Bedell et al. 2017). Modelling suggests planet b holds less than 10% of its mass as hydrogen and helium (Valencia et al. 2013).","system":"All six planets orbit within 0.5 AU, with the inner five on nearly circular, coplanar orbits within 0.25 AU (Quintana et al. 2011).","history":"The packed system is a test case for formation theories, including building planets close to the star or moving them inward (Quintana et al. 2011). Models also follow how hydrogen was lost over time (Lopez et al. 2011).","open":"Whether such planets are scaled-up rocky worlds with thick hydrogen skins or small Neptunes rich in water is not settled (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11b-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11b-Valenciaetal2013","cite":"Valencia et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Diana Valencia, Tristan Guillot, Vivien Parmentier et al.","title":"Bulk Composition of GJ 1214b and Other Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2629","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11b-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11b-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-603 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spectra from the CAFE2 instrument at the Calar Alto Observatory were used to confirm planets around TESS candidates that other follow-up programmes had left aside (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). Measuring the star's wobble gave the planet a mass of about 33 Earth masses on a 16.2-day orbit (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). Hot Jupiter candidates were the focus of this effort because most follow-up resources target low-mass candidates (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi603b-LilloBoxetal2026","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, C. Cifuentes, O. Balsalobre-Ruza et al.","title":"CAFE follow-up of TESS hot Jupiter candidates left behind: I. Five newly confirmed planets and a false positive","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19756","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 69830 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star has a resolved debris disc of leftover rocky and icy debris. If the planet's orbit lies in the same plane as that disc, the planet would be much more massive than its minimum mass, enough to count as a warm or hot Jupiter (Bisht & Jones 2024). Such a planet might be a nearby example of a world that moved inward through recent disc migration (Bisht & Jones 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass is not known; the larger value depends on assuming the planet and the debris disc share one plane (Bisht & Jones 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd69830c-BishtJones2024","cite":"Bisht & Jones 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Deepak Bisht, Hugh R. A. Jones","title":"Radial velocity analysis of stars with debris discs","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 581 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbours, especially GJ 581 d and e. A four-planet model with nearly circular orbits fits the HARPS velocities well and stays stable, while models with a more eccentric planet e become unstable (Vogt et al. 2012). Extra signals hint at more planets (Vogt et al. 2012). Computer models show that a planet like GJ 581 d, thought potentially habitable, could have an atmosphere detectable through water and carbon dioxide bands (von Paris et al. 2011).","system":"The star is a cool M dwarf. Its ultraviolet output, which can change planetary atmospheres and drive gas away, has been modelled (Tilipman et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Whether the habitable-zone planet exists is debated (Vogt et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj581b-Vogtetal2012","cite":"Vogt et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S. S. Vogt, R. P. Butler, N. Haghighipour","title":"GJ 581 update: Additional evidence for a Super-Earth in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.4515","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581b-vonParisetal2011","cite":"von Paris et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"P. von Paris, J. Cabrera, M. Godolt et al.","title":"Spectroscopic characterization of the atmospheres of potentially habitable planets: GL 581 d as a model case study","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3670","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581b-Tilipmanetal2020","cite":"Tilipman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. Tilipman, M. Vieytes, J. L. Linsky","title":"Semi-Empirical Modeling of the M Dwarf Exoplanet Hosts GJ 832, GJ 581, and GJ 876: UV Radiation and Implications for Exoplanet Atmospheres","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-10 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the find, KELT-South, watches bright stars for transits, and this was its first planet (Kuhn et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet is a highly inflated world with a deep transit of 1.4%, one of the deepest in the southern sky for stars of that brightness, so it is well suited to atmosphere studies (Rodriguez 2015). Observations with HARPS found no sodium or hydrogen-alpha absorption in its atmosphere (Steiner et al. 2023). A single transit with the UVES spectrograph had reported sodium, so the two results disagree (Steiner et al. 2023). The planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Steiner et al. 2023).","system":"The star is a bright early G-type star (Kuhn et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere holds sodium is not settled (Steiner et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt10b-Kuhnetal2016","cite":"Kuhn et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Rudolf B. Kuhn, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"KELT-10b: the first transiting exoplanet from the KELT-South survey - a hot sub-Jupiter transiting a V = 10.7 early G-star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.02323","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt10b-Rodriguez2015","cite":"Rodriguez 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez","title":"KELT-10b and KELT-11b: Two Sub-Jupiter Mass Planets well-Suited for Atmospheric Characterization in the Southern Hemisphere","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt10b-Steineretal2023","cite":"Steiner et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michal Steiner","title":"KELT-10b through high-resolution spectroscopy","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 209458 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This is one of the best-studied hot Jupiters, because its transit makes it possible to measure size and atmosphere in detail (Cody & Sasselov 2002). A reanalysis of its spectrum reported the first detection of nitrogen chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere, patchy clouds and less water than expected (MacDonald & Madhusudhan 2017). Helium absorption shows that hydrogen escapes from the upper atmosphere and is almost fully ionised above 2.9 planet radii (Lampon et al. 2020). Titanium and vanadium oxides may cause the temperature to rise with altitude in the upper atmosphere (Desert et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the weak water signal comes from clouds or from truly low water is debated (MacDonald & Madhusudhan 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd209458b-CodySasselov2002","cite":"Cody & Sasselov 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ann Marie Cody, Dimitar D. Sasselov","title":"HD 209458: Physical Parameters of the Parent Star and the Transiting Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0111494","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd209458b-MacDonaldMadhusudhan2017","cite":"MacDonald & Madhusudhan 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ryan J. MacDonald, Nikku Madhusudhan","title":"HD 209458b in new light: evidence of nitrogen chemistry, patchy clouds and sub-solar water","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.01113","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd209458b-Lamponetal2020","cite":"Lampon et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Lampón, M. López-Puertas, L. M. Lara et al.","title":"Modelling the He I triplet absorption at 10 830 Å in the atmosphere of HD 209458 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04872","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd209458b-Desertetal2008","cite":"Desert et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"J.-M. Désert, A. Vidal-Madjar, A. Lecavelier Des Etangs et al.","title":"TiO and VO broad band absorption features in the optical spectrum of the atmosphere of the hot-Jupiter <ASTROBJ>HD 209458b</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.1865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":33,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4468 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with an outer companion and no inner companion, an arrangement not seen before among the roughly ten hot Jupiters with small nearby planets (Livesey & Becker 2026). The star is a K dwarf, and the outer planet is a sub-Neptune on a 7.01-day orbit with a radius of 3.22 Earth radii (Hord et al. 2024). Both planets are statistically validated with ground-based photometry (Hord et al. 2024).","system":"Hot Jupiters usually have no close companions, and companions that do exist tend to lie inside the hot Jupiter's orbit (Hord et al. 2024).","history":"Companions are thought to be kicked out of the transit plane by an early resonance as the star's shape changes, but the outer planet never met that resonance and kept its low tilt (Livesey & Becker 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4468b-LiveseyBecker2026","cite":"Livesey & Becker 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Joseph Livesey, Juliette Becker","title":"The Dearth of Nearby Outer Companions to Hot Jupiters","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4468b-Hordetal2024","cite":"Hord et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Benjamin Hord, Joseph Livesey, Juliette Becker","title":"TOI-4468 and the Emerging Trends in Systems Hosting Hot Jupiters with Nearby Companion Planets","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 100546 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"High-contrast infrared imaging found a source inside the disk around this young Herbig star at about 50 AU; it appears at 3.8 and 4.8 micrometres but not at 2.1 micrometres (Quanz et al. 2015). The emission is a point source surrounded by resolved emission, and the source moves with the star (Quanz et al. 2015). A blackbody fit gives about 932 K and an emitting area of about 6.9 Jupiter radii (Quanz et al. 2015).","system":"The disk reaches about 350 AU, is inclined by about 51 degrees, and has an inner hole about 14 AU in radius (Augereau et al. 2001; Avenhaus et al. 2014). A spiral arm lies in its northeast (Avenhaus et al. 2014). ALMA found 19 molecules in rings out to about 200 AU, and the gas is oxygen-rich (Booth et al. 2024).","history":"The object is seen as direct evidence of a gas giant forming at 50 AU (Quanz et al. 2015).","open":"Part of the light comes from extended material around the point source, so how much of it is the planet itself is not clear (Quanz et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd100546b-Quanzetal2015","cite":"Quanz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Sascha P. Quanz, Adam Amara, Michael R. Meyer et al.","title":"Confirmation and Characterization of the Protoplanet HD 100546 b—Direct Evidence for Gas Giant Planet Formation at 50 AU","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.5173","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd100546b-Augereauetal2001","cite":"Augereau et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"J. C. Augereau, A. M. Lagrange, D. Mouillet et al.","title":"HST/NICMOS2 coronagraphic observations of the circumstellar environment of three old PMS stars: HD 100546, SAO 206462 and MWC 480","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0009496","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd100546b-Avenhausetal2014","cite":"Avenhaus et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Henning Avenhaus, Sascha P. Quanz, Michael R. Meyer et al.","title":"HD100546 Multi-epoch Scattered Light Observations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.6120","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd100546b-Boothetal2024","cite":"Booth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alice S. Booth, Margot Leemker, Ewine F. van Dishoeck et al.","title":"An ALMA Molecular Inventory of Warm Herbig Ae Disks. I. Molecular Rings, Asymmetries, and Complexity in the HD 100546 Disk","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04001","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":65,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"61 Vir b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star and its outer planets. Secular theory, which follows slow changes in orbits, matches full computer simulations of the system even though the eccentricities are quite large (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010). The long axes of the two outer planets' orbits stay roughly aligned (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010). The star itself has been studied for chemical makeup at infrared wavelengths (Senturk et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The alignment of the outer orbits is the most likely behaviour given the planets' masses and spacing, so it does not need tidal evolution to explain it (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-61virb-VanLaerhovenGreenberg2010","cite":"Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Christa Van Laerhoven, R. Greenberg","title":"Secular Behavior In Multi-planet Systems: 61 Virginis, 55 Cancri, Etc.","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #42","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-61virb-Senturketal2026","cite":"Senturk et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. A. Senturk, T. Sahin, C. Kayhan","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 115617 infrared portrait (Senturk+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2818 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's transits have started to arrive earlier than expected, about 8 minutes in the latest year of TESS data, so its period seems to be getting shorter (McKee et al. 2025). The implied decay rate is 1.35 seconds per year, too fast for tidal effects, even for an evolved star (McKee et al. 2025). Velocity and position measurements make a distant companion unlikely (McKee et al. 2025).","system":"The most likely explanation is a hidden planet below about 10 Earth masses, near a resonance with the hot Jupiter (McKee et al. 2025). The host is a slightly evolved G-type star (McKee et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Further Doppler observations could confirm or rule out the companion (McKee et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2818b-McKeeetal2025","cite":"McKee et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Brendan J. McKee, Benjamin T. Montet, Samuel W. Yee et al.","title":"A Planet Candidate Orbiting near the Hot Jupiter TOI-2818 b Inferred through Transit Timing","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04192","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 168-9 b":{"text":{"why":"It was a top target for the mission goal of measuring the masses of 50 small planets (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2020).","observed":"The host is a bright M dwarf, which makes the planet one of the most accessible rocky planets for atmosphere studies (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2020). JWST took the first 3 to 12 micrometre transmission spectrum of the planet, which rules out atmospheres with less than 100 times solar metallicity if clouds are absent and surface pressure is high (Alam et al. 2025). Modelling shows the planet could not have kept a primordial hydrogen-helium atmosphere beyond its first 200 million years (Alam et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Heating from the star probably stripped away any primordial hydrogen-rich atmosphere early (Alam et al. 2025).","open":"Whether it has any atmosphere is not known. Eclipse observations at 15 micrometres could reveal a carbon dioxide atmosphere (Alam et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l1689b-AstudilloDefruetal2020","cite":"Astudillo-Defru et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. Astudillo-Defru, R. Cloutier, S. X. Wang et al.","title":"A hot terrestrial planet orbiting the bright M dwarf L 168-9 unveiled by TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.09175","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l1689b-Alametal2025","cite":"Alam et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Munazza K. Alam, Peter Gao, Jea Adams Redai et al.","title":"JWST COMPASS: The First Near- to Mid-infrared Transmission Spectrum of the Hot Super-Earth L 168-9 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.03154","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 108147 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery report introduced a weighted cross-correlation technique that reduces photon noise (Pepe et al. 2002).","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 1.34 times Saturn's and a high eccentricity of 0.50, which may point to a second companion in the system (Pepe et al. 2002). It was among the lightest planets found at the time, and was spotted using the CORALIE spectrograph, which had reached a velocity precision better than 3 m/s (Pepe et al. 2002).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether a second companion exists is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd108147b-Pepeetal2002","cite":"Pepe et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"F. Pepe, M. Mayor, F. Galland et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets VII. Two short-period Saturnian companions to <ASTROBJ>HD 108147</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 168746</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202457","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-135 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a highly inflated hot Jupiter on a 1.4-day orbit around a Sun-like G5 star (Spake et al. 2016). Intense starlight may be the cause of its puffed-up radius (Spake et al. 2016). Weak evidence suggests the planet is passing angular momentum to its star (Spake et al. 2016). Eight more transits observed from Calar Alto and TUBITAK give a radius of 1.075 Jupiter radii, smaller than the first estimate of 1.30 (Ozturk & Erdem 2021; Spake et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp135b-Spakeetal2016","cite":"Spake et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. J. Spake, D. J. A. Brown, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"WASP-135b: A Highly Irradiated, Inflated Hot Jupiter Orbiting a G5V Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05954","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp135b-OzturkErdem2021","cite":"Ozturk & Erdem 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Oğuz Öztürk, Ahmet Erdem","title":"New photometric analysis of hot Jupiter: WASP-135b","journal":"New Astronomy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newast.2020.101454","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-43 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the closest-orbiting hot Jupiter found at the time, at 0.014 AU, and its star at 0.6 solar masses is the lowest-mass star known to host one (Hellier et al. 2011). Phase curves track brightness over an orbit. Spitzer phase curves show a strong day-night contrast; a high-altitude cloud or haze layer on the nightside could explain this (Stevenson et al. 2017). TESS data show a low reflectivity and no significant dayside clouds, and a brightest point shifted eastward by about 44 degrees (Lally et al. 2026).","system":"The star rotates every 15.6 days (Hellier et al. 2011).","history":"Finding such a planet shows that planets with short remaining lifetimes owing to tidal decay also orbit stars with deep convection zones (Hellier et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp43b-Hellieretal2011","cite":"Hellier et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-43b: the closest-orbiting hot Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1104.2823","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp43b-Stevensonetal2017","cite":"Stevenson et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kevin B. Stevenson, Michael R. Line, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"Spitzer Phase Curve Constraints for WASP-43b at 3.6 and 4.5 μm","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00056","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp43b-Lallyetal2026","cite":"Lally et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Maura Lally, Ryan C. Challener, Elspeth Lee et al.","title":"WASP-43b TESS Phase Curve Mapping: Evidence for a Hot Interior","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10719","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":45,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The Spitzer space telescope devoted more than 1000 hours between 2016 and 2019 to the TRAPPIST-1 star alone. Astronomers analysed 188 transits of the seven planets, seen at infrared wavelengths, to pin down their sizes, masses, compositions and orbital motions (Ducrot et al. 2020). The planets are all Earth-sized and well suited to atmospheric study with the James Webb Space Telescope (Ducrot et al. 2020). Modelling of the planets' interiors finds that possible water mass fractions vary widely between them, and that results depend strongly on which stellar abundance clues are used (Dorn et al. 2018).","system":"TRAPPIST-1 is an ultracool dwarf star 12 parsecs away with seven transiting Earth-sized planets (Ducrot et al. 2020). At least three of them orbit within the classical habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). All the planets sit in a chain of linked orbital resonances, in which orbital periods are close to simple ratios (Teyssandier et al. 2022). Their transit times drift in repeating patterns lasting from days to decades (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","history":"Computer simulations of planets moving inward through a gas disc can build a resonant chain like the observed one, but only for particular disc conditions (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"Three-body resonances should show up in future monitoring of transit times (Teyssandier et al. 2022). Whether any of the planets hold atmospheres is not yet known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1g-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1g-Dornetal2018","cite":"Dorn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Klaus Mosegaard, Simon L. Grimm et al.","title":"Interior Characterization in Multiplanetary Systems: TRAPPIST-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01803","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1g-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 118203 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a search for transiting hot Jupiters that deliberately targeted stars rich in heavy elements (da Silva et al. 2006).","observed":"The host is a slightly evolved subgiant star that was part of a metal-rich sample watched with the ELODIE spectrograph from March 2004 (da Silva et al. 2006). The planet's orbit is noticeably eccentric, with e = 0.31 (da Silva et al. 2006). Checks of the star's spectral line shapes found no sign that stellar activity was faking the signal (da Silva et al. 2006). A later survey of 16 gas giants with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope set the first limits on helium in the planet's upper atmosphere (Orell-Miquel et al. 2026). Helium absorption is a tracer of gas escaping from close-in giants.","system":"A slow drift in the velocity residuals hints at a second companion on a longer orbit (da Silva et al. 2006).","history":"","open":"Whether the longer-period companion exists is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd118203b-daSilvaetal2006","cite":"da Silva et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"R. da Silva, S. Udry, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"Elodie metallicity-biased search for transiting Hot Jupiters. I. Two Hot Jupiters orbiting the slightly evolved stars <ASTROBJ>HD 118203</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 149143</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd118203b-OrellMiqueletal2026","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jaume Orell-Miquel, Kyra Sampson, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"HET/HPF Observations of Helium in Warm, Hot, and Ultrahot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-167 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Measuring the planet's mass was hard because the star's own activity added noise to the velocities. Updates to the HARPS-N data pipeline and a new activity-correction method called CALM, applied to 74 velocity measurements, finally gave a mass of about 6.3 Earth masses (de Beurs et al. 2024). The planet's radius of about 2.33 Earth radii puts it at the upper edge of the radius valley, the gap in sizes between rocky super-Earths and puffier sub-Neptunes (de Beurs et al. 2024). Infrared Spitzer transits confirmed the K2 radius and sharpened the orbital period (Duck et al. 2021). Updated timing from TESS reduces the uncertainty on future transits, which helps in planning James Webb Space Telescope time (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020).","system":"There are hints of a second companion with a period near 22 days (de Beurs et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The 22-day signal needs more velocity measurements to be confirmed (de Beurs et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2167b-deBeursetal2024","cite":"de Beurs et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Zoë L. de Beurs, Andrew Vanderburg, Erica Thygesen et al.","title":"Characterization of K2-167 b and CALM, a new stellar activity mitigation method","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.12276","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2167b-Ducketal2021","cite":"Duck et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alison Duck, Caleb K. Harada, Justin Harrell et al.","title":"K2, Spitzer, and TESS Transits of Four Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08889","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2167b-IkwutUkwaetal2020","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mma Ikwut-Ukwa, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"The K2 and TESS Synergy. I. Updated Ephemerides and Parameters for K2-114, K2-167, K2-237, and K2-261","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.00678","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-23 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was announced together with two other hot Jupiters of about two Jupiter masses from the HATSouth survey (Bento et al. 2017).","observed":"The planet crosses its star on a grazing orbit, so only part of the planet blocks the star's light (Bento et al. 2017). Its radius is large and uncertain, at 1.69 times Jupiter's with an error of 0.24 (Bento et al. 2017). Its mass is about 1.5 Jupiter masses (Bento et al. 2017). The host is a G-dwarf with properties very similar to the Sun's (Bento et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The grazing geometry leaves the planet's true size poorly constrained."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats23b-Bentoetal2017","cite":"Bento et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Bento, B. Schmidt, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-22b, HATS-23b and HATS-24b: three new transiting super-Jupiters from the HATSouth project","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"COCONUTS-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the second find of a programme searching for cool companions on very wide orbits around other stars (Zhang et al. 2021).","observed":"COCONUTS-2 b is a very cold planetary-mass object, with a temperature near 434 K (Zhang et al. 2021). It is the second coldest and second widest imaged exoplanet known (Zhang et al. 2021). Spectra from Gemini show chemistry out of equilibrium, plus clouds or an unusual temperature structure, in its atmosphere (Zhang et al. 2025). Observations with JWST MIRI have since measured a high-quality spectrum, searching for rare isotopes of oxygen and nitrogen (Kühnle et al. 2026). JWST data support a mass of about 7.5 Jupiter masses (Kiman et al. 2026).","system":"The host star, a rapidly spinning, active M dwarf, lies about 6471 au from the planet (Zhang et al. 2021). Its spin axis is nearly edge-on to us (Zhang et al. 2021). The system probably belongs to the Corona of Ursa Major moving group, which would make it about 414 million years old (Kiman et al. 2026).","history":"The planet probably formed in the same way as stars do, by collapse of a gas cloud, rather than inside a disc (Kiman et al. 2026). The nitrogen isotope ratio is a possible test of whether it formed like a star or like a planet (Patapis et al. 2024).","open":"Models disagree on the details of the planet's chemistry. All models predict fainter Y-band light than observed, which points to uncertainties in how sodium and potassium absorb (Zhang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-coconuts2b-Zhangetal2021","cite":"Zhang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Zhoujian Zhang, Michael C. Liu, Zachary R. Claytor et al.","title":"The Second Discovery from the COCONUTS Program: A Cold Wide-orbit Exoplanet around a Young Field M Dwarf at 10.9 pc","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.02805","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-coconuts2b-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Zhoujian Zhang, Sagnick Mukherjee, Michael C. Liu et al.","title":"Disequilibrium Chemistry, Diabatic Thermal Structure, and Clouds in the Atmosphere of COCONUTS-2b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.10939","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-coconuts2b-Khnleetal2026","cite":"Kühnle et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"H. Kühnle, E. C. Matthews, P. Mollière et al.","title":"Oxygen and nitrogen isotopologs on cold COCONUTS-2b observed with MIRI/MRS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26850","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-coconuts2b-Kimanetal2026","cite":"Kiman et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Rocio Kiman, Charles A. Beichman, Azul Ruiz Diaz et al.","title":"The Diversity of Cold Worlds: Age and Characterization of the Exoplanet COCONUTS-2 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-coconuts2b-Patapisetal2024","cite":"Patapis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Polychronis Alexandros Patapis, Paul Molliere, David Barrado Navascues et al.","title":"Testing a new formation tracer for cold gas giant planets with JWST/MIRI","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 3","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3634 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a close-in super-Earth around a cool M dwarf, with a period of about 2.6 days (Bonfils et al. 2011). A chance of about 7 per cent existed that it would cross the star, so a six-hour Spitzer light curve was taken at 4.5 micrometres (Bonfils et al. 2011). No transit appeared, which lowered the odds of the planet transiting to about 0.5 per cent (Bonfils et al. 2011). Its size is therefore not measured. Calculations of tidal spin evolution show its rotation may currently be locked in a non-synchronous resonance, not simple tidal locking (Rodríguez et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is rocky is not known, because its radius has not been measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3634b-Bonfilsetal2011","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"X. Bonfils, M. Gillon, T. Forveille et al.","title":"A short-period super-Earth orbiting the M2.5 dwarf GJ 3634. Detection with HARPS velocimetry and transit search with Spitzer photometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.1420","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3634b-Rodrguezetal2012","cite":"Rodríguez et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"A. Rodríguez, N. Callegari, T. A. Michtchenko et al.","title":"Spin-orbit coupling for tidally evolving super-Earths","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.1580","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 185283 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Mass and orbit were found by combining archival HARPS velocities with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of how the star's motion across the sky speeds up and slows down (Philipot et al. 2023). Combining the two kinds of data constrains the true mass, not only a minimum value (Philipot et al. 2023). HD 185283 b is one of six new planets found this way, with separations between 1 and 6 au (Philipot et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd185283b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, F. Kiefer et al.","title":"A multi-technique approach to identifying and/or constraining radial-velocity substellar companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05417","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4495 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS light curves were modelled with a photodynamical analysis, which fits transit shapes and timing shifts together, to measure both planets' masses (Wang et al. 2026). Planet c has a mass of 23.2 Earth masses and a radius of about 4.0 Earth radii, indicating a gas envelope rich in volatiles (Wang et al. 2026). Observations of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in the star's light during transit, were used to test the alignment of the system (Wang et al. 2026).","system":"A smaller sub-Neptune, planet b, orbits every 2.567 days, close to a 2:1 orbital resonance with planet c (Wang et al. 2026). The pair is aligned with the star's spin (Wang et al. 2026).","history":"The planets likely experienced overstable migration, a drift inward through the disc that pushes planets near, but not into, resonance (Wang et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4495c-Wangetal2026","cite":"Wang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mu-Tian Wang, Fei Dai, Hui-Gen Liu et al.","title":"TOI-4495: A Pair of Aligned, Near-resonant Sub-Neptunes That Likely Experienced Overstable Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02665","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of about 4.2 Jupiter masses and an eccentric orbit, with e = 0.036 detected at 10 sigma (Buchhave et al. 2010). It sits in a sparsely populated part of the mass-radius diagram and matches a 1 Gyr hydrogen-helium gas giant (Buchhave et al. 2010). Spitzer observed two eclipses at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres, with an equilibrium temperature of about 1626 K (McIntyre et al. 2017). Transit times drift in a way that apsidal precession, a slow turning of the orbit's long axis, can explain (Sun et al. 2023).","system":"A statistical study of spin-orbit alignment along the line of sight flagged the system as likely misaligned (Schlaufman 2010).","history":"Alignment between hot Jupiters and their stars may begin random and be reduced by tides (Albrecht et al. 2012).","open":"Planetary companions have not been ruled out as the cause of the timing changes (Sun et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp16b-Buchhaveetal2010","cite":"Buchhave et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"L. A. Buchhave, G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-16b: A 4 M <SUB>J</SUB> Planet Transiting a Bright Star on an Eccentric Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp16b-McIntyreetal2017","cite":"McIntyre et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kathleen McIntyre, Joseph Harrington, Jasmina Blecic et al.","title":"HAT-P-16b: A Bayesian Atmospheric Retrieval","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #49","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp16b-Sunetal2023","cite":"Sun et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"L. Sun, S. Gu, X. Wang et al.","title":"The study on transiting systems HAT-P-13, HAT-P-16, and WASP-32 through combining ground-based and TESS photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08484","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp16b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp16b-Albrechtetal2012","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John A. Johnson et al.","title":"Obliquities of Hot Jupiter Host Stars: Evidence for Tidal Interactions and Primordial Misalignments","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6105","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":16,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-35 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a metal-poor star, with an iron abundance lower than the Sun's ([Fe/H] = -0.15) (Enoch et al. 2011). Follow-up with TESS and a 1-metre ground telescope reanalysed the system and produced improved parameters consistent with earlier results (Bai et al. 2022). The star and planet make a favourable target for transmission spectroscopy, which reads the planet's atmosphere from starlight filtered during transit (Bai et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp35b-Enochetal2011","cite":"Enoch et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"B. Enoch, D. R. Anderson, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-35b, WASP-48b, and HAT-P-30b/WASP-51b: Two New Planets and an Independent Discovery of a Hat Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1104.2827","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp35b-Baietal2022","cite":"Bai et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Lu Bai, Shenghong Gu, Xiaobin Wang et al.","title":"WASP-35 and HAT-P-30/WASP-51: Reanalysis using TESS and Ground-based Transit Photometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02866","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was the first found with a survey that paired professional and amateur astronomers, which helped separate it from many similar false candidates (McCullough et al. 2006).","observed":"XO-1 was the most Sun-like of the ten stars known at the time to host transiting planets, in radius, mass and metal content (McCullough et al. 2006). The planet's mass is about 0.9 Jupiter masses (McCullough et al. 2006). Transit timings collected over many years refined the period, which changed by about 3 seconds from the earlier value (Raetz et al. 2009). Spitzer eclipse data fit models of a carbon-rich atmosphere with a carbon-to-oxygen ratio near 1 (Moses et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"The carbon-rich nature of the atmosphere is only possible, not confirmed (Moses et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo1b-McCulloughetal2006","cite":"McCullough et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"P. R. McCullough, J. E. Stys, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"A Transiting Planet of a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0605414","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo1b-Raetzetal2009","cite":"Raetz et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"St. Raetz, M. Mugrauer, T. O. B. Schmidt et al.","title":"Planetary transit observations at the University Observatory Jena: XO-1b and TrES-1","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.1833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo1b-Mosesetal2013","cite":"Moses et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. I. Moses, N. Madhusudhan, C. Visscher et al.","title":"Chemical Consequences of the C/O Ratio on Hot Jupiters: Examples from WASP-12b, CoRoT-2b, XO-1b, and HD 189733b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.2996","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gliese 48 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet signal was found by reanalysing CARMENES and decade-long HIRES velocity data of a magnetically active M dwarf with a rotation period of about 51.5 days (Conzo et al. 2026). The signal stays coherent over 15 years and looks the same in visible and near-infrared light, both signs that it comes from a planet and not stellar activity (Conzo et al. 2026). TESS saw no transit, and tests show a rocky planet of minimum size would have been seen, so the orbit is tilted from edge-on (Conzo et al. 2026). The planet lies near the inner edge of the conservative habitable zone (Conzo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its composition and true mass are not known (Conzo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gliese48b-Conzoetal2026","cite":"Conzo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Conzo, M. Moriconi, S. A., Jr. Correa","title":"Detection and characterization of the temperate super-earth Gliese 48 b","journal":"Open European Journal on Variable Stars","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11123","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4377 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an evolved star of 1.36 solar masses and 3.52 solar radii (Pereira et al. 2024). The planet's radius of about 1.35 Jupiter radii is inflated (Pereira et al. 2024). Its size follows the trend seen in short-period giants, in which more stellar heating gives a larger planet (Pereira et al. 2024). Interior models suggest that about 2 per cent of the stellar light falling on the planet is deposited in its interior, which puffs it up (Pereira et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4377b-Pereiraetal2024","cite":"Pereira et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Filipe Pereira, Samuel K. Grunblatt, Angelica Psaridi et al.","title":"TESS giants transiting giants V - two hot Jupiters orbiting red giant hosts","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06678","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6029 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a survey of giant planets orbiting giant stars, flagged as a candidate in 2021 and selected for two-minute TESS observations in Sector 58 (Saunders et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet orbits a subgiant star, one that is leaving the main sequence and has cooled to develop a deep outer convective layer (Saunders et al. 2024). Observations of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion in starlight during transit that shows spin-orbit alignment, were made for hot Jupiters around such stars (Saunders et al. 2024). The orbits of those planets, with stars cooler than about 6250 K, are aligned or nearly aligned with the star's spin (Saunders et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Alignment around cooled subgiants points to rapid tidal realignment after the star developed its convective envelope (Saunders et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6029b-Saundersetal2024","cite":"Saunders et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Nicholas Saunders, Samuel K. Grunblatt, Ashley Chontos et al.","title":"TESS Giants Transiting Giants. VI. Newly Discovered Hot Jupiters Provide Evidence for Efficient Obliquity Damping after the Main Sequence","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21650","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 107148 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"An independent check using HIRES, HARPS and CARMENES velocities confirmed both planets in the system (Eberhardt et al. 2022). The two strong periodic signals show no link to stellar activity, which supports their planetary origin (Eberhardt et al. 2022). Planet c is Neptune-mass, with a period of about 18.3 days and a clearly eccentric orbit of about 0.4 (Eberhardt et al. 2022).","system":"The inner planet is the eccentric Neptune-mass planet c. Planet b is Saturn-mass, with a period of about 77.2 days (Eberhardt et al. 2022). The two planets form a moderately compact system (Eberhardt et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd107148c-Eberhardtetal2022","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov, Martin Kürster et al.","title":"Dynamical Architecture of the HD 107148 Planetary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.06702","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-11 hosts six transiting planets in an extremely compact arrangement, a standout among Kepler systems (Quintana et al. 2011). Planet e is among the low-density planets that Kepler revealed in this system (Bedell et al. 2017). A close look at the star found it to be a young solar twin, which raised the planets' densities by 20 to 95 per cent (Bedell et al. 2017). Interior models ask whether such planets are rocky cores with thick hydrogen atmospheres or small Neptunes made mostly of water (Lopez et al. 2011).","system":"All six planets orbit within 0.5 au of the star, and the inner five are on nearly circular, coplanar orbits within 0.25 au (Quintana et al. 2011).","history":"Formation by migration and in-place assembly are both considered possible for such a crowded system (Quintana et al. 2011).","open":"Whether the planet is a super-Earth with a hydrogen envelope or a water-rich mini-Neptune is not settled (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11e-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11e-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11e-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2109 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2109 b is an ultra-hot Jupiter on a 16-hour orbit with a mass of about 5 Jupiter masses (Wong et al. 2021). TESS saw its secondary eclipse and a phase curve with brightness variations around the orbit (Wong et al. 2021). The planet's dayside brightness temperature is about 3631 K (Wong et al. 2021). High-resolution spectra show iron and carbon monoxide emission and a thermal inversion on the dayside, but nothing on the nightside (Cont et al. 2025). A JWST phase curve gives a nightside temperature near 1,700 K and a day-night contrast of about 0.5 (Inglis et al. 2026).","system":"The planet orbits at less than 2.3 stellar radii from its star, the closest relative separation of any hot Jupiter (Harre et al. 2024). The orbit is well aligned with the star's spin (Wong et al. 2021). Timing shifts hint at a nearby outer planet (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026).","history":"Core accretion and disc instability are both plausible formation routes (Inglis et al. 2026). Orbital decay, an inward spiral from tides, is predicted at the fastest rate of any known giant planet (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026).","open":"Whether a companion planet exists is not settled, though sinusoidal timing shifts are strongly favoured (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2109b-Wongetal2021","cite":"Wong et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ian Wong, Avi Shporer, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2109: An Ultrahot Gas Giant on a 16 hr Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12074","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2109b-Contetal2025","cite":"Cont et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Cont, L. Nortmann, F. Lesjak et al.","title":"Retrieving day- and nightside atmospheric properties of the ultra-hot Jupiter TOI-2109b: Detection of Fe and CO emission lines and evidence for inefficient heat transport","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15757","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2109b-Inglisetal2026","cite":"Inglis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Julie Inglis, Nicole Wallack, John Allen et al.","title":"(Nearly) Rolling on its Star: A JWST NIRSpec Phase Curve of the Shortest Orbital Period Transiting Gas Giant Known, TOI-2109b","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2109b-Harreetal2024","cite":"Harre et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J.-V. Harre, A. M. S. Smith, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"Hints of a close outer companion to the ultra-hot Jupiter TOI-2109 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.07797","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2109b-GreklekMcKeonetal2026","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Julie Inglis, Nicole L. Wallack et al.","title":"JWST and HST Confirm Transit Timing Variations for the Ultra-Hot Jupiter TOI-2109 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26894","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1232 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm, Jupiter-mass world on a 14.26-day orbit around a metal-rich G dwarf (Mihaylov et al. 2026). TESS light curves are complicated by a background eclipsing binary with a 1.37-day period, which made the detection harder (Mihaylov et al. 2026). Ground-based follow-up from Las Cumbres, Hazelwood, Brierfield and ASTEP firmly confirmed the planet (Mihaylov et al. 2026). The transits show strong timing variations, a sign of another massive planet in the system (Mihaylov et al. 2026).","system":"The system holds a warm massive pair of planets (Mihaylov et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1232b-Mihaylovetal2026","cite":"Mihaylov et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Deyan P. Mihaylov, Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"A Warm Massive Pair of Planets around TOI-1232 Revealed with Transit-timing Variations and Doppler Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18395","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a low density, which together with its host star's high metal content makes its large radius hard to explain (Kovács et al. 2007). Its orbit is prograde, with a spin-orbit angle of -4.9 degrees plus or minus 11.9, so it moves in the same direction as the star spins (Winn et al. 2011). Spitzer eclipse depths fit inefficient heat redistribution from the day side to the night side and low albedo (Todorov et al. 2013). A search of TESS and ground-based photometry found no extra close-in planets (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"A third body in the system could be a second planet or a companion star (Winn et al. 2011). A companion star, TYC 2569-744-1, has had its chemical abundances measured and compared with the host star's (Saffe et al. 2017).","history":"Aligned orbits around cooler stars fit a picture in which three-body dynamics and tides set the short orbits of many hot Jupiters (Winn et al. 2011).","open":"Whether the third body is a planet or a star is not settled (Winn et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp4b-Kovcsetal2007","cite":"Kovács et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Kovács, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-4b: A Metal-rich Low-Density Transiting Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.0602","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp4b-Winnetal2011","cite":"Winn et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson et al.","title":"Orbital Orientations of Exoplanets: HAT-P-4b is Prograde and HAT-P-14b is Retrograde","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1318","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp4b-Todorovetal2013","cite":"Todorov et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Kamen O. Todorov, Drake Deming, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Photometry of Three Hot Jupiters: HAT-P-3b, HAT-P-4b and HAT-P-12b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.0833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp4b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, M. Fernández, A. Sota et al.","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. III. A Study of Ten Systems Enhanced with New Ground-Based Photometry","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00538","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp4b-Saffeetal2017","cite":"Saffe et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Saffe, E. Jofre, E. Martioli et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HAT-P-4 and TYC 2569-744-1 abundances and spectra (Saffe+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36049004","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5734 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was validated in a survey of K-dwarf hosts, a population less studied than M dwarfs (Morello et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5734 b lies near the upper edge of the radius valley, the gap in planet sizes between rocky and puffy planets (Morello et al. 2026). HARPS-N velocities and TESS photometry were modelled together, using Gaussian processes to tell stellar activity from planetary signals (Filomeno et al. 2026). The result is a hot sub-Neptune with an Earth-like density (Filomeno et al. 2026). Its host is a relatively young K3-K4 dwarf of about 500 million years (Filomeno et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The planet's atmosphere is reconstructed under heavy high-energy irradiation from its young host star (Filomeno et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5734b-Morelloetal2026","cite":"Morello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Morello, A. Peláez-Torres, F. J. Pozuelos et al.","title":"The K-dwarfs survey ─ I. Four validated planets from the radius valley to the Neptune desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag183","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5734b-Filomenoetal2026","cite":"Filomeno et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. Filomeno, T. Trifonov, M. Damasso et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at the TNG: LXXII. TOI-5734b: A hot sub-Neptune orbiting a relatively young K dwarf with an Earth-like density","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18108","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wendelstein-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, Pan-Planets, aimed to find hot Jupiters around low-mass stars and to measure how common they are, because such planets seem to get rarer as stars get lighter (Obermeier et al. 2020).","observed":"Wendelstein-1 b was confirmed in two ways. Radial velocity measurements, which track the star's wobble, showed it is a planet (Obermeier et al. 2020). Observations in many colours, from 4000 to 24 000 Å, showed how the transit depth changes with wavelength, a check called the Transit Color Signature (Obermeier et al. 2020). Secondary eclipse observations, made when the planet passes behind its star, gave its thermal emission (Obermeier et al. 2020). The planet has a radius of 1.03 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.59 Jupiter masses. Its surface temperature is estimated at about 1727 K (Obermeier et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a K7 dwarf, a cool orange star (Obermeier et al. 2020). The same survey found a second hot Jupiter, Wendelstein-2 b, around another late K star (Obermeier et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wendelstein1b-Obermeieretal2020","cite":"Obermeier et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"C. Obermeier, J. Steuer, H. Kellermann et al.","title":"Following the TraCS of exoplanets with Pan-Planets: Wendelstein-1b and Wendelstein-2b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13560","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 204941 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets around this star and three others were found even though the stars show clear signs of magnetic activity (Dumusque et al. 2011).","observed":"HD 204941 b has a minimum mass of 0.27 Jupiter masses and an orbit with an eccentricity below 0.4 (Dumusque et al. 2011). Its star shows a solar-like magnetic cycle, a slow rise and fall of activity over years, and this cycle shifts the measured velocities (Dumusque et al. 2011). The discovery team corrected for it by fitting the star's activity index and applying the same solution to the velocities, which removes much of the extra noise (Dumusque et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd204941b-Dumusqueetal2011","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"X. Dumusque, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXX. Planetary systems around stars with solar-like magnetic cycles and short-term activity variation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.1748","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1173 b":{"text":{"why":"Puffy Neptune-mass planets with very low density are extremely rare, and only a few have been measured precisely with both transit and velocity data (Yana Galarza et al. 2024).","observed":"TOI-1173 b is a low-density super-Neptune with a density below 0.3 g per cubic centimetre, found on a nearly circular orbit (Yana Galarza et al. 2024). It was measured with MAROON-X and HIRES velocities and TESS transits (Yana Galarza et al. 2024). It is the first puffy super-Neptune found in a wide binary system (Yana Galarza et al. 2024). Later velocity work found its eccentricity is below 0.03 to 0.05 at 95% confidence (Yee & Vissapragada 2026). That rules out heating from ongoing eccentricity tides as the cause of its large size (Yee & Vissapragada 2026).","system":"The host is the main star of a wide binary, with the companion star at a projected separation of about 11,400 au (Yana Galarza et al. 2024).","history":"Orbital stability work suggests the planet likely kept a stable orbit over time and may have migrated through the von Zeipel-Lidov-Kozai effect, where a distant companion star tilts and stretches an orbit (Yana Galarza et al. 2024).","open":"Why the planet is so puffy is not settled. Tidal heating was the most promising explanation at discovery (Yana Galarza et al. 2024). Later work points instead to Ohmic dissipation, or to a relic of earlier internal heating followed by slow cooling (Yee & Vissapragada 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1173b-Galarzaetal2024","cite":"Galarza et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jhon Yana Galarza, Thiago Ferreira, Diego Lorenzo-Oliveira et al.","title":"TOI-1173 A b: The First Inflated Super-Neptune in a Wide Binary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06240","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1173b-YeeVissapragada2026","cite":"Yee & Vissapragada 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Shreyas Vissapragada","title":"\"Popcorn Planets\" Are Not Actively Inflated by Eccentricity Tides","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07746","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5800 b":{"text":{"why":"It sits at the edge of the Neptune desert, a region close to stars where few Neptune-size planets are found, thought to be shaped by migration and by atmosphere loss (Jenkins et al. 2025).","observed":"TOI-5800 b has an unusually high eccentricity of 0.39 for such a short orbit (Jenkins et al. 2025). The planet is probably moving inward through tidal migration, and it is heated strongly by the tides as it goes (Jenkins et al. 2025). Velocities from the Planet Finder Spectrograph gave its mass and eccentricity (Jenkins et al. 2025). Independent HARPS and HARPS-N measurements confirmed it as a hot sub-Neptune (Naponiello et al. 2025). It is ranked as a top target for atmosphere studies with the James Webb Space Telescope (Jenkins et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5800b-Jenkinsetal2025","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Sydney A. Jenkins, Andrew Vanderburg, Ritika Sethi et al.","title":"An Eccentric Sub-Neptune Moving Into the Evaporation Desert","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10324","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5800b-Naponielloetal2025","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"L. Naponiello, S. Vissapragada, A. S. Bonomo et al.","title":"The Hot-Neptune Initiative (HONEI): I. Two hot sub-Neptunes on a close-in eccentric orbit (TOI-5800 b) and a farther-out circular orbit (TOI-5817 b)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10123","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5788 b":{"text":{"why":"Few systems have planets on both sides of the radius gap, the dip in planet sizes between rocky and puffier worlds, and fewer still orbit metal-poor stars (Lakeland et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5788 b was measured with six TESS sectors, a dedicated CHEOPS programme and 125 HARPS-N spectra (Lakeland et al. 2026). It has a radius of 1.528 Earth radii and a mass of 3.72 Earth masses (Lakeland et al. 2026). It is the smaller of two planets found around a metal-poor, Sun-like star (Lakeland et al. 2026).","system":"The outer planet, TOI-5788 c, has a radius of 2.272 Earth radii and a mass of 6.4 Earth masses, so the two planets straddle the radius gap (Lakeland et al. 2026). Dynamical analysis says a third planet could exist in a stable orbit between 8 and 14 days, but there is no evidence for one (Lakeland et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5788b-Lakelandetal2026","cite":"Lakeland et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ben S. Lakeland, A. Mortier, R. D. Haywood et al.","title":"Discovery and characterization of two exoplanets orbiting the metal-poor, solar-type star TOI-5788 with TESS, CHEOPS, and HARPS-N","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14045","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-426 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet TOI-426 b. That planet orbits in about 1.32 days near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert, a region close to stars with few Neptune-size planets (Castro-González et al. 2026). It has a radius of 2.19 Earth radii and a mass of 6.7 Earth masses, found from 77 HARPS velocities (Castro-González et al. 2026). The star is active, and its signals were modelled with Gaussian processes to separate them from planetary motion (Castro-González et al. 2026). TOI-426 c was found as a companion in the same velocity data.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi426c-CastroGonzlezetal2026","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Castro-González, O. Barragán, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"Two extremely irradiated volatile-rich sub-Neptunes with companions in the TOI-426 and TOI-1839 systems: Insights into arrival and survival near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05413","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6695 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-6695 c is the outer planet of a pair of warm Jupiter-mass planets, and it is known only from Doppler data (Eberhardt et al. 2025). It has a minimum mass of 1.45 Jupiter masses and a period of about 242.4 days (Eberhardt et al. 2025). The inner planet, TOI-6695 b, transits its star every 80.4 days and has a radius of 0.85 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.21 Jupiter masses (Eberhardt et al. 2025). The two planets pull on each other enough to shift the transit times (Eberhardt et al. 2025).","system":"The pair lies near a 3:1 mean-motion resonance, meaning the outer planet orbits about three times for every orbit of the inner (Eberhardt et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6695c-Eberhardtetal2025","cite":"Eberhardt et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jan Eberhardt, Trifon Trifonov, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"TOI-6695: A Pair of Near-resonant Massive Planets Observed with TESS from the WINE Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/adc44e","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 97658 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 97658 b was first seen as a velocity signal, and photometry showed the star is constant at that period to 0.09 mmag, supporting the planet (Howard et al. 2011). Spitzer observations later confirmed that it transits its star (Van Grootel et al. 2015). Modelling gives a rocky component of at least 60% by mass and at most 2% hydrogen and helium by mass (Van Grootel et al. 2015). CHEOPS later observed its transits and refined its parameters (Maxted et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its atmosphere is not known. Models of hydrogen-rich atmospheres with hydrocarbon haze predict a methane feature at 3.3 microns that the James Webb Space Telescope could detect in planets like it (Kawashima et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd97658b-Howardetal2011","cite":"Howard et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The NASA-UC Eta-Earth Program. III. A Super-Earth Orbiting HD 97658 and a Neptune-mass Planet Orbiting Gl 785","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.0414","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97658b-VanGrooteletal2015","cite":"Van Grootel et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"V. Van Grootel, M. Gillon, D. Valencia et al.","title":"HD 97658 and its super-Earth","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/epjconf/201510102006","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97658b-Maxtedetal2022","cite":"Maxted et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. Ehrenreich, T. G. Wilson et al.","title":"Analysis of Early Science observations with the CHaracterising ExOPlanets Satellite (CHEOPS) using PYCHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08828","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97658b-Kawashimaetal2019","cite":"Kawashima et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Yui Kawashima, Renyu Hu, Masahiro Ikoma","title":"Detectable Molecular Features above Hydrocarbon Haze via Transmission Spectroscopy with JWST: Case Studies of GJ 1214b-, GJ 436b-, HD 97658b-, and Kepler-51b-like Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10151","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":23,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 56414 b":{"text":{"why":"HD 56414 is one of the hottest stars known to host a planet smaller than Jupiter (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"HD 56414 b orbits a young A-type star about 420 million years old (Giacalone et al. 2022). It lies on the boundary of the hot Neptune desert, which suggests it may be losing mass (Giacalone et al. 2022). A model that includes the strong near-ultraviolet light of A stars finds the planet can keep most of its atmosphere over 1 billion years if its mass is 8 Earth masses or more (Giacalone et al. 2022). Planets of this size with masses below 14 Earth masses are at risk of total atmospheric stripping (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass was not measured at discovery, so whether it keeps its atmosphere is not known (Giacalone et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd56414b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, A. García Muñoz et al.","title":"HD 56414 b: A Warm Neptune Transiting an A-type Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.06396","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 110067 c belongs to a system of six sub-Neptunes locked in a resonant chain, where orbital periods form simple ratios (Zak et al. 2024). The ESPRESSO spectrograph watched the star during a transit of planet c and measured a sky-projected obliquity of 6 degrees, with an uncertainty of about 25 degrees, so the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"The star is part of a wide hierarchical triple. A companion, HD 110106, lies at a projected separation of 13,400 au and is itself an 8-year double-lined spectroscopic binary of K3 dwarfs (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"The aligned orbit suggests the system reached its present arrangement through convergent migration, planets drifting inward together, without major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067c-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067c-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 87883 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 87883 b orbits a metal-rich K0 dwarf at 3.6 au with an eccentricity of 0.53, which is a stretched orbit (Fischer et al. 2009). Its minimum mass from velocities alone was 1.78 Jupiter masses (Fischer et al. 2009). Combining velocities with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of the star's motion gave a true mass of 6.31 Jupiter masses, which confirms it is a planet (Li et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd87883b-Fischeretal2009","cite":"Fischer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Debra Fischer, Peter Driscoll, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Five Planets and an Independent Confirmation of HD 196885Ab from Lick Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd87883b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2016-BLG-1836L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was in a high-cadence field, observed about four times per hour by the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network (Yang et al. 2020).","observed":"KMT-2016-BLG-1836L b is a super-Jovian planet with a mass of about 2.2 Jupiter masses, orbiting an M or K dwarf of about 0.49 solar masses (Yang et al. 2020). It lies about 7.1 kpc away, with a projected separation of 3.5 au, beyond the snow line where water ice can form (Yang et al. 2020). Its mass-ratio to the host is about 0.004 (Yang et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass and distance depend on statistical modelling. Future high-resolution images could constrain the lens brightness and so pin down the mass and distance (Yang et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2016blg1836lb-Yangetal2020","cite":"Yang et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Hongjing Yang, Xiangyu Zhang, Kyu-Ha Hwang et al.","title":"KMT-2016-BLG-1836Lb: A Super-Jovian Planet from a High-cadence Microlensing Field","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10011","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-2 S c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"XO-2 S is the southern star of a pair of near-identical metal-rich K dwarfs 31 arcseconds apart that move through space together; its northern twin hosts the transiting hot Jupiter XO-2 N b (Burke et al. 2007). Little is known about this planet beyond its orbit.","system":"The two twin stars have a metallicity of [Fe/H] = 0.45 and likely formed in the metal-rich inner disc of the Galaxy before being scattered into the solar neighbourhood (Burke et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo2sc-Burkeetal2007","cite":"Burke et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, P. R. McCullough, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-2b: Transiting Hot Jupiter in a Metal-rich Common Proper Motion Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0003","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":8,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 192263 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 192263 b is a close-in giant with a minimum mass of 0.73 Jupiter masses on an almost circular orbit at 0.15 au (Santos et al. 2000). Its reality was once doubted. Photometry showed brightness changes matching the 24-day period, and Ca II emission varied at half that period, suggesting star spots rotating in and out of view could fake the velocity signal (Henry et al. 2002). Further measurements found that the velocity variation stays stable in phase, period and amplitude while the brightness signal changes with time, which supports the planet (Santos et al. 2003).","system":"A long-term trend in the velocities may point to another companion in the system (Santos et al. 2003).","history":"","open":"The brightness variations may be unrelated to the planet or caused by interaction between planet and star, and the origin of the long-term trend is unclear (Santos et al. 2003)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd192263b-Santosetal2000","cite":"Santos et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for Southern extra-solar planets. III. A giant planet in orbit around HD 192263","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd192263b-Henryetal2002","cite":"Henry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Gregory W. Henry, Robert A. Donahue, Sallie L. Baliunas","title":"A False Planet around HD 192263","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/344291","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd192263b-Santosetal2003","cite":"Santos et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"N. C. Santos, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XI. The return of the giant planet orbiting HD 192263","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0305434","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 29021 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity measurements alone cannot separate a planet's true mass from the tilt of its orbit. Combining the velocities with precise positions of the star from the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites removes that problem. The result is a mass of about 4.47 Jupiter masses, which confirms HD 29021 b as a planet and not a brown dwarf (Li et al. 2021). The star was monitored with the ELODIE and SOPHIE spectrographs in 66 observing epochs spread over 4 years (Rey et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"Two mirror-image orbital tilts still fit the data, because it is hard to tell whether the planet moves with or against the spin of the star (Li et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd29021b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd29021b-Reyetal2017","cite":"Rey et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Rey, G. Hebrard, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD17674, HD29021, and HD42012 radial velocities (Rey+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36010009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd29021b-Lietal2022","cite":"Li et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1839 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-1839 b. Seventy radial velocity measurements from the HARPS spectrograph were combined with space-telescope brightness data from TESS. The star shows strong activity signals that mimic planet-like velocity changes, so they were modelled and removed (Castro-González et al. 2026). The analysis confirmed the inner planet TOI-1839 b and revealed a second transiting planet, TOI-1839 c, with a radius of 2.50 ± 0.13 Earth radii (Castro-González et al. 2026).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-1839 b, orbits in about 1.42 days and lies near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert, the region close to stars where Neptune-size planets are rare (Castro-González et al. 2026). It measures 2.27 ± 0.10 Earth radii and weighs 7.10 ± 0.78 Earth masses (Castro-González et al. 2026).","history":"Two ideas explain the lower edge of the Neptunian desert. In one, volatile-rich planets are torn apart by tides after being flung onto very elongated orbits. In the other, their hydrogen and helium envelopes are blown away by the star's radiation (Castro-González et al. 2026).","open":"The origin of the lower edge of the Neptunian desert remains an open question (Castro-González et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1839c-CastroGonzlezetal2026","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Castro-González, O. Barragán, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"Two extremely irradiated volatile-rich sub-Neptunes with companions in the TOI-426 and TOI-1839 systems: Insights into arrival and survival near the lower edge of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05413","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 96167 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, the Keck N2K programme, searched for planets around metal-rich stars (Peek et al. 2009).","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 0.68 Jupiter masses and an orbit that is far from circular, with an eccentricity of 0.71 (Peek et al. 2009). Its star is an ageing subgiant of about 1.3 solar masses with a very high content of heavy elements (Peek et al. 2009). Imaging with adaptive optics later found a companion star moving through space together with HD 96167 (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015).","system":"The newly found companions in that imaging survey, around three stars including HD 96167, have masses of 0.21 to 0.56 solar masses and lie about 190 to 510 au from their primaries in projection (Mugrauer & Ginski 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd96167b-Peeketal2009","cite":"Peek et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Kathryn M. G. Peek, John Asher Johnson, Debra A. Fischer et al.","title":"Old, Rich, and Eccentric: Two Jovian Planets Orbiting Evolved Metal-Rich Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0904.2786","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd96167b-MugrauerGinski2015","cite":"Mugrauer & Ginski 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Mugrauer, C. Ginski","title":"High-contrast imaging search for stellar and substellar companions of exoplanet host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stv771","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"PH1 b":{"text":{"why":"Volunteers in the Planet Hunters citizen science project spotted the transits by eye in public Kepler data (Schwamb et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet circles two stars at once. Its orbit lies outside the 20-day orbit of an eclipsing pair made of an F dwarf of 1.528 solar masses and an M dwarf of 0.408 solar masses (Schwamb et al. 2013). Seven transits were seen in the first 11 quarters of Kepler data, about 137 days apart (Schwamb et al. 2013). The planet could not be weighed. Its mass is below 169 Earth masses, or 0.531 Jupiter masses, at 99.7% confidence (Schwamb et al. 2013).","system":"PH1 is a quadruple star system. A previously unknown visual binary, about 1000 au beyond the planet's orbit, is likely bound to the planet's host stars (Schwamb et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ph1b-Schwambetal2013","cite":"Schwamb et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Megan E. Schwamb, Jerome A. Orosz, Joshua A. Carter et al.","title":"Planet Hunters: A Transiting Circumbinary Planet in a Quadruple Star System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.3612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-53 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-53 b is a low-density hot Jupiter. It has 0.595 ± 0.089 Jupiter masses but a radius of 1.340 ± 0.056 Jupiter radii, so it is puffed up for its mass (Henning et al. 2018). Its star has many physical characteristics similar to the Sun (Henning et al. 2018).","system":"Three of four hot Jupiters announced together, including HATS-50 b and HATS-51 b, have low densities and similar orbital periods. The host stars of the other three planets are metal-rich, larger and more massive (Henning et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats53b-Henningetal2018","cite":"Henning et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Th. Henning, L. Mancini, P. Sarkis et al.","title":"HATS-50b through HATS-53b: Four Transiting Hot Jupiters Orbiting G-type Stars Discovered by the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.04324","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5126 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-5126 b was confirmed together with a second, cooler Neptune, TOI-5126 c, which has a radius of 3.86 Earth radii and a 17.9-day orbit (Fairnington et al. 2024). The CHEOPS space telescope gave extra brightness measurements to pin down the size and timing of planet b (Fairnington et al. 2024). Its stellar spin axis and orbit line up well: the sky-projected angle between them is 1 ± 48 degrees (Radzom et al. 2024).","system":"The planet lies in the hot Neptune desert, where planets of this size are rare. Its high equilibrium temperature of about 1442 K points to a cloud-free atmosphere, which makes it a good target for transmission spectroscopy (Fairnington et al. 2024). Small shifts in transit times hint at at least one more planet, though starspot crossings could cause the same signal (Fairnington et al. 2024).","history":"Compact systems with several planets leave little room for violent past events. Their aligned stars, including this one, suggest that such planets keep the tilt of the original disc and that misalignment mostly arises after the disc is gone (Radzom et al. 2024).","open":"More precise brightness measurements are needed to confirm the extra planet suggested by the transit timing shifts (Fairnington et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5126b-Fairningtonetal2024","cite":"Fairnington et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Tyler R. Fairnington, Emma Nabbie, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-5126: a hot super-Neptune and warm Neptune pair discovered by TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08890","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5126b-Radzometal2024","cite":"Radzom et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Brandon T. Radzom, Jiayin Dong, Malena Rice et al.","title":"Evidence for Primordial Alignment: Insights from Stellar Obliquity Measurements for Compact Sub-Saturn Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.06504","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-116 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-116 b is a warm, mildly inflated super-Saturn, with 0.59 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.43 Jupiter radii (Brown et al. 2014). Its host is a cool, metal-poor G0 dwarf with a temperature of 5950 K (Brown et al. 2014). Later work revisited candidates from the WASP survey with space photometry from TESS. Radial velocity measurements gave the planets their masses (Schanche et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp116b-Brownetal2014","cite":"Brown et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, D. R. Anderson, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Three transiting planet discoveries from the Wide Angle Search for Planets: WASP-85 A b; WASP-116 b, and WASP-149 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.7761","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp116b-Schancheetal2025","cite":"Schanche et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Schanche, G. Hébrard, K. G. Stassun et al.","title":"A Swarm of WASP Planets: Nine Giant Planets Identified by the WASP Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08091","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-130 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-130 b is a warm Jupiter with a mass of 1.23 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.89 Jupiter radii. Its size supports the trend that warm Jupiters have smaller radii than hot Jupiters (Hellier et al. 2017). It has the longest orbital period of any planet found by the WASP survey (Hellier et al. 2019). The spin of its star lines up with its orbit, with a sky-projected angle of about 0 to 10 degrees (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025). A search for water in its atmosphere with ESPRESSO found nothing, partly because the planet's velocity changes during transit are too small to separate its signal from the star's (Prinoth et al. 2024).","system":"High-contrast imaging found a candidate companion near the star (Bohn et al. 2020).","history":"Warm Jupiters around single stars are statistically more aligned than hot Jupiters. Of the warm Jupiters, 95% have true 3D tilts below about 25 degrees, which suggests they form in discs that were already aligned with the star (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp130b-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp130b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: 7 WASP-South transiting exoplanets (Hellier+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp130b-EspinozaRetamaletal2025","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Spin─Orbit Alignment of Eight Warm Gas Giant Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08692","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp130b-Prinothetal2024","cite":"Prinoth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Bibiana Prinoth, Elyar Sedaghati, Julia V. Seidel et al.","title":"High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy of Warm Jupiters: An ESPRESSO Sample with Predictions for ANDES","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08558","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp130b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Barnard e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Radial velocity monitoring with the UVES spectrograph found that the star's own motion through space causes a small, steady change in its velocity (Kürster et al. 2003). Stellar activity adds further variability (Kürster et al. 2003). Those data excluded planets above 7.5 Earth masses in circular orbits throughout the habitable zone, between 0.034 and 0.082 au (Kürster et al. 2003). Planets above 2.9 Earth masses were excluded at an orbital period of 2.5 days (Kürster et al. 2003).","system":"Barnard's Star is the nearest star after the Alpha Centauri system, at 1.82 parsecs, and has the largest proper motion known, 10.4 arcseconds per year (Riedel et al. 2005). It is old, 7 to 12 billion years, yet still magnetically active, with X-ray emission from its corona (Riedel et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-barnarde-Krsteretal2003","cite":"Kürster et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"M. Kürster, M. Endl, F. Rouesnel et al.","title":"The low-level radial velocity variability   in Barnard's star (= GJ 699).  Secular acceleration, indications for convective redshift,  and planet mass limits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0303528","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-barnarde-Riedeletal2005","cite":"Riedel et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"A. R. Riedel, E. F. Guinan, L. E. DeWarf et al.","title":"Barnard's Star as a Proxy for Old Disk dM Stars: Magnetic Activity, Light Variations, XUV Irradiances, and Planetary Habitable Zones","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #206","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1346 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1346 b was confirmed statistically, not by measuring its mass. Brightness curves at several colours, mostly in the infrared J band from the 200-inch Hale Telescope, were combined with TESS data and used to estimate the chance that the signal came from something other than a planet. That chance came out below 1.5% (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025). TOI-1346 c in the same system was validated at the same time (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1346b-Barrientosetal2025","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Validation of TESS Planet Candidates with Multicolor Transit Photometry and TRICERATOPS+","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4914 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets show unexpected sizes for their masses. Hot Jupiters look larger than models predict, while warm Jupiters look denser. It is not clear whether formation processes or the chemistry of the host star play a part (Mantovan et al. 2024).","observed":"TOI-4914 b is a warm Jupiter with a mass of 0.72 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.15 Jupiter radii. Its orbit is notably eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.41 ± 0.02 (Mantovan et al. 2024). It was confirmed with 40 HARPS spectra taken between April and September 2023, together with two other giant planets in separate systems (Mantovan et al. 2024).","system":"The host star is more metal-poor than most stars known to have giant planets, with an iron content of -0.13 (Mantovan et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether planet density follows the chemical makeup of the host star is not settled (Mantovan et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4914b-Mantovanetal2024","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Mantovan, T. G. Wilson, L. Borsato et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-4914, TOI-2714 and TOI-2981 RV curves (Mantovan+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 152843 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 152843 c is a superpuff Neptune. It has a mass of about 9 Earth masses but a radius of 5.94 Earth radii, which puts its density in the lowest fifth percentile of known planets (Nicholson et al. 2024). Of all the low-density planets, it has the longest orbital period (Nicholson et al. 2024). Its size falls in the 'Saturn valley', an observed shortage of planets larger than Neptune but smaller than Saturn (Nicholson et al. 2024). The brightness of the star and the low density of the planet make it a key target for atmospheric study (Nicholson et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet, HD 152843 b, has nearly the same mass but a radius of only 3.05 ± 0.11 Earth radii (Nicholson et al. 2024). The two planets orbit near a 5:3 resonance, which could produce shifts in transit times (Nicholson et al. 2024).","history":"The near-resonance hints that the system may once have interacted with a third planet (Nicholson et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd152843c-Nicholsonetal2024","cite":"Nicholson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"B. A. Nicholson, S. Aigrain, N. L. Eisner et al.","title":"HD152843 b & c: the masses and orbital periods of a sub-Neptune and a superpuff Neptune","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15068","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 101581 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 101581 b and its neighbour HD 101581 c are two Earth-size planets that were validated statistically using TESS data and ground-based follow-up (Kunimoto et al. 2025). A third, probable planet signal, TOI-6276.03, has an orbit of 7.87 days (Kunimoto et al. 2025). At V = 7.77, HD 101581 is the brightest star known to host multiple transiting planets smaller than 1.5 Earth radii (Kunimoto et al. 2025).","system":"The planets are remarkably uniform in size and evenly spaced, a 'peas-in-a-pod' layout seen in other compact multiplanet systems (Kunimoto et al. 2025). Planets b and c orbit near a 4:3 resonance, where their periods are close to a ratio of 4 to 3 (Kunimoto et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The system is a promising target for atmospheric study and for comparing small planets (Kunimoto et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd101581b-Kunimotoetal2025","cite":"Kunimoto et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michelle Kunimoto, Zifan Lin, Sarah Millholland et al.","title":"Two Earth-size Planets and an Earth-size Candidate Transiting the nearby Star HD 101581","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08863","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-77 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"JWST measured the planet's dayside heat glow from 2.8 to 5.2 micrometres. It shows water and carbon monoxide, no carbon dioxide, a metal content below solar ([M/H] = -0.91) and a carbon-to-oxygen ratio of 0.36 (August et al. 2023). Ground-based high-resolution spectroscopy had already pointed to similar values (August et al. 2023). Its near-infrared brightness temperature is nearly identical to that of HD 149026 b, yet the two have clearly different compositions (August et al. 2023). In the TESS band, its occultation depth was updated to 94 ppm (Arora & Goyal 2024).","system":"The host star has a K-dwarf companion about 3 arcseconds away that is roughly 2 magnitudes fainter (Maxted et al. 2013). Rotation gives an age of about 1 billion years for the host, but comparison with stellar models gives about 8 billion years (Maxted et al. 2013). Estimated mass loss from the planet's atmosphere is among the strongest of nearby hot Jupiters, exceeding that of HD 209458 b, where an expanded atmosphere has been confirmed (Salz et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"The disagreement between the star's rotation age and its model age is unresolved (Maxted et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp77ab-AroraGoyal2024","cite":"Arora & Goyal 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Rahul Arora, Jayesh Goyal","title":"Constraining planetary albedo of JWST targets in the TESS bandpass, using TESS, HST, and Spitzer eclipse depth observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04804","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp77ab-Maxtedetal2013","cite":"Maxted et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-77 Ab: A Transiting Hot Jupiter Planet in a Wide Binary System","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6033","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp77ab-Salzetal2015","cite":"Salz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Salz, P. C. Schneider, S. Czesla et al.","title":"High-energy irradiation and mass loss rates of hot Jupiters in the solar neighborhood","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.00576","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-172 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Observing one transit with the ESPRESSO spectrograph revealed sodium (5.6 sigma) and hydrogen (19.5 sigma) in the planet's atmosphere, with a tentative detection of iron (4.1 sigma) (Seidel et al. 2023). All of these features are strongly blueshifted, and the shifts vary during the transit, which allows a first study of the planet's atmospheric winds (Seidel et al. 2023). The planet is bloated, with 0.5 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.6 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"The host star is a V = 11.0, F1-type star (Hellier et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp172b-Seideletal2023","cite":"Seidel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. V. Seidel, B. Prinoth, E. Knudstrup et al.","title":"Detection of atmospheric species and dynamics in the bloated hot Jupiter WASP-172 b with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.13622","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp172b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4127 b":{"text":{"why":"Found in a search for stars that show only one transit in a sector of TESS data (Gupta et al. 2023).","observed":"The orbit is very stretched. Its eccentricity is 0.75, so the planet swings far closer to its star than its average distance suggests (Gupta et al. 2023). Two later transits and radial velocity measurements with the NEID and SOPHIE spectrographs confirmed it as a planet (Gupta et al. 2023). Model calculations of how its atmosphere reacts to the sudden heating near closest approach predict only a faint, probably undetectable change in glow that rings on after the close pass (Vanrespaille et al. 2024).","system":"No outer companion has been found (Gupta et al. 2023).","history":"Closest approach to the star is too far out for tides to round off the orbit, so the planet is unlikely to become a hot Jupiter unless an unseen outer companion is exchanging angular momentum with it (Gupta et al. 2023).","open":"Whether an undetected outer companion exists is not known (Gupta et al. 2023). The planet's rotation period is also not known, and it controls how the atmosphere responds to the changing heat (Vanrespaille et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4127b-Guptaetal2023","cite":"Gupta et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Arvind F. Gupta, Jonathan M. Jackson, Guillaume Hébrard et al.","title":"A High-Eccentricity Warm Jupiter Orbiting TOI-4127","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.14570","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4127b-Vanrespailleetal2024","cite":"Vanrespaille et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Vanrespaille, R. Baeyens, A. Schneider et al.","title":"Examining the detectability of ringing on highly eccentric exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.01026","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 23472 c":{"text":{"why":"Comparing planets of one star helps test ideas about how planets form, because all of them grew from the same disc of gas and dust (Barros et al. 2022).","observed":"Radial velocity measurements with the ESPRESSO spectrograph were used to confirm three more planets in the system and to measure masses for all five (Barros et al. 2022). Planet c is the outermost of the five, with an orbit of about 29.8 days and a size of about 1.85 Earth radii (Barros et al. 2022).","system":"Five planets orbit in a compact set, with periods from about 3.98 to 29.80 days (Barros et al. 2022). Three are Earth-sized or smaller, three are described as super-Earths, and two are potential super-Mercuries, meaning planets that may be unusually rich in iron (Barros et al. 2022). Planet e is close to a resonance with planet d, and its small size and the closeness of its transits to those of planet d meant it was found only recently (Barros et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd23472c-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"HD 23472: a multi-planetary system with three super-Earths and two potential super-Mercuries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-17 b":{"text":{"why":"Its relatively low temperature, between 780 and 927 K, makes it an attractive target for studies of its atmosphere (Howard et al. 2012).","observed":"Eclipse measurements with the Spitzer telescope confirmed and refined the eccentric orbit (Wallack et al. 2019). Heat-flow models that match the eclipse depths of the planets in that study suggest efficient circulation of energy to the night side (Wallack et al. 2019). The eccentric orbit may carry the atmosphere across the temperatures where sodium and potassium turn from gas to cloud, which led to a Hubble transmission spectrum campaign (Huitson 2012). Its angle between orbit and stellar spin was measured with HARPS-N (Mancini et al. 2022). A search for escaping helium gave the first limits on a helium signal for this planet (Orell-Miquel et al. 2026).","system":"A second planet, HAT-P-17 c, circles the star every 4.4 years on a nearly circular orbit, with a minimum mass of 1.31 Jupiter masses (Howard et al. 2012). It has not been seen to transit, and its tilt is not known (Howard et al. 2012). The inner planet has an eccentricity of 0.342 (Howard et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp17b-Howardetal2012","cite":"Howard et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"A. W. Howard, G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-17b,c: A Transiting, Eccentric, Hot Saturn and a Long-period, Cold Jupiter","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3898","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp17b-Wallacketal2019","cite":"Wallack et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"Investigating Trends in Atmospheric Compositions of Cool Gas Giant Planets Using Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.00014","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp17b-Huitson2012","cite":"Huitson 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Catherine Huitson","title":"The First Transmission Spectrum of an Eccentric Cool Jupiter","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp17b-Mancinietal2022","cite":"Mancini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXXVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and revising the physical and orbital parameters of the HAT-P-15, HAT-P-17, HAT-P-21, HAT-P-26, HAT-P-29 eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10549","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp17b-OrellMiqueletal2026","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jaume Orell-Miquel, Kyra Sampson, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"HET/HPF Observations of Helium in Warm, Hot, and Ultrahot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-818 b":{"text":{"why":"Undergraduate students at Austin College, searching Kepler data through the citizen-science site Planet Hunters in spring 2012, reported possible transits on this star before it was listed as a planet candidate (Baker et al. 2017).","observed":"The students looked at light curves of over 5000 stars in a research course (Baker et al. 2017). The star they flagged, Kepler 4282872, was promoted to planetary candidate KOI-1325 in 2012 and confirmed as host of Kepler-818 b in 2016 (Baker et al. 2017). Little is known about the planet beyond what its transits show.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler818b-Bakeretal2017","cite":"Baker et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"David Baker, Graham Crannell, James Duncan et al.","title":"Planet Hunters, Undergraduate Research, and Detection of Extrasolar Planet Kepler-818 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #229","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5218 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to the Searching for GEMS survey, which targets giant planets around cool dwarf stars. Such planets are rare because their host stars have low mass (Kanodia et al. 2024).","observed":"Radial velocity measurements from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder on the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and from MAROON-X on Gemini-North gave only an upper limit on the mass, not a precise value (Kanodia et al. 2024). The planet was statistically validated using TESS photometry, ground-based photometry and high-contrast imaging (Kanodia et al. 2024). Its host is a late K dwarf rather than an M dwarf (Clark 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's mass is known only as an upper limit (Kanodia et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5218b-Kanodiaetal2024","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Arvind F. Gupta, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Characterizing Six Giant Planets Around Cool Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14694","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5218b-Clark2024","cite":"Clark 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Catherine Clark","title":"Big Planets around Small Stars: Masses and Upper Limits for Six Giant Planets","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.RHF4LF","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7510 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up with ASTEP, CHEOPS and EulerCam showed that one transit had been misattributed, and that the star hosts three transiting giant planets (Almenara et al. 2025). Photodynamical modelling, which fits the transits and the changes in their timing together, gives this outermost planet a mass of about 0.60 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.94 Jupiter radii (Almenara et al. 2025).","system":"The three giants have periods of 11.5, 22.6 and 48.9 days and radii of 0.65, 0.96 and 0.94 Jupiter radii, making them the largest known trio of transiting planets (Almenara et al. 2025). The orbits lie near a 4:2:1 resonant chain, which causes large shifts in transit times for all three planets (Almenara et al. 2025). Low eccentricities and small tilts between the orbits are favoured (Almenara et al. 2025).","history":"The compact layout makes the system a good test of how tightly packed giant planets form (Almenara et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7510d-Almenaraetal2025","cite":"Almenara et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. M. Almenara, A. Leleu, T. Guillot et al.","title":"TOI-7510: A solar-analog system of three transiting giant planets near a Laplace resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.20074","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-2016-BLG-0005L b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first bound planet found by this technique in space-based data (Specht et al. 2023).","observed":"The Kepler K2 spacecraft observed the event closely enough to resolve the moments when the lens crossed into and out of a caustic, a sharp bright edge in the magnification pattern (Specht et al. 2023). Simultaneous ground-based surveys showed the parallax effect, which allowed the planet's mass to be measured directly (Specht et al. 2023). The planet has about 1.1 Jupiter masses and orbits a star of 0.58 solar masses (Specht et al. 2023). The system lies 5.2 kiloparsecs away towards the Galactic bulge, more than twice as far as the previous most distant planet found by Kepler (Specht et al. 2023).","system":"The planet is about 4.2 au from its star as seen on the sky, which for a circular orbit means a true distance near 4.4 au and a period of roughly 13 years (Specht et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k22016blg0005lb-Spechtetal2023","cite":"Specht et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Specht, R. Poleski, M. T. Penny et al.","title":"Kepler K2 Campaign 9 - II. First space-based discovery of an exoplanet using microlensing","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16959","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-26 b":{"text":{"why":"It was announced together with a second planet, NGTS-27 b, and both orbit stars that heat them strongly (Bouchy et al. 2024).","observed":"Its radius of 1.33 Jupiter radii is much larger than expected for a planet of only 0.29 Jupiter masses, which makes it a Saturn-mass planet with a highly inflated radius (Bouchy et al. 2024). It is one of the largest known objects among planets of similar mass (Bouchy et al. 2024). The host is a G6 main-sequence star (Bouchy et al. 2024). The masses were measured with the CORALIE, FEROS and HARPS spectrographs, and the transits were re-observed from the SAAO 1.0-m and Euler 1.2-m telescopes and by TESS (Bouchy et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Why the planet is so puffed up is not settled; strong irradiation from its star is the likely cause (Bouchy et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts26b-Bouchyetal2024","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Bouchy, E. Gillen, O. Turner et al.","title":"NGTS discovery of a highly inflated Saturn-mass planet and a highly irradiated hot Jupiter. NGTS-26 b and NGTS-27 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202347162","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 367 b":{"text":{"why":"Its high density makes it a test case for how iron-rich, Mercury-like planets form (Brady & Seligman 2026).","observed":"Its dayside temperature is estimated at 1500 to 1800 K, which suggests a permanent magma ocean or magma pond on the surface (Golabek et al. 2024). The measured size and mass imply a density of 6200 to 8500 kilograms per cubic metre, a Mercury-like interior with a thin mantle over a large core (Golabek et al. 2024). Observations of several short-period rocky planets, including this one, suggest that they do not hold substantial atmospheres, which would leave the surface open to direct study (Spaargaren et al. 2025). Simple models used to claim bare rock may not suit planets like this, and its heat redistribution is still debated (Wirth et al. 2026).","system":"The planet circles a small red dwarf star in about 0.32 days (Wirth et al. 2026).","history":"Simulations of high-speed collisions that strip a planet's mantle suggest that dense, iron-rich planets are more likely at low masses and short orbital periods, and GJ 367 b fits that pattern (Brady & Seligman 2026).","open":"Estimates of the age of the star range from 30 million to about 8 billion years, which makes the planet's thermal history uncertain (Golabek et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj367b-BradySeligman2026","cite":"Brady & Seligman 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Madison Brady, Darryl Z. Seligman","title":"The maximum density of a collisionally produced planet is a function of its mass and orbital period","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18336","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367b-Golabeketal2024","cite":"Golabek et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gregor J. Golabek, Tim Lichtenberg, Lotte M. Bartels et al.","title":"Magma oceanography of the dense, ultrashort-period sub-Earth GJ 367 b","journal":"European Geosciences Union General Assembly 2024 (EGU24)","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu24-5832","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367b-Spaargarenetal2025","cite":"Spaargaren et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Rob Spaargaren, Antonio Manjon Cabeza Cordoba, Maxim Ballmer et al.","title":"Modelling surface mineral diversity of atmosphere-free rocky exoplanets for spectroscopic characterisation","journal":"EGU General Assembly Conference Abstracts","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu25-18054","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367b-Wirthetal2026","cite":"Wirth et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Christopher P. Wirth, Diana Powell, Robin Wordsworth","title":"Analytic Modeling of Tidally Locked Rocky Planet Atmospheres across Dynamical Regimes","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12556","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-167 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is a rapidly rotating F1 star, and the planet was announced jointly by two survey teams (Temple et al. 2017).","observed":"The orbit is retrograde: the planet circles the star in the opposite direction to the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of -165 degrees (Temple et al. 2017). Doppler tomography, which follows the planet's shadow across the star's spinning surface, confirmed it as a planet, and only an upper limit of 8 Jupiter masses was set on its mass (Temple et al. 2017). Later work on the atmosphere gave a tentative detection of hydrogen-alpha at 4.6 sigma and of iron at about 3.4 sigma, and a stronger iron signal at 7.3 sigma that is blueshifted (Jiang et al. 2026).","system":"The star rotates faster than the planet orbits, which is rare, and it shows pulsations that make it a delta-Scuti or gamma-Doradus type variable (Temple et al. 2017).","history":"Another hot-Jupiter host, WASP-33, also pulsates, which hints that close-in planets might excite pulsations in their stars (Temple et al. 2017). Hotter stars are more likely to host misaligned orbits (Temple et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp167b-Templeetal2017","cite":"Temple et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"L. Y. Temple, C. Hellier, M. D. Albrow et al.","title":"WASP-167b/KELT-13b: joint discovery of a hot Jupiter transiting a rapidly rotating F1V star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.07771","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp167b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Zewen Jiang, Wei Wang, Shumei Jia et al.","title":"High-resolution Spectroscopic Atmospheric Studies of Five Hot Jupiters Across the Edge of the Neptune Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21273","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-108 b":{"text":{"why":"It was one of four sub-Saturns, planets between Neptune and Saturn in size, measured with the Keck/HIRES spectrograph to study how much of each planet is heavy elements versus gas (Petigura et al. 2017).","observed":"The four planets have similar sizes of 4.5 to 5.5 Earth radii but masses from 16 to 60 Earth masses, so their cores and envelopes must differ (Petigura et al. 2017). K2-108 b is the most massive of the four, which implies a large reservoir of heavy elements of about 50 Earth masses (Petigura et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"Across sub-Saturns, planet mass correlates strongly with the metal content of the host star, and there is little link between mass and size (Petigura et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2108b-Petiguraetal2017","cite":"Petigura et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, Evan Sinukoff, Eric D. Lopez et al.","title":"Four Sub-Saturns with Dissimilar Densities: Windows into Planetary Cores and Envelopes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00013","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"11 Com b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first result from a joint China-Japan search for companions around intermediate-mass giant stars, and it emerged from 300 targets at Okayama Astrophysical Observatory (Liu et al. 2008).","observed":"The host star is a G8 giant. The discovery measurements gave the companion a minimum mass of 19.4 Jupiter masses, well above the deuterium-burning limit, so it is counted as a brown dwarf candidate (Liu et al. 2008). Its orbit is 1.29 au wide with an eccentricity of 0.231 (Liu et al. 2008). Little is known about the companion itself beyond its orbit.","system":"New Okayama data show extra variation in the star's motion, which may point to another companion (Teng et al. 2023). Short-term oscillations of the star itself, with amplitudes of 1 to 7 metres per second, were also detected (Ando et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether a second companion exists is not known (Teng et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-11comb-Liuetal2008","cite":"Liu et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Y.-J. Liu, Bun'ei Sato, G. Zhao et al.","title":"A Substellar Companion to the Intermediate-Mass Giant 11 Comae","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.0645","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-11comb-Tengetal2023","cite":"Teng et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"Revisiting planetary systems in the Okayama Planet Search Program: A new long-period planet, RV astrometry joint analysis, and a multiplicity-metallicity trend around evolved stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05343","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-11comb-Andoetal2010","cite":"Ando et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Hiroyasu Ando, Yusuke Tsuboi, Eiji Kambe et al.","title":"Oscillations in G-Type Giants","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.1286","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-7 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet has very low density, only 0.17 grams per cubic centimetre, among the lowest known when it was found (Latham et al. 2010).","observed":"Its mass is under half that of Jupiter, while its radius is 50 percent larger (Latham et al. 2010). Visible and infrared observations found a relatively high geometric albedo of 0.35, and no heat glow from the planet in two Spitzer bands (Demory et al. 2013). The brightest part of the optical phase curve is shifted westward, which was read as high-altitude reflective clouds west of the point directly under the star, possibly made of silicates (Demory et al. 2013). It gave the first map of clouds in an exoplanet atmosphere (Demory et al. 2013). A separate phase-curve study also found the brightness peak shifted to the morning side (Esteves et al. 2015). A search for lopsided clouds in the transit shape found no convincing signal (von Paris et al. 2016).","system":"The host star is more massive and much larger than the Sun, with 1.35 solar masses and 1.84 solar radii, and is near the end of its main-sequence life (Latham et al. 2010). A statistical study suggests the star's spin may be tilted from the planet's orbit along the line of sight (Schlaufman 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler7b-Lathametal2010","cite":"Latham et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"David W. Latham, William J. Borucki, David G. Koch et al.","title":"Kepler-7b: A Transiting Planet with Unusually Low Density","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0190","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler7b-Demoryetal2013","cite":"Demory et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Brice-Olivier Demory, Julien de Wit, Nikole Lewis et al.","title":"Inference of Inhomogeneous Clouds in an Exoplanet Atmosphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7894","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler7b-Estevesetal2015","cite":"Esteves et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Lisa J. Esteves, Ernst J. W. De Mooij, Ray Jayawardhana","title":"Changing Phases of Alien Worlds: Probing Atmospheres of Kepler Planets with High-precision Photometry","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.2245","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler7b-vonParisetal2016","cite":"von Paris et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. von Paris, P. Gratier, P. Bordé et al.","title":"Inferring asymmetric limb cloudiness on exoplanets from transit light curves","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04362","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler7b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-7 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits in a rare class of hot, large worlds, which makes it a good target for studying atmospheres through transmission spectroscopy (Alsubai et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet lies less than five stellar radii from its host and is heated to an equilibrium temperature of about 2100 K (Alsubai et al. 2019). Its radius of 1.70 Jupiter radii and its temperature put it in the top 6 percent of the hottest and largest known exoplanets (Alsubai et al. 2019). Its mass is 1.88 Jupiter masses (Alsubai et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a relatively massive, metal-rich main-sequence F star, with an estimated age of about 1 billion years (Alsubai et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar7b-Alsubaietal2019","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Khalid Alsubai, Zlatan I. Tsvetanov, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Qatar Exoplanet Survey: Qatar-7b—A Very Hot Jupiter Orbiting a Metal-rich F-Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.05601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-674 b":{"text":{"why":"It sits in the Neptune desert, a gap in the number of Neptune-size planets on short orbits, so it is a test case for how such planets avoid losing their atmospheres (Brande et al. 2022).","observed":"Hubble near-infrared transmission spectroscopy found moderate evidence for water vapour at 2.9 sigma and weak evidence for clouds at 2.2 sigma (Brande et al. 2022). It is a low-density super-Neptune orbiting a nearby M dwarf (Murgas et al. 2021). A JWST program in Cycle 4 was set up to observe it in both transmission and emission as a benchmark for warm Neptunes, at an expected temperature of about 640 K (Piaulet Ghorayeb et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Planets in the desert are thought to have complex histories, shaped by atmospheric loss to the star's radiation or by migration (Brande et al. 2022).","open":"The amount of water is poorly constrained by the data so far (Brande et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi674b-Brandeetal2022","cite":"Brande et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jonathan Brande, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Laura Kreidberg et al.","title":"A Mirage or an Oasis? Water Vapor in the Atmosphere of the Warm Neptune TOI-674 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.04197","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi674b-Murgasetal2021","cite":"Murgas et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"F. Murgas, N. Astudillo-Defru, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"TOI-674b: An oasis in the desert of exo-Neptunes transiting a nearby M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.01246","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi674b-Ghorayebetal2025","cite":"Ghorayeb et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Caroline Piaulet Ghorayeb, Romain Allart, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"Combining Emission and Transmission Spectroscopy to reveal Exo-Neptune Aerosols, Chemistry, and Formation","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-6 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a somewhat larger radius than theory predicts for its mass (Noyes et al. 2008). Spitzer eclipse measurements at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres can be fitted by models with a small or no temperature inversion, a layer where the upper atmosphere gets hotter with height (Todorov et al. 2012). The orbit is circular and the host is a hot late F star (Noyes et al. 2008).","system":"Transit timing studies show no sign of departures from a steady period (Szabo et al. 2010). No small companions in resonant orbits were found, and moderate-mass ones close enough to disturb the timing by 1.5 minutes were ruled out (Dittmann et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp6b-Noyesetal2008","cite":"Noyes et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"R. W. Noyes, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-6b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a Bright F Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.2894","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp6b-Todorovetal2012","cite":"Todorov et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Kamen O. Todorov, Drake Deming, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Observations of Three Hot Exoplanets: XO-4b, HAT-P-6b, and HAT-P-8b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5858","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp6b-Szaboetal2010","cite":"Szabo et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Gy. M. Szabo, O. Haja, K. Szatmary et al.","title":"Limits on Transit Timing Variations in HAT-P-6 and WASP-1","journal":"Information Bulletin on Variable Stars","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.3059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp6b-Dittmannetal2011","cite":"Dittmann et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jason Dittmann, L. Close, L. Scuderi","title":"On the Frequency of Additional Planets in Short Period Hot Jupiter Systems from Transit Timing Variations","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #218","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5720 b":{"text":{"why":"One of the main goals of the TESS mission is to find Earth-like planets around nearby M dwarfs, and this is one of three Earth-sized planets announced together (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is Earth-sized, with a radius of 1.09 Earth radii (Barkaoui et al. 2024). It orbits a nearby M2.5 star about 36 parsecs away every 1.43 days (Barkaoui et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is 708 K, assuming no reflected light, and it receives about 41.7 times the sunlight Earth does (Barkaoui et al. 2024). It was confirmed with ground-based multicolour light curves, spectra, archival images, high-resolution imaging and radial velocity data from Subaru/IRD (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","system":"The same survey announced two sister planets, TOI-6008 b and TOI-6086 b, around other M dwarfs (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5720b-Barkaouietal2024","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. Barkaoui, R. P. Schwarz, N. Narita et al.","title":"Three short-period Earth-sized planets around M dwarfs discovered by TESS: TOI-5720 b, TOI-6008 b, and TOI-6086 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.06350","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 95338 b":{"text":{"why":"A single TESS transit matched the time that radial velocity measurements from Magellan/PFS predicted, confirming that both signals come from the same planet (Díaz et al. 2020).","observed":"It is one of the densest Neptune-size planets known, with a density near 3.98 grams per cubic centimetre (Díaz et al. 2020). Structure models suggest a mixture of ammonia, water and methane, with about 90 percent of its mass in heavy elements, about 38 Earth masses (Díaz et al. 2020). Later TESS and CHEOPS data sharpened the transit timing, with the period known to better than 5 seconds (Saha 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd95338b-Dazetal2020","cite":"Díaz et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Matías R. Díaz, James S. Jenkins, Fabo Feng et al.","title":"The Magellan/PFS Exoplanet Search: a 55-d period dense Neptune transiting the bright (V = 8.6) star HD 95338","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10319","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95338b-Saha2025","cite":"Saha 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Suman Saha","title":"Updating the ephemeris and physical properties of five long-period transiting exoplanets using TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01768","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-35 b":{"text":{"why":"Kepler-35 b is one of the first three planets known to orbit two stars at once, a result taken as direct evidence that planets can form around binaries (Meschiari 2012).","observed":"The two stars' orbit was pinned down with spectra from the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and the 2.7 m Harlan J. Smith Telescope at McDonald Observatory (Welsh et al. 2012). A later reanalysis gave improved orbital parameters, adding the slow turning of the stellar orbit caused by relativity and tides (Thomadis 2016).","system":"The planet circles both stars of an eclipsing pair, and a second planet is known in the system. Stability maps confirm that the orbit of the known transiting circumbinary planets sits in a stable region (Hinse et al. 2012).","history":"Simulations of the similar system Kepler-16 suggest that planet growth is hard to start near binaries over a wide range of distances. Planetary cores may have migrated in from beyond 4 au or formed close to where they now orbit (Meschiari 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler35b-Meschiari2012","cite":"Meschiari 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Stefano Meschiari","title":"Circumbinary Planet Formation in the Kepler-16 System. I. N-body Simulations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1314","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler35b-Welshetal2012","cite":"Welsh et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"W. F. Welsh, J. A. Orosz, J. A. Carter et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Radial velocities of Kepler-34b & Kepler-35b (Welsh+, 2012)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler35b-Thomadis2016","cite":"Thomadis 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Pantelis Chrisovalantis Thomadis","title":"Improved dynamical parameters for transiting circumbinary planets","journal":"Masters Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler35b-Hinseetal2012","cite":"Hinse et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"T. C. Hinse, N. Haghighipour, K. Gozdziewski et al.","title":"Prospects on the detection of additional circumbinary extrasolar planets","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress 2012","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-232 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of only four well-characterized warm Jupiters whose host stars are brighter than V = 10 (Brahm et al. 2018).","observed":"The orbit is eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.258, and the planet has a fairly low equilibrium temperature of 1030 K because of its relatively wide separation from the star (Brahm et al. 2018). Its heavy-element content is estimated at 20 Earth masses, consistent with standard models of giant planet formation (Brahm et al. 2018). The bright late F-type host makes it a good target for follow-up on atmospheres and orbital evolution (Brahm et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2232b-Brahmetal2018","cite":"Brahm et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Brahm, N. Espinoza, A. Jordán et al.","title":"K2-232 b: a transiting warm Saturn on an eccentric P = 11.2 d orbit around a V = 9.9 star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-21 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star has the highest projected rotation speed of any star known to host a transiting hot Jupiter, 146 kilometres per second (Johnson et al. 2018). Doppler tomography confirmed that the planet transits that star, and showed that the orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of -5.6 degrees (Johnson et al. 2018). Only an upper limit of 3.91 Jupiter masses was set on its mass (Johnson et al. 2018). High-resolution transmission spectra from HARPS and HARPS-N found no absorption features (Stangret et al. 2022). Near-infrared spectra found no molecular signals either (Meni-Gallardo et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a metal-poor late A star in a likely hierarchical triple system (Johnson et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"A tentative estimate puts the star's tilt towards us at about 60 degrees (Garai et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt21b-Johnsonetal2018","cite":"Johnson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Marshall C. Johnson, Joseph E. Rodriguez, George Zhou et al.","title":"KELT-21b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting the Rapidly Rotating Metal-poor Late-A Primary of a Likely Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.03241","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt21b-Stangretetal2022","cite":"Stangret et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"M. Stangret, N. Casasayas-Barris, E. Pallé et al.","title":"High-resolution transmission spectroscopy study of ultra-hot Jupiters HAT-P-57b, KELT-17b, KELT-21b, KELT-7b, MASCARA-1b, and WASP-189b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.13064","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt21b-MeniGallardoetal2026","cite":"Meni-Gallardo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Meni-Gallardo, J. Orell-Miquel, G. Fernández-Rodríguez et al.","title":"Cross-correlation transmission spectroscopy of ultra-hot Jupiters WASP-189b, HAT-P-57b, KELT-17b, and KELT-21b with GIANO-B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27221","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt21b-Garaietal2022","cite":"Garai et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Z. Garai, T. Pribulla, J. Kovács et al.","title":"Rapidly rotating stars and their transiting planets: KELT-17b, KELT-19Ab, and KELT-21b in the CHEOPS and TESS era","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.09077","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 94890 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up studies planets around stars that are more massive than the Sun and have since swollen into giants (Fontanet et al. 2025).","observed":"The host is an evolved giant star of intermediate mass. Stars like this are hard to search for planets while they are still on the main sequence, so astronomers wait until they have aged and expanded. The survey behind this planet was started in 2006 and gathered more than ten years of precise velocity measurements for each target (Fontanet et al. 2025). Only about 200 planets were known around giant stars when the work was done (Fontanet et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd94890b-Fontanetetal2025","cite":"Fontanet et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Fontanet, S. Udry, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES): IV. New planetary systems around HD 87816, HD 94890, and HD 102888 and an update on HD 121056","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-39 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has an inflated radius of 1.57 Jupiter radii but a mass of only 0.63 Jupiter masses, so it is very puffy (Bento et al. 2018). Its host is an F-type star, one of several found by the same survey with planets on 2 to 5 day orbits (Bento et al. 2018). Its large size and low density make it a good target for studies of starlight filtering through its atmosphere (Bento et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The cause of the puffiness is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats39b-Bentoetal2018","cite":"Bento et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Bento, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-39b, HATS-40b, HATS-41b, and HATS-42b: three inflated hot Jupiters and a super-Jupiter transiting F stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01623","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2267 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a pair of very cool red dwarf stars, an M5 and an M6, only 0.384 arcseconds apart, which is about 8 au in projected distance (Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025). The data cannot yet show which of the two stars it circles (Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025). Its size depends on that: 1.00 Earth radii around the brighter star, or 1.22 around the fainter one (Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025). The result was checked with space and ground-based photometry plus high-resolution images (Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025).","system":"Two more Earth-sized planets are in the system. Planet c has a 3.49 day orbit (Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025). A third signal at 2.0 days was later confirmed as a planet using new transit observations from the Hale Telescope (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Which star each planet orbits is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2267b-ZigaFernndezetal2025","cite":"Zúñiga-Fernández et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Zúñiga-Fernández, F. J. Pozuelos, M. Dévora-Pajares et al.","title":"Two warm Earth-sized exoplanets and an Earth-sized candidate in the M5V-M6V binary system TOI-2267","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.14176","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2267b-GreklekMcKeonetal2026","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Validation of a Third Earth-sized Planet in the TOI-2267 Binary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.10007","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1080 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a quiet M4 dwarf star, so flares and spots are less of a problem than for many small red stars (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2026). The planet's equilibrium temperature is about 368 K, which makes it temperate (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2026). Its mass is not measured. Radial velocities from the NIRPS spectrograph only show that it weighs less than 10.7 Earth masses (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2026). Its size of 1.2 Earth radii points to a rocky world (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2026).","system":"Searches of the light curves rule out other transiting planets down to 0.9 Earth radii with periods between 0.5 and 7.7 days (Gómez Maqueo Chew et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The mass, and so the true composition, is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1080b-Chewetal2026","cite":"Chew et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Y. Gómez Maqueo Chew, G. Dransfield, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"TOI-1080 b: a temperate, rocky planet orbiting a quiet M4V host","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00385","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158259 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known concerns the whole system, which has six planets found with TESS and 290 velocity measurements of a bright star (Hara et al. 2020). Besides an inner transiting super-Earth, five planets have minimum masses of about 6 Earth masses, on orbits of 3.4, 5.2, 7.9, 12 and 17.4 days (Hara et al. 2020). The 7.9 day signal belongs to this planet. A later study improved the way instrument drifts are removed from the same spectrograph's data (Grouffal et al. 2024).","system":"The planets are packed close together, with orbit lengths near a 3:2 ratio from one planet to the next, a so-called resonance chain (Hara et al. 2020). The outermost signal, at 17.4 days, is still treated as a candidate because it is weaker and close to the star's expected spin period (Hara et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158259e-Haraetal2020","cite":"Hara et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. C. Hara, F. Bouchy, M. Stalport et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVI. HD 158259: A compact planetary system in a near-3:2 mean motion resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd158259e-Grouffaletal2024","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 158259 SOPHIE correction of RV systematics (Grouffal+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"1RXS J160929.1-210524 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known here comes from comparisons made in a study of another object. The infrared colours and brightness of the young star's companion CD-35 2722 B match those of this planet, which is described as an L4 object only 5 million years old and of planetary mass (Wahhaj et al. 2011). L-type objects are cool, dusty and red. The comparison helps show how such young, low-gravity objects look as they age (Wahhaj et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-1rxsj1609291210524b-Wahhajetal2011","cite":"Wahhaj et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Zahed Wahhaj, Michael C. Liu, Beth A. Biller et al.","title":"The Gemini NICI Planet-finding Campaign: Discovery of a Substellar L Dwarf Companion to the Nearby Young M Dwarf CD-35 2722","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.2893","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 251 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a candidate found in years of velocity measurements of a nearby red dwarf only 5.5 parsecs away (Robertson et al. 2026). The data come from the Keck HIRES instrument (78 measurements from 1997 to 2019) and the CARMENES instrument (265 measurements from 2016 to 2020), as well as new data from two other spectrographs (Beard et al. 2025; Robertson et al. 2026). Its mass is in the range expected for a rocky planet, and it orbits within the conservative habitable zone (Robertson et al. 2026). The team compared more than 50 models of the planets and of the star's activity (Robertson et al. 2026).","system":"The star also hosts a known planet, GJ 251 b, whose orbit was refined (Robertson et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"It is still a candidate. Thirty-metre class telescopes may be able to image it directly (Robertson et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj251c-Robertsonetal2026","cite":"Robertson et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Paul Robertson, Corey Beard, Vidya Venkatesan et al.","title":"Discovery of a Nearby Habitable Zone Super-Earth Candidate Amenable to Direct Imaging","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj251c-Beardetal2025","cite":"Beard et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"C. Beard, P. Robertson, J. Lubin et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 251 RVs & candidate exoplanet in habitable zone (Beard+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 168443 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Measurements of the star show two low-mass companions, one of which has a minimum mass of 16.9 Jupiter masses, close to the line between planets and brown dwarfs (Udry et al. 2002). The orbits of the companions in the study were fairly eccentric, with values from 0.22 to 0.43 (Udry et al. 2002). The host star is richer in heavy elements than the Sun (Udry et al. 2002). A separate study found that the habitable zone of this system is mostly chaotic, because of the large masses and eccentricities of the giants (Érdi et al. 2004).","system":"The star has two companions, and both are massive (Udry et al. 2002).","history":"Both companions may have formed together in the star's disc as very massive planets, or they may be brown dwarfs (Udry et al. 2002).","open":"Whether the companions are planets or brown dwarfs is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd168443c-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-700 e":{"text":{"why":"Several years of monitoring by the space telescope were needed to find a planet this small (Gilbert et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is Earth-sized, with a radius of 0.95 Earth radii, and likely rocky (Gilbert et al. 2023). It circles an inactive M2.5 dwarf star at the optimistic edge of the habitable zone, the wider range where liquid water might be possible (Gilbert et al. 2023). Later velocity work shows that its neighbour planet d has a mass of 2.4 Earth masses (Gilbert et al. 2025). Computer models suggest that planet e sits very close to the edge of the habitable zone for tidally locked planets (Nelson & Becker 2026).","system":"The system has four planets. Planet c is a puffy sub-Neptune with a hydrogen and helium atmosphere, and planet d is rocky (Gilbert et al. 2025). Gravity between the planets does not stop either habitable-zone planet from keeping habitable conditions (Nelson & Becker 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether planet e could be habitable is still not known (Nelson & Becker 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi700e-Gilbertetal2023","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Emily A. Gilbert, Andrew Vanderburg, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"A Second Earth-sized Planet in the Habitable Zone of the M Dwarf, TOI-700","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03617","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi700e-Gilbertetal2025","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Emily Gilbert, Jennifer Burt, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"The TOI-700 System at 5 Years: New Radial Velocity Mass Measurements and Updated Transit Fits","journal":"246th Meeting of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi700e-NelsonBecker2026","cite":"Nelson & Becker 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Coleman Nelson, Juliette Becker","title":"Dynamical Interactions and Habitability in the TOI-700 Multi-planet System","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00332","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-53 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was observed again in infrared light with the Spitzer telescope. Together with the earlier data, this improved its size estimate and helped confirm that the signal is a real planet (Livingston et al. 2019). The new timing shrinks the uncertainty in when its transits occur in mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes (Livingston et al. 2019). Its size puts it among the sub-Neptunes in the sample (Livingston et al. 2019). It is a promising target for velocity measurements of its mass with current spectrographs (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k253b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 9689 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a candidate super-Earth around a nearby red dwarf, found in 174 velocity measurements taken over seven years (Maldonado et al. 2021). The star is active, which makes small planets hard to pick out. A signal at 39.31 days was shown to come from the star's rotation (Maldonado et al. 2021). A second signal at 18.27 days has no link to any activity measure, so it is attributed to the planet (Maldonado et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet period is close to half the star's rotation period, so a stellar origin cannot be fully ruled out (Maldonado et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj9689b-Maldonadoetal2021","cite":"Maldonado et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. Maldonado, A. Petralia, M. Damasso et al.","title":"HADES RV programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XIV. A candidate super-Earth orbiting the M-dwarf GJ 9689 with a period close to half the stellar rotation period","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06155","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-29 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up found six new inflated hot Jupiters from three sites on three continents (Espinoza et al. 2016).","observed":"It is one of four typical inflated hot Jupiters in the study, which have radii of 1.17 to 1.26 Jupiter radii and orbit G-type stars every 3.2 to 4.6 days (Espinoza et al. 2016). Its mass lies in the 0.5 to 0.7 Jupiter mass range that is very common for hot Jupiters (Espinoza et al. 2016). It is a good target for studying its atmosphere with future observations (Espinoza et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats29b-Espinozaetal2016","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. Espinoza, D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-25b through HATS-30b: A Half-dozen New Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiters from the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-677 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter on an eccentric orbit, with e = 0.435 in its discovery and a refined 0.460 later (Jordán et al. 2020; Hu et al. 2024). Its orbit is almost exactly aligned with the star's equator: two measurements of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, where a transit distorts the star's spectrum, gave 0.3 and 3.2 degrees (Sedaghati et al. 2023; Hu et al. 2024). Alignment together with high eccentricity is not what existing models predict (Sedaghati et al. 2023). The planet belongs to a class of tidally detached giants, too far from their stars for tides to reshape the orbit (Hu et al. 2024).","system":"A long-term trend in the velocities hints at an outer companion (Jordán et al. 2020). A candidate outer brown dwarf was reported (Sedaghati et al. 2023). The system is described as dynamically hot (Hu et al. 2024).","history":"Forming a high eccentricity without tilting the orbit is a puzzle for giant planet formation models (Hu et al. 2024).","open":"How the orbit became eccentric while staying aligned is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi677b-Jordnetal2020","cite":"Jordán et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm, Néstor Espinoza et al.","title":"TOI-677b: A Warm Jupiter (P = 11.2 days) on an Eccentric Orbit Transiting a Late F-type Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05574","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi677b-Huetal2024","cite":"Hu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Qingru Hu, Malena Rice, Xian-Yu Wang et al.","title":"The PFS View of TOI-677 b: A Spin─Orbit Aligned Warm Jupiter in a Dynamically Hot System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07346","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi677b-Sedaghatietal2023","cite":"Sedaghati et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Elyar Sedaghati, Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"Orbital Alignment of the Eccentric Warm Jupiter TOI-677 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.07598","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 13189 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is among the few found around stars more massive than the Sun, which helps show how stellar mass affects planet formation (Hatzes et al. 2005).","observed":"The host is a K2 bright giant, a star far larger and more luminous than one on the main sequence (Hatzes et al. 2005). Velocity measurements show a 472 day variation with an amplitude of 173 metres per second (Hatzes et al. 2005). The star is quiet in its calcium activity indicator, and that indicator does not vary with the 472 day period, which supports a planet over star spots (Hatzes et al. 2005). Later work found solar-like pulsations in this star, which give a mass consistent with stellar models (Hatzes & Zechmeister 2008).","system":"It was one of only six planets or brown dwarfs then known around intermediate-mass stars, all with masses of several Jupiter masses (Hartmann et al. 2008).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd13189b-Hatzesetal2005","cite":"Hatzes et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"A. P. Hatzes, E. W. Guenther, M. Endl et al.","title":"A giant planet around the massive giant star HD 13189","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20052850","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd13189b-HatzesZechmeister2008","cite":"Hatzes & Zechmeister 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Artie P. Hatzes, Mathias Zechmeister","title":"Stellar oscillations in planet-hosting giant stars","journal":"Journal of Physics Conference Series","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/118/1/012016","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd13189b-Hartmannetal2008","cite":"Hartmann et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"M. Hartmann, A. P. Hatzes, E. W. Guenther et al.","title":"A Survey for Extrasolar Planets Around A F Type Stars","journal":"Precision Spectroscopy in Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-75485-5_70","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1229 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was picked out by a habitability index called SEPHI, which scores planets using seven properties, including mass, radius, orbit and star type (Rodríguez-Mozos & Moya 2017). It ranked among the top three of all planets tested, with Kepler-186 f and Kepler-442 b (Rodríguez-Mozos & Moya 2017). It was the most unexpected of those, because no earlier study had pointed to it as habitable (Rodríguez-Mozos & Moya 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The ranking depends on assumed physical descriptions rather than measurements of the surface."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1229b-RodrguezMozosMoya2017","cite":"Rodríguez-Mozos & Moya 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. M. Rodríguez-Mozos, A. Moya","title":"Statistical-likelihood Exo-Planetary Habitability Index (SEPHI)","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.07986","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 433 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Observations of GJ 433 suggest that the star has a close companion, first hinted at by a wobble in Hipparcos data and then by speckle imaging (Dieterich et al. 2009). The companion's mass is estimated at between 0.088 and 0.122 solar masses, near the limit for hydrogen burning (Dieterich et al. 2009). Its orbit is about 1.5 years long (Dieterich et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the companion is a very low-mass star or a brown dwarf is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj433c-Dieterichetal2009","cite":"Dieterich et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Sergio Dieterich, Todd Henry, David Golimowski","title":"GJ 433: Resolving a Suspected Binary Near the Stellar/Substellar Boundary","journal":"NOAO Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DH Tau b":{"text":{"why":"The companion is young and still gathering material, as is its host star (Hejazi et al. 2025).","observed":"The companion lies 2.3 arcseconds from its star, about 330 au, and shares its motion across the sky (Itoh et al. 2005). Its spectrum shows deep water bands, and its temperature is 2700 to 2800 K (Itoh et al. 2005). The mass was first put at 30 to 50 Jupiter masses, in the brown dwarf range (Itoh et al. 2005). Its spectral type is about M9.5, and young companions like it resemble isolated young brown dwarfs (Bonnefoy et al. 2014). Carbon-to-oxygen ratios of star and companion are both close to the solar value, at 0.555 for the star (Hejazi et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a young T Tauri star with a spin period of 7.2 days (Vrba et al. 1989). Its disc was used to weigh it at 0.101 solar masses, though dust in front of it may affect this (Sheehan et al. 2019).","history":"The matching chemistry suggests the companion formed by fast gravitational collapse, like a small star, rather than by slow core accretion (Hejazi et al. 2025).","open":"Whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dhtaub-Hejazietal2025","cite":"Hejazi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Neda Hejazi, Jerry W. Xuan, David R. Coria et al.","title":"Chemical Links between a Young M-type T Tauri Star and Its Substellar Companion: Spectral Analysis and C/O Measurement of DH Tau A","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15591","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dhtaub-Itohetal2005","cite":"Itoh et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Yoichi Itoh, Masahiko Hayashi, Motohide Tamura et al.","title":"A Young Brown Dwarf Companion to DH Tauri","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0411177","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dhtaub-Bonnefoyetal2014","cite":"Bonnefoy et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Bonnefoy, G. Chauvin, A.-M. Lagrange et al.","title":"A library of near-infrared integral field spectra of young M-L dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dhtaub-Vrbaetal1989","cite":"Vrba et al. 1989","year":"1989","authors":"F. J. Vrba, A. E. Rydgren, P. F. Chugainov et al.","title":"Additional UBVRI Photometric Searches for Periodic Light Variability from T Tauri Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/114998","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dhtaub-Sheehanetal2019","cite":"Sheehan et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Patrick D. Sheehan, Ya-Lin Wu, Josh A. Eisner et al.","title":"High-precision Dynamical Masses of Pre-main-sequence Stars with ALMA and Gaia","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.00032","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-21 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is massive, at about 4 Jupiter masses, and travels on an eccentric orbit with e = 0.228 (Bakos et al. 2010). A later study measured how the orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, for this and four other eccentric systems (Mancini et al. 2022). The star's spin period from its light curve was reported for the first time in another study, which used it to compare gyrochronological and isochronal age estimates (Maxted et al. 2015). Such comparisons test whether tides between planet and star change the star's apparent age (Maxted et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp21b-Bakosetal2010","cite":"Bakos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-20b-HAT-P-23b: Four Massive Transiting Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3388","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp21b-Mancinietal2022","cite":"Mancini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXXVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and revising the physical and orbital parameters of the HAT-P-15, HAT-P-17, HAT-P-21, HAT-P-26, HAT-P-29 eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10549","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp21b-Maxtedetal2015","cite":"Maxted et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, A. M. Serenelli, J. Southworth","title":"Comparison of gyrochronological and isochronal age estimates for transiting exoplanet host stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.09111","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5634 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the Searching for GEMS survey, which targets giant planets around small cool stars, a rare combination (Kanodia et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a giant orbiting an M dwarf. It was checked with radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder, along with photometry and high-contrast imaging from several ground-based telescopes (Kanodia et al. 2024). Its transit signal was statistically validated as a real planet (Kanodia et al. 2024). The velocity data only give an upper limit on its mass, not a measurement (Kanodia et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its true mass is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5634ab-Kanodiaetal2024","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Arvind F. Gupta, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Characterizing Six Giant Planets Around Cool Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2015-BLG-0954L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first reported by the KMTNet and OGLE teams, but those data missed the moment the lensing signal ended (the caustic exit). Data from the MOA team covered that moment and gave a more reliable measure of the source star's size (Bennett et al. 2017). They also gave a new source colour. This changes how fast the lens and the background star move apart on the sky, from 18.4 to 11.8 milliarcseconds per year (Bennett et al. 2017). The new value is at least 100 times more likely a priori and no longer forces the lens system to be closer than 1 kiloparsec (Bennett et al. 2017). The planet's mass, its distance from its star and the distance of the system were therefore revised (Bennett et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2015blg0954lb-Bennettetal2017","cite":"Bennett et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Bennett, I. Bond, F. Abe et al.","title":"MOA Data Reveal a New Mass, Distance, and Relative Proper Motion for Planetary System OGLE-2015-BLG-0954L","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.03937","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2133 b":{"text":{"why":"It belongs to a survey of K dwarfs, because known sub-Neptunes are biased toward M-dwarf hosts, which are easier to observe (Morello et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed as real, without a mass measurement, by combining space-telescope brightness data, spectra and high-resolution images of its star (Morello et al. 2026). It was one of four TESS candidates around K-dwarf stars that were validated together (Morello et al. 2026). TOI-2133 b lies near the upper edge of the radius valley, the size range where planets are scarce between rocky worlds and gas-rich ones (Morello et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planets are suitable for precise mass measurements and atmosphere studies with current facilities, and are meant as test cases for formation models around K dwarfs (Morello et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2133b-Morelloetal2026","cite":"Morello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Morello, A. Peláez-Torres, F. J. Pozuelos et al.","title":"The K-dwarfs survey ─ I. Four validated planets from the radius valley to the Neptune desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag183","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-199 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Masses for the K2-199 planets were refined by adding 12 Keck-HIRES radial velocity measurements to the 33 already available, and by modelling two sets of K2 brightness data (Akana Murphy et al. 2021). The study treats the planet as a data point in the mass-radius plane for sub-Neptunes, a class that TESS keeps showing to be diverse (Akana Murphy et al. 2021). The inner planet K2-199 b is likely rocky (Akana Murphy et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a K5V dwarf with two confirmed planets, with periods of 3.2 days (b) and 7.4 days (c) (Akana Murphy et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2199c-Murphyetal2021","cite":"Murphy et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Molly R. Kosiarek, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"Another Superdense Sub-Neptune in K2-182 b and Refined Mass Measurements for K2-199 b and c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.05502","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-38 b":{"text":{"why":"It lies in the Neptune desert, a range of orbits and masses where few planets are found, and is among the lowest-mass planets found by ground-based wide-field surveys (Jordán et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet's path across its star was followed with the ESPRESSO spectrograph on the VLT, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, which shows how the planet's orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024). The result is about -108 degrees, so the orbit is close to polar (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024). The planet is a low-density hot Neptune (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024). Its star appears old, with an age near 11.9 billion years (Jordán et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"It joins a growing group of hot Neptunes on polar orbits (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats38b-Jordnetal2020","cite":"Jordán et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. Jordán, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-37Ab and HATS-38b: Two Transiting Hot Neptunes in the Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07135","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats38b-EspinozaRetamaletal2024","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Guđmundur Stefánsson, Cristobal Petrovich et al.","title":"HATS-38 b and WASP-139 b Join a Growing Group of Hot Neptunes on Polar Orbits","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18631","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-74 A b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to test core-accretion theory, which predicts that giant planets are rarer around low-mass stars than around Sun-like stars (Jordán et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is a gas giant that transits a late K or early M dwarf (Jordán et al. 2022). The ESPRESSO spectrograph on the VLT was used to confirm it and measure its mass at about 1.46 Jupiter masses, with a radius close to Jupiter's (Jordán et al. 2022). It orbits very close to its star, in under two days (Jordán et al. 2022). It is one of four such giants announced together (Jordán et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Further discoveries are planned to measure how often low-mass stars host giant planets (Jordán et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats74ab-Jordnetal2022","cite":"Jordán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Andrés Jordán, J. D. Hartman, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-74Ab, HATS-75b, HATS-76b, and HATS-77b: Four Transiting Giant Planets Around K and M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01928","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-56 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It has a period of 1002 days and a minimum mass of about 5.6 Jupiter masses, and it does not transit (Otor et al. 2016). It is a massive companion that may explain a puzzle in the system (Otor et al. 2016).","system":"The two inner planets transit and are coplanar, but their orbits are tilted at least 40 degrees from the star's spin axis (Otor et al. 2016). The host is a red giant, and asteroseismology shows its core and envelope spinning in different directions, with the envelope rotating unusually fast (Tokuno 2025).","history":"Dynamical work finds that the large tilt is consistent with a dynamical origin, and that the outer planet may be the cause (Li et al. 2014). Another study suggests the star may have swallowed a hot Jupiter and so gained its fast spin (Tokuno 2025).","open":"The true origin of the tilt and of the star's spin is not settled (Tokuno 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler56d-Otoretal2016","cite":"Otor et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Oderah Justin Otor, Benjamin T. Montet, John Asher Johnson et al.","title":"The Orbit and Mass of the Third Planet in the Kepler-56 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.03627","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler56d-Tokuno2025","cite":"Tokuno 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Takato Tokuno","title":"Engulfment of a hot Jupiter as a possible origin of the rapid spin and internal spin misalignment of the planet-hosting red giant Kepler-56","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.25680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler56d-Lietal2014","cite":"Li et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Gongjie Li, Smadar Naoz, Francesca Valsecchi et al.","title":"The Dynamics of the Multi-planet System Orbiting Kepler-56","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.2249","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1655 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's mass was measured with 95 spectra from HARPS-N on the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo, handled with a method that models the star's own noise (Haywood et al. 2018). The mass rules out an Earth-like composition with 98% confidence, so the planet carries a substantial gas envelope (Haywood et al. 2018). It receives about 155 times the sunlight Earth does and orbits a quiet, Sun-like G0V star (Haywood et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"It falls on the upper edge of the evaporation valley, the sparse transition zone between rocky and gas-rich planets (Haywood et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1655b-Haywoodetal2018","cite":"Haywood et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Raphaëlle D. Haywood, Andrew Vanderburg, Annelies Mortier et al.","title":"An Accurate Mass Determination for Kepler-1655b, a Moderately Irradiated World with a Significant Volatile Envelope","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08820","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 208487 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Two early studies re-analysed the radial velocity data with a Bayesian method that searches for several planets at once, and confirmed the 130-day orbit of this planet (Gregory 2005). They also reported a second planet with a period near 900 to 1000 days (Gregory 2007). A later study with more than 15 years of extra data from HARPS and PFS found a candidate near 923 days with a minimum mass of 0.32 Jupiter masses, a cool Saturn-like world (Rubenstein et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The second planet was proposed and later challenged, and is still only a candidate (Rubenstein et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd208487b-Gregory2005","cite":"Gregory 2005","year":"2005","authors":"P. C. Gregory","title":"A Bayesian Analysis of Extrasolar Planet Data for HD 208487","journal":"Bayesian Inference and Maximum Entropy Methods in Science and Engineering","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0509412","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd208487b-Gregory2007","cite":"Gregory 2007","year":"2007","authors":"P. C. Gregory","title":"A Bayesian Kepler periodogram detects a second planet in HD208487","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609229","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd208487b-Rubensteinetal2025","cite":"Rubenstein et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Rafael I. Rubenstein, James S. Jenkins, Pablo A. Peña R. et al.","title":"Destruction of \"peas in a pod?\": A candidate multi-planet system around the nearby bright star HD 208487","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.12447","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 8799 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HR 8799 was one of the first stars with several planets seen directly in images, using Keck and Gemini telescopes (Marois et al. 2008). The three planets seen first lie at projected distances of 24, 38 and 68 astronomical units, and all move counter-clockwise (Marois et al. 2008). A fourth planet was later imaged closer in (Marois et al. 2008). Infrared colours show thick clouds, and temperatures of 1000 to 1200 K for planet c (Currie et al. 2011). Cloud and chemistry studies of planet e find a cloudy atmosphere shaped by disequilibrium chemistry (Mollière et al. 2020).","system":"The system resembles a scaled-up version of the outer Solar System (Marois et al. 2008). It also has cold and warm debris belts (Marois et al. 2008).","history":"None of the formation models can explain all four planets forming where they are (Marois et al. 2008).","open":"Whether such wide giants form by gravitational instability or by core accretion is still debated (Marois et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr8799c-Maroisetal2008","cite":"Marois et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Christian Marois, Bruce Macintosh, Travis Barman et al.","title":"Direct Imaging of Multiple Planets Orbiting the Star HR 8799","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.2606","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799c-Currieetal2011","cite":"Currie et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Thayne Currie, Adam Burrows, Yoichi Itoh et al.","title":"A Combined Subaru/VLT/MMT 1-5 μm Study of Planets Orbiting HR 8799: Implications for Atmospheric Properties, Masses, and Formation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.1973","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799c-Mollireetal2020","cite":"Mollière et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Mollière, T. Stolker, S. Lacour et al.","title":"Retrieving scattering clouds and disequilibrium chemistry in the atmosphere of HR 8799e","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.09394","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":139,"about":135,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 137388 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its host shows a solar-like magnetic cycle, which causes a slow shift in measured velocities that can look like a planet (Dumusque et al. 2011). The team fitted the activity index and applied the same solution to the velocities, since the two are linearly correlated, which greatly reduced the noise (Dumusque et al. 2011). The planet has a minimum mass of about 0.22 Jupiter masses and an orbit with low eccentricity, under 0.4 (Dumusque et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd137388b-Dumusqueetal2011","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"X. Dumusque, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXX. Planetary systems around stars with solar-like magnetic cycles and short-term activity variation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.1748","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-186 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Tides act fast enough that the four inner planets must have small obliquities and near-synchronous rotation, so one face stays toward the star (Bolmont et al. 2014).","system":"The system has five planets around an early M dwarf, with radii of 1.0 to 1.5 Earth radii and periods of 4 to 130 days (Bolmont et al. 2014). The outermost, Kepler-186f, lies in the habitable zone (Bolmont et al. 2014).","history":"The mix of planet masses can roughly be reproduced if the planets grew in a high-density disc, shaped by an earlier phase of migration (Bolmont et al. 2014). The simulations predict one or two undetected planets between planets e and f (Bolmont et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler186b-Bolmontetal2014","cite":"Bolmont et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Emeline Bolmont, Sean N. Raymond, Philip von Paris et al.","title":"Formation, Tidal Evolution, and Habitability of the Kepler-186 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.4368","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-842 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was a target in a search for artificial radio signals, not in a study of the planet itself. Twelve Kepler planets were watched during their transits with the Green Bank Telescope, and two gave signals of interest that could not be separated from interference (Agosto Reyes 2023). Kepler-842 b was re-observed during transits with the Allen Telescope Array, with 30 minutes of data around mid-transit and a second beam placed off target to check where signals came from (Agosto Reyes 2023). The idea is that a transit could act as a shared meeting point for senders and listeners (Agosto Reyes 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler842b-Reyes2023","cite":"Reyes 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Alanice Agosto Reyes","title":"A Technosignature Follow-Up Search for Two Kepler Planets with the Allen Telescope Array","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 143361 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of four low-mass Jupiter-like companions from the Magellan precision velocity survey, found on eccentric intermediate- or long-period orbits (Minniti et al. 2009). A later HARPS survey refined its orbit, and found a single-planet solution that agrees with the Magellan one (Jenkins et al. 2009). The mass is a minimum value, because only radial velocities are available (Minniti et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd143361b-Minnitietal2009","cite":"Minniti et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Dante Minniti, R. Paul Butler, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"Low-Mass Companions for Five Solar-Type Stars From the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.5348","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd143361b-Jenkinsetal2009","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, H. R. A. Jones, K. Goździewski et al.","title":"First results from the Calan-Hertfordshire Extrasolar Planet Search: exoplanets and the discovery of an eccentric brown dwarf in the desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.2985","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gl 686 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a survey of red dwarfs that uses HARPS-N to find planets of a few Earth masses (Affer et al. 2019).","observed":"The star, an M1 dwarf only 8.2 parsecs away, showed more velocity scatter than its errors allowed, caused by a clean signal repeating every 15.53 days (Affer et al. 2019). Brightness data taken at the same time were used to tell star spots from a planet (Affer et al. 2019). Later CARMENES data confirmed the planet, as a super-Earth with a minimum mass of 6.6 Earth masses (Lalitha et al. 2019). The star itself seems to rotate every 37 days, well apart from the planet's period (Lalitha et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gl686b-Afferetal2019","cite":"Affer et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Affer, M. Damasso, G. Micela et al.","title":"HADES RV program with HARPS-N at the TNG. IX. A super-Earth around the M dwarf Gl 686","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05338","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gl686b-Lalithaetal2019","cite":"Lalitha et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"S. Lalitha, D. Baroch, J. C. Morales et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Detection of a mini-Neptune around LSPM J2116+0234 and refinement of orbital parameters of a super-Earth around GJ 686 (BD+18 3421)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09075","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-95 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey looked at cool low-mass stars in clusters, because Kepler had shown that small planets are common around them (Obermeier et al. 2016).","observed":"This is the first transiting planet found in the Beehive cluster, which is about 800 million years old (Obermeier et al. 2016). Imaging, spectra and archive images were used to rule out false alarms (Obermeier et al. 2016). A Neptune-sized planet is unusual here, since M-dwarf field stars rarely have them (Obermeier et al. 2016). The star is too faint for a radial velocity orbit, so the mass is only limited to below about 1.7 Jupiter masses (Pepper et al. 2017). Its low stellar density makes it a useful test of stellar models at the fully convective boundary (Pepper et al. 2017).","system":"It is the second transiting planet found by K2 in a cluster several hundred million years old, and both orbit mid-M dwarfs (Pepper et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k295b-Obermeieretal2016","cite":"Obermeier et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Christian Obermeier, Thomas Henning, Joshua E. Schlieder et al.","title":"K2 Discovers a Busy Bee: An Unusual Transiting Neptune Found in the Beehive Cluster","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04760","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k295b-Pepperetal2017","cite":"Pepper et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Joshua Pepper, Ed Gillen, Hannu Parviainen et al.","title":"A Low-mass Exoplanet Candidate Detected by K2 Transiting the Praesepe M Dwarf JS 183","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10250","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3929 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Four eclipse observations at 15 micrometres with JWST/MIRI were made as the first full data set of the Rocky Worlds survey, which looks for atmospheres on cool rocky planets (Connors et al. 2026). They show an eclipse depth of 118 parts per million and a day-side brightness temperature of roughly 640 K (Connors et al. 2026). The planet is called an exo-Venus, since it receives about 17 times Earth's sunlight (Beard et al. 2022). Whether such planets around M dwarfs can hold atmospheres is still unknown (Connors et al. 2026).","system":"A second, non-transiting planet is likely a sub-Neptune with a period of 15.04 days and a minimum mass of about 5.7 Earth masses (Beard et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Models suggest impacts could repeatedly rebuild a carbon dioxide atmosphere, which could exist for up to 70% of the planet's history (August et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3929b-Connorsetal2026","cite":"Connors et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nicholas J. Connors, Christopher Monaghan, Björn Benneke et al.","title":"GJ 3929 b as the First Complete Rocky Worlds DDT Data Set","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07511","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3929b-Beardetal2022","cite":"Beard et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Corey Beard, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"GJ 3929: High-precision Photometric and Doppler Characterization of an Exo-Venus and Its Hot, Mini-Neptune-mass Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.10672","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-48 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This hot Jupiter has an inflated radius and may have spun up its slightly evolved host star (Enoch et al. 2011). Its day side was measured with Warm Spitzer and ground-based infrared cameras, and is well described by a blackbody of 2158 K (O'Rourke et al. 2014). The orbit is consistent with circular (O'Rourke et al. 2014). Data cannot tell whether the atmosphere has a temperature inversion (Clark et al. 2018). A search for escaping helium found none, which fits a low mass-loss rate (Bennett et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp48b-Enochetal2011","cite":"Enoch et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"B. Enoch, D. R. Anderson, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-35b, WASP-48b, and HAT-P-30b/WASP-51b: Two New Planets and an Independent Discovery of a Hat Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1104.2827","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp48b-ORourkeetal2014","cite":"O'Rourke et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Joseph G. O'Rourke, Heather A. Knutson, Ming Zhao et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer and Palomar Near-IR Secondary Eclipse Photometry of Two Hot Jupiters: WASP-48b and HAT-P-23b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.0011","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp48b-Clarketal2018","cite":"Clark et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"B. J. M. Clark, D. R. Anderson, N. Madhusudhan et al.","title":"Thermal emission of WASP-48b in the K<SUB>s</SUB>-band","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01913","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp48b-Bennettetal2023","cite":"Bennett et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Katherine A. Bennett, Seth Redfield, Antonija Oklopčić et al.","title":"Nondetection of Helium in the Hot Jupiter WASP-48b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.02465","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the innermost of six in a K2-138 system, whose five inner planets form an unbroken chain of near 3:2 resonances (Christiansen et al. 2018). The HARPS spectra gave a mass of 3.1 Earth masses and a density of 4.9 grams per cubic centimetre, Earth-like (Lopez et al. 2019). CHEOPS timings corrected transit predictions that were off by hours (Vivien et al. 2024).","system":"The six planets are 1.5 to 3.4 Earth radii, with periods of 2.3 to 42 days (Leleu et al. 2026). The system was found by citizen scientists in the Exoplanet Explorers project (Christiansen et al. 2018).","history":"A resonant chain that is this fragile means no scattering or collision has happened since the planets formed and migrated in the disc (Leleu et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138b-Christiansenetal2018","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Geert Barentsen et al.","title":"The K2-138 System: A Near-resonant Chain of Five Sub-Neptune Planets Discovered by Citizen Scientists","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.03874","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138b-Lopezetal2019","cite":"Lopez et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"T. A. Lopez, S. C. C. Barros, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Exoplanet characterisation in the longest known resonant chain: the K2-138 system seen by HARPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.13527","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138b-Vivienetal2024","cite":"Vivien et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. G. Vivien, S. Hoyer, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"New ephemerides and detection of transit-timing variations in the K2-138 system using high-precision CHEOPS photometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18267","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138b-Leleuetal2026","cite":"Leleu et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Leleu, A. Alnajjarine, J.-B. Delisle et al.","title":"Photo-dynamical characterisation of the K2-138 resonant chain: Estimation of a mass─radius relationship for resonant sub-Neptunes","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202558317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":16,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1678 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ground-based follow-up validated the planet, and HARPS velocities give an upper limit of 1.4 Earth masses (Silverstein et al. 2022). It lies in the Venus zone, where it receives much more light than Earth does (Silverstein et al. 2022). Its twin in size and predicted mass is the later-validated planet d, near a 4:3 resonance (Silverstein et al. 2024).","system":"The M2 dwarf has an astrometric companion, probably a brown dwarf, on a decades-long orbit (Silverstein et al. 2022). The star sits in a narrow gap in the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, tied to the fully convective boundary (Silverstein et al. 2024). The ultra-short-period planet b breaks the peas-in-a-pod trend (Silverstein et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The effect of the star's gap position on planet evolution is not known (Silverstein et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1678c-Silversteinetal2022","cite":"Silverstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Michele L. Silverstein, Joshua E. Schlieder, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"The LHS 1678 System: Two Earth-sized Transiting Planets and an Astrometric Companion Orbiting an M Dwarf Near the Convective Boundary at 20 pc","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.12079","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lhs1678c-Silversteinetal2024","cite":"Silverstein et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Michele L. Silverstein, Thomas Barclay, Joshua E. Schlieder et al.","title":"Validation of a Third Planet in the LHS 1678 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.00110","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3288 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets around low-mass stars are uncommon outcomes of planet formation, so each one adds to a small sample (Frensch et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with HARPS and the NIRPS near-infrared spectrograph, using a new way to remove contamination from Earth's atmosphere in the infrared (Frensch et al. 2026). It is a hot Jupiter of about 2.1 Jupiter masses around a K9V star (Frensch et al. 2026). The planets from this kind of survey are suitable for later measures of obliquity and atmosphere (Dransfield et al. 2026).","system":"The host TOI-3288 is a wide binary (Dransfield et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The existence of giants around the lowest-mass stars is hard to reconcile with core accretion (Dransfield et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3288b-Frenschetal2026","cite":"Frensch et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yolanda G. C. Frensch, François Bouchy, Gaspare Lo Curto et al.","title":"TOI-3288 b and TOI-4666 b: Two gas giants transiting low-mass stars characterised by NIRPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11703","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3288b-Dransfieldetal2026","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Dransfield, M. Timmermans, D. Sebastian et al.","title":"MANGOS ─ II. Five new giant planets orbiting low-mass stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1654 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Only two transits were seen, and the second came five days before the second reaction wheel failed and ended Kepler's main mission (Beichman et al. 2018). Imaging, spectra and Keck HIRES velocities confirm it, limiting its mass to below 0.5 Jupiter masses (Beichman et al. 2018). The density is similar to Saturn's or lower (Beichman et al. 2018). Fewer than 20 transiting Kepler planets have periods over a year (Beichman et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a mature G5V star and the orbit is 2.03 astronomical units wide (Beichman et al. 2018). The star's spin appears misaligned with the orbit (Morgan et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1654b-Beichmanetal2018","cite":"Beichman et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"C. A. Beichman, H. A. C. Giles, R. Akeson et al.","title":"Validation and Initial Characterization of the Long-period Planet Kepler-1654 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08945","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1654b-Morganetal2024","cite":"Morgan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Marvin Morgan, Brendan P. Bowler, Quang H. Tran et al.","title":"Signs of Similar Stellar Obliquity Distributions for Hot and Warm Jupiters Orbiting Cool Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18445","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-117 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transit timing changes of several minutes were used with SOPHIE velocities to fit the whole system (Bruno et al. 2015). Planet c has a mass of about 1.84 Jupiter masses, much heavier than planet b at 0.094 (Bruno et al. 2015). Another study of the timings finds about 702 Earth masses and confirms it as a real warm Jupiter (Wu et al. 2018).","system":"The two planets have periods of 18.8 and 50.8 days, low eccentricities and are not in resonance, yet still tug on each other (Bruno et al. 2015). The F8 host star has a best solution that is dynamically stable (Bruno et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler117c-Brunoetal2015","cite":"Bruno et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"G. Bruno, J.-M. Almenara, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. XIV. A joint photometric, spectroscopic, and dynamical analysis of the Kepler-117 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.3934","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler117c-Wuetal2018","cite":"Wu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Dong-Hong Wu, Songhu Wang, Ji-Lin Zhou et al.","title":"TTV-determined Masses for Warm Jupiters and Their Close Planetary Companions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02217","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5350 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to make a complete sample of bright hot Jupiters to learn how often each migration route is taken (Schulte et al. 2025).","observed":"It is one of four new super-Jupiters, each over five times Jupiter's mass (Schulte et al. 2025). The host star of this planet has lithium absorption in its spectrum (Schulte et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"How hot Jupiters form and evolve is not known (Schulte et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5350b-Schulteetal2025","cite":"Schulte et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). II. Super-Jupiters and Lithium-rich host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02666","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 72659 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion was first announced from HIRES and HARPS velocities combined with Gaia astrometry (Ruggieri et al. 2025). Ninety-one HARPS-N spectra from 2013 to 2023 and Gaia data give a mass near 19 Jupiter masses at about 21 astronomical units, inside the brown dwarf desert (Ruggieri et al. 2025). The combination breaks the mass-inclination uncertainty of radial velocities alone (Ruggieri et al. 2025). High-resolution SPHERE imaging was also analysed (Ruggieri et al. 2025).","system":"The Sun-like host also has a gas giant on a roughly 10-year orbit (Ruggieri et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd72659c-Ruggierietal2025","cite":"Ruggieri et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Ruggieri, A. Sozzetti, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG LXVIII. Characterization of the outer substellar companion around HD 72659 with a multitechnique approach<SUP></SUP>","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19063","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"51 Eri b":{"text":{"why":"It came from a large survey of young nearby stars with a new planet-imaging instrument (Macintosh et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet shines by its own heat, which lets astronomers read its atmosphere directly. Its near-infrared spectrum shows strong methane and water-vapour absorption (Macintosh et al. 2015). Models give a temperature of 600 to 750 kelvin, making it among the coolest and faintest planets ever imaged (Macintosh et al. 2015). A second set of observations with a different instrument covered a wider range of wavelengths and compared the planet with other cool, odd dwarfs (Samland et al. 2017). The same team also searched archival images for further companions close to the star (Samland et al. 2017).","system":"The host star is only about 20 million years old, and the planet lies at a projected distance of 13 astronomical units from it (Macintosh et al. 2015).","history":"If the planet formed hot, its mass would be about twice Jupiter's. Its low luminosity also fits a slow, cold-start core-accretion route like the one that may have built Jupiter (Macintosh et al. 2015).","open":"The mass depends on how hot the planet was when it formed, which the observations cannot yet tell apart (Macintosh et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-51erib-Macintoshetal2015","cite":"Macintosh et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"B. Macintosh, J. R. Graham, T. Barman et al.","title":"Discovery and spectroscopy of the young jovian planet 51 Eri b with the Gemini Planet Imager","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.03084","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-51erib-Samlandetal2017","cite":"Samland et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Samland, P. Mollière, M. Bonnefoy et al.","title":"Spectral and atmospheric characterization of 51 Eridani b using VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02987","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":94,"about":33,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MASCARA-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a bright A-type star, and the signal showed up in the combined data of two small wide-field camera networks (Dorval et al. 2020).","observed":"A spectrum taken during transit showed the planet crosses the star on a retrograde orbit, against the star's spin (Dorval et al. 2020). Space-telescope light curves show an uneven transit, caused by the rapidly spinning, squashed star being darker at its equator. This gives a spin-orbit angle of 104 degrees, close to a polar orbit (Ahlers et al. 2020). A faint secondary eclipse at 0.491 of the orbit shows the orbit is slightly eccentric (Ahlers et al. 2020). Transmission spectra reveal hydrogen, sodium and calcium, and later work also found rubidium and samarium, the first time these were seen in an exoplanet (Zhang et al. 2022; Jiang et al. 2023).","system":"The host star spins fast and is flattened into an oval shape, so the planet sees a star that changes shape through its orbit (Ahlers et al. 2020).","history":"The short, polar orbit suggests the planet went through dramatic orbital changes to reach its present place (Ahlers et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-mascara4b-Dorvaletal2020","cite":"Dorval et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Dorval, G. J. J. Talens, G. P. P. L. Otten et al.","title":"MASCARA-4 b/bRing-1 b: A retrograde hot Jupiter around a bright A-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.02733","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara4b-Ahlersetal2020","cite":"Ahlers et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"John P. Ahlers, Ethan Kruse, Knicole D. Colón et al.","title":"Gravity-darkening Analysis of the Misaligned Hot Jupiter MASCARA-4 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05025","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara4b-Zhangetal2022","cite":"Zhang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yapeng Zhang, Ignas A. G. Snellen, Aurèlien Wyttenbach et al.","title":"Transmission spectroscopy of the ultra-hot Jupiter MASCARA-4 b. Disentangling the hydrostatic and exospheric regimes of ultra-hot Jupiters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.11427","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara4b-Jiangetal2023","cite":"Jiang et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Zewen Jiang, Wei Wang, Gang Zhao et al.","title":"Detection of Rubidium and Samarium in the Atmosphere of the Ultrahot Jupiter MASCARA-4b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04948","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-184 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The study presents this planet together with three other hot Jupiters from the same survey. The host star is a post-main-sequence G0 star, 4 to 8 billion years old (Hellier et al. 2019). Because the star has swollen to a larger size, the transits are shallow, dimming it by only 0.7 to 0.9 per cent, even though the planet is moderately inflated (Hellier et al. 2019). The authors give a radius of 1.2 to 1.3 Jupiter radii for the three similar planets in the study (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp184b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"WASP-South hot Jupiters: WASP-178b, WASP-184b, WASP-185b, and WASP-192b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V830 Tau b":{"text":{"why":"The star was monitored as part of a programme on young stars with strong magnetic fields and the survival of close-in giant planets (Donati et al. 2016).","observed":"The star is a very young, magnetically active star, so its spots cause radial-velocity jitter of about 1.2 km/s. The team modelled this jitter and pulled out a planet signal of 68 metres per second (Donati et al. 2016). The star also produced a large flare during the observations (Donati et al. 2016). Models of the stellar wind predict radio emission of 6 to 24 mJy from the planet (Vidotto & Donati 2017). An 8-day radio monitoring of the star found variability by more than a factor of 5 (Osten & Wolk 2025).","system":"The star is only about 2 to 3 million years old and lost its disc recently (Donati et al. 2016; Vidotto & Donati 2017).","history":"It is among the youngest planets claimed, which would make it a test of how close-in giants form and migrate (Donati et al. 2016).","open":"Its existence is disputed. A later independent set of observations with another spectrograph over about 2.5 years was set up to check whether the hot Jupiter is really there (Damasso et al. 2020). The long radio flare may come from the star interacting magnetically with the planet, but this is a hypothesis (Osten & Wolk 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v830taub-Donatietal2016","cite":"Donati et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J.-F. Donati, E. Hébrard, G. A. J. Hussain et al.","title":"Magnetic activity and hot Jupiters of young Suns: the weak-line T Tauri stars V819 Tau and V830 Tau","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.02110","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v830taub-VidottoDonati2017","cite":"Vidotto & Donati 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, J.-F. Donati","title":"Predicting radio emission from the newborn hot Jupiter V830 Tauri b and its host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03622","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v830taub-OstenWolk2025","cite":"Osten & Wolk 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Rachel A. Osten, Scott J. Wolk","title":"The Variable Radio Emission of V830 Tau and Its Putative Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05082","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v830taub-Damassoetal2020","cite":"Damasso et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Damasso, A. F. Lanza, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXVII. Reassessment of a young planetary system with HARPS-N: is the hot Jupiter V830 Tau b really there?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.09445","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":13,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet belongs to a family of at least six transiting planets around a 700-million-year-old G star, all between about 2 and 5 Earth radii (Dai et al. 2023). Their orbital periods sit extremely close to exact resonance, differing by only about 0.01 per cent (Dai et al. 2023). Timing variations in the transits gave masses of 3 to 8 Earth masses and showed the planets are truly locked in resonance (Dai et al. 2023). A measurement during the transit of planet d showed the star's spin is aligned with the orbits (Dai et al. 2023).","system":"The period ratios are near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2. It is the first known resonant chain with a weaker 7:5 resonance between two stronger ones (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"The tidy, aligned layout and lack of a detected stellar companion suggest the chain formed in a quiet, isolated disc without disruption (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136g-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 81688 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey targets giant stars to look for planets around stars heavier than the Sun (Sato et al. 2008).","observed":"The host is a K-type giant in the clump region of the star-colour diagram, with a mass of 2.1 to 2.3 Suns (Sato et al. 2008). The planet has a minimum mass of 2.7 Jupiters and a nearly circular orbit of 184 days, one of the shortest known around evolved stars (Sato et al. 2008). No planet found around such clump giants lies closer than 0.68 astronomical units (Sato et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"Calculations suggest Jupiter-mass planets within about 0.5 astronomical units of 2 to 3 solar-mass stars could be swallowed when the star swells at the tip of the red giant branch (Sato et al. 2008).","open":"The authors cannot tell whether the lack of close-in planets is primordial or due to engulfment (Sato et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd81688b-Satoetal2008","cite":"Sato et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Hideyuki Izumiura, Eri Toyota et al.","title":"Planetary Companions around Three Intermediate-Mass G and K Giants: 18 Delphini, ξ Aquilae, and HD 81688","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.2590","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2025-BLG-1616L b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first bound planet found by the DREAMS survey, which also aims to detect free-floating planets (Yang et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet's signal was caught by two telescope networks. The survey's minute-by-minute observations resolved an ambiguity in the models between a central and a resonant solution (Yang et al. 2026). The background source star is very faint, so its colour was measured from the DREAMS data (Yang et al. 2026). A statistical analysis gives a host star of about 0.3 Suns, a planet of about 40 Earth masses and a projected separation of about 1.6 astronomical units (Yang et al. 2026). The system lies about 7.5 kiloparsecs away (Yang et al. 2026).","system":"The mass ratio between planet and host is about 5 in 10,000 (Yang et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The masses and distance come from a statistical analysis, not a direct measurement (Yang et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2025blg1616lb-Yangetal2026","cite":"Yang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Hongjing Yang, Weicheng Zang, Yoon-Hyun Ryu et al.","title":"KMT-2025-BLG-1616Lb: First Microlensing Bound Planet From DREAMS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11469","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 214823 b":{"text":{"why":"A later survey was built to search for gas giants around metal-rich stars (Ment et al. 2018).","observed":"Orbit data from a French survey found three new companions to Sun-like stars, with orbital periods of three to five years and minimum masses ranging from 1.6 to 19 Jupiters (Díaz et al. 2016). Long-term Keck data later suggested the companion to HD 214823 has a minimum mass of 20 to 30 Jupiters, which is in the brown dwarf range (Ment et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass puts it between a giant planet and a brown dwarf, and the true mass is not known because only a minimum mass comes from radial velocities (Díaz et al. 2016; Ment et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd214823b-Mentetal2018","cite":"Ment et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Kristo Ment, Debra A. Fischer, Gaspar Bakos et al.","title":"Radial Velocities from the N2K Project: Six New Cold Gas Giant Planets Orbiting HD 55696, HD 98736, HD 148164, HD 203473, and HD 211810","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01228","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd214823b-Dazetal2016","cite":"Díaz et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. F. Díaz, J. Rey, O. Demangeon et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XI. Three new companions and an orbit update: Giant planets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.07610","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 25015 b":{"text":{"why":"The long observing time of the survey selects giant companions that suit direct imaging, to help bridge radial-velocity and imaged planets (Rickman et al. 2019).","observed":"A southern spectrograph has monitored stars within 50 parsecs for more than 20 years, which allows it to find companions on very long orbits (Rickman et al. 2019). The five new companions announced in the study have periods of 15.6 to 40.4 years and masses of 2.93 to 26.77 Jupiters. This range straddles the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Rickman et al. 2019). A study of nearby systems with one known giant picked this star as a candidate with a good chance of hosting a habitable-zone Earth-like planet that survives (Kokaia et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd25015b-Rickmanetal2019","cite":"Rickman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"E. L. Rickman, D. Ségransan, M. Marmier et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVIII. Three new massive planets and two low-mass brown dwarfs at greater than 5 AU separation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.01573","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd25015b-Kokaiaetal2020","cite":"Kokaia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Giorgi Kokaia, Melvyn B. Davies, Alexander J. Mustill","title":"Resilient habitability of nearby exoplanet systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07573","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-67 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the inflated Jupiter of a set of ten, with a radius of 1.69 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2019). Its orbit takes only 1.6092 days, the shortest of the ten planets in the study (Hartman et al. 2019). The authors used Gaia distances and broadband photometry to model the stars and to check for blends, where light from a nearby star could mimic a planet (Hartman et al. 2019). Three of the other systems, not this one, were flagged as having possible unresolved companion stars (Hartman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats67b-Hartmanetal2019","cite":"Hartman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-60b-HATS-69b: 10 Transiting Planets from HATSouth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-521 b":{"text":{"why":"It is part of a follow-up programme to understand sub-Neptunes across the whole population (Lacedelli et al. 2026).","observed":"The star is a cool M dwarf, and the planet is a sub-Neptune on a 1.5-day orbit. Its mass was measured with three high-precision spectrographs (Lacedelli et al. 2026). It is nearly identical in mass and radius to the planet around a second, similar M dwarf (Lacedelli et al. 2026). The team also checked for extra planets in the system (Lacedelli et al. 2026). A preliminary conference report suggests an outer, non-transiting companion may exist, and that the transiting planet could be made of rock mixed with similar amounts of water (Ikuta et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The water-rich idea is linked to a third population of planets in the radius valley of M dwarfs (Ikuta et al. 2024).","open":"The outer companion is only suggested so far (Ikuta et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi521b-Lacedellietal2026","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Lacedelli, E. Pallé, R. Luque et al.","title":"Sibling sub-Neptunes around sibling M dwarfs: TOI-521 and TOI-912","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14782","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi521b-Ikutaetal2024","cite":"Ikuta et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kai Ikuta, Norio Narita, Takuya Takarada et al.","title":"Discovery of a short-period sub-Neptune and an outer planetary companion around a mid M-dwarf","journal":"TESS Science Conference III","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13117843","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-156 b":{"text":{"why":"The study aimed to learn how giant planets migrate and where the upper edge of the Neptunian desert lies (Demangeon et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet is a super-Neptune orbiting a K-type star, with a mass of 0.128 Jupiters and a radius of 0.51 Jupiters (Demangeon et al. 2018). New transit spectroscopy shows its orbit is aligned with the star's spin, contrary to an earlier report of a highly misaligned orbit (Lafarga et al. 2026). A search for atmospheric absorption found no significant signal (Jiang et al. 2026). A helium survey placed first limits on helium in its atmosphere (Orell-Miquel et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The aligned orbit gives clues to how short-period Neptune-sized planets arrived (Lafarga et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp156b-Demangeonetal2018","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, F. Faedi, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"The discovery of WASP-151b, WASP-153b, WASP-156b: Insights on giant planet migration and the upper boundary of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06321","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp156b-Lafargaetal2026","cite":"Lafarga et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. Lafarga, J. I. Espinoza-Retamal, H. M. Cegla et al.","title":"The Neptunian ridge planet WASP-156 b does not have a polar orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27291","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp156b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Zewen Jiang, Wei Wang, Shumei Jia et al.","title":"High-resolution Spectroscopic Atmospheric Studies of Five Hot Jupiters Across the Edge of the Neptune Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21273","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp156b-OrellMiqueletal2026","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jaume Orell-Miquel, Kyra Sampson, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"HET/HPF Observations of Helium in Warm, Hot, and Ultrahot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1752 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A search for single transits in the Kepler data, using a neural network, found a new candidate in this system called Kepler 1752.02 (Hansen & Dittmann 2026). Two transit events are consistent with a period of about 778 days (Hansen & Dittmann 2026). The inner planets in the system show transit timing variations (Hansen & Dittmann 2026).","system":"The system joins three others where the inner planets have timing variations and a new long-period candidate (Hansen & Dittmann 2026).","history":"","open":"The long-period signal is still only a candidate (Hansen & Dittmann 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1752b-HansenDittmann2026","cite":"Hansen & Dittmann 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew T. Hansen, Jason A. Dittmann","title":"Exploring Long-period Architectures: Four New Planet Candidates from Kepler with Periods >342 days","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23425","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6651 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a metal-rich sub-giant G star (Baliwal et al. 2024). With a density of 2.52 grams per cubic centimetre, the planet is among the few known dense sub-Saturns and the densest found by TESS (Baliwal et al. 2024). About 87 per cent of its mass is dense material, such as rock and iron in the core, with the rest a low-density envelope (Baliwal et al. 2024). Its orbit is slightly eccentric (Baliwal et al. 2024). The planet fits the trend that more massive planets orbit more metal-rich stars (Baliwal et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6651b-Baliwaletal2024","cite":"Baliwal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sanjay Baliwal, Rishikesh Sharma, Abhijit Chakraborty et al.","title":"Discovery and characterization of a dense sub-Saturn TOI-6651b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.17179","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 4276 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a small M dwarf. The signal is best explained by a Neptune-mass planet on a 13.35-day orbit (Nagel et al. 2019). The data can be fitted either by one eccentric planet or by two planets in circular orbits near a 2:1 period ratio (Nagel et al. 2019). The single eccentric planet is slightly preferred, and a later statistical test also favours it (Nagel et al. 2019; Nixon et al. 2026). If so, it is the most eccentric planet around an M dwarf with such a short period (Nagel et al. 2019).","system":"The star turns slowly, with a rotation period of 65 days, making it one of the slowest rotators in its mass range (Czesla 2018).","history":"","open":"The two models cannot be told apart with the data so far, and further observations at certain orbital phases would help (Nagel et al. 2019; Nixon et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj4276b-Nageletal2019","cite":"Nagel et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"E. Nagel, S. Czesla, J. H. M. M. Schmitt et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. The enigmatic planetary system GJ 4276: one eccentric planet or two planets in a 2:1 resonance?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.02367","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj4276b-Nixonetal2026","cite":"Nixon et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew C. Nixon, Ritvik Basant, Rafael Luque et al.","title":"Enhancing the Interpretability of Radial Velocity Analysis Using Leave-one-out Cross Validation","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07181","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj4276b-Czesla2018","cite":"Czesla 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Stefan Czesla","title":"GJ 4276: Slow rotator with eccentric planet","journal":"XMM-Newton Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 22946 d":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a long-period transiting case where TESS saw only two transits widely separated in time (Garai et al. 2023).","observed":"The first TESS data gave only a single transit of this planet, and the second TESS pass added another, leaving several possible periods (Garai et al. 2023). A space telescope called CHEOPS followed up to choose between them, giving a true period of 47.42489 days (Garai et al. 2023). The planet is a warm sub-Neptune, a type with few confirmed examples (Garai et al. 2023). The same data refined the radii of the other two planets (Garai et al. 2023).","system":"The star is bright and Sun-like and hosts three transiting planets, with the inner two at 4.04 and 9.57 days (Cacciapuoti et al. 2022).","history":"Long-period planets like this may keep more of their primordial traits than close-in ones (Garai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd22946d-Garaietal2023","cite":"Garai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Z. Garai, H. P. Osborn, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Refined parameters of the HD 22946 planetary system and the true orbital period of planet d★","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04468","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd22946d-Cacciapuotietal2022","cite":"Cacciapuoti et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Luca Cacciapuoti, Laura Inno, Giovanni Covone et al.","title":"TESS discovery of a super-Earth and two sub-Neptunes orbiting the bright, nearby, Sun-like star HD 22946","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.09597","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-31 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"It is the first confirmed transiting hot Jupiter from the K2 mission (Grziwa et al. 2016). Its transit is grazing, so its radius is poorly known, between 0.7 and 1.4 Jupiter radii (Grziwa et al. 2016). Its mass is 1.8 Jupiters (Grziwa et al. 2016). Later work found a brightness change over the orbit and a low reflectivity (Niraula et al. 2018). Climate models match its optical brightness best with cloud-free skies or only compact clouds (Adams et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k231b-Grziwaetal2016","cite":"Grziwa et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Sascha Grziwa, Davide Gandolfi, Szilard Csizmadia et al.","title":"K2-31B, a Grazing Transiting Hot Jupiter on a 1.26-day Orbit around a Bright G7V Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.09149","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k231b-Niraulaetal2018","cite":"Niraula et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Seth Redfield, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Discovery of Six Optical Phase Curves with K2","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09227","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k231b-Adamsetal2022","cite":"Adams et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Danica J. Adams, Tiffany Kataria, Natasha E. Batalha et al.","title":"Spatially Resolved Modeling of Optical Albedos for a Sample of Six Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00041","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2421 b":{"text":{"why":"It is part of an effort to build a uniform sample of hot Jupiters for population studies (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based photometry, high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy, coordinated with the TESS follow-up programme (Yee et al. 2022). It is one of ten new hot Jupiters orbiting bright F and G stars, and it is notable for being a Saturn-mass planet (Yee et al. 2022). The ten planets have periods of 2 to 10 days and masses of 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiters (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2421b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63454 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a close-in Jovian planet with a minimum mass of 0.38 Jupiters. It sits at 0.036 astronomical units from its star (Moutou et al. 2005). The host is a young, active K dwarf with strong calcium emission, yet photometrically stable to about 3 millimagnitudes (Kane et al. 2011). The planet had a 14 per cent chance of transiting, but a search found no transit and no sign of magnetic interaction with the star (Kane et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"Metal-rich hosts like this one point to a peculiar formation and migration route for hot Jupiters (Moutou et al. 2005).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63454b-Moutouetal2005","cite":"Moutou et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Mayor, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IV. Three close-in planets around HD 2638, HD 27894 and HD 63454","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20052826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63454b-Kaneetal2011","cite":"Kane et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Diana Dragomir, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"Stellar Variability of the Exoplanet Hosting Star HD 63454","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.6131","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-1599.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The two planets pull on each other, shifting their transit times in opposite directions, which shows they are linked (Panichi et al. 2019). Both have radii of about 1.9 Earth radii (Panichi et al. 2019). They are trapped in a 3:2 orbital resonance with nearly circular orbits (Panichi et al. 2019). The best fit gives masses of 9.0 and 4.6 Earth masses for the inner and outer planet. A second family of solutions gives smaller masses of about 7 and 3.6 Earth masses (Panichi et al. 2019).","system":"The two super-Earths have anti-aligned orbits and a robust 3:2 resonance for both mass solutions (Panichi et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The mass depends on assumptions about eccentricity, so the true masses are uncertain (Panichi et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi159901-Panichietal2019","cite":"Panichi et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"F. Panichi, C. Migaszewski, K. Goździewski","title":"Two Super-Earths in the 3:2 MMR around KOI-1599","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.01435","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2012-BLG-0838L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's signal is well separated from the main peak of the event. Its projected distance from the star is twice the Einstein ring radius, about 4 astronomical units (Poleski et al. 2020). Planets like this around low-mass stars are very hard to find with any other technique (Poleski et al. 2020). The study discusses the model fitting in detail (Poleski et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass and distance of the lens system are not yet measured directly, and the study discusses the prospects for doing so (Poleski et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2012blg0838lb-Poleskietal2020","cite":"Poleski et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. Poleski, D. Suzuki, A. Udalski et al.","title":"A Wide-orbit Exoplanet OGLE-2012-BLG-0838Lb","journal":"Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05466","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-51 b":{"text":{"why":"It is a benchmark super-puff, a class with Jupiter-like radii but sub-Neptune masses (Gao et al. 2026).","observed":"Transit timing variations gave a mass of only 2.1 Earth masses, so the planet has an extremely low density of at most about 0.05 grams per cubic centimetre (Masuda 2014). A Webb spectrum shows an atmosphere largely free of clouds or hazes with clear water vapour absorption, unlike its sibling Kepler-51d (Gao et al. 2026). Its atmosphere is metal-enriched with a low carbon-to-oxygen ratio (Gao et al. 2026). A ring test finds a mild offset in the star's apparent density, which fits unseen compact rings, though this is not proved (Zuluaga et al. 2026).","system":"The star is a Sun-like star under 1 billion years old with three transiting planets. A fourth, outer planet was needed to explain later timing data (Masuda et al. 2024).","history":"One idea is that super-puffs formed at the water snowline, where gas cools easily, and then moved inward (Gao et al. 2026).","open":"Whether rings or hazes inflate the radius is still debated (Gao et al. 2026; Zuluaga et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler51b-Gaoetal2026","cite":"Gao et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Peter Gao, Guangwei Fu, Matthew Nixon et al.","title":"A Clear, Metal-Enriched, and Low C/O Atmosphere for the Benchmark Super-Puff Kepler-51b","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51b-Masuda2014","cite":"Masuda 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Very Low Density Planets around Kepler-51 Revealed with Transit Timing Variations and an Anomaly Similar to a Planet-Planet Eclipse Event","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2885","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51b-Zuluagaetal2026","cite":"Zuluaga et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jorge I. Zuluaga, Sebastian Numpaque, David M. Kipping et al.","title":"Probing Exoplanetary Rings with Asterodensity Profiling: A PhotoRing Analysis of Kepler-51","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25234","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51b-Masudaetal2024","cite":"Masuda et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kento Masuda, Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"A Fourth Planet in the Kepler-51 System Revealed by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01625","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":26,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-428 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three hot Jupiters used to test a data-analysis algorithm that uses Bayesian methods to infer a planet's traits from its light curve (Carado 2016). The returned values for mass, radius and orbital tilt were generally in agreement with published numbers (Carado 2016). For this planet, the dayside and nightside temperatures were also estimated, although there was no other estimate to compare them with (Carado 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler428b-Carado2016","cite":"Carado 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Bertrand Carado","title":"Characterizing Kepler Objects of Interest Using the Algorithm EXONEST","journal":"","url":"https://doi.org/10.54014/c0gf-nkpf","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 117207 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was reported alongside four others, with a test of how likely each Doppler signal is to be noise (Marcy et al. 2005).","observed":"The host is a nearby Sun-like star, followed with Doppler measurements at the Keck Observatory for four to six years (Marcy et al. 2005). All five host stars in that paper are rich in heavy elements (Marcy et al. 2005). Later work used the planet as a test case for a tool that constrains the mass and distance of long-orbit companions from partial orbit data (Van Zandt & Petigura 2024). A magnetic field in the old, inactive host was only marginally detected (Fossati et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd117207b-Marcyetal2005","cite":"Marcy et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"Five New Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/426384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd117207b-VanZandtPetigura2024","cite":"Van Zandt & Petigura 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Judah Van Zandt, Erik A. Petigura","title":"ethraid: A Simple Method for Characterizing Long-period Companions Using Doppler, Astrometric, and Imaging Constraints","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd117207b-Fossatietal2013","cite":"Fossati et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"L. Fossati, O. Kochukhov, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"Detection of a magnetic field in three old and inactive solar-like planet-hosting stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.0879","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-539 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-539 b. Kepler-539 c has not been seen crossing its star. Its presence is inferred from a clear, regular drift in the timing of the 12 transits of the inner planet Kepler-539 b (Mancini et al. 2016). Only a few timings are available, so the properties of planet c are poorly pinned down. Its mass is estimated at 1.2 to 3.6 Jupiter masses, on a very eccentric orbit (eccentricity above 0.4 and up to 0.6) with a period longer than 1000 days (Mancini et al. 2016).","system":"The inner planet, Kepler-539 b, is a dense Jupiter-like world of 0.97 Jupiter masses and 0.747 Jupiter radii. It circles a Sun-like G2 V star in 125.6 days (Mancini et al. 2016). The two giant planets pull on each other, which is what produces the timing drift (Mancini et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The mass, orbit and period of planet c are only roughly known (Mancini et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler539c-Mancinietal2016","cite":"Mancini et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"L. Mancini, J. Lillo-Box, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Kepler-539: A young extrasolar system with two giant planets on wide orbits and in gravitational interaction","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04625","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2719 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2719 b was one of 14 planet candidates examined with an upgraded false-positive tool that adds colour-resolved ground-based light curves, mostly in the J band from the 200-inch Hale Telescope (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025). Its planetary nature was shown statistically: the chance that the signal is a false alarm is below 1.5%, and the chance that a nearby star causes it is below 0.1% (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025). Little else about the planet itself is described.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2719b-Barrientosetal2025","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Validation of TESS Planet Candidates with Multicolor Transit Photometry and TRICERATOPS+","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-65 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet and its sibling HAT-P-66 b were used to test whether giant planets swell as their stars age and brighten. A statistically significant link was found between planet radius and the fractional age of the host star (Hartman et al. 2016).","observed":"HAT-P-65 b is an inflated hot Jupiter: 0.527 Jupiter masses squeezed into a radius of 1.89 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2016). Its star is at the main-sequence turnoff, the point where it is about to leave the main sequence (Hartman et al. 2016). Further transit light curves in four colour bands were later taken with the MuSCAT2 camera (Kang et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The correlation between planet radius and stellar age is fully explained by the known link between radius and present-day equilibrium temperature. Whether planets re-inflate as their stars brighten remains open (Hartman et al. 2016).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp65b-Hartmanetal2016","cite":"Hartman et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, W. Bhatti et al.","title":"HAT-P-65b and HAT-P-66b: Two Transiting Inflated Hot Jupiters and Observational Evidence for the Reinflation of Close-in Giant Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02767","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp65b-Kangetal2025","cite":"Kang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"H. Kang, G. Chen, E. Palle et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Transit light curves of 4 hot Jupiters (Kang+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-270 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler spectra from ESPRESSO and HARPS gave a mass of 6.15 Earth masses for planet c, and its density is lower than Earth's, so it is not a bare rocky world (Van Eylen et al. 2021). Eight observatories tracked its transits between 2018 and 2020 and found timing shifts of about 10 minutes repeating every 3 years or so, caused by the pull of planet d (Kaye et al. 2022). Together with radial velocities, the timing gives a mass of 6.20 Earth masses (Kaye et al. 2022). Computer models suggest one transit with the James Webb Space Telescope could reveal an atmosphere (Chouqar et al. 2020).","system":"Three planets orbit this nearby M dwarf. The planets near a 5:3 and a 2:1 orbit ratio (Kaye et al. 2022). The innermost planet looks rocky, while planets c and d have lower densities, so they sit on opposite sides of the radius valley, a gap in the sizes of close-in planets (Van Eylen et al. 2021).","history":"Interior models suggest planets c and d formed beyond the ice line and gathered plenty of volatile material, while the inner planet is dry and rocky (Deb et al. 2026).","open":"Whether the atmosphere is hydrogen-rich or water-dominated is not known; both are predicted to be detectable (Chouqar et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi270c-VanEylenetal2021","cite":"Van Eylen et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"V. Van Eylen, N. Astudillo-Defru, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"Masses and compositions of three small planets orbiting the nearby M dwarf L231-32 (TOI-270) and the M dwarf radius valley","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01593","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270c-Kayeetal2022","cite":"Kaye et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Laurel Kaye, Shreyas Vissapragada, Maximilian N. Günther et al.","title":"Transit timings variations in the three-planet system: TOI-270","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10763","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270c-Chouqaretal2020","cite":"Chouqar et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Chouqar, Z. Benkhaldoun, A. Jabiri et al.","title":"Properties of sub-Neptune atmospheres: TOI-270 system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12475","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi270c-Debetal2026","cite":"Deb et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sushmita Deb, Kaushal Sharma, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Structural composition and atmospheric analysis of two planetary systems : TOI-270 and TOI-776","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag2095","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 715 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 715 b was first spotted as a single transit in TESS Sector 70, with no orbit known (Venner et al. 2026). Two seasons of radial velocity follow-up then revealed the 56.4-day orbit and gave a mass of 0.635 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.07 Jupiter radii, which makes it a fairly typical warm Jupiter (Venner et al. 2026). TESS saw no transits in Sectors 42 and 43, which narrows the possible orbits (Venner et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a bright subgiant of 1.60 solar masses, one of the most massive stars known to host a transiting warm Jupiter (Venner et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The orbital eccentricity of 0.21 is lower than that of other warm Jupiters around evolved stars, so such systems are more varied than previously thought (Venner et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd715b-Venneretal2026","cite":"Venner et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alexander Venner, Robert A. Wittenmyer, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"HD 715 b, a Warm Jupiter Orbiting a Massive Subgiant Discovered from a Single TESS Transit","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08065","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5806 b":{"text":{"why":"Most ground-based follow-up for the TESS mission goes to low-mass planet candidates, which leaves massive planets such as hot Jupiters without mass measurements (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5806 was one of seven stars with hot-Jupiter candidates monitored with the CAFE2 spectrograph at Calar Alto Observatory (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). The aim was to measure the radial velocity of stars whose candidates had been left behind by follow-up efforts, and so to confirm what the transiting objects really are (Lillo-Box et al. 2026). The radial velocities were fitted together with the TESS light curves to get the planet's properties (Lillo-Box et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5806b-LilloBoxetal2026","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, C. Cifuentes, O. Balsalobre-Ruza et al.","title":"CAFE follow-up of TESS hot Jupiter candidates left behind: I. Five newly confirmed planets and a false positive","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19756","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2406 b":{"text":{"why":"Large sub-Neptunes are uncommon around the coolest stars, and rarer still around metal-poor ones (Wells et al. 2021).","observed":"The host star is a metal-poor M4 V dwarf of the Galaxy's thick disk, 56 pc away, with a temperature of 3100 K (Wells et al. 2021). Multi-band photometry showed that the transit is on the target star and has the same depth in all colours, and the signal was statistically validated as a planet (Wells et al. 2021). Ground-based follow-up came from the SPECULOOS telescope network (Wells et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"The star's low metal content, with the planet's large size and short period, makes TOI-2406 b an unusual outcome of planet formation (Wells et al. 2021).","open":"The large planet-to-star size ratio makes it a good target for infrared transmission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope, but its atmosphere is not yet known (Wells et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2406b-Wellsetal2021","cite":"Wells et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"R. D. Wells, B. V. Rackham, N. Schanche et al.","title":"A large sub-Neptune transiting the thick-disk M4 V TOI-2406","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14125","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-69 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer planet Kepler-69 c. Kepler-69 c is a super-Earth-sized object of 1.7 Earth radii on a 242.5-day orbit, with an equilibrium temperature of 299 K, close to the habitable zone (Barclay et al. 2013). It is the smallest planet found by Kepler in or near the habitable zone of a Sun-like star (Barclay et al. 2013). Kepler-69 b, the inner planet, has a radius of 2.24 Earth radii, and the confidence that both signals are real planets is above 99.3% (Barclay et al. 2013).","system":"Two planets orbit a Sun-like G4V star, at 13.7 days and 242.5 days (Barclay et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler69b-Barclayetal2013","cite":"Barclay et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Thomas Barclay, Christopher J. Burke, Steve B. Howell et al.","title":"A Super-Earth-sized Planet Orbiting in or Near the Habitable Zone around a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4941","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Proxima Cen d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf thought to be fully convective. Seven years of spectra show evidence of a cycle of activity about 442 days long (Cincunegui et al. 2007). The calcium emission index varies by about 130% outside of flares (Cincunegui et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-proximacend-Cincuneguietal2007","cite":"Cincunegui et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"C. Cincunegui, R. F. Díaz, P. J. D. Mauas","title":"A possible activity cycle in <ASTROBJ>Proxima Centauri</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-561 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New CHEOPS and TESS transits pinned down the orbital period of planet e to 77.14399 days, with an uncertainty of 0.00025 days (Piotto et al. 2024). The radius is now known to 2% precision, at 2.517 Earth radii, improved from 5% (Piotto et al. 2024).","system":"TOI-561 hosts at least four transiting planets, with periods of 0.44, 10.8, 25.7 and 77.1 days, plus a fifth candidate of 433 days that does not transit (Piotto et al. 2024). The star is an old member of the Galaxy's thick disc, about 10 billion years old and poor in iron (Brinkman et al. 2023).","history":"Old, metal-poor thick disc stars are less likely to host ultrashort-period super-Earths, so the architecture of this system is of interest (Piotto et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi561e-Piottoetal2024","cite":"Piotto et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Piotto, T. Zingales, L. Borsato et al.","title":"Architecture of TOI-561 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18169","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561e-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-561 b: A Low-density Ultra-short-period \"Rocky\" Planet around a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06665","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-51 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system's atmospheres concerns its neighbours Kepler-51 b and d. Kepler-51 c has a mass of 4.0 Earth masses from transit timing, and a density of about 0.05 g/cm3 or less, among the lowest known (Masuda 2014). Planets this puffy are called super-puffs (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). The planet only grazes its star during transit (Roberts et al. 2018).","system":"The young G dwarf, about 500 million years old, hosts three super-puffs and one lower-mass planet that does not transit (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026). The three transiting planets lie near a 1:2:3 resonance chain (Masuda 2014).","history":"","open":"Why the planets are so puffy is not settled. Ideas for the neighbour Kepler-51 d range from a thick hydrogen-helium envelope, to high-altitude haze, to a tilted ring system (Libby-Roberts et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler51c-Masuda2014","cite":"Masuda 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Very Low Density Planets around Kepler-51 Revealed with Transit Timing Variations and an Anomaly Similar to a Planet-Planet Eclipse Event","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2885","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51c-LibbyRobertsetal2026","cite":"Libby-Roberts et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, Aaron Bello-Arufe, Zachory K. Berta-Thompson et al.","title":"The James Webb Space Telescope NIRSpec-PRISM Transmission Spectrum of the Super-puff, Kepler-51d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler51c-Robertsetal2018","cite":"Roberts et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Jessica Roberts, Zachory K. Berta-Thompson, Jean-Michel Desert et al.","title":"Atmospheres of Two Super-Puffs: Transmission Spectra of Kepler 51b and Kepler 51d","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #232","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"55 Cnc B c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied the system's other star, 55 Cancri A, and its planets. The inner planet 55 Cnc e has an occultation, the dip when it passes behind its star, of 12 ± 3 parts per million in visible light (Demory et al. 2023). The interferometric radius of the primary star is 0.943 solar radii (von Braun et al. 2011).","system":"The primary star hosts five planets, including the transiting super-Earth 55 Cnc e (von Braun et al. 2011). A distant binary companion makes the planetary system precess, so the planets are likely tilted relative to the star's spin axis (Kaib et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-55cncbc-Demoryetal2023","cite":"Demory et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"B.-O. Demory, S. Sulis, E. Meier Valdés et al.","title":"55 Cancri e's occultation captured with CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03582","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncbc-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Theo A. ten Brummelaar et al.","title":"55 Cancri: Stellar Astrophysical Parameters, a Planet in the Habitable Zone, and Implications for the Radius of a Transiting Super-Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-55cncbc-Kaibetal2011","cite":"Kaib et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nathan A. Kaib, Sean N. Raymond, Martin J. Duncan","title":"55 Cancri: A Coplanar Planetary System That is Likely Misaligned with Its Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-5 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up is optimised for short-period planets and is well placed to study the sub-Jovian desert, a region of period and size where few planets are found (Eigmüller et al. 2019).","observed":"NGTS-5 b is a highly inflated warm sub-Saturn of 0.229 Jupiter masses on a 3.357-day orbit, at the edge of the sub-Jovian desert (Eigmüller et al. 2019). Two transits seen with the 10.4 m Gran Telescopio Canarias gave an optical transmission spectrum, which shows signs of contamination by unocculted spots on the star (Wang et al. 2026). After allowing for spots, the atmosphere looks relatively clear, with a metallicity of 0.10 times solar and a carbon-to-oxygen ratio below 0.22 (Wang et al. 2026). Water absorption is strongly indicated when the chemistry is left free (Wang et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts5b-Eigmlleretal2019","cite":"Eigmüller et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Philipp Eigmüller, Alexander Chaushev, Edward Gillen et al.","title":"NGTS-5b: a highly inflated planet offering insights into the sub-Jovian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.02593","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts5b-Wangetal2026","cite":"Wang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Wan-Hao Wang, Guo Chen, Chengzi Jiang et al.","title":"Evidence for stellar contamination and water absorption in NGTS-5b's transmission spectra with GTC/OSIRIS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15704","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AU Mic c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its neighbour, AU Mic b, is a moderately inflated Neptune-size planet near a mean-motion resonance with it (Martioli et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a young, active M dwarf of about 22 million years that flares often, at 6.35 flares per day in TESS data (Martioli et al. 2021; Feinstein et al. 2022). The two planets orbit inside a debris disc (Feinstein et al. 2022). The disc is seen nearly edge-on (Hebb et al. 2007). Ultraviolet flares of 10^29 to 10^31 erg per second were seen with Hubble (Feinstein et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Stellar X-ray and ultraviolet radiation can strip young planets' atmospheres. The mass-loss rate of planet b is around 10^8 g/s in quiet times (Feinstein et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-aumicc-Martiolietal2021","cite":"Martioli et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"E. Martioli, G. Hébrard, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"New constraints on the planetary system around the young active star AU Mic. Two transiting warm Neptunes near mean-motion resonance","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13238","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumicc-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Kevin France, Allison Youngblood et al.","title":"AU Microscopii in the Far-UV: Observations in Quiescence, during Flares, and Implications for AU Mic b and c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09606","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumicc-Hebbetal2007","cite":"Hebb et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Leslie Hebb, Larry Petro, Holland C. Ford et al.","title":"A search for planets transiting the M-dwarf debris disc host, AU Microscopii","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.3584","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-22 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-22 b is a 2.74 Jupiter-mass planet with a radius of 0.953 Jupiter radii, circling a K dwarf of 0.759 solar masses on an eccentric orbit (eccentricity 0.079) (Bento et al. 2017). The planet's high ratio of planet mass to star mass supports the idea that hot Jupiters reach their close orbits after being pushed onto eccentric paths and then having the orbits rounded off by tides (Bento et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"Migration probably involved exciting orbital eccentricity, bringing the planet close to its star, followed by tidal circularisation (Bento et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats22b-Bentoetal2017","cite":"Bento et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Bento, B. Schmidt, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-22b, HATS-23b and HATS-24b: three new transiting super-Jupiters from the HATSouth project","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-290 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-290 c is a warm Jupiter of 0.774 Jupiter masses and 1.006 Jupiter radii, with a period of 48.4 days and eccentricity below 0.241 (Hjorth et al. 2019). Later reanalysis of TESS and CHEOPS data improved the period to better than 5 seconds (Saha 2025).","system":"The star is a late F star in a triple system. Adaptive optics and Gaia data reveal two M-dwarf companions at 113 au and about 2467 au (Hjorth et al. 2019). Inside planet c is a mini-Neptune of 3.06 Earth radii on a 9.2-day orbit, above the radius valley, a gap in the sizes of close-in planets (Hjorth et al. 2019).","history":"The mild eccentricity and the mini-Neptune neighbour suggest that the warm Jupiter formed in place or migrated through the disc (Hjorth et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2290c-Hjorthetal2019","cite":"Hjorth et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Hjorth, A. B. Justesen, T. Hirano et al.","title":"K2-290: a warm Jupiter and a mini-Neptune in a triple-star system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.03716","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2290c-Saha2025","cite":"Saha 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Suman Saha","title":"Updating the ephemeris and physical properties of five long-period transiting exoplanets using TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01768","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1468 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from CARMENES and MAROON-X give TOI-1468 c a mass of 6.64 Earth masses and a radius of 2.06 Earth radii, so its density of 2.00 g/cm3 points to a rocky core with a hydrogen-helium gas envelope (Chaturvedi et al. 2022). Most of what is known about its sibling is from the James Webb Space Telescope, which watched three eclipses of TOI-1468 b at 15 microns (Meier Valdes et al. 2025). Interior models allow planet c to be a water world if its atmosphere is thin (Daspute et al. 2025).","system":"The two planets sit on opposite sides of the radius valley, a gap in the sizes of close-in planets, around an M3 dwarf (Chaturvedi et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether planet c is a water world or has a thick atmosphere is not known. With a thick atmosphere it could be too hot for liquid water (Daspute et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1468c-Chaturvedietal2022","cite":"Chaturvedi et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. Chaturvedi, P. Bluhm, E. Nagel et al.","title":"TOI-1468: A system of two transiting planets, a super-Earth and a mini-Neptune, on opposite sides of the radius valley","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10351","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1468c-Valdesetal2025","cite":"Valdes et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. A. Meier Valdes, B.-O. Demory, H. Diamond-Lowe et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Hot Rocks Survey II. TOI-1468 b fluxes (Meier Valdes+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1468c-Dasputeetal2025","cite":"Daspute et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mangesh Daspute, Amri Wandel, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Potential Interior Structures and Habitability of Super-Earth Exoplanets LHS 1140 b, K2-18 b, TOI-1452 b, and TOI-1468 c","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08476","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 21152 b":{"text":{"why":"The star was chosen because it shows a tiny acceleration in Gaia and Hipparcos astrometry, a sign of an unseen companion (Kuzuhara et al. 2022).","observed":"HIP 21152 B is a brown dwarf, the first one imaged in the Hyades star cluster (Franson et al. 2023). Its spectrum is consistent with the L/T transition, best fitted by an early T dwarf (Kuzuhara et al. 2022). Its dynamical mass is 24 Jupiter masses (Franson et al. 2023), or 27.8 Jupiter masses in another analysis (Kuzuhara et al. 2022). The orbit has a semi-major axis of 17.5 au (Kuzuhara et al. 2022). Models that include evolving clouds best reproduce the mass (Franson et al. 2023).","system":"A wide early-L dwarf near the hydrogen-burning limit moves with the star, at 7.9 × 10^4 au (Franson et al. 2023). Hyades membership gives the system a well-known age (Kuzuhara et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip21152b-Kuzuharaetal2022","cite":"Kuzuhara et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Masayuki Kuzuhara, Thayne Currie, Takuya Takarada et al.","title":"Direct-imaging Discovery and Dynamical Mass of a Substellar Companion Orbiting an Accelerating Hyades Sun-like Star with SCExAO/CHARIS","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02729","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip21152b-Fransonetal2023","cite":"Franson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kyle Franson, Brendan P. Bowler, Mariangela Bonavita et al.","title":"Astrometric Accelerations as Dynamical Beacons: Discovery and Characterization of HIP 21152 B, the First T-dwarf Companion in the Hyades","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09840","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 27894 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its close-in planet with an 18-day orbit. That planet has a minimum mass of 0.62 Jupiter masses and an orbital distance of 0.122 au (Moutou et al. 2005). The radial velocity changes were checked for activity effects (Moutou et al. 2005).","system":"The host star is a main-sequence star, and its planet supports the link between metal-rich stars and planets, especially close-in ones (Moutou et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd27894d-Moutouetal2005","cite":"Moutou et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Mayor, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IV. Three close-in planets around HD 2638, HD 27894 and HD 63454","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20052826","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-74 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-74 b has 0.95 Jupiter masses and a moderately bloated radius of 1.5 Jupiter radii. It orbits a slightly evolved F9 star in about 2 days (Hellier et al. 2015). The star is bright, V = 9.7, one of the brighter systems accessible to southern telescopes (Hellier et al. 2015). Four transits were later observed in 3 to 4 colours at once with MuSCAT2 (Luque et al. 2020), and the planet was among 25 hot Jupiters observed in eclipse with Hubble and Spitzer (Changeat et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp74b-Hellieretal2015","cite":"Hellier et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Three WASP-South Transiting Exoplanets: WASP-74b, WASP-83b, and WASP-89b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.6358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp74b-Luqueetal2020","cite":"Luque et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. Luque, N. Casasayas-Barris, H. Parviainen et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-74 griz<SUB>s</SUB> light curves (Luque+,","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36420050","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp74b-Changeatetal2022","cite":"Changeat et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Q. Changeat, B. Edwards, A. F. Al-Refaie et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: 25 hot-Jupiter properties from HST & Spitzer (Changeat+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.22600003","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 145377 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 145377 is one of six main-sequence stars found with long-period giant companions of 2 to 18 Jupiter masses and 100 to 2000 days (Moutou et al. 2009). The radial velocity changes were checked for effects of stellar activity, and the authors conclude they come from a planet (Moutou et al. 2009). Its system was also used as an example in a web tool for checking whether a small terrestrial planet could stay stable alongside a giant (Pilat-Lohinger & Eggl 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd145377b-Moutouetal2009","cite":"Moutou et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"C. Moutou, M. Mayor, G. Lo Curto et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XV. Six long-period giant planets around BD -17 0063, HD 20868, HD 73267, HD 131664, HD 145377, and HD 153950","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.4662","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd145377b-PilatLohingerEggl2011","cite":"Pilat-Lohinger & Eggl 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elke Pilat-Lohinger, Siegfried Eggl","title":"ExoStab: A www-tool to verify the dynamical stability of Extra-solar planets","journal":"Publications of the Astronomy Department of the Eotvos Lorand University","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-447 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-447 b has an extremely grazing transit: its impact parameter is 1.076, so only about 20% of the planet's disc crosses the star's disc (Lillo-Box et al. 2015). It is one of the few confirmed grazing transits and the first among Kepler's planets (Lillo-Box et al. 2015). The planet has about 1.37 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.65 Jupiter radii, but the radius is uncertain because of the grazing geometry (Lillo-Box et al. 2015).","system":"The star is an active G8V star, and the orbit is slightly eccentric, with eccentricity 0.123 (Lillo-Box et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler447b-LilloBoxetal2015","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, D. Barrado, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"Kepler-447b: a hot-Jupiter with an extremely grazing transit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03267","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"tau Cet h":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Imaging at 10 microns with the VISIR instrument was sensitive to a few Jupiter masses and found no planets around Tau Ceti (Pathak et al. 2021). Tau Ceti is the closest Sun-like star, about 10 billion years old and of type G8V (Di Folco et al. 2014).","system":"The star has the least massive exo-Kuiper belt detected so far, with a total mass only ten times that of the Kuiper belt (Di Folco et al. 2014). Herschel detected a ring-like structure at 15 au (Di Folco et al. 2014). Hot dust is also seen near the star (Di Folco et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the dust belt has been ground down steadily or was recently regenerated by an instability is not known (Di Folco et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tauceth-Pathaketal2021","cite":"Pathak et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Pathak, D. J. M. Petit dit de la Roche, M. Kasper et al.","title":"High-contrast imaging at ten microns: A search for exoplanets around Eps Indi A, Eps Eri, Tau Ceti, Sirius A, and Sirius B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tauceth-DiFolcoetal2014","cite":"Di Folco et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"E. Di Folco, J. Péricaud, J.-C. Augereau et al.","title":"Tau Ceti: our nearest cousin","journal":"SF2A-2014: Proceedings of the Annual meeting of the French Society of Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HAT-P-14 b is a 2.232 Jupiter-mass planet on a slightly eccentric orbit (eccentricity 0.107) with a near-grazing transit (Torres et al. 2010). Its orbit is retrograde: the sky-projected angle to the star's spin is 189.1 degrees (Winn et al. 2011). Models suggest the radius matches a heavy-element fraction of about 50 Earth masses (Torres et al. 2010). Multiband MuSCAT photometry found no variation in transit timing, duration or radius with wavelength (Fukui et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved mid-F star about 1.3 billion years old (Torres et al. 2010).","history":"Hotter stars have a wide range of orbit tilts, which suggests three-body dynamics and tidal dissipation shaped many close-in orbits (Winn et al. 2011). The orbit may also be tilted along the line of sight (Schlaufman 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp14b-Torresetal2010","cite":"Torres et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Torres, G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-14b: A 2.2 M <SUB>J</SUB> Exoplanet Transiting a Bright F Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.2211","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp14b-Winnetal2011","cite":"Winn et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson et al.","title":"Orbital Orientations of Exoplanets: HAT-P-4b is Prograde and HAT-P-14b is Retrograde","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1318","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp14b-Fukuietal2016","cite":"Fukui et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Akihiko Fukui, Norio Narita, Yui Kawashima et al.","title":"Demonstrating High-precision, Multiband Transit Photometry with MuSCAT: A Case for HAT-P-14b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.03997","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp14b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-44 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-44 b was announced with three other short-period giants of 0.26 to 0.90 Jupiter masses (Brahm et al. 2018). The four planets all have radii of about one Jupiter radius, so their bulk densities differ widely (Brahm et al. 2018). The host star HATS-44 is notable for a high metal content, with [Fe/H] of 0.320 (Brahm et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats44b-Brahmetal2018","cite":"Brahm et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Brahm, J. D. Hartman, A. Jordán et al.","title":"HATS-43b, HATS-44b, HATS-45b, and HATS-46b: Four Short-period Transiting Giant Planets in the Neptune-Jupiter Mass Range","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.07093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 79181 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 79181 was one of 57 evolved G-type giant stars watched for three years in a Doppler survey aimed at finding planets around intermediate-mass stars late in their lives (Sato et al. 2005). Its velocity was found to vary in a regular, repeating way, one of only three stars in the sample to do so (Sato et al. 2005). Most targets in that survey were steady to within about 10 to 20 metres per second (Sato et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":"Little is known about the planet itself beyond its repeating velocity signal; the survey left a detailed discussion of these stars to separate studies (Sato et al. 2005)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd79181b-Satoetal2005","cite":"Sato et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eiji Kambe, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Radial-Velocity Variability of G-Type Giants: First Three Years of the Okayama Planet Search Program","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/57.1.97","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 99109 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 99109 b was published in a catalogue of 172 known planets around stars within 200 parsecs, where it was one of five planets reported for the first time (Butler et al. 2006). The catalogue also re-reduced old Lick, Keck and Anglo-Australian velocity measurements to make them more precise (Butler et al. 2006). A separate computer study of fictitious Earth-like bodies sharing a giant planet's orbit found that the HD 99109 system could keep such a companion on a stable path (Schwarz et al. 2007).","system":"Orbit simulations found that an Earth-mass body could stay stable in the same orbit as the giant, ahead of or behind it, in only seven known systems, and HD 99109 is one of them (Schwarz et al. 2007). Whether any such companion exists is not known.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd99109b-Butleretal2006","cite":"Butler et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"R. P. Butler, J. T. Wright, G. W. Marcy et al.","title":"Catalog of Nearby Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0607493","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd99109b-Schwarzetal2007","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. Schwarz, R. Dvorak, Á. Süli et al.","title":"Survey of the stability region of hypothetical habitable Trojan planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20077994","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-11 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a warm, lightly irradiated giant: it receives an incident flux of 1.9 x 10^8 erg per second per square centimetre, making it the third least irradiated transiting planet known at the time (West et al. 2009).","observed":"Its host is an early to mid K dwarf, among the smallest, least massive and faintest stars known to host a transiting planet at the time (West et al. 2009). Follow-up transit timing over 2008 to 2012 found a circular orbit and no sign of timing changes that would hint at other planets (Wang et al. 2014). Spectra taken during transit, using the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, were used to measure how the planet's orbit lines up with the star's spin (Mancini et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"Orbit alignment is used as a clue to how hot giants migrated inward from far out in their discs (Mancini et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp11b-Westetal2009","cite":"West et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"R. G. West, A. Collier Cameron, L. Hebb et al.","title":"The sub-Jupiter mass transiting exoplanet WASP-11b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4597","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp11b-Wangetal2014","cite":"Wang et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Xiao-bin Wang, Sheng-hong Gu, Andrew Collier Cameron et al.","title":"The Refined Physical Properties of Transiting Exoplanetary System WASP-11/HAT-P-10","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-6256/147/4/92","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp11b-Mancinietal2015","cite":"Mancini et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. VIII. Observations of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and characterisation of the transiting planetary systems HAT-P-36 and WASP-11/HAT-P-10","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.01787","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-27 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"NGTS-27 b orbits a slightly evolved G3-type star and is described as a fairly ordinary hot Jupiter (Bouchy et al. 2024). Its radius of 1.40 Jupiter radii is larger than expected for a planet of 0.59 Jupiter masses, which the discovery team calls anomalously inflated (Bouchy et al. 2024). Its transits were re-observed and confirmed with a 1.0-m telescope in South Africa, the 1.2-m Euler telescope and the TESS spacecraft, and its mass came from three spectrographs (Bouchy et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Why strongly irradiated giants like this one have swollen radii is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts27b-Bouchyetal2024","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Bouchy, E. Gillen, O. Turner et al.","title":"NGTS discovery of a highly inflated Saturn-mass planet and a highly irradiated hot Jupiter. NGTS-26 b and NGTS-27 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202347162","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-836.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-836 is a bright, nearby K dwarf with low metal content and a high proper motion (Hawthorn et al. 2023). The planet is the outer of two, a mini-Neptune of 2.59 Earth radii whose mass of 9.6 Earth masses came from radial velocities (Hawthorn et al. 2023). Its transits arrive about 20 minutes early or late in a way that changes over time (Hawthorn et al. 2023). The inner planet shows no such shifts (Hawthorn et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-836 b, is a 1.70 Earth-radius super-Earth in a 3.82-day orbit, sitting inside the so-called radius valley, the scarce range of sizes between rocky planets and puffier ones (Hawthorn et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The timing shifts of the outer planet may be caused by an undetected planet farther out (Hawthorn et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi83601-Hawthornetal2023","cite":"Hawthorn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Faith Hawthorn, Daniel Bayliss, Thomas G. Wilson et al.","title":"TOI-836: A super-Earth and mini-Neptune transiting a nearby K-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07328","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 170469 b":{"text":{"why":"The star was first picked for a survey of metal-rich stars, and it shows the supersolar metal content typical of such targets (Fischer et al. 2007).","observed":"HD 170469 is a G5 subgiant. Its planet has a minimum mass of 0.67 Jupiter masses and a 3.13-year orbit (Fischer et al. 2007). The star first showed little velocity scatter in three early visits, and only follow-up observations over about three years revealed the longer-period signal (Fischer et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd170469b-Fischeretal2007","cite":"Fischer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Steven S. Vogt, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Five Intermediate-Period Planets from the N2K Sample","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.1191","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 160691 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Repeated velocity data from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search were re-analysed with a genetic algorithm and orbital stability tests (Goździewski et al. 2007). Both Jupiter-like companions found early were on nearly circular orbits (Goździewski et al. 2007). Later computer work followed the system's orbits over one million years, noting that without known orbital tilts the planets' true masses are only lower limits (Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the fourth planet exists is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd160691b-PerminovKuznetsov2019","cite":"Perminov & Kuznetsov 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Alexander Perminov, Eduard Kuznetsov","title":"Dynamical evolution of extrasolar planetary systems HD 141399, HD 160691 and HD 39194","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-782 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-782 b is a sub-Neptune orbiting a small red dwarf, found along with three similar planets around other M dwarfs (Hori et al. 2024). Doppler measurements with the Subaru infrared spectrograph gave only an upper limit on its mass, below 19.1 Earth masses at 2 sigma (Hori et al. 2024). A second analysis of the same data gave a mass of about 9.2 Earth masses, with a large error (Clark 2024). Its size and mass together point to volatile material in the interior, such as a thick atmosphere or an icy mantle over a rocky core (Hori et al. 2024). The planet orbits on an eccentric path (Clark 2024).","system":"","history":"Sub-Neptunes sit above the radius valley, and the four planets in the discovery work are thought to keep a sizeable atmosphere or ice layer instead of losing it (Hori et al. 2024).","open":"The planet's true composition, atmosphere or water world, is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi782b-Horietal2024","cite":"Hori et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Yasunori Hori, Akihiko Fukui, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"The Discovery and Follow-up of Four Transiting Short-period Sub-Neptunes Orbiting M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12637","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi782b-Clark2024","cite":"Clark 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Catherine Clark","title":"The Discovery of Four Eccentric Sub-Neptunes Close to M dwarfs from TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.FCEX98","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-357 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from K2-OjOS, a project in which 10 amateur astronomers searched by eye through 20,427 light curves from K2 campaign 18 (Castro-González et al. 2022).","observed":"The amateurs found 42 candidate signals, and the project statistically validated four new planets, including K2-357 b (Castro-González et al. 2022). Follow-up used archived photometry and spectra, new high-resolution images to rule out nearby stars, and a search for timing variations in the transits (Castro-González et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2357b-CastroGonzlezetal2022","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. Castro-González, E. Díez Alonso, J. Menéndez Blanco et al.","title":"The K2-OjOS Project: New and revisited planets and candidates in K2 campaigns 5, 16, & 18","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03346","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5916 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets in tight orbits around low-mass stars are hard to explain: in the most extreme cases their existence cannot be reconciled with current models of core accretion, the slow build-up of a rocky core followed by gas (Dransfield et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5916 b is one of five giant planets reported by MANGOS, a programme dedicated to searching for giant planets, brown dwarfs and low-mass stars around M dwarfs (Dransfield et al. 2026). The five planets have radii of 0.99 to 1.12 Jupiter radii and masses of 0.49 to 1.69 Jupiter masses (Dransfield et al. 2026). A separate team confirmed TOI-5916 b with ground-based photometry and Habitable-zone Planet Finder velocities, and found that it is a Saturn-density planet orbiting an M2 dwarf, with a radius of 1.05 Jupiter radii (O'Brien et al. 2026). The MANGOS planets are suitable targets for later measurements of their orbit tilts and atmospheres (Dransfield et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5916b-Dransfieldetal2026","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Dransfield, M. Timmermans, D. Sebastian et al.","title":"MANGOS ─ II. Five new giant planets orbiting low-mass stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11528","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5916b-OBrienetal2026","cite":"O'Brien et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Shane O'Brien, Amber Wong, Te Han et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-5916 b & TOI-6158 b are Two Saturn-density Planets Orbiting M2 Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11798","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 93385 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 93385 was one of five Sun-like stars monitored for more than 10 years with the HARPS spectrograph, densely enough to detect super-Earths on orbits up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021). The number of planets per star was decided by Bayesian model comparison, a statistical way to weigh one planet count against another, using nested sampling (Unger et al. 2021). For some stars, different models of stellar activity noise were tried to tell planet signals from starspot effects (Unger et al. 2021). Together the five stars gave 12 super-Earths (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd93385d-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-49 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-49 b is a warm giant planet with a mass of 0.353 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.765 Jupiter radii, orbiting a main-sequence K dwarf of 0.7133 solar masses (Hartman et al. 2020). It was announced together with three similar warm giants around K dwarfs (Hartman et al. 2020). The host star is faint, at V magnitude 14.998 (Hartman et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats49b-Hartmanetal2020","cite":"Hartman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. D. Hartman, Andrés Jordán, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-47b, HATS-48Ab, HATS-49b, and HATS-72b: Four Warm Giant Planets Transiting K Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2016-BLG-1337L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found as a short blip on top of the brightening pattern from a pair of low-mass stars acting as a lens (Han et al. 2026). A model with three lensing bodies fits much better than one with a second background star (Han et al. 2026). Two models fit nearly equally well: a planet of about 0.3 Jupiter masses about 4 au from the heavier star, or one of about 7 Jupiter masses about 1.5 au from it (Han et al. 2026). The lens lies about 7 kiloparsecs away toward the Galactic bulge (Han et al. 2026).","system":"The planet orbits within a binary of two early M dwarfs of about 0.54 and 0.40 solar masses, separated by about 3.5 au in projection (Han et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2016blg1337lb-Hanetal2026","cite":"Han et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"KMT-2016-BLG-1337L: A Saturn-mass Planet Orbiting Within a Binary System of Low-mass Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12610","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-19 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets of similar mass can have very different radii, and low-density ones are generally assumed to be inflated by their closeness to the star; finding the cause needs many planets with precisely measured masses and radii (Guenther et al. 2012).","observed":"CoRoT-19 b is a low-density gas giant orbiting an old, magnetically inactive F9 star (Guenther et al. 2012). Alternative explanations for the transit signal were ruled out, and the mass came from four spectrographs (Guenther et al. 2012). Extra transits were watched with the TRAPPIST and Euler telescopes to sharpen the timing of future transits (Guenther et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot19b-Guentheretal2012","cite":"Guenther et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"E. W. Guenther, R. F. Díaz, J.-C. Gazzano et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXI. CoRoT-19b: a low density planet orbiting an old inactive F9V-star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.1035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-418 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-418 b was the first exoplanet confirmed mainly by its transit colour signature, meaning that the dip in starlight was measured at two colours (Tingley et al. 2014). A transit that has the same depth at both colours can only come from a compact, dark body, which ruled out a contaminating eclipsing binary or hierarchical triple (Tingley et al. 2014). The star was found to be blended with an unseen light source that adds about 30 percent to its brightness (Tingley et al. 2014). Correcting for it raised the planet-to-star radius ratio to 0.110, over 25 percent more than the 0.087 measured by Kepler (Tingley et al. 2014). Radial velocities put an upper limit of about 1 Jupiter mass on the planet (Tingley et al. 2014).","system":"The system is one with two proposed planets, and this work confirms one of them (Tingley et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler418b-Tingleyetal2014","cite":"Tingley et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"B. Tingley, H. Parviainen, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Confirmation of an exoplanet using the transit color signature: Kepler-418b, a blended giant planet in a multiplanet system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.5354","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-80 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-80 b transits a cool K7 to M0 dwarf and produces one of the largest transit depths yet reported, which makes it a good target for studying its atmosphere with transmission spectroscopy (Triaud et al. 2013). At the time, WASP-80 was one of only two systems known with a hot Jupiter transiting an M dwarf (Mancini et al. 2014). Spitzer measurements of the planet passing behind its star show a dayside whose colours resemble those of a T4 dwarf, a cool brown dwarf (Triaud et al. 2015). Its transmission spectrum is flat, with no sign of changing radius across wavelengths (Triaud et al. 2015; Mancini et al. 2014). The star shows strong chromospheric activity but no detected starspots (Mancini et al. 2014). X-ray observations place WASP-80 b among the most strongly irradiated planets of a six-planet sample, and its transit was also detected in the near-ultraviolet (King et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The measured spin of the star does not match the Rossiter-McLaughlin amplitude, which fits either an orbit nearly perpendicular to the star's spin or an unknown extra source of line broadening (Triaud et al. 2013). Which one is right is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp80b-Triaudetal2013","cite":"Triaud et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"A. H. M. J. Triaud, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-80b: a gas giant transiting a cool dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.0254","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp80b-Mancinietal2014","cite":"Mancini et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"L. Mancini, J. Southworth, S. Ciceri et al.","title":"Physical properties and transmission spectrum of the WASP-80 planetary system from multi-colour photometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.4982","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp80b-Triaudetal2015","cite":"Triaud et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Michaël Gillon, David Ehrenreich et al.","title":"WASP-80b has a dayside within the T-dwarf range","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.08152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp80b-Kingetal2018","cite":"King et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"George W. King, Peter J. Wheatley, Michael Salz et al.","title":"The XUV environments of exoplanets from Jupiter-size to super-Earth","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.11124","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":34,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"6 Lyn b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"6 Lyn is a K0 subgiant of 1.7 solar masses, the least evolved of the four hosts in the discovery report, and its planet has a minimum mass of 2.4 Jupiter masses in a nearly circular orbit (Sato et al. 2008). Like the other three hosts, the star is thought to have been an early F-type or A-type dwarf while on the main sequence (Sato et al. 2008). All four hosts have solar or lower metal content (Sato et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-6lynb-Satoetal2008","cite":"Sato et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eri Toyota, Masashi Omiya et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: 14 Andromedae, 81 Ceti, 6 Lyncis, and HD167042","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.0268","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86950 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 86950 is a K giant observed with three instruments, and its planet has a minimum mass of 3.6 Jupiter masses at 2.72 au (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). The discovery team calls it typical of the planets found around evolved stars (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). It comes from the Pan-Pacific Planet Search of 164 evolved stars, whose results confirm that planets are more common around stars richer in heavy elements (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86950b-Wittenmyeretal2017","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, M. I. Jones, Jinglin Zhao et al.","title":"The Pan-Pacific Planet Search. VI. Giant Planets Orbiting HD 86950 and HD 222076","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.07648","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2364 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"One transit was watched with the Keck Planet Finder spectrograph on 22 October 2023, with 31 exposures over 4.5 hours covering the full transit and about 2 hours afterwards (Tamburo et al. 2026). A telescope in Arizona caught the transit's start at the same time, and the Tierras telescope monitored the star from October 2024 to March 2025 (Tamburo et al. 2026). TESS saw the star in several sectors, beginning in 2018 (Tamburo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2364b-Tamburoetal2026","cite":"Tamburo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Tamburo, S. W. Yee, J. Garcia-Mejia et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS & KPF: TOI-2364 light & radial velocity curves (Tamburo+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158259 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 158259 is a bright star with a V magnitude of 6.4 observed 290 times with the SOPHIE spectrograph (Hara et al. 2020). Together with TESS, the data reveal six planets: an inner transiting super-Earth and five planets of about 6 Earth masses on orbits of 3.4, 5.2, 7.9, 12 and 17.4 days; this planet is the one on the 5.2-day orbit (Hara et al. 2020). A later study corrected small instrumental drifts in the same SOPHIE data, using pressure and temperature readings from the instrument (Grouffal et al. 2024).","system":"The outer five planets form a compact chain, with neighbouring orbits close to a 3:2 period ratio (Hara et al. 2020). The 17.4-day signal is only classed as a candidate, because it lies close to the star's expected rotation period (Hara et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158259d-Haraetal2020","cite":"Hara et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. C. Hara, F. Bouchy, M. Stalport et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVI. HD 158259: A compact planetary system in a near-3:2 mean motion resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd158259d-Grouffaletal2024","cite":"Grouffal et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Grouffal, A. Santerne, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 158259 SOPHIE correction of RV systematics (Grouffal+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-125 c":{"text":{"why":"A JWST observing programme was designed to compare the carbon-to-oxygen ratios of planets b and c with that of their star, to tell where in the disc they formed and whether they moved across the snow line (Fisher et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-125 c is a mini-Neptune of 2.759 Earth radii and 6.63 Earth masses measured with HARPS, the puffiest of the three planets (Nielsen et al. 2020). Its host is a V = 11.0 K0 dwarf (Nielsen et al. 2020). The planned JWST observations would use a single transit of each of planets b and c with the NIRSpec instrument, since the planets have a similar size and gravity and likely formed in the same disc (Fisher et al. 2023).","system":"Planet b, with a 4.65-day period, is near a 2:1 orbital resonance with planet c at 9.15 days (Nielsen et al. 2020). Planets b and d have unusually high eccentricities of 0.19 and 0.17 (Nielsen et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi125c-Fisheretal2023","cite":"Fisher et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Chloe Fisher, Can Jan Akin, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-125: Comparative Atmospheric Chemistry Within One System","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 2","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi125c-Nielsenetal2020","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, D. Gandolfi, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"Mass determinations of the three mini-Neptunes transiting TOI-125","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08834","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 108236 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The HD 108236 system was observed by the CHEOPS space telescope during the transits of planets b, c, d, e and f (Hoyer et al. 2022). Earlier CHEOPS visits lasted about 17 to 18 hours each, and three of them were gathered during testing and routine operations (Bonfanti et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd108236c-Hoyeretal2022","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Hoyer, A. Bonfanti, A. Leleu et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 108236 system observed with CHEOPS and TESS (Hoyer+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd108236c-Bonfantietal2021","cite":"Bonfanti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Bonfanti, L. Delrez, M. J. Hooton et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 108236 CHEOPS light curves (Bonfanti+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36460157","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 39017 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HIP 39017 b is a companion at the border between a massive planet and a low-mass brown dwarf, around a young star that pulsates as a gamma Doradus variable (Tobin et al. 2024). Its near-infrared spectrum and photometry place it at the L/T transition, where brown dwarf atmospheres change from cloudy to clearer, and cloudy models fit it significantly better than cloudless ones (Tobin et al. 2024). Orbit fitting with star motion from Hipparcos and Gaia gives a semimajor axis of about 23.8 au and a dynamical mass of about 30 Jupiter masses, most consistent with a low-mass brown dwarf (Tobin et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Luminosity models give a lower mass of about 13.8 Jupiter masses because the star is young, no older than about 115 million years (Tobin et al. 2024). The star's pulsations and later Gaia data should help pin down its age and the companion's mass (Tobin et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip39017b-Tobinetal2024","cite":"Tobin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Taylor L. Tobin, Thayne Currie, Yiting Li et al.","title":"Direct-imaging Discovery of a Substellar Companion Orbiting the Accelerating Variable Star HIP 39017","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.04000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-30 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-30 d is a super-puff, a sub-Saturn-mass planet with an extremely low density, below about 0.3 grams per cubic centimetre (Marston et al. 2026). Interior models of 34 cold super-puffs found it, with five others, inconsistent with core accretion theory, which calls for a nonstandard explanation such as rings (Marston et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Core accretion-compatible solutions become possible for most of these planets if an extra heat source is present (Marston et al. 2026).","open":"Whether the planet's low density comes from extra heating, rings, or something else is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler30d-Marstonetal2026","cite":"Marston et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nicholas T. Marston, Juliette Becker, Alex R. Howe","title":"Do Super-puffs Defy Core Accretion? Population-wide Interior Structure Constraints","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11330","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 150706 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found in a follow-up of stars first monitored with the older ELODIE spectrograph, using data spanning close to 15 years to detect long-period planets (Boisse et al. 2012).","observed":"HD 150706 b is a Jupiter-analogue candidate on a 16.1-year orbit at about 6.7 au, with a minimum mass of 2.71 Jupiter masses (Boisse et al. 2012). The host is an active G0 dwarf with a surrounding dust debris disc, and the effect of its starspots on the velocities was partially corrected (Boisse et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd150706b-Boisseetal2012","cite":"Boisse et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"I. Boisse, F. Pepe, C. Perrier et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. V. Follow-up of ELODIE candidates: Jupiter-analogs around Sun-like stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.5835","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1347 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was followed with 120 high-resolution spectra from the HIRES spectrograph on the Keck I telescope, between November 2019 and July 2022, as part of the TESS-Keck Survey (Rubenzahl et al. 2024). Another 14 spectra came from HARPS-N (Rubenzahl et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1347c-Rubenzahletal2024","cite":"Rubenzahl et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"R. A. Rubenzahl, F. Dai, A. W. Howard et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS-Keck Survey. XII. TOI-1347 radial velocity (Rubenzahl+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 357 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 357 d does not transit its star, so only its minimum mass is known, and the real mass may be higher (Kane & Fetherolf 2023). It lies in the habitable zone, receiving about 0.38 times Earth's irradiation, slightly less than Mars gets (Kaltenegger et al. 2019). Models of possible conditions give a frozen rocky world with Earth-like volcanic outgassing, but more carbon dioxide could allow surface water (Kaltenegger et al. 2019). Dynamical simulations suggest that planet d is more likely a source of orbital instability for other possible terrestrial planets in the habitable zone than a habitable world itself (Kane & Fetherolf 2023).","system":"The star hosts three planets with periods of 3.93, 9.13 and 55.7 days, with planet b the only known transiting one (Jenkins et al. 2019; Kaltenegger et al. 2019). The system is probably close to coplanar and stable over millions of years, and orbit analysis caps the mass of planet d at 11.20 Earth masses (Jenkins et al. 2019). The red dwarf host is extremely inactive in X-rays and at least 5 billion years old (Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Whether planet d is rocky or has a thick gas envelope, and whether it could hold liquid water, is not known (Kane & Fetherolf 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj357d-KaneFetherolf2023","cite":"Kane & Fetherolf 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Tara Fetherolf","title":"GJ 357 d: Potentially Habitable World or Agent of Chaos?","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357d-Kalteneggeretal2019","cite":"Kaltenegger et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Kaltenegger, J. Madden, Z. Lin et al.","title":"The Habitability of GJ 357D: Possible Climate and Observability","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.13215","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357d-Jenkinsetal2019","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, F. J. Pozuelos, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"GJ 357: a low-mass planetary system uncovered by precision radial velocities and dynamical simulations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.00831","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357d-ModirroustaGalianetal2020","cite":"Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. Modirrousta-Galian, B. Stelzer, E. Magaudda et al.","title":"GJ 357 b. A super-Earth orbiting an extremely inactive host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.10262","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 6434 b":{"text":{"why":"Survey work with the CORALIE spectrograph placed it among 16 new planet candidates, as one of the shorter-period planets with fairly low eccentricity (Mayor et al. 2004).","observed":"Because the orbit is seen from an unknown angle, the planet might pass in front of its star. The chance of that was calculated at 4%, with a predicted transit lasting about 6 hours (Hinkel et al. 2015). Telescopes at Cerro Tololo in Chile watched 75.4% of the predicted transit window. No transit was seen. A full transit deeper than 0.9% is ruled out, and a grazing one deeper than 1.6% (Hinkel et al. 2015). Its orbit is slightly oval, with an eccentricity of 0.146 (Hinkel et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":"Only a minimum mass is known, because the tilt of the orbit is not measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd6434b-Mayoretal2004","cite":"Mayor et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mayor, S. Udry, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XII. Orbital solutions for 16 extra-solar planets discovered with CORALIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0310316","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd6434b-Hinkeletal2015","cite":"Hinkel et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Natalie R. Hinkel, Stephen R. Kane, Genady Pilyavsky et al.","title":"A New Analysis of the Exoplanet Hosting System HD 6434","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.01746","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1450 A b":{"text":{"why":"It belongs to a survey that measures masses of transiting planets around M dwarfs (small, cool red stars) within 30 parsecs (Brady et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was observed 118 times with the MAROON-X spectrograph on the Gemini-North telescope between April 2021 and July 2023, and its mass was measured to nearly 10% precision (Brady et al. 2024). It is the second-lowest-mass transiting planet with a high-precision mass measurement (Brady et al. 2024). Its density is compatible with an Earth-like composition, slightly below that of Earth (Brady et al. 2024). Because it is heated strongly by its star, the James Webb Space Telescope could test whether it has kept an atmosphere (Brady et al. 2024).","system":"TOI-1450 is a binary system 22.5 parsecs away. The planet orbits the primary star, an M3 dwarf, and a companion star of roughly 3000 K lies in the same system (Brady et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet still holds any atmosphere is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1450ab-Bradyetal2024","cite":"Brady et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Brady, J. L. Bean, A. Seifahrt et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-1450 radial velocities & activity indicators (Brady+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 8799 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has been found in older images that were reanalysed. Hubble Space Telescope data from 1998 show it, with a position consistent with a circular orbit of about 70 AU and low inclination (Lafrenière et al. 2009). Subaru images from 2002 show it too, and confirm it moves counterclockwise around the star (Fukagawa et al. 2009). Its brightness in the near-infrared fits an atmosphere model with intermediate-depth clouds (Lafrenière et al. 2009). Its brightness and colours resemble those of the young planet-mass object 2M1207b, and cloudy atmospheres with low gravity can explain both (Barman et al. 2011).","system":"When three planets had been imaged, they were 5 to 13 Jupiter masses and lay 24 to 68 AU from the star in projection (Lafrenière et al. 2009). The star is a lambda Bootis star, a type with weak metal lines, and it pulses as a gamma Doradus variable. It also shows extra infrared light from a dust shell or disc (Gray & Kaye 1999).","history":"Planets this far out are hard to form. Simulations suggest that building cores beyond 35 AU by core accretion fails, and that planet-planet scattering does not leave stable wide systems like this one. Gravitational instability in the disc is favoured (Dodson-Robinson et al. 2009). For the outermost planet, that route would need about 0.1 solar masses of disc inside its orbit (Nero & Bjorkman 2009).","open":"How the planets formed is still debated."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-Lafrenireetal2009","cite":"Lafrenière et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"David Lafrenière, Christian Marois, René Doyon et al.","title":"HST/NICMOS Detection of HR 8799 b in 1998","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.3247","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-Fukagawaetal2009","cite":"Fukagawa et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Misato Fukagawa, Yoichi Itoh, Motohide Tamura et al.","title":"H-Band Image of a Planetary Companion Around HR 8799 in 2002","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.1919","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-Barmanetal2011","cite":"Barman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Travis S. Barman, Bruce Macintosh, Quinn M. Konopacky et al.","title":"The Young Planet-mass Object 2M1207b: A Cool, Cloudy, and Methane-poor Atmosphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1201","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-GrayKaye1999","cite":"Gray & Kaye 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Richard O. Gray, Anthony B. Kaye","title":"HR 8799: A Link between γ Doradus Variables and λ Bootis Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/301134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-DodsonRobinsonetal2009","cite":"Dodson-Robinson et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Sarah E. Dodson-Robinson, Dimitri Veras, Eric B. Ford et al.","title":"The Formation Mechanism of Gas Giants on Wide Orbits","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.2662","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799b-NeroBjorkman2009","cite":"Nero & Bjorkman 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Nero, J. E. Bjorkman","title":"Did Fomalhaut, HR 8799, and HL Tauri Form Planets Via the Gravitational Instability? Placing Limits on the Required Disk Masses","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1108","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-438 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of 12 small Kepler planets, selected because they were small and possibly in the habitable zone, the range of distances where liquid water could exist (Torres et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet's pull on its star is too small to measure, so its mass could not be confirmed that way. Instead it was confirmed statistically, by simulating many false-alarm cases and comparing them with the Kepler brightness data, with extra spectra and high-resolution images of the star (Torres et al. 2015). Its host star flares violently. Superflares were seen in the star's light curve, and the planet has the highest Earth Similarity Index known so far (Armstrong et al. 2016).","system":"The planet orbits 0.166 au from its star (Armstrong et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Flares and mass ejections from the star could strip the atmosphere. One study found that Kepler-438b was the only one of six well-known habitable-zone planets that might keep its atmosphere against these ejections (Samara et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler438b-Torresetal2015","cite":"Torres et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Guillermo Torres, David M. Kipping, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Validation of 12 Small Kepler Transiting Planets in the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01101","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler438b-Armstrongetal2016","cite":"Armstrong et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Armstrong, C. E. Pugh, A.-M. Broomhall et al.","title":"The host stars of Kepler's habitable exoplanets: superflares, rotation and activity","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05306","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler438b-Samaraetal2021","cite":"Samara et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Evangelia Samara, Spiros Patsourakos, Manolis K. Georgoulis","title":"A Readily Implemented Atmosphere Sustainability Constraint for Terrestrial Exoplanets Orbiting Magnetically Active Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.07837","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110014 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a cool, evolved K giant star. The star has a mass between 1.9 and 2.4 solar masses and is slightly rich in metals (de Medeiros et al. 2009). A reanalysis of archived spectra confirmed the planet but gave different orbital values from those first published (Soto et al. 2015). The same work found a second planet around the star (Soto et al. 2015). Orbit calculations over 100 million years show that the system stays stable within the uncertainties of the earlier solution (Marshall et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110014b-deMedeirosetal2009","cite":"de Medeiros et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. R. de Medeiros, J. Setiawan, A. P. Hatzes et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 110014 (De Medeiros+, 2009)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35040617","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110014b-Sotoetal2015","cite":"Soto et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. G. Soto, J. S. Jenkins, M. I. Jones","title":"RAFT - I. Discovery of new planetary candidates and updated orbits from archival FEROS spectra","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.04796","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110014b-Marshalletal2020","cite":"Marshall et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. P. Marshall, J. Horner, R. A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"Stability analysis of three exoplanet systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13197","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-16 b":{"text":{"why":"It was reported together with three other hot Jupiters, all on orbits shorter than 5 days around Sun-like stars (Tilbrook et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet orbits very close to its star, between 0.039 and 0.052 au like its three siblings, and receives intense radiation (Tilbrook et al. 2021). That puts it in the range where hot Jupiters swell to larger sizes than expected. Comparison with statistical studies shows NGTS-16b is likely inflated in this way (Tilbrook et al. 2021). Current models of inflation do not agree with the data perfectly (Tilbrook et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"What controls radius inflation is still poorly understood (Tilbrook et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts16b-Tilbrooketal2021","cite":"Tilbrook et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Rosanna H. Tilbrook, Matthew R. Burleigh, Jean C. Costes et al.","title":"NGTS 15b, 16b, 17b, and 18b: four hot Jupiters from the Next-Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10302","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2019-BLG-1053L b":{"text":{"why":"Its signal sat unnoticed in survey data until a systematic search of the leftover residuals, after a standard fit, was run on the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network observations (Zang et al. 2021).","observed":"The planetary signal gives the lowest planet-to-host mass ratio of any planetary caustic found to date, 1.25 x 10^-5 (Zang et al. 2021). Modelling gives a planet of about 2.5 Earth masses around a host of about 0.6 solar masses (Zang et al. 2021). They are about 3.4 au apart in projection, and the system lies about 6.8 kiloparsecs away (Zang et al. 2021). These numbers are statistical estimates, since the host star is not seen directly.","system":"","history":"","open":"The host star's light has not been separated out, so its properties are estimated rather than measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2019blg1053lb-Zangetal2021","cite":"Zang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Weicheng Zang, Kyu-Ha Hwang, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. I. OGLE-2019-BLG-1053Lb, a Buried Terrestrial Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.11880","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3082 b":{"text":{"why":"It was one of 11 planets validated in a project that checks TESS candidates statistically instead of by measuring their masses (Mistry et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet was confirmed by examining TESS light curves and calculating the odds of a false alarm with a statistical tool (Mistry et al. 2023). Its star is a K-type dwarf. The planet is listed among those well suited to emission spectroscopy, which measures the light the planet gives off (Mistry et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass has not been measured from the star's motion in this work."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3082b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Aniket Prasad et al.","title":"VaTEST. II. Statistical Validation of 11 TESS-detected Exoplanets Orbiting K-type Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-12 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-density sub-Saturn orbiting a metal-poor K dwarf, with a mean density of 0.295 g/cm^3 (Hartman et al. 2009). James Webb Space Telescope spectra detected carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide and water in its atmosphere, at 12.2, 4.1 and 6.0 sigma (Crouzet et al. 2025). The amounts fit an atmosphere with about 10 times the Sun's metal content, with carbon dioxide made by light-driven chemistry (Crouzet et al. 2025). Methane is not detected and seems to be lacking (Crouzet et al. 2025). Earlier work found a hazy or cloudy veil reaching to low pressures (Barstow et al. 2017). Warm Spitzer observations failed to catch the planet's eclipse, setting upper limits (Todorov et al. 2013).","system":"The host is a faint, metal-poor K4 dwarf (Hartman et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"Missing methane could mean a hot interior with strong vertical mixing, or something else (Crouzet et al. 2025). Weak eclipses might also reflect a highly eccentric orbit (Todorov et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp12b-Hartmanetal2009","cite":"Hartman et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-12b: A Low-Density Sub-Saturn Mass Planet Transiting a Metal-Poor K Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0904.4704","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp12b-Crouzetetal2025","cite":"Crouzet et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Crouzet, B. Edwards, T. Konings et al.","title":"Detection of CO<SUB>2</SUB>, CO, and H<SUB>2</SUB>O in the atmosphere of the warm sub-Saturn HAT-P-12 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.18722","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp12b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp12b-Todorovetal2013","cite":"Todorov et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Kamen O. Todorov, Drake Deming, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Photometry of Three Hot Jupiters: HAT-P-3b, HAT-P-4b and HAT-P-12b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.0833","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 200964 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this system studies how the two planets tug on each other, tracking changes in their orbit sizes and eccentricities over time (Mia & Kushvah 2016). An analytic solution was compared with a numerical one to check it. The resonant angles librate, meaning they swing around a steady value. The authors conclude that the two planets are in a 4:3 mean motion resonance, in which they complete four and three orbits in the same time (Mia & Kushvah 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd200964c-MiaKushvah2016","cite":"Mia & Kushvah 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Rajib Mia, Badam Singh Kushvah","title":"Orbital dynamics of exoplanetary systems Kepler-62, HD 200964 and Kepler-11","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02110","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"PDS 70 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a young giant of about 7 Jupiter masses, sitting 22 au from its star, well inside a gap in the surrounding disc, and it is still growing by taking in gas (Chauvin et al. 2020). Keck spectra detected carbon monoxide and water in its atmosphere. Its spin rate is below 29 km/s, and its carbon-to-oxygen ratio is below 0.63, matching the star (Hsu et al. 2024). The gas in the outer disc has a much higher ratio. The planet's makeup may be dominated by dust and ice rather than disc gas (Hsu et al. 2024).","system":"The 5-million-year-old system is the only one known with protoplanets inside the cavity of the disc they formed from (Hsu et al. 2024). A second planet, PDS 70 c, shares the same gap (Mosqueira 2026). ALMA radio images show a dust gap from 15 to 60 au (Long et al. 2018). The disc is quite flared, with a height of about 15% of its radius at 90 au (Wahhaj et al. 2024).","history":"A simulation shows that a growing planet of about 2.5 Jupiter masses can carve a gap like the observed one on its own, and may grow an eccentric orbit while doing so (Muley et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Chauvinetal2020","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Gael Chauvin, Miriam Keppler, André Muller et al.","title":"PDS70, witnessing a young solar system analog in formation with SPHERE/ALMA","journal":"Planets 2020, Ground and Space Observatories: a Joint Venture to Planetary Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4435565","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Hsuetal2024","cite":"Hsu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Chih-Chun Hsu, Jason J. Wang, Geoffrey A. Blake et al.","title":"PDS 70b Shows Stellar-like Carbon-to-oxygen Ratio","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15117","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Mosqueira2026","cite":"Mosqueira 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ignacio Mosqueira","title":"PDS 70 c and SR 12 c: Observational Constraints on Giant-Planet and Satellite Formation","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Longetal2018","cite":"Long et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Zachary C. Long, Eiji Akiyama, Michael Sitko et al.","title":"Differences in the Gas and Dust Distribution in the Transitional Disk of a Sun-like Young Star, PDS 70","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00529","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Wahhajetal2024","cite":"Wahhaj et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Z. Wahhaj, M. Benisty, C. Ginski et al.","title":"PDS 70 unveiled by star-hopping: Total intensity, polarimetry, and millimeter imaging modeled in concert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11641","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pds70b-Muleyetal2019","cite":"Muley et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Dhruv Muley, Jeffrey Fung, Nienke van der Marel","title":"PDS 70: A Transition Disk Sculpted by a Single Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.07191","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":16,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-663 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-663 d is one of three small transiting planets around an early M dwarf (Cointepas et al. 2024). Its radius is 1.92 Earth radii, and its mass is below 5.2 Earth masses at 99% confidence (Cointepas et al. 2024). The planet lies just above the radius valley, the size gap that separates rocky planets from volatile-rich ones (Cointepas et al. 2024). The three planets are all low-mass mini-Neptunes, too large to be rocky, so water or other volatiles likely dominate (Cointepas et al. 2024).","system":"The two siblings, b and c, have radii near 2.27 Earth radii and masses of 4.45 and 3.65 Earth masses. Orbit calculations show that the system is stable (Cointepas et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi663d-Cointepasetal2024","cite":"Cointepas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cointepas, F. Bouchy, J. M. Almenara et al.","title":"TOI-663: A newly discovered multi-planet system with three transiting mini-Neptunes orbiting an early M star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202346899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A full-orbit Hubble phase curve shows the planet's brightness as it circles its star. The dayside emits at 2894 K and the nightside is below 1430 K, a day-night contrast above 0.96 (Arcangeli et al. 2019). The brightest point is shifted only 4.5 degrees eastward (Arcangeli et al. 2019). Heat barely moves around the planet, which needs strong drag, possibly from a magnetic field as weak as 10 gauss (Arcangeli et al. 2019). Its reflectivity is low, below 0.16, and the data can be explained by thermal emission alone (Blažek et al. 2022).","system":"The planet has 10.3 Jupiter masses and a 22.6 hour orbit at 0.02 au. No rapid orbital decay is seen over nine years, so the star dissipates tides weakly (Wilkins et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Planets like this one were flagged as possible tests for magnetic fields through a bow shock ahead of the planet (Vidotto et al. 2011). Whether one exists is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp18b-Arcangelietal2019","cite":"Arcangeli et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Arcangeli, J.-M. Desert, V. Parmentier et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-18b HST/WFC3 spectroscopic phase curves (Arcangeli+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36250136","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp18b-Blaeketal2022","cite":"Blažek et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Martin Blažek, Petr Kabáth, Anjali A. A. Piette et al.","title":"Constraints on TESS albedos for five hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.03327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp18b-Wilkinsetal2017","cite":"Wilkins et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ashlee N. Wilkins, Laetitia Delrez, Adrian J. Barker et al.","title":"Searching for Rapid Orbital Decay of WASP-18b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.01123","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp18b-Vidottoetal2011","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, M. Jardine, Ch. Helling","title":"Prospects for detection of exoplanet magnetic fields through bow-shock observations during transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.3455","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":29,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-90 i":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its giant neighbours Kepler-90 g and h. Their transits arrive up to 25 hours early or late because of mutual gravity, and that timing shift gives masses of about 15 and 203 Earth masses (Liang et al. 2021). Kepler-90 g has an unusually low density of 0.15 g/cm^3 (Liang et al. 2021). The two planets have periods close to a 2:3 ratio but are not locked in resonance. Their orbits stay stable for billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"Interactions between the planets may have raised their eccentricities and broken a resonance lock after formation (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler90i-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TWA 7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. TWA 7 is a young M2.5 star, about 9 million years old, with a cold debris disc (Flagg et al. 2021). It shows a strong far-infrared excess, 40 times above the star's own light at 70 microns (Low et al. 2005). Carbon monoxide gas sits in the dust belt, the first such detection around an M star, and must be released continuously by comets in the belt (Matrà et al. 2019). Hydrogen gas near the star hints at very low-level accretion (Flagg et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-twa7b-Flaggetal2021","cite":"Flagg et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Laura Flagg, Christopher M. Johns-Krull, Kevin France et al.","title":"Detection of H<SUB>2</SUB> in the TWA 7 System: A Probable Circumstellar Origin","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-twa7b-Lowetal2005","cite":"Low et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Frank J. Low, Paul S. Smith, Michael Werner et al.","title":"Exploring Terrestrial Planet Formation in the TW Hydrae Association","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0506291","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-twa7b-Matretal2019","cite":"Matrà et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Matrà, K. I. Öberg, D. J. Wilner et al.","title":"On the Ubiquity and Stellar Luminosity Dependence of Exocometary CO Gas: Detection around M Dwarf TWA 7","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05004","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-17 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit is tilted -115.9 degrees to the star's spin axis, so it circles against the rotation seen on the sky (Zhou et al. 2016). The planet's shadow was seen directly in the star's spectrum during two transits (Zhou et al. 2016). That tilt let astronomers limit the star's differential rotation, consistent with solid-body or solar-like rates (Zhou et al. 2016). New photometry gave a significantly smaller planet radius than before, and ruled out orbital precession (Garai et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a hot A-star of 7454 K and 1.635 solar masses, spinning at 44.2 km/s. It is among the most massive, hottest and most rapidly rotating planet hosts known (Zhou et al. 2016). It is a chemically peculiar Am star (Saffe et al. 2020). A search found no extra transiting planets down to 2 to 4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt17b-Zhouetal2016","cite":"Zhou et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"George Zhou, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"KELT-17b: A Hot-Jupiter Transiting an A-star in a Misaligned Orbit Detected with Doppler Tomography","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03512","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt17b-Garaietal2022","cite":"Garai et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Z. Garai, T. Pribulla, J. Kovács et al.","title":"Rapidly rotating stars and their transiting planets: KELT-17b, KELT-19Ab, and KELT-21b in the CHEOPS and TESS era","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.09077","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt17b-Saffeetal2020","cite":"Saffe et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"C. Saffe, P. Miquelarena, J. Alacoria et al.","title":"KELT-17: a chemically peculiar Am star and a hot-Jupiter planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.14210","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt17b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0712L b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of four planets announced from a by-eye search of the 2021 Korea Microlensing Telescope Network data, which checks how complete the semi-automated searches are (Ryu et al. 2023).","observed":"Four planets were reported together, with planet-to-host mass ratios from about 0.0005 to 0.006 (Ryu et al. 2023). The by-eye sample brings the total of secure by-eye planets in 2021 to 16 (Ryu et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The properties of the planet's host star are not yet measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0712lb-Ryuetal2023","cite":"Ryu et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Yoon-Hyun Ryu, In-Gu Shin, Hongjing Yang et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets II","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-14 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-14b is an unusually dense very hot Jupiter, with 7.6 Jupiter masses, orbiting an F9 star every 1.5 days (Tingley et al. 2011). Its orbit lies less than 5 stellar radii from the star (Tingley et al. 2011). Only one other known planet of higher mass orbits with a shorter period (Tingley et al. 2011). Tidal forces from such massive close-in planets can alter the star's rotation. That makes using the rotation to estimate the star's age unreliable (Ferraz-Mello et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot14b-Tingleyetal2011","cite":"Tingley et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"B. Tingley, M. Endl, J.-C. Gazzano et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XVI. CoRoT-14b: an unusually dense very hot Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.1899","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot14b-FerrazMelloetal2015","cite":"Ferraz-Mello et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"S. Ferraz-Mello, M. Tadeu dos Santos, H. Folonier et al.","title":"Interplay of Tidal Evolution and Stellar Wind Braking in the Rotation of Stars Hosting Massive Close-In Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04369","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2017-BLG-0165L b":{"text":{"why":"Its planet-to-host mass ratio is 1.3 x 10^-4, 2.5 times the Neptune-to-Sun ratio (Jung et al. 2019).","observed":"A short-lived brightness deviation in the event reveals a super-Neptune-mass planet of about 34 Earth masses orbiting a Sun-like star of about 0.76 solar masses, 4.5 kiloparsecs away (Jung et al. 2019). Planet and star are about 3.45 au apart in projection, beyond the snow line at about 2.1 au where water freezes (Jung et al. 2019). It is the sixteenth microlensing planet with a mass ratio below 3 x 10^-4 (Jung et al. 2019). The light from blended stars matches the expected host (Jung et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2017blg0165lb-Jungetal2019","cite":"Jung et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Youn Kil Jung, Andrew Gould, Weicheng Zang et al.","title":"KMT-2017-BLG-0165Lb: A Super-Neptune-mass Planet Orbiting a Sun-like Host Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01288","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1132 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble observed five transits and found a flat spectrum from 1.1 to 1.7 micrometres. That rules out cloud-free atmospheres rich in heavy elements (metallicity below 300 times solar) at more than 4.8 sigma confidence (Libby-Roberts et al. 2022). Combined with TESS and earlier data, the spectrum stays flat from 0.7 to 4.5 micrometres. So the planet has an atmosphere of heavy molecules, a high haze layer, or almost no atmosphere (Libby-Roberts et al. 2022). A separate analysis of Hubble data reported haze, HCN and methane in a light-weight atmosphere (Swain et al. 2021). Four JWST transits again give a flat spectrum. A thin steam atmosphere also fits, but only because of the first transit, when cool starspots on the star appear to have changed (Bennett et al. 2025).","system":"The planet orbits a bright, slowly rotating M dwarf (Bennett et al. 2025).","history":"Modelling of atmospheric loss suggests the planet lost its original hydrogen and helium envelope, so any atmosphere now present formed again, possibly from gas released by ultra-reduced magma (Swain et al. 2021).","open":"Whether the planet has an atmosphere at all is not settled. Hubble results disagree, and higher-precision data are needed (Libby-Roberts et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1132b-LibbyRobertsetal2022","cite":"Libby-Roberts et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. E. Libby-Roberts, Z. K. Berta-Thompson, H. Diamond-Lowe et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ1132b planet/stellar radius at 0.7-4.5μm (Libby-Roberts+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51640059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1132b-Swainetal2021","cite":"Swain et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Mark R. Swain, Raissa Estrela, Gael M. Roudier et al.","title":"Detection of an Atmosphere on a Rocky Exoplanet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.05657","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1132b-Bennettetal2025","cite":"Bennett et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Katherine A. Bennett, Ryan J. MacDonald, Sarah Peacock et al.","title":"Additional JWST/NIRSpec Transits of the Rocky M Dwarf Exoplanet GJ 1132 b Reveal a Featureless Spectrum","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.10579","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 17674 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with the ELODIE and SOPHIE spectrographs at Haute-Provence Observatory, giving 101 velocity measurements spread over 18 years (Rey et al. 2017). Only the minimum mass is known, because the tilt of the orbit is not measured. The planet is one of three giant planets in the same study with minimum masses between 0.9 and 2.5 Jupiter masses (Rey et al. 2017).","system":"The star has low metallicity, meaning few elements heavier than helium, and shows a single giant companion (Rey et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Its true mass is not known. Its orbit and distance make it a good target for astrometry with the Gaia satellite, which measures tiny sideways wobbles of the star, and preliminary solutions from the first Gaia data release have been discussed (Rey et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd17674b-Reyetal2017","cite":"Rey et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. Rey, G. Hebrard, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD17674, HD29021, and HD42012 radial velocities (Rey+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36010009","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 80653 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was watched by the K2 mission for about 80 days between December 2017 and February 2018, and 108 usable HARPS-N spectra were taken from November 2018 to May 2019 (Frustagli et al. 2020). Later work extended the spectra to 237 and combined them with K2 and TESS light curves. It describes the planet as a rocky super-Earth with an orbital period of 0.720 days (Naponiello et al. 2026).","system":"A second planet, K2-312 c, is a cold Jupiter on a wide, highly eccentric orbit with a period of 871.32 days (Naponiello et al. 2026).","history":"Systems with very short-period planets help to test ideas about how planets form and migrate. An outer giant companion also bears on the dynamical effects it has on inner planets and on pebble accretion, the growth of planets from small solids (Naponiello et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd80653b-Frustaglietal2020","cite":"Frustagli et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Frustagli, E. Poretti, T. Milbourne et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 80653 RV time series (Frustagli+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36330133","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd80653b-Naponielloetal2026","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"L. Naponiello, E. Poretti, K. Rice et al.","title":"Refined parameters, formation, and dynamical stability of the wild exoplanet system K2-312≡HD 80653","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19325","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 37124 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this planet has focused on the stability of its orbit rather than on new observations of the planet itself. Computer studies of the velocity measurements found that the system sits within a large zone of stable orbits (Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002). A later reanalysis searched for orbital arrangements that both fit the data and stay stable over long times (Goździewski et al. 2006). Little else is known about the planet itself.","system":"The star is a so-called red straggler: its position in the brightness-temperature diagram looked odd for a star so rich in heavy elements. Stellar models reproduced its luminosity, temperature and metallicity once its age was fixed from its chromospheric activity (Fernandes & Santos 2004).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd37124b-KiselevaEggletonetal2002","cite":"Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ludmila Kiseleva-Eggleton, Eric Bois, Nicolas Rambaux et al.","title":"Global Dynamics and Stability Limits for Planetary Systems around HD 12661, HD 38529, HD 37124, and HD 160691","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207367","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd37124b-FernandesSantos2004","cite":"Fernandes & Santos 2004","year":"2004","authors":"J. Fernandes, N. C. Santos","title":"Detailed theoretical models for extra-solar planet-host stars. The ``red stragglers'' HD 37124 and HD 46375","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408182","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GPX-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up is designed to search crowded star fields, where blending of transit-like signals makes the job hard for surveys such as WASP, HATNet, XO and KELT (Benni et al. 2021).","observed":"Spectroscopic velocity measurements with SOPHIE show that the object has a mass of 19.7 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.47 Jupiter radii, which makes it a brown dwarf rather than a typical planet (Benni et al. 2021). It was the first such object found by the Galactic Plane eXoplanet survey. TESS observed the star for 30-minute exposures in Sector 18, but the data are blended with a star 3.4 magnitudes brighter lying 42 arcseconds away (Benni et al. 2021). Speckle imaging and ground-based photometry helped to confirm it (Benni et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a fast-rotating F-type star spinning at about 40 km per second at its equator, with an age of roughly 0.27 billion years (Benni et al. 2021).","history":"Its mass lies near the boundary between brown dwarfs and gas giant planets, and it is one of about two dozen transiting brown dwarfs known (Benni et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gpx1b-Bennietal2021","cite":"Benni et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Benni, A. Burdanov, E. Sokov et al.","title":"First Discovery of the Galactic Plane eXoplanet Survey: GPX-1b, a Transiting 15 MJup Companion to a Rapidly Rotating F-type Star (Abstract)","journal":"Journal of the American Association of Variable Star Observers (JAAVSO)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":98,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2458 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about planet c itself; work so far has mostly studied the star and planet b.","system":"The host is an F-type star that is leaving the main sequence.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-1 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The angle between the planet's orbit and the star's spin axis was measured at 77 degrees, give or take 11, which means a strongly tilted system. This needs confirmation, because systematic errors in the velocity data could make the true uncertainty larger (Pont et al. 2010). A jovian-mass planet on a close circular orbit with such a tilt would break the pattern seen in earlier misaligned planets (Pont et al. 2010). Modelling of its optical phase curve gives a scattering albedo between 0.11 and 0.3 and small day-night temperature differences, so energy is moved fairly efficiently to the night side (von Paris et al. 2016). Near-ultraviolet transits show no lopsided shape (Turner et al. 2016).","system":"The system is one of two planets confirmed from the first field CoRoT observed (Carpano et al. 2009).","history":"A strong tilt favours planet-planet scattering as a way to bring giant planets onto very close orbits (Pont et al. 2010). Atmospheric escape estimates suggest the planet may have lost up to 1-4% of its initial mass (Lammer et al. 2009).","open":"Whether the planet has a magnetic field is not known. It is among the best candidates for a bow shock ahead of or behind the planet that could reveal one (Vidotto et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot1b-Pontetal2010","cite":"Pont et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"F. Pont, M. Endl, W. D. Cochran et al.","title":"The spin-orbit angle of the transiting hot Jupiter CoRoT-1b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.3032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot1b-vonParisetal2016","cite":"von Paris et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. von Paris, P. Gratier, P. Bordé et al.","title":"Inferring heat recirculation and albedo for exoplanetary atmospheres: Comparing optical phase curves and secondary eclipse data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.04908","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot1b-Turneretal2016","cite":"Turner et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Kyle A. Pearson, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Ground-based near-UV observations of 15 transiting exoplanets: constraints on their atmospheres and no evidence for asymmetrical transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02587","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot1b-Carpanoetal2009","cite":"Carpano et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"S. Carpano, J. Cabrera, R. Alonso et al.","title":"Planetary transit candidates in Corot-IRa01 field","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.5150","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot1b-Lammeretal2009","cite":"Lammer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"H. Lammer, P. Odert, M. Leitzinger et al.","title":"Determining the mass loss limit for close-in exoplanets: what can we learn from transit observations?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200911922","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot1b-Vidottoetal2011","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, M. Jardine, Ch. Helling","title":"Prospects for detection of exoplanet magnetic fields through bow-shock observations during transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.3455","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":18,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 94771 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The star is one of four lower-confidence candidates for exocomets, comets passing in front of a star, found in a search of about 7500 stars observed with HARPS. The signs are variable absorption in calcium lines (Bendahan-West et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd94771b-BendahanWestetal2025","cite":"Bendahan-West et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Raphaël Bendahan-West, Grant M. Kennedy, David J. A. Brown et al.","title":"Quantifying spectroscopic Ca II exocomet transit occurrence in two decades of HARPS data","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.13253","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 50554 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns a giant companion found earlier by Doppler measurements. Lick Observatory data give it an orbit of 1254 days, an eccentricity of 0.51 and a minimum mass of 3.7 Jupiter masses at 2.2 AU from its star (Fischer et al. 2002). Little is known about planet c itself.","system":"The star is of spectral type F8 V and has a mass of about 1.065 Suns (Fischer et al. 2002).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd50554c-Fischeretal2002","cite":"Fischer et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to HD 136118, HD 50554, and HD 106252","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/341677","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-70 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was confirmed around a relatively massive star, which let its discoverers estimate how often hot Jupiters occur around such stars: about 0.41 ± 0.10% in their sample (Zhou et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet is an ultrahot Jupiter. High-resolution spectra from MAROON-X detected 14 neutral and ionized species, including iron, titanium, calcium, sodium and magnesium. The signals are blueshifted by a few kilometres per second, consistent with winds blowing from the hot day side to the night side (Sun et al. 2026). Daytime emission measured with NIRPS shows iron, magnesium, vanadium, silicon and OH but no significant water. Water appears to be broken apart by heat, depleted by more than 90% in the layers probed (Vaulato et al. 2026).","system":"The planet orbits an A-type star on a retrograde orbit, meaning it circles the star opposite to the star's spin (Zhou et al. 2019). Only an upper limit of 6.78 Jupiter masses is known for its mass (Zhou et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Calcium and titanium appear scarcer than expected. Heat-driven ionization or condensation on the cooler night side could explain this, and the inferred abundance ratios depend strongly on modelling assumptions (Gandhi et al. 2023; Sun et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp70b-Zhouetal2019","cite":"Zhou et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"G. Zhou, C. X. Huang, G. A. Bakos et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Differential photometry & RVs of HAT-P-69 & HAT-P-70 (Zhou+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51580141","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp70b-Sunetal2026","cite":"Sun et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Shi Lin Sun, Stefan Pelletier, Björn Benneke et al.","title":"HAT-P-70b through the Eyes of MAROON-X: Constraining Elemental Abundances of Metals and Insights on Atmosphere Dynamics","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07873","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp70b-Vaulatoetal2026","cite":"Vaulato et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Valentina Vaulato, David Ehrenreich, Stefan Pelletier et al.","title":"Thermal emission spectroscopy of the ultra-hot Jupiter HAT-P-70b: NIRPS constraints on its refractory and volatile content","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18835","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp70b-Gandhietal2023","cite":"Gandhi et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Siddharth Gandhi, Aurora Kesseli, Yapeng Zhang et al.","title":"Retrieval Survey of Metals in Six Ultrahot Jupiters: Trends in Chemistry, Rain-out, Ionization, and Atmospheric Dynamics","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17228","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-10 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, HATSouth, found it together with a second hot Jupiter, HATS-9 b, in the region of sky covered by Field 7 of the K2 mission (Brahm et al. 2015).","observed":"HATS-10 b has a mass of 0.53 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.97 Jupiter radii, giving a mean density of 0.7 g per cubic centimetre (Brahm et al. 2015). It is compact compared with planets that receive similar heating from their host stars (Brahm et al. 2015). Its star lies in the nominal coordinates of K2 Field 7, but did not fall on working detectors, so the Kepler telescope did not observe it (Brahm et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats10b-Brahmetal2015","cite":"Brahm et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"R. Brahm, A. Jordán, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS9-b and HATS10-b: Two Compact Hot Jupiters in Field 7 of the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00062","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LkCa 15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The first claim came from aperture-masking interferometry at three epochs, which revealed a faint point source inside a known gap of the star's disc, surrounded by red, resolved emission. Its brightness corresponded to a mass up to about 6 Jupiter masses, and the planet was thought to be still assembling (Kraus & Ireland 2012). Later imaging with Subaru and Keck did not find these planets. Instead it resolved extended emission from a dust disc with brightness and location similar to the claimed planets, and an inner disc explains the earlier signals better (Currie et al. 2019).","system":"The star is a young solar analog about 2 million years old, surrounded by a disc with a gap, called a transitional disc (Kraus & Ireland 2012).","history":"","open":"Whether any planet exists is disputed. Imaging so far points to dust in the inner disc rather than planets (Currie et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lkca15b-KrausIreland2012","cite":"Kraus & Ireland 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Adam L. Kraus, Michael J. Ireland","title":"LkCa 15: A Young Exoplanet Caught at Formation?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.3808","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lkca15b-Currieetal2019","cite":"Currie et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Thayne Currie, Christian Marois, Lucas Cieza et al.","title":"No Clear, Direct Evidence for Multiple Protoplanets Orbiting LkCa 15: LkCa 15 bcd are Likely Inner Disk Signals","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04322","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-512 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was picked from TESS candidates as part of a programme that follows up transit survey candidates with ESPRESSO, a spectrograph on an 8.2 m telescope (Rodrigues et al. 2025).","observed":"Thirty-seven ESPRESSO spectra combined with TESS data gave a bulk density of 5.62 g per cubic centimetre, typical of a rocky planet (Rodrigues et al. 2025). An interior model suggests a small inner core, making up 0.13 of the solid mass, surrounded by a mantle of 0.69 of the mass. The water layer is limited to at most 0.16 (Rodrigues et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a bright K0-type star with a visual magnitude of 9.74 (Rodrigues et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi512b-Rodriguesetal2025","cite":"Rodrigues et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. Rodrigues, S. C. C. Barros, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"TOI-512: Super-Earth transiting a K-type star discovered by TESS and ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14472","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HS Psc b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a search for young hot Jupiters, aimed at finding when giant planets arrive on close orbits (Tran et al. 2024).","observed":"Four years of velocity measurements from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder reveal a repeating signal of 3.986 days, implying a minimum mass of 1.5 Jupiter masses (Tran et al. 2024). The star spins once every 1.086 days according to TESS, which is not a multiple or alias of the orbital period. That supports the signal being a planet and not starspots (Tran et al. 2024). The planet is still called a candidate.","system":"The host is a K7 star of about 0.7 solar masses and a member of the AB Doradus moving group, which is about 130 million years old (Tran et al. 2024).","history":"A giant planet this close to a star only 130 million years old shows that giants can either migrate in by that age or form in place (Tran et al. 2024).","open":"Its orbit needs refining, and additional planets in the system may be present (Tran et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hspscb-Tranetal2024","cite":"Tran et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Quang H. Tran, Brendan P. Bowler, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"The Epoch of Giant Planet Migration Planet Search Program. II. A Young Hot Jupiter Candidate around the AB Dor Member HS Psc","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02378","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an inflated gas giant with a density of 1.20 g per cubic centimetre and an equilibrium temperature of 2453 K. Its orbit takes under a day, and it is described as nearing tidal disruption (Oberst et al. 2017). It is one of only a handful of giant transiting planets with orbital periods shorter than one day (Oberst et al. 2017). Searches for the slow shrinking of its orbit found no evidence of decay (Alvarado et al. 2024). TESS shows a secondary eclipse and a day-night brightness change (Wong et al. 2021).","system":"The star has a stellar companion, and the system is among six hot Jupiter hosts in binary systems modelled for the Kozai-Lidov mechanism (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","history":"The Kozai-Lidov mechanism is a slow swing in orbital shape and tilt caused by a distant companion star, and it is thought to be one way hot Jupiters reach close orbits (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt16b-Oberstetal2017","cite":"Oberst et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Thomas E. Oberst, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Knicole D. Colón et al.","title":"KELT-16b: A Highly Irradiated, Ultra-short Period Hot Jupiter Nearing Tidal Disruption","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00618","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt16b-Alvaradoetal2024","cite":"Alvarado et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Efrain Alvarado, Kate B. Bostow, Kishore C. Patra et al.","title":"Searching for tidal orbital decay in hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt16b-Wongetal2021","cite":"Wong et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ian Wong, Daniel Kitzmann, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"Visible-light Phase Curves from the Second Year of the TESS Primary Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02610","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt16b-VazquezEastman2025","cite":"Vazquez & Eastman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacqueline Vazquez, Jason Eastman","title":"Understanding Hot Jupiter Migration for Six Binary Star Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0320L b":{"text":{"why":"The search aimed to find short-lived planetary signals in data from current high-cadence microlensing surveys (Han et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is one of four planetary systems with low planet-to-host mass ratios found in the same study. Signals lasted from a few hours to a couple of days. Its mass is about 0.10 times that of Jupiter (Han et al. 2022).","system":"The host stars of three of the four systems are low-mass stars of roughly 0.3 to 0.6 solar masses (Han et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0320lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Doeon Kim, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Four sub-Jovian-mass planets detected by high-cadence microlensing surveys","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10510","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1752 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system comes from a preliminary validation that relied on ground-based photometry. Such photometry is one of the most effective ways to check that a signal around a faint, cool star is a planet and not an eclipsing binary or a brown dwarf (Pelaez Torres et al. 2025). The planet is an ultra-short-period super-Earth.","system":"The system has two planets, the inner super-Earth and a sub-Neptune in the habitable zone (Pelaez Torres et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1752b-Torresetal2025","cite":"Torres et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Pelaez Torres, F. J. Pozuelos, G. Morello et al.","title":"TOI-1752: a GEM System with AN Ultra-Short Super-Earth and a Habitable Zone Sub-Neptune","journal":"Proceedings of the VIII Iberian Congress on Planetary Sciences and Solar System Exploration","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of six transiting planets, all orbiting within 0.5 AU of their star, with the inner five on nearly circular, coplanar orbits within 0.25 AU (Quintana et al. 2011). Spectroscopy of the host star shows it is a young solar twin. Revised stellar parameters raised the densities of the planets by between 20% and 95%, so they are puffier than rocky worlds but denser than first thought (Bedell et al. 2017).","system":"The system is extremely compact and was announced as an unusual, densely packed arrangement (Quintana et al. 2011).","history":"Simulations of the last stages of planet formation in a massive disc tested whether the system could have formed in place (Quintana et al. 2011).","open":"Whether the planets are rocky cores with thick hydrogen-rich atmospheres or miniature Neptunes with mostly water is not settled (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11f-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11f-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11f-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2018-BLG-1988L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up when high-magnification events from past KMTNet data were examined again for planetary signals that had been missed (Han et al. 2022).","observed":"The signal lasted about 6 hours and deviated by 0.2 magnitudes or less from a single-lens light curve. It showed a dip surrounded by weak bumps on both sides (Han et al. 2022). Two possible solutions give a mass of 6.8 or 5.6 Earth masses, placing the planet in the super-Earth regime. It is the 18th known microlensing planet with a mass below the upper limit of a super-Earth (Han et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a disk star of about 0.47 solar masses at a distance of about 4.2 kiloparsecs (Han et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2018blg1988lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrew Gould, Michael D. Albrow et al.","title":"KMT-2018-BLG-1988Lb: Microlensing super-Earth orbiting a low-mass disk dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08955","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-62 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its two outer planets, Kepler-62 e and f, which lie in the habitable zone (Borucki et al. 2013). Kepler-62 d is the largest of the five planets at 1.95 Earth radii and orbits in about 18.2 days (Borucki et al. 2013). The star is a K2V dwarf (Borucki et al. 2013).","system":"The five planets have periods of 5.7, 12.4, 18.2, 122.4 and 267.3 days (Borucki et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler62d-Boruckietal2013","cite":"Borucki et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"William J. Borucki, Eric Agol, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Kepler-62: A Five-Planet System with Planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth Radii in the Habitable Zone","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7387","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-114 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-114 b was characterised together with a close look-alike system, WASP-113. The two host stars are both early G-type stars of about 5900 K with similar heavy-element content (Barros et al. 2016). Both planets are inflated, meaning larger than their mass would suggest. Their radii are similar, yet WASP-114 b is almost four times heavier than WASP-113 b (Barros et al. 2016). WASP-114 b has a mass of 1.77 Jupiter masses and circles its star in about 1.5 days (Barros et al. 2016).","system":"The host star WASP-114 is younger than its twin WASP-113 (Barros et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp114b-Barrosetal2016","cite":"Barros et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, D. J. A. Brown, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"WASP-113b and WASP-114b, two inflated hot Jupiters with contrasting densities","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.02341","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 357 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 357 b is among the closest transiting planets to the Sun, which makes it a prime target for transmission spectroscopy, where starlight filtered through a planet's atmosphere is studied (Luque et al. 2019). Its equilibrium temperature is 525 K (Luque et al. 2019). Its mass and radius point to an Earth-like density (Kaltenegger et al. 2019). A deep X-ray observation showed that the host star is one of the least X-ray active M dwarfs ever measured, with an age of at least 5 billion years (Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020). Working backwards, the planet's original hydrogen-rich atmosphere could have held up to about 38 Earth masses of gas (Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020).","system":"Two more planets orbit the star. GJ 357 c has a 9.1-day orbit and GJ 357 d a 55.7-day orbit that lies in the habitable zone, where liquid water could exist (Luque et al. 2019). Neither of them transits the star (Kane & Fetherolf 2023). Planets b and c lie close to a 7:3 orbital resonance, but no sign of locking into it was found in simulations (Jenkins et al. 2019). The star spins once in 78 days (Luque et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Whether GJ 357 b still holds an atmosphere is not known. The star is quiet, so an atmosphere may have survived (Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj357b-Luqueetal2019","cite":"Luque et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"R. Luque, E. Pallé, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"Planetary system around the nearby M dwarf GJ 357 including a transiting, hot, Earth-sized planet optimal for atmospheric characterization","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.12818","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357b-Kalteneggeretal2019","cite":"Kaltenegger et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Kaltenegger, J. Madden, Z. Lin et al.","title":"The Habitability of GJ 357D: Possible Climate and Observability","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.13215","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357b-ModirroustaGalianetal2020","cite":"Modirrousta-Galian et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. Modirrousta-Galian, B. Stelzer, E. Magaudda et al.","title":"GJ 357 b. A super-Earth orbiting an extremely inactive host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.10262","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357b-KaneFetherolf2023","cite":"Kane & Fetherolf 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Tara Fetherolf","title":"GJ 357 d: Potentially Habitable World or Agent of Chaos?","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357b-Jenkinsetal2019","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, F. J. Pozuelos, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"GJ 357: a low-mass planetary system uncovered by precision radial velocities and dynamical simulations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.00831","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"kap CrB b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Kappa Coronae Borealis is a former A-type dwarf that has become a K-type subgiant (Baines et al. 2014). Interferometry, which combines light from separate telescopes to resolve fine detail, gave the star's size, temperature, luminosity, mass and age (Baines et al. 2014).","system":"The star has an unusual set of companions and dust. It hosts at least one planet and perhaps two, and has either one wide dust ring or two narrow ones (Baines et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the system holds a second planet is not settled, nor whether the dust forms one ring or two (Baines et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kapcrbb-Bainesetal2014","cite":"Baines et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Ellyn K. Baines, J. T. Armstrong, G. van Belle","title":"NPOI Observations of the Exoplanet Host Kappa Coronae Borealis","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #223","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-39 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-39 b orbits an evolved subgiant star about 3.9 times the Sun's radius (Van Eylen et al. 2016). The planet orbits only 3.4 stellar radii from the star's centre (Van Eylen et al. 2016). At 4.6 days, it has the shortest orbit of any known planet around a subgiant (Van Eylen et al. 2016). Such planets are rare, possibly because they are less common around the heavier stars surveyed, or because tides destroy them (Van Eylen et al. 2016). Two teams weighed the planet with radial velocities, which track the star's wobble. They found 50.3 Earth masses (Van Eylen et al. 2016) and 39.8 Earth masses (Petigura et al. 2017). The first team found a low density of 0.50 grams per cubic centimetre (Van Eylen et al. 2016).","system":"The star's motion shows a long-term drift that may come from a distant planet or a stellar companion (Van Eylen et al. 2016). Another team saw no signs of additional planets (Petigura et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The cause of the long-term drift in the star's motion is not known (Van Eylen et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k239b-VanEylenetal2016","cite":"Van Eylen et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Vincent Van Eylen, Simon Albrecht, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"The K2-ESPRINT Project V: A Short-period Giant Planet Orbiting a Subgiant Star*","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.09180","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k239b-Petiguraetal2017","cite":"Petigura et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, Evan Sinukoff, Eric D. Lopez et al.","title":"Four Sub-Saturns with Dissimilar Densities: Windows into Planetary Cores and Envelopes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00013","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3785 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-3785 b is a low-density Neptune, with a density of 0.61 grams per cubic centimetre (Powers et al. 2023). Planets of Neptune size around small, cool M-dwarf stars are rare, with only about 10 confirmed (Powers et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 582 K and its host star is quiet (Powers et al. 2023). Its transmission spectroscopy metric, a score for how easy a planet's atmosphere is to study, is about 150 (Powers et al. 2023). That makes it one of the better low-density M-dwarf Neptunes for atmosphere studies (Powers et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"How fast the planet loses atmosphere is not known. Measuring it could show how gas planets around M dwarfs evolve (Powers et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3785b-Powersetal2023","cite":"Powers et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Luke C. Powers, Jessica Libby-Roberts, Andrea S. J. Lin et al.","title":"TOI-3785 b: A Low-density Neptune Orbiting an M2-dwarf Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.04730","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7149 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-7149 b is a gas giant orbiting a mid-type M dwarf with a mass of 0.344 Suns (Kanodia et al. 2025). Its transit blocks about 12% of the star's light, one of the deepest known for a star on the main sequence (Kanodia et al. 2025). The host is one of the lowest-mass M dwarfs known to have a transiting giant planet (Kanodia et al. 2025). Giant planets around M dwarfs are found mostly around metal-rich stars (Kanodia et al. 2025). Their masses and densities match those of warm Jupiters around Sun-like stars (Kanodia et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"The match in density between giants around mid-type M dwarfs and those around Sun-like stars hints at a shared way of forming (Kanodia et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7149b-Kanodiaetal2025","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Caleb I. Cañas, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-7149 b, an Inflated Giant Planet Causing a 12% Transit of a Fully Convective M-dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.17861","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-815 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The TOI-815 system was watched by the TESS space telescope in three observing periods, in 2018 to 2019, 2021 and 2023 (Psaridi et al. 2024). The CHEOPS space telescope followed it up under two programmes. One aimed to sharpen planet radii in multi-planet systems found by TESS. The other aimed to pin down the orbital periods of long-period TESS candidates (Psaridi et al. 2024). Ground-based telescopes also watched for a transit of planet b in 2021 (Psaridi et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi815c-Psaridietal2024","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Psaridi, H. Osborn, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-815 light curves and RV sources (Psaridi+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 179079 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 179079 b is a hot Neptune with a minimum mass of 27.5 Earth masses on a 14.5-day orbit that is nearly circular (Valenti et al. 2009). Its host is a G5 subgiant, a star beginning to swell as it ages. The star is metal-rich and shows little magnetic activity (Valenti et al. 2009). The star's wobble from the planet is small, about 6.6 metres per second (Valenti et al. 2009). The discovery team used the case to warn that low-amplitude signals tend to give orbits that look more eccentric than they are (Valenti et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd179079b-Valentietal2009","cite":"Valenti et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Jeff A. Valenti, Debra Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Two Exoplanets Discovered at Keck Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 15906 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 15906 c showed only two transits in the TESS data, about 734 days apart, so its period could be any of 36 values (Tuson et al. 2023). The CHEOPS space telescope was used to find the true period of 21.58 days (Tuson et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 532 K, assuming no reflection and full heat sharing around the planet (Tuson et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, HD 15906 b, has a 10.9-day period and a radius of 2.24 Earth radii (Tuson et al. 2023). The system is one of only six with two warm sub-Neptunes transiting a bright star (Tuson et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd15906c-Tusonetal2023","cite":"Tuson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Tuson, D. Queloz, H. P. Osborn et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 15906 CHEOPS PIPE and WASP lightcurves (Tuson+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gl 410 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Gl 410, also called DS Leonis, is an active M dwarf with starspots, dark patches that come and go as the star spins (Fekel & Henry 2000). Its brightness varied with periods of 13.99 and 15.71 days in two observing seasons (Fekel & Henry 2000). The planet's signal was measured in the star's velocity, using the SPIRou instrument from 2020 to 2023 and SOPHIE in 2010 to 2012 and 2021 to 2023 (Carmona et al. 2025).","system":"The star has a strong, changing magnetic field whose shape has been mapped over many years (Bellotti et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gl410b-FekelHenry2000","cite":"Fekel & Henry 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Francis C. Fekel, Gregory W. Henry","title":"Chromospherically Active Stars. XVIII. Sorting Out the Variability of HD 95559 and Gliese 410=DS Leonis","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/316866","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gl410b-Carmonaetal2025","cite":"Carmona et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Carmona, X. Delfosse, M. Ould-Elhkim et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Gl 410 radial velocity curves (Carmona+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gl410b-Bellottietal2024","cite":"Bellotti et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Bellotti, J. Morin, L. T. Lehmann et al.","title":"Long-term monitoring of large-scale magnetic fields across optical and near-infrared domains with ESPaDOnS, Narval, and SPIRou. The cases of EV Lac, DS Leo, and CN Leo","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08590","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-168 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-168 b is a bloated hot Jupiter, with a mass of 0.42 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.5 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2019). Its transit is grazing, meaning the planet only clips the edge of the star's disc as seen from Earth (Hellier et al. 2019). The host star is an F9 star of magnitude 12.1 (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp168b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 284149 AB b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Despite its catalogue label, the object HD 284149 b is a brown dwarf, an object between a planet and a star in mass (Bonavita et al. 2017). The SPHERE instrument, which blocks out starlight to image faint companions, showed that its host is a binary. A low-mass star of about 0.16 Suns lies about 0.1 arcseconds from the main star (Bonavita et al. 2017). Spectra of the companion refined its temperature and spectral type, and a new look at the system's age and distance gave a better mass and orbit size (Bonavita et al. 2017). Keck observations in K-band light were made in November 2017 (Hoch et al. 2024).","system":"The system has three members: a Sun-like star, a close low-mass star and a distant brown dwarf (Bonavita et al. 2017). The close star matches earlier differences in the star's velocity and motion on the sky (Bonavita et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd284149abb-Bonavitaetal2017","cite":"Bonavita et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Bonavita, V. D'Orazi, D. Mesa et al.","title":"Orbiting a binary. SPHERE characterisation of the HD 284149 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06238","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd284149abb-Hochetal2024","cite":"Hoch et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. K. W. Hoch, Q. M. Konopacky, C. A. Theissen et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K-band spectroscopy of HD 284149b (Hoch+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 436 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 436 b is a warm Neptune-sized planet whose atmosphere is escaping. Hydrogen gas around it forms a comet-like tail that hides part of the star's disc for more than 10 hours after the transit (Villarreal D'Angelo et al. 2021). In the Lyman-alpha line, a colour of light sensitive to hydrogen, transits show about 56% absorption (Villarreal D'Angelo et al. 2021). No absorption was seen in the H-alpha line (Villarreal D'Angelo et al. 2021). Simulations fit these data with a stellar wind of 250 to 460 kilometres per second and a planet mass-loss rate of about 6 to 10 billion grams per second (Villarreal D'Angelo et al. 2021). Hubble data predicted the hydrogen cloud before it was seen (Ehrenreich et al. 2011). Archive infrared transit spectra showed no conclusive molecular features (Gibson et al. 2011).","system":"The star is a quiet M dwarf (Ehrenreich et al. 2011). The orbit is slightly eccentric (Lewis et al. 2011). Timing searches for extra planets found no statistically significant deviations (Cáceres et al. 2009).","history":"","open":"What the planet is made of is unclear: hydrogen and helium gas, ice, or rock were all possible (Butler et al. 2004). The planet may have an atmosphere about 30 times richer in heavy elements than the Sun's (Lewis et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj436b-DAngeloetal2021","cite":"D'Angelo et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Carolina Villarreal D'Angelo, Aline A. Vidotto, Alejandro Esquivel et al.","title":"GJ 436b and the stellar wind interaction: simulations constraints using Ly α and H α transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.05128","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj436b-Ehrenreichetal2011","cite":"Ehrenreich et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. Ehrenreich, A. Lecavelier Des Etangs, X. Delfosse","title":"HST/STIS Lyman-α observations of the quiet M dwarf <ASTROBJ>GJ 436</ASTROBJ>. Predictions for the exospheric transit signature of the hot Neptune <ASTROBJ>GJ 436b</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.0009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj436b-Gibsonetal2011","cite":"Gibson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"N. P. Gibson, F. Pont, S. Aigrain","title":"A new look at NICMOS transmission spectroscopy of HD 189733, GJ-436 and XO-1: no conclusive evidence for molecular features","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.1753","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj436b-Lewisetal2011","cite":"Lewis et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"N. K. Lewis, A. P. Showman, J. J. Fortney et al.","title":"Atmospheric Dynamics of Two Eccentric Transiting Planets: GJ 436b and HD 17156b","journal":"Molecules in the Atmospheres of Extrasolar Planets","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj436b-Cceresetal2009","cite":"Cáceres et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"C. Cáceres, V. D. Ivanov, D. Minniti et al.","title":"High cadence near infrared timing observations of extrasolar planets. I. GJ 436b and XO-1b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.1728","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj436b-Butleretal2004","cite":"Butler et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"A Neptune-Mass Planet Orbiting the Nearby M Dwarf GJ 436","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408587","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":60,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 7449 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 7449 b is a massive planet on a very elongated orbit, with an eccentricity of about 0.8 and a distance from its star of about 2.3 AU (Rodigas et al. 2016). Its minimum mass is about 1.1 Jupiter masses (Rodigas et al. 2016). Adaptive-optics images, which sharpen blurring from the atmosphere, found the source of a long-term drift in the star's motion. It is an M dwarf of 0.1 to 0.2 Suns at about 0.54 arcseconds from the star (Rodigas et al. 2016).","system":"The M dwarf companion lies about 18 AU from the star and is likely bound to it (Rodigas et al. 2016).","history":"The M dwarf may be pushing the planet's orbit back and forth between circular and stretched, in what is called Kozai oscillation. That could explain the high eccentricity (Rodigas et al. 2016).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd7449b-Rodigasetal2016","cite":"Rodigas et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"T. J. Rodigas, P. Arriagada, J. Faherty et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: MILO. I. HD 7449 radial velocities (Rodigas+, 2016)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.18180106","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-9 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-9 d. Kepler-9 was the first system in which transit timing variations were used to confirm planets (Dreizler & Ofir 2014). Timing variations are shifts in when a planet crosses its star, caused by the pull of a neighbour. Kepler-9 was also the first system with several confirmed transiting planets (Ragozzine & Holman 2018). Planets b and c have anti-correlated timing changes that signal a near 2:1 orbital resonance (Holman et al. 2010). A later study using far more data found lower masses than first thought, with Kepler-9 b at 45.1 Earth masses (Dreizler & Ofir 2014). Densities came out about a third of the first value, at about 0.18 grams per cubic centimetre (Dreizler & Ofir 2014).","system":"Three planets are known. Kepler-9 c has a 38.9-day orbit (Holman et al. 2010). A small planet on a 1.6-day orbit, Kepler-9 d, was found later (Holman et al. 2010). Kepler-9 d was reported larger, at about 2 Earth radii, in a later study (Dreizler & Ofir 2014).","history":"","open":"There are hints of an outer, non-transiting planet in the velocity data (Dreizler & Ofir 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler9b-DreizlerOfir2014","cite":"Dreizler & Ofir 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Stefan Dreizler, Aviv Ofir","title":"Kepler-9 revisited 60% the mass with six times more data","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1372","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9b-RagozzineHolman2018","cite":"Ragozzine & Holman 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Matthew J. Holman","title":"Kepler-9: The first multi-transiting system and the first transit timing variations","journal":"New Astronomy Reviews","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newar.2019.03.003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9b-Holmanetal2010","cite":"Holman et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Matthew J. Holman, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Darin Ragozzine et al.","title":"Kepler-9: A System of Multiple Planets Transiting a Sun-Like Star, Confirmed by Timing Variations","journal":"Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1195778","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 17156 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a search of metal-rich stars that had first looked quiet, and follow-up over about three years revealed it (Fischer et al. 2007). Its star is one of five with supersolar metal content that gained Jupiter-mass planets in the same report (Fischer et al. 2007).","observed":"The orbit is very stretched: its eccentricity is 0.67, one of the highest known for a planet with such a short period (Fischer et al. 2007). A first transit search came up empty, but it covered only 25% of the possible search space (Fischer et al. 2007). Transits were seen later, in September 2007, by three independent observers working with a network of amateur and small-telescope groups (Barbieri et al. 2007). The dip in starlight is 0.0062 deep and lasts 186 minutes, which fits a Jupiter-sized planet crossing the star off-centre (Barbieri et al. 2007). Because of the stretched orbit, the planet receives 26 times more starlight at its closest than at its farthest (Barbieri et al. 2007).","system":"The star is a G0-type dwarf and the system has one known planet. Sound waves in the star have been used to estimate its age, and different methods give a spread of values (Lebreton 2012).","history":"","open":"The planet is a good target for studying how an atmosphere reacts to a huge swing in heating around each orbit (Barbieri et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd17156b-Fischeretal2007","cite":"Fischer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Steven S. Vogt, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Five Intermediate-Period Planets from the N2K Sample","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.1191","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd17156b-Barbierietal2007","cite":"Barbieri et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Barbieri, R. Alonso, G. Laughlin et al.","title":"HD 17156b: a transiting planet with a 21.2-day period and an eccentric orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.0898","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd17156b-Lebreton2012","cite":"Lebreton 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Y. Lebreton","title":"Ages of Exoplanet Host-stars from Asteroseismology: HD 17156, a Case Study","journal":"Progress in Solar/Stellar Physics with Helio- and Asteroseismology","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.6153","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":22,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-15 b was the first planet from the Kepler mission whose mass was measured with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope, using 24 velocity measurements from it plus 6 from a telescope in the Canary Islands (Endl et al. 2011). The planet has 0.66 Jupiter masses and 0.96 Jupiter radii, giving a density of 0.9 grams per cubic centimetre (Endl et al. 2011). Its size sits at the low end for planets of similar mass and heating, which points to a large amount of heavy elements inside, about 30 to 40 Earth masses (Endl et al. 2011).","system":"The host is a metal-rich, Sun-like star with a surface temperature of 5515 K (Endl et al. 2011).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler15b-Endletal2011","cite":"Endl et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Michael Endl, Phillip J. MacQueen, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"Kepler-15b: A Hot Jupiter Enriched in Heavy Elements and the First Kepler Mission Planet Confirmed with the Hobby-Eberly Telescope","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0067-0049/197/1/13","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-2 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is very massive, about 9 times the mass of Jupiter, with a density near 12 grams per cubic centimetre, denser than Earth rock (Bakos et al. 2007). Matching its mass and size may need a large core of more than about 100 Earth masses (Bakos et al. 2007). Its orbit is stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.52, so starlight on the planet varies ninefold between closest and farthest approach (Bakos et al. 2007). Velocity measurements during a transit show that the orbit runs in the same direction as the star's spin and is closely aligned with it (Loeillet et al. 2008). Because the orbital speed changes so much, the planet cannot spin in step with its orbit. Tides are expected to leave it spinning about once every 1.96 days (Bakos et al. 2007).","system":"The orbit points almost edge-on to us along its long axis. About 13 hours after transit the planet reaches its closest approach, and it passes behind the star about 13 hours after that (Bakos et al. 2007). Starlight at the planet rises from about 1600 to 3600 times what Earth gets (Bakos et al. 2007).","history":"Tide calculations find that it is the only transiting giant planet known to have a stable tidal end state, while the others are spiralling toward their stars (Levrard et al. 2009). Simulations of star clusters show that stellar close passes can produce hot Jupiters on stretched orbits like this one (Shara et al. 2016).","open":"Models of its heat output over an orbit suggest the timing of its brightness peak would reveal the direction of its winds (Cowan & Agol 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp2b-Bakosetal2007","cite":"Bakos et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, G. Kovács, G. Torres et al.","title":"HD 147506b: A Supermassive Planet in an Eccentric Orbit Transiting a Bright Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0126","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp2b-Loeilletetal2008","cite":"Loeillet et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"B. Loeillet, A. Shporer, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"Refined parameters and spectroscopic transit of the super-massive planet HD 147506b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.0679","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp2b-Levrardetal2009","cite":"Levrard et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"B. Levrard, C. Winisdoerffer, G. Chabrier","title":"Falling Transiting Extrasolar Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2048","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp2b-Sharaetal2016","cite":"Shara et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Michael M. Shara, Jarrod R. Hurley, Rosemary A. Mardling","title":"Dynamical Interactions Make Hot Jupiters in Open Star Clusters","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.7061","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp2b-CowanAgol2011","cite":"Cowan & Agol 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Nicolas B. Cowan, Eric Agol","title":"A Model for Thermal Phase Variations of Circular and Eccentric Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.0428","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-38 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about the mass and size of Saturn, 0.27 Jupiter masses and 0.82 Jupiter radii, which makes it one of the closest Saturn matches among known planets (Sato et al. 2012). Its transmission spectrum from the Hubble Space Telescope shows a fairly clear atmosphere with water clearly detected (Bruno et al. 2018). A second planet of nearly the same temperature, WASP-67 b, shows a muted water signal, which means either higher clouds or more heavy elements. The difference supports the idea that giant-planet atmospheres carry traces of how the planets formed (Bruno et al. 2018). Heat given off by the planet's day side was measured with the Spitzer Space Telescope, and the orbit was confirmed to be circular (Wallack et al. 2021). An amateur observatory in Finland recorded its transits in autumn 2020 (Haukka et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Observations in visible light and in the mid-infrared are still needed to learn what its clouds or hazes are made of (Bruno et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp38b-Satoetal2012","cite":"Sato et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Joel D. Hartman, Gáspár Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-38b: A Saturn-Mass Planet Transiting a Late G Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.5075","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp38b-Brunoetal2018","cite":"Bruno et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Giovanni Bruno, Nikole K. Lewis, Kevin B. Stevenson et al.","title":"A Comparative Study of WASP-67 b and HAT-P-38 b from WFC3 Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.03384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp38b-Wallacketal2021","cite":"Wallack et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Drake Deming","title":"Trends in Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp38b-Haukkaetal2021","cite":"Haukka et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Harri Haukka, Veli-Pekka Hentunen, Markku Nissinen et al.","title":"Taurus Hill Observatory season 2020/2021 exoplanet review. HAT-P-38b (Hiisi) and secondary eclipse of the HAT-P-32b exoplanet.","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2021-107","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-20 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star hosts five repeating dips in its brightness, which the discovery work interpreted as three sub-Neptune planets and two Earth-size candidates (Gautier et al. 2012). For three of the signals, a careful check of blended-star false alarms, using sharp images, colours and spectra, found that planets were far more likely than false positives (Gautier et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a Sun-like star with a surface temperature of 5455 K and a metal content close to the Sun's (Gautier et al. 2012). It has a mass of 0.912 times the Sun and a radius of 0.944 times the Sun (Gautier et al. 2012). Six planets are now known around it.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler20d-Gautieretal2012","cite":"Gautier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas N., III Gautier, David Charbonneau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-20: A Sun-like Star with Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets and Two Earth-size Candidates","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.4514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2012-BLG-505L b":{"text":{"why":"A 15-minute observing schedule in the survey picked up the short and subtle signal of the planet (Nagakane et al. 2017).","observed":"The microlensing event lasted only about 10 days, the second shortest event known to show a planet signal (Nagakane et al. 2017). The signal fits two solutions that cannot be told apart: the planet may lie slightly beyond or slightly inside the Einstein ring, at 1.1 or 0.9 times its radius (Nagakane et al. 2017). Statistical modelling gives a planet of about 6.7 Earth masses, with a wide range of uncertainty (Nagakane et al. 2017). It orbits a brown dwarf or a very faint red dwarf, at a distance of about 0.9 astronomical units (Nagakane et al. 2017).","system":"The host star has only about 0.10 solar masses (Nagakane et al. 2017). The system lies about 7.2 kiloparsecs away, which probably puts it in the Galactic bulge (Nagakane et al. 2017). Low-mass planetary systems in the bulge are rare finds.","history":"","open":"The planet's exact mass, the host star's type and the planet's orbital distance remain uncertain, with wide error ranges (Nagakane et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2012blg505lb-Nagakaneetal2017","cite":"Nagakane et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Nagakane, T. Sumi, N. Koshimoto et al.","title":"MOA-2012-BLG-505Lb: A Super-Earth-mass Planet That Probably Resides in the Galactic Bulge","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.10769","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-48 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was reported together with three other warm giant planets around K dwarf stars (Hartman et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet has about 0.243 Jupiter masses and 0.800 Jupiter radii, and circles its star every 3.13 days (Hartman et al. 2020). Its host is a main-sequence K dwarf of 0.7279 solar masses and a faint visual brightness of V = 14.35 (Hartman et al. 2020). Planets of this kind, giants that are not extreme hot Jupiters, are the focus of the report that announced it (Hartman et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats48ab-Hartmanetal2020","cite":"Hartman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. D. Hartman, Andrés Jordán, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-47b, HATS-48Ab, HATS-49b, and HATS-72b: Four Warm Giant Planets Transiting K Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.05776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-747 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Whether Kepler-747 b is a real planet is in doubt. A new analysis used improved star sizes from the Gaia satellite and the way the system's brightness changes around the orbit (Niraula et al. 2022). The companion's physical size, worked out from those results, is too large for a planet that gets that much starlight (Niraula et al. 2022). The same work disproved three other validated planets and called Kepler-747 b possibly not a planet either (Niraula et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"It is not settled whether the object is a planet or a small star."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler747b-Niraulaetal2022","cite":"Niraula et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Avi Shporer, Ian Wong et al.","title":"Revisiting Kepler Transiting Systems: Unvetting Planets and Constraining Relationships among Harmonics in Phase Curves","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.07312","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2010 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first seen as a single transit in TESS data, so its orbital period was unknown (Mann et al. 2023). Such single-transit planets are hard to confirm because follow-up observations cannot be scheduled (Mann et al. 2023).","observed":"Nearly three years of velocity measurements pinned down the period well enough to search for another transit with the Near-Earth Object Surveillance Satellite, which found one (Mann et al. 2023). A later TESS sector gave a further transit and fixed the final values (Mann et al. 2023). The planet has 1.29 Jupiter masses and 1.05 Jupiter radii, with a period of 141.83 days and a mildly stretched orbit of eccentricity 0.21 (Mann et al. 2023). With no reflection and perfect heat sharing, its temperature would run from about 360 K at the farthest point to about 450 K at the closest (Mann et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2010b-Mannetal2023","cite":"Mann et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Christopher R. Mann, Paul A. Dalba, David Lafrenière et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. III. Recovery and Confirmation of a Temperate, Mildly Eccentric, Single-transit Jupiter Orbiting TOI-2010","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10232","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219077 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a survey of southern main-sequence stars within 50 parsecs, run with the CORALIE spectrograph since 1998 (Marmier et al. 2013). After more than 14 years of data, the survey can now find Jupiter-like planets on Jupiter-like periods, which helps test formation and migration ideas beyond the ice line (Marmier et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet was reported along with six other new long-period giants, with periods from 2200 to 5500 days and masses between 1 and 10.5 Jupiter masses (Marmier et al. 2013). A few extra observations from the HARPS spectrograph supported the CORALIE data (Marmier et al. 2013). Among those seven new planets, five have eccentricities above 0.57, which was a surprise (Marmier et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219077b-Marmieretal2013","cite":"Marmier et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Marmier, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVII. New and updated long period and massive planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6444","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-151 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was announced with two other transiting planets from the same survey, selected because their masses and radii could be measured to better than 15% (Demangeon et al. 2018). Precise values like these are needed to understand features of the planet population, such as the edge of the Neptunian desert, the gap in the number of Neptune-sized planets close to their stars (Demangeon et al. 2018).","observed":"WASP-151 b is a hot Saturn with a mass of 0.31 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.13 Jupiter radii (Demangeon et al. 2018). Its density is only 0.22 times Jupiter's, and its equilibrium temperature is about 1290 K (Demangeon et al. 2018).","system":"The host star is an early G-type star of visual brightness around V = 13 (Demangeon et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp151b-Demangeonetal2018","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, F. Faedi, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"The discovery of WASP-151b, WASP-153b, WASP-156b: Insights on giant planet migration and the upper boundary of the Neptunian desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.06321","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-481 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet joins a sparsely populated group of transiting gas giants with orbits longer than 10 days (Brahm et al. 2020).","observed":"TOI-481 b has 1.53 Jupiter masses and 0.99 Jupiter radii, and orbits every 10.33 days (Brahm et al. 2020). Its mass was measured with a broad set of ground-based telescopes, including seven different spectrographs (Brahm et al. 2020). A second planet, TOI-892 b, has nearly the same size and period but a much lower mass of 0.95 Jupiter masses (Brahm et al. 2020).","system":"The star is a metal-rich G-type star with 1.14 solar masses and 1.66 solar radii (Brahm et al. 2020). At an age of 6.7 billion years, it is at the turn-off point, where a star is just leaving the main sequence (Brahm et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi481b-Brahmetal2020","cite":"Brahm et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Louise D. Nielsen, Robert A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"TOI-481 b and TOI-892 b: Two Long-period Hot Jupiters from the Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.08881","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-70 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-70 b is a brown dwarf, an object between a planet and a star, at the limit where deuterium burning starts (Zhou et al. 2019). It has 12.9 Jupiter masses and 1.384 Jupiter radii, and circles its star every 1.89 days (Zhou et al. 2019). Its host spins fast, so the planet's passage across the star was traced with Doppler tomography, which tracks the starlight distortion as the object crosses the star (Zhou et al. 2019). The orbit is closely aligned with the star's spin, within about 9 degrees (Zhou et al. 2019).","system":"The host star is an A-type star of 1.78 solar masses, spinning at about 40 kilometres per second along our line of sight (Zhou et al. 2019).","history":"Other massive planets and brown dwarfs show the same low tilt, which is surprising because all of them orbit stars hotter than the Kraft break, the temperature above which stars spin fast (Zhou et al. 2019). The pattern is tentatively at odds with chaotic migration for such massive companions (Zhou et al. 2019).","open":"The strong tides from massive companions make it hard to say what the original tilt distribution was (Zhou et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats70b-Zhouetal2019","cite":"Zhou et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"G. Zhou, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-70b: A 13 MJ Brown Dwarf Transiting an A Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.06925","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-87 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit is closely aligned with its star's spin, with a measured sky-projected angle of -8 degrees, give or take 11 degrees (Addison et al. 2016). The host is a hot mid-F star of 6450 K, a type linked to many planets on tilted orbits, so the alignment is notable (Addison et al. 2016). Heat given off by the planet's day side was measured with the Spitzer Space Telescope at two infrared wavelengths, for the first time for this planet (Garhart et al. 2020).","system":"The host star is a visual binary with a mid-G star (Anderson et al. 2014). High-contrast imaging detected a new candidate companion near the star (Bohn et al. 2020). The star is metal-poor, with an iron content ratio [Fe/H] of -0.41 (Anderson et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp87b-Addisonetal2016","cite":"Addison et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"B. C. Addison, C. G. Tinney, D. J. Wright et al.","title":"Spin-orbit Alignment for Three Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-103b, WASP-87b, and WASP-66b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.05754","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp87b-Garhartetal2020","cite":"Garhart et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Emily Garhart, Drake Deming, Avi Mandell et al.","title":"Statistical Characterization of Hot Jupiter Atmospheres Using Spitzer's Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.07040","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp87b-Andersonetal2014","cite":"Anderson et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. R. Anderson, D. J. A. Brown, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Six newly-discovered hot Jupiters transiting F/G stars: WASP-87b, WASP-108b, WASP-109b, WASP-110b, WASP-111b & WASP-112b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.3449","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp87b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6041 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the inner planet, TOI-6041 b, a warm Neptune of 4.55 Earth radii on a 26.05-day orbit (Heidari et al. 2026). That planet's transits arrive early or late by about 1 hour from peak to peak, which suggests a gravitational tug from another planet (Heidari et al. 2026). Velocity measurements reveal TOI-6041 c on an 88-day orbit, with a minimum mass of 0.25 Jupiter masses (Heidari et al. 2026). To explain the transit timing shifts, the orbit of planet c must be fairly stretched, with an eccentricity of about 0.3 (Heidari et al. 2026).","system":"The star is a bright G7-type star of visual brightness 9.84 (Heidari et al. 2026). Orbit calculations show that the stretched-orbit arrangement could be stable for a planet c mass up to 0.8 Jupiter masses (Heidari et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The true mass of TOI-6041 c is not known, and other causes of the timing shifts have not been ruled out (Heidari et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6041c-Heidarietal2026","cite":"Heidari et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"N. Heidari, A. Alnajjarine, H. P. Osborn et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT'EM) survey: VII. TOI-6041: A multi-planet system including a warm Neptune exhibiting strong transit-timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-158 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-158 b is a relatively massive hot Jupiter of 2.8 Jupiter masses and 1.1 Jupiter radii, orbiting every 3.66 days (Hellier et al. 2019). It was announced in a batch of eight new hot Jupiters, which came from a southern-sky transit survey followed up with the Euler/CORALIE and TRAPPIST-South telescopes (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"The host is an F6-type star of visual brightness V = 12.1 (Hellier et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp158b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-54 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-54 b is one of five hot Jupiters announced together from the HATSouth survey (Espinoza et al. 2019). It counts as a prototypical short-period hot Jupiter, alongside HATS-55 b and HATS-58A b, with orbits of 2.5 to 4.2 days and sizes of about 1.1 to 1.2 Jupiter radii (Espinoza et al. 2019). The three planets span temperatures from 1350 to 1750 K, which puts them in the range where the swelling of hot Jupiters is proposed to work most efficiently (Espinoza et al. 2019). Spectrographs named FEROS and HARPS were used to confirm and weigh the planets (Espinoza et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats54b-Espinozaetal2019","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Espinoza, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-54b-HATS-58Ab: Five New Transiting Hot Jupiters Including One with a Possible Temperate Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-45 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-45 b is one of four short-period giant planets announced together. Their masses range from 0.26 to 0.90 Jupiter masses, yet their radii are all close to one Jupiter radius, so their densities differ widely (Brahm et al. 2018). Orbital periods in the group run from 2.7 to 4.7 days, and the host stars range from late F to early K in type (Brahm et al. 2018). All four stars are moderately bright, which leaves room for detailed follow-up observations (Brahm et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats45b-Brahmetal2018","cite":"Brahm et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Brahm, J. D. Hartman, A. Jordán et al.","title":"HATS-43b, HATS-44b, HATS-45b, and HATS-46b: Four Short-period Transiting Giant Planets in the Neptune-Jupiter Mass Range","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.07093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 10647 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star q1 Eridani, also called HD 10647, is surrounded by a bright disc of cold dust left over from colliding rocks and ice. Far-infrared images from the Herschel space telescope resolved the disc (Liseau et al. 2010). Radio images from the ALMA array show a broad belt spanning 34 to 134 au, tilted by 76.7 degrees and brightest at about 81.6 au (Lovell et al. 2021). The planet itself orbits at about 2 au, far too close in to shape a belt that distant (Lovell et al. 2021).","system":"The belt is lopsided: one side is brighter and lies closer to the star, and scattered starlight seen with the Hubble Space Telescope is also uneven (Lovell et al. 2021). The disc has the highest fractional luminosity among the closest 300 sun-like stars (Lovell et al. 2021). The star is about 1.4 billion years old (Lovell et al. 2021).","history":"The lopsided belt fits a second, more distant planet at about 60 au, with a mass between 8 Earth masses and 11 Jupiter masses, that may have migrated outwards like Neptune in the Solar System (Lovell et al. 2021).","open":"An early check of the star's velocity data found only weak evidence for the planet and could not confirm it, because the signal was about as large as the star's own jitter (Jones et al. 2004). Whether a planet sits at 60 au is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd10647b-Liseauetal2010","cite":"Liseau et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"R. Liseau, C. Eiroa, D. Fedele et al.","title":"Resolving the cold debris disc around a planet-hosting star . PACS photometric imaging observations of q<SUP>1</SUP> Eridani (HD 10647, HR 506)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.3137","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10647b-Lovelletal2021","cite":"Lovell et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"J. B. Lovell, S. Marino, M. C. Wyatt et al.","title":"High-resolution ALMA and HST images of q<SUP>1</SUP> Eri: an asymmetric debris disc with an eccentric Jupiter","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.05975","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10647b-Jonesetal2004","cite":"Jones et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"H. R. A. Jones, R. P. Butler, C. G. Tinney et al.","title":"HD10647 and the Distribution of Exoplanet Properties with Semi-major Axis","journal":"Extrasolar Planets: Today and Tomorrow","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-52 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-52 c is one of three planets, with Kepler-52 b and Kepler-57 b, whose measured masses of 30 to 100 Earth masses imply densities higher than iron planets of the same size (Mocquet et al. 2013). Such densities are hard to explain with ordinary rock and metal.","system":"","history":"One proposal is that these planets are the naked solid cores of gas giants that lost their atmospheres, for instance while migrating towards their star (Mocquet et al. 2013). Work has looked at the conditions under which such a core could stay close to its original, highly squeezed state (Mocquet et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler52c-Mocquetetal2013","cite":"Mocquet et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"A. Mocquet, O. Grasset, C. Sotin","title":"Super-dense remnants of gas giant exoplanets","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-13 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transit observations with the RISE camera on the 2-m Liverpool Telescope pinned down the tilt of the orbit at about 85 degrees, giving a planet radius of 1.39 Jupiter radii (Barros et al. 2012). Its density is only 0.17 that of Jupiter, and its radius is too large for standard models of heating by starlight (Barros et al. 2012). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1500 K (Barros et al. 2012). Infrared thermal emission from the planet was measured with the Spitzer telescope (Kilpatrick et al. 2017). A first high-resolution search for its atmosphere used four transits (Sicilia et al. 2026).","system":"The host star has swollen to about 1.56 times the Sun's radius and has evolved off the main sequence into its hydrogen-shell-burning phase (Barros et al. 2012). The orbit is prograde and consistent with alignment with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of 8 degrees (Brothwell et al. 2014).","history":"The aligned orbit around a cool star supports the general trend that aligned systems are found preferentially around cool hosts (Brothwell et al. 2014).","open":"The size of the planet is larger than standard irradiation models can explain (Barros et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp13b-Barrosetal2012","cite":"Barros et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, D. L. Pollacco, N. P. Gibson et al.","title":"High-precision transit observations of the exoplanet WASP-13b with the RISE instrument","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3996","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp13b-Kilpatricketal2017","cite":"Kilpatrick et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Brian M. Kilpatrick, Nikole K. Lewis, Tiffany Kataria et al.","title":"Spitzer Secondary Eclipse Depths with Multiple Intrapixel Sensitivity Correction Methods Observations of WASP-13b, WASP-15b, WASP-16b, WASP-62b, and HAT-P-22b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.08708","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp13b-Siciliaetal2026","cite":"Sicilia et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"D. Sicilia, M. Basilicata, G. Guilluy et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG LXXXII. The first atmospheric investigation of the gas giant WASP-13 b at high resolution with GIARPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12677","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp13b-Brothwelletal2014","cite":"Brothwell et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"R. D. Brothwell, C. A. Watson, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"A window on exoplanet dynamical histories: Rossiter-McLaughlin observations of WASP-13b and WASP-32b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.4095","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"alf Ari b":{"text":{"why":"The signal came from a survey of 55 giant stars with the BOES spectrograph in Korea, searching for planets around intermediate-mass stars and for star pulsations (Lee et al. 2011).","observed":"The host is an oscillating K2 giant star. Its velocity varies by 41 m/s over 381 days, in an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.25 (Lee et al. 2011). The variation does not track chromospheric activity indicators, and a bisector analysis shows no obvious link either (Lee et al. 2011). The bisector does show a periodic component that may come from the star's rotation (Lee et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"A planet is the likely cause, but spots on the star's surface have not been fully ruled out, and the discoverers describe the planet as likely rather than confirmed (Lee et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-alfarib-Leeetal2011","cite":"Lee et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, David E. Mkrtichian, In-Woo Han et al.","title":"Search for extrasolar planets around K-giants: \\alpha Arietis - planet or surface features?","journal":"Bulletin of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2136 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2136 b is a sub-Neptune with a radius of about 2.2 Earth radii and a mass of 6.4 Earth masses (Gan et al. 2022). Its mass and radius fit a broad range of compositions, from water-ice worlds to gas-dominated ones (Gan et al. 2022). The planet lies close to the radius valley predicted for M dwarfs, the gap where atmosphere loss splits rocky planets from puffier ones (Gan et al. 2022). A search for helium escaping from its atmosphere found no statistically significant absorption (Kawauchi et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a nearby M dwarf, with a rotation period of about 75 days (Gan et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Its interior structure and atmosphere are not yet known (Gan et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2136b-Ganetal2022","cite":"Gan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Tianjun Gan, Abderahmane Soubkiou, Sharon X. Wang et al.","title":"TESS discovery of a sub-Neptune orbiting a mid-M dwarf TOI-2136","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.10024","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2136b-Kawauchietal2026","cite":"Kawauchi et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Kiyoe Kawauchi, Norio Narita, Yuichi Ito et al.","title":"A search for helium in the atmospheres of three sub-Neptunes and a super-Earth around M-dwarfs","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19030","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 109246 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 109246 b has a minimum mass of 0.77 Jupiter masses and an orbital eccentricity of 0.12 (Boisse et al. 2010). Its 68-day period places it in a sparsely populated region of the period distribution of known planets (Boisse et al. 2010). The host is a G0 star slightly richer in metals than the Sun (Boisse et al. 2010). The discovery announcement also presented a correction for the so-called seeing effect that disturbs velocities from the SOPHIE spectrograph (Boisse et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd109246b-Boisseetal2010","cite":"Boisse et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"I. Boisse, A. Eggenberger, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. III. A Jupiter-mass companion around HD 109246","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.4984","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-81 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A study of 268 hot-Jupiter host stars found that the star of WASP-81 b belongs to the Milky Way's thick disk, an older population rich in alpha elements, which had not been recognised before (DiTomasso et al. 2026). None of the planets around such thick-disk stars in the sample orbits in less than 2.7 days, while 22% of those around thin-disk stars do (DiTomasso et al. 2026). The thick-disk planets are also less massive than the typical thin-disk planet (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Differences between disk populations may reflect different giant-planet formation across galactic environments, later evolution, or differing stellar properties (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp81b-DiTomassoetal2026","cite":"DiTomasso et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Victoria DiTomasso, Samuel W. Yee, Romy Rodriguez Martinez et al.","title":"A Sample of Thick-Disk Stars Hosting Hot Jupiters","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22254","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 9827 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 9827 b belongs to a tightly packed system of three small planets whose orbital periods of about 1.2, 3.6 and 6.2 days lie near a 1:3:5 ratio (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). Combining space photometry with velocity measurements from three spectrographs gave the planet a density of about 5.1 g per cubic centimetre (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018). GJ 9827 is the nearest planetary system, at about 30 pc, found by the Kepler or K2 missions, and its star is bright, which makes detailed study possible (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018).","system":"Multi-planet systems are valued because all the planets formed under the same initial conditions (Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj9827b-PrietoArranzetal2018","cite":"Prieto-Arranz et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Prieto-Arranz, E. Palle, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Mass determination of the 1:3:5 near-resonant planets transiting GJ 9827 (K2-135)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.09557","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 98-59 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"L 98-59 b is the lowest-mass planet measured so far with radial velocities, with about half the mass of Venus (Demangeon et al. 2021). Four transits observed with the JWST NIRSpec instrument gave a transmission spectrum (Bello-Arufe et al. 2025). A flat spectrum, meaning no atmosphere, fits the data adequately, but a sulphur dioxide atmosphere is preferred at 3.6 sigma (Bello-Arufe et al. 2025). Such an atmosphere would likely be in a steady state in which volcanic outgassing balances escape to space (Bello-Arufe et al. 2025).","system":"The star has at least four planets, three of which transit, plus a fourth non-transiting planet with a 12.8-day period and a possible fifth with a 23-day period that would sit in the middle of the habitable zone (Demangeon et al. 2021). Planets c and d lie very close to a 2:1 orbital resonance (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023).","history":"","open":"If the sulphur dioxide is real, the planet would need at least eight times as much volcanism and tidal heating per unit mass as Jupiter's moon Io, and possibly a magma ocean beneath the surface (Bello-Arufe et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l9859b-Demangeonetal2021","cite":"Demangeon et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"O. D. S. Demangeon, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: L 98-59 (TOI-175) ESPRESSO observations (Demangeon+, 2021)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36530041","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859b-BelloArufeetal2025","cite":"Bello-Arufe et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Aaron Bello-Arufe, Mario Damiano, Katherine A. Bennett et al.","title":"Evidence for a Volcanic Atmosphere on the Sub-Earth L 98-59 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859b-SchwarzDreizler2023","cite":"Schwarz & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler","title":"L98-59 (TOI-175) a case study for a multiple exoplanet system with small planets near resonance","journal":"EAS2023, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 4113 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 4113 b moves on a very eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.903 at about 1.28 au from its metal-rich G5 host (Tamuz et al. 2008). Its minimum mass is 1.6 Jupiter masses (Tamuz et al. 2008). Both this planet and the one found with it around HD 156846 have very high eccentricities, which can be explained by Kozai oscillations, a slow trade between orbital tilt and eccentricity driven by another body (Tamuz et al. 2008).","system":"The star shows an additional velocity drift of 28 m/s per year over 8 years, which points to a more distant companion (Tamuz et al. 2008). High-contrast imaging found no main-sequence star there, so the companion is a probable brown dwarf or a faint white dwarf (Tamuz et al. 2008). A companion too light to be a star, HD 4113 C, has since been directly imaged (Feng et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd4113b-Tamuzetal2008","cite":"Tamuz et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"O. Tamuz, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XV. Discovery of two eccentric planets orbiting <ASTROBJ>HD 4113</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 156846</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.5028","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd4113b-Fengetal2022","cite":"Feng et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Fabo Feng, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"3D Selection of 167 Substellar Companions to Nearby Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.12720","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LTT 3780 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system's recent study concerns the pair of planets together. LTT 3780 b is a rocky super-Earth, and LTT 3780 c is a temperate sub-Neptune, the two lying on opposite sides of the radius valley (Tremblay et al. 2026). A radio search for narrowband technosignatures used the Allen Telescope Array and the Very Large Array for about 30 hours in the 1 to 10 GHz range, and found no candidate signals (Tremblay et al. 2026).","system":"The outer planet, LTT 3780 c, has been proposed as a candidate Hycean world, a planet with a hydrogen atmosphere over a deep ocean, and has recent JWST atmospheric observations (Tremblay et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ltt3780b-Tremblayetal2026","cite":"Tremblay et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Chenoa D. Tremblay, Sofia Z. Sheikh, Vishal Gajjar et al.","title":"A Search for Narrowband Technosignatures from LTT 3780 with the Allen Telescope Array and the Karl G. Jansky Very Large Array","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07182","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 207897 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 207897 b is a dense sub-Neptune, one of the relatively rare dense ones, with a radius of 2.50 Earth radii (Heidari et al. 2022). Its mass is either 14.4 or 15.9 Earth masses with nearly equal probability, because the two solutions match two possible periods for the star's own activity cycle (Heidari et al. 2022). The density is accordingly either 5.1 or about 5.5 g per cubic centimetre (Heidari et al. 2022). The bright K0 host star is only 28 pc away (Heidari et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"A dense planet only 0.12 au from its star is unusual among sub-Neptunes, and the most likely explanation is that it migrated to its current position (Heidari et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd207897b-Heidarietal2022","cite":"Heidari et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"N. Heidari, I. Boisse, J. Orell-Miquel et al.","title":"HD 207897 b: A dense sub-Neptune transiting a nearby and bright K-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.08597","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 171238 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 171238 b follows a 1523-day orbit with an eccentricity of 0.40 at about 2.5 au from its G8 host (Ségransan et al. 2010). The radial velocities gave a minimum mass of 2.6 Jupiter masses (Ségransan et al. 2010). Adding absolute astrometry from the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites removed that uncertainty and gave a true mass of about 8.8 Jupiter masses, confirming that it is a planet (Li et al. 2021). The host star is rich in metals, which further supports the idea that massive planets tend to form around metal-rich stars (Ségransan et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd171238b-Sgransanetal2010","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Ségransan, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVI. Discovery of a planetary system around HD 147018 and of two long period and massive planets orbiting HD 171238 and HD 204313","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1479","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd171238b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 93385 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 93385 b comes from a HARPS survey of five solar-type stars that together yielded 12 super-Earths (Unger et al. 2021). More than 10 years of fairly dense observations made the survey sensitive to low-mass planets on orbits of up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021). Bayesian model comparison, a statistical way to weigh how many planets the data support, was used to decide how many planets each star has (Unger et al. 2021). For some stars, different models of stellar noise were compared to separate planet signals from activity (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd93385b-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-43 c":{"text":{"why":"The signal was missed because of instrumental noise, search biases and star variability, three factors that can confound planet hunting (Hedges et al. 2019).","observed":"K2-43 c is one of four small planets, with K2-168 c, K2-198 c and K2-198 d, that went undetected in several large K2 planet searches although their transit signals had a signal-to-noise ratio of 10 or more (Hedges et al. 2019). Fast open-source tools for handling light curves recovered them (Hedges et al. 2019). All four were confirmed as true planets, and two of the four are new discoveries (Hedges et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k243c-Hedgesetal2019","cite":"Hedges et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christina Hedges, Nicholas Saunders, Geert Barentsen et al.","title":"Four Small Planets Buried in K2 Systems: What Can We Learn for TESS?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.08244","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 147513 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Observations of HD 147513 with a spectro-polarimeter, an instrument that reads magnetic signatures in starlight, show a weak surface magnetic field of 1.0 to 3.2 G (Hussain et al. 2016). Night-to-night velocity variations follow the same timescale as the magnetic field, so they likely come from active regions rotating with the star rather than from the planet's 528-day orbit (Hussain et al. 2016). The star's rotation period is about 10 days, and the star is seen at a low inclination of about 18 degrees (Hussain et al. 2016).","system":"Chemical analysis suggests that the star went through a barium-enrichment phase because of a higher-mass companion (Hussain et al. 2016). The star still has a fairly typical activity level for its rotation (Hussain et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Simulations of hypothetical terrestrial planets sharing the giant planet's orbit in the habitable zone have mapped where such Trojan planets could stay stable (Funk et al. 2012). Whether any exist is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd147513b-Hussainetal2016","cite":"Hussain et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"G. A. J. Hussain, J. D. Alvarado-Gómez, J. Grunhut et al.","title":"A spectro-polarimetric study of the planet-hosting G dwarf, HD 147513","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.02127","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd147513b-Funketal2012","cite":"Funk et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"B. Funk, R. Schwarz, Á. Süli et al.","title":"On the stability of possible Trojan planets in the habitable zone: an application to the systems HD 147513 and HD 210277","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2966.2012.21121.x","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-164 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-164 b is a hot Jupiter of 2.13 Jupiter masses and 1.13 Jupiter radii orbiting a G2 star every 1.8 days (Lendl et al. 2019). Its orbit is close in but still tidally stable, meaning the star's pull should not drag it in quickly (Lendl et al. 2019). Among four planets announced together, it was singled out as a good target for emission spectroscopy, which measures light from the planet's own heat (Lendl et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp164b-Lendletal2019","cite":"Lendl et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Lendl, D. R. Anderson, A. Bonfanti et al.","title":"WASP-147b, 160Bb, 164b, and 165b: two hot Saturns and two Jupiters, including two planets with metal-rich hosts","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06973","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"bet Pic c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star and disc, and most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour bet Pic b. Beta Pictoris is the best-studied normal star ringed by a dust disc, which holds much more dust and many more rock fragments than the Solar System does today (Artymowicz 1997). Images in polarised light reveal the disc between about 6 and 33 au, nearly edge-on, with an inner edge near 23.6 au (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015).","system":"The inner disc is tilted slightly from the main outer disc (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015). Planet b has a semimajor axis of about 9.2 au and an eccentricity of at most 0.26 (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015). Its orbit constrains the star's mass to 1.60 solar masses (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015).","history":"The disc is thought to be shaped by collisions among orbiting solid bodies, with tiny debris blown out by starlight pressure (Artymowicz 1997).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-betpicc-Artymowicz1997","cite":"Artymowicz 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Pawel Artymowicz","title":"Beta Pictoris: an Early Solar System?","journal":"Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.25.1.175","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-betpicc-MillarBlanchaeretal2015","cite":"Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maxwell A. Millar-Blanchaer, James R. Graham, Laurent Pueyo et al.","title":"Beta Pictoris' Inner Disk in Polarized Light and New Orbital Parameters for Beta Pictoris b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.04787","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-88 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Imaging of the star with the SPHERE high-contrast camera on the Very Large Telescope, part of a survey of 45 planet-host stars, turned up a new candidate companion near WASP-88 (Bohn et al. 2020). Whether it is a true companion of the star is a separate question that the survey tests with common-motion checks. The planet's discovery data combined transit photometry from the TRAPPIST and Euler-Swiss telescopes with radial velocities from the Euler/CORALIE spectrograph (Delrez et al. 2014).","system":"Two other hot Jupiters, WASP-68 b and WASP-73 b, orbit similar evolved late-F or early-G stars in 4-5 day orbits, yet all three planets have very different bulk properties (Delrez et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp88b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp88b-Delrezetal2014","cite":"Delrez et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"L. Delrez, V. Van Grootel, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-68b, WASP-73b, WASP-88b transits (Delrez+, 2014)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35630143","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp88b-Delrezetal2016","cite":"Delrez et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Laetitia Delrez","title":"Detection and Characterization of Transiting Hot Jupiters","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-201 b":{"text":{"why":"Warm Jupiters have uncertain origins. Their spin tilts could tell scattering or migration through resonances, which can tilt them strongly, from formation in place, which should give low tilts (Lammers & Winn 2026).","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet comes from its place in a larger system and from a search for signs of flattening. Its transit light curve shows no sign that the planet is squashed at the poles, and fits a spherical planet equally well (Lammers & Winn 2026). Because oblateness and tilt of the spin axis trade off against each other, the light curve alone cannot say how tilted the spin is (Lammers & Winn 2026).","system":"A super-Earth, TOI-201 c, with a radius of 1.64 Earth radii, orbits closer in every 5.85 days around the same young, active star (Mireles et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"If warm Jupiters are as flattened as Saturn, their typical spin tilt is below 12 degrees with 90% confidence, but Jupiter-like flattening and larger tilts are still allowed (Lammers & Winn 2026). JWST observations could tighten this (Lammers & Winn 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi201b-LammersWinn2026","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Constraining the Planetary Obliquity Distribution of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01599","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi201b-Mirelesetal2023","cite":"Mireles et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ismael Mireles, Diana Dragomir, Katharine Hesse","title":"TOI-201 c and TOI-1670 d: two new planets in warm Jupiter systems discovered by TESS","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #242","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-60 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of seven hot Jupiters and Saturns found around bright Sun-like stars by a ground-based survey (Bakos et al. 2021). Its host star is especially bright, with a visual magnitude of 9.710, which makes it an excellent target for further follow-up (Bakos et al. 2021). The planet's radius is about 1.63 times Jupiter's, while its mass is only 0.57 times Jupiter's, so it is a swollen, low-density world (Bakos et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp60b-Bakosetal2021","cite":"Bakos et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti et al.","title":"HAT-P-58b-HAT-P-64b: Seven Planets Transiting Bright Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4600 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the inner of two giant planets around an early K dwarf. Transits of the system were first spotted by a TESS group that looks for single-transit candidates (Mireles et al. 2023). Ground-based spectroscopy, photometry and high-resolution imaging were combined with the space data to confirm both planets (Mireles et al. 2023). With an equilibrium temperature of 347 K, the planet is a temperate giant, bridging the gap between hot or warm Jupiters and the solar system's gas giants (Mireles et al. 2023).","system":"The outer planet, TOI-4600 c, orbits every 482.82 days and is the longest-period confirmed or validated planet that TESS has found so far (Mireles et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"More transit and precise radial-velocity observations could measure the planets' masses and orbits and search for further non-transiting planets (Mireles et al. 2023). Both planets are suitable for atmospheric study with JWST (Mireles et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4600b-Mirelesetal2023","cite":"Mireles et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ismael Mireles, Diana Dragomir, Hugh P. Osborn et al.","title":"TOI-4600 b and c: Two Long-period Giant Planets Orbiting an Early K Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.15572","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4504 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-4504 d. The planet shows the largest transit timing variations known, with a peak-to-node amplitude of about 2 days and a repeating pattern, or superperiod, of about 930 days (Vítková et al. 2025). Transits come early or late by this much because the planet is tugged by a neighbour in a resonant orbit. Combining the timing shifts with radial-velocity data from the FEROS spectrograph gave a mass of 3.77 Jupiter masses, a radius of 0.99 Jupiter radii and an osculating period of 82.54 days (Vítková et al. 2025).","system":"A non-transiting giant, TOI-4504 d, orbits every 40.56 days with a mass of 1.42 Jupiter masses; the two giants are in resonance (Vítková et al. 2025). A smaller inner planet, TOI-4504 b, has a radius of 2.69 Earth radii and a 2.4261-day period (Vítková et al. 2025).","history":"The timing shifts, together with the radial velocities, revealed the third planet, TOI-4504 d, which does not transit (Vítková et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4504c-Vtkovetal2025","cite":"Vítková et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michaela Vítková, Rafael Brahm, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"TOI-4504: Exceptionally Large Transit Timing Variations Induced by Two Resonant Warm Gas Giants in a Three-planet System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05609","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 154088 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of 12 super-Earths found around five Sun-like stars in a radial-velocity survey that spanned more than 10 years with fairly dense sampling (Unger et al. 2021). The survey can detect low-mass planets down to super-Earths on orbits up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021). Bayesian model comparison, a statistical method that weighs how well different numbers of planets fit the data, was used to decide how many planets each star hosts, and for some stars different noise models were compared to separate planet signals from stellar activity (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd154088b-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 162020 b":{"text":{"why":"Considerations of tidal dissipation in this short-period system point towards a brown-dwarf nature for the companion (Udry et al. 2002).","observed":"The discovery measurements gave a minimum mass of 14.4 Jupiter masses, near the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Udry et al. 2002). The orbit is fairly eccentric, and the host star is poor in heavy elements compared with the Sun, unlike most stars with planets (Udry et al. 2002). A search for X-ray changes in the star found variability that follows the companion's orbit, but only at marginal significance (Pillitteri et al. 2013). The companion was also one of the targets of a radio search for emission from hot Jupiters at 150 MHz (Winterhalter et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a brown dwarf is not settled (Udry et al. 2002)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd162020b-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd162020b-Pillitterietal2013","cite":"Pillitteri et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Ignazio Pillitteri, Scott Wolk, Javier Lopez-Santiago et al.","title":"Study of the X-ray Emission of Stars with Hot Jupiters.","journal":"Protostars and Planets VI Posters","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd162020b-Winterhalteretal2005","cite":"Winterhalter et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"D. Winterhalter, WA Majid, TBH Kuiper et al.","title":"Search for Radio Emissions from Extrasolar Planets at 150 MHz","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-30 b":{"text":{"why":"Long-period transiting planets bridge the gap between close-in transit discoveries and Doppler discoveries, and they receive less starlight, so they should keep more of their original atmospheres (Battley et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was followed up from a single transit seen by TESS, using photometry from TESS, NGTS and EulerCam and spectroscopy from CORALIE, FEROS, HARPS and PFS to find its orbit and mass (Battley et al. 2024). Its host star is only about 1.1 billion years old, so the planet is one of the youngest well-studied long-period exoplanets (Battley et al. 2024). Interior modelling gives a heavy-element mass fraction of 0.23, about 20 times the enrichment of its host star (Battley et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts30b-Battleyetal2024","cite":"Battley et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. P. Battley, K. A. Collins, S. Ulmer-Moll et al.","title":"NGTS-30b/TOI-4862b: An  1 Gyr old 98-day transiting warm Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02974","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"4 UMa b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to understand the diverse nature of the radial-velocity variations that K giants show (Döllinger et al. 2007).","observed":"The host is a K giant, a star that has swollen after leaving the main sequence. It was one of 62 K giants monitored for three years for signs of companions (Döllinger et al. 2007). The star's radial velocity varies with a period of 269.3 days and a semi-amplitude of 216.8 metres per second (Döllinger et al. 2007). An orbit with an eccentricity of 0.43 is the most reasonable explanation, giving a minimum companion mass of 7.1 Jupiter masses (Döllinger et al. 2007).","system":"The host star has a metallicity of -0.25, lower in heavy elements than the Sun, and a mass of 1.23 solar masses (Döllinger et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-4umab-Dllingeretal2007","cite":"Döllinger et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. P. Döllinger, A. P. Hatzes, L. Pasquini et al.","title":"Discovery of a planet around the K giant star 4 Ursae Majoris","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703672","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2022-BLG-033L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet appeared as a short dip in the light curve of a magnified background star, close to the peak of the event (Han et al. 2025). The dip arose as the background star passed through a region of reduced brightness behind the central caustic, along the line joining planet and host (Han et al. 2025). Modelling gives a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 7.5 x 10^-5 (Han et al. 2025). Estimates of the system's physical properties come from statistical analysis of the light-curve measurements (Han et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Two solutions fit the light curve equally, one with the planet closer to its host and one farther away, so the planet's projected separation from its host star is not known (Han et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2022blg033lb-Hanetal2025","cite":"Han et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cheongho Han, Ian A. Bond, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"MOA-2022-BLG-033Lb, KMT-2023-BLG-0119Lb, and KMT-2023-BLG-1896Lb: Three low mass-ratio microlensing planets detected through dip signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02193","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5789 d":{"text":{"why":"Sub-Neptunes are the most common planets around Sun-like stars on short orbits, and whether they are mostly gas dwarfs or water worlds is unclear (Bonomo et al. 2026).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour TOI-5789 c. That sub-Neptune has a radius of 2.86 Earth radii, a mass of 5.00 Earth masses and a density of 1.16 grams per cubic centimetre (Bonomo et al. 2026). It transits a bright, magnetically inactive K1 dwarf and was measured with 141 radial velocities from the HARPS-N spectrograph (Bonomo et al. 2026).","system":"The system has several planets whose orbits are tilted relative to each other (Bonomo et al. 2026). The inner planet TOI-5789 c is a promising target for atmospheric study with JWST and, later, Ariel (Bonomo et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5789d-Bonomoetal2026","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, L. Naponiello, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG: LXXI. A sub-Neptune suitable for atmospheric characterization in a multiplanet and mutually inclined system orbiting the bright K dwarf TOI-5789 (HIP 99452)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86264 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a metal-rich F7 dwarf. It is one of five long-period planets reported from Doppler data from Lick Observatory, whose typical eccentricities are higher than the average for known exoplanets (Fischer et al. 2009). The measurements give a minimum mass of 7.0 Jupiter masses, a separation of 2.86 AU and an eccentricity of 0.7 (Fischer et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86264b-Fischeretal2009","cite":"Fischer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Debra Fischer, Peter Driscoll, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Five Planets and an Independent Confirmation of HD 196885Ab from Lick Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1596","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-15 b":{"text":{"why":"The low density and large atmosphere make it a good candidate for transmission spectroscopy (Petit dit de la Roche et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet is one of the least dense transiting exoplanets known, with a density of 0.247 grams per cubic centimetre (West et al. 2009). The host star is of spectral type around F5 and slightly poor in heavy elements (West et al. 2009). The angle between the planet's orbit and the star's spin axis was measured through the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the distortion in the starlight caused by the transit (Southworth et al. 2013). New multi-colour transit photometry refined the planet's properties and orbit timing (Southworth et al. 2013). An atmosphere study with the VLT used a statistical technique to remove instrument noise and produce the first transmission spectrum of the planet (Petit dit de la Roche et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Calculations suggest the planet may have lost up to 1-4% of its initial mass through atmospheric escape (Lammer et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp15b-Rocheetal2021","cite":"Roche et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Dominique Petit dit de la Roche, Mario van den Ancker, Paulo Miles Páez","title":"The first atmospheric characterisation of Wasp-15b with Gaussian Process modelling","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2021-693","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp15b-Westetal2009","cite":"West et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"R. G. West, D. R. Anderson, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The Low Density Transiting Exoplanet WASP-15b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.2651","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp15b-Southworthetal2013","cite":"Southworth et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"John Southworth, L. Mancini, P. Browne et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocusing - V. WASP-15 and WASP-16","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3509","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp15b-Lammeretal2009","cite":"Lammer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"H. Lammer, P. Odert, M. Leitzinger et al.","title":"Determining the mass loss limit for close-in exoplanets: what can we learn from transit observations?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200911922","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1130 b":{"text":{"why":"The atmosphere's makeup is read as evidence that the planet formed beyond the water ice line (Barat et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet's atmosphere was observed with JWST and shows water (7.5 sigma), carbon dioxide (3.3 sigma) and sulphur dioxide (3.6 sigma), plus a tentative sign of methane (Barat et al. 2026). Its spectrum has a strong slope towards blue light, in line with TESS and CHEOPS transit depths (Barat et al. 2026). The atmosphere is rich in heavy elements, about log(Z/Zsun) = 1.8, with a carbon-to-oxygen ratio below 0.75 and a high mean molecular weight (Barat et al. 2026).","system":"The planet is in a 2:1 orbital resonance with the hot Jupiter TOI-1130 c; this is the first known system with a hot Jupiter and an inner lower-mass planet locked in resonance (Korth et al. 2023). Radial velocities from HARPS and PFS spectrographs confirmed the planet, which had only been validated before (Korth et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1130b-Baratetal2026","cite":"Barat et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Saugata Barat, Tyler Fairnington, Shelby Courreges et al.","title":"JWST Unveils a High Mean Molecular Weight Atmosphere for Mini-Neptune TOI-1130 b: Evidence for Formation Beyond the Water Ice Line","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02036","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1130b-Korthetal2023","cite":"Korth et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Korth, D. Gandolfi, J. Šubjak et al.","title":"TOI-1130: A photodynamical analysis of a hot Jupiter in resonance with an inner low-mass planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15565","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-34 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit is prograde, meaning it circles in the same direction as the star spins, and well aligned with the star's spin axis, with an angle of -1 degrees (with an uncertainty of about 10 degrees), measured through the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Hirano et al. 2016). Its mass of 1.773 Jupiter masses was measured with intensive radial-velocity follow-up (Hirano et al. 2016). Imaging with the Subaru telescope found a faint companion candidate 0.36 arcseconds from the star (Hirano et al. 2016). Further observations are needed to show whether it is physically associated with K2-34 (Hirano et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a metal-rich F-type star (Hirano et al. 2016). An independent study of the system refined the planet's parameters using velocities from several groups (Brahm et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k234b-Hiranoetal2016","cite":"Hirano et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Grzegorz Nowak, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"The K2-ESPRINT Project IV. A Hot Jupiter in a Prograde Orbit with a Possible Stellar Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.00638","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k234b-Brahmetal2016","cite":"Brahm et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Matías Jones, Néstor Espinoza et al.","title":"An Independent Discovery of Two Hot Jupiters from the K2 Mission","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.01721","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0912L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet showed up as a very short anomaly in the light curve of a microlensing event (Han et al. 2022). Four explanations were tested: three with a lens made of two masses and one in which the background source is a double star (Han et al. 2022). The double-source model was ruled out because the faint source companion would have to be bigger than its primary (Han et al. 2022). Two solutions survive, with planet-to-host mass ratios of about 2.8 x 10^-5 and 1.1 x 10^-5 (Han et al. 2022).","system":"A subtle deviation near the centre of the event hints at a third lens component, either a companion to the host at a very large or very small separation, or a second planet near the Einstein ring (Han et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether a third body exists is not settled, because the fit improves only marginally and the data sets disagree (Han et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0912lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Ian A. Bond, Jennifer C. Yee et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-0912Lb: a microlensing super Earth around a K-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.04861","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2048 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was among 20 worlds checked with high-resolution spectrographs for helium or hydrogen gas streaming from their atmospheres, as part of a survey of young planets' atmospheric escape (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). New helium detections were reported for TOI-1268 b and TOI-2018 b, and a hydrogen detection for TOI-1136 d, with hints for a few others, but TOI-2048 b is not among them (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Across 70 planets studied, no evidence was found that young planets lose their atmospheres faster (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2048b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-34 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet crosses its star at a high impact parameter of 0.90, so it only just clips the stellar disc, and it may be the first known transiting planet to undergo grazing transits, with 80% confidence (Smalley et al. 2011). Two eclipse observations with the Spitzer Space Telescope measured the planet's dayside glow (Challener et al. 2022). The spectrum most resembles a blackbody, with no constraint on molecules, and it is redder than other warm Jupiters of similar temperature, which hints at unusual chemistry (Challener et al. 2022). The eccentricity is consistent with zero (Challener et al. 2022).","system":"A steady drift in the star's velocity of 55 metres per second per year points to a third body with a mass of at least 0.45 Jupiter masses at least 1.2 AU away (Smalley et al. 2011). It may be a low-mass star, a white dwarf or another planet (Smalley et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"The nature of the distant third body and the grazing geometry are both still uncertain (Smalley et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp34b-Smalleyetal2011","cite":"Smalley et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"B. Smalley, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-34b: a near-grazing transiting sub-Jupiter-mass exoplanet in a hierarchical triple system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.2278","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp34b-Challeneretal2022","cite":"Challener et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Ryan C. Challener, Joseph Harrington, Patricio E. Cubillos et al.","title":"Spitzer Dayside Emission of WASP-34b","journal":"The Planetary Science Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04101","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-90 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of eight K2 candidates observed in transit with the Spitzer Space Telescope at 4.5 micrometres (Livingston et al. 2019). Combining the K2 and Spitzer light curves gave improved radii and multi-wavelength information that helps confirm planets as real (Livingston et al. 2019). The Spitzer timings sharpen the predicted transit times, reducing the uncertainty in mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes for the sample (Livingston et al. 2019). K2-90 b is named as a promising target for radial-velocity mass measurement (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k290b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"eps Tau b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The host is one of the giant stars of the Hyades cluster, and its boron content is greatly reduced below the solar value, which is a clue to deep mixing and mass loss inside the star (Duncan et al. 1994). Its brightness and velocity changes have also been recorded in time series from the K2 mission and the SONG telescope network (Arentoft et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epstaub-Duncanetal1994","cite":"Duncan et al. 1994","year":"1994","authors":"D. K. Duncan, R. C. Peterson, J. A. Thorburn et al.","title":"The Boron Abundance of the Hyades Giants","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #184","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epstaub-Arentoftetal2019","cite":"Arentoft et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"T. Arentoft, F. Grundahl, T. R. White et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: eps Tau intensity and RV time-series data (Arentoft+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36220190","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 c":{"text":{"why":"The system was presented as a test of how rare such tightly packed planetary systems are (Lissauer & Kepler Science Team 2011).","observed":"Kepler-11 has six transiting planets that all orbit closer to their star than Venus does to the Sun (Lissauer & Kepler Science Team 2011). A detailed Keck spectrum later showed that the star is a young solar twin with a chemical makeup close to the Sun's (Bedell et al. 2017). Planet densities rose by 20% to 95% after that revision, which makes them more typical of the \"puffy\" close-in planets seen elsewhere (Bedell et al. 2017).","system":"All six planets orbit within 0.5 AU of the star, and the inner five lie on nearly circular, flat orbits within 0.25 AU (Quintana et al. 2011).","history":"Computer simulations of planet building in a massive disc of gas and dust have tested whether the planets could have formed where they are now (Quintana et al. 2011).","open":"It is not settled whether such low-density planets are rocky worlds wrapped in thick hydrogen-rich atmospheres or small Neptunes with much of their mass in water (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11c-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11c-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11c-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 150010 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up searches for planets around giant stars in the northern sky near the celestial pole, using the Bohyunsan Optical Astronomy Observatory (Jeong et al. 2022).","observed":"The host is a giant star whose radial velocity, its motion toward and away from us, varies slowly over 562 days (Jeong et al. 2022). Brightness changes and distortions in the star's spectral lines were checked for the same period, and none were found (Jeong et al. 2022). The signal is therefore most likely caused by a planet of at least 2.4 Jupiter masses (Jeong et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Some interpretations are unclear, and recent studies may call some planet claims around giant stars into question (Jeong et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd150010b-Jeongetal2022","cite":"Jeong et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Jeong, B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park et al.","title":"Search for exoplanets around northern circumpolar stars. V. Three likely planetary companions to the giant stars HD 19615, HD 150010, and HD 174205","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-25 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was announced together with CoRoT-26b, another low-density giant (Almenara et al. 2013).","observed":"Follow-up photometry and radial velocity measurements from the ground confirmed the planetary nature of the transiting body (Almenara et al. 2013). The planet has a mass of 0.27 Jupiter masses and a radius near 1.08 Jupiter radii, which gives a low mean density of about 0.15 g per cubic centimetre (Almenara et al. 2013). Evolution models suggest a core of a few tens of Earth masses (Almenara et al. 2013).","system":"The host is an F9 main-sequence star of solar metallicity, about 4.5 billion years old, and faint at V magnitude 15.0 (Almenara et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot25b-Almenaraetal2013","cite":"Almenara et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. M. Almenara, F. Bouchy, P. Gaulme et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXIV. CoRoT-25b and CoRoT-26b: two low-density giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201321462","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-60 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Models of core-powered mass loss, in which heat leaking from the planet's core slowly drives its atmosphere away, list this planet among those that could be losing atmosphere today at a rate of at least 10 million grams per second (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). That holds only if the mechanism mainly shapes the gap between super-Earths and sub-Neptunes (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether core-powered mass loss is the main process behind the gap in planet sizes is still debated (Gupta & Schlichting 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler60b-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-144 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has 0.44 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.85 Jupiter radii, and it circles a K2 star every 2.27 days (Hellier et al. 2019). The star is faint, at V magnitude 12.9, and its brightness varies with a 21-day period as it rotates (Hellier et al. 2019). Seven other hot Jupiters were announced in the same report, found with the WASP-South survey and confirmed with the Euler/CORALIE and TRAPPIST-South telescopes (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp144b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1467 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of eight possible super-Earths confirmed together with statistical tools, not with a measured mass (Mistry et al. 2024).","observed":"Ground-based telescope data and high-resolution imaging were combined with a statistical tool called TRICERATOPS, which estimates how likely a signal is to be a false alarm (Mistry et al. 2024). The result supports a planet radius of about 1.83 Earth radii (Mistry et al. 2024). Model transmission spectra were also computed for the Hubble and James Webb space telescopes to see how the atmospheres could look (Mistry et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1467b-Mistryetal2024","cite":"Mistry et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Aniket Prasad, Mousam Maity et al.","title":"VaTEST III: Validation of eight potential super-earths from TESS data","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 338 B b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is one half of a wide pair of similar M dwarfs, and the planet circles the star GJ 338 B (González-Álvarez et al. 2020). The CARMENES spectrograph took 159 velocity measurements of that star between January 2016 and October 2018 (González-Álvarez et al. 2020). A signal near 24.45 days shows no counterpart in the star's activity indicators, which points away from starspots (González-Álvarez et al. 2020). A separate 16.61-day signal is blamed on the star's rotation (González-Álvarez et al. 2020).","system":"Two M0 dwarfs orbit each other, with dynamical masses of 0.64 and 0.69 solar masses for GJ 338 B and GJ 338 A, found from 200 years of position measurements (González-Álvarez et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj338bb-Gonzlezlvarezetal2020","cite":"González-Álvarez et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. González-Álvarez, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, J. A. Caballero et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. A super-Earth planet orbiting HD 79211 (GJ 338 B)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.13052","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-159 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot stars spin fast and pulsate, which makes planets around them hard to find, so such systems remain little explored (Mantovan et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet orbits a young star of about 150 million years that pulsates as a gamma Doradus variable (Mantovan et al. 2026). Its orbit is measurably eccentric, with e = 0.24, and at about 1900 K it is the hottest hot Jupiter with a significant eccentricity found so far (Mantovan et al. 2026). A low-resolution transmission spectrum was made as an exploratory look for an atmosphere (Mantovan et al. 2026).","system":"The planet is of the S-type, meaning it circles one star of a close binary (Mantovan et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has an atmosphere is only explored so far, with a preliminary analysis (Mantovan et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi159b-Mantovanetal2026","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Mantovan, A. Llancaqueo Albornoz, A. Psaridi et al.","title":"TOI-159 b: An eccentric hot-Jupiter planet around a young, pulsating γ Doradus star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.04149","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-7 b was the first super-Earth with a measured radius, found from very shallow dips of about 0.03% in the light of an active G9 star (Léger et al. 2009). Follow-up work ruled out nearly all false alarms, and the radius is 1.68 Earth radii (Léger et al. 2009). Stellar activity made early velocity measurements hard to read, so a clear mass came later (Fressin et al. 2009). HARPS data gave a mass of 4.8 Earth masses and a density equal to Earth's, suggesting a silicate-dominated makeup (Rouan et al. 2010).","system":"A second planet with about twice the mass lies on a slightly larger orbit, still extremely close to the star (Rouan et al. 2010).","history":"Tides may have pulled the planet inward from a wider orbit that received less starlight (Barnes et al. 2010).","open":"Tidal heating could dominate the planet's geology and drive extreme volcanism, making it a \"super-Io\" (Barnes et al. 2010). Interior models allow high water fractions even for planets that may be rocky (Dorn et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot7b-Lgeretal2009","cite":"Léger et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. Léger, D. Rouan, J. Schneider et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. VIII. CoRoT-7b: the first super-Earth with measured radius","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0241","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7b-Fressinetal2009","cite":"Fressin et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Francois Fressin, Suzanne Aigrain, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"CoRoT-Exo-7b: Confirming the first transiting rocky planet","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7b-Rouanetal2010","cite":"Rouan et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Rouan, A. Léger, J. Schneider et al.","title":"CoRoT-7b: The First Transiting Super-Earth Fully Characterized in Radius and Mass","journal":"Pathways Towards Habitable Planets","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7b-Barnesetal2010","cite":"Barnes et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Rory Barnes, Sean N. Raymond, Richard Greenberg et al.","title":"CoRoT-7b: Super-Earth or Super-Io?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1337","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7b-Dornetal2017","cite":"Dorn et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Natalie R. Hinkel, Julia Venturini","title":"Bayesian analysis of interiors of HD 219134b, Kepler-10b, Kepler-93b, CoRoT-7b, 55 Cnc e, and HD 97658b using stellar abundance proxies","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.03909","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":41,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-129 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-129 b is one of two transiting sub-Neptunes, with a radius of 2.40 Earth radii, and its mass is only limited to under 20 Earth masses (Zhang et al. 2021). Six years of Keck velocity data revealed a massive outer companion, Kepler-129 d, of at least 8.3 Jupiter masses on a 7.2-year orbit (Zhang et al. 2021). Asteroseismology, which reads the star's internal oscillations, gives tentative evidence that the inner planets are tilted at least 38 degrees from the star's spin axis (Zhang et al. 2021).","system":"The system is a hierarchy: two tightly packed inner planets and one massive outer planet (Zhang et al. 2021).","history":"The outer giant could have tilted the inner orbits if its own orbit is inclined by at least 19 degrees to them (Zhang et al. 2021).","open":"The mutual tilt between the outer giant and the inner planets is not well known (Zhang et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler129b-Zhangetal2021","cite":"Zhang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jingwen Zhang, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"Long-period Jovian Tilts the Orbits of Two sub-Neptunes Relative to Stellar Spin Axis in Kepler-129","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03446","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 876 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known from these studies concerns its neighbour GJ 876 d. Eight years of Keck velocity monitoring of the nearby 4.69-parsec red dwarf revealed a third planet, d, with a period of 1.94 days (Rivera et al. 2005). Its mass is about 7.5 Earth masses if the system is tilted near 50 degrees, which at the time made it by far the lowest-mass companion found around a main-sequence star other than the Sun (Rivera et al. 2005). The star spins once every 96.7 days (Rivera et al. 2005).","system":"The star already had two Jupiter-mass companions, b and c, before planet d was found (Rivera et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj876e-Riveraetal2005","cite":"Rivera et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, Jack J. Lissauer, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"A ~7.5 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet Orbiting the Nearby Star, GJ 876","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510508","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4602.01":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits in the radius valley, the dip in planet sizes between super-Earths and sub-Neptunes, and is a key target for future atmospheric study (Di Maio et al. 2026).","observed":"The HARPS-N spectrograph at the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo watched the star for the signal of the planet's pull as part of the ArMS programme (Di Maio et al. 2026). The result is the first mass measurement of a sub-Neptune using data collected entirely within that programme (Di Maio et al. 2026). The planet has a radius of 2.5 Earth radii (Di Maio et al. 2026). Its mass and radius are meant to guide atmospheric follow-up with the James Webb Space Telescope and the future Ariel satellite (Di Maio et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi460201-DiMaioetal2026","cite":"Di Maio et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"C. Di Maio, S. Benatti, D. Locci et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG: LXXIII. Confirmation of the hot sub-Neptune TOI-4602 b (HD 25295 b), a key target for future atmospheric characterization","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 e":{"text":{"why":"The system is a test of whether resonant chains, in which planets tug on each other at fixed orbit ratios, can survive for long (Dai et al. 2023).","observed":"The star is only about 700 million years old and hosts at least six transiting planets of 2 to 5 Earth radii (Dai et al. 2023). Their period ratios sit within 0.01% of exact resonance, much closer than the 1% typical of Kepler near-resonant systems (Dai et al. 2023). Timing changes in the transits gave masses of 3 to 8 Earth masses (Dai et al. 2023). Spin-orbit measurements on planet d show the star's rotation is aligned with the orbits (Dai et al. 2023).","system":"Period ratios near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2 make it the first known resonant chain that includes a delicate 7:5 link between two other resonances (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"No binary companion has been detected, and the aligned orbits suggest the chain formed in an isolated, quiet disc (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136e-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-21 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is metal-poor and likely belongs to the Milky Way's thick disc, an older stellar population (Bouchy et al. 2010).","observed":"WASP-21 b is a hot Saturn with a radius of 1.07 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.30 Jupiter masses, giving a low density of 0.24 that of Jupiter (Bouchy et al. 2010). The host star has a metal content of [Fe/H] of -0.46, and the planet supports a link between planet density and host metallicity among five known Saturn-like transiting planets (Bouchy et al. 2010). Three transits seen with the William Herschel Telescope gave an optical transmission spectrum (Alderson et al. 2020).","system":"The planet is the first transiting planet likely to belong to the thick disc (Bouchy et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere is clear or cloudy was being tested, since WASP-39 b, which has similar temperature and gravity, is cloud-free (Alderson et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp21b-Bouchyetal2010","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"F. Bouchy, L. Hebb, I. Skillen et al.","title":"WASP-21b: a hot-Saturn exoplanet transiting a thick disc star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2605","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp21b-Aldersonetal2020","cite":"Alderson et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. Alderson, J. Kirk, M. Lopez-Morales et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-21b light curves (Alderson+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-210 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-210 is an active star with two short-period, Neptune-sized planets (Ioannidis et al. 2014). Properties of the host star derived from each planet's transits disagree with one another and with expected values (Ioannidis et al. 2014). Both planets show transit timing variations, shifts in when transits occur (Ioannidis et al. 2014). A simple explanation is that the inner planets have substantial eccentricities and that another, longer-period planet pulls on them (Ioannidis et al. 2014).","system":"N-body simulations modelled the timing shifts and checked that the system is stable (Ioannidis et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"The suspected third planet has not been confirmed (Ioannidis et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler210b-Ioannidisetal2014","cite":"Ioannidis et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"P. Ioannidis, J. H. M. M. Schmitt, Ch. Avdellidou et al.","title":"Kepler-210: An active star with at least two planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.3238","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 104067 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Combined velocity data from HIRES and HARPS revealed a planet of about Uranus mass in an eccentric 14-day orbit (Kane et al. 2024). Injection-recovery tests, which hide fake signals in the data to see what would be seen, exclude Saturn-mass and Jupiter-mass planets out to 3 and 8 AU (Kane et al. 2024). TESS also shows a possible terrestrial planet of 1.30 Earth radii with a 2.2-day period (Kane et al. 2024).","system":"A giant planet circles the K dwarf in an eccentric orbit of about 55 days (Kane et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The innermost candidate is not yet confirmed as a planet (Kane et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd104067c-Kaneetal2024","cite":"Kane et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Tara Fetherolf, Zhexing Li et al.","title":"A Perfect Tidal Storm: HD 104067 Planetary Architecture Creating an Incandescent World","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.17062","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-553 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to measure masses of giant planets on long orbits, at the edge of what transit searches can reach (Dalba et al. 2024).","observed":"Long-term Doppler spectroscopy of 11 stars, with observation baselines from a few years to a decade, gave a new precise mass for this planet (Dalba et al. 2024). Velocity data were modelled jointly with transit photometry (Dalba et al. 2024). Results for other systems in the same survey show that several confirmed planets are in fact not planetary at all (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler553c-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2022-BLG-091L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was reported together with KMT-2024-BLG-1209Lb, whose event shows very similar anomalies (Han et al. 2025).","observed":"The microlensing light curve has an anomaly near its peak, where the lensing is moderately high (Han et al. 2025). The signal is a weak caustic crossing, with minimal distortion while the background star stays inside the caustic (Han et al. 2025). Binary-lens modelling showed the anomaly comes from a planet (Han et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The exact interpretation is complicated by a newly identified degeneracy tied to the uncertain angle at which the source crosses the planet-host axis (Han et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2022blg091lb-Hanetal2025","cite":"Han et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"MOA-2022-BLG-091Lb and KMT-2024-BLG-1209Lb: Microlensing planets detected through weak caustic-crossing signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22951","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-3680 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity follow-up with the SOPHIE spectrograph at Observatoire de Haute-Provence, gathered over 2 years, showed that a promising Kepler transit candidate is a real planet (Hébrard et al. 2019). The orbit is highly eccentric, with e = 0.50 ± 0.03, around an early G dwarf star (Hébrard et al. 2019). As of 2019, it was the transiting giant planet with the longest period characterized around a single star (Hébrard et al. 2019). Only a few giants with long periods are well characterized, so it lets astronomers extend studies that mostly covered planets close to their stars (Hébrard et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi3680b-Hbrardetal2019","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"G. Hébrard, A. S. Bonomo, R. F. Díaz et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. XIX. The transiting temperate giant planet KOI-3680b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09580","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-969 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity data from the HARPS, PFS and CORALIE spectrographs, covering more than two years, were combined with TESS and ground-based light curves to confirm and measure this planet (Lillo-Box et al. 2023). Its mass is 9.1 Earth masses and its radius 2.765 Earth radii, and its equilibrium temperature is 941 ± 31 K (Lillo-Box et al. 2023). That places it on the lower boundary of the hot-Neptune desert, a range of very close-in orbits where Neptune-size planets are scarce (Lillo-Box et al. 2023). Modelling of its interior shows it is rich in volatiles (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","system":"The velocity data also favour a second, massive body further out, described as an eccentric cold Jupiter (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","history":"Its volatile-rich makeup suggests it moved inward after forming (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi969b-LilloBoxetal2023","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, D. Gandolfi, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-969: a late-K dwarf with a hot mini-Neptune in the desert and an eccentric cold Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08996","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-64 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with nine others from the HATSouth survey (Hartman et al. 2019). Its host star, at 1.56 solar masses, is the most massive of the ten hosts (Hartman et al. 2019). Distances and brightness measurements from Gaia suggest that the star may have an unresolved companion star too close to it to be seen separately (Hartman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the star really has a binary companion is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats64b-Hartmanetal2019","cite":"Hartman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-60b-HATS-69b: 10 Transiting Planets from HATSouth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1695 b":{"text":{"why":"A planet of this size around a small red star helps show whether the gap in planet sizes is made by atmospheric loss or by how planets form (Cherubim et al. 2023).","observed":"Two teams measured the planet's mass independently. Year-long monitoring with the SPIRou infrared spectrograph gave 5.5 ± 1.0 Earth masses and a radius of 2.03 ± 0.18 Earth radii (Kiefer et al. 2023). Forty-nine measurements with the HARPS-N spectrograph gave 6.36 ± 1.00 Earth masses (Cherubim et al. 2023). Its density is too low for a purely rocky planet of iron and magnesium silicate, so it is likely rich in water (Cherubim et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 590 ± 90 K (Kiefer et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Its size and orbit sit between the predictions of models where heat drives atmospheric loss and models where planets form with little gas (Cherubim et al. 2023).","open":"Whether the planet is water-rich or has a hydrogen-helium envelope is not settled; one analysis allows about 0.28 per cent hydrogen and helium or 23 ± 12 per cent water (Kiefer et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1695b-Cherubimetal2023","cite":"Cherubim et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Collin Cherubim, Ryan Cloutier, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"TOI-1695 b: A Water World Orbiting an Early-M Dwarf in the Planet Radius Valley","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06445","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1695b-Kieferetal2023","cite":"Kiefer et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Kiefer, G. Hébrard, E. Martioli et al.","title":"A sub-Neptune planet around TOI-1695 discovered and characterized with SPIRou and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06205","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2005-BLG-169L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's host star gives off no light that has been separated from the background. Work on this planet showed how the Hubble Space Telescope could detect the host star, unless it is a stellar remnant (Bennett et al. 2007). Seeing the star would measure how fast the star moves relative to the background source star, which turns the microlensing signal into real masses and a real separation in physical units (Bennett et al. 2007). Observations in several colours would give a cross-check (Bennett et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":"Until the host star is seen, the planet's mass and the star's mass remain estimates from the light curve."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2005blg169lb-Bennettetal2007","cite":"Bennett et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"David P. Bennett, Jay Anderson, B. Scott Gaudi","title":"Characterization of Gravitational Microlensing Planetary Host Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0611448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1303L b":{"text":{"why":"The planetary signal was one of four found by searching high-cadence microlensing data for short-lived events (Han et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet's signal lasted only from a few hours to a couple of days, yet the combined data from the lensing surveys detected it clearly (Han et al. 2022). Its mass is about 0.38 times that of Jupiter, so it is a sub-Jovian planet (Han et al. 2022). The host star is a low-mass star in the range of roughly 0.3 to 0.6 solar masses (Han et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1303lb-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Doeon Kim, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Four sub-Jovian-mass planets detected by high-cadence microlensing surveys","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10510","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TIC 393818343 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Only one transit of this planet was seen at first, with a duration of 3.957 hours and a depth of 0.1196 per cent, so its orbital period could not be worked out (Conzo & Moriconi 2024). The host star was later shown to belong to a visual binary of two solar-type stars (Conzo et al. 2025).","system":"The star has a companion, TOI-6883B, at a projected separation of about 616 au (Conzo et al. 2025). The pair is physically bound and would take roughly 15,000 years to orbit each other (Conzo et al. 2025). The planet's orbit is long-term stable, although Kozai-Lidov perturbations, slow tilting and stretching of an orbit by a distant star, remain possible (Conzo et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Further observations are needed to pin down the binary orbit and to judge how the companion star affects the planet (Conzo et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tic393818343b-ConzoMoriconi2024","cite":"Conzo & Moriconi 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Conzo, M. Moriconi","title":"TOI-6883.01: A Single-transit Planet Candidate Detected from TESS","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ad2c85","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tic393818343b-Conzoetal2025","cite":"Conzo et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"G. Conzo, M. Moriconi","title":"TOI-6883.01: A Single-transit Planet Candidate Detected from TESS","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ad2c85","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2016-BLG-227L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's signal was well covered because the event lay near the field that many telescopes watched during the K2 Campaign 9 observations (Koshimoto et al. 2017). The planet is massive for its star, with a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 9 × 10^-3 (Koshimoto et al. 2017). Adaptive-optics images from the Keck telescope show extra light at the position of the source star (Koshimoto et al. 2017). That light could come from the host star, from a companion to the source or host, or from an unrelated star (Koshimoto et al. 2017). A statistical analysis suggests that little of it is likely to come from the host (Koshimoto et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host star's mass is uncertain because the source of the extra light is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2016blg227lb-Koshimotoetal2017","cite":"Koshimoto et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"N. Koshimoto, Y. Shvartzvald, D. P. Bennett et al.","title":"MOA-2016-BLG-227Lb: A Massive Planet Characterized by Combining Light-curve Analysis and Keck AO Imaging","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.01724","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-25 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its density is only about 0.32 times Jupiter's, which is low for a planet with a mass below Jupiter's (Enoch et al. 2010). Its radius is well explained by its temperature and its star's metal content, as with most low-mass planets (Enoch et al. 2010). The angle between the star's spin and the planet's orbit, as seen on the sky, is 14.6 ± 6.7 degrees, and a circular orbit fits the data (Brown et al. 2012). An optical transmission spectrum, which probes starlight filtered through the atmosphere, shows no strong features (McGruder et al. 2023). Models of that spectrum favour stellar activity, with spots 500 to 3000 K cooler than the rest of the star (McGruder et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"One study predicts that the planet has a high level of hazes or clouds, based on its host star's metal content, but this is tentative (McGruder et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp25b-Enochetal2010","cite":"Enoch et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"B. Enoch, A. Collier Cameron, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-25b: a 0.6 M<SUB>J</SUB> planet in the Southern hemisphere","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5917","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp25b-Brownetal2012","cite":"Brown et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. Collier Cameron, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Rossiter-McLaughlin effect measurements for WASP-16, WASP-25 and WASP-31","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp25b-McGruderetal2023","cite":"McGruder et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Chima D. McGruder, Mercedes López-Morales, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"The Similar Seven: A Set of Very Alike Exoplanets to Test Correlations between System Parameters and Atmospheric Properties","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.04174","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-561 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities and TESS data showed that this planet is real. It has a mass of 11.9 ± 1.3 Earth masses and a radius of 2.53 ± 0.13 Earth radii, which makes it a mini-Neptune (Lacedelli et al. 2021). An early reading of the TESS data suggested an outer transiting planet that velocities could not confirm. Two transits first thought to belong to that planet were shown to be single transits of two other planets (Lacedelli et al. 2021). Later CHEOPS and TESS observations showed that the 25.7-day planet transits (Piotto et al. 2024).","system":"The star hosts at least four transiting planets, with periods of 0.44, 10.8, 25.7 and 77.1 days, plus a fifth, non-transiting candidate at 433 days (Piotto et al. 2024). The innermost planet is an ultra-short-period super-Earth with a very low density (Lacedelli et al. 2021).","history":"The host is an old star of the galactic thick disc, about 10 billion years old and poor in iron (Brinkman et al. 2023). Such stars are less likely to host ultra-short-period super-Earths (Piotto et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi561d-Lacedellietal2021","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"G. Lacedelli, L. Malavolta, L. Borsato et al.","title":"An unusually low density ultra-short period super-Earth and three mini-Neptunes around the old star TOI-561","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.02332","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561d-Piottoetal2024","cite":"Piotto et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Piotto, T. Zingales, L. Borsato et al.","title":"Architecture of TOI-561 planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18169","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi561d-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Fei Dai et al.","title":"TOI-561 b: A Low-density Ultra-short-period \"Rocky\" Planet around a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06665","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-62 f":{"text":{"why":"Planets of 1.4 to 1.6 Earth radii in the habitable zone were among the smallest then known to lie there (Borucki et al. 2013).","observed":"It receives 0.41 ± 0.05 times the sunlight that Earth gets, which puts it in the habitable zone of its orange star (Borucki et al. 2013). Models for a star age of about 7 billion years suggest it could be solid, either rocky or mostly solid water (Borucki et al. 2013). Only four transits were seen during Kepler's observations, and the first three were found by an independent search after the pipeline missed it (Borucki et al. 2018). A later automatic catalogue labelled it a false positive, which its discoverers argued was a misclassification (Borucki et al. 2018).","system":"It is the outermost of five planets, with the others orbiting at periods of 5.7, 12.4, 18.2 and 122.4 days (Borucki et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Climate modelling suggests the surface is warm enough for liquid water only under certain conditions. With 3 bar of carbon dioxide, it would need a high tilt of 60 to 90 degrees and an orbit at the upper end of the stable eccentricities, up to 0.32 (Shields et al. 2016). Long-period giant planets, if present, could change the tilt by up to 60 degrees (Quarles et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler62f-Boruckietal2013","cite":"Borucki et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"William J. Borucki, Eric Agol, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Kepler-62: A Five-Planet System with Planets of 1.4 and 1.6 Earth Radii in the Habitable Zone","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.7387","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler62f-Boruckietal2018","cite":"Borucki et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"William Borucki, Susan E. Thompson, Eric Agol et al.","title":"Kepler-62f: Kepler's first small planet in the habitable zone, but is it real?","journal":"New Astronomy Reviews","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newar.2019.03.002","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler62f-Shieldsetal2016","cite":"Shields et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Aomawa L. Shields, Rory Barnes, Eric Agol et al.","title":"The Effect of Orbital Configuration on the Possible Climates and Habitability of Kepler-62f","journal":"Astrobiology","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.01272","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler62f-Quarlesetal2018","cite":"Quarles et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Billy Quarles, Jason W. Barnes, Jack Lissauer et al.","title":"Obliquity Variations of a Potentially Habitable Kepler-62f","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #50","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 216536 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a K0 giant star, a star that has swollen as it aged, at 0.609 au in 148.6 days (Niedzielski et al. 2015). The orbit is eccentric, with e = 0.38 (Niedzielski et al. 2015). Its minimum mass is 1.47 times that of Jupiter (Niedzielski et al. 2015). The star has a mass of 1.36 solar masses (Niedzielski et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet is very close to the engulfment zone, the distance within which the swelling star could swallow it, and is prone to ingestion in the near future (Niedzielski et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd216536b-Niedzielskietal2015","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan, G. Nowak et al.","title":"Three Red Giants With Substellar-Mass Companions","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07076","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2018-BLG-0506L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was picked out by an automated search of 2018 to 2019 light curves from six KMTNet fields (Hwang et al. 2022). Its signal is a subtle dip that appears as the background source star crosses the line joining the host and planet on the opposite side from the planet (Hwang et al. 2022). Earlier searches by eye missed such dips (Hwang et al. 2022). The planet is one of six new planets with a planet-to-host mass ratio below 2 × 10^-4 (Hwang et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2018blg0506lb-Hwangetal2022","cite":"Hwang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Kyu-Ha Hwang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. II. Six New q < 2 × 10<SUP>-4</SUP> Mass-ratio Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06686","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-10 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the hot Jupiter with the shortest orbital period known, 0.7668944 days (McCormac et al. 2020). Its orbit is just 1.46 ± 0.18 Roche radii from its host star, a K5V star about 10.4 billion years old that shows moderate signs of activity (McCormac et al. 2020). With a stellar tidal quality factor of 2 × 10^7, the median remaining inspiral time is 38 million years and the orbital period could shrink by 7 seconds over a decade (McCormac et al. 2020). Another study of its decay finds that the timescale depends on how tidal energy is lost inside the star (Alvarado-Montes et al. 2021). Its star rotates slowly, which has been used to set limits on how strongly the star and planet exert tides on each other (Tokuno et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"How fast the orbit is shrinking is not known, because it depends on how efficiently the star dissipates tidal energy (Alvarado-Montes et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts10b-McCormacetal2020","cite":"McCormac et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"James McCormac, Edward Gillen, James A. G. Jackman et al.","title":"NGTS-10b: the shortest period hot Jupiter yet discovered","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.12424","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts10b-AlvaradoMontesetal2021","cite":"Alvarado-Montes et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jaime A. Alvarado-Montes, Mario Sucerquia, Carolina García-Carmona et al.","title":"The impact of tidal friction evolution on the orbital decay of ultra-short-period planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.05967","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts10b-Tokunoetal2024","cite":"Tokuno et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Takato Tokuno, Akihiko Fukui, Takeru K. Suzuki","title":"A Novel Method to Constrain Tidal Quality Factor from A Nonsynchronized Exoplanetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07375","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of the outer five planets in the Kepler-80 system that sit in a chain of three-body resonances, where each set of three neighbouring planets has orbital periods that repeat in a linked pattern (Weisserman et al. 2023). The planets do not appear to be in resonance in pairs (Weisserman et al. 2023). The system's mass estimates come from transit timing variations, small shifts in when the transits occur (Weisserman et al. 2023). A model of atmospheric escape powered by heat from the planet's core predicts that Kepler-80 e could be losing its atmosphere today, at a rate of at least 10^7 grams per second (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","system":"The system packs about four planets into periods within a factor of about 3, and radial velocity surveys had not detected any such tightly packed system (Ragozzine et al. 2015). A sixth planet, Kepler-80 g, has an estimated mass of about 1 Earth mass (Weisserman et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Adding a newly found planet to the fit changes the masses derived from the timing shifts, so conclusions from incomplete systems need caution (Weisserman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80e-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80e-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80e-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-289 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system comes from transit timing variations, the small shifts in when transits occur, in a three-planet system (Schmitt et al. 2014). The middle planet's timing shifts reach about 5 hours, which is why earlier searches missed or mismeasured it (Schmitt et al. 2014). A strong shorter-period wobble in the timing, called chopping, breaks the usual uncertainty between mass and eccentricity (Schmitt et al. 2014). The masses of the inner, middle and outer planets come out as 7.3 ± 6.8, 4.0 ± 0.9 and 132 ± 17 Earth masses (Schmitt et al. 2014).","system":"The planets are not in a 1:2:4 Laplace resonance, a locked chain where the three orbital periods keep a fixed ratio (Schmitt et al. 2014). The outermost planet is a giant with deep transits of about 1.3 per cent, which pins down the host star's properties very tightly (Schmitt et al. 2014). The host star is a young, Sun-like star, about 1 billion years old (Schmitt et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler289b-Schmittetal2014","cite":"Schmitt et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Joseph R. Schmitt, Eric Agol, Katherine M. Deck et al.","title":"Planet Hunters. VII. Discovery of a New Low-mass, Low-density Planet (PH3 C) Orbiting Kepler-289 with Mass Measurements of Two Additional Planets (PH3 B and D)","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.8114","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-5 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is very puffy: its radius and mass give a mean density of only 0.217 g/cm3, far below water (Rauer et al. 2009). Its transits were picked up quickly in the satellite's alarm mode, which flags deep dips while monitoring continues. They came from the first long-duration field pointed toward the galactic anti-centre (Rauer et al. 2009). Ground telescopes later timed transits of CoRoT-5 b and five other CoRoT planets, 5 to 9 years after discovery, to refine the orbital timing (Raetz et al. 2019).","system":"The planet crosses dark spots on its star during transits, and each crossing leaves a small bump in the light curve. Tracking such bumps gives the star's spot properties and spin (Valio 2017). For CoRoT-5 b, tides appear to dominate the interaction between star and planet (Silva & Valio 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot5b-Raueretal2009","cite":"Rauer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"H. Rauer, D. Queloz, Sz. Csizmadia et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. VII. The ``hot-Jupiter''-type planet CoRoT-5b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.3397","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot5b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot5b-Valio2017","cite":"Valio 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Adriana Valio","title":"Starspots properties and stellar activity from planetary transits","journal":"Living Around Active Stars","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921317004094","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot5b-SilvaValio2017","cite":"Silva & Valio 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Douglas Silva, Adriana Valio","title":"Interaction of extra solar planets with their host star","journal":"Living Around Active Stars","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921317004501","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KIC 5437945 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind it aimed to fill a gap, since the census of planets beyond 1 AU is incomplete (Wang et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet is one of 41 long-period candidates in 38 systems spotted by volunteers of the Planet Hunters project in archival Kepler data (Wang et al. 2015). Of the candidates, 17 show a single transit, 14 show two and 10 show more (Wang et al. 2015). Follow-up adaptive optics images were taken for 33 of the host stars to look for blended background or companion stars, and six stars have companions within 4 arcseconds (Wang et al. 2015). Most of the candidates have orbits corresponding to 1 to 3 AU from their stars (Wang et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kic5437945b-Wangetal2015","cite":"Wang et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Ji Wang, Debra A. Fischer, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Planet Hunters. VIII. Characterization of 41 Long-period Exoplanet Candidates from Kepler Archival Data","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.02559","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-849 b":{"text":{"why":"The Doppler survey behind the mass measurement targets giant planets on orbits longer than 100 days, the outer edge of what transit searches can handle (Dalba et al. 2024).","observed":"Years of Doppler spectroscopy, with baselines from a few years to a decade, were modelled together with transit photometry to weigh the planet (Dalba et al. 2024). The result is a new precise mass for Kepler-849 b, one of four long-period giants measured that way in the study (Dalba et al. 2024). Long orbits and the geometry of transits make such planets hard to follow up (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler849b-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1438 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star hosts two transiting sub-Neptunes plus a tentative, long-period Jupiter-like planet, which makes the system rare (Persson et al. 2025). Masses came from 85 HARPS-N and 20 HIRES velocity measurements taken over about five years (Persson et al. 2025). Planet c has a density of 2.9 g/cm3, which points to a volatile-rich interior (Persson et al. 2025). Interior models allow no more than 0.2% of its mass as hydrogen and helium, much less than planet b, which could hold up to 2.5% (Persson et al. 2025).","system":"The inner planet b has a radius of 3.04 Earth radii, a mass of 9.4 Earth masses and a 5.1-day orbit (Persson et al. 2025). It receives about 145 times Earth's sunlight, against 65 times for planet c (Persson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether the outer Jupiter-like planet is real is still tentative (Persson et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1438c-Perssonetal2025","cite":"Persson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Carina M. Persson, Emil Knudstrup, Ilaria Carleo et al.","title":"TOI-1438: A rare system with two short-period sub-Neptunes and a tentative long-period Jupiter-like planet orbiting a K0V star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 273 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found in a long HARPS monitoring programme of small M dwarfs, aimed at low-mass planets and long periods (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017).","observed":"No transits have been seen, so only a minimum mass is known (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Simulations of the first 100 million years explored how the system formed and how its planets could have gained water (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Further modelling covered the stability of the orbits and the effects of tides, namely spin-orbit alignment, pseudo-synchronisation and tidal heating (Pozuelos et al. 2019).","system":"The star lies only about 3.75 parsecs away and has two confirmed planets and two further candidates (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Planet b sits in the habitable zone (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Orbits that could hold asteroid-belt-like bodies were also mapped (Pozuelos et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The true masses are not known, because the tilt of the orbit is not measured (Pozuelos et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj273b-AstudilloDefruetal2017","cite":"Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"N. Astudillo-Defru, T. Forveille, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLI. A dozen planets around the M dwarfs GJ 3138, GJ 3323, GJ 273, GJ 628, and GJ 3293","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05386","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj273b-Pozuelosetal2020","cite":"Pozuelos et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Francisco J. Pozuelos, Juan C. Suárez, Gonzalo C. de Elía et al.","title":"GJ 273: on the formation, dynamical evolution, and habitability of a planetary system hosted by an M dwarf at 3.75 parsec","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.09403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj273b-Pozuelosetal2019","cite":"Pozuelos et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"F. J. Pozuelos, J. C. Suárez, G. C. de Elía et al.","title":"GJ 273: Formation, dynamical evolution and habitability of one of the most interesting planetary systems","journal":"Highlights on Spanish Astrophysics X","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-12 b":{"text":{"why":"The companions offer clues to how warm Jupiters, giant planets on orbits of roughly 10 to 100 days, form (Masuda 2017).","observed":"Changes in the timing of its transits reveal an unseen outer companion, Kepler-448 c. Modelling gives it a mass of about 22 Jupiter masses, a highly eccentric orbit (eccentricity 0.65) and a closest approach of 1.5 au (Masuda 2017). The transiting planet also has a moderately eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.34 (Masuda 2017). Its radius is 1.23 Jupiter radii (Masuda 2017).","system":"Kepler-448 c, the companion, is massive enough to sit near the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Masuda 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi12b-Masuda2017","cite":"Masuda 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Eccentric Companions to Kepler-448b and Kepler-693b: Clues to the Formation of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-9 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-9 d was not flagged by the Kepler pipeline. It turned up during analysis of the system's two bigger planets (Ragozzine & Holman 2018). Its signal is shallow, so a statistical test called BLENDER ruled out a large share of false alarms, but not all of them (Torres et al. 2011). No mass has been measured from velocities. A reanalysis found a radius of about 2 Earth radii, larger than first thought, which suggests a low-mass, low-density planet (Dreizler & Ofir 2014). Earlier work gave 1.5 to 1.64 Earth radii (Stevick et al. 2013).","system":"Kepler-9 was the first system with several confirmed transiting planets and the first to show clear transit timing variations (Ragozzine & Holman 2018). Two Saturn-size planets near a 2:1 orbital resonance tug on each other (Holman et al. 2010). Later work put their masses at 45.1 and 31.0 Earth masses, with low densities (Dreizler & Ofir 2014).","history":"Models of the planet's makeup with silicate and carbon-rich interiors were used to limit its mass and composition, in light of formation theory (Gong & Zhou 2012).","open":"The mass and composition are not known (Gong & Zhou 2012). The radius is still debated (Dreizler & Ofir 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-RagozzineHolman2018","cite":"Ragozzine & Holman 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Matthew J. Holman","title":"Kepler-9: The first multi-transiting system and the first transit timing variations","journal":"New Astronomy Reviews","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newar.2019.03.003","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-Torresetal2011","cite":"Torres et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Guillermo Torres, François Fressin, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"Modeling Kepler Transit Light Curves as False Positives: Rejection of Blend Scenarios for Kepler-9, and Validation of Kepler-9 d, A Super-earth-size Planet in a Multiple System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.4393","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-DreizlerOfir2014","cite":"Dreizler & Ofir 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Stefan Dreizler, Aviv Ofir","title":"Kepler-9 revisited 60% the mass with six times more data","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.1372","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-Stevicketal2013","cite":"Stevick et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Justin Stevick, W. F. Welsh, J. A. Orosz et al.","title":"The Radius of the Super-Earth Kepler-9d","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #221","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-Holmanetal2010","cite":"Holman et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Matthew J. Holman, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Darin Ragozzine et al.","title":"Kepler-9: A System of Multiple Planets Transiting a Sun-Like Star, Confirmed by Timing Variations","journal":"Science","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1195778","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler9d-GongZhou2012","cite":"Gong & Zhou 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Yan-Xiang Gong, Ji-Lin Zhou","title":"The silicate and carbon-rich models of CoRoT-7b, Kepler-9d and Kepler-10b","journal":"Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.6554","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 190007 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three new candidates in a survey of small planets using the Automated Planet Finder and Keck HIRES (Burt et al. 2021). Its star is a slightly metal-rich, active K4 dwarf (Burt et al. 2021). The velocity wobble has a semi-amplitude of 5.64 m/s, which gives a minimum mass of about 16.5 Earth masses (Burt et al. 2021). The study also tested how well such surveys can recover small planets by injecting fake signals into the data (Burt et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd190007b-Burtetal2021","cite":"Burt et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jennifer Burt, Fabo Feng, Bradford Holden et al.","title":"A Collage of Small Planets from the Lick-Carnegie Exoplanet Survey: Exploring the Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Mass Regime","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08867","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-292 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was once thought to be one of the densest sub-Neptunes known. New Doppler data from HARPS, HARPS-N and HIRES show it is nearly one-third as massive: 8.8 Earth masses (with an upper limit of 15.4 at 98% confidence), against 24.5 reported earlier (Akana Murphy et al. 2025). The first measurement was probably inflated by a second signal in the data, possibly the star's rotation, which added to the planet's signal (Akana Murphy et al. 2025). The host is a chromospherically inactive G dwarf (Akana Murphy et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The cause of the original wrong mass is not certain (Akana Murphy et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2292b-Murphyetal2025","cite":"Murphy et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Rafael Luque, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"HD 119130 b Is Not an \"Ultradense\" Sub-Neptune","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.02518","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 42618 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found in a search for Neptune-mass planets around nearby Sun-like stars (Fulton et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 15.4 Earth masses, an orbit of 0.55 au and an equilibrium temperature of 337 K (Fulton et al. 2016). It is the first planet found around its star, a solar analogue (Fulton et al. 2016). A transit search with CoRoT space data and ground-based telescopes found no transits (Fulton et al. 2016).","system":"The star is the closest to the Sun, in its basic properties, ever observed by the CoRoT satellite, and its vibrations were detected (Barban et al. 2013). It is not strictly a solar twin: it is slightly metal-poor and perhaps younger than the Sun (Morel et al. 2013). Later modelling instead found evidence it is older than the Sun but rich in lithium (Castro et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd42618b-Fultonetal2016","cite":"Fulton et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Benjamin J. Fulton, Andrew W. Howard, Lauren M. Weiss et al.","title":"Three Temperate Neptunes Orbiting Nearby Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00007","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd42618b-Barbanetal2013","cite":"Barban et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"C. Barban, S. Deheuvels, M. J. Goupil et al.","title":"Solar-like oscillations in distant stars as seen by CoRoT : the special case of HD 42618, a solar sister","journal":"Journal of Physics Conference Series","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1742-6596/440/1/012030","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd42618b-Moreletal2013","cite":"Morel et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"T. Morel, M. Rainer, E. Poretti et al.","title":"Abundance study of the two solar-analogue CoRoT targets HD 42618 and HD 43587 from HARPS spectroscopy","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.3172","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd42618b-Castroetal2021","cite":"Castro et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"M. Castro, F. Baudin, O. Benomar et al.","title":"Modeling of two CoRoT solar analogues constrained by seismic and spectroscopic analysis","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.01163","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5788 c":{"text":{"why":"The star is metal-poor, and few systems with planets on both sides of the radius gap are known around such stars (Lakeland et al. 2026).","observed":"Six TESS sectors and a dedicated CHEOPS programme revealed two transiting planets, and 125 HARPS-N velocity spectra gave their masses (Lakeland et al. 2026). Planet c has a radius of 2.272 Earth radii and a mass of 6.4 Earth masses (Lakeland et al. 2026). The inner planet b is smaller, at 1.528 Earth radii and 3.72 Earth masses (Lakeland et al. 2026). The pair straddle the radius gap, a dearth of planets at intermediate sizes (Lakeland et al. 2026).","system":"Orbit calculations show a third planet could stay stable between 8 and 14 days, but no evidence of one has been found (Lakeland et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5788c-Lakelandetal2026","cite":"Lakeland et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ben S. Lakeland, A. Mortier, R. D. Haywood et al.","title":"Discovery and characterization of two exoplanets orbiting the metal-poor, solar-type star TOI-5788 with TESS, CHEOPS, and HARPS-N","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14045","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-440 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most work so far has been about the star and about modelling, not direct observation of the planet. The host is one of 13 stars studied for rotation, flares, mass loss and activity in a sample of Kepler's most Earth-like planet hosts (Armstrong et al. 2016). Simulations of tidal spin evolution expect Kepler-440 b to be tidally trapped, with one side kept facing its star (Camposeo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler440b-Armstrongetal2016","cite":"Armstrong et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Armstrong, C. E. Pugh, A.-M. Broomhall et al.","title":"The host stars of Kepler's habitable exoplanets: superflares, rotation and activity","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05306","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler440b-Camposeoetal2026","cite":"Camposeo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Salvatore Camposeo, Francesco De Paolis, Vincenzo Orofino et al.","title":"Simulation of the Spin Evolution of Some Selected Exoplanets and Inferences on Their Climate","journal":"Universe","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11780","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-25 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star belongs to the Hyades cluster, so its age is known: 727 million years (Rockcliffe et al. 2021).","observed":"Membership of the Hyades lets the star's distance, radius and mass be fixed to 5-10% (Mann et al. 2016). Adaptive optics images and spectra ruled out eclipsing binaries as the source of the transits (Mann et al. 2016). Two Hubble transits were watched in Lyman-alpha light for escaping hydrogen, and none was seen (Rockcliffe et al. 2021). The star's radiation could still strip about 0.56 Earth masses per billion years, five times the rate estimated for GJ 436 b (Rockcliffe et al. 2021). Its light curve shows a spot-driven signal at 1.88 days (Mann et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":"Simulations suggest future giant telescopes could detect molecules in the atmosphere of K2-25 b (Dubey et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k225b-Rockcliffeetal2021","cite":"Rockcliffe et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Keighley E. Rockcliffe, Elisabeth R. Newton, Allison Youngblood et al.","title":"A Lyα Transit Left Undetected: the Environment and Atmospheric Behavior of K2-25b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04376","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k225b-Mannetal2016","cite":"Mann et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Andrew W. Mann, Eric Gaidos, Gregory N. Mace et al.","title":"Zodiacal Exoplanets in Time (ZEIT). I. A Neptune-sized Planet Orbiting an M4.5 Dwarf in the Hyades Star Cluster","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.00483","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k225b-Dubeyetal2025","cite":"Dubey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Dwaipayan Dubey, Liton Majumdar, Charles Beichman et al.","title":"Quantified Estimation of Molecular Detections across Different Classes of Neptunian Atmospheres Using Cross-correlation Spectroscopy: Prospects for Future Extremely Large Telescopes with High-resolution Spectrographs","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01142","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 212301 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known beyond the planet's orbit concerns a stellar companion. Imaging found a close red dwarf, HD 212301 B, of 0.35 solar masses, about 4.4 arcseconds from the host star, which is about 230 AU in projected separation (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009). The star therefore has a companion star, as do roughly 17% of known exoplanet hosts (Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009). The star's calcium emission, a sign of chromospheric activity, was also measured among hot-Jupiter hosts (Krejcova & Budaj 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd212301b-MugrauerNeuhuser2009","cite":"Mugrauer & Neuhäuser 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser","title":"The multiplicity of exoplanet host stars. New low-mass stellar companions of the exoplanet host stars HD 125612 and HD 212301","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.2561","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd212301b-KrejcovaBudaj2012","cite":"Krejcova & Budaj 2012","year":"2012","authors":"T. Krejcova, J. Budaj","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CaIIHK emission in stars with close-in planets (Krejcova+, 2012)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35400082","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Ross 128 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ross 128 is a quiet M4 dwarf only 3.4 parsecs away (Bonfils et al. 2018). The planet gets less than 1.5 times Earth's sunlight, with an equilibrium temperature between 269 K for an Earth-like reflectivity and 213 K for a Venus-like one (Bonfils et al. 2018). The planet is placed close to the inner edge of the habitable zone (Bonfils et al. 2018). Interior models suggest it may hold an extended liquid water reservoir (Boldog et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether flares from the star threaten the surface is debated. Surface UV light should stay below early-Earth levels even with an eroded atmosphere (O'Malley-James & Kaltenegger 2019). In the worst flares, the radiation dose at the lowest atmospheric depth could reach fatal levels (Yamashiki et al. 2019). Its atmosphere, if any, is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ross128b-Bonfilsetal2018","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"X. Bonfils, N. Astudillo-Defru, R. Díaz et al.","title":"A temperate exo-Earth around a quiet M dwarf at 3.4 parsec","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.06177","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ross128b-Boldogetal2024","cite":"Boldog et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ádám Boldog, Vera Dobos, László L. Kiss et al.","title":"Water content of rocky exoplanets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.01893","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ross128b-OMalleyJamesKaltenegger2019","cite":"O'Malley-James & Kaltenegger 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jack T. O'Malley-James, L. Kaltenegger","title":"Lessons from early Earth: UV surface radiation should not limit the habitability of active M star systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.03956","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ross128b-Yamashikietal2019","cite":"Yamashiki et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Yosuke A. Yamashiki, Hiroyuki Maehara, Vladimir Airapetian et al.","title":"Impact of Stellar Superflares on Planetary Habitability","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.06797","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1736 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a solar analogue, which makes the system a useful test of how planets form in Solar System-like settings (Martioli et al. 2023). Radial velocities from the SOPHIE spectrograph give the sub-Neptune a mass of 12.8 Earth masses and a radius of 2.44 Earth radii (Martioli et al. 2023).","system":"Long-term velocity changes reveal an outer companion, TOI-1736 c, with a minimum mass of 8.09 Jupiter masses (Martioli et al. 2023). Gaia astrometry puts its mass at about 8.7 Jupiter masses (Martioli et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1736b-Martiolietal2023","cite":"Martioli et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Martioli, G. Hébrard, L. de Almeida et al.","title":"TOI-1736 and TOI-2141: Two systems including sub-Neptunes around solar analogs revealed by TESS and SOPHIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.07011","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3090 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is among the most favourable mini-Neptunes for studying atmospheres, thanks to its bright host star (Almenara et al. 2022). Its low density of 1.89 g/cm3 implies volatiles (Almenara et al. 2022). Modelling of how the atmosphere has evolved is inconsistent with pure hydrogen and helium and favours a heavy mean molecular weight atmosphere (Almenara et al. 2022). Six transits observed with NIRPS and HARPS show the orbit is retrograde, with a 3D obliquity of 136 degrees, the first such planet around an M dwarf (Carteret et al. 2026).","system":"No massive outer planet or wide stellar companion has been found, which argues against migration driven by gravitational interactions with a third body (Carteret et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"How the planet came to be on a retrograde orbit is not known (Carteret et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3090b-Almenaraetal2022","cite":"Almenara et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. M. Almenara, X. Bonfils, J. F. Otegi et al.","title":"GJ 3090 b: one of the most favourable mini-Neptune for atmospheric characterisation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.14121","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3090b-Carteretetal2026","cite":"Carteret et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yann Carteret, Vincent Bourrier, Léna Parc et al.","title":"Upside down: GJ 3090 b the first retrograde exoplanet around an M dwarf detected with NIRPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24870","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-5 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's surface gravity of 22 m/s2 is large for its orbital period compared with most transiting planets (Burke et al. 2008). Independent observations confirmed it and found no significant stellar activity (Pál et al. 2009). Later transit photometry extended the timing baseline by nearly four years, fixing the orbital period to a precision of 50 milliseconds (Smith 2015). Mid-infrared excess suggests dust rings around the star at about 0.1 to 25 AU (Krivov et al. 2011).","system":"By coincidence, the star lies in front of a huge hydrogen gas plume from the interacting galaxies NGC 2444 and NGC 2445 (Burke et al. 2008).","history":"Debris dust may help constrain how hot Jupiters form and migrate (Krivov et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo5b-Burkeetal2008","cite":"Burke et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, P. R. McCullough, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-5b: A Transiting Jupiter-sized Planet with a 4 Day Period","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.2399","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo5b-Pletal2009","cite":"Pál et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. Pál, G. Á. Bakos, J. Fernandez et al.","title":"Independent Confirmation and Refined Parameters of the Hot Jupiter XO-5b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.0260","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo5b-Smith2015","cite":"Smith 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. M. S. Smith","title":"The Properties of XO-5b and WASP-82b Redetermined Using New High-Precision Transit Photometry and Global Data Analyses","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.0451","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo5b-Krivovetal2011","cite":"Krivov et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. V. Krivov, M. Reidemeister, S. Fiedler et al.","title":"Warm dust in systems with transiting planets","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2011","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2016-BLG-0613L AB b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The brightness curve of this event looked like a typical one for a pair of stars acting as a gravitational lens, with two sharp spikes (Han et al. 2017). A jump in the trough between the spikes could not be explained by the stars alone. It was produced by a planet orbiting the pair (Han et al. 2017). Four families of solutions fit the data, and one was ruled out because it fit poorly (Han et al. 2017). In the other three the main star has a mass of about 0.7 Suns, and the planet is a super Jupiter, heavier than Jupiter (Han et al. 2017).","system":"The system lies in the Galactic disk, about halfway toward the Galactic bulge (Han et al. 2017). In one family of solutions the second star is a low-mass brown dwarf, with mass ratios of 1:0.03:0.003 (Han et al. 2017). In the other two, the two stars have similar masses (Han et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Whether the second star is a brown dwarf or an ordinary star is not known. Around 2024 the lens and the background star were expected to have drifted far enough apart to be seen separately, which would settle it (Han et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2016blg0613labb-Hanetal2017","cite":"Han et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Han, A. Udalski, A. Gould et al.","title":"OGLE-2016-BLG-0613LABb: A Microlensing Planet in a Binary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.00924","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1898 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Later transit timing from the TESS and CHEOPS space telescopes was combined with earlier data to re-measure when this planet crosses its star (Saha 2025). The orbital period is now known to better than 5 seconds (Saha 2025). The analysis used noise-reduction methods to cut the effect of stellar variability and instrument noise on the light curve (Saha 2025). Transits of long-period planets are rare, so small errors in the timing grow with time and can make future follow-up observations hard to schedule (Saha 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1898b-Saha2025","cite":"Saha 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Suman Saha","title":"Updating the ephemeris and physical properties of five long-period transiting exoplanets using TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01768","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2017-BLG-2509L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's signal came from a faint background star crossing a resonant caustic, a ring of sharp brightness peaks formed by a giant planet near the Einstein ring of its host star, the circle of light that a lens star bends around itself (Han et al. 2021). Two other planets in the same study share this trait (Han et al. 2021). The solution for this event is unique, with no competing explanation (Han et al. 2021).","system":"The three planets in the study orbit hosts of 0.45 to 0.59 Suns, from early M to late K dwarfs, so the stars are less massive than the Sun (Han et al. 2021). The planets have 2.1 to 6.2 times the mass of Jupiter and lie beyond the snow line, the distance where water freezes (Han et al. 2021).","history":"Planets like these support the idea that massive gas giants form easily beyond the snow line of their stars (Han et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2017blg2509lb-Hanetal2021","cite":"Han et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Doeon Kim et al.","title":"Three faint-source microlensing planets detected via the resonant-caustic channel","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07068","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-47 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet transits two stars that eclipse each other every 7.45 days: a Sun-like star and a companion about one-third its size (Orosz et al. 2012). The outer planet has an orbital period of 303.2 days and a radius 4.6 times Earth's (Orosz et al. 2012). It lies inside the classical habitable zone, where liquid water could exist on an Earth-like planet, but the planet is not Earth-like (Orosz et al. 2012). Its mass is between 2 and 5 Earth masses, and its density is below 0.26 g per cubic centimetre, so it is very light for its size (Orosz et al. 2019).","system":"Kepler-47 shows that close binary stars can host complete planetary systems (Orosz et al. 2012). A third planet orbits between the two first ones, with a period of 187.4 days and a radius of about 7 Earth radii (Orosz et al. 2019). All four orbits, the binary and the three planets, are within about 2 degrees of each other (Welsh et al. 2015). The two outer planets are tightly packed, so no other planet could stably orbit between them (Orosz et al. 2019).","history":"Low eccentricities and flat orbits argue against violent scattering and suggest gentle migration in the disc of gas and dust around the young stars (Orosz et al. 2019).","open":"Simulations show that Earth-mass Trojan planets, which share an orbit with a larger planet, could be stable in the habitable zone of systems like this one (Sudol & Haghighipour 2015). None has been detected in any system."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler47c-Oroszetal2012","cite":"Orosz et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Joshua A. Carter et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Transiting Circumbinary Multiplanet System","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5489","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47c-Oroszetal2019","cite":"Orosz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Nader Haghighipour et al.","title":"Discovery of a Third Transiting Planet in the Kepler-47 Circumbinary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07255","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47c-Welshetal2015","cite":"Welsh et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"William Welsh, Jerome Orosz, Billy Quarles et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Three-Planet Circumbinary System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47c-SudolHaghighipour2015","cite":"Sudol & Haghighipour 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Jeffrey Sudol, Nader Haghighipour","title":"On the Possibility of Habitable, Trojan Planets in the Kepler Circumbinary Planetary Systems","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 221287 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is one of about 1400 Sun-like stars monitored with the HARPS spectrograph for small shifts in their motion caused by orbiting companions (Naef et al. 2007). The survey aims to find more planets in a volume-limited sample, meaning all suitable stars within a fixed distance, to improve the measured spread of planet orbits (Naef et al. 2007). Better numbers are meant to give tighter tests of planet-formation models (Naef et al. 2007). The orbit of this planet was published together with those of planets around HD 100777 and HD 190647 (Naef et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd221287b-Naefetal2007","cite":"Naef et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, W. Benz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. IX. Exoplanets orbiting <ASTROBJ>HD 100777</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 190647</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 221287</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.0917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 g":{"text":{"why":"The planet was spotted in a survey data set searched with the help of volunteers in the Exoplanet Explorers project (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","observed":"The K2 telescope saw only two transits of this planet, too few to be sure (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Follow-up with the Spitzer Space Telescope confirmed it, giving a period of 41.966 days and a radius of 3.44 Earth radii (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). K2 was typically limited to planets with periods below 40 days (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). With six planets, K2-138 is the K2 system with the most known planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","system":"The inner planets are one super-Earth and four sub-Neptunes whose orbits sit near 3:2 resonances, where the periods form simple ratios (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). A large gap separates the outer two planets, since the fifth planet orbits every 12.76 days (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","history":"Computer models give dynamical evidence that three of the planets were captured together into a three-planet resonance during migration (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024).","open":"Smaller or non-transiting planets may sit in the gap between the fifth and sixth planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Because the star is bright and the planets are near resonance, the system could become a benchmark for measuring planet masses (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138g-HardegreeUllmanetal2021","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie L. Christiansen, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Planet for the Citizen Science System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08978","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138g-CerioniBeaug2024","cite":"Cerioni & Beaugé 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cerioni, C. Beaugé","title":"K2-138 and the capture in 1st-order 3P-MMRs","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2020-BLG-108L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The brightness curve of this event strayed strongly from that of a single lens star (Satoh et al. 2026). Two solutions fit equally well: a wide one and a close one, with a companion-to-host mass ratio of about 0.02 (Satoh et al. 2026). Finite-source effects, where the background star is large enough that its disc is resolved as the lens passes, gave an angular Einstein radius of 0.7 mas (Satoh et al. 2026). That value links the mass and distance of the lens (Satoh et al. 2026). The planet is a giant lying beyond the snow line of a low-mass host (Satoh et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Which of the wide and close solutions is correct is not known (Satoh et al. 2026). The planet sits near the lower edge of the mass-ratio desert, a range of mass ratios where few companions are found (Satoh et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2020blg108lb-Satohetal2026","cite":"Satoh et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yuki K. Satoh, David P. Bennett, Takahiro Sumi et al.","title":"MOA-2020-BLG-108Lb: A giant planet beyond the snow line of a low-mass lens near the lower boundary of the mass-ratio desert","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.12081","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-25 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey is HATSouth, a global network of automated telescopes at three sites in Chile, Namibia and Australia (Espinoza et al. 2016).","observed":"HATS-25 b is one of six new inflated hot Jupiters announced together, with radii of 1.17 to 1.75 times Jupiter's (Espinoza et al. 2016). Its radius is larger than expected for a planet of its mass, which is why it counts as inflated (Espinoza et al. 2016). It is a good target for atmospheric studies (Espinoza et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats25b-Espinozaetal2016","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. Espinoza, D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-25b through HATS-30b: A Half-dozen New Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiters from the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 581 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was already known to host three planets when this one was added with data from HARPS, a high-resolution spectrograph (Mayor et al. 2009). The planet's mass was reported as about 1.9 Earth masses (Mayor et al. 2009). A later study argued that an eccentricity of 0.32 reported for it cannot be correct, because such an orbit would make the system unstable (Vogt et al. 2012). It found that a four-planet model with circular or nearly circular orbits fits the data well and is stable (Vogt et al. 2012).","system":"The star is a cool red dwarf of type M3 (Tilipman et al. 2020). Ultraviolet light from such stars drives chemistry in planetary atmospheres and can blow gas away (Tilipman et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The precise shape of the orbit is debated (Vogt et al. 2012). Extra signals left in the data hint at one or more further planets (Vogt et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj581e-Mayoretal2009","cite":"Mayor et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Mayor, X. Bonfils, T. Forveille et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 581 radial velocity curve (Mayor+, 2009)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35070487","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581e-Vogtetal2012","cite":"Vogt et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S. S. Vogt, R. P. Butler, N. Haghighipour","title":"GJ 581 update: Additional evidence for a Super-Earth in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.4515","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581e-Tilipmanetal2020","cite":"Tilipman et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"D. Tilipman, M. Vieytes, J. L. Linsky","title":"Semi-Empirical Modeling of the M Dwarf Exoplanet Hosts GJ 832, GJ 581, and GJ 876: UV Radiation and Implications for Exoplanet Atmospheres","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-419 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first noticed as a candidate whose transit shape revealed a large eccentricity of 0.81, meaning a very stretched orbit, found with a method called the photoeccentric effect (Dawson et al. 2012). Its host is a rapidly rotating F star, and the chance that it is a false alarm is 3.1% (Dawson et al. 2012). Its transits arrive an hour early or late, which points to an unseen companion (Dawson et al. 2012). Clearly non-sinusoidal timing variations were also found by a later search (Masuda 2017).","system":"The system holds two nearly coplanar, highly eccentric gas giants whose elliptical orbits point in nearly opposite directions (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","history":"Models reproduce this layout if the birth disc of gas and dust held at least 95 Jupiter masses and faded over at least 10000 years (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020). Such a disc would be gravitationally stable, so the two planets must have grown by core accretion and been prone to migration (Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020).","open":"The orbit was first proposed as a possible hot Jupiter in the making, one that has not yet shrunk and rounded (Dawson et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler419b-Dawsonetal2012","cite":"Dawson et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Rebekah I. Dawson, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"The Photoeccentric Effect and Proto-hot Jupiters. II. KOI-1474.01, a Candidate Eccentric Planet Perturbed by an Unseen Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.5579","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler419b-Masuda2017","cite":"Masuda 2017","year":"2017","authors":"K. Masuda","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Transit times of Kepler-448b and Kepler-693b (Masuda, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51540064","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler419b-AliDibPetrovich2020","cite":"Ali-Dib & Petrovich 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mohamad Ali-Dib, Cristobal Petrovich","title":"Constraining protoplanetary discs with exoplanetary dynamics: Kepler-419 as an example","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.06448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2374 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a sub-Saturn, a world between Neptune and Saturn in size, in the Neptunian desert, a region close to stars where such planets are rare (Hacker et al. 2024). It is hot and highly irradiated, with an equilibrium temperature of about 745 K, assuming a Bond albedo of 0.5 (Hacker et al. 2024). A transit seen with the Keck Planet Finder showed that the orbit is tilted nearly over the star's poles, with a projected angle of about 81 degrees from the star's spin (Yee et al. 2025). The signal had an amplitude of 3 metres per second (Yee et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Planets in the Neptunian ridge, a peak in planet numbers at periods of 3 to 6 days, may have formed like hot Jupiters through a dynamically excited route, in which orbits get tilted and stretched (Yee et al. 2025).","open":"Whether most hot Neptunes follow tilted orbits is not known, so one more measurement of this kind helps (Yee et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2374b-Hackeretal2024","cite":"Hacker et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alejandro Hacker, Rodrigo F. Díaz, David J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-2374 b and TOI-3071 b: two metal-rich sub-Saturns well within the Neptunian desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12996","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2374b-Yeeetal2025","cite":"Yee et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Patrick Tamburo, Gudmundur Stefánsson et al.","title":"The Polar Orbit of TOI-2374 b, a Planet in the Neptunian Ridge","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11565","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-216.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a gas giant on a nearly circular orbit (Nesvorný et al. 2022). It was confirmed together with a second large planet, and the two lie in or slightly wide of the 2:1 resonance, where one planet circles twice for every orbit of the other (Dawson et al. 2019). Two families of solutions fit the data, and the favoured one has a Jupiter-sized planet in resonance with a sub-Saturn-mass planet (Dawson et al. 2019).","system":"The inner planet is Neptune-class and orbits at 0.12 au every 17 days, with an eccentricity of 0.16 (Nesvorný et al. 2022). The orbits are compatible with stirring by other, undetected planets (Dawson et al. 2019).","history":"Models of migration in the gas disc suggest the inner planet's migration was halted near its current orbit so that the two could move into resonance together (Nesvorný et al. 2022). They suggest the inner disc was removed quickly, in about 100000 years (Nesvorný et al. 2022).","open":"Which family of solutions is right was left open, and better constraints need more follow-up transits (Dawson et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi21601-Nesvornetal2022","cite":"Nesvorný et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"David Nesvorný, Ondřej Chrenko, Mario Flock","title":"TOI-216: Resonant Constraints on Planet Migration","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09577","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi21601-Dawsonetal2019","cite":"Dawson et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Rebekah I. Dawson, Chelsea X. Huang, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"TOI-216b and TOI-216 c: Two Warm, Large Exoplanets in or Slightly Wide of the 2:1 Orbital Resonance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.11852","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-25 b":{"text":{"why":"The study also looked at whether hot Jupiters have enough irradiation from their stars to become inflated, meaning puffier than simple models predict (Jackson et al. 2023).","observed":"NGTS-25 b was one of three new hot Jupiters found in one survey, and orbits a K-type star every 2.823 days (Jackson et al. 2023). Its mass is 0.64 Jupiter masses and its radius is 1.02 Jupiter radii, so it is between the inflated and non-inflated cases (Jackson et al. 2023). All four planets in the study orbit close enough, at 0.037 to 0.050 au, to receive enough starlight to drive inflation (Jackson et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The physical processes behind radius inflation are still poorly understood (Jackson et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts25b-Jacksonetal2023","cite":"Jackson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"David G. Jackson, Christopher A. Watson, Ernst J. W. de Mooij et al.","title":"The discovery of three hot Jupiters, NGTS-23b, 24b, and 25b, and updated parameters for HATS-54b from the Next Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.01044","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11112 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. The star is old and Sun-like, and its motion shows a long-term trend from a massive companion on a wide orbit (Rodigas et al. 2016). Direct images from the Magellan adaptive optics system show a faint object, HD 11112B, at a separation of 2.2 arcseconds, which is 100 au (Rodigas et al. 2016). It is most likely a cool white dwarf bound to the star, with a mass of 0.9 to 1.1 Suns (Rodigas et al. 2016).","system":"The white dwarf's age is hard to match with the age of the star (Rodigas et al. 2016). One explanation is that it formed from two white dwarfs that merged (Rodigas et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Its orbit would have to be very eccentric and almost edge-on (Rodigas et al. 2016). Further monitoring with radial velocities and direct imaging was proposed (Rodigas et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11112b-Rodigasetal2016","cite":"Rodigas et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Timothy J. Rodigas, P. Bergeron, Amélie Simon et al.","title":"MagAO Imaging of Long-period Objects (MILO). II. A Puzzling White Dwarf around the Sun-like Star HD 11112","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.02576","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 112570 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a red giant that has swollen to 9.85 times the Sun's radius, so its star is far larger than the Sun (Xiao et al. 2024). The star is low in metals, with a metallicity [Fe/H] of -0.46 (Xiao et al. 2024). Combining radial velocities with astrometry from Hipparcos and Gaia, which measures the star's position on the sky, gives a true mass of about 3.42 Jupiter masses (Xiao et al. 2024). The orbit has a period of 2615 days and a moderate eccentricity of 0.20 (Xiao et al. 2024).","system":"The same study reported a second planet, HD 154391 b, around a different giant star (Xiao et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd112570b-Xiaoetal2024","cite":"Xiao et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Guang-Yao Xiao, Huan-Yu Teng, Jianzhao Zhou et al.","title":"Two Long-period Giant Planets around Two Giant Stars: HD 112570 and HD 154391","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.01388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-421 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets of this size and hotness around a Sun-like star had not been studied before; earlier JWST sub-Neptunes orbit late K and M stars (Davenport et al. 2025).","observed":"A spectrum from JWST covering 0.83 to 5 micrometres shows that this planet has a clear, hydrogen-rich atmosphere, with water features that match a solar metallicity and no appreciable haze (Davenport et al. 2025). Its equilibrium temperature is about 920 K (Davenport et al. 2025). Hints of sulphur dioxide and carbon monoxide are present, but not strongly enough to draw firm conclusions (Davenport et al. 2025). The mass is about 7.17 Earth masses and the radius about 2.68 Earth radii (Carleo et al. 2020).","system":"A warm Neptune with a period of about 16 days and a mass of about 16 Earth masses orbits further out (Carleo et al. 2020). The star is a G9 dwarf in a visual binary system (Carleo et al. 2020).","history":"Sub-Neptunes hotter than about 850 K appear not to form hydrocarbon hazes, because they have too little methane to break down (Davenport et al. 2025). The planet also fits the idea that mass loss shapes the radius valley, a dip in planet sizes (Davenport et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi421b-Davenportetal2025","cite":"Davenport et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Brian Davenport, Eliza M.-R. Kempton, Matthew C. Nixon et al.","title":"TOI-421 b: A Hot Sub-Neptune with a Haze-free, Low Mean Molecular Weight Atmosphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.01498","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi421b-Carleoetal2020","cite":"Carleo et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Davide Gandolfi, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"The Multiplanet System TOI-421","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10095","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 233604 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a red giant with a mass of 1.5 Suns (Nowak et al. 2013). It has a minimum mass of 6.6 Jupiter masses, a period of 192 days and a semi-major axis of only 0.75 au (Nowak et al. 2013). That makes it one of the least distant planets known around a giant star (Nowak et al. 2013). Chemical analysis shows the star has a relatively high abundance of lithium-7 (Nowak et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"The lithium may be a sign of an early evolutionary stage of the star or of a recent engulfment of another planet (Nowak et al. 2013).","open":"The planet lies close to the zone where growing giant stars swallow their planets (Nowak et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd233604b-Nowaketal2013","cite":"Nowak et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Nowak, A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan et al.","title":"BD+15 2940 and HD 233604: Two Giants with Planets Close to the Engulfment Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6755","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGC 2682 Sand 1429 b":{"text":{"why":"The star belongs to the open cluster M 67, where stars share a similar age and chemical makeup, which makes planet populations easier to compare (Thomas et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter orbiting a turn-off point star, one just about to leave the main sequence (Thomas et al. 2024). An earlier survey of 88 stars in the cluster found five giant planets, three of them hot Jupiters with periods below 10 days (Thomas et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngc2682sand1429b-Thomasetal2024","cite":"Thomas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Luis Thomas, Roberto Saglia, Luca Pasquini et al.","title":"Search for giant planets in M 67 V: A warm Jupiter orbiting the turn-off star S1429","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28109 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet c is one of three planets that cross the face of their star. Its transit lasts about 9 hours, and the whole event was caught from Antarctica by the ASTEP telescope, which confirmed that the dips seen by TESS were real (Dransfield et al. 2022). The planet's mass was measured from the way its transit times drift, together with precise radial velocities from the ESPRESSO and HARPS spectrographs (Dransfield et al. 2022). The result is a mass of about 7.9 Earth masses and a radius of about 4.2 Earth radii, so the planet is Neptune-like (Dransfield et al. 2022).","system":"Two of the planets, with periods of about 56 and 84 days, sit close to a 3:2 orbital resonance, meaning the inner one circles almost three times while the outer one circles twice. Their pull on each other shifts transit times by up to 60 minutes (Dransfield et al. 2022). A third, inner planet has a period of 22.9 days (Dransfield et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28109c-Dransfieldetal2022","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Georgina Dransfield, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Tristan Guillot et al.","title":"HD 28109 hosts a trio of transiting Neptunian planets including a near-resonant pair, confirmed by ASTEP from Antarctica","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09046","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-87 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-87 c has a neighbour, Kepler-87 b, with a period of about 114 days. Both planets show transit times that drift in opposite directions, which shows they belong to the same system (Ofir et al. 2014). Computer simulations that reproduce the drifts gave the planets' masses (Ofir et al. 2014). The result is an extremely low density for a planet of this mass: Kepler-87 c is puffed up far beyond what its few Earth masses would suggest (Ofir et al. 2014).","system":"The two known transiting planets have periods of about 114 and 191 days and lie close to a 5:3 resonance (Ofir et al. 2014). Kepler-87 b is a giant with a radius of 13.49 Earth radii (Ofir et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Some astronomers have questioned whether the transit-timing masses could be read in another way. Later work argues that very low density planets of 2 to 6 Earth masses are not uncommon in compact multi-planet systems (Ford 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler87c-Ofiretal2014","cite":"Ofir et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Aviv Ofir, Stefan Dreizler, Mathias Zechmeister et al.","title":"An independent planet search in the Kepler dataset. II. An extremely low-density super-Earth mass planet around Kepler-87","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2064","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler87c-Ford2015","cite":"Ford 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Eric B. Ford","title":"Securing the Extremely Low-Densities of Low-Mass Planets Characterized by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 625 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its star is a quiet M2 dwarf only 6.5 parsecs away (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). A second, slower signal of 74 to 85 days also appears in the data. Checks of calcium and hydrogen emission lines, line widths and brightness variations link it to the star's rotation (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). No activity indicator is tied to the short signal, which supports the planet reading (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017). Only a minimum mass is known.","system":"The planet orbits at the inner edge of the star's habitable zone, at 0.078 AU (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj625b-Mascareoetal2017","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, J. I. González Hernández, R. Rebolo et al.","title":"HADES RV Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. V. A super-Earth on the inner edge of the habitable zone of the nearby M dwarf GJ 625","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.06537","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1736 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1736 c is a massive outer companion to a transiting sub-Neptune. Years of radial velocity measurements with the SOPHIE spectrograph show a strong signal from it, with a semi-amplitude of 201.1 metres per second (Martioli et al. 2023). That gives a minimum mass of 8.09 Jupiter masses (Martioli et al. 2023). Small errors in the star's position recorded by Gaia constrain the true mass to about 8.7 Jupiter masses (Martioli et al. 2023). The star is a solar analog, meaning it is similar to the Sun (Martioli et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-1736 b, is a sub-Neptune with a 7.07-day period, a radius of 2.44 Earth radii and a mass of 12.8 Earth masses (Martioli et al. 2023). The velocities also show a slow linear drift on top of the two planets (Martioli et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1736c-Martiolietal2023","cite":"Martioli et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Martioli, G. Hébrard, L. de Almeida et al.","title":"TOI-1736 and TOI-2141: Two systems including sub-Neptunes around solar analogs revealed by TESS and SOPHIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.07011","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it, the Qatar Exoplanet Survey, reported it together with Qatar-3 b and Qatar-5 b, all hot Jupiters (Alsubai et al. 2017).","observed":"Follow-up spectroscopy of the star revealed a heavy planet, about 6 Jupiter masses, with a radius only slightly larger than Jupiter's (Alsubai et al. 2017). Its host is a low-mass main-sequence star, smaller than the Sun (Alsubai et al. 2017). Later transit timings from small robotic telescopes and TESS pinned down the orbit very precisely, to a period of 1.80536560 days with an error of two ten-millionths of a day (Hewitt et al. 2026). Such timings help later missions schedule their observations. A model study of radio emission from planets with magnetic fields lists it among four planets that the Square Kilometre Array might detect (Bagheri et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar4b-Alsubaietal2017","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Khalid Alsubai, Dimitris Mislis, Zlatan I. Tsvetanov et al.","title":"Qatar Exoplanet Survey : Qatar-3b, Qatar-4b, and Qatar-5b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06882","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar4b-Hewittetal2026","cite":"Hewitt et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"H. B. Hewitt, M. Chamlee, T. Hockman et al.","title":"Ephemeris Refinement for Qatar-4 b, HAT-P-18 b, and CoRoT-1 b with Small Telescope and TESS Observations","journal":"Journal of the American Association of Variable Star Observers (JAAVSO)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.15391","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar4b-Bagherietal2024","cite":"Bagheri et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Fatemeh Bagheri, Anshuman Garga, Ramon E. Lopez","title":"Exploring radio emissions from confirmed exoplanets using SKA","journal":"Frontiers in Astronomy and Space Sciences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14468","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 16760 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 16760 b is a heavy companion with a minimum mass of 14.3 Jupiter masses, close to the dividing line between planets and brown dwarfs (Bouchy et al. 2009). Because only the minimum mass is known, the true mass may be higher. It was one of the first results from the SOPHIE spectrograph on the 1.93-metre telescope at Haute-Provence in France, and the discovery report also described the instrument and its planet-search programme (Bouchy et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"The orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.067, which supports formation in a gas disc around the young star in the way planets form (Bouchy et al. 2009).","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a small brown dwarf is not settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd16760b-Bouchyetal2009","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"F. Bouchy, G. Hébrard, S. Udry et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets . I. A companion around HD 16760 with mass close to the planet/brown-dwarf transition","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200912427","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2498 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets in the Neptune desert, a range of masses and orbital periods where few planets are found, help show where the desert's edges lie and how it formed (Frame et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-2498 b is a hot, puffed-up planet with a density of 0.86 grams per cubic centimetre, less than that of water at about 1 (Frame et al. 2023). It lies on the edge of the Neptune desert (Frame et al. 2023). Its star is a Sun-like G star about 3.6 billion years old (Frame et al. 2023). The planet is also a good target for future atmosphere studies, searching for species such as water or sodium with high-resolution optical spectra (Frame et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Modelling of its evaporation history suggests the planet has shrunk from a Saturn-sized world to its present radius over about 3.6 billion years as starlight stripped gas from it (Frame et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2498b-Frameetal2023","cite":"Frame et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ginger Frame, David J. Armstrong, Heather M. Cegla et al.","title":"TOI-2498 b: a hot bloated super-Neptune within the Neptune desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06950","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1649 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery work aimed at finding Earth-sized planets that could be compared with Venus, because it remains unclear what made Earth's and Venus's atmospheres so different (Angelo et al. 2017).","observed":"Kepler-1649 b is an Earth-sized planet around a nearby M5 dwarf. It receives about as much starlight as Venus does from the Sun, which led astronomers to call it an exo-Venus (Angelo et al. 2017). The planet was confirmed by combining point-spread-function photometry, ground-based spectroscopy and imaging of the star (Angelo et al. 2017). A climate study using a three-dimensional model of its atmosphere found that, in every simulation, the air drifted away from temperate conditions toward a runaway greenhouse with rapidly rising surface temperature (Kane et al. 2018). The same study computed spectra that the James Webb Space Telescope could use to test this (Kane et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"No atmosphere has been measured. The planet's true climate is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1649b-Angeloetal2017","cite":"Angelo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Isabel Angelo, Jason F. Rowe, Steve B. Howell et al.","title":"Kepler-1649b: An Exo-Venus in the Solar Neighborhood","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.03136","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1649b-Kaneetal2018","cite":"Kane et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Alma Y. Ceja, Michael J. Way et al.","title":"Climate Modeling of a Potential ExoVenus","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.10072","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 79098 AB b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, the B-star Exoplanet Abundance Study, examines how common planets, brown dwarfs and discs are around B-type stars, the most massive stars within 150 parsecs (Janson et al. 2019).","observed":"HIP 79098 AB b was seen in direct images of the young Scorpius-Centaurus association. It had been reported before but dismissed as a background object because of its odd colours (Janson et al. 2019). Its colours match those of several young, low-mass brown dwarfs, including companions to other stars in the same association (Janson et al. 2019). Fifteen years of images show it moving through space with its host, which proves it belongs to the system (Janson et al. 2019). Its mass is estimated at 16 to 25 Jupiter masses, and it lies about 345 AU from its host in projection (Janson et al. 2019).","system":"The host is a close pair of stars, a B9-type spectroscopic binary, and the companion circles both of them (Janson et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip79098abb-Jansonetal2019","cite":"Janson et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Markus Janson, Ruben Asensio-Torres, Damien André et al.","title":"The B-Star Exoplanet Abundance Study: a co-moving 16-25 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> companion to the young binary system HIP 79098","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.02787","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-148 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No transits of the outer planet were detected. Its minimum mass is 0.40 Jupiter masses, and its true mass is below 0.60 Jupiter masses (Hébrard et al. 2020). The tilt between the two orbits cannot be more than 35° (Hébrard et al. 2020).","system":"Both giant planets have eccentric orbits near a 4:1 resonance, where the inner planet circles four times for every orbit of the outer one (Hébrard et al. 2020). Their tugs on each other are strong enough to shift the inner planet's transit times by about 15 minutes, the first such interacting system detected with ground-based light curves (Hébrard et al. 2020). The arrangement is stable over long times (Hébrard et al. 2020). The star is a slowly rotating, inactive late-G dwarf (Hébrard et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp148c-Hbrardetal2020","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Hébrard, R. F. Díaz, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"Discovery and characterization of the exoplanets WASP-148b and c. A transiting system with two interacting giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14645","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-79 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-79 b has a 10.99-day orbit, close to the periods of signals caused by magnetic activity on its active G-type star, which can distort a mass measurement (Nava et al. 2022). A new method to separate the two signals, applied to HARPS-N data from several observing seasons, found a mass of 11.8 Earth masses and a radius of 4.09 Earth radii (Nava et al. 2022). Models then call it a highly irradiated Uranus analog (Nava et al. 2022). Spitzer observations later confirmed the radius from K2 independently and sharpened the orbital period by more than a factor of ten (Duck et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its density falls in a low range that fits either a rocky core with a thick gas layer or a water-rich world, and the data cannot yet tell which (Palethorpe et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k279b-Navaetal2022","cite":"Nava et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Chantanelle Nava, Mercedes López-Morales, Annelies Mortier et al.","title":"K2-79b and K2-222b: Mass Measurements of Two Small Exoplanets with Periods beyond 10 days that Overlap with Periodic Magnetic Activity Signals","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02608","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k279b-Ducketal2021","cite":"Duck et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alison Duck, Caleb K. Harada, Justin Harrell et al.","title":"K2, Spitzer, and TESS Transits of Four Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08889","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k279b-Palethorpeetal2026","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Larissa Palethorpe, Annelies Mortier, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Constraining small planet compositions for future missions","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14552","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 674 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 674 b was found while sorting out a puzzle. HARPS radial velocities of the spotted M dwarf GJ 674 showed two signals, of 4.69 and 35 days (Bonfils et al. 2007). The 35-day signal matches the star's rotation, so it comes from dark spots and not from a planet, while the 4.69-day signal is caused by the planet (Bonfils et al. 2007). A re-analysis with more data confirmed the planet's eccentric orbit (Liebing et al. 2024). The star also produced a very large ultraviolet flare, seen with the Hubble Space Telescope, with a hot blue glow at about 40,000 K (Froning et al. 2019).","system":"The star is an M2.5 dwarf only 4.5 parsecs away (Bonfils et al. 2007). Flares like the one seen change the chemistry and heating of any atmosphere on its planet (Froning et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"A study of radio emission from planets around M dwarfs picks GJ 674 b as one of the best candidates for detection, but the result depends on the star's unknown wind (Vidotto et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj674b-Bonfilsetal2007","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"X. Bonfils, M. Mayor, X. Delfosse et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. X. A m sin i = 11 M_⊕ planet around the nearby spotted M dwarf <ASTROBJ>GJ 674</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.0270","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj674b-Liebingetal2024","cite":"Liebing et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Liebing, S. V. Jeffers, P. Gorrini et al.","title":"RedDots: Limits on habitable and undetected planets orbiting nearby stars GJ 832, GJ 674, and Ross 128","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01173","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj674b-Froningetal2019","cite":"Froning et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Cynthia S. Froning, Adam Kowalski, Kevin France et al.","title":"A Hot Ultraviolet Flare on the M Dwarf Star GJ 674","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08647","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj674b-Vidottoetal2019","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, N. Feeney, J. H. Groh","title":"Can we detect aurora in exoplanets orbiting M dwarfs?","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.07089","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219828 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 219828 c is a long-period giant on a very eccentric orbit, with a period of 13.1 years and an eccentricity of 0.81 (Santos et al. 2016). Radial velocities from HARPS, SOPHIE and ELODIE give a minimum mass of 15.1 Jupiter masses (Santos et al. 2016). Only the minimum mass is known. The same data also confirm a hot Neptune, HD 219828 b, with a 3.83-day period and a minimum mass of 21 Earth masses (Santos et al. 2016).","system":"A close-in Neptune-mass planet together with a distant massive companion is an unusual combination, and the discovery report calls the system extreme (Santos et al. 2016). Computer simulations show the system is stable (Santos et al. 2016). The host star is bright and rich in heavy elements (Santos et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219828c-Santosetal2016","cite":"Santos et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. C. Santos, A. Santerne, J. P. Faria et al.","title":"An extreme planetary system around HD 219828. One long-period super Jupiter to a hot-Neptune host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.06942","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-104 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found together with WASP-106 b. Its orbit shows no significant eccentricity, which is expected for a planet so close to its star (Smith et al. 2014).","observed":"WASP-104 b is a hot Jupiter that is darker than almost any planet known. Precise K2 brightness measurements of its star show the star's light varying slightly as the planet circles it (Močnik et al. 2018). They give a visual reflectivity below 0.03 at 95% confidence, so the planet reflects very little light (Močnik et al. 2018). The measurements also set tight limits on transit timing changes, starspot crossings and additional transiting planets (Močnik et al. 2018).","system":"The star rotates about every 23 or 46 days (Močnik et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp104b-Smithetal2014","cite":"Smith et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, D. R. Anderson, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"WASP-104b and WASP-106b: two transiting hot Jupiters in 1.75-day and 9.3-day orbits","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.0887","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp104b-Moniketal2018","cite":"Močnik et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"T. Močnik, C. Hellier, J. Southworth","title":"WASP-104b is Darker Than Charcoal","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05334","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 121504 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has largely studied its star. The discovery survey placed the planet among its shorter-period finds, with a lower orbital eccentricity than most of the long-period giants it reported (Mayor et al. 2004). Its star was used as a test case when several research groups each measured ten element abundances in its spectrum, alongside three other stars, to compare their methods (Hinkel et al. 2016). A 2002 estimate of reflected light listed the planet among those best suited to searches for small brightness changes (Vid'Machenko et al. 2002).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd121504b-Mayoretal2004","cite":"Mayor et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mayor, S. Udry, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XII. Orbital solutions for 16 extra-solar planets discovered with CORALIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0310316","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd121504b-Hinkeletal2016","cite":"Hinkel et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Natalie R. Hinkel, Patrick A. Young, Michael D. Pagano et al.","title":"A Comparison of Stellar Elemental Abundance Techniques and Measurements","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.03130","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd121504b-VidMachenkoetal2002","cite":"Vid'Machenko et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"A. P. Vid'Machenko, V. N. Krushevskaya, Yu. G. Kuznetsova","title":"Brightness variations of an extrasolar giant planet rotating around its central star","journal":"Kinematika i Fizika Nebesnykh Tel","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-407 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New radial velocity measurements with the MAROON-X and KPF spectrographs updated the masses of five small rocky planets, Kepler-407 b among them (Brinkman et al. 2025). The new masses suggest compositions closer to Earth's than earlier results for every planet in the sample (Brinkman et al. 2025). Iron-to-rock ratios in the planets also resemble those of their host stars, with no steep relationship between planet and star (Brinkman et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler407b-Brinkmanetal2025","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"The Compositions of Rocky Planets in Close-in Orbits Tend to Be Earth-like","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00213","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3540 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of ten hot Jupiters reported in the first paper of the TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey (Yee et al. 2022). All of them were first spotted in TESS brightness data. Follow-up transit light curves from many ground-based telescopes, including small observatories and the Las Cumbres network, refined the transit properties and orbital timing (Yee et al. 2022). Little else is known about the planet itself.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3540ab-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. W. Yee, J. N. Winn, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey I. 10 planets (Yee+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51640070","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"FU Tau b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the brighter brown dwarf FU Tau A, which orbits with FU Tau b. The pair are young brown dwarfs, about a million years old, separated by about 800 AU, and the chance that two unrelated brown dwarfs would lie so close in the sky is only about 3 in 10,000 (Luhman et al. 2009). Spectral types of M7.25 and M9.25 correspond to masses of about 0.05 and 0.015 solar masses, with FU Tau b the smaller (Luhman et al. 2009). Unlike its partner, FU Tau b matches the brightness expected for a one-million-year-old object (Stelzer et al. 2010).","system":"The pair are a wide brown dwarf binary in the Barnard 215 dark cloud, apparently born in isolation (Luhman et al. 2009). FU Tau A drives a molecular outflow (Monin et al. 2013). Its radio emission suggests a thermal jet, as seen in young low-mass stars (Rodríguez et al. 2017).","history":"FU Tau A is brighter than expected for its age, which suggests the two components did not form at the same time (Stelzer et al. 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-futaub-Luhmanetal2009","cite":"Luhman et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"K. L. Luhman, E. E. Mamajek, P. R. Allen et al.","title":"Discovery of a Wide Binary Brown Dwarf Born in Isolation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0425","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-futaub-Stelzeretal2010","cite":"Stelzer et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"B. Stelzer, A. Scholz, C. Argiroffi et al.","title":"The enigmatic young brown dwarf binary FUTau: accretion and activity","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2717","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-futaub-Moninetal2013","cite":"Monin et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J.-L. Monin, E. T. Whelan, B. Lefloch et al.","title":"A molecular outflow driven by the brown dwarf binary FU Tauri","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.4387","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-futaub-Rodrguezetal2017","cite":"Rodríguez et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Luis F. Rodríguez, Luis A. Zapata, Aina Palau","title":"JVLA Observations of Young Brown Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03999","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-21 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star, HD 179070, is the brightest planet host that Kepler has turned up (Howell et al. 2012). Its slow brightness pulsations, caused by sound waves inside the star, gave a precise mass, radius and an age of about 2.84 billion years (Howell et al. 2012). At first only an upper limit of about 10 Earth masses could be set for the planet (Howell et al. 2012). A later campaign of 82 new velocity measurements with HARPS-N detected the planet's pull on its star and gave a mass of 5.1 +/- 1.7 Earth masses and a density of 6.4 +/- 2.1 g/cm3 (López-Morales et al. 2016). Such a planet is likely made of iron and rock and has lost most of its gas (López-Morales et al. 2016). A 2024 study placed it in the radius valley and ruled out a water-world composition (Beard et al. 2024).","system":"A long-period super-Jupiter candidate, Kepler-21 (c), has been identified from a slow trend in the star's velocities combined with Gaia astrometry (Beard et al. 2024). Imaging also turned up a previously unknown stellar companion to the host star (Ginski et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Kepler-21 (c) is still only a candidate (Beard et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler21b-Howelletal2012","cite":"Howell et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Steve B. Howell, Jason F. Rowe, Stephen T. Bryson et al.","title":"Kepler-21b: A 1.6 R <SUB>Earth</SUB> Planet Transiting the Bright Oscillating F Subgiant Star HD 179070","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.2165","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler21b-LpezMoralesetal2016","cite":"López-Morales et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Mercedes López-Morales, Raphaëlle D. Haywood, Jeffrey L. Coughlin et al.","title":"Kepler-21b: A Rocky Planet Around a V = 8.25 Magnitude Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.07617","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler21b-Beardetal2024","cite":"Beard et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Corey Beard, Paul Robertson, Mark R. Giovinazzi et al.","title":"Utilizing Photometry from Multiple Sources to Mitigate Stellar Variability in Precise Radial Velocities: A Case Study of Kepler-21","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02873","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler21b-Ginskietal2016","cite":"Ginski et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"C. Ginski, M. Mugrauer, M. Seeliger et al.","title":"A lucky imaging multiplicity study of exoplanet host stars - II","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.01524","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2236 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up aims to build a uniform sample of hot Jupiters, giant planets on very short orbits, to study where they come from (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-2236 b is one of ten new hot Jupiters reported as the first results of the TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey (Yee et al. 2022). The survey combines new TESS detections with earlier ground-based transit surveys, so that the origins of hot Jupiters can be studied across a large sample (Yee et al. 2022). Each candidate was checked with ground-based time-series photometry, high-resolution imaging and high-resolution spectroscopy (Yee et al. 2022). The ten planets orbit fairly bright F and G stars, have orbital periods of 2 to 10 days and masses of 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiter masses (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2236b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4529 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements with the CARMENES spectrograph followed up the TESS signal (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The radius is well measured at about 1.77 Earth radii (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The planet's pull on its star was too weak to measure, so only upper limits exist: a 3-sigma mass limit of 4.9 Earth masses and a density limit of 0.88 times Earth's (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). The host is an early M dwarf of type M1.5 V (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026). Planets of this size and low density fall in a region where rocky planets with thick atmospheres and gas-rich planets cannot be told apart (Poultourtzidis et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is mostly rock or carries a deep gas layer is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4529b-Poultourtzidisetal2026","cite":"Poultourtzidis et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"E. Poultourtzidis, G. Lacedelli, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Characterization of two new transiting sub-Neptunes and a terrestrial planet around M-dwarf hosts","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07414","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2013-BLG-220L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet could have been found and measured using follow-up observations alone, which matters for present and future microlensing surveys (Yee et al. 2014).","observed":"The planet has a mass ratio of about 3.0 thousandths relative to its host star, which makes it a super-Jupiter (Yee et al. 2014). Two separate lines of evidence place both planet and host in the Galactic disk (Yee et al. 2014). The system moves across the sky unusually fast for a microlensing planet, at 12.5 +/- 1 milliarcseconds a year (Yee et al. 2014). As a result, the host star was expected to be separately resolvable within about 7 years (Yee et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2013blg220lb-Yeeetal2014","cite":"Yee et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. C. Yee, C. Han, A. Gould et al.","title":"MOA-2013-BLG-220Lb: Massive Planetary Companion to Galactic-disk Host","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.2134","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1625 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of the attention on this planet concerns a possible moon, Kepler-1625b I, a Neptune-sized satellite suggested around a long-period Jupiter-like planet (Kreidberg et al. 2019). A new analysis of the Hubble observations found that a planet-only model fits the transit well and that adding a moon does not improve the fit (Kreidberg et al. 2019). The moon signal was judged likely to be an artifact of the data reduction (Kreidberg et al. 2019). A later modelling study also found no firm evidence of a moon, because noise that varies slowly in time can mimic a moon's signal (Kálmán et al. 2024). The original authors defend the result, saying their fits of the Hubble data again recover a moon-like dip (Kipping et al. 2025).","system":"If the moon exists, it would be about as massive as Neptune and orbit at about 40 planetary radii, around a planet of roughly ten Jupiter masses (Moraes & Vieira Neto 2020). Orbit calculations have also asked how far from such a moon a moon-of-a-moon could stay stable (Rosario-Franco et al. 2019).","history":"A moon this large on a wide orbit was first thought to have been captured (Moraes & Vieira Neto 2020). Simulations show it could instead have formed around the planet from a massive disc of material, with wider star-planet distances needing more solid material in the disc (Moraes & Vieira Neto 2020).","open":"Whether the exomoon is real is still disputed."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1625b-Kreidbergetal2019","cite":"Kreidberg et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Laura Kreidberg, Rodrigo Luger, Megan Bedell","title":"No Evidence for Lunar Transit in New Analysis of Hubble Space Telescope Observations of the Kepler-1625 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10618","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1625b-Klmnetal2024","cite":"Kálmán et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Szilárd Kálmán, Szilárd Csizmadia, Attila E. Simon et al.","title":"Modelling the light curves of transiting exomoons: a zero-order photodynamic agent added to the Transit and Light Curve Modeller","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.04647","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1625b-Kippingetal2025","cite":"Kipping et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"David Kipping, Alex Teachey, Daniel Yahalomi et al.","title":"Concerning the possible exomoons around Kepler-1625b and Kepler-1708b","journal":"Dryad Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.5061/DRYAD.ZS7H44JH4","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1625b-MoraesVieiraNeto2020","cite":"Moraes & Vieira Neto 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. A. Moraes, E. Vieira Neto","title":"Exploring formation scenarios for the exomoon candidate Kepler 1625b I","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.10138","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1625b-RosarioFrancoetal2019","cite":"Rosario-Franco et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Marialis Rosario-Franco, Billy Quarles, Zdzislaw E. Musielak et al.","title":"Determining Stability Conditions for Submoons Orbiting Exomoon Candidate: Kepler 1625-b-I","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #50","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-266 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the system as a whole and its innermost planet. The discovery team reports a compact system of six sub-Neptune-sized planets around a nearby K-type star at about 78 parsecs (Rodriguez et al. 2018). It holds the most planets found in one system by the K2 mission (Rodriguez et al. 2018). Its ultra-short-period super-Earth, with a 0.66 day orbit, is tilted by more than 12 degrees relative to the other planets (Rodriguez et al. 2018). Because that planet transits, it can be compared directly with its neighbours (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","system":"A transiting planet candidate was also identified around a nearby companion star that moves through space with K2-266 (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"The tilted inner planet may reveal how it reached its current orbit (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2266e-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Juliette Becker, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"K2-266: A Compact Multi-Planet System With A Significantly Misaligned Ultra Short Period Planet","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 101930 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a Saturn-mass world with a minimum mass of 0.30 Jupiter masses and an orbital period of 70.5 days (Lovis et al. 2005). Checks of stellar activity showed no sign that starspots could fake the velocity signal, which supports the planetary explanation (Lovis et al. 2005). The velocity scatter left after fitting the orbit is only 1.8 m/s, showing how precise the HARPS instrument is (Lovis et al. 2005). The host star is rich in metals, matching the known trend that planets are more common around such stars (Lovis et al. 2005).","system":"The host star has a wide companion, HD 101930 B, an M0 to M1 dwarf of about 0.7 solar masses, roughly 73 arcseconds away, or about 2200 au in projected separation (Mugrauer et al. 2007). Calculations show that a hypothetical terrestrial planet could stay stable for a long time at the Lagrange points of this planet's orbit, which sits partly in the star's habitable zone (Schwarz et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd101930b-Lovisetal2005","cite":"Lovis et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"C. Lovis, M. Mayor, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. III. Three Saturn-mass planets around HD 93083, HD 101930 and HD 102117","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0503660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd101930b-Mugraueretal2007","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Mugrauer, A. Seifahrt, R. Neuhäuser","title":"The multiplicity of planet host stars - new low-mass companions to planet host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.1767","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd101930b-Schwarzetal2007","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. Schwarz, R. Dvorak, Á. Süli et al.","title":"Stability of fictitious Trojan planets in extrasolar systems","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/asna.200710789","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-50 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-50 has two transiting planets close to a 6:5 orbital ratio, so they tug on each other and shift their transit times (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). Fits to those timing shifts give a mass of 2.35 Earth masses for Kepler-50 b and 4.43 Earth masses for Kepler-50 c (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). The fits also show that the two orbits are measurably different in shape (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). Both planets lie in the radius valley and are sub-Neptunes by size (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026).","system":"Leftover timing shifts are attributed to a third, non-transiting planet near a resonance with the other two (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). Its properties are poorly pinned down, with 55 possible regions for an outer planet and 15 for an inner one (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"About 68 percent of the likely stable solutions have the two transiting planets locked in a resonance, so the timing data do not confirm it (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler50b-JontofHutteretal2026","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jason F. Rowe, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-50: Two Planets in Close Resonance Perturbed by a Non-transiting Planet","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-57 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-57 b is a massive, dense hot Jupiter of 3.15 Jupiter masses and 1.14 Jupiter radii, with a mean density of 2.65 +/- 0.21 g/cm3 (Espinoza et al. 2019). It circles its star every 2.35 days (Espinoza et al. 2019). The host star is very active, with about 2 percent peak-to-peak variation in its brightness (Espinoza et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats57b-Espinozaetal2019","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Espinoza, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-54b-HATS-58Ab: Five New Transiting Hot Jupiters Including One with a Possible Temperate Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-42 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star, which has the survey name KOI 961. The star was compared with the nearby, well-studied Barnard's Star, and the two match closely in colour and spectrum (Muirhead et al. 2012). That comparison gave the star a mass of 0.13 +/- 0.05 solar masses and a radius of 0.17 +/- 0.04 solar radii (Muirhead et al. 2012). A new fit of the Kepler light curve significantly revised the transit parameters of the three planets (Muirhead et al. 2012).","system":"The star hosts three small, short-period planets (Muirhead et al. 2012). Like Barnard's Star, it is old, metal-poor, fast-moving and has no steady Halpha emission, a sign of low activity (Muirhead et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler42d-Muirheadetal2012","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, John Asher Johnson, Kevin Apps et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. III. KOI 961: A Small Star with Large Proper Motion and Three Small Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2189","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1846 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed using TESS photometry, ground-based multicolour photometry, high-resolution imaging and statistical validation (Soubkiou et al. 2025). Its radius is 1.79 +/- 0.07 Earth radii, in the radius valley, the range of sizes between rocky super-Earths and puffier sub-Neptunes (Soubkiou et al. 2025). Only a predicted mass of about 4.4 Earth masses exists (Soubkiou et al. 2025). The host is a nearby M dwarf, 47 parsecs away, with a temperature of about 3568 K (Soubkiou et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's mass has not been measured, so whether it is rocky or gas-rich is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1846b-Soubkiouetal2025","cite":"Soubkiou et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Abderahmane Soubkiou, Khalid Barkaoui, Zouhair Benkhaldoun et al.","title":"TOI-1846 b: a super-Earth in the radius valley orbiting a nearby M dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.18550","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-942 b":{"text":{"why":"Young stars and systems with several planets help test ideas about how planets form and change (Carleo et al. 2021).","observed":"The host star is young, about 50 million years old, and relatively active, with a rotation period of 3.39 days (Carleo et al. 2021). Spectroscopic observations of two partial transits showed that the planet orbits in the same direction as the star spins (Wirth et al. 2021). Its projected tilt is 1 degree with large uncertainty, and the true 3D tilt is 2 degrees (Wirth et al. 2021). TOI-942 b is one of the youngest planets to have its orbital tilt measured (Wirth et al. 2021).","system":"A second Neptune-sized planet orbits further out, with a period of 10.1 days (Wirth et al. 2021).","history":"Young systems of similar age studied so far have shown no strongly misaligned orbits, and TOI-942 b follows that pattern (Wirth et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi942b-Carleoetal2021","cite":"Carleo et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"I. Carleo, S. Desidera, D. Nardiello et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXVIII. A pair of hot-Neptunes orbiting the young star TOI-942","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13795","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi942b-Wirthetal2021","cite":"Wirth et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christopher P. Wirth, George Zhou, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"TOI-942b: A Prograde Neptune in a 60 Myr Old Multi-transiting System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14968","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-23 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Warm Spitzer observations of the planet passing behind its star measured its dayside light at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres and in the K band, with eclipse depths of 0.248, 0.309 and 0.234 percent (O'Rourke et al. 2014). Its dayside glow matches a blackbody of 2154 +/- 90 K (O'Rourke et al. 2014). The eclipse timing is consistent with a circular orbit (O'Rourke et al. 2014). Transit timing shows no evidence that the orbit is shrinking from tidal decay (Alvarado et al. 2024). The planet is one of four massive transiting planets, of 2 to 7 Jupiter masses, announced together (Bakos et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether its very short orbit will eventually decay is still being tested with longer timing records (Alvarado et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp23b-ORourkeetal2014","cite":"O'Rourke et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Joseph G. O'Rourke, Heather A. Knutson, Ming Zhao et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer and Palomar Near-IR Secondary Eclipse Photometry of Two Hot Jupiters: WASP-48b and HAT-P-23b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.0011","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp23b-Alvaradoetal2024","cite":"Alvarado et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Efrain Alvarado, Kate B. Bostow, Kishore C. Patra et al.","title":"Searching for tidal orbital decay in hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp23b-Bakosetal2010","cite":"Bakos et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-20b-HAT-P-23b: Four Massive Transiting Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+60 1417 b":{"text":{"why":"The companion lies more than 1000 au from its star, so its light is free of contamination from the host, which makes it a rare target for studying how such worlds form (Phillips et al. 2024).","observed":"The companion is an L7-type object of about 15 +/- 5 Jupiter masses, close to the boundary of about 13 Jupiter masses where deuterium burning starts (Vierling & Bardalez Gagliuffi 2023). It is one of the reddest brown-dwarf-like objects studied so far, with a J-Ks colour of 2.72, which points to thick clouds (Phillips et al. 2024). Atmospheric retrieval strongly favours a cloudy model over a cloudless one (Phillips et al. 2024). The retrieval could not pin down the gas abundances or the carbon-to-oxygen ratio (Phillips et al. 2024).","system":"The companion orbits a K0 star at a separation of about 1662 au (Vierling & Bardalez Gagliuffi 2023). The star's own elemental abundances were measured for the first time with high-resolution spectra (Phillips et al. 2024).","history":"The companion may have formed in a disc or in a collapsing cloud, and the two routes could leave different chemical fingerprints (Vierling & Bardalez Gagliuffi 2023).","open":"The companion's composition and formation route are not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd601417b-Phillipsetal2024","cite":"Phillips et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Caprice L. Phillips, Jacqueline K. Faherty, Ben Burningham et al.","title":"Retrieving Young Cloudy L Dwarfs: A Nearby Planetary-mass Companion BD+60 1417B and its Isolated Red Twin W0047","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01694","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-bd601417b-VierlingBardalezGagliuffi2023","cite":"Vierling & Bardalez Gagliuffi 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Sarah Vierling, Daniella Bardalez Gagliuffi","title":"Searching for Spectroscopic Features of Brown Dwarf Companion Formation: BD+60 1417 Case Study","journal":"AGU Fall Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-177 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-177 c is a super-puff, a planet of sub-Saturn mass with an extremely low density (Marston et al. 2026). Interior models of 34 cold super-puffs found that Kepler-177 c is one of six planets that cannot be reproduced by standard core-accretion models, based on their observed masses and radii (Marston et al. 2026). Extra heat from within the planet or from late impacts could make it puffier, but radioactive heating alone is insufficient (Marston et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The planet's low density challenges core-accretion theory for giant-planet formation (Marston et al. 2026).","open":"A non-standard explanation, such as rings, may be needed (Marston et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler177c-Marstonetal2026","cite":"Marston et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nicholas T. Marston, Juliette Becker, Alex R. Howe","title":"Do Super-puffs Defy Core Accretion? Population-wide Interior Structure Constraints","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11330","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-771 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet belongs to a project that studies the composition of sub-Neptunes across star types, using JWST (Lacedelli et al. 2025).","observed":"Thirty-one ESPRESSO velocity measurements, along with TESS, SPECULOOS, TRAPPIST and MEarth photometry, gave a radius of 1.36 +/- 0.10 Earth radii and a mass of 2.47 Earth masses (Lacedelli et al. 2025). It is a rocky super-Earth orbiting an M dwarf 25 parsecs away (Lacedelli et al. 2025). The planet had first been validated statistically with TESS data (Mistry et al. 2024). Predicted transmission spectra were also calculated for HST and JWST (Mistry et al. 2024).","system":"A second, non-transiting sub-Neptune orbits the same star (Lacedelli et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi771b-Lacedellietal2025","cite":"Lacedelli et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"G. Lacedelli, E. Palle, Y. T. Davis et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-771 RV datasets (Lacedelli+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi771b-Mistryetal2024","cite":"Mistry et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Aniket Prasad, Mousam Maity et al.","title":"VaTEST III: Validation of eight potential super-earths from TESS data","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.00688","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-76 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets are expected to be rarer around low-mass stars than around Sun-like stars, a key prediction of core-accretion theory, in which planets grow by building up a solid core that then pulls in gas (Jordán et al. 2022). The survey that turned up this planet aims to count such giants to test that idea (Jordán et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet transits a late K or early M dwarf, a small, cool star (Jordán et al. 2022). Follow-up with TESS and other telescopes confirmed the transits, and the ESPRESSO spectrograph on the Very Large Telescope measured its mass at 2.629 Jupiter masses (Jordán et al. 2022). Its radius is 1.079 Jupiter radii (Jordán et al. 2022). It was announced together with three other giant planets around similar stars (Jordán et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether giant planets are truly rare around low-mass stars is still being measured, and each new find adds to the count (Jordán et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats76b-Jordnetal2022","cite":"Jordán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Andrés Jordán, J. D. Hartman, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-74Ab, HATS-75b, HATS-76b, and HATS-77b: Four Transiting Giant Planets Around K and M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01928","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-715 b":{"text":{"why":"Its radius of 1.55 Earth radii falls exactly between two measured positions of the radius valley for M dwarfs (Dransfield et al. 2024). The radius valley is a scarcity of planets at certain sizes that separates rocky worlds from gas-rich ones. Measuring this planet's mass and composition would help show where the valley truly lies for low-mass stars (Dransfield et al. 2024).","observed":"TESS first picked out the transits. Ground-based photometry, high-resolution imaging and statistical checks then validated the planet (Dransfield et al. 2024). It receives 0.67 times the starlight Earth does, which puts it inside the most conservative definitions of the habitable zone for rocky planets (Dransfield et al. 2024). A second, smaller candidate on a 25.6-day orbit was proposed, but its signal failed to grow with more data, so it is unlikely to be real (Jenkins et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a nearby M4 star close to the ecliptic South Pole, about 42 parsecs away (Dransfield et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Its mass and composition are not yet measured. The star is bright enough that radial-velocity measurements and transmission spectroscopy look possible (Dransfield et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi715b-Dransfieldetal2024","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Georgina Dransfield, Mathilde Timmermans, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"A 1.55 R<SUB>⊕</SUB> habitable-zone planet hosted by TOI-715, an M4 star near the ecliptic South Pole","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06206","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi715b-Jenkinsetal2024","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jon M. Jenkins","title":"TIC271971130.02 is Unlikely to be a TransitingPlanet: How Smoothing Light Curves Prior to Transit Searches Can Artificially Boost SNR","journal":"TESS Science Conference III","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13287265","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-42 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system comes from studying its host star, which carries the name KOI 961 in the Kepler catalogue (Muirhead et al. 2012). The star was compared with Barnard's Star, a nearby, well-studied M dwarf, and the two agree closely in colour and spectra, implying similar temperatures and metal content (Muirhead et al. 2012). Both are metal-poor and old, with large proper motion and high speed along the line of sight (Muirhead et al. 2012). The star's mass is estimated at 0.13 solar masses and its radius at 0.17 solar radii (Muirhead et al. 2012). The transit signals of its three small planets were refit, and each was tested for the chance of being a false alarm (Muirhead et al. 2012).","system":"Three small planets circle this small star on short orbits (Muirhead et al. 2012). The star's motion through the Galaxy matches that of an old population at larger heights above the galactic plane (Muirhead et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler42b-Muirheadetal2012","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, John Asher Johnson, Kevin Apps et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. III. KOI 961: A Small Star with Large Proper Motion and Three Small Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2189","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-454 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour Kepler-454 b. That planet was first estimated to have a radius between the two common groups of small planets, the rocky ones and the lower-density, gas-rich ones (Kohler 2016). Only 12 Kepler planets between 1 and 2.7 Earth radii had precise masses measured at the time (Kohler 2016). Kepler-454 b belongs to the six larger ones, whose lower densities point to a thick envelope of water, hydrogen or helium (Kohler 2016). Little is known about Kepler-454 d itself.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler454d-Kohler2016","cite":"Kohler 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"Kepler-454b: Rocky or Not?","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-21 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The faint F8 subgiant host, magnitude 16, was watched by the CoRoT space telescope in the constellation Monoceros from October 2007 to March 2008 (Pätzold et al. 2012). Radial-velocity follow-up came mainly from the 10-metre Keck telescope in January 2010 (Pätzold et al. 2012). The planet has 2.26 Jupiter masses and 1.30 Jupiter radii on a circular orbit, and its density is very similar to Jupiter's (Pätzold et al. 2012). The host star is a subgiant of 1.29 solar masses and 1.95 solar radii (Pätzold et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot21b-Ptzoldetal2012","cite":"Pätzold et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Pätzold, M. Endl, Sz. Csizmadia et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXIII. CoRoT-21b: a doomed large Jupiter around a faint subgiant star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201118425","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2018-BLG-0977L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a systematic search of 2018 and 2019 KMTNet light curves from about 13 square degrees of sky, run by an automated program called AnomalyFinder (Hwang et al. 2022). The search found 11 planets with a low planet-to-host mass ratio, six of them new (Hwang et al. 2022).","observed":"This planet's signal came from a planetary caustic event, a bright spike made when the background star passes near the planet's own lensing region (Hwang et al. 2022). The other five new planets in that search were revealed by a subtle dip in the light curve, which earlier searches by eye had missed (Hwang et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"The host star is poorly known, because a microlensing event cannot be repeated."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2018blg0977lb-Hwangetal2022","cite":"Hwang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Kyu-Ha Hwang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. II. Six New q < 2 × 10<SUP>-4</SUP> Mass-ratio Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06686","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 23472 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet e has a radius of about 0.82 Earth radii and an estimated mass of 0.76 Earth masses (Barros et al. 2022). Its small size, a transit close in time to that of planet d, and a near resonance with d meant that it was found only recently (Barros et al. 2022). ESPRESSO radial-velocity measurements helped confirm it along with two other Earth-size planets in the system (Barros et al. 2022).","system":"Five planets circle this K dwarf in a compact system, with periods of about 3.98, 7.90, 12.16, 17.67 and 29.80 days (Barros et al. 2022). Two were confirmed earlier, and three are Earth-size (Barros et al. 2022). Two of the planets are potential super-Mercuries, meaning worlds with a large iron core (Barros et al. 2022).","history":"Planets in one system formed from the same disc, so comparing them helps test theories of how planets form and change (Barros et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd23472e-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"HD 23472: a multi-planetary system with three super-Earths and two potential super-Mercuries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1670 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer neighbour, the warm Jupiter TOI-1670 c. Planet b is a sub-Neptune of about 2.06 Earth radii on a 10.9-day orbit (Tran et al. 2022). Its signal was not found in the radial-velocity data, which limits its mass to under 0.13 Jupiter masses at 99% confidence (Tran et al. 2022).","system":"Planet c has about 0.987 Jupiter radii and 0.63 Jupiter masses and circles the star every 40.7 days on a nearly circular orbit (Tran et al. 2022). Its orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a tilt of -0.3 ± 2.2 degrees (Lubin et al. 2023).","history":"A warm Jupiter with an inner companion on a nearly circular orbit fits gentle formation, such as migration through the disc or growth in place, better than a violent high-eccentricity path (Tran et al. 2022). The alignment of planet c supports a calm history (Lubin et al. 2023).","open":"Planet b's mass is not measured."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1670b-Tranetal2022","cite":"Tran et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Quang H. Tran, Brendan P. Bowler, Michael Endl et al.","title":"TOI-1670 b and c: An Inner Sub-Neptune with an Outer Warm Jupiter Unlikely to Have Originated from High-eccentricity Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.04334","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1670b-Lubinetal2023","cite":"Lubin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jack Lubin, Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice et al.","title":"TOI-1670 c, a 40 day Orbital Period Warm Jupiter in a Compact System, Is Well Aligned","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16237","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-56 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a highly inflated hot Jupiter, with 1.7 Jupiter radii but only 0.6 Jupiter masses (Espinoza et al. 2019). Its F dwarf host is relatively bright, at V = 11.6, which makes the planet an excellent target for atmosphere studies (Espinoza et al. 2019). The star's radial velocities also show a significant quadratic trend, a slow drift over the years (Espinoza et al. 2019).","system":"The slow drift in the star's motion points to something else in the system, perhaps a more distant companion (Espinoza et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"What causes the slow drift in the star's motion is not yet settled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats56b-Espinozaetal2019","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Espinoza, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-54b-HATS-58Ab: Five New Transiting Hot Jupiters Including One with a Possible Temperate Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"17 Sco b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims to learn how planet populations change as the host star evolves off the main sequence (Tala Pinto et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet is detected through 12 years of precise radial velocities of the host star, a K giant (Tala Pinto et al. 2020). Its minimum mass is 4.3 Jupiter masses (Tala Pinto et al. 2020). More than 100 planets are known around K and G giants, and their properties differ considerably from those of planets around Sun-like and late-type main-sequence stars (Tala Pinto et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-17scob-Pintoetal2020","cite":"Pinto et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Marcelo Tala Pinto, Sabine Reffert, Andreas Quirrenbach et al.","title":"Precise radial velocities of giant stars. XIV. Evidence of planetary companions around HD 25723, 17 Sco, 3 Cnc, and 44 UMa","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202038285","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-133 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transits were first spotted by the WASP-South survey and confirmed with photometry from EulerCam on the Swiss Euler 1.2-metre telescope and from the TRAPPIST telescope, plus CORALIE spectroscopy (Maxted et al. 2016). The host star has an enhanced surface lithium abundance compared with other old G-type stars, and particularly other planet hosts (Maxted et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":"Why the star has so much lithium is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp133b-Maxtedetal2016","cite":"Maxted et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Five transiting hot Jupiters discovered using WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST: WASP-119 b, WASP-124 b, WASP-126 b, WASP-129 b, and WASP-133 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.01740","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2525 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its transits arrive hours early or late. The size of the shift is about 6 hours and was strong enough to confirm the planet (Trifonov et al. 2023). A dynamical model of those timing shifts, combined with a full light-curve fit and radial velocities from FEROS and PFS, gives a mass of 0.709 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.98 Jupiter radii (Trifonov et al. 2023).","system":"Planet c has an inner neighbour, planet b, with 0.088 Jupiter masses and 0.88 Jupiter radii and a period of 23.288 days (Trifonov et al. 2023). Planet c orbits every 49.260 days (Trifonov et al. 2023). The period ratio is close to 2:1, but simulations suggest the pair lies outside the resonance (Trifonov et al. 2023). The star is a K dwarf of 0.849 solar masses observed by TESS in 22 sectors (Trifonov et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2525c-Trifonovetal2023","cite":"Trifonov et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Trifon Trifonov, Rafael Brahm, Andrés Jordán et al.","title":"TOI-2525 b and c: A Pair of Massive Warm Giant Planets with Strong Transit Timing Variations Revealed by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet concerns the TRAPPIST-1 system as a whole. The Spitzer program Red Worlds spent more than 1000 hours on the star from February 2016 to October 2019 and analysed 188 transits (Ducrot et al. 2020). At least three of the seven planets orbit in the classical habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). Climate models have been run for TRAPPIST-1 d as a tidally locked world, one that always shows the same face to its star (Carone et al. 2018).","system":"The seven Earth-size planets appear to form a resonant chain, in which neighbouring orbits repeat in simple ratios (Teyssandier et al. 2022). All of them are in two-planet and three-planet resonances, apart from the innermost pair (Teyssandier et al. 2022). Their transit times shift in patterns that repeat over days to decades (Teyssandier et al. 2022). Studies of their interiors allow a wide range of possible water contents (Dorn et al. 2018).","history":"Computer simulations in which planets migrate through a disc can form a chain of resonances like the observed one, for particular disc conditions (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"The composition and atmosphere of planet d are not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1d-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1d-Caroneetal2018","cite":"Carone et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"L. Carone, R. Keppens, L. Decin et al.","title":"Stratosphere circulation on tidally locked ExoEarths","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.11446","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1d-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1d-Dornetal2018","cite":"Dorn et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Klaus Mosegaard, Simon L. Grimm et al.","title":"Interior Characterization in Multiplanetary Systems: TRAPPIST-1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.01803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 99492 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of five found from 4 to 6 years of Doppler measurements at the Keck Observatory (Marcy et al. 2005). It has the lowest minimum mass of the five (Marcy et al. 2005). All five host stars are metal-rich (Marcy et al. 2005). The work set a 1% false-alarm probability as the criterion for accepting candidate planets (Marcy et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd99492b-Marcyetal2005","cite":"Marcy et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"Five New Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/426384","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 720 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from HADES, a HARPS-N survey of northern M dwarfs that reaches the precision needed to detect planets of a few Earth masses (González-Álvarez et al. 2021).","observed":"Stellar activity can mimic a planet, so the team checked activity indicators and brightness measurements from APACHE, EXORAP, TESS, MEarth and SuperWASP to tell the star's variations from the planet's (González-Álvarez et al. 2021). The star is 15.56 parsecs away (González-Álvarez et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj720ab-Gonzlezlvarezetal2021","cite":"González-Álvarez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"E. González-Álvarez, A. Petralia, G. Micela et al.","title":"HADES RV Programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XIII. A sub-Neptune around the M dwarf GJ 720 A","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.09643","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-166 b":{"text":{"why":"A super-Neptune sitting this close to its star is rare, and it appears to be an object in the Neptune desert (Hellier et al. 2019). The Neptune desert is a lack of Neptune-sized planets on close orbits. Despite its close orbit, the planet kept its atmosphere, which makes it a benchmark for atmosphere studies in the desert (Lafarga et al. 2023).","observed":"JWST spectra detected water vapour at 15.2 sigma and carbon dioxide at 14.7 sigma, the first detection of carbon dioxide on this planet (Mayo et al. 2025). Possible hints of ammonia (2.3 sigma) and a cloud layer at intermediate pressure (2.6 sigma) were also found (Mayo et al. 2025). The atmosphere is richer in heavy elements than the star (Mayo et al. 2025). From the ground, ESPRESSO tentatively saw water shifted by about 5 km/s toward the observer, with a low water amount or high clouds (Lafarga et al. 2023). The planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Hellier et al. 2019). Archival JWST data are being searched for escaping helium, in the first such study of this planet (Bell & Dos Santos 2026).","system":"The host is a bright F9 star of V = 9.36, with a rotation period of about 12 days whose magnetic activity affects radial-velocity measurements (Hellier et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere is escaping is not yet known (Bell & Dos Santos 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp166b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"WASP-166b: a bloated super-Neptune transiting a V = 9 star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.05292","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp166b-Lafargaetal2023","cite":"Lafarga et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Lafarga, M. Brogi, S. Gandhi et al.","title":"The hot Neptune WASP-166 b with ESPRESSO - III. A blue-shifted tentative water signal constrains the presence of clouds","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04794","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp166b-Mayoetal2025","cite":"Mayo et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Andrew W. Mayo, Charles D. Fortenbach, Dana R. Louie et al.","title":"Detection of H<SUB>2</SUB>O and CO<SUB>2</SUB> in the Atmosphere of the Hot Super-Neptune WASP-166b with JWST","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00609","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp166b-BellDosSantos2026","cite":"Bell & Dos Santos 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Aislyn Bell, Leonardo Dos Santos","title":"First Look from The Unintentional JWST He* Escape Survey (TUNES): WASP-166b","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 83443 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from four instruments over more than two decades show a giant planet with a minimum mass of 1.35 Jupiter masses on a 22-year orbit with eccentricity 0.76 and a distance of 8.0 au (Errico et al. 2022). Combining these with Gaia and Hipparcos measurements of the star's motion gives a true mass of about 1.5 Jupiter masses (Errico et al. 2022).","system":"The system also hosts a hot Jupiter, a rare combination with an outer companion (Errico et al. 2022). Simulations show stable regions where more planets could survive, including some in the habitable zone of the star (Errico et al. 2022).","history":"The outer planet's high eccentricity suggests that a scattering event may have flung the hot Jupiter into its close orbit and left planet c on a wide, eccentric path (Errico et al. 2022).","open":"Whether further planets exist in the stable regions is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd83443c-Erricoetal2022","cite":"Errico et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adriana Errico, Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jonathan Horner et al.","title":"HD 83443c: A Highly Eccentric Giant Planet on a 22 yr Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.05711","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106315 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble's WFC3 camera detected water vapour in its atmosphere, with an abundance of about 10 to the power -2.1 and a significance of 5.68 sigma (Guilluy et al. 2021). There is a possible hint of ammonia, but at under 2 sigma it is not significant (Guilluy et al. 2021). Other studies conclude that cloud-free or low-metallicity atmospheres are ruled out for it (Pelaez-Torres et al. 2025).","system":"The star hosts a sub-Neptune on a 9.5-day orbit and this Neptune on a 21-day orbit (Rodriguez et al. 2018). The two are near a 2:1 period ratio (Huang et al. 2020). The star is bright and rotates unusually slowly, so planet c is among the best small planets for measuring how its orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"Simulations show that both planets could have started near 0.4 and 0.8 au and migrated to their present places, ending near the 2:1 resonance (Huang et al. 2020). A misaligned orbit would suggest a more violent migration history (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106315c-Guilluyetal2021","cite":"Guilluy et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Gloria Guilluy, Amélie Gressier, Sam Wright et al.","title":"ARES IV: Probing the Atmospheres of the Two Warm Small Planets HD 106315c and HD 3167c with the HST/WFC3 Camera","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03221","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106315c-PelaezTorresetal2025","cite":"Pelaez-Torres et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Pelaez-Torres, A. Sanchez-Lopez, M. Lopez-Puertas et al.","title":"Tightest Constraints to Date on the Cloudy Atmosphere of GJ 436 B with Carmenes","journal":"Proceedings of the VIII Iberian Congress on Planetary Sciences and Solar System Exploration","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106315c-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Andrew Vanderburg, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"HD 106315 and GJ 9827: Understanding the Formation and Evolution of Small Planets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106315c-Huangetal2020","cite":"Huang et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"X. M. Huang, J. H. Ji, Y. Dong","title":"Near 2:1 Mean Motion Resonance Capture and Orbital Evolution of A Hot-Neptune System: HD 106315","journal":"Acta Astronomica Sinica","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 111591 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from two search programs for planets around giant stars: one at the Bohyunsan observatory in Korea monitoring 55 early K giants, and one at Okayama in Japan monitoring 190 G and K giants (Jeong et al. 2018).","observed":"Long-term radial-velocity changes of the K giant host led to the conclusion that a companion is the most likely cause (Jeong et al. 2018). The orbit has a period of 1056.4 days, a distance of 2.5 au and a minimum mass of 4.4 Jupiter masses (Jeong et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd111591b-Jeongetal2018","cite":"Jeong et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"G. Jeong, B.-C. Lee, I. Han et al.","title":"Detection of planet candidates around K giants. HD 40956, HD 111591, and HD 113996","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.07173","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 175167 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 7.8 Jupiter masses and an eccentric orbit of 0.54, around a metal-rich G5 star (Arriagada et al. 2010). A joint study of Gaia's astrometry and Doppler data suggested a companion mass of 14.8 Jupiter masses, which would make it a brown dwarf (Gan 2023). Adding more radial-velocity data lowers this to 10.2 Jupiter masses, with a period of 1275.8 days and an orbit tilted 38.6 degrees from edge-on (Gan 2023). The Gaia and Doppler solutions disagree somewhat for this star (Winn 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a brown dwarf depends on its exact mass. The Gaia and Doppler data disagree for this star, possibly because of how the Gaia uncertainties were treated (Winn 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd175167b-Arriagadaetal2010","cite":"Arriagada et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Pamela Arriagada, R. Paul Butler, Dante Minniti et al.","title":"Five Long-period Extrasolar Planets in Eccentric Orbits from the Magellan Planet Search Program","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4093","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd175167b-Gan2023","cite":"Gan 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Tianjun Gan","title":"Gaia Astrometry and MIKE+PFS Doppler Data Joint Analysis Reveals that HD 175167b is a Massive Cold Jupiter","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14568","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd175167b-Winn2022","cite":"Winn 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Joshua N. Winn","title":"Joint Constraints on Exoplanetary Orbits from Gaia DR3 and Doppler Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.05516","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4773 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the MEEP survey, which aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters brighter than G = 12.5 to measure how often different migration paths occur (Schulte et al. 2025).","observed":"TOI-4773 b is one of four new super-Jupiters, each more than five times the mass of Jupiter (Schulte et al. 2025). Its host star shows lithium absorption in its spectrum (Schulte et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"How hot Jupiters form and evolve is not well known, and lithium-rich host stars may bear on that question (Schulte et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4773b-Schulteetal2025","cite":"Schulte et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). II. Super-Jupiters and Lithium-rich host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02666","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-424 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters usually have no nearby companion planets and are called lonely, possibly because of a violent past (Endl et al. 2014).","observed":"This hot Jupiter is accompanied by a more massive planet, Kepler-424 c, on an eccentric orbit of 223 days with e = 0.3 (Endl et al. 2014). The outer planet does not transit. Its mass and orbit, and the mass of the hot Jupiter, come from radial velocities taken with the High Resolution Spectrograph on the Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Endl et al. 2014).","system":"Kepler-424 resembles upsilon Andromedae, another system with a hot Jupiter in a multiple-planet system (Endl et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler424b-Endletal2014","cite":"Endl et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Michael Endl, Douglas A. Caldwell, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Kepler-424 b: A \"Lonely\" Hot Jupiter that Found a Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8156","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"IRAS 04125+2902 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star, also called TIDYE-1, is about 3 million years old, and hosts the youngest known transiting planet (Mori et al. 2026). The planet is Jupiter-sized and orbits edge-on, while the surrounding disc looks closer to face-on, at about 30 degrees (Mori et al. 2026). Two transits seen with Subaru's IRD instrument give a spin-orbit angle of -20 degrees, with a spread of +13 and -18 degrees, so the orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Mori et al. 2026). Monitoring for transit timing shifts was also carried out (Mori et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Alignment of planet and star supports the idea that the outer disc is the part that is misaligned with the inner system (Mori et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-iras041252902b-Morietal2026","cite":"Mori et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mayuko Mori, Teruyuki Hirano, Kosei Usami et al.","title":"Quiet Dynamical History of the Youngest Transiting Planetary System","journal":"23rd Cambridge Workshop on Cool Stars, Stellar Systems, and the Sun","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20520527","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TIC 46432937 b":{"text":{"why":"Most short-period giant planets around M dwarfs may form by high-eccentricity migration, in which a massive companion drives a planet onto a stretched orbit that later shrinks (Weisserman et al. 2026). Most systems, however, lack a known companion that could drive it (Weisserman et al. 2026).","observed":"The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a small distortion in the star's radial velocity as the planet crosses it, was measured with Gemini-North's MAROON-X spectrograph (Weisserman et al. 2026). It shows a sky-projected obliquity, the angle between the orbit and the star's spin, of 3 degrees with a spread of +4 and -3, so the orbit is well aligned (Weisserman et al. 2026). Archival data rule out massive companions between about 1 and 10 au (Weisserman et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"A well-aligned orbit and no massive companions argue against high-eccentricity migration for this planet (Weisserman et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tic46432937b-Weissermanetal2026","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Drew Weisserman, Ryan Cloutier, Alexandra Rochon et al.","title":"New and Updated Rossiter-McLaughlin Measurements for Three Hot Jupiter-Hosting M Dwarfs","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03031","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5646 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies in the Neptune desert, a region of orbital periods shorter than about 3.2 days where Neptune-sized planets are scarce (Mancini et al. 2026). It orbits the largest host star found so far for a desert planet (Mancini et al. 2026).","observed":"HARPS-N radial velocities confirmed the TESS candidate (Mancini et al. 2026). The planet has a mass of 45.9 Earth masses, a radius of 6.6 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of 1935 K (Mancini et al. 2026). Its orbit is compatible with a circle (Mancini et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a metal-rich F8IV-V subgiant with an effective temperature of 6136 K (Mancini et al. 2026).","history":"How planets end up in the desert, the ridge or the savanna is still poorly understood (Mancini et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5646b-Mancinietal2026","cite":"Mancini et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"L. Mancini, F. Manni, D. Kubyshkina et al.","title":"The Hot Neptune Initiative (HONEI): III. An ultra-hot super Neptune orbiting the metal-rich subgiant star TOI-5646: the largest host for a desert dweller","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24311","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1408 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet c is a sub-Neptune of 2.22 Earth radii and 7.6 Earth masses on a 2.2-day orbit, inside the orbit of the hot Jupiter TOI-1408 b (Korth et al. 2024). Its transits arrive early or late by 15% of its orbital period, the largest such shift relative to the orbit measured to date (Korth et al. 2024). The inner planet's size and mass come from a photodynamical model, which fits the timing shifts, light curves and radial velocities together (Korth et al. 2024).","system":"The hot Jupiter has 1.86 Jupiter masses on a 4.42-day orbit and shows grazing transits (Korth et al. 2024). The two planets are near a 2:1 period ratio, and the inner planet's resonant angle librates, swinging back and forth (Korth et al. 2024). Radial velocities support a third body with a period of several thousand days (Korth et al. 2024). Hot Jupiters with an inner low-mass planet are rare (Korth et al. 2024). The hot Jupiter's radius is poorly constrained, from about 1.2 to 3.5 Jupiter radii, because of the near-tangential transit (Baluev 2026). The star is metal-rich, with a strong lithium excess of +1.46 dex (Galazutdinov et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The hot Jupiter's radius is not well pinned down."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1408c-Korthetal2024","cite":"Korth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Judith Korth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Hannu Parviainen et al.","title":"TOI-1408: Discovery and Photodynamical Modeling of a Small Inner Companion to a Hot Jupiter Revealed by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.17798","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1408c-Baluev2026","cite":"Baluev 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Roman V. Baluev","title":"TOI-1408: the limb darkening models, photometric and Doppler noises, and the exoplanetary system","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02434","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1408c-Galazutdinovetal2026","cite":"Galazutdinov et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. A. Galazutdinov, S. A. Korotin, D. B. Poklad et al.","title":"TOI-1408: a metal-rich star with lithium overabundance hosting a multiplanet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag1918","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 96700 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was watched with the HARPS spectrograph for more than 10 years with fairly dense sampling, which made the survey sensitive to super-Earths on orbits of up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021). The study covered five Sun-like stars and found 12 super-Earths in total (Unger et al. 2021). Statistical model comparison was used to decide how many planets each star really has, and some stars were also tested with different models of stellar activity noise to separate planet signals from starspot effects (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd96700b-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-160 B b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned up this planet was a search for hot giant planets (Lendl et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet has about 0.28 Jupiter masses and a radius of about 1.09 Jupiter radii, almost the same as its relative WASP-147b, but it is less strongly heated by its star (Lendl et al. 2019). It is a good target for transmission spectroscopy, where starlight filtering through its atmosphere during transit is analysed (Lendl et al. 2019). Updated planet properties were later worked out from new observations (Jofré et al. 2021). Searches of TESS space-telescope data limit what other transiting planets could be present around the star (Jofré et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a metal-rich K star that has a near equal-mass partner star 28.5 arcsec away on the sky (Lendl et al. 2019). No planet has been reported around that partner, WASP-160A (Jofré et al. 2021). Detailed chemical measurements of 25 elements show the planet's star has fewer volatile elements and more refractory (rock-forming) elements than its partner (Jofré et al. 2021). Only one other pair of stars with planets, WASP-94, was known to show this pattern (Jofré et al. 2021).","history":"The chemical difference between the two stars may be a fingerprint of how planets formed and evolved around one of them (Jofré et al. 2021).","open":"Why the two stars differ in chemistry is still debated, and several formation and evolution explanations remain possible (Jofré et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp160bb-Lendletal2019","cite":"Lendl et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Lendl, D. R. Anderson, A. Bonfanti et al.","title":"WASP-147b, 160Bb, 164b, and 165b: two hot Saturns and two Jupiters, including two planets with metal-rich hosts","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06973","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp160bb-Jofretal2021","cite":"Jofré et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Emiliano Jofré, Romina Petrucci, Yilen Gómez Maqueo Chew et al.","title":"The Peculiar Chemical Pattern of the WASP-160 Binary System: Signatures of Planetary Formation and Evolution?","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04590","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TYC 8998-760-1 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about the atmospheres in this system concerns its neighbour planet c. High-resolution spectra from the CRIRES+ instrument were used to measure the chemical makeup and spin of both super-Jupiters in the system (Zhang et al. 2024). Planet c was observed with the JWST space telescope, giving the first direct detection of a cloud species in an exoplanet atmosphere: a silicate absorption feature between 9 and 11 microns (Hoch et al. 2025). Planet c is about 6 Jupiter masses and orbits a Sun-like star about 17 million years old (Hoch et al. 2025).","system":"Two super-Jupiters orbit the same Sun-like star, which allows a uniform comparison of how giant planets form and evolve (Zhang et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tyc89987601b-Zhangetal2024","cite":"Zhang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Yapeng Zhang, Wenhao Xuan, Ignas Snellen et al.","title":"Atmospheric characterization of super-Jovian exoplanets through the high-res eyes of CRIRES and KPIC","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tyc89987601b-Hochetal2025","cite":"Hoch et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Kielan Hoch, Melanie Rowland, Simon Petrus et al.","title":"Direct Detection of Silicate Clouds in a Multiplanet System around a Sunlike Star","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-88 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planets b and c. Planet b, with a period of about 10.92 days, shows transit timing variations of about 12 hours, which earned it the nickname king of transit variations (Barros et al. 2014). Those timing shifts revealed a non-transiting companion, planet c, with about 0.63 Jupiter masses (Nesvorný et al. 2013). Radial velocity observations later confirmed planet c, the first such confirmation of a non-transiting planet found from transit timing (Barros et al. 2014).","system":"Planets b and c have a period ratio of 2.03, just wide of the 2:1 orbital resonance (Nesvorný et al. 2013). Planet b is Neptune-class (Nesvorný et al. 2013).","history":"The near-resonant orbits of planets b and c can be used to test theories of how near-resonant planet pairs form (Nesvorný et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler88d-Barrosetal2014","cite":"Barros et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, R. F. Díaz, A. Santerne et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. X. KOI-142 c: first radial velocity confirmation of a non-transiting exoplanet discovered by transit timing","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4335","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler88d-Nesvornetal2013","cite":"Nesvorný et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"David Nesvorný, David Kipping, Dirk Terrell et al.","title":"KOI-142, The King of Transit Variations, is a Pair of Planets near the 2:1 Resonance","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4283","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about 13 Jupiter masses, close to the deuterium-burning limit of 13 Jupiter masses that is proposed as a boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Johns-Krull et al. 2008). It is one of the most massive planets known with an orbital period under 10 days, and its orbit is unusually eccentric for such a short period, with an eccentricity of 0.26 (Johns-Krull et al. 2008). Its radius of about 1.95 Jupiter radii is best explained if tidal heating from the eccentric orbit is included (Liu et al. 2008). A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement, which tracks the transit across the star's spinning surface, shows the orbit is misaligned with the star's spin (Rusznak et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an F5 star with a fast spin, about 18.5 km/s along the line of sight, and somewhat low in metals (Johns-Krull et al. 2008). It is the only known system with a high planet-to-star mass ratio and a misaligned orbit (Rusznak et al. 2025). An unusually large positional error in Gaia data hints that an undetected companion star may be present (Rusznak et al. 2025).","history":"Heavy planets around hot stars are usually aligned with the star's spin, so the misalignment here is an outlier (Rusznak et al. 2025). A hidden companion star might have helped tilt the orbit (Rusznak et al. 2025).","open":"Whether XO-3 b is a planet or a brown dwarf cannot be settled from the observations (Johns-Krull et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo3b-JohnsKrulletal2008","cite":"Johns-Krull et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Christopher M. Johns-Krull, Peter R. McCullough, Christopher J. Burke et al.","title":"XO-3b: A Massive Planet in an Eccentric Orbit Transiting an F5 V Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0712.4283","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo3b-Liuetal2008","cite":"Liu et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Xin Liu, Adam Burrows, Laurent Ibgui","title":"Theoretical Radii of Extrasolar Giant Planets: The Cases of TrES-4, XO-3b, and HAT-P-1b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.1733","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo3b-Rusznaketal2025","cite":"Rusznak et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jace Rusznak, Xian-Yu Wang, Malena Rice et al.","title":"From Misaligned Sub-Saturns to Aligned Brown Dwarfs: The Highest M<SUB>p</SUB>/M<SUB>*</SUB> Systems Exhibit Low Obliquities, Even around Hot Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.04438","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":26,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5007 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from MANGOS, a programme that searches for giant planets, brown dwarfs and low-mass stars around M dwarfs (Dransfield et al. 2026). Giant planets on short orbits around low-mass stars are hard to explain with current core accretion models, and more examples are needed to overcome small-number statistics (Dransfield et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-5007 b is one of five giant planets announced together, all with radii of 0.99 to 1.12 Jupiter radii and orbital periods of 1.43 to 2.91 days (Dransfield et al. 2026). The planets were first spotted by TESS and confirmed with ground-based photometry and spectroscopy (Dransfield et al. 2026). A small but significant orbital eccentricity was detected for TOI-5007 b (Dransfield et al. 2026). The five planets are well suited to follow-up measurements of their orbit tilt relative to the star and of their atmospheres (Dransfield et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"How giant planets form around such low-mass stars is not settled (Dransfield et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5007b-Dransfieldetal2026","cite":"Dransfield et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Dransfield, M. Timmermans, D. Sebastian et al.","title":"MANGOS ─ II. Five new giant planets orbiting low-mass stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-411 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transit timing variations, small shifts in when the planets cross the star, gave a mass of 25.6 Earth masses for this planet (Sun et al. 2019). The same method confirmed planets c and d and revealed a non-transiting planet e on a 31.5-day orbit that strongly interacts with planet d (Sun et al. 2019). Planets c and d have low densities of 1.71 and 2.32 grams per cubic centimetre (Sun et al. 2019).","system":"The host star is an active K dwarf with a rotation period of 10.52 days (Araújo & Valio 2021). Tracking starspots hidden by transiting planets revealed 198 spots, with an average radius of about 17,000 km and a temperature of about 3800 K (Araújo & Valio 2021). The star produced 65 superflares lasting 8 to 260 minutes, and larger spot areas go with more energetic flares (Araújo & Valio 2021). Its equator spins faster than its higher latitudes, as on the Sun, and the planets cross the star at latitudes of -11, -21 and -49 degrees (Araújo & Valio 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler411b-Sunetal2019","cite":"Sun et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Sun, P. Ioannidis, S. Gu et al.","title":"Kepler-411: a four-planet system with an active host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.09719","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler411b-ArajoValio2021","cite":"Araújo & Valio 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alexandre Araújo, Adriana Valio","title":"Kepler-411 Differential Rotation from Three Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.07692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 246851721 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler tomography, which follows the planet's shadow across the spinning star, shows the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with a projected tilt of about -1.5 degrees (Yu et al. 2018). Starspot-driven brightness changes give the star's rotation period, which converts this into a true tilt of about 3.7 degrees (Yu et al. 2018). The planet has about 3 Jupiter masses and a radius of about 1.05 Jupiter radii (Yu et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a rapidly rotating F dwarf, moving at about 75 km/s along the line of sight, and the fourth hottest star known to host a Jovian planet on an orbit longer than 5 days with a measured tilt (Yu et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic246851721b-Yuetal2018","cite":"Yu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Liang Yu, George Zhou, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"EPIC 246851721 b: A Tropical Jupiter Transiting a Rapidly Rotating Star in a Well-aligned Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.10298","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-434 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has been studied through the small brightness changes of its system over each orbit, which are unusually smooth and weak for a planet this far from its star (Dittmann et al. 2020). The total light from the system rises by about 32 ppm (parts per million) when the planet is closer than 0.1147 astronomical units to its star, and takes about 7.7 hours to change state (Dittmann et al. 2020). Earlier work found a rise of about 30 ppm near the point where the planet's equilibrium temperature is 1000 K (Dittmann et al. 2019).","system":"The planet follows an eccentric orbit around a Sun-like star, so the heat it receives changes strongly through each orbit (Dittmann et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Model atmospheres suggest the upper atmosphere crosses the condensation point of potassium chloride and sodium chloride, so clouds may form and dissipate along the orbit (Dittmann et al. 2020). Warm Jupiters on 10 to 20 day orbits have temperatures near the brown dwarf transition between cloud-covered and clear atmospheres, where brown dwarfs vary most (Dittmann 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler434b-Dittmannetal2020","cite":"Dittmann et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Dittmann, P. Gao, J. Faherty et al.","title":"Detection of dynamic cloud formation in the atmosphere of an extrasolar planet","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler434b-Dittmannetal2019","cite":"Dittmann et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jason Dittmann, Jacqueline Faherty, Daniella Bardalez Gagliuffi et al.","title":"Detecting analogues of the L-T transition in eccentric warm Jupiters.","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler434b-Dittmann2024","cite":"Dittmann 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jason A. Dittmann","title":"Detecting Signs of Dynamic Cloud Formation in Eccentric Warm Jupiters in Kepler","journal":"NASA XRP proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-444 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star was dated with asteroseismology, which reads the star's vibrations to measure its age, and comes out at 11.2 billion years, with an uncertainty of 1.0 billion years (Campante et al. 2015). The star belongs to the old Galactic thick disk and is metal-poor (Campante et al. 2015). Five planets orbit it, with sizes between Mercury and Venus, making this the oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets (Campante et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"The star formed when the Universe was less than 20 percent of its present age, so Earth-size planets have been forming for most of cosmic history (Campante et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler444e-Campanteetal2015","cite":"Campante et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"T. L. Campante, T. Barclay, J. J. Swift et al.","title":"KOI-3158: The oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07869","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 357 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner transiting neighbour GJ 357 b. Planet c shows up only as a 9.12-day signal in radial velocity data from several spectrographs, with a minimum mass of about 3.4 Earth masses (Luque et al. 2019). TESS data show that planets c and d do not transit, so their true masses may be higher than the minimum values (Kane & Fetherolf 2023).","system":"The three planets orbit a nearby M2.5 dwarf (Luque et al. 2019). Planet c receives about 4.4 times the radiation Earth gets from the Sun (Kaltenegger et al. 2019). The outer planet d lies in the habitable zone, but a small eccentricity for planet d would make most of that zone unstable for an Earth-mass planet (Kane & Fetherolf 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj357c-Luqueetal2019","cite":"Luque et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"R. Luque, E. Pallé, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"Planetary system around the nearby M dwarf GJ 357 including a transiting, hot, Earth-sized planet optimal for atmospheric characterization","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.12818","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357c-KaneFetherolf2023","cite":"Kane & Fetherolf 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Tara Fetherolf","title":"GJ 357 d: Potentially Habitable World or Agent of Chaos?","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02327","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj357c-Kalteneggeretal2019","cite":"Kaltenegger et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Kaltenegger, J. Madden, Z. Lin et al.","title":"The Habitability of GJ 357D: Possible Climate and Observability","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.13215","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 107773 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied the star's surroundings. Infrared imaging with the SPHERE instrument on the Very Large Telescope found a stellar companion only 0.38 arcsec from the star, about 40 astronomical units apart in projection (Ginski et al. 2021). The companion is among the closest ever found around a planet host star, and explains the steady drift seen in the star's radial velocities (Ginski et al. 2021).","system":"A star this close probably had a strong influence on the planet-forming disc around the main star (Ginski et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip107773b-Ginskietal2021","cite":"Ginski et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Ginski, M. Mugrauer, C. Adam et al.","title":"How many suns are in the sky? A SPHERE multiplicity survey of exoplanet host stars. I. Four new close stellar companions including a white dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10363","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Proxima Cen b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known comes from studies of its star and from models of the planet's air. Proxima Centauri is a dM5.5e red dwarf thought to be fully convective, yet it shows a possible activity cycle of about 442 days (Cincunegui et al. 2007). Its calcium emission varies by about 130 percent outside flares over that cycle (Cincunegui et al. 2007). Computer models of the planet suggest its atmosphere could be stripped away: ion escape rates would be about two orders of magnitude higher than for the rocky planets of the Solar System if the planet has no magnetic field (Dong et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"A magnetic field of its own would lower the loss rate but not to Solar System values, and most of the planet's relevant properties remain wholly or partly unknown (Dong et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-proximacenb-Cincuneguietal2007","cite":"Cincunegui et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"C. Cincunegui, R. F. Díaz, P. J. D. Mauas","title":"A possible activity cycle in <ASTROBJ>Proxima Centauri</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703514","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-proximacenb-Dongetal2017","cite":"Dong et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Chuanfei Dong, Manasvi Lingam, Yingjuan Ma et al.","title":"Is Proxima Centauri b Habitable? A Study of Atmospheric Loss","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.04089","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2567 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was reported as part of the TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey, an effort to build a large, uniform sample of hot Jupiters for studying their origins (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-2567 b is one of 10 new hot Jupiters announced together, all confirmed with ground-based time-series photometry, high-resolution imaging and high-resolution spectroscopy after TESS flagged them (Yee et al. 2022). The ten planets orbit F and G stars with periods of 2 to 10 days and masses of 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiter masses (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Many questions about the origin of hot Jupiters remain (Yee et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2567b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-19 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its larger planets b and c. K2-19 was the first multi-planet system found by the K2 mission to show transit timing variations (Barros et al. 2016). Planets b and c lie very close to a 3:2 orbital resonance (Barros et al. 2016). K2 watched the star from May to August 2014, Spitzer measured two more transits of each planet, and 51 Keck spectra taken from 2015 to 2017 gave radial velocities (Petigura et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k219d-Barrosetal2016","cite":"Barros et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, J. M. Almenara, O. Demangeon et al.","title":"K2-19, The first K2 muti-planetary system showing TTVs","journal":"IAU Focus Meeting","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921316002386","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k219d-Petiguraetal2020","cite":"Petigura et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. A. Petigura, J. Livingston, K. Batygin et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2-19b and c transit times and radial velocities (Petigura+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51590002","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4010 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its three transiting inner planets. TOI-4010 e is a companion with more than a Jupiter mass found through radial velocity observations, on a long, eccentric orbit of about 762 days and eccentricity about 0.26 (Kunimoto et al. 2023). These values are based on the observations available so far (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","system":"The three inner planets are TOI-4010 b, a sub-Neptune of about 11 Earth masses in the hot-Neptune desert, and the two sub-Saturns c and d of about 20 and 38 Earth masses (Kunimoto et al. 2023). The star is a metal-rich K dwarf, and few systems with multiple short-period sub-Saturns are known (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Planet b is one of the few hot-Neptune desert planets with known companions (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4010e-Kunimotoetal2023","cite":"Kunimoto et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michelle Kunimoto, Andrew Vanderburg, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-4010: A System of Three Large Short-period Planets with a Massive Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05308","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"61 Vir c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known comes from computer studies of how the planets in the system interact over long times. Secular theory, which averages out fast orbital motions to follow slow changes, reproduces the behaviour seen in full numerical simulations even though the orbital eccentricities are quite large (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010). The long axes of the two outer planets' orbits swing back and forth around a mutual alignment (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010).","system":"","history":"The alignment is the most likely outcome given the planets' masses and spacing, so it does not show that tides have reshaped the orbits (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-61virc-VanLaerhovenGreenberg2010","cite":"Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Christa Van Laerhoven, R. Greenberg","title":"Secular Behavior In Multi-planet Systems: 61 Virginis, 55 Cancri, Etc.","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #42","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-776 c":{"text":{"why":"Sitting close to the size gap seen for planets around small red stars, TOI-776 c helps test how that gap forms (Luque et al. 2021).","observed":"Transits seen by a space telescope were combined with velocity measurements from the HARPS spectrograph and ground-based transit follow-up. Together they gave a mass and radius whose density allows a wide range of interiors and atmospheres, though the planet has kept a significant atmosphere (Luque et al. 2021). Later Hubble observations found a giant cloud of atomic hydrogen escaping from both TOI-776 c and its inner sibling (Loyd et al. 2024). From the escape rates, the atmospheres look hydrogen-dominated rather than water-dominated, likely wrapped around iron and rock (Loyd et al. 2024). TOI-776 c is expected to stay a sub-Neptune rather than shrink to a bare rocky core (Loyd et al. 2024).","system":"The star is a bright M dwarf with a second transiting planet, TOI-776 b, on an 8.25-day orbit (Luque et al. 2021). Star spots carry a signal near 34 days in the velocity data, matching the star's spin (Luque et al. 2021).","history":"Hydrogen-dominated atmospheres on planets of this size challenge common formation models (Loyd et al. 2024). They support the idea that most planets smaller than Neptune on orbits under 100 days are mostly iron and rock (Loyd et al. 2024).","open":"Whether such planets are gas-covered rocky worlds or water-rich is still debated. TOI-776 c is one of eight M-dwarf sub-Neptunes with a JWST spectrum used to probe atmosphere weight (Pagés Navarrete 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi776c-Luqueetal2021","cite":"Luque et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"R. Luque","title":"TOI-776: a multi-planetary system around a bright M dwarf optimal for JWST","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi776c-Loydetal2024","cite":"Loyd et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Parke Loyd, Ethan Schreyer, James E. Owen et al.","title":"Observed hydrogen escape from two sibling ~2 R⊕ exoplanets, TOI-776 b & c","journal":"The Astrobiology Science Conference (AbSciCon) 2024","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi776c-Navarrete2026","cite":"Navarrete 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Juan F. Pagés Navarrete","title":"Beyond gas dwarfs and water worlds: hybrid envelopes among the M-dwarf sub-Neptunes characterised with JWST","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34635","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2583 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of 20 hot Jupiters first flagged as transit candidates in TESS data for a survey of such planets (Yee et al. 2023). Its star was picked for faster, 2-minute observations during TESS's first extended mission (Yee et al. 2023). Older ground-based WASP survey data covering 2004 to 2010 also show transits at the same period as TESS (Yee et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2583ab-Yeeetal2023","cite":"Yee et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"S. W. Yee, J. N. Winn, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Surveys. II. (Yee+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6884 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Despite the planet label, TOI-6884 b is a low-mass brown dwarf, an object heavier than any planet but too light to be a star, with a mass of 26.32 Jupiter masses (Khandelwal et al. 2026). TESS light curves first suggested an orbit of about 14.42 days. Ground-based velocity measurements and repeated brightness monitoring showed that value to be a false harmonic, and the true period is 4.808264 days (Khandelwal et al. 2026). Its radius is 0.927 Jupiter radii and its orbit is nearly circular (Khandelwal et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a late F-type star that has begun to evolve off the main sequence, with a mass of 1.41 Suns (Khandelwal et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6884b-Khandelwaletal2026","cite":"Khandelwal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Akanksha Khandelwal, Shubhendra Nath Das, Rishikesh Sharma et al.","title":"TOI-6884b: a low-mass brown dwarf transiting a slightly evolved star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29666","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TrES-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"At discovery, TrES-4 b had the largest radius and lowest density of any known transiting planet, with 1.674 Jupiter radii but only 0.84 Jupiter masses (Mandushev et al. 2007). Velocity measurements ruled out a blend with an eclipsing pair of stars as the cause of the dips (Mandushev et al. 2007). Brightness measurements in four colour bands in 2017 gave a first hint of a cloudy, opaque atmosphere (McGough et al. 2018). Infrared dayside data fit equally well with and without a thermal inversion, a layer where air gets hotter with height (Madhusudhan & Seager 2010).","system":"The host star is much larger than the Sun, at 1.738 solar radii (Mandushev et al. 2007). Statistical modelling suggests the star's spin axis may be tilted along the line of sight relative to the planet's orbit (Schlaufman 2010).","history":"The puffed-up size challenged models of hot Jupiter structure (Mandushev et al. 2007). One proposed explanation is electric currents driven by atmospheric winds, which could heat the interior enough to keep the radius inflated (Batygin & Stevenson 2010).","open":"The cause of the inflated radius, the presence of a temperature inversion and the cloud cover are all unsettled."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tres4b-Mandushevetal2007","cite":"Mandushev et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Georgi Mandushev, Francis T. O'Donovan, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"TrES-4: A Transiting Hot Jupiter of Very Low Density","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.0834","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres4b-McGoughetal2018","cite":"McGough et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Aylin Marie McGough, Jackson Lane Cole, Cristilyn N. Gardner et al.","title":"Characterizing Giant Exoplanets through Multi-wavelength Transit Observations: TrES-4b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres4b-MadhusudhanSeager2010","cite":"Madhusudhan & Seager 2010","year":"2010","authors":"N. Madhusudhan, S. Seager","title":"On the Inference of Thermal Inversions in Hot Jupiter Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.4585","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres4b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres4b-BatyginStevenson2010","cite":"Batygin & Stevenson 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Konstantin Batygin, David J. Stevenson","title":"Inflating Hot Jupiters with Ohmic Dissipation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.3650","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 91258 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up was a search for planets around northern solar-type stars (Moutou et al. 2014).","observed":"Velocity measurements show a planet with a minimum mass of 1.068 Jupiter masses and a 5.0505-day orbit (Moutou et al. 2014). The velocities also show a slow quadratic drift, which points to a more distant companion, either a planet or a star, that has not yet been characterised (Moutou et al. 2014). The host is a G5V main-sequence star with moderate activity (Moutou et al. 2014).","system":"The system is likely to hold at least one more body orbiting farther out (Moutou et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"The nature of the outer companion is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip91258b-Moutouetal2014","cite":"Moutou et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"C. Moutou, G. Hébrard, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. VI. Three new hot Jupiters in multi-planet extrasolar systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.5271","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-444 b":{"text":{"why":"The system became notable as a test of whether small planets could form early in cosmic history, since small planets can form around stars with few heavy elements (Campante et al. 2015).","observed":"Kepler spacecraft observations show five planets with sizes between Mercury and Venus around a metal-poor Sun-like star from the old thick disk of the Galaxy (Campante et al. 2015). Asteroseismology, which reads a star's age from its sound-wave vibrations, gives the host an age of 11.2 billion years (Campante et al. 2015). Kepler-444 b belongs to the oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets (Campante et al. 2015).","system":"The five planets are all smaller than Venus (Campante et al. 2015).","history":"The star formed when the Universe was less than 20 percent of its present age, so Earth-size planets have formed through most of cosmic history (Campante et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler444b-Campanteetal2015","cite":"Campante et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"T. L. Campante, T. Barclay, J. J. Swift et al.","title":"KOI-3158: The oldest known system of terrestrial-size planets","journal":"European Physical Journal Web of Conferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.07869","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28471 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Nineteen years of HARPS velocity data were searched with a method that treats the number of planets as unknown (Stevenson et al. 2025). Three short-period signals look like genuine planets, at about 3.16, 6.12 and 11.68 days, with minimum masses of 3.7, 5.7 and 4.9 Earth masses (Stevenson et al. 2025). Planet b is the innermost of the three (Stevenson et al. 2025).","system":"The three planets are packed close together and lie near orbital resonances (Stevenson et al. 2025). A fourth signal at about 1500 days is strongly detected, but it may come from a short magnetic cycle of the star rather than a cold giant planet (Stevenson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"More data are needed to cover the long-period signal and decide whether a distant giant planet exists."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28471b-Stevensonetal2025","cite":"Stevenson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. T. Stevenson, C. A. Haswell, J. R. Barnes et al.","title":"HD 28471: a near-resonant compact multiplanet system with a possible cold giant planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-35 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-35 b is one of five hot Jupiters announced together from the HATSouth survey, a network of small automated telescopes at three southern sites (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). It has the shortest orbit of the five, at 1.8209993 days (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). Its radius of 1.464 Jupiter radii is moderately inflated, which makes it useful for testing ideas on why hot Jupiters swell (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). A circular orbit fits the data better than an eccentric one (de Val-Borro et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a relatively young F star, 2.13 billion years old (de Val-Borro et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats35b-deValBorroetal2016","cite":"de Val-Borro et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"M. de Val-Borro, G. Á. Bakos, R. Brahm et al.","title":"HATS-31b through HATS-35b: Five Transiting Hot Jupiters Discovered By the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00006","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 221420 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities alone give only a minimum mass. Combining them with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of the star's motion shows the true mass is 22.9 Jupiter masses, well above the minimum (Venner et al. 2021). Such a body can be read as a high-mass giant planet or a low-mass brown dwarf (Venner et al. 2021).","system":"Its orbit has a size of about 10 au (Venner et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"Whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf is not settled. At about 10 au it is a good target for direct imaging (Venner et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd221420b-Venneretal2021","cite":"Venner et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alexander Venner, Andrew Vanderburg, Logan A. Pearce","title":"True Masses of the Long-period Companions to HD 92987 and HD 221420 from Hipparcos-Gaia Astrometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13941","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1883 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Brightness measurements in several colours, taken with ground-based cameras, helped rule out false alarms and confirm the planet around its M dwarf (Peláez-Torres et al. 2024). Velocity measurements with the Subaru telescope's InfraRed Doppler instrument later gave a mass of 13.7 Earth masses and a very low density of 0.4 grams per cubic centimetre (Fukuda et al. 2026). Such a fluffy super-Neptune is among the least dense planets of its size (Fukuda et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The planet lies in the ridge, a band of orbital periods between 3.2 and 5.7 days where Neptune-sized planets are scarce (Fukuda et al. 2026). Each part of this thinly populated region likely reflects a different evolutionary path (Fukuda et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1883b-PelezTorresetal2024","cite":"Peláez-Torres et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Peláez-Torres, E. Esparza-Borges, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Validation of up to seven TESS planet candidates through multi-colour transit photometry using MuSCAT2 data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.07400","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1883b-Fukudaetal2026","cite":"Fukuda et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Izuru Fukuda, Norio Narita, Akihiko Fukui et al.","title":"The mass of TOI-1883 b: A low-density super-Neptune in the ridge regime transiting an early-M dwarf","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.06868","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4155 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-4155 b was confirmed as a real planet by statistical validation, not by measuring its mass. Brightness measurements in several colours, mainly in the J band from the 200-inch Hale Telescope at Palomar, were combined with TESS data and high-resolution images (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025). The analysis gave a false-positive probability below 1.5 percent (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4155b-Barrientosetal2025","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Validation of TESS Planet Candidates with Multicolor Transit Photometry and TRICERATOPS+","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02782","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 89345 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is bright and shows solar-like oscillations, so asteroseismology could fix its properties and age at 9.4 billion years, and shows it has recently left the main sequence (Van Eylen et al. 2018). The planet is a warm Saturn with a mass of 35.7 Earth masses, on an eccentric orbit with e of about 0.2 (Van Eylen et al. 2018). Its equilibrium temperature is 1100 K and its density is low (Yu et al. 2018). Despite a lifetime near the irradiation level at which giant planets are thought to inflate, it shows no sign of an inflated radius (Yu et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The tilt of the star's spin relative to the planet's orbit is poorly constrained, because Rossiter-McLaughlin observations, which track the planet's transit across the spinning star, gave a broad result (Van Eylen et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd89345b-VanEylenetal2018","cite":"Van Eylen et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"V. Van Eylen, F. Dai, S. Mathur et al.","title":"HD 89345: a bright oscillating star hosting a transiting warm Saturn-sized planet observed by K2","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.01860","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd89345b-Yuetal2018","cite":"Yu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Liang Yu, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Jason D. Eastman et al.","title":"Two Warm, Low-density Sub-Jovian Planets Orbiting Bright Stars in K2 Campaigns 13 and 14","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02858","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 160.2 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up re-analysed archived velocities of 41 nearby M dwarfs to estimate how common low-mass planets are around them (Tuomi et al. 2014).","observed":"Archive velocities from the UVES spectrograph were combined with new values drawn from public HARPS spectra, and a Bayesian search was run (Tuomi et al. 2014). GJ 160.2 was one of the stars in which a new planet candidate was reported, among eight in the sample (Tuomi et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1602b-Tuomietal2014","cite":"Tuomi et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Mikko Tuomi, Hugh R. A. Jones, John R. Barnes et al.","title":"Bayesian search for low-mass planets around nearby M dwarfs - estimates for occurrence rate based on global detectability statistics","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.0430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 581 c":{"text":{"why":"The star is a cool M dwarf, a type that hosts many more very low-mass planets than Sun-like stars do (Udry et al. 2007).","observed":"The discovery paper announced two super-Earths in this system at once, one near the warm edge and one near the cold edge of the star's habitable zone, the band where liquid water could exist (Udry et al. 2007). Later work added a fourth planet and corrected the period of the outermost one to 66.8 days (Mayor et al. 2009). Dynamical stability of the four planets limits how tilted the orbits can be, so none can be more than about 1.6 times its minimum mass (Mayor et al. 2009). Direct measurement of the star's size with an interferometer put planets d and g in or on the edge of the habitable zone (von Braun et al. 2011).","system":"The first planet found here was a Neptune-mass world on a 5.4-day orbit, only the third planetary system known around an M dwarf (Bonfils et al. 2005). The star has a radius of 0.299 solar radii and a temperature of about 3500 K (von Braun et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"A 2024 re-analysis of CARMENES, HARPS and HIRES velocities tested several orbital layouts, because some of the planets are still disputed (von Stauffenberg et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj581c-Udryetal2007","cite":"Udry et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"S. Udry, X. Bonfils, X. Delfosse et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Radial velocities of Gl 581 (Udry+, 2007)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.34699043","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581c-Mayoretal2009","cite":"Mayor et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Mayor, X. Bonfils, T. Forveille et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XVIII. An Earth-mass planet in the GJ 581 planetary system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.2780","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581c-vonBraunetal2011","cite":"von Braun et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Stephen R. Kane et al.","title":"Astrophysical Parameters and Habitable Zone of the Exoplanet Hosting Star GJ 581","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0237","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581c-Bonfilsetal2005","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"X. Bonfils, T. Forveille, X. Delfosse et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. VI. A Neptune-mass planet around the nearby M dwarf Gl 581","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0509211","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj581c-vonStauffenbergetal2024","cite":"von Stauffenberg et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. von Stauffenberg, T. Trifonov, A. Quirrenbach et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Revisiting the GJ 581 multi-planetary system with new Doppler measurements from CARMENES, HARPS, and HIRES","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11520","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":38,"about":34,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2011-BLG-0265L b":{"text":{"why":"A Jupiter-mass planet around an M dwarf is very rare among known systems, so it matters for theories of planet formation (Skowron et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet was seen as a short blip on the brightening of a background star as the lens star passed in front of it. High-cadence coverage of that blip, plus observations through the whole event, allowed an accurate light-curve model (Skowron et al. 2015). The solution is still ambiguous. In the preferred one the planet has 0.9 Jupiter masses and the star 0.22 solar masses. In the other, 2 sigma away, the planet has 0.6 Jupiter masses and the star 0.14 solar masses (Skowron et al. 2015).","system":"The system lies in the Galactic disk, 3 to 4 kiloparsecs away toward the bulge. The orbit is 1.5 to 2.0 AU wide, well beyond the snow line, so this is a cold Jupiter (Skowron et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"The data cannot yet pick between the two solutions, but later observations may settle it (Skowron et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2011blg0265lb-Skowronetal2015","cite":"Skowron et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. Skowron, I.-G. Shin, A. Udalski et al.","title":"OGLE-2011-BLG-0265Lb: A Jovian Microlensing Planet Orbiting an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.8252","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-42 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star, then called KOI 961, was studied by comparing it with Barnard's Star, a well-known nearby M dwarf. Colours and spectra match closely, so both are old, metal-poor red dwarfs (Muirhead et al. 2012). The comparison gave the star a mass of 0.13 solar masses and a radius of 0.17 solar radii (Muirhead et al. 2012). A new fit to the public Kepler light curve significantly revised the transit parameters of all three planets, and a false-positive probability was calculated for each (Muirhead et al. 2012).","system":"Kepler-42 is the one known system with both very short orbital periods and neighbouring planets close together. A statistical study expected about 17 such pairs and found only this one (Steffen & Farr 2013). Short-period small planets also seem to be commoner as single candidates than as members of multiple systems (Steffen & Farr 2013).","history":"","open":"Models of rotation in tightly packed super-Earth systems, including KOI-961, study whether orbital resonances can disturb synchronous spin (Ribeiro & Callegari 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler42c-Muirheadetal2012","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, John Asher Johnson, Kevin Apps et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. III. KOI 961: A Small Star with Large Proper Motion and Three Small Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2189","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler42c-SteffenFarr2013","cite":"Steffen & Farr 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jason H. Steffen, Will M. Farr","title":"A Lack of Short-period Multiplanet Systems with Close-proximity Pairs and the Curious Case of Kepler-42","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3526","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler42c-RibeiroCallegari2014","cite":"Ribeiro & Callegari 2014","year":"2014","authors":"F. B. Ribeiro, N., Jr. Callegari","title":"Spin-Orbit Resonances in Super-Earth Systems Close to Mean-motion Commensurabilities","journal":"Revista Mexicana de Astronomia y Astrofisica Conference Series","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-6 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is unusually rich in heavy elements, with an iron abundance of +0.34 (Dunham et al. 2010). The planet's low density of 0.35 g per cubic cm is typical for such a planet (Dunham et al. 2010).","observed":"Kepler-6b was observed as it passed behind its star, with the Warm Spitzer telescope and with Kepler (Desert et al. 2011). The infrared brightness gives a temperature of 1660 K, and the visible-light reflectivity is low, with a geometric albedo of 0.11 (Desert et al. 2011). The Bond albedo, the share of all starlight reflected, is at most 0.17 (Desert et al. 2011). The timing of the eclipse fits a circular orbit (Desert et al. 2011). A separate Kepler study also detected its full brightness curve through the orbit, and found a low albedo (Esteves et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"Models fit best when most of the absorbed energy stays on the dayside, with less than 10% carried to the night side (Desert et al. 2011).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler6b-Dunhametal2010","cite":"Dunham et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Edward W. Dunham, William J. Borucki, David G. Koch et al.","title":"Kepler-6b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter Orbiting a Metal-rich Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0333","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler6b-Desertetal2011","cite":"Desert et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jean-Michel Désert, David Charbonneau, Jonathan J. Fortney et al.","title":"The Atmospheres of the Hot-Jupiters Kepler-5b and Kepler-6b Observed during Occultations with Warm-Spitzer and Kepler","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0555","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler6b-Estevesetal2013","cite":"Esteves et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Lisa J. Esteves, Ernst J. W. De Mooij, Ray Jayawardhana","title":"Optical Phase Curves of Kepler Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3271","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HN Peg b":{"text":{"why":"Its spectrum is used to test models of the change from L-type to T-type brown dwarfs, as clouds clear (Leggett et al. 2008).","observed":"Near-infrared spectra show methane absorption, which marks a T dwarf, a cool brown dwarf. Its spectral type is T2.5 (Luhman et al. 2007). The brown dwarf moves across the sky with its star, which confirms they are a pair (Luhman et al. 2007). Models suggest a mass of 0.021 solar masses, and a temperature lower than other T dwarfs of similar type (Luhman et al. 2007). Hubble data show its brightness varies with a period of 15.4 hours, by about 1.2% (Zhou et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a young Sun-like G0 star, about 0.3 billion years old (Luhman et al. 2007). Adaptive-optics images show the T dwarf is a single object, with no close companion (Leggett et al. 2008).","history":"","open":"Fits to the spectrum work only if the cloud layer is thin and there is strong vertical mixing in the atmosphere (Leggett et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hnpegb-Leggettetal2008","cite":"Leggett et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"S. K. Leggett, D. Saumon, Loic Albert et al.","title":"HN Peg B: A Test of Models of the L to T Dwarf Transition","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0804.1386","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hnpegb-Luhmanetal2007","cite":"Luhman et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"K. L. Luhman, B. M. Patten, M. Marengo et al.","title":"Discovery of Two T Dwarf Companions with the Spitzer Space Telescope","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609464","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hnpegb-Zhouetal2018","cite":"Zhou et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Yifan Zhou, Dániel Apai, Stanimir Metchev et al.","title":"Cloud Atlas: Rotational Modulations in the L/T Transition Brown Dwarf Companion HN Peg B","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.09757","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":32,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1107 b":{"text":{"why":"Core-accretion models predict giant planets should be more common around more massive stars, which this survey tests (Psaridi et al. 2022).","observed":"Stars this hot rotate fast and show few, broadened spectral lines, so measuring their wobble is hard. Surveys had therefore covered them poorly (Psaridi et al. 2022). Follow-up used the CORALIE, CHIRON, TRES, FEROS and MINERVA-Australis spectrographs, plus ground-based photometry (Psaridi et al. 2022).","system":"Stars above the Kraft break of 6200 K have thin outer convective layers and weak magnetic winds. They therefore keep spinning quickly (Psaridi et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1107b-Psaridietal2022","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Angelica Psaridi, François Bouchy, Monika Lendl et al.","title":"Three new brown dwarfs and a massive hot Jupiter revealed by TESS around early-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10854","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2014-BLG-0221L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The signal comes from a microlensing event seen in 2014 and analysed years later. Two models fit about equally well, with event timescales of about 70 and 110 days (Kirikawa et al. 2024). Both give a mass ratio near one to a thousand, a Jupiter mass ratio (Kirikawa et al. 2024). These long timescales mean the host is fairly massive. The shorter one gives 65% odds of a late-type star and 25% of a white dwarf, and the longer one favours a black hole (Kirikawa et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"If the host is a stellar remnant, this would be only the second planet found by microlensing around one (Kirikawa et al. 2024).","open":"The source and lens stars are now 41 to 139 milliarcseconds apart, so high-resolution imaging could now tell which host it is (Kirikawa et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2014blg0221lb-Kirikawaetal2024","cite":"Kirikawa et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"R. Kirikawa, T. Sumi, D. P. Bennett et al.","title":"OGLE-2014-BLG-0221Lb: A Jupiter Mass Ratio Companion Orbiting Either a Late-type Star or a Stellar Remnant","journal":"Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13066","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"RR Cae b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"RR Cae is a close pair of a white dwarf and an M dwarf that eclipse each other. The planet is inferred from the eclipse times, which drift in a cycle of 11.9 years with an amplitude of 14.3 seconds. A third body pulling on the pair would cause this (Qian et al. 2012). The implied minimum mass is 4.2 Jupiter masses at about 5.3 AU (Qian et al. 2012). A 2023 study added 430 new eclipse times. One body fits a 16.6-year cycle and 3.4 Jupiter masses, and two bodies fit cycles of 15.0 and 39 years (Rattanamala et al. 2023).","system":"The white dwarf has a temperature of 7540 K and the M dwarf is about spectral type M4 (Maxted et al. 2007). The pair orbit in 7.29 hours and the M dwarf is highly active (Ribeiro et al. 2013). The metal-rich white dwarf suggests it is taking in wind from its companion (Ribeiro et al. 2013).","history":"The M dwarf is larger than expected for its mass, possibly from a previous common-envelope phase, when one star swallowed the other (Bruch 1999).","open":"An upward trend in the timing may be part of a longer cycle, hinting at a second giant planet (Qian et al. 2012). The measurements allow one or two bodies (Rattanamala et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-rrcaeb-Qianetal2012","cite":"Qian et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"S.-B. Qian, L. Liu, L.-Y. Zhu et al.","title":"A circumbinary planet in orbit around the short-period white dwarf eclipsing binary RR Cae","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.4205","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-rrcaeb-Rattanamalaetal2023","cite":"Rattanamala et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"R. Rattanamala, S. Awiphan, S. Komonjinda et al.","title":"Eclipse timing variations in the WD + dM eclipsing binary RR Cae","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.09397","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-rrcaeb-Maxtedetal2007","cite":"Maxted et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. O'Donoghue, L. Morales-Rueda et al.","title":"The mass and radius of the M-dwarf in the short-period eclipsing binary RR Caeli","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0702005","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-rrcaeb-Ribeiroetal2013","cite":"Ribeiro et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"T. Ribeiro, R. Baptista, S. Kafka et al.","title":"Accretion and activity on the post-common-envelope binary RR Caeli","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.5921","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-rrcaeb-Bruch1999","cite":"Bruch 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Albert Bruch","title":"The Precataclysmic Binary RR Caeli Revisited: Spectroscopy and Light-Curve Synthesis","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/300904","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11506 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet was found by re-analysing published velocity data of the star with a Bayesian method, a statistical approach that weighs many possible orbit solutions. The star's own jitter was left as a free parameter (Tuomi & Kotiranta 2009). The change gave a new orbit for the known planet and a high-confidence signal of a second one, of 0.8 Jupiter masses with a period of about half a year (Tuomi & Kotiranta 2009). The N2K survey, built to find short-period planets, later confirmed it. Its period is 223.6 days, and the outer planet orbits every 1627.5 days (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"The system holds a confirmed outer giant planet and this inner one. HARPS-N has also gathered 72 spectra of the star between 2012 and 2023 (Ruggieri et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11506c-TuomiKotiranta2009","cite":"Tuomi & Kotiranta 2009","year":"2009","authors":"M. Tuomi, S. Kotiranta","title":"Bayesian analysis of the radial velocities of HD 11506 reveals another planetary companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.2997","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd11506c-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd11506c-Ruggierietal2024","cite":"Ruggieri et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Ruggieri, S. Desidera, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 75898 and HD 11506 radial velocities (Ruggieri+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-4777.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler photometry, near-infrared Doppler spectroscopy with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder, and adaptive-optics imaging were combined to confirm the planet (Cañas et al. 2022). It is about half the size of Earth, similar to Mars, and orbits its M dwarf every 9.9 hours (Cañas et al. 2022). It is the smallest validated ultra-short-period planet known (Cañas et al. 2022). The velocity data show no sign of other massive companions (Cañas et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass is below 0.34 Earth masses if it is less dense than iron, and measuring it is beyond current instruments (Cañas et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi477701-Caasetal2022","cite":"Cañas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Suvrath Mahadevan, William D. Cochran et al.","title":"A Hot Mars-sized Exoplanet Transiting an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-36 b":{"text":{"why":"Evaporation by stellar radiation could have turned two similar planets into different ones. The inner one is consistent with a stripped core, and the outer kept some gas because its core is more massive (Owen & Morton 2016). The mass of the core controls the loss (Lopez & Fortney 2013).","observed":"Kepler-36 holds two planets whose orbits differ in distance by only 10% but whose densities differ by a factor of 8. One is a likely rocky super-Earth and the other is Neptune-like (Carter et al. 2012). Their spacing is 20 times closer than any adjacent pair of solar-system planets (Carter et al. 2012). Their orbits are at 0.115 and 0.128 AU (Owen & Morton 2016). Their mean-motion resonance is 7:6, the most compact known (Batygin & Morbidelli 2026).","system":"The inner planet has an initial gas share of at most 10% and a core of about 4.4 Earth masses (Owen & Morton 2016).","history":"Disk turbulence can bring such pairs into tight orbits during migration (Paardekooper et al. 2013). A 2026 theory says capture into this resonance could not have happened near the disk's inner edge, and points to long-range migration (Batygin & Morbidelli 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler36b-OwenMorton2016","cite":"Owen & Morton 2016","year":"2016","authors":"James E. Owen, Timothy. D. Morton","title":"The Initial Physical Conditions of Kepler-36 b and c","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.07385","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36b-LopezFortney2013","cite":"Lopez & Fortney 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Eric D. Lopez, Jonathan J. Fortney","title":"The Role of Core Mass in Controlling Evaporation: The Kepler Radius Distribution and the Kepler-36 Density Dichotomy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.0269","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36b-Carteretal2012","cite":"Carter et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Joshua A. Carter, Eric Agol, William J. Chaplin et al.","title":"Kepler-36: A Pair of Planets with Neighboring Orbits and Dissimilar Densities","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.4718","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36b-BatyginMorbidelli2026","cite":"Batygin & Morbidelli 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Konstantin Batygin, Alessandro Morbidelli","title":"Origins of Compact Mean-Motion Resonances: Evidence for Long-Range Migration and the Case of Kepler-36","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.27093","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler36b-Paardekooperetal2013","cite":"Paardekooper et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Sijme-Jan Paardekooper, Hanno Rein, Willy Kley","title":"The formation of systems with closely spaced low-mass planets and the application to Kepler-36","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4762","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"24 Sex b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a subgiant, a massive evolved star, and part of a survey of retired A stars. Radial velocities showed a pair of Jovian planets, with periods of 452.8 and 883.0 days (Johnson et al. 2010). Brightness monitoring found the star constant to within 0.002 magnitudes, which supports the planet reading (Johnson et al. 2010). Most stable solutions have crossing orbits, so the planets must be locked in a resonance that prevents close encounters (Johnson et al. 2010).","system":"The best orbit puts the two planets very near a 2:1 period ratio. The system is stable only in a narrow range of orbits where the planets lie in the same plane (Wittenmyer et al. 2012). Tilting one orbit makes it quickly unstable (Wittenmyer et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-24sexb-Johnsonetal2010","cite":"Johnson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"John Asher Johnson, Matthew Payne, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"Retired A Stars and Their Companions. VI. A Pair of Interacting Exoplanet Pairs Around the Subgiants 24 Sextanis and HD 200964","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4552","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-24sexb-Wittenmyeretal2012","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jonathan Horner, C. G. Tinney","title":"Resonances Required: Dynamical Analysis of the 24 Sex and HD 200964 Planetary Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1078","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is near the end of its main-sequence life, with 1.223 solar masses and 1.487 solar radii (Borucki et al. 2010). Asteroseismology, which uses starquakes, refined host star properties (Huber et al. 2013).","observed":"Kepler's early light curve showed a 3.95-hour transit and Keck velocities showed a low-eccentricity orbit (Borucki et al. 2010). Its density is similar to that of Neptune and GJ 436b even though it is 800,000 times more irradiated (Borucki et al. 2010). A reanalysis found an eccentric orbit model at 2 sigma, with e of 0.25 (Kipping & Bakos 2011). A secondary eclipse was later found near phase 0.7 and a revised eccentric model was proposed (Sheets 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"An eccentric orbit is surprising, as tides should have made it circular (Sheets 2019). The eccentric solution is not firmly confirmed (Kipping & Bakos 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler4b-Boruckietal2010","cite":"Borucki et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"William J. Borucki, David G. Koch, Timothy M. Brown et al.","title":"Kepler-4b: A Hot Neptune-like Planet of a G0 Star Near Main-sequence Turnoff","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.0604","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler4b-Huberetal2013","cite":"Huber et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Daniel Huber, William J. Chaplin, Jørgen Christensen-Dalsgaard et al.","title":"Fundamental Properties of Kepler Planet-candidate Host Stars using Asteroseismology","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2624","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler4b-KippingBakos2011","cite":"Kipping & Bakos 2011","year":"2011","authors":"David Kipping, Gáspár Bakos","title":"An Independent Analysis of Kepler-4b Through Kepler-8b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.3538","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler4b-Sheets2019","cite":"Sheets 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Holly Sheets","title":"Revised Orbital Parameters for Kepler-4b with Secondary Eclipse","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 367 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A 3-year HARPS campaign with 371 measurements found two non-transiting companions to the inner planet, at about 11.5 and 34 days (Goffo et al. 2023). The minimum masses are 4.13 and 6.03 Earth masses, and the periods are near 3:1 (Goffo et al. 2023). Only a minimum mass is known for each, because they do not transit (Goffo et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet GJ 367 b is an ultra-short-period sub-Earth with a 7.7-hour orbit and a very high density (Goffo et al. 2023). A 2026 ESPRESSO study lowers its mass to 0.503 Earth masses (Lee et al. 2026). X-ray data suggest the star is old and quiet, so planet b has likely lost any atmosphere (Poppenhaeger et al. 2024). Imaging shows no warm dust and no stellar companion beyond 0.4 AU (Brandner et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The star's age is uncertain, between 30 million and about 8 billion years (Golabek et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj367c-Goffoetal2023","cite":"Goffo et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Elisa Goffo, Davide Gandolfi, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Company for the Ultra-high Density, Ultra-short Period Sub-Earth GJ 367 b: Discovery of Two Additional Low-mass Planets at 11.5 and 34 Days","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09181","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367c-Leeetal2026","cite":"Lee et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Rena A. Lee, Fei Dai, Ellen M. Price et al.","title":"Revisiting the Exo-Mercury Candidate GJ 367 b with ESPRESSO and a Self-consistent Tidal Distortion Model","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6cec","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367c-Poppenhaegeretal2024","cite":"Poppenhaeger et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. Poppenhaeger, L. Ketzer, N. Ilic et al.","title":"The high-energy environment of the heavy sub-Earth GJ 367 b indicates likely complete evaporation of its atmosphere","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.17302","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367c-Brandneretal2022","cite":"Brandner et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Wolfgang Brandner, Per Calissendorff, Neige Frankel et al.","title":"High-contrast, high-angular resolution view of the GJ 367 exoplanet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02998","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj367c-Golabeketal2024","cite":"Golabek et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gregor J. Golabek, Tim Lichtenberg, Lotte M. Bartels et al.","title":"Magma oceanography of the dense, ultrashort-period sub-Earth GJ 367 b","journal":"European Geosciences Union General Assembly 2024 (EGU24)","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-egu24-5832","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4311 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS first spotted the 15-day sub-Neptune. Follow-up with CHEOPS and HARPS refined the radii and confirmed it as a planet (Eschen et al. 2026). It is 2.47 Earth radii wide (Eschen et al. 2026). The system also has an ultra-short-period super-Earth of 1.38 Earth radii with a period near 1 day, and its mass is 4.5 Earth masses (Eschen et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a K dwarf with motion between the Galactic thick disc and the Hercules stream (Eschen et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"A third periodic signal in the HARPS velocities cannot be linked to stellar activity (Eschen et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4311c-Eschenetal2026","cite":"Eschen et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yoshi Nike Emilia Eschen, Thomas G. Wilson, Andrew Collier Cameron et al.","title":"An ultra-short-period super-Earth and a sub-Neptune orbiting the K dwarf TOI-4311","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12324","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-132 b":{"text":{"why":"It lies in the Neptune desert, a region of radius and period where short-period Neptune-like planets are rare (Díaz et al. 2020).","observed":"TESS saw transits about 1400 parts per million deep every 2.11 days. HARPS confirmed the planet with a velocity change of 11.38 m/s (Díaz et al. 2020). The planet's density is 3.08 g per cubic cm. Structure models suggest most of its mass is a rocky core, with an atmosphere making up about 4.3% of its mass (Díaz et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"It is a TESS Level 1 Science Requirement candidate, so follow-up may find more planets in the system (Díaz et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi132b-Dazetal2020","cite":"Díaz et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Matías R. Díaz, James S. Jenkins, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"TOI-132 b: A short-period planet in the Neptune desert transiting a V = 11.3 G-type star<SUP>★</SUP>","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02012","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-37 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HAT-P-37 b was announced alongside three other transiting planets heavier than Jupiter, and its properties are typical of hot Jupiters (Bakos et al. 2012). Later timing work found a possible extra planet from small transit-time shifts of 1.74 minutes (A-thano et al. 2022). That study excluded a Saturn-mass planet with a period under 6 days. Another found no significant timing signal and ruled out perturbers above 200 Earth masses near several resonances (Yang et al. 2021). Broadband spectra favour a cloudy atmosphere (A-thano et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"The transit depth in blue light was smaller than in the near-infrared, which may hint at TiO or VO absorption (Turner et al. 2017).","open":"The two timing studies disagree on a second planet, and the blue-light hint needs follow-up (Turner et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp37b-Bakosetal2012","cite":"Bakos et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-34b-HAT-P-37b: Four Transiting Planets More Massive than Jupiter Orbiting Moderately Bright Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.0659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp37b-Athanoetal2022","cite":"A-thano et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Napaporn A-thano, Ing-Guey Jiang, Supachai Awiphan et al.","title":"The Transit Timing and Atmosphere of Hot Jupiter HAT-P-37b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.04724","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp37b-Yangetal2021","cite":"Yang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jia-Mei Yang, Xian-Yu Wang, Kai Li et al.","title":"Photometric follow-up observations and transit timing analysis of HAT-P-37b","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/psab059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp37b-Turneretal2017","cite":"Turner et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Robin M. Leiter, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Investigating the physical properties of transiting hot Jupiters with the 1.5-m Kuiper Telescope","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07909","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2145 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS found a giant planet on a 10.26-day orbit with significant eccentricity, 0.208, around a subgiant star (Rodriguez et al. 2023). Its mass is 5.26 Jupiter masses (Rodriguez et al. 2023). Despite very high irradiation it shows no sign of inflation, probably thanks to its high mass (Rodriguez et al. 2023). New NEID Rossiter-McLaughlin data show its orbit is nearly aligned with the star's spin (Dong et al. 2025).","system":"The host is an evolved A star (Dong et al. 2025).","history":"Super Jupiters may form with high masses, or grow through collisions, and simulations allow either route for this planet (Dong et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2145b-Rodriguezetal2023","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Another shipment of six short-period giant planets from TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2145b-Dongetal2025","cite":"Dong et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jiayin Dong, Ashley Chontos, George Zhou et al.","title":"Origins of Super Jupiters: TOI-2145b has a Moderately Eccentric and Nearly Aligned Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01356","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-13 c":{"text":{"why":"Whether the orbits are coplanar tells astronomers about the inner planet's interior (Winn et al. 2010).","observed":"HAT-P-13 c is a massive outer companion on a highly eccentric orbit, found beyond a transiting hot Jupiter (Bakos et al. 2009). It has not been seen to transit, and may not (Bakos et al. 2009). Velocity data show the inner planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Winn et al. 2010). A long-term trend suggests a third body, perhaps another planet or a low-mass star (Winn et al. 2010).","system":"Spitzer eclipse data give a second Love number of 0.81 for the inner planet, which challenges interior models (Hardy et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"Early transit timing variations were not confirmed (Fulton et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp13c-Winnetal2010","cite":"Winn et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, John Asher Johnson, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"The HAT-P-13 Exoplanetary System: Evidence for Spin-Orbit Alignment and a Third Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.4512","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp13c-Bakosetal2009","cite":"Bakos et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, A. W. Howard, R. W. Noyes et al.","title":"HAT-P-13b,c: A Transiting Hot Jupiter with a Massive Outer Companion on an Eccentric Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.3525","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp13c-Hardyetal2017","cite":"Hardy et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ryan A. Hardy, Joseph Harrington, Matthew R. Hardin et al.","title":"Secondary Eclipses of HAT-P-13b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.00828","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp13c-Fultonetal2011","cite":"Fulton et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Benjamin J. Fulton, Avi Shporer, Joshua N. Winn et al.","title":"Long-term Transit Timing Monitoring and Refined Light Curve Parameters of HAT-P-13b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.5599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":15,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-756 b":{"text":{"why":"This is the first result of a NIRPS program on sub-Neptunes around M dwarfs (Parc et al. 2025).","observed":"A cold eccentric giant planet, TOI-756 c, was found in velocity data (Parc et al. 2025). The star is an M1 dwarf, and a special method corrected for Earth's air absorption when the star's velocity matched Earth's motion (Parc et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi756b-Parcetal2025","cite":"Parc et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Léna Parc, François Bouchy, Neil J. Cook et al.","title":"NIRPS and TESS reveal a peculiar system around the M dwarf TOI-756: A transiting sub-Neptune and a cold eccentric giant","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.14927","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2019-BLG-1492L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This planet was found by an automated search of KMTNet light curves from 2018 and 2019 (Hwang et al. 2022). The method is AnomalyFinder. It is one of six new planets with mass ratios below 2 in 10,000 (Hwang et al. 2022). The signal is a small dip in the light curve, made as the source star crosses the line between host and planet on the far side (Hwang et al. 2022). These faint signals were missed in earlier by-eye searches (Hwang et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2019blg1492lb-Hwangetal2022","cite":"Hwang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Kyu-Ha Hwang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Systematic KMTNet Planetary Anomaly Search. II. Six New q < 2 × 10<SUP>-4</SUP> Mass-ratio Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.06686","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28185 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CORALIE found a giant planet with a minimum mass of 5.7 Jupiter masses. Its period is about 383 days, and one of the first long-period planets in a nearly circular orbit (Santos et al. 2001). A 2024 re-analysis of 22 years of velocity data plus astrometry confirmed a temperate giant with an Earth-like incident flux (Venner et al. 2024).","system":"A more distant companion first thought a brown dwarf is a super-Jovian planet of 6.0 Jupiter masses and a 24.9-year period (Venner et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"Studies of hypothetical moons for this planet find the heating depends on how long it stays in the habitable zone (Hinkel & Kane 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28185b-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets VI. New long period giant planets around HD 28185 and HD 213240","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20011366","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd28185b-Venneretal2024","cite":"Venner et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alexander Venner, Qier An, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"HD 28185 revisited: an outer planet, instead of a brown dwarf, on a Saturn-like orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.14218","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd28185b-HinkelKane2013","cite":"Hinkel & Kane 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Natalie R. Hinkel, Stephen R. Kane","title":"Habitability of Exomoons at the Hill or Tidal Locking Radius","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.4760","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WISPIT 2 c":{"text":{"why":"The host is a young, solar-type star of about 5 million years (van Capelleveen et al. 2025).","observed":"VLTI/GRAVITY spectra show CO band-head absorption and a continuum like a young giant planet (Lawlor et al. 2026). The fit gives 1500 to 2600 K, and a mass of 8 to 12 Jupiter masses, about twice WISPIT 2b (Lawlor et al. 2026). Astrometry rules out a background source and marginally detects orbital motion (Lawlor et al. 2026).","system":"The disc has multiple rings reaching 380 AU (van Capelleveen et al. 2025). Planet b lies in a gap at 57 AU (Lawlor et al. 2026). ALMA shows an empty cavity within the orbit of c and a ring between the two planets (Benisty et al. 2026). A single narrow ring lies at 144.4 AU (Facchini et al. 2026). The host is also a spectroscopic binary with a 4.8-day period (Alqubelat et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Facchini et al. 2026 doubt planet b alone drives the dust structure."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wispit2c-vanCapelleveenetal2025","cite":"van Capelleveen et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Richelle F. van Capelleveen, Christian Ginski, Matthew A. Kenworthy et al.","title":"WIde Separation Planets In Time (WISPIT): A Gap-clearing Planet in a Multi-ringed Disk around the Young Solar-type Star WISPIT 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19053","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2c-Lawloretal2026","cite":"Lawlor et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Chloe Lawlor, Richelle F. van Capelleveen, Guillaume Bourdarot et al.","title":"Direct Spectroscopic Confirmation of the Young Embedded Protoplanet WISPIT 2c","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.22085","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2c-Benistyetal2026","cite":"Benisty et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Myriam Benisty, Stefano Facchini, Richard Teague et al.","title":"Mapping the WISPIT 2 Planet-hosting Cavity at Sub-Hill-radius Scales","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04946","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2c-Facchinietal2026","cite":"Facchini et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Stefano Facchini, Pietro Curone, Myriam Benisty et al.","title":"A 2 au Resolution View by ALMA of the Planet-hosting WISPIT 2 Disk","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15948","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wispit2c-Alqubelatetal2026","cite":"Alqubelat et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"H. Alqubelat, C. J. Bürgy, C. F. Manara et al.","title":"The Orbital Parameters of the Spectroscopic Binary WISPIT 2","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/aebe24","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-55 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-55 has an unusual resonant layout. The two inner planets, d and e, form a resonant pair, and so do the two outer planets, b and c. Planet f sits between them and is in no resonance (Sendao & MacDonald 2025). N-body simulations with added planets show a planet between e and f spoils the inner resonance, so a full chain would need other changes (Sendao & MacDonald 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether planets are missing, which could complete a resonant chain, is under search (Sendao & MacDonald 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler55e-SendaoMacDonald2025","cite":"Sendao & MacDonald 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jake Sendao, Mariah MacDonald","title":"A Thorough Investigation of the Kepler-55 System: Are We Missing Planets?","journal":"246th Meeting of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4600 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS caught the planet in a single transit and it was validated by TESS single-transit working group (Mireles et al. 2023). Its 482.8-day period makes it the longest-period confirmed or validated TESS planet at that time (Mireles et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is 191 K (Mireles et al. 2023). MAROON-X velocities give a mass of 212.5 Earth masses and eccentricity 0.219, and both planets show transit timing variations of about an hour (Hu et al. 2026).","system":"The inner planet orbits in 82.7 days and has a mass of 74.7 Earth masses (Hu et al. 2026).","history":"Both planets are targets for JWST (Mireles et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4600c-Mirelesetal2023","cite":"Mireles et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ismael Mireles, Diana Dragomir, Hugh P. Osborn et al.","title":"TOI-4600 b and c: Two Long-period Giant Planets Orbiting an Early K Dwarf","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.15572","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4600c-Huetal2026","cite":"Hu et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Tong Hu, Zitao Lin, Sharon X. Wang et al.","title":"RV and TTV Measurements of Two Transiting Long-Period Giants around TOI-4600","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26426","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-141 c":{"text":{"why":"The discovery team was mainly after the inner planet's mass, to test whether very hot, very close-in planets are stripped gas giants or born rocky (Malavolta et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet passes only across the edge of its star, a grazing transit, so its size is hard to pin down (Malavolta et al. 2018). Its pull on the star was not detected in the velocity data, so its mass and density are only loosely limited. The authors judge it likely to be Neptune-like (Malavolta et al. 2018). It was confirmed as a planet by statistical validation rather than by a measured mass (Malavolta et al. 2018).","system":"The inner planet, K2-141 b, circles the star in about 0.28 days and is rocky, with no thick atmosphere (Malavolta et al. 2018). Its surface is hotter than 2000 K, and its infrared glow hints at a thin atmosphere of rock vapour (Zieba et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Its density, and so whether it is a gas-rich Neptune or something denser, is still not measured (Malavolta et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2141c-Malavoltaetal2018","cite":"Malavolta et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Luca Malavolta, Andrew W. Mayo, Tom Louden et al.","title":"An Ultra-short Period Rocky Super-Earth with a Secondary Eclipse and a Neptune-like Companion around K2-141","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.03502","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2141c-Ziebaetal2022","cite":"Zieba et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Zieba, M. Zilinskas, L. Kreidberg et al.","title":"K2 and Spitzer phase curves of the rocky ultra-short-period planet K2-141 b hint at a tenuous rock vapor atmosphere","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.00370","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-289 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its transits come earlier or later than a clock would predict, because the planets tug on each other. The planet's very deep transits, about 1.3 per cent of the starlight, tightly pin down the star's properties (Schmitt et al. 2014). Two more transits were later recorded with the 200-inch Hale Telescope using a diffuser, which spreads starlight to give space-like precision from the ground. They extended the timing record by 7.5 years (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2023). Modelling these timings gave its mass, and the result was two or more times more precise than before (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2023).","system":"Two smaller planets, both sub-Neptunes, orbit closer in. Each neighbouring pair has periods close to a 2:1 ratio, which is why the timings shift (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2023). The three planets are not locked in a 1:2:4 chain of resonances (Schmitt et al. 2014). The star is young, about 1 billion years old, and Sun-like (Schmitt et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler289c-Schmittetal2014","cite":"Schmitt et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Joseph R. Schmitt, Eric Agol, Katherine M. Deck et al.","title":"Planet Hunters. VII. Discovery of a New Low-mass, Low-density Planet (PH3 C) Orbiting Kepler-289 with Mass Measurements of Two Additional Planets (PH3 B and D)","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.8114","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler289c-GreklekMcKeonetal2023","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson, Shreyas Vissapragada et al.","title":"Constraining the Densities of the Three Kepler-289 Planets with Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.00022","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 6860 b":{"text":{"why":"The search targeted bright, cool red giant stars that vary in brightness over long periods (Lee et al. 2023).","observed":"The companion was spotted in 16 years of spectra, from 2005 to 2022, taken at Bohyunsan Observatory (Lee et al. 2023). The slow wobble of the star implies a body of about 28 Jupiter masses at 2.03 au (Lee et al. 2023). That is heavy for a planet, and the study calls it a companion lighter than a star rather than simply a planet. It circles a red giant, a swollen, evolved star (Lee et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd6860b-Leeetal2023","cite":"Lee et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Hee-Jin Do, Myeong-Gu Park et al.","title":"Long-period radial velocity variations of nine M red giants. The detection of substellar companions around HD 6860 and HD 112300","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15897","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1002 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found in 139 velocity measurements made between 2017 and 2021 with two spectrographs, ESPRESSO and CARMENES. The authors modelled the star's own activity signals so they would not be mistaken for planets (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2023). The host is a quiet, faint red dwarf only 4.84 parsecs from the Sun. The planet is about Earth's mass at least, receives about 0.67 times Earth's starlight, and lies in the habitable zone (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2023). Because it does not transit, its size is unknown.","system":"A second planet, GJ 1002 c, orbits every 20.2 days with a minimum mass of 1.36 Earth masses (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"A later check of the data found the periodic signal may not be statistically significant, and more extreme-precision observations are needed (Castro Nieva et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1002b-Mascareoetal2023","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, E. González-Álvarez, M. R. Zapatero Osorio et al.","title":"Two temperate Earth-mass planets orbiting the nearby star GJ 1002","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07332","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1002b-Nievaetal2025","cite":"Nieva et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Paula Andrea Castro Nieva, Sarah Dodson-Robinson, Victor Ramirez Delgado","title":"Validation of Two Earth-mass Planets Orbiting GJ 1002","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/adb612","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 18742 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star was re-measured by listening to its brightness oscillations with TESS, a technique called asteroseismology (Pope et al. 2024). For this star the new mass and radius disagreed significantly with earlier values from spectra and stellar models (Pope et al. 2024). Since the planet's mass is derived from the star's mass, the authors caution that the planet's properties may need revising (Pope et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass of the star, and so of the planet, remains uncertain (Pope et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd18742b-Popeetal2024","cite":"Pope et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Myles Pope, Joleen K. Carlberg, Jeff Valenti et al.","title":"TESS Asteroseismic Masses and Radii of Red Giants with (and without) Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02051","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28185 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Twenty-two years of velocity data combined with precise position measurements from the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites were used to re-examine this companion (Venner et al. 2024). It had earlier been claimed to be a brown dwarf, an object between planet and star. The new mass, 6.0 Jupiter masses, leads the authors to reclassify it as a planet (Venner et al. 2024). Its orbit takes about 24.9 years, similar to Saturn's (Venner et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet, HD 28185 b, takes 385.92 days to orbit and receives about as much light as Earth does (Venner et al. 2024). It was one of the first long-period giants found on a nearly circular orbit (Santos et al. 2001).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28185c-Venneretal2024","cite":"Venner et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alexander Venner, Qier An, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"HD 28185 revisited: an outer planet, instead of a brown dwarf, on a Saturn-like orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.14218","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd28185c-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets VI. New long period giant planets around HD 28185 and HD 213240","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20011366","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-113 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a mass of 0.48 Jupiter masses but a puffed-up radius, with a radius anomaly of 0.35, meaning it is much larger than models of gas giants predict (Barros et al. 2016). Its atmosphere extends about 950 km in scale height, which makes it a good target for atmosphere observations (Barros et al. 2016).","system":"The star is an early G-type star at about 5900 K and is older than the similar star WASP-114. WASP-114 hosts a planet almost four times heavier than WASP-113 b but of similar radius (Barros et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp113b-Barrosetal2016","cite":"Barros et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, D. J. A. Brown, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"WASP-113b and WASP-114b, two inflated hot Jupiters with contrasting densities","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.02341","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 204313 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity surveys of the star have revealed its inner giants: the first was found by the CORALIE survey (Ségransan et al. 2010), and eight years of velocity data from McDonald Observatory then revealed a second giant on a longer orbit (Robertson et al. 2012).","system":"The star hosts a Neptune-mass planet at 0.2 au, a giant of about 3.5 Jupiter masses on a 1900-day orbit, and a 1.6 Jupiter-mass planet on a 2800-day orbit (Robertson et al. 2012). The two giants appear to be locked in a 3:2 orbital resonance (Robertson et al. 2012). The star is metal rich (Ségransan et al. 2010).","history":"The star's richness in heavy elements adds to evidence that massive planets tend to form around such stars (Ségransan et al. 2010).","open":"A dynamical test found no region where an Earth-like planet could stay stable in the habitable zone for long (Thilliez et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd204313e-Sgransanetal2010","cite":"Ségransan et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Ségransan, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVI. Discovery of a planetary system around HD 147018 and of two long period and massive planets orbiting HD 171238 and HD 204313","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1479","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd204313e-Robertsonetal2012","cite":"Robertson et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Paul Robertson, J. Horner, Robert A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"A Second Giant Planet in 3:2 Mean-motion Resonance in the HD 204313 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.3689","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd204313e-Thilliezetal2014","cite":"Thilliez et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"E. Thilliez, L. Jouvin, S. T. Maddison et al.","title":"A dynamical test for terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of HD 204313","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.2728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-182 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from re-processing survey data to find 23 faint, borderline transit candidates, of which it was the only one confirmed (Pont et al. 2008).","observed":"Proving it was a planet took a large investment of time on the best telescopes available, including the VLT (Pont et al. 2008). The authors say this shows how hard confirmation is for transit candidates from the OGLE survey, and they map the zone where confirmation is difficult or impossible (Pont et al. 2008). It is a typical hot Jupiter, with about 1.01 Jupiter masses (Pont et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr182b-Pontetal2008","cite":"Pont et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"F. Pont, O. Tamuz, A. Udalski et al.","title":"A transiting planet among 23 new near-threshold candidates from the OGLE survey - OGLE-TR-182","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.5278","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-42 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has an equilibrium temperature of 995 K, and the orbit shows some evidence for a small but non-zero eccentricity of 0.060 (Lendl et al. 2012). Later follow-up with defocused telescopes, which blur starlight to improve precision, gave the first new observations since discovery (Southworth et al. 2016). They found no transit timing variations, and the planet's radius is inflated above what models of gas giants predict (Southworth et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp42b-Lendletal2012","cite":"Lendl et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Lendl, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-42 b and WASP-49 b: two new transiting sub-Jupiters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2757","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp42b-Southworthetal2016","cite":"Southworth et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"John Southworth, J. Tregloan-Reed, M. I. Andersen et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VIII. WASP-22, WASP-41, WASP-42 and WASP-55","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-139 f":{"text":{"why":"It illustrates how an outer giant planet can tilt inner orbits and reduce the number of transiting planets we see (Lammers & Winn 2025).","observed":"The planet does not transit its star. It was revealed through timing shifts in the transits of its neighbours, together with velocity data (Lammers & Winn 2025). Its orbit takes 355 days and its mass is about 36 Earth masses (Lammers & Winn 2025). Dynamical simulations show that the pull of the outer giant on its orbit lowers the chance that an observer sees transits of all four inner planets (Lammers & Winn 2025).","system":"It sits just outside three transiting planets and inside a giant planet (Lammers & Winn 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler139f-LammersWinn2025","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Discovery and Dynamics of the Nontransiting Planet Kepler-139f","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13160","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-55 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-55 b is one of three prototypical short-period hot Jupiters in a batch of five, with radii of about 1.1 to 1.2 Jupiter radii. These planets span effective temperatures of 1350 to 1750 K, which puts them in the range proposed for the most efficient radius inflation (Espinoza et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats55b-Espinozaetal2019","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Espinoza, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-54b-HATS-58Ab: Five New Transiting Hot Jupiters Including One with a Possible Temperate Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 10180 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with HARPS, a spectrograph that detects velocity changes below one metre per second. It showed at least five Neptune-like planets with minimum masses between 12 and 25 Earth masses, at 0.06 to 1.4 au (Lovis et al. 2011). The system was the most populated known when found. The planets are densely packed but well separated (Lovis et al. 2011).","system":"A sixth signal probably comes from a 65 Earth-mass object, and a seventh body of about 1.4 Earth masses may orbit very close to the star (Lovis et al. 2011). A re-analysis found evidence for up to nine planets (Tuomi 2012). Studies of hypothetical comets in this system find a range of outcomes, from planet encounters to ejections (Loibnegger et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The number of planets is debated, and without transits the orbital tilt is unknown, which changes the true masses (Kane & Gelino 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd10180e-Lovisetal2011","cite":"Lovis et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Lovis, D. Ségransan, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXVIII. Up to seven planets orbiting HD 10180: probing the architecture of low-mass planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.4994","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10180e-Tuomi2012","cite":"Tuomi 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Tuomi","title":"Evidence for nine planets in the HD 10180 system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1254","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10180e-Loibneggeretal2017","cite":"Loibnegger et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Birgit Loibnegger, Rudolf Dvorak, Manfred Cuntz","title":"Case Studies of Exocomets in the System of HD 10180","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.02386","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd10180e-KaneGelino2014","cite":"Kane & Gelino 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Dawn M. Gelino","title":"On the Inclination and Habitability of the HD 10180 System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.4150","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":13,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 100508 b":{"text":{"why":"The study aimed to expand the known population of cold Jupiters, whose formation is still uncertain (Wu et al. 2026).","observed":"Velocity data were combined with Gaia astrometry, which measures the star's drift across the sky over a 25-year baseline, to constrain the planet's mass and orbit tilt (Wu et al. 2026). This reduces the ambiguity of velocity-only mass estimates. The planet orbits in 5681 days and is the first confirmed planet around its star (Wu et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"How cold Jupiters form is still uncertain (Wu et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd100508b-Wuetal2026","cite":"Wu et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yiyun Wu, Guang-Yao Xiao, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Detection of Four Cold Jupiters through Combined Analyses of Radial Velocity and Astrometry Data","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11280","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-833 b":{"text":{"why":"It was one of 13 hot, possibly rocky planets validated so that the James Webb Space Telescope could study their atmospheres and surfaces through heat emission (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was confirmed using ground-based follow-up through the TESS Follow-up Observing Program and two vetting tools, DAVE and TRICERATOPS, which estimate how likely a signal is a false alarm (Giacalone et al. 2022). It was validated as a planet statistically rather than by a measured mass in that paper (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi833b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-10 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first rocky planet found by Kepler (Batalha et al. 2011).","observed":"Forty Doppler measurements confirmed the planet. The star is bright enough for asteroseismology, which reads its oscillations: 19 frequencies were found, giving precise stellar properties and showing an old star of about 11.9 billion years (Batalha et al. 2011). More than 100 further measurements with HARPS-N narrowed the mass to 3.33 Earth masses and a density of 5.8 g per cubic cm, close to an Earth-like interior (Dumusque et al. 2014).","system":"A second planet on a 45-day orbit was validated (Fressin et al. 2011). It is a solid Neptune-mass planet (Dumusque et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Interior models suggest a large iron core and a Mercury-like bulk composition (Wagner et al. 2012), while the mass measurement points to an Earth-like interior (Dumusque et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler10b-Batalhaetal2011","cite":"Batalha et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Natalie M. Batalha, William J. Borucki, Stephen T. Bryson et al.","title":"Kepler's First Rocky Planet: Kepler-10b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0605","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10b-Dumusqueetal2014","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Xavier Dumusque, Aldo S. Bonomo, Raphaëlle D. Haywood et al.","title":"The Kepler-10 Planetary System Revisited by HARPS-N: A Hot Rocky World and a Solid Neptune-Mass Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.7881","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10b-Fressinetal2011","cite":"Fressin et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"François Fressin, Guillermo Torres, Jean-Michel Désert et al.","title":"Kepler-10 c: a 2.2 Earth Radius Transiting Planet in a Multiple System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.4647","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler10b-Wagneretal2012","cite":"Wagner et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"F. W. Wagner, N. Tosi, F. Sohl et al.","title":"Rocky super-Earth interiors. Structure and internal dynamics of CoRoT-7b and Kepler-10b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201118441","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":60,"about":28,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-142 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transit durations also vary. These two changes together revealed an unseen companion of about 0.63 Jupiter masses, KOI-142 c, just outside the 2:1 resonance (Nesvorný et al. 2013). Velocity data later confirmed this companion, the first radial velocity confirmation of a planet found from transit timing (Barros et al. 2014). The planet is Neptune-class (Nesvorný et al. 2013).","system":"A third planet, Kepler-88 d, has a minimum mass of 3.04 Jupiter masses on a 1403-day, eccentric orbit (Weiss et al. 2020).","history":"The system can be used to test formation theories for near-resonant pairs of planets (Nesvorný et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi142b-Nesvornetal2013","cite":"Nesvorný et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"David Nesvorný, David Kipping, Dirk Terrell et al.","title":"KOI-142, The King of Transit Variations, is a Pair of Planets near the 2:1 Resonance","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4283","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi142b-Barrosetal2014","cite":"Barros et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, R. F. Díaz, A. Santerne et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. X. KOI-142 c: first radial velocity confirmation of a non-transiting exoplanet discovered by transit timing","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4335","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi142b-Weissetal2020","cite":"Weiss et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Eric Agol et al.","title":"The Discovery of the Long-Period, Eccentric Planet Kepler-88 d and System Characterization with Radial Velocities and Photodynamical Analysis","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.02427","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-138 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A later modelling of the light curves found a size between Mars and Earth (Almenara et al. 2018). The authors infer that planet b has a thick volatile layer, likely enriched in heavier gases (Almenara et al. 2018).","system":"The middle planet has an Earth-like density, and the outer planet has less than half Earth's density (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015). The star is much fainter and cooler than the Sun (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015). Spectra of the outer planet are flat and compatible with a water world (Piaulet et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler138b-Almenaraetal2018","cite":"Almenara et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. M. Almenara, R. F. Díaz, C. Dorn et al.","title":"Absolute densities in exoplanetary systems: photodynamical modelling of Kepler-138","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.12520","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138b-JontofHutteretal2015","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jason F. Rowe, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"The mass of the Mars-sized exoplanet Kepler-138 b from transit timing","journal":"Nature","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.07067","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138b-Piauletetal2021","cite":"Piaulet et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Piaulet, B. Benneke, J. Almenara et al.","title":"Spectroscopic Characterization of Kepler-138d: a temperate Earth-mass exoplanet that might be a water world","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #237","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-33 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its mass is only an upper limit, below 8.2 Earth masses, from modelling how the planets' transit times shift. That gives a density below 0.4 g per cubic cm, very low (Sikora et al. 2022). The study confirms low densities for planets d, e and f (Sikora et al. 2022).","system":"Five transiting planets orbit between 5 and 41 days, in nearly coplanar orbits with remarkably similar transit durations (Sikora et al. 2022). The outer three are closely packed (Sikora et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler33d-Sikoraetal2022","cite":"Sikora et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"James Sikora, Jason Rowe, Daniel Jontof-Hutter et al.","title":"Refining the Masses and Radii of the Star Kepler-33 and its Five Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00703","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 113103 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planets were followed up with the CHEOPS space telescope, which watched both transiting for about 17.5 hours (Lowson et al. 2024). Over three years, transit timing variations were limited to 4.5 minutes or less for planet b and 2.5 minutes or less for c, which constrains the orbit shapes (Lowson et al. 2024). Planet c is a mini-Neptune. The authors simulated how JWST, HST and Twinkle would see its atmosphere (Lowson et al. 2024).","system":"The star is an adolescent K-dwarf. Planet b, inside it, sits in the radius gap and may have lost or still be losing its primordial atmosphere, and the two planets orbit near a 2:1 resonance (Lowson et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip113103c-Lowsonetal2024","cite":"Lowson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"N. Lowson, G. Zhou, C. X. Huang et al.","title":"Two mini-Neptunes transiting the adolescent K-star HIP 113103 confirmed with TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.04137","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1260 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is moderately active with a complex pattern, so Gaussian process regression, a statistical method, was used to separate its signal from the planets (Georgieva et al. 2021). The authors find the planets are classic cases of the composition ambiguity typical for 2 to 3 Earth-radius planets, and tested their survival against evaporation (Georgieva et al. 2021). Modelling later concluded that planet b most likely lost all of its hydrogen-dominated envelope (Lam et al. 2023).","system":"It orbits a low-metallicity, late K-dwarf (Georgieva et al. 2021). Planet c has a 7.49-day orbit, and a third transiting planet orbits every 16.6 days (Lam et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1260b-Georgievaetal2021","cite":"Georgieva et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"I. Y. Georgieva, C. M. Persson, O. Barragán et al.","title":"Hot planets around cool stars - two short-period mini-Neptunes transiting the late K-dwarf TOI-1260","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.05653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1260b-Lametal2023","cite":"Lam et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"K. W. F. Lam, J. Cabrera, M. J. Hooton et al.","title":"Discovery of TOI-1260d and the characterization of the multiplanet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04307","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's spin-orbit angle was measured with Keck/KPF through the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the shift in starlight as the planet crosses a spinning star. It is well aligned, at 3.7 degrees (Lubin et al. 2024). Its mass of about 10 Earth masses is low for its size compared with similar planets (Lubin et al. 2022). A Hubble transmission spectrum shows no significant features, which argues against a cloud-free, solar-metallicity atmosphere (Gapp et al. 2026).","system":"Six planets orbit this star: the three transiting sub-Neptunes in near resonance, a Saturn-mass planet, a planet near the habitable zone and a super-Jupiter (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020; Orell-Miquel et al. 2023; Lubin et al. 2024). They are well aligned and nearly coplanar (Lubin et al. 2024).","history":"The aligned, flat layout suggests the system formed from a well-aligned disc (Lubin et al. 2024).","open":"The flat spectrum could come from clouds, haze or a supersolar metal content (Gapp et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939b-Lubinetal2024","cite":"Lubin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Lubin, Erik A. Petigura, Judah Van Zandt et al.","title":"The HD 191939 Exoplanet System is Well Aligned and Flat","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.06795","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939b-Lubinetal2022","cite":"Lubin et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jack Lubin, Judah Van Zandt, Rae Holcomb et al.","title":"TESS-Keck Survey. IX. Masses of Three Sub-Neptunes Orbiting HD 191939 and the Discovery of a Warm Jovian plus a Distant Substellar Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02208","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939b-Gappetal2026","cite":"Gapp et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Cyril Gapp, Lorena Acuña-Aguirre, Reza Ashtari et al.","title":"The SPACE Program. II. No Discernible Spectral Features in the Transmission Spectrum of the Sub-Neptune HD 191939 b Observed with HST/WFC3","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05962","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939b-BadenasAgustietal2020","cite":"Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mariona Badenas-Agusti, Maximilian N. Günther, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"HD 191939: Three Sub-Neptunes Transiting a Sun-like Star Only 54 pc Away","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939b-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Brightness dips from the CoRoT satellite were combined with velocity measurements from the HARPS and HIRES spectrographs to weigh the planet. Its density is low, about 0.44 g/cm3 (Ollivier et al. 2012). The orbit appears to be stretched: a fit with no prior assumptions gives an eccentricity of 0.33 ± 0.1 (Ollivier et al. 2012). Close-in giant planets usually have circular orbits, because tides from the star smooth out any stretch, so a non-circular orbit is very uncommon here (Ollivier et al. 2012).","system":"The host is a faint G5 star, with a visual magnitude of 15.63 (Ollivier et al. 2012). Its age is estimated at 6.73 ± 2.8 billion years, old enough that tides would normally have circularised the orbit (Ollivier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"Whether the eccentricity is real is still open."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot16b-Ollivieretal2012","cite":"Ollivier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Ollivier, M. Gillon, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXII. CoRoT-16b: a hot Jupiter with a hint of eccentricity around a faint solar-like star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201117460","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1246 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Mass was measured from 125 velocity observations taken with Keck/HIRES and TNG/HARPS-N (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The planet is 2.47 ± 0.08 Earth radii across and 8.8 ± 1.2 Earth masses, the second of four transiting planets in its system (Turtelboom et al. 2022). All four are sub-Neptunes, meaning planets between Earth and Neptune in size (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","system":"TOI-1246 is one of the brightest stars with four known transiting planets, and one of only six systems with measured masses and radii for all four (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The outermost of the four, TOI-1246 e, is much heavier than the three inner planets (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The two outer planets lie just beyond a 2:1 orbital resonance, with periods in a ratio of 2.03, and tug on each other enough to shift their transit times (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1246c-Turtelboometal2022","cite":"Turtelboom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emma V. Turtelboom, Courtney Dressing, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"TOI-1246: Mass Measurements for Four Transiting sub-Neptunes","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 70573 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star's velocity varies with a period of 852 ± 12 days and a semi-amplitude of 149 ± 6 m/s (Setiawan et al. 2007). Starspot activity and stellar pulsations were checked and ruled out as the cause (Setiawan et al. 2007). A low-mass companion with a minimum mass of 6.1 Jupiter masses and an orbit of about 1.76 AU was the best explanation (Setiawan et al. 2007).","system":"The host is a young G-type dwarf, about 100 million years old (Setiawan et al. 2007).","history":"","open":"The planet is in doubt. A later re-analysis of the archived FEROS spectra found no evidence of a companion around this star (Soto et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd70573b-Setiawanetal2007","cite":"Setiawan et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"J. Setiawan, P. Weise, Th. Henning et al.","title":"Evidence for a Planetary Companion around a Nearby Young Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.0441","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd70573b-Sotoetal2015","cite":"Soto et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. G. Soto, J. S. Jenkins, M. I. Jones","title":"RAFT - I. Discovery of new planetary candidates and updated orbits from archival FEROS spectra","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.04796","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1728 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet adds a data point to the sparse mass-radius map of M-dwarf planets, between Neptune-sized and Jupiter-sized worlds (Kanodia et al. 2020).","observed":"Near-infrared velocity measurements with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder gave the planet a mass of 26.78 Earth masses and a radius of 5.05 Earth radii (Kanodia et al. 2020). Its orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of about 0.057 (Kanodia et al. 2020). The team searched for helium absorption in the planet's atmosphere during transit and saw none, with an upper limit of 1.1% (Kanodia et al. 2020). Helium has been seen around GJ 3470 b, a comparable gas planet (Kanodia et al. 2020).","system":"The host is an old, inactive M0 dwarf (Kanodia et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet holds on to a helium-rich atmosphere is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1728b-Kanodiaetal2020","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Caleb I. Cañas, Gudmundur Stefansson et al.","title":"TOI-1728b: The Habitable-zone Planet Finder Confirms a Warm Super-Neptune Orbiting an M-dwarf Host","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14546","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-286 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is 2.1 ± 0.2 Earth radii across, close to a gap in the size distribution of small planets between 1.5 and 2.0 Earth radii (Díez Alonso et al. 2019). Its equilibrium temperature is 347 K, cooler than most small planets with well-measured masses (Díez Alonso et al. 2019). Orbiting near the inner edge of the habitable zone, it was expected to tug its star by only about 1.9 m/s (Díez Alonso et al. 2019). Its mass has not yet been measured.","system":"The host is an M0 dwarf that is less active than other M dwarfs with planets (Díez Alonso et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The star is relatively bright, so follow-up work on the planet's mass and on starlight filtered through its atmosphere is possible. The expected atmospheric signal is only about 5.0 ± 3.0 parts per million (Díez Alonso et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2286b-Alonsoetal2019","cite":"Alonso et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"E. Díez Alonso, J. I. González Hernández, B. Toledo-Padrón et al.","title":"A transiting super-Earth close to the inner edge of the habitable zone of an M0 dwarf star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.04739","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The object is either a low-mass brown dwarf or a super-massive planet, with a mass of about 27 Jupiter masses (Siverd et al. 2012). It was the first transiting low-mass companion found by the KELT-North survey (Siverd et al. 2012).","observed":"Spitzer eclipse measurements point to a strong hotspot on the star-facing side and little heat sharing around the globe (Beatty et al. 2014). TESS measured a phase curve, the change in brightness through one orbit. It suggests the dayside reflects about half the light that hits it, possibly from silicate clouds that form on the night side and drift onto the day side (Beatty et al. 2020). The hottest spot may be shifted east of the point under the star, by 18.3 ± 7.4 degrees (Beatty et al. 2020).","system":"The host star is slightly evolved and is stretched by the companion's tides, which adds a signal of 399 ± 19 ppm to the light curve (Beatty et al. 2020). The orbit takes about 29 hours (Siverd et al. 2012). A density of 22.1 g/cm3 made it the densest planet in one study of revised sizes (Johns et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Whether clouds really cause the high reflectivity is not settled, since simple cloud-free models cannot fit the data (Beatty et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt1b-Siverdetal2012","cite":"Siverd et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Robert J. Siverd, Thomas G. Beatty, Joshua Pepper et al.","title":"KELT-1b: A Strongly Irradiated, Highly Inflated, Short Period, 27 Jupiter-mass Companion Transiting a Mid-F Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1635","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt1b-Beattyetal2014","cite":"Beatty et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Thomas G. Beatty, Karen A. Collins, Jonathan Fortney et al.","title":"Spitzer and z' Secondary Eclipse Observations of the Highly Irradiated Transiting Brown Dwarf KELT-1b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7585","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt1b-Beattyetal2020","cite":"Beatty et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Thomas G. Beatty, Ian Wong, Tara Fetherolf et al.","title":"The TESS Phase Curve of KELT-1b Suggests a High Dayside Albedo","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10292","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt1b-Johnsetal2018","cite":"Johns et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Daniel Johns, Connor Marti, Madison Huff et al.","title":"Revised Exoplanet Radii and Habitability Using Gaia Data Release 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.04533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4010 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a sub-Saturn, 5.93 Earth radii across with a mass of 20.31 Earth masses, measured with HARPS-N velocities (Kunimoto et al. 2023). It is among the most massive sub-Saturns in multi-planet systems with short orbits (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Its orbit takes 5.4 days (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","system":"Four planets are known in the system. Planet b is a sub-Neptune in the hot-Neptune desert, a region with few planets, on a 1.3-day orbit (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Planet d is a sub-Saturn of similar size to c with a 14.7-day orbit (Kunimoto et al. 2023). A super-Jupiter-mass companion, planet e, has a long, eccentric orbit of about 762 days (Kunimoto et al. 2023). The host is a metal-rich K dwarf (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4010c-Kunimotoetal2023","cite":"Kunimoto et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michelle Kunimoto, Andrew Vanderburg, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-4010: A System of Three Large Short-period Planets with a Massive Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05308","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6330 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of two super-Jupiters found by the GEMS survey, which looks for giant planets around M dwarfs (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet has a mass of 10.00 ± 0.31 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.97 ± 0.03 Jupiter radii, measured with ground-based photometry and Habitable-zone Planet Finder velocities (Hotnisky et al. 2025). Its host is an M2 dwarf (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"A planet this heavy around a small star needs a very massive disc of gas and dust. Either the disc was unstable and collapsed directly into the planet, or it was among the most massive protoplanetary discs known and the planet grew by core accretion (Hotnisky et al. 2025). The orbital eccentricity may show how the planet moved inward (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","open":"Which of the two formation routes made the planet is not known (Hotnisky et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6330b-Hotniskyetal2025","cite":"Hotnisky et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Andrew Hotnisky, Shubham Kanodia, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Two Super-Jupiters Around M Dwarfs that May have Formed via Gravitational Instability","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/add2ef","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2368 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a survey that aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters around FGK stars brighter than Gaia magnitude 12.5, analysed in one uniform way, to test how hot Jupiters formed and moved inward (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy (Schulte et al. 2024). It is one of nine newly confirmed hot Jupiters in the first paper of the Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets survey (Schulte et al. 2024). Most of the nine have no detectable orbital eccentricity, and the two that do are other planets (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Three main ideas exist for how hot Jupiters got so close to their stars: forming in place, moving through the gas disc, and being flung onto stretched orbits that tides then shrink (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2368b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2368b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5005 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits in the Neptunian savanna near the ridge. The ridge is an excess of planets with orbital periods of about 3 to 5 days, between the sparse desert and the savanna (Castro-González et al. 2024).","observed":"Analysis used 38 HARPS velocity measurements, three sectors of TESS photometry and two ground-based transits (Castro-González et al. 2024). Several models, including circular and eccentric orbits, long-term drifts and correlated noise, were compared on how well each fit the data (Castro-González et al. 2024). The planet is a super-Neptune, 6.25 ± 0.24 Earth radii across with a mass of 32.7 ± 5.9 Earth masses (Castro-González et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a moderately bright solar-type G2 star (Castro-González et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The desert, ridge and savanna are thought to be shaped by dynamical and atmospheric processes, but their roles are not yet well understood (Castro-González et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5005b-CastroGonzlezetal2024","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Castro-González, J. Lillo-Box, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-5005 b: A super-Neptune in the savanna near the ridge","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.18129","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-103 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits less than 20% beyond its Roche limit, the distance inside which tides tear a body apart (Gillon et al. 2014). It may be strongly stretched by tides and may lose mass by spilling gas onto its star (Gillon et al. 2014).","observed":"CHEOPS observations of 12 transits detected the planet's tidal deformation at a significance of 3 sigma (Barros et al. 2022). Visible-light transmission spectra show strong sodium and potassium absorption in the cores of their lines, in a planet at about 2500 K (Lendl et al. 2017). Ground-based eclipse searches in blue light saw no dimming, so the planet reflects little light of that colour. Geometric albedos for it and two other hot Jupiters range from zero to 0.18 (Mallonn et al. 2022). Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements show the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, within large uncertainty (Addison et al. 2016).","system":"The host is an F8 star of 1.22 solar masses (Gillon et al. 2014). The planet's measured spin-orbit angle is 3 ± 33 degrees (Addison et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"How quickly the orbit is shrinking and what the planet's interior is like are goals of the tidal measurements (Barros et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp103b-Gillonetal2014","cite":"Gillon et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Gillon, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-103 b: a new planet at the edge of tidal disruption","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2784","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp103b-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, B. Akinsanmi, G. Boué et al.","title":"Detection of the tidal deformation of WASP-103b at 3 σ with CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.03328","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp103b-Lendletal2017","cite":"Lendl et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Lendl, P. E. Cubillos, J. Hagelberg et al.","title":"Signs of strong Na and K absorption in the transmission spectrum of WASP-103b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.05737","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp103b-Mallonnetal2022","cite":"Mallonn et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthias Mallonn, Enrique Herrero, Carolina von Essen","title":"Geometric Albedos at Short Optical Wavelengths for the Hot Jupiters WASP-43b, WASP-103b, and TrES-3b","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.06680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp103b-Addisonetal2016","cite":"Addison et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"B. C. Addison, C. G. Tinney, D. J. Wright et al.","title":"Spin-orbit Alignment for Three Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-103b, WASP-87b, and WASP-66b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.05754","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":28,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 96700 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet c is one of 12 super-Earths found around five solar-type stars in a HARPS survey with a time span of more than 10 years and fairly dense sampling (Unger et al. 2021). The survey is sensitive to low-mass planets on orbits up to 100 days (Unger et al. 2021). The number of planets around each star was chosen by Bayesian model comparison, a statistical way to weigh different fits. For some stars, models of stellar activity noise were compared to separate real planet signals from starspots (Unger et al. 2021).","system":"The star HD 96700 has three known planets (Unger et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd96700c-Ungeretal2021","cite":"Unger et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"N. Unger, D. Ségransan, D. Queloz et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVI. 12 super-Earths around the solar type stars HD 39194, HD 93385, HD 96700, HD 154088, and HD 189567","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10198","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 23472 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet b is one of two super-Earths confirmed earlier in a compact five-planet system. Velocities from ESPRESSO later confirmed three Earth-size planets (Barros et al. 2022). Planet b has a radius of about 2.01 Earth radii and a period of about 17.67 days (Barros et al. 2022).","system":"Five planets have periods of about 3.98, 7.90, 12.16, 17.67 and 29.80 days (Barros et al. 2022). Two of the planets are potential super-Mercuries, meaning iron-rich worlds (Barros et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd23472b-Barrosetal2022","cite":"Barros et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, O. D. S. Demangeon, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"HD 23472: a multi-planetary system with three super-Earths and two potential super-Mercuries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5688 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-density giant with a mass of 124 ± 24 Earth masses and a radius of 10.4 ± 0.7 Earth radii (Reji et al. 2025). Ground-based transits and velocities from two large telescopes were used to characterise it (Reji et al. 2025).","system":"The host star is a metal-rich M2 dwarf, with [Fe/H] of 0.47 ± 0.16 (Reji et al. 2025). A wide companion star, an M4 dwarf called TOI-5688 B, lies about 5 arcseconds away, or 1110 au in projection (Reji et al. 2025).","history":"The star's high metal content favours formation by core accretion, where a solid core grows first and then pulls in gas (Reji et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5688ab-Rejietal2025","cite":"Reji et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Varghese Reji, Shubham Kanodia, Joe P. Ninan et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-5688 A b, a Low-density Giant Orbiting a High-metallicity Early M-dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01371","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 220074 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star's velocity varies with a period of 672.1 days, and surface activity on the star is not related to the changes (Lee et al. 2013). The best explanation is a companion with a minimum mass of 11.1 Jupiter masses, orbiting at about 1.6 AU with an eccentricity of 0.14 (Lee et al. 2013). The host is an M giant, and this was the first M giant found with a planetary companion (Lee et al. 2013).","system":"The star was found to be an M giant rather than the K dwarf first thought (Lee et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd220074b-Leeetal2013","cite":"Lee et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Inwoo Han, Myeong-Gu Park","title":"Planetary companions orbiting K giant HD 208527 and M giant HD 220074","journal":"Bulletin of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 54515 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first flagged by precise measurements of the star's motion from the Hipparcos-Gaia Catalogue of Accelerations, which hinted that something massive was pulling on it (Currie et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was detected with SCExAO/CHARIS in five epochs, at 0.145 to 0.192 arcseconds from the star (Currie et al. 2026). It sits only about 3 to 4 times the smallest angle the telescope can resolve (Currie et al. 2026). It moves clockwise around its star (Currie et al. 2026). It lies near the M/L transition, a spectral-type change for cool, faint objects (Currie et al. 2026). Its mass is about 17.7 Jupiter masses, from dynamical modelling (Currie et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a young A5 star (Currie et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip54515b-Currieetal2026","cite":"Currie et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Thayne Currie, Yiting Li, Mona El Morsy et al.","title":"SCExAO/CHARIS and Gaia Direct Imaging and Astrometric Discovery of a Superjovian Planet 3─4 λ/D from the Accelerating Star HIP 54515","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02159","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2497 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive giant, and its orbit is stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.195 (Rodriguez et al. 2023). It shows no sign of being puffed up despite very high irradiation (Rodriguez et al. 2023). The lack of inflation may be explained by its high mass (Rodriguez et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a subgiant, a star starting to swell as it ages, with a surface temperature of 7360 K (Rodriguez et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2497b-Rodriguezetal2023","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Another shipment of six short-period giant planets from TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05709","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6303 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of two super-Jupiters found by the GEMS survey, which looks for giant planets around M dwarfs (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet has a mass of 7.84 ± 0.31 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.03 ± 0.06 Jupiter radii, measured with ground-based photometry and Habitable-zone Planet Finder velocities (Hotnisky et al. 2025). Its host is an M0 dwarf (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"A planet this heavy around a small star needs a very massive disc of gas and dust. Either the disc was unstable and collapsed directly into the planet, or it was among the most massive protoplanetary discs known and the planet grew by core accretion (Hotnisky et al. 2025). The orbital eccentricity may show how the planet moved inward (Hotnisky et al. 2025).","open":"Which of the two formation routes made the planet is not known (Hotnisky et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6303b-Hotniskyetal2025","cite":"Hotnisky et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Andrew Hotnisky, Shubham Kanodia, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Two Super-Jupiters Around M Dwarfs that May have Formed via Gravitational Instability","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/add2ef","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up uses a global network of autonomous wide-field telescopes (Penev et al. 2013).","observed":"The discovery light curve has near-continuous coverage over several multi-day spans, which shows the strength of using telescopes spread around the world (Penev et al. 2013). The host star is a V = 12.05 G dwarf (Penev et al. 2013). Later follow-up with the LCOGT telescope network refined the planet's transit times, and the planet is a viable target for atmospheric study (Edwards et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats1b-Penevetal2013","cite":"Penev et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"K. Penev, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-1b: The First Transiting Planet Discovered by the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1524","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats1b-Edwardsetal2021","cite":"Edwards et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Billy Edwards, Cynthia Ho, Hannah Osborne et al.","title":"Original Research By Young Twinkle Students (ORBYTS): Ephemeris Refinement of Transiting Exoplanets III","journal":"Astronomy: Theory, Observations and Methods","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10350","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3664 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters have rarely been studied around stars late in their main-sequence life, so the planet was confirmed to help fill that gap (Battley et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-3664 b was confirmed with follow-up spectra from three instruments: CARMENES, CORALIE and MINERVA-Australis (Battley et al. 2026). It was announced together with two other new hot Jupiters, TOI-4034 b and TOI-6564 b (Battley et al. 2026). Its measured size is 1.22 Jupiter radii and its mass is 0.36 Jupiter masses, so it is puffy for its weight (Battley et al. 2026). It is one of the few hot Jupiters known around a star nearing the end of its main-sequence life (Battley et al. 2026).","system":"Its star is approaching the terminal age main sequence, the point where hydrogen in the core runs low and the star begins to swell (Battley et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"How hot Jupiters change as their stars age is still poorly explored (Battley et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3664b-Battleyetal2026","cite":"Battley et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew P. Battley, Marina Lafarga, Edward Gillen et al.","title":"TOI-3664 b, TOI-4034 b, and TOI-6564 b: three new hot Jupiters around stars approaching the terminal age main sequence","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.05295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-288 B b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first spotted by volunteers on the Exoplanet Explorers project, not by the automated search (Feinstein et al. 2019). An earlier systematics correction had trimmed one transit from the start of the data, which kept the automated search from flagging it (Kohler 2019). Follow-up observations indicate it likely orbits the fainter of two low-mass stars, every 31.39 days (Feinstein et al. 2019). Its equilibrium temperature is 226 K, which places it in or near the habitable zone of its star (Feinstein et al. 2019). Its size of 1.9 Earth radii falls in a gap in the observed planet-size distribution where planets are less common (Feinstein et al. 2019).","system":"The two host stars are both low-mass M dwarfs, separated by about 55 au as seen on the sky (Feinstein et al. 2019). The planet orbits one of them at more than 0.1 au (Feinstein et al. 2019).","history":"Planets of this size may be in a short-lived phase of changing radius (Feinstein et al. 2019).","open":"Which of the two stars the planet orbits is likely but not certain, and further follow-up may show how it formed (Feinstein et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2288bb-Feinsteinetal2019","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Joshua E. Schlieder, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"K2-288Bb: A Small Temperate Planet in a Low-mass Binary System Discovered by Citizen Scientists","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.02789","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2288bb-Kohler2019","cite":"Kohler 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"Citizen Scientists Find an Unexpected Planet","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-17 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"NGTS-17 b was announced together with three other hot Jupiters from the Next-Generation Transit Survey: NGTS-15 b, 16 b and 18 b (Tilbrook et al. 2021). All four circle Sun-like stars in under 5 days, at 0.039 to 0.052 au, so all are strongly irradiated (Tilbrook et al. 2021). That heating is high enough for mechanisms that puff up giant planets to work (Tilbrook et al. 2021). Comparison with statistical studies shows NGTS-17 b is likely inflated, meaning larger than its mass alone would give, although some models disagree (Tilbrook et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The relationships that govern how much giant planets swell under heating remain poorly understood (Tilbrook et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts17b-Tilbrooketal2021","cite":"Tilbrook et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Rosanna H. Tilbrook, Matthew R. Burleigh, Jean C. Costes et al.","title":"NGTS 15b, 16b, 17b, and 18b: four hot Jupiters from the Next-Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10302","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63433 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Mass measurements of HD 63433 c rely on a radial-velocity study of its young star, giving 15.5 Earth masses with an uncertainty of 3.9 (Mallorquín Díaz et al. 2024). With a radius of 2.7 Earth radii, the planet is a mini-Neptune of intermediate age (Mallorquín Díaz et al. 2024). A helium and hydrogen-alpha search for escaping atmosphere covered both inner planets, and hints of helium showed up only for planet b (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Computer simulations suggest future giant telescopes could detect molecules in its atmosphere using high-resolution spectra (Dubey et al. 2025).","system":"The star is about 400 million years old and hosts two close-in sub-Neptunes with periods near 7.1 and 20.5 days (Damasso et al. 2023). Its youth lets astronomers watch planets early in their evolution (Damasso et al. 2023).","history":"Models of atmospheric loss through photoevaporation, where starlight strips gas from a planet, were used to follow how its mass and radius have changed and will change (Damasso et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63433c-Dazetal2024","cite":"Díaz et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Manuel Mallorquín Díaz, Nicolas Lodieu, Víctor J. Sánchez Béjar et al.","title":"Mass and radius determination of young planets and the implications for their gas envelope evolution","journal":"EAS2024, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433c-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433c-Dubeyetal2025","cite":"Dubey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Dwaipayan Dubey, Liton Majumdar, Charles Beichman et al.","title":"Quantified Estimation of Molecular Detections across Different Classes of Neptunian Atmospheres Using Cross-correlation Spectroscopy: Prospects for Future Extremely Large Telescopes with High-resolution Spectrographs","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01142","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd63433c-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Locci, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XLII. A characterisation study of the multi-planet system around the 400 Myr-old star HD 63433 (TOI-1726)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15242","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 65 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HIP 65 A b is a massive planet of 3.213 Jupiter masses that crosses the edge of its star in a grazing transit, so its radius is poorly constrained at about 2.03 Jupiter radii (Nielsen et al. 2020). It orbits at less than twice the distance at which the star's gravity would tear it apart, and is expected to spiral into the star in 80 million years to a few billion years (Nielsen et al. 2020). The light curve from TESS shows brightness changes from the planet's lit side, from tidal stretching of the star, and from Doppler boosting (Nielsen et al. 2020). An apparent change in transit depth over time is probably an artefact of the grazing geometry (Wang & Espinoza 2024). A search of transit times in four hot-Jupiter systems including this one looked for signs of orbital shrinking (Griffiths et al. 2026).","system":"The star has a companion, HIP 65 B, about 269 au away, seen 3.95 arcseconds apart on the sky (Nielsen et al. 2020).","history":"Theory predicts the tidal process needed for rapid orbital decay only in one of the four systems checked, WASP-173 (Griffiths et al. 2026).","open":"Whether the planet's orbit is shrinking is not settled, and its true radius is uncertain (Nielsen et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip65ab-Nielsenetal2020","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, R. Brahm, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"Three short-period Jupiters from TESS. HIP 65Ab, TOI-157b, and TOI-169b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.05932","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65ab-WangEspinoza2024","cite":"Wang & Espinoza 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gavin Wang, Néstor Espinoza","title":"A Blind Search for Transit Depth Variability with TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02154","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip65ab-Griffithsetal2026","cite":"Griffiths et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. W. Griffiths, J. Southworth, L. Alegre et al.","title":"A search for transit-timing variations in the transiting hot-Jupiter systems HIP 65, NGTS-6, NGTS-10, and WASP-173","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14634","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-79 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-79 e is the outermost of four planets whose masses were measured from small shifts in their transit times, caused by the planets tugging on each other (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). All four have low densities for their sizes, and the system is a compact one with periods near a 1:2:4:6 chain, from 13.5 to 81.1 days (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Timing changes also showed all four orbit the same star (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","system":"The four planets are 3.5 to 7 times Earth's size and lie close to resonance, where their orbital periods are simple ratios (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014). Kepler-79 d has the lowest bulk density yet found among planets below Saturn's mass (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014).","history":"Models of the three planets known at first suggest they did not form where they are now and migrated inward through the gas disc, stopping at its inner edge (Wang et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler79e-JontofHutteretal2014","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jack J. Lissauer, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-79's Low Density Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2642","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler79e-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Su Wang, Jianghui Ji, Ji-Lin Zhou","title":"Predicting the Configuration of a Planetary System: KOI-152 Observed by Kepler","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2431","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-108 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant-planet systems are mostly flat, like the Solar System, and few have had their orbit tilts measured (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","observed":"Kepler-108 b is one of two roughly Saturn-mass planets with periods of about 49 and 190 days (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). Their orbits are tilted against each other by about 24 degrees, with a moderate eccentricity of at least 0.1 (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). Both are unusual for giant planets (Mills & Fabrycky 2017). The orbital layout is hard to explain by migration through a gas disc alone (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","system":"The star has a distant binary companion about 300 au away (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","history":"Planet-planet scattering, where close encounters between planets fling them onto tilted, eccentric orbits, could explain the tilt (Mills & Fabrycky 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler108b-MillsFabrycky2017","cite":"Mills & Fabrycky 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky","title":"Kepler-108: A Mutually Inclined Giant Planet System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04485","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-291 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The mass of K2-291 b came from 75 radial-velocity measurements taken with two spectrographs, one on Keck I and one on the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo (Kosiarek et al. 2019). The star's own magnetic activity creates false signals, so the team modelled it with a statistical tool called a Gaussian process, which also sharpened the mass (Kosiarek et al. 2019). Adaptive-optics imaging searched for stellar companions (Kosiarek et al. 2019). The mass is 6.49 Earth masses and the bulk density is about 8.8 g/cm3, consistent with an Earth-like mix of rock and iron and no substantial gas envelope (Kosiarek et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2291b-Kosiareketal2019","cite":"Kosiarek et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Molly R. Kosiarek, Sarah Blunt, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"K2-291b: A Rocky Super-Earth in a 2.2 day Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.04558","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 60779 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the inner planet HD 60779 b. It transits its bright, nearby Sun-like star every 30 days and is a sub-Neptune of about 3.25 Earth radii with a mass of about 14 Earth masses (DiTomasso et al. 2026). A second, more massive planet orbits farther out, and it is HD 60779 c (DiTomasso et al. 2026). The star is bright at V = 7.2 and 35 parsecs away, which suits precise radial-velocity work (DiTomasso et al. 2026). The system was measured with 313 radial-velocity measurements from the HARPS-N spectrograph (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","system":"The star is Sun-like, with 1.05 solar masses (DiTomasso et al. 2026). Planet b transits it every 29.99 days (DiTomasso et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd60779c-DiTomassoetal2026","cite":"DiTomasso et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Victoria DiTomasso, David Charbonneau, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"The HD 60779 Planetary System: A Transiting Sub-Neptune on a 30 Day Orbit and a More Massive Outer World","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.16805","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 176986 c":{"text":{"why":"The RoPES radial-velocity program targets quiet G and K stars, in a search for very low-mass planets on close orbits (Suarez Mascareno et al. 2017).","observed":"HD 176986 c is a super-Earth with a mass of 9.18 Earth masses, on a 16.82-day orbit at 0.119 au (Nari et al. 2025). A second super-Earth, HD 176986 b, of 5.74 Earth masses, orbits closer in (Nari et al. 2025). Both orbits are consistent with circular (Nari et al. 2025). The star has been monitored for years with the HARPS and HARPS-N spectrographs, from mid-2004 onward (Suarez Mascareno et al. 2017).","system":"The star is a K2.5 dwarf that is only modestly active, yet its activity pattern is complex (Nari et al. 2025). Its magnetic cycle and rotation were studied alongside the planets (Nari et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether analysis techniques can separate such small planet signals from stellar activity is still being tested on this star (Nari et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd176986c-Mascarenoetal2017","cite":"Mascareno et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez, R. Rebolo et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS + HARPS-N data (Suarez Mascareno+, 2018)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36120041","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd176986c-Narietal2025","cite":"Nari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Nari, A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS Rvs and activity indicators (Nari+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-32 c":{"text":{"why":"The system is the richest known set of transiting planets around an M dwarf, so it offers a model for how compact systems form around the most common kind of star (Swift et al. 2013).","observed":"Five planets transit Kepler-32, and new observations ruled out false-positive explanations, allowing the whole system to be validated at once (Swift et al. 2013). It has the richest known set of transiting planets around an M dwarf (Swift et al. 2013). The authors used its architecture to argue how such systems form, and presented it as a prototype for compact systems throughout the Galaxy (Swift et al. 2013).","system":"All five planets orbit within one-third the size of Mercury's orbit, and the innermost sits only 4.3 stellar radii from the star's surface (Swift et al. 2013). Three adjacent planets have orbital periods close to simple ratios (Swift et al. 2013). Kepler-32 is a typical M dwarf, the kind that makes up about 70% of all stars in the Galaxy (Swift et al. 2013).","history":"The formation argument rests on the dust sublimation radius, a minimum-mass protoplanetary nebula, and the near-commensurability of three planets (Swift et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler32c-Swiftetal2013","cite":"Swift et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Jonathan J. Swift, John Asher Johnson, Timothy D. Morton et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. IV. Kepler-32 as a Prototype for the Formation of Compact Planetary Systems throughout the Galaxy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-49 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HAT-P-49 b is a hot Jupiter of 1.73 Jupiter masses and 1.41 Jupiter radii, and it orbits one of the 25 brightest stars known to host a transiting planet (Bieryla et al. 2014). Its star spins fast, at 16 km/s, which makes it a strong target for Rossiter-McLaughlin follow-up, a measurement of the angle between the orbit and the star's spin (Bieryla et al. 2014). The Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement later showed a polar orbit, with a projected angle of -85.3 degrees plus or minus 1.7 (Balkóová et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a slightly evolved F star with 1.54 solar masses and 1.83 solar radii (Bieryla et al. 2014). It is the fourth most massive star known to host a transiting planet with well-determined mass and radius (Bieryla et al. 2014).","history":"How hot Jupiters migrate inward is not yet fully understood, and orbit angles such as this one help constrain the processes involved (Balkóová et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp49b-Bierylaetal2014","cite":"Bieryla et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. Bieryla, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-49b: A 1.7 M <SUB>J</SUB> Planet Transiting a Bright 1.5 M <SUB>☉</SUB> F-star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.5460","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp49b-Balkovetal2026","cite":"Balkóová et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Z. Balkóová, J. Žák, M. Skarka et al.","title":"Aligned, Misaligned, and Polar Orbits of Hot Jupiters: Measuring Spin-orbit Angles via Doppler Tomography with HARPS-N","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11313","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-384 f":{"text":{"why":"The validation effort aimed to identify promising new K2 planets for further characterization (Christiansen et al. 2022).","observed":"K2-384 f was validated, meaning shown statistically to be a real planet, with 59 others in a batch of K2 results (Christiansen et al. 2022). It is among the planets best suited to transmission spectroscopy, where starlight passes through a planet's atmosphere (Christiansen et al. 2022). The planet comes from a validation that used reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging and checks on shifts of the star's image during transits (Christiansen et al. 2022).","system":"K2-384 has a compact, TRAPPIST-1-like system of five small planets around a mid-M dwarf (Christiansen et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2384f-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-34 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-34 b was re-measured using space-based TESS light curves and high-precision parallax from the Gaia mission, which gives the star's distance and so its size (Louden & Hartman 2021). The revised planet has 0.951 Jupiter masses and 1.282 Jupiter radii (Louden & Hartman 2021). The uncertainties in some parameters shrank by as much as a factor of 7 (Louden & Hartman 2021). The revised host star has 0.952 solar masses and 0.9381 solar radii (Louden & Hartman 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats34b-LoudenHartman2021","cite":"Louden & Hartman 2021","year":"2021","authors":"E. M. Louden, J. D. Hartman","title":"HATS-34b and HATS-46b: Exoplanet Re-characterization Using TESS and Gaia","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1231 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1231 b is a temperate planet of 3.65 Earth radii and about 15.5 Earth masses, with an equilibrium temperature of 330 K (Burt et al. 2021). It is one of the coolest small planets reachable for atmospheric study so far (Burt et al. 2021). Its star is bright in the near infrared, which makes it a target for the Hubble and James Webb space telescopes (Burt et al. 2021). A Hubble program was set up to observe its transmission spectrum and the star's ultraviolet emission (Kreidberg et al. 2020). Atmospheric work could allow the first comparison with K2-18 b in the 250 to 350 K range (Burt et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a nearby M3 dwarf, and a kinematic age of about 13 billion years makes it an outlier among six M dwarfs studied, although the uncertainty is 4 to 6 billion years (Sairam & Madhusudhan 2026). Its high systemic velocity of 70.5 km/s may allow escaping hydrogen to be detected (Burt et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"What the atmosphere is made of is not yet known (Burt et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1231b-Burtetal2021","cite":"Burt et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Jennifer A. Burt, Diana Dragomir, Paul Mollière et al.","title":"TOI-1231 b: A Temperate, Neptune-sized Planet Transiting the Nearby M3 Dwarf NLTT 24399","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.08077","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1231b-Kreidbergetal2020","cite":"Kreidberg et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Laura Kreidberg, Jennifer Burt, Ian Crossfield et al.","title":"Exploring the Demographics of Exo-Neptunes: Atmospheric Characterization of a Cool Sub-Neptune from TESS","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1231b-SairamMadhusudhan2026","cite":"Sairam & Madhusudhan 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Lalitha Sairam, Nikku Madhusudhan","title":"Stellar ages of M dwarf hosts of temperate sub-Neptunes","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17505","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6255 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-6255 b is an Earth-sized planet of 1.079 Earth radii and 1.44 Earth masses whose orbit takes only 5.7 hours (Dai et al. 2024). It lies just outside its Roche limit, the distance inside which a planet would be torn apart, with an orbital period 1.13 times the limiting period (Dai et al. 2024). Strong tides likely stretch it into an egg-like shape with a long axis about 10% longer than its short axis (Dai et al. 2024). Tidal decay should bring it to the Roche limit in roughly 400 million years (Dai et al. 2024). The mass came from two instruments, the Keck Planet Finder and CARMENES (Dai et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"A planet torn apart this way could produce the signs of engulfment seen in stars with unusually high levels of heavy elements compared with their twin companions (Dai et al. 2024). Magnetic interaction with the star might melt the interior and speed the decay (Dai et al. 2024).","open":"It is a top target for phase-curve observations with the James Webb Space Telescope (Dai et al. 2024). Models for a JWST program covering five rocky planets, this one included, mostly favour bare rock without a detectable atmosphere (Sastre et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6255b-Daietal2024","cite":"Dai et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Fei Dai, Andrew W. Howard, Samuel Halverson et al.","title":"An Earth-sized Planet on the Verge of Tidal Disruption","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21167","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi6255b-Sastreetal2026","cite":"Sastre et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mariana Sastre, Tim Lichtenberg, Lisa Dang et al.","title":"Thermal Evolution of Lava Planets Across System Ages: Predictions for Hell of a Survey","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20155","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 57274 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HIP 57274 b is a super-Earth with a minimum mass of 11.6 Earth masses, found by Doppler observations from Keck Observatory (Fischer et al. 2012). Its orbit is slightly eccentric, with e = 0.19 plus or minus 0.1 (Fischer et al. 2012). Chance of a transit across the star is only 6.5% (Fischer et al. 2012).","system":"Two more planets orbit farther out: one of at least 0.4 Jupiter masses every 32.0 days, and one of at least 0.53 Jupiter masses every 432 days (Fischer et al. 2012). The star is a K4 dwarf (Fischer et al. 2012).","history":"Planets with masses below 12 Earth masses are often found in multi-planet systems, at 56% plus or minus 18% against 20% plus or minus 8% for Jupiter-mass planets (Fischer et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip57274b-Fischeretal2012","cite":"Fischer et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Eric Gaidos, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"M2K. II. A Triple-planet System Orbiting HIP 57274","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.2926","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4342 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The mass of TOI-4342 b is 7.3 Earth masses, from precise radial velocities measured with the ESPRESSO spectrograph (Parc et al. 2026). It is a sub-Neptune of 2.33 Earth radii, with a period of 5.54 days (Parc et al. 2026). The photometry was refined with data from TESS and the LCOGT network, and the host star was characterised with the NIRPS and ESPRESSO spectrographs (Parc et al. 2026). The aim was to learn the planet's density and possible composition (Parc et al. 2026).","system":"The M0 dwarf hosts a second sub-Neptune of nearly the same size, 2.35 Earth radii, on a 10.69-day orbit with a mass of 4.8 Earth masses (Parc et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4342b-Parcetal2026","cite":"Parc et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Léna Parc, Charles Cadieux, Nolan Grieves et al.","title":"Densities of small planets around the M dwarfs TOI-4336 A and TOI-4342 with ESPRESSO: Three sub-Neptunes, one super-Earth, and a Neptune-mass candidate","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22115","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 191939 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 191939 g is a non-transiting planet found in radial-velocity data, with a minimum mass of 13.5 Earth masses and a period of about 280 days (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). It lies within the conservative habitable zone of its star and near a 1:3 resonance with planet e (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). The result came from 362 radial-velocity measurements from four high-resolution spectrographs (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","system":"Three sub-Neptunes transit the star, plus two distant Jupiter-like planets (Lubin & Petigura 2024). All of the planets are within about 10 degrees of one shared orbital plane, and planet b's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Lubin & Petigura 2024). The transiting planets have similar radii of 3.2 to 3.4 Earth radii (Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd191939g-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, G. Nowak, F. Murgas et al.","title":"HD 191939 revisited: New and refined planet mass determinations, and a new planet in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00667","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939g-LubinPetigura2024","cite":"Lubin & Petigura 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Lubin, Erik Petigura","title":"HD 191939: a multi-planet system with a shared and aligned orbital plane","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd191939g-BadenasAgustietal2020","cite":"Badenas-Agusti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Mariona Badenas-Agusti, Maximilian N. Günther, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"HD 191939: Three Sub-Neptunes Transiting a Sun-like Star Only 54 pc Away","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.03958","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 196067 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the discovery was built to test whether stars with outer giant planets are more or less likely to have small inner planets (Delisle et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet turned up in a long-term survey with the CORALIE, HARPS and ESPRESSO spectrographs. The survey was designed so that stars with and without outer giant planets are observed in the same way and can be compared fairly (Delisle et al. 2025). HD 196067 belongs to the group that has an outer giant planet. Its inner planet is one of the first three detections of that kind in the sample (Delisle et al. 2025).","system":"The star has an outer giant planet in addition to this inner one (Delisle et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether outer giant planets and inner light planets tend to occur together is debated. Formation models predict either a link or the opposite, and earlier surveys disagreed (Delisle et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd196067c-Delisleetal2025","cite":"Delisle et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J.-B. Delisle, J. P. Faria, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"Architecture of planetary systems with and without outer giant planets: I. Inner planet detections around HD 23079, HD 196067, and HD 86226","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26232","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-355 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up was a collaboration between professional and amateur astronomers (Castro-González et al. 2022).","observed":"Ten amateur astronomers searched the brightness records of 20 427 stars from the last K2 campaign in this study by eye (Castro-González et al. 2022). They found 42 planet candidates, 18 of them new. The follow-up work used older images and spectra plus new sharp images, and ended with four newly confirmed planets, K2-355 b among them, and 14 candidates (Castro-González et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2355b-CastroGonzlezetal2022","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. Castro-González, E. Díez Alonso, J. Menéndez Blanco et al.","title":"The K2-OjOS Project: New and revisited planets and candidates in K2 campaigns 5, 16, & 18","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.03346","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"pi Men c":{"text":{"why":"Astronomers have treated it as one of the best candidates for a water-rich world (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019).","observed":"Hubble observing time was requested to watch two transits in far-ultraviolet light, looking for hydrogen, oxygen and carbon absorbing starlight high above the planet (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019). Finding oxygen and carbon would help show whether the planet is rich in water or other heavy gases (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019). Models say the strong starlight drives a vigorous outflow of gas from the planet (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019).","system":"The system also has an outer giant planet, whose orbit was analysed with satellite position measurements of the star and radial velocities (De Rosa et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Its bulk density is consistent with 50 to 100 per cent water by volume, but this has not been confirmed (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019). One model of atmospheric escape predicts it could be losing gas at 10 million grams per second or more today (Gupta & Schlichting 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pimenc-Munozetal2019","cite":"Munoz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Antonio Garcia Munoz, Juan Cabrera, Luca Fossati et al.","title":"Pi Men c: The first confirmed water world?","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pimenc-DeRosaetal2020","cite":"De Rosa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. J. De Rosa, R. Dawson, E. L. Nielsen","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: pi Men radial velocity curves (De Rosa+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36400073","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pimenc-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-11 has six transiting planets, all orbiting closer to their star than Venus is to the Sun, which made it unique among more than 160 000 stars observed at the time (Lissauer & Team 2011). The host star turned out to be a young solar twin, and a fresh analysis raised the densities of all six planets by between 20 and 95 per cent (Bedell et al. 2017). Even so, the planets are in the class of puffy, low-density worlds (Bedell et al. 2017).","system":"All six planets orbit within 0.5 AU of the star. The inner five move on nearly circular, coplanar orbits within 0.25 AU (Quintana et al. 2011).","history":"Computer simulations tested whether such a tightly packed system could have formed in place from a massive disc of gas and dust (Quintana et al. 2011).","open":"Whether planets like this are scaled-up rocky worlds wrapped in thick hydrogen-rich gas, or miniature Neptunes made mostly of water, is still being worked out (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11d-LissauerTeam2011","cite":"Lissauer & Team 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Jack J. Lissauer, Kepler Science Team","title":"Kepler-11: Oddball Or Extreme Member Of A Class Of Densely-packed Planetary Systems?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11d-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11d-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11d-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-58 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-density, inflated giant, meaning it is larger than its mass would suggest (Hébrard et al. 2013). A search of space-telescope brightness records found no further planets in the system, down to the size of Neptune-like worlds (Maciejewski 2022). Transit timing, which would reveal unseen planets tugging on this one, showed nothing either (Maciejewski 2022).","system":"","history":"Such an empty neighbourhood fits with giant planets reaching tight orbits by being thrown onto stretched orbits first and settling later (Maciejewski 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp58b-Hbrardetal2013","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Hebrard, Cameron A. Collier, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Four new WASP transiting close-in giant planets (Hebrard+, 2013)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35490134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp58b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-56 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Brightness measurements during the planet's passage behind its star gave the first optical-light detection of heat from an exoplanet made with ground-based telescopes (Sing & López-Morales 2009). The glow corresponds to a temperature near 2718 K, with a very low reflectivity and little sign of strong winds carrying heat around (Sing & López-Morales 2009). Detailed modelling of the candidate's light and velocity changes ruled out a hidden pair of stars as the cause of the signal (Torres et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"Calculations suggest the planet could be a survivor of mass loss as it filled its Roche lobe, the region within which its gas is held (Sasselov 2003).","open":"Measurements are not yet precise enough to tell a simple glowing blackbody from an atmosphere with strong absorbers such as titanium oxide (Sing & López-Morales 2009). Models need 0.1 to 0.5 per cent of the incoming starlight to be turned into heat deep inside to explain the large radius (Chabrier et al. 2004)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr56b-SingLpezMorales2009","cite":"Sing & López-Morales 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. K. Sing, M. López-Morales","title":"Ground-based secondary eclipse detection of the very-hot Jupiter OGLE-TR-56b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.1876","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr56b-Torresetal2005","cite":"Torres et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Guillermo Torres, Maciej Konacki, Dimitar D. Sasselov et al.","title":"Testing Blend Scenarios for Extrasolar Transiting Planet Candidates. II. OGLE-TR-56","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0410157","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr56b-Sasselov2003","cite":"Sasselov 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Dimitar D. Sasselov","title":"The New Transiting Planet OGLE-TR-56b: Orbit and Atmosphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0303403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr56b-Chabrieretal2004","cite":"Chabrier et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"G. Chabrier, T. Barman, I. Baraffe et al.","title":"The Evolution of Irradiated Planets: Application to Transits","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0401487","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6054.02":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is half of a pair of candidate sub-Neptunes orbiting an F-type star, with periods near 7.5 and 12.6 days (Kroft & Beatty 2024). Mass measurements are being made with the NEID spectrograph and the Keck Planet Finder. Early joint fits suggest both planets have low bulk densities, so they could carry atmospheres rich in hydrogen (Kroft & Beatty 2024).","system":"The other planet in the pair, TOI-6054.01, orbits closer in, with a period near 7.5 days (Kroft & Beatty 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi605402-KroftBeatty2024","cite":"Kroft & Beatty 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Max Kroft, Thomas Beatty","title":"A Pair of Hot Sub-Neptunes Around a Sun-like Star","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #244","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-137 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet circles its star in just 4.3 hours, which was the second shortest orbital period known when it was reported, only 4 minutes longer than that of KOI 1843.03, another planet around an M dwarf (Smith et al. 2018). Older images, sharp adaptive-optics images, velocity measurements and light-curve fits showed that no plausible pair of eclipsing stars could explain the dips, which confirmed the planet (Smith et al. 2018). The planet must contain more than 45 per cent iron by mass (Smith et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2137b-Smithetal2018","cite":"Smith et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, J. Cabrera, Sz Csizmadia et al.","title":"K2-137 b: an Earth-sized planet in a 4.3-h orbit around an M-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6478 b":{"text":{"why":"Planet formation around very low-mass M dwarfs is of particular interest because it tests how outcomes scale with the star's mass (Scott et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet is a cold, Neptune-like world with an equilibrium temperature of 204 K around a very small M5 star that belongs to the Milky Way's thick disc (Scott et al. 2025). Velocity measurements give only an upper mass limit of 9.9 Earth masses, so the planet is less dense than expected for its size (Scott et al. 2025). Hubble observations found a spectral feature at 1.4 micrometres with a signal-to-noise ratio of about 6, the strongest yet seen in a sub-Neptune with that instrument (Triantafillides et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Hubble's narrow wavelength range cannot tell whether the feature comes from water or methane, although atmosphere models favour methane (Triantafillides et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6478b-Scottetal2025","cite":"Scott et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Madison G. Scott, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud, Khalid Barkaoui et al.","title":"TOI-6478 b: a cold underdense Neptune transiting a fully convective M dwarf from the thick disc","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.06848","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi6478b-Triantafillidesetal2026","cite":"Triantafillides et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Anastasia Triantafillides, Jason S. Pero, Angelique Kahle et al.","title":"The SPACE Program IV: Atmospheric Features on the Cold Transiting Sub-Neptune TOI-6478 b","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25230","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-151 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed as real using light-curve analysis, adaptive-optics imaging, and optical spectra of its low-mass star (Hirano et al. 2018). It is the one planet in that study for which ground-based transit photometry was added, which sharpened the timing of its transits (Hirano et al. 2018). It is one of 12 newly validated planets around cool, low-mass stars observed by K2 (Hirano et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2151b-Hiranoetal2018","cite":"Hirano et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Fei Dai, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"Exoplanets around Low-mass Stars Unveiled by K2","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.03239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 213240 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a giant on an eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.45 and a minimum mass of 4.5 Jupiter masses in the discovery data (Santos et al. 2001). Its orbit was described as typical of the long-period giant planets known at that time (Santos et al. 2001).","system":"Computer models of the star's disc suggest that if a giant planet moved inwards during planet formation, any rocky planets would end up with more magnesium-rich silicates and much more water (Carter-Bond et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd213240b-Santosetal2001","cite":"Santos et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"N. C. Santos, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets VI. New long period giant planets around HD 28185 and HD 213240","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20011366","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd213240b-CarterBondetal2014","cite":"Carter-Bond et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Jade C. Carter-Bond, David P. O'Brien, Sean N. Raymond","title":"Migration & Extra-solar Terrestrial Planets: Watering the Planets","journal":"Formation, Detection, and Characterization of Extrasolar Habitable Planets","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S174392131301288X","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 74890 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the discovery aimed to find how planets depend on the mass and metal content of their stars (Jones et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet orbits a giant star, one of four new planetary systems found around such stars in a velocity survey of 166 giants (Jones et al. 2016). The survey used the FEROS and CHIRON spectrographs over six years to follow the stars (Jones et al. 2016). Giant planets are known to be found more often around metal-rich stars and around more massive stars (Jones et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip74890b-Jonesetal2016","cite":"Jones et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"M. I. Jones, J. S. Jenkins, R. Brahm et al.","title":"Four new planets around giant stars and the mass-metallicity correlation of planet-hosting stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.03738","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 41004 B b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The object orbits the fainter star of a double-star system, and is described as a brown dwarf of about 18.6 Jupiter masses at a distance of 0.0177 AU (Satyal & Musielak 2016). A planet of about 2.5 Jupiter masses orbits the brighter star of the pair (Zucker et al. 2003). Space-telescope brightness records show a 31-hour variation that probably comes from the faint star, which seems to be spun up to match its close companion's orbit and to be very magnetically active (Ayres & Buzasi 2021).","system":"The two stars are 22 AU apart and have masses of 0.7 and 0.4 times the Sun (Satyal & Musielak 2016). The planet around the brighter star orbits at 1.7 AU with an eccentricity of 0.39, and calculations found regular, stable orbits for it (Satyal & Musielak 2016).","history":"","open":"The red dwarf may someday swallow its brown dwarf companion (Ayres & Buzasi 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd41004bb-SatyalMusielak2016","cite":"Satyal & Musielak 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. Satyal, Z. E. Musielak","title":"Stability of a planet in the HD 41004 binary system","journal":"Astronomische Nachrichten","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/asna.201512305","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd41004bb-Zuckeretal2003","cite":"Zucker et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"S. Zucker, T. Mazeh, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"Multi-order TODCOR: Application to observations taken with the CORALIE echelle spectrograph. II. A planet in the system <ASTROBJ>HD 41004</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20040384","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd41004bb-AyresBuzasi2021","cite":"Ayres & Buzasi 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Tom Ayres, Derek Buzasi","title":"HD 41004: A Photometric Sheep in Wolf's Clothing?","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ac3255","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 37605 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has an eccentric orbit of about 55 days and a Jupiter-like mass (Wang et al. 2012). A search using the predicted time of transit, with ground photometry and the MOST satellite, found that the planet does not transit its star (Wang et al. 2012). The brightness records show the star is old and inactive (Wang et al. 2012).","system":"A second planet, HD 37605 c, was found with a period of about 7.5 years, a low eccentricity and a minimum mass near 3.4 Jupiter masses (Wang et al. 2012). Calculations show very little interaction between the two planets and no significant transit timing variation (Wang et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd37605b-Wangetal2012","cite":"Wang et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Xuesong, Sharon Wang, Jason T. Wright, William Cochran et al.","title":"The Discovery of HD 37605c and a Dispositive Null Detection of Transits of HD 37605b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.6985","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 806 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about the system concerns its inner planet GJ 806 b, which transits every 0.93 days and has a radius of 1.331 Earth radii (Palle et al. 2023).","system":"GJ 806 is a bright, nearby M dwarf 12 parsecs away (Palle et al. 2023). A third signal at 13.6 days shows up in the velocities, but it could be a third planet of about 8.5 Earth masses or stellar activity (Palle et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the 13.6-day signal is a planet or stellar activity is not settled (Palle et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj806c-Palleetal2023","cite":"Palle et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"E. Palle, J. Orell-Miquel, M. Brady et al.","title":"GJ 806 (TOI-4481): A bright nearby multi-planetary system with a transiting hot low-density super-Earth","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06873","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1860 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was validated as part of a search for hot, possibly rocky planets that the James Webb Space Telescope could study through their emitted light (Giacalone et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet was shown very likely to be real using ground-based follow-up and two statistical vetting tools, DAVE and TRICERATOPS (Giacalone et al. 2022). Its host star is the youngest solar twin known to host a planet, at 133 million years old, give or take 26 million (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1860b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2084 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed to be very likely real using spectra of the star, sharp imaging from Gemini telescopes, archived images and statistical tests (Barkaoui et al. 2023). It has an equilibrium temperature of 527 K and receives 12.8 times the sunlight that Earth does (Barkaoui et al. 2023). Its size places it near the radius valley, which suggests diverse planet formation around low-mass stars (Soubkiou et al. 2026).","system":"The M2 host star has a wide companion, a dim M8 star moving through space with it at a projected distance of 1400 AU (Barkaoui et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2084b-Barkaouietal2023","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"K. Barkaoui, M. Timmermans, A. Soubkiou et al.","title":"TOI-2084 b and TOI-4184 b: Two new sub-Neptunes around M dwarf stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.15095","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2084b-Soubkiouetal2026","cite":"Soubkiou et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Soubkiou, K. Barkaoui, T. Gan et al.","title":"Discovery of Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets Orbiting M Dwarfs with TESS","journal":"Planetary Science and Exoplanets in the Era of JWST","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S174392132400262X","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-663 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three transiting mini-Neptunes around an early M dwarf, with a radius of 2.27 Earth radii and a mass of 4.45 Earth masses (Cointepas et al. 2024). The planets were validated with ground-based photometry, precise velocity measurements and high-resolution imaging (Cointepas et al. 2024). Their radii are well above those of rocky planets, so volatile materials such as water must make up much of them (Cointepas et al. 2024).","system":"The system holds three planets just above the radius valley, the size gap between rocky and volatile-rich planets (Cointepas et al. 2024). A dynamical analysis confirmed that the system is stable (Cointepas et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi663b-Cointepasetal2024","cite":"Cointepas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cointepas, F. Bouchy, J. M. Almenara et al.","title":"TOI-663: A newly discovered multi-planet system with three transiting mini-Neptunes orbiting an early M star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202346899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Ross 508 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found with near-infrared velocity measurements from the Subaru IRD instrument, which shows that this approach can find low-mass planets around cool M dwarfs (Harakawa et al. 2022). The velocity signal shows no matching variation in stellar activity indicators or in brightness data, so it is not caused by the star (Harakawa et al. 2022). The orbit-averaged sunlight is about 1.4 times Earth's, which places the planet near the inner edge of its star's habitable zone (Harakawa et al. 2022).","system":"The star may have an additional unconfirmed companion on a wide orbit (Harakawa et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The planet's orbit may be highly eccentric; this was explored but not settled (Harakawa et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ross508b-Harakawaetal2022","cite":"Harakawa et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Hiroki Harakawa, Takuya Takarada, Yui Kasagi et al.","title":"A super-Earth orbiting near the inner edge of the habitable zone around the M4.5 dwarf Ross 508","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11986","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer planets Kepler-90 g and Kepler-90 h. Their orbits tug on each other and shift transit times by up to 25 hours, which allowed their masses to be measured (Liang et al. 2021). Planet g has a mass of 15.0 Earth masses and an unusually low apparent density of 0.15 grams per cubic centimetre, while h has 203 Earth masses (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"The two outer planets have periods close to a 2:3 ratio but are not locked in resonance. Their arrangement is stable over billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"The dynamical history suggests that planet interactions raised the orbits' eccentricities and broke the resonant lock after the planets formed (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351b-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1150L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was studied to check whether odd degeneracies, meaning several different explanations fitting the data, persist when a planet's signal is covered densely and continuously (Han et al. 2023).","observed":"A short anomaly in the lensing event's brightness curve was covered densely and continuously. It could be explained only by a planet with a mass ratio to its star near 1.1 thousandths (Han et al. 2023). Only one ambiguity remained, the known inner-outer degeneracy, which gives two solutions with a scaled separation of 1.297 or 1.242 (Han et al. 2023). Both solutions give almost the same mass ratio (Han et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The two solutions cannot be told apart from the light curve, so the planet's position relative to its star is uncertain (Han et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1150lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Youn Kil Jung, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-1150Lb: Microlensing planet detected through a densely covered planetary-caustic signal","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15628","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-120 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive giant of 4.85 Jupiter masses around an F5 star, and its orbit is likely slightly eccentric (Turner et al. 2016). Measurements of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of the star's spectrum as the planet crosses it, show the orbit is aligned with the star's spin, with an angle of -2° give or take 4° (Zak et al. 2024). Transit spectra of this planet and five others showed no strong atomic absorbers, probably because high clouds or scattering mute them (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"A high-contrast imaging survey found a candidate companion near the star (Bohn et al. 2020). A search of space-telescope records found no further planets down to the size of sub-Neptunes (Maciejewski 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp120b-Turneretal2016","cite":"Turner et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"O. D. Turner, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-120 b, WASP-122 b, AND WASP-123 b: Three Newly Discovered Planets from the WASP-South Survey","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.02210","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp120b-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, A. Bocchieri, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"Stellar obliquity measurements of six gas giants. Orbital misalignment of WASP-101b and WASP-131b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15631","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp120b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp120b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 791-18 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour LP 791-18 b. Webb Space Telescope observations of a transit found strong methane absorption and hazes that block light at short wavelengths (Roy et al. 2024). No sign of carbon dioxide was seen, unlike expectations for similar planets, and a tentative hint of hydrogen sulfide may point to strong vertical mixing (Roy et al. 2024). The methane level matches that measured on K2-18 b, a planet of similar size and temperature (Roy et al. 2024).","system":"The star has two other transiting planets. LP 791-18 b has a radius of 1.2 Earth radii and a period of 0.95 days, and LP 791-18 d has 1.03 Earth radii and a period of 2.76 days (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Planets c and d tug on each other, shifting transit times, and this was used to measure the mass of d (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The planet's temperature is estimated at 324 K, and models say its carbon dioxide to methane ratio could reveal how much water lies in its deep interior (Yang & Hu 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp79118c-Royetal2024","cite":"Roy et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Pierre-Alexis Roy, Björn Benneke, Caroline Piaulet et al.","title":"JWST reveals abundant methane and depleted carbon dioxide on the temperate sub-Neptune LP791-18c","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lp79118c-GreklekMcKeonetal2025","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson, W. Garrett Levine et al.","title":"Updated Mass, Eccentricity, and Tidal Heating Constraints for the Earth-sized Planet LP 791-18 d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18700","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lp79118c-YangHu2024","cite":"Yang & Hu 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jeehyun Yang, Renyu Hu","title":"Chemical Mapping of Temperate Sub-Neptune Atmospheres: Constraining the Deep Interior H<SUB>2</SUB>O/H<SUB>2</SUB> Ratio from the Atmospheric CO<SUB>2</SUB>/CH<SUB>4</SUB> Ratio","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01955","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11506 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the discovery was first designed to find short-period planets but has since shifted to planets far from their stars, beyond the ice line, where gas giants are thought to form (Giguere et al. 2015).","observed":"Radial-velocity monitoring in the N2K survey confirmed a second planet in the system and refined the orbits. The outer planet has an orbital period of 1627.5 days, and the new inner planet takes 223.6 days (Giguere et al. 2015). The four planets announced in the study have minimum masses between 0.4 and 2.1 Jupiter masses (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"The system now contains an outer giant and an inner planet with a period of 223.6 days (Giguere et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11506b-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4438 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity measurements from CARMENES and ground-based transit photometry gave a mass of 5.4 Earth masses and a radius of 2.52 Earth radii, so the planet is volatile-rich (Goffo et al. 2024). An interior model with a pure water envelope needs at least 46 per cent water by mass (Goffo et al. 2024). Later SPIRou observations improved the mass precision by 53 per cent and the radius precision by 22 per cent (Serrano Bell et al. 2026).","system":"The M3.5 star shows weaker and intermittent magnetic activity, and its rotation period was inferred from magnetic field variations (Serrano Bell et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4438b-Goffoetal2024","cite":"Goffo et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"E. Goffo, P. Chaturvedi, F. Murgas et al.","title":"TOI-4438 b: a transiting mini-Neptune amenable to atmospheric characterization","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4438b-Belletal2026","cite":"Bell et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Serrano Bell, G. Hébrard, E. Martioli et al.","title":"Revisiting TOI-4438 and TOI-442 planetary systems with new observations from SPIRou and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13333","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4495 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the inner member of a pair of sub-Neptune and Neptune-like planets with periods of 2.567 and 5.185 days, close to a 2:1 orbital resonance (Wang et al. 2026). Modelling the light curves together gave masses of 7.7 Earth masses for this planet and 23.2 for the outer one (Wang et al. 2026). The masses and radii show volatile-rich gaseous envelopes on both planets (Wang et al. 2026).","system":"The orbits of both planets are aligned with the F-type star's spin (Wang et al. 2026).","history":"The planets likely experienced overstable migration, a drift through the disc that leaves them near but not exactly in resonance (Wang et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4495b-Wangetal2026","cite":"Wang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mu-Tian Wang, Fei Dai, Hui-Gen Liu et al.","title":"TOI-4495: A Pair of Aligned, Near-resonant Sub-Neptunes That Likely Experienced Overstable Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02665","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2211.01":{"text":{"why":"The planet was studied as a possible hot water world, a close-in planet that could keep a heavy, water-rich atmosphere despite strong starlight (Egger et al. 2026).","observed":"Brightness data from TESS and CHEOPS, together with velocities from HARPS, ESPRESSO and KPF, gave a radius of 1.457 Earth radii and a mass of 2.69 Earth masses (Egger et al. 2026). Those values fit neither a bare rocky planet nor one with a thick hydrogen layer, so the planet probably holds a heavier atmosphere. An evaporation analysis tested whether such an envelope could survive (Egger et al. 2026).","system":"The velocities also show an outer, non-transiting planet candidate with a period of about 15.6 days and a minimum mass of 8.53 Earth masses (Egger et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi221101-Eggeretal2026","cite":"Egger et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. A. Egger, P. Cortés-Zuleta, D. Kubyshkina et al.","title":"Searching for hot water world candidates with CHEOPS: II. A low-density super-Earth orbiting the K dwarf TOI-2211","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08922","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 89307 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a minimum mass of 1.78 Jupiter masses, an orbit of 3.3 AU and an eccentricity of 0.24 (Fischer et al. 2009). Its host is a G0 dwarf star with slightly lower than solar metal content, unlike most other hosts in the same Lick Observatory study, which are metal rich (Fischer et al. 2009). The massive long-period planets in that study often have higher eccentricities than the average known exoplanet (Fischer et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd89307b-Fischeretal2009","cite":"Fischer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Debra Fischer, Peter Driscoll, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Five Planets and an Independent Confirmation of HD 196885Ab from Lick Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1596","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2018-BLG-0567L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A short anomaly in the lensing event's brightness was covered densely and continuously by two high-cadence surveys. Its shape shows the background star crossing a planetary caustic, a region of strongly magnified light, produced by the planet (Jung et al. 2021). The scaled planet-host separation is 1.81 and the mass ratio is about 1.24 thousandths (Jung et al. 2021). Estimates give a host of about 0.25 solar masses and a planet of about 0.32 Jupiter masses (Jung et al. 2021).","system":"The system lies at about 7.06 kiloparsecs, so it is probably near the Galactic bulge (Jung et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"Host and planet masses come from statistical estimates and carry large uncertainties (Jung et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2018blg0567lb-Jungetal2021","cite":"Jung et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Youn Kil Jung, Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"OGLE-2018-BLG-0567Lb and OGLE-2018-BLG-0962Lb: Two Microlensing Planets through the Planetary-caustic Channel","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.07338","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 791-18 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer neighbour LP 791-18 d. Three new transit observations of the sub-Neptune LP 791-18 c, made with the Palomar WIRC camera, gave transit times precise to under 6 seconds (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Small shifts in those transit times come from the planets pulling on each other, and they were used to weigh planet d at 0.91 ± 0.19 Earth masses (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","system":"LP 791-18 b is a super-Earth on a 0.95-day orbit around a late M dwarf (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025). Beyond it lie the temperate Earth-sized planet d, with a 2.76-day orbit, and the sub-Neptune c, with a 4.99-day orbit (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Planet d may be kept slightly eccentric by its pull from planet c, which would heat its interior through tides and could drive volcanic outgassing (Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp79118b-GreklekMcKeonetal2025","cite":"Greklek-McKeon et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Greklek-McKeon, Heather A. Knutson, W. Garrett Levine et al.","title":"Updated Mass, Eccentricity, and Tidal Heating Constraints for the Earth-sized Planet LP 791-18 d","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18700","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gl 49 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spots and bright patches on a star's surface create repeating signals in radial velocities, in the same period range where temperate planets of small stars are expected, so they can mimic or hide a real planet (Perger et al. 2019). Twenty-two years of data from the HARPS-N and CARMENES spectrographs, plus brightness monitoring, were analysed with models for the star's activity to separate the two (Perger et al. 2019). Gl 49 b emerged as a super-Earth in this work (Perger et al. 2019).","system":"Gl 49 is an M1.5 dwarf (Perger et al. 2019). Its hydrogen and calcium emission lines show it is fairly active, with calcium emission almost as strong as in flaring dM1e stars (Houdebine 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gl49b-Pergeretal2019","cite":"Perger et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. Perger, G. Scandariato, I. Ribas et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Gl 49 radial velocities and activity indicators (Perger+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36240123","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gl49b-Houdebine2010","cite":"Houdebine 2010","year":"2010","authors":"E. R. Houdebine","title":"Observation and modelling of main-sequence star chromospheres . IX. Two-component model chromospheres for nine M1 dwarfs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200811602","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1994 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1994 b is a brown dwarf, an object between a planet and a star, with a mass of 22.1 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.22 Jupiter radii (Page et al. 2024). Radial-velocity measurements from MINERVA-Australis and CHIRON gave full coverage of its orbit (Page et al. 2024). Its orbit is moderately eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.341 (Page et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a hot subgiant, a star that has started to swell as it ages, with a temperature of 7700 K (Page et al. 2024).","history":"Other transiting brown dwarfs of similar mass and period have less eccentric orbits (Page et al. 2024). Low-mass brown dwarfs might resemble planetary systems if they formed the same way, but the short period and high eccentricity here may contradict that idea (Page et al. 2024).","open":"An evolved host with a well-known age, mass and radius makes it possible to test models of how brown dwarfs change over time (Page et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1994b-Pageetal2024","cite":"Page et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Emma Page, Joshua Pepper, Duncan Wright et al.","title":"TOI-1994b: A Low-mass Eccentric Brown Dwarf Transiting A Subgiant Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08836","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-178 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet concerns the system as a whole. Follow-up observations with CHEOPS, NGTS and SPECULOOS revealed six transiting planets, all smaller than Neptune (Hooton et al. 2022). Precise velocity measurements of the star with ESPRESSO showed that the planets' densities vary from planet to planet (Hooton et al. 2022). Later monitoring with 27 new CHEOPS visits, 8 NGTS observations and 4 more from Euler tracked timing shifts of the five outer planets (Leleu et al. 2024).","system":"Five of the six planets form a chain of Laplace resonance, where several planets are locked in linked orbital rhythms (Hooton et al. 2022). Chains like this are rare and are also seen in TRAPPIST-1 and the Galilean moons of Jupiter (Hooton et al. 2022). Early TESS data had suggested two planets sharing one orbit (Hooton et al. 2022).","history":"A resonance chain is fragile, so its survival limits how the planets could have formed and moved (Hooton et al. 2022).","open":"Density does not fall steadily with distance from the star, unlike in most systems, and this is hard to explain theoretically (Hooton et al. 2021). JWST observations were awarded for planets b, d and g (Hooton et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi178f-Hootonetal2022","cite":"Hooton et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178f-Leleuetal2024","cite":"Leleu et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Leleu, J.-B. Delisle, L. Delrez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-178 light curves (Leleu+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi178f-Hootonetal2021","cite":"Hooton et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Matthew Hooton, Vardan Adibekyan, Yann Alibert et al.","title":"TOI-178: the best laboratory for testing planetary formation theories","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 1","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 145457 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up searched evolved stars of intermediate mass (Sato et al. 2010).","observed":"Measurements at Subaru Telescope and Okayama Astrophysical Observatory showed a planet with a minimum mass of 2.9 Jupiter masses and an orbit of 176 days with an eccentricity of 0.11 (Sato et al. 2010). Its host is a K0 giant of 1.9 solar masses (Sato et al. 2010). Planets close to evolved stars of this kind are rare, and this one is among the innermost known (Sato et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd145457b-Satoetal2010","cite":"Sato et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya, Yujuan Liu et al.","title":"Substellar Companions to Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: HD 145457 and HD 180314","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2860","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-8 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Two transits were observed in 2017 July and August with the IMACS instrument on the Magellan Baade 6.5 m telescope (May et al. 2020). On both nights the planet's atmosphere scattered blue light more strongly than a pure Rayleigh gas would, and unocculted starspots cannot fully explain it (May et al. 2020). Tiny manganese sulphide particles could explain up to 80% of the slope if the planet is warmer than its equilibrium temperature, but the slope is steeper than even the most extreme haze considered (May et al. 2020).","system":"A reference star of nearly equal brightness lies just 19 arcseconds away in the sky (Bayliss et al. 2015). The host star is rich in heavy elements, which argues against the idea that low-density planets form around metal-poor stars (Bayliss et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"What makes the scattering slope so steep is not known (May et al. 2020). The low density and large radius give an atmospheric scale height of almost 1000 km, which makes the planet a good target for further atmosphere studies (Bayliss et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats8b-Baylissetal2015","cite":"Bayliss et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-8b: A Low-density Transiting Super-Neptune","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01334","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-132 c":{"text":{"why":"The discovery tested whether hot Jupiters can have close planetary companions, which they are generally thought to lack (Hord et al. 2022).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour WASP-132 b. A planet of 1.78 ± 0.10 Earth radii was seen in TESS data on a 1.01-day orbit inside the hot Jupiter WASP-132 b, and checked with ground-based follow-up (Hord et al. 2022). Statistical tests gave false-positive probabilities of 9 × 10^-5 and 0.0107 (Hord et al. 2022). Archival CORALIE velocities set an upper limit of 32.65 Earth masses at 3 sigma, so its mass is not measured (Hord et al. 2022).","system":"The hot Jupiter WASP-132 b has a 7.1-day orbit around a K4 star, and is among the least irradiated and coolest of the WASP planets (Hellier et al. 2017). Simulations show the system stays stable for about 100 million years (Hord et al. 2022).","history":"A close companion makes it highly unlikely that the hot Jupiter reached its orbit by high-eccentricity migration, in which a planet is flung onto a stretched orbit and then circularises (Hord et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp132c-Hordetal2022","cite":"Hord et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Benjamin Hord, Knicole Colon, Travis Berger et al.","title":"TOI-822.02 is a Nearby Planetary Companion to Hot Jupiter WASP-132 b","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp132c-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 75784 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, N2K, was first designed for short-period planets but now looks for worlds at large separations (Giguere et al. 2015). The goal is to learn how common giant planets are beyond the ice line, where gas giants are thought to form (Giguere et al. 2015).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the inner planet, which has a 341.7-day orbit (Giguere et al. 2015). Radial-velocity monitoring also shows evidence for a longer-period signal from the same star (Giguere et al. 2015). HD 75784 was not known to host planets before this survey (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd75784c-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"61 Vir d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Secular theory describes slow, long-term changes in orbits caused by the planets' mutual pull. For 61 Virginis it reproduces the behaviour seen in full numerical simulations, even though the eccentricities are quite large (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010). The long axes of the two outer planets, which include this one, swing back and forth around a shared alignment (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010).","system":"","history":"The alignment of the outer orbits is the most likely outcome given the planets' masses and spacings, so it does not show that tides shaped the system (Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-61vird-VanLaerhovenGreenberg2010","cite":"Van Laerhoven & Greenberg 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Christa Van Laerhoven, R. Greenberg","title":"Secular Behavior In Multi-planet Systems: 61 Virginis, 55 Cancri, Etc.","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #42","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1811 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1811 b is one of six short-period giant planets announced together from TESS (Rodriguez et al. 2023). Time-series photometry and spectroscopy from the TESS Follow-up Observing Program Working Group were used to confirm them and measure their masses (Rodriguez et al. 2023). All six orbit bright stars, and TOI-1811 has the coolest host of the group at 4766 K (Rodriguez et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1811b-Rodriguezetal2023","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Another shipment of six short-period giant planets from TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05709","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 37124 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most work on this system tests whether the fitted orbits are stable. A stability map of the orbits around the measured values found HD 37124 sits within large stability zones (Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002). The radial-velocity data were reanalysed with N-body simulations, which follow the planets' mutual pulls, to derive stable configurations and limits on the planets' parameters (Goździewski et al. 2006). The host star has also been modelled; its position in the diagram of star brightness against temperature looked odd for its high metal content (Fernandes & Santos 2004).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd37124c-KiselevaEggletonetal2002","cite":"Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ludmila Kiseleva-Eggleton, Eric Bois, Nicolas Rambaux et al.","title":"Global Dynamics and Stability Limits for Planetary Systems around HD 12661, HD 38529, HD 37124, and HD 160691","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207367","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd37124c-FernandesSantos2004","cite":"Fernandes & Santos 2004","year":"2004","authors":"J. Fernandes, N. C. Santos","title":"Detailed theoretical models for extra-solar planet-host stars. The ``red stragglers'' HD 37124 and HD 46375","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408182","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-96 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-96 b is a hot Saturn with a well-studied atmosphere. Hubble WFC3 data show a strong water signature, but ground-based VLT data give transit depths more than 1100 ppm different, so combining instruments is risky (Yip et al. 2021). A new JWST NIRSpec transit combined with archival NIRISS and VLT FORS2 spectra shows water, carbon dioxide and sodium (Radica et al. 2026). The atmosphere holds 2 to 6 times the star's share of heavy elements, with a carbon-to-oxygen ratio close to the star's (Radica et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The heavy-element content and carbon-to-oxygen ratio suggest the planet may have formed by core accretion beyond the water snowline and then taken in volatile-rich material (Radica et al. 2026).","open":"Carbon monoxide is not constrained, so metallicity cannot be fixed from free retrievals alone, and the signs of photochemistry are only tentative (Radica et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp96b-Yipetal2021","cite":"Yip et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kai Hou Yip, Quentin Changeat, Billy Edwards et al.","title":"On the Compatibility of Ground-based and Space-based Data: WASP-96 b, an Example","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10438","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp96b-Radicaetal2026","cite":"Radica et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Michael Radica, Jake Taylor, Yoav Rotman et al.","title":"Supersolar Metallicity and Tentative Evidence for Photochemistry on WASP-96 b from JWST and Ground-based VLT Transmission Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.05049","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its 10.9-day orbit lies in the sparsely populated \"period valley\" (Kovács et al. 2010). Spitzer looked for its secondary eclipse at 3.6 and 4.5 micrometres, but found none in either band, unlike the other cool giant planets in that study (Wallack et al. 2019). HARPS-N spectra taken during transits were used to measure how the orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin, in a study of five eccentric systems including this one (Mancini et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Models need about 10 Earth masses of heavy elements, 2% of its mass, to explain the planet's radius (Kovács et al. 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp15b-Kovcsetal2010","cite":"Kovács et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Kovács, G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-15b: A 10.9 Day Extrasolar Planet Transiting a Solar-type Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.5300","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp15b-Wallacketal2019","cite":"Wallack et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"Investigating Trends in Atmospheric Compositions of Cool Gas Giant Planets Using Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.00014","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp15b-Mancinietal2022","cite":"Mancini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXXVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect and revising the physical and orbital parameters of the HAT-P-15, HAT-P-17, HAT-P-21, HAT-P-26, HAT-P-29 eccentric planetary systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.10549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 832 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour GJ 832 c. GJ 832 b is a Jupiter analogue on a nearly circular orbit of 9.4 years around an M dwarf (Wittenmyer et al. 2014). Refined orbital elements from HARPS data confirm earlier eccentricity estimates, and no further planets were found in the data (Liebing et al. 2024). The star's extreme-ultraviolet output cannot be observed directly, so a model guided by Hubble far- and near-ultraviolet spectra was built to predict it (Peacock et al. 2019).","system":"A second planet, GJ 832 c, was reported near the inner edge of the habitable zone (Wittenmyer et al. 2014). Together the system was described as a miniature version of the solar system (Wittenmyer et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Stellar activity may account for the signal of GJ 832 c, because the star's rotation period of 37.5 days is close to the 35.68-day signal (Gorrini et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj832b-Wittenmyeretal2014","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Mikko Tuomi, R. P. Butler et al.","title":"GJ 832c: A Super-Earth in the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.5587","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj832b-Liebingetal2024","cite":"Liebing et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"F. Liebing, S. V. Jeffers, P. Gorrini et al.","title":"RedDots: Limits on habitable and undetected planets orbiting nearby stars GJ 832, GJ 674, and Ross 128","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01173","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj832b-Peacocketal2019","cite":"Peacock et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Sarah Peacock, Travis Barman, Evgenya L. Shkolnik et al.","title":"Predicting the Extreme Ultraviolet Radiation Environment of Exoplanets around Low-mass Stars: GJ 832, GJ 176, and GJ 436","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.08053","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj832b-Gorrinietal2022","cite":"Gorrini et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. Gorrini, N. Astudillo-Defru, S. Dreizler et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: GJ 832 stellar activity (Gorrini+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36640064","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-730 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A statistically validated hot Jupiter with a 6.49-day orbit shares its system with a candidate Earth-sized planet on a 2.85-day orbit (Cañas et al. 2019). Spectroscopic velocities from APOGEE-2N, adaptive-optics contrast curves and Gaia distances were used to rule out binary-star explanations for the small signal (Cañas et al. 2019). Kepler-730 b has a radius of 1.100 Jupiter radii, while the inner companion is about 1.57 Earth radii (Cañas et al. 2019).","system":"Kepler-730 is only the second compact system known to host a hot Jupiter with an inner transiting planet (Cañas et al. 2019). WASP-47 was the first (Zhu et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"The small companion could still transit a different star than the hot Jupiter's host, and follow-up with 10-m telescopes could settle this (Zhu et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler730b-Caasetal2019","cite":"Cañas et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Songhu Wang, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"Kepler-730: A Hot Jupiter System with a Close-in, Transiting, Earth-sized Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.08358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler730b-Zhuetal2018","cite":"Zhu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Wei Zhu, Fei Dai, Kento Masuda","title":"Kepler-730b is Probably a Hot Jupiter with a Small Companion","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00007","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4137 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up aims to build a uniform sample of hot Jupiters for population studies, by combining new TESS detections with earlier ground-based surveys (Yee et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-4137 b is one of ten new hot Jupiters reported together (Yee et al. 2022). Each was first flagged from periodic dips in TESS brightness, then confirmed with ground-based photometry, high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy (Yee et al. 2022). The ten planets orbit relatively bright F and G stars, with periods of 2 to 10 days and masses of 0.2 to 2.2 Jupiter masses (Yee et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4137b-Yeeetal2022","cite":"Yee et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. I. Ten TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09728","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 60292 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, SENS, searches stars near the northern celestial pole using the BOES spectrograph on a 1.8-m telescope in Korea (Lee et al. 2020).","observed":"The host is a giant of about 1.7 solar masses, and its velocity varies with a period of 495 ± 3 days (Lee et al. 2020). Giants can show similar variations from surface features, but the authors argue the signal is better explained by an orbiting companion (Lee et al. 2020). Its minimum mass is 6.5 ± 1.0 Jupiter masses (Lee et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd60292b-Leeetal2020","cite":"Lee et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Myeong-Gu Park, Inwoo Han et al.","title":"A Search for Exoplanets around Northern Circumpolar Stars. VI. Detection of Planetary Companions Orbiting the Giants HD 60292 and HD 112640","journal":"Journal of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.5303/JKAS.2020.53.1.27","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Luhman 16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Luhman 16 is the closest known pair of brown dwarfs, about 2 parsecs from the Sun (Garcia et al. 2017). Positions measured over 31 years, plus radial velocities from CRIRES, gave masses of 27.9 Jupiter masses for the T dwarf and 34.2 for the L dwarf (Garcia et al. 2017). The pair is made of an L7.5 and a T0.5 dwarf, spanning the change from cloudy to clearer atmospheres (de Regt et al. 2025). High-resolution CRIRES+ spectra detect water, potassium, sodium, iron hydride and, for the first time in the J band, hydrogen fluoride (de Regt et al. 2025).","system":"The two brown dwarfs are unlikely members of any known young nearby group (Mamajek 2013).","history":"","open":"Clouds are found in both dwarfs, with patchy clearing thought to be behind their variable brightness (de Regt et al. 2025). The brightness varies by about 0.1 mag and changes rapidly, possibly from fast-evolving weather (Street 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-luhman16b-Garciaetal2017","cite":"Garcia et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"E. Victor Garcia, S. Mark Ammons, Maissa Salama et al.","title":"Individual, Model-independent Masses of the Closest Known Brown Dwarf Binary to the Sun","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.02714","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-luhman16b-deRegtetal2025","cite":"de Regt et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. de Regt, I. A. G. Snellen, N. F. Allard et al.","title":"The ESO SupJup Survey: VII. Clouds and line asymmetries in CRIRES<SUP>+</SUP> J-band spectra of the Luhman 16 binary","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21266","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-luhman16b-Mamajek2013","cite":"Mamajek 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Eric E. Mamajek","title":"On the Nearby Binary Brown Dwarf WISE J104915.57-531906.1 (Luhman 16)","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5345","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-luhman16b-Street2015","cite":"Street 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Rachel Street","title":"Extended Baseline Photometry of Rapidly Changing Weather Patterns on the Brown Dwarf Binary, Luhman-16","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 251 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour GJ 251 c. Radial velocity measurements (tiny shifts in the star's light caused by orbiting planets) from five spectrographs were combined. They improved the orbit of GJ 251 b to a period of 14.2370 days and a minimum mass of 3.85 Earth masses (Beard et al. 2025).","system":"GJ 251 c is a candidate super-Earth with a 53.647-day orbit and a minimum mass of 3.84 Earth masses, placing it in the star's habitable zone (Beard et al. 2025). It is described as a good target for direct imaging (Beard et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"GJ 251 c is still only a candidate (Beard et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj251b-Beardetal2025","cite":"Beard et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Corey Beard, Paul Robertson, Jack Lubin et al.","title":"Discovery of a Nearby Habitable Zone Super-Earth Candidate Amenable to Direct Imaging","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19956","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 176986 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with the HARPS and HARPS-N spectrographs from 2004 onwards (Suarez Mascareno et al. 2017). Its later observations came from a programme that watches quiet sun-like stars nearly every night to find very light planets on close orbits (Suarez Mascareno et al. 2017). The measurements revealed two super-Earths with masses of 5.74 and 9.18 Earth masses, periods of 6.49 and 16.82 days, and orbits consistent with circular (Nari et al. 2025). The host is a K2.5 dwarf with modest magnetic activity but a complex activity pattern, and its magnetic cycle and rotation were studied alongside the planets (Nari et al. 2025).","system":"Two super-Earths orbit at 0.063 and 0.119 AU (Nari et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The star is a useful test case for methods that separate stellar activity from planet signals (Nari et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd176986b-Mascarenoetal2017","cite":"Mascareno et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez, R. Rebolo et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS + HARPS-N data (Suarez Mascareno+, 2018)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36120041","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd176986b-Narietal2025","cite":"Nari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Nari, A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS Rvs and activity indicators (Nari+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3473 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"GJ 3473 c does not transit, so it is known only from the wobble it causes in its star. The wobble was measured with the CARMENES, IRD and HARPS spectrographs and gives a minimum mass of 7.41 Earth masses and an orbit of 15.509 days (Kemmer et al. 2020). Its orbit is described as temperate (Kemmer et al. 2020).","system":"The inner planet, GJ 3473 b, crosses the face of its M4 dwarf star and is an Earth-sized world of 1.264 Earth radii and 1.86 Earth masses (Kemmer et al. 2020). It is one of the hottest transiting Earth-sized planets known, which makes it a good target for studying its heat emission (Kemmer et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Only a minimum mass is known for GJ 3473 c, because its orbit is not seen edge-on (Kemmer et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3473c-Kemmeretal2020","cite":"Kemmer et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"J. Kemmer, S. Stock, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"Discovery of a hot, transiting, Earth-sized planet and a second temperate, non-transiting planet around the M4 dwarf GJ 3473 (TOI-488)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.10432","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2012-BLG-0563L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The microlensing event was very bright, which allowed a clear planet signal, with a planet-to-star mass ratio of about 1 in 1000 (Fukui et al. 2015). Microlensing alone gave no distance or mass for the host star. Infrared images from the Subaru telescope's adaptive optics system caught the star's own light, so the system's size could be pinned down (Fukui et al. 2015). The system lies about 1.3 kpc away. An M dwarf of 0.34 solar masses is orbited by a Saturn-mass planet of 0.39 Jupiter masses (Fukui et al. 2015).","system":"The planet's separation from its star is about 0.74 AU in the close model or 4.3 AU in the wide model (Fukui et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Which separation is right is not known. There is a 17% chance that light from another star contaminates the host's flux, which would lower the masses by up to a factor of three (Fukui et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2012blg0563lb-Fukuietal2015","cite":"Fukui et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. Fukui, A. Gould, T. Sumi et al.","title":"OGLE-2012-BLG-0563Lb: A Saturn-mass Planet around an M Dwarf with the Mass Constrained by Subaru AO Imaging","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.08850","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-80 b is one of six planets in a very compact system, studied through small shifts in the timing of their transits (Weisserman et al. 2023). Those timing changes show that the outer five planets sit in a chain of linked three-body orbital resonances, but they are not locked in pairs (Weisserman et al. 2023). The system was highlighted as unusual because planets this tightly packed, with about four periods within a factor of three, have not been seen by radial velocity surveys (Ragozzine et al. 2015).","system":"A sixth planet, Kepler-80 g, has a mass of about 0.8 to 1.0 Earth masses (Weisserman et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Adding the extra planet changes the masses worked out from transit timing, so conclusions from incomplete systems need caution (Weisserman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80b-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80b-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-170 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity follow-up with the Euler/CORALIE spectrograph and transit light curves from several telescopes gave a mass of 1.7 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.14 Jupiter radii. The planet orbits a G1V star every 2.344 days (Barkaoui et al. 2019). It receives about 10^9 erg per second per square centimetre of starlight. At that irradiation and mass, its size matches classical models of heated giant planets (Barkaoui et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp170b-Barkaouietal2019","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"K. Barkaoui, A. Burdanov, C. Hellier et al.","title":"Discovery of Three New Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-161 b, WASP-163 b, and WASP-170 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06548","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 220773 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A giant planet on an eccentric orbit was announced around HD 220773. About 130 HARPS-N measurements, taken to search for extra small planets, refuted it (Carleo et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether any planet orbits the star is not known; the announced giant planet was not confirmed (Carleo et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd220773b-Carleoetal2024","cite":"Carleo et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ilaria Carleo, Silvano Desidera, Alessandro Sozzetti et al.","title":"Disproving the High-eccentricity Planet HD 220773b","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/ad57b5","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-500 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-500 b was one of 18 potentially terrestrial planet candidates picked as ideal for emission spectroscopy with the James Webb Space Telescope, which could probe the atmospheres and surfaces of hot rocky planets (Giacalone et al. 2022). Ground-based observations and two vetting tools, DAVE and TRICERATOPS, were used to show that it is very likely a real planet (Giacalone et al. 2022). It is one of seven ultra-short-period planets among the 13 validated (Giacalone et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi500b-Giacaloneetal2022","cite":"Giacalone et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Steven Giacalone, Courtney D. Dressing, Christina Hedges et al.","title":"Validation of 13 Hot and Potentially Terrestrial TESS Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.12661","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-8 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 191122, also called DMPP-8, was observed as part of the Dispersed Matter Planet Project. This radial velocity survey targets stars with unusual activity signs that suggest close-in planets are evaporating (Standing et al. 2026). The survey reported seven new planet signals in five systems, including DMPP-8 b (Standing et al. 2026). Detection limits were worked out for 24 stars, and the occurrence rate of planets of 3 to 10 Earth masses with periods under 50 days came out at about 83 per cent (Standing et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp8b-Standingetal2026","cite":"Standing et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, John R. Barnes, Carole A. Haswell et al.","title":"The dispersed matter planet project sample ─ detection limits, occurrence rates, and new planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18207","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148164 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The N2K survey was designed to find giant planets around metal-rich stars, because such stars are more likely to host them (Ment et al. 2018). It measured 378 stars with the HIRES spectrograph at Keck Observatory between 2004 and 2017 (Ment et al. 2018). HD 148164 hosts two giant planets, with minimum masses of 1.23 and 5.16 Jupiter masses (Ment et al. 2018). The new giants in the survey have orbits of 1.0 to 6.2 AU and eccentricities from 0.13 to 0.71 (Ment et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148164b-Mentetal2018","cite":"Ment et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Kristo Ment, Debra A. Fischer, Gaspar Bakos et al.","title":"Radial Velocities from the N2K Project: Six New Cold Gas Giant Planets Orbiting HD 55696, HD 98736, HD 148164, HD 203473, and HD 211810","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01228","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 92788 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour HD 92788 b. That planet has an orbit of 326.7 days, an eccentricity of 0.30, a minimum mass of 3.34 Jupiter masses and a distance of 0.95 AU from its star (Fischer et al. 2001). The star shows little chromospheric (outer atmosphere) activity (Fischer et al. 2001). Little is known about HD 92788 c itself; work so far has mostly studied its neighbour.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd92788c-Fischeretal2001","cite":"Fischer et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to HD 12661, HD 92788, and HD 38529 and Variations in Keplerian Residuals of Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/320224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1178 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-1178 b is among small, long-period Kepler planets whose statistical confirmation was re-examined (Burke et al. 2019). The re-examination used final Kepler data and a better understanding of false alarms caused by the telescope's data processing in this regime (Burke et al. 2019). Kepler-452 b and Kepler-186 f no longer reach 99% confidence, and Kepler-1178 b is named among further planets affected when simple processing variations are included (Burke et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"How firmly Kepler-1178 b counts as a confirmed planet is under scrutiny (Burke et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1178b-Burkeetal2019","cite":"Burke et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, F. Mullally, Susan E. Thompson et al.","title":"Re-evaluating Small Long-period Confirmed Planets from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.00506","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2005-BLG-071L b":{"text":{"why":"Core accretion theory predicts such super-Jupiters around M dwarfs to be rare (Bennett et al. 2020).","observed":"Hubble Space Telescope images and measurements from the microlensing light curve showed the host is an M dwarf of 0.46 solar masses about 3.2 kpc away. The planet has a mass of 3.8 Jupiter masses at a projected separation of 3.6 AU and an equilibrium temperature of about 55 K (Dong et al. 2009). Later Keck adaptive-optics images resolved the host star as it separated from the brighter background star. They gave a host mass of 0.426 solar masses and a planet mass of 3.27 Jupiter masses at 3.4 au (Bennett et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"A second model gives a smaller separation of 2.1 AU and a temperature of about 71 K (Dong et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2005blg071lb-Bennettetal2020","cite":"Bennett et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"David P. Bennett, Aparna Bhattacharya, Jean-Philippe Beaulieu et al.","title":"Keck Observations Confirm a Super-Jupiter Planet Orbiting M Dwarf OGLE-2005-BLG-071L","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.04740","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogle2005blg071lb-Dongetal2009","cite":"Dong et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Subo Dong, Andrew Gould, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"OGLE-2005-BLG-071Lb, the Most Massive M Dwarf Planetary Companion?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0804.1354","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"14 Her b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is very rich in heavy elements, with an iron abundance (relative to hydrogen) of [Fe/H]=0.50 (Gonzalez et al. 1999). It is nearly identical in bulk properties to the star rho1 Cancri, which suggests the process that formed their planets may have altered the stars (Gonzalez et al. 1999). A dynamical analysis of the radial velocities found a second Jupiter-like planet, 14 Her c, in a 3:1 or 6:1 resonance with 14 Her b (Goździewski et al. 2006).","system":"","history":"The star's heavy-element richness hints that planet formation left a mark on the star itself (Gonzalez et al. 1999).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-14herb-Gonzalezetal1999","cite":"Gonzalez et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, George Wallerstein, Steven H. Saar","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. IV. 14 Herculis, HD 187123, and HD 210277","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/311847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-162 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity follow-up showed a massive planet of 5.2 Jupiter masses on a relatively long and highly eccentric orbit, with a period of 9.6 days and an eccentricity of 0.43. It orbits a V = 12.2 K0 star (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp162b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2012-BLG-0026L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Four solutions fit because of a close versus wide ambiguity. In the best fit, the two planets are about 3.8 and 4.6 AU away in projection (Han et al. 2013). The system lies about 4.1 kpc away toward the Galactic centre, and it is the only multi-planet system other than our own whose masses are measured free of the minimum-mass limit (Han et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true separations are larger than those seen in projection and the order of the two planets may be reversed (Han et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2012blg0026lb-Hanetal2013","cite":"Han et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"C. Han, A. Udalski, J.-Y. Choi et al.","title":"The Second Multiple-planet System Discovered by Microlensing: OGLE-2012-BLG-0026Lb, c—A Pair of Jovian Planets beyond the Snow Line","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.4265","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 143105 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is one of the brightest known to host a hot Jupiter, which makes follow-up studies easier even though the planet does not transit (Hébrard et al. 2016).","observed":"Because the planet does not cross in front of its star, its true mass was long unknown. Infrared spectra from the Keck telescope gave the first detection of its atmosphere (Finnerty et al. 2025). The signal fixed the orbital tilt and gave a true mass of 1.23 ± 0.10 Jupiter masses (Finnerty et al. 2025). The atmosphere is cloud free down to about 1 bar of pressure and dominated by water vapour (Finnerty et al. 2025). Carbon monoxide is only limited from above, and the carbon-to-oxygen ratio is below 0.2 (Finnerty et al. 2025). Heavy elements are at about 0.1 times the solar level (Finnerty et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Iron and magnesium abundances are not constrained by the observations (Finnerty et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd143105b-Hbrardetal2016","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"G. Hébrard, L. Arnold, T. Forveille et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. X. Detection and characterization of giant planets by the dozen","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04622","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd143105b-Finnertyetal2025","cite":"Finnerty et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Luke Finnerty, Yinzi Xin, Jerry W. Xuan et al.","title":"True Mass and Atmospheric Composition of the Nontransiting Hot Jupiter HD 143105 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.04552","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-38 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first seen as a single dip in TESS data from Sector 33 (Rodel et al. 2026). A campaign of 228 nights of monitoring with NGTS caught a transit egress, and velocity measurements from CORALIE and HARPS showed the orbit lasts 180.5 days (Rodel et al. 2026). The velocities also showed the companion is a planet, with a mass of 4.77 Jupiter masses (Rodel et al. 2026). Its orbit is moderately eccentric, with e = 0.3086 (Rodel et al. 2026). Extra transit observations coordinated by the TESS follow-up programme refined the period (Rodel et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a bright, metal-rich F6 to F7 star with [Fe/H] = 0.33 (Rodel et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts38b-Rodeletal2026","cite":"Rodel et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Toby Rodel, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Samuel Gill et al.","title":"TIC-65910228 b / NGTS-38 b, a 180 d transiting warm super-Jupiter","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-163 b":{"text":{"why":"The star lies at the edge of the region of sky that JWST can watch all year (Kossakowski et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with follow-up photometry from several telescopes, spectra from FEROS and CORALIE, and speckle imaging (Kossakowski et al. 2019). It is inflated, with a radius of 1.478 Jupiter radii for a mass of 1.219 Jupiter masses (Kossakowski et al. 2019). Planets this puffy have sizes larger than simple models of their mass predict. Another hot Jupiter, TOI-150 b, was announced in the same study (Kossakowski et al. 2019). Both planets orbit F-type stars and are good targets for further study (Kossakowski et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi163b-Kossakowskietal2019","cite":"Kossakowski et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Diana Kossakowski, Néstor Espinoza, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"TOI-150b and TOI-163b: two transiting hot Jupiters, one eccentric and one inflated, revealed by TESS near and at the edge of the JWST CVZ","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09866","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MASCARA-1 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found by the MASCARA all-sky camera survey (Talens et al. 2017).","observed":"The host is a bright, rapidly rotating A8 star with no obvious pulsations, so the planet is a good target for atmospheric work (Talens et al. 2017). Its equilibrium temperature of 2570 K was one of the highest known for a hot Jupiter at the time (Talens et al. 2017). Infrared spectra of the dayside show iron, carbon monoxide and water (Ramkumar et al. 2023). The carbon-to-oxygen ratio is about 0.68, consistent with the Sun (Ramkumar et al. 2023). Keck spectra show the light is dominated by carbon monoxide emission, with weak water or hydroxyl features that fit water being broken apart by heat (Finnerty et al. 2026). Transmission spectra at visible wavelengths showed no absorption features (Stangret et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"The orbit is tilted relative to the star's spin, which may be a sign of how the planet formed and moved inward (Talens et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-mascara1b-Talensetal2017","cite":"Talens et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"G. J. J. Talens, S. Albrecht, J. F. P. Spronck et al.","title":"MASCARA-1 b. A hot Jupiter transiting a bright m<SUB>V</SUB> = 8.3 A-star in a misaligned orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04262","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara1b-Ramkumaretal2023","cite":"Ramkumar et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Swaetha Ramkumar, Neale P. Gibson, Stevanus K. Nugroho et al.","title":"High-resolution emission spectroscopy retrievals of MASCARA-1b with CRIRES+: strong detections of CO, H<SUB>2</SUB>O, and Fe emission lines and a C/O consistent with solar","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07157","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara1b-Finnertyetal2026","cite":"Finnerty et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Luke Finnerty, Michael P. Fitzgerald, Yinzi Xin et al.","title":"Atmospheric Characterization of Six Ultrahot Jupiters from K-band High-resolution Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.03992","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mascara1b-Stangretetal2022","cite":"Stangret et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"M. Stangret, N. Casasayas-Barris, E. Pallé et al.","title":"High-resolution transmission spectroscopy study of ultra-hot Jupiters HAT-P-57b, KELT-17b, KELT-21b, KELT-7b, MASCARA-1b, and WASP-189b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.13064","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-20 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocity measurements from HARPS-N and Keck gave a mass of 9.70 Earth masses and a mean density of 8.2 g per cubic centimetre (Buchhave et al. 2016). That is dense enough for a rocky planet, even though its radius of 1.87 Earth radii is above the size of about 1.6 Earth radii where planets were proposed to switch from rocky to gassy (Buchhave et al. 2016). Studies of other low-mass planets, including this one, find less than 10 percent hydrogen and helium by mass (Valencia et al. 2013).","system":"The star hosts five transiting planets packed within the distance of Mercury from the Sun, plus a sixth planet that does not transit (Buchhave et al. 2016). Early work found three sub-Neptune planets and two Earth-size candidates (Gautier et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler20b-Buchhaveetal2016","cite":"Buchhave et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Lars A. Buchhave, Courtney D. Dressing, Xavier Dumusque et al.","title":"A 1.9 Earth Radius Rocky Planet and the Discovery of a Non-transiting Planet in the Kepler-20 System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.06836","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler20b-Valenciaetal2013","cite":"Valencia et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Diana Valencia, Tristan Guillot, Vivien Parmentier et al.","title":"Bulk Composition of GJ 1214b and Other Sub-Neptune Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2629","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler20b-Gautieretal2012","cite":"Gautier et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas N., III Gautier, David Charbonneau, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"Kepler-20: A Sun-like Star with Three Sub-Neptune Exoplanets and Two Earth-size Candidates","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.4514","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0119L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is about 6 Jupiter masses and orbits an early M dwarf or a K dwarf (Shin et al. 2023). It was analysed together with two other planets from the 2021 microlensing season, all found by eye rather than by the automated search (Shin et al. 2023). Those by-eye detections give a useful comparison sample for the algorithm-selected planets (Shin et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The type of host star is not pinned down, and could be an early M dwarf or a K dwarf (Shin et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0119lb-Shinetal2023","cite":"Shin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"In-Gu Shin, Jennifer C. Yee, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets. III. Analysis of Three Giant Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.03886","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+03 2562 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is an evolved giant star with very low metallicity, [Fe/H] = -0.71 (Villaver et al. 2017).","observed":"The planet is a massive Jupiter-like world found around an aging giant star (Villaver et al. 2017). The star was followed with 46 epochs of Hobby-Eberly Telescope data over 3380 days and 19 epochs from HARPS-N over 919 days (Villaver et al. 2017). The velocity changes were checked against stellar activity, rotation and brightness variations and interpreted as the orbit of a planet (Villaver et al. 2017). The star has a mass of 1.14 Sun masses (Villaver et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"Evolved stars with planets help show how planet formation depends on the mass and metallicity of the parent star (Villaver et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd032562b-Villaveretal2017","cite":"Villaver et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"E. Villaver, A. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan et al.","title":"Tracking Advanced Planetary Systems (TAPAS) with HARPS-N. V. A Massive Jupiter orbiting the very-low-metallicity giant star BD+03 2562 and a possible planet around HD 103485","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.01278","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 136118 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was one of five targets of a Hubble Space Telescope program that tracks the tiny wobble of a star to measure true planet masses, rather than only a minimum mass (Benedict et al. 2008). The Fine Guidance Sensors reach a precision of about a thousandth of an arcsecond per observation (Benedict et al. 2008). About 2 years of Hubble data were combined with longer-running velocity measurements to cover orbits much longer than that span (Benedict et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd136118b-Benedictetal2008","cite":"Benedict et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, B. E. McArthur, E. Martioli et al.","title":"Exoplanet Masses from HST/FGS Astrometry - Progress Report","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #40","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-412 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is an inflated hot Jupiter, meaning it is larger than its mass would suggest (Deleuil et al. 2014). Kepler light curves from quarters 1 to 15 were combined with velocity measurements from the SOPHIE spectrograph (Deleuil et al. 2014). The light curve also gave the star's rotation period and the planet's phase function, which is how its brightness changes around the orbit, including the secondary eclipse when it passes behind the star (Deleuil et al. 2014). Later checks of its transit times found they fit a steady, linear schedule (Püsküllü & Ozturk 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler412b-Deleuiletal2014","cite":"Deleuil et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Deleuil, J.-M. Almenara, A. Santerne et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates XI. Kepler-412 system: probing the properties of a new inflated hot Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.6811","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler412b-PskllOzturk2023","cite":"Püsküllü & Ozturk 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Çağlar Püsküllü, Oguz Ozturk","title":"Investigating the Variation of Selected Kepler Objects Mid-Transit Times","journal":"Journal of Advanced Research in Natural and Applied Sciences","url":"https://doi.org/10.28979/jarnas.1201714","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 189733 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Three transits seen in ultraviolet hydrogen light with Hubble showed a 5.05 percent dip, deeper than the planet's disk alone can cause (Lecavelier Des Etangs et al. 2010). An extended cloud of hydrogen around the planet shows it is losing gas, at a rate between 10^9 and 10^11 grams per second (Lecavelier Des Etangs et al. 2010). Models of its transmission spectrum match some infrared data and help place a haze layer in its atmosphere (Fortney et al. 2010). Three-dimensional simulations predict that winds mix its chemistry from place to place (Zamyatina et al. 2023). Photochemical models have been used to test the effects of sulfur and carbon condensation (Tsai et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd189733b-Etangsetal2010","cite":"Etangs et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"A. Lecavelier Des Etangs, D. Ehrenreich, A. Vidal-Madjar et al.","title":"Evaporation of the planet HD 189733b observed in H I Lyman-α","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.2206","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd189733b-Fortneyetal2010","cite":"Fortney et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"J. J. Fortney, M. Shabram, A. P. Showman et al.","title":"Transmission Spectra of Three-Dimensional Hot Jupiter Model Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.2350","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd189733b-Zamyatinaetal2023","cite":"Zamyatina et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Maria Zamyatina, Eric Hébrard, Benjamin Drummond et al.","title":"Observability of signatures of transport-induced chemistry in clear atmospheres of hot gas giant exoplanets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09071","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd189733b-Tsaietal2021","cite":"Tsai et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Shang-Min Tsai, Matej Malik, Daniel Kitzmann et al.","title":"A Comparative Study of Atmospheric Chemistry with VULCAN","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.01790","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":37,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet belongs to one of the most tightly packed planetary systems known, with a chain of resonances found from changes in transit times (Weisserman et al. 2023). Timing analysis of the system gives masses for the planets, including 0.8 to 1.0 Earth masses for the newly found Kepler-80 g (Weisserman et al. 2023).","system":"The outer five planets are in successive three-body resonances, where three neighbours have linked orbital periods (Weisserman et al. 2023). The planets do not appear to be in two-body resonances (Weisserman et al. 2023). Such packed systems are hard to see with radial velocity surveys (Ragozzine et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Masses from transit timing can shift when a system may be missing a planet, so conclusions need caution (Weisserman et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80f-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80f-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 143811 AB b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a close pair of stars, which is rare among stars with imaged planets (Peck et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet is about 6.1 Jupiter masses and orbits at a projected distance of about 60 au (Squicciarini et al. 2025). It was spotted in archival SPHERE and GPI data reanalysed with new image-processing methods, and a new SPHERE observation confirmed it (Squicciarini et al. 2025). Gemini and Keck images from 2016, 2019 and 2022 show it moves with its host (Jones et al. 2025). Its orbit is mostly face-on, with a semimajor axis of about 64 au and an eccentricity near 0.23 (Jones et al. 2025).","system":"The two stars circle each other every 18.59 days, with a mass ratio of 0.886 (Peck et al. 2025). The system is a member of the Sco-Cen star-forming region, aged 13 ± 4 million years (Peck et al. 2025).","history":"Precise stellar properties are needed to understand how the planet formed and evolved (Peck et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd143811abb-Pecketal2025","cite":"Peck et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Anne E. Peck, William Roberson, Eric L. Nielsen et al.","title":"Characterization of the Host Binary of the Directly Imaged Exoplanet HD 143811 AB b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06727","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd143811abb-Squicciarinietal2025","cite":"Squicciarini et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"V. Squicciarini, J. Mazoyer, C. Wilkinson et al.","title":"GPI+SPHERE detection of a 6.1 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> circumbinary planet around HD 143811","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd143811abb-Jonesetal2025","cite":"Jones et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Nathalie K. Jones, Jason J. Wang, Eric L. Nielsen et al.","title":"HD 143811 AB b: A Directly Imaged Planet Orbiting a Spectroscopic Binary in Sco-Cen","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06729","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-67 b":{"text":{"why":"It is among the lowest-density gas giants known, at the boundary between hot and ultrahot planets (Bello-Arufe et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is a puffy, Saturn-mass world with a radius of about 2.14 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.45 ± 0.15 Jupiter masses (Wang et al. 2025). Its host star is a rapidly rotating subgiant, which makes the mass hard to measure (Zhou et al. 2017). Infrared and optical transit spectra show calcium and sodium in its atmosphere (Bello-Arufe et al. 2023). Hydrogen and helium absorption varied strongly, with early ingress and late egress, which suggests an extended outflow of gas with a mass loss rate near 10^13 grams per second (Bello-Arufe et al. 2023). The orbit is aligned with the star's spin, within a few degrees (Sicilia et al. 2024).","system":"The star rotates every 5.40 days, close to the planet's orbital period (Wang et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Low-density hot Saturns were expected to lose their atmospheres within 100 million years, yet this planet still holds one well after 1 billion years, and how it avoided mass loss is not settled (Wang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp67b-BelloArufeetal2023","cite":"Bello-Arufe et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Aaron Bello-Arufe, Heather A. Knutson, João M. Mendonça et al.","title":"Transmission Spectroscopy of the Lowest-density Gas Giant: Metals and a Potential Extended Outflow in HAT-P-67b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06356","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp67b-Wangetal2025","cite":"Wang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Gavin Wang, William O. Balmer, Laurent Pueyo et al.","title":"A Revised Density Estimate for the Largest Known Exoplanet, HAT-P-67 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13997","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp67b-Zhouetal2017","cite":"Zhou et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"G. Zhou, G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-67b: An Extremely Low Density Saturn Transiting an F-subgiant Confirmed via Doppler Tomography","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00106","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp67b-Siciliaetal2024","cite":"Sicilia et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"D. Sicilia, G. Scandariato, G. Guilluy et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. LVI. Characterisation of the low-density gas giant HAT-P-67 b with GIARPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.03317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 76920 b":{"text":{"why":"It has the most eccentric orbit of any planet known around an evolved star (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","observed":"The eccentricity is 0.8782, from 63 new velocity measurements concentrated around the closest approach to the star (Bergmann et al. 2021). At closest approach the planet comes within 2.4 stellar radii of the surface of its giant host (Bergmann et al. 2021). The minimum mass is 3.13 Jupiter masses and the period is 415.891 days (Bergmann et al. 2021). The planet is not currently showing significant orbital decay (Bergmann et al. 2021). Stellar evolution and tides are predicted to engulf it within about 100 million years (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"No unseen binary companion is indicated, which favours a scattering event over Kozai oscillations as the cause of the eccentric orbit (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd76920b-Wittenmyeretal2017","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, M. I. Jones, Jonathan Horner et al.","title":"The Pan-Pacific Planet Search. VII. The Most Eccentric Planet Orbiting a Giant Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05378","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd76920b-Bergmannetal2021","cite":"Bergmann et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Bergmann, M. I. Jones, J. Zhao et al.","title":"HD 76920 b pinned down: A detailed analysis of the most eccentric planetary system around an evolved star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08902","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 41378 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star hosts five confirmed transiting planets, with periods from 15 to over 542 days, a wide system comparable to the Solar System (Leonardi et al. 2025). Planets b and c are near a 2:1 period ratio, within about 1.8 percent, and pull on each other enough to shift transit times (Leonardi et al. 2025). Modelling these timing shifts together with 311 HARPS spectra confirmed a non-transiting sixth planet (Leonardi et al. 2025). Planet b has a radius of 2.45 Earth radii (Leonardi et al. 2025).","system":"The outer planet d has transited only twice, three years apart (Sulis et al. 2024). A CHEOPS search at the predicted time saw no transit (Sulis et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The true period of planet d is not settled; if it is not 278.36 days, 101.22 or 371.14 days would be the likeliest alternatives (Sulis et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip41378b-Leonardietal2025","cite":"Leonardi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"P. Leonardi, L. Borsato, L. Pagliaro et al.","title":"Transit timing variations in HIP 41378: CHEOPS and TESS confirm a non-transiting sixth planet in the system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14156","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip41378b-Sulisetal2024","cite":"Sulis et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"S. Sulis, L. Borsato, S. Grouffal et al.","title":"HIP 41378 observed by CHEOPS: Where is planet d?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20077","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3323 b":{"text":{"why":"Red dwarfs are currently the best targets for finding rocky planets in the habitable zone of their star (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017). Long-term monitoring of a small sample aims to reach lower planet masses and longer orbits than earlier samples (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017).","observed":"Many high-precision HARPS spectra of the red dwarf GJ 3323 were collected over years, together with four other red dwarfs (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017). From the spectra, radial velocities (the star's back-and-forth motion along our line of sight) and spectroscopic activity indicators were derived (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017). Activity indicators show whether a signal comes from starspots rather than a planet, so the planet's signal could be checked against the star's own variability (Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3323b-AstudilloDefruetal2017","cite":"Astudillo-Defru et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"N. Astudillo-Defru, T. Forveille, X. Bonfils et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLI. A dozen planets around the M dwarfs GJ 3138, GJ 3323, GJ 273, GJ 628, and GJ 3293","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05386","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-71 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"During a transit, the planet blocks part of the spinning star, which distorts the star's spectrum slightly. This Rossiter-McLaughlin effect was detected at high significance with the CORALIE spectrograph (Smith et al. 2013). It gave the sky-projected angle between the star's spin axis and the planet's orbit as 20.1 +/- 9.7 degrees, so the orbit is counted as aligned with the star's spin (Smith et al. 2013). Later Doppler tomography, which maps the star's surface from the same distortion, gave -1.9 degrees (+7.1, -7.5), again aligned (Brown et al. 2017). The planet is larger than Jupiter, at 1.46 Jupiter radii, yet less dense than Jupiter, at 0.71 of its density (Smith et al. 2013).","system":"The host is an evolved F8 star with an effective temperature of about 6059 K and a mass of 1.56 solar masses (Smith et al. 2013).","history":"Only stars hotter than 6250 K had been seen to host planets in misaligned orbits, and WASP-71 fits that pattern (Smith et al. 2013). With a mass of 1.56 solar masses, the star was hotter than 6250 K earlier in its life (Smith et al. 2013).","open":"Only the sky-projected angle is known, so whether the spin and orbit are misaligned along the line of sight is not known (Smith et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp71b-Smithetal2013","cite":"Smith et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"WASP-71b: a bloated hot Jupiter in a 2.9-day, prograde orbit around an evolved F8 star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.3045","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp71b-Brownetal2017","cite":"Brown et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. H. M. J. Triaud, A. P. Doyle et al.","title":"Rossiter-McLaughlin models and their effect on estimates of stellar rotation, illustrated using six WASP systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00600","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MXB 1658-298 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its host system, which is a strong X-ray source rather than an ordinary star. The system shines steadily at about 30 mCrab (a unit of X-ray brightness based on the Crab Nebula) and has produced X-ray bursts, one seen in September 1999 (Heise et al. 1999). It belongs to the group of X-ray binaries called dippers, systems seen close to the plane of their accretion disc, where gas swirls around the compact object (Boirin & Parmar 2003). Narrow iron absorption lines have been reported in its X-ray spectrum (Boirin & Parmar 2003).","system":"The system has two stars, and the planet orbits both of them.","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-mxb1658298b-Heiseetal1999","cite":"Heise et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"J. Heise, L. Natalucci, M. Cocchi et al.","title":"SAX J1719.6-4254, MXB 1658-298, and SAX J1819.3-2525","journal":"International Astronomical Union Circular","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-mxb1658298b-BoirinParmar2003","cite":"Boirin & Parmar 2003","year":"2003","authors":"L. Boirin, A. N. Parmar","title":"Discovery of narrow X-ray absorption features from the low-mass X-ray binary X 1254-690 with XMM-Newton","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0306526","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The K2-138 system was first spotted in K2 data by volunteers in the Exoplanet Explorers citizen science project, who helped identify one super-Earth and four sub-Neptune sized planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). All of them orbit near 3:2 mean-motion resonances, meaning the inner planets circle the star in close whole-number ratios of their periods (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Radial velocity masses have already been obtained for the inner four planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). A sixth planet at 41.966 days was confirmed with Spitzer (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","system":"With six known planets, K2-138 is the K2 system with the most known planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). The fifth planet orbits at 12.76 days, and the sixth at 41.966 days, which leaves a sizeable gap that might hold planets that do not transit (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). The host is a moderately bright early K dwarf (Hardegree-Ullman & Christiansen 2019).","history":"Dynamical work found evidence that three of the planets sit in a first-order three-planet resonance, the first such case linked to a real system (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024). Simulations of planets migrating through a gas disc show that capture into such a resonance is favoured by short-period planets of a few Earth masses (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024).","open":"Whether additional, smaller or non-transiting planets sit in the gap between the outer two planets is not known (Hardegree-Ullman & Christiansen 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138e-HardegreeUllmanetal2021","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie L. Christiansen, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Planet for the Citizen Science System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08978","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138e-HardegreeUllmanChristiansen2019","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman & Christiansen 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Kevin Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie Christiansen","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Sub-Neptune for the Citizen Science System","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138e-CerioniBeaug2024","cite":"Cerioni & Beaugé 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cerioni, C. Beaugé","title":"K2-138 and the capture in 1st-order 3P-MMRs","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1708 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet carries an exomoon candidate named Kepler-1708 b-i, reported from just two transits in Kepler data (Cassese & Kipping 2022). The candidate has a 1 per cent false-positive probability but still needs follow-up to be confirmed or rejected (Cassese & Kipping 2022). A Hubble observation was planned for the transit window in March 2023, but injection tests recovered the moon in only 7 of 75 trials, a true-positive chance of about 10 per cent (Cassese & Kipping 2022). Hubble's systematics and the moon's tight orbit blur the signal, while James Webb's NIRSpec should recover it easily (Cassese & Kipping 2022).","system":"The candidate moon's orbit is compact (Cassese & Kipping 2022). Simulations of the star, planet and moon found stable regions for the proposed moon parameters (Patel et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether the moon exists is not known; it remains a candidate (Cassese & Kipping 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1708b-CasseseKipping2022","cite":"Cassese & Kipping 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Ben Cassese, David Kipping","title":"Kepler-1708 b-i is likely undetectable with HST","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.13290","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1708b-Pateletal2025","cite":"Patel et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Shaan D. Patel, Billy Quarles, Manfred Cuntz","title":"Orbital stability of hierarchical three- and four-body systems with inclination: results for Kepler-1625, 1708, and HD 23079","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12258","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1683.01":{"text":{"why":"Young planets may lose their atmospheres faster, so the survey checked whether helium and hydrogen are escaping from 20 planets (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet, listed as TOI-1683 b, was observed with the high-resolution spectrographs CARMENES and GIARPS, searching for helium (He I) and hydrogen (H-alpha) light that would show an escaping atmosphere (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). The survey reported new helium detections for TOI-1268 b and TOI-2018 b and a hydrogen detection for TOI-1136 d (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). TOI-1683 b was not among the planets with a detection or a hint, so no sign of an escaping atmosphere is reported for it (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi168301-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148164 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up targeted stars rich in heavy elements, to exploit the link between metal-rich stars and giant planets (Ment et al. 2018).","observed":"Keck HIRES radial velocities of 378 stars, collected between 2004 and 2017, revealed a double-planet system around HD 148164 with minimum masses of 1.23 and 5.16 Jupiter masses (Ment et al. 2018). The new gas giants from that survey have orbits between 1.0 and 6.2 au across and eccentricities from 0.13 to 0.71 (Ment et al. 2018). Planets like this are called cold gas giants, since they orbit far from their star (Ment et al. 2018).","system":"HD 148164 hosts two giant planets (Ment et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148164c-Mentetal2018","cite":"Ment et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Kristo Ment, Debra A. Fischer, Gaspar Bakos et al.","title":"Radial Velocities from the N2K Project: Six New Cold Gas Giant Planets Orbiting HD 55696, HD 98736, HD 148164, HD 203473, and HD 211810","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01228","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-1 b":{"text":{"why":"Close-in giant planets around red dwarfs challenge ideas of planet formation, since few have been found (Bayliss et al. 2018).","observed":"With a mass of 0.812 Jupiter masses, NGTS-1 b is the most massive planet seen transiting an M dwarf (Bayliss et al. 2018). The transit is grazing, meaning the planet only partly crosses the star's disc, so its radius of 1.33 Jupiter radii is uncertain and rests on a density assumption from known gas giants (Bayliss et al. 2018). The transit is deep, at 2.5 per cent (Bayliss et al. 2018).","system":"The host is an early M dwarf with a temperature of 3916 K that shows no signs of activity (Bayliss et al. 2018). Its motion through the galaxy hints that it belongs to the old thick disc population (Bayliss et al. 2018).","history":"It is the third transiting giant planet found around an M dwarf, which supports the idea that close-in gas giants form and migrate in a similar way around red dwarfs as around Sun-like stars (Bayliss et al. 2018).","open":"The planet's composition is not known; James Webb transmission spectroscopy could probe it (Bayliss et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts1b-Baylissetal2018","cite":"Bayliss et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Daniel Bayliss, Edward Gillen, Philipp Eigmüller et al.","title":"NGTS-1b: a hot Jupiter transiting an M-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11099","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106270 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Among all planets that orbit inside the habitable zone, the zone where liquid water could exist on a rocky surface, HD 106270 b is one of the two most massive that is below 13 Jupiter masses (Hill et al. 2023). It is highlighted as an extreme case that tests how well ideas about habitability hold up outside the Earth-like standard (Hill et al. 2023). It is called an ideal target for learning about the hosts of possible habitable-zone moons (Hill et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has any moons is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106270b-Hilletal2023","cite":"Hill et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michelle L. Hill, Kimberly Bott, Paul A. Dalba et al.","title":"A Catalog of Habitable Zone Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/aca1c0","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1547L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was picked out by a search through earlier KMTNet data for anomalies that had no precise explanation (Han et al. 2023).","observed":"A binary-lens model with a giant planet roughly fitted the anomaly in the light curve, but left leftover signals unexplained (Han et al. 2023). Tests showed the leftovers come from a faint companion to the background star being magnified, and not from an extra lens component (Han et al. 2023). The fit that includes a second source star is better than the best three-lens fit by 24.7 in chi-squared, a statistical measure of how well a model matches the data (Han et al. 2023). In that solution, the planet has a mass of about 1.47 Jupiter masses (Han et al. 2023).","system":"The lens is a planetary system with one giant planet and a host star (Han et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1547lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Weicheng Zang, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-1547Lb: Giant microlensing planet detected through a signal deformed due to source binarity","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01280","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1410.01":{"text":{"why":"Hot sub-Neptunes are outliers that challenge models of planet formation and atmospheric escape (Whitsett et al. 2026).","observed":"A Hubble WFC3 transmission spectrum from 1.1 to 1.7 micrometres was made by comparing starlight filtered through the planet's atmosphere (Whitsett et al. 2026). Three separate data pipelines all show a flat spectrum, which suggests a high-metallicity planet, meaning an atmosphere rich in heavy elements, with significant aerosols such as clouds or haze (Whitsett et al. 2026). The planet is hot, with a temperature of 1236 K at a distance of 0.207 au from its star (Whitsett et al. 2026).","system":"TOI-1410 c is a warm Jupiter at 0.239 au from the star, close to this planet's orbit (Whitsett et al. 2026). A joint fit of TESS and HIRES radial velocity data studied the dynamics of the two (Whitsett et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi141001-Whitsettetal2026","cite":"Whitsett et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nathan Whitsett, Tansu Daylan, John Livingston et al.","title":"WFC3 Transmission Spectroscopy and Dynamics of TOI-1410.01","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 82943 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 82943 is one of only six stars known to host both multiple planets and dusty debris disks (Moro-Martin et al. 2010). Modelling of the dust's infrared brightness, including two new Spitzer spectra, constrained where the dust-producing rocky and icy bodies orbit (Moro-Martin et al. 2010). Long-term orbit simulations also tested which belt orbits stay stable (Moro-Martin et al. 2010). Separate simulations found that the two planets' orbits slowly rotate in step, in an alignment or opposite alignment of their long axes (Ji et al. 2003).","system":"Two planets orbit the star, and a disc of dust from colliding small bodies surrounds the system (Moro-Martin et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd82943c-MoroMartinetal2010","cite":"Moro-Martin et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Amaya Moro-Martín, Renu Malhotra, Geoffrey Bryden et al.","title":"Locating Planetesimal Belts in the Multiple-planet Systems HD 128311, HD 202206, HD 82943, and HR 8799","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd82943c-Jietal2003","cite":"Ji et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Jianghui Ji, Lin Liu, Hiroshi Kinoshita et al.","title":"The Librating Companions in HD 37124, HD 12661, HD 82943, 47 Ursa Majoris, and GJ 876: Alignment or Antialignment?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0305448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1136 hosts at least six transiting planets between about 2 and 5 Earth radii around a star 700 +/- 150 million years old (Dai et al. 2023). Their orbital period ratios sit within 10^-4 of exact whole-number ratios, a hundred times closer than the typical 10^-2 seen in Kepler systems (Dai et al. 2023). Transit-timing analysis gave planet masses of 3 to 8 Earth masses and showed the planets are in true resonances (Dai et al. 2023). A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement on planet d shows the star's spin is aligned with the orbits (Dai et al. 2023). Radial velocities from Keck HIRES (155 spectra), HARPS-N and APF were later collected (Beard et al. 2024).","system":"Period ratios near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2 make it the first known resonant chain with a 7:5 link between two 3:2 links (Dai et al. 2023). No binary companion was detected (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"Disc migration is suspected of building resonant chains, and later events could disrupt them (Dai et al. 2023). The chain's neat state and the star's spin alignment suggest it formed in an isolated, quiet disc, without a stellar flyby or disc warp (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136c-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1136c-Beardetal2024","cite":"Beard et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Beard, P. Robertson, F. Dai et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS-Keck Survey. XVII. TOI-1136 radial velocities (Beard+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 649 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Infrared interferometry, which combines light from several telescopes to measure the width of a star's disc, was used to measure the radius and temperature of GJ 649 directly (von Braun et al. 2014). Uncertainties are 0.5 to 5 per cent for radius and 0.2 to 2 per cent for temperature across the 11 stars studied (von Braun et al. 2014). The measurements fix where each system's habitable zone lies (von Braun et al. 2014).","system":"The star is one of 11 exoplanet host stars measured this way (von Braun et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj649b-vonBraunetal2014","cite":"von Braun et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Kaspar von Braun, Tabetha S. Boyajian, Gerard T. van Belle et al.","title":"Stellar diameters and temperatures - V. 11 newly characterized exoplanet host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1792","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"bet Pic d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its debris disc and its innermost planet beta Pic b. The star is the best studied normal star surrounded by a disc of dust (Artymowicz 1997). Gemini Planet Imager observations in polarized light traced the disc between about 6 and 33 au and found planet b orbiting at 9.2 au with an eccentricity of 0.26 or less (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015). The orbit's orientation is offset from both the outer main disc and the inner disc (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015). The orbit also fixes the star's mass at 1.60 +/- 0.05 solar masses (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015).","system":"The star has an inclined debris disc, seen almost edge-on at an angle of 85.27 degrees (Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015). The disc has very little gas, so it is more evolved than the nebula that made the Sun (Artymowicz 1997). Collisions between orbiting solid bodies grind them into dust, which radiation pressure removes (Artymowicz 1997).","history":"Dust and planetesimals around the star resemble a young Solar System in the clearing stage (Artymowicz 1997).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-betpicd-Artymowicz1997","cite":"Artymowicz 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Pawel Artymowicz","title":"Beta Pictoris: an Early Solar System?","journal":"Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.earth.25.1.175","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-betpicd-MillarBlanchaeretal2015","cite":"Millar-Blanchaer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maxwell A. Millar-Blanchaer, James R. Graham, Laurent Pueyo et al.","title":"Beta Pictoris' Inner Disk in Polarized Light and New Orbital Parameters for Beta Pictoris b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.04787","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 85390 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets of intermediate mass, between 30 and 100 Earth masses, should be rare inside a few au according to core accretion formation models (Mordasini et al. 2011).","observed":"HARPS radial velocities show a companion with a projected mass of 42.0 Earth masses on a 788-day orbit at 1.52 au, with an eccentricity of 0.41 (Mordasini et al. 2011). No analogue exists in the Solar System (Mordasini et al. 2011). Planets of this mass are not expected to be common at this distance in core-accretion models (Mordasini et al. 2011).","system":"A drift in the velocity data points to another companion on a long-period orbit, which the data do not cover (Mordasini et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"The outer companion is not yet characterised (Mordasini et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd85390b-Mordasinietal2011","cite":"Mordasini et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Mordasini, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIV. Companions to HD 85390, HD 90156, and HD 103197: a Neptune analog and two intermediate-mass planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.0856","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-56 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-56 has two inner planets on orbits in nearly the same plane, and the star's spin axis is strongly tilted compared with that plane (Li et al. 2014). An outer companion of about 3.3 Jupiter masses is also known, and the tilt of its orbit is not known (Li et al. 2014). Calculations from the observed line-of-sight angles were used to estimate the star's true spin tilt and the outer planet's orbit tilt (Li et al. 2014). If the star's spin started out aligned with the inner planets, the outer planet's orbit is tilted against theirs by anywhere from 20 to 160 degrees (Li et al. 2014). If the star's spin started out aligned with the whole system's total spin, a tilt near 40 or 130 degrees is favoured (Li et al. 2014).","system":"Two coplanar inner planets sit well out of line with their star's spin, and a massive outer planet of unknown orbit tilt accompanies them (Li et al. 2014).","history":"Physical processes that can tilt a star relative to its planets are the focus of the work, and the two starting conditions tried give different answers for the outer planet's orbit (Li et al. 2014).","open":"Measuring the outer planet's orbit tilt, or the star's true spin tilt from starquakes and from the planet's shadow crossing the star's spinning face, would tell the two starting conditions apart and show how the system formed (Li et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler56b-Lietal2014","cite":"Li et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Gongjie Li, Smadar Naoz, Francesca Valsecchi et al.","title":"Kepler 56: Present and Future Configuration and Obliquity","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #224","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"7 CMa b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it looks for planets around giant and sub-giant stars in the southern sky, using a sample of metal-rich sub-giants (Wittenmyer et al. 2016; Wittenmyer et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet, also known as HD 47205 b, was the first planet found by the Pan-Pacific Planet Search (Wittenmyer et al. 2016). The search measures tiny back-and-forth shifts in starlight with the 3.9 m Anglo-Australian Telescope (Wittenmyer et al. 2013). The star is extremely bright, so exposures lasted only 100 to 500 seconds (Wittenmyer et al. 2013). The first discovery data came from 21 observations spanning 917 days, with an average velocity uncertainty of 6.5 m/s (Wittenmyer et al. 2013). Updated velocities later allowed a refined orbit for the planet (Wittenmyer et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-7cmab-Wittenmyeretal2016","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. A. Wittenmyer, R. P. Butler, L. Wang et al.","title":"The Pan-Pacific Planet Search III: five companions orbiting giant stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.04343","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-7cmab-Wittenmyeretal2013","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"R. A. Wittenmyer, M. Endl, L. Wang et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Pan-Pacific Planet Search (PPPS) I. 7 CMa (Wittenmyer+, 2011)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.17430184","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-18 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour K2-18 b, a sub-Neptune that orbits in the habitable zone of the same red dwarf. Hubble transit observations of K2-18 b showed a strong signature of water vapour, the first molecular signal from the atmosphere of a super-Earth (Tsiaras 2021). A new analysis of the JWST spectrum of K2-18 b robustly detected methane, found only weak hints of carbon dioxide, and tested whether earlier claims of other molecules hold up (Fernández-Rodríguez et al. 2026). Little is known about K2-18 c itself.","system":"Planet b is a temperate sub-Neptune that may be a water world if its atmosphere is thin or absent (Daspute et al. 2025). Models suggest hydrogen and water may be separating inside it, which could explain why no water features show in its JWST spectrum (Howard et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Interior models of K2-18 b with gradual changes in composition allow a much wider range of structures than layered models, so its makeup is far from settled (Morf & Helled 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k218c-Tsiaras2021","cite":"Tsiaras 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Tsiaras","title":"K2-18b: first water vapour detection in a habitable-zone planet","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218c-FernndezRodrguezetal2026","cite":"Fernández-Rodríguez et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Gareb Fernández-Rodríguez, Giuseppe Morello, Jonathan C. Tan et al.","title":"The atmospheric composition of the sub-Neptune K2-18b and insights into its formation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18098","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218c-Dasputeetal2025","cite":"Daspute et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mangesh Daspute, Amri Wandel, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Potential Interior Structures and Habitability of Super-Earth Exoplanets LHS 1140 b, K2-18 b, TOI-1452 b, and TOI-1468 c","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.08476","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218c-Howardetal2025","cite":"Howard et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"S. Howard, R. Helled, A. Bergermann et al.","title":"The possibility of hydrogen-water demixing in Uranus, Neptune, K2-18 b and TOI-270 d","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06288","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k218c-MorfHelled2026","cite":"Morf & Helled 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Luca Morf, Ravit Helled","title":"Composition gradients in sub-Neptunes: K2-18 b and TOI-270 d as case studies","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04318","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148156 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of eight companions found around low-activity, sun-like stars in a volume-limited sample searched with the HARPS spectrograph. Across the eight, minimum masses run from 0.58 to 2.54 Jupiter masses, orbital periods from slightly under one year to almost seven years, and eccentricities from 0.08 to above 0.8 (Naef et al. 2010). Later work used Gaia DR2 distances to recompute the radiation the planet receives from its star. With the new numbers, HD 148156 b was added to the catalogue of potentially habitable worlds (Johns et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148156b-Naefetal2010","cite":"Naef et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Naef, M. Mayor, G. Lo Curto et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extrasolar planets. XXIII. 8 planetary companions to low-activity solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.4600","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd148156b-Johnsetal2018","cite":"Johns et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Daniel Johns, Connor Marti, Madison Huff et al.","title":"Revised Exoplanet Radii and Habitability Using Gaia Data Release 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.04533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-446 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-446 is a mid-M dwarf star, with a metal content lower than the Sun's, and it has three short-period, likely rocky transiting planets (Muirhead et al. 2015). The planets were first assigned wrongly large sizes relative to their star, as happened with the similar Kepler-42 system (Muirhead et al. 2015). Both stars are metal-poor and move fast through the galaxy (Muirhead et al. 2015). Sizes were refined by fixing the star's density from spectroscopy while fitting the short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"Compact systems of several small, close-in planets are common around mid-M dwarfs: about 21 percent of such stars host them, from a count combining Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and Kepler-42 (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler446b-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3071 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies well within the Neptunian desert, the range of short orbits where planets of Neptune to Saturn size are rare (Hacker et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with photometry, precise radial-velocity monitoring and high-resolution imaging (Hacker et al. 2024). It is a sub-Saturn, with a mass of about 68 Earth masses and a radius of about 7.2 Earth radii, orbiting an F8 star (Hacker et al. 2024). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1812 K, assuming a Bond albedo of 0.5 (Hacker et al. 2024). Of all known planets with masses between 10 and 300 Earth masses and radii under 1.5 Jupiter radii, it has the hottest equilibrium temperature (Hacker et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3071b-Hackeretal2024","cite":"Hacker et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Alejandro Hacker, Rodrigo F. Díaz, David J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-2374 b and TOI-3071 b: two metal-rich sub-Saturns well within the Neptunian desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12996","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planets g and h of this star pull on each other so strongly that their transits come up to 25 hours early or late (Liang et al. 2021). Analysing those timing shifts gave a single solution for the orbits and masses (Liang et al. 2021). Planet g has a mass of about 15 Earth masses but a very low apparent density of 0.15 g per cubic centimetre, so it is called a super-puff (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"Planets g and h have periods close to a 2:3 resonance but are not locked in it, and the system is stable over billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"Interactions between the planets may have raised their eccentricities and broken the resonant lock after the planets first formed (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351g-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 30177 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive Jupiter analog, orbiting at about 3.8 au with a minimum mass of 9.7 Jupiter masses (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). Further monitoring from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search revealed a second, more distant giant planet in the system (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","system":"The second planet, HD 30177 c, is a massive Saturn analog at about 9.9 au with a minimum mass of 7.6 Jupiter masses (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). Its orbit of about 32 years has not yet been fully observed (Wittenmyer et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The best-fit orbit for the outer planet, at about 7 au, was found unstable in dynamical simulations, so a longer-period, more circular orbit was adopted (Wittenmyer et al. 2017). More monitoring is needed to find distant giant planets near 10 au (Wittenmyer et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd30177b-Wittenmyeretal2017","cite":"Wittenmyer et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Robert A. Wittenmyer, Jonathan Horner, M. W. Mengel et al.","title":"The Anglo-Australian Planet Search. XXV. A Candidate Massive Saturn Analog Orbiting HD 30177","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.02072","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2714 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was confirmed in a study of how bulk density of giant planets may relate to the chemistry of their host stars (Mantovan et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with a radius of about 1.22 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.72 Jupiter masses, on a nearly circular orbit lasting about 2.5 days (Mantovan et al. 2024). Its star is metal-rich, with an iron abundance of 0.30 on a logarithmic scale where the Sun is zero (Mantovan et al. 2024). The mass came from follow-up with the HARPS spectrograph, which took 40 spectra of this and two other stars between April and September 2023 (Mantovan et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2714b-Mantovanetal2024","cite":"Mantovan et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Mantovan, T. G. Wilson, L. Borsato et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-4914, TOI-2714 and TOI-2981 RV curves (Mantovan+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-100 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's mass was re-measured using 187 high-precision radial velocities from the MAROON-X and KPF spectrographs, covering several rocky planets together (Brinkman et al. 2025). Kepler-100 b had been called a super-Mercury, a small planet that seemed to be made mostly of iron (Brinkman et al. 2025). The new mass is lower, which suggests a less iron-rich composition closer to that of Earth (Brinkman et al. 2025). Comparing the planet's iron-to-rock ratio with the star's, planets and their host stars appear to have similar ratios, with no steep relationship (Brinkman et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler100b-Brinkmanetal2025","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"The Compositions of Rocky Planets in Close-in Orbits Tend to Be Earth-like","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00213","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-102 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner and middle planets. Planets d and e had their masses re-measured with 111 new radial velocities from Keck/HIRES and HARPS-N, using a statistical model to separate the star's activity from the planets' tugs (Brinkman et al. 2023). Planet d, a super-Earth, has a best-fit mass of 2.5 Earth masses with a density consistent with rock like Earth's, and planet e, a sub-Neptune, has a mass of 4.7 Earth masses (Brinkman et al. 2023).","system":"Kepler-102 is an active star with tightly packed transiting planets, with orbital periods of 5 to 28 days (Brinkman et al. 2023; Volk & Malhotra 2020). The planets lie near, but are not locked in, mutual resonances (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","history":"Computer simulations of packed systems like this one tend to go unstable within 100 million years, yet the star is thought to be over 2 billion years old, so the real system must have avoided that fate (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","open":"The cause of the instability in simulations is not resonances between the planets (Volk & Malhotra 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler102f-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, James Cadman, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"Kepler-102: Masses and Compositions for a Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Orbiting an Active Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05196","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler102f-VolkMalhotra2020","cite":"Volk & Malhotra 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Volk, R. Malhotra","title":"Kepler-102: a case study for using dynamical constraints to characterize exoplanet systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 d":{"text":{"why":"The star is only about 700 million years old, so its planets are seen soon after they formed (Dai et al. 2023).","observed":"Planet d was studied in several ways. Transit-timing analysis measured the masses of the star's planets at 3 to 8 Earth masses (Dai et al. 2023). A Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement of planet d, which tracks the planet's shadow crossing the star's spinning face, showed the star's rotation is aligned with the planets' orbits (Dai et al. 2023). Helium and hydrogen-alpha observations looking for escaping atmosphere found a hydrogen-alpha signal from planet d (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Keck and HARPS-N observations collected over 2019 to 2022 give the radial velocities of the star (Beard et al. 2024).","system":"The star hosts at least six transiting planets of about 2 to 5 Earth radii in a chain of resonances, with period ratios near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2 (Dai et al. 2023). The planets are in true resonances with librating resonant angles, and the system is nearly coplanar (Dai et al. 2023). It is the first known resonant chain with a second-order resonance (7:5) between two first-order ones (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"Such a delicate chain suggests formation in an isolated, quiet disc, with no stellar flyby, disc warp or significant lopsidedness, and no detected binary companion (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136d-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1136d-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1136d-Beardetal2024","cite":"Beard et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Beard, P. Robertson, F. Dai et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS-Keck Survey. XVII. TOI-1136 radial velocities (Beard+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5344 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a giant orbiting a small red dwarf, a rare pairing, and its star is unusually rich in metals (Han et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based photometry from the Red Buttes Observatory 0.6 m telescope, radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and speckle imaging (Han et al. 2024). It is a Saturn-like giant with a radius of 9.7 Earth radii, a mass of about 135 Earth masses and a density of 0.80 g per cubic centimetre (Han et al. 2024). Its orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity of 0.06 (Han et al. 2024).","system":"The M0 host star has a high iron abundance, with [Fe/H] = 0.48, consistent with giant planet formation by core accretion (Han et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"M-dwarf hosts of giant planets appear to differ in metal content from hosts of smaller planets, but sample problems prevent a firm statistical test (Han et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5344b-Hanetal2024","cite":"Han et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Te Han, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"TOI-5344 b: A Saturn-like Planet Orbiting a Super-solar Metallicity M0 Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.20634","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-8 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a dense sub-Saturn on a circular orbit at 0.063 au, with a radius of 0.57 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.22 Jupiter masses (Bordé et al. 2010). Its mean density of 1.6 g per cubic centimetre is comparable to Neptune's (Bordé et al. 2010). Follow-up transit observations from 5 to 9 years after discovery showed transit times differing from predictions by up to about 115 minutes in five of six CoRoT planets studied, allowing refined timing of the planet's orbit (Raetz et al. 2019). Dark starspots crossing the star's face during transits have also been used to study the star (Valio 2017).","system":"The planet's heavy elements are estimated at 47 to 63 Earth masses, and its hydrogen-helium envelope at 7 to 23 Earth masses (Bordé et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Thermal loss of hydrogen from the planet at its orbit should be no more than 0.1 percent over an assumed lifetime of 3 billion years (Bordé et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot8b-Bordetal2010","cite":"Bordé et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"P. Borde, F. Bouchy, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CoRoT-8b light and RV curves (Borde+, 2010)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35200066","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot8b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot8b-Valio2017","cite":"Valio 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Adriana Valio","title":"Starspots properties and stellar activity from planetary transits","journal":"Living Around Active Stars","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921317004094","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 363-38 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up searched blindly for planets around nearby stars within 11 parsecs that had few or no previous velocity measurements, to find targets for future large telescopes (Sartori et al. 2023).","observed":"Its host is a nearby M dwarf of 0.21 solar masses, 10.23 parsecs away (Sartori et al. 2023). TESS photometry and archival VLT/NaCo high-contrast images were used to limit the orbit's tilt and the possible architecture of other planets in the system (Sartori et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l36338b-Sartorietal2023","cite":"Sartori et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Lia F. Sartori, Christophe Lovis, Jean-Baptiste Delisle et al.","title":"L 363-38 b: A planet newly discovered with ESPRESSO orbiting a nearby M dwarf star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.12710","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-446 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-446 is a mid-M dwarf star that hosts three short-period, likely rocky transiting planets (Muirhead et al. 2015). Its planets were first assigned wrongly large sizes relative to their star, as in the similar Kepler-42 system, and the sizes were refined by fixing the star's density from spectroscopy while fitting the short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015). The star is metal-poor and moves fast through the galaxy (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"About 21 percent of mid-M dwarfs host compact multiples, from a count combining Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and Kepler-42 (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler446c-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-60 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was independently detected and characterised using time-series data from the K2 mission combined with spectroscopic observations from FIES at the Nordic Optical Telescope and HARPS-N at the Telescopio Nazionale Galileo (Eigmüller et al. 2017). It is a hot sub-Jovian planet with a radius of 0.683 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.426 Jupiter masses, orbiting a G4 star every 3.003 days (Eigmüller et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k260b-Eigmlleretal2017","cite":"Eigmüller et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Philipp Eigmüller, Davide Gandolfi, Carina M. Persson et al.","title":"K2-60b and K2-107b. A Sub-Jovian and a Jovian Planet from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.03704","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-58 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a typical short-period hot Jupiter, with a period of 4.2 days and a radius of about 1.1 to 1.2 Jupiter radii (Espinoza et al. 2019). Its effective temperature lies between 1350 and 1750 K, in the range proposed for maximum efficiency of radius inflation (Espinoza et al. 2019).","system":"The system has two stars, HATS-58 A and HATS-58 B, picked out using Gaia DR2 data, and the planet orbits the brighter star, HATS-58 A (Espinoza et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats58ab-Espinozaetal2019","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Espinoza, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-54b-HATS-58Ab: Five New Transiting Hot Jupiters Including One with a Possible Temperate Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07668","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AU Mic d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Hubble far-ultraviolet observations of AU Mic over 5 hours found 13 flares with energies of 10^29 to 10^31 erg (Feinstein et al. 2022). In each flare, the flux at wavelengths below 1100 angstroms rose about 100 times (Feinstein et al. 2022). The star shows spot-driven brightness changes with a period of 4.847 days (Hebb et al. 2007).","system":"AU Mic is a young M dwarf, about 22 million years old, with a debris disc seen nearly edge-on outside its planets (Feinstein et al. 2022; Hebb et al. 2007). The baseline atmospheric mass-loss rate for planet b is about 10^8 g per second (Feinstein et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-aumicd-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Kevin France, Allison Youngblood et al.","title":"AU Microscopii in the Far-UV: Observations in Quiescence, during Flares, and Implications for AU Mic b and c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09606","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aumicd-Hebbetal2007","cite":"Hebb et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Leslie Hebb, Larry Petro, Holland C. Ford et al.","title":"A search for planets transiting the M-dwarf debris disc host, AU Microscopii","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.3584","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"47 UMa d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about this outer planet itself; work so far has studied its star and the system as a whole. The star shows no strong magnetic activity, which makes it a quiet target for precise velocity measurements (Henry et al. 1997). Its chemical make-up is roughly like the Sun's, and its age is similar to the Sun's (Gonzalez 1996). Computer simulations have tested whether an Earth-like planet could keep a stable orbit in the star's habitable zone, the region where liquid water could exist (Asghari et al. 2004).","system":"The system's middle planet, 47 UMa c, is a planned target for direct imaging in reflected light with the Roman Space Telescope's coronagraph (Smith et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-47umad-Henryetal1997","cite":"Henry et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Gregory W. Henry, Sallie L. Baliunas, Robert A. Donahue et al.","title":"Properties of Sun-like Stars with Planets: 51 Pegasi, 47 Ursae Majoris, 70 Virginis, and HD 114762","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/303451","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umad-Gonzalez1996","cite":"Gonzalez 1996","year":"1996","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez","title":"Spectroscopic Analyses of the Planetary System Candidates: 55 Cnc, 51 Peg, 47 UMa, 70 Vir, and HD 114762","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9609148","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umad-Asgharietal2004","cite":"Asghari et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"N. Asghari, C. Broeg, L. Carone et al.","title":"Stability of terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of Gl 777 A, HD 72659, Gl 614, 47 Uma and HD 4208","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0403152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umad-Smithetal2022","cite":"Smith et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adam JRW Smith, Eric Nielsen, Bruce Macintosh et al.","title":"47 Ursae Majoris c: Exploring Roman CGI Observation Strategies","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-712 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is young, with an age between about 500 million and 1.1 billion years, worked out from its 12.48-day spin period (Vach et al. 2022). Planets around young stars show how planets change in their first billion years. TOI-712 b is one of three mini-Neptunes, planets a little larger than Earth with thick gas envelopes, that were seen crossing the star (Vach et al. 2022). The star is bright, which makes follow-up study easier (Vach et al. 2022).","system":"The system has three transiting mini-Neptunes at 9.5, 51.7 and 84.8 days (Vach et al. 2022). A fourth, Earth-sized candidate with a 4.32-day period, TOI-712.05, was found after modelling the three planets (Vach et al. 2022). The outermost, TOI-712 d, lies near the inner edge of the star's habitable zone (Vach et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi712b-Vachetal2022","cite":"Vach et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sydney Vach, Samuel N. Quinn, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TOI-712: A System of Adolescent Mini-Neptunes Extending to the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.02416","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-443 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Kepler-443 is one of 13 stars in a study of the hosts of the most Earth-like planets found by Kepler (Armstrong et al. 2016). The study measured how fast each star spins, how active it is, how strongly it flares and how fast it loses mass (Armstrong et al. 2016). It also considered what that activity means for the planets' magnetic fields and habitability (Armstrong et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler443b-Armstrongetal2016","cite":"Armstrong et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Armstrong, C. E. Pugh, A.-M. Broomhall et al.","title":"The host stars of Kepler's habitable exoplanets: superflares, rotation and activity","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05306","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 163 b":{"text":{"why":"The search aimed to find planets of 1 to 10 Earth masses in the habitable zones of cool stars (Bonfils et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet was found in 150 velocity measurements gathered over eight years (Bonfils et al. 2013). The data showed five repeating signals. Activity on the star could explain two of them, so only three were accepted as planets (Bonfils et al. 2013). Planet b is the innermost of these three (Bonfils et al. 2013).","system":"Planet c has a 25.63-day period and planet d a 604-day period (Bonfils et al. 2013). The two inner planets have a period ratio of 2.97, close to 3, but a dynamical analysis shows they lie outside the 3:1 resonance (Bonfils et al. 2013). The inner planet pair is therefore not locked in a resonance.","history":"","open":"Two further signals, at 19.4 and 108 days, are not yet interpreted. More data are needed to say whether they are planets (Bonfils et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj163b-Bonfilsetal2013","cite":"Bonfils et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"X. Bonfils, G. Lo Curto, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXXIV. A planetary system around the nearby M dwarf <ASTROBJ>GJ 163</ASTROBJ>, with a super-Earth possibly in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.0904","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3988 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found by combining 153 spectra from the CARMENES spectrograph on the 3.5 m Calar Alto telescope with infrared velocities from the Subaru telescope's IRD instrument (Gorrini et al. 2023). The best-fitting model for the star has one planet only (Gorrini et al. 2023). Its orbit is circular and lies within 0.05 au of the star (Gorrini et al. 2023). Only a minimum mass is known, because the orbit's tilt is not measured.","system":"The star is a small, cool M dwarf. A second M dwarf, GJ 724, was announced in the same study with its own planet, which has the most eccentric orbit of any single planet around an M dwarf known at the time (Gorrini et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3988b-Gorrinietal2023","cite":"Gorrini et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"P. Gorrini, J. Kemmer, S. Dreizler et al.","title":"Planetary companions orbiting the M dwarfs GJ 724 and GJ 3988. A CARMENES and IRD collaboration","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-7 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour CoRoT-7 b. The star's spin period is 23 days, and the signal from planet b at 0.8535 days has an amplitude of 3.3 m/s in the velocity data (Queloz et al. 2009). Planet c matters mostly for what it does to planet b. Its pull on b could keep b's orbit slightly eccentric if c's own eccentricity is above about 0.0001 (Barnes et al. 2010).","system":"A slightly eccentric orbit for CoRoT-7 b would make tides heat it strongly, possibly driving extreme volcanism like Jupiter's moon Io (Barnes et al. 2010). Tides from the star may also have shrunk b's orbit over time, possibly carrying it through orbital resonances with c (Jackson et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot7c-Quelozetal2009","cite":"Queloz et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Queloz, F. Bouchy, C. Moutou et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CoRoT-7 radial velocities (Queloz+, 2009)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35060303","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7c-Barnesetal2010","cite":"Barnes et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Rory Barnes, Sean N. Raymond, Richard Greenberg et al.","title":"CoRoT-7b: Super-Earth or Super-Io?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1337","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7c-Jacksonetal2010","cite":"Jackson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Brian Jackson, Neil Miller, Rory Barnes et al.","title":"The roles of tidal evolution and evaporative mass loss in the origin of CoRoT-7 b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2186","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-733 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet sits in the small-planet radius valley, the gap in sizes where planets change from rocky to gas-rich (Georgieva et al. 2023). Its density is 3.98 g/cm3, between those of rocky planets and planets with thick hydrogen-helium envelopes (Georgieva et al. 2023). Interior and atmosphere models point to an outer layer rich in volatiles, most likely a secondary one formed after the planet itself (Georgieva et al. 2023). Its equilibrium temperature is about 1055 K (Georgieva et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi733b-Georgievaetal2023","cite":"Georgieva et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Iskra Y. Georgieva, Carina M. Persson, Elisa Goffo et al.","title":"TOI-733 b: A planet in the small-planet radius valley orbiting a Sun-like star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06655","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-257 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm sub-Saturn, a world lighter than Saturn that is not especially hot, with a bulk density of about 0.65 g/cm3 (Addison et al. 2021). Warm sub-Saturns are rare among known planets (Addison et al. 2021). Its star is a slightly evolved late F-type star, whose oscillations were measured to pin down its size and mass (Addison et al. 2021). The star is among only about 100 planet hosts studied this way (Addison et al. 2021). The orbit is mildly eccentric, with e about 0.24 (Addison 2021).","system":"Velocity data hint at a second, non-transiting sub-Saturn-mass planet on a roughly 71-day orbit (Addison et al. 2021). Its orbit would be close to a 1:4 resonance with TOI-257 b (Addison 2021).","history":"","open":"The second planet is a hint in the data and is not yet confirmed (Addison et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi257b-Addisonetal2021","cite":"Addison et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Brett C. Addison, Duncan J. Wright, Belinda A. Nicholson et al.","title":"TOI-257b (HD 19916b): a warm sub-saturn orbiting an evolved F-type star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.07345","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi257b-Addison2021","cite":"Addison 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Brett C. Addison, Duncan J. Wright, Belinda A. Nicholson et al.","title":"TOI-257b (HD 19916b): a warm sub-saturn orbiting an evolved F-type star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.07345","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KIC 3558849 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from Planet Hunters, a project in which volunteers look through archived Kepler light curves for transits that the automatic searches missed (Wang et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet was one of three validated planets that showed only a single transit in the Kepler data (Wang et al. 2015). Its orbital period is therefore estimated from the length of the transit and the properties of the star (Wang et al. 2015). Adaptive-optics images of the star were taken to look for blending sources, and the planet was validated statistically at over 3 sigma confidence (Wang et al. 2015). Planets from this sample mostly lie 1 to 3 au from their stars, farther than most Kepler planets (Wang et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kic3558849b-Wangetal2015","cite":"Wang et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Ji Wang, Debra A. Fischer, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Planet Hunters. VIII. Characterization of 41 Long-period Exoplanet Candidates from Kepler Archival Data","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.02559","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-99 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with an orbit compatible with a perfect circle (Hellier et al. 2014). Its transit is the shallowest yet found by the WASP-South survey, with the star dimming by only 0.4 per cent (Hellier et al. 2014). That makes it a good test of how well the survey finds smaller or fainter signals (Hellier et al. 2014). The star is of spectral type F2 to G8, like the other six planets announced in the same study (Hellier et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp99b-Hellieretal2014","cite":"Hellier et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Transiting hot Jupiters from WASP-South, Euler and TRAPPIST: WASP-95b to WASP-101b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5630","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-52 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with three others from the same ground-based survey, one of them a hot Saturn and two others hot Jupiters (Hartman et al. 2015). The star is faint, at magnitude V = 14.1 (Hartman et al. 2015). Its orbit is consistent with a circle (Hartman et al. 2015). The planet's mass of 0.82 Jupiter masses and radius of 1.01 Jupiter radii were measured to better than 10 per cent (Hartman et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp52b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-50b, HAT-P-51b, HAT-P-52b, and HAT-P-53b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters and a Transiting Hot Saturn From the HATNet Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04149","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 183579 b":{"text":{"why":"A search of archived velocity data for 18 single-planet TESS candidates found one with a matching signal at the right period and phase. HD 183579 b was the one, and it was confirmed without new observations (Palatnick et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet is a warm sub-Neptune, a planet smaller than Neptune with a thick envelope, around a solar twin (Gan et al. 2021). It is among the most accessible small transiting planets for atmospheric study, and its star is the fifth brightest known to host a sub-Neptune (Palatnick et al. 2021; Gan et al. 2021). Two CHEOPS transits were later used to refine its radius (Bonfanti et al. 2023). Detailed comparison of the star's spectrum shows no significant loss of refractory (rock-forming) elements (Gan et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Two earlier analyses of nearly the same HARPS data gave masses that differed by about 2 sigma. A longer HARPS series and CHEOPS transits were used to settle the mass at 20.4 Earth masses (Bonfanti et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd183579b-Palatnicketal2021","cite":"Palatnick et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Skyler Palatnick, David Kipping, Daniel Yahalomi","title":"Validation of HD 183579b Using Archival Radial Velocities: A Warm Neptune Orbiting a Bright Solar Analog","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.12137","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd183579b-Ganetal2021","cite":"Gan et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Tianjun Gan, Megan Bedell, Sharon Xuesong Wang et al.","title":"HD 183579b: a warm sub-Neptune transiting a solar twin detected by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14015","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd183579b-Bonfantietal2023","cite":"Bonfanti et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Bonfanti, D. Gandolfi, J. A. Egger et al.","title":"TOI-1055 b: Neptunian planet characterised with HARPS, TESS, and CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.10664","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1416 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transits are very shallow, with a depth of about 455 parts per million (Deeg et al. 2023). Velocity data from five instruments confirmed the planet (Deeg et al. 2023). Its density is slightly below Earth's, at 4.50 g/cm3 (Deeg et al. 2023). The planet has an equilibrium temperature of about 1570 K (Deeg et al. 2023). A molten magma interior holding a lot of dissolved water would explain its make-up (Deeg et al. 2023). Its atmosphere could be studied with JWST (Deeg et al. 2023).","system":"A second, tentative planet with a period of 27.4 or 29.5 days appears in the velocity data. A signal from the Moon's 29.53-day cycle probably contaminates it, so it cannot be verified (Deeg et al. 2023).","history":"Its place in the radius-period diagram suggests that planets with periods under one day are not a separate group but the short-period end of the super-Earth population (Deeg et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1416b-Deegetal2023","cite":"Deeg et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"H. J. Deeg, I. Y. Georgieva, G. Nowak et al.","title":"TOI-1416: A system with a super-Earth planet with a 1.07 d period","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18542","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164604 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities combined with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of the star's motion give it a true mass of about 9.5 Jupiter masses, or 7.6 depending on which of two orbital orientations is right (Xiao et al. 2026). The orbit has a radius of about 5.6 au and an eccentricity of about 0.2 (Xiao et al. 2026).","system":"The inner planet, HD 164604 b, has an eccentric orbit, with e = 0.479 (Xiao et al. 2026). The two planets could orbit almost in the same plane, tilted by about 5 degrees. They could instead move in opposite directions, tilted by about 162 degrees (Xiao et al. 2026).","history":"If the orbits are opposed, the system had a violent past, possibly with von Zeipel-Lidov-Kozai cycles, slow tilting swings driven by an outer body, or with planets scattering each other. If the orbits lie in one plane, gentle migration through the disc fits better (Xiao et al. 2026).","open":"Which orientation is true is not known yet. Most opposed-orbit models stay stable for at least 10 million years, while same-direction models may turn chaotic or eject a planet (Xiao et al. 2026). Future Gaia DR4 astrometry is expected to settle the question (Xiao et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164604c-Xiaoetal2026","cite":"Xiao et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Guang-Yao Xiao, Fabo Feng, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"HD 164604 c: A Second Giant Planet on a 15 Yr Orbit and Constraints on the Planet─Planet Mutual Inclination","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae4490","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-74 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a close-in giant, found around an F8-type star (Hébrard et al. 2013). It was among the first planets characterised with the HARPS-N spectrograph (Hébrard et al. 2013). Its orbit is eccentric, and it lies right at the border where tides can and cannot make orbits circular (Hébrard et al. 2013). Later modelling of Kepler's finer-sampled transit data refined its parameters (Bonomo et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"Hot Jupiters are thought to form far from their stars and move inward, either slowly through the disc, which keeps orbits circular, or by interacting with other bodies, which stretches orbits (Welch et al. 2024). Kepler-74 b was picked as the pilot case for a uniform re-analysis of all transiting giant planets (Welch et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler74b-Hbrardetal2013","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Hébrard, J.-M. Almenara, A. Santerne et al.","title":"KOI-200 b and KOI-889 b: Two transiting exoplanets detected and characterized with Kepler, SOPHIE, and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.6002","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler74b-Bonomoetal2015","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, A. Sozzetti, A. Santerne et al.","title":"Improved parameters of seven Kepler giant companions characterized with SOPHIE and HARPS-N","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.02653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler74b-Welchetal2024","cite":"Welch et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shaniylah Welch, Noah Vowell, Joseph Rodriguez et al.","title":"Kepler-74 b: A Pilot Study for the Comprehensive Reanalysis of all Transiting Giant Planets","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #243","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-260 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with velocities from the ESPRESSO spectrograph on the VLT, combined with TESS and ground-based photometry, high-resolution imaging, and HARPS and HIRES velocities (Hobson et al. 2024). The planet is a super-Earth, a rocky world larger than Earth, around a late K dwarf (Hobson et al. 2024). Its mass of 4.23 Earth masses carries a large uncertainty of 1.60 (Hobson et al. 2024). Its radius is 1.71 Earth radii (Hobson et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi260b-Hobsonetal2024","cite":"Hobson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. J. Hobson, F. Bouchy, B. Lavie et al.","title":"Three super-Earths and a possible water world from TESS and ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06278","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-45 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter with 0.89 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.43 Jupiter radii, orbiting a star of V = 12.8 (Hartman et al. 2014). The velocity data show extra variations that might mean a second, unseen companion, but there are not enough data to justify a two-planet model (Hartman et al. 2014). A single planet is preferred. The star needs a relatively high jitter of 22.5 m/s to fit, high for this type of star (Hartman et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether another planet orbits the star is not known (Hartman et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp45b-Hartmanetal2014","cite":"Hartman et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-44b, HAT-P-45b, and HAT-P-46b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters in Possible Multi-planet Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2937","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-421 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is Uranus-sized and orbits a G9/K0 dwarf in a near-circular orbit (Kipping et al. 2014). It receives the same insolation as a body at about 2 au in the Solar System (Kipping et al. 2014). With a Uranus-like reflectivity, its temperature would be about 180 K (Kipping et al. 2014). Its mass is not yet measured, only limited from above (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"A model of the star's planet-forming disc suggests the planet's present orbit was beyond the snow line, where water freezes, after about 3 million years. Kepler-421 b may therefore have formed where it is now (Kipping et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler421b-Kippingetal2014","cite":"Kipping et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. M. Kipping, G. Torres, L. A. Buchhave et al.","title":"Discovery of a Transiting Planet near the Snow-line","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.4807","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler421b-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-99 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit was poorly pinned down until 60 new radial velocity measurements (tiny back-and-forth shifts of the star's light) from three spectrographs were added (Smith et al. 2022). They show a minimum mass of 8.4 Jupiter masses and an eccentric orbit (Smith et al. 2022). A transit across the star was thought to have a good chance of showing up in TESS observations in 2022, but only if the two planets' orbits lie in nearly the same plane (Smith et al. 2022). An unexplained steady drift in the star's velocity had earlier pointed to a third body, perhaps a brown dwarf with a period of several hundred days (Smith et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a metal-rich subgiant, a star that has swollen as it ages, with a radius of 2.55 times the Sun's (Smith et al. 2022). Planet b is a warm Jupiter that crosses the star every 18.25 days (Smith et al. 2017). It is not puffed up, with a radius of 1.06 Jupiter radii (Smith et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether the outer planet transits is not known, because that depends on how closely the two orbits are aligned (Smith et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k299c-Smithetal2022","cite":"Smith et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, S. N. Breton, Sz Csizmadia et al.","title":"K2-99 revisited: a non-inflated warm Jupiter, and a temperate giant planet on a 522-d orbit around a subgiant","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.14660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k299c-Smithetal2017","cite":"Smith et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, D. Gandolfi, O. Barragán et al.","title":"K2-99: a subgiant hosting a transiting warm Jupiter in an eccentric orbit and a long-period companion","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.00239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TRAPPIST-1 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spitzer Space Telescope watched the star for more than 1000 hours between February 2016 and October 2019 (Ducrot et al. 2020). Astronomers analysed 188 transits, with the planets crossing the star at 3.6 or 4.5 micrometres, and eight occultations of planet c, where the planet passes behind the star, to constrain how hot its dayside is (Ducrot et al. 2020). Timing shifts in the transits show that all seven planets are locked in a chain of orbital resonances, where their periods form simple ratios (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","system":"The star is a nearby ultracool dwarf, a very small and dim red star, 12 pc away (Ducrot et al. 2020). It has seven Earth-sized planets, and at least three of them orbit inside the classical habitable zone (Ducrot et al. 2020). All seven are seen as good targets for atmosphere studies with JWST (Ducrot et al. 2020).","history":"Computer simulations of planets moving inward through a gas disc can build a resonant chain like the observed one, but only for particular disc conditions (Teyssandier et al. 2022).","open":"Whether planet c has an atmosphere is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-trappist1c-Ducrotetal2020","cite":"Ducrot et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. Ducrot, M. Gillon, L. Delrez et al.","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Global results of the Spitzer Exploration Science Program Red Worlds","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13826","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-trappist1c-Teyssandieretal2022","cite":"Teyssandier et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Teyssandier, A.-S. Libert, E. Agol","title":"TRAPPIST-1: Dynamical analysis of the transit-timing variations and origin of the resonant chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.03340","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 35843 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour HD 35843 c, a sub-Neptune on a 46.96-day orbit that was first spotted by volunteers in the Planet Hunters TESS project (Hesse et al. 2025). While measuring that neighbour's mass with the ESPRESSO spectrograph, astronomers found an extra signal in the star's radial velocities, the tiny back-and-forth shifts of its light (Hesse et al. 2025). The signal reveals this inner planet, with a period of 9.90 days and a minimum mass of 5.84 Earth masses (Hesse et al. 2025). Its radius is not known, because it has not been seen to transit.","system":"The host is a bright, metal-poor, Sun-like star (Hesse et al. 2025). Planet c has a radius of 2.54 Earth radii and a mass of 11.32 Earth masses, so it may be rocky with a thick hydrogen atmosphere or a water world (Hesse et al. 2025). Its temperature of about 480 K places it among the coolest 5% of planets found by TESS (Hesse et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Planet c is a promising target for atmosphere studies (Hesse et al. 2025). Its inner companion has no measured radius or composition."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd35843b-Hesseetal2025","cite":"Hesse et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Katharine Hesse, Ismael Mireles, François Bouchy et al.","title":"HD 35843: A Sun-like Star Hosting a Long-period Sub-Neptune and Inner Super-Earth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00898","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-763 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star is a fairly bright, high proper motion Sun-like star that TESS watched during Sector 10 (Fridlund et al. 2020). Two transit signals in its light curve were confirmed as planets with radial velocity follow-up from the HARPS spectrograph (Fridlund et al. 2020). Planet b has a mass of 9.8 Earth masses and a radius of 2.37 Earth radii (Fridlund et al. 2020). Both numbers place it among the sub-Neptunes, planets between Earth and Neptune in size (Fridlund et al. 2020).","system":"A second transiting planet, c, orbits every 12.3 days with a radius of 2.87 Earth radii (Fridlund et al. 2020). A third planet candidate turned up by chance in the radial velocity data, with a period of about 48 days and a minimum mass of 9.5 Earth masses (Fridlund et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The outermost candidate is not yet confirmed as a planet (Fridlund et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi763b-Fridlundetal2020","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Fridlund, J. Livingston, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"The TOI-763 system: sub-Neptunes orbiting a Sun-like star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.12535","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1246 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Astronomers collected 125 radial velocity measurements with the Keck and HARPS-N spectrographs to weigh all four transiting planets in this system (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Planet d has a radius of 3.46 Earth radii and a mass of 5.3 Earth masses, the lowest mass of the four (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Its low mass for its size makes it a fluffy sub-Neptune, a planet between Earth and Neptune in size (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","system":"TOI-1246 is a moderately bright K dwarf with four transiting sub-Neptunes on orbits of 4.31, 5.90, 18.66 and 37.92 days (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The outermost planet, e, is much heavier than the three inner ones, at 15.2 Earth masses (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Planets d and e lie just outside a 2:1 resonance, where one orbit takes twice as long as the other, and tug on each other enough to shift their transit times (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Only six systems have measured masses and radii for all four transiting planets, and TOI-1246 is one of them (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1246d-Turtelboometal2022","cite":"Turtelboom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emma V. Turtelboom, Courtney Dressing, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"TOI-1246: Mass Measurements for Four Transiting sub-Neptunes","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-111 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-111 c was weighed using years of radial velocity measurements, tiny shifts in the star's light, combined with its transit light curve (Dalba et al. 2024). Long-period giants like it are hard to study, because transits are rare and the observations must span many years (Dalba et al. 2024). The planet's mass was measured precisely for the first time as part of a survey of 11 stars thought to host cool giant planets (Dalba et al. 2024). The same survey showed that several other supposed planets are not planets at all (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler111c-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Paul A. Dalba, Stephen R. Kane, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT 'EM) Survey. IV. Long-term Doppler Spectroscopy for 11 Stars Thought to Host Cool Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03021","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-44 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The survey that turned up this planet announced three hot Jupiters at once, each with signs of further bodies in the system (Hartman et al. 2014). The star's velocity measurements show systematic drifts that the transiting planet cannot explain (Hartman et al. 2014). The best model has a second, outer planet with a period of 872 days, an eccentricity of 0.494 and a minimum mass of 4.0 Jupiter masses (Hartman et al. 2014). Gaps in the data mean periods of 220 or 438 days cannot be ruled out (Hartman et al. 2014). Later ground-based transit and monitoring observations were collected to search for extra planets in hot Jupiter systems (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the outer planet exists, and on which orbit, is not settled (Hartman et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp44b-Hartmanetal2014","cite":"Hartman et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-44b, HAT-P-45b, and HAT-P-46b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters in Possible Multi-planet Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2937","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp44b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, M. Fernandez, A. Sota et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems (Maciejewski+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1749 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1749 d is the outermost of three small planets circling an early M dwarf at a distance of about 100 pc (Fukui et al. 2021). Follow-up included multiband transit photometry, adaptive optics imaging and low-resolution spectroscopy (Fukui et al. 2021). Shifts in the planets' transit times set a 95% upper limit of 15 Earth masses on planet d (Fukui et al. 2021). The two outer planets, c and d, have a period ratio within 1% of 2, so one orbit takes almost exactly twice as long as the other (Fukui et al. 2021). The trio resembles other planet trios found around M dwarfs, such as TOI-175 and TOI-270 (Fukui et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"Sizes and periods fit a picture in which all three planets began with a hydrogen envelope on a rocky core, and only the innermost planet has since lost its envelope to heating from the star or from its own core (Fukui et al. 2021).","open":"Only upper limits on the masses are known (Fukui et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1749d-Fukuietal2021","cite":"Fukui et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Fukui, J. Korth, J. H. Livingston et al.","title":"TOI-1749: an M dwarf with a Trio of Planets including a Near-resonant Pair","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05430","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-222 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New transit observations from the CHEOPS space telescope were combined with earlier K2, TESS and ground-based photometry and with radial velocity data from several spectrographs (Palethorpe et al. 2026). Together they give precise bulk densities for six systems (Palethorpe et al. 2026). K2-222 b falls in the low-density group, which is consistent with a gas dwarf, a rocky planet with a thin hydrogen envelope, or with a water world (Palethorpe et al. 2026).","system":"Several of the systems in the study show signs of extra companions in the radial velocities that are not seen in transit (Palethorpe et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is a gas dwarf or a water world is not settled (Palethorpe et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2222b-Palethorpeetal2026","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Larissa Palethorpe, Annelies Mortier, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Constraining small planet compositions for future missions","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14552","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 67522 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"JWST measured a 3 to 5 micrometre transmission spectrum, which shows starlight filtered through the planet's atmosphere during a transit (Thao et al. 2024). Absorption features are 30% to 50% deeper than the planet's overall transit depth, and point to a mass below 20 Earth masses (Thao et al. 2024). A fit to the spectrum gives 13.8 Earth masses (Thao et al. 2024). With a density below 0.10 g per cubic centimetre, it is one of the lowest-density planets known (Thao et al. 2024). Water and carbon dioxide are strongly detected, and carbon monoxide more modestly (Thao et al. 2024). A ground-based VLT/CRIRES+ spectrum detected water at 20 sigma and carbon monoxide at 5 sigma (Lavail et al. 2026). Winds blowing from the day side to the night side show up as a velocity offset of 2.9 km per second (Lavail et al. 2026). The planet's spin is slow enough to be consistent with tidal locking (Lavail et al. 2026).","system":"The star is only 17 million years old (Thao et al. 2024). The planet is the youngest Jupiter-sized planet known with an orbit well aligned to its star's spin axis (Heitzmann et al. 2022).","history":"JWST results challenge the older label of hot Jupiter and instead make it a forerunner of the more common sub-Neptunes (Thao et al. 2024).","open":"The ground-based spectrum suggests a heavier planet than the JWST fit, so the mass is still in question (Lavail et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip67522b-Thaoetal2024","cite":"Thao et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Pa Chia Thao, Andrew W. Mann, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"The Featherweight Giant: Unraveling the Atmosphere of a 17 Myr Planet with JWST","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16355","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip67522b-Lavailetal2026","cite":"Lavail et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"A. Lavail, F. Debras, B. Klein et al.","title":"Atmospheric characterisation of HIP 67522 b with VLT/CRIRES+: VLT/CRIRES+ suggests a heavier planet and hints at deuterium fractionation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03498","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip67522b-Heitzmannetal2022","cite":"Heitzmann et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alexis Heitzmann, Stephen Marsden, George Zhou et al.","title":"Young planets are key: HIP 67522 b and TOI-4562 b","journal":"The 21st Cambridge Workshop on Cool Stars, Stellar Systems, and the Sun","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7322896","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-60 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transits of WASP-60 b are shallow, which makes the planet harder to study than its relatives (Hébrard et al. 2013). Observations of its transits with the 1.5-m Kuiper Telescope were the first follow-up since discovery (Turner et al. 2017). They were used to update the planet's properties and to look for transit timing variations, which could point to a third body, and for changes in transit depth with wavelength, which would reveal its atmosphere (Turner et al. 2017).","system":"The star is a G-type dwarf, one of four hosts presented together in the discovery announcement (Hébrard et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp60b-Hbrardetal2013","cite":"Hébrard et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"G. Hébrard, A. Collier Cameron, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"WASP-52b, WASP-58b, WASP-59b, and WASP-60b: Four new transiting close-in giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.0810","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp60b-Turneretal2017","cite":"Turner et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Robin M. Leiter, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Investigating the physical properties of transiting hot Jupiters with the 1.5-m Kuiper Telescope","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07909","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-80 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet g was a later addition to a system already known for a chain of resonances, where neighbouring planets tug on each other in a regular rhythm (Weisserman et al. 2023). Variations in transit times, the small early or late arrivals of transits, give planet g a mass of 0.8 Earth masses if all orbits are circular and 1.0 Earth masses if they are not (Weisserman et al. 2023). Adding g changes the masses derived for other planets, so conclusions from timing data should be treated with caution when a system may be missing members (Weisserman et al. 2023).","system":"The outer five planets sit in successive three-body resonances, where three planets share a linked rhythm (Weisserman et al. 2023). They do not appear to be in two-body resonances (Weisserman et al. 2023). The system's extremely tight packing is thought to be hard for radial velocity surveys to detect (Ragozzine et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler80g-Weissermanetal2023","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Weisserman, J. C. Becker, A. Vanderburg","title":"Kepler-80 Revisited: Assessing the Participation of a Newly Discovered Planet in the Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08695","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler80g-Ragozzineetal2015","cite":"Ragozzine et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Darin Ragozzine, Mariah MacDonald, Eric B. Ford","title":"Kepler-80 and the Frequency of STIPs","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2328 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-2328 b is a warm sub-Saturn, a planet smaller than Saturn but heavier than Neptune, that crosses the star in the light curves of the TESS space telescope (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). Ground-based photometry and spectroscopy confirmed the transits as a planet (Tala Pinto et al. 2025). It was announced together with three warm Jupiters on eccentric orbits around other stars (Tala Pinto et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2328b-Pintoetal2025","cite":"Pinto et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Marcelo Tala Pinto, Andrés Jordán, Lorena Acuña et al.","title":"Three warm Jupiters orbiting TOI-6628, TOI-3837, and TOI-5027 and one sub-Saturn orbiting TOI-2328","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.02069","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1278 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The object has a mass of 18.5 Jupiter masses and is classed as a brown dwarf, a body between planets and stars (Artigau et al. 2021). The transit is grazing, with the object barely clipping the star's disc (Artigau et al. 2021). The SPIRou instrument at the Canada-France-Hawaii Telescope measured its pull on the star at 2306 metres per second, in step with the 14.5-day period (Artigau et al. 2021). A mass ratio of about 31 at such a short separation of 0.095 au is unique among known M-dwarf systems (Artigau et al. 2021). Radio emission from its magnetic field was estimated to be detectable with the Square Kilometre Array telescope (Bagheri et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1278b-Artigauetal2021","cite":"Artigau et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Étienne Artigau, Guillaume Hébrard, Charles Cadieux et al.","title":"TOI-1278 B: SPIRou Unveils a Rare Brown Dwarf Companion in Close-in Orbit around an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04536","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1278b-Bagherietal2024","cite":"Bagheri et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Fatemeh Bagheri, Anshuman Garga, Ramon E. Lopez","title":"Exploring radio emissions from confirmed exoplanets using SKA","journal":"Frontiers in Astronomy and Space Sciences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14468","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 213885 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is bright, the second brightest known to host a transiting planet this close in, after 55 Cancri (Espinoza et al. 2020). Bright stars make atmosphere studies easier, and the planet is a good target for comparing short-period, highly irradiated super-Earths (Espinoza et al. 2020).","observed":"Radial velocities from three spectrographs (FEROS, HARPS and CORALIE) gave a precise mass of 8.8 ± 0.6 Earth masses for a radius of 1.74 ± 0.05 Earth radii (Espinoza et al. 2020). Those two numbers point to a bulk composition similar to Earth's but enriched in iron (Espinoza et al. 2020). The radius, mass and starlight received are very similar to those of 55 Cancri e (Espinoza et al. 2020).","system":"The same radial velocities show a second signal every 4.78 days, interpreted as a non-transiting planet, HD 213885 c, with a minimum mass of 19.9 ± 1.4 Earth masses, consistent with a Neptune-mass planet (Espinoza et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd213885b-Espinozaetal2020","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Néstor Espinoza, Rafael Brahm, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"HD 213885b: a transiting 1-d-period super-Earth with an Earth-like composition around a bright (V = 7.9) star unveiled by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.07694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 21520 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The system was first alerted as two planet candidates, with periods of 703.6 and 46.4 days (Nies et al. 2024). Further analysis supports a single planet with a period of 25.1292 days instead (Nies et al. 2024). Three full transits and one partial transit in separate TESS sectors match that period (Nies et al. 2024). Transits were also seen with CHEOPS and LCOGT, and high-resolution imaging found no nearby companion stars (Nies et al. 2024). Radial velocities rule out a hidden spectroscopic binary star as the source of the signal (Nies et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd21520b-Niesetal2024","cite":"Nies et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Molly Nies, Ismael Mireles, François Bouchy et al.","title":"HD 21520 b: a warm sub-Neptune transiting a bright G dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09595","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-52 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Transits of K2-52 b were re-observed at 4.5 micrometres with the Spitzer space telescope, as part of a follow-up of eight planet candidates from the K2 mission (Livingston et al. 2019). Combining the two data sets improved the planet radii and gave colour information that helps confirm the signals are planets (Livingston et al. 2019). The Spitzer data also made the orbit timing far more precise, cutting the uncertainty in predicting transit times in mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes across the sample (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k252b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-849 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the few found in the hot-Neptune desert, a region close to stars where Neptune-sized planets are rare, and is the densest Neptune-sized planet found there so far (Pezzotti et al. 2021).","observed":"Models of the planet and its star suggest it lacks a massive hydrogen-helium envelope on top of a rocky core of about 40.8 Earth masses (Pezzotti et al. 2021). Its extreme closeness to the star raises the question of how the star shaped the planet's history (Pezzotti et al. 2021). Observations of three secondary eclipses (the planet passing behind its star) with the JWST NIRSpec instrument were planned to measure the dayside temperature and to look for water and carbon dioxide (Roy et al. 2024).","system":"Another study groups it with LTT 9779 b and K2-100 as ultrahot Neptunes, which are above the roughly 10 Earth-mass threshold for runaway gas accretion and are inferred to hold hydrogen-helium or other volatile envelopes (Dai et al. 2021).","history":"Calculations that follow the star's spinning history, including tides raised in its outer layers and its high-energy radiation, were used to test how the planet's orbit and atmosphere evolved (Pezzotti et al. 2021). Planets like this are thought to be exposed versions of the cores at the centre of giant planets (Roy et al. 2024).","open":"Whether the planet is a bare, metal-enriched core, and what that says about its formation, is still to be tested with spectra (Roy et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi849b-Pezzottietal2021","cite":"Pezzotti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"C. Pezzotti, M. Attia, P. Eggenberger et al.","title":"The key impact of the host star's rotational history on the evolution of TOI-849b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09364","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi849b-Royetal2024","cite":"Roy et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Pierre-Alexis Roy, Bjorn Benneke, Louis-Philippe Coulombe et al.","title":"Exploring the desert: Thermal characterization of an exposed planetary core","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 3","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi849b-Daietal2021","cite":"Dai et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Fei Dai, Andrew W. Howard, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"TKS X: Confirmation of TOI-1444b and a Comparative Analysis of the Ultra-short-period Planets with Hot Neptunes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.08844","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 128356 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 128356 b was one of eight new giant planets reported together from the Calan-Hertfordshire Extrasolar Planet Search, a survey of nearby dwarf and subgiant stars with the CORALIE, HARPS and MIKE spectrographs (Jenkins et al. 2017). The batch includes two eccentric giant planets, HD 128356 b and HD 154672 b (Jenkins et al. 2017). Across the batch, masses run from 1.1 to 5.4 Jupiter masses and eccentricities from 0.0 to 0.6 (Jenkins et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd128356b-Jenkinsetal2017","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, H. R. A. Jones, M. Tuomi et al.","title":"New planetary systems from the Calan-Hertfordshire Extrasolar Planet Search","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09391","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-24 c":{"text":{"why":"The system was the first candidate with more than one transiting planet found in the CoRoT mission (Alonso et al. 2014).","observed":"Two transit-like dips, repeating every 5.11 and 11.76 days, were seen around the same star, corresponding to objects of 3.7 ± 0.4 and 5.0 ± 0.5 Earth radii (Alonso et al. 2014). Radial velocities gave a tentative mass of 28 ± 11 Earth masses for the 11.76-day object, and an upper limit of 5.7 Earth masses for the 5.11-day one (Alonso et al. 2014). Such low densities imply a significant gaseous envelope (Alonso et al. 2014). Statistical tests find real Neptune-sized planets over 26 times more likely than a blend involving one transiting planet, and over 900 times more likely than two blends and no planets (Alonso et al. 2014).","system":"The radial velocities also show a slow long-term drift that might come from a 1.5 Jupiter-mass planet orbiting at 1.8 AU from the star (Alonso et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"More data are needed to pin down the orbit of the possible outer giant planet (Alonso et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot24c-Alonsoetal2014","cite":"Alonso et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"R. Alonso, C. Moutou, M. Endl et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXVI. CoRoT-24: a transiting multiplanet system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.1270","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-40 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-40 b is the largest of four hot Jupiters reported together, with a radius of 1.58 Jupiter radii, which makes it an inflated planet (Bento et al. 2018). All four orbit moderately bright F dwarf stars with periods of 2 to 5 days (Bento et al. 2018). Their masses and densities differ by more than a factor of ten from planet to planet (Bento et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats40b-Bentoetal2018","cite":"Bento et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"J. Bento, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-39b, HATS-40b, HATS-41b, and HATS-42b: three inflated hot Jupiters and a super-Jupiter transiting F stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01623","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2525 b":{"text":{"why":"The pair is notable for strong transit timing variations, which let the planets' masses be measured from how they tug on each other (Trifonov et al. 2023).","observed":"Transits of the two planets arrive up to about 6 hours early or late, with a semi-amplitude of about 6 hours (Trifonov et al. 2023). Analysis of those timing shifts, together with radial velocities from FEROS and PFS, confirmed both as planets (Trifonov et al. 2023). The inner planet has a mass of 0.088 Jupiter masses, similar to Neptune's, yet a radius of 0.88 Jupiter radii (Trifonov et al. 2023).","system":"The outer planet, TOI-2525 c, has 0.709 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.98 Jupiter radii, with a period of 49.260 days (Trifonov et al. 2023). The period ratio is close to 2:1, but simulations suggest the pair lies outside a mean-motion resonance (Trifonov et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2525b-Trifonovetal2023","cite":"Trifonov et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Trifon Trifonov, Rafael Brahm, Andrés Jordán et al.","title":"TOI-2525 b and c: A Pair of Massive Warm Giant Planets with Strong Transit Timing Variations Revealed by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05694","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 95735 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from four spectrographs (APF, HIRES, SOPHIE and CARMENES) confirm a signal with a period near 2900 days around the nearby red dwarf Gliese 411 (Hurt et al. 2022). The signal had been disputed as either a planet or a long-period magnetic cycle of the star (Hurt et al. 2022). Activity measurements support the planet interpretation, a Neptune-like planet (Hurt et al. 2022).","system":"A further signal near 215 days may be another planet, with a minimum mass of 3.89 ± 0.84 Earth masses at 0.5142 ± 0.0042 au, between the other two planets and beyond the outer edge of the habitable zone (Hurt et al. 2022). A planetary origin cannot be ruled out for it (Hurt et al. 2022).","history":"Astrometric work on the star, the sideways motion measured on photographic plates, had earlier reported an unseen companion with a period near 8 years (Lippincott 1960). Later astrometry reported accelerations suggesting a planetary system, including a shorter component of about 5.8 years (Gatewood 1996).","open":"Whether the 215-day signal is a real planet is not settled (Hurt et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd95735c-Hurtetal2022","cite":"Hurt et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Spencer A. Hurt, Benjamin Fulton, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Confirmation of the Long-period Planet Orbiting Gliese 411 and the Detection of a New Planet Candidate","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.09087","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95735c-Lippincott1960","cite":"Lippincott 1960","year":"1960","authors":"Sarah Lee Lippincott","title":"The Unseen Companion of the Fourth Nearest Star, Lalande 21185.","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/108260","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd95735c-Gatewood1996","cite":"Gatewood 1996","year":"1996","authors":"G. Gatewood","title":"Lalande 21185","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #188","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-111 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it checked 42 transit candidates in the constellation Carina with high-resolution spectra, to see which were true planets (Pont et al. 2004).","observed":"Spectroscopic follow-up of 42 transit candidates in Carina confirmed three as planets: OGLE-TR-111, OGLE-TR-113 and OGLE-TR-132 (Pont et al. 2004). Of the other 39, most turned out to be eclipsing binary stars in varied configurations, including small stars crossing in front of F and G dwarfs, and some triple and quadruple star systems (Pont et al. 2004). The result shows how many different cases can mimic a planetary transit, and why spectroscopic follow-up matters (Pont et al. 2004).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr111b-Pontetal2004","cite":"Pont et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"F. Pont, F. Bouchy, C. Melo et al.","title":"Doppler follow-up of OGLE planetary transit candidates in Carina","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0501615","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-30 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's orbit is strongly tilted relative to the star's spin, matching a pattern that tilted orbits are more common around stars hotter than about 6250 K (Johnson et al. 2011).","observed":"Radial velocities taken during a transit show the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion that reveals the angle between the orbit and the star's spin. The angle is 73.5 ± 9.0 degrees, a highly oblique orbit (Johnson et al. 2011). Its mean density is low, 0.37 ± 0.05 grams per cubic centimetre (Johnson et al. 2011). Later work finds a bloated planet with a radius of about 1.5 Jupiter radii (Saeed et al. 2022). Transit times show a possible drift that tidal decay of the orbit cannot explain (Bai et al. 2022).","system":"Adaptive optics imaging found a companion star 3.7 arcseconds away, 2.9 magnitudes fainter in the infrared (Adams et al. 2013). Depending on the resolution of the transit photometry, such a companion may require the planet's measured properties to be reassessed (Adams et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Apsidal precession (a slow turning of the orbit's long axis) or an additional perturbing body could explain the timing signal (Bai et al. 2022). The star's unusually low chromospheric activity may be due to absorption by gas lost from the planet, though absorption between the stars may contribute (Staab et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp30b-Johnsonetal2011","cite":"Johnson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"John Asher Johnson, J. N. Winn, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-30b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter on a Highly Oblique Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.3825","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp30b-Saeedetal2022","cite":"Saeed et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"M. I. Saeed, S. N. Goderya, F. A. Chishtie","title":"Multicolor photometry and parameters estimation of three transiting Jupiter-sized exoplanets; TrES-3b, WASP-2b and HAT-P-30b","journal":"New Astronomy","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.newast.2021.101680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp30b-Baietal2022","cite":"Bai et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Lu Bai, Shenghong Gu, Xiaobin Wang et al.","title":"WASP-35 and HAT-P-30/WASP-51: Reanalysis using TESS and Ground-based Transit Photometry","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02866","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp30b-Adamsetal2013","cite":"Adams et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"E. R. Adams, A. K. Dupree, C. Kulesa et al.","title":"Adaptive Optics Images. II. 12 Kepler Objects of Interest and 15 Confirmed Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.6548","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp30b-Staabetal2017","cite":"Staab et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Staab, C. A. Haswell, Gareth D. Smith et al.","title":"SALT observations of the chromospheric activity of transiting planet hosts: mass-loss and star-planet interactions<SUP>★</SUP>","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01739","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 74698 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey aims at long-period companions, since only 6% of the planets discovered so far have periods longer than 3 years (Frensch et al. 2023).","observed":"HD 74698 c is one of five Jupiter-mass planets with orbits of 5.2 to 17.4 years, reported from a HARPS radial velocity survey that has run since 2003 and has a 19-year baseline (Frensch et al. 2023). Its orbit takes 9.4 years (Frensch et al. 2023). Long signals can be confused with the star's magnetic cycle, so the stellar activity indicators were examined to rule that out (Frensch et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd74698c-Frenschetal2023","cite":"Frensch et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Y. G. C. Frensch, G. Lo Curto, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XLVII. Five Jupiter-mass planets in long-period orbits, one highly irradiated Neptune, one brown dwarf, and five stellar binaries","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04420","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-9 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind the discovery aims mainly at planets of Neptune size or larger (Costes et al. 2023).","observed":"NGTS-9 b was observed with a single camera of the Next Generation Transit Survey over a 234-night baseline, from October 2016 to May 2017 (Costes et al. 2023). Follow-up light curves were taken in December 2018 and January 2019 (Costes et al. 2023). Along with NGTS-8 b, it is described as a non-inflated hot Jupiter (Costes et al. 2023).","system":"A theoretical study of ten systems including NGTS-9 b found that the Lagrangian points, stable-looking spots along the orbit, are unstable, but may still hold minor planets, asteroids or debris (Hasan & Shah 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts9b-Costesetal2023","cite":"Costes et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. C. Costes, C. A. Watson, C. Belardi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: NGTS-8b and NGTS-9b non-inflated hot Jupiters (Costes+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts9b-HasanShah2024","cite":"Hasan & Shah 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Syed Najamul Hasan, Priya Shah","title":"Exploring the collinear Lagrangian points of exoplanet systems with P-R drag and oblateness.","journal":"32nd General Assembly International Union (IAUGA 2024)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD-06 1339 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this star, which also carries the name GJ 221, found a companion on a 125-day orbit with a minimum mass of 53.2 Earth masses (Arriagada et al. 2013). That mass falls in a range where very few planet candidates have been found, nicknamed the sub-Saturn desert (Arriagada et al. 2013). The signal showed up in 4.4 years of public HARPS spectra, and later velocity measurements from Magellan/PFS confirmed the system (Arriagada et al. 2013).","system":"A second, much smaller companion circles the star every 3.87 days, with a mass of 6.5 Earth masses (Arriagada et al. 2013).","history":"The star has slightly less iron than the Sun. Two low-mass companions around it support the view that slightly reduced metal content does not stop planets from forming in the super-Earth to sub-Saturn mass range (Arriagada et al. 2013).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd061339c-Arriagadaetal2013","cite":"Arriagada et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Pamela Arriagada, Guillem Anglada-Escudé, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Two Planetary Companions around the K7 Dwarf GJ 221: A Hot Super-Earth and a Candidate in the Sub-Saturn Desert Range","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2203","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"pi Men d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbours, the giant outer planet and the transiting planet pi Men c. The outer giant's orbit was worked out by combining radial velocities with Hipparcos and Gaia position measurements of the star (De Rosa et al. 2020). The star was also watched with the ESPRESSO spectrograph, which gathered 275 spectra over 37 nights between September 2018 and March 2019 (Damasso et al. 2020).","system":"Planet c is strongly irradiated by its solar-type star, and its density fits a composition of 50 to 100 percent water by volume (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019). Observers asked for ten Hubble orbits to look for hydrogen, oxygen and carbon escaping from its upper atmosphere (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-pimend-DeRosaetal2020","cite":"De Rosa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. J. De Rosa, R. Dawson, E. L. Nielsen","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: pi Men radial velocity curves (De Rosa+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36400073","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pimend-Damassoetal2020","cite":"Damasso et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Damasso, A. Sozzetti, C. Lovis et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: pi Men radial velocity curves (Damasso+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36420031","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-pimend-Munozetal2019","cite":"Munoz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Antonio Garcia Munoz, Juan Cabrera, Luca Fossati et al.","title":"Pi Men c: The first confirmed water world?","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-297 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-297 c. Three planets in this system pass in front of their star. Their transits arrive slightly early or late because the planets tug on each other, and those timing shifts were re-analysed from Kepler data and new Spitzer observations of Kepler-297 c (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015). The pull between the planets lets astronomers estimate their mass ratios (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015).","system":"The timing shifts are best fitted by a model with a fourth planet that does not transit, orbiting outside the three that do (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015). The outermost transiting planet is confirmed as Kepler-297 d by constraints on its orbit (Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler297b-DiamondLoweetal2015","cite":"Diamond-Lowe et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Hannah Diamond-Lowe, Kevin B. Stevenson, Daniel Fabrycky et al.","title":"Determining the architecture of the Kepler-297 system using transit timing variations","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2008-BLG-379L b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned up the planet was a systematic search through binary-lens events that the MOA group had already flagged (Suzuki et al. 2014). A faint background star crossed a large resonant caustic, a curve where the lensing brightening is extreme, so the planet's signal dominated the light curve and its planetary nature was not noticed at first (Suzuki et al. 2014).","observed":"The brightening has a mass ratio between planet and host of 6.9 × 10^-3 (Suzuki et al. 2014). A statistical model of the Galaxy gives a host star of about 0.56 solar masses, a planet of about 4.1 Jupiter masses, an orbital separation of about 3.3 AU and a distance of about 3.3 kiloparsecs (Suzuki et al. 2014). These values are estimates with wide error bars, not direct measurements.","system":"","history":"","open":"The lens is faint and moves slowly against the background, so follow-up with adaptive optics or the Hubble telescope is expected to be needed to see the host star (Suzuki et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2008blg379lb-Suzukietal2014","cite":"Suzuki et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"D. Suzuki, A. Udalski, T. Sumi et al.","title":"MOA-2008-BLG-379Lb: A Massive Planet from a High Magnification Event with a Faint Source","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.3424","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 12661 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Computer simulations of how the two planets of this star pull on each other's orbits found that they undergo apsidal alignment or antialignment (Ji et al. 2003). In plain terms, the long axes of the two elliptical orbits slowly swing round together, pointing either in the same direction or in opposite directions (Ji et al. 2003). Four other planetary systems showed the same behaviour in those simulations (Ji et al. 2003).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd12661b-Jietal2003","cite":"Ji et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Jianghui Ji, Lin Liu, Hiroshi Kinoshita et al.","title":"The Librating Companions in HD 37124, HD 12661, HD 82943, 47 Ursa Majoris, and GJ 876: Alignment or Antialignment?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0305448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2134 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Long-period transiting planets are valuable targets for future instruments, but their transits are rare, so their orbital timing is poorly known. New TESS and CHEOPS observations were combined with earlier ones for this planet and four others, and noise in the light curves was reduced with wavelet filtering and Gaussian-process fitting (Saha 2025). The orbital period of this planet is now known to better than 5 seconds (Saha 2025). Other transit properties were updated too (Saha 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2134c-Saha2025","cite":"Saha 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Suman Saha","title":"Updating the ephemeris and physical properties of five long-period transiting exoplanets using TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01768","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 11506 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its two inner giant planets. A planet at 1627.5 days and a newly found one at 223.6 days were reported from the Next 2000 stars (N2K) Doppler survey (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"N2K began by looking for short-period planets and later turned to wider orbits, to learn how common giant planets are beyond the ice line, where gas giants are thought to form (Giguere et al. 2015). Its results confirmed a second planet around HD 11506 (Giguere et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd11506d-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-413 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet circles both stars of a close pair, a K dwarf and an M dwarf that orbit each other every 10.11615 days (Kostov et al. 2014). Its own orbit takes about 66 days, is slightly eccentric (0.118) and lies about 2.5° off the plane of the two stars (Kostov et al. 2014). The tilt makes the orbit wobble with a period of about 11 years, so the planet often misses crossing the face of the main star (Kostov et al. 2014). Stretches of hundreds of days pass with no transits, and the next one was predicted for 2020 (Kostov et al. 2014).","system":"The two stars have masses of about 0.82 and 0.54 times the Sun and orbit each other on a nearly circular path (Kostov et al. 2014).","history":"Known planets around binaries often sit near the edge of orbital stability, and attempts to form them there have failed, which supports the idea that they formed far out and migrated inward (Haghighipour 2017).","open":"Many more circumbinary planets may exist on tilted orbits that never transit (Haghighipour 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler413b-Kostovetal2014","cite":"Kostov et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"V. B. Kostov, P. R. McCullough, J. A. Carter et al.","title":"Kepler-413b: A Slightly Misaligned, Neptune-size Transiting Circumbinary Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.7275","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler413b-Haghighipour2017","cite":"Haghighipour 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Nader Haghighipour","title":"Dynamical Evolution of Circumbinary Disks and Embedded Planets","journal":"NASA XRP proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-56 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a ground-based find that space data made firm: K2 photometry greatly improved the confidence that it is a planet and the accuracy of its measured size (Huang et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet has 2.18 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.47 Jupiter radii, one of the largest known for a planet above 2 Jupiter masses (Huang et al. 2015). It crosses its star on a near-grazing path, so only part of the planet blocks the star's light (Huang et al. 2015). Transit timings gathered from 2016 to 2020 at two Turkish telescopes show no convincing sign of additional objects in the system (Sonbas et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a bright F-type star whose K2 light curve suggests it is a gamma Doradus variable, a star that pulses slightly in brightness (Huang et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp56b-Huangetal2015","cite":"Huang et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"C. X. Huang, J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-56b: An Inflated Massive Hot Jupiter Transiting a Bright F Star Followed Up with K2 Campaign 0 Observations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01776","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp56b-Sonbasetal2022","cite":"Sonbas et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. Sonbas, N. Karaman, A. Özdönmez et al.","title":"Probing Transit Timing Variations of three hot Jupiters: HATP-36b, HATP-56b, and WASP-52b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.05220","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-762 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm sub-Saturn with 0.251 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.744 Jupiter radii, crossing a mid-M dwarf of 0.442 solar masses (Hartman et al. 2024). Giant planets are rare around such small stars. Velocities from the ESPRESSO spectrograph confirmed it as a planet (Hartman et al. 2024).","system":"The host star has a resolved companion star, TOI 762 B, about 3.2 arcseconds away, or roughly 319 au, with an estimated mass of 0.227 solar masses (Hartman et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi762ab-Hartmanetal2024","cite":"Hartman et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Joel D. Hartman, Daniel Bayliss, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"TOI 762 A b and TIC 46432937 b: Two Giant Planets Transiting M-dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07187","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-628 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a search of TESS full-frame images, which cover many stars at lower time resolution than the pre-selected target lists (Rodriguez et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter and is the most massive one found by TESS up to that work (Rodriguez et al. 2021). It was confirmed through photometric and spectroscopic follow-up by the TESS Follow-up Observing Program, together with four other hot or warm Jupiters (Rodriguez et al. 2021). The five planets are all Jupiter-sized, with masses of 0.85 to 6.33 Jupiter masses (Rodriguez et al. 2021).","system":"The host stars of the five systems are F and G stars and relatively bright, so they are well suited to detailed follow-up (Rodriguez et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi628b-Rodriguezetal2021","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn, George Zhou et al.","title":"TESS Delivers Five New Hot Giant Planets Orbiting Bright Stars from the Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01726","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-883 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Starlight passing through the Neptune-sized planet's transit was used to measure how its orbit lines up with the spin of its star, a technique called the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). The sky-projected angle between the two is 22° with an uncertainty of about 15°, and the 10-day orbit is eccentric, with e = 0.16 ± 0.03 (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). It is one of the first two planets measured in the POSEIDON survey of how transiting Neptunes ended up where they are (Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026). Measured mass is 0.123 Jupiter masses, radius 0.604 Jupiter radii and equilibrium temperature 1086 ± 19 K (Rojas et al. 2026).","system":"A neighbouring planet around another star, TOI-899 b, has a similar mass, period and host star but a different internal make-up, which could point to distinct formation histories (Rojas et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Both planets are suitable targets for atmospheric studies to further test how giant planets form (Rojas et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi883b-EspinozaRetamaletal2026","cite":"Espinoza-Retamal et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Juan I. Espinoza-Retamal, Joshua N. Winn, Rafael Brahm et al.","title":"POSEIDON I: The Dynamical Origins of Transiting Neptunes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18553","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi883b-Rojasetal2026","cite":"Rojas et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Felipe I. Rojas, Rafael Brahm, Andrés Jordán et al.","title":"Two warm sub-Saturn mass planets identified from the TESS Full Frame Images","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16646","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1201 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm mini-Neptune with a radius of 2.415 Earth radii and a mass of 6.28 Earth masses, giving a bulk density of about 2.45 grams per cubic centimetre (Kossakowski et al. 2021). Its mass came from CARMENES velocities (Kossakowski et al. 2021). Its star is relatively young, about 600 to 800 million years old (Kossakowski et al. 2021). The planet is an excellent target for atmospheric studies (Kossakowski et al. 2021).","system":"The host is an early M dwarf with an equal-mass companion star about 8 arcseconds away (Kossakowski et al. 2021). A 19-day signal in the velocities is attributed to the star's rotation, although it could belong to the companion, and a slower variation of unknown origin is also present (Kossakowski et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"The origin of the long-term velocity variation is not known (Kossakowski et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1201b-Kossakowskietal2021","cite":"Kossakowski et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"D. Kossakowski, J. Kemmer, P. Bluhm et al.","title":"TOI-1201 b: A mini-Neptune transiting a bright and moderately young M dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.09346","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1928 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this planet, listed there as KOI-3876 b, placed its star in a young group of stars called MELANGE-3, found by searching near Kepler planet hosts with high lithium (Barber et al. 2022). The group is 105 ± 10 million years old, which fixes the planet's age far better than usual (Barber et al. 2022). The planet is 2.0 ± 0.1 Earth radii on a 19.58-day orbit (Barber et al. 2022). Its star was once flagged as an eclipsing binary (Barber et al. 2022).","system":"Another Kepler planetary system, Kepler-970, belongs to the same young group (Barber et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1928b-Barberetal2022","cite":"Barber et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Madyson G. Barber, Andrew W. Mann, Jonathan L. Bush et al.","title":"Transit Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). VIII. A Pleiades-age Association Harboring Two Transiting Planetary Systems from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08383","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-1 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has 0.80 to 0.98 Jupiter masses and a radius of at least 1.33 Jupiter radii, so it is puffed up (Collier Cameron et al. 2007). Its host star is rich in metals and lithium, with an age of 1 to 3 billion years (Stempels et al. 2007). A similar star hosts HD 149026 b, the densest planet then known, yet the two planets differ strongly in density, so planet density does not depend simply on a star's metal content or on irradiation (Stempels et al. 2007). Measurements during transit show only a weak Rossiter-McLaughlin signal (Albrecht et al. 2011).","system":"A weak signal is best explained if the star is seen almost pole-on, which would put its spin axis at a high angle to the planet's edge-on orbit (Albrecht et al. 2011). A separate statistical study also flags the system as probably misaligned along the line of sight (Schlaufman 2010).","history":"","open":"The star's tilt relative to the orbit is inferred indirectly and has not been measured directly (Albrecht et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp1b-Cameronetal2007","cite":"Cameron et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"A. Collier Cameron, F. Bouchy, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"WASP-1b and WASP-2b: two new transiting exoplanets detected with SuperWASP and SOPHIE","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609688","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp1b-Stempelsetal2007","cite":"Stempels et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"H. C. Stempels, A. Collier Cameron, L. Hebb et al.","title":"WASP-1: a lithium- and metal-rich star with an oversized planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.1677","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp1b-Albrechtetal2011","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John Asher Johnson et al.","title":"Two Upper Limits on the Rossiter-Mclaughlin Effect, with Differing Implications: WASP-1 has a High Obliquity and WASP-2 is Indeterminate","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.2548","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp1b-Schlaufman2010","cite":"Schlaufman 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Kevin C. Schlaufman","title":"Evidence of Possible Spin-orbit Misalignment Along the Line of Sight in Transiting Exoplanet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2851","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158259 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star HD 158259 was monitored with 290 SOPHIE radial-velocity measurements and with TESS photometry (Hara et al. 2020). HD 158259 b is the innermost of six planets: a super-Earth on a 2.18-day orbit that TESS saw transiting. Further out, the velocities show five planets with minimum masses of about 6 Earth masses on orbits of 3.4 to 17.4 days (Hara et al. 2020).","system":"The outer planets form a compact chain with orbits close to 3:2 resonance, so each completes about three orbits for every two of the next one in (Hara et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158259b-Haraetal2020","cite":"Hara et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"N. C. Hara, F. Bouchy, M. Stalport et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XVI. HD 158259: A compact planetary system in a near-3:2 mean motion resonance chain","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13296","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The system was the first candidate with more than one transiting planet found by the CoRoT mission, with signals at 5.11 and 11.76 days (Alonso et al. 2014). This planet has a radius of 3.7 Earth radii, and velocity measurements give only an upper limit of 5.7 Earth masses (Alonso et al. 2014). Such low mass for its size implies a low-density planet with a significant gas envelope (Alonso et al. 2014). Among CoRoT's finds, it has the lowest mass limit (Schneider & CoRot Team 2016).","system":"The neighbour on the 11.76-day orbit is 5.0 Earth radii, with a tentative mass of 28 ± 11 Earth masses (Alonso et al. 2014). The velocities also show a slow drift that might come from a 1.5 Jupiter-mass planet at 1.8 AU (Alonso et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"More data are needed to pin down the orbit of the possible outer giant planet (Alonso et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot24b-Alonsoetal2014","cite":"Alonso et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"R. Alonso, C. Moutou, M. Endl et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XXVI. CoRoT-24: a transiting multiplanet system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.1270","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot24b-SchneiderCoRotTeam2016","cite":"Schneider & CoRot Team 2016","year":"2016","authors":"J. Schneider, CoRot Team","title":"III.8 Exoplanets versus brown dwarfs: The CoRoT view and the future","journal":"The CoRoT Legacy Book: The Adventure of the Ultra High Precision Photometry from Space","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.00917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DS Tuc A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a young Sun-like star in the 45-million-year-old Tucana-Horologium group, so its age is well known (Newton et al. 2019). Observations of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect during three transits show the orbit is well aligned with the star's equator, with a projected angle of 2.5° (Zhou et al. 2020). The same observations revealed large starspots, with angular sizes of tens of degrees, on the star (Zhou et al. 2020). Spitzer photometry and Gemini adaptive-optics imaging helped confirm the planet and found no other companions or planetary signals (Newton et al. 2019).","system":"The host star has a visual companion, and the planet orbits the primary, a G6 star (Newton et al. 2019). The primary produces energetic superflares, with an X-ray flare of 2.8 × 10^35 erg recorded by NICER (Dethero et al. 2023). Hubble ultraviolet data show about 20 smaller flares in 11 hours (Sextro et al. 2025). Mapping of its magnetic field was done for the first time, followed by simulations of the wind that hits its planets (Bellotti et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dstucab-Newtonetal2019","cite":"Newton et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Elisabeth R. Newton, Andrew W. Mann, Benjamin M. Tofflemire et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME): A Planet in the 45 Myr Tucana-Horologium Association","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.10703","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dstucab-Zhouetal2020","cite":"Zhou et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Zhou, J. N. Winn, E. R. Newton et al.","title":"A Well-aligned Orbit for the 45 Myr-old Transiting Neptune DS Tuc Ab","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.04095","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dstucab-Detheroetal2023","cite":"Dethero et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Mary Geer Dethero, Jeremy Hare, Vladimir S. Airapetian et al.","title":"Energetic Superflare from a Young Solar Analog, DS Tucanae A, Observed with NICER","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/2515-5172/acfda2","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dstucab-Sextroetal2025","cite":"Sextro et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Tristen Sextro, Garrett Brady, Randall McEntaffer et al.","title":"Far UV Observations of Superflares from a Young Solar Analog, DS Tuc A","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-dstucab-Bellottietal2026","cite":"Bellotti et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. Bellotti, A. Lavail, D. Evensberget et al.","title":"Spectropolarimetric characterisation of exoplanet host stars in preparation of the Ariel mission: II. The magnetised wind environment of TOI-1860, DS Tuc A, and HD 63433","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02708","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":21,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Oph 11 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The object is the fainter member of a very wide pair of very low-mass objects, separated by 243 au, the widest known for such a binary (Close et al. 2007). Spectra and photometry give temperatures of 2375 ± 175 K for the brighter member, Oph 11 A, and 2175 ± 175 K for Oph 11 B (Close et al. 2007). Model masses are 17 and 14 Jupiter masses, higher than earlier estimates of 13 to 15 and 7 to 8 Jupiter masses (Close et al. 2007). The system is therefore unlikely to be a planetary-mass binary, but it has the second lowest mass and lowest binding energy of any known binary (Close et al. 2007).","system":"The pair is about 5 ± 2 million years old and lies near the star-forming clouds of Ophiuchus (Close et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-oph11b-Closeetal2007","cite":"Close et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Laird M. Close, B. Zuckerman, Inseok Song et al.","title":"The Wide Brown Dwarf Binary Oph 1622-2405 and Discovery of a Wide, Low-Mass Binary in Ophiuchus (Oph 1623-2402): A New Class of Young Evaporating Wide Binaries?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0608574","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-51 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has 0.31 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.29 Jupiter radii, so it is a puffy, low-density Saturn-mass world (Hartman et al. 2015). Low jitter from the star and a favourable planet-to-star size ratio make it a promising target for measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, which shows how the orbit lines up with the star's spin (Hartman et al. 2015). A search of transit timings among 144 hot Jupiters classed it as a potential candidate for orbital decay, a slow spiral toward the star (Yeh et al. 2024). Multicolour transit photometry with MuSCAT2 refined its orbit and transit parameters (Kang et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the orbit is truly shrinking is not settled (Yeh et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp51b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HAT-P-50b, HAT-P-51b, HAT-P-52b, and HAT-P-53b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters and a Transiting Hot Saturn From the HATNet Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04149","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp51b-Yehetal2024","cite":"Yeh et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Li-Chin Yeh, Ing-Guey Jiang, Napaporn A-thano","title":"Searching for candidates of orbital decays among transit exoplanets","journal":"New Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08953","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp51b-Kangetal2024","cite":"Kang et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. Kang, G. Chen, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Simultaneous multicolour transit photometry of hot Jupiters HAT-P-19b, HAT-P-51b, HAT-P-55b, and HAT-P-65b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.03715","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-71 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was first flagged by the ground-based HATSouth survey, then confirmed with ground photometry, spectroscopy, imaging and TESS Sector 1 data (Bakos et al. 2020).","observed":"The host, an M3 dwarf, is the coolest M dwarf known to host a hot Jupiter (Bakos et al. 2020). The planet blocks 4.7 percent of the star's light during transit, more than in any other confirmed transiting planet system (Bakos et al. 2020). Its radius is 1.024 ± 0.018 Jupiter radii, and its mass is 0.37 ± 0.24 Jupiter masses, with a 95 percent upper limit of 0.80 Jupiter masses (Bakos et al. 2020).","system":"The star has 0.4861 solar masses and a radius of 0.4783 solar radii, so it is not much bigger than the planet (Bakos et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The planet's mass is only loosely measured (Bakos et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats71b-Bakosetal2020","cite":"Bakos et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss, J. Bento et al.","title":"HATS-71b: A Giant Planet Transiting an M3 Dwarf Star in TESS Sector 1","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ab8ad1","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 67537 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Revised distances and stellar sizes from Gaia's second data release changed how much starlight the planet is calculated to receive. With the new numbers, its orbit counts as lying in the habitable zone of its star, and it was added to a catalogue of potentially habitable worlds (Johns et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip67537b-Johnsetal2018","cite":"Johns et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Daniel Johns, Connor Marti, Madison Huff et al.","title":"Revised Exoplanet Radii and Habitability Using Gaia Data Release 2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.04533","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-411 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-411 e does not cross the face of its star, so it is never seen in transit. Its existence rests on how strongly it interacts with the neighbouring planet Kepler-411 d, and on assuming that the planets' orbits lie nearly in one plane, with a mutual tilt below 30 degrees (Sun et al. 2019). Its host star is very active: it is covered in starspots and produced 65 superflares, lasting 8 to 260 minutes, in about 600 days of Kepler observation (Araújo & Valio 2021).","system":"Kepler-411 hosts at least four planets, three of which transit (Sun et al. 2019). Transits of those three planets were used to map 198 starspots and to measure how the star's rotation speed changes with latitude, as the Sun's does (Araújo & Valio 2021).","history":"","open":"Whether planet e exists as described depends on the assumed near-flat orbital arrangement; it has not been seen directly (Sun et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler411e-Sunetal2019","cite":"Sun et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Sun, P. Ioannidis, S. Gu et al.","title":"Kepler-411: a four-planet system with an active host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.09719","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler411e-ArajoValio2021","cite":"Araújo & Valio 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alexandre Araújo, Adriana Valio","title":"Kepler-411 Differential Rotation from Three Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.07692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2180 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was followed up as part of the TESS-Keck Survey and later included in a survey of giant planets on long, transiting orbits (Dalba et al. 2022; Dalba et al. 2024).","observed":"The star was monitored with the Automated Planet Finder telescope at Lick Observatory to follow the slowly emerging Doppler signal of the planet. In August 2020 wildfires shut Lick Observatory, and observations moved for a time to the Keck I telescope's HIRES spectrograph to keep the coverage unbroken (Dalba et al. 2022). Later work combined TESS photometry, Keck velocities spanning years and new Automated Planet Finder velocities to measure the masses of long-period transiting giants such as this one (Dalba et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2180b-Dalbaetal2022","cite":"Dalba et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. A. Dalba, S. R. Kane, D. Dragomir et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: The TESS-Keck Survey. VIII. TOI-2180 radial velocity (Dalba+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51630061","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2180b-Dalbaetal2024","cite":"Dalba et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"P. A. Dalba, S. R. Kane, H. Isaacson et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Giant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass survey. IV. (Dalba+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 93963 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet's transits were too faint to be flagged in the survey data: its signal in the TESS light curve had a signal-to-noise ratio of about 6.7, so it was not listed as a candidate until the star was studied in detail (Serrano et al. 2022).","observed":"HD 93963 A b is a super-Earth that orbits in only 1.04 days. CHEOPS watched eight transits of the planet to confirm it (Serrano et al. 2022). Later velocity measurements lowered its mass, so it is less iron-rich than first thought. It had been called a super-Mercury, a planet with an unusually large iron core, but the new mass points to a composition much closer to Earth's (Brinkman et al. 2025).","system":"The star belongs to a visual binary, with its companion star 5.9 arcseconds away (Teng et al. 2026). A second planet, HD 93963 A c, is a sub-Neptune on a 3.65-day orbit with 19.2 Earth masses (Serrano et al. 2022). Observations in 2024 caught the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of the starlight during transit that reveals the orbit's alignment, for planet c (Teng et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"How iron-rich small close-in planets really are is still debated; revised masses for several of them suggest Earth-like makeup is the norm (Brinkman et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd93963ab-Serranoetal2022","cite":"Serrano et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. M. Serrano, D. Gandolfi, S. Hoyer et al.","title":"The HD 93963 A transiting system: A 1.04 d super-Earth and a 3.65 d sub-Neptune discovered by TESS and CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.13920","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd93963ab-Brinkmanetal2025","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, Lauren M. Weiss, Daniel Huber et al.","title":"The Compositions of Rocky Planets in Close-in Orbits Tend to Be Earth-like","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00213","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd93963ab-Tengetal2026","cite":"Teng et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"H.-Y. Teng, F. Dai, A. W. Howard et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 93963 radial velocities from Keck/KPF (Teng+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The star lies within the continuous viewing zone of the Hubble Space Telescope, so it can be watched without the interruptions caused by Earth blocking the view (McCullough et al. 2008).","observed":"Observations of the planet passing behind its star at infrared wavelengths with the Spitzer Space Telescope showed a deeper dip at 4.5 micrometres than at 3.6 micrometres. Such a pattern fits an atmosphere whose temperature rises with height, called a temperature inversion (Todorov et al. 2012). The eclipse timing also gave an upper limit on how elongated the orbit is (Todorov et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"The rotation periods of the three hottest stars known to host transiting giant planets are close to 2.0, 1.1 and 2.0 times the planets' orbital periods, which raised the possibility of a resonant interaction between star and planet (McCullough et al. 2008).","open":"Whether hot-Jupiter atmospheres develop a temperature inversion depends on how active the star is, and the XO-4 result agrees with that idea without strongly confirming it (Todorov et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo4b-McCulloughetal2008","cite":"McCullough et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"P. R. McCullough, Christopher J. Burke, Jeff A. Valenti et al.","title":"XO-4b: An Extrasolar Planet Transiting an F5V Star","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.2921","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo4b-Todorovetal2012","cite":"Todorov et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Kamen O. Todorov, Drake Deming, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Observations of Three Hot Exoplanets: XO-4b, HAT-P-6b, and HAT-P-8b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5858","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":29,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-138 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's measured size agrees with what standard models of giant planets predict. Matching those models requires a core of heavy elements of about 10 Earth masses (Lam et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp138b-Lametal2017","cite":"Lam et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"K. W. F. Lam, F. Faedi, D. J. A. Brown et al.","title":"From dense hot Jupiter to low-density Neptune: The discovery of WASP-127b, WASP-136b, and WASP-138b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.07859","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-19 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet b and its neighbour K2-19 c sit a mere 0.1 per cent outside a 3:2 orbital resonance, meaning b orbits three times for every two of c, almost exactly. Despite this, the planets are not locked into resonance: the angles that describe their gravitational lock circulate rather than librate (Petigura et al. 2020). Their orbits are eccentric, about 0.20, with the long axes of both ellipses lined up (Petigura et al. 2020). Because the planets nearly line up, transits of the two sometimes happen at the same moment (Armstrong et al. 2015).","system":"K2-19 is a compact system with three planets (Nespral et al. 2017). Planet b has a mass of 32.4 ± 1.7 Earth masses and planet c has 10.8 ± 0.6 Earth masses (Petigura et al. 2020).","history":"Standard formation by planets migrating through their disc predicts nearly circular orbits with the ellipses pointing in opposite directions. The measured orbits contradict that, so the system poses a challenge to planet assembly by convergent migration (Petigura et al. 2020).","open":"Mass measurements have not agreed: radial velocities alone gave about 54 Earth masses for planet b (Nespral et al. 2017), well above the later joint analysis (Petigura et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k219b-Petiguraetal2020","cite":"Petigura et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, John Livingston, Konstantin Batygin et al.","title":"K2-19b and c are in a 3:2 Commensurability but out of Resonance: A Challenge to Planet Assembly by Convergent Migration","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12899","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k219b-Armstrongetal2015","cite":"Armstrong et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"David J. Armstrong, Alexandre Santerne, Dimitri Veras et al.","title":"One of the closest exoplanet pairs to the 3:2 mean motion resonance: K2-19b and c","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00692","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k219b-Nespraletal2017","cite":"Nespral et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Nespral, D. Gandolfi, H. J. Deeg et al.","title":"Mass determination of K2-19b and K2-19c from radial velocities and transit timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.01265","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-6 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, the Dispersed Matter Planet Project, targets stars whose activity looks like the effect of close-in planets losing material to evaporation (Standing et al. 2026).","observed":"Radial-velocity monitoring revealed two planetary signals around this star, which is also catalogued as HD 67200 (Standing et al. 2026). Across the survey's 24 well-observed stars, planets of 3 to 10 Earth masses on orbits under 50 days turn out to be common, with an occurrence rate of about 83 per cent, though with large uncertainty (Standing et al. 2026). Only a minimum mass is known for this planet.","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the star's unusual activity signature really comes from an evaporating planet is the question the survey was built to test (Standing et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp6b-Standingetal2026","cite":"Standing et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, John R. Barnes, Carole A. Haswell et al.","title":"The dispersed matter planet project sample ─ detection limits, occurrence rates, and new planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18207","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 141937 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion was first reported with a minimum mass close to the dividing line between planets and brown dwarfs, 9.7 Jupiter masses, and an eccentric orbit. The study named it as either a brown dwarf or a superplanet (Udry et al. 2002). Combining Gaia astrometry with the star's wobble later suggested a mass above 13.5 Jupiter masses, which would place it in the brown dwarf range (Kiefer et al. 2021). A 2026 analysis using Hipparcos and Gaia positions instead concluded it is likely a planet (Piccinini et al. 2026). Models predict faint radio emission from it that the Square Kilometre Array could, in principle, detect (Mousavi-Sadr et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Companions of this kind may form like planets in the star's disc, as superplanets, or like brown dwarfs (Udry et al. 2002).","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a brown dwarf remains disputed, because the true mass depends on the orbit's tilt, which is hard to measure (Kiefer et al. 2021; Piccinini et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd141937b-Udryetal2002","cite":"Udry et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. VIII. The very low-mass companions of <ASTROBJ>HD 141937</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 162020</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 168443</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 202206</ASTROBJ>: Brown dwarfs or ``superplanets''?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0202458","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd141937b-Kieferetal2021","cite":"Kiefer et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"F. Kiefer, G. Hébrard, A. Lecavelier des Etangs et al.","title":"Determining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia. Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14164","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd141937b-Piccininietal2026","cite":"Piccinini et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. Piccinini, A. Petralia, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"True masses using radial velocity data with HIPPARCOS and Gaia astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09401","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd141937b-MousaviSadretal2025","cite":"Mousavi-Sadr et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Mahdiyar Mousavi-Sadr, Fatemeh S. Tabatabaei, Alexander Wolszczan et al.","title":"Exoplanetary radio emission predictions and detectability in the SKA era","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-454 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour Kepler-454 b. That planet was first measured as being of a size between the two common small-planet groups: rocky planets below about 1.6 Earth radii and lower-density planets between 2 and 2.7 Earth radii. Its measured mass placed it with the lower-density group, which likely carries a thick gas or water envelope (Kohler 2016). Little is known about planet c itself.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler454c-Kohler2016","cite":"Kohler 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"Kepler-454b: Rocky or Not?","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-44 b":{"text":{"why":"The orbit was fitted as circular because the measurements agree with a near-circular path, as expected for a Jupiter-mass planet so close to its star (Anderson et al. 2011).","observed":"Follow-up observations in several colours found no clear change in the times of its transits, so no hidden companion is suggested (Moyano et al. 2017). The planet's apparent size is nearly the same at all colours, giving a mostly flat broad-band spectrum (Moyano et al. 2017). Transits observed in the near-ultraviolet showed no lopsided shapes (Turner et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp44b-Andersonetal2011","cite":"Anderson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-44b, WASP-45b and WASP-46b: three short-period, transiting extrasolar planets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3179","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp44b-Moyanoetal2017","cite":"Moyano et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"M. Moyano, L. A. Almeida, C. von Essen et al.","title":"Multi-band characterization of the hot Jupiters: WASP-5b, WASP-44b and WASP-46b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.05700","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp44b-Turneretal2016","cite":"Turner et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Jake D. Turner, Kyle A. Pearson, Lauren I. Biddle et al.","title":"Ground-based near-UV observations of 15 transiting exoplanets: constraints on their atmospheres and no evidence for asymmetrical transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02587","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"2MASS J0249-0557 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet-mass object, about 11.6 Jupiter masses, lies 1950 ± 200 au from its hosts, 40 arcseconds away on the sky. It orbits a close pair of brown dwarfs only 2.17 au apart, whose masses are about 48 and 44 Jupiter masses (Dupuy et al. 2018). The host pair was resolved as a tight binary only with adaptive-optics aperture-masking observations at Keck (Dupuy et al. 2018). The object's mass, brightness and spectral type are remarkably similar to the planet β Pictoris b, making β Pictoris b the first directly imaged planet to have a twin (Dupuy et al. 2018).","system":"The system is a bound triple: the planet-mass object orbits the binary of two low-mass objects (Dupuy et al. 2018). Another free-floating object, 2MASS J2208+2921, may belong to the same moving group and look similar (Dupuy et al. 2018).","history":"The host binary belongs to the β Pictoris moving group, a family of stars about 22 ± 6 million years old, and is the first ultracool binary confirmed in that group (Dupuy et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-2massj02490557c-Dupuyetal2018","cite":"Dupuy et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Trent J. Dupuy, Michael C. Liu, Katelyn N. Allers et al.","title":"The Hawaii Infrared Parallax Program. III. 2MASS J0249-0557 c: A Wide Planetary-mass Companion to a Low-mass Binary in the β Pic Moving Group","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05235","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6086 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey aim was to find Earth-like planets around nearby small red stars, the M dwarfs (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed together with two other Earth-sized planets, TOI-5720 b and TOI-6008 b, each around a nearby M dwarf. Confirmation combined space-telescope data with multicolour ground-based light curves, optical and infrared spectra, high-resolution imaging and velocity measurements from the Subaru IRD instrument (Barkaoui et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6086b-Barkaouietal2024","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"K. Barkaoui, R. P. Schwarz, N. Narita et al.","title":"Three short-period Earth-sized planets around M dwarfs discovered by TESS: TOI-5720 b, TOI-6008 b, and TOI-6086 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.06350","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-99 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-99 b orbits a subgiant star, a star that has begun to swell as it ages. The star was first thought to be 3.1 times the Sun's radius, but a study of its pulsations showed it to be smaller, 2.55 solar radii (Smith et al. 2022). With the corrected star, the planet is a warm Jupiter that is not inflated, with a radius of 1.06 Jupiter radii (Smith et al. 2022). Its orbit is eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.19 ± 0.04 (Smith et al. 2017).","system":"A second giant, K2-99 c, circles the star every 522.2 days with a minimum mass of 8.4 Jupiter masses and an eccentricity of 0.210 (Smith et al. 2022). It was first hinted at by a steady acceleration of the star (Smith et al. 2017). TESS observations in 2022 were expected to have a good chance of catching its transit if the two orbits are nearly in the same plane (Smith et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Whether K2-99 c transits is not known, and the mutual tilt of the two orbits is unconstrained (Smith et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k299b-Smithetal2022","cite":"Smith et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, S. N. Breton, Sz Csizmadia et al.","title":"K2-99 revisited: a non-inflated warm Jupiter, and a temperate giant planet on a 522-d orbit around a subgiant","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.14660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k299b-Smithetal2017","cite":"Smith et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, D. Gandolfi, O. Barragán et al.","title":"K2-99: a subgiant hosting a transiting warm Jupiter in an eccentric orbit and a long-period companion","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.00239","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 220674823 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is the inner one of a two-planet system also known as K2-106. Mass and size give a high density of about 13.1 g per cubic centimetre, so it is a metal-rich world (Guenther et al. 2017). Later work using CHEOPS space-telescope transits and many velocity measurements classes K2-106 b as a rocky planet near the radius valley, the gap between rocky planets and puffier ones (Palethorpe et al. 2026). Its mass and radius were also refined using stellar properties from the GALAH survey (Clark et al. 2022).","system":"The outer planet, K2-106 c, orbits every 13.3 days with about the same mass but a much lower density of about 2.0 g per cubic centimetre (Guenther et al. 2017). The two planets make a good test of why similar-mass planets can end up so different (Guenther et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic220674823b-Guentheretal2017","cite":"Guenther et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"E. W. Guenther, O. Barragán, F. Dai et al.","title":"K2-106, a system containing a metal-rich planet and a planet of lower density","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04163","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epic220674823b-Palethorpeetal2026","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Larissa Palethorpe, Annelies Mortier, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Constraining small planet compositions for future missions","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14552","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epic220674823b-Clarketal2022","cite":"Clark et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jake T. Clark, Duncan J. Wright, Robert A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"The GALAH Survey: improving our understanding of confirmed and candidate planetary systems with large stellar surveys","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.14883","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-223 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-223 is a rare chain of four planets whose orbital periods fall in the ratios 8:6:4:3, linked by 4:3, 3:2 and 2:1 resonances (Mills et al. 2015). Neighbouring planets tug on each other, shifting transit times from predictions. Those timing changes were modelled as librations of resonant angles, the slow swinging that marks a locked orbit, and used to measure the planets' masses (Mills et al. 2016).","system":"The four planets are all sub-Neptunes (Mills et al. 2015).","history":"The finely tuned architecture is too delicate to have formed by planets scattering, but arises naturally if the planets migrated inward through the gas disc and were captured into resonance (Mills et al. 2016). The shape of the resonance chain also holds clues to turbulence during the gas-disc phase (Hühn et al. 2021). Only a small mass of rocky debris crossing the orbits would be enough to break such a chain (Moore et al. 2013).","open":"Tidal dissipation in the planets, or a wide-scale instability, may eventually turn resonant chains like this one into the more common type of planetary system (Mills et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler223b-Millsetal2015","cite":"Mills et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Sean M. Mills, Daniel Clark Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223b-Millsetal2016","cite":"Mills et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Sean Mills, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Cezary Migaszewski et al.","title":"Kepler-223: A Resonant Chain of Four Transiting, Sub-Neptune Planets","journal":"AAS/Division of Dynamical Astronomy Meeting #47","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223b-Hhnetal2021","cite":"Hühn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"L.-A. Hühn, G. Pichierri, B. Bitsch et al.","title":"Kepler-223 resonance holds information about turbulence during the gas-disk phase","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.13835","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler223b-Mooreetal2013","cite":"Moore et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Alexander Moore, Imran Hasan, Alice C. Quillen","title":"Limits on orbit-crossing planetesimals in the resonant multiple planet system, KOI-730","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 15906 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 15906 b is the inner of two warm sub-Neptunes that cross the face of a bright K-type star. Its period was clear from the start, but the outer planet showed only two transits about 734 days apart, which left 36 possible periods. The CHEOPS space telescope was used to find the true period of the outer planet and sharpen both planets' radii (Tuson et al. 2023). The equilibrium temperature of planet b is 668 ± 13 K, assuming no reflected light and full sharing of heat between day and night sides (Tuson et al. 2023).","system":"The outer planet, HD 15906 c, is larger, at 2.93 Earth radii, and orbits every 21.58 days (Tuson et al. 2023). The system is one of only six multiplanet systems with two warm sub-Neptune-sized planets transiting a bright star (Tuson et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd15906b-Tusonetal2023","cite":"Tuson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Tuson, D. Queloz, H. P. Osborn et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 15906 CHEOPS PIPE and WASP lightcurves (Tuson+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"CoRoT-3 b sits near the boundary between planets and brown dwarfs. Its star is hot and lacks a thick convective envelope, and the planet orbits very close to it. Studies of tidal damping of stellar tilt suggest that planets like it, in misaligned systems, must have arrived in their close orbits only after a few tens of millions of years (Spalding & Winn 2022).","system":"A possible stellar companion near the host was re-observed in a lucky-imaging search (Wöllert et al. 2015).","history":"If the star's spin and the orbit are misaligned, the planet must have moved into its tight orbit late, after the star lost its thick convective envelope (Spalding & Winn 2022).","open":"Whether the object is a planet or a brown dwarf is not settled (Cabrera et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot3b-SpaldingWinn2022","cite":"Spalding & Winn 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Christopher Spalding, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Tidal Erasure of Stellar Obliquities Constrains the Timing of Hot Jupiter Formation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.03653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot3b-Wllertetal2015","cite":"Wöllert et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maria Wöllert, Wolfgang Brandner, Carolina Bergfors et al.","title":"A Lucky Imaging search for stellar companions to transiting planet host stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01938","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot3b-Cabreraetal2009","cite":"Cabrera et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. Cabrera, M. Fridlund, M. Ollivier et al.","title":"Planetary transit candidates in CoRoT-LRc01 field","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/200912684","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":16,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-1 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is one member of a wide pair of G-type stars called ADS 16402, about 3 billion years old and separated by roughly 1550 au on the sky (Bakos et al. 2007). When reported, the planet was at least as large and less dense than any planet known at the time (Bakos et al. 2007). Its transmission spectrum is consistent with some aerosols, either Rayleigh-scattering haze or a grey cloud (Barstow et al. 2017). Strict limits show its carbon-to-oxygen ratio is below 0.9, and its water content is consistent with solar. Clouds, not a lack of water, make its water features shallow (Benneke 2015). Its secondary eclipse was also observed in the infrared K band (de Mooij et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"An attempt to detect the planet's reflected light with Hubble was granted time, but the camera failed before the observations could be made (Holman 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp1b-Bakosetal2007","cite":"Bakos et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, R. W. Noyes, G. Kovács et al.","title":"HAT-P-1b: A Large-Radius, Low-Density Exoplanet Transiting One Member of a Stellar Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609369","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp1b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp1b-Benneke2015","cite":"Benneke 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Björn Benneke","title":"Strict Upper Limits on the Carbon-to-Oxygen Ratios of Eight Hot Jupiters from Self-Consistent Atmospheric Retrieval","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.07655","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp1b-deMooijetal2011","cite":"de Mooij et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"E. J. W. de Mooij, R. J. de Kok, S. V. Nefs et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HAT-P-1b Ks-band secondary eclipse (de Mooij +, 2011)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35280049","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp1b-Holman2009","cite":"Holman 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Matthew Holman","title":"HAT-P-1: A Direct Glimpse into the Atmosphere of a Hot Jupiter","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-12 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is faint, at magnitude 15.5, and similar to the Sun (Gillon et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet's large size and low density, 1.44 Jupiter radii for 0.92 Jupiter masses, can be explained by standard models for strongly irradiated planets, so no special inflation mechanism is needed (Gillon et al. 2010). Ground-based transit follow-up years after discovery was used to refine its orbital timing (Raetz et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot12b-Gillonetal2010","cite":"Gillon et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"M. Gillon, A. Hatzes, Sz. Csizmadia et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. XII. CoRoT-12b: a short-period low-density planet transiting a solar analog star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2497","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot12b-Raetzetal2019","cite":"Raetz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"St Raetz, A. M. Heras, M. Fernández et al.","title":"Transit analysis of the CoRoT-5, CoRoT-8, CoRoT-12, CoRoT-18, CoRoT-20, and CoRoT-27 systems with combined ground- and space-based photometry","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.01536","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1338 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first planet around two stars that is suited to atmosphere studies with the James Webb Space Telescope (Standing et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet circles both stars of an eclipsing pair: a star of 1.1 solar masses and a red dwarf of 0.3 solar masses that orbit each other every 14.6 days (Kostov et al. 2020). Three transits across the larger star had similar depths but different durations, a common signature of planets that circle two stars (Kostov et al. 2020). The planet's orbit lies within about 1 degree of the plane of the two stars' orbit (Kostov et al. 2020). Radial velocity data have not detected the planet itself (Standing et al. 2023).","system":"A second planet, TOI-1338 c, has 65.2 Earth masses and a 215.5-day orbit (Standing et al. 2023). Spectra that reveal the faint red dwarf gave the masses of both stars about four times more precisely than before (Sebastian et al. 2024).","history":"The larger star spins in line with the orbits of the stars and the planet, which suggests they formed from a single disc (Kunovac Hodžić et al. 2020). Formation simulations find that only discs with low turbulence produce systems like this one (Coleman et al. 2024).","open":"Only an upper limit of 21.8 Earth masses exists for the planet's mass (Standing et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1338b-Standingetal2023","cite":"Standing et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Matthew R. Standing, Lalitha Sairam, David V. Martin et al.","title":"Radial-velocity discovery of a second planet in the TOI-1338/BEBOP-1 circumbinary system","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10794","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1338b-Kostovetal2020","cite":"Kostov et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Veselin B. Kostov, Jerome A. Orosz, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"TOI-1338: TESS' First Transiting Circumbinary Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07783","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1338b-Sebastianetal2024","cite":"Sebastian et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"D. Sebastian, A. H. M. J. Triaud, M. Brogi et al.","title":"The EBLM project - XIII. The absolute dynamical masses of the circumbinary planet host TOI-1338/BEBOP-1, and applications to the study of exoplanet atmospheres","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.06449","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1338b-Colemanetal2024","cite":"Coleman et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Gavin A. L. Coleman, Richard P. Nelson, Amaury H. M. J. Triaud et al.","title":"Constraining the formation history of the TOI-1338/BEBOP-1 circumbinary planetary system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11898","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-23 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spectra taken during a transit with the HARPS spectrograph detected the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a small shift in starlight as a planet crosses a spinning star that shows how the orbit is tilted against the star's spin (Triaud et al. 2011). The signal was weak, so the tilt is not well fixed: two methods gave conflicting rotation speeds of 2.2 and 1.35 kilometres per second, and the planet might be aligned or misaligned (Triaud et al. 2011). One transit watched in seven colour bands at once was used to build a transmission spectrum, a test for strong absorbers in the planet's atmosphere (Nikolov et al. 2013). A search of TESS data found no further transiting planets larger than 2 to 4 Earth radii (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"The orbit shows no sign of eccentricity, and the star shows no radial velocity drift that would point to a distant companion (Triaud et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"Whether the orbit is aligned with the star's spin is not known (Triaud et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp23b-Triaudetal2011","cite":"Triaud et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. H. M. J. Triaud, D. Queloz, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-23b: a transiting hot Jupiter around a K dwarf and its Rossiter-McLaughlin effect","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.2603","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp23b-Nikolovetal2013","cite":"Nikolov et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"N. Nikolov, G. Chen, J. J. Fortney et al.","title":"Refined physical properties and g', r', i', z', J, H, K transmission spectrum of WASP-23b from the ground","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.4565","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp23b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-45 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities, adaptive optics imaging and near-infrared spectroscopy confirmed that the transits come from a planet (Johnson et al. 2012). The planet is the first hot Jupiter known around an M-type dwarf star (Johnson et al. 2012). Kepler transit light curves have since been used to map dark spots and bright patches, called faculae, on the star. Spots and faculae differ from the quiet surface by a few hundred degrees, with spots near the centre of the disc and faculae toward the edge (Zaleski et al. 2020). The map shows an activity cycle of about 295 days and a star rotation of 15.52 days at the latitude the planet crosses (Zaleski et al. 2020).","system":"The host star is rich in heavy elements, as is common for M dwarfs that harbour giant planets (Johnson et al. 2012). Its equator spins faster than its poles, by about 7.8 per cent (Zaleski et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler45b-Johnsonetal2012","cite":"Johnson et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"John Asher Johnson, J. Zachary Gazak, Kevin Apps et al.","title":"Characterizing the Cool KOIs. II. The M Dwarf KOI-254 and Its Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1112.0017","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler45b-Zaleskietal2020","cite":"Zaleski et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. M. Zaleski, A. Valio, B. D. Carter et al.","title":"Activity and differential rotation of the early M dwarf Kepler-45 from transit mapping","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/staa103","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-444 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was vetted and confirmed with follow-up observations as part of a study of ten small worlds seen by both TESS and the CHEOPS satellite (Oddo et al. 2023). The set was chosen because the planets border the radius valley, a feature in small-planet sizes that bears on how atmospheres form and evolve (Oddo et al. 2023). CHEOPS transits gave depth measurements as good as between 2 and 12 TESS transits, averaging 5.9 (Oddo et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi444b-Oddoetal2023","cite":"Oddo et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Dominic Oddo, Diana Dragomir, Alexis Brandeker et al.","title":"Characterization of a Set of Small Planets with TESS and CHEOPS and an Analysis of Photometric Performance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08162","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CT Cha b":{"text":{"why":"Disc material around giant planets supplies the ingredients for moons and the last mass a planet gains, so the chemistry of such discs tells how giant planets finish forming (Cugno & Grant 2025).","observed":"The James Webb Space Telescope took a mid-infrared spectrum of the disc of gas and dust around the companion. The disc shows carbon-rich chemistry, with seven carbon-bearing molecules, up to benzene, and a gas carbon-to-oxygen ratio above 1 (Cugno & Grant 2025). Ratios that high are unlike those usually measured in the atmospheres of directly imaged giant planets (Cugno & Grant 2025). Radio observations at 0.88 millimetres found no dust at the companion's position, with limits of 120 to 210 microjanskys (Wu et al. 2020).","system":"The host star CT Cha A has a mass of 0.796 solar masses, measured from the rotation of its own disc (Sheehan et al. 2019). Its disc shows no carbon-bearing molecules at all (Cugno & Grant 2025).","history":"The difference between the two discs points to rapid, divergent chemical change over about a million years (Cugno & Grant 2025).","open":"Why the companion's disc is so faint in radio light is not clear: fast inward drift of dust, a lack of dust traps, or tidal effects like those in binary stars could all play a part (Wu et al. 2020). Another idea is a very compact disc, smaller than 1000 Jupiter radii and thick enough to hide its dust (Wu et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ctchab-CugnoGrant2025","cite":"Cugno & Grant 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Gabriele Cugno, Sierra L. Grant","title":"A Carbon-rich Disk Surrounding a Planetary-mass Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15209","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ctchab-Wuetal2020","cite":"Wu et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ya-Lin Wu, Brendan P. Bowler, Patrick D. Sheehan et al.","title":"ALMA 0.88 mm Survey of Disks around Planetary-mass Companions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.08658","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ctchab-Sheehanetal2019","cite":"Sheehan et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Patrick D. Sheehan, Ya-Lin Wu, Josh A. Eisner et al.","title":"High-precision Dynamical Masses of Pre-main-sequence Stars with ALMA and Gaia","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.00032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ctchab-Wuetal2017","cite":"Wu et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ya-Lin Wu, Laird M. Close, Josh A. Eisner et al.","title":"An Explanation of the Very Low Radio Flux of Young Planet-mass Companions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.07489","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":9,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-27 b":{"text":{"why":"Sub-Saturns range from about 6 to 60 Earth masses whatever their size, so planets of similar size can hold very different amounts of heavy elements (Petigura et al. 2017).","observed":"Radial velocity monitoring with the Keck HIRES spectrograph measured the planet's mass, in a campaign on four sub-Saturns of 4.5 to 5.5 Earth radii (Petigura et al. 2017). K2-27 b came out at 30.9 Earth masses, much heavier than the 16.5 Earth masses of K2-32 b in the same set (Petigura et al. 2017). Radial velocities show no sign of additional planets in the system (Petigura et al. 2017). Across the group, planet mass correlates strongly with the heavy-element content of the host star (Petigura et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k227b-Petiguraetal2017","cite":"Petigura et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Erik A. Petigura, Evan Sinukoff, Eric D. Lopez et al.","title":"Four Sub-Saturns with Dissimilar Densities: Windows into Planetary Cores and Envelopes","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00013","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 278555 b":{"text":{"why":"Warm Jupiters sit between cold giant planets and hot Jupiters, so their orbits can show how giant planets form and move inward (Ball et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet first appeared as a single transit in a TESS light curve, which left its period unknown (Ball et al. 2026). Further TESS data and radial velocities from the NEID spectrograph confirmed the signal (Ball et al. 2026). A joint fit of both data sets gives a mass of 0.84 Jupiter masses and a low-eccentricity, near-circular orbit (Ball et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd278555b-Balletal2026","cite":"Ball et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"James Ball, Arvind F. Gupta, Diana Dragomir et al.","title":"Confirmation of the Low-eccentricity Warm Jupiter HD 278555 b with NEID","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae8a50","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-93 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is bright and has been watched for years, so the planet's size is known to within about 120 kilometres (Ballard et al. 2014).","observed":"Sound waves in the star, measured as tiny brightness changes, give the star a density of 1.652 grams per cubic centimetre (Ballard et al. 2014). The transit gives a consistent but less precise density, which supports the planetary interpretation (Ballard et al. 2014). The transit depth is the same in Kepler and Spitzer light, which also supports it (Ballard et al. 2014). Seven Spitzer transits were observed, three of them in a new pointing mode that halved the uncertainty on the planet-to-star size ratio (Ballard et al. 2014).","system":"The star's mass is 0.911 solar masses, one of the lowest among stars studied with sound-wave seismology (Ballard et al. 2014). It is a benchmark for the future PLATO mission (Bétrisey et al. 2022).","history":"Revised stellar parameters were used to update the planet's properties and to simulate how tides and atmospheric evaporation shape its orbit over time (Bétrisey et al. 2022).","open":"The interior is not well fixed: modelling of six small planets that includes this one allows high water fractions even for most planets that might be rocky (Dorn et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler93b-Ballardetal2014","cite":"Ballard et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Sarah Ballard, William J. Chaplin, David Charbonneau et al.","title":"Kepler-93b: A Terrestrial World Measured to within 120 km, and a Test Case for a New Spitzer Observing Mode","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.3659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler93b-Btriseyetal2022","cite":"Bétrisey et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Bétrisey, C. Pezzotti, G. Buldgen et al.","title":"Kepler-93: A testbed for detailed seismic modelling and orbital evolution of super-Earths around solar-like stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.13686","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler93b-Dornetal2017","cite":"Dorn et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Natalie R. Hinkel, Julia Venturini","title":"Bayesian analysis of interiors of HD 219134b, Kepler-10b, Kepler-93b, CoRoT-7b, 55 Cnc e, and HD 97658b using stellar abundance proxies","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.03909","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-138 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet concerns its crowded system. The planet is the fifth of six known planets around K2-138, found with the help of citizen scientists in the Exoplanet Explorers project (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Its orbit near 12.76 days sits in a gap before the outer planet, which circles every 41.966 days (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","system":"The system holds one super-Earth and four sub-Neptunes near 3:2 orbital resonances, plus a sixth planet confirmed with Spitzer, and has the most known planets of any K2 system (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). The wide gap between the outer two planets leaves room for further non-transiting planets (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021). Radial velocity masses exist for the inner four planets, and the near-resonance makes the system a benchmark for measuring masses from transit timing (Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021).","history":"Dynamical work finds evidence for the first known capture of a real system in a first-order three-planet resonance (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024). Capture is favoured by short-period planets of a few Earth masses and by lower-density, wider discs (Cerioni & Beaugé 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2138f-HardegreeUllmanetal2021","cite":"Hardegree-Ullman et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Kevin K. Hardegree-Ullman, Jessie L. Christiansen, David R. Ciardi et al.","title":"K2-138 g: Spitzer Spots a Sixth Planet for the Citizen Science System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08978","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2138f-CerioniBeaug2024","cite":"Cerioni & Beaugé 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Cerioni, C. Beaugé","title":"K2-138 and the capture in 1st-order 3P-MMRs","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4562 b":{"text":{"why":"Young giant planets are rare, and this one's long, stretched orbit gives a view of the early life of giant planets around Sun-like stars (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet orbits a young F7-type star, younger than the Praesepe and Hyades clusters, which are about 700 million years old (Heitzmann et al. 2023). Its 225-day period is unusually long for a transiting planet with a measured mass, and its orbit is strongly eccentric, with e = 0.76 (Heitzmann et al. 2023). TESS watched the star through 25 sectors near its southern viewing zone, so the period came from TESS alone (Heitzmann et al. 2023). The measured radius of 1.118 Jupiter radii fits models of how young giant planets contract (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","system":"Transit times vary by about 20 minutes across five transits, a hint that a companion planet may tug on it (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","history":"A companion, if confirmed, could explain how the planet reached such an eccentric orbit (Heitzmann et al. 2023).","open":"The suspected companion has not been confirmed (Heitzmann et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4562b-Heitzmannetal2023","cite":"Heitzmann et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Alexis Heitzmann, George Zhou, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"TOI-4562b: A Highly Eccentric Temperate Jupiter Analog Orbiting a Young Field Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.10854","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-892 b":{"text":{"why":"Transiting gas giants with orbits longer than 10 days are scarce, so each one adds to a thinly populated region (Brahm et al. 2020).","observed":"The planet's mass of 0.95 Jupiter masses was measured with a wide range of ground-based telescopes and spectrographs (Brahm et al. 2020). It was announced together with TOI-481 b, which has a similar size and period but a mass of 1.53 Jupiter masses (Brahm et al. 2020). The host is an F-type dwarf, rich in heavy elements with [Fe/H] = +0.24 (Brahm et al. 2020).","system":"The sibling planet TOI-481 b orbits a more evolved star at the turn-off point of the main sequence, while TOI-892 is still an F-type dwarf (Brahm et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi892b-Brahmetal2020","cite":"Brahm et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Rafael Brahm, Louise D. Nielsen, Robert A. Wittenmyer et al.","title":"TOI-481 b and TOI-892 b: Two Long-period Hot Jupiters from the Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.08881","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-167 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer giant planet, Kepler-167 e. Long-term radial velocity monitoring gave that planet a mass of 1.01 Jupiter masses on a 1071-day orbit, making it a Jupiter analogue (Chachan et al. 2022). Kepler-167 is the only known system with both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant beyond 1 au (Chachan et al. 2022). A refit of the Kepler light curves gave updated radii for all four planets (Chachan et al. 2022).","system":"Planet e holds about 66 Earth masses of solids, so it is rich in heavy elements compared with its solar-metallicity star (Chachan et al. 2022).","history":"Pebble-accretion models show that massive, metal-rich discs can form giant planets and also deliver enough solids to the inner disc for a set of super-Earths (Chachan et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler167c-Chachanetal2022","cite":"Chachan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yayaati Chachan, Paul A. Dalba, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-167e as a Probe of the Formation Histories of Cold Giants with Inner Super-Earths","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00747","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-28 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced with five others as part of a set of six new inflated hot Jupiters (Espinoza et al. 2016). It is one of the typical ones: a radius between 1.17 and 1.26 Jupiter radii, a G-type host star and a short orbit of 3.2 to 4.6 days (Espinoza et al. 2016). Its mass lies in the well-populated range of 0.5 to 0.7 Jupiter masses (Espinoza et al. 2016). The planet is a good target for future atmosphere studies (Espinoza et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats28b-Espinozaetal2016","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. Espinoza, D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-25b through HATS-30b: A Half-dozen New Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiters from the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-424 c":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters usually have no nearby planetary companions, so a hot Jupiter with a more massive outer companion is unusual (Endl et al. 2014).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour Kepler-424 b. The planet does not transit its star, so its orbit and mass come from precise radial velocities taken with the High Resolution Spectrograph on the Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Endl et al. 2014). It moves on an eccentric orbit of 223 days with e = 0.3 and is more massive than the transiting hot Jupiter Kepler-424 b inside it (Endl et al. 2014). False positives were ruled out with follow-up observations (Endl et al. 2014).","system":"The inner planet, Kepler-424 b, is a hot Jupiter on a 3.31-day orbit (Endl et al. 2014). The system resembles upsilon Andromedae, another hot Jupiter with outer companions (Endl et al. 2014).","history":"The usual loneliness of hot Jupiters might result from a highly dynamical past (Endl et al. 2014).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler424c-Endletal2014","cite":"Endl et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Michael Endl, Douglas A. Caldwell, Thomas Barclay et al.","title":"Kepler-424 b: A \"Lonely\" Hot Jupiter that Found a Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.8156","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 219134 b":{"text":{"why":"When found, it was the nearest known transiting planet, which makes the star a prime target for follow-up (Motalebi et al. 2015).","observed":"Spitzer observations caught the planet crossing its star, a dip of 359 parts per million (Motalebi et al. 2015). Combined with the radial velocity signal, the transit gives a mean density of 5.76 grams per cubic centimetre, which suggests a rocky composition (Motalebi et al. 2015). Observations of the star's magnetic field and wind suggest the strong wind hitting the planet may knock material from its surface (Folsom et al. 2022). Models of core-powered mass loss predict the planet could be losing its atmosphere today (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","system":"The star hosts six reported planets found by radial velocity, and planets b and c show transits (Bailey 2023). TESS rules out transits of planet f (Bailey 2023).","history":"Planet b may belong to a class of super-Earths that formed from high-temperature condensates, which are rich in calcium and aluminium minerals and have no core (Dorn et al. 2019). Planet b's density is 10 per cent lower than planet c's, but 11 per cent uncertainties cannot settle whether the difference is real (Dorn et al. 2019).","open":"Water mass fractions allowed by interior models stay high, even for planets that are probably rocky (Dorn et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-Motalebietal2015","cite":"Motalebi et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"F. Motalebi, S. Udry, M. Gillon et al.","title":"The HARPS-N Rocky Planet Search. I. HD 219134 b: A transiting rocky planet in a multi-planet system at 6.5 pc from the Sun","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.08532","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-Folsometal2022","cite":"Folsom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Colin P. Folsom, Aline A. Vidotto, Luca Fossati","title":"Stellar magnetic fields for star-planet interactions: HD 219134 and 55 Cnc","journal":"The 21st Cambridge Workshop on Cool Stars, Stellar Systems, and the Sun","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7592884","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-Bailey2023","cite":"Bailey 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Vanessa Bailey","title":"HD 219134 Revisited: Planet d Transit Upper Limit and Planet f Transit Non-Detection with ASTERIA and TESS","journal":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory Data Set","url":"https://doi.org/10.48577/JPL.MTOJHC","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-Dornetal2019","cite":"Dorn et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"C. Dorn, J. H. D. Harrison, A. Bonsor et al.","title":"A new class of Super-Earths formed from high-temperature condensates: HD219134 b, 55 Cnc e, WASP-47 e","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07222","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd219134b-Dornetal2017","cite":"Dorn et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Caroline Dorn, Natalie R. Hinkel, Julia Venturini","title":"Bayesian analysis of interiors of HD 219134b, Kepler-10b, Kepler-93b, CoRoT-7b, 55 Cnc e, and HD 97658b using stellar abundance proxies","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.03909","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 285968 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Also known as GJ 176 b, after another catalogue name of its star. Radial velocity data were reanalysed with a stellar-activity model, which cut the mass uncertainty to 6.9 per cent from earlier values of 15 and 12 per cent (Cote et al. 2024). The planet is now one of the most precise radial velocity mass measurements for a small, cool planet (Cote et al. 2024). The planet has been named as a target for the LIFE mission (Cote et al. 2024).","system":"Its host, GJ 176, is an M2 dwarf, and models of its ultraviolet and extreme-ultraviolet light, which cannot be observed directly, were built to understand the radiation the planet receives (Peacock et al. 2019).","history":"The orbit is mildly eccentric even though the star is old, about 8 billion years, and the planet is close in (Veyette & Muirhead 2018). Tides inside the planet appear to dissipate energy as in the ice giants of the Solar System (Veyette & Muirhead 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd285968b-Coteetal2024","cite":"Cote et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Melanie Cote, Victoria DiTomasso, Mercedes Lopez-Morales","title":"Refined Mass Measurement of sub-Neptune Exoplanet GJ 176 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #243","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd285968b-Peacocketal2019","cite":"Peacock et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Sarah Peacock, Travis Barman, Evgenya L. Shkolnik et al.","title":"Predicting the Extreme Ultraviolet Radiation Environment of Exoplanets around Low-mass Stars: GJ 832, GJ 176, and GJ 436","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.08053","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd285968b-VeyetteMuirhead2018","cite":"Veyette & Muirhead 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Mark J. Veyette, Philip S. Muirhead","title":"Chemo-kinematic Ages of Eccentric-planet-hosting M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06017","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"eps CrB b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first reported in 2010 as a possible companion to a K2 III giant star, found among 55 K giants checked for velocity changes with the BOES spectrograph at Bohyunsan Observatory (Lee et al. 2010). Measurements from 2005 to 2010 gave an orbit of 419 days with e = 0.14 (Lee et al. 2010). The velocity swings of 137 metres per second imply a minimum mass of 4.2 Jupiter masses, if the star has 0.77 solar masses (Lee et al. 2010). The planet's mass depends on the star's assumed mass, and it orbits about 1 au from its star (Lee et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epscrbb-Leeetal2010","cite":"Lee et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, In-Woo Han, Kang-Min Kim et al.","title":"Search for extrasolar planets around K-giants: \\varepsilon CrB - possible planet","journal":"Bulletin of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 273 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this planet concerns its system as a whole. The planet is one of two confirmed planets and two candidates around an M dwarf only a few parsecs away (Pozuelos et al. 2020). No transits are seen, so only minimum masses are known; planet c has a minimum of 1.18 Earth masses (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Simulations followed formation and water delivery during the first 100 million years and tested the system's stability (Pozuelos et al. 2020).","system":"Planet b sits in the habitable zone (Pozuelos et al. 2020). Work in progress studied tides, spin-orbit locking and tidal heating, and regions where asteroid-belt analogues could survive in stable orbits (Pozuelos et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The true masses are unknown because the orbits' tilt has not been measured (Pozuelos et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj273c-Pozuelosetal2020","cite":"Pozuelos et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Francisco J. Pozuelos, Juan C. Suárez, Gonzalo C. de Elía et al.","title":"GJ 273: on the formation, dynamical evolution, and habitability of a planetary system hosted by an M dwarf at 3.75 parsec","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.09403","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj273c-Pozuelosetal2019","cite":"Pozuelos et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"F. J. Pozuelos, J. C. Suárez, G. C. de Elía et al.","title":"GJ 273: Formation, dynamical evolution and habitability of one of the most interesting planetary systems","journal":"Highlights on Spanish Astrophysics X","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-161 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the CORALIE spectrograph and transit light curves from the TRAPPIST, SPECULOOS, NITES and Euler telescopes gave the planet a mass of 2.5 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.14 Jupiter radii (Barkaoui et al. 2019). It orbits an F6-type star in 5.406 days (Barkaoui et al. 2019). Given the planet's irradiation of about 10 to the power of 9 erg per second per square centimetre and its mass, the size agrees well with classical models of irradiated giant planets (Barkaoui et al. 2019).","system":"WASP-163 b and WASP-170 b were announced in the same work (Barkaoui et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp161b-Barkaouietal2019","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"K. Barkaoui, A. Burdanov, C. Hellier et al.","title":"Discovery of Three New Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-161 b, WASP-163 b, and WASP-170 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06548","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-19 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is part of a compact three-planet system whose two larger planets are close to a 3:2 orbital resonance, so they tug on each other and shift each other's transit times (Nespral et al. 2017). Fitting radial velocities and transit times together gave planet c a mass of 7.5 Earth masses, while radial velocities alone gave only a marginal detection near 5.9 Earth masses (Nespral et al. 2017). Planet b is much heavier at 54.4 Earth masses (Nespral et al. 2017). Spitzer added transit observations of both planets, and Keck HIRES added 51 spectra (Petigura et al. 2020).","system":"K2-19 was the first K2 multi-planet system to show transit timing variations (Barros et al. 2016). The slow swings of the timing last more than 1.5 years, far longer than the 80-day K2 observations, but a photo-dynamical model detects the short-period timing changes caused by conjunctions between planets (Barros et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The mass of planet c is uncertain, partly because radial velocity precision is limited and unrecognised systematics may remain (Nespral et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k219c-Nespraletal2017","cite":"Nespral et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. Nespral, D. Gandolfi, H. J. Deeg et al.","title":"Mass determination of K2-19b and K2-19c from radial velocities and transit timing variations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.01265","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k219c-Petiguraetal2020","cite":"Petigura et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"E. A. Petigura, J. Livingston, K. Batygin et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2-19b and c transit times and radial velocities (Petigura+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.51590002","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k219c-Barrosetal2016","cite":"Barros et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. C. C. Barros, J. M. Almenara, O. Demangeon et al.","title":"K2-19, The first K2 muti-planetary system showing TTVs","journal":"IAU Focus Meeting","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921316002386","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-37 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is a metal-poor G2 dwarf with a temperature of 5800 K and [Fe/H] = -0.40 (Simpson et al. 2010). Few stars with so low a heavy-element content are known to host a transiting planet, and WASP-37 is one of the lowest (Simpson et al. 2010). The planet has 1.80 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.16 Jupiter radii, which gives a mean density of 1.15 times Jupiter's (Simpson et al. 2010). Each transit lasts about 0.13 days (Simpson et al. 2010).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp37b-Simpsonetal2010","cite":"Simpson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"E. K. Simpson, F. Faedi, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-37b: A 1.8 M <SUB>J</SUB> Exoplanet Transiting a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.3096","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-17 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is bright, so follow-up can measure the orbit's geometry and the planet's atmosphere (Brahm et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet is the first transiting warm Jupiter found by the HATSouth network and the longest-period transiting planet from any ground-based survey (Brahm et al. 2016). Its orbit is circular, with a period of 16.2546 days (Brahm et al. 2016). Its radius of 0.777 Jupiter radii is very compact for a mass of 1.338 Jupiter masses (Brahm et al. 2016). Up to 50 per cent of the planet's mass may be heavy elements to explain such a high density (Brahm et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a bright, metal-rich G-type star with [Fe/H] = +0.3 (Brahm et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats17b-Brahmetal2016","cite":"Brahm et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"R. Brahm, A. Jordán, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-17b: A Transiting Compact Warm Jupiter in a 16.3 Day Circular Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.05758","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-189 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is the brightest known host of a transiting hot Jupiter (Anderson et al. 2018).","observed":"The planet's orbit passes over the poles of its star, at an angle of 89.3 degrees, around a rapidly spinning A-type star of 8000 K (Anderson et al. 2018). The dayside shows iron lines in emission, direct evidence of a temperature inversion, with 4320 K at the top of the inversion against an equilibrium temperature of 2641 K (Yan et al. 2020). Titanium is present but at a sub-solar ratio to iron (Pelletier et al. 2026). Near-ultraviolet transit depth is about twice the visual one, which points to an extended upper atmosphere at about 15,000 K, losing mass at about 4 times 10 to the power of 8 kilograms per second (Sreejith et al. 2023).","system":"Of eight hot-Jupiter systems with stars hotter than 7000 K, seven have strongly misaligned orbits (Anderson et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp189b-Andersonetal2018","cite":"Anderson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"D. R. Anderson, L. Y. Temple, L. D. Nielsen et al.","title":"WASP-189b: an ultra-hot Jupiter transiting the bright A star HR 5599 in a polar orbit","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.04897","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp189b-Yanetal2020","cite":"Yan et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"F. Yan, E. Pallé, A. Reiners et al.","title":"A temperature inversion with atomic iron in the ultra-hot dayside atmosphere of WASP-189b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.02716","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp189b-Pelletieretal2026","cite":"Pelletier et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"S. Pelletier, R. Allart, V. Vaulato et al.","title":"The breaking of cold traps and onset of titanium in ultra-hot Jupiter atmospheres: WASP-189b in context","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15934","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp189b-Sreejithetal2023","cite":"Sreejith et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. G. Sreejith, Kevin France, Luca Fossati et al.","title":"CUTE Reveals Escaping Metals in the Upper Atmosphere of the Ultrahot Jupiter WASP-189b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05726","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-64 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has 1.271 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.271 Jupiter radii (Gillon et al. 2013). It circles a V = 12.3 G7-type dwarf of 1.004 solar masses on a very short, circular orbit of 1.57 days at 0.02648 au (Gillon et al. 2013). Its size is typical of hot Jupiters of similar mass (Gillon et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp64b-Gillonetal2013","cite":"Gillon et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Gillon, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-64 b and WASP-72 b: two new transiting highly irradiated giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.4257","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-264 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets in clusters have known ages, and this is the second known cluster star with multiple transiting planets (Livingston et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet orbits an M2 dwarf in the Praesepe open cluster, also called the Beehive Cluster, with an age of 600 to 800 million years (Livingston et al. 2019). Its radius is 2.2 Earth radii and its equilibrium temperature about 496 K (Livingston et al. 2019). Unlike some planets in clusters, it does not appear to have an inflated radius compared with field planets around stars of similar mass (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"A second sub-Neptune orbits every 19.7 days, with a radius of 2.7 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of 331 K (Livingston et al. 2019). The system is valuable for testing theories of photoevaporation in multi-planet systems (Livingston et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"Planet masses have not been measured, and follow-up with near-infrared spectrographs could provide them (Livingston et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2264b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Fei Dai, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"K2-264: a transiting multiplanet system in the Praesepe open cluster","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01968","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 39091 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet, pi Men c. Planet b, the outer giant, has its orbit measured with a joint analysis of radial velocities and astrometry from the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites (De Rosa et al. 2020). Radial velocities come from ESPRESSO, with 275 spectra over 37 nights, and from CORALIE, with 60 spectra between 1998 and 2020 (Damasso et al. 2020).","system":"Planet c is strongly irradiated and has a bulk density consistent with 50 to 100 per cent water by volume, so it may be a water world (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019). Models suggest its irradiation sustains a vigorous atmospheric outflow (Garcia Munoz et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd39091b-DeRosaetal2020","cite":"De Rosa et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. J. De Rosa, R. Dawson, E. L. Nielsen","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: pi Men radial velocity curves (De Rosa+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36400073","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd39091b-Damassoetal2020","cite":"Damasso et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Damasso, A. Sozzetti, C. Lovis et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: pi Men radial velocity curves (Damasso+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36420031","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd39091b-Munozetal2019","cite":"Munoz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Antonio Garcia Munoz, Juan Cabrera, Luca Fossati et al.","title":"Pi Men c: The first confirmed water world?","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 72659 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HARPS-N collected 91 spectra of the star between 2013 and 2023, giving high-precision radial velocities and activity indicators (Ruggieri et al. 2025). A dynamical study of five systems, including this one, tested whether a terrestrial planet could keep a stable orbit in the habitable zone next to a giant planet. Orbits were integrated over 1 million years, with the giant's eccentricity varied over 80 values (Asghari et al. 2004).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd72659b-Ruggierietal2025","cite":"Ruggieri et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Ruggieri, A. Sozzetti, S. Desidera et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 72659 RV, S and BIS curves (Ruggieri+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd72659b-Asgharietal2004","cite":"Asghari et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"N. Asghari, C. Broeg, L. Carone et al.","title":"Stability of terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of Gl 777 A, HD 72659, Gl 614, 47 Uma and HD 4208","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0403152","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-107 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a highly inflated Jovian planet, with a radius of 1.44 Jupiter radii for a mass of 0.84 Jupiter masses (Eigmüller et al. 2017). Its host is an F9 IV star near the end of its main-sequence life (Eigmüller et al. 2017). A phase curve, the small brightness change as the planet's day and night sides rotate into view, was later detected in the K2 light curve (Niraula et al. 2018). A mass estimated from the phase curve agrees with the radial velocity mass within 3 sigma (Niraula et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2107b-Eigmlleretal2017","cite":"Eigmüller et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Philipp Eigmüller, Davide Gandolfi, Carina M. Persson et al.","title":"K2-60b and K2-107b. A Sub-Jovian and a Jovian Planet from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.03704","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2107b-Niraulaetal2018","cite":"Niraula et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Seth Redfield, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Discovery of Six Optical Phase Curves with K2","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09227","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 19615 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Precise radial velocities from the Bohyunsan Observatory, part of a survey of northern circumpolar stars, show a signal at 402 days and a minimum mass of 8.5 Jupiter masses (Jeong et al. 2022). The host is a giant star, and no variability with the same period appears in photometry or line shapes, so the signal is most likely a planet (Jeong et al. 2022). Masses and periods like these are typical for planets around intermediate-mass stars (Jeong et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd19615b-Jeongetal2022","cite":"Jeong et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Jeong, B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park et al.","title":"Search for exoplanets around northern circumpolar stars. V. Three likely planetary companions to the giant stars HD 19615, HD 150010, and HD 174205","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08899","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 77900 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The object is a young, late-M brown dwarf that has been claimed as a companion of the star HIP 77900 in the Upper Scorpius association, which is 5 to 11 million years old (Petrus et al. 2020). Spectra from the X-shooter instrument at the VLT, covering ultraviolet, optical and near-infrared light, were used to measure its chemistry and physical properties (Petrus et al. 2020). The spectra were compared with those of free-floating analogues in the same association (Petrus et al. 2020). Hydrogen emission lines were examined for signs of ongoing accretion (Petrus et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip77900b-Petrusetal2020","cite":"Petrus et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Petrus, M. Bonnefoy, G. Chauvin et al.","title":"A new take on the low-mass brown dwarf companions on wide orbits in Upper-Scorpius","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.00347","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-31 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a very low density, with 0.48 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.55 Jupiter radii, second only to WASP-17 b (Anderson et al. 2011). Its host is a metal-poor, late-F-type dwarf that is part of a common proper motion pair (Anderson et al. 2011). The light was corrected for blending with a star 4.1 magnitudes fainter, 35 arcseconds away (Anderson et al. 2011). Transmission spectra were taken with the Hubble Space Telescope and Spitzer (Sing et al. 2017). Retrievals fit the data better with a grey cloud than with other aerosol models (Barstow et al. 2017), and show no clear evidence for hazes (Arfaux & Lavvas 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp31b-Andersonetal2011","cite":"Anderson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-31b: a low-density planet transiting a metal-poor, late-F-type dwarf star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.5882","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp31b-Singetal2017","cite":"Sing et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"D. K. Sing, H. R. Wakeford, A. P. Showman et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP-31b:HST/Spitzer transmission spectral survey (Sing+, 2015)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp31b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp31b-ArfauxLavvas2022","cite":"Arfaux & Lavvas 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Anthony Arfaux, Panayotis Lavvas","title":"A large range of haziness conditions in hot-Jupiter atmospheres","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13134","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":12,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-351 h":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Also known as Kepler-90 h. Transit times of Kepler-90 g and h shift by up to 25 hours because the two planets pull on each other (Liang et al. 2021). The best fit gives planet h a mass of 203 Earth masses (Liang et al. 2021). Their periods are close to a 2:3 resonance, but the planets are not locked in it (Liang et al. 2021). The configuration is stable over billions of years (Liang et al. 2021).","system":"Planet g has a mass of 15 Earth masses and an unusually low apparent density of 0.15 grams per cubic centimetre (Liang et al. 2021).","history":"Interactions between the planets after formation could have raised their eccentricities and broken the resonant lock (Liang et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi351h-Liangetal2021","cite":"Liang et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yan Liang, Jakob Robnik, Uroš Seljak","title":"Kepler-90: Giant Transit-timing Variations Reveal a Super-puff","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08515","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148797 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns both of its planets together. HD 148797 c and its inner neighbour HD 148797 b both pass in front of their star, and both were watched by the TESS and CHEOPS space telescopes (Almenara et al. 2026). Their transits arrive slightly early or late in opposite directions, and the size of those shifts gave a mass of 39.6 Earth masses for planet c (Almenara et al. 2026). Its density of 0.377 grams per cubic centimetre is low, so it puffs out more than a Neptune-like planet of the same mass would (Almenara et al. 2026).","system":"The two planets orbit with a period ratio of 1.619, very close to the golden mean, which sits near several strong orbital resonances (Almenara et al. 2026). Systems of several planets are common among planets of this kind, and most contain at least one pair of neighbours whose orbits are less than three times apart (Almenara et al. 2026).","history":"Crowded, calm systems like this one fit gentle inward drift of planets through the gas disc around the young star, rather than violent scattering (Almenara et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148797c-Almenaraetal2026","cite":"Almenara et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. M. Almenara, A. Castro-González, R. Mardling et al.","title":"HD 148797: A bright F-type star with two moderate-period low-density sub-Jovian planets. Compact multi-planet architectures are common in the Neptunian savanna","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09656","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 5278 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour HD 5278 b, a transiting sub-Neptune on a 14.3-day orbit (Sozzetti et al. 2021). HD 5278 c does not pass in front of its star and is known only from the tug it exerts on the star, seen in 43 spectra from the ESPRESSO spectrograph on the Very Large Telescope (Sozzetti et al. 2021). Its minimum mass is Neptune-like (Sozzetti et al. 2021). Because it does not transit, its true size is not known.","system":"The star is a bright, late F-type dwarf with a temperature of 6203 K (Sozzetti et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd5278c-Sozzettietal2021","cite":"Sozzetti et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. Sozzetti, M. Damasso, A. S. Bonomo et al.","title":"A sub-Neptune and a non-transiting Neptune-mass companion unveiled by ESPRESSO around the bright late-F dwarf HD 5278 (TOI-130)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.12300","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1627 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets only a few tens of millions of years old are very rare, so this one shows what young planets are like.","observed":"The star belongs to a group of stars called the delta Lyrae cluster, shown to be 38 million years old by Gaia motions, TESS spin periods and lithium in the starlight (Bouma et al. 2022). That makes the planet the youngest with a precisely known age found by the main Kepler mission (Bouma et al. 2022). Its transits show two oddities: the average dip is lopsided, and individual transit times may follow the slope of the star's light curve (Bouma et al. 2022).","system":"The planet sits in a scattered group of stars stretching from Cepheus to Hercules, which includes other young Kepler planets (Bouma et al. 2022).","history":"Many processes in planet evolution happen in the first few million years after the disc disperses, but nearly all known planets are billions of years old (Bouma et al. 2022).","open":"The cause of the lopsided transit and the shifting transit times is not settled (Bouma et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1627b-Boumaetal2022","cite":"Bouma et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. G. Bouma, J. L. Curtis, K. Masuda et al.","title":"A 38 Million Year Old Neptune-sized Planet in the Kepler Field","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.14776","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-295 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-295 is a K5 dwarf, a cool orange star, and its planet is slightly smaller than Jupiter (Smith et al. 2019). Radial velocity measurements from the HARPS and FIES spectrographs gave the planet's mass, which is similar to that of Saturn (Smith et al. 2019). Roughly Jupiter-sized but only Saturn-massive, the planet is on the puffy side for a gas giant.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2295b-Smithetal2019","cite":"Smith et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, Sz. Csizmadia, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"K2-295 b and K2-237 b: Two Transiting Hot Jupiters","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.05865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 149026 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is unusually dense for its size.","observed":"The planet is a hot Saturn with a mass of 0.36 Jupiters but a radius of only 0.725 Jupiters (Sato et al. 2005). Models suggest a core of about 67 Earth masses made of elements heavier than hydrogen (Sato et al. 2005). Its star was the second-brightest known to host a transiting planet at the time (Sato et al. 2005). Comparing Spitzer infrared data with models points to a hot upper atmosphere that gets warmer with height, a so-called thermal inversion (Burrows et al. 2008). High-resolution near-infrared spectra show water vapour at about 4.8 times the noise level (Rafi et al. 2024). A uniform study of nine planets found no convincing sign of unusual carbon-to-oxygen ratios (Line et al. 2014).","system":"The star is metal-rich, with iron at [Fe/H]=+0.36, and chromospherically quiet, meaning it shows little magnetic activity (Sato et al. 2005).","history":"","open":"The water signal appears at a speed well off from what theory predicts, which may mean unusual winds in the atmosphere or a slightly eccentric orbit (Rafi et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd149026b-Satoetal2005","cite":"Sato et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Debra A. Fischer, Gregory W. Henry et al.","title":"The N2K Consortium. II. A Transiting Hot Saturn around HD 149026 with a Large Dense Core","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0507009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd149026b-Burrowsetal2008","cite":"Burrows et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"A. Burrows, J. Budaj, I. Hubeny","title":"Theoretical Spectra and Light Curves of Close-in Extrasolar Giant Planets and Comparison with Data","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.4080","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd149026b-Rafietal2024","cite":"Rafi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sayyed A. Rafi, Stevanus K. Nugroho, Motohide Tamura et al.","title":"Evidence of Water Vapor in the Atmosphere of a Metal-rich Hot Saturn with High-resolution Transmission Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01266","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd149026b-Lineetal2014","cite":"Line et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Michael R. Line, Heather Knutson, Aaron S. Wolf et al.","title":"A Systematic Retrieval Analysis of Secondary Eclipse Spectra. II. A Uniform Analysis of Nine Planets and their C to O Ratios","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.6663","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":29,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-31 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-31 b is one of five hot Jupiters announced together from the HATSouth network of automated telescopes (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). Its radius of 1.64 Jupiters makes it moderately inflated, meaning larger than a gas giant of its mass is expected to be (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). Planets like this help test the ideas about what puffs up hot Jupiters (de Val-Borro et al. 2016). The data favour a circular orbit over an eccentric one (de Val-Borro et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats31b-deValBorroetal2016","cite":"de Val-Borro et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"M. de Val-Borro, G. Á. Bakos, R. Brahm et al.","title":"HATS-31b through HATS-35b: Five Transiting Hot Jupiters Discovered By the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00006","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-178 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the best ultrahot Jupiters to study from the Southern Hemisphere (Hellier et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet orbits an A1 star at 9350 K, the second-hottest star known to host a transiting planet, and is bloated to 1.81 Jupiter radii with an estimated temperature of 2470 K (Hellier et al. 2019). Hubble data show an enormous rise in absorption in the near-ultraviolet, driven by some mix of ionised iron, magnesium and silicon monoxide (Lothringer et al. 2025). Hubble and JWST also found water and carbon monoxide, with water mostly broken apart by heat (Lothringer et al. 2025). ESPRESSO spectra revealed sodium, hydrogen, magnesium, neutral iron and ionised iron (Damasceno et al. 2024). Dayside spectra gave the first definite detection of neutral iron on the planet's day side (Bell et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"The mix of elements suggests solar to supersolar oxygen and silicon but subsolar carbon, perhaps from rocky material added near the water ice line (Lothringer et al. 2025).","open":"The amount of each element depends strongly on the data and model used, so the planet's composition is not settled (Lothringer et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp178b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"WASP-South hot Jupiters: WASP-178b, WASP-184b, WASP-185b, and WASP-192b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11667","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp178b-Lothringeretal2025","cite":"Lothringer et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Joshua D. Lothringer, Katherine A. Bennett, David K. Sing et al.","title":"Refractory and Volatile Species in the UV-to-IR Transmission Spectrum of Ultra-hot Jupiter WASP-178b with HST and JWST","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.15472","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp178b-Damascenoetal2024","cite":"Damasceno et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Y. C. Damasceno, J. V. Seidel, B. Prinoth et al.","title":"The atmospheric composition of the ultra-hot Jupiter WASP-178 b observed with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08348","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp178b-Belletal2026","cite":"Bell et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caitlin Bell, Emily Deibert, Adam Langeveld et al.","title":"High-Resolution Dayside Spectroscopy of Ultra-Hot Jupiter WASP-178b with GHOST/Gemini South","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 37124 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star and the stability of the system. Computer experiments on the orbits fitted to the radial velocity data found that the system sits well inside large zones of stability, so the planets can survive for a long time (Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002). A later re-analysis of the radial velocities searched for stable orbit sets that reproduce the signals (Goździewski et al. 2006).","system":"The host star is one of two nicknamed 'red stragglers', whose position in the brightness-and-colour diagram seemed at odds with their high metal content, until stellar models reproduced it (Fernandes & Santos 2004).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd37124d-KiselevaEggletonetal2002","cite":"Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ludmila Kiseleva-Eggleton, Eric Bois, Nicolas Rambaux et al.","title":"Global Dynamics and Stability Limits for Planetary Systems around HD 12661, HD 38529, HD 37124, and HD 160691","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207367","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd37124d-FernandesSantos2004","cite":"Fernandes & Santos 2004","year":"2004","authors":"J. Fernandes, N. C. Santos","title":"Detailed theoretical models for extra-solar planet-host stars. The ``red stragglers'' HD 37124 and HD 46375","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408182","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3757 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets around red dwarfs are rare, and this one is the lowest-density transiting planet known around such a star (Kanodia et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-3757 b is a Jupiter-sized planet of 12.0 Earth radii and 85.3 Earth masses, with a density of only 0.27 grams per cubic centimetre (Kanodia et al. 2022). Its host is an M0 dwarf with roughly solar metal content, and about ten giant planets are known around such stars (Kanodia et al. 2022). Its low density and large atmosphere make it a good target for transmission spectroscopy, which reads starlight filtered through the atmosphere (Kanodia et al. 2022). The orbit is slightly eccentric, with e = 0.14 (Kanodia et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Two ideas explain the low density: the star's metal content, about 0.3 dex lower than the median for M dwarfs with giant planets, may have delayed the growth of a heavy core, or tidal heating from the eccentric orbit may inflate the planet (Kanodia et al. 2022).","open":"Which of the two ideas holds is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3757b-Kanodiaetal2022","cite":"Kanodia et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Shubham Kanodia, Jessica Libby-Roberts, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"TOI-3757 b: A Low-density Gas Giant Orbiting a Solar-metallicity M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.07178","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-1783.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was observed in infrared light with a ground-based camera on the Hale 200 inch telescope at Palomar, using an engineered diffuser that spreads the light to reduce noise (Vissapragada et al. 2020). The observations matched or beat the precision of the Spitzer space telescope, and the transit was detected at more than 3 sigma (Vissapragada et al. 2020). Combined with earlier data, the new transit confirmed the planetary nature of the signal (Vissapragada et al. 2020). The Kepler transits are shifted by pulls from another planet, and TESS recovered further transits of this system (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2022).","system":"KOI-1783 is one of four systems in which planets tug on each other's orbits enough to shift their transit times (Vissapragada et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi178301-Vissapragadaetal2020","cite":"Vissapragada et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Shreyas Vissapragada, Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"Diffuser-assisted Infrared Transit Photometry for Four Dynamically Interacting Kepler Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.04445","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi178301-JontofHutteretal2022","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Paul A. Dalba, John H. Livingston","title":"TESS Observations of Kepler Systems with Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6019 b":{"text":{"why":"Warm Jupiters, with periods of 10 to 200 days, show a wide range of orbit shapes and system layouts, which hints at several ways they form and migrate (Thomas et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-6019 b has a radius of 12.3 Earth radii and a mass of 149 Earth masses, and its 14.5-day orbit is strongly eccentric, with e = 0.48 (Thomas et al. 2026). The star is a slightly evolved, solar-metallicity sub-giant, a star beginning to swell as it ages (Thomas et al. 2026). The mass came from radial velocities measured with the MaHPS spectrograph at Wendelstein Observatory (Thomas et al. 2026). Interior models indicate that tidal heating likely contributes to the mildly inflated radius (Thomas et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6019b-Thomasetal2026","cite":"Thomas et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Luis Thomas, Louise D. Nielsen, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"TOI-2147 b and TOI-6019 b: Two eccentric warm Jupiters detected and characterized with TESS and MaHPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.20224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2010-BLG-328L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The best fit gives a planet of 9.2 Earth masses orbiting a very late M dwarf of 0.11 solar masses, about 0.81 kiloparsecs away, at a projected separation of 0.92 AU (Furusawa et al. 2013). Its host would be unusually close for a lensing star, so the team checked whether the signal that fixes the host's mass and distance might instead come from the source star's own orbital motion (Furusawa et al. 2013). The result favours the first, nearby-host solution (Furusawa et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"Future high-resolution measurements of the star's position could decide between the two solutions (Furusawa et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2010blg328lb-Furusawaetal2013","cite":"Furusawa et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"K. Furusawa, A. Udalski, T. Sumi et al.","title":"MOA-2010-BLG-328Lb: A Sub-Neptune Orbiting very Late M Dwarf?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.7714","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-266 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet on a 0.66-day orbit. The team reported a compact system of six planets around a nearby K star, the most planets found in one system by the K2 mission (Rodriguez et al. 2018). The 0.66-day super-Earth is tilted by more than 12 degrees from the orbits of the other five, which is a rare chance to compare a misaligned planet with its neighbours (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","system":"A transiting planet candidate was also found around a nearby companion star that moves through space with K2-266 (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"How the inner planet reached its tilted orbit is a question the system may help answer (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2266c-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Juliette Becker, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"K2-266: A Compact Multi-Planet System With A Significantly Misaligned Ultra Short Period Planet","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 8799 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HR 8799 e is the innermost of four giant planets directly imaged around the star. Near-infrared colours of the planets differ from those of brown dwarfs, and thick cloud layers match them far better (Currie et al. 2011). Temperatures of 1000 to 1200 K were inferred for planets c, d and e (Currie et al. 2011).","system":"Planet e cannot be in a circular, coplanar 4:2:1 resonance with planets c and d, but a 5:2 resonance is allowed (Esposito et al. 2013). The system is highly unstable if the planet masses are about 5 and 7 Jupiter masses (Esposito et al. 2013). The star is metal-poor and has excess infrared light from dust (Gray & Kaye 1999). Inner and outer belts of small bodies feed the planets with a steady rain of impacts (Frantseva et al. 2020).","history":"The outermost planet may have formed by fragmentation of the disc, which would require about 0.1 solar masses of disc inside its orbit (Nero & Bjorkman 2009).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr8799e-Currieetal2011","cite":"Currie et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Thayne Currie, Adam Burrows, Yoichi Itoh et al.","title":"A Combined Subaru/VLT/MMT 1-5 μm Study of Planets Orbiting HR 8799: Implications for Atmospheric Properties, Masses, and Formation","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.1973","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799e-Espositoetal2013","cite":"Esposito et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"S. Esposito, D. Mesa, A. Skemer et al.","title":"LBT observations of the HR 8799 planetary system. First detection of HR 8799e in H band","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.2735","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799e-GrayKaye1999","cite":"Gray & Kaye 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Richard O. Gray, Anthony B. Kaye","title":"HR 8799: A Link between γ Doradus Variables and λ Bootis Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/301134","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799e-Frantsevaetal2020","cite":"Frantseva et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Frantseva, M. Mueller, P. Pokorný et al.","title":"Enrichment of the HR 8799 planets by minor bodies and dust","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13562","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hr8799e-NeroBjorkman2009","cite":"Nero & Bjorkman 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Nero, J. E. Bjorkman","title":"Did Fomalhaut, HR 8799, and HL Tauri Form Planets Via the Gravitational Instability? Placing Limits on the Required Disk Masses","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1108","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1972 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The Kepler database listed the 11.32-day signal of this star as a false positive flagged 'not transit-like' (Leleu et al. 2022). Researchers applied a method called RIVERS to the star KIC 4725681, which had three Kepler candidates with periods of 7.54, 4.02 and 11.32 days, using the timing of the transits (Leleu et al. 2022). Timing variations are small shifts in when transits happen, caused by planets tugging on each other.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1972c-Leleuetal2022","cite":"Leleu et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"A. Leleu, J.-B. Delisle, R. Mardling et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Kepler-1972 transit timing variations (Leleu+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36610141","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-199 b":{"text":{"why":"It is the first warm exo-Saturn with a precisely measured mass and radius (Hobson et al. 2023).","observed":"TOI-199 b has a 104.854-day period, a mass of 0.17 Jupiters and a radius of 0.810 Jupiters (Hobson et al. 2023). A full 6.5-hour transit was caught by the ASTEP telescope in Antarctica, and the transits show strong timing variations (Hobson et al. 2023). Its transit light curve is consistent with a spherical planet, so any oblateness, a flattening from spin, is not detected (Lammers & Winn 2026). JWST observations were planned to measure its oblateness and atmosphere together (Acuna et al. 2025).","system":"The timing variations point to a second, non-transiting planet, TOI-199 c, with a 273.69-day period and a mass of 0.28 Jupiters, within the conservative habitable zone (Hobson et al. 2023).","history":"Warm Jupiters with large obliquities, meaning tilted spin axes, would point to scattering or migration, while low obliquities fit formation in place (Lammers & Winn 2026).","open":"Whether the planet has a diluted core, with heavy elements mixed into the envelope as in Jupiter and Saturn, is not known (Acuna et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi199b-Hobsonetal2023","cite":"Hobson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Melissa J. Hobson, Trifon Trifonov, Thomas Henning et al.","title":"TOI-199 b: A Well-characterized 100 day Transiting Warm Giant Planet with TTVs Seen from Antarctica","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14915","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi199b-LammersWinn2026","cite":"Lammers & Winn 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Caleb Lammers, Joshua N. Winn","title":"Constraining the Planetary Obliquity Distribution of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01599","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi199b-Acunaetal2025","cite":"Acuna et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Lorena Acuna, David Berardo, Juan Ignacio Espinoza-Retamal et al.","title":"All you need is Love numbers: simultaneous measurement of the oblateness and atmospheric composition of a warm gas giant","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-155 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-155 is a bright early M dwarf, a small cool red star, that the K2 mission watched during its Campaign 13. Follow-up included speckle imaging, which looks for hidden nearby stars, and high-resolution optical spectroscopy with radial velocity measurements (Hirano et al. 2018). Together with the K2 light curve, the data gave this inner planet a radius of about 1.55 Earth radii (Hirano et al. 2018). The star is metal-poor, meaning it has few elements heavier than helium (Hirano et al. 2018).","system":"Three super-Earths transit the star. The middle planet has a radius of about 1.95 Earth radii and a 13.85-day orbit (Hirano et al. 2018). The outer planet, K2-155 d, has a 40.7-day orbit and receives about 1.67 times Earth's sunlight, which puts it near the habitable zone (Hirano et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Climate simulations of the outer planet assumed it is tidally locked and has an Earth-like air and composition (Hirano et al. 2018). Its radius sits between those of small rocky planets and larger gas-rich ones, so its makeup is not settled (Hirano et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2155b-Hiranoetal2018","cite":"Hirano et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Fei Dai, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"K2-155: A Bright Metal-poor M Dwarf with Three Transiting Super-Earths","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06957","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 17092 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was monitored with precise radial velocity measurements, which track the small back-and-forth motion a planet causes in its star. The data set holds 69 measurements spread over 2014 days, about 5.5 years, ten more than were used in the first analysis of the planet (Adamow et al. 2023). The star's velocity swings with an amplitude of 108.15 m/s, and the typical uncertainty of one measurement is 5.09 m/s (Adamow et al. 2023). Little else about the planet is reported beyond this velocity curve.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd17092b-Adamowetal2023","cite":"Adamow et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Adamow, A. T. Niedzielski, A. Wolszczan et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 17092 radial velocity curve (Adamow+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-41 b":{"text":{"why":"Follow-up of the star was meant to explore links between hot Jupiter properties and how magnetically active their host stars are (Maxted et al. 2011).","observed":"WASP-41 is a G8 dwarf with moderate magnetic activity, seen both in emission from calcium in its atmosphere and in a roughly 1 percent brightness wobble every 18.4 days (Maxted et al. 2011). Later transit observations caught the planet passing in front of dark starspots, which show up as small brightness bumps during the transit (Southworth et al. 2016). Two such bumps seen 3.1 days apart are very likely the same spot, which allowed a stellar rotation period of 18.6 days and a sky-projected orbital tilt of 6 ± 11 degrees (Southworth et al. 2016). No variations in transit timing were seen (Southworth et al. 2016).","system":"The star's spin gives it an age of about 1.8 billion years (Maxted et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"The planet's radius is larger than models of gas giants predict (Southworth et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp41b-Maxtedetal2011","cite":"Maxted et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-41b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter Planet Orbiting a Magnetically Active G8V Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.2977","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp41b-Southworthetal2016","cite":"Southworth et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"John Southworth, J. Tregloan-Reed, M. I. Andersen et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VIII. WASP-22, WASP-41, WASP-42 and WASP-55","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6016 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the first confirmed with the MaHPS spectrograph, which can detect planets around stars as faint as V = 12 (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","observed":"Confirming the planet took spectra from the MaHPS spectrograph on the 2.1 m telescope at Wendelstein Observatory, together with the NEID and TRES instruments, which measured its mass (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). TOI-6016 b was confirmed alongside three other hot Jupiters, and all four have orbits of 2.4 to 4.0 days (Ehrhardt et al. 2024). The four planets span 1.29 to 1.64 Jupiter radii, and all fit current models of radius inflation, the puffing-up of hot giant planets (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","system":"A search for further planets in the system found none (Ehrhardt et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6016b-Ehrhardtetal2024","cite":"Ehrhardt et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Juliana Ehrhardt, Luis Thomas, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"Confirmation of four hot Jupiters detected by TESS using follow-up spectroscopy from MaHPS at Wendelstein together with NEID and TRES","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04383","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 135625 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was identified in archival spectra from the HARPS instrument, combined with measurements of how the star's motion across the sky changed between the Hipparcos and Gaia satellites (Philipot et al. 2023). A sky-motion change of this kind can signal an unseen companion. Fitting both kinds of data together gave the orbit and mass (Philipot et al. 2023). It is one of six new planets reported in the same study, with separations of 1 to 6 au and masses of 0.6 to 100 Jupiter masses across the whole group of new companions (Philipot et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd135625b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, F. Kiefer et al.","title":"A multi-technique approach to identifying and/or constraining radial-velocity substellar companions","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05417","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-167 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer giant planet Kepler-167 e. Kepler-167 is the only system known to hold both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed transiting gas giant beyond 1 au (Chachan et al. 2022). Long-term radial velocity monitoring gave the giant a mass of about 1.01 Jupiter masses on a 1071-day orbit, making it a Jupiter analogue (Chachan et al. 2022). Refitting the Kepler data gave updated radii for all four planets (Chachan et al. 2022).","system":"Planetary structure models suggest the outer giant holds 66 ± 19 Earth masses of heavy elements, far more than a solar-metallicity star would suggest (Chachan et al. 2022).","history":"Simple disc models show that massive, metal-rich discs, the best places to build giant planets, can also deliver enough solid material to the inner disc to form super-Earths (Chachan et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler167b-Chachanetal2022","cite":"Chachan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yayaati Chachan, Paul A. Dalba, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-167e as a Probe of the Formation Histories of Cold Giants with Inner Super-Earths","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00747","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-763 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-763 c is one of two planets seen crossing the star in TESS data from Sector 10, and radial velocity measurements from HARPS confirmed both (Fridlund et al. 2020). The measurements gave masses, radii and densities for both planets (Fridlund et al. 2020). It has a mass of about 9.3 Earth masses and an orbit of 12.3 days (Fridlund et al. 2020). The host is a high-proper-motion G-type dwarf slightly smaller and somewhat older than the Sun, with similar metal content (Fridlund et al. 2020).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-763 b, has a 5.6-day orbit and a mass of about 9.8 Earth masses (Fridlund et al. 2020). A third planet candidate turned up by chance in the radial velocity data, with an orbit near 48 days (Fridlund et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"The 48-day candidate has not been confirmed as a planet (Fridlund et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi763c-Fridlundetal2020","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"M. Fridlund, J. Livingston, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"The TOI-763 system: sub-Neptunes orbiting a Sun-like star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.12535","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6894 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is the smallest star known to have a transiting giant planet, and the planet blocks about 17 percent of its light during transit (Zhang et al. 2025). Its equilibrium temperature is about 410 K (Zhang et al. 2025). A JWST observing programme was designed to capture one transit with the NIRSpec PRISM instrument across 0.7 to 5.2 microns (Zhang et al. 2025). It is expected to show absorption from ammonia, methane and water (Zhang et al. 2025). The long ingress and egress, the slow start and end of the transit, may allow the two limbs of the planet to be studied separately (Zhang et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Measured metal content and ratios of carbon, oxygen and nitrogen could show whether the planet formed like a failed star, a Y dwarf analogue, or by core accretion that enriched it in heavy elements (Zhang et al. 2025).","open":"It is not known whether methane is depleted at these cool temperatures, as is often seen on hotter planets (Zhang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6894b-Zhangetal2025","cite":"Zhang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Zhang, Jacob L. Bean, Madison Brady et al.","title":"Ammonia and methane on a temperate giant planet orbiting a late M dwarf","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-0171L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in a high-magnification event flagged by HighMagFinder, a system that alerts observers to such events using live data from the KMTNet telescopes, so follow-up could be done more uniformly (Yang et al. 2022).","observed":"The signal comes from a very small planet-to-star mass ratio, about 4.7 × 10^-5 or 2.2 × 10^-5 depending on which solution is correct (Yang et al. 2022). The two solutions are hard to tell apart because of a known confusion, the central-resonant caustic degeneracy, in which different lens geometries produce similar light curves (Yang et al. 2022). Four cases with this confusion were found in the 2021 season alone, so such solutions may have been missed in earlier studies (Yang et al. 2022). It is one of few planets with a mass ratio below 10^-4 found through a high-magnification signal (Zhang et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Which of the two mass ratios is right is not settled (Yang et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0171lb-Yangetal2022","cite":"Yang et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Hongjing Yang, Weicheng Zang, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-0171Lb and KMT-2021-BLG-1689Lb: two microlensing planets in the KMTNet high-cadence fields with followup observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12584","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg0171lb-Zhangetal2023","cite":"Zhang et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jiyuan Zhang, Weicheng Zang, Youn Kil Jung et al.","title":"KMT-2022-BLG-0440Lb: A new q < 10<SUP>-4</SUP> microlensing planet with the central-resonant caustic degeneracy broken","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06779","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7189 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the Searching for GEMS survey, which looks for giant planets around early M dwarfs, giving a controlled comparison of outcomes around low-mass stars (Premnath et al. 2026).","observed":"TESS and ground-based photometry were combined with precise radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID spectrographs to fit the orbit and physical properties (Premnath et al. 2026). TOI-7189 b has a mass of 0.50 Jupiter masses and is Saturn-like in mass and density (Premnath et al. 2026). Across the four planets reported together, radii fall between 0.95 and 1.02 Jupiter radii (Premnath et al. 2026). Its host is an early M dwarf with higher metal content than the Sun (Premnath et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7189b-Premnathetal2026","cite":"Premnath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Pranav H. Premnath, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Three Warm Saturns and a Super-Jupiter Orbiting Four Early M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27064","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-163 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from the Euler/CORALIE spectrograph gave the planet's mass, and transit light curves from the TRAPPIST, SPECULOOS, NITES and Euler telescopes gave its size (Barkaoui et al. 2019). It is about 1.9 Jupiter masses and 1.2 Jupiter radii, and its host is a G8 dwarf with V = 12.5 (Barkaoui et al. 2019). Given its mass and the starlight it receives, the planet's size agrees well with classical models of irradiated giant planets (Barkaoui et al. 2019). It was announced together with two other new hot Jupiters, WASP-161 b and WASP-170 b (Barkaoui et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp163b-Barkaouietal2019","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"K. Barkaoui, A. Burdanov, C. Hellier et al.","title":"Discovery of Three New Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-161 b, WASP-163 b, and WASP-170 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06548","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-1077L c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The brightening event showed a subtle, complex anomaly around its peak that ordinary models with one lens star and one planet, or a double source star, could not explain (Han et al. 2022). A model with both lens and source as double systems still left noticeable residuals (Han et al. 2022). Only a triple-lens model, with one star and two companions, explained all the major features (Han et al. 2022). The companions have mass ratios to the star of about 1.56 × 10^-3 and 1.75 × 10^-3, about 1.6 and 1.8 times the Jupiter-to-Sun ratio (Han et al. 2022).","system":"The star hosts two giant planets, making it the fifth confirmed multiplanet system found by microlensing (Han et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg1077lc-Hanetal2022","cite":"Han et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrew Gould, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-1077L: The fifth confirmed multiplanetary system detected by microlensing","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16734","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1437 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star lies in TESS's northern Continuous Viewing Zone, which the satellite has watched exhaustively, including Sectors 14 to 26 and many more (Pidhorodetska et al. 2025). Radial velocities came from the HIRES spectrograph at Keck, with 88 nights of data, and from the Levy Spectrograph on the Automated Planet Finder telescope (Pidhorodetska et al. 2025). The planet is a sub-Neptune with a radius of about 2.24 Earth radii and a mass of 9.6 ± 3.9 Earth masses (Pidhorodetska et al. 2024). It is one of about 50 known transiting sub-Neptunes around a solar-mass star with a radial velocity mass (Pidhorodetska et al. 2024). The host is a near-solar analogue (Pidhorodetska et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"How worlds of this kind form is still an open question, and a precise mass may help answer it (Pidhorodetska et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1437b-Pidhorodetskaetal2025","cite":"Pidhorodetska et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Pidhorodetska, E. A. Gilbert, S. R. Kane et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: The TESS-Keck survey. XXII. TOI-1437 (Pidhorodetska+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1437b-Pidhorodetskaetal2024","cite":"Pidhorodetska et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daria Pidhorodetska, Emily A. Gilbert, Stephen R. Kane et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. XXII. A Sub-Neptune Orbiting TOI-1437","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5261 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from the Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP) survey, which aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters around stars brighter than G = 12.5, to measure how often different migration routes occur (Schulte et al. 2025).","observed":"TOI-5261 b is one of four new super-Jupiters reported together, each more than five times Jupiter's mass (Schulte et al. 2025). Its mass is 11.49 Jupiter masses, nearly enough to ignite deuterium fusion, the burning that marks the start of brown dwarfs (Schulte et al. 2025). Its orbit is eccentric, with e = 0.1585, meaning it is slightly stretched rather than circular (Schulte et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Hot Jupiters were the first exoplanets found around Sun-like stars, yet how they form and evolve is not known (Schulte et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5261b-Schulteetal2025","cite":"Schulte et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). II. Super-Jupiters and Lithium-rich host stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02666","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-244 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was characterised with both TESS and the CHEOPS space telescope, which gives high-precision transit measurements (Oddo et al. 2023). Separate work with 57 radial velocity measurements from the ESPRESSO spectrograph, plus TESS and HARPS data, found a radius of 1.52 ± 0.12 Earth radii and a mass of 2.68 ± 0.30 Earth masses (Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023). Its density, 4.2 ± 1.1 g/cm^3, is below what an Earth-like mix of iron and rock would give (Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023). Atmospheric loss may have stripped away any hydrogen envelope it once had (Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023).","system":"The host, GJ 1018, is a bright, nearby M2.5 dwarf about 22 parsecs away (Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Small planets of unusually low density, like this one, are a new group not matching the usual iron-and-rock composition (Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi244b-Oddoetal2023","cite":"Oddo et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Dominic Oddo, Diana Dragomir, Alexis Brandeker et al.","title":"Characterization of a Set of Small Planets with TESS and CHEOPS and an Analysis of Photometric Performance","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08162","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi244b-CastroGonzalezetal2023","cite":"Castro-Gonzalez et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Castro-González, O. D. S. Demangeon, J. Lillo-Box et al.","title":"An unusually low-density super-Earth transiting the bright early-type M-dwarf GJ 1018 (TOI-244)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.04922","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4010 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the whole group of planets, which were confirmed with radial velocities from the HARPS-N spectrograph (Kunimoto et al. 2023). The masses of the three transiting planets were measured to 8 to 12 percent precision (Kunimoto et al. 2023). TOI-4010 d is a sub-Saturn with a radius of about 6.18 Earth radii and a mass of about 38 Earth masses (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a metal-rich K dwarf (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Planet b is a sub-Neptune on a 1.3-day orbit inside the hot-Neptune desert, a region where such planets are rare, and planet c is a similar sub-Saturn on a 5.4-day orbit (Kunimoto et al. 2023). A super-Jupiter-mass companion, TOI-4010 e, circles on a long, eccentric orbit of about 762 days (Kunimoto et al. 2023). Few known systems hold multiple short-period sub-Saturns (Kunimoto et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4010d-Kunimotoetal2023","cite":"Kunimoto et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Michelle Kunimoto, Andrew Vanderburg, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TOI-4010: A System of Three Large Short-period Planets with a Massive Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05308","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-446 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-446 is a mid-M dwarf with three short-period transiting planets, probably rocky (Muirhead et al. 2015). The planets were first assigned wrongly large planet-to-star radius ratios, and the fix was to impose a strict prior on stellar density, which removes correlations that arise for very short transits (Muirhead et al. 2015). The star is metal-poor with a large galactic space velocity, much like Kepler-42 (Muirhead et al. 2015).","system":"Kepler-446 and its neighbours Kepler-445 and Kepler-42 suggest that about 21 percent of mid-M dwarf stars host compact multiples, systems of several small planets in tight orbits (Muirhead et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler446d-Muirheadetal2015","cite":"Muirhead et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Philip S. Muirhead, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"Kepler-445, Kepler-446 and the Occurrence of Compact Multiples Orbiting Mid-M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 128311 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star was observed with the High Resolution Spectrograph on the Hobby-Eberly Telescope at McDonald Observatory, using an iodine cell to measure tiny velocity shifts (McArthur et al. 2017). A total of 355 spectra were collected between 2005 April and 2011 January, at 161 separate epochs (McArthur et al. 2017). Hubble Space Telescope astrometry, which tracks the star's position on the sky, was added with 29 orbits of observations between 2007 December and 2009 August (McArthur et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd128311b-McArthuretal2017","cite":"McArthur et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"B. E. McArthur, G. F. Benedict, G. W. Henry et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 128311 radial velocity and astrometric data (McArthur+, 2014)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.17950041","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-6 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters around fast-spinning stars are rare, so the system gives a rare test of how close-in giant planets reach their orbits (Crouzet et al. 2015).","observed":"Follow-up observations of XO-6 b included Rossiter-McLaughlin measurements, which show how the planet's orbit is tilted relative to the spin of its star by tracking distortions in the starlight as the planet crosses (Crouzet et al. 2015). The host star spins fast. Its rotation period is under 1.41 days, shorter than the planet's 3.77-day orbit, which is true of very few hot Jupiter systems (Crouzet et al. 2015). A later search in TESS data found no additional planets in the system down to the size of sub-Neptunes (Maciejewski 2022).","system":"No other planets were found in the system, down to sub-Neptune size (Maciejewski 2022).","history":"The absence of other planets agrees with statistical studies that favour high-eccentricity migration, in which a giant planet is flung onto a stretched orbit and then settles close to its star (Maciejewski 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo6b-Crouzetetal2015","cite":"Crouzet et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Nicolas Michael Crouzet, Peter McCullough, Pilar Montañés-Rodríguez et al.","title":"XO-6b: A transiting hot Jupiter around a fast rotating star","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo6b-Maciejewski2022","cite":"Maciejewski 2022","year":"2022","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. II. Constraints on Planetary Companions in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 166724 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets on long orbits are important for models of formation and migration, because few constraints exist beyond the ice line, the distance where water freezes (Marmier et al. 2013).","observed":"HD 166724 b was found in a long-running search for planets around Sun-like stars within 50 parsecs of the Sun in the southern sky, using the CORALIE spectrograph at La Silla Observatory since 1998 (Marmier et al. 2013). After more than 14 years of observing, the survey can now see planets with orbits as long as Jupiter's. Seven new planets were announced together, with periods of 2200 to 5500 days and masses of 1 to 10.5 times Jupiter's (Marmier et al. 2013). Five of the seven have very high orbital eccentricities, above 0.57 (Marmier et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd166724b-Marmieretal2013","cite":"Marmier et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Marmier, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XVII. New and updated long period and massive planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.6444","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2458 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star shows a short magnetic activity cycle of about 54 days (Šubjak et al. 2025). Other F stars with such short cycles spin faster than their ages would suggest (Šubjak et al. 2025). The star's spin axis is tilted only about 10.6 degrees from our line of sight, so the planet's orbit appears to pass over the star's poles (Šubjak et al. 2025; Subjak & Gandolfi 2025).","system":"","history":"A hot Jupiter forming in place, inside the orbit of this mini-Neptune, could explain both the star's fast spin and the polar orbit (Šubjak et al. 2025). Engulfment of a planet by the star is also proposed as the cause of the short activity cycle (Subjak & Gandolfi 2025).","open":"Whether a hot Jupiter ever existed in the system is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2458b-SubjakGandolfi2025","cite":"Subjak & Gandolfi 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ján Šubjak, Davide Gandolfi, Elisa Goffo et al.","title":"TOI-2458 b: A mini-Neptune consistent with in situ hot Jupiter formation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.17532","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-1257 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"KOI-1257 b was first flagged by Kepler as a candidate and then validated with radial-velocity measurements from the SOPHIE spectrograph (Santerne et al. 2014). Its orbit is very stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.772 (Santerne et al. 2014). The planet has a mass of 1.45 times Jupiter's and a radius of 0.94 times Jupiter's, giving a density of 2.1 g/cm3 (Santerne et al. 2014). Its equilibrium temperature is 511 K, though that value is only an estimate for a planet whose distance from its star changes a lot (Santerne et al. 2014).","system":"The planet orbits the main star of a metal-rich, relatively old binary. The two stars have masses of 0.99 and 0.70 times the Sun's (Santerne et al. 2014). The binary is constrained by modelling, and further observations are needed to confirm it (Santerne et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"The existence of the companion star still needs to be confirmed (Santerne et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi1257b-Santerneetal2014","cite":"Santerne et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"A. Santerne, G. Hébrard, M. Deleuil et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. XII. KOI-1257 b: a highly eccentric three-month period transiting exoplanet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.6172","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-107 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-107 c is on a list of ten planets that may be losing their atmospheres today, if a process called core-powered mass loss is what mostly shapes them (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). In that process, heat left over from the planet's formation slowly pushes its gas envelope into space. The models predict an escape rate of at least 10^7 grams per second for these planets, at 50 per cent confidence (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). The prediction rests on the published mass, radius and star properties, and has not been tested by direct observation of escaping gas.","system":"","history":"Core-powered mass loss is one explanation for the radius valley, the gap in sizes that separates super-Earths from sub-Neptunes (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler107c-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1685 b":{"text":{"why":"Ultra-short-period planets like this one let astronomers study both the dynamical history of planetary systems and how strongly irradiated planets evolve (Bluhm et al. 2021).","observed":"Early measurements of TOI-1685 b disagreed, so it could be read either as a hot bare rock or as a water world made of about 50 per cent water (Burt et al. 2024). Updated analysis, using more TESS data, ground-based photometry and radial velocities from three instruments, finds the planet smaller, lower in mass and denser than first reported, with a radius of 1.468 Earth radii (Burt et al. 2024). Its host star is very metal-rich (Burt et al. 2024). JWST observed the planet for about 19 hours with its NIRSpec instrument in February 2024, covering a secondary eclipse, when the planet passes behind its star (Luque et al. 2026). An earlier study also reported an Earth-like composition (Hirano et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is a bare hot rock or has a thick water-rich envelope has driven three separate JWST programmes, two assuming a water world and one a hot rocky planet (Burt et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1685b-Bluhmetal2021","cite":"Bluhm et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Bluhm, E. Pallé, K. Molaverdikhani et al.","title":"An ultra-short-period transiting super-Earth orbiting the M3 dwarf TOI-1685","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01016","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1685b-Burtetal2024","cite":"Burt et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jennifer A. Burt, Matthew J. Hooton, Eric E. Mamajek et al.","title":"TOI-1685 b Is a Hot Rocky Super-Earth: Updates to the Stellar and Planet Parameters of a Popular JWST Cycle 2 Target","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14895","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1685b-Luqueetal2026","cite":"Luque et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"R. Luque, B. P. Coy, Q. Xue et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-1685b transit spectra from JWST (Luque+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1685b-Hiranoetal2021","cite":"Hirano et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, John H. Livingston, Akihiko Fukui et al.","title":"Two Bright M Dwarfs Hosting Ultra-Short-Period Super-Earths with Earth-like Compositions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.12760","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-942 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour TOI-942 b. Both planets are Neptune-sized. The star is young, about 50 million years old, and relatively active, with a rotation period of 3.39 days (Carleo et al. 2021).","system":"Two Neptune-sized planets orbit a young K2.5 star, on orbits of 4.3 and 10.1 days (Wirth et al. 2021). The inner planet, TOI-942 b, has a prograde orbit, aligned with the star's spin, within a margin of tens of degrees (Wirth et al. 2021). Few young multi-planet systems have such a measurement (Wirth et al. 2021).","history":"Young systems like this one help to test theories of how planetary systems form and evolve (Carleo et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi942c-Carleoetal2021","cite":"Carleo et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"I. Carleo, S. Desidera, D. Nardiello et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXVIII. A pair of hot-Neptunes orbiting the young star TOI-942","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13795","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi942c-Wirthetal2021","cite":"Wirth et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christopher P. Wirth, George Zhou, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"TOI-942b: A Prograde Neptune in a 60 Myr Old Multi-transiting System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14968","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-11 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is bright enough that the system is a valuable target for atmosphere studies, and it is expected to become a benchmark for inflated planets (Pepper et al. 2017).","observed":"KELT-11 b is one of the most inflated planets known, with a density of 0.093 g/cm3 and an exceptionally large atmospheric scale height of 2763 km (Pepper et al. 2017). Scale height measures how thick the atmosphere's visible layer is. The expected atmospheric signal during transit is 5.6 per cent of the starlight (Pepper et al. 2017). Hubble data show water vapour and extra absorption at wavelengths longer than 1.5 micrometres, which could come from carbon-bearing molecules, with carbon dioxide detected whenever it was included in the models (Changeat et al. 2020). Hubble results depend strongly on how the data are processed, and different choices lead to different interpretations of the atmosphere (Edwards et al. 2024).","system":"The host star is a bright subgiant, an ageing star that has swollen to 2.72 times the Sun's radius (Pepper et al. 2017). It is the brightest known transiting planet host in the southern hemisphere by more than a magnitude (Pepper et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The exact composition of the atmosphere remains uncertain, because the carbon-bearing signals are weak (Changeat et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt11b-Pepperetal2017","cite":"Pepper et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Joshua Pepper, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"KELT-11b: A Highly Inflated Sub-Saturn Exoplanet Transiting the V = 8 Subgiant HD 93396","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.01755","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt11b-Changeatetal2020","cite":"Changeat et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Q. Changeat, B. Edwards, A. F. Al-Refaie et al.","title":"KELT-11 b: Abundances of Water and Constraints on Carbon-bearing Molecules from the Hubble Transmission Spectrum","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.01310","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt11b-Edwardsetal2024","cite":"Edwards et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Billy Edwards, Angelos Tsiaras, Quentin Changeat et al.","title":"On the difficulties of obtaining absolute transit depths with HST WFC3: KELT-11 b, an example","journal":"RAS Techniques and Instruments","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/rasti/rzae023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-201 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A planet on a 5.85-day orbit around this star was announced in 2023 under the name TOI-201 c, with a radius of 1.64 Earth radii, making it a super-Earth (Mireles et al. 2023). It was validated using TESS data together with follow-up observations (Mireles et al. 2023). The star is young and active (Mireles et al. 2023).","system":"The planet orbits interior to the warm Jupiter TOI-201 b (Mireles et al. 2023). The system adds to a growing list of warm Jupiters with nearby companions (Mireles et al. 2023).","history":"Systems like this one may reveal how warm Jupiter systems formed and evolved (Mireles et al. 2023).","open":"The planet's mass, orbit and composition are not yet fully characterised (Mireles et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi201d-Mirelesetal2023","cite":"Mireles et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ismael Mireles, Diana Dragomir, Katharine Hesse","title":"TOI-201 c and TOI-1670 d: two new planets in warm Jupiter systems discovered by TESS","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #242","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 187123 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star HD 187123 is metal rich, with an iron abundance of [Fe/H] = 0.16 (Gonzalez et al. 1999). It is nearly identical to 51 Pegasi, whose planet has a similar orbital period and mass (Gonzalez et al. 1999). Tidal theory limits the mass of HD 187123 b to below 0.59 Jupiter masses, because the planet's orbit has been tidally circularised and its star has not been spun up (Trilling 2000). A small search for brightness changes in the star, suspected to be caused by tides from the planet, found possible variability (Golovin et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet really causes the possible variability in the star is not known (Golovin et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd187123b-Gonzalezetal1999","cite":"Gonzalez et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, George Wallerstein, Steven H. Saar","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. IV. 14 Herculis, HD 187123, and HD 210277","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/311847","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd187123b-Trilling2000","cite":"Trilling 2000","year":"2000","authors":"D. E. Trilling","title":"Tidal Constraints on the Masses of Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/312746","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd187123b-Golovinetal2007","cite":"Golovin et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Alex V. Golovin, Viktoriia N. Krushevska, Yuliana G. Kuznyetsova","title":"Photometric Detection of Chromospheric Activity of Extrasolar Planets with Short Orbital Periods: HD 187123, HD 68988, HD168746","journal":"JENAM-2007, \"Our Non-Stable Universe\"","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4153 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up aims to build a complete sample of hot Jupiters around FGK stars brighter than Gaia magnitude 12.5, analysed in the same way, so that ideas about how these planets form can be tested statistically (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"TOI-4153 b was confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy, as one of nine new hot Jupiters from TESS (Schulte et al. 2024). The nine planets range in mass from 0.55 to 3.88 times Jupiter's and in radius from 0.967 to 1.438 times Jupiter's (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Three ideas compete for the origin of hot Jupiters: forming in place, migrating through the gas disc, and high-eccentricity tidal migration (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4153b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4153b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7393 b":{"text":{"why":"The Searching for GEMS survey targets giant planets around early M dwarfs, so that giant-planet outcomes can be compared around similar low-mass stars (Premnath et al. 2026).","observed":"TOI-7393 b was confirmed by combining TESS and ground-based photometry with precise radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID spectrographs (Premnath et al. 2026). Its mass of 0.61 Jupiter masses and Jupiter-like radius make it similar to Saturn in mass and density (Premnath et al. 2026). The host star, an early M dwarf, has an iron abundance of -0.35, the lowest of any GEMS host so far, and moves through the galaxy in a way that approaches the transition between the thin and thick disc (Premnath et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7393b-Premnathetal2026","cite":"Premnath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Pranav H. Premnath, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: Three Warm Saturns and a Super-Jupiter Orbiting Four Early M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27064","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-480 b":{"text":{"why":"The Small, Low-mass Oblique Planets Experiment (SLOPE) uses the Keck Planet Finder to measure obliquity, the angle between a star's spin and a planet's orbit, for planets smaller than Saturn (Handley et al. 2026).","observed":"The sky-projected obliquity of TOI-480 b was measured with the Keck Planet Finder spectrograph and is consistent with spin-orbit alignment (Handley et al. 2026). It is one of four planets measured in the first results of the survey (Handley et al. 2026). Obliquity helps reveal how planets migrate and evolve, and small planets reflect system dynamics better than giants because they hardly disturb their stars (Handley et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi480b-Handleyetal2026","cite":"Handley et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Luke B. Handley, Andrew W. Howard, Fei Dai et al.","title":"The KPF SLOPE Survey: Small, Compact Multiplanet Systems Appear Spin-Orbit Aligned","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23713","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-261.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-261.01 is the innermost of three mini-Neptunes around an F8 star, observed by TESS in Sectors 3 and 30 (Grziwa et al. 2022). Radial-velocity follow-up with the HARPS spectrograph confirmed the detection of this planet, with a period of 3.4 days (Grziwa et al. 2022). The three planets have masses between 8 and 11 Earth masses (Grziwa et al. 2022).","system":"The second candidate, TOI-261.02, was found to have a period of 10.2 days, not the 13 days first announced (Grziwa et al. 2022). A third, outer planet shows a single transit in Sector 3 and possibly one in Sector 30, and radial velocities fix its period at 22.8 days (Grziwa et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi26101-Grziwaetal2022","cite":"Grziwa et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Sascha Grziwa, Davide Gandolfi, Luisa Maria Serrano et al.","title":"One for all and all for one: the TESS-HARPS synergy unveils three mini-Neptunes transiting TOI-261","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"UZ For c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself. Work so far has studied the central binary, UZ Fornacis, a cataclysmic variable, in which a white dwarf pulls gas from a small companion star (Dai et al. 2010). Timing the eclipses of the white dwarf shows that the orbital period is growing (Dai et al. 2010). The eclipse times also show a possible cyclical change with a period of about 23.4 years (Dai et al. 2010).","system":"The light travel-time effect, in which a third body tugs the binary and shifts eclipse times, is one possible cause of the 23.4-year cycle (Dai et al. 2010). Magnetic activity cycles in the late-type companion star are the other probable cause (Dai et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"Whether a planet or a magnetic cycle causes the timing changes is not settled (Dai et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-uzforc-Daietal2010","cite":"Dai et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Z.-B. Dai, S.-B. Qian, E. Fernández Lajús et al.","title":"Orbital period analyses for two cataclysmic variables: UZ Fornacis and V348 Puppis inside the period gap","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.4070","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-700 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer neighbours TOI-700 d and e. TOI-700 is a nearby, quiet M2.5 dwarf, observed by TESS for 21 sectors over two years of the mission (Gilbert et al. 2023). Little is known about TOI-700 b itself beyond its transits.","system":"The system has four planets. TOI-700 e is a likely rocky planet of 0.95 Earth radii on a 27.8-day orbit inside the optimistic habitable zone, the range of distances where liquid water could possibly exist on a rocky surface (Gilbert et al. 2023). TOI-700 d is also a small habitable-zone planet (Gilbert et al. 2023). The star is bright enough for follow-up studies of masses and atmospheres (Gilbert et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi700b-Gilbertetal2023","cite":"Gilbert et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Emily A. Gilbert, Andrew Vanderburg, Joseph E. Rodriguez et al.","title":"A Second Earth-sized Planet in the Habitable Zone of the M Dwarf, TOI-700","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.03617","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2020-BLG-208L b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies near the peak of the mass-ratio function derived by the MOA collaboration, and near the edge of the survey's sensitivity (Olmschenk et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is a cool, sub-Saturn-mass world, and its mass ratio to its star is 3.17 times 0.0001 (Olmschenk et al. 2023). Its mass and its host star's depend on assumptions. If all stars are equally likely to host planets, the planet weighs 46 Earth masses around a 0.43 solar-mass star at 7.49 kiloparsecs (Olmschenk et al. 2023). If planets favour heavier stars, the planet weighs 69 Earth masses around a 0.66 solar-mass star at 7.81 kiloparsecs (Olmschenk et al. 2023). Its projected separation is 1.38 times the Einstein ring radius (Olmschenk et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet and star masses remain uncertain, because the two assumptions give different answers (Olmschenk et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2020blg208lb-Olmschenketal2023","cite":"Olmschenk et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Greg Olmschenk, David P. Bennett, Ian A. Bond et al.","title":"MOA-2020-BLG-208Lb: Cool Sub-Saturn-mass Planet within Predicted Desert","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02436","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-2 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet HAT-P-2 b, also named HD 147506 b. That planet is massive, at 9.04 times Jupiter's mass, with a radius close to Jupiter's and a density of about 12 g/cm3, denser than rocky planets such as the Earth (Bakos et al. 2007). Its mass and radius may require a large core of more than about 100 Earth masses (Bakos et al. 2007).","system":"The inner planet orbits a bright F8 star every 5.63 days, with an eccentricity of 0.520 (Bakos et al. 2007). Its eccentric orbit makes the heat it receives vary ninefold between closest and farthest points (Bakos et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp2c-Bakosetal2007","cite":"Bakos et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, G. Kovács, G. Torres et al.","title":"HD 147506b: A Supermassive Planet in an Eccentric Orbit Transiting a Bright Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0126","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1252 b":{"text":{"why":"The star's proximity and brightness, low activity and the planet's short orbit make it an attractive target for detailed study (Shporer et al. 2020).","observed":"Ground-based photometry, spectroscopy, Gaia astrometry and high-resolution imaging were used to rule out every false-positive explanation in which an eclipsing pair of stars mimics the transit (Shporer et al. 2020). Radial velocities gave a tentative mass of 2.09 Earth masses, later refined to 1.54 Earth masses with the NIRPS spectrograph (Shporer et al. 2020; Weisserman 2025). The planet's iron-rich core is smaller than predicted from its star's abundances, which is tentative evidence for water-rich formation (Weisserman 2025). It is likely airless, and models of rough regolith surfaces may help interpret its heat emission (Tenthoff et al. 2024).","system":"GJ 1252 is an M3 dwarf, and chemical and motion analysis flags it as a potential thick-disc star (Maas et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The surface of the planet largely remains mysterious (Tenthoff et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1252b-Shporeretal2020","cite":"Shporer et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Avi Shporer, Karen A. Collins, Nicola Astudillo-Defru et al.","title":"GJ 1252 b: A 1.2 R<SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet Transiting an M3 Dwarf at 20.4 pc","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05556","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1252b-Weisserman2025","cite":"Weisserman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Drew Weisserman","title":"Super-Earth Masses and Stellar Abundances from NIRPS Reveal Tentative Evidence for Water-Rich Formation around M Dwarfs","journal":"Masters Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1252b-Tenthoffetal2024","cite":"Tenthoff et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Moritz Tenthoff, Kay Wohlfarth, Christian Wöhler et al.","title":"Reflectance and Emission Modelling of Airless Exoplanets","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2024-649","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj1252b-Maasetal2026","cite":"Maas et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Zachary G Maas, Natalie R. Hinkel, Gregory N. Mace et al.","title":"The Composition of M-dwarf Exoplanet Hosts: Atmospheric Parameters and O, Mg, Si, and Fe Abundances from IR Spectroscopy","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07037","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-539 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-539 b was confirmed with radial velocities from the CAFE spectrograph, and the fit shows a dense, Jupiter-like planet of 0.97 Jupiter masses and 0.747 Jupiter radii (Mancini et al. 2016). Its orbit is circular, and the planet lies about 0.5 au from its star, around 0.45 au from the habitable zone (Mancini et al. 2016). Kepler recorded 12 of its transits, and their timings vary in a clear modulated way, revealing another planet in the system (Mancini et al. 2016).","system":"The varying transit times point to a second planet, Kepler-539 c, with a mass of 1.2 to 3.6 Jupiter masses on a very eccentric orbit (0.4 < e <= 0.6) longer than 1000 days (Mancini et al. 2016). The two giant planets interact gravitationally, and the star is young (Mancini et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"The properties of Kepler-539 c are poorly constrained, because few timings are available (Mancini et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler539b-Mancinietal2016","cite":"Mancini et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"L. Mancini, J. Lillo-Box, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Kepler-539: A young extrasolar system with two giant planets on wide orbits and in gravitational interaction","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04625","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-157 c":{"text":{"why":"Ultra-short-period rocky planets are widely thought to have moved inward through interactions with outer neighbours, so the survey looked for such companions (Castro-González et al. 2025).","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour K2-157 b. Astronomers measured 49 radial velocities (small back-and-forth shifts in the star's light) with the ESPRESSO spectrograph and tested several models of the signal (Castro-González et al. 2025). Planet c has a minimum mass of 30.8 Earth masses and sits in the warm Neptunian savanna, a band of orbits where such planets are found (Castro-González et al. 2025). Its true mass and size are not known.","system":"A second super-Neptune-mass planet, K2-157 d, orbits every 66.5 days with a minimum mass of 23.3 Earth masses (Castro-González et al. 2025). The innermost planet, K2-157 b, has a mass of about 1.14 Earth masses, measured at the 2.7 sigma level (Castro-González et al. 2025).","history":"How ultra-short-period rocky planets form is poorly understood (Castro-González et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2157c-CastroGonzlezetal2025","cite":"Castro-González et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. Castro-González, F. Bouchy, A. C. M. Correia et al.","title":"Two neighbours of the ultra-short-period Earth-sized planet K2-157 b in the warm Neptunian savanna","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20999","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TCP J05074264+2447555 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was revealed by a microlensing event first spotted by the amateur astronomer Tadashi Kojima, making it the first planet found in an event discovered by an amateur (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022). Its host is the brightest star of any planetary microlensing event (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022). It was also the first planetary event where the Einstein radius and relative proper motion were measured by interferometry, and the first to use the VLTI/GRAVITY instrument (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022). Keck aperture-masking images taken about two years after the event confirmed the GRAVITY results (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022). The mass ratio between planet and star is about 1 in 10,000, typical for microlensing planets (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Spectra of the bright host star are expected to be a future target (Rau & Kojima-1 Analysis Team 2022)."},"refs":[],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 161178 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. HD 161178 was one of 57 late-G giant stars watched in the first three years of the Okayama Planet Search Program, which looks for planets around intermediate-mass stars in their evolved stages (Sato et al. 2005). Its radial velocity varied by more than 30 metres per second, but not in an obviously periodic way (Sato et al. 2005). Most stars in the sample were much steadier than that (Sato et al. 2005).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd161178b-Satoetal2005","cite":"Sato et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eiji Kambe, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Radial-Velocity Variability of G-Type Giants: First Three Years of the Okayama Planet Search Program","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/57.1.97","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2266 b":{"text":{"why":"Validation relied on transit light curves taken in several colours at once, which give a firm upper limit on the size of the transiting object (Parviainen et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed as a planet without a mass measurement. Light curves in several colours from MuSCAT2, MuSCAT3 and HiPERCAM, plus TESS and LCOGT data, were used to rule out a larger object (Parviainen et al. 2024). The host is a faint M5 dwarf (Parviainen et al. 2024). The radius of 1.54 Earth radii puts the planet at the edge of the M dwarf radius valley, the transition zone between rocky planets, water-rich planets and sub-Neptunes (Parviainen et al. 2024). Its 2.33-day orbit also places it in a 'keystone planet' wedge of the period-radius plane (Parviainen et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"A mass measurement with red-sensitive spectrographs on large telescopes, such as MAROON-X and Keck Planet Finder, is possible and would help show whether the planet is rocky or watery (Parviainen et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2266b-Parviainenetal2024","cite":"Parviainen et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"H. Parviainen, F. Murgas, E. Esparza-Borges et al.","title":"TOI-2266 b: A keystone super-Earth at the edge of the M dwarf radius valley","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.11879","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 24064 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up targeted about 200 northern circumpolar stars, chosen for brightness and steady light (Lee et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet came from a radial velocity survey of northern stars near the celestial pole, run with the 1.8 m telescope at Bohyunsan Observatory and its BOES spectrograph (Lee et al. 2015). Monitoring began in 2010, and the stars were screened for other causes of velocity changes, such as stellar activity and line shapes (Lee et al. 2015). HD 24064 showed signs of a giant planet in Keplerian motion, meaning a regular orbit (Lee et al. 2015). It was announced together with planets around HD 11755, HD 12648 and 8 Ursae Minoris (Lee et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd24064b-Leeetal2015","cite":"Lee et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"B.-C. Lee, M.-G. Park, S.-M. Lee et al.","title":"Search for exoplanet around northern circumpolar stars. Four planets around HD 11755, HD 12648, HD 24064, and 8 Ursae Minoris","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.09012","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up with radial velocities, ground-based photometry and spectra showed the planet has a circular orbit, a radius of 1.19 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.72 Jupiter masses (Moutou et al. 2008). It is a gas giant with a normal internal structure made mainly of hydrogen and helium (Moutou et al. 2008). When found, it had the second longest period of any known transiting planet and filled an empty area in the mass-period diagram (Moutou et al. 2008). Dips in its transits show the planet passing in front of dark spots on the star, which can be used to measure the star's rotation (Valio 2017).","system":"The host is a late F-type star of 1.16 solar masses and 1.17 solar radii (Moutou et al. 2008). Its surface has three main active longitudes, regions of spots that last 30 to 60 days and rotate nearly in step with the planet's orbit (Lanza et al. 2009). Different rates among them point to differential rotation, with the equator spinning faster than higher latitudes (Lanza et al. 2009).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot4b-Moutouetal2008","cite":"Moutou et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"C. Moutou, H. Bruntt, T. Guillot et al.","title":"Transiting exoplanets from the CoRoT space mission. V. CoRoT-Exo-4b: stellar and planetary parameters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.3739","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot4b-Valio2017","cite":"Valio 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Adriana Valio","title":"Starspots properties and stellar activity from planetary transits","journal":"Living Around Active Stars","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S1743921317004094","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot4b-Lanzaetal2009","cite":"Lanza et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. F. Lanza, S. Aigrain, S. Messina et al.","title":"Photospheric activity and rotation of the planet-hosting star CoRoT-4a","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.4618","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-504 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet beyond its size and orbit. One study computed an Earth Similarity Index, a score of physical likeness to Earth, from available telescope data (Bhaumik & Sethi 2024). Kepler-504 b scored 71.23 per cent, high compared with the other planets in that study, and its host is an M-type star, a small red dwarf (Bhaumik & Sethi 2024). The score is a measure of resemblance, not evidence of life or of conditions at the surface (Bhaumik & Sethi 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler504b-BhaumikSethi2024","cite":"Bhaumik & Sethi 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Sattik Bhaumik, Geetanjali Sethi","title":"Studying habitability of the exoplanets Kepler-504 b, Kepler-315 b, and Kepler-315 c","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.06269","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-111 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star lies behind the Hyades cluster, at least four times farther away than the cluster, and its motion suggests it belongs to the old thick disc of the Galaxy (Fridlund et al. 2017). It is metal-poor and rich in alpha elements, and mildly evolved (Fridlund et al. 2017; Mortier et al. 2020). Radial velocities from HARPS-N and ESPRESSO give the planet a radius of 1.82 Earth radii and a mass of 5.29 Earth masses, a density slightly lower than Earth's (Mortier et al. 2020). The composition fits a terrestrial planet with a smaller iron core than Earth's (Mortier et al. 2020). A later CHEOPS-based study found a density that needs a steam-rich or mixed volatile envelope (Palethorpe et al. 2026).","system":"A second planet, K2-111 c, does not transit. It orbits every 15.68 days with a minimum mass of 11.3 Earth masses, close to a 3:1 resonance with planet b (Mortier et al. 2020).","history":"The system is very old (Mortier et al. 2020).","open":"Whether the planet is bare rock or carries a steam or volatile envelope is still debated (Mortier et al. 2020; Palethorpe et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2111b-Fridlundetal2017","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Malcolm Fridlund, Eric Gaidos, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"K2-111 b - a short period super-Earth transiting a metal poor, evolved old star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.08284","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2111b-Mortieretal2020","cite":"Mortier et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. Mortier, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, L. Malavolta et al.","title":"K2-111: an old system with two planets in near-resonance","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.01993","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2111b-Palethorpeetal2026","cite":"Palethorpe et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Larissa Palethorpe, Annelies Mortier, Jo Ann Egger et al.","title":"Constraining small planet compositions for future missions","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14552","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-757 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities from HARPS, ESPRESSO and PFS gave a mass of 10.5 Earth masses, and CHEOPS space photometry tightened the radius to 2.5 Earth radii (Alqasim et al. 2024). The bulk density is 3.6 grams per cubic centimetre (Alqasim et al. 2024). The orbit is highly eccentric, with e = 0.39, making the planet one of very few highly eccentric mini-Neptunes with precisely measured properties (Alqasim et al. 2024). The host is a bright star with V = 9.7 (Alqasim et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"Models of the interior could not pin down the composition, so atmospheric observations are important (Alqasim et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi757b-Alqasimetal2024","cite":"Alqasim et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Alqasim, N. Grieves, N. M. Rosário et al.","title":"TOI-757 b: an eccentric transiting mini-Neptune on a 17.5-d orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.20525","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-19 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler tomography, which maps the planet's shadow across the spinning star, shows the orbit is retrograde: the planet circles the star against the direction the star rotates, with a projected angle of about -180 degrees (Siverd et al. 2018). Radial velocities limit its mass to at most 4.1 Jupiter masses (Siverd et al. 2018). The planet is about 1.91 Jupiter radii wide, with an equilibrium temperature near 1935 K (Siverd et al. 2018). CHEOPS and TESS data later gave a significantly smaller radius, and ruled out long-term changes in transit duration from orbital precession (Garai et al. 2022).","system":"The host is a 7500 K A8V star that looks like an Am star, a type with odd metal abundances: rich in metals but low in calcium (Siverd et al. 2018). It rotates slowly compared with similar stars, at 84.8 km/s (Siverd et al. 2018). A distant stellar companion is also present (Siverd et al. 2018).","history":"The distant stellar companion may have helped push the planet inward through the Kozai-Lidov mechanism, in which a companion makes a planet's eccentricity and tilt oscillate; this is being tested on six hot Jupiters in binaries, KELT-19 among them (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt19ab-Siverdetal2018","cite":"Siverd et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Robert J. Siverd, Karen A. Collins, George Zhou et al.","title":"KELT-19Ab: A P ∼ 4.6-day Hot Jupiter Transiting a Likely Am Star with a Distant Stellar Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07010","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt19ab-Garaietal2022","cite":"Garai et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Z. Garai, T. Pribulla, J. Kovács et al.","title":"Rapidly rotating stars and their transiting planets: KELT-17b, KELT-19Ab, and KELT-21b in the CHEOPS and TESS era","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.09077","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt19ab-VazquezEastman2025","cite":"Vazquez & Eastman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacqueline Vazquez, Jason Eastman","title":"Understanding Hot Jupiter Migration for Six Binary Star Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"gam Cep b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Gamma Cephei is a bright star that showed regular velocity changes in the 1980s, which were suspected to be a Jupiter-mass companion (Walker et al. 1992). Eleven years of data gave a period of 2.52 years, but the star is a yellow giant, so its own rotation could also cause the signal (Walker et al. 1992). Both explanations stayed open for years. Studies of orbit stability found that a planet at this distance stays on a stable orbit when the binary's orbit is not too eccentric (Haghighipour 2006).","system":"The planet's host is a K1 red giant of about 1.3 solar masses and 4.7 solar radii (Tripathi & Roth 2025). It has a red-dwarf companion on a 66.8-year orbit (Tripathi & Roth 2025). A stable Earth-like planet could exist only at 0.3 to 0.8 au from the giant, and its habitable zone at about 3.05 to 3.7 au is unstable (Haghighipour 2006).","history":"Simulations suggest giant-planet cores could still form in a binary like this one (Turrini et al. 2005).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gamcepb-Walkeretal1992","cite":"Walker et al. 1992","year":"1992","authors":"Gordon A. H. Walker, David A. Bohlender, Andrew R. Walker et al.","title":"Gamma Cephei: Rotation or Planetary Companion?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/186524","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gamcepb-Haghighipour2006","cite":"Haghighipour 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Nader Haghighipour","title":"Dynamical Stability and Habitability of the γ Cephei Binary-Planetary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0509659","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gamcepb-TripathiRoth2025","cite":"Tripathi & Roth 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Aashana Tripathi, Markus Roth","title":"Asteroseismic Modelling of the Red Giant HD222404 (γ Cephei A)","journal":"TASC9/KASC16 9th TESS/16th Kepler Asteroseismic Science Consortium","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17287466","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gamcepb-Turrinietal2005","cite":"Turrini et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"D. Turrini, M. Barbieri, F. Marzari et al.","title":"Planetary Formation and Orbital Stability in Binary Star Systems","journal":"Memorie della Societa Astronomica Italiana Supplementi","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":23,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-2 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star's youth may matter. Models of the star point either to a very young system of 20 to 40 million years, or to a young main-sequence star of 40 to 500 million years (Guillot & Havel 2009).","observed":"The planet is among the most anomalously large transiting planets known: a radius near 1.5 Jupiter radii for a mass of 3.3 Jupiter masses, which standard evolution models and recipes that work for other puffy planets cannot explain (Guillot & Havel 2009). Extra internal energy of at least 3 x 10^28 erg/s is needed to keep it that large (Guillot & Havel 2009). Spitzer secondary-eclipse data are consistent with a carbon-rich atmosphere, with carbon-to-oxygen ratio near 1 (Moses et al. 2013). The orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, at a projected angle of 4.7 degrees (Nutzman et al. 2011).","system":"The host is an active, rapidly rotating star with 7 to 20 per cent of its surface covered in spots and a rotation period of 4.5 days (Guillot & Havel 2009). The planet's transits cross star spots, which allowed the alignment to be measured from the light curve alone (Nutzman et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"Tidal heating may inflate the planet, but the cause of its size is not settled (Guillot & Havel 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot2b-GuillotHavel2009","cite":"Guillot & Havel 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Tristan Guillot, M. Havel","title":"CoRoT-2b: a Tidally Inflated, Young Exoplanet?","journal":"AAS/Division for Planetary Sciences Meeting Abstracts #41","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot2b-Mosesetal2013","cite":"Moses et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"J. I. Moses, N. Madhusudhan, C. Visscher et al.","title":"Chemical Consequences of the C/O Ratio on Hot Jupiters: Examples from WASP-12b, CoRoT-2b, XO-1b, and HD 189733b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.2996","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot2b-Nutzmanetal2011","cite":"Nutzman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Philip A. Nutzman, Daniel C. Fabrycky, Jonathan J. Fortney","title":"Using Star Spots to Measure the Spin-orbit Alignment of Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.2106","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":31,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"BD+48 740 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a giant star with an unusually high lithium abundance, A(Li) = 2.33, a rare trait in giants (Adamów et al. 2012). Radial velocity changes fit a 1.6 Jupiter-mass companion on a highly eccentric orbit, with e = 0.67 and a distance of 1.89 au, a period of 771 days (Adamów et al. 2012). High eccentricity is uncommon among planets orbiting evolved stars (Adamów et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"A second, closer planet that the star swallowed could explain both the high lithium and the eccentric orbit (Adamów et al. 2012). If so, the system would be the first remnant of a multiple-planet system recently changed by stellar evolution (Adamów et al. 2012).","open":"The planet itself still needed confirmation by future radial velocity observations when it was reported (Adamów et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-bd48740b-Adamwetal2012","cite":"Adamów et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"M. Adamów, A. Niedzielski, E. Villaver et al.","title":"BD+48 740—Li Overabundant Giant Star with a Planet: A Case of Recent Engulfment?","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.4938","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star appears close to leaving the main sequence, or may already be doing so, which makes it interesting because hot Jupiters are rarely seen around sub-giant stars (Jackson et al. 2023). The planet has a mass of 0.52 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.21 Jupiter radii (Jackson et al. 2023). That radius is larger than non-inflated models expect, but smaller than current Bayesian inflation models predict (Jackson et al. 2023). The orbit is small enough, and the star luminous enough, that the planet receives more light than the threshold at which inflation mechanisms work (Jackson et al. 2023).","system":"The host is an F-type star (Jackson et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"The physical processes that make hot Jupiters swell are still poorly understood (Jackson et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts24b-Jacksonetal2023","cite":"Jackson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"David G. Jackson, Christopher A. Watson, Ernst J. W. de Mooij et al.","title":"The discovery of three hot Jupiters, NGTS-23b, 24b, and 25b, and updated parameters for HATS-54b from the Next Generation Transit Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.01044","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 180314 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The companion has a minimum mass of 22 Jupiter masses, which falls in the brown-dwarf regime, so it may not be a true planet (Sato et al. 2010). Its host is a K0 giant of 2.6 solar masses, and the companion orbits every 396 days with an eccentricity of 0.26 (Sato et al. 2010). When announced it was the seventh candidate brown-dwarf companion found around evolved intermediate-mass stars (Sato et al. 2010). The star has a relatively high lithium abundance, likely left over from its main-sequence phase (Zuloaga et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Giants with brown-dwarf companions seem on early evidence to be metal-poor (Zuloaga et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd180314b-Satoetal2010","cite":"Sato et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya, Yujuan Liu et al.","title":"Substellar Companions to Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: HD 145457 and HD 180314","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1005.2860","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd180314b-Zuloagaetal2023","cite":"Zuloaga et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"C. Zuloaga, E. Jofré, R. Petrucci et al.","title":"Espectroscopía Gemini-GRACES de estrellas evolucionadas con enanas marrones","journal":"Boletin de la Asociacion Argentina de Astronomia La Plata Argentina","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-2016-BLG-1190L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's mass, 13.4 Jupiter masses, sits right at the deuterium-burning limit, the usual boundary between planets and brown dwarfs (Ryu et al. 2018). It is likely the first Spitzer microlensing planet in the Galactic bulge or bar, and this can be confirmed with two epochs of high-resolution imaging of the source and lens together (Ryu et al. 2018). Data from Kepler's K2 Campaign 9 broke the degeneracy in the lens mass, the first successful use of that approach (Ryu et al. 2018). The host is a G dwarf of 0.89 solar masses, and the planet orbits at about 2.0 au (Ryu et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"Whether such objects form in the disc around a star like planets, or by gas fragmenting like failed stars, is a question the Spitzer program may answer by comparing disc and bulge planets (Ryu et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogle2016blg1190lb-Ryuetal2018","cite":"Ryu et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Y.-H. Ryu, J. C. Yee, A. Udalski et al.","title":"OGLE-2016-BLG-1190Lb: The First Spitzer Bulge Planet Lies Near the Planet/Brown-dwarf Boundary","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09974","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1246 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-1246 b is the innermost of four transiting sub-Neptunes around a moderately bright K dwarf. The mass came from 125 radial velocity observations with Keck/HIRES and HARPS-N (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Refitting 14 sectors of TESS light curves gave a radius of 2.97 Earth radii, and the planet has a mass of 8.3 Earth masses (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","system":"The four transiting planets orbit every 4.31, 5.90, 18.66 and 37.92 days, and the outermost, TOI-1246 e, is much more massive than the three inner planets, at 15.2 Earth masses (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The two outer planets lie just outside a 2:1 resonance and show transit timing variations (Turtelboom et al. 2022). The system is one of only six with measured masses and radii for all four transiting planets, and one of the brightest stars with four known transiting planets (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1246b-Turtelboometal2022","cite":"Turtelboom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emma V. Turtelboom, Courtney Dressing, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"TOI-1246: Mass Measurements for Four Transiting sub-Neptunes","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2196 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies in the hot Neptune desert, a region where short-period Neptune-size planets are scarce; highly irradiated planets are usually small and rocky or gas giants (Persson et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is a sub-Neptune on a 1.2-day orbit, with a radius of 3.51 Earth radii and a mass of 26.0 Earth masses from 41 HARPS radial velocities (Persson et al. 2022). The density is about twice Neptune's (Persson et al. 2022). Its equilibrium temperature is 1860 K (Persson et al. 2022). Amateur astronomers helped analyse the follow-up data through the TESS follow-up programme (Zambelli & Papini 2023).","system":"A trend in the HARPS velocities points to a distant companion, with a period of at least 220 days and a mass of at least 0.65 Jupiter masses (Persson et al. 2022).","history":"Depending on the star's activity in its youth, the planet may have lost up to 50 per cent of its original atmosphere and still kept much of it (Persson et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2196b-Perssonetal2022","cite":"Persson et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Carina M. Persson, Iskra Y. Georgieva, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"TOI-2196 b: Rare planet in the hot Neptune desert transiting a G-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.05797","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2196b-ZambelliPapini2023","cite":"Zambelli & Papini 2023","year":"2023","authors":"R. Zambelli, R. Papini","title":"TOI-2196 b: il contributo degli astrofili alla scoperta di nuovi pianeti extrasolari all'interno della missione spaziale TESS","journal":"Astronomia. La rivista dell' Unione Astrofili Italiani","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-452 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has the longest orbital period of any small transiting planet found up to its announcement, 384.843 days for a radius under 2 Earth radii (Jenkins et al. 2015). The transit lasts 10.5 hours (Kohler 2015). The chance that it is rocky is estimated at 49 to 62 per cent (Jenkins et al. 2015). It receives about 10 per cent more light than Earth does, so it is inside the optimistic habitable zone but slightly outside the conservative one (Jenkins et al. 2015). Climate models for a rocky version found habitable conditions mostly only for carbon dioxide pressure below about 0.04 bar (Silva et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a G2 star slightly larger and older than the Sun, at about 6 billion years old (Jenkins et al. 2015). The planet has probably always been in the habitable zone and should stay there for about 3 billion more years (Jenkins et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"The composition is unknown. Interior models for some habitable-zone planets, Kepler-452 b among them, suggest a very high water content (Boldog et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler452b-Jenkinsetal2015","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Jon M. Jenkins, Joseph D. Twicken, Natalie M. Batalha et al.","title":"Discovery and Validation of Kepler-452b: A 1.6 R <SUB>⨁</SUB> Super Earth Exoplanet in the Habitable Zone of a G2 Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.06723","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler452b-Kohler2015","cite":"Kohler 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Susanna Kohler","title":"Selections from 2015: Earth-Sized Planet Found in Star's Habitable Zone","journal":"AAS Nova Highlights","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler452b-Silvaetal2017","cite":"Silva et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Laura Silva, Giovanni Vladilo, Giuseppe Murante et al.","title":"Quantitative estimates of the surface habitability of Kepler-452b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.01224","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler452b-Boldogetal2024","cite":"Boldog et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ádám Boldog, Vera Dobos, László L. Kiss et al.","title":"Water content of rocky exoplanets in the habitable zone","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.01893","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-778 b":{"text":{"why":"Most planets confirmed from TESS follow-up orbit slow-rotating stars with sharp spectral lines, so this one adds a rapid rotator to the sample (Clark et al. 2023).","observed":"The host star spins fast, with its surface moving at about 35 km per second along our line of sight (Clark et al. 2023). Fast spin smears the star's spectral lines, which makes it harder to confirm a planet and weigh it. The team still obtained a mass from velocity measurements with four spectrographs, and used several ground-based telescopes to catch extra transits (Clark et al. 2023). They also watched a transit spectroscopically to measure how the orbit is tilted compared with the star's spin. The sky-projected angle came out at 18 degrees, with an uncertainty of 11 degrees, which is consistent with an aligned system (Clark et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi778b-Clarketal2023","cite":"Clark et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jake T. Clark, Brett C. Addison, Jack Okumura et al.","title":"Spinning up a Daze: TESS Uncovers a Hot Jupiter Orbiting the Rapid Rotator TOI-778","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08242","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1660 AB b":{"text":{"why":"Transit searches favour planets that orbit in the same plane as their binary, and all known transiting circumbinary planets are within 4.5 degrees of it, so tilted orbits can only be found by other means (Goldberg et al. 2023). This is the first confirmed circumbinary planet found this way around a main-sequence binary (Goldberg et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet does not cross in front of its stars. Earlier work suggested a heavier planet, up to 7.7 times Jupiter's mass, on an orbit tilted by 120 degrees to the binary (Goldberg et al. 2023). This study added the first radial velocities of the binary and also examined how the eclipse depths change. Together these confirm a planet on a 239.5-day orbit, but with a lower mass of about 4.9 Jupiter masses, orbiting in the same plane as the two stars (Goldberg et al. 2023). Tilted orbits are ruled out because the eclipse depths do not vary (Goldberg et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a pair of stars that eclipse each other every 18.6 days (Goldberg et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1660abb-Goldbergetal2023","cite":"Goldberg et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Max Goldberg, Daniel Fabrycky, David V. Martin et al.","title":"A 5M<SUB>Jup</SUB> non-transiting coplanar circumbinary planet around Kepler-1660AB","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09255","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-65 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper was the first to use asteroseismology, the study of starquakes seen as brightness ripples, to measure how a planet system is tilted relative to its star's spin (Chaplin et al. 2013).","observed":"The host star shows rich solar-like oscillations, which let the team work out the direction of its spin axis. The axis lies nearly perpendicular to our line of sight (Chaplin et al. 2013). Statistical arguments favour planet orbits that lie in one flat plane, and suggest the orbits line up with the star's spin (Chaplin et al. 2013). Spectra of the star from Keck were later used to measure the amounts of 17 elements in it, as part of a study of how star chemistry relates to planets (Vaz et al. 2015).","system":"The star has several small super-Earth-sized planets that transit it (Chaplin et al. 2013).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler65b-Chaplinetal2013","cite":"Chaplin et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"W. J. Chaplin, R. Sanchis-Ojeda, T. L. Campante et al.","title":"Asteroseismic Determination of Obliquities of the Exoplanet Systems Kepler-50 and Kepler-65","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.3728","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler65b-Vazetal2015","cite":"Vaz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Zachary A. Vaz, Simon C. Schuler, Orlando J. Katime Santrich et al.","title":"Metallicity Analysis of Kepler-65, Kepler-93, Kepler-99, Kepler-102, Kepler-406, and Kepler-409","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #225","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 876 c":{"text":{"why":"The first two planets found around this star were locked in a 2:1 orbital resonance, unprecedented among major planets but common among moons and asteroids (Marcy et al. 2001).","observed":"Six years of precise Doppler measurements from two observatories showed two planets. The inner one had been missed at first because its 2:1 period ratio with the outer one made its signal look like extra eccentricity of the outer orbit (Marcy et al. 2001). Later HARPS data, combined with Keck data, pinned down the tilt of the orbits, giving true masses of about 2.64 and 0.83 Jupiter masses for planets b and c (Correia et al. 2010). The two giants are nearly in the same plane (Correia et al. 2010).","system":"Two more planets have been found: a planet of about 7.5 Earth masses on a 1.94-day orbit (Rivera et al. 2005) and a Uranus-mass planet on a 126.6-day orbit, in a Laplace configuration, a chain of linked resonances (Rivera et al. 2010). The resonances keep the system stable over at least 5 billion years (Correia et al. 2010).","history":"The resonance libration, the small back-and-forth swing of the orbits, is under 2 degrees, which suggests some dissipative process damped it while the planets formed (Correia et al. 2010). Dynamical models place the planets deep in the resonance (Lee & Peale 2002).","open":"How the planets came to be so close and locked in resonance is an unsolved question (Marcy et al. 2001)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj876c-Marcyetal2001","cite":"Marcy et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler, Debra Fischer et al.","title":"A Pair of Resonant Planets Orbiting GJ 876","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/321552","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876c-Correiaetal2010","cite":"Correia et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"A. C. M. Correia, J. Couetdic, J. Laskar et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XIX. Characterization and dynamics of the GJ 876 planetary system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.4774","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876c-Riveraetal2005","cite":"Rivera et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, Jack J. Lissauer, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"A ~7.5 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet Orbiting the Nearby Star, GJ 876","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510508","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876c-Riveraetal2010","cite":"Rivera et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, Gregory Laughlin, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"The Lick-Carnegie Exoplanet Survey: a Uranus-Mass Fourth Planet for GJ 876 in an Extrasolar Laplace Configuration","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.4244","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876c-LeePeale2002","cite":"Lee & Peale 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Man Hoi Lee, S. J. Peale","title":"Dynamics and Origin of the 2:1 Orbital Resonances of the GJ 876 Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/338504","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":53,"about":52,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-776 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies near the radius gap for M dwarfs, the size range where planets become rare, so it helps test how that gap forms around low-mass stars (Luque et al. 2021).","observed":"JWST measured a 2.8 to 5.2 micron transmission spectrum, which shows starlight filtered through the atmosphere, in two transits of this roughly 520 K planet (Alderson et al. 2025). The spectrum is flat in both visits, but the two visits differ in structure. It rules out atmospheres richer in heavy elements than about 100 times the Sun's, down to a cloud-top pressure of 0.001 bar (Alderson et al. 2025). CHEOPS transits sharpened the planets' radii and densities (Fridlund et al. 2024). The bright star makes the system a good JWST target (Luque et al. 2021).","system":"A second planet, TOI-776 c, orbits every 15.66 days. A third Doppler signal near 34 days comes from the star's rotation, not a planet (Luque et al. 2021). Interior modelling suggests both planets are mostly rocky (Deb et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The planets' densities allow a wide range of interiors and atmospheres, though both appear to have kept a significant atmosphere (Luque et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi776b-Luqueetal2021","cite":"Luque et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"R. Luque, L. M. Serrano, K. Molaverdikhani et al.","title":"A planetary system with two transiting mini-Neptunes near the radius valley transition around the bright M dwarf TOI-776","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.08338","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi776b-Aldersonetal2025","cite":"Alderson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Lili Alderson, Sarah E. Moran, Nicole L. Wallack et al.","title":"JWST COMPASS: NIRSpec/G395H Transmission Observations of the Super-Earth TOI-776 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi776b-Fridlundetal2024","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Fridlund, I. Y. Georgieva, A. Bonfanti et al.","title":"Planets observed with CHEOPS. Two super-Earths orbiting the red dwarf star TOI-776","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202243838","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi776b-Debetal2026","cite":"Deb et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Sushmita Deb, Kaushal Sharma, Ravi Kumar Kopparapu et al.","title":"Structural composition and atmospheric analysis of two planetary systems : TOI-270 and TOI-776","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag2095","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-111 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit, so it shows up only as a second signal in velocity data. That signal appears independently in both the HARPS-N and ESPRESSO spectrographs when each is fitted alone (Mortier et al. 2020). The planet circles its star about once for every three orbits of the transiting planet K2-111 b, a near-3:1 resonance (Mortier et al. 2020). The same paper characterised the host star in detail, using K2, WASP and ASAS-SN brightness data and spectra from both instruments (Mortier et al. 2020).","system":"The host is an old, iron-poor but alpha-element-rich star of the Galaxy's thick disc, and it lies far behind the Hyades cluster (Mortier et al. 2020; Fridlund et al. 2017). Planet b is probably terrestrial, with a smaller iron core fraction than Earth (Mortier et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2111c-Mortieretal2020","cite":"Mortier et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. Mortier, M. R. Zapatero Osorio, L. Malavolta et al.","title":"K2-111: an old system with two planets in near-resonance","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.01993","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2111c-Fridlundetal2017","cite":"Fridlund et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Malcolm Fridlund, Eric Gaidos, Oscar Barragán et al.","title":"K2-111 b - a short period super-Earth transiting a metal poor, evolved old star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.08284","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 3167 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its orbit lies between those of planets b and c. Because no transits of d are seen, orbits within 1.3 degrees of the plane of b and c are ruled out (Christiansen et al. 2017). The star is bright and nearby, so the system is a good target for follow-up (Vanderburg et al. 2016).","system":"Planet b is a hot, rocky super-Earth on a 0.96-day orbit, and a JWST eclipse measurement suggests it has an atmosphere that cools its dayside (Coy et al. 2026). Planet c is a larger, low-density planet with a thick gas envelope (Gandolfi et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd3167d-Christiansenetal2017","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Andrew Vanderburg, Jennifer Burt et al.","title":"Three’s Company: An Additional Non-transiting Super-Earth in the Bright HD 3167 System, and Masses for All Three Planets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.01892","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167d-Vanderburgetal2016","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Allyson Bieryla, Dmitry A. Duev et al.","title":"Two Small Planets Transiting HD 3167","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.05248","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167d-Coyetal2026","cite":"Coy et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Brandon Park Coy, Qiao Xue, Megan Weiner Mansfield et al.","title":"Evidence for an Atmosphere on the Ultra-short-period Super-Earth HD 3167 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11911","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167d-Gandolfietal2017","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Davide Gandolfi, Oscar Barragán, Artie P. Hatzes et al.","title":"The Transiting Multi-planet System HD 3167: A 5.7 M <SUB>⊕</SUB> Super-Earth and an 8.3 M <SUB>⊕</SUB> Mini-Neptune","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02532","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":24,"about":24,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery paper reports this planet together with HATS-16 b, as two massive hot Jupiters around old stars of about 10 billion years (Ciceri et al. 2016). The host is a G9 dwarf, faint at magnitude 14.8, and the planet circles it in about 1.7 days. The sister planet's star spins faster than its age would suggest, and the authors say tides from the planet may have spun it up (Ciceri et al. 2016). The planet is described as a hot Jupiter with about twice Jupiter's mass (Ciceri et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats15b-Cicerietal2016","cite":"Ciceri et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"S. Ciceri, L. Mancini, T. Henning et al.","title":"HATS-15b and HATS-16b: Two Massive Planets Transiting Old G Dwarf Stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06305","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 156846 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has a very eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.85, so it swings between about 0.16 au and far out from its star (Tamuz et al. 2008; Kane et al. 2011). Its closest point is nearly aligned with our line of sight, which gives a transit chance of 5.4% (Kane et al. 2011). The planet would be heated suddenly as it passes the bright star (Kane et al. 2011). Better velocity data cut the predicted transit time uncertainty to 20 minutes, but one monitored window under poor conditions did not rule out a transit (Kane et al. 2011).","system":"The star is the main member of a wide binary, with an M4 dwarf companion more than 250 au away (Tamuz et al. 2008).","history":"The very high eccentricity can be explained by Kozai oscillations, a slow exchange between tilt and stretch of an orbit driven by a distant body (Tamuz et al. 2008).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd156846b-Tamuzetal2008","cite":"Tamuz et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"O. Tamuz, D. Ségransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XV. Discovery of two eccentric planets orbiting <ASTROBJ>HD 4113</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 156846</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.5028","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd156846b-Kaneetal2011","cite":"Kane et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Stephen R. Kane, Andrew W. Howard, Genady Pilyavsky et al.","title":"Improved Orbital Parameters and Transit Monitoring for HD 156846b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.4127","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2022-BLG-1013L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The brief signal of the planet was only partly covered by observations, so the team tested several explanations, including a pair of stars lensing and a binary source (Han et al. 2023). For this event, only a planetary lens fits, with a planet-to-host mass ratio of about 1.6 in a thousand (Han et al. 2023). The study analysed three planetary events in all.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2022blg1013lb-Hanetal2023","cite":"Han et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Weicheng Zang et al.","title":"KMT-2021-BLG-2010Lb, KMT-2022-BLG-0371Lb, and KMT-2022-BLG-1013Lb: Three microlensing planets detected via partially covered signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03871","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1203 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"An intensive campaign with the HARPS spectrograph and the CHEOPS space telescope revealed two low-mass planets, at 13.1 and 204.6 days, in a system already known to host a transiting sub-Neptune (Gandolfi et al. 2025). Planet c does not transit and has a minimum mass of 5.46 Earth masses (Gandolfi et al. 2025). The same data also revealed a transiting super-Earth on a 4.2-day orbit and confirmed the 25.5-day sub-Neptune (Gandolfi et al. 2025).","system":"The star hosts four planets. The outer planet e has an eccentric orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.15 (Gandolfi et al. 2025).","history":"The star is about 12.5 billion years old and belongs to the old, alpha-element-rich thick disc population of the Galaxy (Gandolfi et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1203c-Gandolfietal2025","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Gandolfi, A. Alnajjarine, L. M. Serrano et al.","title":"A four-planet system orbiting the old thick disk star TOI-1203","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10136","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-285 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Four transiting planets were found, ranging from twice the size of Earth to nearly the size of Neptune (Palle et al. 2019). Velocity measurements with the CARMENES and HARPS-N spectrographs gave masses to better than 15% for the two inner planets. Only upper limits were placed on the masses of the other planets (Palle et al. 2019). Planets in this size range are the most common around Sun-like stars, but have no counterpart in the Solar System (Palle et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2285d-Palleetal2019","cite":"Palle et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"E. Palle, G. Nowak, R. Luque et al.","title":"Detection and Doppler monitoring of K2-285 (EPIC 246471491), a system of four transiting planets smaller than Neptune","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.00575","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-16 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of the star's spectrum during a transit, was weak, which suggests the star is a slow rotator and so old (Brown et al. 2012). The star fits the idea that cooler stars tend to have planets orbiting in line with their spin (Brown et al. 2012). A circular orbit is consistent with the data (Brown et al. 2012). Later transit observations found the star and planet larger and less massive than first thought (Southworth et al. 2013). A search of TESS data flagged new planet candidates in this system (Dévora-Pajares et al. 2024).","system":"The host is a near-solar-metallicity star similar to the Sun, of intermediate age (Lister et al. 2009).","history":"The planet is one of many hot Jupiters used to show that their host stars' spin tilts look random at first and later align through tides (Albrecht et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp16b-Brownetal2012","cite":"Brown et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. Collier Cameron, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Rossiter-McLaughlin effect measurements for WASP-16, WASP-25 and WASP-31","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp16b-Southworthetal2013","cite":"Southworth et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"John Southworth, L. Mancini, P. Browne et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocusing - V. WASP-15 and WASP-16","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.3509","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp16b-DvoraPajaresetal2024","cite":"Dévora-Pajares et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Martín Dévora-Pajares, Francisco J. Pozuelos, Antoine Thuillier et al.","title":"The SHERLOCK pipeline: new exoplanet candidates in the WASP-16, HAT-P-27, HAT-P-26, and TOI-2411 systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14602","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp16b-Listeretal2009","cite":"Lister et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"T. A. Lister, D. R. Anderson, M. Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-16b: A New Jupiter-Like Planet Transiting a Southern Solar Analog","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0297","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp16b-Albrechtetal2012","cite":"Albrecht et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Simon Albrecht, Joshua N. Winn, John A. Johnson et al.","title":"Obliquities of Hot Jupiter Host Stars: Evidence for Tidal Interactions and Primordial Misalignments","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6105","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7166 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet sits near the inner edge of its star's habitable zone, and the bright star makes it a target for atmosphere studies (Barkaoui et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet was confirmed using TESS data, multicolour ground-based photometry, spectra, high-contrast imaging, archival images and statistical arguments (Barkaoui et al. 2025). Multicolour validation checks that the transit depth varies with wavelength in the way a real planet would, which rejects look-alike false signals (Jiang et al. 2026). It is a benchmark temperate mini-Neptune, with an equilibrium temperature of 249 K if it reflects no starlight (Barkaoui et al. 2025; Jiang et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7166b-Barkaouietal2025","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Khalid Barkaoui, Francisco J. Pozuelos, Benjamin V. Rackham et al.","title":"TOI-7166 b: a habitable zone mini-Neptune planet around a nearby low-mass star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi7166b-Jiangetal2026","cite":"Jiang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Chengzi Jiang, Aleksandra Selezneva, Hannu Parviainen et al.","title":"Multicolour validation of two temperate mini-Neptunes around M-dwarf habitable zones","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06817","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 86728 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came from the NEID Earth Twin Survey, which watches bright stars that are quiet in velocity, in the hope of finding Earth analogues (Gupta et al. 2025; Gupta 2023).","observed":"HD 86728 b is the first planet found by that survey, and it confirms a candidate signal reported earlier by Hirsch and colleagues (Gupta et al. 2025). The signal is tiny, and careful tests of the velocities and the spectral activity indicators show that it comes from a planet on a circular orbit, not from the star (Gupta et al. 2025). The star is quiet at about the 1 m per second level, with granulation, the boiling of its surface, as the likely source (Gupta et al. 2025). It is among the few planets of its mass and period with no known siblings (Gupta et al. 2025).","system":"The star has a M dwarf companion, probably itself an unresolved pair of stars, which is old, metal-rich and active (Gizis et al. 2000).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd86728b-Guptaetal2025","cite":"Gupta et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Arvind F. Gupta, Jacob K. Luhn, Jason T. Wright et al.","title":"The NEID Earth Twin Survey. I. Confirmation of a 31 Day Planet Orbiting HD 86728","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12315","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd86728b-Gupta2023","cite":"Gupta 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Arvind F. Gupta","title":"Harnessing the Potential of Radial Velocity Exoplanet Surveys","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd86728b-Gizisetal2000","cite":"Gizis et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"John E. Gizis, David G. Monet, I. Neill Reid et al.","title":"Two nearby M dwarf binaries from the Two Micron All Sky Survey","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-8711.2000.03060.x","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-124 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spitzer measured the planet's transit at 4.5 microns, together with seven other planet candidates from K2 (Livingston et al. 2019). Combining Spitzer and K2 light curves refined the radius and gave multi-wavelength information that helps confirm the planetary nature of these objects (Livingston et al. 2019). The observations also improved timing precision by an order of magnitude, cutting the uncertainty on transit times in mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2124b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1624 b":{"text":{"why":"Gas giants that have resonant or near-resonant companions are rare, and such systems help constrain how giants form and migrate (Im et al. 2025).","observed":"Kepler data had shown small shifts in transit times of about 2 minutes, which hinted at a nearby companion that does not transit (Im et al. 2025). A new analysis added TESS data and three new ground-based transits from Palomar and Las Cumbres, extending the baseline by 11 years (Im et al. 2025). The timing shifts turned out much weaker, and a one-planet model is preferred over a two-planet one (Im et al. 2025).","system":"The planet is a sub-Saturn orbiting an M dwarf (Im et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The earlier hint of a companion is not supported, which shows the need for care with weak timing signals in automated catalogues (Im et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1624b-Imetal2025","cite":"Im et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Haedam Im, Morgan Saidel, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-1624b Has No Significant Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17709","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-2016-BLG-319L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event's light curve has a brief anomaly of about 3 days near its peak, caused by minor-image perturbations (Han et al. 2018). Two different solutions fit almost equally well, because the source star passed on different sides of the planet's caustic, the curve of strongly magnified light (Han et al. 2018). Such confusion is rare for minor-image perturbations, and it follows from source paths that crossed the star-planet line at about right angles (Han et al. 2018). A statistical analysis suggests a giant planet of about 0.6 Jupiter masses orbiting a low-mass M dwarf (Han et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The two solutions cannot be told apart, so the planet's properties remain uncertain (Han et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moa2016blg319lb-Hanetal2018","cite":"Han et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Cheongho Han, Ian A. Bond, Andrew Gould et al.","title":"MOA-2016-BLG-319Lb: Microlensing Planet Subject to Rare Minor-image Perturbation Degeneracy in Determining Planet Parameters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07898","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 164922 d":{"text":{"why":"Finding out how often inner terrestrial planets sit alongside outer gas giants, as in the Solar System, is still an open question, and this survey targeted such systems (Benatti et al. 2020).","observed":"More than 300 HARPS-N spectra were collected, and a Gaussian-process model, a flexible statistical tool, was used to separate the star's activity from planet signals (Benatti et al. 2020). The team combined almost 700 velocities, including data from the literature, and found the close-in super-Earth (Benatti et al. 2020). A dynamical analysis checked that the system remains stable and where more planets might fit (Benatti et al. 2020).","system":"The star also has a gas giant on a wide orbit of about 1200 days and a Neptune-mass planet on a 76-day orbit (Benatti et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd164922d-Benattietal2020","cite":"Benatti et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Benatti, M. Damasso, S. Desidera et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XXIII. HD 164922 d: close-in super-Earth discovered with HARPS-N in a system with a long-period Saturn mass companion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.03368","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"OGLE-TR-113 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of two confirmed from velocity follow-up of OGLE transit candidates in Carina (Bouchy et al. 2004).","observed":"With a period of 1.43 days, it is one of the \"very hot Jupiters\", which show that the pile-up of periods near 3 days in velocity surveys is not an absolute limit (Bouchy et al. 2004; Konacki et al. 2004). Near-infrared photometry shows a flat-bottomed transit, which points to weaker limb darkening in the infrared than in visible light, and gave a tentative detection of the planet's own emission (Snellen & Covino 2006). Nineteen transits over 13 years show no clear orbital decay (Hoyer et al. 2015). Earlier hints of a shrinking period were tentative (Adams et al. 2010).","system":"Timing data rule out additional planets more massive than about 0.5 to 3 Earth masses in the main resonances with this planet (Hoyer et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ogletr113b-Bouchyetal2004","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"F. Bouchy, F. Pont, N. C. Santos et al.","title":"Two new ``very hot Jupiters'' among the OGLE transiting candidates","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0404264","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr113b-Konackietal2004","cite":"Konacki et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"Maciej Konacki, Guillermo Torres, Dimitar D. Sasselov et al.","title":"The Transiting Extrasolar Giant Planet around the Star OGLE-TR-113","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0404541","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr113b-SnellenCovino2006","cite":"Snellen & Covino 2006","year":"2006","authors":"I. Snellen, E. Covino","title":"K‐band transit and secondary eclipse photometry of exoplanet OGLE‐TR‐113b","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0611877","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr113b-Hoyeretal2015","cite":"Hoyer et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"S. Hoyer, M. López-Morales, P. Rojo et al.","title":"TraMoS – IV. Discarding the Quick Orbital Decay Hypothesis for OGLE-TR-113b","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.03790","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ogletr113b-Adamsetal2010","cite":"Adams et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"E. Adams, M. López-Morales, J. Elliot et al.","title":"SIX HIGH-PRECISION TRANSITS OF OGLE-TR-113b","journal":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1008.1799","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":19,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1246 e":{"text":{"why":"The system is one of the brightest with four transiting planets, and one of only a handful with measured masses and radii for all four (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","observed":"130 velocity measurements with Keck and HARPS-N gave the masses of four sub-Neptunes of similar size (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Planet e is substantially more massive than the three inner planets (Turtelboom et al. 2022). It and planet d lie just outside a 2:1 resonance, with a period ratio of 2.03, and their transit times shift measurably (Turtelboom et al. 2022). Densities across the four planets range from 0.70 to 3.21 grams per cubic centimetre, which points to a wide mix of bulk and atmospheric compositions (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","system":"The planets orbit a K dwarf with periods of 4.31, 5.90, 18.66 and 37.92 days, with two tightly packed inner planets (Turtelboom et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"The system is a testing ground for ideas on mass loss and formation, because similar-sized planets differ so much in mass (Turtelboom et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1246e-Turtelboometal2022","cite":"Turtelboom et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Emma V. Turtelboom, Lauren M. Weiss, Courtney D. Dressing et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. XI. Mass Measurements for Four Transiting Sub-Neptunes Orbiting K Dwarf TOI-1246","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11895","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-138 e":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet e is the outermost known planet in a system studied with 13 Hubble and Spitzer transit observations and new Keck velocities (Piaulet et al. 2023). Its neighbour Kepler-138 d, at 1.51 Earth radii and about 350 K, appears to be a water world, with roughly 11% of its mass in volatiles such as water (Piaulet et al. 2023). Transit timing and a flat transmission spectrum support that view (Piaulet et al. 2023). Earlier work found that the Mars-sized inner planet gave the first density measurement for an exoplanet smaller than Earth (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015).","system":"The star is cooler and fainter than the Sun (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler138e-Piauletetal2023","cite":"Piaulet et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Caroline Piaulet, Björn Benneke, Jose M. Almenara et al.","title":"Evidence for the volatile-rich composition of a 1.5-Earth-radius planet","journal":"Nature Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08477","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler138e-JontofHutteretal2015","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jason F. Rowe, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"The mass of the Mars-sized exoplanet Kepler-138 b from transit timing","journal":"Nature","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.07067","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-836 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Two transits were observed with the JWST space telescope, giving a spectrum of starlight filtering through the planet's atmosphere from 2.8 to 5.2 microns. The spectrum is flat, with no features. Combining both visits rules out atmospheres with metal content below 250 times solar, so the planet does not have a light, hydrogen-dominated atmosphere (Alderson et al. 2024). Its size places it directly within the radius valley, a gap in the sizes of known small planets (Hawthorn et al. 2023).","system":"The outer planet, TOI-836 c, is a 2.59 Earth-radius mini-Neptune in an 8.60-day orbit. Its transit times drift by about 20 minutes, which may be caused by an undetected exterior planet (Hawthorn et al. 2023). The two planets sit on either side of the radius valley, so they can be compared directly (Wallack et al. 2024).","history":"The two planets formed in the same environment but ended up different, which makes the pair a test of how small planets evolve (Wallack et al. 2024).","open":"The outer planet may keep a hydrogen-rich atmosphere, in contrast to this one (Alderson et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi836b-Aldersonetal2024","cite":"Alderson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Lili Alderson, Natasha E. Batalha, Hannah R. Wakeford et al.","title":"JWST COMPASS: NIRSpec/G395H Transmission Observations of the Super-Earth TOI-836b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00093","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi836b-Hawthornetal2023","cite":"Hawthorn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Faith Hawthorn, Daniel Bayliss, Thomas G. Wilson et al.","title":"TOI-836: A super-Earth and mini-Neptune transiting a nearby K-dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07328","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi836b-Wallacketal2024","cite":"Wallack et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Natasha E. Batalha, Lili Alderson et al.","title":"JWST COMPASS: A NIRSpec/G395H Transmission Spectrum of the Sub-Neptune TOI-836c","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01264","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 154345 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is described as a twin of Jupiter: a minimum mass of 0.95 Jupiter masses on a circular orbit lasting 9.2 years, at 4.2 times the Earth-Sun distance (Wright et al. 2007). It was called the best Jupiter analog known at the time (Wright 2007). The star shows a roughly nine-year activity cycle, both in brightness and in its chromospheric emission. The authors ruled out this cycle as the cause of the velocity wobble by comparison with other cycling late G dwarfs (Wright et al. 2007). Later data from two spectrographs refined the orbit (Boisse et al. 2012).","system":"Little or no evidence of other planets was found, though smaller or more distant ones cannot be ruled out (Wright et al. 2007).","history":"","open":"Whether smaller or exterior planets exist is not yet settled (Wright et al. 2007)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd154345b-Wrightetal2007","cite":"Wright et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"J. T. Wright, G. W. Marcy, D. A. Fischer et al.","title":"Four New Exoplanets and Hints of Additional Substellar Companions to Exoplanet Host Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0611658","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd154345b-Wright2007","cite":"Wright 2007","year":"2007","authors":"J. T. Wright, G. W. Marcy, R. P. Butler et al.","title":"The Jupiter Twin HD 154345b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1731","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd154345b-Boisseetal2012","cite":"Boisse et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"I. Boisse, F. Pepe, C. Perrier et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. V. Follow-up of ELODIE candidates: Jupiter-analogs around Sun-like stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.5835","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 145675 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. Spectroscopy showed that the star is very rich in heavy elements, with an iron content of [Fe/H] = 0.50, about three times the Sun's (Gonzalez et al. 1999). It is among about half a dozen nearby stars with such high levels, and it is nearly identical to the planet-hosting star rho1 Cancri.","system":"The close match with rho1 Cancri, combined with the extreme metal content, led the authors to suggest that the star's properties were affected by the process that formed its planets (Gonzalez et al. 1999).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd145675c-Gonzalezetal1999","cite":"Gonzalez et al. 1999","year":"1999","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, George Wallerstein, Steven H. Saar","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. IV. 14 Herculis, HD 187123, and HD 210277","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/311847","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1544 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"No observations of the planet's atmosphere are described. Two modelling studies consider its possible climate. One places it among five planets that stay in a photosynthetic habitable zone, where both liquid water and oxygen-producing photosynthesis could occur, and predicts a day length of 9 to 11 hours (Hall et al. 2023). Another follows tidal spin evolution and finds that the planet is expected to rotate fast rather than keep one face to its star, whatever its orbital shape (Camposeo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's real rotation and atmosphere have not been measured; the day length is a prediction (Hall et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1544b-Halletal2023","cite":"Hall et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"C. Hall, P. C. Stancil, J. P. Terry et al.","title":"A New Definition of Exoplanet Habitability: Introducing the Photosynthetic Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.13836","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1544b-Camposeoetal2026","cite":"Camposeo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Salvatore Camposeo, Francesco De Paolis, Vincenzo Orofino et al.","title":"Simulation of the Spin Evolution of Some Selected Exoplanets and Inferences on Their Climate","journal":"Universe","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11780","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2000 b":{"text":{"why":"The system is unusual because hot giant planets are almost always alone, and only four of hundreds known have inner companions (Sha et al. 2023).","observed":"The system has a mini-Neptune inside a hot Saturn-mass planet, and both transit their star. Radial velocity measurements from three spectrographs weighed both planets directly, which confirmed them as real (Sha et al. 2023). The host is a metal-rich G dwarf, and the team also followed the system up with ground-based photometry, spectroscopy and speckle imaging to rule out other explanations (Sha et al. 2023).","system":"The outer planet, TOI-2000 c, is a hot Saturn with about 8 Earth radii and 82 Earth masses in a 9.13-day orbit (Sha et al. 2023).","history":"Inner companions to hot giants help show how the giant got close to its star. They rule out high-eccentricity tidal migration, which would have disrupted the inner planet (Sha et al. 2023).","open":"Less is known about inner companions to hot Saturn-mass planets than to hot Jupiters (Sha et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2000b-Shaetal2023","cite":"Sha et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Lizhou Sha, Andrew M. Vanderburg, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"TESS spots a mini-neptune interior to a hot saturn in the TOI-2000 system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.14396","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 1461 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found in 12.8 years of precision velocity data from the Keck telescope. The same star was watched for 12.2 years in brightness, and it is photometrically very stable (Rivera et al. 2010). If the planet is Neptune-like, a transit would dim the star by only about 0.5 millimagnitudes, and the chance that the orbit is aligned to transit is about 8 percent (Rivera et al. 2010).","system":"The data also hint at two more planets, with periods of 446 and 5017 days and minimum masses of 28 and 87 Earth masses. Test integrations suggest the arrangement would be stable (Rivera et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"The two outer signals are not confirmed. Small brightness variations with similar periods cast suspicion on them as true planets (Rivera et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd1461b-Riveraetal2010","cite":"Rivera et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, R. Paul Butler, Steven S. Vogt et al.","title":"A Super-Earth Orbiting the Nearby Sun-like Star HD 1461","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.2566","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2076 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is young, about 210 million years old, and its spots cause strong periodic dimming (Hedges et al. 2021; Barber et al. 2025). Spectroscopy during transit measured the tilt of the orbit relative to the star's spin. The orbit is well aligned, with a true tilt below 34 degrees at 95 percent confidence (Frazier et al. 2023). The planet is one of three transiting planets that are all JWST targets for atmosphere studies (Barber et al. 2025).","system":"Planets c and d have periods of 21.03 and 35.13 days, pinned down after only two transits had been seen of each (Osborn et al. 2022). A smaller planet, TOI-2076 e, with 1.35 Earth radii, orbits inside in 3.02 days (Barber et al. 2025). The star shares its motion with the star TOI-1807, which also hosts a planet (Hedges et al. 2021).","history":"The low tilt and the transit timing variations suggest the system formed by migrating together through an initially well-aligned disc (Frazier et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2076b-Hedgesetal2021","cite":"Hedges et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christina Hedges, Alex Hughes, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2076 and TOI-1807: Two Young, Comoving Planetary Systems within 50 pc Identified by TESS that are Ideal Candidates for Further Follow Up","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01311","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076b-Barberetal2025","cite":"Barber et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Madyson G. Barber, Andrew W. Mann, Andrew Vanderburg et al.","title":"TESS Investigation—Demographics of Young Exoplanets (TI-DYE). III. An Inner Super-Earth in TOI 2076","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076b-Frazieretal2023","cite":"Frazier et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Robert C. Frazier, Gudmundur Stefánsson, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"NEID Reveals That the Young Warm Neptune TOI-2076 b Has a Low Obliquity","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.06266","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2076b-Osbornetal2022","cite":"Osborn et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"H. P. Osborn, A. Bonfanti, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"Uncovering the true periods of the young sub-Neptunes orbiting TOI-2076","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.03194","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":14,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"47 UMa b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this planet so far is theoretical, not new observations. A formation model finds that a planet of 2.6 Jupiter masses at 2.1 times the Earth-Sun distance, and another of 0.89 Jupiter masses at 3.95 times, could both build up within a few million years. This needs a strong build-up of solid material in the disc between 1 and 4 times that distance (Kornet et al. 2002). Stability studies ask whether smaller planets could survive in the system's habitable zone (Goździewski 2002; Asghari et al. 2004).","system":"Two Jupiter-size planets lie beyond the outer edge of the habitable zone, making the system resemble our own Solar System more closely than other systems known then (Cuntz et al. 2003). A third Jupiter-type planet is now known (Cuntz et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Earth-type habitable planets are possible in principle, if they formed and stayed stable (Cuntz et al. 2003). No such planet is described."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-47umab-Kornetetal2002","cite":"Kornet et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"K. Kornet, P. Bodenheimer, M. Różyczka","title":"Models of the formation of the planets in the 47 UMa system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0209613","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umab-Asgharietal2004","cite":"Asghari et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"N. Asghari, C. Broeg, L. Carone et al.","title":"Stability of terrestrial planets in the habitable zone of Gl 777 A, HD 72659, Gl 614, 47 Uma and HD 4208","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0403152","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umab-Cuntzetal2003","cite":"Cuntz et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"Manfred Cuntz, Werner von Bloh, Christine Bounama et al.","title":"On the possibility of earth-type habitable planets around 47 UMa","journal":"Icarus","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0019-1035(02)00058-1","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-47umab-Cuntzetal2018","cite":"Cuntz et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Manfred Cuntz, Birgit Loibnegger, Rudolf Dvorak","title":"Exocomets in the 47 UMa System: Theoretical Simulations Including Water Transport","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09579","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":19,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5108 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a sub-Saturn, a size between Neptune and Saturn, that sits just within the Neptune desert. This is a region close to stars where planets of this size are rare (Thomas et al. 2025). Models of its interior estimate that hydrogen-helium gas makes up 38 percent of its mass. With a model that includes tidal effects, the gas fraction could be much lower, about 20 percent (Thomas et al. 2025). The host is a moderately bright G-type star.","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's gas fraction depends on how tides are treated in the interior model (Thomas et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5108b-Thomasetal2025","cite":"Thomas et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Luis Thomas, Guillaume Hébrard, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"TOI-5108 b and TOI 5786 b: Two transiting sub-Saturns detected and characterized with TESS, MaHPS, and SOPHIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.03803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-323 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"New velocity measurements from the Keck telescope, combined with earlier ones, gave a mass of 6.5 Earth masses for this planet. Its density is high, consistent with a rocky composition (Thomas et al. 2024). The study compares the two planets of this system with the three planets of Kepler-104, which are more massive and appear to be rocky cores wrapped in gas (Thomas et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet, Kepler-323 b, is only about 2 Earth masses and is also consistent with a rocky composition (Thomas et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler323c-Thomasetal2024","cite":"Thomas et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"C. Alexander Thomas, Lauren M. Weiss, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"A Tale of Two Peas in a Pod: The Kepler-323 and Kepler-104 Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13386","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 66074 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star was first flagged as having a super-Jupiter companion from Gaia astrometry. Early velocity data seemed to disagree, with a signal 15 times smaller than predicted (Sozzetti et al. 2023).","observed":"Intensive monitoring with the HARPS-N spectrograph found the companion at the period Gaia had reported, but on an extremely stretched orbit, with an eccentricity of 0.948. The minimum mass is 0.79 Jupiter masses (Sozzetti et al. 2023). The mismatch is explained by the first velocity data being sparse, and by biases in the Gaia solution. It underestimated the eccentricity and misjudged the tilt, which is close to face-on (Sozzetti et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Gaia and ground-based velocities disagree for six of 12 candidates checked, and some of these are false positives from pairs of similar stars (Marcussen & Albrecht 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip66074b-Sozzettietal2023","cite":"Sozzetti et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Sozzetti, M. Pinamonti, M. Damasso et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XLVII. A conundrum resolved: HIP 66074b/Gaia-3b characterised as a massive giant planet on a quasi-face-on and extremely elongated orbit","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08653","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip66074b-MarcussenAlbrecht2023","cite":"Marcussen & Albrecht 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Marcus L. Marcussen, Simon H. Albrecht","title":"Spectroscopic Follow-up of Gaia Exoplanet Candidates: Impostor Binary Stars Invade the Gaia DR3 Astrometric Exoplanet Candidates","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08623","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 285507 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This is the first hot Jupiter found in the Hyades open cluster. Its orbit is slightly eccentric, with a minimum mass of 0.92 Jupiter masses. Follow-up photometry indicates it does not transit (Quinn et al. 2014). Later observations in visible and infrared light confirmed it. Young stars are magnetically active, and a true planet signal is the same at all wavelengths while activity signals are not (Carleo et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"The time needed for tides to circularize the orbit is longer than the age of the Hyades. The eccentricity may therefore come from migration through interactions with a third body (Quinn et al. 2014). The authors conclude that planet-planet scattering plays an important role in hot Jupiter migration (Quinn et al. 2014).","open":"The planet shows that a dense star cluster does not stop giant planets from forming and migrating (Quinn et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd285507b-Quinnetal2014","cite":"Quinn et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Samuel N. Quinn, Russel J. White, David W. Latham et al.","title":"HD 285507b: An Eccentric Hot Jupiter in the Hyades Open Cluster","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7328","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd285507b-Carleoetal2020","cite":"Carleo et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"I. Carleo, L. Malavolta, A. F. Lanza et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XXI. A GIARPS case study of known young planetary candidates: confirmation of HD 285507 b and refutation of AD Leonis b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10562","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-94 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A mass of 106 Earth masses came from 26 velocity measurements at Keck, combined with the light curve. The planet is not inflated, unlike many hot Jupiters, although it gets 2700 times the radiation Jupiter does (Weiss et al. 2013). The system is the first known in which two planets were seen partly overlapping during a double transit, a planet-planet eclipse (Masuda et al. 2013).","system":"KOI-94 has at least three smaller transiting planets. Their reality is supported by gravitational tugs that shift transit times (Weiss et al. 2013). A faint companion candidate, probably an M-type star, lies about 0.6 arcseconds away (Takahashi et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Transit timing gives a mass of 52 Earth masses, about half the velocity value (Masuda et al. 2013). The two methods disagree."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi94d-Weissetal2013","cite":"Weiss et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Lauren M. Weiss, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Jason F. Rowe et al.","title":"The Mass of KOI-94d and a Relation for Planet Radius, Mass, and Incident Flux","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.2150","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi94d-Masudaetal2013","cite":"Masuda et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Kento Masuda, Teruyuki Hirano, Atsushi Taruya et al.","title":"Characterization of the KOI-94 System with Transit Timing Variation Analysis: Implication for the Planet-Planet Eclipse","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5771","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi94d-Takahashietal2013","cite":"Takahashi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Yasuhiro H. Takahashi, Norio Narita, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"A Discovery of a Candidate Companion to a Transiting System KOI-94: A Direct Imaging Study for a Possibility of a False Positive","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.2559","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-11 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A decade of Keck velocity measurements revealed a planet similar to Jupiter in minimum mass (1.6 Jupiter masses) and orbital period (9.3 years). Unlike Jupiter, its orbit is much more eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.60 (Yee et al. 2018). The star's own activity adds only about 7 metres per second, compared with 31 metres per second from this planet (Yee et al. 2018).","system":"The inner planet, HAT-P-11 b, is Neptune-sized and orbits tilted relative to the star's spin (Winn et al. 2010; Yee et al. 2018). Planet c could explain that tilt through precession of planet b's orbit over million-year timescales (Yee et al. 2018).","history":"One proposal is that planet-planet scattering gave both planets eccentric, tilted orbits. That tilt then drove planet b inward through von Zeipel-Lidov-Kozai cycles (Lu et al. 2025).","open":"Why planet c's orbit is so eccentric remains a question (Yee et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp11c-Yeeetal2018","cite":"Yee et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Erik A. Petigura, Benjamin J. Fulton et al.","title":"HAT-P-11: Discovery of a Second Planet and a Clue to Understanding Exoplanet Obliquities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09352","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp11c-Winnetal2010","cite":"Winn et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, John Asher Johnson, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"The Oblique Orbit of the Super-Neptune HAT-P-11b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5671","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp11c-Luetal2025","cite":"Lu et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Tiger Lu, Qier An, Gongjie Li et al.","title":"Planet─Planet Scattering and Von Zeipel─Lidov─Kozai Migration—The Dynamical History of HAT-P-11","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.19511","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":33,"about":33,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-62 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HATS-62 b is a super-Neptune, one of ten planets announced together by the HATSouth survey. It is the least massive planet discovered so far with a radius larger than Jupiter's (Hartman et al. 2019). The study used Gaia distances and colours to check each system. It found that HATS-62, HATS-64 and HATS-65 may each have an unresolved binary companion star (Hartman et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The possible companion star in this system is not yet confirmed (Hartman et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats62b-Hartmanetal2019","cite":"Hartman et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-60b-HATS-69b: 10 Transiting Planets from HATSouth","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01048","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-32 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"NGTS-32 b is one of two inflated hot Jupiters announced together. It orbits a slightly evolved star, a subgiant, and has a radius of 1.42 Jupiter radii for a mass of 0.57 Jupiter masses (Vines et al. 2025). The planet receives far more stellar flux than the threshold above which hot planets swell. Its equilibrium temperature is higher than other hot Jupiters of similar mass and radius (Vines et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether other inflation mechanisms play a role in puffing up planets like this one is still an open question (Vines et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts32b-Vinesetal2025","cite":"Vines et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jose I. Vines, James S. Jenkins, David R. Anderson et al.","title":"NGTS-31b and NGTS-32b: two inflated hot Jupiters orbiting subgiant stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stae2616","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 81991 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey behind it aims to find the most promising giant-planet host stars before the Gaia DR4 data release, so that candidates can be checked and their orbits and masses pinned down (Van Zandt et al. 2026).","observed":"The companion was identified from a long-term pull on its host star. The survey combines absolute star positions from Hipparcos and Gaia with adaptive optics imaging and precise velocity measurements to limit companion masses and orbits (Van Zandt et al. 2026). Systems that show both sideways and line-of-sight acceleration are the most informative, because the combination narrows down the companion's mass and orbit.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip81991b-VanZandtetal2026","cite":"Van Zandt et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"J. Van Zandt, B. Bowler, E. Petigura et al.","title":"Gaia Exoplanet Orbits, Demographics, and Evolution Survey (GEODES). I. Characteristics of Three Long-period Companions Accelerating their Host Stars","journal":"Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23018","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1643 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star belongs to a dispersed group of stars about 40 million years old, spread across the sky from Cepheus to Hercules. The planet's star is 46 million years old, based on its membership in a dense subcluster of this group (Bouma et al. 2022). This makes it one of the youngest planets from the Kepler mission, and a first demonstration that mini-Neptunes of about 2 Earth radii exist at such ages (Bouma et al. 2022). A search for timing shifts in its transits found a candidate signal (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The timing signal is sparsely sampled and needs longer monitoring to confirm (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026). Young planets show such shifts more often than older ones (Lopez Murillo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1643b-Boumaetal2022","cite":"Bouma et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. G. Bouma, R. Kerr, J. L. Curtis et al.","title":"Kepler and the Behemoth: Three Mini-Neptunes in a 40 Million Year Old Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01112","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1643b-Murilloetal2026","cite":"Murillo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Ana Isabel Lopez Murillo, Andrew W. Mann, Madyson G. Barber et al.","title":"Searching for Transit Timing Variations in Young Transiting Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 57274 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Doppler observations at the Keck Observatory showed a planet of at least 0.4 Jupiter masses in a 32-day, nearly circular orbit (Fischer et al. 2012). It is the middle planet of a triple system found with a survey of nearby stars. The study uses the system to show that super-Earths are often in multi-planet systems, in 56 percent of cases against 20 percent for Jupiter-mass planets (Fischer et al. 2012).","system":"The inner planet, HIP 57274 b, is a super-Earth with a minimum mass of 11.6 Earth masses in an 8.135-day orbit. The outer planet, d, has a minimum mass of 0.53 Jupiter masses and a 432-day orbit (Fischer et al. 2012).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip57274c-Fischeretal2012","cite":"Fischer et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Eric Gaidos, Andrew W. Howard et al.","title":"M2K. II. A Triple-planet System Orbiting HIP 57274","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.2926","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-75 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper notes that giant planets are expected to be rare around low-mass stars, a key prediction of core-accretion theory (Jordán et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet is a giant that transits a late K or early M dwarf. Its mass was measured with the ESPRESSO spectrograph (Jordán et al. 2022). Three JWST transit observations gave a spectrum from 0.6 to 5.3 microns. The spectrum shows a slightly larger transit depth at shorter wavelengths, which could be hazes or contamination from starspots and faculae (bright patches) on the star. Evidence such as stellar rotation and spot crossings favours the stellar explanation. This leads to a remarkably low atmospheric metallicity, below solar (Ashtari et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Hazes and starspot contamination can both fit the spectrum, so the atmosphere is not settled (Ashtari et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats75b-Jordnetal2022","cite":"Jordán et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Andrés Jordán, J. D. Hartman, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"HATS-74Ab, HATS-75b, HATS-76b, and HATS-77b: Four Transiting Giant Planets Around K and M Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01928","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats75b-Ashtarietal2026","cite":"Ashtari et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Reza Ashtari, Jacob Lustig-Yaeger, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"GEMS JWST: HATS-75 b A Giant Planet with a Subsolar Metallicity Atmosphere Orbiting an M Dwarf","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07268","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-26 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a small M dwarf. Its transit lasts too long for a circular orbit around a star of this measured size. The authors say the long transits are likely due to the photoeccentric effect, and the transit fits hint at an eccentric orbit (Schlieder et al. 2016). The planet receives low incident flux from its host (Schlieder et al. 2016). Observations with the Spitzer telescope later confirmed the system and refined the planet's orbit and size (Beichman et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":"The eccentricity is only hinted at by the transit fits (Schlieder et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k226b-Schliederetal2016","cite":"Schlieder et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Joshua E. Schlieder, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Erik A. Petigura et al.","title":"Two Small Temperate Planets Transiting Nearby M Dwarfs in K2 Campaigns 0 and 1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02706","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k226b-Beichmanetal2016","cite":"Beichman et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Charles Beichman, John Livingston, Michael Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer Observations of Exoplanets Discovered with the Kepler K2 Mission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.01934","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1221 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was validated, which means statistically shown to be real, rather than weighed. Velocity data from the CHIRON spectrograph only set an upper limit on the mass of 1.1 Jupiter masses at 1 sigma and 3.5 at 3 sigma (Mann et al. 2023). The catalogue mass is therefore a limit, not a measurement. The 91.7-day period makes it one of few known planets with a period over 50 days around a star bright enough for follow-up. Its transit times shift, and its equilibrium temperature is about 440 K (Mann et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its true mass is not yet measured (Mann et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1221b-Mannetal2023","cite":"Mann et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Christopher Mann, David Lafreniére, Diana Dragomir et al.","title":"Validation of TOI-1221 b: A Warm Sub-Neptune Exhibiting Transit Timing Variations around a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.13651","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 20794 d":{"text":{"why":"The star was one of ten of the quietest from the HARPS programme, chosen to be watched with dense, continuous velocity data. The aim was to find tiny signals from very light planets in the habitable zone (Pepe et al. 2011).","observed":"Its star is a nearby, bright G dwarf poor in heavy elements, and the star's planetary system has been extensively debated (Nari et al. 2025). A reanalysis of more than 20 years of data, including new ESPRESSO measurements, confirms three planets with periods of 18.3, 89.7 and 647.6 days. The 647.6-day planet lies in the habitable zone (Nari et al. 2025). Stellar models suggest the star is about 9 billion years old (Medhi et al. 2026).","system":"An earlier analysis found four signals, at 18, 89, 147 and 330 days (Feng et al. 2017). The first three planets were reported with the discovery data (Pepe et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"A 330-day signal near the habitable zone is close to the one-year sampling period and needs more data to confirm (Feng et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd20794d-Pepeetal2011","cite":"Pepe et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Pepe, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Very low-mass planets around <ASTROBJ>HD 20794</ASTROBJ>, <ASTROBJ>HD 85512</ASTROBJ>, and <ASTROBJ>HD 192310</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3447","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794d-Narietal2025","cite":"Nari et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Nari, X. Dumusque, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"Revisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794: Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17092","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794d-Medhietal2026","cite":"Medhi et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mrinmay Medhi, Mami Deka, Krishna Saha et al.","title":"A theoretical study of the structure and elemental abundances of HD 20794","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd20794d-Fengetal2017","cite":"Feng et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"F. Feng, M. Tuomi, H. R. A. Jones","title":"Evidence for at least three planet candidates orbiting HD 20794","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.05124","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":13,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AF Lep b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found because its young host star shows an unexplained acceleration between Hipparcos and Gaia. It is the lowest-mass planet found by coupling such acceleration with direct imaging (Mesa et al. 2023; Franson et al. 2023). The combined orbit fit gives a mass of 3.2 Jupiter masses, 8.4 times the Earth-Sun distance, and a slightly eccentric orbit (Franson et al. 2023). JWST spectroscopy detected carbon dioxide, hydrogen sulfide, methane, carbon monoxide and water. Carbon, oxygen and sulfur are all enriched, at 2.9, 3.7 and 4.7 times solar (Xuan et al. 2026).","system":"The star is 24 million years old and belongs to the beta Pictoris moving group. A debris disc lies at about 50 times the Earth-Sun distance, but the planet is unlikely to have shaped it, so more planets may exist farther out (Franson et al. 2023). Star spin and orbit appear aligned (Franson et al. 2023).","history":"The sulfur enrichment implies the planet swallowed a lot of disc solids, about 56 Earth masses. The ratios fit formation near where it is now, not beyond the carbon monoxide snow line (Xuan et al. 2026).","open":"Possible outer planets that might sculpt the debris disc have not been found (Franson et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-aflepb-Mesaetal2023","cite":"Mesa et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"D. Mesa, R. Gratton, P. Kervella et al.","title":"AF Lep b: The lowest-mass planet detected by coupling astrometric and direct imaging data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06213","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aflepb-Fransonetal2023","cite":"Franson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kyle Franson, Brendan P. Bowler, Yifan Zhou et al.","title":"Astrometric Accelerations as Dynamical Beacons: A Giant Planet Imaged inside the Debris Disk of the Young Star AF Lep","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05420","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-aflepb-Xuanetal2026","cite":"Xuan et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jerry W. Xuan, William O. Balmer, Yayaati Chachan et al.","title":"A Sulfur-rich Atmosphere for the Young Jupiter Analog AF Lep b Reveals Significant Solid Accretion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10068","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":52,"about":29,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-238 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Velocity data from the ESPRESSO and HARPS spectrographs detected the planet's pull on its star and revealed a second transiting planet, TOI-238 c (Suárez Mascareño et al. 2024). A later study used CHEOPS to refine the radius, and tested whether the planet falls in a Hot Water World region of mass and radius. Planets there must hold some heavier volatiles, because a light gas envelope would not survive the star's radiation (Egger et al. 2025). Models of its upper atmosphere show that adding water or metals raises both heating and cooling, changing how fast gas escapes (Kubyshkina et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet carries a water-rich or metal-rich atmosphere is not settled (Kubyshkina et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi238b-Mascareoetal2024","cite":"Mascareño et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Suárez Mascareño, V. M. Passegger, J. I. González Hernández et al.","title":"TESS and ESPRESSO discover a super-Earth and a mini-Neptune orbiting the K-dwarf TOI-238*","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04113","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi238b-Eggeretal2025","cite":"Egger et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. A. Egger, D. Kubyshkina, Y. Alibert et al.","title":"Searching for hot water world candidates with CHEOPS: Refining the radii and analysing the internal structures and atmospheric lifetimes of TOI-238 b and TOI-1685 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07887","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi238b-Kubyshkinaetal2026","cite":"Kubyshkina et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Daria Kubyshkina, Jo Ann Egger, Caroline Piaulet-Ghorayeb","title":"Escape of water- and metal-enriched atmospheres from compact hot mini-Neptunes with CHAIN","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02083","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-4 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up was built to find Neptune-size and smaller planets around bright stars (Turner 2019).","observed":"NGTS-4 b orbits inside the Neptunian Desert, a zone of very short orbits where Neptune-size planets are scarce (West et al. 2019). Its density of 3.45 g per cubic centimetre fits either pure water or a rocky core wrapped in a volatile envelope (West et al. 2019). Its transit is only 0.13 per cent deep, the shallowest transiting system found from the ground and the smallest planet found by a wide-field ground-based survey (West et al. 2019). Confirming such a faint dip needed follow-up photometry from four different facilities (West et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Strong extreme-ultraviolet and X-ray light from the star is likely to strip mass from the planet (West et al. 2019). It may survive because of an unusually massive core, or because it moved inward only after the star's early active phase had faded (West et al. 2019).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts4b-Turner2019","cite":"Turner 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Oliver Turner","title":"New Discoveries and high precision photometric follow-up from NGTS","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts4b-Westetal2019","cite":"West et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Richard G. West, Edward Gillen, Daniel Bayliss et al.","title":"NGTS-4b: A sub-Neptune transiting in the desert","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00678","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts4b-Westetal2022","cite":"West et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"R. G. West, E. Gillen, D. Bayliss et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: NGTS-4b A sub-Neptune transiting in the desert (West+, 2019)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 40307 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 40307 is a bright, nearby K2 dwarf that was known to host three very low-mass planets (Gillon et al. 2008). Astronomers planned 10.5 hours of Spitzer Space Telescope observations at the expected transit time of the innermost planet, hoping to catch the first transiting super-Earth (Gillon et al. 2008). With a minimum mass of 4.21 Earth masses and a 4.3-day orbit, the chance of a transit was only 8 per cent if the orbit is randomly tilted (Gillon et al. 2008). Radial-velocity measurements with the HARPS spectrograph, together with star-activity indicators, are public (Diaz et al. 2015).","system":"The system has several low-mass planets around a nearby K dwarf, 12.8 parsecs away (Gillon et al. 2008).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd40307b-Gillonetal2008","cite":"Gillon et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"Michael Gillon, Drake Deming, Brice-Olivier Demory et al.","title":"HD 40307b, the first transiting Super-Earth?","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd40307b-Diazetal2015","cite":"Diaz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"R. F. Diaz, D. Segransan, S. Udry et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD1461, HD40307, and HD204313 radial velocities (Diaz+, 2016)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35850134","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 88133 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up chose stars rich in heavy elements, because the fraction of stars with planets rises steeply with metal content (Fischer et al. 2005).","observed":"Light from the planet itself was reported earlier in several observing runs with the Keck NIRSPEC spectrograph in the infrared L band (Buzard et al. 2021). A reanalysis found that six such runs would not have revealed the planet unless it were unreasonably large (Buzard et al. 2021). Many more than six runs would be needed for a strong detection, partly because the star is large and cool (Buzard et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether its light has truly been detected is in doubt (Buzard et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd88133b-Fischeretal2005","cite":"Fischer et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"D. A. Fischer, G. W. Marcy, R. P. Butler et al.","title":"N2K: A Targeted Search for Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd88133b-Buzardetal2021","cite":"Buzard et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Cam Buzard, Danielle Piskorz, Alexandra C. Lockwood et al.","title":"Reinvestigation of the Multiepoch Direct Detections of HD 88133 b and Upsilon Andromedae b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.13275","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-22 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Ground-based radial-velocity measurements could not confirm CoRoT-22 b, because its signal is too small to reach their limits (Moutou et al. 2014). Instead, the planet was validated statistically. Radial-velocity limits, adaptive-optics imaging and the shape of the transit were combined with the known frequency of stellar populations, planets and multiple star systems (Moutou et al. 2014). The planet explanation came out at least 1400 times more probable than any false-positive explanation (Moutou et al. 2014). The star is a metal-rich, solar-like dwarf of 1.099 solar masses (Moutou et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":"Its mass has not been measured directly (Moutou et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot22b-Moutouetal2014","cite":"Moutou et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"C. Moutou, J. M. Almenara, R. F. Díaz et al.","title":"CoRoT-22 b: a validated 4.9 R<SUB>⊕</SUB> exoplanet in 10-d orbit","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.2576","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-182 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-182 b is a bloated planet of sub-Saturn mass, 0.148 Jupiter masses but 0.850 Jupiter radii, so its gas envelope is puffed up and its density is low (Nielsen et al. 2019). It orbits a metal-rich G5 star with iron abundance [Fe/H] of 0.27 (Nielsen et al. 2019). Radial velocities from CORALIE and HARPS, and follow-up photometry from EulerCam, TRAPPIST-North and -South, and SPECULOOS, pinned down its properties (Nielsen et al. 2019). It was announced together with three hot Jupiters from WASP-South (Nielsen et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp182b-Nielsenetal2019","cite":"Nielsen et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. D. Nielsen, F. Bouchy, O. D. Turner et al.","title":"WASP-169, WASP-171, WASP-175, and WASP-182: three hot Jupiters and one bloated sub-Saturn mass planet discovered by WASP-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10388","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HR 5183 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey was designed to observe the star before, during and after the planet's closest approach, which gave tight limits on its orbit (Blunt et al. 2019).","observed":"HR 5183 b has a minimum mass of about 3 Jupiter masses and a very eccentric orbit, with eccentricity near 0.84 (Blunt et al. 2019). It swings from inside Jupiter's orbit to beyond Neptune's (Blunt et al. 2019). Two decades of radial velocities from Keck, McDonald Observatory and the Automated Planet Finder caught the 80 metres per second signal from its closest approach, which happened in January 2018 (Blunt et al. 2019). Its orbital period is about 74 years, with wide uncertainty (Blunt et al. 2019). It was reported as the longest-period planet found with radial velocities (Blunt et al. 2019).","system":"A bound star more than 15,000 au away is too distant to affect the planet (Blunt et al. 2019).","history":"No planet or star was found that could have stirred up the eccentric orbit, which may mean the culprit was ejected from the system (Blunt et al. 2019).","open":"Its path takes it beyond 30 au from the star, so direct imaging at 10 microns and Gaia astrometry both look promising (Blunt et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hr5183b-Bluntetal2019","cite":"Blunt et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Sarah Blunt, Michael Endl, Lauren M. Weiss et al.","title":"Radial Velocity Discovery of an Eccentric Jovian World Orbiting at 18 au","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09925","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":0,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 111998 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looked at 108 AF-type stars of 1.1 to 3.6 solar masses, a group that earlier radial-velocity surveys avoided because their few spectral lines and fast spin make precise measurements difficult (Borgniet et al. 2017).","observed":"HD 111998 b has a minimum mass of 4.51 Jupiter masses and orbits an F6 dwarf (Borgniet et al. 2017). The survey aimed to estimate how often giant planets and brown dwarfs occur around such stars (Borgniet et al. 2017). A later study of F-type stars found that the planet stays inside its star's habitable zone throughout its orbit (Patel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd111998b-Borgnietetal2017","cite":"Borgniet et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"S. Borgniet, A.-M. Lagrange, N. Meunier et al.","title":"Extrasolar planets and brown dwarfs around AF-type stars. IX. The HARPS southern sample","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.08257","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd111998b-Pateletal2024","cite":"Patel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Shaan D. Patel, Manfred Cuntz, Nevin N. Weinberg","title":"Statistics and Habitability of F-type Star─Planet Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15826","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-24 b orbits 0.037 au from its F8-9 star, and the star is estimated to be 3.8 billion years old, though with only weak limits (Street et al. 2010). Measurements of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the distortion in starlight as the planet crosses a spinning star, show a prograde orbit well aligned with the star's spin (Simpson et al. 2011). The host is cooler than 6250 K and fits the trend that cooler stars have low obliquities (Simpson et al. 2011). Spitzer has also observed the planet, and thirteen transits across three systems were re-measured with defocussed telescopes (Southworth et al. 2014).","system":"A star near WASP-24 on the sky is an eclipsing binary (Southworth et al. 2014).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp24b-Streetetal2010","cite":"Street et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"R. A. Street, E. Simpson, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"WASP-24 b: A New Transiting Close-in Hot Jupiter Orbiting a Late F-star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637X/720/1/337","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp24b-Simpsonetal2011","cite":"Simpson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"E. K. Simpson, D. Pollacco, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"The spin-orbit angles of the transiting exoplanets WASP-1b, WASP-24b, WASP-38b and HAT-P-8b from Rossiter-McLaughlin observations","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.5664","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp24b-Southworthetal2014","cite":"Southworth et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"John Southworth, T. C. Hinse, M. Burgdorf et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VI. WASP-24, WASP-25 and WASP-26","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.6253","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-131 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-131 b is a bloated Saturn-mass planet of 0.27 Jupiter masses and 1.22 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2017). Its large scale height, the thickness of its atmosphere, and its bright V = 10.1 host star make it a good target for atmospheric studies (Hellier et al. 2017). ESPRESSO observations of two transits showed that its orbit is misaligned and nearly retrograde, with a projected obliquity of 162.4 degrees (Doyle et al. 2023). The true three-dimensional tilt is 123.7 degrees (Doyle et al. 2023).","system":"The host star rotates differentially, with a shear of 0.61, and is tilted about 41 degrees to our line of sight (Doyle et al. 2023). Imaging found a candidate faint companion near the star (Bohn et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp131b-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp131b-Doyleetal2023","cite":"Doyle et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"L. Doyle, H. M. Cegla, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-131 b with ESPRESSO - I. A bloated sub-Saturn on a polar orbit around a differentially rotating solar-type star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12163","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp131b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 36384 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 36384 is an M giant, and five years of radial velocities from the Bohyunsan spectrograph show a long-period variation compatible with a planet or brown dwarf companion (Lee et al. 2017). However, the star also brightens and dims with a period very close to that of the radial-velocity signal, and the amplitude changes with time (Lee et al. 2017). Such behaviour points to stellar pulsations as the cause of the variation instead of a planet (Lee et al. 2017). The star sits on the asymptotic giant branch, after the helium flash and leaving the giant clump (Lee et al. 2017).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether a planet exists here, or the signal comes from the star itself, is not settled (Lee et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd36384b-Leeetal2017","cite":"Lee et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Gwanghui Jeong, Myeong-Gu Park et al.","title":"Search for Exoplanets around Northern Circumpolar Stars. II. The Detection of Radial Velocity Variations in M Giant Stars HD 36384, HD 52030, and HD 208742","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10009","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-411 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-411 d, a candidate with a 58-day period, was confirmed by transit timing, the shifts in transit times caused by the pull of neighbouring planets (Sun et al. 2019). Its mass is 15.2 Earth masses (Sun et al. 2019). Assuming the system is nearly flat, the same data revealed a non-transiting planet, Kepler-411 e, of 10.8 Earth masses on a 31.5-day orbit that interacts strongly with Kepler-411 d (Sun et al. 2019). Kepler-411 d crosses the star at a latitude of -49 degrees, and its transits across starspots were used to measure how the star's rotation changes with latitude (Araújo & Valio 2021).","system":"The star hosts at least four planets and is very active, with 198 starspots and 65 superflares recorded over about 600 days (Araújo & Valio 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler411d-Sunetal2019","cite":"Sun et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. Sun, P. Ioannidis, S. Gu et al.","title":"Kepler-411: a four-planet system with an active host star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.09719","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler411d-ArajoValio2021","cite":"Araújo & Valio 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Alexandre Araújo, Adriana Valio","title":"Kepler-411 Differential Rotation from Three Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.07692","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-102 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbours Kepler-102 d and e. Kepler-102 has five tightly packed transiting planets with periods of 5 to 28 days (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The star is active, which makes radial-velocity masses hard to measure (Brinkman et al. 2023). Kepler-102 d has a mass of about 2.5 Earth masses and a density like Earth's, while Kepler-102 e has a mass of 4.7 Earth masses (Brinkman et al. 2023).","system":"Computer simulations with plausible masses make such packed systems unstable within 100 million years, yet the star is over 2 billion years old, so the planets must have avoided that (Volk & Malhotra 2020). The planets lie near, but not locked in, orbital resonances (Volk & Malhotra 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler102c-VolkMalhotra2020","cite":"Volk & Malhotra 2020","year":"2020","authors":"K. Volk, R. Malhotra","title":"Kepler-102: a case study for using dynamical constraints to characterize exoplanet systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler102c-Brinkmanetal2023","cite":"Brinkman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Casey L. Brinkman, James Cadman, Lauren Weiss et al.","title":"Kepler-102: Masses and Compositions for a Super-Earth and Sub-Neptune Orbiting an Active Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05196","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 112300 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up looked for faint, long-period velocity changes in bright M giants over 16 years (Lee et al. 2023).","observed":"HD 112300 is a red giant, and its companion has a minimum mass of about 15.8 Jupiter masses on a 466.63-day orbit at 1.33 au (Lee et al. 2023). Nine M giants were watched with the Bohyunsan spectrograph from 2005 to 2022 (Lee et al. 2023). Two of them showed companions, this one and another around HD 6860 (Lee et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd112300b-Leeetal2023","cite":"Lee et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Byeong-Cheol Lee, Hee-Jin Do, Myeong-Gu Park et al.","title":"Long-period radial velocity variations of nine M red giants. The detection of substellar companions around HD 6860 and HD 112300","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15897","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3090 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its minimum mass is 10.0 Earth masses on a 15.9-day orbit (Lamontagne et al. 2026). The star rotates every 18 days, close to the planet's period, so starspots could mimic a planet signal. A model combining TESS photometry and stellar temperature measurements separated the two (Lamontagne et al. 2026).","system":"The inner planet GJ 3090 b transits, and is a good candidate for a water-rich world with a volatile envelope (Lamontagne et al. 2026). It is on a retrograde orbit, with a three-dimensional tilt of about 136 degrees (Carteret et al. 2026). No massive outer planets or wide stellar companions were found (Carteret et al. 2026). A further non-transiting candidate with a 12.7-day period remains under study (Lamontagne et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3090c-Lamontagneetal2026","cite":"Lamontagne et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Pierrot Lamontagne, Drew Weisserman, Charles Cadieux et al.","title":"NIRPS tightens the mass estimate of GJ 3090 b and detects a planet near the stellar rotation period","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09330","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3090c-Carteretetal2026","cite":"Carteret et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Yann Carteret, Vincent Bourrier, Léna Parc et al.","title":"Upside down: GJ 3090 b the first retrograde exoplanet around an M dwarf detected with NIRPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24870","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-100 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-100 b is a highly irradiated, bloated hot Jupiter orbiting a hot F2 star of 6900 K (Addison et al. 2018). Its orbit is nearly polar, with a sky-projected spin-orbit angle of 79 degrees (Addison et al. 2018). TESS phase-curve observations found marginal evidence that the brightest region of its atmosphere is shifted eastward from the point facing the star (Wong et al. 2020). Searches in TESS data found no additional transiting planets down to mini-Neptune or Neptune size, and no transit-timing variations (Maciejewski 2020).","system":"","history":"The lack of companions fits the idea that hot Jupiters reached tight orbits by high-eccentricity migration (Maciejewski 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp100b-Addisonetal2018","cite":"Addison et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"B. C. Addison, Songhu Wang, M. C. Johnson et al.","title":"Stellar Obliquities and Planetary Alignments (SOPA). I. Spin-Orbit Measurements of Three Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-72b, WASP-100b, and WASP-109b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00314","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp100b-Wongetal2020","cite":"Wong et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ian Wong, Avi Shporer, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"Systematic Phase Curve Study of Known Transiting Systems from Year One of the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06407","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp100b-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 6718 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Combining radial velocities with Gaia astrometry, HD 6718 b was found to have a true mass above 13.5 Jupiter masses at 3 sigma confidence, which would put it among brown dwarfs rather than planets (Kiefer et al. 2021). Radial velocities alone give only a minimum mass, because they cannot show how tilted the orbit is (Kiefer et al. 2021). The system was also listed among nearby stars where Earth-like planets in the habitable zone could survive on stable orbits (Kokaia et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf depends on its true mass (Kiefer et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd6718b-Kieferetal2021","cite":"Kiefer et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"F. Kiefer, G. Hébrard, A. Lecavelier des Etangs et al.","title":"Determining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia. Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14164","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd6718b-Kokaiaetal2020","cite":"Kokaia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Giorgi Kokaia, Melvyn B. Davies, Alexander J. Mustill","title":"Resilient habitability of nearby exoplanet systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07573","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":0,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4201 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-4201 b has a mass of 2.59 Jupiter masses and is one of the most massive planets transiting an M dwarf (Delamer et al. 2023). It is about 0.4 per cent of its star's mass (Delamer et al. 2023). Its heavy-element content may be comparable to the total dust in a typical protoplanetary disc (Delamer et al. 2023). Transit-shape measurements with the MAROON-X spectrograph found the orbit well aligned with the star's spin, with a true tilt of 21.3 degrees. This was the first such measurement for an M dwarf with a hot Jupiter (Gan et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Forming such a giant stretches core accretion, so it must have formed much earlier in the disc's life, or through gravitational instability (Delamer et al. 2023). Close-in giants around M dwarfs tend to favour metal-rich stars (Hartman et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4201b-Delameretal2023","cite":"Delamer et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Megan Delamer, Shubham Kanodia, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"TOI-4201: An Early M Dwarf Hosting a Massive Transiting Jupiter Stretching Theories of Core Accretion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06880","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4201b-Ganetal2023","cite":"Gan et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Megan Delamer, Shubham Kanodia, Caleb I. Cañas et al.","title":"TOI-4201: An Early M Dwarf Hosting a Massive Transiting Jupiter Stretching Theories of Core Accretion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06880","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4201b-Hartmanetal2023","cite":"Hartman et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, Z. Csubry et al.","title":"TOI 4201 b and TOI 5344 b: Discovery of Two Transiting Giant Planets around M-dwarf Stars and Revised Parameters for Three Others","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06809","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 238 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is about the size of Mars: its radius is 1.064 times that of Mars (Tey et al. 2024). The star sits close to the southern ecliptic pole, so the TESS space telescope can watch it almost continuously when it observes the southern sky (Tey et al. 2024). The signal was confirmed by ruling out or making highly unlikely each of the false-positive explanations, using archival data and ground-based follow-up (Tey et al. 2024). The small size of the star and its high proper motion, meaning it drifts quickly across the sky, made this check easier (Tey et al. 2024).","system":"The host is an M2.5 dwarf, a small, cool red star, at 15.2 pc (Tey et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj238b-Teyetal2024","cite":"Tey et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Evan Tey, Avi Shporer, Zifan Lin et al.","title":"GJ 238 b: A 0.57 Earth Radius Planet Orbiting an M2.5 Dwarf Star at 15.2 pc","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18199","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-432 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour Kepler-432 b. Planet c does not cross the face of its star. It was picked up by years of radial velocity monitoring, which tracks the star's back-and-forth wobble, and it has a minimum mass of 2.43 Jupiter masses and an orbit of 406.2 days (Quinn et al. 2015). Planet b is a giant of 5.41 Jupiter masses on a stretched orbit with an eccentricity of 0.51 (Quinn et al. 2015).","system":"The host is an evolved star that has swollen to 4.06 times the radius of the Sun and is climbing the red giant branch (Quinn et al. 2015; Ciceri et al. 2015). A faint star lies 1.1 arcseconds from it on the sky, too faint to disturb the transits of planet b (Ciceri et al. 2015). Kepler-432 occupies an almost empty region of parameter space, together with Kepler-56 and Kepler-91 (Ciceri et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Models of how planets fare as their star evolves predict that planet b will most likely be swallowed by the star before it reaches the tip of the red giant branch (Ortiz et al. 2015). A steady drift in the radial velocities suggests a further, third object in the system (Ortiz et al. 2015)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler432c-Quinnetal2015","cite":"Quinn et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Samuel N. Quinn, Timothy. R. White, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Kepler-432: A Red Giant Interacting with One of its Two Long-period Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4666","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler432c-Cicerietal2015","cite":"Ciceri et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"S. Ciceri, J. Lillo-Box, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Kepler-432 b: a massive planet in a highly eccentric orbit transiting a red giant","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.2999","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler432c-Ortizetal2015","cite":"Ortiz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Mauricio Ortiz, Davide Gandolfi, Sabine Reffert et al.","title":"Kepler-432 b: a massive warm Jupiter in a 52-day eccentric orbit transiting a giant star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.3000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 32963 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up is a long-term radial velocity programme, and the team stresses the value of such surveys because Jupiter shaped the early Solar System (Rowan et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet is a Jupiter analog, a giant planet on a long orbit around a Sun-like star. The signal repeats every 6.49 years and implies a minimum mass of 0.7 Jupiter masses (Rowan et al. 2016). The same survey covered 1122 stars and, after correcting for the planets it could not have seen, estimated that about 3% of such stars have a Jupiter analog (Rowan et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd32963b-Rowanetal2016","cite":"Rowan et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Dominick Rowan, Stefano Meschiari, Gregory Laughlin et al.","title":"The Lick-Carnegie Exoplanet Survey: HD 32963—A New Jupiter Analog Orbiting a Sun-like Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.00417","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-39 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-39 b is a puffed-up, low-density giant. Its density is only 0.14 times that of Jupiter, and only WASP-17 b and WASP-31 b were known to be less dense when it was announced (Faedi et al. 2011). Its equilibrium temperature, the heat expected from starlight alone, is 1116 K (Faedi et al. 2011). It was one of the first giant planets observed in the JWST Early Release Science programme (Carone et al. 2023). Modelling suggests the atmosphere is cool enough for clouds to form all around the planet, and that a mix of silicates and metal oxides makes up the cloud tops (Carone et al. 2023). Earlier Hubble-era spectra were better matched by a grey, featureless cloud layer than by fine haze (Barstow et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a late G-type dwarf of about 9 billion years, with slightly less iron than the Sun (Faedi et al. 2011). Its orbital alignment with the star's spin was measured with HARPS-N (Mancini et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Different analysis methods give different answers for the planet's metal content and mineral ratios (Carone et al. 2023). Clouds can explain the JWST data without requiring more than 100 times the solar metal content (Carone et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp39b-Faedietal2011","cite":"Faedi et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Faedi, S. C. C. Barros, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-39b: a highly inflated Saturn-mass planet orbiting a late G-type star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.1375","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp39b-Caroneetal2023","cite":"Carone et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Ludmila Carone, David A. Lewis, Dominic Samra et al.","title":"WASP-39b: exo-Saturn with patchy cloud composition, moderate metallicity, and underdepleted S/O","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.08492","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp39b-Barstowetal2017","cite":"Barstow et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"J. K. Barstow, S. Aigrain, P. G. J. Irwin et al.","title":"A Consistent Retrieval Analysis of 10 Hot Jupiters Observed in Transmission","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01841","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp39b-Mancinietal2018","cite":"Mancini et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"L. Mancini, M. Esposito, E. Covino et al.","title":"The GAPS programme with HARPS-N at TNG. XVI. Measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect of transiting planetary systems HAT-P-3, HAT-P-12, HAT-P-22, WASP-39, and WASP-60","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.03859","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":39,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 9618 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HIP 9618 b is a warm Neptune with a mass of 10.0 Earth masses (Osborn et al. 2023). Interior models give it a hydrogen-rich gas layer making up about 6.8 per cent of its mass (Osborn et al. 2023). Its 20.77-day period was clear from the start. For its neighbour HIP 9618 c, only two transits were seen with a 680-day gap between them, so the true period was unclear until the CHEOPS space telescope targeted the possible periods and found 52.56 days (Osborn et al. 2023).","system":"A steady drift in the radial velocities, combined with imaging and astrometry, points to a low-mass companion of about 0.08 solar masses orbiting at about 26 au (Osborn et al. 2023). The host is a bright, Sun-like star (Osborn et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip9618b-Osbornetal2023","cite":"Osborn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"H. P. Osborn, G. Nowak, G. Hebrard et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HIP 9618 light and radial velocity curves (Osborn+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-266 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-266 b is an ultra-short-period planet, circling its star every 0.66 days (Rodriguez et al. 2018). Its orbit is tilted by more than 12 degrees relative to the other planets in the system (Rodriguez et al. 2018). Because it is so close to the star, it still crosses the star's face as seen from Earth, which gives a rare chance to compare a misaligned planet directly with its neighbours (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","system":"The system is compact and holds several small planets around a nearby K star, a set of sub-Neptunes and super-Earths (Rodriguez et al. 2018). It is the system with the most planets found by the K2 mission (Rodriguez et al. 2018). A transiting planet candidate was also identified around a nearby companion star that moves through space with K2-266 (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","history":"How the planet reached its tilted orbit is a key question (Rodriguez et al. 2018).","open":"How the misaligned orbit came about is not known (Rodriguez et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2266b-Rodriguezetal2018","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Joseph Rodriguez, Juliette Becker, Jason Eastman et al.","title":"K2-266: A Compact Multi-Planet System With A Significantly Misaligned Ultra Short Period Planet","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #233","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 328 c":{"text":{"why":"The work targeted systems that already host a cold Jupiter, a giant planet on a long orbit, to see whether smaller planets also lie closer in (Pinamonti et al. 2023).","observed":"The star was one of three late-type dwarfs, each known to host a long-period giant planet, that were monitored with high-cadence, high-precision radial velocities from HARPS-N (Pinamonti et al. 2023). The team combined the new data with earlier measurements to refine the known orbits and to search for additional inner planets, while modelling out the star's own activity signals (Pinamonti et al. 2023). The search looked for short-period sub-Neptunes with minimum masses below 30 Earth masses (Pinamonti et al. 2023). The results were used to estimate how often sub-Neptunes occur in systems with cold Jupiters, compared with the rate around ordinary M dwarfs (Pinamonti et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj328c-Pinamontietal2023","cite":"Pinamonti et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Pinamonti, D. Barbato, A. Sozzetti et al.","title":"The GAPS programme at TNG. XLVI. Deep search for low-mass planets in late-dwarf systems hosting cold Jupiters","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04419","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1634 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour TOI-1634 b. Planet b is an ultra-short-period planet with a 0.989-day orbit and a radius of about 1.79 Earth radii (Cloutier et al. 2021). Its size and period place it inside the M-dwarf radius valley, a size range where few planets are found around small red stars (Cloutier et al. 2021). Its composition can help decide whether such planets form late as rocky worlds or lose a primordial gas envelope to heating (Cloutier et al. 2021). Mass measurements used 32 radial velocity measurements from HARPS-N (Cloutier et al. 2021).","system":"The host is a nearby M2 dwarf with a radius of 0.450 times that of the Sun (Cloutier et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1634c-Cloutieretal2021","cite":"Cloutier et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ryan Cloutier, David Charbonneau, Keivan G. Stassun et al.","title":"TOI-1634 b: An Ultra-short-period Keystone Planet Sitting inside the M-dwarf Radius Valley","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.12790","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-8 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up, NGTS, set out to find Neptune-sized or bigger planets (Costes et al. 2023).","observed":"NGTS-8 b has about Jupiter's mass and size, 0.93 Jupiter masses and 1.09 Jupiter radii, giving a density of 0.89 g per cubic centimetre (Costes et al. 2020). Statistically, planets of this mass and heating are expected to be puffed up beyond their normal size (Costes et al. 2020). Instead, its radius matches theoretical models of non-inflated planets (Costes et al. 2020). Its star was watched by a single NGTS camera over a 227-night span in 2016 (Costes et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a K0 dwarf (Costes et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts8b-Costesetal2023","cite":"Costes et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. C. Costes, C. A. Watson, C. Belardi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: NGTS-8b and NGTS-9b non-inflated hot Jupiters (Costes+, 2020)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ngts8b-Costesetal2020","cite":"Costes et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Jean C. Costes, Christopher A. Watson, Claudia Belardi et al.","title":"NGTS-8b and NGTS-9b: two non-inflated hot Jupiters","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02814","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5786 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-5786 b is Saturn-like, with a radius of 8.54 Earth radii and a mass of 73 Earth masses (Thomas et al. 2025). Its 12.78-day orbit puts it just above the upper edge of the Neptune desert, a zone close to stars where Neptune-sized planets are scarce (Thomas et al. 2025). Interior models suggest that a hydrogen-helium envelope makes up about 74% of its mass (Thomas et al. 2025). It was characterised together with a second sub-Saturn, TOI-5108 b, using spectrographs at the Wendelstein and Haute-Provence observatories (Thomas et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a slightly dim F-type star of visual magnitude 10.2 (Thomas et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5786b-Thomasetal2025","cite":"Thomas et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Luis Thomas, Guillaume Hébrard, Hanna Kellermann et al.","title":"TOI-5108 b and TOI 5786 b: Two transiting sub-Saturns detected and characterized with TESS, MaHPS, and SOPHIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.03803","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-530 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant planets around M dwarfs are predicted to be infrequent by core accretion theory, where planets grow by piling up solid material, so each one helps test how such planets form and migrate (Gan et al. 2022).","observed":"TOI-530 b is a Saturn-like planet with a radius of 0.83 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.37 Jupiter masses (Gan et al. 2022). It is the sixth transiting giant planet known around an M-type star (Gan et al. 2022). Its star, at V = 15.4, is one of the faintest accessible to ground-based spectroscopy (Gan et al. 2022). The planet was verified with multiwavelength photometry, SPIRou spectroscopy and high-resolution imaging (Gan et al. 2022).","system":"The host is an M0.5 dwarf with a mass of 0.53 solar masses (Gan et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"A possible shortage of hot, massive giant planets around M dwarfs is noted, for separations below 0.1 au (Gan et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi530b-Ganetal2022","cite":"Gan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Tianjun Gan, Zitao Lin, Sharon Xuesong Wang et al.","title":"TOI-530b: a giant planet transiting an M-dwarf detected by TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04220","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-167 e":{"text":{"why":"Systems with outer giants and inner super-Earths help test how planets form, and Kepler-167 is the only known system with both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant beyond 1 au (Chachan et al. 2022).","observed":"Kepler-167 e was reported as the first validated transiting Jupiter analog (Kipping et al. 2016). It has a radius of 0.91 Jupiter radii, a low orbital eccentricity of 0.06 and an equilibrium temperature of 131 K (Kipping et al. 2016). Radial velocity monitoring gave a mass of 1.01 Jupiter masses (Chachan et al. 2022). Structure models suggest it holds about 66 Earth masses of solids, which is far richer in heavy elements than its Sun-like host (Chachan et al. 2022). Two transits and continuous coverage fixed the period at 1071.2 days (Kipping et al. 2016).","system":"Three super-Earths on compact orbits accompany it, leaving a large gap in transiting planets out to the habitable zone (Kipping et al. 2016). Its host is a K4 dwarf (Kipping et al. 2016).","history":"Pebble accretion models, where a planet's core grows from small solid grains, show that massive, metal-rich discs can form both a giant and inner super-Earths (Chachan et al. 2022).","open":"A JWST transit could detect whether the planet is flattened by its spin, if the spin is tilted by about 20 degrees or more relative to the orbit (Liu et al. 2025). Rings, moons and atmospheric species of cold giants may also be detectable (Liu et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler167e-Chachanetal2022","cite":"Chachan et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yayaati Chachan, Paul A. Dalba, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-167e as a Probe of the Formation Histories of Cold Giants with Inner Super-Earths","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.00747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler167e-Kippingetal2016","cite":"Kipping et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. M. Kipping, G. Torres, C. Henze et al.","title":"A Transiting Jupiter Analog","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.00042","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler167e-Liuetal2025","cite":"Liu et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Quanyi Liu, Wei Zhu, Yifan Zhou et al.","title":"Detecting Planetary Oblateness in the Era of JWST: A Case Study of Kepler-167e","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11644","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-4 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"KELT-4 A b is an inflated hot Jupiter, with a radius of 1.699 Jupiter radii and a mass of 0.902 Jupiter masses (Eastman et al. 2016). At V of about 10, its star is the brightest host among known inflated hot Jupiters, those larger than 1.5 Jupiter radii (Eastman et al. 2016). That brightness makes it a good target for studying why such planets swell (Eastman et al. 2016).","system":"The host is an F star that is the brightest part of a hierarchical triple: a close star pair, KELT-4 BC, lies at a projected distance of 328 au (Eastman et al. 2016). Its closeness to Earth allows dynamical studies with continued imaging and radial velocities (Eastman et al. 2016).","history":"Work on six hot Jupiters in binary systems, KELT-4 among them, examined whether Kozai-Lidov migration could explain their close orbits (Vazquez & Eastman 2025). In that process, a stellar companion makes the planet's orbital eccentricity and tilt swing back and forth, and tides then pull it into a close circular orbit (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt4ab-Eastmanetal2016","cite":"Eastman et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Jason D. Eastman, Thomas G. Beatty, Robert J. Siverd et al.","title":"KELT-4Ab: An Inflated Hot Jupiter Transiting the Bright (V ∼ 10) Component of a Hierarchical Triple","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.00015","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt4ab-VazquezEastman2025","cite":"Vazquez & Eastman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacqueline Vazquez, Jason Eastman","title":"Understanding Hot Jupiter Migration for Six Binary Star Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-31 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"NGTS-31 b is a hot Jupiter with a radius of 1.61 Jupiter radii and a mass of 1.12 Jupiter masses, and it orbits a slightly evolved subgiant star (Vines et al. 2025). The heating it receives is well above the level at which planets are known to inflate, and it shows signs of being inflated (Vines et al. 2025). Its equilibrium temperature is significantly lower than that of other hot Jupiters of similar mass and radius (Vines et al. 2025). That raises the question of how important other inflation mechanisms are (Vines et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a subgiant, a star that has started to swell as it ages (Vines et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"What causes the unusually large radius of NGTS-31 b is not settled (Vines et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts31b-Vinesetal2025","cite":"Vines et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jose I. Vines, James S. Jenkins, David R. Anderson et al.","title":"NGTS-31b and NGTS-32b: two inflated hot Jupiters orbiting subgiant stars","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stae2616","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-39 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-39 b, also called KOI-423 b, sits in the overlap between massive planets and brown dwarfs, with a mass of 18.0 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.22 Jupiter radii (Bouchy et al. 2011). It orbits an F7 subgiant star every 21 days on a slightly eccentric orbit of 0.12 (Bouchy et al. 2011). The shape of its transit gave the first tentative detection of an oblate, flattened planet outside the Solar System, with an oblateness of 0.22 (Zhu et al. 2014). Gaia distances later changed its radius estimate significantly (Carmichael 2023).","system":"KOI-423 b and KOI-522 b belong to a growing population of massive companions close to F-type stars (Bouchy et al. 2011).","history":"","open":"A large oblateness would need the brown dwarf's tidal dissipation to be two orders of magnitude weaker than in Jupiter and Saturn, otherwise tides would have slowed its spin (Zhu et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler39b-Bouchyetal2011","cite":"Bouchy et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"F. Bouchy, A. S. Bonomo, A. Santerne et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. III. KOI-423b: an 18 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> transiting companion around an F7IV star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.3225","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler39b-Zhuetal2014","cite":"Zhu et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Wei Zhu, Chelsea X. Huang, George Zhou et al.","title":"Constraining the Oblateness of Kepler Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.0361","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler39b-Carmichael2023","cite":"Carmichael 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Theron W. Carmichael","title":"Improved radius determinations for the transiting brown dwarf population in the era of Gaia and TESS","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.02502","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-56 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-56 has two inner planets that orbit in nearly the same plane, and that plane is strongly tilted relative to the spin of the parent star (Li et al. 2014). A third, outer planet of 3.3 Jupiter masses is also known, but the tilt of its orbit is not known (Li et al. 2014). Modelling of the measured line-of-sight angles can narrow down the true tilts, but the answer depends on how the system started out (Li et al. 2014).","system":"Two inner planets lie close together in one plane, with an outer planet of 3.3 Jupiter masses farther out (Li et al. 2014).","history":"If the star's spin began aligned with the two inner planets, the outer planet's orbit must be tilted by 20 to 160 degrees from theirs (Li et al. 2014). If the spin began aligned with the combined spin of the whole system, a tilt near 40 or 130 degrees is favoured (Li et al. 2014).","open":"The true angle between the star's spin and the planets' orbits has not been measured. Measuring it with starquakes (asteroseismology) and the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the way a transit distorts the star's light, would give tighter limits (Li et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler56c-Lietal2014","cite":"Li et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Gongjie Li, Smadar Naoz, Francesca Valsecchi et al.","title":"Kepler 56: Present and Future Configuration and Obliquity","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #224","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4479 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was confirmed by comparing its transits in several colours of light, which rules out blending with a nearby star as the cause of the dip (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022).","observed":"Light curves from the TESS space telescope and from ground-based instruments (MuSCAT2, MuSCAT3 and SINISTRO) were analysed together, using transits seen in several colours at once (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022). This set limits on contamination from other stars and showed that the dips are caused by a planet-sized body (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022). With an orbit of 1.15890 days, the planet lies within the Neptune desert, a range of short orbits where Neptune-sized planets are scarce (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022). It is the largest nearly ultra-short-period planet known around an M dwarf (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Spectroscopic follow-up and studies of how planets form and evolve are expected to benefit from this rare case among M dwarf planets (Esparza-Borges et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4479b-EsparzaBorgesetal2022","cite":"Esparza-Borges et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"E. Esparza-Borges, H. Parviainen, F. Murgas et al.","title":"A hot sub-Neptune in the desert and a temperate super-Earth around faint M dwarfs. Color validation of TOI-4479b and TOI-2081b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.10643","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TYC 8998-760-1 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The James Webb Space Telescope has been used to take spectra of young giant planets and brown dwarfs at wide separations from Sun-like stars, using its NIRSpec integral field unit (Hoch et al. 2024). The programmes included the system YSES-1, which has two young Jupiter-like planets far from a Sun-like star (Hoch et al. 2024). The spectra reach wavelengths beyond 3 microns, where atmospheric gases can be measured to test how giant planets form (Hoch et al. 2024).","system":"Two young Jupiter-like planets orbit far from a Sun-like star (Hoch et al. 2024).","history":"Planets of 2 to 14 Jupiter masses at separations of about 10 to 100 AU remain a puzzle for both core accretion and gravitational instability, the two main formation models (Hoch et al. 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tyc89987601c-Hochetal2024","cite":"Hoch et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Kielan Hoch, Marshall Perrin, Jean-Baptiste Ruffio et al.","title":"Direct Imaging Spectroscopy of Substellar Companions with JWST","journal":"AASTCS10, Extreme Solar Systems V","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 158996 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that found it, Search for Exoplanets around Northern circumpolar Stars, targets giant stars to learn how planetary systems change in the late stages of stellar evolution (Bang et al. 2018).","observed":"The host is a K giant that was observed with the BOES spectrograph on a 1.8 m telescope in Korea, with 24 spectra taken from 2010 to 2017 (Bang et al. 2018). The star's velocity varies with a long period of 820.2 days (Bang et al. 2018). Giant stars can mimic planets through pulsation and surface activity, so the team checked calcium and hydrogen-alpha lines, Hipparcos photometry and line shapes (Bang et al. 2018). For the K giant, none of these showed a matching signal or any correlation with the velocity changes (Bang et al. 2018). A second giant observed in the same survey, HD 18438, was judged to be pulsating instead (Bang et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd158996b-Bangetal2018","cite":"Bang et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Tae-Yang Bang, Byeong-Cheol Lee, Gwang-hui Jeong et al.","title":"Search for Exoplanets around Northern Circumpolar Stars III. Long-Period Radial Velocity Variations in HD 18438 and HD 158996","journal":"Journal of Korean Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07213","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2095 b":{"text":{"why":"The mission's main goal is to find planets smaller than Neptune around stars bright enough for follow-up, and M dwarfs are prime targets for small planets in or near the habitable zone (Murgas et al. 2023).","observed":"Two planet candidates found by TESS were confirmed by combining photometry with radial velocity measurements from the CARMENES spectrograph (Murgas et al. 2023). The fit allowed for variability of the star in both the light curves and the velocities (Murgas et al. 2023). The orbital periods are 17.66484 days for TOI-2095 b and 28.17232 days for TOI-2095 c (Murgas et al. 2023).","system":"The star is a nearby M dwarf with a temperature of 3759 K and a mass and radius of 0.44 times the Sun's (Murgas et al. 2023). Two transiting planets orbit it (Murgas et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2095b-Murgasetal2023","cite":"Murgas et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Murgas, A. Castro-González, E. Pallé et al.","title":"Two super-Earths at the edge of the habitable zone of the nearby M dwarf TOI-2095","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09220","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-121 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits only about 1.15 times farther out than its Roche limit, the distance inside which tides would tear it apart, so it may be close to tidal disruption (Delrez et al. 2016). Its glow was detected in one infrared band, with an occultation depth of 603 parts per million (Delrez et al. 2016). Models predict that on the dayside most molecules, including water, break apart, but carbon monoxide survives (Parmentier et al. 2018). Webb Space Telescope spectra show signs of water and hydrogen breaking apart on the dayside, and silicon monoxide detected at 5.2 sigma (Gapp et al. 2025). Water appears more abundant on the nightside than on the dayside (Gapp et al. 2025).","system":"The star is an active F6-type main-sequence star with a temperature of about 6460 K (Delrez et al. 2016). Its spin may be strongly misaligned with the planet's orbit, based on a sky-projected angle of 257.8 degrees (Delrez et al. 2016).","history":"High-resolution spectra show a carbon-to-oxygen ratio well above the star's, and a moderately raised ratio of rock-forming to gas-forming elements (Smith et al. 2024). The combination fits formation between the soot line and the water snow line, but a region farther out cannot be ruled out (Smith et al. 2024).","open":"No single 3D model includes all the physics at work, such as magnetic drag on winds and clouds on the nightside (Frazier et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp121b-Delrezetal2016","cite":"Delrez et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"L. Delrez, A. Santerne, J.-M. Almenara et al.","title":"WASP-121 b: a hot Jupiter close to tidal disruption transiting an active F star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02471","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp121b-Parmentieretal2018","cite":"Parmentier et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Vivien Parmentier, Mike R. Line, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"From thermal dissociation to condensation in the atmospheres of ultra hot Jupiters: WASP-121b in context","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.00096","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp121b-Gappetal2025","cite":"Gapp et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cyril Gapp, Thomas M. Evans-Soma, Joanna K. Barstow et al.","title":"WASP-121 b's Transmission Spectrum Observed with JWST/NIRSpec G395H Reveals Thermal Dissociation and SiO in the Atmosphere","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.02199","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp121b-Smithetal2024","cite":"Smith et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Peter C. B. Smith, Jorge A. Sanchez, Michael R. Line et al.","title":"The Roasting Marshmallows Program with IGRINS on Gemini South. II. WASP-121 b has Superstellar C/O and Refractory-to-volatile Ratios","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.19017","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp121b-Frazieretal2026","cite":"Frazier et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Robert Frazier, Emily Rauscher, Jared Splinter et al.","title":"WASP-121b Under the Watch of JWST: How 3D Models Compare to its Spectroscopic Phase Curve","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":52,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 740 b":{"text":{"why":"The star belongs to the HADES and CARMENES searches of northern M dwarfs, which aim to measure planet properties in a statistical sense (Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021). It was picked from stars without reported planetary companions (Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021).","observed":"The star was followed for 10 years with 129 HARPS-N spectra over 6 years, 57 HARPS spectra over 4 years and 32 CARMENES spectra over more than 1 year (Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021). Photometry from the ASAS survey and the EXORAP project was used to check the star's own variability (Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021). A short-period velocity signal remained that fits a planetary companion (Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021). The HARPS-N velocities have a typical error of 0.8 m/s against a scatter of 5.8 m/s (Toledo-Padron et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj740b-ToledoPadrnetal2021","cite":"Toledo-Padrón et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"B. Toledo-Padrón, A. Suárez Mascareño, J. I. González Hernández et al.","title":"A super-Earth on a close-in orbit around the M1V star GJ 740. A HADES and CARMENES collaboration","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09441","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj740b-ToledoPadronetal2021","cite":"Toledo-Padron et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"B. Toledo-Padrón, A. Suárez Mascareño, J. I. González Hernández et al.","title":"A super-Earth on a close-in orbit around the M1V star GJ 740. A HADES and CARMENES collaboration","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09441","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 206893 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck Planet Imager and Characterizer spectra at high resolution in the K band detected the object at more than 8 sigma in two epochs (Sappey et al. 2025). It is an exceptionally red companion at the transition between L-type and T-type objects (Sappey et al. 2025). The fitted temperature is 1634 K and the carbon-to-oxygen ratio is 0.57, close to the Sun's (Sappey et al. 2025). Evolution models give an age of 112 million years, a mass of 22.7 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.11 Jupiter radii (Sappey et al. 2025). The velocity data were also used to fit its orbit (Sappey et al. 2025).","system":"It belongs to a system with more than one planet (Sappey et al. 2025).","history":"A tentative trend shows companions above 4 Jupiter masses with near-solar carbon-to-oxygen ratios and lighter ones with higher ratios, and this object fits it (Sappey et al. 2025).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd206893b-Sappeyetal2025","cite":"Sappey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ben Sappey, Quinn Konopacky, Clarissa R. Do Ó et al.","title":"HD 206893 B at High Spectral Resolution with the Keck Planet Imager and Characterizer","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 38529 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer companion HD 38529 c. Hubble astrometry, which tracks the star's tiny sideways wobble, combined with 11 years of velocities refined the orbits of both companions (Benedict et al. 2010). The outer companion has a period of 2136.1 days and an orbit tilted 48.3 degrees, giving a mass of 17.6 Jupiter masses, above the 13 Jupiter-mass limit for burning deuterium (Benedict et al. 2010). Earlier Hipparcos data had already suggested the outer companion has a mass in the brown dwarf range (Reffert & Quirrenbach 2006).","system":"The star spins every 31.65 days, and spots on it can explain the remaining scatter in the velocities (Benedict et al. 2010). Computer tests show the system sits within a large zone of stable orbits (Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd38529b-Benedictetal2010","cite":"Benedict et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"G. Fritz Benedict, Barbara E. McArthur, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"The Mass of HD 38529c from Hubble Space Telescope Astrometry and High-precision Radial Velocities","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.0421","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd38529b-ReffertQuirrenbach2006","cite":"Reffert & Quirrenbach 2006","year":"2006","authors":"S. Reffert, A. Quirrenbach","title":"Hipparcos astrometric orbits for two brown dwarf companions: HD 38529 and HD 168443","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20054611","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd38529b-KiselevaEggletonetal2002","cite":"Kiseleva-Eggleton et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Ludmila Kiseleva-Eggleton, Eric Bois, Nicolas Rambaux et al.","title":"Global Dynamics and Stability Limits for Planetary Systems around HD 12661, HD 38529, HD 37124, and HD 160691","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0207367","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 102195 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found with the Exoplanet Tracker, a new high-throughput Doppler instrument that uses an interferometer (Ge et al. 2006). A 0.9 m telescope was enough to find a planet around a star of magnitude 8.05, the first time for a star fainter than magnitude 8 (Ge et al. 2006).","observed":"The star's velocity changes by 63.4 m/s every 4.11 days (Ge et al. 2006). The signal was confirmed with the 2.1 m Kitt Peak telescope and the 9 m Hobby-Eberly Telescope (Ge et al. 2006). Later modelling of disequilibrium chemistry cites a first robust methane detection on an irradiated planet for HD 102195 b, which places it in a range of 800 to 1500 K where methane is expected to be easier to detect (Molaverdikhani et al. 2019). Simulations of the host star's wind suggest a bow shock around the planet and a magnetic bubble of 4.1 to 5.6 planetary radii, if its magnetic field is similar to Jupiter's (Vidotto et al. 2015).","system":"The host is a young star with solar metal content that may belong to a local association of stars (Ge et al. 2006).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd102195b-Geetal2006","cite":"Ge et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"Jian Ge, Julian van Eyken, Suvrath Mahadevan et al.","title":"The First Extrasolar Planet Discovered with a New-Generation High-Throughput Doppler Instrument","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0605247","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd102195b-Molaverdikhanietal2019","cite":"Molaverdikhani et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Karan Molaverdikhani, Thomas Henning, Paul Mollière","title":"From Cold to Hot Irradiated Gaseous Exoplanets: Fingerprints of Chemical Disequilibrium in Atmospheric Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09847","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd102195b-Vidottoetal2015","cite":"Vidotto et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"A. A. Vidotto, R. Fares, M. Jardine et al.","title":"On the environment surrounding close-in exoplanets","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.05711","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":112,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is the third found by the KELT survey, and its star is one of the 20 brightest known to host transiting planets, which makes it promising for detailed study (Pepper et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet is a moderately inflated hot Jupiter (Pepper et al. 2013). Its host star is evolved, yet the planet has probably always received enough starlight to be puffed up (Pepper et al. 2013). A search for hydrogen-alpha absorption from an escaping atmosphere found none during two transits (Cauley et al. 2017). Ultraviolet transits with Swift gave a marginal detection, and the size derived in that band was 50 to 100 percent larger than the optical size (Corrales et al. 2021). Spin-orbit measurements show the orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Polanski et al. 2026).","system":"The star has a likely companion star (Pepper et al. 2013). The star's companion makes this a binary system, and the Kozai-Lidov effect, in which a companion star shifts a planet's orbit, has been examined as a way to bring such planets close in (Vazquez & Eastman 2025).","history":"The planet's alignment with its star in a binary system was tested against oscillations driven by the companion star, a mechanism often invoked for misaligned planets in multi-star systems (Polanski et al. 2026).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt3b-Pepperetal2013","cite":"Pepper et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Joshua Pepper, Robert J. Siverd, Thomas G. Beatty et al.","title":"KELT-3b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a V = 9.8 Late-F Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1031","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt3b-Cauleyetal2017","cite":"Cauley et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"P. Wilson Cauley, Seth Redfield, Adam G. Jensen","title":"A Search for Hα Absorption around KELT-3 b and GJ 436 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.05945","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt3b-Corralesetal2021","cite":"Corrales et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Lia Corrales, Sasikrishna Ravi, George W. King et al.","title":"Five New Hot Jupiter Transits Investigated with Swift-UVOT","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.01579","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt3b-Polanskietal2026","cite":"Polanski et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alex S. Polanski, Malena Rice, Catherine A. Clark et al.","title":"CHIANTE I: Obliquity Measurements of Four High-Priority Ariel Targets in Binaries","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23662","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt3b-VazquezEastman2025","cite":"Vazquez & Eastman 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Jacqueline Vazquez, Jason Eastman","title":"Understanding Hot Jupiter Migration for Six Binary Star Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-8 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Saturn with a mass of 0.37 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.3 Jupiter radii (Alsubai et al. 2019). Years of TESS photometry were searched for additional planets around its star, both as extra transits and as shifts in transit times (Maciejewski 2020).","system":"No sign of other planets was found around the star (Maciejewski 2020).","history":"Hot Jupiters with no close companions fit high-eccentricity migration, in which a giant planet is thrown onto a stretched orbit that later shrinks (Maciejewski 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar8b-Alsubaietal2019","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Khalid Alsubai, Zlatan I. Tsvetanov, Stylianos Pyrzas et al.","title":"Qatar Exoplanet Survey: Qatar-8b, 9b, and 10b—A Hot Saturn and Two Hot Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.09258","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar8b-Maciejewski2020","cite":"Maciejewski 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Maciejewski","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. I. No Companions in Planetary Systems KELT-18, KELT-23, KELT-24, Qatar-8, WASP-62, WASP-100, WASP-119, and WASP-126","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11977","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 18606 b":{"text":{"why":"Giant stars are good hunting grounds for giant planets beyond about 0.5 AU (Jenkins et al. 2025). The planet came from combining two long-running surveys, EXPRESS and PPPS, with a Bayesian method (Jenkins et al. 2025).","observed":"The host is a naked-eye K giant of magnitude 5.8 (Jenkins et al. 2025). The star's velocity shows a planet with a period of about 675 days and a circular orbit (Jenkins et al. 2025). Its minimum mass is 0.8 Jupiter masses, which makes it a sub-Jupiter gas giant (Jenkins et al. 2025). Among the 37 giant stars shared by the two surveys, 13 planetary systems have now been found (Jenkins et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip18606b-Jenkinsetal2025","cite":"Jenkins et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"J. S. Jenkins, M. I. Jones, J. I. Vines et al.","title":"Sub-Jupiter gas giants orbiting giant stars uncovered using a Bayesian framework","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02507","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1408 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet TOI-1408 c. The hot Jupiter has a period of 4.42 days, a mass of 1.86 Jupiter masses and grazing transits, so only the edge of the planet crosses the star (Korth et al. 2024). The grazing path leaves its radius poorly known, anywhere from about 1.2 to 3.5 Jupiter radii (Baluev 2026). The two planets sit near a 2:1 period ratio and tug on each other, shifting transit times (Korth et al. 2024).","system":"The inner planet TOI-1408 c is a sub-Neptune of 2.22 Earth radii on a 2.2-day orbit (Korth et al. 2024). Its transit times shift by 15 percent of its orbital period, the largest such shift measured (Korth et al. 2024). Velocities point to a third body with a period of several thousand days (Korth et al. 2024). The star is metal-rich and has a large excess of lithium (Galazutdinov et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The planet's radius is still uncertain because of the near-tangential transit geometry (Baluev 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1408b-Korthetal2024","cite":"Korth et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Judith Korth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Hannu Parviainen et al.","title":"TOI-1408: Discovery and Photodynamical Modeling of a Small Inner Companion to a Hot Jupiter Revealed by Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.17798","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1408b-Baluev2026","cite":"Baluev 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Roman V. Baluev","title":"TOI-1408: the limb darkening models, photometric and Doppler noises, and the exoplanetary system","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02434","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1408b-Galazutdinovetal2026","cite":"Galazutdinov et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"G. A. Galazutdinov, S. A. Korotin, D. B. Poklad et al.","title":"TOI-1408: a metal-rich star with lithium overabundance hosting a multiplanet system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stag1918","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 3167 c":{"text":{"why":"The star is nearby and bright, one of the closest and brightest hosts of several transiting planets (Vanderburg et al. 2016). It is also quiet, with low activity and slow rotation, which suits velocity measurements of planet masses (Vanderburg et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet has a radius of 2.9 Earth radii, large enough that it likely has a thick gaseous envelope (Vanderburg et al. 2016). Mass measurements show the inner planet is rocky and planet c is rich in volatiles, a mini-Neptune (Lendl 2016). Hubble observations of one transit of planet c were planned to look for hydrogen escaping in the Lyman-alpha line (Lendl 2016). A large Hubble survey also targets it with a 1.1 to 1.6 micron spectrum, where water and methane absorb, and gives its temperature as about 600 K (Mikal-Evans 2020).","system":"The inner planet HD 3167 b has a radius of 1.6 Earth radii and an ultra-short period of 0.96 days (Vanderburg et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is losing its atmosphere to space was the question the Hubble study was built to answer (Lendl 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd3167c-Vanderburgetal2016","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Allyson Bieryla, Dmitry A. Duev et al.","title":"Two Small Planets Transiting HD 3167","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.05248","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167c-Lendl2016","cite":"Lendl 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Monika Lendl","title":"Atmospheric escape from a mini-Neptune","journal":"HST Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd3167c-MikalEvans2020","cite":"Mikal-Evans 2020","year":"2020","authors":"T. Mikal-Evans","title":"The Atmospheric Diversity of Mini-Neptunes in Multi-planet Systems","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #235","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 40307 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was proposed as a habitable-zone super-Earth candidate in a six-planet system around a K dwarf (Tuomi et al. 2013). It came from a fresh Bayesian analysis of public HARPS velocities, which had previously shown three super-Earths (Tuomi et al. 2013). The analysis found signals at 34 and 51 days and a further candidate near 320 days (Tuomi et al. 2013). Signals linked to the star's chromospheric emission depend strongly on wavelength, so stellar activity can imitate planets (Tuomi et al. 2013).","system":"The system has no massive planets and was considered a likely home for further low-mass planets (Tuomi et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"Its existence has been questioned."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd40307g-Tuomietal2013","cite":"Tuomi et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Tuomi, G. Anglada-Escudé, E. Gerlach et al.","title":"Habitable-zone super-Earth candidate in a six-planet system around the K2.5V star HD 40307","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1617","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-55 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A transit of the planet was observed with the Spitzer space telescope, confirming the original K2 timing for future follow-up (Dressing et al. 2018). Fitting Spitzer and K2 data together gave a radius of 4.41 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of about 900 K (Dressing et al. 2018). Twelve velocity measurements with the Keck I telescope gave a mass of 43.13 Earth masses (Dressing et al. 2018). The bulk density is 2.8 grams per cubic centimetre, which fits a rocky planet with a modest hydrogen-helium envelope (Dressing et al. 2018).","system":"The host is a K7 dwarf of 0.688 solar masses, characterised with infrared spectra (Dressing et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k255b-Dressingetal2018","cite":"Dressing et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Courtney D. Dressing, Evan Sinukoff, Benjamin J. Fulton et al.","title":"Characterizing K2 Candidate Planetary Systems Orbiting Low-mass Stars. III. A High Mass and Low Envelope Fraction for the Warm Neptune K2-55b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.05148","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"AB Aur b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star and the disc around it. The star is a young Herbig Ae star with a prototypical transition disc showing spiral arms, dust cavities and dust traps, features linked to planet formation (Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2020). Radio observations resolve a disc 780 AU across, seen at an inclination of 21.5 degrees, and confirm its spiral structure (Corder et al. 2005). Hydrogen sulfide is found in a ring from about 109 to 275 AU (Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2022).","system":"The disc's spiral arms show density contrasts in the gas and dust (Corder et al. 2005).","history":"The disc's chemistry sets the starting ingredients for planetary atmospheres (Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2026). Sulfur is strongly depleted and the carbon-to-oxygen ratio is around 1 or higher (Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2026).","open":"No single chemistry model fits all the molecules seen in the disc (Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-abaurb-RivireMarichalaretal2020","cite":"Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"P. Rivière-Marichalar, A. Fuente, R. Le Gal et al.","title":"AB Aur, a Rosetta stone for studies of planet formation. I. Chemical study of a planet-forming disk","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.00751","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abaurb-Corderetal2005","cite":"Corder et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Stuartt Corder, Josh Eisner, Anneila Sargent","title":"AB Aurigae Resolved: Evidence for Spiral Structure","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0502131","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abaurb-RivireMarichalaretal2022","cite":"Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"P. Rivière-Marichalar, A. Fuente, G. Esplugues et al.","title":"AB Aur, a Rosetta stone for studies of planet formation. II. H<SUB>2</SUB>S detection and sulfur budget","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.06716","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-abaurb-RivireMarichalaretal2026","cite":"Rivière-Marichalar et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Rivière-Marichalar, A. Fuente, R. le Gal et al.","title":"AB Aur, a Rosetta stone for studies of planet formation: IV. C/O estimates from CS and SO interferometric observations","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06551","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":70,"about":12,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1266 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Computer models have explored what its atmosphere could be like, because its mass and radius allow it to be a sub-Neptune that has lost much of its gas (Harman et al. 2022). Photochemical, climate and atmospheric escape models were used to describe a water-dominated atmosphere (Harman et al. 2022). If the planet is such a world, it could show how a rocky planet passes through a runaway greenhouse, the way Venus may have (Harman et al. 2022).","system":"The host is an early M dwarf (Harman et al. 2022).","history":"Worlds like this could be stuck in a runaway greenhouse state for billions of years (Harman et al. 2022).","open":"Whether the planet is a depleted sub-Neptune with a steam atmosphere is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1266c-Harmanetal2022","cite":"Harman et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Chester E. Harman, Ravi Kopparapu, Gudmundur Stefansson et al.","title":"A Snowball in Hell: the Potential Steam Atmosphere of TOI-1266c","journal":"The Astrobiology Science Conference (AbSciCon) 2022","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3929 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planet GJ 3929 b, an Earth-sized planet in its star's Venus zone. Planet c does not transit and is most likely a sub-Neptune (Beard et al. 2022). A velocity study with the NEID spectrometer revised its period from an alias at 14.30 days to 15.04 days, with a minimum mass of 5.71 Earth masses (Beard et al. 2022).","system":"The inner planet GJ 3929 b is an Earth-sized planet on a 2.616-day orbit (Beard et al. 2022). Four JWST eclipse observations of it were used to probe for an atmosphere (Connors et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The orbital period was first estimated at 14.303 days from earlier velocity data (Kemmer et al. 2022) and later revised (Beard et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3929c-Beardetal2022","cite":"Beard et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Corey Beard, Paul Robertson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"GJ 3929: High-precision Photometric and Doppler Characterization of an Exo-Venus and Its Hot, Mini-Neptune-mass Companion","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.10672","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3929c-Connorsetal2026","cite":"Connors et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Nicholas J. Connors, Christopher Monaghan, Björn Benneke et al.","title":"GJ 3929 b as the First Complete Rocky Worlds DDT Data Set","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07511","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3929c-Kemmeretal2022","cite":"Kemmer et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"J. Kemmer, S. Dreizler, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"Discovery and mass measurement of the hot, transiting, Earth-sized planet, GJ 3929 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00970","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 40979 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star and the stars around it. A search for companions of planet-host stars found that HD 40979 has a very wide companion, about 6400 AU away (Mugrauer et al. 2007). The companion is itself a close pair of dwarf stars, of roughly 0.8 and 0.4 solar masses, about 130 AU apart (Mugrauer et al. 2007).","system":"The planet's star is one of the widest planet-hosting stellar systems known, and a rare triple-star system. When it was reported, only three other planet-host triple-star systems were known (Mugrauer et al. 2007).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd40979b-Mugraueretal2007","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser, T. Mazeh","title":"The multiplicity of exoplanet host stars. Spectroscopic confirmation of the companions GJ 3021 B and HD 27442 B, one new planet host triple-star system, and global statistics","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0703795","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2049 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters may form in more than one way, and well-measured planets are needed before studies of their atmospheres and orbits can narrow down the ideas (Guenther et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed together with four other hot or warm Jupiters, by combining space-telescope brightness data with follow-up from ground-based telescopes to measure each planet's mass and radius (Guenther et al. 2026). Of the five planets, three have equilibrium temperatures of about 1800 K and two have about 1000 K (Guenther et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2049b-Guentheretal2026","cite":"Guenther et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Eike W. Guenther, William D. Cochran, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"The Discovery of Five Hot Jupiters by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6449","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1656 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour Kepler-1656 b, a planet on an extremely stretched orbit with an eccentricity of 0.8 (Angelo et al. 2022). Velocity measurements of the star confirmed a second planet, Kepler-1656 c, with a mass of 0.40 Jupiter masses and an orbit lasting 1919 days (Angelo et al. 2022).","system":"The inner planet has about 5 Earth radii and a 32-day orbit. It has an eccentricity of 0.84, the largest measured for any planet under 100 Earth masses (Brady et al. 2018). About 80% of its mass is thought to be dense rock and iron (Brady et al. 2018).","history":"Computer simulations of the inner planet's motion, with the outer planet included, fit a picture in which planet b formed where it is now. The outer planet's gravity later stretched its orbit gently, without dragging it closer to the star (Angelo et al. 2022). An earlier suggestion was that one or more scattering and merger events shaped the inner planet (Brady et al. 2018).","open":"Despite its high eccentricity, there is no evidence that the inner planet is moving inward or ever did (Angelo et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1656c-Angeloetal2022","cite":"Angelo et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Isabel Angelo, Smadar Naoz, Erik Petigura et al.","title":"Kepler-1656b's Extreme Eccentricity: Signature of a Gentle Giant","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.00019","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler1656c-Bradyetal2018","cite":"Brady et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Madison T. Brady, Erik A. Petigura, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Kepler-1656b: A Dense Sub-Saturn with an Extreme Eccentricity","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08436","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1694 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed as real by checking its space-telescope light curves and calculating how unlikely it is that a false alarm would mimic the signal (Mistry et al. 2023). It is one of 11 planets validated this way around orange K-type stars (Mistry et al. 2023). It is judged suitable for atmosphere studies by both transmission spectroscopy, which reads starlight filtered through the atmosphere, and emission spectroscopy, which reads the planet's own glow (Mistry et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1694b-Mistryetal2023","cite":"Mistry et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Priyashkumar Mistry, Kamlesh Pathak, Aniket Prasad et al.","title":"VaTEST. II. Statistical Validation of 11 TESS-detected Exoplanets Orbiting K-type Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09865","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-33 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is highly inflated, meaning it is puffed up to a radius of 1.686 Jupiter radii at a mass of 0.762 Jupiter masses (Hartman et al. 2011). The host star is a late-F dwarf, with 1.38 solar masses and a radius of 1.64 solar radii (Hartman et al. 2011). The star's surface is restless, causing velocity noise of about 50 m/s (Hartman et al. 2011). Because of this noise, the planet's orbital eccentricity is poorly constrained. Astronomers cut the noise to about 35 m/s by using a measured link between the star's line shapes and its velocity jitter (Hartman et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":"The orbit's eccentricity is not well known, and the planet's mass and radius depend on it (Hartman et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp33b-Hartmanetal2011","cite":"Hartman et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-32b and HAT-P-33b: Two Highly Inflated Hot Jupiters Transiting High-jitter Stars","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1212","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-28 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a G3 dwarf star and has a mass of 0.63 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.21 Jupiter radii, giving a low average density of 0.44 g/cm3 (Buchhave et al. 2011). A survey with a fast camera that takes very sharp images found a new stellar source within 1 arcsecond of HAT-P-28 (Wöllert & Brandner 2015). Any nearby star can distort the measured transit, so such sources matter for the planet's measured size.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp28b-Buchhaveetal2011","cite":"Buchhave et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"L. A. Buchhave, G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"Hat-P-28b and Hat-P-29b: Two Sub-Jupiter Mass Transiting Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.1813","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp28b-WllertBrandner2015","cite":"Wöllert & Brandner 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Maria Wöllert, Wolfgang Brandner","title":"A Lucky Imaging search for stellar sources near 74 transit hosts","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.05456","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2641 b":{"text":{"why":"Planets around hot, early-type stars are still few, and they help show how planets form around stars of different mass. They also show how strong radiation affects hot Jupiters (Psaridi et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is one of three Saturn-mass planets found around main-sequence F-type stars (Psaridi et al. 2023). It has a mass of 0.39 Jupiter masses (Psaridi et al. 2023). Its transit is grazing, with an impact parameter of 1.04, so the planet only partly crosses the face of its star (Psaridi et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2641b-Psaridietal2023","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"A. Psaridi, F. Bouchy, M. Lendl et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-615, TOI-622, TOI-2641 RVs + ground phot. (Psaridi+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"MOA-bin-1L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was found as a by-product of a search for short single-lens events that could be caused by free-floating planets (Bennett et al. 2012).","observed":"Light from a background star was briefly magnified, almost entirely by a sharp caustic feature, meaning a sharp-edged pattern of focused light (Bennett et al. 2012). This was the first planetary event in which the host star shows up only through its effects on the lensing of the light (Bennett et al. 2012). The planet-to-star mass ratio is 4.9 × 10^-3 (Bennett et al. 2012). A statistical model of the Galaxy puts the planet at 3.7 Jupiter masses, orbiting a star of about 0.75 solar masses at about 8.3 AU (Bennett et al. 2012). That makes it one of the most massive and widest-orbit planets found by microlensing (Bennett et al. 2012).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass and distance are statistical estimates with wide uncertainties (Bennett et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-moabin1lb-Bennettetal2012","cite":"Bennett et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. P. Bennett, T. Sumi, I. A. Bond et al.","title":"Planetary and Other Short Binary Microlensing Events from the MOA Short-event Analysis","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4560","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1199 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial-velocity measurements fixed the planet's mass to a precision of 8.4%, giving 0.239 Jupiter masses and a radius of 0.938 Jupiter radii (Serrano Bell et al. 2024). Its average density is 0.358 g/cm3, among the lowest known for planets of this mass (Serrano Bell et al. 2024). The star is bright, at about magnitude 11, which makes the planet a good target for studies of its atmosphere (Serrano Bell et al. 2024). A second hot Saturn, TOI-1273 b, was reported in the same study (Serrano Bell et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1199b-Belletal2024","cite":"Bell et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Serrano Bell, R. F. Díaz, G. Hébrard et al.","title":"TOI-1199 b and TOI-1273 b: Two new transiting hot Saturns detected and characterized with SOPHIE and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07861","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4409 b":{"text":{"why":"Long-period transiting planets are valuable test cases for how planets form and change, but their rare transits make them hard to find (Reller et al. 2026).","observed":"Confirmation needed eight space-telescope transits plus ground-based light curves, including some from the Antarctic ASTEP telescope, and velocity measurements gathered from 2018 to 2024 (Reller et al. 2026). The planet is a puffy warm super-Neptune with a mass of 0.047 Jupiter masses and a very low density of 0.20 g/cm3 (Reller et al. 2026). The mass is uncertain, with a 3-sigma upper limit of 0.095 Jupiter masses (Reller et al. 2026).","system":"Small shifts in the transit times, with a semi-amplitude of about 10 minutes, hint that another body may be in the system (Reller et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether a companion planet exists is not yet settled (Reller et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4409b-Relleretal2026","cite":"Reller et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Reller, R. Brahm, B. Merin et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-4409 b TESS light curves (Reller+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-43 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of seven transiting planets monitored with Thai telescopes in Thailand, Chile, China, the USA and Australia from 2015 to 2019 (Awiphan et al. 2019). Earlier work had reported large shifts in its transit times, which can be a sign of another body in the system. The planet was observed again to check those results (Awiphan et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp43b-Awiphanetal2019","cite":"Awiphan et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Supachai Awiphan, Eamonn Kerins, Jake Morgan et al.","title":"Transit Timing Variations and Transmission Spectroscopic Studies of Seven Exoplanets with Thai Telescopes","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-448 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet does not transit its star. Its presence was worked out from the way the inner planet, Kepler-448 b, arrives early or late at each transit, and from changes in transit length (Masuda 2017). The mass of Kepler-448 c is 22 Jupiter masses, and its orbit is strongly stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.65 (Masuda 2017). At closest approach it comes within 1.5 AU of the star (Masuda 2017).","system":"The inner planet, Kepler-448 b, is a warm Jupiter with a 17.9-day orbit and 1.23 Jupiter radii (Masuda 2017). Its own orbit is moderately eccentric, at 0.34 (Masuda 2017).","history":"Eccentric companions like this one offer clues to how warm Jupiters, which sit too far out to be heated into place by their stars, came to be where they are (Masuda 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler448c-Masuda2017","cite":"Masuda 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Kento Masuda","title":"Eccentric Companions to Kepler-448b and Kepler-693b: Clues to the Formation of Warm Jupiters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.04990","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-1783.02":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour KOI-1783.01. Planets in this system pull on each other, which shifts their transit times (Vissapragada et al. 2020). Infrared transit observations of KOI-1783.01 with the 200-inch Palomar telescope, using a light-spreading diffuser to reduce noise, confirmed that system's planetary nature (Vissapragada et al. 2020). A single new transit, observed 4 to 5 years after Kepler ended, can cut uncertainties on dynamically measured masses by up to a factor of 3 in systems like this one (Vissapragada et al. 2020). Later space-telescope observations were used to update the transit timing and mass limits for the KOI-1783 system (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi178302-Vissapragadaetal2020","cite":"Vissapragada et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Shreyas Vissapragada, Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Avi Shporer et al.","title":"Diffuser-assisted Infrared Transit Photometry for Four Dynamically Interacting Kepler Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.04445","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-koi178302-JontofHutteretal2022","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Paul A. Dalba, John H. Livingston","title":"TESS Observations of Kepler Systems with Transit Timing Variations","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08917","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2023-BLG-1454L b":{"text":{"why":"The event was picked out of a search of high-cadence microlensing survey data for short signals that were only partly covered by observations (Han et al. 2024).","observed":"Because the planetary signal was only partially covered, several different models fit it. Four local solutions were found, with planet-to-host mass ratios between 1.7 × 10^-3 and 4.3 × 10^-3 (Han et al. 2024). The planet's physical properties come from a statistical model based on how long the event lasted and the size of its lensing ring (Han et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet's exact mass and distance from its host remain uncertain because of the several competing solutions (Han et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2023blg1454lb-Hanetal2024","cite":"Han et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Chung-Uk Lee et al.","title":"KMT-2023-BLG-0416, KMT-2023-BLG-1454, KMT-2023-BLG-1642: Microlensing planets identified from partially covered signals","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.08904","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2285 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was first thought to orbit every 27.27 days, but later space-telescope data showed that the true period is 13.64 days, half as long (Fukui 2025). The mistake came from the weakness of individual transit signals and the small number of transits seen at first (Fukui 2025). Its equilibrium temperature is therefore 358 K, not the 284 K first estimated (Fukui 2025). Its mass has only an upper limit, below 19.5 Earth masses (Fukui et al. 2022). The star is bright in the near-infrared, which makes the planet a good target for atmosphere work (Fukui et al. 2022).","system":"The new data also show another planet candidate, with a 9.67-day orbit and a radius of 1.5 Earth radii, that still needs confirmation (Fukui 2025).","history":"","open":"If the planet has a layer of water under a hydrogen-rich atmosphere, liquid water could exist on the water layer's surface, depending on the planet's mass and water fraction (Fukui et al. 2022). Climate simulations over a range of surface and water assumptions found cases where the planet stays temperate (Miles et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2285b-Fukui2025","cite":"Fukui 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Akihiko Fukui","title":"Hidden Transits: TOI-2285 b is a Warmer Sub-Neptune Likely with a Super-Earth Companion","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22009","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2285b-Fukuietal2022","cite":"Fukui et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Akihiko Fukui, Tadahiro Kimura, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"TOI-2285b: A 1.7 Earth-radius planet near the habitable zone around a nearby M dwarf","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.10215","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi2285b-Milesetal2025","cite":"Miles et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Emma L. Miles, Colby Ostberg, Stephen R. Kane et al.","title":"Interior and Climate Modeling of the Venus Zone Planet TOI-2285 b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10647","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-72 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Jupiter orbiting a bright, moderately evolved F7 star, at an extreme irradiation of about 5.5 × 10^9 erg/s/cm2 (Gillon et al. 2013). Its size of 1.27 Jupiter radii is moderate for that heating, which may point to a core enriched in heavy elements, though the errors were too large to be sure (Gillon et al. 2013). The planet's orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of about -7 degrees (Addison et al. 2018). Its glow was detected by the Spitzer space telescope in at least one infrared band, and the orbit is circular (Wallack et al. 2021). Space-telescope observations of the full orbit also found significant secondary eclipse or brightness changes (Wong et al. 2020).","system":"A high-contrast imaging survey found a new candidate companion near the star (Bohn et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp72b-Gillonetal2013","cite":"Gillon et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"M. Gillon, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier-Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-64 b and WASP-72 b: two new transiting highly irradiated giant planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.4257","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp72b-Addisonetal2018","cite":"Addison et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"B. C. Addison, Songhu Wang, M. C. Johnson et al.","title":"Stellar Obliquities and Planetary Alignments (SOPA). I. Spin-Orbit Measurements of Three Transiting Hot Jupiters: WASP-72b, WASP-100b, and WASP-109b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.00314","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp72b-Wallacketal2021","cite":"Wallack et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Nicole L. Wallack, Heather A. Knutson, Drake Deming","title":"Trends in Spitzer Secondary Eclipses","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15833","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp72b-Wongetal2020","cite":"Wong et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ian Wong, Avi Shporer, Tansu Daylan et al.","title":"Systematic Phase Curve Study of Known Transiting Systems from Year One of the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06407","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp72b-Bohnetal2020","cite":"Bohn et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"A. J. Bohn, J. Southworth, C. Ginski et al.","title":"A multiplicity study of transiting exoplanet host stars. I. High-contrast imaging with VLT/SPHERE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4458 b":{"text":{"why":"Hot Jupiters may form in several ways, and well-measured examples are needed before atmospheric and dynamical studies can narrow the ideas (Guenther et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with four other hot or warm Jupiters, using space-telescope data combined with ground-based observations (Guenther et al. 2026). It lies in the northern field of the PLATO space mission, which is expected to observe it again (Guenther et al. 2026). Three of the five planets have equilibrium temperatures of about 1800 K and two have about 1000 K (Guenther et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4458b-Guentheretal2026","cite":"Guenther et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Eike W. Guenther, William D. Cochran, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"The Discovery of Five Hot Jupiters by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ae6449","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-129 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of five hot Jupiters confirmed in one study using ground-based photometry and spectroscopy (Maxted et al. 2016). The group has orbital periods of 2.17 to 5.75 days and masses of 0.3 to 1.2 Jupiter masses (Maxted et al. 2016). Little is known about WASP-129 b itself. Work so far has mostly studied its star. The star, WASP-129 A, is unusually dense, which suggests it is rich in helium, like HAT-P-11 A (Maxted et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp129b-Maxtedetal2016","cite":"Maxted et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Five transiting hot Jupiters discovered using WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST: WASP-119 b, WASP-124 b, WASP-126 b, WASP-129 b, and WASP-133 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.01740","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-140 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about Jupiter's mass at 1.019 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.095 Jupiter radii (Giles et al. 2018). The host star spins once in about 16.3 days (Giles et al. 2018). It is slightly inflated, but much less than other planets that receive similar heating, which makes it useful for studying why hot Jupiters swell (Giles et al. 2018). The discovery was the ninth hot Jupiter from the K2 mission (Giles et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The orbit's shape is disputed. One analysis found an eccentricity of 0.120 (Giles et al. 2018). A later analysis found that the available velocity data are consistent with a circular orbit (Korth et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2140b-Gilesetal2018","cite":"Giles et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"H. A. C. Giles, D. Bayliss, N. Espinoza et al.","title":"K2-140b - an eccentric 6.57 d transiting hot Jupiter in Virgo","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06865","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2140b-Korthetal2019","cite":"Korth et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Korth, Sz Csizmadia, D. Gandolfi et al.","title":"K2-140b and K2-180b - Characterization of a hot Jupiter and a mini-Neptune from the K2 mission","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04601","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 60532 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work on this system has studied how its two giant planets pull on each other. The best fit to the star's velocity data puts the pair in a 3:1 resonance, meaning the outer planet circles once for every three orbits of the inner one (Laskar & Correia 2009). The system stays stable for at least 5 billion years, and stability is still possible over a wide range of orbits (Laskar & Correia 2009).","system":"If the orbital plane is tilted to about 20 degrees from the sky plane, the true masses are 3.1 Jupiter masses for the inner planet and 7.4 for the outer one (Laskar & Correia 2009).","history":"","open":"The tilt of the orbits is not measured, so the true masses of both planets are still uncertain (Laskar & Correia 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd60532b-LaskarCorreia2009","cite":"Laskar & Correia 2009","year":"2009","authors":"J. Laskar, A. C. M. Correia","title":"HD 60532, a planetary system in a 3:1 mean motion resonance","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 714-47 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet sits in the 'hot Neptune desert', a range of short orbits where Neptune-sized planets are rare (Dreizler et al. 2020). Its mass and radius are both measured with low uncertainties, so it can be compared meaningfully with models of planet interiors (Dreizler et al. 2020). Between February 2019 and January 2020, six spectrographs gathered 102 spectra of the star to weigh the planet (Dreizler et al. 2020). Adaptive optics imaging and many ground-based telescopes helped confirm the signal (Dreizler et al. 2020).","system":"The host is a slowly rotating early M dwarf, with a rotation period of about 33 days (Dreizler et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp71447b-Dreizleretal2020","cite":"Dreizler et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"S. Dreizler, I. J. M. Crossfield, D. Kossakowski et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. LP 714-47 b (TOI 442.01): populating the Neptune desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.01716","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2025-BLG-0975L b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up watched the Galactic bulge at high cadence during the 2025 season (Han et al. 2026).","observed":"A short-lived brightening near the peak of the lensing light curve revealed the planet (Han et al. 2026). The ratio of planet mass to host mass came out at 8.6 x 10^-4 (Han et al. 2026). Finite-source effects were detected, which allowed the angular size of the lensing ring on the sky to be measured (Han et al. 2026). The planet lies beyond the snow line, the distance from its star where water ice can form (Han et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"The mass is poorly pinned down: the estimate is 29.8 Earth masses, with a range from about 14 to about 80 Earth masses (Han et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2025blg0975lb-Hanetal2026","cite":"Han et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Cheongho Han, Chung-Uk Lee, Andrzej Udalski et al.","title":"KMT-2025-BLG-0975Lb and KMT-2025-BLG-1160Lb: Two Uranus-Mass Planets Beyond the Snow Line Discovered by Microlensing","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25259","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5489 b":{"text":{"why":"Earth-sized planets around M dwarfs are among the best targets for studying the atmospheres and bulk makeup of rocky worlds with current telescopes (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed statistically, by showing that false explanations are very unlikely, using TESS light curves, colour-filtered observations from Palomar and Las Cumbres Observatory, high-resolution imaging and stellar spectroscopy (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). It is a super-Earth about 1.40 times the radius of Earth (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026).","system":"A second planet, TOI-5489 c, has a similar size of 1.28 Earth radii and orbits in 4.921 days, compared with 3.152 days for planet b (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5489b-Barrientosetal2026","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Heather A. Knutson, Morgan Saidel et al.","title":"From Earths to Super-Earths: Five New Small Planets Transiting M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11971","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5232 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of nine hot Jupiters announced together as the first results of a survey aiming at a complete sample of such planets around Sun-like stars brighter than Gaia magnitude 12.5 (Schulte et al. 2024). Using the same methods for every planet is meant to make the sample reliable for statistics (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy after being flagged by the TESS satellite (Schulte et al. 2024). The nine planets in the announcement range from 0.55 to 3.88 Jupiter masses and from 0.967 to 1.438 Jupiter radii (Schulte et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"Three ideas compete to explain hot Jupiters: forming where they are, migrating inward through the gas disc, or being flung inward on a stretched orbit that tides later circularise (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","open":"Where hot Jupiters come from is still debated (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5232b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5232b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CHXR 73 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Its infrared spectrum shows strong steam absorption, and the shape of the spectrum shows that it is young and not a background star (Luhman et al. 2006). Its mass was estimated by comparing its brightness with evolutionary models (Luhman et al. 2006). Infrared Spitzer images show light consistent with the object's own surface alone, so it is unlikely to have a disc around it (Martinez & Kraus 2022).","system":"The host is a young, low-mass star about 2 million years old and about 0.35 solar masses (Luhman et al. 2006). The chance that the two are unrelated members of the cluster is about 0.001 (Luhman et al. 2006).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-chxr73b-Luhmanetal2006","cite":"Luhman et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"K. L. Luhman, J. C. Wilson, W. Brandner et al.","title":"Discovery of a Young Substellar Companion in Chamaeleon","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0609187","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-chxr73b-MartinezKraus2022","cite":"Martinez & Kraus 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Raquel A. Martinez, Adam L. Kraus","title":"A Mid-infrared Study of Directly Imaged Planetary-mass Companions Using Archival Spitzer/IRAC Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-82 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its other planets. Planets b and c show regular timing shifts in their transits because they pull on each other near a 2:1 orbital resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Extra small jitter in the timing of planet c hints at a further planet near a 3:2 or 3:1 resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Modelling of the timings revealed a new planet, Kepler-82 f, with a mass of about 21 Earth masses, which does not transit (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","system":"The system was followed up by a worldwide network of telescopes that completed the transit-timing curves after Kepler stopped looking at its original field in 2013 (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler82d-Freudenthaletal2019","cite":"Freudenthal et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Freudenthal, C. von Essen, A. Ofir et al.","title":"Kepler Object of Interest Network. III. Kepler-82f: a new non-transiting 21 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> planet from photodynamical modelling","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.06534","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 106252 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities alone give only a minimum mass. Combining them with Hipparcos and Gaia measurements of the star's tiny sideways motion gave a true mass of about 10.00 Jupiter masses, which confirms that the companion is a planet (Li et al. 2021). A two-fold ambiguity in the orbit's tilt remains, because forward and backward motion on the orbit cannot be told apart (Li et al. 2022).","system":"The star's chemical makeup matches the Sun's (Sadakane et al. 2002).","history":"","open":"The tilt of the orbit is known only up to a mirror-image ambiguity (Li et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd106252b-Lietal2021","cite":"Li et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106252b-Lietal2022","cite":"Li et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Yiting Li, Timothy D. Brandt, G. Mirek Brandt et al.","title":"Precise Masses and Orbits for Nine Radial-velocity Exoplanets","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.10422","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd106252b-Sadakaneetal2002","cite":"Sadakane et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Kozo Sadakane, Michiko Ohkubo, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Abundance Analyses of 12 Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets Observed with the SUBARU/HDS","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/54.6.911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4616 b":{"text":{"why":"Rocky planets are common around M dwarfs, and the small size and low brightness of these stars make terrestrial planets easier to detect and to study for atmospheres (Lang et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed statistically by combining TESS photometry with multi-band ground-based transit observations, high-resolution imaging, and optical and near-infrared spectroscopy (Lang et al. 2026). The SAINT-EX telescope, built to study planets around very cool stars, took part in characterising it (Lang & Demory 2026). The planet is Earth-sized, about 1.22 Earth radii, and receives roughly 40 times the sunlight that Earth does (Lang et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a nearby mid-M dwarf about 28.10 parsecs away, with a radius of 0.1889 solar radii (Lang et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4616b-Langetal2026","cite":"Lang et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"F. Zong Lang, B. O. Demory, Y. Gómez Maqueo Chew et al.","title":"TOI-4616 b: an Earth-sized planet transiting a nearby M4 dwarf","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.10905","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi4616b-LangDemory2026","cite":"Lang & Demory 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Francis Zong Lang, Brice-Olivier Demory","title":"Constraining The Parameters of an Earth-Sized Exoplanet Orbiting an M3 Star","journal":"EPSC-DPS Joint Meeting 2025","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc-dps2025-857","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-431 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a sub-Neptune with a measured density of 1.36 grams per cubic centimetre, low enough to suggest a thick, extended atmosphere (Osborn et al. 2021). Its mass is about 9.90 Earth masses and its radius is about 3.29 Earth radii (Osborn et al. 2021). It is one of the best TESS finds for studying an atmosphere (Osborn et al. 2021).","system":"The star is a bright early K dwarf that rotates every 30.5 days (Osborn et al. 2021). Planet b is a super-Earth on a 0.49-day orbit with a density of 8.0 grams per cubic centimetre (Osborn et al. 2021).","history":"The inner planet b may be a stripped core, a world that lost its atmosphere (Osborn et al. 2021). Planets b and d lie on opposite sides of the gap in planet sizes, which makes the system a case study for how atmospheres evolve (Osborn et al. 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi431d-Osbornetal2021","cite":"Osborn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Ares Osborn, David J. Armstrong, Bryson Cale et al.","title":"TOI-431/HIP 26013: a super-Earth and a sub-Neptune transiting a bright, early K dwarf, with a third RV planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.02310","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1136 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the whole chain of planets. The star hosts at least six transiting planets between about 2 and 5 Earth radii (Dai et al. 2023). Timing shifts of the transits gave planet masses of 3 to 8 Earth masses (Dai et al. 2023). The period ratios sit within about 10^-4 of exact resonance, much closer than the roughly 10^-2 seen in typical Kepler systems (Dai et al. 2023).","system":"Period ratios are near 3:2, 2:1, 3:2, 7:5 and 3:2, making this the first known resonant chain with a 7:5 link between two simpler ones (Dai et al. 2023). The star is about 700 million years old (Dai et al. 2023). Its spin lines up with the planets' orbits, judging from a measurement on planet d (Dai et al. 2023).","history":"Orbits in resonance are thought to arise when planets migrate inward together through a gas disc. The orderly system suggests an isolated, calm disc, with no passing star and no disc warp (Dai et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1136b-Daietal2023","cite":"Dai et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Fei Dai, Kento Masuda, Corey Beard et al.","title":"TOI-1136 is a Young, Coplanar, Aligned Planetary System in a Pristine Resonant Chain","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09283","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2005 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet follows a stretched orbit, with eccentricity about 0.59, around a rapidly rotating F star (Bieryla et al. 2025). Spectroscopy taken during a transit shows that the orbit is nearly aligned with the star's spin (Bieryla et al. 2025). Its temperature at closest approach is about 2100 K, and it swings by almost 1000 K over one orbit (Bieryla et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"Few planets are known with both a high eccentricity and an aligned orbit, which may come from migration inward along a stretched orbit that stayed in the same plane (Bieryla et al. 2025).","open":"The fast heating and cooling could reveal how quickly the atmosphere responds to changes in starlight (Bieryla et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2005b-Bierylaetal2025","cite":"Bieryla et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Allyson Bieryla, Jiayin Dong, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2005b: An Eccentric Warm Jupiter in Spin-orbit Alignment","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20069","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-9 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits far enough from its star to have a large zone of gravitational control, about 3.5 million kilometres in radius, where rings and moons could survive (Hebrard 2010).","observed":"It is one of the rare transiting giants with a long period, 95.3 days, and a measured mass (Bonomo et al. 2017). Five years of radial-velocity monitoring and three more transits seen by CoRoT and Spitzer revealed a small but real eccentricity and no sign of further planets (Bonomo et al. 2017). Searches for rings and moons were planned with Spitzer observations of its transits (Hebrard 2010).","system":"","history":"Computer simulations show that the eccentricity may come from planet-planet scattering, in which a planet of 50 Earth masses was thrown out of the system (Bonomo et al. 2017). That scattering would not tilt the orbit away from the star's equator (Bonomo et al. 2017).","open":"A large tilt between the orbit and the star's spin would mean the original disc was tilted (Bonomo et al. 2017). Whether the planet has rings or moons is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot9b-Hebrard2010","cite":"Hebrard 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Guillaume Hebrard, Alain Lecavelier Des Etangs, Jean-Michel Desert et al.","title":"Search for rings and satellites around the temperate exoplanet CoRoT-9b (continued)","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot9b-Bonomoetal2017","cite":"Bonomo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. S. Bonomo, G. Hebrard, S. N. Raymond et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CoRoT-9 radial velocity curve (Bonomo+, 2017)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36030043","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":10,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-815 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The system was watched by TESS in three stretches, from December 2018 to January 2019, in March 2021 and in March 2023 (Psaridi et al. 2024). The CHEOPS space telescope added observations of 8 to 11 hours each, to sharpen planet sizes in multi-planet systems and to confirm the orbits of long-period candidates (Psaridi et al. 2024). Ground telescopes in the Las Cumbres network and the ASTEP telescope took part, and 34 high-resolution spectra were collected (Psaridi et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi815b-Psaridietal2024","cite":"Psaridi et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"A. Psaridi, H. Osborn, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-815 light curves and RV sources (Psaridi+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 63433 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planets b and c, two sub-Neptunes on orbits of about 7.1 and 20.5 days (Damasso et al. 2023). Two years of radial-velocity monitoring with HARPS-N gave the first mass measurements for those two planets (Damasso et al. 2023). Little is known about planet d itself.","system":"The star is only about 400 million years old, so the planets are at an early stage of their evolution (Damasso et al. 2023).","history":"Planets b and c may be losing atmosphere under the star's radiation, and their past and future mass loss was modelled (Damasso et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd63433d-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Locci, S. Benatti et al.","title":"The GAPS Programme at TNG. XLII. A characterisation study of the multi-planet system around the 400 Myr-old star HD 63433 (TOI-1726)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15242","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-105 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a hot Neptune-sized world; its transit depth points to a radius of about 3.59 Earth radii (Narita et al. 2017). Radial velocities from the Subaru telescope set a 3-sigma upper limit of 90 Earth masses on the mass (Narita et al. 2017). Adaptive-optics imaging rules out faint companions near the star, and the chance that the signal is a false alarm is below one in a million (Narita et al. 2017). Transit light from K2 and from multi-colour ground observations agree on the depth (Narita et al. 2017).","system":"The host is a metal-rich G dwarf, with an iron abundance of 0.26 on a logarithmic scale relative to the Sun (Narita et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2105b-Naritaetal2017","cite":"Narita et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Norio Narita, Teruyuki Hirano, Akihiko Fukui et al.","title":"The K2-ESPRINT project. VI. K2-105 b, a hot Neptune around a metal-rich G-dwarf","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.01294","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-155 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is near the habitable zone and receives 1.67 times the sunlight Earth gets (Hirano et al. 2018). Its size falls between smaller rocky planets and larger gas-rich ones, so its makeup is uncertain (Hirano et al. 2018). Three-dimensional climate simulations were run, assuming a tidally locked planet with an Earth-like composition and atmosphere (Hirano et al. 2018).","system":"The star is a relatively bright, metal-poor early M dwarf, and three super-Earths orbit it with periods of 6.34, 13.85 and 40.68 days (Hirano et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is rocky or gas-rich, and what atmosphere it has, is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2155d-Hiranoetal2018","cite":"Hirano et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Teruyuki Hirano, Fei Dai, John H. Livingston et al.","title":"K2-155: A Bright Metal-poor M Dwarf with Three Transiting Super-Earths","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06957","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 196885 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits the brighter star of a close pair, and is a giant with a period of 1349 days (Correia et al. 2008). A 386-day planet suggested earlier could not be confirmed (Correia et al. 2008). Radial velocities now span almost 40 years, and high-resolution images taken between 2015 and 2020 resolve the stellar companion (Chauvin et al. 2023).","system":"The companion star is a low-mass M dwarf about 23 AU from the main star, one of the closest resolved binaries known to host a planet (Chauvin et al. 2007).","history":"Planets in such a perturbed region challenge theories of planet formation and long-term stability (Chauvin et al. 2023). Simulations suggest the system is more stable if the planet's orbit is steeply tilted relative to the stellar pair, a Kozai-type arrangement (Chauvin 2011).","open":"The true mass is not settled. Models that include the stars' mutual pull give a minimum mass differing by more than 0.2 Jupiter masses from simple orbit fits (Huang et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd196885ab-Correiaetal2008","cite":"Correia et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"A. C. M. Correia, S. Udry, M. Mayor et al.","title":"The ELODIE survey for northern extra-solar planets. IV. HD 196885, a close binary star with a 3.7-year planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20078908","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196885ab-Chauvinetal2023","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Chauvin, M. Videla, H. Beust et al.","title":"Chasing extreme planetary architectures. I. HD 196885 Ab, a super-Jupiter dancing with two stars?","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00994","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196885ab-Chauvinetal2007","cite":"Chauvin et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Chauvin, A.-M. Lagrange, S. Udry et al.","title":"Characterization of the long-period companions of the exoplanet host stars: HD 196885, HD 1237 and HD 27442. VLT/NACO and SINFONI near-infrared, follow-up imaging and spectroscopy","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20067046","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196885ab-Chauvin2011","cite":"Chauvin 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Gael Chauvin","title":"Hd196885, The Most Extreme Planetary System?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196885ab-Huangetal2025","cite":"Huang et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Xiumin Huang, Jianghui Ji, Chunhui Bao et al.","title":"Closeby Habitable Exoplanet Survey (CHES). III. Retrieval of Planetary Masses in Binaries Using the N-body Model with Radial Velocity and Astrometry Synergy","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17090","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":13,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 94235 b":{"text":{"why":"Young stars bright enough for TESS are valued as benchmarks for testing theories of how planets evolve (Zhou et al. 2022).","observed":"TESS saw three transits that block only 0.06% of the starlight (Zhou et al. 2022). Starspots make the young host vary by 2% peak to peak, so the transits are dwarfed by the star's own changes (Zhou et al. 2022). Follow-up with the CHEOPS satellite confirmed the dips and kept the transit times predictable (Zhou et al. 2022). Hubble searched for escaping neutral hydrogen and saw none, limiting the loss to 10^13 g/s (Morrissey et al. 2024). Neutral hydrogen leaving the planet would be ionised within about 15 minutes, which may hide it (Morrissey et al. 2024). A search for escaping helium with CRIRES+ at the Very Large Telescope also found nothing, limiting the loss to 10^11 g/s, and no water was detected (Morrissey et al. 2025).","system":"The host star belongs to a 50 au binary with a lower-mass companion, HIP 94235 B, whose orbit was constrained with 11 years of imaging and with Hipparcos and Gaia motions (Zhou et al. 2022). The star shows spot modulation, lithium absorption and X-ray emission, all signs of youth (Zhou et al. 2022).","history":"The planet sits at the end of a roughly 100 million year phase of intense X-ray and ultraviolet irradiation (Morrissey et al. 2024). Mass-loss models suggest it will shrink into a super-Earth within 1 billion years (Morrissey et al. 2024).","open":"The atmosphere probably lacks a large hydrogen-helium envelope, but no molecule has been detected in it (Morrissey et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip94235b-Zhouetal2022","cite":"Zhou et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"George Zhou, Christopher P. Wirth, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"A Mini-Neptune from TESS and CHEOPS Around the 120 Myr Old AB Dor Member HIP 94235","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11975","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip94235b-Morrisseyetal2024","cite":"Morrissey et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Ava Morrissey, George Zhou, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"Searching for Neutral Hydrogen Escape from the 120 Myr Old Sub-Neptune HIP94235b using HST","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02170","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip94235b-Morrisseyetal2025","cite":"Morrissey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ava Morrissey, George Zhou, Chelsea X. Huang et al.","title":"Searching for Neutral Hydrogen Escape from the 120 Myr Old Sub-Neptune HIP94235b using HST","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02170","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 196050 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from the Anglo-Australian Planet Search, which measures stellar velocities with an iodine cell for precision (Jones et al. 2002).","observed":"Years of Doppler measurements show a velocity cycle of about 1300 days, with an orbit mildly eccentric (e = 0.19) and a minimum mass of 2.8 Jupiter masses (Jones et al. 2002). The orbit has a semi-major axis near 2.4 au (Jones et al. 2002). A later survey with the CORALIE spectrograph counted the planet among typical Jupiter-like planets on intermediate or long, fairly eccentric orbits (Mayor et al. 2004). A search for water-maser radio emission from the system detected none (Greenhill et al. 2002).","system":"The star has a wide companion, HD 196050 B, an early M dwarf (Mugrauer et al. 2005). Planets with periods longer than 100 days tend to look alike around single stars and around stars in binaries (Mugrauer et al. 2005).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd196050b-Jonesetal2002","cite":"Jones et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Hugh R. A. Jones, R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Extrasolar planets around HD 196050, HD 216437 and HD 160691","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0206216","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196050b-Mayoretal2004","cite":"Mayor et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"M. Mayor, S. Udry, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. XII. Orbital solutions for 16 extra-solar planets discovered with CORALIE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0310316","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196050b-Greenhilletal2002","cite":"Greenhill et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"L. Greenhill, P. Kondratko, J. Lovell et al.","title":"epsilon Eridani, upsilon Andromedae, 51 Pegasi, HD 209458, HD 196050","journal":"International Astronomical Union Circular","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd196050b-Mugraueretal2005","cite":"Mugrauer et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"M. Mugrauer, R. Neuhäuser, A. Seifahrt et al.","title":"Four new wide binaries among exoplanet host stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0507101","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-3 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery report also describes the low- and medium-resolution spectroscopy used to sort the survey's many transit candidates and reject eclipsing binary stars (Bayliss et al. 2013).","observed":"The planet is inflated, meaning its radius of 1.38 Jupiter radii is large for its mass of 1.07 Jupiter masses (Bayliss et al. 2013). Its F-type host star spins with a projected speed of 9.0 km/s, making the system a good target for measuring whether the orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Bayliss et al. 2013). The system was among the hot Jupiter targets of a programme to measure such spin-orbit angles with the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect (Addison et al. 2015). Ground-based follow-up transits were also taken to keep its transit times well known for future atmosphere studies (Edwards et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats3b-Baylissetal2013","cite":"Bayliss et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"D. Bayliss, G. Zhou, K. Penev et al.","title":"HATS-3b: An Inflated Hot Jupiter Transiting an F-type Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.0624","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats3b-Addisonetal2015","cite":"Addison et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Brett Christopher Addison, Chris Tinney, Duncan Wright et al.","title":"Probing the Migration Histories of Exoplanets Through Measurements of Their Spin-Orbit Alignments","journal":"IAU General Assembly","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats3b-Edwardsetal2021","cite":"Edwards et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Billy Edwards, Cynthia Ho, Hannah Osborne et al.","title":"Original Research By Young Twinkle Students (ORBYTS): Ephemeris Refinement of Transiting Exoplanets III","journal":"Astronomy: Theory, Observations and Methods","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10350","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1807 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is young, bright, cool and nearby, which makes the planet an excellent target for follow-up (Hedges et al. 2021).","observed":"The orbit lasts only 0.549 days, so the planet circles very close to its star (Hedges et al. 2021). Spectroscopy with HARPS-N and TESS light curves were used to characterise both the planet and the star's activity (Nardiello et al. 2022). The planet was one of 20 searched for escaping helium and hydrogen-alpha light with CARMENES and GIARPS; the new detections in that survey went to other planets (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). Models of five hot rocky planets, including TOI-1807 b, for a JWST programme mostly lean towards bare rock with no thick atmosphere (Sastre et al. 2026).","system":"TOI-1807 has a comoving partner star, TOI-2076, which hosts three transiting planets (Hedges et al. 2021). The host is a young K star in a moving group about 300 million years old (Nardiello et al. 2022). Starspots make its brightness vary periodically (Hedges et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet keeps any atmosphere over a molten surface is still to be tested with JWST (Sastre et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1807b-Hedgesetal2021","cite":"Hedges et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Christina Hedges, Alex Hughes, George Zhou et al.","title":"TOI-2076 and TOI-1807: Two Young, Comoving Planetary Systems within 50 pc Identified by TESS that are Ideal Candidates for Further Follow Up","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01311","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1807b-Nardielloetal2022","cite":"Nardiello et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"D. Nardiello, L. Malavolta, S. Desidera et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TOI-1807 RV and flux curves (Nardiello+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36640163","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1807b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1807b-Sastreetal2026","cite":"Sastre et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mariana Sastre, Tim Lichtenberg, Lisa Dang et al.","title":"Thermal Evolution of Lava Planets Across System Ages: Predictions for Hell of a Survey","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20155","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-145 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The transit is grazing, so the planet only partly crosses the face of its star (Hellier et al. 2019). The planet has 0.89 Jupiter masses and a 1.77-day orbit (Hellier et al. 2019). The host is a K2 star of magnitude 11.5 (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"The host has a nearby companion star 5 arcseconds away that is 1.4 magnitudes fainter (Hellier et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp145ab-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"New transiting hot Jupiters discovered by WASP-South, Euler/CORALIE, and TRAPPIST-South","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.02224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 231701 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an F8 dwarf with more heavy elements than the Sun, and the planet has at least 1.08 Jupiter masses (Fischer et al. 2007). The planet was reported along with four other Jupiter-mass planets, all around stars rich in heavy elements (Fischer et al. 2007). Several of those stars looked quiet in early observations, and about three years of follow-up revealed longer-period planets (Fischer et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd231701b-Fischeretal2007","cite":"Fischer et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Steven S. Vogt, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"Five Intermediate-Period Planets from the N2K Sample","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0704.1191","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-20 b":{"text":{"why":"A second survey reported the planet independently under the name MASCARA-2 b (Talens et al. 2018). Its star is a bright, rapidly rotating A star (Lund et al. 2017).","observed":"High-resolution spectra taken during transits show absorption from calcium, iron, sodium and hydrogen in the atmosphere (Casasayas-Barris et al. 2019). Neutral iron is shifted by -3.4 km/s from the planet's rest frame, which points to a strong wind from the day side to the night side (Nugroho et al. 2020). Iron also shows up in emission, a sign that the temperature rises with height (Johnson et al. 2023). Searches for titanium oxide, vanadium oxide, iron hydride and calcium hydride found none (Johnson et al. 2023). The mass is only limited, to below about 3.5 Jupiter masses (Lund et al. 2017).","system":"The host star spins fast, at about 120 km/s, and is younger than 600 million years (Lund et al. 2017). The planet's orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, which is unusual for a hot Jupiter around a hot star (Talens et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"What causes the hot upper atmosphere is unknown. Titanium oxide and calcium hydride are ruled out, and vanadium oxide only if its line list is accurate (Johnson et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt20b-Talensetal2018","cite":"Talens et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"G. J. J. Talens, A. B. Justesen, S. Albrecht et al.","title":"MASCARA-2 b. A hot Jupiter transiting the m<SUB>V</SUB> = 7.6 A-star HD 185603","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01500","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt20b-Lundetal2017","cite":"Lund et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Michael B. Lund, Joseph E. Rodriguez, George Zhou et al.","title":"KELT-20b: A Giant Planet with a Period of P ∼ 3.5 days Transiting the V ∼ 7.6 Early A Star HD 185603","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01518","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt20b-CasasayasBarrisetal2019","cite":"Casasayas-Barris et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"N. Casasayas-Barris, E. Pallé, F. Yan et al.","title":"Atmospheric characterization of the ultra-hot Jupiter MASCARA-2b/KELT-20b. Detection of CaII, FeII, NaI, and the Balmer series of H (Hα, Hβ, and Hγ) with high-dispersion transit spectroscopy","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12491","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt20b-Nugrohoetal2020","cite":"Nugroho et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Stevanus K. Nugroho, Neale P. Gibson, Ernst J. W. de Mooij et al.","title":"Searching for thermal inversion agents in the transmission spectrum of KELT-20b/MASCARA-2b: detection of neutral iron and ionised calcium H&K lines","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04856","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt20b-Johnsonetal2023","cite":"Johnson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Marshall C. Johnson, Ji Wang, Anusha Pai Asnodkar et al.","title":"The PEPSI Exoplanet Transit Survey (PETS). II. A Deep Search for Thermal Inversion Agents in KELT-20 b/MASCARA-2 b with Emission and Transmission Spectroscopy","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12162","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":35,"about":14,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-442 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was one of 12 small Kepler candidates in the habitable zone whose masses are too small to measure by Doppler shifts, so they were confirmed by statistics instead (Torres et al. 2015).","observed":"Confirmation used the BLENDER technique, which simulates many false positives and compares them with the Kepler light curve. It was backed by spectroscopy, adaptive-optics imaging and speckle imaging (Torres et al. 2015). The planet's mass has not been measured. Its size suggests a rocky world of about 2.3 Earth masses, and the starlight it receives is about 0.81 times what Earth gets (Guinan et al. 2023).","system":"The host is a K5 dwarf of 15th magnitude (Guinan et al. 2023). Studies of the star's age, X-ray and ultraviolet output and stellar wind were made to judge the planet's habitability (Guinan et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"Whether the planet has an atmosphere, or how it is affected by stellar wind, is not known (Guinan et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler442b-Torresetal2015","cite":"Torres et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Guillermo Torres, David M. Kipping, Francois Fressin et al.","title":"Validation of 12 Small Kepler Transiting Planets in the Habitable Zone","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01101","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler442b-Guinanetal2023","cite":"Guinan et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Edward Guinan, Claire Finley, Scott Engle et al.","title":"Kepler-442 b: Earth-size planet orbiting in the Goldilocks Zone of its old \"Goldilocks\" K5V host star: Age, XUV Irradiances and Potential for advanced alien Life","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #241","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":1,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TrES-1 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is a fairly bright K0 dwarf, and the transit was caught by a multisite survey (Alonso et al. 2004).","observed":"The planet has a smaller radius than HD 209458 b at the same mass, so its size fits standard models without extra heat deep inside (Alonso et al. 2004). Spitzer measured its dayside heat. Models fit to those data show no hot stratosphere (Burrows et al. 2008). Its emission spectrum is well described with water in absorption, and it orbits an active star (Knutson et al. 2010). Hot-planet atmospheres may lose their stratospheres to the extra ultraviolet from active stars (Knutson et al. 2010). Transit and eclipse timing shows its orbit changing over years (Hagey et al. 2025).","system":"Radial velocities reveal a candidate companion on a wide, eccentric orbit, though it is unlikely to cause the orbital changes (Hagey et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Apsidal precession at 4 degrees per year would need an undetected close-in planet, which is not seen (Hagey et al. 2025). An alternative is orbital decay of about 7 milliseconds per year, driven by tides on a tilted planet (Hagey et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tres1b-Alonsoetal2004","cite":"Alonso et al. 2004","year":"2004","authors":"Roi Alonso, Timothy M. Brown, Guillermo Torres et al.","title":"TrES-1: The Transiting Planet of a Bright K0 V Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0408421","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres1b-Burrowsetal2008","cite":"Burrows et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"A. Burrows, J. Budaj, I. Hubeny","title":"Theoretical Spectra and Light Curves of Close-in Extrasolar Giant Planets and Comparison with Data","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.4080","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres1b-Knutsonetal2010","cite":"Knutson et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Heather A. Knutson, Andrew W. Howard, Howard Isaacson","title":"A Correlation Between Stellar Activity and Hot Jupiter Emission Spectra","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.2702","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tres1b-Hageyetal2025","cite":"Hagey et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Simone R. Hagey, Billy Edwards, Angelos Tsiaras et al.","title":"TrES-1 b: A Case Study in Detecting Secular Evolution of Exoplanet Orbits","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15075","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":29,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 791-18 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Work so far consists of models rather than direct measurements of the atmosphere. The planet is estimated to receive continuous tidal heating from the orbital configuration of its system, so volcanic activity is expected (Gkouvelis et al. 2025). Models of an outgassing atmosphere put the mantle temperature at about 1680 to 1880 K (Gkouvelis et al. 2025). The gas mix depends on the oxygen content of the interior rather than on the overall abundance of heavy elements (Gkouvelis et al. 2025).","system":"Tidal heating comes from the planet's orbital arrangement with its neighbours (Gkouvelis et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"Whether a stable atmosphere can survive depends on how oxidised the interior is, which is not known (Gkouvelis et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp79118d-Gkouvelisetal2025","cite":"Gkouvelis et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Leonardos Gkouvelis, Francisco J. Pozuelos, Thomas Drant et al.","title":"Interior redox state effects on the stability of secondary atmospheres and observational manifestations: LP 791-18 d as a case study for outgassing rocky exoplanets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.02188","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"ROXs 12 b":{"text":{"why":"Candidate companions were noted in earlier surveys but neglected for over ten years until follow-up showed they move with their stars (Kraus et al. 2014).","observed":"Direct imaging gives a mass of 16 Jupiter masses at a projected 210 au from its star (Kraus et al. 2014). Spectra reveal low surface gravity, weak alkali lines and a triangular H-band shape, all signs of youth (Bowler et al. 2017). The companion shows no accretion emission, and its brightness and the host's age of about 6 million years give a mass of 17.5 Jupiter masses (Bowler et al. 2017). ALMA found no dust disc around it down to a limit of 120 to 210 microjanskys (Wu et al. 2020).","system":"A wide third object, 2MASS J16262774-2527247, lies 5100 au away and is accreting heavily and varying irregularly (Bowler et al. 2017). The host's spin axis and the companion's orbit are likely misaligned (Bowler et al. 2017).","history":"Misalignment suggests the companion formed more like fragmenting binary stars than like planets in a flat disc (Bowler et al. 2017).","open":"Its mass sits near the deuterium-burning limit, so whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf is unclear (Bowler et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-roxs12b-Krausetal2014","cite":"Kraus et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Adam L. Kraus, Michael J. Ireland, Lucas A. Cieza et al.","title":"Three Wide Planetary-mass Companions to FW Tau, ROXs 12, and ROXs 42B","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.7664","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-roxs12b-Bowleretal2017","cite":"Bowler et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Brendan P. Bowler, Adam L. Kraus, Marta L. Bryan et al.","title":"The Young Substellar Companion ROXs 12 B: Near-infrared Spectrum, System Architecture, and Spin-Orbit Misalignment","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07611","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-roxs12b-Wuetal2020","cite":"Wu et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Ya-Lin Wu, Brendan P. Bowler, Patrick D. Sheehan et al.","title":"ALMA 0.88 mm Survey of Disks around Planetary-mass Companions","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.08658","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-432 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet occupies an almost empty region of parameter space with Kepler-56 and Kepler-91, giants transiting evolved stars, which helps constrain how such systems evolve (Ciceri et al. 2015).","observed":"Three teams measured velocities with the CAFE spectrograph and others, giving masses from 4.87 to 5.84 Jupiter masses (Ciceri et al. 2015; Quinn et al. 2015; Ortiz et al. 2015). The orbit is highly eccentric, with e between about 0.48 and 0.54 (Ortiz et al. 2015; Ciceri et al. 2015). An independent method using stellar density gave a matching eccentricity (Quinn et al. 2015). At 0.303 au, the planet is the first warm Jupiter confirmed to transit a giant star (Ortiz et al. 2015).","system":"A non-transiting outer planet, Kepler-432 c, has a minimum mass of 2.43 Jupiter masses and a 406-day period (Quinn et al. 2015). A faint star lies 1.1 arcseconds from the host but is too dim to affect the transit (Ciceri et al. 2015).","history":"Evolution models predict the planet will most likely be engulfed by its host before the star reaches the tip of the red giant branch (Ortiz et al. 2015).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler432b-Cicerietal2015","cite":"Ciceri et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"S. Ciceri, J. Lillo-Box, J. Southworth et al.","title":"Kepler-432 b: a massive planet in a highly eccentric orbit transiting a red giant","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.2999","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler432b-Quinnetal2015","cite":"Quinn et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Samuel N. Quinn, Timothy. R. White, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Kepler-432: A Red Giant Interacting with One of its Two Long-period Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4666","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler432b-Ortizetal2015","cite":"Ortiz et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Mauricio Ortiz, Davide Gandolfi, Sabine Reffert et al.","title":"Kepler-432 b: a massive warm Jupiter in a 52-day eccentric orbit transiting a giant star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.3000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-264 c":{"text":{"why":"Planets in star clusters have well-known ages, a rare window on how planets evolve (Livingston et al. 2019).","observed":"The planet is the outer of two warm sub-Neptunes transiting an M dwarf in the Praesepe cluster, which is 600 to 800 million years old (Livingston et al. 2019). Its equilibrium temperature is 331 K (Livingston et al. 2019). Compared with planets around field stars of similar mass, its radius is not inflated (Livingston et al. 2019). Follow-up with near-infrared spectrographs could measure the masses (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"The inner planet orbits every 5.8 days, with a radius of 2.2 Earth radii and an equilibrium temperature of 496 K (Livingston et al. 2019). K2-264 is only the second known cluster star with multiple transiting planets (Livingston et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"The system is a useful test of photoevaporation in multi-planet systems, but the masses are not yet known (Livingston et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2264c-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Fei Dai, Teruyuki Hirano et al.","title":"K2-264: a transiting multiplanet system in the Praesepe open cluster","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.01968","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-55 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up light curves show the planet is inflated, with a radius of 1.34 Jupiter radii for 0.63 Jupiter masses, the largest mismatch with models among four planets studied (Southworth et al. 2016). No transit timing variations were seen (Southworth et al. 2016). K2 data were searched for timing changes, starspots and extra planets (Clark et al. 2018). Magellan high-resolution spectroscopy found no excess absorption from hydrogen-alpha, calcium or sodium, with limits between under 2% and under 8% across three planets (Wang & Spake 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether it has an extended escaping atmosphere is unclear, and higher signal-to-noise data would be needed (Wang & Spake 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp55b-Southworthetal2016","cite":"Southworth et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"John Southworth, J. Tregloan-Reed, M. I. Andersen et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VIII. WASP-22, WASP-41, WASP-42 and WASP-55","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05549","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp55b-Clarketal2018","cite":"Clark et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"B. J. M. Clark, D. R. Anderson, C. Hellier et al.","title":"An Analysis of Transiting Hot Jupiters Observed with K2: WASP-55b and WASP-75b","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.02132","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp55b-WangSpake2026","cite":"Wang & Spake 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Gavin Wang, Jessica Spake","title":"Searching for Atmospheric Escape with the Magellan Inamori Kyocera Echelle Spectrograph","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2016-BLG-2364L b":{"text":{"why":"Microlensing planets around faint source stars give weak signals and can go unreported, so the survey hunted for them to build a complete demographic census (Han et al. 2020).","observed":"The signal came from an event with a faint background source star. Analysis of this and three other faint-source events found four giant planets around stars less massive than the Sun (Han et al. 2020). Across the four systems, host masses lie between 0.50 and 0.85 solar masses, planet masses between 0.5 and 13.2 Jupiter masses, and lenses between 3.8 and 6.4 kiloparsecs (Han et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2016blg2364lb-Hanetal2020","cite":"Han et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Doeon Kim et al.","title":"Four microlensing planets with faint-source stars identified in the 2016 and 2017 season data","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.09258","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-8 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its neighbour WASP-8 b. WASP-8 b, of 2.25 Jupiter masses, transits on an eccentric 8.15-day orbit strongly inclined to the star's equator and moving in a retrograde direction against the star's rotation (Queloz et al. 2010). Spitzer eclipse measurements show no temperature inversion in its atmosphere (Cubillos et al. 2013). Little is known about planet c itself.","system":"The host star is in a multiple stellar system with two other companions (Queloz et al. 2010).","history":"The dynamical complexity suggests the Kozai mechanism or a formation differing from classical disc migration (Queloz et al. 2010).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp8c-Quelozetal2010","cite":"Queloz et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"D. Queloz, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-8b: a retrograde transiting planet in a multiple system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.5089","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp8c-Cubillosetal2013","cite":"Cubillos et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Patricio Cubillos, Joseph Harrington, Nikku Madhusudhan et al.","title":"WASP-8b: Characterization of a Cool and Eccentric Exoplanet with Spitzer","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5468","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 10442 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up was designed for short-period planets and later extended to wide orbits, to measure how common giant planets are beyond the ice line (Giguere et al. 2015).","observed":"The star was not known to host planets before this detection (Giguere et al. 2015). The planet is one of four gas giants reported together, with minimum masses from 0.4 to 2.1 Jupiter masses (Giguere et al. 2015). Its 1032-day orbit lies beyond the ice line, where gas giants are thought to form (Giguere et al. 2015).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd10442b-Giguereetal2015","cite":"Giguere et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Matthew J. Giguere, Debra A. Fischer, Matthew J. Payne et al.","title":"Newly Discovered Planets Orbiting HD 5319, HD 11506, HD 75784 and HD 10442 from the N2K Consortium","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5374","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 29442 c":{"text":{"why":"The star was followed up to check a sub-Neptune candidate already known around it, and that work revealed two more close-in planets (Damasso et al. 2023).","observed":"Planet c’s transits are very faint in TESS data, with low signal-to-noise, so the planet was confirmed by combining the transit light curve with precise radial velocities from the ESPRESSO spectrograph (Damasso et al. 2023). CHEOPS, a space telescope built for measuring planet sizes, watched the star for 9.6 days in total (Egger et al. 2024). Joint modelling with TESS data gave planet c a radius of 1.551 ± 0.045 Earth radii (Egger et al. 2024).","system":"HIP 29442 is an evolved K0V star with three close-in planets: two super-Earths and one sub-Neptune, with periods of 13.6, 3.5 and 6.4 days for planets b, c and d (Egger et al. 2024). Planet c is the innermost. Their sizes straddle the radius valley, the gap between small rocky planets and larger gas-rich ones, so the system is a good test of explanations for it (Egger et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"CHEOPS gave planet d a radius that differs from the earlier TESS-only value by more than 3 sigma, so sizes of small planets from TESS alone call for caution (Egger et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip29442c-Damassoetal2023","cite":"Damasso et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"M. Damasso, J. Rodrigues, A. Castro-González et al.","title":"A compact multi-planet system transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) discovered by TESS and ESPRESSO. Radial velocities lead to the detection of transits with low signal-to-noise ratio","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.13310","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hip29442c-Eggeretal2024","cite":"Egger et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. A. Egger, H. P. Osborn, D. Kubyshkina et al.","title":"Unveiling the internal structure and formation history of the three planets transiting HIP 29442 (TOI-469) with CHEOPS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18653","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":3,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5728 b":{"text":{"why":"Earth-sized planets around M dwarfs, small cool red stars, are among the best targets for studying the atmospheres and make-up of rocky worlds with current telescopes (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026).","observed":"The planet was confirmed statistically rather than by weighing it. Its TESS transits were checked against false alarms with multicolour observations from Palomar and Las Cumbres Observatory, high-resolution imaging and stellar spectroscopy (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). Its radius is 1.31 ± 0.05 Earth radii, which makes it a super-Earth (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). Its equilibrium temperature is at most 400 K, cool for a planet on such a short orbit (Gomez Barrientos et al. 2026). A machine-learning search of TESS candidates flagged the signal, labelled TOI-5728.01, as one of the most Earth-like unconfirmed candidates (Islam 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5728b-Barrientosetal2026","cite":"Barrientos et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Jonathan Gomez Barrientos, Heather A. Knutson, Morgan Saidel et al.","title":"From Earths to Super-Earths: Five New Small Planets Transiting M Dwarf Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.11971","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5728b-Islam2026","cite":"Islam 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Md. Rashadul Islam","title":"ExoNet: Calibrated Multimodal Deep Learning for TESS Exoplanet Candidate Vetting using Phase-Folded Light Curves, Stellar Parameters, and Multi-Head Attention","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.15560","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-122 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TOI-122 b receives 8.8 ± 1.0 times the sunlight that Earth does, a little more than the 6.7 times that Mercury gets from the Sun (Waalkes et al. 2021). That makes it one of the cooler planets TESS has found, even on a 5.08-day orbit (Waalkes et al. 2021). Its radius is 2.72 ± 0.18 Earth radii (Waalkes et al. 2021). Its star is an inactive M dwarf (Waalkes et al. 2021).","system":"A second warm planet, TOI-237 b, was reported alongside it, orbiting a different inactive M dwarf (Waalkes et al. 2021). Together the two planets span the small-planet radius valley, so they are useful for studying how volatile materials, those that evaporate easily, evolve around M dwarfs (Waalkes et al. 2021).","history":"","open":"At about 62 pc the planet is near enough for mass measurements and atmosphere studies, but these may need a large investment of time on big telescopes (Waalkes et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi122b-Waalkesetal2021","cite":"Waalkes et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"William C. Waalkes, Zachory K. Berta-Thompson, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"TOI 122b and TOI 237b: Two Small Warm Planets Orbiting Inactive M Dwarfs Found by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15905","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-29 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet’s transit is deep, 20 mmag, and its star is fairly bright, so it is a good target for transmission spectroscopy, which reads starlight filtered through a planet’s atmosphere, from space and from the ground, especially in the near-infrared where both stars are bright (Santerne et al. 2016).","observed":"The Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, a distortion of the starlight as the planet crosses the spinning star, shows that the orbit is aligned with the star’s spin (Santerne et al. 2016). The planet is inflated, with a radius of 1.19 ± 0.02 Jupiter radii for a mass of 0.73 ± 0.04 Jupiter masses (Santerne et al. 2016). An independent confirmation using radial velocities from three telescopes found a slightly eccentric orbit, with eccentricity 0.084 (Johnson et al. 2016).","system":"The host is a relatively bright G7 dwarf with a nearby K5V companion star, forming a young visual binary (Santerne et al. 2016). Stellar rotation and lithium abundance suggest the system might be as young as about 450 million years (Santerne et al. 2016). The host star’s spots make its brightness vary with a rotation period of 10.777 days (Johnson et al. 2016).","history":"","open":"Studies differ on the planet’s size and mass: 1.19 Jupiter radii and 0.73 Jupiter masses in one, 1.000 Jupiter radii and 0.613 Jupiter masses in another (Santerne et al. 2016; Johnson et al. 2016)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k229b-Santerneetal2016","cite":"Santerne et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"A. Santerne, G. Hébrard, J. Lillo-Box et al.","title":"K2-29 b/WASP-152 b: An Aligned and Inflated Hot Jupiter in a Young Visual Binary","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.07680","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k229b-Johnsonetal2016","cite":"Johnson et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Marshall C. Johnson, Davide Gandolfi, Malcolm Fridlund et al.","title":"Two Hot Jupiters from K2 Campaign 4","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.07844","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-12 b":{"text":{"why":"The star’s chromospheric emission, a glow from its outer atmosphere, is anomalously low, making it an extreme outlier among thousands of stars (Haswell 2018). A diffuse gas shroud around the whole system, probably fed by mass lost from the planet, may be absorbing it (Haswell 2018).","observed":"When found, WASP-12 b had the largest radius of any known transiting planet, 1.79 Jupiter radii, and was the most heavily irradiated planet and the one with the shortest period (Hebb et al. 2009). Its equilibrium temperature is 2516 K (Hebb et al. 2009). A Hubble transit spectrum showed a broad absorption feature at 1.4 micrometres, most likely water, but weaker than earlier Spitzer data suggested, possibly because of hazes or an unusual composition (Mandell et al. 2013). The planet is wrapped in a translucent gas envelope that overfills its Roche lobe, the region it gravitationally controls, and absorbs near-ultraviolet light (Haswell 2018). The planet is losing mass (Haswell 2018).","system":"WASP-12 is a hierarchical quadruple system: two distant companions, both consistent with M dwarfs, orbit each other and the planet’s host star (Bechter et al. 2014). The two are 84.3 ± 0.6 milliarcseconds apart, about 21 AU (Bechter et al. 2014).","history":"The orbit fits a past in which gravitational interactions within the system made it highly eccentric, after which tides circularised it (Haswell 2018).","open":"Transit timing shows an apparent shortening of the orbital period, from either orbital decay or apsidal precession, a slow turning of the orbit (Maciejewski 2018). An undetected 17 Earth-mass planet on a 3.2-day orbit could mimic it (Maciejewski 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp12b-Haswell2018","cite":"Haswell 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Carole A. Haswell","title":"WASP-12b: A Mass-Losing Extremely Hot Jupiter","journal":"Handbook of Exoplanets","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.00693","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp12b-Hebbetal2009","cite":"Hebb et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"L. Hebb, A. Collier-Cameron, B. Loeillet et al.","title":"WASP-12b: The Hottest Transiting Extrasolar Planet Yet Discovered","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.3240","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp12b-Mandelletal2013","cite":"Mandell et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Avi M. Mandell, Korey Haynes, Evan Sinukoff et al.","title":"Exoplanet Transit Spectroscopy Using WFC3: WASP-12 b, WASP-17 b, and WASP-19 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2949","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp12b-Bechteretal2014","cite":"Bechter et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Eric B. Bechter, Justin R. Crepp, Henry Ngo et al.","title":"WASP-12b and HAT-P-8b are Members of Triple Star Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6857","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp12b-Maciejewski2018","cite":"Maciejewski 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Gracjan Maciejewski","title":"WASP-12 b - an exoplanet falling onto its host star?","journal":"XXXVIII Polish Astronomical Society Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":66,"about":10,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2322 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a K4 star with strong activity signals, and its 20.2255-day period is very close to the star’s rotation period of 21.28 ± 0.08 days (Hobson et al. 2025). Starspots can produce radial velocity signals that repeat at the rotation period and mimic planets. Separating the two needed a multivariate Gaussian process, a statistical model of correlated noise, fitted to ESPRESSO and HARPS data together with TESS photometry (Hobson et al. 2025). Planet c has a mass of 18.10 Earth masses, with an uncertainty of about 5, and a radius of 1.874 Earth radii (Hobson et al. 2025).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-2322 b, orbits every 11.307 days, close to half the stellar rotation period, with a radius of 0.994 Earth radii and a mass of at most 2.03 Earth masses (Hobson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2322c-Hobsonetal2025","cite":"Hobson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"M. J. Hobson, A. Suárez Mascareño, C. Lovis et al.","title":"TOI-2322: Two transiting rocky planets close to the stellar rotation period and its first harmonic","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18094","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"2MASS J01033563-5515561 AB b":{"text":{"why":"Accretion implies a gas disc in the system, likely with a circumplanetary disc around the planet (Ringqvist et al. 2023). Gas discs normally disperse within 5–10 million years, so accretion at an age of about 30–45 million years is surprising (Betti et al. 2022).","observed":"Delorme 1 (AB)b is still gaining gas (Eriksson et al. 2020). Spectra show very strong Hα and Hβ emission plus helium emission, signs of active accretion, and give a likely mass of 12–15 Jupiter masses (Eriksson et al. 2020). Near-ultraviolet hydrogen lines were resolved for the first time in a super-Jovian protoplanet, and the analysis strongly favours a planetary accretion shock, where infalling gas heats on impact (Ringqvist et al. 2023). Near-infrared hydrogen lines from the planet were also detected, while the host binary shows none (Betti et al. 2022). Line ratios fit a planetary accretion shock better than the models used for accreting stars (Betti et al. 2022).","system":"The planet orbits a pair of M5 low-mass stars, making it circumbinary (Ringqvist et al. 2023). It lies about 2 arcseconds from the binary (Perrin et al. 2022).","history":"Strong accretion might point to a younger age than the 30–40 million years expected from likely membership of the Tucana-Horologium group, and the central binary is overluminous, which also hints at youth (Eriksson et al. 2020).","open":"Whether the system is younger than 30–40 million years is not settled (Eriksson et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-2massj010335635515561abb-Ringqvistetal2023","cite":"Ringqvist et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Simon C. Ringqvist, Gayathri Viswanath, Yuhiko Aoyama et al.","title":"Resolved near-UV hydrogen emission lines at 40-Myr super-Jovian protoplanet Delorme 1 (AB)b. Indications of magnetospheric accretion","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03207","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-2massj010335635515561abb-Bettietal2022","cite":"Betti et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. K. Betti, K. B. Follette, K. Ward-Duong et al.","title":"Near-infrared Accretion Signatures from the Circumbinary Planetary-mass Companion Delorme 1 (AB)b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.05016","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-2massj010335635515561abb-Erikssonetal2020","cite":"Eriksson et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Simon C. Eriksson, Rubén Asensio Torres, Markus Janson et al.","title":"Strong Hα emission and signs of accretion in a circumbinary planetary mass companion from MUSE","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11725","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-2massj010335635515561abb-Perrinetal2022","cite":"Perrin et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Marshall Perrin, William Balmer, Christine Chen et al.","title":"Imaging Spectroscopy of the Coldest Imaged Exoplanet and a Low-Mass Accreting Protoplanet","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 2","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-289 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Spitzer measured the planet’s transit at 4.5 micrometres in the infrared, and the data were analysed together with the K2 light curves (Livingston et al. 2019). Seeing the transit at more than one wavelength helped validate the candidate EPIC 205686202.01 as a real planet, K2-289 b, which had been unvalidated before (Livingston et al. 2019). The joint fit also improved the planet’s radius (Livingston et al. 2019). For the whole set of eight candidates, Spitzer timing cut the typical uncertainty on transit times in mid-2021 from several hours to about a dozen minutes (Livingston et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The planet is a promising target for radial velocity follow-up to measure its mass (Livingston et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2289b-Livingstonetal2019","cite":"Livingston et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"John H. Livingston, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Michael W. Werner et al.","title":"Spitzer  Transit Follow-up of Planet Candidates from the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.05855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 28471 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet d is one of three short-period planets found in about 19 years of HARPS radial velocity data, searched with KIMA, an algorithm that treats the number of planets as a free parameter (Stevenson et al. 2025). Planet d has a minimum mass of 4.9 Earth masses and an 11.68-day orbit (Stevenson et al. 2025). Checks of the star’s activity indicators suggest all three short-period signals are genuine planets (Stevenson et al. 2025). Masses are minimums because the tilt of the orbits is not known.","system":"Three planets with periods of about 3.16, 6.12 and 11.68 days, and minimum masses of 3.7, 5.7 and 4.9 Earth masses, form a compact, near-resonant system in the super-Earth and sub-Neptune range (Stevenson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"A fourth signal at about 1500 days could be a cold giant planet, but may instead come from a short stellar magnetic cycle; more data are needed (Stevenson et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd28471d-Stevensonetal2025","cite":"Stevenson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. T. Stevenson, C. A. Haswell, J. R. Barnes et al.","title":"HD 28471: a near-resonant compact multiplanet system with a possible cold giant planet","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18000","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"CoRoT-7 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns the innermost planet, CoRoT-7 b. CoRoT-7 b was the first-discovered rocky exoplanet, about 70% larger than Earth, orbiting a Sun-like star (Barnes et al. 2010). Its 0.8535-day orbit was picked out of radial velocity data against the star’s 23-day rotation signal (Queloz et al. 2009). Stellar activity contaminates such velocities and can hide small planets, so tools like SCALPELS were built to separate planet-induced Doppler shifts from changes in the shape of spectral lines (John et al. 2022). Little else is known about planet d itself.","system":"Tides could heat CoRoT-7 b enough to drive Io-like volcanism, but only if perturbations from CoRoT-7 c keep its orbit slightly eccentric (Barnes et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-corot7d-Barnesetal2010","cite":"Barnes et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Rory Barnes, S. N. Raymond, R. Greenberg et al.","title":"CoRoT-7 b: Super-Earth or Super-Io?","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #215","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7d-Quelozetal2009","cite":"Queloz et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Queloz, F. Bouchy, C. Moutou et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: CoRoT-7 radial velocities (Queloz+, 2009)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35060303","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-corot7d-Johnetal2022","cite":"John et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Ancy Anna John, Andrew Collier Cameron, Thomas Wilson","title":"Mitigating effects of stellar activity in RVs using SCALPELS","journal":"44th COSPAR Scientific Assembly. Held 16-24 July","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-44 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Planet c is a candidate outer companion rather than a planet seen directly (Hartman et al. 2014). The star’s radial velocities show leftover variations once the transiting planet’s pull is removed, and the best-fitting two-planet model puts planet c on an 872-day orbit with eccentricity 0.494 ± 0.081 and a minimum mass of 4.0 Jupiter masses (Hartman et al. 2014). Aliasing, false periods created by gaps in the observations, leaves 220 or 438 days possible instead (Hartman et al. 2014).","system":"The inner planet, HAT-P-44 b, transits every 4.3012 days, with a mass of 0.35 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.24 Jupiter radii (Hartman et al. 2014).","history":"","open":"Whether the extra signal is a planet, and which period is right, is not known (Hartman et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp44c-Hartmanetal2014","cite":"Hartman et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, G. Torres et al.","title":"HAT-P-44b, HAT-P-45b, and HAT-P-46b: Three Transiting Hot Jupiters in Possible Multi-planet Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2937","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"V1298 Tau d":{"text":{"why":"Because the system is young, its four planets, with sizes from 0.5 to 0.9 Jupiter radii, offer a rare chance to compare how radii evolve at different distances from the same star (Feinstein et al. 2022).","observed":"Planet d belongs to a system only 20–30 million years old (Feinstein et al. 2022). TESS re-observed the star six years after K2, which refreshed the planets’ orbit timings and sizes (Feinstein et al. 2022). Transits of planets b, c and d are shallower in TESS’s redder bandpass, at the 1–2 sigma level, possibly because of starspot crossings or contamination from faint nearby stars (Feinstein et al. 2022). Hubble and NICER observed the star’s ultraviolet and X-ray output between 2020 and early 2022 (Duvvuri et al. 2024).","system":"V1298 Tau is a solar-mass K star in the Taurus region with four transiting planets: two Neptune-sized, one Saturn-sized and one Jupiter-sized (Suárez Mascareño 2022). Planet e’s period is most probably near a 2:1 resonance with planet b (Feinstein et al. 2022).","history":"Very young giant planets are expected to start large and low in density, and to contract over several hundred million years (Suárez Mascareño 2022).","open":"The star’s extreme activity makes planet masses hard to measure; the signal of planet b was measured, and a second signal may be planet e (Suárez Mascareño 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-v1298taud-Feinsteinetal2022","cite":"Feinstein et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Adina D. Feinstein, Trevor J. David, Benjamin T. Montet et al.","title":"V1298 Tau with TESS: Updated Ephemerides, Radii, and Period Constraints from a Second Transit of V1298 Tau e","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.08660","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taud-Duvvurietal2024","cite":"Duvvuri et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. M. Duvvuri, P. W. Cauley, F. Cruz Aguirre et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: V1298 Tau high energy spectrum (Duvvuri+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-v1298taud-Mascareo2022","cite":"Mascareño 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Alejandro Suárez Mascareño","title":"V1298 Tau - Rapid contraction of giant planets?","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":4,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-73 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"WASP-73 b is more massive than Jupiter, at 1.88 Jupiter masses, and slightly larger, at 1.16 Jupiter radii (Delrez et al. 2014). Despite its high irradiation, its mean density is high, 1.20 times Jupiter’s, which suggests the planet is enriched in heavy elements (Delrez et al. 2014). Follow-up used the TRAPPIST and Euler-Swiss telescopes for transits and the CORALIE spectrograph for radial velocities (Delrez et al. 2014).","system":"The host is an evolved F9 star of 1.34 solar masses and 2.07 solar radii (Delrez et al. 2014). Two other hot Jupiters, WASP-68 b and WASP-88 b, were reported alongside it. All three orbit evolved late-F or early-G stars every 4–5 days, yet have very different bulk properties (Delrez 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp73b-Delrezetal2014","cite":"Delrez et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"L. Delrez, V. Van Grootel, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"Transiting planets from WASP-South, Euler, and TRAPPIST. WASP-68 b, WASP-73 b, and WASP-88 b, three hot Jupiters transiting evolved solar-type stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1827","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp73b-Delrez2016","cite":"Delrez 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Laetitia Delrez","title":"Detection and Characterization of Transiting Hot Jupiters","journal":"Ph.D. Thesis","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 8152 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was one of eight newly confirmed sub-Neptunes from the TESS-Keck Survey that are particularly suited to atmospheric follow-up with JWST (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). Radial velocities from Keck-HIRES and the Automated Planet Finder gave a mass of 8.9 ± 1.7 Earth masses, measured to better than 5-sigma precision (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). The planet is about 2.7 Earth radii in size and orbits a bright star, V = 10.1 mag (Akana Murphy et al. 2023). It is also known as TOI-266 b (Akana Murphy et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip8152b-Murphyetal2023","cite":"Murphy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Joseph M. Akana Murphy, Natalie Batalha, TESS-Keck Survey","title":"The Masses and Radii of Eight Sub-Neptunes Amenable to Atmospheric Characterization","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 3154 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star LHS 3154 is a low-mass M dwarf observed with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder, a near-infrared spectrograph on the 10-m Hobby-Eberly Telescope in Texas (Stefansson et al. 2024). It was part of a survey designed to search for planets around very low-mass dwarfs (Stefansson et al. 2024). In all, 137 spectra were taken between January 2020 and April 2022, from which the radial velocities were measured (Stefansson et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs3154b-Stefanssonetal2024","cite":"Stefansson et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"G. Stefansson, S. Mahadevan, Y. Miguel et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: LHS 3154 RV and activity indicators (Stefansson+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog (other)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-36 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a campaign that monitored 25 HATSouth candidates with the Kepler spacecraft during K2 Campaign 7, combining ground-based imaging and spectroscopy with higher-precision space photometry (Bayliss et al. 2018).","observed":"HATS-36 b is an eccentric hot Jupiter, with eccentricity 0.105 ± 0.028, a mass of 3.216 ± 0.062 Jupiter masses and a radius of 1.235 ± 0.043 Jupiter radii (Bayliss et al. 2018). It transits a solar-type G0V star of magnitude 14.386 (Bayliss et al. 2018). It is also known as EPIC 215969174 b and K2-145 b (Bayliss et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats36b-Baylissetal2018","cite":"Bayliss et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman, G. Zhou et al.","title":"HATS-36b and 24 Other Transiting/Eclipsing Systems from the HATSouth-K2 Campaign 7 Program","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03858","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Gl 378 b":{"text":{"why":"As one of only a few such planets around M dwarfs (small, cool red stars), it gives clues to how close-in planets of this kind evolve (Hobson et al. 2019).","observed":"Gl 378 was watched with the SOPHIE spectrograph as part of a sub-programme dedicated to finding planets around M dwarfs (Hobson et al. 2019). The star is an M1 dwarf with a metal content like the Sun's (Hobson et al. 2019). Only one planet was detected. Its minimum mass of 13.02 Earth masses and 3.82-day orbit place it at the lower boundary of the hot Neptune desert, a range of mass and orbit where few Neptune-sized planets are found close to their stars (Hobson et al. 2019).","system":"Only one planet was detected around the star (Hobson et al. 2019).","history":"The orbit is slightly stretched, with an eccentricity of 0.1, which may point to high-eccentricity migration, where a planet is flung onto an elongated orbit and later settles close to its star (Hobson et al. 2019).","open":"The planet may have lost part of its gas envelope because of the radiation from its star (Hobson et al. 2019). Whether it did is not known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gl378b-Hobsonetal2019","cite":"Hobson et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"M. J. Hobson, X. Delfosse, N. Astudillo-Defru et al.","title":"The SOPHIE search for northern extrasolar planets. XV. A warm Neptune around the M dwarf Gl 378","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.05998","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6448 b":{"text":{"why":"Young, close-in planets are needed to understand how the mature transiting planets seen by Kepler came to be (Barber et al. 2026).","observed":"The star was observed by the TESS satellite in four separate stretches between 2020 and 2025, and transits were also watched from the ground with the Las Cumbres telescope network and TRAPPIST-South (Barber et al. 2026). The planet has been validated, meaning shown to be a real planet, with a radius of 8.8 +/- 0.8 Earth radii (Barber et al. 2026). The host star belongs to a young group called the Vela Association, and the star, and so the planet, is 34 +/- 3 million years old (Barber et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Young planets tend to land in a region of size and orbit with few older planets, and the planet fits earlier findings of an excess of planets 5 to 11 Earth radii across around young stars (Barber et al. 2026).","open":"Searches of Vela, Taurus-Auriga, Sco-Cen and Orion are expected to reveal dozens more transiting planets younger than 50 million years (Barber et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6448b-Barberetal2026","cite":"Barber et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"M. G. Barber, A. W. Mann, A. Vanderburg et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TI-DYE. IV. TOI-6448 comoving star candidates (Barber+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 176986 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner planets HD 176986 b and c. They are super-Earths with masses of 5.74 and 9.18 Earth masses, on orbits of 6.49 and 16.82 days that are consistent with circular (Nari et al. 2026). The star was followed with the HARPS and HARPS-N spectrographs as part of the RoPES programme, which searches for short-period rocky planets around Sun-like stars (Nari et al. 2026). Little is known about the planet HD 176986 d itself.","system":"The host is a K2.5 dwarf with modest magnetic activity but a complex activity pattern, and its magnetic cycle and rotation have been studied (Nari et al. 2026). The star has been monitored since mid-2004 (Suarez Mascareno et al. 2017).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd176986d-Narietal2026","cite":"Nari et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"N. Nari, A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS Rvs and activity indicators (Nari+, 2026)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd176986d-Mascarenoetal2017","cite":"Mascareno et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"A. Suarez Mascareno, J. I. Gonzalez Hernandez, R. Rebolo et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 176986 HARPS + HARPS-N data (Suarez Mascareno+, 2018)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36120041","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-89 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet has about 6 Jupiter masses and an orbit with an eccentricity of 0.2 (Hellier et al. 2015). Planets like it, such as XO-3b and HAT-P-2b, orbit F stars, whereas WASP-89 is a K star (Hellier et al. 2015). Star spots, darker patches on the star's surface, are visible in the transits as the planet crosses them (Hellier et al. 2015). There are indications that the planet's orbit is aligned with the star's spin (Hellier et al. 2015).","system":"The host star is magnetically active and spins once in 20.2 days (Hellier et al. 2015).","history":"","open":"Star spots make WASP-89 a good target for an extensive study of spot crossings during transits (Hellier et al. 2015). A theoretical study of ten systems including this one found that points of balance along the orbit are unstable, and could hold minor planets, asteroids or debris (Hasan & Shah 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp89b-Hellieretal2015","cite":"Hellier et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"Three WASP-South Transiting Exoplanets: WASP-74b, WASP-83b, and WASP-89b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.6358","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp89b-HasanShah2024","cite":"Hasan & Shah 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Syed Najamul Hasan, Priya Shah","title":"Exploring the collinear Lagrangian points of exoplanet systems with P-R drag and oblateness.","journal":"32nd General Assembly International Union (IAUGA 2024)","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4552 b":{"text":{"why":"Ultra-short-period planets, which orbit in less than a day, are rare around M dwarfs, and only ten have both mass and radius measured (Srivastava et al. 2026).","observed":"Ground-based transit observations from LCO, ExTrA and SPECULOOS checked the planet's size, and speckle imaging with Zorro at Gemini-South ruled out eclipsing binary stars as a false alarm (Srivastava et al. 2026). The NIRPS and HARPS spectrographs then measured its mass as 1.83 +/- 0.47 Earth masses, with a radius of 1.11 +/- 0.04 Earth radii (Srivastava et al. 2026).","system":"The host is a nearby M4.5 dwarf that is quiet: it shows no short-term changes linked to its spin in light or in velocity, and long-term data also suggest no activity (Srivastava et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4552b-Srivastavaetal2026","cite":"Srivastava et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Avidaan Srivastava, René Doyon, François Bouchy et al.","title":"TOI-4552 b: A new ultra-short-period rocky world revealed by NIRPS and TESS","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18233","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":2,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Qatar-6 b":{"text":{"why":"Few exoplanets are known in a grazing transit, so the planet is a good target for looking for other bodies in the system (Alsubai et al. 2018).","observed":"Light curves taken in several colours show that the transit is grazing: the planet only clips the edge of the star's disc as it passes (Alsubai et al. 2018). Grazing transits make small changes in transit depth and duration easy to notice, which could reveal additional bodies in the system (Alsubai et al. 2018). A search of ten hot Jupiter systems including this one, using TESS and new ground-based light curves, found no close-in low-mass companions (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"Hot Jupiters are often alone, which supports formation of such systems through high-eccentricity migration (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-qatar6b-Alsubaietal2018","cite":"Alsubai et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Khalid Alsubai, Zlatan I. Tsvetanov, David W. Latham et al.","title":"Qatar Exoplanet Survey: Qatar-6b—A Grazing Transiting Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.03216","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-qatar6b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, M. Fernández, A. Sota et al.","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. III. A Study of Ten Systems Enhanced with New Ground-Based Photometry","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00538","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":2,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3593 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of 30 giant planets announced together by the TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey (Yee et al. 2025). The 30 planets have orbits of 1.6 to 8.2 days and radii of 0.9 to 1.7 Jupiter radii, and their masses span more than a factor of ten (Yee et al. 2025). The orbit of TOI-3593 b is measurably non-circular, with an eccentricity of about 0.11 (Yee et al. 2025). Only two planets in the group showed such a significant eccentricity, the other being TOI-4961 b (Yee et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3593b-Yeeetal2025","cite":"Yee et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Joshua N. Winn, Joel D. Hartman et al.","title":"The TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Survey. III. Thirty More Giant Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Supplement Series","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01855","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2021-BLG-2478L b":{"text":{"why":"The by-eye sample is an important check of how complete the semi-automated detections are, which are the basis for statistics on microlensing planets (Ryu et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet is one of four reported together from the 2021 data of the Korea Microlensing Telescope Network, whose planet-to-host mass ratios span a factor of about ten (Ryu et al. 2023). The programme publishes every planet and possible planet found by eye in the network's online data (Ryu et al. 2023). Together with earlier ones, the secure by-eye planets from 2021 now number 16 (Ryu et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2021blg2478lb-Ryuetal2023","cite":"Ryu et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Yoon-Hyun Ryu, In-Gu Shin, Hongjing Yang et al.","title":"Mass Production of 2021 KMTNet Microlensing Planets II","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07295","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"DMPP-3 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits the K0 star DMPP-3A, which has a close and unusual companion of very low mass at the hydrogen-burning limit, the boundary between star and brown dwarf (Stevenson et al. 2023). Further HARPS velocities confirmed the planet's orbit and the binary orbit, and Gaia astrometry independently confirmed the binary (Stevenson et al. 2023).","system":"The two stars orbit each other every 507 days on a highly eccentric orbit (e about 0.6), with a semi-major axis of 1.2 au (Stevenson et al. 2023). The binary orbit is inclined by 63.89 +/- 0.78 degrees (Stevenson et al. 2023).","history":"","open":"A slow signal of about 800 days, once taken as a possible outer planet, is probably caused by stellar activity (Stevenson et al. 2023). Simulations show it cannot be an orbiting body (Stevenson et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-dmpp3ab-Stevensonetal2023","cite":"Stevenson et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Adam T. Stevenson, Carole A. Haswell, John R. Barnes et al.","title":"DMPP-3: confirmation of short-period S-type planet(s) in a compact eccentric binary star system, and warnings about long-period RV planet detections","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06263","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 50554 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its outer giant planet HD 50554 b. It orbits an F8 star with a period of 1254 +/- 34 days, an eccentricity of 0.51 +/- 0.06 and a minimum mass of 3.7 Jupiter masses at 2.2 au (Fischer et al. 2002). Little is known about HD 50554 d itself.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd50554d-Fischeretal2002","cite":"Fischer et al. 2002","year":"2002","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to HD 136118, HD 50554, and HD 106252","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/341677","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 68988 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its star. In 2007, brightness measurements in blue, visual and red light from the Crimean Astrophysical Observatory looked for variability in the star linked to its close giant planet (Golovin et al. 2007). The idea was that tides raised by a giant planet on a short orbit could drive changes in the strongest calcium lines of the star's outer atmosphere (Golovin et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd68988c-Golovinetal2007","cite":"Golovin et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Alex V. Golovin, Viktoriia N. Krushevska, Yuliana G. Kuznyetsova","title":"Photometric Detection of Chromospheric Activity of Extrasolar Planets with Short Orbital Periods: HD 187123, HD 68988, HD168746","journal":"JENAM-2007, \"Our Non-Stable Universe\"","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-37 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-37 is known to host three transiting planets, and Kepler-37 d produces a very weak wobble in its star (Rajpaul et al. 2021). Two statistical methods were used to separate that signal from magnetic activity on the star, such as spots, and together they allowed a definitive detection with the HARPS-N spectrograph (Rajpaul et al. 2021). The wobble has a semi-amplitude of 1.22 +/- 0.31 metres per second, giving a mass of 5.4 +/- 1.4 Earth masses (Rajpaul et al. 2021). Only 18 exoplanets with a signal below 2 metres per second had their masses measured directly when that work was done (Rajpaul et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler37d-Rajpauletal2021","cite":"Rajpaul et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"V. M. Rajpaul, L. A. Buchhave, G. Lacedelli et al.","title":"A HARPS-N mass for the elusive Kepler-37d: a case study in disentangling stellar activity and planetary signals","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13900","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-7169 b":{"text":{"why":"Most known planets orbit metal-rich stars, so it is unclear whether planet formation has a lower metallicity limit (Simon et al. 2026).","observed":"The host star is metal-poor, with an iron abundance of -0.72 +/- 0.05 on a logarithmic scale, so well below the Sun's (Simon et al. 2026). Photometry from TESS and ground-based imaging gave a radius of 1.475 +/- 0.029 Jupiter radii (Simon et al. 2026). Spectra from TRES gave a mass of 0.41 +/- 0.14 Jupiter masses (Simon et al. 2026). The planet is therefore inflated, meaning puffed up and of low density (Simon et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":"How planets born in low-metallicity places differ from metal-rich ones is still not well known (Simon et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi7169b-Simonetal2026","cite":"Simon et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Joshua D. Simon, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Jhon Yana Galarza et al.","title":"TOI-7169 b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a Metal-poor Star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25787","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 97166 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was picked out in a systematic search of TESS candidates for eccentric orbits, based on a mismatch between the observed transit duration and the duration expected for a circular orbit (MacDougall et al. 2021). A joint analysis of radial velocities and photometry showed that the short transit came mostly from a high impact parameter of 0.84 +/- 0.03, meaning the planet crosses the star off-centre, with only a moderate eccentricity of 0.16 +/- 0.03 (MacDougall et al. 2021). That is above average for sub-Neptunes with well-measured eccentricities (MacDougall et al. 2021).","system":"A second planet that does not transit, HIP 97166 c, has a minimum mass of 10 +/- 2 Earth masses and a 16.8-day orbit (MacDougall et al. 2021). Requiring the system to stay stable for more than 100,000 orbits limits its eccentricity to below 0.25 (MacDougall et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip97166b-MacDougalletal2021","cite":"MacDougall et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Mason G. MacDougall, Erik A. Petigura, Isabel Angelo et al.","title":"The TESS-Keck Survey. VI. Two Eccentric Sub-Neptunes Orbiting HIP-97166","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.05628","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-40 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was confirmed with radial-velocity measurements from the SOPHIE spectrograph, aided by public Kepler photometry (Santerne et al. 2011). The host star is an F5 subgiant, a star that has started to swell as it ages, with a radius of 2.13 +/- 0.06 times the Sun's (Santerne et al. 2011). At the time, it was the largest and most evolved star found with a transiting planet (Santerne et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler40b-Santerneetal2011","cite":"Santerne et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"A. Santerne, R. F. Díaz, F. Bouchy et al.","title":"SOPHIE velocimetry of Kepler transit candidates. II. KOI-428b: a hot Jupiter transiting a subgiant F-star","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0196","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-41 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour WASP-41 b, a hot Jupiter that crosses the face of the star every 3.05 days (Maxted et al. 2011). For WASP-41 c itself, the work behind its discovery gathered radial-velocity measurements of the star with the Euler/CORALIE and ESO/HARPS spectrographs (Neveu-Vanmalle et al. 2015). Radial velocity means measuring the tiny back-and-forth motion of the star caused by an unseen companion.","system":"The host star is a moderately bright G8 dwarf with a metal content close to the Sun's and signs of moderate magnetic activity (Maxted et al. 2011). Starspots on it are crossed by planet b during transits, which gave a spin period for the star of 18.6 days (Southworth et al. 2016). The orbit of planet b is well aligned with the star's spin (Southworth et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp41c-Maxtedetal2011","cite":"Maxted et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"P. F. L. Maxted, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-41b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter Planet Orbiting a Magnetically Active G8V Star","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.2977","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp41c-NeveuVanmalleetal2015","cite":"Neveu-Vanmalle et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Neveu-Vanmalle, D. Queloz, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: WASP41 and WASP47 photometric and RV data (Neveu-VanMalle+, 2016)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.35860093","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp41c-Southworthetal2016","cite":"Southworth et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"John Southworth, J. Tregloan-Reed, M. I. Andersen et al.","title":"High-precision photometry by telescope defocussing - VIII. WASP-22, WASP-41, WASP-42 and WASP-55","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05549","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 92788 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's orbit is noticeably stretched rather than circular, with an eccentricity of 0.30 ± 0.06 (Fischer et al. 2001). Its distance from the star therefore changes substantially over each 326.7-day orbit. The star shows no sign of magnetic activity in its calcium emission lines, so the Doppler signal is not a false alarm from starspots (Fischer et al. 2001). The announcement covered this planet together with companions around two other stars, HD 12661 and HD 38529.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd92788b-Fischeretal2001","cite":"Fischer et al. 2001","year":"2001","authors":"Debra A. Fischer, Geoffrey W. Marcy, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to HD 12661, HD 92788, and HD 38529 and Variations in Keplerian Residuals of Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/320224","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-77 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-77 b was one of 20 planets checked for signs of gas escaping from their atmospheres, using helium and hydrogen-alpha light seen with the CARMENES and GIARPS spectrographs (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). New helium detections were reported for TOI-1268 b and TOI-2018 b, and a hydrogen detection for TOI-1136 d (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024). No detection was reported for K2-77 b. The survey, called MOPYS, aims to test whether young planets lose their atmospheres faster, and found no evidence that they do (Orell-Miquel et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k277b-OrellMiqueletal2024","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, F. Murgas, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The MOPYS project: A survey of 70 planets in search of extended He I and H atmospheres: No evidence of enhanced evaporation in young planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16732","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-47 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-47 b circles two stars at once. Its transits were seen 18 times, which allowed a detailed measurement of its orbit and those of the stars (Orosz et al. 2012). The two stars are a Sun-like star and a companion roughly one-third its size, eclipsing each other every 7.45 days (Orosz et al. 2012). Kepler-47 b is the inner planet of the system and has a radius of about 3 Earth radii (Orosz et al. 2019). Its mass is only loosely known, with a 1-sigma upper limit of 26 Earth masses (Orosz et al. 2019).","system":"Kepler-47 is the only known circumbinary system with more than one transiting planet (Orosz et al. 2019). A middle planet of about 7 Earth radii orbits every 187.4 days, and an outer planet of about 4.7 Earth radii orbits every 303.2 days (Orosz et al. 2019). The outer planet lies in the classical habitable zone, though it is not Earth-like (Orosz et al. 2012). All the orbits, including the stars' orbit, lie within about 2 degrees of one another (Welsh et al. 2015).","history":"Low eccentricities and nearly flat orbits argue against violent scattering between planets and suggest gentle migration within the disc of gas and dust around the young stars (Orosz et al. 2019).","open":"Masses are only roughly constrained. The middle and outer planets have very low densities, below 0.68 and 0.26 g/cm3 respectively (Orosz et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler47b-Oroszetal2012","cite":"Orosz et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Joshua A. Carter et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Transiting Circumbinary Multiplanet System","journal":"Science","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5489","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47b-Oroszetal2019","cite":"Orosz et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh, Nader Haghighipour et al.","title":"Discovery of a Third Transiting Planet in the Kepler-47 Circumbinary System","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07255","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler47b-Welshetal2015","cite":"Welsh et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"William Welsh, Jerome Orosz, Billy Quarles et al.","title":"Kepler-47: A Three-Planet Circumbinary System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":5,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-654.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Follow-up work measured the planet's mass using the InfraRed Doppler instrument on the Subaru telescope, and refined its size from extra transit observations with TESS and the MuSCAT cameras (Ikuta et al. 2025). The star is a mid-M dwarf, and the planet is a suitable target for atmospheric observations (Ikuta et al. 2025). Its measured density suggests either a rocky and volatile-rich core, or a rocky core wrapped in a small amount of hydrogen and helium gas (Ikuta et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the planet is a volatile-rich world or a rocky core with a thin gas envelope is not yet settled; the density alone cannot tell them apart."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi65401-Ikutaetal2025","cite":"Ikuta et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Kai Ikuta, Norio Narita, Takuya Takarada et al.","title":"The mass of TOI-654 b: A short-period sub-Neptune transiting a mid-M dwarf","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16222","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-141 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-141 b orbits its star in only 6.7 hours, which made it the shortest-period planet with a precisely measured mass at the time (Barragán et al. 2018). Its mean density of about 8 g/cm3 suggests a rocky and iron composition, with iron making up no more than 70% of the mass (Barragán et al. 2018). The star is an active K7 dwarf (Barragán et al. 2018). The planet is hot enough to count among the lava worlds, and its light varies over the orbit in TESS data, detected at better than 3 sigma (Coy et al. 2026). Lava worlds in general reflect more light than hot Jupiters at similar temperatures (Coy et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"Lava planets likely did not form in their current orbits. Models of the migration of K2-141 b and six similar planets find that reaching such a tight orbit from about 0.1 au needs starting eccentricities of at least 0.9 (Herath et al. 2026).","open":"Dayside temperatures measured for lava worlds are lower than expected for dark bare rock. Either a thick atmosphere carries heat to the night side or the surface is highly reflective, and the data cannot yet tell which (Coy et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2141b-Barragnetal2018","cite":"Barragán et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"O. Barragán, D. Gandolfi, F. Dai et al.","title":"K2-141 b. A 5-M<SUB>⊕</SUB> super-Earth transiting a K7 V star every 6.7 h","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.02097","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2141b-Coyetal2026","cite":"Coy et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Brandon Park Coy, Michael Zhang, Fei Dai et al.","title":"Revealing the Shiny Nature of Lava Worlds: Small Planet Phase Curves with TESS","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26874","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2141b-Herathetal2026","cite":"Herath et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Mahesh Herath, Nicolas B. Cowan, Charles-Édouard Boukaré et al.","title":"Coupled Orbital and Interior Structure Evolution of Lava Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.18682","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-6 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system concerns its inner neighbour KELT-6 b, a mildly inflated, Saturn-mass planet that crosses its star every 7.8 days (Collins et al. 2014). Radial-velocity data indicated a possible third body on a longer period, and adaptive-optics imaging at the Keck telescope detected no companion, which limits the mass and period of the unseen object (Collins et al. 2013).","system":"The host is a metal-poor, slightly evolved late-F star with an iron content well below the Sun's, [Fe/H] = -0.28 (Collins et al. 2014). Planet b receives almost the same incident flux as the well-studied HD 209458 b, making it a metal-poor twin for comparing atmospheres (Collins et al. 2013).","history":"","open":"The nature and exact orbit of the outer companion are not well known."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt6c-Collinsetal2014","cite":"Collins et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Karen A. Collins, Jason D. Eastman, Thomas G. Beatty et al.","title":"KELT-6b: A P ~ 7.9 Day Hot Saturn Transiting a Metal-poor Star with a Long-period Companion","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2296","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt6c-Collinsetal2013","cite":"Collins et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Karen A. Collins, R. Siverd, T. G. Beatty et al.","title":"KELT-6b: A Transiting Mildly-Inflated Saturn with a Metal-Poor Host","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NY Vir b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Little is known about the planet itself; work so far has studied its host binary star. The system is a close eclipsing pair, a rapidly pulsating hot subdwarf B star with an M5 companion (Vučković et al. 2009). The hot star pulsates with periods of about 3 minutes, and the pulsation pattern has changed substantially over the years (Kilkenny et al. 2003). Measurements put the hot star's mass at 0.466 or 0.389 solar masses, with no unique solution possible (Vučković et al. 2007).","system":"","history":"The hot subdwarf star probably formed when it lost its outer layers in a common-envelope phase, in which the companion spiralled inside the swollen star (Hu et al. 2007).","open":"Whether the planet exists at all is in question."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-nyvirb-Kilkennyetal2003","cite":"Kilkenny et al. 2003","year":"2003","authors":"D. Kilkenny, M. D. Reed, D. O'Donoghue et al.","title":"A Whole Earth Telescope campaign on the pulsating subdwarf B binary system PG 1336-018 (NY Vir)","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-8711.2003.07007.x","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-nyvirb-Huetal2007","cite":"Hu et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"Haili Hu, G. Nelemans, R. Østensen et al.","title":"An evolutionary study of the pulsating subdwarf B eclipsing binary <ASTROBJ>PG 1336-018</ASTROBJ> (NY Virginis)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.0106","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":7,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 876 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck radial-velocity monitoring over eight years found that GJ 876 holds more planets than b and c alone, including a very light third companion of about 7.5 Earth masses (Rivera et al. 2005). Fits that assume all orbits lie in one plane give the best result for a system tilted about 50 degrees from the plane of the sky (Rivera et al. 2005). The orbit of planet b is well enough known that its position on the sky can be predicted for future direct imaging in reflected light with giant telescopes (Pearce et al. 2025).","system":"The host is a nearby red dwarf, about 4.7 parsecs away, rotating once every 96.7 days (Rivera et al. 2005). Planets b and c are Jupiter-mass companions that lie in or near a mean-motion resonance, where their orbital periods form a simple ratio (Dawson 2017).","history":"Resonant orbits like those of GJ 876 b and c were taken as evidence that planets migrate inward through the disc of gas around a young star (Dawson 2017).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj876b-Riveraetal2005","cite":"Rivera et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Eugenio J. Rivera, Jack J. Lissauer, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"A ~7.5 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> Planet Orbiting the Nearby Star, GJ 876","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510508","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876b-Pearceetal2025","cite":"Pearce et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Logan A. Pearce, Jared R. Males, Mary Anne Limbach","title":"Direct Detection of Known Exoplanets in Reflected Light: Predicting Sky Position with Literature Orbit Solutions","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj876b-Dawson2017","cite":"Dawson 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Rebekah Ilene Dawson","title":"Assessing the Hallmarks of Migration and In Situ Formation in Multi-Exoplanet Systems","journal":"NASA XRP proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 238914 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a lithium-rich giant. Such stars were picked out on purpose for the search (Adamów et al. 2018).","observed":"The star belongs to a group of the 15 giant stars richest in lithium in the PennState-Toruń Planet Search sample, which were studied in depth (Adamów et al. 2018). The aims were to look for low-mass companions in the stars' radial velocities and to work out how evolved each star is (Adamów et al. 2018). The team used spectra from the Hobby-Eberly Telescope and from HARPS-N (Adamów et al. 2018). HD 238914 and TYC 3318-01333-1 were reported as two new planetary systems around lithium-rich giants (Adamów et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd238914b-Adamwetal2018","cite":"Adamów et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. Adamów, A. Niedzielski, K. Kowalik et al.","title":"Tracking Advanced Planetary Systems (TAPAS) with HARPS-N. VI. HD 238914 and TYC 3318-01333-1: two more Li-rich giants with planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.04379","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LHS 1678 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is a target for measuring the magnetic fields of close-in planets, a key to their habitability (Watson et al. 2025).","observed":"Radio telescopes have listened to this star for signs of the planet. The Australia Telescope Compact Array recorded 1 to 3 GHz radio waves for 1.3 orbits of the planet (Watson et al. 2025). A planet's orbit disturbing the star's magnetic field should cause radio bursts (Watson et al. 2025). The star was not detected in total brightness, but it showed circularly polarised radio emission of 72 microjanskys (Watson et al. 2025). Two points in the polarised time series rose above the 3-sigma threshold, which the authors call a promising sign (Watson et al. 2025).","system":"The star is inactive, which reduces radio bursts from stellar activity and makes it a good target (Watson et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The authors plan further cleaning of the data to remove contamination from background radio sources (Watson et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lhs1678b-Watsonetal2025","cite":"Watson et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Ariana Watson, Jackie Villadsen, J. Sebastian Pineda et al.","title":"The Search for Habitable Planets Through Uninhabitable Planets: Measuring Radio Emissions from LHS 1678","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #245","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HIP 9618 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet's period was a puzzle. TESS saw only two transits, separated by a 680-day gap, so many periods fitted (Osborn et al. 2023). CHEOPS then made targeted observations at the candidate periods and found the true one (Osborn et al. 2023). Radial velocities from HARPS-N, SOPHIE and CAFE show that the planet is lighter than its inner neighbour, with a 3-sigma upper limit of 18 Earth masses (Osborn et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, HIP 9618 b, has a mass that suggests a gas fraction of about 6.8 per cent (Osborn et al. 2023). A long-term trend in the radial velocities, combined with imaging and astrometry, points to a low-mass companion of about 0.08 solar masses orbiting at roughly 26 au (Osborn et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hip9618c-Osbornetal2023","cite":"Osborn et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"H. P. Osborn, G. Nowak, G. Hebrard et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HIP 9618 light and radial velocity curves (Osborn+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-14 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet lies in the Neptunian desert, a region with few intermediate-mass planets on short orbits (Smith et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet is about 30 per cent larger than Neptune and about 70 per cent more massive (Smith et al. 2021). Its radius came from transits seen by the Next Generation Transit Survey, ground-based follow-up and TESS, and its mass from HARPS radial velocities (Smith et al. 2021). Its orbit is just far enough out that it may have kept at least some of its original atmosphere (Smith et al. 2021).","system":"A possible stellar companion on a long-period orbit, NGTS-14B, was identified. The authors used Gaia to compare the binarity of planet hosts inside and outside the desert (Smith et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts14ab-Smithetal2021","cite":"Smith et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"A. M. S. Smith, J. S. Acton, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"NGTS-14Ab: a Neptune-sized transiting planet in the desert","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01470","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 94890 c":{"text":{"why":"The survey that turned it up studies intermediate-mass stars after they have left the main sequence, to learn how common their planets are (Fontanet et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet comes from the CORALIE search for companions around evolved stars, called CASCADES, which began in 2006 (Fontanet et al. 2025). The team collected high-precision radial velocities for more than ten years with the CORALIE spectrograph on the Euler telescope in Chile (Fontanet et al. 2025). They searched the data for repeating signals and fitted orbits to them (Fontanet et al. 2025). Only about 200 planets were known around giant stars at the time, so each new system adds to a small sample (Fontanet et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd94890c-Fontanetetal2025","cite":"Fontanet et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"E. Fontanet, S. Udry, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"CORALIE radial-velocity search for companions around evolved stars (CASCADES): IV. New planetary systems around HD 87816, HD 94890, and HD 102888 and an update on HD 121056","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14317","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 110067 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"HD 110067 has six sub-Neptunes locked in a resonant chain, where their orbital periods form simple ratios (Zak et al. 2024). Observations with the ESPRESSO spectrograph during a transit of planet c showed that its orbit is well aligned with the star's spin, with a sky-projected angle of 6 degrees, give or take about 25 (Zak et al. 2024). The authors read this as a sign that the system reached its layout by migrating inward together, without major disruptive events (Zak et al. 2024).","system":"The host is not a single star. It has a wide companion, HD 110106, at a projected separation of 13,400 au, which is itself a spectroscopic binary with an 8-year period (Apps & Luque 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd110067f-Zaketal2024","cite":"Zak et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"J. Zak, H. M. J. Boffin, E. Sedaghati et al.","title":"HD 110067 c has an aligned orbit. Measuring the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect inside a resonant multi-planet system with ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12409","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd110067f-AppsLuque2023","cite":"Apps & Luque 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Kevin Apps, Rafael Luque","title":"HD 110067 is a Wide Hierarchical Triple System","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.04599","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-47 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This is the long-period giant planet of a rare system. It was measured with precise radial velocities, which gave the planets' masses and orbits (Vanderburg et al. 2018). A dynamical analysis suggests that it likely orbits near the same plane as the inner transiting planets (Vanderburg et al. 2018). The same analysis for five other hot Jupiter systems with distant companions gave similar results (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","system":"WASP-47 also has a hot Jupiter, a super-Earth on a 0.79-day orbit and a Neptune-sized planet on a 9-day orbit (Bryant & Bayliss 2022). It was the first hot Jupiter found with such close companions (Becker & Adams 2016).","history":"Flat orbits disfavour hot Jupiter models that rely on strong gravitational interactions, such as Kozai-Lidov migration (Vanderburg et al. 2018).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp47c-Vanderburgetal2018","cite":"Vanderburg et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Andrew Vanderburg, Juliette Becker, David W. Latham et al.","title":"WASP-47 and the Origin of Hot Jupiters","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47c-BryantBayliss2022","cite":"Bryant & Bayliss 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Edward M. Bryant, Daniel Bayliss","title":"Revisiting WASP-47 with ESPRESSO and TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12747","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp47c-BeckerAdams2016","cite":"Becker & Adams 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Juliette C. Becker, Andrew Vanderburg, Fred C. Adams et al.","title":"WASP-47: A Hot Jupiter System with Two Additional Planets Discovered by K2","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.02411","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":4,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-57 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits a fast-spinning A-type star, and a Doppler shadow seen during a partial transit with Keck helped confirm it (Hartman et al. 2015). The early data allowed two possible spin-orbit angles (Hartman et al. 2015). A later study found practically no deviation between the planet's orbit and the star's equator (Balkóová et al. 2026). Searches for atmospheric absorption with HARPS-N found none (Stangret et al. 2022). A near-infrared study reported a tentative 3.8-sigma detection of water (Meni-Gallardo et al. 2026). Early multicolour transits hinted that the measured radius varies with wavelength, but this needs confirming (Garver et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The atmosphere is not yet settled: one study found no features and another found a tentative water signal (Stangret et al. 2022; Meni-Gallardo et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp57b-Hartmanetal2015","cite":"Hartman et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"J. D. Hartman, G. Á. Bakos, L. A. Buchhave et al.","title":"HAT-P-57b: A Short-period Giant Planet Transiting a Bright Rapidly Rotating A8V Star Confirmed Via Doppler Tomography","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.08839","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp57b-Balkovetal2026","cite":"Balkóová et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Z. Balkóová, J. Žák, M. Skarka et al.","title":"Aligned, Misaligned, and Polar Orbits of Hot Jupiters: Measuring Spin-orbit Angles via Doppler Tomography with HARPS-N","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11313","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp57b-Stangretetal2022","cite":"Stangret et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"M. Stangret, N. Casasayas-Barris, E. Pallé et al.","title":"High-resolution transmission spectroscopy study of ultra-hot Jupiters HAT-P-57b, KELT-17b, KELT-21b, KELT-7b, MASCARA-1b, and WASP-189b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.13064","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp57b-MeniGallardoetal2026","cite":"Meni-Gallardo et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"P. Meni-Gallardo, J. Orell-Miquel, G. Fernández-Rodríguez et al.","title":"Cross-correlation transmission spectroscopy of ultra-hot Jupiters WASP-189b, HAT-P-57b, KELT-17b, and KELT-21b with GIANO-B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27221","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp57b-Garveretal2018","cite":"Garver et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Bethany Ray Garver, Jackson Lane Cole, Cristilyn N. Gardner et al.","title":"Characterizing Giant Exoplanets through Multiwavelength Transit Observations: HAT-P-57 b","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts #231","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 16417 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The campaign showed that long, continuous runs of observations are needed to detect low-amplitude planets on short orbits (O'Toole et al. 2009). Further Doppler data from the Anglo-Australian Telescope and from the Keck Planet Search confirmed it (O'Toole et al. 2009). The orbit is slightly eccentric, at 0.20 (O'Toole et al. 2009). At the time, it brought the number of published planets with minimum masses below 25 Earth masses to 18 (O'Toole et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd16417b-OTooleetal2009","cite":"O'Toole et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Simon O'Toole, C. G. Tinney, R. Paul Butler et al.","title":"A Neptune-mass Planet Orbiting the Nearby G Dwarf HD 16417","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.4024","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-110 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of the most massive known with a sub-Neptune radius, and its density is pinned down to better than 30 per cent (Osborn et al. 2017). The mass came from 28 HARPS and HARPS-N spectra (Osborn et al. 2017). Its density fits an Earth-like interior of about 16.5 Earth masses under a hydrogen atmosphere of only about 0.2 Earth masses, though it could also hold a lot of water (Osborn et al. 2017).","system":"The star is an old, metal-poor K dwarf, about 8 billion years old (Osborn et al. 2017).","history":"The planet probably migrated inward through the disc, which makes the lack of a thick hydrogen atmosphere puzzling (Osborn et al. 2017).","open":"Evaporation by stellar ultraviolet light is unlikely to explain the thin atmosphere (Osborn et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2110b-Osbornetal2017","cite":"Osborn et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"H. P. Osborn, A. Santerne, S. C. C. Barros et al.","title":"K2-110 b: a massive mini-Neptune exoplanet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.04291","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3819 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of 20 planets in the second paper of the TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Surveys (Yee et al. 2023).","observed":"The planet was first flagged as a transit candidate in TESS photometry (Yee et al. 2023). It was one of six targets picked from the main mission data for faster 2-minute observations in the first extended mission (Yee et al. 2023). Archival photometry from the WASP transit survey was also available for its star (Yee et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3819b-Yeeetal2023","cite":"Yee et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"S. W. Yee, J. N. Winn, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: TESS Grand Unified Hot Jupiter Surveys. II. (Yee+, 2023)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 90156 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Neptune analogue, with a minimum mass of 1.05 Neptune masses (Mordasini et al. 2011). Its orbit is noticeably eccentric, at 0.31 (Mordasini et al. 2011). It was found with the HARPS spectrograph on the ESO 3.6-m telescope at La Silla, in a programme that also turned up two other planets of intermediate mass around other stars (Mordasini et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd90156b-Mordasinietal2011","cite":"Mordasini et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"C. Mordasini, M. Mayor, S. Udry et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXIV. Companions to HD 85390, HD 90156, and HD 103197: a Neptune analog and two intermediate-mass planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.0856","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"eps Eri b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what has been observed here concerns the star's dust. Spitzer and submillimetre images show a ring at 35 to 90 au (Backman et al. 2009). The ring is mainly large grains, with smaller grains inside and beyond it (Backman et al. 2009). Two further narrow dust belts sit inside the ring's central gap, and the innermost, at about 3 au, is made of silicate dust (Backman et al. 2009). Mid-infrared imaging with the VLT found no planet, but ruled out several-Jupiter-mass planets at wide separations in the inner sky (Pathak et al. 2021).","system":"A simple dynamical model suggests the dust could come from about 11 Earth masses of colliding planetesimals in the ring (Backman et al. 2009). An early study noted that the densest dust lies close to the likely orbit of the planet (Das & Backman 1992).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epserib-Backmanetal2009","cite":"Backman et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"D. Backman, M. Marengo, K. Stapelfeldt et al.","title":"Epsilon Eridani's Planetary Debris Disk: Structure and Dynamics Based on Spitzer and Caltech Submillimeter Observatory Observations","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.4564","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epserib-Pathaketal2021","cite":"Pathak et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"P. Pathak, D. J. M. Petit dit de la Roche, M. Kasper et al.","title":"High-contrast imaging at ten microns: A search for exoplanets around Eps Indi A, Eps Eri, Tau Ceti, Sirius A, and Sirius B","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13032","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-epserib-DasBackman1992","cite":"Das & Backman 1992","year":"1992","authors":"S. R. Das, D. E. Backman","title":"Epsilon Eridani's Planetary Companion and Circumstellar Dust","journal":"American Astronomical Society Meeting Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":48,"about":9,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 148427 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was found with Doppler data from Lick Observatory, along with several other long-period giants whose eccentricities tend to be higher than the average for known planets (Fischer et al. 2009). The star is a metal-rich K0 subgiant (Fischer et al. 2009). A study that combined radial velocities with Gaia astrometry found that its true mass exceeds 13.5 Jupiter masses at 3-sigma, which puts it in the brown dwarf range (Kiefer et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass is in doubt, since the object may be a brown dwarf and not a planet (Kiefer et al. 2021)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd148427b-Fischeretal2009","cite":"Fischer et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"Debra Fischer, Peter Driscoll, Howard Isaacson et al.","title":"Five Planets and an Independent Confirmation of HD 196885Ab from Lick Observatory","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1596","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd148427b-Kieferetal2021","cite":"Kiefer et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"F. Kiefer, G. Hébrard, A. Lecavelier des Etangs et al.","title":"Determining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia. Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.14164","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"L 98-59 f":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The outer planet shows up only in radial velocities, since the three inner planets transit but this one does not (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023). A signal near 23 days had earlier been a candidate, and it was unclear whether it was a planet or an artefact of stellar activity, because the star's rotation period was not well known (Schwarz et al. 2022). The confirmation combines 25 sectors of TESS photometry from 2018 to 2025 with 66 ESPRESSO spectra and 166 HARPS spectra (Cadieux et al. 2025).","system":"The M3 dwarf hosts four confirmed planets and this one. Planets c and d lie close to a 2:1 orbital resonance, and the transiting planets have been observed with Hubble for atmospheric studies (Schwarz & Dreizler 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-l9859f-SchwarzDreizler2023","cite":"Schwarz & Dreizler 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler","title":"L98-59 (TOI-175) a case study for a multiple exoplanet system with small planets near resonance","journal":"EAS2023, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859f-Schwarzetal2022","cite":"Schwarz et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Paul Schwarz, Stefan Dreizler, René Heller","title":"Disentangling the stellar activity and the radial velocity signatures of L 98-59 (TOI-175)","journal":"EAS2022, European Astronomical Society Annual Meeting","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-l9859f-Cadieuxetal2025","cite":"Cadieux et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"C. Cadieux, A. L'Heureux, C. Piaulet-Ghorayeb et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: L 98-59 f confirmed with TESS, HARPS, ESPRESSO (Cadieux+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":5,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-94 B b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits one star of a wide binary whose two similar stars each host a hot Jupiter (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). The stars are 15 arcseconds apart, a projected separation of 2700 au (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). The planets' orbital planes are inclined relative to each other, so at least one is tilted relative to the plane of the stellar binary (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). This bears on how close-in giants form and on the role of stellar multiplicity (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014).","system":"The twin planet, WASP-94 A b, moves on a retrograde orbit (Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014). JWST spectra show it has a muted morning limb and a clear evening limb (Fu et al. 2025). Ground-based spectra suggest sodium absorption and a blue slope from Rayleigh scattering (Ahrer 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp94bb-NeveuVanMalleetal2014","cite":"Neveu-VanMalle et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"M. Neveu-VanMalle, D. Queloz, D. R. Anderson et al.","title":"WASP-94 A and B planets: hot-Jupiter cousins in a twin-star system","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.7566","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp94bb-Fuetal2025","cite":"Fu et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Guangwei Fu, Sagnick Mukherjee, Kevin B. Stevenson et al.","title":"Overcast Mornings and Clear Evenings in Hot Jupiter Exoplanet Atmospheres","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.15854","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp94bb-Ahrer2021","cite":"Ahrer 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Eva-Maria Ahrer","title":"LRG-BEASTS: sodium absorption and Rayleigh slope in the atmosphere of WASP-94A b using NTT/EFOSC2","journal":"Atmospheres, Atmospheres! Do I Look Like I Care About Atmospheres?","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5544521","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-2337 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is one of three hot Jupiters found around evolved, intermediate-mass stars by combining TESS with ground-based photometry and radial velocities (Grunblatt et al. 2022). It has the shortest period of any planet found around a red giant star so far (Grunblatt et al. 2022). Unlike its two companion discoveries, it shows no sign of inflation, and is one of the densest hot Jupiters known (Grunblatt et al. 2022).","system":"The orbit is nearly circular, with an eccentricity below 0.2 (Grunblatt et al. 2022).","history":"Hot Jupiters around evolved stars are expected to be inflated and eventually engulfed by their hosts (Grunblatt et al. 2022).","open":"How planet inflation depends on mass, radius and orbit is complex and not well known (Grunblatt et al. 2022)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi2337b-Grunblattetal2022","cite":"Grunblatt et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Samuel K. Grunblatt, Nicholas Saunders, Meng Sun et al.","title":"TESS Giants Transiting Giants. II. The Hottest Jupiters Orbiting Evolved Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.04140","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-34 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet orbits two stars at once. Its discovery helped show that such systems are not rare (Welsh 2012).","observed":"Over a decade of Kepler and TESS data has since been searched for timing variations in the eclipses that an extra planet would cause (Spalding & Orosz 2024). Of the fourteen eclipsing binaries known to host a transiting circumbinary planet, only two show strong evidence of additional planets, and this is not one of them (Spalding & Orosz 2024).","system":"Simulations suggest that the eccentric orbits of the binary and planets may stop a ring of debris from forming alongside the planet (Demidova & Shevchenko 2018).","history":"The planet probably did not form where it is. Simulations show that growing planets from small bodies there is hard, so it was likely assembled farther out in the disc and migrated inward (Paardekooper et al. 2012).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler34b-Welsh2012","cite":"Welsh 2012","year":"2012","authors":"William F Welsh, Jerome A Orosz","title":"Stellar Astrophysics Via Exoplanet Transits","journal":"NSF Award","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler34b-SpaldingOrosz2024","cite":"Spalding & Orosz 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Patricia Spalding, Jerome Orosz","title":"A Search for Additional Planets in Kepler-16, Kepler-34, and Kepler-1647","journal":"56th Annual Meeting of the Division for Planetary Sciences","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler34b-DemidovaShevchenko2018","cite":"Demidova & Shevchenko 2018","year":"2018","authors":"T. V. Demidova, I. I. Shevchenko","title":"Simulations of the Dynamics of the Debris Disks in the Systems Kepler-16, Kepler-34, and Kepler-35","journal":"Astronomy Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.07390","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler34b-Paardekooperetal2012","cite":"Paardekooper et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Sijme-Jan Paardekooper, Zoë M. Leinhardt, Philippe Thébault et al.","title":"How Not to Build Tatooine: The Difficulty of In Situ Formation of Circumbinary Planets Kepler 16b, Kepler 34b, and Kepler 35b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.3484","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":3,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-64 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of seven hot Jupiters and Saturns announced together from the HATNet survey, all orbiting bright Sun-like stars (Bakos et al. 2021).","observed":"The planet has a radius of about 1.70 Jupiter radii but a mass of only 0.58 Jupiter masses, so it is puffed up compared with Jupiter (Bakos et al. 2021). The study gives accurate stellar parameters for the host stars of all seven planets (Bakos et al. 2021).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp64b-Bakosetal2021","cite":"Bakos et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"G. Á. Bakos, J. D. Hartman, W. Bhatti et al.","title":"HAT-P-58b-HAT-P-64b: Seven Planets Transiting Bright Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05528","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-26 b":{"text":{"why":"The six planets announced together were all inflated hot Jupiters from the HATSouth survey (Espinoza et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet has a radius of 1.75 Jupiter radii and orbits a slightly evolved F star just after the turn-off point (Espinoza et al. 2016). It is among the least dense hot Jupiters known, at 0.153 grams per cubic centimetre (Espinoza et al. 2016). The authors say it is a good target for future atmospheric studies (Espinoza et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats26b-Espinozaetal2016","cite":"Espinoza et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"N. Espinoza, D. Bayliss, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS-25b through HATS-30b: A Half-dozen New Inflated Transiting Hot Jupiters from the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.00023","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-564 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet barely crosses the edge of its star's disc. The transit impact parameter is 0.994, making it one of only about 20 known grazing transit systems and one of the brightest hosts among them (Davis et al. 2020). Because the radius is so uncertain in a grazing transit, it is measured as 1.02 Jupiter radii with large error bars (Davis et al. 2020).","system":"Grazing transits are very sensitive to small changes in orbit, so the system is a good place to look for extra non-transiting, smaller planets (Davis et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi564b-Davisetal2020","cite":"Davis et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Allen B. Davis, Songhu Wang, Matias Jones et al.","title":"TOI 564 b and TOI 905 b: Grazing and Fully Transiting Hot Jupiters Discovered by TESS","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10186","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 97048 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is inferred from the motion of gas in a disc around a young star. The star, a Herbig Ae star in the Chamaeleon I dark cloud, has a large flaring disc seen at an inclination (Doucet et al. 2007). Hubble images show scattered light from material between about 360 and 720 au, with filaments like spiral arms (Doering et al. 2007). Such structure has been attributed to orbiting planets or stellar encounters (Doering et al. 2007). Herschel detected CH+ emission from the disc, only the second such detection in a Herbig Ae star (Meeus et al. 2012).","system":"A search with Gaia and Hipparcos astrometry found no evidence of stellar-mass companions between about 4 and 25 au (Blakely et al. 2026).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd97048b-Doucetetal2007","cite":"Doucet et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"C. Doucet, E. Habart, E. Pantin et al.","title":"HD 97048: a closer look at the disk","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:20066610","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97048b-Doeringetal2007","cite":"Doering et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"R. L. Doering, M. Meixner, S. T. Holfeltz et al.","title":"HD 97048's Circumstellar Environment as Revealed by a Hubble Space Telescope ACS Coronagraphic Study of Disk Candidate Stars","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0701576","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97048b-Meeusetal2012","cite":"Meeus et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"G. Meeus, B. Montesinos, I. Mendigutía et al.","title":"Observations of Herbig Ae/Be stars with Herschel/PACS. The atomic and molecular contents of their protoplanetary discs","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.3413","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd97048b-Blakelyetal2026","cite":"Blakely et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Dori Blakely, William Thompson, Doug Johnstone et al.","title":"Dynamical Mass Constraints on Transition Disk Perturbers with the G23H Catalog","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07731","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":30,"about":4,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 13931 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Radial velocities alone give only a minimum mass. Combining them with absolute astrometry removes that uncertainty and gives a true mass in the planetary range (Philipot et al. 2023).","system":"The star shows evidence for a single planet in Keplerian motion (Howard et al. 2010).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd13931b-Philipotetal2023","cite":"Philipot et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"F. Philipot, A.-M. Lagrange, P. Rubini et al.","title":"Updated characterization of long-period single companion by combining radial velocity, relative astrometry, and absolute astrometry","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01263","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd13931b-Howardetal2010","cite":"Howard et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The California Planet Survey. I. Four New Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3488","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5293 A b":{"text":{"why":"The planet is one of the coolest gas giants among hot-Jupiter-sized planets orbiting M dwarfs (Cañas et al. 2023).","observed":"A temperate gas giant orbits a mid-M dwarf that has a wide M dwarf companion (Cañas et al. 2023). The team used ground and space photometry, speckle imaging, and radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder and NEID spectrographs (Cañas et al. 2023). The planet is a favourable target for atmospheric studies and for measuring the tilt of its orbit relative to the star's spin (Cañas et al. 2023).","system":"A later analysis found tentative evidence in Gaia data for a new massive companion around the host star, which needs follow-up to confirm (Weisserman et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"Whether a second companion exists and how the planet reached its close orbit are not yet settled (Weisserman et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5293ab-Caasetal2023","cite":"Cañas et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Shubham Kanodia, Jessica Libby-Roberts et al.","title":"TOI-3984 A b and TOI-5293 A b: Two Temperate Gas Giants Transiting Mid-M Dwarfs in Wide Binary Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07714","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi5293ab-Weissermanetal2026","cite":"Weisserman et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Drew Weisserman, Ryan Cloutier, Alexandra Rochon et al.","title":"New and Updated Rossiter-McLaughlin Measurements for Three Hot Jupiter-Hosting M Dwarfs","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.03031","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KOI-7368 b":{"text":{"why":"The star belongs to a loose, spread-out group of young stars called the Cep-Her complex (Bouma et al. 2022).","observed":"Gaia measurements of stellar positions and velocities, spin periods of the star measured by TESS, and spectra were used to show that this star is about 36 million years old (Bouma et al. 2022). It lies in a diffuse part of the group the authors call CH-2 (Bouma et al. 2022). The transit shape and high-resolution images make a planet by far the most likely explanation (Bouma et al. 2022). Most known planets are billions of years old, so a planet this young is rare (Bouma et al. 2022).","system":"Another young Kepler planet host, KOI-7913, sits in the same diffuse region and has a similar age (Bouma et al. 2022).","history":"Together with two neighbours, it gives the first direct evidence that mini-Neptunes about twice Earth's size already exist at ages near 40 million years (Bouma et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-koi7368b-Boumaetal2022","cite":"Bouma et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. G. Bouma, R. Kerr, J. L. Curtis et al.","title":"Kepler and the Behemoth: Three Mini-Neptunes in a 40 Million Year Old Association","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01112","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 235088 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is a young, active K-type star, about 600 to 800 million years old (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","observed":"Observers saw the star's light dim at the helium line during the planet's transit, which shows that helium gas surrounds the planet (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). The absorption was about 0.9 percent, and it may vary from one transit to the next (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). Models of this signal point to an upper atmosphere that escapes into space, losing mass at roughly 10 billion grams per second in a relatively cold outflow of about 3125 K (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023). It is the smallest planet with a solid detection of any atom or molecule in its atmosphere (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023).","system":"Strong activity on the star complicates the measurement of the planet's mass, so the authors modelled the star's signal and the planet together (Nardiello et al. 2025).","history":"A later study followed the planet's atmosphere back and forward in time (Hall et al. 2026). It found that the planet may have lost anywhere from almost none to a large part of its envelope, depending on how fast the star spun when young (Hall et al. 2026).","open":"A survey of 70 planets found no sign that young planets lose their atmospheres faster than old ones (Orell-Miquel et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd235088b-OrellMiqueletal2023","cite":"Orell-Miquel et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Orell-Miquel, M. Lampón, M. López-Puertas et al.","title":"Confirmation of an He I evaporating atmosphere around the 650-Myr-old sub-Neptune HD 235088 b (TOI-1430 b) with CARMENES","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05191","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd235088b-Nardielloetal2025","cite":"Nardiello et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Nardiello, J. M. Akana Murphy, R. Spinelli et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: Spectroscopic data of HD 235088 (TOI-1430) (Nardiello+, 2025)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd235088b-Halletal2026","cite":"Hall et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Stephanie Hall, Rory Barnes, Sean Matt","title":"The Fate of Planets: Coupling Stellar Evolution to Planetary Atmospheric Lifetimes for TOI-1430 b","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":5,"about":2,"read":5},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 30856 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Imaging with the Keck telescope found that the planet's host star has a stellar companion that was not known before (Ngo et al. 2017). The star was one of 144 systems with giant planets searched for companions in the largest survey of its kind so far (Ngo et al. 2017). The survey found no evidence that having a companion star changes the orbits or other properties of planets between 0.1 and 5 au from their star (Ngo et al. 2017).","system":"The planet's star is part of a multiple-star system (Ngo et al. 2017).","history":"","open":"The companions might still affect planets farther out, but the radial velocity data give only weak limits on such planets (Ngo et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd30856b-Ngoetal2017","cite":"Ngo et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Henry Ngo, Heather A. Knutson, Marta L. Bryan et al.","title":"No Difference in Orbital Parameters of RV-detected Giant Planets between 0.1 and 5 au in Single versus Multi-stellar Systems","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02326","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-3919 b":{"text":{"why":"It is one of nine hot Jupiters from a survey that aims to build a complete, evenly analysed sample of such planets around bright Sun-like stars (Schulte et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet was confirmed with ground-based imaging and spectroscopy after the space telescope TESS first flagged it (Schulte et al. 2024). Its orbit is measurably eccentric, meaning it is not a perfect circle (Schulte et al. 2024). That is notable for a planet so close to its star, because tides tend to make such orbits round over time.","system":"","history":"Hot Jupiters may have formed where they are, moved inward through the gas disc, or been flung onto stretched orbits that tides then shrank (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024). A uniform sample is meant to test these ideas (Schulte & Rodriguez 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi3919b-Schulteetal2024","cite":"Schulte et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi3919b-SchulteRodriguez2024","cite":"Schulte & Rodriguez 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Jack Schulte, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"Migration and Evolution of giant ExoPlanets (MEEP). I. Nine Newly Confirmed Hot Jupiters from the TESS Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05923","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-132 b":{"text":{"why":"It is among the least irradiated and coolest planets found by the WASP survey, with a 7.1-day orbit around a K4 star (Hellier et al. 2017).","observed":"The planet's mass is about 0.41 Jupiter masses and its radius about 0.87 Jupiter radii (Hellier et al. 2017). Later work found a smaller planet closer to the star, a world about 1.78 times Earth's radius that circles in about one day (Hord et al. 2022). Such a close companion to a hot Jupiter is unusual.","system":"The inner planet, called WASP-132 c, was validated from TESS and ground-based data (Hord et al. 2022). Computer simulations show the two planets can stay stable for about 100 million years (Hord et al. 2022). The star may show a faint 0.4 millimagnitude brightness wobble from its rotation (Hellier et al. 2017).","history":"Hot Jupiters were thought to lack close companions, which was read as evidence they reached their orbits on stretched paths that would fling companions away (Hord et al. 2022). The inner planet makes that route highly unlikely here (Hord et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp132b-Hellieretal2017","cite":"Hellier et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"C. Hellier, D. R. Anderson, A. Collier Cameron et al.","title":"WASP-South transiting exoplanets: WASP-130b, WASP-131b, WASP-132b, WASP-139b, WASP-140b, WASP-141b and WASP-142b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.04195","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp132b-Hordetal2022","cite":"Hord et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Benjamin Hord, Knicole Colon, Travis Berger et al.","title":"TOI-822.02 is a Nearby Planetary Companion to Hot Jupiter WASP-132 b","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 128311 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was followed with two kinds of data. The Hobby-Eberly Telescope took 355 high-resolution spectra between 2005 and 2011, using an iodine cell to measure tiny shifts in the star's motion (McArthur et al. 2017). The Hubble Space Telescope also took 29 orbits of astrometry in 2007 to 2009, which measure the star's position on the sky directly (McArthur et al. 2017). Combining the two kinds of data can show the true orbit rather than only the motion toward and away from us.","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd128311c-McArthuretal2017","cite":"McArthur et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"B. E. McArthur, G. F. Benedict, G. W. Henry et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD 128311 radial velocity and astrometric data (McArthur+, 2014)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.17950041","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 75289 b":{"text":{"why":"The star is metal-rich, with iron about twice the Sun's level, and every hot-Jupiter host studied then was metal-rich too (Gonzalez & Laws 2000).","observed":"The orbit is circular, and the minimum mass of 0.42 Jupiter masses was the lowest then known for a planet around a Sun-like star (Udry et al. 2000). Tidal theory limits the true mass to less than 1.21 Jupiter masses (Trilling 2000). A snapshot technique may have found a shallow transit, which would make this the brightest transiting hot Jupiter system and a prime target for later atmosphere studies (Crossfield et al. 2017). It has also been a target for attempts to detect the planet's own light in the near infrared (Barnes et al. 2008).","system":"The star was once misclassified as a supergiant, but it is a metal-rich dwarf (Gratton et al. 1989).","history":"","open":"Transits were not found until the possible shallow signal, and it still needed confirmation (Crossfield et al. 2017)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-GonzalezLaws2000","cite":"Gonzalez & Laws 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Guillermo Gonzalez, Chris Laws","title":"Parent Stars of Extrasolar Planets. V. HD 75289","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9909453","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-Udryetal2000","cite":"Udry et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"S. Udry, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extra-solar planets. II. The short-period planetary companions to <ASTROBJ>HD 75289</ASTROBJ> and <ASTROBJ>HD 130322</ASTROBJ>","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-Trilling2000","cite":"Trilling 2000","year":"2000","authors":"D. E. Trilling","title":"Tidal Constraints on the Masses of Extrasolar Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/312746","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-Crossfieldetal2017","cite":"Crossfield et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Ian Crossfield, Jessica Krick, Nick Cowan et al.","title":"Confirming the Brightest Transiting Gas Giant","journal":"Spitzer Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-Barnesetal2008","cite":"Barnes et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"J. R. Barnes, H. R. A. Jones, Travis S. Barman et al.","title":"Near Infrared Spectroscopic Characterisation Of Close Orbiting Extrasolar Giant Planets","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress 2008","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd75289b-Grattonetal1989","cite":"Gratton et al. 1989","year":"1989","authors":"Raffaele G. Gratton, Paola Focardi, Rino Bandiera","title":"A spectroscopic analysis of three supergiants.","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/237.4.1085","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":11,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LP 890-9 c":{"text":{"why":"It orbits a nearby, very small M6 dwarf star (Delrez et al. 2022).","observed":"The inner planet, LP 890-9 b, was found first by TESS. Intensive monitoring with the SPECULOOS telescopes then revealed this second planet, which TESS had missed (Delrez et al. 2022). Its period was confirmed with MuSCAT3 (Delrez et al. 2022). The planet receives about the same flux as modern Earth, which puts it close to the inner edge of the habitable zone (Kaltenegger et al. 2023).","system":"Two super-Earths transit a low-activity star, and both are temperate (Delrez et al. 2022).","history":"","open":"Models show it could be a hot, wet Earth, a steamy planet in a runaway greenhouse, or a Venus-like world, and JWST spectra could tell these apart (Kaltenegger et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-lp8909c-Delrezetal2022","cite":"Delrez et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"L. Delrez, C. A. Murray, F. J. Pozuelos et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: LP 890-9 (TOI-4306) light curves and RVs (Delrez+, 2022)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"https://doi.org/10.26093/cds/vizier.36670059","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-lp8909c-Kalteneggeretal2023","cite":"Kaltenegger et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"L. Kaltenegger, R. C. Payne, Z. Lin et al.","title":"Hot Earth or Young Venus? A nearby transiting rocky planet mystery","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.03105","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1647 b":{"text":{"why":"It defies the trend of circumbinary planets orbiting close to the stability limit of their stars (Kostov et al. 2016).","observed":"The planet orbits two stars at once, in about 1100 days. It crossed in front of them only twice during Kepler's lifetime, producing three transits (Kostov et al. 2016). One transit happened during an eclipse of one star by the other (Kostov et al. 2016). The planet also shifted the timing of the stellar eclipses, which gave its mass of about 1.5 Jupiter masses (Kostov et al. 2016). It is the largest and longest-period transiting circumbinary planet found by Kepler (Kostov et al. 2016).","system":"The two stars are each about as massive as the Sun and orbit each other every 11 days on a mildly eccentric orbit (Kostov et al. 2016). They are spin-synchronized (Kostov et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1647b-Kostovetal2016","cite":"Kostov et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Veselin B. Kostov, Jerome A. Orosz, William F. Welsh et al.","title":"Kepler-1647b: The Largest and Longest-period Kepler Transiting Circumbinary Planet","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.00189","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 104985 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet-host star found in the Okayama survey, which looks for planets around evolved late-G giant stars of intermediate mass (Sato et al. 2005).","observed":"The host is a giant star, not a Sun-like dwarf, and such stars tend to show larger natural jitter in their motion, around 6 to 20 metres per second (Sato et al. 2005). The planet's signal still stands out as a periodic variation (Sato et al. 2005). It belongs to a small group of planets around intermediate-mass stars, all of which have masses of several Jupiters (Hartmann et al. 2008).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd104985b-Satoetal2005","cite":"Sato et al. 2005","year":"2005","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Eiji Kambe, Yoichi Takeda et al.","title":"Radial-Velocity Variability of G-Type Giants: First Three Years of the Okayama Planet Search Program","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/pasj/57.1.97","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd104985b-Hartmannetal2008","cite":"Hartmann et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"M. Hartmann, A. P. Hatzes, E. W. Guenther et al.","title":"A Survey for Extrasolar Planets Around A F Type Stars","journal":"Precision Spectroscopy in Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-75485-5_70","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TIC 139270665 b":{"text":{"why":"Citizen scientists spotted the first hints of the planet in TESS data from sector 47 (Peluso et al. 2024).","observed":"Only a single transit was seen in the TESS data, so its period was unknown (Peluso et al. 2024). Radial velocity data from the Automated Planet Finder suggested about 23.6 days, and the Unistellar citizen science network of amateur telescopes then caught a second transit (Peluso et al. 2024). With a bulk density of 2.13 grams per cubic centimetre, it is the densest warm sub-Saturn among TESS planets (Peluso et al. 2024). The campaign also served as a test of an education programme for young students (Peluso et al. 2024).","system":"It orbits a metal-rich G2 star, and the data show evidence of a second planet with a period near 1010 days and a minimum mass of about 4.9 Jupiter masses (Peluso et al. 2024).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tic139270665b-Pelusoetal2024","cite":"Peluso et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Daniel O'Conner Peluso, Paul A. Dalba, Duncan Wright et al.","title":"Confirming the Warm and Dense Sub-Saturn TIC 139270665 b with the Automated Planet Finder and Unistellar Citizen Science Network","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-3881/ad2772","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-5 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is bright and photometrically quiet, which makes it a good target for transmission spectroscopy (Zhou et al. 2014).","observed":"A ground-based optical transmission spectrum with the Magellan telescope, built from five transits, shows a tentative detection of water (Allen et al. 2022). The planet's temperature of about 1025 K puts it near the range where atmospheres are predicted to be clearer, between where silicate clouds and hydrocarbon hazes form (Allen et al. 2022). It sits at the dividing point between warm and hot giant planets (Allen et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":"Among close-in giants, the size of hot Jupiter-mass planets depends most strongly on temperature, while metallicity shows no clear effect (Zhou et al. 2014)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats5b-Zhouetal2014","cite":"Zhou et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"G. Zhou, D. Bayliss, K. Penev et al.","title":"HATS-5b: A Transiting Hot Saturn from the HATSouth Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1582","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats5b-Allenetal2022","cite":"Allen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Natalie Allen, Néstor Espinoza, Andres Jordan et al.","title":"ACCESS: Tentative detection of H2O in the ground-based optical transmission spectrum of the low density hot-Saturn HATS-5b","journal":"Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5153 b":{"text":{"why":"The aim was to confirm planets that TESS saw transit only once, using ground-based follow-up (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","observed":"The Next Generation Transit Survey watched the star for additional transits, and spectrographs measured the planet's mass (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022). The planet has a long period and an eccentric orbit (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022). Long-period planets are useful because their atmospheres stay largely unchanged by tides and radiation from the star (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"Wide orbits like this one reflect a less extreme migration history than that of close-in planets (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5153b-UlmerMolletal2022","cite":"Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Ulmer-Moll, M. Lendl, S. Gill et al.","title":"Two long-period transiting exoplanets on eccentric orbits: NGTS-20 b (TOI-5152 b) and TOI-5153 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.03911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-27 b":{"text":{"why":"The host star is a metal-rich G8 dwarf (Béky et al. 2011).","observed":"Among 30 known transiting planets of 0.3 to 0.8 Jupiter masses, larger planets orbit stars with less metal, and radius also depends on temperature (Béky et al. 2011). The planet was independently found by another survey, WASP, where it is called WASP-40b (Anderson et al. 2011). Follow-up of 46 transits found no sign of timing variations (Seeliger et al. 2015). A recent search of TESS data suggests new planet candidates in the system (Dévora-Pajares et al. 2024).","system":"The alignment of the planet's orbit with the star's spin is poorly constrained, at about 24 degrees with a large uncertainty (Brown et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"The spin-orbit angle remains essentially unmeasured because of noisy data (Brown et al. 2012)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp27b-Bkyetal2011","cite":"Béky et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"B. Béky, G. Á. Bakos, J. Hartman et al.","title":"HAT-P-27b: A Hot Jupiter Transiting a G Star on a 3 Day Orbit","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.3511","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp27b-Andersonetal2011","cite":"Anderson et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"D. R. Anderson, S. C. C. Barros, I. Boisse et al.","title":"WASP-40b: Independent Discovery of the 0.6 M<SUB>Jup</SUB> Transiting Exoplanet HAT-P-27b","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.4643","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp27b-Seeligeretal2015","cite":"Seeliger et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"M. Seeliger, M. Kitze, R. Errmann et al.","title":"Ground-based transit observations of the HAT-P-18, HAT-P-19, HAT-P-27/WASP40 and WASP-21 systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.06215","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp27b-DvoraPajaresetal2024","cite":"Dévora-Pajares et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Martín Dévora-Pajares, Francisco J. Pozuelos, Antoine Thuillier et al.","title":"The SHERLOCK pipeline: new exoplanet candidates in the WASP-16, HAT-P-27, HAT-P-26, and TOI-2411 systems","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14602","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp27b-Brownetal2012","cite":"Brown et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"D. J. A. Brown, A. Collier Cameron, R. F. Díaz et al.","title":"Analysis of Spin-Orbit Alignment in the WASP-32, WASP-38, and HAT-P-27/WASP-40 Systems","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5649","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":5,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1181 b":{"text":{"why":"The host is an early subgiant, a star that has just begun to swell after its main sequence life (Kabath et al. 2022).","observed":"The planet orbits in about 2.1 days and has a mass of about 1.18 Jupiter masses (Kabáth et al. 2022). Its star is an F9 subgiant, and the system is estimated to be 2 to 5 billion years old (Kabáth et al. 2022). Radial velocity follow-up was done mostly with telescopes of 2 to 3 metres, which shows that mid-sized telescope networks can confirm TESS gas giants (Kabáth et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1181b-Kabathetal2022","cite":"Kabath et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Petr Kabáth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"TOI-2046b, TOI-1181b, and TOI-1516b, three new hot Jupiters from TESS: planets orbiting a young star, a subgiant, and a normal star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01860","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1181b-Kabthetal2022","cite":"Kabáth et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Petr Kabáth, Priyanka Chaturvedi, Phillip J. MacQueen et al.","title":"TOI-2046b, TOI-1181b, and TOI-1516b, three new hot Jupiters from TESS: planets orbiting a young star, a subgiant, and a normal star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01860","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-193 b":{"text":{"why":"It is among the least dense transiting planets known, with a density of 0.060 grams per cubic centimetre (Yee et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet is about as large as 1.46 Jupiters but only 0.14 Jupiter masses, which standard models of irradiated gas giants cannot reproduce, even with no core (Barkaoui et al. 2022). A hidden heat source, such as electric currents or tidal heating, may be needed (Barkaoui et al. 2022). Interior models later fit the planet with about equal parts metals and hydrogen-helium gas (Yee et al. 2025). The planet is probably reinflated by its host star's evolution and is expected to be losing mass now (Yee et al. 2025).","system":"The orbit is well aligned with the star's equator, unlike several other super-puffs, which are misaligned (Yee et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"A planned JWST survey will test whether super-puffs are inflated by internal heat or by haze made by starlight (Radica et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp193b-Yeeetal2025","cite":"Yee et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Samuel W. Yee, Gudmundur Stefánsson, Daniel Thorngren et al.","title":"The Super-puff WASP-193 b is on a Well-aligned Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.17610","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp193b-Barkaouietal2022","cite":"Barkaoui et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Khalid Barkaoui, Francisco J. Pozuelos, Michaël Gillon et al.","title":"WASP-193b: An extremely low-density super-Neptune","journal":"European Planetary Science Congress","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/epsc2022-709","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp193b-Radicaetal2025","cite":"Radica et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michael Radica, Caroline Piaulet Ghorayeb, Jacob L. Bean et al.","title":"Unveiling the Nature of Super-Puffs: A Panchromatic Transmission Spectroscopy Survey","journal":"JWST Proposal. Cycle 4","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 3470 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble watched three transits in the light of hydrogen's Lyman-alpha line and saw the planet's upper atmosphere block 35% of the starlight in one wing of the line and 23% in the other (Bourrier et al. 2018). Hot gas of neutral hydrogen extends beyond the planet's Roche lobe, the region where its own gravity holds material. Later, JWST detected water, methane, sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide in its air (Beatty et al. 2024). It is the lowest-mass and coldest planet known to show a clear sulfur dioxide feature (Beatty et al. 2024). The water, carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide point to an atmosphere about 100 times richer in heavy elements than the Sun (Beatty et al. 2024).","system":"The planet sits at the edge of the \"evaporation desert\", a zone where few Neptune-sized planets survive close to their star (Bourrier et al. 2018).","history":"","open":"Models say the helium signal should be strong, yet it is muted, possibly because excited hydrogen molecules destroy the helium ions that cause it (García Muñoz et al. 2025). The methane is lower than chemistry in balance would predict (Beatty et al. 2024). An amateur-led group reported a possible second planet on a 66-day orbit, but it is unconfirmed (Scott et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj3470b-Bourrieretal2018","cite":"Bourrier et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"V. Bourrier, A. Lecavelier des Etangs, D. Ehrenreich et al.","title":"Hubble PanCET: an extended upper atmosphere of neutral hydrogen around the warm Neptune GJ 3470b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.05119","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3470b-Beattyetal2024","cite":"Beatty et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Thomas G. Beatty, Luis Welbanks, Everett Schlawin et al.","title":"Sulfur Dioxide and Other Molecular Species in the Atmosphere of the Sub-Neptune GJ 3470 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04450","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3470b-Muozetal2025","cite":"Muñoz et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"A. García Muñoz, D. De Fazio, D. J. Wilson et al.","title":"Vibrationally excited H<SUB>2</SUB> muting the He I triplet line at 1.08 μm on warm exo-Neptunes","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21966","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-gj3470b-Scottetal2020","cite":"Scott et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Phillip Scott, Bradley Walter, Quanzhi Ye et al.","title":"GJ 3470 c: A Saturn-like Exoplanet Candidate in the Habitable Zone of GJ 3470","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07373","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":27,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 40956 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet turned up in two surveys of giant stars, one watching 55 early K giants and one watching 190 G and K giants (Jeong et al. 2018).","observed":"The host is a K giant, a star that has swollen as it aged. Its velocity wobbles over 578.6 days, and the authors checked the cause and concluded that a companion is the most likely explanation (Jeong et al. 2018). The orbit is about 1.4 AU wide and the minimum mass is about 2.7 Jupiter masses (Jeong et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"The true mass is unknown, since only a minimum mass comes from the velocity signal (Jeong et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd40956b-Jeongetal2018","cite":"Jeong et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"G. Jeong, B.-C. Lee, I. Han et al.","title":"Detection of planet candidates around K giants. HD 40956, HD 111591, and HD 113996","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.07173","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-280 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a low-density sub-Saturn, with a density of about 0.48 grams per cubic centimetre, much less than water-rich Neptunes (Nowak et al. 2020). Its orbit is moderately eccentric, with an eccentricity of 0.35 (Nowak et al. 2020). The host is a mildly evolved G7 star with more iron than the Sun (Nowak et al. 2020).","system":"","history":"Sub-Saturn planets are absent from our Solar System, and K2-280 b helps test how they form (Nowak et al. 2020).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2280b-Nowaketal2020","cite":"Nowak et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Grzegorz Nowak, Enric Palle, Davide Gandolfi et al.","title":"K2-280 b - a low density warm sub-Saturn around a mildly evolved star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07939","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 126614 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a long Keck survey that gathered Doppler data for a decade, aimed at giant planets near or beyond the ice line (Howard et al. 2010).","observed":"The planet orbits the metal-rich star HD 126614 A at about 2.3 AU with a period of 3.41 years and a minimum mass of 0.38 Jupiter masses (Howard et al. 2010). The same data also revealed a faint M dwarf companion star, HD 126614 B, in the system (Howard et al. 2010).","system":"The star is a metal-rich primary with a faint M dwarf companion (Howard et al. 2010).","history":"","open":"The authors suggest following the planet up with astrometry, imaging and space-based spectroscopy (Howard et al. 2010)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd126614b-Howardetal2010","cite":"Howard et al. 2010","year":"2010","authors":"Andrew W. Howard, John Asher Johnson, Geoffrey W. Marcy et al.","title":"The California Planet Survey. I. Four New Giant Exoplanets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3488","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 70642 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet itself has not been imaged. Computer simulations placed hypothetical Earth-mass planets in the habitable zone of the system, and found that giant planets on circular orbits disturb such worlds least (Hinse et al. 2008). The host star has a weak magnetic field, detected with a polarisation-sensitive spectrograph, and is old and inactive like the Sun (Fossati et al. 2013).","system":"The giant planet's orbit shapes whether a small planet could stay stable in the habitable zone (Hinse et al. 2008).","history":"","open":"No Earth-mass planet is known here; the study only tests whether one could survive (Hinse et al. 2008)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd70642b-Hinseetal2008","cite":"Hinse et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"T. C. Hinse, R. Michelsen, U. G. Jørgensen et al.","title":"Dynamics and stability of telluric planets within the habitable zone of extrasolar planetary systems. Numerical simulations of test particles within the HD 4208 and HD 70642 systems","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361:200809822","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd70642b-Fossatietal2013","cite":"Fossati et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"L. Fossati, O. Kochukhov, J. S. Jenkins et al.","title":"Detection of a magnetic field in three old and inactive solar-like planet-hosting stars","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.0879","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 240210 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found by a search with the 9.2 m Hobby-Eberly Telescope, which studies stars of many types (Niedzielski et al. 2009).","observed":"The host is a K3 giant whose velocity changes in a way that would need several orbits to model, but the data are not yet enough to do so (Niedzielski et al. 2009). A tentative one-planet model gives a minimum mass of 5.2 Jupiter masses in a 502-day orbit (Niedzielski et al. 2009).","system":"","history":"","open":"The one-planet solution is only tentative, and more companions may be present (Niedzielski et al. 2009)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd240210b-Niedzielskietal2009","cite":"Niedzielski et al. 2009","year":"2009","authors":"A. Niedzielski, G. Nowak, M. Adamów et al.","title":"Substellar-mass Companions to the K-dwarf BD+14 4559 and the K-giants HD 240210 and BD+20 2457","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.1804","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-24 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"K2-24 b and its neighbour K2-24 c orbit close to a 2:1 resonance, in which one planet circles twice for each orbit of the other (Giuppone et al. 2023). Their gravitational tugs shift the transit times, and the system has been observed again with the CHEOPS space telescope along with K2 and Hubble data (Nascimbeni et al. 2024).","system":"The system is one of three used to map how a 2:1 resonance behaves for different masses and orbit shapes (Giuppone et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k224b-Giupponeetal2023","cite":"Giuppone et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Cristian Giuppone, Adrián Rodríguez, Viviam Alencastro et al.","title":"Mapping the structure of the planetary 2:1 mean motion resonance: the TOI-216, K2-24, and HD27894 systems","journal":"Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14198","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k224b-Nascimbenietal2024","cite":"Nascimbeni et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"V. Nascimbeni, L. Borsato, P. Leonardi et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: K2-24 planetary system revisited by CHEOPS (Nascimbeni+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4504 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Most of what is known about this system so far concerns its neighbour TOI-4504 c. The planet transits every 2.4 days, but its own mass could not be measured because the star's velocity scatter was too large (Vítková et al. 2025). Its two giant neighbours, TOI-4504 c and d, are in resonance and pull on each other. TOI-4504 c shows the largest transit timing variations known, with a swing of about 2 days (Vítková et al. 2025). Those shifts revealed a third, non-transiting giant planet, TOI-4504 d (Vítková et al. 2025).","system":"Two resonant warm gas giants of about 3.8 and 1.4 Jupiter masses orbit outside the small planet, on periods of about 82.5 and 40.6 days (Vítková et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The mass of the inner planet is still unknown (Vítková et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4504b-Vtkovetal2025","cite":"Vítková et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Michaela Vítková, Rafael Brahm, Trifon Trifonov et al.","title":"TOI-4504: Exceptionally Large Transit Timing Variations Induced by Two Resonant Warm Gas Giants in a Three-planet System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05609","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-408 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Using starquakes, which reveal the tilt of a star's spin, astronomers found the star's rotation axis is tilted about 42 degrees (Kamiaka et al. 2019). The planet's orbit is therefore misaligned with the star's spin. It is by far the smallest planet known with a clearly misaligned orbit (Kamiaka et al. 2019). Earlier studies disagreed, and the new work traced the conflict to how background noise was modelled (Kamiaka et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler408b-Kamiakaetal2019","cite":"Kamiaka et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Shoya Kamiaka, Othman Benomar, Yasushi Suto et al.","title":"The Misaligned Orbit of the Earth-sized Planet Kepler-408b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.02057","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"NGTS-20 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet came from a search for single transits in space-telescope data, followed up from the ground to catch more transits (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","observed":"Long-period transiting planets keep atmospheres little changed by their star, and their orbits reflect a less extreme migration history than close-in planets (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022). The planet's orbit is eccentric (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"The orbit may reflect gentler migration than hot Jupiters undergo (Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ngts20b-UlmerMolletal2022","cite":"Ulmer-Moll et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"S. Ulmer-Moll, M. Lendl, S. Gill et al.","title":"Two long-period transiting exoplanets on eccentric orbits: NGTS-20 b (TOI-5152 b) and TOI-5153 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.03911","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-52 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Keck detected neutral helium in the planet's atmosphere, absorbing 3.44% of the starlight and extending across 66 scale heights (Kirk et al. 2022). The planet loses about 0.5% of its mass per billion years (Kirk et al. 2022). Hubble and Spitzer spectra show a composition between 0.1 and 10 times solar with no sign of clouds or hazes (Bruno et al. 2020). The star is active, with dark spots that the planet crosses during transit (Bruno et al. 2020).","system":"The host is an active K dwarf with starspots covering about 5% of its surface (Bruno et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp52b-Kirketal2022","cite":"Kirk et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"James Kirk, Leonardo A. Dos Santos, Mercedes López-Morales et al.","title":"Keck/NIRSPEC Studies of He I in the Atmospheres of Two Inflated Hot Gas Giants Orbiting K Dwarfs: WASP-52b and WASP-177b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11579","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp52b-Brunoetal2020","cite":"Bruno et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Giovanni Bruno, Nikole K. Lewis, Munazza K. Alam et al.","title":"WASP-52b. The effect of star-spot correction on atmospheric retrievals","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05179","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":17,"about":8,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-11 g":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-11 holds six transiting planets, all closer to their star than Venus is to the Sun (Lissauer & Kepler Science Team 2011). A new study of the star found it is a young solar twin, which raised the planets' densities by 20% to 95% (Bedell et al. 2017). Planet g is only weakly coupled to the inner five and shows no transit timing shifts from outer neighbours (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2017).","system":"The inner five planets lie on near-circular, coplanar orbits within 0.25 AU, and g is the outermost (Quintana et al. 2011). There is a dynamical gap between f and g, and no Jupiter-mass planet orbits within 2 AU (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2017).","history":"Simulations tested whether such a compact system could form in place within a massive disc (Quintana et al. 2011).","open":"It is unclear whether the planets are rocky with thick hydrogen atmospheres or water-rich mini-Neptunes (Lopez et al. 2011)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler11g-Bedelletal2017","cite":"Bedell et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Megan Bedell, Jacob L. Bean, Jorge Meléndez et al.","title":"Kepler-11 is a Solar Twin: Revising the Masses and Radii of Benchmark Planets via Precise Stellar Characterization","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.06239","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11g-JontofHutteretal2017","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2017","year":"2017","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Brian P. Weaver, Eric B. Ford et al.","title":"Outer Architecture of Kepler-11: Constraints from Coplanarity","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.08829","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11g-Quintanaetal2011","cite":"Quintana et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Elisa V. Quintana, J. E. Chambers, J. J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-11: On the Formation of an Extremely Compact Six-Planet System","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kepler11g-Lopezetal2011","cite":"Lopez et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Eric Lopez, J. J. Fortney, N. Miller et al.","title":"Kepler 11: A System Of Super-Earths Or Mini-Neptunes?","journal":"AAS/Division for Extreme Solar Systems Abstracts","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":8,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"tau Boo b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Searches for light reflected from the planet found none, setting an upper limit that the albedo, the fraction of light reflected, is below 0.22 for an assumed size (Collier Cameron et al. 2000). The data also suggest the star's rotation is synchronised with the planet's orbit, and put its mass between 5.5 and 10 Jupiter masses (Collier Cameron et al. 2000). The star's magnetic field switches polarity with a cycle of only two years (Poppenhaeger 2011).","system":"The host has been studied for a chromospheric cycle of about 117 days in addition to its 3.3-day rotation (Maulik et al. 1997).","history":"The discovery paper suggested such close-in planets are a class with circular orbits (Butler et al. 1997).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-tauboob-Cameronetal2000","cite":"Cameron et al. 2000","year":"2000","authors":"Andrew Collier Cameron, Keith Horne, David James et al.","title":"tau Boo b: Not so bright, but just as heavy","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0012186","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tauboob-Poppenhaeger2011","cite":"Poppenhaeger 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Katja Poppenhaeger","title":"tau Boo's magnetic polarity switch in X-rays - continued monitoring","journal":"Chandra Proposal","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tauboob-Mauliketal1997","cite":"Maulik et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"Davesh Maulik, Robert A. Donahue, Sallie L. Baliunas","title":"Persistent Sub-Yearly Chromospheric Variations in Lower Main-Sequence Stars: Tau Booe and alpha Com","journal":"NASA Contractor Report","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-tauboob-Butleretal1997","cite":"Butler et al. 1997","year":"1997","authors":"R. Paul Butler, Geoffrey W. Marcy, Eric Williams et al.","title":"Three New ``51 Pegasi-Type'' Planets","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://doi.org/10.1086/310444","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":25,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HAT-P-3 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is smaller than expected for its mass, which implies about 75 Earth masses of heavy elements, roughly a third of its total mass (Torres et al. 2007). A later study confirmed that it is smaller than a pure hydrogen-helium planet (Chan et al. 2011). Spitzer measured the heat radiated by its day side, consistent with weak heat transfer to the night side and low reflectivity (Todorov et al. 2013).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether the atmosphere has a temperature inversion remains inconclusive (Todorov et al. 2013)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hatp3b-Torresetal2007","cite":"Torres et al. 2007","year":"2007","authors":"G. Torres, G. Á. Bakos, G. Kovács et al.","title":"HAT-P-3b: A Heavy-Element-rich Planet Transiting a K Dwarf Star","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0707.4268","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp3b-Chanetal2011","cite":"Chan et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"Tucker Chan, Mikael Ingemyr, Joshua N. Winn et al.","title":"The Transit Light-curve Project. XIV. Confirmation of Anomalous Radii for the Exoplanets TrES-4b, HAT-P-3b, and WASP-12b","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.3078","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hatp3b-Todorovetal2013","cite":"Todorov et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Kamen O. Todorov, Drake Deming, Heather A. Knutson et al.","title":"Warm Spitzer Photometry of Three Hot Jupiters: HAT-P-3b, HAT-P-4b and HAT-P-12b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.0833","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":15,"about":6,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-451 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The star belongs to a 120-million-year-old stream of stars, confirmed from its motion and lithium content (Newton et al. 2021). It shows an infrared excess that points to a debris disc (Newton et al. 2021). The system has three small planets that may still be evolving their atmospheres (Newton et al. 2021).","system":"A wide companion, TOI 451 B, is itself likely an M-dwarf binary (Newton et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi451b-Newtonetal2021","cite":"Newton et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Elisabeth R. Newton, Andrew W. Mann, Adam L. Kraus et al.","title":"TESS Hunt for Young and Maturing Exoplanets (THYME). IV. Three Small Planets Orbiting a 120 Myr Old Star in the Pisces-Eridanus Stream","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06049","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 9404 b":{"text":{"why":"A survey of M dwarfs with the HARPS-N spectrograph had listed it as a candidate (Baycroft et al. 2023).","observed":"Eccentric orbits are not sine curves, which affects how the false alarm probability is calculated in standard period searches (Baycroft et al. 2023). Re-analysis with Keplerian orbit functions shows the signal is a real planet with a highly eccentric orbit, detected with high confidence (Baycroft et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj9404b-Baycroftetal2023","cite":"Baycroft et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Thomas A. Baycroft, Harry Badnell, Samuel Blacker et al.","title":"GJ 9404 b: A Confirmed Eccentric Planet, and not a Candidate","journal":"Research Notes of the American Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12309","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 88986 b":{"text":{"why":"Transiting planets with periods longer than 40 days are extremely rare among the 5000+ known planets (Heidari et al. 2024).","observed":"The planet has the longest orbit among known small transiting planets, those under 4 Earth radii, with a precise mass (Heidari et al. 2024). More than 25 years of velocity data, plus TESS and CHEOPS, hint at two possible single transits that match the predicted timing (Heidari et al. 2024). The star is bright, with V = 6.5 (Heidari et al. 2024).","system":"A massive companion orbits further out (Heidari et al. 2024).","history":"","open":"The transit is not yet certain, only potential (Heidari et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd88986b-Heidarietal2024","cite":"Heidari et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"N. Heidari, I. Boisse, N. C. Hara et al.","title":"VizieR Online Data Catalog: HD88986 radial velocity curves (Heidari+, 2024)","journal":"VizieR Online Data Catalog","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1899 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a warm Jupiter orbiting an M dwarf, and the star is the lowest-mass one known to host a transiting warm Jupiter (Cañas et al. 2020). Later data refined its radius to 0.99 Jupiter radii, and its equilibrium temperature is about 380 K (Lin et al. 2023). Its orbit is consistent with circular at 2 sigma (Lin et al. 2023). It is the coolest known Jupiter-sized transiting planet around an M dwarf (Lin et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":"It could reveal how such gas giants form and migrate around small stars, through JWST spectra and studies of tides (Lin et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1899b-Caasetal2020","cite":"Cañas et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Caleb I. Cañas, Gudmundur Stefansson, Shubham Kanodia et al.","title":"A Warm Jupiter Transiting an M Dwarf: A TESS Single-transit Event Confirmed with the Habitable-zone Planet Finder","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07098","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi1899b-Linetal2023","cite":"Lin et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Andrea S. J. Lin, Jessica E. Libby-Roberts, Jaime A. Alvarado-Montes et al.","title":"The Unusual M-dwarf Warm Jupiter TOI-1899 b: Refinement of Orbital and Planetary Parameters","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10837","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":0,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-403 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was not confirmed by measuring its mass directly. Instead it was validated, meaning shown to be very probably a real planet, by combining reconnaissance spectra, high-resolution imaging, checks of how the star's image shifts during transits, and a statistical likelihood analysis (Christiansen et al. 2022). It is one of 60 new planets validated together in that work. The authors single it out as a good target for transmission spectroscopy, where starlight filtered through a planet's atmosphere is studied during transit (Christiansen et al. 2022).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2403b-Christiansenetal2022","cite":"Christiansen et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Jessie L. Christiansen, Sakhee Bhure, Jon K. Zink et al.","title":"Scaling K2. V. Statistical Validation of 60 New Exoplanets From K2 Campaigns 2─18","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02087","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-4 b":{"text":{"why":"It was the first planet found by the SuperWASP-South and CORALIE collaboration, and the first around a star brighter than 16th magnitude found in the southern hemisphere (Wilson et al. 2008).","observed":"Its predicted surface temperature of 1761 K made it a good candidate for detecting the secondary eclipse, when the planet passes behind its star, in infrared light (Wilson et al. 2008). Years later, transit times seemed to show the 1.3 day orbit shrinking: TESS transits came 81.6 seconds earlier than predicted (Bouma et al. 2019). Radial velocity data then showed the whole system accelerating toward the Sun, which can mimic a shrinking period (Bouma et al. 2020).","system":"A distant, unseen companion is the likely cause of the acceleration. One study favours a body of about 7 Jupiter masses at about 8 au (Winn & Stefánsson 2025). Another allows 10 to 300 Jupiter masses at 10 to 100 au (Bouma et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Whether any true tidal orbital decay is happening is debated. One study finds no need for decay (Winn & Stefánsson 2025), while another still finds a slow trend that could be tidal (Baluev et al. 2020)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp4b-Wilsonetal2008","cite":"Wilson et al. 2008","year":"2008","authors":"D. M. Wilson, M. Gillon, C. Hellier et al.","title":"WASP-4b: A 12th Magnitude Transiting Hot Jupiter in the Southern Hemisphere","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.1509","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp4b-Boumaetal2019","cite":"Bouma et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"L. G. Bouma, J. N. Winn, C. Baxter et al.","title":"WASP-4b Arrived Early for the TESS  Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02573","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp4b-Boumaetal2020","cite":"Bouma et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. G. Bouma, J. N. Winn, A. W. Howard et al.","title":"WASP-4 Is Accelerating toward the Earth","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.00637","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp4b-WinnStefnsson2025","cite":"Winn & Stefánsson 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Joshua N. Winn, Guđmundur Stefánsson","title":"Orbital Decay Candidates Reconsidered: WASP-4 b Is Not Decaying and Kepler-1658 b Is Not a Planet","journal":"The Planetary Science Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05229","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-wasp4b-Baluevetal2020","cite":"Baluev et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"R. V. Baluev, E. N. Sokov, S. Hoyer et al.","title":"WASP-4 transit timing variation from a comprehensive set of 129 transits","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09109","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":39,"about":11,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-43 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was announced together with three others of similar size but very different masses, so their densities vary widely (Brahm et al. 2018). Its orbit appears to be slightly non-circular (Brahm et al. 2018). Its star is moderately bright, and the expected transmission signal of 2350 ppm makes it a particularly good target for studying its atmosphere as it passes in front of the star (Brahm et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats43b-Brahmetal2018","cite":"Brahm et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Brahm, J. D. Hartman, A. Jordán et al.","title":"HATS-43b, HATS-44b, HATS-45b, and HATS-46b: Four Short-period Transiting Giant Planets in the Neptune-Jupiter Mass Range","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.07093","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-15 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is a G0 star near the blue hook, a short stage of evolution before the star swells toward a giant, and the planet is an inflated hot Jupiter, larger than its mass would suggest (Rodriguez et al. 2016). A later search of TESS space-telescope data found no additional transiting planets down to about 2 to 4 Earth radii in this and 11 other hot Jupiter systems (Maciejewski et al. 2023).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt15b-Rodriguezetal2016","cite":"Rodriguez et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Joseph E. Rodriguez, Knicole D. Colón, Keivan G. Stassun et al.","title":"KELT-14b and KELT-15b: An Independent Discovery of WASP-122b and a New Hot Jupiter","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.08953","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt15b-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":1,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-4 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is among the densest known planets with masses between 1 and 2 Jupiter masses (Jordán et al. 2014). Its host star is very rich in elements heavier than helium, with [Fe/H] of 0.43, and the planet is likely to hold about 75 Earth masses of heavy elements (Jordán et al. 2014). The discovery paper also describes how the HATSouth survey reduced and used spectra from the CORALIE spectrograph to confirm such planets (Jordán et al. 2014).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats4b-Jordnetal2014","cite":"Jordán et al. 2014","year":"2014","authors":"Andrés Jordán, Rafael Brahm, G. Á. Bakos et al.","title":"HATS-4b: A Dense Hot Jupiter Transiting a Super Metal-rich G star","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.6546","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 142022 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Six years of Doppler measurements covered only one full orbit, and the closest approach to the star was missed because the star was too low on the horizon (Eggenberger et al. 2006). The orbit's eccentricity and the strength of the star's wobble are therefore uncertain, and the solution is preliminary (Eggenberger et al. 2006). The host is a metal-rich K dwarf with little magnetic activity (Eggenberger et al. 2006).","system":"The host is the main star of a wide binary, which makes this a candidate planet in a binary (Eggenberger et al. 2006).","history":"The planet's high eccentricity might come from secular interactions, slow long-term gravitational tugs, with the distant stellar companion (Eggenberger et al. 2006).","open":"The true eccentricity is still uncertain (Eggenberger et al. 2006)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd142022ab-Eggenbergeretal2006","cite":"Eggenberger et al. 2006","year":"2006","authors":"A. Eggenberger, M. Mayor, D. Naef et al.","title":"The CORALIE survey for southern extrasolar planets. XIV. HD 142022 b: a long-period planetary companion in a wide binary","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0510561","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-1661 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet orbits two stars, and its orbit precesses, or turns around, with a period of only 35 years (Socia et al. 2020). This changes the tilt of its orbit relative to the stars' orbit, so it is seen crossing the stars only about 7% of the time from Earth (Socia et al. 2020). Like several other Kepler planets around two stars, it orbits close to the radius inside which orbits would be unstable, and near the hot edge of the habitable zone (Socia et al. 2020).","system":"The stars are 0.84 and 0.26 solar masses, circling each other every 28.2 days in a slightly eccentric orbit (Socia et al. 2020). They eclipse each other only by grazing, and starspots on them affect the measured eclipse depths (Socia et al. 2020). The system is about 1 to 3 billion years old (Socia et al. 2020).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler1661b-Sociaetal2020","cite":"Socia et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Quentin J. Socia, William F. Welsh, Jerome A. Orosz et al.","title":"Kepler-1661 b: A Neptune-sized Kepler Transiting Circumbinary Planet around a Grazing Eclipsing Binary","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.02840","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HATS-9 b":{"text":{"why":"The survey field lay in the planned footprint of the K2 mission, though only this planet's star fell on working detector area (Brahm et al. 2015).","observed":"The planet is compact compared with other planets receiving similar starlight, and its star is old, about 10.8 billion years (Brahm et al. 2015). A search of K2 space-telescope data later found its phase curve, the small brightening and dimming as its day and night sides rotate into view (Niraula et al. 2018). A mass from that signal agreed with the radial velocity mass within 1 sigma, and the planet reflects comparatively little light (Niraula et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hats9b-Brahmetal2015","cite":"Brahm et al. 2015","year":"2015","authors":"R. Brahm, A. Jordán, J. D. Hartman et al.","title":"HATS9-b and HATS10-b: Two Compact Hot Jupiters in Field 7 of the K2 Mission","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00062","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hats9b-Niraulaetal2018","cite":"Niraula et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"Prajwal Niraula, Seth Redfield, Julien de Wit et al.","title":"Discovery of Six Optical Phase Curves with K2","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.09227","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-185 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The orbit is eccentric, with e of 0.24, and long for a hot Jupiter at 9.4 days (Hellier et al. 2019). The host is an older G0 star that has left the main sequence and grown large, so transits are shallow, about 0.7 to 0.9 per cent, even though the planet is moderately inflated (Hellier et al. 2019). A faint star 4.6 arcseconds away might be physically associated with it (Hellier et al. 2019).","system":"A faint star 4.4 magnitudes dimmer than the host may be a bound companion (Hellier et al. 2019).","history":"","open":"It is not settled whether the nearby faint star is a true companion (Hellier et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp185b-Hellieretal2019","cite":"Hellier et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Coel Hellier, D. R. Anderson, K. Barkaoui et al.","title":"WASP-South hot Jupiters: WASP-178b, WASP-184b, WASP-185b, and WASP-192b","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11667","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"WASP-194 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is also known as HAT-P-71 b, so two surveys flagged it independently (Schanche et al. 2025). It is one of nine giant planets confirmed together: WASP candidates were weighed with radial velocity measurements, then refined with TESS space photometry and ground-based follow-up (Schanche et al. 2025). The study reports that the nine planets have radii of 0.9 to 1.4 Jupiter radii and orbital periods of 1.3 to 6.6 days (Schanche et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wasp194b-Schancheetal2025","cite":"Schanche et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"N. Schanche, G. Hébrard, K. G. Stassun et al.","title":"A Swarm of WASP Planets: Nine Giant Planets Identified by the WASP Survey","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08091","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KELT-2 A b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is the bright primary star of the HD 42176 binary, a slightly evolved late F star at a brief stage of evolution called the blue hook (Beatty et al. 2012). At discovery it was the third brightest star with a transiting planet found by ground-based surveys (Beatty et al. 2012). The planet is mildly inflated compared with models of irradiated giants at the system's age (Beatty et al. 2012). A recent measurement of the Rossiter-McLaughlin effect, the distortion of starlight as the planet crosses the spinning star, finds its orbit aligned with the star's spin (Polanski et al. 2026).","system":"The planet orbits the primary of a binary, and the motion of the second star was measured over many years (Beatty et al. 2012).","history":"","open":"A short-period shift in transit times has the traits of the so-called exomoon corridor, but the authors say more observations are needed (Maciejewski et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kelt2ab-Beattyetal2012","cite":"Beatty et al. 2012","year":"2012","authors":"Thomas G. Beatty, Joshua Pepper, Robert J. Siverd et al.","title":"KELT-2Ab: A Hot Jupiter Transiting the Bright (V = 8.77) Primary Star of a Binary System","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.1592","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt2ab-Polanskietal2026","cite":"Polanski et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alex S. Polanski, Malena Rice, Catherine A. Clark et al.","title":"CHIANTE I: Obliquity Measurements of Four High-Priority Ariel Targets in Binaries","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23662","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-kelt2ab-Maciejewskietal2023","cite":"Maciejewski et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"G. Maciejewski, J. Sierzputowska, J. Golonka","title":"Search for Planets in Hot Jupiter Systems with Multi-Sector TESS Photometry. IV. Null Detections in 12 Systems","journal":"Acta Astronomica","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03319","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"KMT-2024-BLG-0404L b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The event's light curve had a complex shape with two sharp spikes, two bumps and a brief break between the spikes. A two-body lens explained most of it but not the break, which needed a third component of planetary mass (Han et al. 2025). The result is a planet of about 17 Earth masses, a Uranus-like planet, orbiting a pair made of a late M dwarf and a brown dwarf (Han et al. 2025). The system lies about 7.2 kiloparsecs away (Han et al. 2025).","system":"The planet orbits a binary of a late M dwarf and a brown dwarf, an object too light to be a true star (Han et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"All three masses are uncertain by large factors (Han et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kmt2024blg0404lb-Hanetal2025","cite":"Han et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"Cheongho Han, Andrzej Udalski, Chung-Uk Lee et al.","title":"KMT-2024-BLG-0404L: A triple microlensing system consisting of a star, a brown dwarf, and a planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.07326","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-559 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet is a massive giant of about 6 Jupiter masses on a slightly eccentric 6.98 day orbit around an older G dwarf (Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2022). A later Rossiter-McLaughlin measurement, which reads the tilt of the orbit from distorted starlight, found its orbit aligned with the star's spin (Wang 2024). It was one of six warm Jupiters whose alignment suggests that, for single stars, misalignment is not universal or primordial (Wang 2024).","system":"","history":"Alignment around a single star argues against some theories of how giant planets end up on tilted orbits (Wang 2024).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi559b-IkwutUkwaetal2022","cite":"Ikwut-Ukwa et al. 2022","year":"2022","authors":"Mma Ikwut-Ukwa, Joseph E. Rodriguez, Samuel N. Quinn et al.","title":"Two Massive Jupiters in Eccentric Orbits from the TESS Full-frame Images","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.02222","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi559b-Wang2024","cite":"Wang 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Xian-Yu Wang","title":"Single-Star Warm-Jupiter Systems Tend to Be Aligned, Even Around Hot Stars","journal":"TESS Science Conference III","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13629411","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":0,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-5817 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery came from the HONEI programme, which uses the HARPS and HARPS-N spectrographs to measure masses of TESS Neptune-sized candidates so they can be studied with the James Webb Space Telescope (Naponiello et al. 2025).","observed":"The planet is one of two hot sub-Neptunes confirmed in that paper (Naponiello et al. 2025). It lies on a farther-out circular orbit, in contrast with TOI-5800 b, which is closer in and eccentric (Naponiello et al. 2025). Both help test ideas about the Neptune desert, a region with few short-period Neptune-sized planets (Naponiello et al. 2025).","system":"","history":"","open":"How Neptune-sized planets formed and evolved is still poorly understood (Naponiello et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi5817b-Naponielloetal2025","cite":"Naponiello et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"L. Naponiello, S. Vissapragada, A. S. Bonomo et al.","title":"The Hot-Neptune Initiative (HONEI): I. Two hot sub-Neptunes on a close-in eccentric orbit (TOI-5800 b) and a farther-out circular orbit (TOI-5817 b)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10123","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-969 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet was inferred from radial velocity data, taken with HARPS, PFS and CORALIE over more than two years, which favour a second massive body in the system (Lillo-Box et al. 2023). It is described as an eccentric cold Jupiter, far from its star compared with the inner planet (Lillo-Box et al. 2023). The inner planet is a mini-Neptune at the lower edge of the hot-Neptune desert (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","system":"The inner planet, TOI-969 b, orbits every 1.82 days and is volatile-rich (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","history":"The inner planet's makeup suggests it migrated inward, and the study treats the system's layout as a clue to its past (Lillo-Box et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi969c-LilloBoxetal2023","cite":"Lillo-Box et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"J. Lillo-Box, D. Gandolfi, D. J. Armstrong et al.","title":"TOI-969: a late-K dwarf with a hot mini-Neptune in the desert and an eccentric cold Jupiter","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08996","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-308 b":{"text":{"why":"The star was first thought to belong to the 1 billion year old open cluster NGC 1817, based on its motion and distance, which made the planet a rare candidate for a giant planet in a cluster (Rampalli et al. 2019).","observed":"New Gaia DR2 motions and a measured radial velocity showed the star is not in the cluster (Rampalli et al. 2019). Three stars fell inside the K2 aperture, so the authors used seeing-limited photometry to find which one hosts the planet, then ruled out false alarms with adaptive optics imaging and radial velocities (Rampalli et al. 2019). The false-positive probability is 0.01 per cent (Rampalli et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"The search for a giant transiting planet in an open cluster continues (Rampalli et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2308b-Rampallietal2019","cite":"Rampalli et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Rayna Rampalli, Andrew Vanderburg, Allyson Bieryla et al.","title":"A Hot Saturn Near (but Unassociated with) the Open Cluster NGC 1817","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.02395","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-136 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is a K dwarf in the Hyades cluster, about 700 million years old, with three transiting planets (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). X-ray measurements from XMM-Newton show the star is less active than models predicted, but typical of Hyades stars (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). Because the three planets share the same history of high-energy radiation, they can be compared to test models of atmosphere loss (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023). Planet d needs a mass near the top of its allowed range to have kept any gas envelope (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023).","system":"The star has an M dwarf companion about 40 au away, projected on the sky (Ciardi et al. 2018). The planets are about 1.0, 3.0 and 1.5 Earth radii, and the middle one sits above the radius valley, the gap in planet sizes (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023).","history":"The study finds all three planets could have begun with gas envelopes, with the innermost losing it within a few million years (Fernández Fernández et al. 2023).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2136d-Fernndezetal2023","cite":"Fernández et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Jorge Fernández Fernández, Peter J. Wheatley, George W. King","title":"The shared evaporation history of three sub-Neptunes spanning the radius-period valley of a hyades star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.12705","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-k2136d-Ciardietal2018","cite":"Ciardi et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"David R. Ciardi, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Adina D. Feinstein et al.","title":"K2-136: A Binary System in the Hyades Cluster Hosting a Neptune-sized Planet","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.10398","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 7199 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet was found in a HARPS paper about stars with solar-like magnetic cycles, so the work was as much about handling stellar activity as about the planets (Dumusque et al. 2011).","observed":"The host star shows clear signs of magnetic activity, including slow cycles like the Sun's, yet the planet was detected anyway (Dumusque et al. 2011). Such cycles cause slow drifts in the measured radial velocity, and the authors propose removing them by fitting the activity index and applying the same fit to the velocities (Dumusque et al. 2011). The planet's orbit has low eccentricity, under 0.4 (Dumusque et al. 2011).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd7199b-Dumusqueetal2011","cite":"Dumusque et al. 2011","year":"2011","authors":"X. Dumusque, C. Lovis, D. Ségransan et al.","title":"The HARPS search for southern extra-solar planets. XXX. Planetary systems around stars with solar-like magnetic cycles and short-term activity variation","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1107.1748","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"EPIC 249893012 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet appears on a shortlist of planets that may be losing their atmospheres right now, if core-powered mass loss, where heat from the planet's own core drives gas away, shapes planet sizes (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). Their models give a loss rate of at least 10 million grams per second at 50 per cent confidence (Gupta & Schlichting 2021). This is a prediction from published masses, radii and star properties, not a direct observation of escaping gas (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","system":"","history":"The mass-loss idea is used to explain the radius valley, the gap between super-Earths and sub-Neptunes (Gupta & Schlichting 2021).","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-epic249893012b-GuptaSchlichting2021","cite":"Gupta & Schlichting 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Akash Gupta, Hilke E. Schlichting","title":"Caught in the act: core-powered mass-loss predictions for observing atmospheric escape","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.08785","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-50 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-50 has two transiting planets close to a 6:5 orbital ratio, and their transit times drift in a way the pair alone cannot explain (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). The authors attribute the extra signal to a third planet that does not transit and lies near resonance (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). Fits to the transit times give masses of 2.35 Earth masses for planet b and 4.43 for planet c (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026). About 68 per cent of stable solutions have the pair locked in resonance (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026).","system":"Both planets are small and lie in the radius valley, the gap in planet sizes (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026).","history":"","open":"The third planet's properties are degenerate, meaning many different solutions fit equally well (Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler50c-JontofHutteretal2026","cite":"Jontof-Hutter et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Daniel Jontof-Hutter, Jason F. Rowe, Jack J. Lissauer et al.","title":"Kepler-50: Two Planets in Close Resonance Perturbed by a Non-transiting Planet","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25035","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"XO-7 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The discovery came from a team of amateur and professional astronomers who ran photometric and radial velocity follow-up together (Crouzet et al. 2020). The star is bright and the planet's atmosphere is puffed up, so the system is well suited to studying the planet's air (Crouzet et al. 2020). The star's motion also shows a slow drift of about 100 metres per second over two years, which points to a second, more distant body (Crouzet et al. 2020).","system":"The distant companion has a minimum mass of 4 Jupiter masses and could be a planet, a brown dwarf or a low-mass star (Crouzet et al. 2020). The host star is rich in heavy elements (Crouzet et al. 2020).","history":"The close orbit of the hot Jupiter and the wide companion together suggest the planet was pushed inward by a stretched-out, high-eccentricity path (Crouzet et al. 2020).","open":"Later radial velocities did not confirm the drift and showed only a marginal slope the other way, so the companion is unproven (Garai et al. 2024). If it exists, its orbit is probably longer than about 7900 days (Garai et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-xo7b-Crouzetetal2020","cite":"Crouzet et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Nicolas Crouzet, Brian F. Healy, Guillaume Hébrard et al.","title":"XO-7 b: A Transiting Hot Jupiter with a Massive Companion on a Wide Orbit","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02821","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-xo7b-Garaietal2024","cite":"Garai et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Z. Garai, T. Pribulla, R. Komžík","title":"Search for the wide-orbit massive companion of XO-7b in the follow-up radial-velocity and transit-timing data: no significant clues","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01402","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":1,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"VHS J125601.92-125723.9 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Hubble watched this planet for 8.5 hours and saw its near-infrared brightness change by 19.3 percent, and by 24.7 percent at one wavelength, which rivals the most variable brown dwarf known (Bowler et al. 2020). The brightness was still rising when the run ended, so the true swing is larger (Bowler et al. 2020). The implied spin of about 21 to 24 hours is unusually slow for such objects (Bowler et al. 2020). Deep radio and millimetre images found no sign of a dust disc around it (Zakhozhay et al. 2023).","system":"The planet orbits a close pair of brown dwarfs or very low-mass stars that circle each other every 7.31 years on a very elongated path (Dupuy et al. 2023). Their combined mass is 0.141 solar masses (Dupuy et al. 2023).","history":"The planet's orbit is also eccentric and tilted by 115 degrees against the pair, which fits a violent past of gravitational scattering (Dupuy et al. 2023). That tilt could in turn have stretched the pair's own orbit (Dupuy et al. 2023).","open":"At an age of about 140 million years, its mass sits on the borderline where deuterium fusion starts, so it is not clear whether it is a planet or a brown dwarf (Dupuy et al. 2023)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-vhsj125601921257239b-Bowleretal2020","cite":"Bowler et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"Brendan P. Bowler, Yifan Zhou, Caroline V. Morley et al.","title":"Strong Near-infrared Spectral Variability of the Young Cloudy L Dwarf Companion VHS J1256-1257 b","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.05170","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-vhsj125601921257239b-Zakhozhayetal2023","cite":"Zakhozhay et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Olga V. Zakhozhay, María Rosa Zapatero Osorio, Víctor J. S. Béjar et al.","title":"New constraints on the presence of debris disks around G 196-3 B and VHS J125601.92-125723.9 b","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.12163","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-vhsj125601921257239b-Dupuyetal2023","cite":"Dupuy et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Trent J. Dupuy, Michael C. Liu, Elise L. Evans et al.","title":"On the masses, age, and architecture of the VHS J1256-1257AB b system","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.08448","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":6,"about":0,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-283 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"TESS flagged this planet as a candidate in 2019, and a long mass-measuring campaign with the ESPRESSO spectrograph followed (Murgas et al. 2025). The host is a bright K-type star, and the planet's density of 2.81 grams per cubic centimetre was measured (Murgas et al. 2025). With a temperature near 600 K, it sits where either water-rich or hydrogen-and-helium-rich outer layers would be expected (Murgas et al. 2025). The star's motion also shows a slow long-term trend (Murgas et al. 2025).","system":"The trend is probably caused by stellar activity, but it could come from an additional companion (Murgas et al. 2025).","history":"","open":"The mass and size alone do not say whether the envelope is water or hydrogen-rich (Murgas et al. 2025)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi283b-Murgasetal2025","cite":"Murgas et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"F. Murgas, E. Pallé, A. Suárez Mascareño et al.","title":"TOI-283 b: A transiting mini-Neptune in a 17.6-day orbit discovered with TESS and ESPRESSO","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15084","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":2,"about":2,"read":2},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-6158 b":{"text":{"why":"The planet comes from a search for giant exoplanets around M dwarfs, called GEMS (O'Brien et al. 2026).","observed":"This is one of two giant planets around M2 dwarf stars confirmed in the same study (O'Brien et al. 2026). Both have about the density of Saturn (O'Brien et al. 2026). Ground-based brightness measurements from the Swope telescope and radial velocities from the Habitable-zone Planet Finder confirmed it (O'Brien et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi6158b-OBrienetal2026","cite":"O'Brien et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Shane O'Brien, Amber Wong, Te Han et al.","title":"Searching for GEMS: TOI-5916 b & TOI-6158 b are Two Saturn-density Planets Orbiting M2 Dwarfs","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11798","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-837 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planet and its star belong to the young cluster IC 2602 and are only about 35 million years old (Bouma et al. 2020). The transits just graze the edge of the star, which usually raises fears of a false alarm, but follow-up data made those unlikely (Bouma et al. 2020). Later radial velocities gave a mass of about 0.38 Jupiter masses and showed a heavy core of roughly 70 Earth masses, about 60 percent of the planet (Barragán et al. 2024).","system":"","history":"The planet's large core is hard to explain with current core-formation models (Barragán et al. 2024). Population simulations were used to trace its formation and possible interior (Damasso et al. 2024). The planet is a snapshot of a system soon after birth, when evolution is still under way (Damasso et al. 2024).","open":"Its atmosphere and how much it loses to evaporation are open questions, and JWST could help (Damasso et al. 2024)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi837b-Boumaetal2020","cite":"Bouma et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"L. G. Bouma, J. D. Hartman, R. Brahm et al.","title":"Cluster Difference Imaging Photometric Survey. II. TOI 837: A Young Validated Planet in IC 2602","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.07845","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi837b-Barragnetal2024","cite":"Barragán et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Oscar Barragán, Haochuan Yu, Alix Violet Freckelton et al.","title":"TOI-837 b is a young Saturn-sized exoplanet with a massive 70 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> core","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13750","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-toi837b-Damassoetal2024","cite":"Damasso et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"M. Damasso, D. Polychroni, D. Locci et al.","title":"TOI-837 b: Characterisation, formation, and evolutionary history of an infant warm Saturn-mass planet","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08949","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":4,"about":3,"read":4},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-82 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"Kepler-82 c and its neighbour b pull on each other near a 2:1 orbital resonance, which makes their transit times swing back and forth in a smooth wave (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Ground-based telescopes of a global network added new transit data to complete these timing curves (Freudenthal et al. 2019). Modelling both together gave the first dynamical analysis of the system (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","system":"A smaller, fast jitter in the timing of Kepler-82 c hinted at a further planet near the 3:2 or 3:1 resonance (Freudenthal et al. 2019). The model found a non-transiting planet of about 21 Earth masses, named Kepler-82 f (Freudenthal et al. 2019).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler82c-Freudenthaletal2019","cite":"Freudenthal et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"J. Freudenthal, C. von Essen, A. Ofir et al.","title":"Kepler Object of Interest Network. III. Kepler-82f: a new non-transiting 21 M<SUB>⊕</SUB> planet from photodynamical modelling","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.06534","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Wolf 1061 d":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The planets of this system were found by re-examining archived spectra with a custom template tailored to the star (Wright et al. 2016). This removed the strong yearly wobble found in the standard velocities and could help dig more small planets out of old data (Wright et al. 2016). No activity signals appeared near the planetary periods, which supports the planetary reading (Wright et al. 2016).","system":"The star hosts three planets of low mass that may all be rocky (Wright et al. 2016). One of them lies in the star's habitable zone, and another sits just outside its outer edge (Wright et al. 2016).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-wolf1061d-Wrightetal2016","cite":"Wright et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"D. J. Wright, R. A. Wittenmyer, C. G. Tinney et al.","title":"Three Planets Orbiting Wolf 1061","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.05154","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"Kepler-441 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A re-check of small, long-period Kepler planets found that their statistical confirmation is shakier than first thought (Burke et al. 2019). Allowing for processing variations, several single-planet systems, including Kepler-441 b, are named as no longer clearly passing the 99 percent confidence mark (Burke et al. 2019). The authors say it is hard to prove any one such candidate is a real planet rather than a false alarm from systematic noise (Burke et al. 2019).","system":"","history":"","open":"Whether Kepler-441 b is a real planet is less certain than when it was announced (Burke et al. 2019)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-kepler441b-Burkeetal2019","cite":"Burke et al. 2019","year":"2019","authors":"Christopher J. Burke, F. Mullally, Susan E. Thompson et al.","title":"Re-evaluating Small Long-period Confirmed Planets from Kepler","journal":"The Astronomical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.00506","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-4940 b":{"text":{"why":"The discovery paper notes that transiting gas giants are scarce beyond 10-day orbits, and long orbits make good tests of how planets form and migrate (Kendall et al. 2026).","observed":"This planet is part of a trio of long-period planets, a Neptune and two Saturns, reported together (Kendall et al. 2026). Each first showed up as a single transit in TESS data and was pinned down by extra transits seen from the ground with NGTS and ASTEP (Kendall et al. 2026). Radial velocities from CORALIE and HARPS then confirmed the masses (Kendall et al. 2026).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi4940b-Kendalletal2026","cite":"Kendall et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Alicia Kendall, Solène Ulmer-Moll, Samuel Gill et al.","title":"A 43 d transiting Neptune and two 25 d Saturns from TESS, NGTS, and ASTEP","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07716","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-9 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"A second team independently confirmed this planet using spectra and sharp adaptive-optics images of the host star, which is a nearby M dwarf at about 110 parsecs (Schlieder et al. 2016). It was first spotted by an earlier team (Schlieder et al. 2016). The planet and its sibling in the same study both receive low incident flux from their cool stars (Schlieder et al. 2016).","system":"","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k29b-Schliederetal2016","cite":"Schlieder et al. 2016","year":"2016","authors":"Joshua E. Schlieder, Ian J. M. Crossfield, Erik A. Petigura et al.","title":"Two Small Temperate Planets Transiting Nearby M Dwarfs in K2 Campaigns 0 and 1","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02706","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":22,"about":2,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"K2-260 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"This is the first K2 hot Jupiter with a secondary eclipse, the dip when the planet goes behind its star, seen in the Kepler band (Johnson et al. 2018). The eclipse is only 71 parts per million deep, which suggests the planet reflects about 20 percent of light that hits it (Johnson et al. 2018). The host is a slightly evolved F star with a radius of 1.69 solar radii (Johnson et al. 2018).","system":"A faint candidate companion star lies 0.6 arcseconds away and is very likely physically tied to the system, as an M5 to M6 dwarf about 400 au away (Johnson et al. 2018).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-k2260b-Johnsonetal2018","cite":"Johnson et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"M. C. Johnson, F. Dai, A. B. Justesen et al.","title":"K2-260 b: a hot Jupiter transiting an F star, and K2-261 b: a warm Saturn around a bright G star","journal":"Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06099","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-1203 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host star is about 12.5 billion years old and enriched in alpha elements, marking it as a member of the old thick disc of the Galaxy (Gandolfi et al. 2025). Planet b is a transiting super-Earth that was newly discovered in this work (Gandolfi et al. 2025). A long HARPS and CHEOPS campaign revealed it in a system that already had one known transiting sub-Neptune (Gandolfi et al. 2025).","system":"The system holds four planets, with orbits of 4.2, 13.1, 25.5 and 204.6 days (Gandolfi et al. 2025). The outer planet follows a mildly eccentric orbit, and a stability analysis supports the arrangement (Gandolfi et al. 2025).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi1203b-Gandolfietal2025","cite":"Gandolfi et al. 2025","year":"2025","authors":"D. Gandolfi, A. Alnajjarine, L. M. Serrano et al.","title":"A four-planet system orbiting the old thick disk star TOI-1203","journal":"arXiv e-prints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10136","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":0,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"HD 120084 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is an evolved giant star of about 2.4 solar masses (Sato et al. 2013). Its planet has one of the most eccentric orbits found around such stars, at 0.66, while almost all others are below 0.4 (Sato et al. 2013). Starquake measurements from TESS showed for the first time that the host is a red clump star, one burning helium in its core (Lin et al. 2024).","system":"Combining radial velocities with the star's astrometric motion helped fix the orbit more tightly (Teng et al. 2023).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-hd120084b-Satoetal2013","cite":"Sato et al. 2013","year":"2013","authors":"Bun'ei Sato, Masashi Omiya, Hiroki Harakawa et al.","title":"Planetary Companions to Three Evolved Intermediate-Mass Stars: HD 2952, HD 120084, and ω Serpentis","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.4328","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd120084b-Linetal2024","cite":"Lin et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Wen-Xu Lin, Sheng-Bang Qian, Li-Ying Zhu","title":"Revealing the Fate of Exoplanet Systems: Asteroseismic Identification of Host Star in the Red Clump or Red Giant Branch","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04947","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-hd120084b-Tengetal2023","cite":"Teng et al. 2023","year":"2023","authors":"Huan-Yu Teng, Bun'ei Sato, Masayuki Kuzuhara et al.","title":"Revisiting planetary systems in the Okayama Planet Search Program: A new long-period planet, RV astrometry joint analysis, and a multiplicity-metallicity trend around evolved stars","journal":"Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.05343","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":3,"about":1,"read":3},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"GJ 1265 b":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is a cool M4.5 dwarf, and the planet is almost a twin of GJ 3779 b around another M dwarf (Luque et al. 2018). Both have low-eccentricity orbits of about 3 to 4 days and minimum masses near 7 to 8 Earth masses (Luque et al. 2018). A separate 95-day signal in the data comes from the star's rotation, not a planet (Luque et al. 2018).","system":"","history":"","open":"Adding these planets to a mass-period diagram for M dwarfs suggests a bimodal spread, with a lack of short-period planets of 2 to 5 Earth masses (Luque et al. 2018)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-gj1265b-Luqueetal2018","cite":"Luque et al. 2018","year":"2018","authors":"R. Luque, G. Nowak, E. Pallé et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. The warm super-Earths in twin orbits around the mid-type M dwarfs Ross 1020 (GJ 3779) and LP 819-052 (GJ 1265)","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.07572","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"TOI-682.01":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"The host is the G star HD 97260 (Quinn et al. 2021). Radial velocities from the Planet Finder Spectrograph confirmed the planet and gave its mass (Quinn et al. 2021). Its equilibrium temperature of 950 K lies where few Neptune-sized atmospheres have been studied (Quinn et al. 2021). The large transit depth, modest density and bright star should give clear transmission spectra (Quinn et al. 2021).","system":"A second signal at 16.1 days comes from a non-transiting planet of at least 9.6 Earth masses (Quinn et al. 2021). Both planets show clearly non-zero eccentricities and interact dynamically (Quinn et al. 2021).","history":"","open":""},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-toi68201-Quinnetal2021","cite":"Quinn et al. 2021","year":"2021","authors":"Samuel N. Quinn, Jonathan Horner, Karen A. Collins et al.","title":"TOI-682: two mini-Neptunes, one transiting","journal":"Posters from the TESS Science Conference II (TSC2)","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5131353","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":1,"about":1,"read":1},"researched":"2026-11-20"},"LTT 3780 c":{"text":{"why":"","observed":"LTT 3780 c and its inner partner sit on opposite sides of the radius gap, the dip in the number of planets between rocky and puffy sizes (Nowak et al. 2020). Its star is a nearby M dwarf about 22 parsecs away (Nowak et al. 2020). A ground-based infrared spectrum of one transit gave marginal evidence of methane at 2.2 sigma (Cabot et al. 2024). A JWST spectrum from 0.9 to 12 micrometres then showed strong evidence for methane in a hydrogen-dominated atmosphere at about 1 percent, with no sign of ammonia (Rigby et al. 2026).","system":"The inner planet is an ultra-short-period world with a rocky density (Nowak et al. 2020).","history":"","open":"Constraints on water, carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide are only nominal so far (Rigby et al. 2026)."},"refs":[{"key":"ctx-ltt3780c-Nowaketal2020","cite":"Nowak et al. 2020","year":"2020","authors":"G. Nowak, R. Luque, H. Parviainen et al.","title":"The CARMENES search for exoplanets around M dwarfs. Two planets on opposite sides of the radius gap transiting the nearby M dwarf LTT 3780","journal":"Astronomy and Astrophysics","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.01140","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt3780c-Cabotetal2024","cite":"Cabot et al. 2024","year":"2024","authors":"Samuel H. C. Cabot, Nikku Madhusudhan, Savvas Constantinou et al.","title":"High-resolution Spectroscopic Reconnaissance of a Temperate Sub-Neptune","journal":"The Astrophysical Journal Letters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.18891","kind":"paper","cited":true},{"key":"ctx-ltt3780c-Rigbyetal2026","cite":"Rigby et al. 2026","year":"2026","authors":"Frances Rigby, Nikku Madhusudhan, Subhajit Sarkar et al.","title":"A JWST Transmission Spectrum of the Temperate Sub-Neptune TOI-732 c","journal":"AASTCS 11: Exoplanet Atmospheres 2026","url":"","kind":"paper","cited":true}],"counts":{"found":7,"about":1,"read":6},"researched":"2026-11-20"}};
